ok me out to the country, I managed to pee whilst keeping my pants mostly on, and so I was very happy.  (I figured all you swinney people would like to know that, just as I posted it here after I had my first orgasm. )',1,798,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(15407,2586,'2002-04-01 17:33:25',81,'felicity','I am entirely with you on this one.  I, too, was a much transplanted child/adolescent.  Now I, too, have been in upstate New York for a touch longer than I have been raised to feel comfortable with.  I love this area, near Corning/Elmira, because it is so similar to the climate and lanscape that I grew up with in Germany, but 7 years is by far too long to hang out in one spot.',2,379,0,0,'216.42.123.44','en'),(15408,2584,'2002-04-01 18:27:02',83,'adamajah','<p>i had no problems in china, but that\\\'s because i was always in urban china, not rural china.  some people in my group caved in and used the pay-toilet at Tienenmen Square.  They had to squat over a festering pit of shit in a dark, hot room.  I always saved my stew for hotels.  Hotels are the safest place to take the throne.</p><p>good to hear from you, \\\'austhai\\\'.</p>',1,381,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(15409,2559,'2002-04-02 00:18:49',93,'chiamattt','i miss my cat already. i wonder where he thinks i am. ',0,54,0,0,'211.216.216.47','en'),(15410,2584,'2002-04-02 00:56:16',92,'kini','my work here is done.',0,21,123,0,'152.91.9.43','en'),(15411,2584,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','ACTUALLY i was kidding about the nuts part.<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni mean really, people.<br>',1,81,89,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15412,2582,'2002-04-02 17:26:50',218,'da','<a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2471.txt\\\">kite related posts</a> are just so #1 for some reason.',1,124,0,0,'165.121.116.117','en'),(15413,2582,'2002-04-02 18:58:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt>thats because kites #1</tt>',1,35,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15414,2577,'2002-04-02 20:18:11',180,'auntie','i\\\'ve been married 29 years, so i\\\'m a bit of an expert.  mark and i adore each other.<br><br>\r\n\r\neven when you love and adore and admire and are madly in lust with the person you marry, marriage is HARD.<br><br>\r\n\r\na man who keeps a secret, for whatever reason, is a man who will find reasons to ALWAYS keep secrets.<br><br>\r\n\r\nif you don\\\'t mind that, fine, but if you do, then DON\\\'T MARRY HIM unless you truly feel that there is no other man out there who will worship you as much as he does, and vice versa.<br><br>\r\n',3,530,0,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(15415,2577,'2002-04-03 00:38:26',239,'Poonlarp','and whats wrong with keeping secrets?',1,37,180,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15416,2587,'2002-04-03 02:25:44',59,'s5','oh god, the webbies. yet another way for a small friendly site to get flooded by the deer hunter crowd.',5,103,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15417,2590,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','oh austie :(:(:(:(:(',1,20,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15418,2577,'2002-04-03 05:17:15',180,'auntie','a man who hides the fact of a prior marriage is likely to be less than truthful about anything during marriage that he believes will upset his wife or her family, or his family, etc. <br><br>\r\n\r\na man who cannot trust his future wife with news of this sort will not trust her after the marriage, either. <br><br>\r\n\r\nmarriage is not a business deal, it\\\'s all about sharing yourself totally with another person, for a lifetime. <br><br>\r\n\r\nthe rewards can be amazingly wonderful - but if both parties are not in it for the long haul from the beginning, which means no secrets, then it is bound to turn into a nightmare before the divorce.',3,639,239,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(15419,2577,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','if i was a lotus, i would probably wait. lost rental hall deposit be damned, etc. what\\\'s 300$ to my lifetime of happiness and self-worth?',0,140,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15420,2577,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','you seem to be pretty happy, or at least resigned to the idea of marriage to this guy.  is that so?',2,99,156,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15421,2582,'2002-04-03 09:06:19',120,'joyrider','being 12, flying a box kite with papa.  the string was alive, the kite bouncing over a white gold montana valley.  thanks for reminding me.',0,139,0,0,'24.205.201.4','en'),(15422,2583,'2002-04-03 09:32:05',187,'kandrer','i know you were sincere (i have been unclear again). lotus is right about you :)',1,80,62,0,'63.208.130.228','en'),(15423,2583,'2002-04-03 09:42:33',187,'kandrer','ok, about the clothes thing, i really meant that i\\\'m jealous of \\\"how women look in their clothes\\\"; but it\\\'s more about the body than the clothes. i never thought a man body could look <i>that</i> good.<br>\r\ni think i am like these 13 years old boys (in a movie i saw) who say \\\"hey, if i was a girl, i would all day long look at myself naked in a mirror\\\".<br>\r\ni don\\\'t think i am a perverse; women bodies are prettier, that\\\'s all. :)',1,456,0,0,'63.208.130.228','en'),(15424,2583,'2002-04-03 09:46:14',187,'kandrer','you are a sweetheart too.',1,25,156,0,'63.208.130.228','en'),(15425,2581,'2002-04-03 10:11:45',187,'kandrer','i can\\\'t say that i understood everything (since i need a dictionary sometimes to read you) but i liked your post. what goes through my mind now:<br>\r\n- i loved the part with the building \\\'bouncer\\\'.<br>\r\n- like other people said, several \\\'small\\\' troubles are making much more damage on you than one \\\'big\\\' trouble.<br>\r\n- a few years ago, i have been unemployed for 10 months. i now there is a moment where you start doubting about yourself (however confident you were feeling at the beginning). try to remember the more you can that your success on the job market and your personal value are not correlated. i know these are only words, but hold on.<br>\r\n- i forbid myself to drink when i am feeling down, except if good friends are around to take care of \\\'what\\\'s left of me\\\'<br>\r\n- the brain is a dangerous machine if it\\\'s left untied. don\\\'t let yours go wherever it wants. thinking about what life can bring me \\\'in the end\\\' always ends up depressing me. in the end, we die; let\\\'s think about other things if we can, ok? :)<br>\r\ntake care.<br>\r\nuncle kandrer (am i so older than you?)\r\n',1,1131,176,0,'63.208.130.228','en'),(15426,2586,'2002-04-03 12:22:37',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ive lived in nyc for 7 years; 7 is a good amount of years. maybe ill live here for 7 years more, and even 7 more after that.</tt>',1,137,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15427,2579,'2002-04-03 14:11:21',156,'lotus','a random list. why i am crying:<br> <br>\r\n* this post hit me hard<br>\r\n* i feel guilty<br>\r\n* i want to help, and i don\\\'t know how<br>\r\n* i read so many amazing dazzling TRUE things on swinney, and then guiltily go to the next article because i don\\\'t know how to comment. i refuse to do that this time. hence this lame comment. i just want you to know you are heard. and what you wrote matters very much<br>\r\n* it snowed 10 cm last night. in april! it is supposed to be spring, dammit.<br>\r\n* i know that last one was trivial. but having so much of the day be in darkness really makes me sad. and yes, i am so self-centred.<br>\r\n* i know people in afganistan<br>\r\n* why do people kill their own tiny children?<br>\r\n* this world is so terrible<br>\r\n* this world is so lovely<br>',0,783,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15428,2583,'2002-04-03 14:15:01',156,'lotus','i think you\\\'re very very endearing. i just read a slew of your posts and comments, and find your expressions (\\\'perverse\\\' instead of \\\'pervert\\\') so, so...ahem. what i mean to say is both what you say and how you say it are very adorable.',0,250,187,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15429,2577,'2002-04-03 14:20:12',156,'lotus','i\\\'ve been thinking and thinking about kandrer\\\'s comments for days now. i\\\'m assuming (please correct me if i\\\'m wrong) that you went back and read an earlier post i made about the marriage, because your second comment seemed to be a response to that other post.\r\n<br><Br>\r\nso, i\\\'ve been thinking. and i feel almost to bursting with all the things i want to say. i just don\\\'t know how to say them.\r\n<br><br>\r\ndearest o,<br>\r\nyes, i think i am resigned to this marriage. why? a very long story.\r\n<br><br>\r\nauntie, are you really le\\\'s mom? you\\\'re so great. i\\\'ve been reading your posts since i joined swinney. thank you for your concern and knowledge. \r\n<br><br>\r\nonce i feel i can articulate the cloud inside me, i will try writing more on this again. currently, the wedding is set for may 25. i will get to meet and know the groom after april 26, once our \\\'akht\\\' ceremony has been peformed in the mosque. before then, i can only see him while chaperoned. my headhurts.',0,998,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15430,2590,'2002-04-03 14:55:58',93,'chiamattt','dude. come teach english in south korea. the women are fine, and the water is drinkable. i think. actually i only drink bottled water here. ok never mind my idea. the jig is up. ',0,178,0,0,'61.72.197.151','en'),(15431,2577,'2002-04-03 15:19:47',65,'le','auntie is really my mom.',4,24,0,0,'165.247.218.143','en'),(15432,2577,'2002-04-03 15:24:08',65,'le','lotus. i have a lot of respect for you.',0,39,0,0,'165.247.218.143','en'),(15433,2590,'2002-04-03 16:18:27',17,'austin','uh, is chiamattt in south korea?\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n*destabilizing*',4,59,93,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(15434,2590,'2002-04-03 17:58:45',82,'simon','yeah, our little chiamattt has left the nest for the far seas.',0,62,17,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15435,2590,'2002-04-03 18:06:21',122,'hool','<p>math isn\\\'t lame.  i used to like math in highschool because i liked problem solving.  after taking more thoeretical mathematics courses at higher levels in university, i know that math is fucking cool.   i also understand why math is taught the way it is highschool.  it\\\'s just building a very basic alphabet.  it takes a long time later to learn to write sentences.. and then paragraphs and then stories.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Godel,Escher,Bach: An Eternal Golden Braid by Hofstadter(sp?) is the strongest confirmation that math is not lame.</p>',0,545,17,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(15436,2577,'2002-04-03 18:09:33',180,'auntie','yes, i am le\\\'s mom. isn\\\'t she great?\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\njust remember that you aren\\\'t married until you say the \\\"i do\\\" -- at any time before that, you can always cut and run.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nif you are resigned to the marriage, then you need to gird up your loins and begin as you mean to go on.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\ndecide how you wish to live your daily lives, and for a woman, that means money issues, child bearing issues, etc. be sure to tell your future husband how you feel about these things NOW and ask how he feels about them.\r\n<br><br>\r\n if he has a child from a previous marriage, a son, who will inherit his estate 50 year from now?  what if you bear only daughters?\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nattempting to live within a traditional religious marriage as a woman with a modern education will be very hard.  you will often find yourself hard-pressed to find joy when you are feeling frustrated and angry by the conventions of the relationship you have agreed to abide by.\r\n<br><br>\r\nmark and i have a traditional marriage.  i did not work until the children were much older, and even then, it was not a good choice, being a parent and wife requires full time attention when you have brilliant children.\r\n<br><br>\r\nwith great love and affection.. auntie\r\n\r\n',2,1257,156,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(15437,2587,'2002-04-03 18:35:54',129,'moebius','in most busy street intersections you\\\'ll usually find natural \\\"dead spots\\\"--parts of the roadway that fill with excess dirt and dust because most car trajectories pass by them to one side or the other.\r\n\r\nin filipino towns you will encounter dogs that manage to find those odd \\\"dead spots\\\" and settle down within them. cars and honking jeepneys and trucks full of chickens will go by in every direction and they\\\'ll just hang there all day long, seemingly fearless, doing a sort of dog=zen act under the hot tropical sun. \r\n\r\ni loved those dogs when i was a kid.',1,579,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(15438,2590,'2002-04-03 19:07:26',83,'adamajah','<p>Without mathematics, we wouldn\\\'t have Crazy Train by Ozzy Osbourne.</p>',3,77,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(15439,2587,'2002-04-03 19:15:07',17,'austin','bloody fucking hell,\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni am super hungry.  when is lunch today?  i cant work at all.  we are supposed to have a pizza lunch today, non-dot-com style where i have to buy my own fucking shitty pizza.  bastard.  son of a bitch.',4,233,0,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(15440,2587,'2002-04-03 19:35:22',83,'adamajah','<P>it\\\'s not just you.  i have to buy my own lunch on Team Lunch day too.  :\\\\</p>',2,86,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(15441,2590,'2002-04-03 19:56:07',66,'Peter','<br><tt>math rocks; my entire worldview was changed by partial dif. equations</tt>',1,82,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15442,2589,'2002-04-03 20:01:40',160,'Shane McGowan','When it\\\'s Summer in Siam\r\nAnd the moon is full of rainbows\r\nWhen it\\\'s Summer in Siam\r\nAnd we go through many changes\r\nWhen it\\\'s Summer in Siam\r\nThen all I really know\r\nIs that I truly am\r\nIn the Summer in Siam\r\n',0,220,0,0,'198.185.18.207','en'),(15443,2589,'2002-04-03 20:02:30',160,'Shane McGowan','When it\\\'s Summer in Siam<br>\r\nAnd the moon is full of rainbows<br>\r\nWhen it\\\'s Summer in Siam<br>\r\nAnd we go through many changes<br>\r\nWhen it\\\'s Summer in Siam<br>\r\nThen all I really know<br>\r\nIs that I truly am<br>\r\nIn the Summer in Siam<br>\r\n',0,252,0,0,'198.185.18.207','en'),(15444,2589,'2002-04-03 20:06:48',160,'Exene Cervenka','<p>The facts we hate: we\\\'ll never meet. \r\nwalking down the road. Everybody yelling\r\n\\\"hurry up!\\\" \\\"hurry up!\\\" But i\\\'m waiting\r\nfor you, I must go slow. I must not \r\nthink bad thoughts. When is this \r\nworld coming to? Both sides are \r\nright but both sides murder. I \r\ngive up; why can\\\'t they? I must \r\nnot think bad thoughts. The civil \r\nwars and the uncivilized wars. \r\nconflagrations leap out of every \r\npoor furnace. The food cooks \r\npoorly and everyone goes hungry. \r\nfrom then on it\\\'s dog eat dog, dog \r\neat body, & body eat dog. I can\\\'t \r\ngo down there. I can\\\'t understand it.\r\nI\\\'m a no good coward & an american too.\r\na north american, that is not a south \r\nor a central. Or a native american. \r\noh i must not think bad thoughts. I\\\'m\r\nguilty of murder of innocent men, \r\ninnocent women, innocent children, \r\nthousands of them! My planes, my guns,\r\nmy money, my soldiers, my blood on ny \r\nhands, it\\\'s all my fault! I must not \r\nthink bad thoughts. I must not think \r\nbad thoughts. The facts we hate: \r\nyou\\\'ll never hear us. \r\n\\\"I hear the radio is finally gonna \r\nplay new music, you know, the \r\nbritish invasion.\\\" But what about \r\nthe Minutemen, Flesh Eaters, DOA, \r\nBig Boys. And the Black Flag? Will \r\nthe last american band to get played \r\non the radio please bring the flag? \r\nplease bring the flag! Glitter-disco-\r\nsynthesizer night school. All this \r\nnoble savage drum, drum, drum. \r\nastronauts going back in time to hang \r\nout with the cave people. It\\\'s about \r\ntime, it\\\'s about space. It\\\'s about \r\nsome people in the strangest place. \r\nwoody guthrie sang about b-e-e-t-s\r\nnot b-e-a-t-s. I must not think \r\nbad thoughts, I must not think\r\nbad thoughts, the facts we hate:',0,1738,0,0,'198.185.18.207','en'),(15445,2590,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','math is a way to understand patterns, and patterns are cool. see: trees',0,71,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15446,2577,'2002-04-03 23:46:22',17,'austin','i just finnished reading The Count Of Monte Cristo.  i recommend it to you.  a good one half of the book is devoted to arranged marriages of several of the characters and how they dont end up happening for various reasons.',3,222,156,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(15447,2590,'2002-04-04 00:00:55',17,'austin','<P>did i say math was lame?</P><P>what i meant was <i>i am lame</i>, as math makes so clear.  there are a few areas in life that i feel limited by my lack of understanding in the math department.  </P><P>i do not have the alphabet for math that you speak of.  i was never into challenges in school.  math problems always seemed to take me longer than they should.  i think i got behind early on and then my self esteem took a hit so i started avoiding it all together, or thinking i was horrible at math was cool.  it is clear to me that i have to start out with kindergarden kumon worksheets as i dont know what the Pythagorean theorem is.</P><P>some combination of your website and seeing josh praystation at sxsw has me assuming some practical application.  well, not practical in the monetary sense, but that there is room for it in my life.  also, i saw a book at the store: <a href=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0262032937/ref=cm_wl_topnav_gateway/ref=cm_wed_ovu-pg.1-pos.1/102-1656586-8620909?coliid=I1I8YEO4FNM1ME\\\">Introduction to Algorithms, Second Edition</a>.  it contains a different approach to math than i am familiar with, but in a format that i am familiar with, programming.  the book contains explanations behind some functionality that is programmed into high level programming languages, and through explaining them i hope will impart to me the ability to use some lower level programming languages.</P><P>in addition to those factors, i have a math class left that i need to pass in order to graduate from college.  this class that i have dropped late twice and failed once.</P>',4,1615,122,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(15448,2595,'2002-04-04 00:07:43',88,'deb','I suppose a broken heart can hurt just as much in a dream as in reality. ',3,73,0,0,'198.139.249.121','en'),(15449,2593,'2002-04-04 00:12:05',88,'deb','That reminds me of my time in school; picked on, picked last, etc.  It got so bad that by the time I was done junior high, I switched schools. It didn\\\'t get much better, but it didn\\\'t get worse...',2,202,0,0,'198.139.249.121','en'),(15450,2589,'2002-04-04 00:17:17',88,'deb','Love is a drug. In the end, the drugs kill... \r\n*angry, bitter person today*',5,76,0,0,'198.139.249.121','en'),(15451,2586,'2002-04-04 02:24:06',124,'gus','I lived in the same house from birth to age 18. then I went to college in that same town, 5 miles away from home. Now I live 40 miles from there. weird huh? but i\\\'ve been other places.. I just like this place best. ',0,218,0,0,'64.172.56.208','en'),(15452,2588,'2002-04-04 02:26:21',124,'gus','Every song I ever hear is a dance inside my head. (If it isnt, I wont listen to that song again). ',0,98,0,0,'64.172.56.208','en'),(15453,2594,'2002-04-04 02:30:26',239,'Poonlarp','Can i take your name<br>\r\nThank you, what is your complaint?<br> \r\nGenital Herpes<br><br>\r\n\r\nWill you take a seat <br>\r\nin waiting room number two <br>\r\nWait for Doctor Duane<br><br> \r\n\r\nRead some magazines <br>\r\nGardener\\\'s World, TV Times <br>\r\nChild is sick on floor <br><br>\r\n\r\nGenital Herpes? <br>\r\nYes Doctor Duane that is me<br> \r\nAh, please walk this way <br><br>\r\n\r\nThis looks serious <br>\r\nTake these tablets twice a day <br>\r\nAnd topical cream <br><br>\r\n\r\nNo more sex for you <br>\r\nKeep your legs closed if you want <br>\r\nTo be cured this time \r\n<br><br>',1,567,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15454,2590,'2002-04-04 02:33:16',124,'gus','I bet math is cool, and useful. I just never got along well with it. My teachers didnt think girls needed to know it so well. They also didnt catch on that I was cheating on the tests. So, that makes me think Im smart, to pass math without knowing math, how could a dum kid do that?\r\n<p>\r\nelliot smith. yah. that guy has a broken heart, and a broken life... but I could listen to <i>st ides heaven</i> or <i>the biggest lie</i> or <i>pisteleh</i> or <i>angeles</i> a hundred thousand times.',0,490,0,0,'64.172.56.208','en'),(15455,2592,'2002-04-04 02:35:33',124,'gus','peep',0,4,0,0,'64.172.56.208','en'),(15456,2586,'2002-04-04 02:40:06',82,'simon','yeah, i\\\'ve lived a whole bunch of places and now i live a mere 4 blocks from where i started.',0,96,124,0,'216.175.90.98','en'),(15457,2585,'2002-04-04 04:34:00',183,'lin','<p>Dreams tell us what we want, when can\\\'t see everything as plain as day. Dremas tell us who we are when we don\\\'t know, and sometimes dreams tell us what\\\'s going on in our lives, when we are too close, and the picture is blurred. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I used to think my dremas meant nothing. Just a bunch of random symbols. Hey, we have to think of something while we sleep right? But my dremas are often like dramatic sets or scits where people I know perform scenes for me. The times when I pay attnetion to what is happening (usually when I\\\'m really upset, or don\\\'t know what else to do) things have become more clear. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>In retrospect, I feel validated knowing that some part of me is picking up on things, even though I can\\\'t always be aware. It\\\'s not always easy to remember your dreams. It helps if you have someone to tell them too, or if you don\\\'t mind losing a little sleep, writing them down right away, if you wake up.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> I do not however, believe in dream interpretation symbols, that tell you what your dreams mean. I had a dream once, where I was running through a war zone with my friend Katya, who was helping me out. I got shot in the side, but kept running. Then I ran up to a man who had a rake and a hose, who sprayed me with his hose, which knocked me flat, and eventually I had to will him to stop spraying me so I could get up. Then someone shot me in the chest machine gun style, but with a shotgun. I kept running, and Katya was going to drive me to the hospital, which was at home in Sarnia, and as I sat in the car I finally registered that I have been shot multiple times, and I look down at my chest because I can feel the blood draining out of my body. I had a star bullet holes on my chest over my heart in a very elaborate pattern. Then I woke up. I dare any dream book to tell me what that means.</p>\r\n',2,1867,0,0,'24.42.108.124','en'),(15458,2577,'2002-04-04 07:49:10',187,'kandrer','i thought about <a ref=\\\"http://www.filmsite.org/nightof.html\\\">the Night of the Hunter</a>. i thought the fact that the wedding is \\\"arranged\\\" is not what scares me. what scares me is to think you could be tied to someone bad. tyrannic people are able to hide it; that\\\'s one of their first weapons. because you are sensitive and gentle, i believe you can make sure by yourself (if you\\\'re given enough time) that he is for real a kind person. examples of useful questions: does he have friends? how does he behave with his family? etc. it might be hard to know this in an arranged wedding, i guess.</p><p>\r\ni don\\\'t think that the fact of having secrets is really bad in itself (though auntie\\\'s comments are great even on that point). the question in my opinion is more why the secret has been kept and what kind it is. in your case we can guess why a first wedding was not mentionned. but i think it would be great to hear what happened to make the first marriage fail. how does he speaks about it? does he still respect his first wife? etc. people who blame things on someone else have more chances to put it on yourself later on. so i just hope you know enough about this, or you will get to know before the wedding. if you still feel confident about him, go for it!</p><p>\r\ni wish you the greatest hapiness anyhow, little lotus :)',1,1354,156,0,'64.156.228.84','en'),(15459,2597,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','*cough*<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni like the plants in my little garden.  i am glad weeb can tell me and steve what they are.',2,110,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15460,2597,'2002-04-04 08:35:35',124,'gus','worms, yes. buy more worms! I used to stay at a friend on 23rd st near york over by SF general, someone next door kept chickens, the roosters would wake me up in the morning. No one ever said you couldnt keep chickens in the mission. you can! you can!',0,251,0,0,'64.172.56.208','en'),(15461,2597,'2002-04-04 12:23:54',66,'Peter','<br><tt>if you remove the grass and first few inches of soily roots from the earth under the compost heap, more than enough big ol\' worms will relocate to your compost; it takes about a month. as soon as they move in, youll see them get noticeably fatter and jollier after a few weeks when you turn the pile.<br><br>someone kept chickens in a vacant lot across the street from my old place; suffice to say that alot of people that keep roosters and chickens in urban areas do it for cock-fighting. the nypd came and busted them up, but they kept coming back. so, the police spread the word around hte neighborhood and told us that we should notify them if we ever saw any fowl that appeared to have their legs shaved (they do this strictly for the fighting). interestingly, folks were always loitering around the lot, perusing the animal\'s legs.<br><br>i always figured that, with gazillions of acres of rural american sprawl, no one needed to raise farm animals in cities. rural america keeps the animals, and the cities keep all the guidos and pimps and pushers out of their hair; its a good symbiotic relationship.</tt> ',1,1129,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15462,2577,'2002-04-04 12:30:26',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i lived in a house once that had a coal-chute that fed from out by the road into the basement where the boiler/heater used to be. The house was built in the 1890s. The coal-fired equipment had long been replaced with modern electric/gas equipment, but the chute remained.  The chute, although quite quaint with its well-crafted cast-iron design, was arcane and no longer served a modern purpose. It also severely intruded into the room it was in, and took up too much space for something so outdated. I often hit my head on the chute, so it was doubly a pain in the neck as well as a physical obstacle in the room.<br><br>one day, i took a power-saw and removed the chute. I left the pretty hinged trap-door, but expunged the useless, rusty utilitarian chute. After taking action, the room was so much prettier and much more useful and functional.<br><br><i>[vague metaphor re: arcane practices here, btw]</i></tt>',1,922,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15463,2595,'2002-04-04 12:35:20',239,'Poonlarp','this is fascinating. i hope you will tell us more.',1,50,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15464,2596,'2002-04-04 12:48:13',122,'hool','agreed. i know plenty of twenty somethings who have never seemed to graduate from emotional highschool.  funny how in highschool the kids who unwittingly express their insecurity most fashionably are the most popular, but those same kids are pretty transparent once highschool is done.',0,285,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(15465,2577,'2002-04-04 14:06:30',156,'lotus','fragments:<br><br>\r\ni was going to email this auntie personally, but: i had already considered myself, the money and children issues, but the son-inheritance part didn\\\'t even occur to me. i\\\'m so grateful she wrote about it, because this morning, i asked my dad what Islam\\\'s regulations would be regarding this situation.<br><br>\r\ni think, arranged marriages, done right, are a really good thing. but the key phrase is: \\\'done right\\\'. at the end, if you\\\'re happy, who cares how you met or how it was initiated? i don\\\'t think it matters if your parents introduced you, or if you met in a bookstore or whatever. but i really think/wish that after being set up, the bride and groom could have time to get to know one another, to get deeper into the interaction, instead of having to settle for surface compatibility checks (e.g. background, religion, status in society, family). i don\\\'t know. my parents, (and culture?) think it is weird, abstract, to require more than this, to want to know about favourite movies and values, and things that make you laugh, and what you want to do with the future outside of work.<br><br>\r\nthank you to everyone for their good wishes. and an extra thank you to auntie for giving me something important and practical.',0,1270,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15466,2577,'2002-04-04 14:07:29',156,'lotus','thank you for your kindness. ',0,29,187,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15467,2577,'2002-04-04 14:09:01',156,'lotus','i admire you. especially your way with words. and you have such a cool mom! :)<br><Br>\r\nbtw, i\\\'ve been thinking of the whole online journal thing. i sort of have one too. and why? what are my motivations? how does it change things? i am thinking of taking a vacation from it.',0,278,65,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15468,2577,'2002-04-04 14:09:42',156,'lotus','i read the abridged version when i was a little kid. it is now on my book list. thank you.',0,90,17,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15469,2577,'2002-04-04 14:11:03',156,'lotus','your comments are always so well thought out, and so, so positive. i am not just referring to this comment, but to all the comments of yours that i have read.',0,158,66,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15470,2592,'2002-04-04 14:11:57',156,'lotus','cheep. :)',0,9,124,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15471,2577,'2002-04-04 14:39:03',66,'Peter','<br><tt>thanks, lotus! one of my resolutions for this year has been to be more positive (ive been quite a downer inthe past)and to be nice to people, acting in ways that generally cause people to act nicer to each other, raising the standards.<br><br>eschewing alot of intellectual sarcasm has helped. being more observant of my surroundings and community of people has also. <i>kind kicks ass</i>, and i hope more people realize this. its making my world better.</tt>',1,468,156,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15472,2590,'2002-04-04 16:04:41',151,'stella','I like math but I have dyslexia which makes me kinda not like math. \r\nI like the movie \\\"pi\\\" which is all about math and numbers and patterns. It\\\'s a good movie, but it makes my head hurt sometimes when I watch it. ',0,223,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(15473,2597,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<p>i\\\'ve been adding worms from the garden to the compost heap. we\\\'ll probably have a big yield of worms from that in a couple of weeks. yay! peter, i used to live near a very unkind person who kept a rooster in the house next door. this was while i was working the night shift. it was an epic (yet polite) battle and eventually the chickens went away. now i feel badly about forcing him to get rid of his chickens, but goddamn it, i just wanted the goddamn things to stop crowing! mmm city living.\r\n</p><p>\r\npeep peep!</p>',0,528,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15474,2597,'2002-04-04 17:17:57',66,'Peter','<br><tt>bok! bok! bok!</tt>',1,27,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15475,2597,'2002-04-04 19:41:01',82,'simon','i once bought two chickens from the slausson indoor swap meet in south central and they lived in my backyard in echo park for a few months before they upgraded and moved to more fashionable silver lake to peck and squawk in my friend gill\\\'s spacious yard.',0,258,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15476,2597,'2002-04-04 19:42:29',82,'simon','also, watch out for fowl play!',0,30,82,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15477,2596,'2002-04-04 19:55:21',176,'Scapegoat','<b>To Newfragile_yellows:</b><br>\r\nPretension abounds be it related to music or any other endevour. It\\\'s annoying.<br><br>\r\n<b>To Hool</b><br>\r\nEmotional maturity is IMHO were society runs the biggest deficits.<br><br>\r\nOn a more whimsical note, lunch tables in high school is something I haven\\\'t thought about in so long. I used to sit at all the different tables but home base would have been the one populated by \\\'the hoods\\\' or criminal element. Fond and somewhat scary memories of highschool evoked.     ',0,520,0,0,'64.229.160.203','en'),(15478,2581,'2002-04-04 20:28:19',176,'Scapegoat','I am 25. Due to your writing it seems to me that you would be a bit older then me just because you have been a lot of different places. Your words are noted and appreciated.',0,173,187,0,'64.229.160.203','en'),(15479,2596,'2002-04-04 20:53:44',148,'jeeff','when i went to the cafeteria in high school (only grade 9) i always sat with a friend named matt.  every day he brought an orange in his lunch.  did he like oranges?  no.  but each day he\\\'d take out a pen knife and carve it into a jack-o-lantern.  then he left it on the table for the next people to enjoy.  the jack-ol-orange.\r\n\r\n<p>i switched schools next year.</p>',0,370,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(15480,2596,'2002-04-04 20:58:32',66,'Peter','<br><tt>Emotional maturity is were <i>intelligent</i> society runs the biggest deficits; <i>basic human behavior</i> is where the rest need some improvement, heh...</tt>',1,169,176,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15481,2597,'2002-04-04 20:59:01',148,'jeeff','i\\\'ve never had a chicken.  or a big garden.  i had a big cactus once, but somehow i managed to kill it.  this summer will be my first with a garden since i\\\'ve been living on my own.  i\\\'m afraid for the plants.',0,218,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(15482,2598,'2002-04-04 21:17:13',82,'simon','hi al! thanks for cheering me up with a little story about my two favorite topics: making out and CARS!\r\n<br>\r\nfond memories include:<br>\r\nmaking out in the back of a small orange datsun pick-up while parked in front of the landslide cafe in san anselmo, ca<br>\r\ncopping a feel in a faggy jeep<br>\r\na \\\'65 mustang convertible<br>\r\na lotus esprit<br>\r\nan olds convertible<br>\r\nand making out endlessly in the el camino, of course. <br>\r\n<br>\r\nbut, i must admit, i dated a forest green 4runner for a really long time. don\\\'t tell!',1,532,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15483,2596,'2002-04-04 21:29:30',129,'moebius','<br><br>for a long period of my life i was guilty of copping the \\\"indier-than-thou\\\" attitude. eventually i woke up and realized that: a) just because people don\\\'t know/like/understand the music i like doesn\\\'t have anything to do with whether or not they\\\'re good people at heart, and b) this attitude was alienating me from everyone around me, even my other \\\"indier-than-thou\\\" friends.<br><br>some of my friendships have been forever affected by this; one friend, for example, still gets overly defensive when he talks about music with me. to paraphrase a recent email from him: <i>oh, i wanted to tell you i really like [this band] but you probably have everything by them since you are way beyond me.</i> <br><br>it was a band i\\\'d heard of but never heard. seems silly that two guys over 30 should still communicate like this.',0,851,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(15484,2598,'2002-04-04 21:49:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt><b>two auto-related incidents from my experiences in an all-male high-school:</b><br><br><b>1.</b> a varsity wrestler and a jass-band trumpeter discovered in the back of one silver \'82 dodge omni, sharing a special kind of love.<br><b>2.</b> class valedictorian discovered in the senior parking-lot pleasuring himself via a \'93 forest green audi\'s tailpipes.<br><br><i>auto</i>eroticism at its best (worst?).<br><br>2002\'s. yum. i dated one of those once, an orange one. also, a white olds, a \'62 impala droptop, a 93 caddie. saddest? an \'89 suburban with lift kit.</tt>',1,430,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15485,2598,'2002-04-04 22:15:11',148,'jeeff','cars.  blurgh.',0,14,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(15486,2597,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i would really like a chicken to come live in the back yard. we\\\'re afraid mika would eat it, though.',2,103,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15487,2598,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i lost my virginity in the back of a mazda rx7. here are some of the cars i\'ve dated throughout the years:<br>\r\n<br>\r\n- various makes of mini<br>\r\n- holden gemini<br>\r\n- ford laser<br>\r\n- mazda 323<br>\r\n- ford escort mkII<br>\r\n- mitsubishi cordia turbo<br>\r\n- lotus 7 replica<br>\r\n- vw jetta<br>\r\n<br>\r\nby far, my favourite experiences in these cars are trying to have sex in the back seat of a mini with a 6\'2\" guy, and s5 pulling over his jetta to the side of the highway somewhere in the middle of the usa so we could make out.<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni love cars.  i love making out.<br>',2,588,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15488,2598,'2002-04-04 23:13:30',82,'simon','a holden gemini! those are cute.',1,32,62,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15489,2597,'2002-04-04 23:19:18',129,'moebius','<br>i\\\'ve found it\\\'s pretty hard to convince cats to go vegan, especially when there\\\'s edible critters running about. they\\\'ll suffer the reformulated birdseed you feed \\\'em, but an actual live bird will beat that dish, paws down, every time.',2,254,62,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(15490,2597,'2002-04-04 23:29:48',124,'gus','a chicken will totally peck a cat\\\'s eye out. ',0,48,0,0,'64.172.56.208','en'),(15491,2597,'2002-04-04 23:41:53',82,'simon','heh. i\\\'ve never had plants either but then i started dating this girl who came over one day and gave me a plant. so far, the plant is actually doing well. i\\\'m shocked i haven\\\'t fucked it up.',0,199,148,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15492,2598,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','this is totally making my day. \r\n\r\n1- would you believe a dodge aries? a more responsible, boxy, hair parted in the middle, two rubbers kind of guy couldn\\\'t be found. \r\n2- a cherokee jeep. rich and tasteful\r\n3- a tan chevette/dodge neon. reliable, yet responsive!\r\n\r\ngoddamn. this is making me realize i\\\'ve dated a lot of boring cars. but nice boys.',0,355,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15493,2598,'2002-04-05 00:15:33',17,'austin to gus','<P>i will let you barrow my white 81 sting ray to make out with whomever you choose for a night.</P>\r\n<P>then it will turn back into a pumpkin.</P>',2,147,0,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(15494,2598,'2002-04-05 00:16:06',82,'simon','this is making my day too. but i\\\'m also realizing that i\\\'m the one with the cars, usually. i\\\'m not even gonna get started on the list of running and partially running cars i\\\'ve had....',0,196,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15495,2598,'2002-04-05 00:16:55',83,'adamajah','<p>i\\\'ve only <i>really</i> made out in a brown ford mavrick and some mazda 323 hatchback thing.  the cops put a stop to that one.</p>  ',3,138,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(15496,2598,'2002-04-05 00:19:27',17,'austin','which reminds me:<BR><BR>\r\none of the first times i was hanging out with ix, we were getting out of a movie.  he, egg and i were walking back to my car and i was going to give him a ride home.  there was a black and silver (sunburst type paint job) \'80 vette parked next to my decidedly lame honda civic and i said, \"here we go,\" motioning to the vette.  ix said, \"oh, i can take a cab, it alright.\"</P>',3,417,0,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(15497,2596,'2002-04-05 01:09:49',224,'newfragile_yellows','<p>awhile back i wrote a poem about the people who sat at our lunch table in high school my junior year. some of the best years of my life were those \\\"penultimate\\\" years in school before   most of my friends managed to end up with the same lunch period and a few others sort of joined the group as time went on. it weren\\\'t the \\\"popular table\\\" but in our own rite it was where we felt popular.</p>\r\n\r\n<i>\r\nWe were loose and cool: hung <br>\r\nt-shirts on our bodies too<br>\r\nLow<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWords we swore<br>\r\nWe would never use <br>\r\nstuck in our tongues<br>\r\ncoughed mouthfuls of gas<br>\r\nstation cigarettes<br>\r\n<br>\r\nharbored notebooks from ourselves <br>\r\nuntil lunch, the round table<br>\r\namong us: a dancer, the musicians, a daydreamer,  <br>\r\nthe free-writer in love with the daydreamer, <br>\r\ncomedians...</i><br>\r\n\r\n',0,838,0,0,'24.209.71.12','en'),(15498,2596,'2002-04-05 01:12:15',224,'newfragile_yellows','<p>oops i screwed up the last post. i meant to say:<br>\r\n<br>\r\nawhile back i wrote a poem about the people who sat at our lunch table in high school my junior year. some of the best years of my life were those \\\"penultimate\\\" years in school before where i was just getting comfortable but looking forward to moving on to some bigger stage in life.  <br>\r\n<br>\r\nmost of my friends managed to end up with the same lunch period and a few others sort of joined the group as time went on. it weren\\\'t the \\\"popular table\\\" but in our own rite it was where we felt popular.</p>\r\n\r\n',0,586,0,0,'24.209.71.12','en'),(15499,2596,'2002-04-05 01:13:03',224,'newfragile_yellows','ok i screwed up that one, too. ',0,31,0,0,'24.209.71.12','en'),(15500,2331,'2002-04-05 01:28:04',224,'newfragile_yellows','<p>i think this piece is really beautiful. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>in the written text, you discuss people bringing their feelings to you and you mention that these individuals are \\\"fractured\\\". you also talk about how these people are so absorbed in what they are feeling that they don\\\'t really listen to what you have to offer them, as if asking someone for advice or help was really just an opportunity to unload your feelings on them.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the picture, in my mind, is much like a response to the text. the picture is fractured, just like the people mentioned in the text. that is not just a picture of a random object or scene, it is a picture of a person (you?). in conjunction with the text, it feels like the picture represents the idea that we all have our own issues and frustrations and with all the time we spend not really truly listening to other peoples problems, we only have ourselves to truly listen to the internal conflict that we are dealing with.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>it is almost impossible these days to feel like the other side of the phone is truly listening.  there is so much stimuli around us and we usualy only think of situations in terms of how it will benefit ourselves. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>the amazing part about this poem, with all of the elements of image and text working together and the image being a response to the text and/or vice versa and the subject matter of the piece itself, is that exactly what you are talking about in the piece - the frustration with feeling heard - comes through loud and clear.</p>\r\n\r\n',2,1536,0,0,'24.209.71.12','en'),(15501,2598,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','this particular gemmi was even better than your average, because it was GOLD.',2,77,82,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15502,2598,'2002-04-05 02:38:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>my parents sold their matching \\\'71 convertible stingrays (which i might have inherited?) to make the payment on my childhood home. sigh.</tt>',1,152,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15503,2599,'2002-04-05 03:41:19',195,'matt','i\\\'m fascinated by steam engines. i\\\'d like like to learn to build them, machine the parts myself. the parts have erotic names like \\\"glands\\\" and \\\"ballcocks.\\\"',1,173,0,0,'209.208.17.34','en'),(15504,2596,'2002-04-05 03:44:39',206,'josta','it\\\'s ok newfragile, it was a good poem and we got the basic idea of the explanation. i\\\'m a high school soph and my lunch table is sort of like that right now (the one last year kind of sucked so it\\\'s a real relief to finally be having fun at lunch now)... we have the punk, the preppy, the Nice Guy, the comedian, the hacker (me) and the actress... every day Nice Guy eats a bagel for lunch; he cuts it in half, spreads cream cheese over it and arranges three of the comedian\\\'s pretzels on either side, then we time him on eating it. so far his record is just under three minutes. <br>\r\n<br>\r\ni confess to being indier-than-thou when facing the storm of \\\"blink-182 and/or sum41 = punk\\\" types... i\\\'m working on it...',2,736,0,0,'64.12.106.43','en'),(15505,2598,'2002-04-05 04:16:45',124,'gus','Ok maybe I was hasty with corvette. maybe what I need me is kick ass muscle car of some tuffer-than-thou variety. yah. that.',0,124,0,0,'64.172.56.208','en'),(15506,2598,'2002-04-05 08:01:18',64,'Joanna','Because making out for me generally involves strangers, and strangers generally involve alcohol, and alcohol should never ever involve cars, I\\\'ve only ever made out in a car once. It was the back seat of a taxi and I\\\'m oblivious to what kind of car it was, just like we were oblivious to whatever the driver was thinking. I still rate it as one of the hottest experiances in my life. I want to make out in a car again please. Consider this my Personals ad.',1,462,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(15507,2594,'2002-04-05 09:11:25',17,'austin','i have had one great experience at a health clinic.  the doctor there was  the only real doctor i have ever been to.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ndr: \\\'drop your pants\\\'\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nas: drops pants and stands there\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ndr: walks off\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nas: sits down\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ndr: walks back in, \\\'stand up\\\'\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nas: stands up\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ndr: \\\'never sit down in a VD clinic.\\\'\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nas: \\\'oh.\\\'',3,402,0,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(15508,2599,'2002-04-05 14:11:16',251,'Jamie','<p>a kid, i was always trying to take electronic equipment to bits. Walkmans mainly, to the bemusement of my parents. They were expensive items back in the day. </p>\r\n<p>I\\\'d spend hours on end trying to get inside the cases, eventually resorting to throwing it at walls/floors. When i\\\'d get the thing apart it rip out all the capacitors, resistors, transistors etc. that i could. I kept them in a box. I liked the pretty colours.</p>',3,439,0,0,'217.159.16.30','en'),(15509,1926,'2002-04-05 14:16:46',251,'Jamie','<p>ah! but crack doesn\\\'t mean the same thing in ireland as it does in the us/uk. Crack means <i>\\\'a good time\\\'</i>... Come to think of it i suppose it <i>does</i> mean the same thing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>No, but seriously. I\\\'d prefer a nice warm hypodermic syringe full o good old fashioned smack to a crack pipe any day of the week</p>',1,337,0,0,'217.159.16.30','en'),(15510,2600,'2002-04-05 14:38:14',155,'blue stocking','this is deliciously good. thank you.',0,36,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15511,2598,'2002-04-05 15:21:18',224,'newfragile_yellows','i made out with a guy in his antique truck that had modern day novelty pedal shaped like a foot.\r\n\r\n',0,100,0,0,'198.185.18.207','en'),(15512,2594,'2002-04-05 17:49:31',66,'Peter','<br><tt>the clap?</tt>',1,22,17,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15513,2598,'2002-04-05 18:35:36',148,'jeeff','<b>newfragileyellow:</b> hahaha!  that comment was like a ray of sunshine breaking through the dark clouds of my hangover.',0,122,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(15514,2602,'2002-04-05 19:21:30',129,'moebius','<br>now that\\\'s a cool word to know. it\\\'s related to another favorite word of mine: glossolalia. speaking in tongues.',2,122,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(15515,2599,'2002-04-05 20:00:08',129,'moebius','<br>when i was a kid i was fascinated by magnets. i eventually took apart all of my machine-oriented toys in hopes of finding magnets inside. then i\\\'d take the magnets & spend hours playing w/them and collecting bags full of iron filings.<br><br>several years later i had a friend who swore he could repair my broken walkman. after questioning him over & over about his mechanical abilities i finally decided that there was nothing to lose. i gave it to him. weeks went by. after much hassling, the guy showed up at school with bag full of itsy-bitsy little walkman parts, which he handed to me sheepishly. he\\\'d gone & disassembled the entire thing, and couldn\\\'t figure out how to put it back together. ',0,712,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(15516,2598,'2002-04-05 20:08:12',188,'bark','This girl leaned over to kiss me at a stoplight in Wesport.  Her foot came off the  Suburban\\\'s brake and we rolled uphill into the car in front of us.  A lot of Trans Am fiberglass died that day.  Luckily her dad was a big deal and he paid for everything.  We might have been 16.',0,282,0,0,'12.110.65.208','en'),(15517,2598,'2002-04-05 20:42:13',82,'simon','bark, my dear, it\\\'s so nice when you come by and comment.',0,60,188,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15518,2602,'2002-04-05 21:08:34',175,'Paul G','Both beautiful and also true - a rare combination.',0,50,0,0,'67.248.78.227','en'),(15519,2599,'2002-04-05 21:10:45',218,'da','<p>the walkman thing may be something of a tangent, but since it\\\'s come up...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i fixed walkmen when i was a kid too - but i actually managed to fix most of the ones i worked on.  opening them up was the worst part, but with a set of small screwdrivers and the knack that i eventually developed for figuring out when a plastic bit could successfully be forced without shattering, it was possible.  inside, it was usually a belt that went, which could sometimes be replaced by appropriately sized rubberbands.  or a gear would have slid off its post and just needed to be put back on with a dab of 2-part epoxy.  i never had too much trouble reassembling them for some reason.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni was on this circuit city warranty plan where i could exchange a broken walkman for a fancier model just by paying the upgrade cost. (they don\\\'t make warranties like this anymore, i think)  since i went through about one a year, by the time i was a junior in high school i was carrying around one damned refined piece of machinery.  it was roughly the size of a tape case, and had solid state controls that would activate with just a brush of your fingers.  but it was really fragile and didn\\\'t live long.  since it was clearly alien technology of some kind, i didn\\\'t even try to open it up.  i just took it back to circuit city and replaced it with one of those waterproof sony models and a nice pair of headphones.\r\n</p>',2,1431,0,0,'128.111.96.65','en'),(15520,2602,'2002-04-05 21:33:48',187,'kandrer','<p>i heard that twins (how often?) elaborate a special language as they grow up. i was happy to discover this since it explained why i couldn\\\'t get a word of some of the songs of the Cocteau Twins.</p><p>\r\n\r\nH and i already share a set of english mistakes. Sondre made fun of us one day: he imagined one of us asking the other <i>hey, how would you say that?</i> and the other would answer a mistake <i>i\\\'m quite sure this is like this...</i><br>\r\nfrom that day we have fun saying we should call him on his mobile phone at any time of day or night. <i>hi Sondre, how would you say that?</i><br></p><p>\r\nanyway, all this is far less romantic than your post, dear lotus. :)',1,677,0,0,'206.15.1.206','en'),(15521,2602,'2002-04-05 23:02:03',59,'s5','(although only one cocteau twin sings - the other twin is a ghost who exists only by studio magic)',3,98,187,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15522,2598,'2002-04-06 05:17:39',185,'orion','I have made out in many places, but oddly enough, never in a vehicle.\r\n<br><br>\r\nAm I missing something?  :^)',1,109,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(15523,2603,'2002-04-06 05:49:18',70,'Fuzzy','Um... hi.  Do you have a fan club?  I am interested in a membership package.',1,76,0,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(15524,2603,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','BOK!  this ROCKS!',6,17,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15525,2598,'2002-04-06 08:51:16',255,'qq','Wankel Rotary Engine',0,20,0,0,'64.81.241.248','en'),(15526,2604,'2002-04-06 19:58:35',66,'Peter','<br><tt>Manny Swindle Used Autos, Lisnaskea Co, Fermanagh, Northern Ireland.</tt>  ',1,83,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15527,2600,'2002-04-06 20:24:46',65,'le','yes, i really like this.',4,24,0,0,'165.247.207.75','en'),(15528,2598,'2002-04-06 20:50:39',17,'austin','you have to be shitting us.  nobody has ever had two corvettes! certainly not matching ones.  :-(',4,97,66,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(15529,2598,'2002-04-06 22:15:38',66,'Peter','<br><tt>one was a metaflake green drop-top, one was the same colour, but t-top. my mom\\\'s had silvery tinted windows. i remember going out for rides when i was little. they were the spoils of mny mom\\\'s first husband\\\'s life-insurance payment when he died. they quickly turned into payment for our house. if you ask me, id rather have lived in a lesser house and kept the \\\'71 stingray. ooooh. but such is the story of my family. nothing good lasted, heh.</tt>',1,468,17,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15530,2598,'2002-04-06 22:51:01',175,'Paul G','- except for you.',0,17,66,0,'67.248.113.124','en'),(15531,2598,'2002-04-06 23:14:51',66,'Peter','<br><tt>true, im managing to persist, albeit 1000 miles from my family, heh.</tt>',1,81,175,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15532,2605,'2002-04-07 07:41:44',65,'le','jamie == jamazon?',6,17,0,0,'209.86.3.157','en'),(15533,2277,'2002-04-07 08:44:20',17,'austin','oh my,<BR><BR>i just experienced a moment of truth, the fruit of reflection.<BR><BR>i have a san francisco beatnik lifestyle!  i am feeling sudden pangs of awe, horror, and i have to say the word, <B>grateful</B> feelings.',4,222,0,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(15534,2602,'2002-04-07 09:24:57',187,'kandrer','i don\\\'t get it; they are actually 2 and the only one singing is using their \\\'private\\\' language, right?',1,111,59,0,'166.90.35.135','en'),(15535,2605,'2002-04-07 14:35:21',89,'sawks','aye',3,3,65,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(15536,2602,'2002-04-07 19:23:32',69,'starlajo','nope, it\\\'s just liz fraser.',0,30,187,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(15537,2602,'2002-04-07 19:25:43',69,'starlajo','well, and Robin Guthrie, but he\\\'s the guitarist.',0,51,187,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(15538,2603,'2002-04-07 20:13:49',88,'deb','I wish I had read that this morning; before my contact lens tried to go off on his own to meet the soft squishy part of my eye. I think I stopped him just in time. ',4,164,0,0,'64.158.0.242','en'),(15539,2605,'2002-04-07 21:33:36',251,'Jamie','yep jamie = jamazon<br><br> vous etes correcte.',1,47,0,0,'62.252.192.6','en'),(15540,2598,'2002-04-07 22:13:06',74,'jo','sigh. i have never really made out in a car. cars have always been the intermediary ground. like <i>oh my god we have to leave here as quick as possible, we will kiss on the way to the car, in the car, in the parking lot, on the landing of the steps, everywhere we have to stop before getting to bed.</i><BR>\r\ni obviously need to go on a road trip with my boy, since i missed out on all this furtive high school sex.',0,416,0,0,'66.32.36.29','en'),(15541,1226,'2002-04-08 01:55:48',65,'le','is this erin gael, who writes me email about chakras? ',0,54,0,0,'165.247.202.86','en'),(15542,2608,'2002-04-08 02:18:13',66,'Peter','<br><tt>:(  but taking time to handle these things is very wise. no one could have anything but respect for someone who focuses on finding continuity and resolution for the disparate parts of their life. good for you, and good luck.</tt>',1,237,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15543,2607,'2002-04-08 02:22:14',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i like the part that says: <i>My life has become a perpetual carousel of the dull and mundane from which I cannot escape. I am the horse shackled to my humdrum existence with rusty carriage-bolts, my non-conforming lead paint paring off, my wooden torso rotting away as I gallop aimlessly into eternity. The music plays on.</i></tt>',1,340,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15544,2608,'2002-04-08 02:49:00',65,'le','why did you choose to post this in a public forum?',6,50,0,0,'165.247.202.86','en'),(15545,2608,'2002-04-08 03:01:03',239,'Poonlarp','why not post it in a public forum?',1,34,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15546,2608,'2002-04-08 03:29:35',65,'le','i don\\\'t know why or why not, that\\\'s why i asked.',6,54,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(15547,1025,'2002-04-08 04:06:19',66,'Peter','<br><tt>nice</tt>',1,17,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15548,2608,'2002-04-08 04:32:38',175,'Paul G','Good luck, Matt.',0,16,0,0,'67.248.78.243','en'),(15549,2609,'2002-04-08 05:59:03',17,'austin','<font size=2>\r\n<PRE>\r\n[22:56] heenie> i wrote a swinney and no one cares.\r\n[22:56] heenie> no comments.\r\n[22:56] heenie> everyone hates me.\r\n[22:56] da> been there\r\nlife sucks   \r\nmy last post has nothing too\r\n[22:56] heenie> it must have sucked.\r\nit is a heartless world\r\n[22:56] heenie> i thought my return to swinney would be heralded with trumpets\r\n[22:56] heenie> and parades.\r\n[22:56] heenie> apparently i\'m just not cool anymore.\r\nit is sunday night\r\n[22:57] heenie> austin what the fuck.\r\npeter is probably alseep\r\n[22:57] heenie> you and me, we\'re the SHIT.\r\n[22:57] <heenie> i mean, it\'s US.\r\nim not the shit, i am a loser\r\n[22:57] <heenie> and these fucking ungrateful shits can\'t even throw us a mercy\r\n[22:57] <heenie> commnent?\r\n[22:57] <heenie> or a comment out of RESPECT for our LEGACY?\r\n[22:57] <heenie> don\'t the early adopters get any props?\r\n[22:57] <heenie> what the fuck?\r\n[22:58] <heenie> i am filled with rage.\r\n</PRE></FONT>',5,975,0,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(15550,2609,'2002-04-08 06:01:48',65,'le','fuck you austin, i knew you were posting that. now everyone will know how \\\"strangely mean\\\" (tm alanna 2002) i really am.',6,126,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(15551,2609,'2002-04-08 06:07:45',20,'jim','the Add a Comment button shouldn\\\'t be above the posts even when there\\\'s only two.  I forgot what else I was going  to say.',6,128,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(15552,2609,'2002-04-08 06:35:11',17,'austin','OCD.',4,4,20,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(15553,2609,'2002-04-08 07:42:57',187,'kandrer','i\\\'ve been to the moma last week. it sucked 100%. well, i should precise we\\\'ve been there on wednesday, it\\\'s the closing day. art likes to make me suffer.',1,162,0,0,'64.156.229.98','en'),(15554,2609,'2002-04-08 08:03:56',175,'Paul G','The weirdness goes to 11.\r\n\r\nAnd what is that I hear?  A Frenchman complaining?  Such a thing has no precedent!',0,111,0,0,'67.248.78.243','en'),(15555,2609,'2002-04-08 10:35:04',176,'Scapegoat','I\\\'m confused but congrads on the post-illness, I too am recently post-illness and not cool. Not cool is the new black I think, we\\\'re just  early adopters.<br><br>\r\nThis comment needs a deluded attempt at humour category, or maybe a self depreciating humour.<br>    ',1,271,0,0,'64.229.160.243','en'),(15556,2599,'2002-04-08 10:55:08',176,'Scapegoat','I find using machines sometimes to be an extremely  altering experience. I can\\\'t explain it very well but when I\\\'m driving for example its like my body extends into the car. My nervous system extends out through the steering wheel, pedals, and seat with every vibration or rattle sent up the frame of the car straight into my brain, just like an itch or something I might feel on my arm.<br><br>\r\nReading this over it seems a little flaky, so I figure I may as well go all out and mention that each machine exerts its own influence as well its not like my being expands into an object that is simply empty.',0,612,0,0,'64.229.160.243','en'),(15557,2599,'2002-04-08 10:59:47',176,'Scapegoat','I can only imagine the influence a back hoe or front end loader might have on me. I think a helicopter would be the top on my list of machines I would like to learn to use/experience followed closely by a welding torch.',0,219,0,0,'64.229.160.243','en'),(15558,2599,'2002-04-08 12:23:56',66,'Peter','<br><tt>welding is nice; i think it would be fun to fly a helecopter, too, and then jump out of it.</tt>',1,104,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15559,2611,'2002-04-08 13:04:55',66,'Peter','<br><tt>a <a href=\"http://www.erowid.org/experiences/exp.php3?ID=11245\">cure</a>. <a href=\"http://www.ambient.ca/bodmod/hole.html\">heh</a> heh <a href=\"http://www.brainbloodvolume.com/blood_ego.htm\">heh</a>.</tt>',1,105,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15560,2612,'2002-04-08 13:18:36',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ive dreamed about a similar endless/non-functional bathroom, before. odd. in my version, theres lots of marble, and i keep thinking to myself: <i>a bathroom that looks this nice should work. whats wrong here?</i> soemtimes, i whistle in the endless bathroom, and listen to the echo.</tt>',1,295,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15561,2603,'2002-04-08 13:51:56',155,'blue stocking','*grin* actually...with you twisting my arm and everything, i confess that there is a very basic fan club in place. you can join it <a href=\\\"http://originalbs.pitas.com\\\">here</a>\r\n',0,185,70,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15562,2611,'2002-04-08 13:53:43',89,'sawks','hey bro i enjoyed this bit of writing<br>\r\n(PHOTONS!)<br>\r\n<br>\r\nbut if no coffee == headache, maybe you need to fix that?<br>\r\ni like to drink some coffee once in a while when i am tired, but all you cafe culture motherfuckers might as well be sucking dick for crack :P<br>\r\n',2,276,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(15563,2611,'2002-04-08 14:06:06',155,'blue stocking','re: blurb<br>\r\nwe do all need a good whinge, but few of us can do it as well as you. i really enjoyed reading this, though i feel bad enjoying it. up until recently, i never even had headaches. when i got my first one, i couldn\\\'t believe the pain. so i can\\\'t even imagine what migraine sufferers go through. ',0,314,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15564,2603,'2002-04-08 14:08:12',155,'blue stocking','tee hee! thank you! whenever something bizarre (good bizarre, bad bizarre, doesn\\\'t matter) happens to me, a tiny part of me is consoled by the fact that i can always rant about it.',0,183,62,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15565,2603,'2002-04-08 14:09:35',155,'blue stocking','hi! i have a contact lens case still unopened from the optician sitting on my bookshelf. for good reason. i think.',0,114,88,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15566,2602,'2002-04-08 14:10:44',155,'lotus','i\\\'ve never heard of the Cocteau twins. thank you for reading this very old piece everyone!',0,93,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15567,2601,'2002-04-08 15:09:24',123,'dcc','<tt>\"It fits her in all the right places, hugging her ample bosom like a joey to it\'s marsupial mother.\"</tt><BR>\r\n\r\nthis brought back a difficult memory. a new challenge to my day in emotion. i\'m not sure if i should thank you or damn you to hell, but i still enjoyed reading this.',4,292,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(15568,2612,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i just woke up. i dreamed that julie andrews was a pickup artist. <a href=\\\"http://www.eek.org\\\">oof</a> was confronting her about it, and i woke up just as she was shrieking in her inimitable voice, \\\"me? a pickup ahrtist? how daaahre you!\\\" they were arguing inside a theatre. oof was really mad at being played for a sucker. don\\\'t play the oof. *yawn*\r\n',1,367,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15569,2609,'2002-04-08 15:29:51',74,'jo','i didn\\\'t comment because i was really drunk when i read it so my brain kept deleting sentences and zooming in on others like missing egg and seeing the exhibit with stefan and descriptions of barfing and then when i woke up this morning and came here to say something, this article wasn\\\'t listed on the front page and so i started thinking that i had <i>dreamed</i> it. oh whiskey. fucking hell, i have to call the texas motherfucking workforce commission today, i have to call them today from <b>chicago</b> and be like, \\\"yup, looking for jobs, looking for jobs, send my check, yup.\\\"',4,596,0,0,'66.32.36.29','en'),(15570,2612,'2002-04-08 17:34:54',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ive just woken from an accidental nap. i had a 5 second dream, right before i woke: a tugboat steering up the harlem river from the hudson, partially obscured by teh bridge abutments there. someone on the shore pointing it out to his kid, saying \\\"look at the tugboat go!\\\". then, we all suddenly noticed that the tugboat was sinking, once it cleared the bridges, spinning around in an underwater-bound vorted, making this horriffic panic-whistling sound.</tt>',1,472,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15571,2609,'2002-04-08 17:39:45',124,'gus','dude. no one ever comments when I write real posts. people only comment if I talk about bullshit. that is life. you will not be rewarded for the good you do in life, you gotta do it for the sake of good. you WILL however be rewarded for every little shit you take. ahh... america!',5,280,0,0,'64.160.53.160','en'),(15572,2609,'2002-04-08 17:49:40',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yeah, i wrote one lately that i thought was one of my all time best; people had zilch to say. ahh, god bless the comment-free fraternity of the well-written post.</tt>',1,171,124,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15573,2608,'2002-04-08 18:07:06',195,'matt','i\\\'m not really sure why i chose to put it on swinney as opposed to e-mailing peter. maybe it was a half-thought out gesture at openness. i\\\'m not really sure.',1,163,0,0,'209.208.17.98','en'),(15574,2609,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','sometimes i want to comment but i have nothing amazing to share. i shouldn\'t write things like \"ZOWIE THAT WAS NEATO KEEN I LOVES IT\" but sometimes that\'s all i have to offer. and also, Peter, that entry you are referring to, it bowled me over, it was so good. soo good, that i couldn\'t sully it with a lameass comment. but it was incredibly written. the words had a beautiful rhythm.<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni don\'t think we need to rate posts. i hope we\'re not going in that direction. <br>\r\n<br>\r\ndeep breaths. repeat after me:<br> \r\n<br>\r\ni am a rockstar.<br>\r\neverybody loves me.<br>\r\npeople want to steal my panties.<br>\r\n<br>\r\netc etc<br>',1,631,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15575,2611,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','this rocked. more entertaining whinging i have never read. perversely, i will tell you now that your posting made me go do yoga this morning and it was heavenly good. i am two inches taller. you should get your yoga room setup and have meditation hours. mmmm! also, yoga videos are really nice too. no need to leave the house.',1,326,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15576,2612,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i only dream about enclosed spaces when i\\\'m depressed. i have the tendency to ignore being unhappy until my dreams force me to realize that i need a change. do you need change, dear le? perhaps an adventure will cure what ails ya...',1,235,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15577,2612,'2002-04-08 18:46:45',124,'gus','the other night I had this dream about a small european town. I was with some others, we were all sort of mysterious. we couldnt find out hotel, turned out it was becuase you couldnt SEE the hotel, it was just there, you had to know the right spot and just walk into it.\r\n<p>\r\nThen later in the dream, I was with some trench coated men investigating a femme fatale type. We went into a narrow stall with a corrugated tin roof, it had a toilet, must have been an outhouse of sorts. We were searching for clues about this women, and she had some important things to do with meat products. the meat products were the clues, the woman had vanished. The walls were hung with various cuts of meat, sausage, and the like.',0,714,0,0,'64.160.53.160','en'),(15578,23,'2002-04-08 19:10:40',196,'jasonp','\nYes I believe in all of the above.\nThat was very cleverly and insightfully written also.\nBuddhist philosophy kicks ass.\n\na little Pre-historic swinney treasure...',0,163,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(15579,2612,'2002-04-08 20:07:24',175,'Paul G','Sometimes a sausage is just a sausage.',0,38,124,0,'67.248.113.79','en'),(15580,2598,'2002-04-08 20:13:16',17,'austin','i went to the grocery market at lunch and parked to the left of a filthy baby-blue late 70\\\'s american sedan.  i looked out the passenger window and saw a young highschool girl in the driver\\\'s  seat talking to herself.  then when i got out of my car i saw that her boyfriend was laying down on the bench seat with his head in her lap.  i took a moment to remember that highschool was nice sometimes.',1,404,0,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(15581,2612,'2002-04-08 20:23:48',82,'vaginasausage.com','and sometimes it\\\'s a VAGINA SAUSAGE!',0,39,175,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15582,2612,'2002-04-08 20:37:04',196,'jasonp','Very short armchair analysis:\nProblems with guilt and being unable at controlling privacy/personal boundaries.\n\nI had a really fucked up dream this morning. Here it is fresh from memory:\n\nI\\\'m in some kind of restaraunt after eating breakfast, and I\\\'m supposed to be stepping out only for a minute.\nPeople are expecting me back but I feel like I want to get away for some reason.\nI go through a back entrance connected to a bar to get away without being seen.\nI end up in a sort of mall type building with different bars and clubs in it.\nIt seem to be a collection of places I remember from the past.\nI\\\'m walking through crowds and feeling slightly intrigued, yet also anxiety in trying to get out. \nIt turns dark out and there is a growing feeling of anxiety about the people who I was supposed to meet back with after breakfast.\n\nThen I need to use the bathroom. I go into one place where it\\\'s dark lined with glowing \nflourescent colors with fishbowl type windows with people in bondage gear looking out.\nAs I go in further it looks more like a hospital with all kind of people in medical type contraptions.\nThey are restrained and have tubes, open wounds, bandages, etc. It is very disturbing and I start to feel freaked out. They seem to be willingly chosing their being there like some kind of \nwierd SM/body modification thing.\nAll throughout there are the sounds of people screaming and shrieking that\\\'s very disturbing.\nI go to what I think is the bathroom. People are attached to the wall in something looking looking like astronaut suits which functions as a toilet. There are no dividers so they all can see each other.\nThe faces of the people are doped up and it\\\'s not clear whether they are patients in a hospital, being tortured or choosing it voluntarilly.\nAt this point it\\\'s too much and I have to leave immediately no matter what.\n\n<BR><BR>\n<B>Then, I wake up feeling extremely creeped out and glad to be awake.</B>\nI\\\'m going through some <B>issues</B> lately. My dreams aren\\\'t that often fucked up.',0,2043,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(15583,2611,'2002-04-08 21:04:27',75,'tyler','i used to get migraines. now i get excedrine migraines which work oh so well. good luck.\r\n<p>\r\nps-they also contain caffeine ',1,125,0,0,'165.247.164.211','en'),(15584,2612,'2002-04-08 21:28:11',65,'le','\\\" Problems with guilt and being unable at controlling privacy/personal boundaries\\\"\r\n<br><br>\r\noh that\\\'s not me at all.',6,127,196,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(15585,2597,'2002-04-08 23:06:08',200,'chiclet','chickens are pretty unobtrusive as long as you don\\\'t keep a rooster.  my mom used to have chickens in her backyard.  it was neat to sit out there while they were wandering around and listen to their little clucking and cooing noises.  hypnotism by chicken.',0,259,0,0,'12.233.47.46','en'),(15586,2609,'2002-04-09 01:00:03',124,'gus','you dork, you just gave him a beautiful comment right there. you could have SAID THAT! heh. hehe.',0,97,57,0,'64.160.53.160','en'),(15587,2611,'2002-04-09 01:27:08',64,'Joanna','mmmm sucking dick for crack!',0,28,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(15588,2609,'2002-04-09 01:39:49',187,'kandrer','to Paul G and all:<br>\r\nyou don\\\'t have a clue about how little clue i have. wasn\\\'t complaining, man; just read m-o-m-a and it reminded me i failed to get in there. wanted to make it look like complain, yes.<br>\r\n<b>weeb</b>, though was clear as the fresh water i never can drink cause my teeth would hurt. and true, so true... the comments after a post have something in common with the comments after making love.<br>\r\n<i>(please, write stupid, write for me your litl\\\' frenchie to understand :])</i>',1,509,0,0,'208.223.155.15','en'),(15589,2616,'2002-04-09 02:50:10',124,'gus','here it is:\r\n<img src=\\\"http://floozy.com/allison/laoswaterfall.jpg\\\">',0,74,0,0,'64.160.53.160','en'),(15590,2608,'2002-04-09 04:33:11',75,'tyler','i for one am glad you did post it, because it made me think. good luck, man. ',1,77,0,0,'165.247.164.211','en'),(15591,2615,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>but a war could, and did come. a war so terrible that no one could have anticipated it\\\'s destructive force.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>a year ago, old farmer macy had perfected his time machine.  people scoffed at him while he tinkered in his barn.  \\\"old farmer macy,\\\" they tittered, \\\"he thinks he can time travel!\\\"  little did they know, macy had already achieved time travel, had seen the future in it\\\'s full glory.  little did they know he\\\'d learned the secret of harnessing nuclear fission, and was on his way to making the first nuclear bomb...</p>',6,560,0,0,'165.228.130.12','en'),(15592,2609,'2002-04-09 06:08:23',175,'Paul G','Kandrer, mon frÃ¨re, ne sont pas alarmÃ©s. Vous Ãªtes avez bien aimÃ©. I, d\\\'autre part, AM pas aussi populaire. Mes mots sont de peu de consÃ©quence. J\\\'ai fait une tentative faible d\\\'humeur, rien davantage. Et, s\\\'il vous plaÃ®t, pardonnez ma propre injustice Ã  votre langage. DÃ©sirent ardemment de phase la diffÃ©rence. Paul.',0,330,187,0,'67.248.113.26','en'),(15593,2612,'2002-04-09 07:11:12',175,'Paul G','Oh I say, no pics?  I was going to offer a friendly: \\\"Well played,\\\" but I\\\'m a bit unsure now.',0,102,82,0,'67.248.113.26','en'),(15594,2616,'2002-04-09 07:22:51',86,'egg','the Laos are some of my people.',0,31,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(15595,2612,'2002-04-09 12:04:32',180,'auntie','when i was pregnant with you i visited an apartment just like you described.  in greece.  this is very strange.  boxy basement apartment with more stairs leading down. what is up with that?<br><br>\r\n\r\nbut there weren\\\'t any bodies involved that i remember. perhaps all the recent deaths in the family are causing you to dream about death?<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhen i was 3, i began having a recurring dream of cartoon characters walking up and down stairs in an unfinished attic space.  they looked like miss peach comic people (they had giant heads on little bodies)  the weird thing was....\r\n\r\nall the little comic people had their heads chopped off and they were hanging behind them and bouncing off their backs, held by a flap of skin and they were bleeding yellow blood.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni have had this dream for 45 years, about once a year, and the only thing i have ever been able to attach it to is when i feel moving anxiety of some sort.',4,934,65,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(15596,2616,'2002-04-09 13:13:53',123,'dcc','<i>\\\"In a town ON the river, at a restaurant where I could SEE the river, I ordered fish. I was told they were out.\\\"</i>\r\n<p>wow, that made me laugh a lot. i am glad you went elsewhere though. that would have disturbed me greatly',0,234,124,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(15597,2612,'2002-04-09 17:30:58',82,'vaginasausage.com','<a href=\\\"http://www.vaginasausage.com/mascot.jpg\\\">hi!</a>',0,63,175,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15598,2612,'2002-04-09 18:20:58',124,'gus','you remind me of my horrifying recurring nightmare as a child. not cartoon characters, but the fuzzy happy friendly seasame street monsters. \r\n<p>\r\nexcept... They were not friendly, they were the same monsters, and they were still puppets, but they had BIG SHARP TEETH, in my dream my entire field of vision was a black 2 dimensional tv screen, the monsters were crammed all together. they were growling, gnashing their teeth, and moving slighty right to left to right to left with every step, moving like one unit. I always had to run into mom and dad\\\'s room after that one. ',0,579,180,0,'64.160.53.160','en'),(15599,2612,'2002-04-09 20:40:40',175,'Paul G','Good day to you, sir.  Or, ma\\\'am.  Very pleased to have made your acquaintance.  You must have a busy day ahead.  Carry on.',0,126,82,0,'67.248.52.174','en'),(15600,2617,'2002-04-09 21:31:37',186,'ook','<P>The smell of fresh rain is one of those things that I have always thought was over-romaticised in poems. It smells like worm guts. You are not the only killer. Frequently they drown in mass puddle graves all by themselves. Tragique!</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Similarly, in many climates spring itself does not smell sweetly of flowers and young love, but of dog poo and dead grass that has been stored for months under the snow and is now composting in a great ode to the fecundity of the season.</P>',2,487,0,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(15601,2609,'2002-04-10 00:01:02',187,'kandrer','[x] goodest friendly :]',1,23,175,0,'208.223.155.15','en'),(15602,2616,'2002-04-10 00:23:11',90,'leonard','i used to work with a girl (manny tang) and she was from laos. she smelled like a plastic-coated couch and the inside of a tennis ball can.',2,139,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(15603,2615,'2002-04-10 00:36:44',185,'orion','I think with a title like that I just have to post a comment classified as \\\"BURN! BURN!\\\".  ;^)',6,100,0,0,'216.201.248.18','en'),(15604,2609,'2002-04-10 00:46:26',129,'moebius','well, i\\\'m useless; i\\\'m posting a comment for your article, dear le, and i\\\'ve not read it yet. i\\\'m only getting around to the last five days worth of swinney articles this afternoon and it\\\'s late in the day and i\\\'m tired and all i have patience for at the moment are everyone else\\\'s short, odd comments about your piece. which are all very interesting in their own sake.<br><br>but i\\\'m sure your piece is lovely. how could it not be? :D<br><br>now it\\\'s time for vegan chocolate cake, if such a thing truly exists.',1,539,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(15605,2609,'2002-04-10 00:59:16',129,'moebius','<br>i went ahead & read this piece anyway. and hoogasian flowers, it\\\'s great. makes me a)want to go to MOMA right now and b)aspire to write pieces of equivalent entertainment value. so there ya go, le.<br><br>also, peter, i loved yr piece also. i also generally adore most everything by lotus, hool, austin, s5, egg, gus, and even ix. ix is a good writer. consider yourselves complimented in advance. and those of you who didn\\\'t make that list: you\\\'re all my favorites, just for showing up here and giving it a try.<br><br>cake.',1,537,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(15606,2614,'2002-04-10 01:04:02',124,'gus','ooh. and they have tap. <br> if I wasnt so out of shape/practice I\\\'d do modern classes. it\\\'s just depressing when your body wont do what it used to!',0,154,0,0,'64.160.53.160','en'),(15607,2617,'2002-04-10 01:07:11',124,'Dorothy Parker','<b>Thought for a Sunshiny Morning</b>\r\n<p>\r\nIt costs me never a stab nor squirm<br>\r\nTo tread by chance upon a worm.</br>\r\n\\\"Aha, my little dear,\\\" I say,</br>\r\n\\\"Your clan will pay me back one day.\\\"',0,208,0,0,'64.160.53.160','en'),(15608,2612,'2002-04-10 01:56:41',206,'josta','when i was 4 i had a dream that there was a new monster puppet on sesame street and oscar, in a fit of jealous hatred, wanted to off him. oscar found out that this new puppet was allergic to vanilla extract, so he had an unwitting Luis put some in New Guy\\\'s cereal. upon eating it the new puppet became a scared-looking mosaic in the sidewalk outside oscar\\\'s trash can. luis, horrified, went to visit his grandmother to get some turquoise (apparently the antidote). he was halfway up the mountain when i woke up. i was terrified. i woke up my parents but when they asked what the dream was about i was too embarassed to tell them so i made up something about being shot by a man in a trenchcoat. this is the only vivid memory i have of being 4.',2,750,124,0,'152.163.195.198','en'),(15609,2599,'2002-04-10 03:02:05',176,'Scapegoat','When I was a wee lad my Fathers squadron used to have family days out in the bush in northern Alberta. They would fly out members of the military\\\'s family to this cabin/lodge that the department of defense owned. The high point in the day after the BBQ was the candy drop from the helicopter. All the kids would be corralled on top of this hill and then a helicopter would fly around the hill in a circle dropping candy like rain. When they were sure the kids wouldn\\\'t be hurt from falling rock candy they\\\'d open the gate and it was a race to pick up the candy.<br><br>\r\nThey used to take you for rides in the helicopter. My father was the guy that would sit half in the doorway half on the skid while they were flying. I just remember it being really it feeling smooth compared to planes. As for jumping out I can see the appeal but I would probably opt to jump from a plane if I was going to sky dive. ',0,913,0,0,'64.229.166.164','en'),(15610,2599,'2002-04-10 03:15:17',66,'Peter','<br><tt>there weren\\\'t many helicopters in my small town, growing up; but, there was <i>one</i>. the <i>Life Force</i> rescue copter, used to shuttle victims to nearby biggertown\\\'s hospital. so, over the years, you learnt that the only time you saw a helecopter flying was when there had been some sort of terrible accident or wreck or something.<br><br>\r\nit got to be the in-joke after a while that, anytime you saw someone get hurt, a bike wreck, an injury on the football field, etc... you were like \\\"ooh, free helicopter ride!\\\".</tt>',1,548,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15611,2611,'2002-04-10 04:35:22',261,'sine','horrendous vomitous migraines.\r\n\r\nyour brain blood vessels shrink up and your brain is deprived of blood. this tends to hurt. ouch!\r\n\r\nI have found the best remedy for me. Strong strong coffee and a headstand for as long as I can stand it. Usually the headstand first, then the coffee, along with 3 tylenol will do the trick.\r\n\r\ncopious water seems to help everything.\r\n\r\nbut seriously, try a headstand. get a nice flat pillow, scoot it up against a wall, and raise your body up against said wall and relax into the pose. it will totally get the blood flowing in your brain again, pronto. the caffeine further dilates the blood vessels, and then the tylenole or whatnot will take away any residual pain. water, you know, water is good.\r\n\r\nok! immitrex is so evil. i had the nose squirty kind a few years back and it caused me to vomit for 2 days. not fun.',1,855,0,0,'205.179.65.211','en'),(15612,2592,'2002-04-10 04:40:57',130,'treetop','Hi Lotus,\r\n\r\n<p>I really like this.  I am instantly sucked into anything that involves the moon and glitter.  I dont have much time to comment on articles anymore.  Today I took a little time and as I was scanning through the names of articles, \\\"Grace\\\" stuck out.  I have a friend named Grace.  She likes to eat Red Vines and drink coffee.  She is the goddess of her knitting circle.  She is beautiful, inside and out.',3,424,156,0,'172.131.128.3','en'),(15613,2615,'2002-04-10 04:55:32',75,'tyler','Behind old farmer macy\\\'s barn, however, a different kind of bomb was about to go off. George whispered softly in Alexandra\\\'s ear, and she knelt behind him and began to undo the two buttons on the ass flap of his overalls. \\\"Darling, you know what I want you to do,\\\" he said softly. \\\"Remember the horses, Alexandra. The horses, that night.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nAlexandra remembered. ',1,385,0,0,'165.247.164.211','en'),(15614,2615,'2002-04-10 05:17:20',130,'treetop','nice!',4,5,0,0,'172.131.128.3','en'),(15615,2609,'2002-04-10 05:47:52',175,'Paul G','Kandrer, je ne peux pas parler franÃ§ais. Ce beaucoup que vous pouvez voir. Ainsi, pourquoi ne pas faire un Godzilla pour moi Ã  l\\\'intÃ©rieur de de mon pantalon ?',0,162,187,0,'67.248.113.131','en'),(15616,2611,'2002-04-10 06:11:46',63,'olivia','my doc sez that it is too much blood flowing through the veins.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nthat\\\'s why blocking blood at your temples makes you feel better.<br>',6,146,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15617,2615,'2002-04-10 07:46:00',176,'Scapegoat','Joe the crow perched a top farmer Macy\\\'s barn watched as Alexandra and George rushed inside the barn up to the loft to get away from the prying eyes of Nicholas. Turning his neck Joe watched while the two of them proceeded to do what to Joe bird brain could only possibly be something relate to fornication.<br><br>\r\nThis is the only act that would justify in Joe\\\'s brain how the two of them didn\\\'t notice the pile of hay they had chosen was starting to slide. Sure enough their own activities provided the motion that lead to the two of them sliding over the edge and falling to the floor of the barn admist the pile of hay that moments ago was the third partner in their menage a trois.<br><br>\r\nGeorge and Alexandra didn\\\'t notice that they had fallen right into the part of the barn that farmer Macy\\\'s used for a lab. George trying to lift himself up reached a hand up onto the work bench and mistakenly pushed a large red button an a remote of some kind. Joe noticed a look of concern on Alexandra\\\'s face, as she noticed the two strange antennae that create the time warp bubble charge up, just before it zapped both her and George buck naked into some unknown time and nearly blinded Joe with the flash.',0,1226,0,0,'64.229.160.244','en'),(15618,2611,'2002-04-10 08:34:01',260,'Layla','I\\\'ve heard of actually getting up and about to get blood moving.  Also putting your hands in warm water.. opens up the blood vessels.  ',2,138,0,0,'62.64.227.24','en'),(15619,2598,'2002-04-10 08:52:11',64,'Joanna','We were sitting in his car tonight, some kind of stationwagon pulled up in front of my apartment building.  My door was open and the ignition was still on when suddenly we started to have one of those defining our relationships conversations which wasn\\\'t made any easier by the fact that he suddenly kissed me in the middle of it, so we kept on kissing for a while, all the while trying to agree on the fact that it wouldn\\\'t be a good idea for him to come up.  He apologised when he stroked my hair, saying maybe that was the wrong thing to do, and I said to him \\\"you just kissed me - how is stroking my hair any worse?\\\" - although it does feel worse.  ',1,665,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(15620,2615,'2002-04-10 08:55:43',59,'s5','<p>\r\nalexandra pressed snooze for the final time. she brushed away the\r\ncigarette ashes from the sheets and the remains of a fucked up dream\r\nfrom the corners of her eyes. she coughed four times and scratched herself.\r\n</p><p>\r\na neat pile of twenty dollar bills sat on the milkcrate by the bed.\r\nwritten on the back of the ATM receipt: \\\"keep the change luv - macy\r\n(the doc) ps. don\\\'t call me\\\"\r\n</p><p>\r\n\\\"FUCK YOU YOU FUCKING PIECE OF SHIT\\\"\r\n</p><p>\r\natlanta was no different to her than los angeles. the air left the\r\nsame foul sheen on everything it touched. (but it never occurred to\r\nalexandra that she should stop smoking.) and the people, fake and\r\ndelusional, stuck to her like the genital sores she caught from\r\ngeorge. that he caught from macy.\r\n</p><p>\r\nalexandra clenched her fists stuffed with the twenty dollar bills and\r\nscreamed until her face turned red, then purple. \\\"FUCK YOU YOU FUCKING\r\nPIECE OF SHIT.\\\" she kicked the milkcrate, twice, jumped on it, twice,\r\nand threw the money with all her weight at the lamp in the corner of\r\nthe room. dissatisfied with the anemic destructive force of paper, she picked\r\nup the lamp and smashed the telephone repeatedly, as if to drive a stake\r\nthrough the heart of the floor.\r\n</p><p>\r\non cue, the telephone rang.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe effect of a ringing telephone is no different than an alarm clock:\r\nit reminds us that reality is looking for us on the other side. alexandra\r\nstopped and absorbed her renewed self-awareness. she stared at the telephone\r\n(shattered lamp parts hanging by wire still wrapped around her wrist), and\r\nwatched how the receiver would jump a fraction of an inch with each ring.\r\n</p><p>\r\n\\\"hi this is nicholas and alexandra, leave a message after the\r\nbeep. and if you\\\'re calling about the mustang we sold it so don\\\'t\r\nleave a message. umm. okay. (beep)\\\"\r\n</p><p>\r\n\\\"alex. answer the fucking phone. it\\\'s nick. george got parole and he\\\'s\r\nleaving los angeles. he\\\'s coming to atlanta, alex. (pause) look just fucking\r\ncall me back if you get this. i\\\'m at work.\\\"\r\n</p><p>\r\nalexandra continued to breathe heavily like a jogger suffering through\r\nan atlanta august. she didn\\\'t call nick.\r\n</p><p>\r\nshe called macy.\r\n</p>',1,2246,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15621,2615,'2002-04-10 11:16:09',196,'jasonp','\nHer brow was furrowed and sweaty while she nervously flipped her air and looked up at the ceiling.\n<BR>\n\\\"Okay, godammit...\\\", she muttered.\n<BR>\nShe picked up the battered phone and called Macy.\n<BR>\nMacy happened to be staring at the ceiling with a beer in his hand while sitting in his barca lounger. If it weren\\\'t for his shaved head and tweety bird T-shirt that was ridiculously too small to fit his oversized waist his appearance and dwelling would be so much the stereotypical southerner white trash slob that it would be unbelievable. He picked up the phone.<BR>\n\\\"Macy. Macy darlin\\\', are you cross at me sugar?\\\". Her voice wavered while waiting for the response she hoped for but knew probably wouldn\\\'t get.\n<BR>\n\\\"Not cross at all. Bitch, I got me the DZT5 now. Whatchu gone do about it? I got it all. Fifty nine different varieties, blue and purple too. Me and the pied piper are going to town.\\\"\n<BR>\nDZT5 was the new designer drug that had a variety of unique and amazing effects. Most notable was the intensification and extension of the orgasm which led to trance like states conducive to clairavoyance and psychic time travel. There were also a long series of bizzare mental side effects that progressed with use. Eventually it destroyed the minds ability to distinguish the psychic level impressions from mundane reality which led to psychosis. Only fourteen people knew of it\\\'s existence or potential.<BR>\n<BR>\nHe then muttered under his breath in a conpiratorial tone, \\\"Where\\\'s my beer? You got my beer?...\\\"\nHe looked at the beer in his hand at opened his mouth mock horror then giggled like a little girl. He composed himself and continued in a business like tone, \\\"You can tell your hebrew friend George I don\\\'t care how many cops, or lawyers, or whatever he buys off, or what kind of doughnuts he\\\'s eatin, or anything. Nothin. I\\\'ve got the formula, and I\\\'ve got the formula. And so that means... YOU do NOT have the formula, nor the fifty nine varieties!\\\"\n<BR>\nFrom the other end of the phone Alexandra gritted her teeth. Her face contorted in rage while her eyes rolled to the back of her head and then back down like two lasers glaring down at the phone. Her breathing came out in labored hisses. The trembling look of put upon gentility had entirely shattered. Something like a grunt came out of her throat which then turned into a growl of pure animal agression which ended in a shriek. After a few more grunts she paused to let him continue.<BR>\n\\\"Girl, now you know you are good. And I\\\'d like to have you along and all, but we know about the... current situation, right? A man\\\'s got to do what a man\\\'s got to do. You know...\\\"<BR>\n\\\"You shut the fuck up! SHUTUP! I\\\'m gonna get you... you crazy fucking redneck asshole!\\\"<BR>\n\\\"Girl let me tell you this. There are many ways to get there. One is by travel. There are many things to do. And you can do them. We all try to do our best so don\\\'t let it get you down. I reckon it\\\'s too damn late. But that\\\'s allright cuz it will all work out somehow. We all go through it. I don\\\'t know what\\\'s in store for either of us, but it will somehow work out I reckon. Just go out and take a look at the moon and see what I mean.\\\"<BR>\nAll she could bear to do was press the receiver into her forehead, which left a red mark. Her body convulsed as she sobbed and tears welled up. Finally she heard the click of the phone hanging up.\n',0,3487,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(15622,2615,'2002-04-10 11:56:11',65,'le','she lay there on the floor for some time, reveling in her own self pity. she was proud, really, that she could have sunk this low. the phone emitted a shrill beeping sound near her ear and the synthetic carpet began to make her bare skin itch.\r\n<br><br>\r\nsome time later, who could say how long, she began to stir. she lifted herself off the floor and limped into the bathroom. in times of upheaval, a bath and some lines of DZT7 can cure anything.\r\n<br><br>\r\non the side of the tub, she carefully unstrapped the elaborate prosthetics from the scarred stumps that used to be her legs. she turned one of the false limbs over in her hands. a day didn\\\'t go by that she didn\\\'t spend time admiring these prosthetics. they were truly a work of art, a technological wonder. the gunmetal finish, the transparent knee windows so she could view the titanium gears in action inside, the LED display. there was nothing these fake legs couldn\\\'t do. absentmindedly she checked the bullet chamber. yep, loaded. \r\n<br><br>\r\n<i>it would be fucked up if i killed myself with my own body</i>, she thought, morbidly, fingering the weapon control panel on the prosthetic usually belonging to her right stump. things had been shitty. so much had been shitty. <i>but i\\\'m not going to let  the bastards get me down</i>.\r\n<br><br>\r\nshe punched the water temperature into the keypad near the tub, and waited as the tub began to fill. staring into space, she remembered a time when macy never would have dared betray her... \r\n<br><br>',6,1519,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(15623,2603,'2002-04-10 13:14:11',155,'blue stocking','for anybody who cares, i changed my proto-webspace so the fan club is now here:\r\n\r\n<a href=\\\"http://lotusbasic.pitas.com\\\">visit</a>',0,136,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15624,2592,'2002-04-10 13:57:57',155,'lotus','hi treetop. thank you so much for reading and writing. i too, am instantly in love with anything involving moon and glitter. :) your friend sounds great.',0,153,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15625,2617,'2002-04-10 13:59:57',155,'blue stocking','you are so right. i had to step over hundreds of rain-wet earth worms yesterday.',0,80,186,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15626,2609,'2002-04-10 14:04:44',155,'lotus','*swoon* now i\\\'m all happy. i used to read every new article posted here, but now i can\\\'t keep up. still, there are a few writers i read religiously.',0,154,129,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15627,2571,'2002-04-10 14:09:27',155,'lotus','thank you. i am a big fan of your poems.',0,40,195,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15628,2615,'2002-04-10 15:28:36',75,'tyler','With a sigh, George rolled over on his side and relaxed his body, starting at his feet and working his way up, muscle by muscle. There was a time, before his new job and his new responsibilities, maybe back in college, where he didn\\\'t have to masturbate just to fall asleep...well, he did do it every night in college, but not for the same reasons. And the fantasies definitely weren\\\'t this fucked up. \\\"Man, that was fucked up,\\\" he whispered to himself just before drifting away into the womb of sleep. \\\"Remember the horses? What was that all about? And prosthetic legs? Jesus Christ.\\\" ',1,604,0,0,'165.247.164.211','en'),(15629,2614,'2002-04-10 16:15:48',246,'erb','i actually heard last night that Dance Mission is better.  saturday mornings at 10, Shemaine will rock your out-of-shape ass!  join me for some wigglin, folks.',2,159,0,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(15630,2614,'2002-04-10 16:41:45',156,'lotus','you write so beautifully i sometimes want to memories entire paragraphs. i was going to quote my favourite lines just now, but when i went and reread this whole piece, i couldn\\\'t just cut words so heartlessly. thank you so much for posting this. i really really hate DST. i am still woozy.',0,292,246,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15631,2621,'2002-04-10 16:47:06',17,'austin','i had a highschool dream this morning that involved an earthquake.<BR><BR>when i left the house the sun was shining and i sang o\\\' what a beautiful morning...<BR><BR>i got to work and there was a single krispy kreme on my desk.',1,229,0,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(15632,2211,'2002-04-10 16:47:53',156,'lotus','i\\\'m giving myself a bittersweet present. i am reading and rereading all of erb\\\'s writing. and wondering -- is this all there is? i want more.',0,147,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15633,2619,'2002-04-10 17:04:18',76,'chi chi','actually when i was younger and even before me in the sixties and early seventies they used to tell arabs about to go to the US to look for Kosher butchers to find \\\"halal\\\" meats. in essence they are the same, prayed over and butchered.',0,241,0,0,'64.160.46.14','en'),(15634,2621,'2002-04-10 17:23:54',86,'egg','\\\"fin\\\"? what are you, french?',0,34,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(15635,2619,'2002-04-10 17:46:30',156,'lotus','afaik, muslims can eat kosher meat.',0,35,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15636,2619,'2002-04-10 18:04:38',176,'Scapegoat','Good to know this. I have always been curious why Muslims and Jews have special way to prepare food but Christians not so much. Anyone know the answer to this?',0,159,0,0,'64.229.160.244','en'),(15637,2609,'2002-04-10 18:05:48',175,'Paul G','There are exceptions, of course, but in general the value of a comment is directly proportional to the merit of the commentator from whom it proceeded.',0,151,0,0,'67.248.78.214','en'),(15638,2621,'2002-04-10 18:14:46',83,'adamajah','<p>there is a partially eaten krispy kreme in my drawer.  no dream.</p>',0,71,17,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(15639,2621,'2002-04-10 18:31:36',260,'Layla','<p>There was a girl.  She dreamt for a period of 3 months the same recurring dream almost every night.</p>  \r\n<br>\r\n<p>She is stuck in a tall skyscraper, and everytime she dreams this place she is standing among office cubicles on the top floor.  She has to leave,  she must leave.  So she heads for the lifts.  But everytime, everynight when she tries she is always blocked by obstacles, people, more office cubicles, furniture.  This happens everynight, even though each time, she tries different routes.  Then one night, she makes it, the lifts open, she gets in.</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p><i>The dream has not returned since. </i></p>',1,626,0,0,'62.64.223.221','en'),(15640,2595,'2002-04-10 19:24:37',260,'Layla','..that something is still missing? unfulfilled?',3,47,0,0,'62.64.213.154','en'),(15641,2621,'2002-04-10 20:20:29',195,'matt','last night i dreamed my ears were made of pepperoni, and cops were chasing me trying to eat them off my head.',1,109,0,0,'67.209.229.149','en'),(15642,2367,'2002-04-10 20:32:11',17,'austin','<P>this is the first swinney article that i have ever thought of randomly.  i was somewhere yesterday walking and i remembered the visualization of Menka\\\'s vividly and wanted to go there because that is where i used to hang out.</P>\r\n<P>this piece  captures the highschool hang out so well that my own experiences seemed to connect perfectly.</P>\r\n<P>i also saw it in terms of a movie.  like when i remember a scene from a movie. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> with the combination of the first and second types of rememberences that i mention, the effect is very much like deja v&#250;.</P>',1,575,67,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(15643,2615,'2002-04-10 21:09:00',63,'olivia','<p>with a start, alexandra woke up.  she shook the cobwebs out of her head.  falling asleep, thinking of macy, having a fucked up dream of george, dreaming about her?  if only the last year was a dream, she thought.</p>\r\n<p>looking down at her chilling body in the now lukewarm water, she berated herself for her stupidity.  first the legs, now almost killing herself by falling asleep in the bath.</p>',6,402,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15644,2619,'2002-04-10 21:14:36',122,'hool','well, a devout christian will eat fish on fridays..\r\n\r\ni\\\'m picturing captain highliner marketing \\\'Crucifish\\\' - fish sticks in the shape of the cross.  now with 25% more repentance.\r\n\r\njokes aside, christians also do the communion thing with unleavened bread, so they do have some food relationships with their religion, but not to the degree of kosher or halal.',0,370,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(15645,2619,'2002-04-10 21:16:35',122,'hool','so in conclusion, i didn\\\'t answer your question at all. ',0,59,176,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(15646,2619,'2002-04-10 21:23:56',156,'lotus','someone told me that christians (catholics?) aren\\\'t supposed to eat pork (just like muslims). i think maybe christianity also had food-rules, but they aren\\\'t followed as much. but i have no idea.',0,201,176,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15647,2624,'2002-04-10 22:24:41',124,'gus','I have something to say about this, but I dont know how yet. This speaks to me today. Even the lip balm is relevant.',0,116,0,0,'64.160.53.160','en'),(15648,2619,'2002-04-10 22:27:30',76,'chi chi','also halal is just the slaughter of the animal.. it has to be prayed over, then butchered in a precise manner that allowes the blood to drain. that and the no pork ever. but i think kosher is the strictest cause they\\\'re not to eat the swine of the sea, and certain foods can\\\'t be on the same plate/ touch each other',0,321,0,0,'64.160.46.14','en'),(15649,2624,'2002-04-10 22:29:03',124,'gus','oh, but I can say this. it\'s years old though.\r\n<p>\r\nIt\'s October<br>\r\nand I let you move me<br>\r\nas clouds pile up against the sky.<br>\r\nI can see the mountains peak,<br>\r\nthe way the sun splashes highness everywhere,<br>\r\nyour warm body, <br>\r\njumping off the rocks.<br>\r\nAnd how I always wished<br>\r\nI could touch your hair.',0,269,0,0,'64.160.53.160','en'),(15650,2611,'2002-04-10 23:51:42',124,'gus','I thought caffiene was one of those AVOID AVOID things for migraines? However... it is a good thing to try if you\\\'re having some kinda asthma attack and have no other medicine. the caffiene will open up those little lungy vessels nad things and you\\\'ll be able to breathe again. (even a pepsi was highly effective at 13,000 feet).',0,335,0,0,'64.160.53.160','en'),(15651,2615,'2002-04-11 00:14:27',65,'le','OK there have been far too many dallas-finale oh god it was just a dream cop outs here! ',6,88,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(15652,2615,'2002-04-11 00:35:54',124,'gus','yah it was going good and I was laughing outloud about to pee, but now... its RUINED! ALL RUINED!\r\n<p>\r\nOR IS IT?',0,113,0,0,'64.160.53.160','en'),(15653,2619,'2002-04-11 02:14:27',156,'lotus','well, certain mazhabs (schools of thought/scholars) say muslims cannot eat predatory creatures (e.g. a lion) and even those from the sea with claws etc',0,151,0,0,'64.26.139.140','en'),(15654,2184,'2002-04-11 03:46:11',17,'austin','<B>WAS THIS POST MADE BY <FONT SIZE=5>JESUS</FONT>?</B>',6,55,0,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(15655,2184,'2002-04-11 03:51:30',65,'le','i don\\\'t know,  it doesn\\\'t really match <a href=\\\"http://realjesus.diaryland.com\\\">jesus\\\'s writing style</A>.',6,121,0,0,'165.247.208.20','en'),(15656,2615,'2002-04-11 04:03:24',130,'treetop','Meanwhile, in another corner of the globe.\r\n\r\n<p>Matthias is leaving Ferihegy airport after a brief meeting with Macy in the States.  The two go back a long way. They spent several years together while in boarding school together in Bern.  They were actually both expelled together for boobie-trapping the head masters water closet.  But that\\\'s another story.  Now the two get together about once or twice a year to catch up on stories and exchange some US lsd for some Euro mdma.  Psychoactive drugs have been their pleasure ever since boarding school.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Now, Macy had given him something new.  Something that not many people had tried.  Matthias decided that he wanted to try it as soon as possible.  He was happy to be back in his own county.  Since he only had a day pack for luggage from his trip he decided to try out this new substance on his way home.  He poured the vial of almost clear powder in his mouth chased it with some water.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Matthias home is located on the Pest of the Duna River.  He decide that he would make his way to the Szechenyl Bridge fist though.  Once there, a girl with dreadlocks offered him some grass.  After close inspection of the herb, Matthias handed it back to her and shook his head.  She turned to walk away and he heard, \\\"you just wasted my time you fucking tool!\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Matthias was astounded.  He had heard the words, but her lips did not move.  He bagan to feel this warm glowing feeling inside his bones and he turned to look out over the water from the bridge.  His vision began to blur slowly.  The natural colors of the world were changing to the opposite colors as if he was looking at a photo negative.  He had never experienced this sort of hallucination before on acid or mushrooms.  He bagan to hear what people were thinking as they passed behind him as if they were talking out loud.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>He thought, \\\"What has Macy given me?  How long will this last?\\\"  At first he was a little freaked out.  But then he quickly adjusted to the experience and tried to make the best out of it.  He also wondered what else this stuff had in store for him.  He began to walk home.  He hoped that he could make it to his house on Kazinczy u before it fully kicked in..</p>',2,2249,0,0,'172.159.147.126','en'),(15657,2626,'2002-04-11 04:33:14',65,'le','sigh. jason, please use the edit article feature to fix your missing end bold tag.\r\n<br><br>\r\nalso, my incidence of synchronicity is that ever since oof explained jung\\\'s theory of synchronicity to me, it keeps coming up, everyone keeps mentioning it.\r\n ',6,256,0,0,'165.247.208.20','en'),(15658,2615,'2002-04-11 05:27:13',63,'olivia','ungrateful bitches.',6,19,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15659,2625,'2002-04-11 05:27:21',176,'Scapegoat','This had me grinning mischievously remembering a deliquent youthfulness that I narrowly escaped.',0,96,0,0,'64.229.160.244','en'),(15660,2619,'2002-04-11 06:03:26',74,'jo','lotus, most christians don\\\'t follow dietary laws and back this up with various biblical sources. peter, in acts, had this vision where god told him to rise and slay and eat and showed him this great sheet in the sky and the unclean animals were made clean. (see: peter is a fucking loon.) paul, less of a fucking loon, in his letter to the romans, said that no food was unclean. also, noah was allowed to eat any animal he wanted to and in fact, there existed no dietary laws pre-abraham in the bible, leading many scholars to believe that the dietary laws came about for sanitary reasons rather than religious and thus wouldn\\\'t be relevant for modern believers. personally? i\\\'m always going to go with the argument that following all the laws, particularly those in leviticus and deuteronomy, would be near impossible in our society now, what with the laws about shaving and mixing fabrics and how women should behave when menstruating, etc. etc.',2,956,156,0,'172.144.166.246','en'),(15661,2619,'2002-04-11 06:29:42',65,'le','thank you, jo. i <tt><3</tt> information',1,40,74,0,'165.247.208.20','en'),(15662,2615,'2002-04-11 07:48:14',64,'Joanna','Man, what kind of bodice rippers were you all raised on?  And how else did you learn about sex if not from reading about \\\"throbbing manhoods\\\" in \\\'My Lady, My Vixen\\\' age seven? ',0,188,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(15663,2615,'2002-04-11 07:48:16',64,'Joanna','Man, what kind of bodice rippers were you all raised on?  And how else did you learn about sex if not from reading about \\\"throbbing manhoods\\\" in \\\'My Lady, My Vixen\\\' age seven? ',1,188,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(15664,2621,'2002-04-11 07:50:26',65,'le','yes. for many years i had driving dreams. in the dreams i\\\'d be in a car that was driving itself, and i would have no way to steer it and it was pretty no-good. then i started dreaming that i was steering the car but i couldn\\\'t do it properly, i made a lot of wrong turns and drove off the road. then one day i dreamt that i drove, parked the car, and got out. and since then i haven\\\'t had driving dreams anymore.',4,421,260,0,'165.247.208.20','en'),(15665,2619,'2002-04-11 07:51:20',176,'Scapegoat','<b>To Hool</b> The Crucifish idea makes me laugh. I\\\'m excited about you being here this summer it\\\'s going to be good.<br><br>\r\n<b>To Jo</b> Thanks for the low down on the Christians. <br><br>\r\n<b>To chi chi</b> What is the swine of the sea?<br><br>\r\n<b>To Lotus</b> What would be the reasoning behind not eating predatory creatures?<br><br>\r\nI apologize for all the questions but curiosity has been peaked. ',0,413,0,0,'64.229.160.244','en'),(15666,2615,'2002-04-11 07:58:03',65,'le','when i was 14, we stayed with my aunt in florida, and i learned all about sex by sneaking her copies of \\\"the joy of sex\\\" and \\\"our bodies, ourselves\\\" into my room. i never told my mom that. hi mom.',0,208,64,0,'165.247.208.20','en'),(15667,2615,'2002-04-11 08:06:21',176,'Scapegoat','I wasn\\\'t raised on bodice rippers at all, didn\\\'t even know what they were until we read one in highschool my last year. I learned about sex from movies. The literature I grew up with was a solid diet of Sci-fi, Fantasy, and Spy Novels. I read comic books more then anything else though.  ',0,294,64,0,'64.229.160.244','en'),(15668,2627,'2002-04-11 08:07:19',65,'le','so good.',1,8,0,0,'165.247.208.20','en'),(15669,2626,'2002-04-11 10:06:34',196,'jasonp','Wow, I just tried to un-categorize this as \\\"lesbian\\\" and it just vanished...\n\n',0,84,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(15670,2628,'2002-04-11 11:02:27',196,'jasonp','you\\\'re in korea huh?\nI spent a day in Pusan to get my visa renewed for japan.\n<BR>\nsignificant impressions:<BR>\n<UL>\n<LI>the ferry across, it could hold a few hundred people at least. most people got a mat and slept on the floor for the overnight trip. I spent a lot of time walking around on the roof looking around\n<LI> the smell of the entire city, i guess from burning oil and kim chee and who knows what else they put in the food\n<LI> scores of tacky russians buying leather shoes and imitation designer brands\n<LI>Koreans are different than Japanese. They\\\'re taller, noisier, less polite. (broad generalization of course)\n<LI> people have a different concept of where personal body boundaries are -- they\\\'ll bump into like you were a twig or something and not even acknowledge it.\n<LI> a guy at a park who approached to talk to me about politics to practive his english. actually much better than most japanese students I had.\n</UL>',0,947,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(15671,2626,'2002-04-11 11:11:00',196,'jasonp','<b> \nBy an unfortunate series of events this article has disappeared and stubbornly refuses to come back as an updated edition.<BR>\nUnless anyone really wants back, consider it a victim of fate that it has now vanished.<BR>\n</b>',0,228,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(15672,2621,'2002-04-11 11:26:11',196,'jasonp','Cool, I had the same kind of recurring car dream.<BR>\nI would always be in the backseat looking around out window as the car drove around by itself.<BR>\nI don\\\'t think I ever drove the car myself. The dream had already stopped by the time I knew how to drive.',0,261,65,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(15673,2626,'2002-04-11 11:52:10',65,'le','it\\\'s kind of nice as just a title. minimalist work.',4,54,0,0,'165.247.208.20','en'),(15674,2626,'2002-04-11 12:17:59',122,'hool','',0,0,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(15675,2626,'2002-04-11 12:18:04',122,'hool',' ',0,1,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(15676,2627,'2002-04-11 12:37:58',156,'lotus','swinney has more than its share of spectacular writers.',0,55,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15677,2626,'2002-04-11 13:04:02',196,'jasonp',' <a href=\\\"http://www.jasonp.org/swin-synch.html\\\">\n-></a>\n\n',0,64,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(15678,2619,'2002-04-11 14:19:22',74,'jo','welcome. sometimes this pesky religion degree comes in handy.',0,61,0,0,'172.131.194.121','en'),(15679,2626,'2002-04-11 15:57:21',239,'Poonlarp','why all the hoopla and fuss? why not just repost the article? just a thought...',1,79,196,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15680,2619,'2002-04-11 15:59:33',66,'Peter','<br><tt>hey, dont be callin\\\' my namesake a loon! heh.</tt>',1,61,74,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15681,2624,'2002-04-11 16:58:49',156,'lotus','i reread this before posting it (it was written oct 2000) and considered cutting out the lip balm part! did you write that poem, gus? and may i please send it to someone? it really deeply moved me.',1,197,124,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15682,2628,'2002-04-11 17:37:18',83,'adamajah','<p>reading this reminds me of my own foreign adventures.  what memories.  it also reminds me of how long it\\\'s been since i\\\'ve had that humble yet empowering feeling you get when you are a guest in a foreign place.  it\\\'s like a whole different realm.  so many parallels in lifestyle and structure, but it feels so alien.  travelling is definitely one of the most worthwhile things i\\\'ve ever done.</p>',2,411,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(15683,2626,'2002-04-11 19:11:10',196,'jasonp','too easy<BR>\nIt\\\'s back now anyway. I\\\'ll ascribe that to mysterious forces.',0,80,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(15684,2621,'2002-04-11 19:49:06',260,'Layla','<p><i>So what did you achieve besides gaining a parking space?</i></p>',0,70,65,0,'62.64.225.91','en'),(15685,2630,'2002-04-11 19:51:48',66,'Peter','<br><tt><b>serious collections:</b> stamps, records, flyers, prints from <a href=\"http://www.petermax.com/\">peter max</a> (the ones i can steal from the subway ads or banners fro ma lamp-post, also, heh).<br><br><b>random silly collections:</b> \'found art\' from the street, old machine parts, retro odds and ends.<br><br><b>sentimental but meaningless collections:</b> notes and letters from ex, random ticket stubs, random pictures, meaningles odds and ends/old parts to things i dont own anymore.</tt>',1,322,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15686,2615,'2002-04-11 20:05:54',17,'austin','i believe those books were on the family book shelf in the living room when i was growing up.',4,93,65,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(15687,2615,'2002-04-11 20:08:20',17,'austin','except our \\\"our bodies our selves\\\" which i bought my little sister three years ago in order to circumvent my mom\\\'s fucked up mormon decision to not enroll her in sex education.<BR><BR>\r\n<H1>hi mom, that was an idiotic decision, i hope you changed your mind</H1>',6,270,65,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(15688,2608,'2002-04-11 20:11:29',180,'auntie','the first step in any treatment is to announce you have a problem.  every 12 step program requires you make this step to get yourself going.  my prayers will be with you while you undergo your treatment.  just remember - one day at a time.  with great love and affection.',1,271,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(15689,2630,'2002-04-11 20:16:00',86,'egg','collecting is a bad business, it is like marriage and employment, just a setup for destruction and disappointment. I had a roommate who collected paperweights because her father had given her some special ones as a kid. When we were robbed they took all her paperweights. Now, why would someone rob your paperweight collection? Collections are  monuments to preciousness. who gives a shit. If I had money, I\\\'d buy art.',0,421,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(15690,2630,'2002-04-11 20:28:31',82,'simon','hmm, i don\\\'t think i agree.<br>\r\nbut also, if i had money, i\\\'d start with buying more of the materials i use to make art before i started buying art.',0,155,86,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15691,2630,'2002-04-11 20:43:30',124,'gus','stamps, disney stamps to be exact. I have a barely filled binder of them somewhere... \r\n<p>\r\nlittle t shirts. old ones, logo ones, tourist ones. I have a ton. I like them in child size large, they\\\'re cheaper and nice and tight.<p>\r\nbooks, any books. used books. I never throw out books. plays, poetry. books.\r\n<p>\r\nhats. I have some hats from various countries, kazakstan, uzbekestan, thailand hill tribe..\r\n<p>\r\nand then there are my bud hats. the yarn and beercan bud hat, the fishing style bud hat, and the straw cowboyhat with budweiser bandana. that collection was accidental, but its a good one! ',0,605,0,0,'64.160.53.160','en'),(15692,2619,'2002-04-11 20:45:15',148,'jeeff','swine of the sea = pigs with gills.  very rare.  when they oink, bubbles shoot out of the snout.',0,96,176,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(15693,2630,'2002-04-11 20:47:19',66,'Peter','<br><tt>collections are good, for me, cause they let me force order and finite-ness to the random flux of shit around me. most of my collecitons are interactive- cataloging/mounting stamps, playing/mixing/digitizing the records, etc. fun.</tt>',1,243,86,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15694,2619,'2002-04-11 20:59:22',76,'chi chi','haha. actually swine of the sea is shrimp.',0,42,176,0,'64.160.46.14','en'),(15695,2630,'2002-04-11 21:31:30',176,'Scapegoat','My main collection thus far in life would have to be comic books. Mainly Image titles from the beginning of the company and a few Dark Horse and lesser known companies.<br><br>\r\nThe second runner up is my junk collection. I have this tendency to collect bizarre junk in boxes I have tons of little bits from different electronic devices that have broken down and then been dissected by yours truly. Add in a variety of weird military garbage from my days as a base brat and a then a bunch of those white board that electronic engineers do their homework on and all the accompanying resistors and capacitators etc.<br><br>\r\nAlso I have a few hockey cards from 1980-1982  ',0,670,0,0,'64.229.160.244','en'),(15696,2619,'2002-04-11 21:58:09',74,'jo','sorry, peter, but he was a fucking loon. and wholly responsible for all the crazy shit southern baptists refer to, out of context, to defend their gay-bashing, women-oppressing, fundamentalist ways. way to go, st. peter, you jackass!',5,233,66,0,'172.164.155.7','en'),(15697,2599,'2002-04-11 22:12:29',260,'Layla','<p>My friend had a broken walkman.... I said jokingly that there wasn\\\'t a mechanical thing that I hadn\\\'t fixed yet... she took me seriously...</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p><b>So I fixed the damn thing.. and it worked! I have hardly ever touched one before, let alone opened one up.. but it\\\'s pretty straight forward when you analyse it\\\'s organs for a little while</b></p>\r\n<p><i>The End</i></p>',0,391,0,0,'62.64.201.39','en'),(15698,2619,'2002-04-11 22:14:24',66,'Peter','<br><tt>didnt he cut off someone\\\'s ear, once? i think jesus had to put it back. yikes!</tt>',1,94,74,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15699,2630,'2002-04-11 22:30:29',59,'s5','i collect snowglobes from US states and foreign countries. the rule is that i can never directly purchase a snowglobe; each must be a gift from a friend who has recently traveled. the tackier, the better. if obtained from a foreign country, it must be accompanied by a story in which the checkout person at the tourist shop has sneered at the purchaser for buying trashy americana.',0,381,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15700,2626,'2002-04-11 22:36:13',59,'s5','at the exact moment your article disappeared, my house lost power. freaky, eh?',0,78,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15701,2630,'2002-04-11 22:49:40',82,'simon','i just remembered that i inherited a collection of  souvenir glasses from across the US (mostly places like yosemite, yellow stone, wyoming, niagra falls, etc.). my great grandmother collected them, died and left them to me. i keep them in boxes until  i have the proper glass cabinet in which to display them.',1,310,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15702,2621,'2002-04-11 22:50:10',246,'erb','<p>scene from my dream from last night:</p>\r\n<p>\r\n i am on a big red horse, in a meadow, talking to someone on the ground.  i have been riding the horse and we are resting.  suddenly the horse begins to buck and prance around.  i say, \\\"whoa, WHOA,\\\" but the horse wants me off now, and pitches me forward over his head.  i am not trying to stay on anymore, but as i slide forward, i realize that my leg is trapped at the calf in the stirrup, so i am stuck hanging by my right leg.  i swing myself back in the saddle, but am pitched forward again.   finally i wrench my leg free and slide into the green grass, relieved.</p>\r\n<p>the episode spoke to me of the anger, and being a slave to the emotion, being trapped in it, no longer having control.  i had a revelation in yoga class last night that i am in control of where i focus my energy.  this anger has had a hold on me because i let go of the reins, because i have been entangled in it.  i want to sit in the meadow.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n',1,986,0,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(15703,2619,'2002-04-11 23:09:35',74,'jo','yeah, he cut off the ear of the servant of a high priest who was coming to arrest jesus. see: fucking loon.',0,107,0,0,'172.140.238.65','en'),(15704,2630,'2002-04-11 23:23:04',76,'chi chi','shit that reminds me that last august i bought you a snow globe from england.',0,77,59,0,'64.160.46.14','en'),(15705,2630,'2002-04-11 23:24:28',86,'egg','that makes me feel like we should have some kind of party and all be friends. ',0,78,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(15706,2630,'2002-04-11 23:28:17',76,'chi chi','also i like to collect beverage containers. i have a small collection of Coffee People (portland) travel mugs; and hard rock cafe glasses spanning 4 continents.',0,160,0,0,'64.160.46.14','en'),(15707,2630,'2002-04-11 23:28:23',82,'simon','i think i might be into that.',0,29,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15708,2626,'2002-04-11 23:30:58',17,'austin','dude, it is all about the number 26!\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ndestabilizing',4,61,59,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(15709,2630,'2002-04-11 23:32:21',82,'simon','also, i was just thinking, there are a fair number of people out in the world who collect vintage levi\\\'s. if i were one of those people, i couldn\\\'t even pretend to be satisfied, no matter how extensive my collection, if i did not count among my possessions, a 1977 AMC Pacer DL wagon with the Levi\\\'s interior option. sigh....if only.',1,342,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15710,2624,'2002-04-11 23:48:46',124,'gus','I wrote it, but a long time ago. sure you can send it. (the lipbalm is only randomly relevant to me, maybe not to anyone else. heh).',0,132,0,0,'216.175.110.165','en'),(15711,2621,'2002-04-12 00:09:52',65,'le','i\\\'ve jinxed myself! i totally dreamt i was in an out of control car last night again! it was the ol\\\' \\\"can\\\'t reach the break\\\" dream. fuck that! ',6,158,260,0,'209.86.0.3','en'),(15712,2630,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','art can be pretty precious. the kind of art that costs a million dollars and made of baby foreskins, for example.\r\n\r\ni guess i don\\\'t collect anything, but i do have lots of stuff. boatloads of fabric and books. and a decent corner dedicated to afroken courtesy of the nice s5. but! i did just buy an inflatable cockring for jeff at maruwa and lots of yummy mochiballs...\r\n\r\nbut the collecting? i\\\'m too scared to give my obsessiveness free rein like that, i would start buying those things up to the exclusion of rent and food. i guess i\\\'ll have to pick something really really hard to collect, like maturity, happiness and the ability to be cool like fonzy.',1,666,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15713,2619,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<strong>Meerschweine</strong> (sea pig) is the actual german word for \"guinea pig.\" demonstrated here, is the classic and preferred way to traverse a room: <a href=\"http://www.dasmeerschwein.de/20020211/Seiten/Spiegel.htm\">\"the nose to butt method\"</a>',1,262,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15714,2630,'2002-04-12 00:54:16',263,'sparrowe','test',0,4,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15715,2630,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<a href=\\\"http://www.ce-review.org/00/41/nezmah41.html\\\">collecting obscenities</a>.',1,88,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15716,2630,'2002-04-12 01:14:48',186,'ook','<P>1. hardcore genre nerds referring to collections as My Collection is very great, especially when their collection is made up of complete sets of sci-fi novels based on tv shows or something.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>2. any collections i manage to make don\\\'t last long before they get made into something else. it is more like inventory. right now i seem to be accumulating a lot of stolen cards from board games, because the local thrift barn seems to have hit a gold mine of oddball games from ago. not sure what they can turn into yet.</P>',0,532,0,0,'209.148.183.210','en'),(15717,2624,'2002-04-12 03:59:10',122,'hool','i thought of you standing at a phone booth, upset.  i thought about you having to bear the burden of the transitional generation.  \r\n\r\nto me this speaks in terms of love the conflict between your traditional home and the modern world you have to live in.  that you had to use a payphone to talk to your lover tells me how unable your home is to reconcile your outside life with itself - it won\\\'t even let contact come through a telephone wire - even if it\\\'s love. \r\n\r\nand you write in terms of seasons, and you write about yourself, and i see you as autumn, standing fast between your torpid home and the sun that beats down on you each time you step outside.',0,665,156,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(15718,2619,'2002-04-12 04:42:52',175,'Paul G','In Judaism huge restrictions are placed upon the consumption of seafood: only those fish that have both fins and scales may be eaten.  Shrimp, clams, mussels, lobster, eel and such are all prohibited.\r\n\r\n<p>In no case is the blood of any animal to be consumed.  Animal flesh must not only come from a properly killed beast, but it must also be cooked/cured in such a manner as to remove all blood; this usually involves grilling and salt packing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Some Christian sects still maintain the dietary restrictions established by Judaism; most do not keep them.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Most of the dietary restrictions appear to have served two purposes: 1) to set one group of people apart from another [and the earth]; and 2) to preserve good health.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The process of conversion to Judaism [rare] involves adoption into the tribe of Abraham.  One might consider one\\\'s self to be Jewish by virtue of birth or adoption even though one didn\\\'t keep any of the dietary restrictions or even if one was an atheist.  Any child born to a Jewish mother will be considered to be Jewish.  But, having a Jewish father alone is not traditionally enough.  Ultra-Orthodox, Orthodox, Conservative, and Reformed Jews disagree upon such issues even as Roman Catholic, Methodist, Baptist, and Seventh Day Adventists disagree.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Similarly, Muslims might not everywhere and always agree about the exact manner in which even the 5 pillars of Islam: 1) the acknowledgment that there is no God but Allah and that Muhammad is his prophet; 2) daily prayer toward the Kaaba; 3) the giving of alms; 4) the fast of Ramadan; and 5) the pilgrimage to Mecca ought to be understood and performed.  And certainly there is no consensus about the interpretation of the Koran or succession.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Arabs are Semites: they cannot be anti-Semitic [bonus].</p>',0,1835,0,0,'67.248.52.214','en'),(15719,2621,'2002-04-12 08:52:17',260,'Layla','<p><i>Heh! heh! heh!</i></p>\r\n<p>That\\\'s quite funny.</p>',1,59,65,0,'62.64.203.43','en'),(15720,2635,'2002-04-12 10:39:13',86,'egg','today I proclaimed, \\\"austin, you have no romance in you.\\\" I take it back.',0,79,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(15721,2635,'2002-04-12 10:51:28',65,'le','here is a blurry picture of a snail that peter and i met on the sidewalk of iririki resort in vanuatu. this snail was unperturbed by our poking. it was peter\\\'s birthday. it was also xep\\\'s birthday, she was on some other resort. i think everyone involved in this paragraph was drinking, except me and this snail.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://still.hungry.com/~le/blursnail.jpg\\\" width=\\\"300\\\" height=\\\"225\\\" alt=\\\"snail\\\">\r\n<br><br>\r\ni liked your article austin.',2,483,0,0,'209.162.48.145','en'),(15722,2627,'2002-04-12 12:11:47',232,'necklace','<p>indeed. :)</p>',1,17,156,0,'193.250.74.231','en'),(15723,2635,'2002-04-12 16:57:16',156,'lotus','this is an awesome article. if i wasn\\\'t on dial up, i would post my own snail story. thanks, austin.',1,103,0,0,'64.26.139.247','en'),(15724,2633,'2002-04-12 17:02:20',156,'lotus','every time i see your stuff it makes me want to buy a camera. <br>\r\np.s. you better not leave ottawa without seeing me.',0,119,0,0,'64.26.139.247','en'),(15725,2633,'2002-04-12 17:59:20',93,'chiamattt','my   screen is full of korean characters. without the poem the photo would be nothing. ',0,87,0,0,'61.72.197.151','en'),(15726,2633,'2002-04-12 18:02:19',223,'shanE','Did you take this photo in our neighbourhood? It seems so familiar.. except that your poem encourages the construction whereas I would say \\\"Go to hell! Don t you realize that it s 7 am!\\\"',6,192,0,0,'61.72.197.151','en'),(15727,2633,'2002-04-12 18:15:58',66,'Peter','<br><tt>that <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2599.txt\\\">machine</a> is called a <i>putzmeisterâ„¢</i> [pump master]. it pumps the concrete up to where the mullet-workers want it. <i>chug chug, pump pump</i></tt>',1,236,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15728,2633,'2002-04-12 18:51:33',82,'simon','putzmeister! i kept seeing that on the side of those things and giggling.',0,73,66,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15729,2635,'2002-09-05 18:40:19',18,'ix','at nirvana, the mailbox is a hole in the brick wall that surrounds the front yard. snails like to climb into the mailbox. whenever i retrieve the mail on a rainy day, there are sure to be three or four snails sticking to the envelopes. i have to scrape them off onto the wall.',0,276,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(15730,2634,'2002-04-12 18:54:09',82,'simon','i want to see this',0,18,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15731,2633,'2002-04-12 19:06:15',122,'hool','<p>meaning, your neighborhood when you lived in O dot? it\\\'s from the market. they\\\'re building condos down there, across from the dominion. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>also, i\\\'m not encouraging construction, just commenting on it in general.</p>\r\n\r\n',0,240,223,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(15732,2635,'2002-04-12 19:33:23',63,'olivia','you can just take what you need out of the envelope and leave it on the front door step outside what was previously my room.  that way they snail off all by themselves.  then you can throw out the envelope the next day.',6,219,16,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15733,2633,'2002-04-12 19:52:09',66,'Peter','<br><tt>god, i was so happy when i was dating a mechanical/structural <a href=\"http://www.rhodamine.org/futurecity/madison1.jpg\">engineer</a>. she taught me about concrete-compression tests and the various ends and outs of steel erection and concrete pumping.<i>sigh</i>.</tt>',1,213,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15734,2633,'2002-04-12 20:07:36',82,'simon','heh, steel erection.',0,20,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15735,2635,'2002-04-12 23:30:15',187,'kandrer','mmm... ix, where\\\'s that door you will leave the envelopes? we french like snails too. >:)<br>\r\nand, austin, we were in santa cruz last week-end and we didn\\\'t see you there. while i\\\'m thinking of it, we didn\\\'t notice any snail either. (you can always count on me for useful comments)',1,294,0,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(15736,2635,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','snails :(((((((((((((((((((((',1,29,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15737,2367,'2002-04-13 05:09:20',67,'trouble','wow, thanks austin.  the place was/is one of a kind.',1,52,17,0,'68.47.202.98','en'),(15738,2637,'2002-04-13 05:35:20',180,'auntie','what i hate is when they don\\\'t check the damn drip and your arm turns black while you sleep.  bleh!  hospitals suck.',1,119,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(15739,2630,'2002-04-13 07:11:03',93,'chiamattt','logging into this site from korean game rooms really pisses me the fuck right off. ok back to collecting. \r\n\r\nwell i collect pubic hair i find in public washrooms. i have a pretty good collection, and when its done im going to have my grandma knit me a pair of socks with them. ',0,278,0,0,'211.216.216.47','en'),(15740,2637,'2002-04-13 17:39:53',75,'tyler','nice article! i wish i knew more about what you were talking about though. i think, myself, that i would get extremely rude and loud in a situation where my condition was being ignored like that. i used to think that made me an asshole, but you\\\'ve gotta do what you\\\'ve gotta do.',1,284,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15741,2637,'2002-04-13 20:10:21',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\\\"youre so <b>vein</b>, you prob\\\'ly think this piece is about youuuu...\\\"</i></tt>',1,100,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15742,2633,'2002-04-13 23:09:28',176,'Scapegoat','Condos across from the dom? Things are definitely changing. Condos are increasing becoming signs of the neighborhood going all to hell in my eyes. This building that I live in is always lobbying against different businesses ie - bars, pubs, etc. saying it speaks on behalf of all its residents. ',0,295,0,0,'64.229.161.73','en'),(15743,2639,'2002-04-14 03:24:37',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i like how this rhymes:<br><br><i>I\\\'m in my early 40\\\'s. I like to tell stories</i></tt>',1,101,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15744,2639,'2002-04-14 19:11:36',265,'chuckspeed','peter-\r\n\r\nthanks.  swinney posted a link from a motorcyle egroup, and...you know the rest of the story.\r\n\r\nFWIW, I\\\'m from detroit, like machines, (as long as they have motors and make lotsa noise) and have an appreciation for tools.  Go figure. ',0,248,0,0,'172.143.61.240','en'),(15745,2549,'2002-04-14 21:39:49',75,'tyler','you know, even though this got edited out, i am more proud of this article than anything i have written in a long time. of course, it could just be nicotine withdrawal.',0,168,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15746,2640,'2002-04-14 23:37:11',74,'jo','hi, this is crazy good. mmm, narrative in the disguise of lists. <a href=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0231073372/\\\">shonagon</a> would be proud.',1,160,0,0,'172.148.68.160','en'),(15747,2640,'2002-04-15 00:25:17',65,'le','yeah, this is really good. much more lucid than your other stuff here, matt. i like it. how do you get internet from there? ',2,124,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(15748,2608,'2002-04-15 00:26:20',65,'le','this is one of those cases where i really respect \\\"i don\\\'t know\\\" as an answer',2,86,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(15749,2632,'2002-04-15 14:42:59',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this was fascinating; thanks for posting it.</tt>',1,57,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15750,2632,'2002-04-15 15:03:10',82,'simon','yeah, i thought so too. i really enjoyed reading it.',0,52,0,0,'12.236.164.112','en'),(15751,2641,'2002-04-15 16:36:45',82,'simon','good morning, egg. were you the one telling me about \\\"eyeliner people\\\" and how you can tell if someone\\\'s an eyeliner person or not and what a difference that makes? i just then started singing \\\"what a difference a day makes\\\" in my head.',1,251,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15752,2642,'2002-04-15 17:18:35',66,'Peter','<br><tt>my grandma used to have a \\\'71 chrysler with an over-bored hemi; i think it was the 392; my grandfather used to tool on it all the time.  i remember endlessly driving it around the block when i was 13, marvelling at the powerful gurgling sounds it made, like any good 13 year old southern male should.</tt>',1,316,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15753,2641,'2002-04-15 17:20:45',66,'Peter','<br><tt>on a completely unrelated note: you said <i>good morning, egg</i> and that reminded me of this place i used to go eat breakfast at when i was little; they\\\'d always give you stickers with a happy-face on them that said just that: \\\"good morning, egg!\\\". i always thought that was an odd thing to print on a smiley-face sticker.</tt>',1,346,82,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15754,2642,'2002-04-15 17:30:13',82,'simon','fuckin\\\' a! me and old man swinney were just discussing how nice it is to have other car kids around.',0,103,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15755,2641,'2002-04-15 17:40:10',82,'smiley face','GOOD MORNING, EGG! I\\\'ll be eating you now! on toast! bwahahaha',0,65,66,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15756,2642,'2002-04-15 17:42:56',66,'Peter','<br><tt>car kids rock; such types understand the draw of a bored hemi with dual-exhaust and glass-packs! the car even had blue velour interior. damn, why does my family always sell all the good cars?!</tt>',1,205,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15757,2641,'2002-04-15 17:44:15',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i will hide under my hat;</tt>',1,38,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15758,2632,'2002-04-15 18:07:34',265,'chuckspeed','damn that was good.',0,19,0,0,'208.5.114.84','en'),(15759,2642,'2002-04-15 20:06:23',265,'chuckspeed','old rides look better in the rearview mirror of the mind.',0,57,0,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(15760,2641,'2002-04-15 20:44:51',65,'le','i haven\\\'t read this article yet, but i assume it\\\'s about egg not being able to sleep last night. i just found this in my chat window, left there from about 3am, and i wanted to put it somewhere anyway, so it\\\'s going here. ok:\r\n<br><br>\r\n<pre>\r\n[=Arrive=] egg (egg@atari.saturn5.com) entered group\r\n&lt;egg&gt; cmon man\r\n[=Beep!=] egg sent you a beep!\r\n&lt;egg&gt; i was having a heartfelt conversation irl\r\n&lt;egg&gt; have i missed everything\r\n&lt;egg&gt; i see, you bed down with your man when its time to schnoozey paw\r\n&lt;egg&gt; do you know what schnoozy paw is\r\n&lt;egg&gt; thats what my german stepfather calls the motion of my dog\\\'s\r\npaws when\r\n&lt;egg&gt; she is in dreamland\r\n&lt;egg&gt; she thrusts them slightly into the air like a ballerina\r\n&lt;egg&gt; well, she\\\'s dead now\r\n&lt;egg&gt; she doesn\\\'t do anything\r\n&lt;egg&gt; but she was handsome, in the same way you two are handsome\r\n&lt;egg&gt; she was part wolf, part siberian husky\r\n&lt;egg&gt; named \\\"mischka\\\"\r\n&lt;egg&gt; that means like \\\"honey cake\\\" or something in colloquial russian\r\n&lt;egg&gt; my sister was a russian major\r\n&lt;egg&gt; now shes in sales\r\n&lt;egg&gt; hows that for sagittarius\r\n&lt;egg&gt; ok bye you guys\r\n[=Depart=] egg (egg@atari.saturn5.com) just left\r\n',4,1279,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(15761,2641,'2002-05-24 17:02:54',86,'egg','i am an ass hat. we will throw this one away.',0,45,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(15762,2641,'2002-04-15 20:51:11',65,'le','hey, have you ever heard of the omega institute? you can go there and live in a tent and they feed you in return for arranging chairs and riding on golf carts. maybe you could go there for the summer and get your angst out.',4,223,17,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(15763,2641,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','hi. this made me happy, a lot. eyeliner people. i vaguely remember wearing eyeliner in high school, and applying it on the bus every morning. early sunrises, watching the sun come up, wishing your friends were awake... that\\\'s my life yo. the only cure for that mood is late night snacks for breakfast. ice cream + nachos. chocolate sundaes + hot chocolate. ',1,360,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15764,2641,'2002-04-16 02:21:41',187,'kandrer','to kandrer: remind to ask americans if \\\"the cure for that\\\" is a typical american expression. usually followed by a list of (sometimes disgusting) food and sometimes alcohol.',1,179,0,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(15765,2641,'2002-05-24 17:02:54',86,'egg','i cant go there. its not for me. i like misery.',0,47,65,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(15766,2633,'2002-04-16 06:57:57',93,'chiamattt','if thats a steel erection...that dude has a serious case of \\\"the hook\\\"',0,76,0,0,'61.72.197.151','en'),(15767,2641,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','kanderer: the only cure for french people asking that question is peanut butter. peanut butter on white bread, followed by light beer.',0,134,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15768,2632,'2002-04-16 08:17:10',187,'kandrer','yeah, it was great! though, i read because of the nice comments - the title itself misled me. what would you think of... mmm... <i>reservoir puppies</i> ? :/',1,157,0,0,'205.184.158.245','en'),(15769,2641,'2002-04-16 08:32:29',187,'kandrer',':)) excellent. but... how did u guess for peanut butter?',1,56,57,0,'205.184.158.245','en'),(15770,2641,'2002-04-16 13:39:54',93,'chiamattt','i had hallucinations the other night. hung over to a point i dont really want to think about i dreamt i was in a large house trying to find the owners. i wanted to kill them because all around this large house were slums and people with want in their face. not want....need. need in their face. the owners of the house when i found them were already dead. they had died long ago and all those hands clutching at the iron fence protecting the mansion has no idea the owners were dead. they just knew that someone owned the house. i woke up really pissy because throughout the whole dream i didnt once open the gates. i dont even recall thinking about letting them in. i just explored and looked around in annoyance. fuck the rich. fuck the facade we call the modern world. all we do is come up with new ways to hide the truth. more ways to instill fear and greed and more ways to ignore the real issues that are affecting everyone. ME ME ME never WE WE WE. fuck the upper crust who we entrust with the wealth and the power. vote for me. vote for him. vote for her. fuck voting. voting is another facade. facades everywhere. lets rename all the malls in the world after disney land. they offer nothing but smoke and mirrors. if the information doesnt shine or blow up who cares. FUCKING COMMUNISTS!!!! the fucking communists ruined europe i have to listen to all day long. THE FUCKING COMMUNISTS. cuba is poor because of the FUCKING COMMUNISTS. i say this. THEY NEVER CLAIMED TO BE COMMUNIST NATIONS. they claimed to be socialist states on the road towards communism. the manifesto never spoke of an arms race. the manifesto never spoke of a man demonizing another because of political ideology...the manifesto demonized those who chose to have more than the whole in the name of rich vs poor. im mad at the world and im mad at the rich. FUCK ALL THE RICH FUCKERS WHO DONT SHARE, DONT CARE, AND DONT TAKE RESPONSIBILTY FOR ALL THE POVERTY, SICKNESS, AND MALNUTRITION IN THE WORLD. fuck man. those poor bastards living in abject poverty should just get a job, work harder, goto school....HELL life wasnt peachy for me but i still worked hard. fuck you. fuck you and that idea. fuck everyone who thinks the poor are JUST lazy. fuck those who think women have babies to get a bigger welfare check, and fuck those who think that having a liquer store on the corner of every poor neighbourhood in the world is \\\"just\\\" and \\\"proper\\\". id like to see someone try and open up a malt likker liquidation warehouse in poshmansion america....fuckers. \r\n',5,2548,0,0,'61.72.197.151','en'),(15771,2643,'2002-04-16 13:51:27',265,'chuckspeed','Relax don\\\'t do it\r\nWhen you want to go to it\r\n-frankie goes to hollywood',0,75,0,0,'208.5.114.75','en'),(15772,2632,'2002-04-16 15:34:26',265,'chuckspeed','Yah.  originally passed it up on account of I thought it was some sorta Pete Townsend/Roger Daltrey commentary.  glad it wasn\\\'t. ',0,132,0,0,'208.5.114.93','en'),(15773,615,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<pre>full of scraggly</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>i love that.</p>',6,50,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15774,2644,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>this article had me wracking my brains trying to think of some solution...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>needless to say, i can\\\'t come up with one.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>some thoughts i have, though:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>kids may be kids but they are old enough to know about responsibility for actions and appropriateness.  it\\\'s something you learn young, when you\\\'re trying to take off your pants in the supermarket, or grabbing for some foreign breast on the bus much to your mother\\\'s embarrasment.  schools counselling kids rarely works. if the kids are going to do something awful to your daughter at school, chances are they have no respect for the people employed by it.  they don\\\'t feel threatened.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>perhaps if your daughter feels safe enough you could enter into some kind of discussion involving yourself, your daughter and the boys (perhaps their parents?, intending to explain the impact their actions had on her?  it may not do any visible good, and if they are truly very bad kids they will probably be happy they\\\'ve caused pain, but it is really the only form of non-violent resolution that i can think of right now.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>perhaps conceed to the idea that a lot of boys around that age are just out of control balls of hormonal scariness, and that they will either outgrow it or go to jail, and either way they will feel sorry for their actions later.  work on your daughter and try and rebuild her self esteem.  give her some books by the dalai lama.  interest her in an activity that can calm her mind and help her feel proud of her body and it\\\'s changes.  self-defence classes?  yoga?  meditation?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>hope some of this might help.</p>',6,1653,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15775,2643,'2002-04-16 17:28:10',83,'adamajah','<p>this is totally something i want to watch on a screen.  not sure why.  foofoo!</p>',2,85,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(15776,2644,'2002-04-16 17:37:01',265,'chuckspeed','<p>Thanks.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There were two boys involved; one is remorseful while the other don\\\'t give a shit.  He\\\'s already assaulted a teacher this year.  We\\\'ve known he was a problem - we just didn\\\'t know how much of one until recently.  We believe in the public school system on account of we both had problems in Catholic school (read reality is just f\\\'n weird), but what is done with a hardened miscreant?  Where do they go when they\\\'ve gone bad?  Okay - this sounds goofy - I don\\\'t hate the kid.  I just think he\\\'s fucked up and needs help.  Prob is, I don\\\'t think he\\\'s gonna get it - which means he\\\'s a danger to my daughter when he gets back from \\\'vacation\\\'.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Thanks, o.  You\\\'re right.  I\\\'ll concentrate on my daughter.  Got a lot invested in her as it is.</p>',0,816,62,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(15777,2642,'2002-04-16 18:53:08',129,'moebius','<br>that was a great piece. speaking as someone who\\\'s been dealing with a freeway commute nearly every working day for the past eight years, it certainly fulfilled a few fantasies of mine. in real life, though, i learned (eventually) to just let maniacs like the T/A driver go. nowadays i\\\'m mostly oblivious to them.',0,322,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(15778,2644,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<p>this made me really angry. is this how you would react if that kid attacked her with a knife? broke her knee? did some lasting harm <i>that you could see?</i> just because it\'s sexual assault doesn\'t mean it\'s less harmful, usually it means a long term mental effects that can last a lot longer than physical damage.\r\n</p><p>\r\nhave you sat down with your daughter and told her you know it\'s not her fault? that what those boys did was wrong, wrong WRONG, and a violation her space and body? if you\'ve ever had someone make you feel that way, you would up in arms, telling everyone what heinous crime has been done TO her. not BY her.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwould you react this way if that boy scarred her for life physically? ignoring things or hushing it up creates a sense of shame. does your daughter know she\'s not to blame for this? are you sending her mixed signals by not being as mad as your wife is? \r\n</p><p>\r\nplease think carefully about this. it is affecting how your daughter will think about herself, her body, and self esteem for the rest of her life. and also it shows what is and isn\'t acceptable to all her 11 year old peers, male and female.\r\n</p><p>\r\nif i were a father, i would torch that boy\'s house. barring that, i would have a man to man talk with the boy\'s father and son, demanding a public apology and also a one-on-one apology. barring that, a meeting would be hard, but necessary as o described.\r\n</p><p>\r\nit is hard to deal with people like that, but sometimes you have to try. even if it\'s only to make your daughter feel better about being attacked. <strong>and to do it as soon as possible.</strong>\r\n</p><p>\r\nbeing sexually assaulted is to stripped of your dignity as well as control of your body. it\'s like feeling someone has taken your clothes off, and spit in your face. it varies in the degree of humiliation, but if it sounds like she\'s blaming herself (dressing in baggy clothes, etc); trust me, your daughter is thinking about it and replaying it over and over in her head. she\'s trying to make sense of why it happened and what she could have done to prevent it. give her hugs. tell her over and over again how she didn\'t do anything to deserve this, because otherwise, it\'s easy to blame yourself for other people\'s hurtful actions. don\'t let her start on that cycle of self-abuse, because that will open up the possibility of her becoming used to that sexual dynamic when she gets older.\r\n</p><p>\r\noh? and don\'t blame yourself for not knowing how to respond. it\'s really hard, and i recommend talking to your wife about it with an open heart, and to give yourself time to heal too. it\'s really sad, but my best wishes go to you and your daughter. you\'ll both make it through fine, if you make it clear you\'re on *her* side.\r\n</p><p>\r\nlove. weeb. wow this was long. sorry.</p>\r\n\r\n',1,2690,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15779,2644,'2002-04-16 20:34:27',175,'Paul G','<p>You love your daughter, and so you feel what she feels.  She is confused now; and so you are confused too.  How could it be any other way?  No one expects things like this to happen; but they do.  No one expects the attackers to be so young; but they are.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When you love someone you are made vulnerable through him or her.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You feel paralyzed by indecision.  You feel that you ought to practice vendetta; that is what a \\\"real\\\" man - am man of your father\\\'s generation - would do.  You feel that you have failed to protect your daughter; you feel that you have failed as a man.  But the way that the system works now if you take violent action then you will be the one who is incarcerated and not the true criminals.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Your daughter needs you - a good and caring parent - now more than ever.  Don\\\'t rob her of more than she has already lost.  Until age 13, in most states, children can literally get away with murder.  We have some real problems in the United States.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Your initial response was a great one: you showed her love.  She needs you.  If you have vacation time: take it.  Be with her.  Don\\\'t forget that this is going to take time for her to understand.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>It is an easy thing to kill, and a much more difficult thing to nurture and grow.  You aren\\\'t going to make the events of the past \\\"un-happen\\\".  What you can do is convince yourself and your daughter that there is a better future ahead, and then make it real.  You need to act like a family now more than ever.  Don\\\'t fight; don\\\'t yell.  Show your strength by building your daughter up.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There is a difference between giving and taking.  Help your daughter to understand, in time, that she has something good to share with someone who cares for her.  What was taken from her was by violence.  She should know that she is not alone; women and men of all ages have felt what she is feeling.  Can you go to counseling, religious or secular?  Have you thought of looking for a book or books on the subject, finding one that you feel comfortable talking with her about, and then giving it to her?  Is moving an option?  If you\\\'re in doubt, support her in what she chooses, as much as it is healthy to do so.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>To follow on Weeb\\\'s analogy: if she had been cut, you would take her to a place where her wound would be healed by a doctor who treated the body.  Your daughter\\\'s heart has been broken.  You, moreso than any other man, can help to restore her sense of self-worth.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>I am so sorry for all of you.</p> ',2,2585,0,0,'67.248.52.49','en'),(15780,2640,'2002-04-16 20:36:42',195,'matt','thank you. there\\\'s a computer in the activity room.',1,54,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(15781,2644,'2002-04-16 21:23:43',65,'le','this is really sad. i would say, make sure that you talk with her about it, or that your wife does. it\\\'s really important that you don\\\'t act ashamed of what happened or let her act too ashamed, make sure it\\\'s okay for her to say stuff about it. if you keep quiet about it or encourage anyone else to keep quiet about it, it will make her believe that it\\\'s some horrible shameful secret that should never be mentioned. i think as long as there is open talking about it that will keep her from having too much grey area to ruminate on in private and feel bad about. \r\n<br><br>\r\ni know when i was a kid, the stuff i was always most confused about or ashamed of was stuff we never talked about.\r\n<br><br>\r\nyou may be tempted to put her in therapy or something, but i\\\'d caution you not to do too many  things that will make her feel like she\\\'s Wrong or Different. making a really big deal out of the whole thing may have just as shitty ramifications as acting like it\\\'s no big deal. \r\n<br><br>\r\nalso, the feelings she\\\'s experiencing about her early bloomer body would probably be happening whether some stupid asshole kid assaulted her or not - the other kids probably already say shit about her boobs and  stuff, and make her feel like she is some kind of freak. this tomboy thing she is doing is a totally normal puberty thing, probably completely exascerbated by this event. when i was her age and until i was about 14 or 15 i tried to make myself as ugly as possible because the power of my own feminity was really unweildly. i didn\\\'t like the attention. \r\n<br><br>\r\ni would like to recommend a really great book to you about raising girls into women. it\\\'s called <a href=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0345392825/\\\">reviving ophelia</a> and it\\\'s about what happens to girls during puberty, not physically, but in their peer groups and in their heads. it\\\'s a really great book, it really helped me understand what happened to me around this time of my life, and it\\\'s very engagingly written with lots of real life examples. it definitely covers this tomboy behavior you describe, and talks about ways to help adolescent girls feel good about themselves. i think it would be a great read for you and your wife and give you a lot of insight into what your daughters face! it changed my whole outlook about the horrible things that i went through when i was their age.\r\n<br><br>\r\nsorry for all the long winded answers!! i hope between all of us you find some useful ideas. \r\n',2,2524,0,0,'209.86.0.8','en'),(15782,2644,'2002-04-16 23:23:57',75,'tyler','maybe you could both go to a self-defense class together. also, maybe get her a pepper spray keychain and teach her how to use it!',1,130,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15783,2644,'2002-04-16 23:42:08',65,'le','tyler, you suck. \\\"well honey, if you\\\'re gonna be a victim all your life, you may as well get some pepper spray and learn how to protect yourself!\\\" ',6,156,0,0,'209.86.0.8','en'),(15784,2327,'2002-04-17 00:20:41',266,'spleenless','ok, i\\\'m sure nobody\\\'s reading this any more but i like to obsess about things like this.  mercury isn\\\'t in retrograde and never has been.  retrograde motion of the planets is something that was thought up when people still thought the earth was the center of the universe.  it sometimes appears from earth that planets are moving backwards.  they\\\'re not.  they\\\'re just cruising around in their orbits like usual.\r\n<br><br>\r\nof course, people probably know that and just say that, during this time, that the planets are in retrograde but i wish they\\\'d say something else.',4,588,0,0,'66.92.184.86','en'),(15785,2644,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>i don\\\'t think self defense classes are that bad an idea!  it\\\'s not teaching your daughter to be a victim to acknowledge that sometimes you need to physically defend yourself.  i don\\\'t think it\\\'s ever a bad idea to help your kids be in a position to kick someone\\\'s ass if they\\\'re ever in real trouble.</p>',6,325,65,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15786,2630,'2002-04-17 02:37:20',148,'jeeff','i don\\\'t collect anything.  hm.  maybe thats where the gaping hole in my life is.',0,83,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(15787,2635,'2002-04-17 02:59:47',148,'jeeff','great story!  but.. people are actually afraid of snails?  or grossed out by them?  i used to play with them all the time.  sometimes i\\\'d let them crawl on my face as a kid.  nice and slimy!',0,193,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(15788,2644,'2002-04-17 03:34:32',65,'le','i dunno, it\'s not that taking self defense classes is a bad idea, something about the context of the comment bugged me. pepper spray especially is such a bullshit thing marketed to frightened women. i think coming up to this 11 year old girl and saying \"hey, since you got assaulted and all, maybe you should learn to fight back.\" it seems like saying \"since this will happen again\" and that\'s a pretty bad attitude to instill in an 11 year old kid! \r\n<br><br>\r\n<a href=\"http://www.emory.edu/WOMENS_CENTER/lornefa98.html\">here\'s a really interesting article about women\'s self defense</a>. try and look past the slightly sensationalistic language, there are some really good points.',4,619,0,0,'165.247.205.74','en'),(15789,2644,'2002-04-17 03:34:34',75,'tyler','how does teaching your daughter to  protect herself make her a victim? that idea doesn\\\'t make any sense. i won\\\'t attack you personally though.',1,148,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15790,2644,'2002-04-17 03:45:49',75,'tyler','i think that article is a little ridiculous by the way. how often (other than i guess in prison) are men the targets of random sexual abuse or sexual predators? also, the concept of \\\"women\\\'s self-defense\\\" is not sexist--women typically are weaker than men, and therefore have to use different techniques when defending themselves against a physical attack. a mugger etc. would probably consider a woman an easier target, <i>for the very reason</i> that the average woman <i>hasn\\\'t</i> had any self-defense training, not to mention a million schoolyard fights, football camp, etc. ',1,592,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15791,2644,'2002-04-17 03:46:51',65,'le','i don\\\'t have anything against people learning to protect themselves, i just think in the context of this article it\\\'s kind of a lame thing to say.  if i was a kid who had just been attacked and my dad told me it was time to take some self defense classes, and got me some pepper spray, i\\\'d think he was trying to prepare me for a long future of being attacked by people. or trying to say that if i had protected myself this wouldn\\\'t have happened to me.\r\n<br><br>\r\nin general i think it would be good if kids learned ways to protect themselves from attackers, and especially if young women learn to have a strong sense of self worth and even entitlement, so that when someone starts something with them they KNOW it\\\'s wrong immediately. women are raised to be demure and inviting, and that makes it harder for them to defend themselves. \r\n\r\n',3,856,75,0,'165.247.205.74','en'),(15792,2644,'2002-04-17 03:48:49',65,'le','sure, but don\'t you think the real question is why is our society fucked up enough that women NEED to take self defense classes to protect themselves from men? why don\'t we have men taking some classes that are like \"WHY YOU SHOULDN\'T RAPE PEOPLE 101\" instead?',3,279,75,0,'165.247.205.74','en'),(15793,2644,'2002-04-17 03:57:28',74,'jo','i <i>really</i> can\\\'t get my mind past the phrase \\\"with intent to arouse.\\\" that phrase is making my mind do angry backflips. and my stomach is joining in.',0,163,0,0,'172.158.178.186','en'),(15794,2644,'2002-04-17 03:57:50',75,'tyler','there\\\'s a question for the ages. should we disband the military because there shouldn\\\'t be war? should cops turn in their guns because there shouldn\\\'t be crime? why do we have locks on our houses and cars? we even had to get passwords for swinney.org for christ\\\'s sake. \r\n<p>\r\nattacks can happen. in this case one did happen. what would you rather do in a situation like that? reason it out, over coffee maybe, or spray pepper juice in someone\\\'s eyes?',1,466,65,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15795,2640,'2002-04-17 04:01:10',75,'tyler','you sound like you are handling this very well, matt. i bet most people in your situation would want to just sit around and mope, but you are working and being creative, and that\\\'s rare and special. ',1,202,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15796,2644,'2002-04-17 04:03:50',65,'le','again, i\'m not saying that people shouldn\'t learn to defend themselves, tyler, all i\'m saying is that we need to hold men accountable more often instead of saying \"oh she should learn self defense\"  without also saying \"and her male attacker should learn not to use violence.\" \r\n<br><br>\r\ni just wish that for everytime someone said that a woman should take a self defense course, someone also said that men should be forced to take anger and violence management classes. it\'s not fair that in high schools and colleges women are encouraged to take self defense classes when men are rarely made to take any sort of course that would help them to understand that attacking or otherwise victimizing women is not acceptable.\r\n',5,746,0,0,'165.247.205.74','en'),(15797,2644,'2002-04-17 04:42:51',75,'tyler','most men understand that attacking or otherwise harming women is not acceptable. the tiny fraction which does not innately understand this is not likely to benefit from a million college courses on the subject. ',1,211,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15798,2644,'2002-04-17 04:51:06',65,'le','i disagree, i think there\\\'s still a societal problem wherein men think it\\\'s ok to physically dominate women, or do it without thinking. this may not be the case in my incredibly hippie hometown, but pretty much everywhere else in the world it is. i\\\'m not just talking about outright attacks, i\\\'m talking about leering, catcalls, even just getting in a woman\\\'s space in a threatening way. ',5,403,0,0,'165.247.205.74','en'),(15799,2644,'2002-04-17 04:51:16',17,'austin','<a href=\"http://www.sustainedaction.org/Images_Photos/Carter.JPG\" border=10 alt=\"visualize your enemy\">image of a woman in back stance.</A>',6,83,0,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(15802,2644,'2002-04-17 05:09:55',187,'kandrer','<p>it seems to me that the problem of chuckspeed and his family is unfortunately much more <i>practical</i> than self defense issues.\r\n</p><p>\r\nchuckspeed, i think it\\\'s normal that you ask \\\"what should i feel?\\\" because with what happened you were expecting from yourself a \\\"clear\\\" reaction (like kick someone\\\'s butt). and you are here in a situation where there is no clear path to choose. for example, should you:<br>\r\n- bring you daughter to a therapist of some kind ?<br>\r\n- meet the parents of these kids? <br>\r\n- show your daughter that you\\\'re angry against these kids / the school ? <br>\r\n- move to a different neighbourhood ?<br>\r\n</p><p>\r\ni agree with others here: be very close to your daughter. talk with her. she is old enough to talk with you (and the rest of the family) about what happened and what you all want to do against it (even if there might be no easy solution).\r\n</p><p>\r\na simple first question: can the school make sure that your daughter will be safe from the same boys <i>inside</i> the school itself? if they can\\\'t you have either to see the boys themselves (or their parents if they have any control on them, which is not sure) or to put her in another school. seeing the boys might not even be a good idea; you could go to jail just for threatening them, i guess. oh my god, it sucks so much :(( and what do the cops suggested for inside the school?\r\n</P>',1,1410,0,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(15800,2644,'2002-04-17 04:54:44',180,'auntie','i was sexually assaulted the first time when i was 13.  i was also an early bloomer.  my assaulter was also another kid - a boy from my church.  Back then, 13 was like 11 today.  i had never been kissed, never thought about being kissed. <br><br>\r\n i didn\\\'t even know for sure how babies were made. i was very innocent until then.  everyone, adults and peers, blamed ME for the incident, said i was \\\"coming on to him\\\" -- i was flirting the way all clueless innocents flirt. <br><br>\r\n i was not asking to be assaulted.  it was NOT my fault.  <br><br>\r\n\r\nto this day i know that my mother believes that it WAS my fault.  that\\\'s because when i was 15 my stepfather attempted to rape me, and she thought that was my fault, too.  <br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\nthe only person who matters here is your daughter.<br><br>\r\n\r\nself-defense is in knowing your body is your own, it doesn\\\'t take pepper spray or karate classes.<br><br>\r\n\r\nself-defense is knowing that you are vulnerable in certain situations, and learning how to avoid them.<br><br>\r\n\r\nle, what didn\\\'t we talk about?\r\n\r\n',3,1077,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(15801,2646,'2002-04-17 04:55:22',65,'le','yes, they are made of stars and asteroids. and the motion of the atoms in your body changes every moment in time, like the motion of the stars in the sky and the planets in the universe changes. if we took a snapshot of the location of the atoms in one of your cells, we could define the moment in time by that snapshot. if we took a snapshot of the planets in the sky, we could define the moment in time by those planets. there\\\'s a word for that. it\\\'s \\\"astrology.\\\"\r\n<br><br>\r\nlove,<br>\r\nelly',4,504,0,0,'165.247.205.74','en'),(15803,2644,'2002-05-24 17:02:54',86,'egg','<p>I have a friend in marin who has a teenage daughter. When she was around 10 or 11 he and other parents in his town discovered that a teacher at their school was molesting one of the students. The parents immediately sent their daughters to karate class. His daughter, who is now 18, is a fierce black belt. </p>\r\n<p>It is difficult to find mitigating ground between suggesting that girls learn how to fight, and making sure she still feels safe, and directing energy towards educating the boys. I believe that sexual assault is not only  passively overlooked, but in fact subtley approved by our culture. In the end, a smart girl does have to live with the fact that she is in danger.  I know I do.  ANd that might be the fact forever. But your daughter is smart enough to know when survival tactics are being imparted and when she is being held personally culpable for being a victim. </p>\r\n<p>There are SO MANY reasons why your daughter was assaulted, so many elements of a gendered education that stretches back through hundreds of years of history and perpetuates itself every new minute. Your daughter was assaulted because young boys are angry, because they are afraid of girls, because they are afraid of their parents. Your daughter was assaulted because of the tv show \\\"the bachelor\\\". Your daughter was assaulted because of Kid Joe, and Eminem, and Ronald Reagan, and St. Augustine.  You can only do so much to unravel this machine that churns and groans over us every day. Small practical examples, such as telling her its not her fault, and joining your community not just to condemn but to educate these boys - and ALL THE BOYS. but the education cannot just be about touching someone someplace, it has to comprehensively address the violence against women that happens every day- in your house, and in the world.</p>\r\n<p>I dont think theres anything wrong with suggesting that a young girl learn self defense after a horrible incident. Just as assault is not merely physical, the right kind of training is not just physical; it arms you with an overall confidence that comes with being aware of the power of your body.</p>\r\n',0,2146,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(15804,2644,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>the only person chuckspeed has any real influence over is his daughter.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>saying he should arm his daughter with self-confidence, knowledge and a little kick-ass to back herself up is not condoning any actions by any violators anywhere.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>in many cases there is nothing you can do to place accountability on the shoulders of an 11 year old boy.  law is not currently set up to deal, and it seems obvious that the parents of the kid are not either.  this is glaringly obvious, and although this may be fixable, no one voice screaming can fix it right now.  in many cases all you can do is try to ensure your own family is decent, well-adjusted and capable.  you can educate your family and whoever else will listen.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"why you shouldn\\\'t rape someone 101\\\" is a course you are taught all your life in most \\\"civilised\\\" countries - through parents who scold children for fighting with their siblings, through religion which preaches peace and goodwill, through media which condemns crime and through school where you are punished for violence against others.  some people cannot be taught and they are dealt with by society.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>being prepared for the worst is not dictating that the worst will happen, let alone again and again.  it is merely a tool for confidence in the awful event of personal violence.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>......</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>that the majority of male humans do not condone violence towards women is moot.  i would say that the vast majority on this continent do not, in their minds, condone violence to women.  whether or not this is mirrored in their actions is another thing - but school or college courses on less violence to women would only reinforce what most men believe to be true anyway.  i believe that, in this society, most men hitting a woman do not feel proud of what they have done.  they do not believe it to be a positive action.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>okay this is far too long.</p>',6,1941,65,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15805,2646,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>your fingernail is more majestic than your conciousness?  ah really?  but where did your conciousness come from?  and what is it made of?  and how old is it really?</p>',6,171,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15806,2643,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','it\\\'s a hard thing to walk past, chia.',6,40,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15807,2644,'2002-04-17 07:49:39',65,'le','i wasn\\\'t really even talking about chuckspeed and his daughter at all anymore, i was just taking a more general issue with the popular concept of \\\"women\\\'s self defense courses.\\\" tho i do feel somewhat concerned that putting a recently violated kid into a self defense course might not go over that well. but maybe it would, you all seem to think it\\\'s a good idea, so perhaps i\\\'m wrong. if it were me i\\\'d probably not want to think about it anymore and not want to be forced to take self defense courses. but maybe doing something less obvious, like just putting the kid into aikido instead of \\\"women\\\'s self defense\\\" courses would be a good solution to buff her up and give her confidence, and have it not be directly related to the attack itself.\r\n',4,778,62,0,'165.247.205.74','en'),(15808,1712,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','wow.  that site is so great.  it completely kicks the ass of tubcat.',6,68,66,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15809,2644,'2002-04-17 08:11:52',65,'le','ps.\r\n<br>\r\nmy dad got me a pepper spray keychain when i was a kid and also taught me to go straight for the eyes if anyone ever attacked me. also he taught me how to use handguns. and i turned out good. so despite all my feminist GRANDSTANDING i think giving a kid a pepper spray keychain is a-ok.\r\n<br><br>\r\nalso, i\\\'d like to invoke incitor at this point.',4,359,0,0,'165.247.205.74','en'),(15810,2644,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>i don\\\'t think anyone wants to suggest that the girl should be forced to accept anything more than she has had to already.  i think people just thought it might make her feel more safe and self-confident.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i completely agree that there should be no violence to women or anyone at all for that matter, but that is kind of a given, really, isn\\\'t it, in the context of this thread?  i guess i just don\\\'t understand why you seemed to assume that tyler or anyone thought the boy should get off scot free and shouldn\\\'t be educated about non-violence. simply not having a solution to put forth for what happens to the attacker is not condoning or ignoring his actions, and it seems pretty insulting to assume that it is.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the article you pointed to seems to be as insulting to men as the examples within are to women.  it assumes that all men are given the message that they have free reign over the world and can walk unchallenged down any dark alleyway.  i know bugger all men who would walk fearlessly through a scary park at night, considering the world their domain.  violence isn\\\'t specifically targeted against women.  men have fear too, and yet the article suggests that not only do they not have fear, but women have men to blame for being fearful themselves.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the article singles out examples of women in court being asked the same quality of questions that men are asked when they\\\'re on the stand after being attacked by another man (why were you getting drunk at the bar anyway?  were you looking for a fight, etc).  the hypocrisy presented within that article was such that i found it of little value!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it is not fair to demonise men by  asserting that attacks are made by a gender rather than a calibre of person, and it is not fair to insult someone who is trying to help because they did not present a full solution to a problem.</p>',6,1898,65,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15811,2644,'2002-04-17 08:40:56',65,'le','i\'m not standing behind that article 100%, i just thought it was interesting and somewhat related so i linked it.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni am bailing out of this argument now because i don\'t think i actually have any real opinions or information about the issue beyond what i\'ve already said. thanks for listening.',4,183,0,0,'165.247.205.74','en'),(15812,2644,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i have been hearing all sorts of interesting things in this discussion, thank you all. the scariest and most disheartening thing i\\\'ve heard though, is the fact that we can\\\'t educate a boy who is 11 years old about the fact that what he\\\'s done is unacceptable. he\\\'s 11 years old. a child. yet we accept that he is an immoral monster without much more than a shrug.\r\n\r\nthis is disgusting to me. on that happy note, it\\\'s time for bed.',1,446,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15813,2644,'2002-04-17 09:13:14',187,'kandrer','i don\\\'t know if it adds something, but i thought that kids of 11 being violent are suffering of what psychology calls \\\"conduct disorder\\\". of course this is another huge area to discuss, but for what i read about it, it seems that there is no easy solution to apply to these 2 boys either (as chuckspeed understood). that\\\'s also why i thought we might have wanted to focus here on practical details that are the things chuckspeed needs right now. (btw, it\\\'s $2 for the lecture ;))<br>\r\n[x] nice like hell',1,518,0,0,'205.184.183.17','en'),(15814,2644,'2002-04-17 09:25:14',187,'kandrer','my comment wasn\\\'t the answer to yours, weeb - i didn\\\'t see yours before submitting mine.</p><p>\r\nalso: <i>everybody</i> here is disgusted, u know. still a few humans are more difficult to \\\"educate\\\" (socialize?) than others.',1,235,57,0,'205.184.183.17','en'),(15815,2610,'2002-04-17 10:33:07',200,'chiclet','people don\\\'t comment much on poetry here...  i just wanted to say - i like this.',0,83,0,0,'12.233.47.46','en'),(15816,2624,'2002-04-17 10:47:09',200,'chiclet','for me the lip balm is particularly poignant.  i find this piece quite moving.',0,78,0,0,'12.233.47.46','en'),(15817,1712,'2002-04-17 11:33:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i dunno! you\\\'d need a pretty big foot to kick the ass of tubcat :D</tt>',1,82,62,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15818,2635,'2002-04-17 12:47:33',123,'dcc','i want a pet snail.',0,19,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(15819,2610,'2002-04-17 12:57:23',156,'lotus','yes, this is very good. your use of whitespace is excellent.',1,60,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15820,2644,'2002-04-17 13:09:39',156,'lotus','my heart aches for your daughter. i can\\\'t imagine someone going through that at 11. <br><br>\r\ni keep writing and then deleting what i write, because it is not coming out right. what i want to say is, it is important to be there for her, in any and every way (listen, listen, listen, just spend time with her, let her feel safe) for a <i>long long time</i>, perhaps longer than you woudl expect. i am not talking of weeks or months, but years.<br><Br>',1,453,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15821,2645,'2002-04-17 13:10:29',123,'dcc','wow. thank you, erb. so illustrious you write. evoked many images in my mind.',0,77,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(15822,2640,'2002-04-17 13:19:58',156,'lotus','this is *spectacular*\r\n<br>\r\ni feel bad being in awe of something that is rooted in pain, but there it is. you write so well. i don\\\'t know many people who could pull off a narrative structure like this, but in your piece it seems organic, just right. i send you many good wishes and hope.',0,291,195,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15823,2637,'2002-04-17 13:23:59',156,'lotus','i tried commenting on this article over the weekend but kept getting a database error, thus this delay. i\\\'ve spent too much time in hospitals this past winter, practically living there with my siblings to take care of my mom. there are not enough nurses, and she needed 24 hour care, so they encouraged us to camp out in her semi-private room. i saw first hand what nurses do. some are wonderful, changing soiled bedsheets so sweetly my mother cried in gratitude. they went out of their way to make her comfortable. others were just horrible. they treated her so badly, especially when it came to pushing needles into her skin, it made me fierce.',0,649,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15824,2630,'2002-04-17 13:34:29',156,'lotus','i don\\\'t consciously collect anything anymore. as a kid, i had awesome collections of:\r\n<br> <br>\r\n* marbles, (i loved playing the games we invented, but i loved more the feel of them in my palm, all the different colours and kinds: cat\\\'s eyes, pearlies, rainbows) <br>\r\n* stickers, (i spent lots of my allowance on this, and peeled the stickers and stuck them into photo albums)<br>\r\n* stamps, (yay, penpals around the world!) <br>\r\n* airline tickets, (we love(d) travelling) <br>\r\n* erasers (you know, the fancy kind, those that looked like lipsticks, those int he shape of the alphabet etc) <br>\r\n<br><br>\r\nnow i don\\\'t actively seek out anything, but looking in my room, i see i have a disgustingly large selection of shower gels; blue shirts in different shades; and last summer, my sister was so bored she counted all my books, and reached up to 1324 before giving up. i love books.\r\n',1,897,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15825,2644,'2002-04-17 13:35:51',265,'chuckspeed','\r\n\r\n<p>It\\\'s really heartening to see such insightful and considered comments - thanks, all.  I really appreciate it, as it provides perspective in moving forward.  Here\\\'s an update:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My wife met with the detective yesterday - a mom, BTW.  What the boys did was criminal sexual misconduct - there WILL be an investigation, and there WILL be a probate hearing, at the very least.  In the case of one of the boys, the charges will be assualt and battery, as he told my daughter earlier in the day what he was going to do - and then he did it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The mother of the boy (single mom) appears to be a real handful - she is being VERY uncooperative.  She called the house last night - we\\\'ve been instructed to avoid contact with the alleged perpetrators (and their parents) by the police.  The detective also informed us that when it comes to sexual crimes, an 11-year old can, and will, be treated as a much older individual.  We can go so far as to get a restraining order, if need be.  If this happens - the other family has to move, not us.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I am, by default, a silverback alpha male.  You don\\\'t become an executive manager of a multimillion dollar global corp by letting folks run over your ass.  This is my family, though...I like to analyze things - solicit input, and then decide.  I didn\\\'t start this fight - nor did I ask for it - but you can be damned sure I\\\'ll finish it.  The trick is to use the resources available and to stay within the limits of the law.  My job as the father of two wonderful daughters is to provide them with empowering life lessons.  My older daughter isn\\\'t afraid of kicking ass and taking names - her jr. high has recognized this, and has been selected to be part of peer conflict resolution.  I\\\'m proud of that - I\\\'m growing a leader.\r\n\r\n<p>The younger daughter has more compassion than I\\\'ve ever seen in an individual. She has trouble eating meat - pretty sure she\\\'ll go vegan B4 she hits high school.  Everybody loves her - and this is perhaps why this angry, bitter, alienated child singled her out.  We\\\'ve had many talks - in short, digestible segments - since this happened.  She knows that we\\\'re NOT going to allow anything bad to happen to her, and she knows that she\\\'s responsible for standing up for herself.  She\\\'s gone back to wearing her favorite clothes, although whe won\\\'t take the baseball cap off.  If it gives her confidence and security, I\\\'m okay with that.</p>   \r\n\r\n<p>And Tyler...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Two things.  First, men DO get sexually assualted, and not in prison.  They get assaulted at the same age as my daughter usually - by men.  To say that they don\\\'t is a sweeping generalization, and flat untrue.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Second, our state has enforced a \\\'zero tolerance\\\' policy with regard to weapons - even those of self-defense.  There is a documented case of a child bringing a small baseball bat to school so as to defend himself against a bully - the possessor of the bat was expelled, while the bully remained to terrorize yet another day.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>While there\\\'s nothing wrong with self-defense, the answer is not that simple. Pepper spray is a glib response - how effective is it against a PCP-fueled sociopath with a Glock?  That\\\'s a rhetorical question - I already know the answer.  BTDT.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I guess the part that I have to come to grips with is that life\\\'s stage has changed.  This is a process.  The event and its repercussions will be with us for many years to come.  The trick is to lay out effective strategies and contingencies - with the \\\'roadmap\\\' in place, my kids will continue to grow in an environment where they feel safe.</P>\r\n\r\n<p> Thanks again, all.  You\\\'ve been a tremendous help. Really.  I owe a debt of gratitude.</p> \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',0,3807,0,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(15826,2645,'2002-04-17 13:37:57',156,'lotus to erb','please \\\"make more\\\" <br> i love reading your stuff. it is not like reading, it is like, well, it isn;t made of words, but sounds, images, feelings, thoughts.',1,162,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15827,2646,'2002-04-17 13:49:15',265,'chuckspeed','<p>torn off my fingernails on more than one occasion - they grow back.</p>\r\n\r\n<p/>been busted flat in baton rouge - to quote janis - and managed thru.  Keep your eye on the horizon, and your foot in the gas.  The desert passes more quickly than you realize.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n',0,267,0,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(15828,2642,'2002-04-17 14:03:49',265,'chuckspeed','<p>thanks.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s my personal fave.  the really sad part is its 97% true - a compliation of a decade\\\'s worth of street racing compressed into a single narrative.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The T/A fight was conducted with a Nova SS - the high speed blast across the desert floor was inna Caddy limo the wife and I bought as an, ahem...marital aid.  The blown motor was a race against a Maserati (motor blew under an overpass) and the failed valvetrain was a race against a Jag out on the freeway.  Closest I ever got to a \\\'57 Desoto was a \\\'56 Chrysler I bought for my wife - pink and white, Mabel was.  She ran outta steam at about 115MPH, tho.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>wrote the piece in a linear format about six years ago while listening to the instrumental in Prince\\\'s \\\'Darlin\\\' Nikki\\\' over and over again.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>come back nikki, come back...</p>',0,853,129,0,'208.5.114.85','en'),(15829,2644,'2002-04-17 14:16:18',75,'tyler','god luck. i didn\\\'t say that by the way.',1,42,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15830,2644,'2002-04-17 15:32:43',151,'stella','I think that with a Dad like you, your daughter will be *just* fine...',0,70,265,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(15831,2646,'2002-04-17 15:46:41',151,'stella','<p>I liked this post. It made me smile...</P>\r\n<p> Elly, yes. It\\\'s also called \\\'Natural Pantheism\\\' <br>\r\nhttp://members.aol.com/heraklit1/index.htm</P>',0,160,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(15832,2642,'2002-04-17 16:56:08',82,'chuck berry','<p>\r\nAs I was motivatin\\\' over the hill<br>\r\nI saw Maybelline in a coup de ville<br>\r\nA Cadillac a-rollin\\\' on the open road<br>\r\nNothin\\\' will outrun my V8 Ford<br>\r\nThe cadillac doin\\\' \\\'bout ninety-five<br>\r\nShe\\\'s bumper to bumber rollin\\\' side by side</p>',1,274,265,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15833,2642,'2002-04-17 17:05:12',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i saw a documentary about chuck berry once, and his supposed shit-fetish. there was footage of him, in his pool, coercing a voluptuous female to do the deed on his chest. think of <i>that</i> next time you hear <i>johnny b. goode</i>!</tt>',1,247,82,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15834,2630,'2002-05-24 17:02:54',86,'egg','i just remembered something. I used to collect dinosaur drawings by people I know. I have one by simon. the artist as a young simon.',0,132,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(15835,2642,'2002-04-17 17:41:06',239,'Golden Fountain','I spied a golden fountain once<br> \r\nits spires arched high and true <br>\r\nbut when i took a closer look, <br>\r\ni saw my shattered bliss <br>\r\ninstead of just a golden font <br>\r\nits pipes pumped only piss<br>',1,209,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15836,2644,'2002-04-17 18:13:23',17,'austin','<P>a word about \"self defense\".  having messed up through middle school enough to make high school an unavailable option, i ended up in a military school in rural Texas.  i spent the first semester getting hit in the balls every morning and being made to do chores for other people.  i was a bitch.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>the bitch shit stopped abruptly after a semester.  i think i just got tired of it.  other guys remained bitches for the entire four years.  some guys were never bitches.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i feel like i am in the fourth dimension even thinking about this.  i do not like to think about that time in my life, freshman year in military school.  it was pretty brutal, a state of nature.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>to this day i hate people who tell me what to do.  i think i always did and that is how i ended up at that school.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>the only significant change in my status as someone who is angry at being told what to do, or even allowing the assumption that i am under compulsion, has been karate.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>there have always been karate practitioners around whom i saw and respected for a certain command over themselves and their immediate area.  i never saw them execute any karate moves.  they  exude karate moves in their presence.</P>\r\n<P>i started a few months, perhaps 8, when a friend of mine who is a black belt and knew i was interested pointed out a good dojo near where i lived.  during the last 8 months, i have gone to class between 2 and 5 times a week.  there are physiological results that are nice.  i am in better shape.  i can stretch.  i can touch my toes while sitting down.  i can almost do the splits.  moreover than that, i know things about the realm of physical contact that most people have never imagined.  the simplest stance can render vulnerable someone who may not be attacking per se, but is violating my own personal bounds.  a shift of the feet and the advantage is mine to do what i will with.  that might be telling them to back off.  it might be spearing them in the ribs, or palming their nose.  it could be, \"excuse me, but your actions are inappropriate.\"</P> \r\n\r\n<P>also, most of the black belts at the dojo that i practice are old enough to have kids.  quite a few of them practice with their kids.  there are also parent and daughter groups.  there is for instance a black belt and her two black belt daughters.  they all started at the same time.  there is a father and son, who are brown belts, they both started at the same time.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>it is good to think about how we can make society more equal and teach kids to have respect for others.  karate is one way of reaching that goal for the individual and for society as a whole.  karate practitioners are an example of good conduct in most cases.  and in some rare cases, they teach lessons to those who fall short.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>p.s. it is really fun too!</P>\r\n** nice supportive **\r\n',2,2885,0,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(15837,2641,'2002-04-17 18:53:56',151,'stella','phew! I was feeling all over-sugary and anxious until I read your post, now I\\\'m drained and feel like a just ran a 5 minute mile.... thanks!',0,143,93,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(15838,2644,'2002-04-17 19:39:48',265,'chuckspeed','<p>thanks for sharing, man.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> got a few bitch stories myself - took many years of writing them down and then burning the book to set my world right side up again.  helped me at least...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>funny you should mention the karate thing - just got back from meeting with the ass\\\'t superintendent of the school district.  We talked about many things, including empowerment.  I want my daughter to feel empowered - it\\\'s why I\\\'ve bought her a car to restore (father-daughter thing) Figure if she drives to school in a car she\\\'s restored (a Subaru van, BTW) it will give her the confidence she *knows* things many boys don\\\'t.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>that being said, the karate thing is a good idea.  Never considered it as a father-daughter actvity; perhaps she\\\'d like that. I\\\'ll ask her - thanks.<p/>\r\n\r\n<p>and finally - really cool site, man.  thanks for making it available.  you know I ride (or used to) the snot outta motorcycles, too...</p>  ',0,962,17,0,'152.163.213.58','en'),(15839,2630,'2002-04-17 20:57:18',129,'moebius','comix, books, music of every kind predominate. something\\\'s starting to get going with DVDs.<br><br>for awhile i was collecting those silly taco bell dog dolls, mainly because they seemed kitschy and amusingly annoying at the time. now they\\\'re just annoying, so i\\\'ll probably give \\\'em to goodwill.<br><br>postcards. racy photos of drew barrymore. random polaroids found on the street.<br><br>movie & concert tickets. i like to use them as bookmarks. sometimes i\\\'ll return to an old book and find that odd ticket for a superchunk show or something & get nostalgic about it.<br><br>a couple years ago i started up with action figures, mostly from comix i liked. but this fascination is also fading out, partly because the cats keep breaking them and hiding their detatchable swords under the bed.',1,808,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(15840,2647,'2002-04-17 21:28:31',187,'kandrer','reminded me of <i>fear of music</i> by talking heads, in a way. <i>i won\\\'t listen animals advice...</i><br>\r\nexcept i\\\'m not scared by blank documents :)',1,158,0,0,'130.212.118.16','en'),(15841,2646,'2002-04-17 21:48:40',187,'kandrer','<p>(dreaming) i was about to ask <i>why choose your fingernail?</i> but i realized that, just by not choosing your penis instead, you\\\'re already making it more original than a big portion of the male population. oh, but i just supposed you\\\'re a male... what part of their body are most women  obsessed with? oh please, make that it\\\'s not boobs...\r\n</p><p>\r\nbtw, there is a very good post from s5 somewhere on swinney about this cycle of materials.\r\n</p>',1,462,0,0,'130.212.118.16','en'),(15842,2646,'2002-04-17 22:40:01',88,'deb','I think boobs are the part of the female body that most men are obscessed with; aside from others. I don\\\'t know if I\\\'d say obscessed, but I am pretty attached to mine...',0,175,0,0,'198.139.249.86','en'),(15843,2646,'2002-04-17 23:28:52',82,'simon','don\\\'t forget the ass-men of the world',0,40,88,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15844,2608,'2002-04-17 23:57:28',88,'deb','good luck',2,9,0,0,'198.139.249.98','en'),(15845,2648,'2002-04-18 01:57:17',190,'dahlia','*weeps*',0,7,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(15846,2646,'2002-04-18 02:38:42',59,'s5','was <a href=\\\"http://saturn5.diaryland.com/020302_59.html\\\">this</a> the post you were thinking of?',0,103,187,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15847,2510,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i like intestines.',0,18,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15848,2630,'2002-04-18 11:26:53',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i am endlessly enchanted by <i>random photos found on the street</i>; i find them frequently here in nyc. maybe we should start a gallery of these- there was a radom guy in my neighborhood who used to tape up polaroids of himself wearing nothing but a santa beard/hat and sunglasses. they\\\'d be all over bus stops and overpasses and newspaper machines.</tt>',1,367,129,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15849,2630,'2002-04-18 12:40:51',156,'lotus','<a href=<http://www.spillway.com/index.html\\\">this is marvellous</a>',1,70,66,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15850,2630,'2002-04-18 12:41:49',156,'lotus','uh...i\\\'m hopeless. here\\\'s the correct link: http://www.spillway.com/index.html',1,84,66,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15851,2630,'2002-04-18 12:45:07',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ooh, neat. i also like <a href=\\\"http://www.christop.com/found/\\\">this</a>, about found-photos</tt>',1,111,156,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15852,2649,'2002-04-18 14:29:22',265,'chuckspeed','<p>well written.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There are three seminal events in the temple of the body.  The first one occurs when the reproductive hardware gets switched on. Man, what a ride.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The second occurs when one discovers they have more in common with Mr. Glass in \\\'Unbreakable\\\' than they do with Bruce Willis\\\' character. (I forget his name...sorry)  You\\\'ll have plenty of time to think about the ramifications - fortunately, time does heal both wounds of the body and spirit.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> The third event occurs when one realizes one\\\'s fragility can be permanent, if not fatal.  The blown disk in my back (a motorcycle accident) was foreshadowing - gestalt occurred on an ER bed struggling for breath after an allergic reaction to a yellowjacket sting.  \\\"*this* is it?\\\" was my thought as my tongue swelled up to softball size...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>mortal</p>\r\n<p>fragile</P>\r\n<p>transient</p>\r\n\r\n<p>whatcha gonna leave when yer gone?  How didja treat those you love?</p>',0,980,0,0,'172.157.199.178','en'),(15853,2630,'2002-04-18 15:56:58',82,'simon','there\\\'s also a magazine out now called found. i think they have a website somewhere. it\\\'s full of found photos and notes and things. also, i\\\'m so glad everyone posted so many things about their various collections. thanks, guys.',1,237,66,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15854,2646,'2002-04-18 16:13:58',266,'spleenless','i like the post, s5.  one thing struck me as funny.  in the diagram you talk about, you end up with a line labeled \\\'life\\\' that points to \\\'shit\\\'.  life is shit?  (i\\\'m kidding.  i\\\'m quite happy with life in general.  it\\\'s not perfect but it\\\'s far better than any alternatives i\\\'ve heard of.)\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\none other thing:  those crazy oceanographer people have found that there are food chains at the bottom of the sea living off of chemosynthesis rather than photosynthesis.  there are critters than can produce energy from the heat and chemicals that come from deep geothermal vents.  no sunlight involved.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nthat\\\'s so fucking cool.  i\\\'m going to quit my job and go search for giant squid.  i\\\'m not kidding.',2,754,59,0,'66.92.184.86','en'),(15855,2630,'2002-04-18 16:49:16',66,'Peter','<br><tt>info on <i>found</i>? sounds interesting!</tt>',1,54,82,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15856,2630,'2003-03-05 23:09:34',129,'moebius','oooh, this reminds me of another collection i discovered on the web the other day: glossosaurus\\\' collection of <a href=\\\"http://www.glossosaurus.com/music/answering_machine/index.php\\\">found telephone answering message tapes</a>. weird, strange, hillarious.',1,264,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(15857,2630,'2002-04-18 18:11:39',272,'glossosaurus','hi.  thanks for the mention.\r\nthe magazine you are thinking of, Found Magazine, can be found on the Web at www.foundmagazine.com - good zine stores should have it too. (Quimby\\\'s carries it if you\\\'re in Chicago.)  Issue #2 of Found is being printed now and should be available in May.',0,289,0,0,'65.42.130.248','en'),(15858,2630,'2002-04-18 19:56:02',74,'jo','<a href=\\\"http://www.filling.com/reminder/index.html\\\">more found stuff</a>.',0,80,0,0,'172.135.4.78','en'),(15859,2630,'2002-04-18 20:17:31',82,'simon','i bet see hear would have copies of found magazine, if it\\\'s still on 7th and A. or St. Marks Books is a good bet as well.',1,124,66,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15860,2650,'2002-04-18 21:20:37',195,'matt','i always wanted to be a projectionist - see all those movies.',1,61,0,0,'209.208.77.105','en'),(15861,2646,'2002-04-18 22:32:34',59,'s5','i would never claim that life is shit.',4,38,266,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15862,2630,'2002-04-18 23:32:20',66,'Peter','<br><tt>mmm, <i>see hear</i>. also of note: <A href=\\\"http://www.abcnorio.org/\\\">abc no rio</a> has a zine library now. how cool.</tt>',1,138,82,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15863,2630,'2002-04-18 23:37:43',82,'simon','i heard a rumor that abc no rio was gone. interesting...',0,56,66,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15864,2630,'2002-04-18 23:49:45',265,'chuckspeed','<p>collecting?</p>\r\n\r\n<P>Life xperiences.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Don\\\'t carry a camera - write them down in the notebook of the mind</p> ',0,126,0,0,'205.188.200.41','en'),(15865,2630,'2002-04-19 01:39:38',66,'Peter','<br><tt>from their website: <i>\"Reports of ABC No Rio\'s death have been greatly exaggerated. \"Every time we send out a mailing or post something on the internet about the shows here, the same thing happens--all these people come back to us and say they thought we weren\'t here anymore,\" says Esneider, a long-time ABC No Rio volunteer, perhaps best known as the lead singer in Huasipungo.\"</i><br><br>\r\n\r\ntheyre still fighting ongoing eviction stuff, and are low-key as ever, but very much still around...</tt>\r\n\r\n',1,629,82,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15866,2646,'2002-04-19 02:36:09',187,'kandrer','<p>oh, thanks, that\\\'s why i couldn\\\'t find it! yes, i love your conclusion :) thinking about the <i>tiny backs of photons</i> is nice and fun (shit, i\\\'d like soo much to have more words in my vocabulary than nice and fun :().</p>\r\n<p>\r\na remark about this one though:<br>\r\n<i>what i\\\'m saying is, sunlight is the same exact thing as life.</i><br>\r\nam i wrong or you should have come to sunlight being the <i>opposite</i> of life?',1,439,59,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(15867,2646,'2002-04-19 04:38:42',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i think that <i>nice</i> and <i>fun</i> are about two of the best things you can say about something!</tt>',6,114,187,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15868,2650,'2002-04-19 05:46:11',75,'tyler','i didn\\\'t really get to watch them. there were ten theaters and one tyler. they were staggered so that I just had time to go from one to the next through all of them, and then it was only 30 minutes until the first one ended and had to be rewound. So I watched <i>Titanic</i> in bits and pieces.',1,297,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15869,2646,'2002-04-19 06:30:55',59,'s5','sure. the same thing or the opposite thing. however you want to look at it. i don\\\'t really know what i\\\'m talking about anyway.',4,132,187,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15870,2649,'2002-04-19 08:14:36',65,'le','you\\\'re a trooper, cat.',2,25,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(15871,2649,'2002-04-19 12:38:08',151,'stella','*ouch*',0,6,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(15872,2641,'2002-05-24 17:02:54',86,'egg','hi, Im supposed to be packing but i feel like posting something instead. joanne asked me if when my brain melts, does it harden again in the same blobby shape? and I think she is correct, which is why I asked her if it is tank top weather in LA even though I dont have any clean tank tops right now. I was really just making sure that its not too hot. I cant take the heat. I might be moving into the kitchen though. ',0,417,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(15873,2646,'2002-04-19 20:13:05',187,'kandrer','then here is another:<br>\r\n- what is the contrary of <i>sometimes</i>? it\\\'s <i>never</i><br>\r\n- what is the contrary of <i>always</i>? it\\\'s <i>never</i><br>\r\nso <i>sometimes</i> and <always> are the same thing.<br>\r\n[x] trying to be fun',1,242,59,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(15874,2646,'2002-04-19 20:15:25',187,'kandrer','<i>sometimes</i> and <i>always</i>, i meant.',1,44,59,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(15875,953,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','sine!  this is a really rad post!',6,33,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15876,2651,'2002-04-20 00:07:52',70,'Fuzzy','This was great.<br><br>It put a smile on my face.',1,49,0,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(15877,2549,'2002-04-20 00:54:51',187,'kandrer','what about if the type writter is a cursed one, that transforms anything you wanna write into something boring to death? shit, i\\\'m already bored by this idea, it must be my own keyboard i\\\'m talking about... :]<br>\r\nanyway, you\\\'re paper was for friday, today, so you would need a typewritter that just types stories instantaneously to type this...<br>\r\nwhat about a genie that only suggests useless ideas? :o)<br>\r\nok, i stop...',1,436,75,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(15878,2655,'2002-04-20 03:24:52',187,'kandrer','great!<br>\r\n(how can i have be so wrong thinking that muni was only wide and slow. it is <i>also</i> malicious).\r\n',1,114,0,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(15879,2651,'2002-04-20 03:47:01',175,'Paul G','Did you, or were you able, or did you even desire to connect with reality in the same way you did before your experience, after you experience?  How critical was your exposure to nature: ocean, cold, tree, etc., relative to the whole experience?  You employed chemical triggers.  Do you suppose that through manipulation of your senses by other means that you would have been able to achieve similar results?  Would the experience have been the same without the regimentation of the military to compare it to?  \r\n\r\n<p>Did both things: the military and the acid, serve to expand your consciousness: illustrating alternate and extreme modes of organizing reality, so that what was critical was really the juxtaposition of the two things in your life: alternate poles, if you will?',0,778,0,0,'67.248.52.158','en'),(15880,2651,'2002-04-20 03:53:18',175,'Paul G','military----------acid<br>\r\norder-------------chaos<br>\r\nroom--------------beach/ocean<br>\r\nfamily------------you<br>\r\ncallous-----------sensitive',0,146,0,0,'67.248.52.158','en'),(15881,2655,'2002-09-05 18:40:19',18,'ix','i recommend taxis! altho muni has been quite pleasant since i got back from australia.',0,86,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(15882,2652,'2002-04-20 06:17:48',175,'Paul G','Wow: great story!',0,17,0,0,'67.248.52.157','en'),(15883,2652,'2002-04-20 15:23:02',122,'hool','<p>agreed. this was really well told. i read it from start to finish, with no pauses in between.  this is a good example of telling a detailed story without going into a lot of literal detail.  i liked the part about the beetle best.</p>',0,237,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(15884,2651,'2002-04-20 15:52:19',75,'tyler','being in the military mostly served to teach me that what i really wanted to do was go for the outer limits of human experience and joy. i was sort of like that before i had ever joined though, because of my negative experiences in school(grammer/middle/high). being in the navy was being surrounded by ignorance etc., but to really go into that would require a whole other article.\r\n<p>\r\ni have never really connected the same way with reality since this night, but not just because of the acid/mushrooms, which i had taken before. it\\\'s the juxtaposition of what is happening at this point in the story and what happens next.',1,629,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15885,2651,'2002-04-20 18:52:20',196,'jasonp','I always find acid stories interesting. There are certain similarities you find and then there are the things unique to the individual and the moment.\n\nOf all my experiences on it the most significant was at the beach also. We started out along the cliffs that were by the beach watching the sunset. Later that evening we ended up on the beach itself. There was a full moon out and a few clouds in the sky which could still be seen. I was standing along a sea wall watching the waves come crashing into a tide pool area.\nThe sky was amazing it was varying in shifting waves to purple and blue. The clouds stood in extreme high contrast. The sky seemed alive and electric. The most amazing thing was the sight and sound of the waves coming in the moonlight. The whitewater was like a static TV pattern, very mesmerizing. What really got to me was the constant crash and roar of the waves. It totally surrounded me and turned into a kind of drone that made my body vibrate. At one point it felt like my body was a live wire with a ball of energy at the base of my spine. After a few minutes I had this \\\"at one with the universe\\\" experience where \\\"I\\\" lost all concept of identity. It was like being in heaven for a minute. \n\nThat feeling I had stayed fresh for years after that. I got into all kinds of eastern religion, because of it to try and figure out what I went through. It\\\'s been 12 years now so I remember what happened but that tangible feeling I had has entirely faded.',2,1492,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(15886,2656,'2002-04-20 21:49:32',187,'kandrer','<i>Magnolia</i>',1,15,0,0,'207.93.61.103','en'),(15887,2651,'2002-04-20 22:39:18',175,'Paul G','Well, I really connected with the article.  \r\n\r\n<p>The idea of making a transition from a military/para-military/institutional environment to some sort of time/space where you were able to re-connect with the sensual flux of nature really resonated with me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When swinney.org is at it\\\'s best I think that it offers that type of experience.  Many, if not most, of us are \\\"starved\\\" for something to counter the routine of our existence.  Oh well.',0,462,0,0,'64.156.106.61','en'),(15888,2656,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>sometimes people hurt you so badly that they will never again have a place in your life.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i used to think that everyone should always try and make ammends, but now i know that some hurts can never be forgiven.</p>',6,225,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15889,2651,'2002-04-20 22:52:50',66,'Peter','<br><tt>are you kidding? im in <i>love</i> with my routine!</tt>',1,64,175,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(15890,2652,'2002-04-20 23:17:19',187,'kandrer','<p>i agree that the beetle must have been a lucky sign - even though H, my only (and very dear) japanese-at-hand doesn\\\'t know about such belief (that might vary among places in japan, for sure).</p><p>\r\ni agree because, the only time i had the fine idea to leave my window open during 5 minutes of the deadly warm japanese summer, i got one of these <i>huge</i> flying cockroach inside my apartment. the beast was sometimes on the floor as well but i wasn\\\'t \\\"brave\\\" enough to step on it (what crunching noise it must make, <i>brrrr</i>). after half an hour chasing through the rooms looking at the ceiling or crawling to check under the furnitures, i considered the idea of giving up the apartment to it.\r\n</p><p>\r\nso yeah, a beetle must be good luck...',1,765,0,0,'207.93.61.103','en'),(15891,2656,'2002-04-20 23:50:12',187,'kandrer','i\\\'m a bit scared to write something longer than one word here, since this is \\\'sensitive\\\' stuff. hope i won\\\'t say something too wrong...</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbut no, o, i think <i>everything</i> can be <i>understood</i>, and what\\\'s the distance between understanding and forgiving? i\\\'m asking, since i don\\\'t know myself; forgiveness seems to have links with the ability to love someone; this doesn\\\'t help to clear the notion, i guess...<br>\r\nunderstanding means you know why people could became what they are. that doesn\\\'t imply that you love them this way.\r\n</p><p>\r\nin cynn story, if i understood correctly, the hate has been <i>transmitted</i>. that\\\'s very important, i think, especially when one think about forgiving (or understanding).\r\n</p><p>\r\nanother thing: it took me many years to realize that many things i was \\\"knowing\\\" about my separated parents had been told to me when i was a kid. i didn\\\'t know these things from my personal experience.<br>\r\ni am not saying these things were lies, but they might have been different, and i couldn\\\'t know for sure (i was 8 when they separated).<br>\r\ncynn might not be in that case but he was young too.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni did not meet, but they may exist, people that call themselves \\\"bastard\\\" without having a little piece of a good heart. cynn, i think you did well anyway to go and see him, and maybe you got more than what you expected from it.\r\n<p>\r\n<i>amen</i> (self-denigration)<br>\r\n[x] best friendly\r\n</p>',1,1493,0,0,'207.93.61.103','en'),(15892,1510,'2002-04-21 00:13:47',239,'Poonlarp','this is fucking terrifying. i might never fly again. thanks alot!',4,65,66,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(15893,2658,'2002-04-21 01:22:49',74,'jo','wow. <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2575\\\">i sense</a> <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2560\\\">a theme</a>. or a series, perhaps.',2,183,0,0,'172.154.53.111','en'),(15894,2658,'2002-04-21 01:38:39',239,'Poonlarp','and you think this is a coincidence? i have news for you- its <i>all about the tuna</i> baby!',1,93,74,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(15895,2656,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>understanding and forgiveness are not the same thing.  you can understand someone\\\'s motivations and never condone them.</p>',6,129,187,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15896,2658,'2002-04-21 06:06:34',74,'jo','no! not a coincidence! that\\\'s what i\\\'m saying! link up your shit, baby!<BR>\r\nalso: i loooooove tuna. love it. can\\\'t get enough. the tuna salad that my mom makes (which i can\\\'t possibly reproduce, not even when she gives me the recipe, not even when i make it in her kitchen with her supervision) is the best <b>ever</b>.',1,332,239,0,'172.165.151.55','en'),(15897,2658,'2002-04-21 08:20:50',176,'Scapegoat','After the last tuna article I included four tins in my weekly grocery budget. This week with the new findings related to sex I\\\'m upping my allotment to six. I\\\'m sold on the tuna ',0,184,0,0,'64.229.164.75','en'),(15898,2658,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>i am vegan and therefore poonlarp\\\'s sworn enemy.</p>\r\n<p><img src=\\\"http://saturn5.com/olivia/fighting_ladies.jpg\\\"></p>\r\n<p>fight to the death.</p>',6,158,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15899,2658,'2002-04-21 13:01:40',64,'Joanna','Does that mean that your phrase \\\"All girls should think of me when they\\\'re eating tuna\\\" should no longer apply, o?',1,123,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(15900,2658,'2002-04-21 16:27:40',239,'Poonlarp','is cunnilingus and/or fellatio off limits for vegans and vegetarians?',6,69,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(15901,2657,'2002-04-21 16:45:31',75,'tyler','you should say thank you and mean it.',1,37,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15902,2658,'2002-04-21 16:54:31',65,'le','no one has EVER asked that question before',5,42,239,0,'63.178.200.188','en'),(15903,2657,'2002-04-21 17:15:48',65,'le','maybe you should get some pepper spray.',4,39,0,0,'63.178.200.16','en'),(15904,2658,'2002-04-21 20:04:03',59,'s5','the answer is \\\'no\\\'',3,24,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15905,2658,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>you have to be really careful though.  you can\\\'t swallow, definitely not.  and going down on a girl is tricky because you have to make sure none of her secretions get into your mouth.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s so tricky that most vegans don\\\'t bother.  i gave up giving head years ago.</p>',6,289,239,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15906,2658,'2002-04-21 22:36:56',65,'le','yep. and after visiting the horrid sperm farms, who would WANT to swallow? i mean, rows and rows of men, and some women (there\'s a lower demand for female jism), all in tiny cages. so small they can\'t even turn around to clean themselves. there\'s a slatted floor so their pee and poo falls through, but the slats cut their feet. and i won\'t even go into the machines they use to milk the sperm. it\'s so vile and inhumane. free range sperm farms are only marginally better. :( :( :( after i found out about the sperm farms i cried all day. then i gave up swallowing jizz forever. that was the same day i joined the socialist party, threw out all my leather shoes, and bought a huge economy size bag of brown rice. and the following week, i followed through on my newfound political leanings by attending the free mumia rally and hoe-down in dolores park.\r\n',5,574,239,0,'63.178.201.75','en'),(15907,2658,'2002-04-21 22:48:29',65,'le','i think my comment page, when i\\\'m logged in, should have another comment category: \\\"strangely mean\\\"',6,108,0,0,'63.178.201.75','en'),(15908,2658,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','yesterday there was a free mumia rally in dolores park.  this one was for palestine.',6,84,65,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15909,2658,'2002-04-22 04:33:58',66,'Peter','<br><tt>someone hastily marker-tagged <i>free mumia!</i> on the side of my buddy\\\'s van last summer. its still there.</tt>',1,124,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(15910,2664,'2002-04-22 04:59:54',17,'austin','homesteader?',3,12,0,0,'64.174.154.109','en'),(15911,2664,'2002-04-22 05:01:59',65,'le','is that yours?',0,14,0,0,'168.191.185.44','en'),(15912,2660,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','you\\\'re welcome to come to california. we have *good* pot here.',1,65,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15913,2660,'2002-04-22 05:24:02',75,'tyler','awww, gee. we have really good pot here too. i just didn\\\'t have any then.',1,76,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15914,2664,'2002-09-05 18:40:19',18,'ix','my brother is executing my grandmother\\\'s estate. selling her house.',0,70,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(15915,1248,'2002-04-22 06:01:50',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i love how, sometimes, the folks here can form a very interesting support-group; cool.</tt>',1,99,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(15916,1248,'2002-04-22 06:22:07',65,'le','i\\\'d like an update from trouble on the state of affairs since he wrote this article.',2,87,0,0,'168.191.185.44','en'),(15917,2664,'2002-04-22 11:15:07',66,'Peter','<br><tt>the house that i grew up in, which was built in what is now two centuries ago, has an odd deed; seems someone who lived there back then had some sort of allergy to cow\\\'s milk, but not to goat\\\'s milk. thus, the deed provides that, despite the land being within the city limits, the owner could \\\"be in possession of one (1) domestic, lactating goat\\\". i always wondered if they ever sent around someone to check to see if the goat was actually lactating or not.</tt>',1,483,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15918,2660,'2002-04-22 11:19:36',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\\\"I decided that I had had enough\\\"</i>... powerful epiphanies, these sorts.</tt>',1,96,75,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15919,2657,'2002-04-22 12:27:44',75,'tyler','you don\\\'t love me, you just love my doggystyle.',1,50,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15920,2662,'2002-04-22 12:31:59',75,'tyler','when i have those dreams, i get up in the middle of the night and make up tai chi moves until i feel better.',1,108,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15921,2664,'2002-04-22 12:35:38',75,'tyler','sorry about your grandmother.',1,29,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15922,2664,'2002-09-05 18:40:19',18,'ix','thanks. she actually died years ago, but it\\\'s taking forever to get the house sold. as you probably know, kershaw county isn\\\'t exactly the most attractive place to buy a house.',0,182,75,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(15923,2663,'2002-04-22 16:13:22',66,'Peter','<br><tt><a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=542.txt\\\">i see a series here...</a></tt>',1,112,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15924,2665,'2002-04-22 17:31:58',75,'tyler','\\\"weird\\\" is an understatement.',1,35,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15925,2664,'2002-04-22 17:34:07',75,'tyler','yeah, i guess it\\\'s not. still, the suburbs of columbia are getting HUGE and it won\\\'t be long before that\\\'s prime real estate most likely. you should see the clemson road exit.',1,184,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15926,2664,'2002-04-22 17:34:43',75,'tyler','oh wait. that\\\'s farther away than i thought.',0,47,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15927,2665,'2002-04-22 17:57:12',65,'le','your fears seem justified. luckily, if you are honest and rational, and if he can be that way too, hopefully you\\\'ll sort out a true and steady way of being with each other. it might take a while. sounds like time is something you have though.',2,245,0,0,'63.178.200.194','en'),(15928,2656,'2002-04-22 19:19:29',151,'stella','<P>Also, when it comes to the pain that an addict can inflict on their family (emotional, physical and psychological) to understand the behavior sometimes is worse. I understand that my father is an alcoholic and I also understand that he has made the decision to kill himself very slowly so that we all get to watch. I understand that he no longer cares what we think or what we want. I understand that he has given up. That hurts more than you can imagine. Understanding really only means that you can no longer fool yourself to think that maybe the addict just doesn\\\'t realize the pain that they inflict. Understanding means that you can\\\'t deny that they just don\\\'t care.</P>',0,687,187,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(15929,2610,'2002-04-22 19:31:44',241,'linus van pelt','thanks for the positive feedback. this is the first proper poem i\\\'ve ever written, so it\\\'s especially nice that it was well-received. i wasn\\\'t sure i knew what i was doing.',0,181,0,0,'64.228.74.202','en'),(15930,2655,'2002-04-22 21:08:55',82,'simon','i used to work with a guy who also had a deep hatred of the MUNI and had to take a bus and then bart to work every day. he called in late to work one day because he had woken up and just couldn\\\'t imagine another day on the MUNI so he treated himself to a cab. and about halfway to the bart station, a MUNI bus hit his cab.',0,325,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15931,2659,'2002-04-22 21:15:17',82,'simon','in the mornings these days, i\\\'m the one who gets up and leaves. i usually get up quietly (and quickly cuz i\\\'m always late) take a shower and get dressed. when i come back over to say bye i usually get pulled back into bed for a minute and she mumbles, have a good day baby and pulls the covers over her head.',0,314,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15932,2662,'2002-04-22 22:51:23',224,'newfragile_yellows','that makes sense to me. i woke up talking a hundred miles a minute on saturday morning because i went into a cold sweat and all of these worries hit me all at once and my body and my brain couldn\\\'t keep up with one another so i paced around until it all got back in order. well relatively in order.',0,301,75,0,'24.209.71.12','en'),(15933,2625,'2002-04-22 23:13:51',75,'tyler','that was my 22nd birthday.',1,26,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15934,2659,'2002-04-22 23:25:54',65,'le','at night, peter is almost always asleep before me. i climb into bed and arrange him however i want, he sleeps so completely that i can mold him to my whim. roll him over or move his arms into my pillows. sometimes, when i am luckiest, he wakes up slightly and smiles at me. he tries to whisper affection but the words don\\\'t form. in moments of true blessedness, he notices my arrival and spoons me sleepily before descending back into his dreamworld. \r\n<br><br>\r\ni like some of the things about not living with my lover, though. i like waking up to his key in my apartment door on weekend mornings, when he\\\'s come to wake me up and bring me coffee. it\\\'s special to live together but it\\\'s so nice to be visited, too.\r\n',2,729,0,0,'168.191.185.12','en'),(15935,2665,'2002-04-23 00:20:02',83,'dza','going out on a limb, sometimes i wonder about that though -  judging people by how they look.  a few times i have tried to judge whether or not a stranger was a \\\"nice person\\\" just by looking at a photo of them, just for a fun little game  (-_*).  i know that\\\'s kind of psycho, but really think about it.  maybe our appearance really is shaped by our character more than we think.  it\\\'s mostly in the eyes and mouth, i think.   ?',3,440,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(15936,2656,'2002-04-23 03:23:21',187,'kandrer','<p>\r\no, i was thinking about a \\\'deeper way\\\' of understanding: not just gessing motivations but realizing that if you \r\r\nwere this person, having been raised the way he/she has, and so on, you might be acting exactly the same \r\r\n(i am sure many people expressed that before me in a much clearer language, BTW).</p><p>\r\nand, stella, i was specially thinking about understanding as remedy against hate, not against pain. forgiving \r\r\nsomeone won\\\'t take pain and regrets away, that\\\'s for sure. but maybe give the peace that is needed to \r\r\nheal.<br>\r\ni know this is a cliche\\\': i\\\'ve heard it many times, i think this would work with me, but there must be several \r\r\nways to balance one\\\'s psyche. only in the movies i saw people building \\\"positive\\\" things from hate (usually \r\r\na revenge :/ ).</p>',1,818,0,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(15937,2665,'2002-04-23 03:44:44',187,'kandrer','i don\\\'t get it: that\\\'s bad because love at first sight is something wrong? or romantism is not allowed because it\\\'s an arranged marriage? it does not seem so weird to me, if he saw you and liked you some time ago, if he talked to you a few times, if all this time you\\\'ve been sweet like you are when you write here, and if he knew that he could possibly marry you. why wouldn\\\'t he be feeling <i>some</i> love? it doesn\\\'t have to be lust! what about imagination? it doesn\\\'t have to be a bad thing.',1,517,0,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(15938,2656,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>harbouring hate is not healthy. but:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you can understand why someone committed acts, but it doesn\\\'t mean that you have to forgive it.  understanding does not mean forgiveness!  non-forgiveness does not mean living a bitter life of hatred!  you can heal without forgiving people who abused you.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>as an aside, being raised a certain way is no excuse (in my book) for acting in a harmful manner to those you love.  being able to let go of hate for someone who abused you is healthy and good.  sometimes forgiving is not healthy.  sometimes it is not necessary.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>sorry. i am re-covering territory.</p>',6,630,187,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15939,2665,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>i agree with kandrer\\\'s statement, but if you\\\'re getting creepy oompa loompa vibes, that\\\'s the way it is.  sometimes you\\\'ve got to listen to your instinct.</p>',6,173,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15940,2665,'2002-04-23 04:48:57',175,'Paul G','<a href=\\\"http://www.bobthealien.com/oompaloompa/pix6.html\\\">Creepy?</a>',0,76,0,0,'67.248.78.204','en'),(15941,2509,'2002-04-23 04:54:31',175,'Paul G','<a href=\\\"http://www.bobthealien.com/oompaloompa/pix6.html\\\">Creepy?</a>',0,76,0,0,'67.248.78.204','en'),(15942,2666,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','so cute!',6,8,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15943,2658,'2002-04-23 06:17:06',148,'jeeff','my favorite graffiti is \\\"super chloe #1\\\" written in the sidewalk cement outside the YMCA.  hooray for the internet!  \r\n\r\n<p>..and down with sperm farms.  i was stuck in one for months.  it left me a shell of my former self.</p>',0,233,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(15944,2659,'2002-04-23 06:21:14',148,'jeeff','when i was with jenie, she lived up in the top floor of an old house down by the highway, and her bedroom was in the attic.  there was a tiny attic window at the front of the house.  she had a custom-made coat-rack propped in the window, with big wooden letters that said \\\'jennifer\\\'.  whenever i\\\'d leave in the morning, i\\\'d look up and see her face above the coat rack.  it always made me smile, like a big name-tag.',0,428,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(15945,2659,'2002-04-23 06:40:54',74,'jo','i don\\\'t mind the leaving. i wouldn\\\'t want him to stay forever. it\\\'s the not staying the night which is bothering me. it\\\'s nice to wake up with someone.',0,163,0,0,'172.155.133.136','en'),(15946,2658,'2002-04-23 06:42:19',175,'Paul G','<a href=\\\"http://www.scifi.com/sfw/issue95/classic.html\\\">Vic is first overjoyed, then horrified as they laugh in his face and then strap him into a sexual milking machine.</a>',0,180,0,0,'67.248.52.59','en'),(15947,2657,'2002-04-23 06:48:11',175,'Paul G','<a href=\\\"http://www.udap.com/product.htm\\\">Down boy.</a>',0,61,0,0,'67.248.52.59','en'),(15948,2666,'2002-04-23 13:07:49',156,'lotus','dear austin,<br><br>\r\nthis was awesome.<br><br>\r\nl. <br>\r\nlotus',1,63,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15949,2665,'2002-04-23 13:09:45',156,'lotus','no, no, i don\\\'t think love at first sight is wrong or right, i just don\\\'t think it exists. he has only talked to me in person once, and on the phone a few times. we are not supposed to meet (in person) before we get married. so i don\\\'t see how he could fall for me. i think he\\\'s deluding himself. i don\\\'t know. it is all very dizzying. ',1,351,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15950,2663,'2002-04-23 13:10:53',156,'lotus','i wrote a ltter yesterday. i thought of your post while watching my handwriting (which looked like a foreign object)',1,116,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15951,2665,'2002-04-23 13:47:59',180,'auntie','my grandmother told me one time that if you have no expectations, you can have no disappointments in life.<br><br>\r\n\r\nshe also said that life without expectations can be frightening, but very exciting, because everything becomes an adventure.<br><br>\r\n\r\ncreative people have difficulty with this, often it is impossible not to project within the mind\\\'s eye what is ahead, whether it is the mix of people on the bus ride you are about to embark upon, or the conversation you will have with a friend about an item of mutual interest.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhen i was younger, i always tried to anticipate and expect the very worst from life.  my theory was that if the worst did not happen, i could be pleasantly surprised.<br><br>\r\n\r\nas i grew older, people told me i was negative, so i began to expect the best things would happen, and was often unpleasantly rewarded.<br><br>\r\n\r\nnow, as i near the half-century mark, i have no expectations of anything at all from anyone. the only person who does not surprise me is my husband of almost 30 years.  that is a comfort.  30 years from now, i hope you will be comforted by the lack of surprise, also.<br><br> ',1,1148,156,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(15952,2656,'2002-04-23 13:55:22',151,'stella','Hi. I know that\\\'s what you were getting at. In my personal experiences, the hate is gone, which is why I defaulted to speaking about pain. And I think that with cynn, more than likely that hate *was* pain, just in disguise. <br>\r\nI think that when you\\\'re talking about this kind of situation, where there\\\'s an addict involved (or an abuser) the hate is a mask for the pain. As the relationship is developing, or as the abuse gets worse, the pain is shoved down deep and covered with thick, wooden layers of hate that keeps the pain locked in. Because pain, in these situations, is a weakness that can be used against you. Understanding is definately important; so is empathy. But all the empathy in the world doesn\\\'t excuse or forgive. Sometimes people don\\\'t deserve to be forgiven. It\\\'s possible to reach this conclusion in a healthy way, which I belive is O\\\'s point.  ',0,891,187,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(15953,2665,'2002-04-23 14:18:08',151,'stella','<p> Hi Lotus! I don\\\'t know you, but it sounds to me that you were attracted to the idea of an arranged marriage because of the anonimity (meaning that the romantic relationships that you had had were nice, but that you were more into the idea of not having to deal with love) and the focus and emphasis on security. If that\\\'s true, I would hazard to guess that you\\\'re feeling uneasy because if he starts to allow himself to \\\'fall\\\' for you, that would remove the construct of security and put you back to a place that you were looking to avoid. Again, I don\\\'t know you, so I\\\'m sure that the probability of my being way off is quite high. </P>\r\n\r\n<p> Also, I\\\'d like to know what a \\\'creepy oompla loompa\\\' vibe is... </P>\r\n',0,749,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(15954,2659,'2002-04-23 15:24:35',156,'lotus','what you didn\\\'t know is that i like to sleep in just as much as you do, only when i was living with you, i had to get up earlier than you. you see, i\\\'d much rather watch you waking up than catch the last few cobwebs of sleep. <br><br>what you didn\\\'t know, as you exclaimed in surprise that i always woke before you, is that i would trace the air over your face, i would watch your eyelids move, i would admire you, and only after you left, would i fill up on my sleep.',0,477,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15955,2665,'2002-04-23 16:38:36',265,'chuckspeed','<p>to be honest, the process of \\\'falling\\\' and then committing to another was the most terrifying thing I\\\'ve ever done...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'m glad it\\\'s past.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s okay to be scared.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i remember the exact moment i first saw her - what she was wearing, what she was doing, the expression on her face...didn\\\'t know at the time she was \\\'the one\\\' tho.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>that was eighteen years ago.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>when it started to happen for me - i freaked. somehow, i convinced myself it was a sham - she really couldn\\\'t feel the same way.  soo...we talked about it.  nearly twenty years later, that ranks as the most important conversation i\\\'ve ever had.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>she\\\'s still beautiful.</p> ',0,726,156,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(15956,2660,'2002-04-23 16:46:22',265,'chuckspeed','<p>my heart was doing 160bpm</p>\r\n<p>i was floating near the ceiling</p>\r\n<p>my head was a cabbage.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>that was it. no mas.</p>\r\n',0,136,75,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(15957,1561,'2002-04-23 17:22:20',239,'Poonlarp','the Â£ is definitely gay, while the $ is holding a staunch hetero worldview. the euro is copping out by claiming bisexuality. damn euro.',1,135,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15958,2665,'2002-04-23 20:13:54',187,'kandrer','long time after submitting my comment, i was still thinking about it, a bit amazed by what i wrote. remember, at first, i was freaking out about your arranged marriage with an unknown person... and i think i am still scared for you. so i agree 100% with o (o is cool) about the bad \\\'vibes\\\'. i trust intuition, too, and in that way, bad vibes might mean that you unconsciously noticed nasty details. and i am paranoid too, so it\\\'s mmm... my duty :) to tell you to be careful like hell, ok?</p><p>\r\nbut, but, but, and this is i guess why i wrote my first comment, i have always been what french call \\\"an artichoke heart\\\". this means a person who falls in love quite fast. i don\\\'t know if it\\\'s good; i perfectly see reasons why it\\\'s bad (you mentionned some of them). but that\\\'s why i was feeling like defend your husband-to-be.<br>\r\nthe second time i saw her, i told my wife i was falling in love. she took months to believe me. we\\\'ve been together for 2 years. i can\\\'t say if the way i feel (and the way i felt) is the good one. i don\\\'t think i am deluding myself, though, or i have been doing this all my life...\r\n</p>\r\nwell, be careful anyway >:).',1,1184,156,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(15959,2665,'2002-04-23 20:18:40',59,'s5','an artichoke heart! can i use that phrase even though i\\\'m not french?',1,72,187,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(15960,2665,'2002-04-23 20:20:33',265,'chuckspeed','en francais, s\\\'vous plait?',0,29,0,0,'208.5.114.119','en'),(15961,2667,'2002-04-23 20:50:14',151,'stella','In the little spaces in between, my friend. ',0,44,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(15962,2656,'2002-04-23 21:01:10',187,'kandrer','no, *i* am sorry, since i am far to know enough about this to be arguing here. this is one of my defects: i like to argue, rather for the fun of it. so don\\\'t take me too seriously. :)<br>\r\na few notions i would need to be sure about: forgiving, understanding, hate, pain, empathy.\r\n</p><p>\r\nfor example, in my idea, i would understand/forgive anybody, but <u>that doesn\\\'t mean i will love them or even trust them afterward</u>. i thought of forgiving/understanding as \\\"do not feel hate anymore\\\".<br>\r\nand my idea of hate was: \\\"to wish bad things to someone, or favorize these bad things to happen\\\"<br>\r\nonce again, i don\\\'t think i am right; i am just trying to explain my definitions (and i know i am quite a sick person in my way).\r\n</p><p>\r\nwith these definitions:<br>\r\nunderstanding = forgiving<br>\r\nnon-forgiveness = bitter life of hatred<br>\r\nhating = non understanding => difficulty to heal (since you don\\\'t understand your pain)<br>\r\netc...\r\n</p><p>\r\ni found stella\\\'s description of \\\"layers of hate\\\" excellent. i kinda \\\'learned\\\' that stuff too in my time. that\\\'s specially useful when you still have to deal with these harmful people (in cynn\\\'s case it\\\'s finished).<br>\r\nthough i don\\\'t agree with \\\"Sometimes people don\\\'t deserve to be forgiven\\\". i think of forgiving/understanding as something you do for yourself, <i>not</i> for the people who abused you. so i would say \\\"sometimes people don\\\'t deserve to be trusted anymore\\\", but it\\\'s always healthier for yourself to forgive them.<br>\r\nwhen i said \\\"only in movies\\\", i meant i can\\\'t figure out one example where, as you say, o, \\\"let go of hate\\\" is healthy. oh, wait, i see 2: one if you have to fight against this person (hate will make you stronger), and two if you accumulated unexpressed hate fealings for years (you have to release it, the earlier the better).\r\n</p><p>\r\nand... i really enjoy talking with you two, thanks :). i don\\\'t know if cynn will even come back and read anything here anyway, btw.\r\n</p>',1,2061,0,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(15963,2665,'2002-04-23 21:11:13',187,'kandrer','- s5, yes, but this will make people\\\'s eyebrows rise (raise?), like when my french manager said in a meeting (here in the US) \\\"we\\\'re gonna do it <i>fingers in the nose</i>\\\" (that means it\\\'s easy in colored french)<br>\r\n- en francais, \\\"un coeur d\\\'artichaud\\\" (the o and the e are merged)\r\n',1,311,0,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(15964,2667,'2002-04-23 21:33:03',83,'adamajah','go bungee jumping in one of those little spaces',0,47,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(15965,2659,'2002-04-23 21:40:12',124,'gus','The staying. I am addicted to the last squeeze and the final \\\"sweetheart\\\" right before I fall asleep with the spoon in my back. ',0,134,0,0,'64.160.55.127','en'),(15966,2666,'2002-04-23 21:53:04',90,'leonard','austin, next time can you sit on your scanner and scan your nuts for us. i\\\'ve got a friend here who, instead of reading palms, reads the lines in nut sacs. she even dabbles in a bit of teabagging, which i hear you take w/ two lumps.  ',1,237,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(15967,2667,'2002-04-23 21:55:23',124,'gus','living and writing lists never go together. I have never understood the people who make lists. Not that this post is super listlike, but it is a little. I especially dont understand the list of \\\"things I want to do before 30/50/death\\\". We live when we arent making lists. We do things or we dont do them by the time we\\\'re 30/50/dead. \r\n<p>\r\nMake a list and you will only notice what you have NOT done. That isnt living.',0,428,0,0,'64.160.55.127','en'),(15968,2666,'2002-04-23 22:09:24',17,'austin','uh, i believe your fiance already read me my fortune while staring at my nuts through her ARABIAN GOGGLES.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nas for tea bagging, the only tea bags i take are used tampons.',4,180,90,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(15969,2667,'2002-04-23 22:12:04',129,'moebius','<br>there is nothing wrong with making lists. we\\\'ve all been touched by the need, at some point, to sort, categorize, order, and list things. (even if we never actually write them down.) it\\\'s a way of making sense of the world on one hand, and of trying to ensure that certain minutiae won\\\'t be forgotten on the other.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni personally ought to make <i>more</i> lists; i\\\'m terribly unorganized and forgetful. but i always forget to make lists. i\\\'m going to have to put that down as #1 on my List Of Things To Do To Get Organized, when i get around to it.\r\n<br><br>\r\nin fact, a few people are so obsessed with the lists, they\\\'ve created a whole magazine<a href=\\\"http://www.todolistmagazine.com/\\\">magazine</a> about them. go lists!',3,760,124,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(15970,2665,'2002-04-23 22:26:01',129,'moebius','<br>holeymoley auntie, that\\\'s good advice you gave up there. it kind of accurately reflects the space i\\\'ve been in for several months now, but i\\\'ve not been able to put my finger on what\\\'s changed. all i know is i\\\'m fairly happy to be in the space i\\\'m in now, and i\\\'m taking a few more risks than i would\\\'ve taken a few years ago. accepting opportunities given, letting go of those taken away, and not reading much of anything into either situation.<br><br>and what\\\'s new? lack of expectation. just day to day life and all the possibilities entailed in such. right on.',2,595,180,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(15971,2667,'2002-04-23 22:36:36',124,'gus','I think I will have to just agree to disagree!\r\n<p>\r\nor something. hi.',0,70,129,0,'64.160.55.127','en'),(15972,2669,'2002-04-23 23:07:46',82,'simon','i can think of lots of things to say here but instead i\\\'ll reduce it all to a food anecdote and tell you that my grandmother buys the following when she knows i\\\'ll visit, she loves me and she\\\'s proud of herself for remembering: marinated mushrooms, individually wrapped cheese slices, frosted oatmeal cookies, TAB, and saltines. ',0,338,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15973,2669,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','*love*\r\n\r\noh le. this made me love and commiserate with you so so much. i admire your bravery in saying these things in a public forum, where your mom reads it, no less. it\\\'s so hard to say no to a parent\\\'s love, even when its in the form of hydrogenated fats and processed sugars. i find myself letting my parents browbeat me, because that\\\'s how they love. that\\\'s how they know how to give me attention. i can only gently remind them once a while that i am me, and they can\\\'t change how i act. i can\\\'t change how they act either. and somewhere in the middle we co-exist, somehow. tethered by common dna and a sense of obligation. parental love.\r\n\r\ni think everyone has an image of the perfect parent, and mine would be pretty close to the ones i have... with some minor and impossible changes. if i were a parent, i would have a happy, healthy and loving child, and everything else would be optional. \r\n\r\nlove to you and auntie. ',1,948,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15974,2666,'2002-04-23 23:48:29',90,'leonard','that\\\'s it... you\\\'ve been added to my bozo list. you RAT BASTARD, and you\\\'re getting a goddamn kid\\\'s meal and the piece of cake i shove my dirty thumb into.',6,167,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en');
INSERT INTO `comments` VALUES (15975,2668,'2002-04-24 00:15:45',65,'le','just today, i had expressed a desire to explore dark writing. wanting to write things that people hate but are good, well written, affecting. little did i know that while i was expressing this desire, you were carrying it out.',2,226,0,0,'168.191.184.215','en'),(15976,2667,'2002-04-24 00:26:32',265,'chuckspeed','<p>it struck me whilst waiting for the police report on my daugter\\\'s attack that we do an awful lot of waiting.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>unaware, we can spend our whole lives...waiting.  Waiting for a better job, a cuter girlfriend, a nicer house, a better offer...Before you know it, we\\\'ve waited our lives away</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n',0,313,124,0,'152.163.201.199','en'),(15977,2667,'2002-04-24 01:09:06',124,'gus','That reminds me of an old boyfriend of mine. He loved me but was always looking/waiting for something \\\"better\\\". I kept asking him, \\\"if you\\\'re always looking for something better, how will you know when you\\\'ve found it?\\\" (He found out later, the answer was: when it\\\'s gone). live in the moment!  \r\n',0,318,265,0,'64.160.55.127','en'),(15978,2667,'2002-04-24 01:42:18',65,'le','i make a lot of lists. i think it\\\'s kind of OCD. here\\\'s a list of lists i\\\'ve made, started to make, or conceived of making when i should have been sleeping:\r\n<br><br>\r\n<pre>\r\n- everyone i ever kissed\r\n- everyone i ever fucked\r\n- every aries i ever dated\r\n- everyone i ever met\r\n- every place i\\\'ve ever used the internet\r\n- every place i\\\'ve been, by country\r\n- every place i\\\'ve been, by city\r\n- every place i\\\'ve been, anecdotal\r\n- every school i ever went to\r\n- <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=464.txt\\\">things i\\\'ve chewed</a>\r\n- everything i own, with related stories\r\n- every bottle in my bottle collection\r\n- everywhere i ever peed that wasn\\\'t in a toilet\r\n</pre>\r\n<br><br>\r\nthere are a lot of listmakers on swinney. soon i will make a \\\"lists\\\" series.\r\n\r\n',2,817,124,0,'63.178.200.174','en'),(15979,2669,'2002-04-24 01:48:36',86,'egg','<p>does the title \\\"the rest of america\\\" imply that those on swinney.org or people you/we know in the Bay Area are not excessive in their consumption, vegan, sweatshop-banning, solar power-utilising, compost-building as some may be? thats a laugh. but even though I just ate some peanut butter pancake, and may later eat some pork, i have to say that after reading this I have decided not to read and watch tv at the same time. </p>',0,437,0,0,'216.244.19.127','en'),(15980,2669,'2002-04-24 01:55:41',65,'le','egg, am i losing my touch? you\\\'re supposed to feel bad.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni don\\\'t know what  the title means, i should really leave the word \\\"america\\\" out of things. i think i meant, everywhere i don\\\'t usually see. not saying people in the bay area are superior, just maybe that i feel more understood there? i don\\\'t know.',4,333,0,0,'63.178.200.174','en'),(15981,2669,'2002-04-24 02:00:08',174,'Mad Moll','<p>It is an ugly system, but I admire its efficiency and its ability to both exploit our Slave Colonies and dupe the consumers into buying the trash that comes from them, and then to market and deploy all of it in a single, universal and convenient nexus which, to the average hausfrau, appears completely spotless with respect to the box of horrors needed to make things so cheap E-Z.</p>\r\n',3,391,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(15982,2668,'2002-04-24 02:05:38',75,'tyler','wtf',1,3,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(15983,2668,'2002-04-24 02:10:18',174,'Mad Moll','This reminded me a lot of this:\r\n\r\nhttp://www.barcelonareview.com/23/e_tc.htm',1,77,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(15984,2665,'2002-04-24 02:30:57',156,'lotus to everyone','i have so many good people in my life, here and elsewhere. thank you everyone for responding, for your kindness, and for helping me think things through. ',0,154,0,0,'24.156.95.178','en'),(15985,2668,'2002-04-24 02:36:25',156,'blue stocking','dear le. you know what? i wrote this a couple of days after my mom told me what happened to the girl. (my aunt in the states, in texas, where it happened, actually knew the girl) but when i posted it on the creative writing forum i co-founded, i got burned so badly, that i eventually only put it under a locked diaryland. the funny thing is, for years people kept complaining i was \\\"too nice in [my] writing\\\" and then, when i decide to tell the truth about something, to not try to prettify it, they freaked out. i don\\\'t know. i was called names, and was accused of sensationalising or deriving sick pleasure or what. i don\\\'t knpow. i just know i had to write it. this was my way of shouting back. the backlash against muslims, and against dark skinned people, was tremendous in the weeks and months after sept 11, even in canada. okay this is turning into a huge incoherent ramble, i don\\\'t even know what i am trying to say except your support means so much to me and i feel like bawling and.',1,1009,0,0,'24.156.95.178','en'),(15986,2668,'2002-04-24 02:38:06',155,'blue stocking','? what does \\\'wtf\\\' refer to? please note that i did put \\\'warning: graphic\\\' in the blurb. please reply.',0,113,75,0,'24.156.95.178','en'),(15987,2668,'2002-04-24 02:40:09',155,'blue stocking','thanks for the link to the barcelona review. i looked around (just in that issue) and mark anthony jarman is one of my aboslute favourite writers. i\\\'ve got a bunch of his books, and his shrot stories are mind blowing. sly and funny and convoluted and so open-to-meaning and just such fun to read. the thomas glave story (heard of this author but never read him) was...i don\\\'t know what to think. disturbing. why did i write this? what did i expect to get out of it? i don\\\'t know.',0,488,174,0,'24.156.95.178','en'),(15988,2669,'2002-04-24 02:43:31',155,'blue stocking','they built a walmart five minutes from our house. there used to be only two in ottawa. now i don\\\'t know how many there are. this article made me feel both profoundly sad and extremely grateful, all at the same time. there are many things sparked off in my head as a result of reading it, but...no words. sorry. on a side note, a couple of good friends introduced me to an awesome site today, and i was thinking of taking a break from swinney to go check it out and well, immerse myself in it. but then i read this, le, and i thought, God, no, i can\\\'t risk missing writing like this. love to you and yours.',1,611,65,0,'24.156.95.178','en'),(15989,2667,'2002-04-24 02:53:34',155,'blue stocking','lists rock. i couldn\\\'t live without them. i have journals full of lists. my outlook folder at work is full of lists on those fake digital post it notes. i have a never ending list of all the books i want to buy. i have lists of movies to watch. i have lists of things to write about, mostly just one or two word jottings because i don\\\'t have time at the moment to write the whole thing, but if i ignore the impulse correctly, i forget it. and that goes to the heart of my listmaking. i have the world\\\'s second worst memory. even when something is terribly important to me, i\r\nm liable to forget it if i don\\\'t write it down. so i have to make lists. it is a form of survival. it makes my life more fun, more useful, good. heck, my life is composed of scraps of paper, the baacks of envelopes, receipts, movie stubs, and post-it-notes.',1,845,0,0,'24.156.95.178','en'),(15990,2668,'2002-04-24 03:09:29',65,'le','yeah, blue_.  what you describe is the writing version of virgin whore complex. if you won\\\'t put out, you\\\'re a prude, but if you spread your legs and show em the real meat, you\\\'re a whore. \r\n<br><br>\r\nit\\\'s funny to me that you\\\'re writing on another site where people got offended at this work. when i was thinking about writing some exploratory dark stuff today, i was feeling slightly concerned because there\\\'s a lot of mutual support and backpatting around swinney and i thought maybe it\\\'d be shitty of me to really challenge people\\\'s happy go lucky well being with something ugly. obviously i need not worry, because no one here seems too disturbed by this piece and you say elsewhere they were.\r\n a lot of times, though, if you write disturbing shit, everyone acts all offended or annoyed, but you know what? life is disturbing. the darkest parts of life are sometimes the most beautiful though. i have been reading a lot of alan moore lately, and he really runs that point home in his writing. i don\\\'t know how he does it, but it\\\'s something to aspire to. he paints the horrors of human existence rather brutally and specifically, then redeems it all by showing how they are part of a greater whole, and how the greater whole is beautiful in its intricate balance. it\\\'s amazing, his writing should by all means be depressing but leaves me feeling so thankful to exist.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni\\\'m totally rambling did you notice?\r\n\r\n',4,1461,0,0,'168.191.184.149','en'),(15991,2668,'2002-04-24 03:36:12',66,'Peter','<br><tt>and sometimes its the <i>offended or annoyed</i> that really need to be disturbed</tt>',1,94,65,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(15992,2656,'2002-04-24 03:55:48',132,'cynn','i\\\'ve been following the entire discussion that\\\'s taken place since i posted my article.  i didn\\\'t post any comments initially because i wanted to see what people had to say without my opinion getting in the way.  it\\\'s interesting to hear other people\\\'s take on understanding and forgiveness.  i may post my thoughts on that at some point.',0,353,0,0,'216.8.154.50','en'),(15993,2669,'2002-04-24 04:32:38',86,'egg','wait, what am i supposed to feel bad about? doritos? walmart? pancakes? not being skinny? if there is a specific agenda youre going for, im all for it. i do prefer specifics when it comes to feeling bad. id rather not just feel general guilt. i dont want to sound cynical and apathetic either, im just not sure what exactly is at stake here. ',0,342,65,0,'216.244.18.57','en'),(15994,2669,'2002-04-24 04:42:04',65,'le','i was joking, i don\\\'t actually have any agenda and there\\\'s nothing at stake. ',3,83,86,0,'168.191.185.174','en'),(15995,2669,'2002-04-24 05:30:33',74,'jo','<p>i can relate to this a lot. my parents offer comfort and love in the form of food, too. my first instinct is to guess that it has something to do with living through wars and being poor and growing up through times when families couldn\\\'t afford frivolties so doing so is both an offer of comfort and of stability?</p>\r\n<p>i definitely knew things were okay at home when we could afford brand name cereals. likewise, i knew they were bad when the freezer was filled with sams club mini-quiches, or 20-packs of ramen.<BR>\r\nbut this diversion is more about economy than using food as a panacea, which is a whole other thing, and which i also have a lot of thoughts on, particularly in regards to my mom.</p>\r\n<p>my dad was diagnosed with diabetes several years ago. he learned how diabetics should eat, he bought the right foods, became hyper-vigilant, and lost about 50 lbs in 5 months, which excited me, because while he didn\\\'t eat foods that were bad for him, per se, i want him to live for a long time, and this disease forced him to learn how to take care of his body.</p>\r\n<p>my mom, on the other hand, still eats a lot of shit, and will do things that blow my mind. like when she wants to prepare a healthy vegetarian side for me at easter dinner so she makes broccoli and rice casserole, which is 2/3 velveeta cheese and a cardiac arrest waiting to happen, and is surprised when i don\\\'t eat any of it.</p>\r\n<p>i was thinking about this today, actually, because a jenny craig commercial was immediately followed by a slim fast commercial, <b>immediately</b> followed by one of those \\\"eat subway twice a day!\\\" commercials, and i was enraged because none of those \\\"diets\\\" actually TEACH you anything about taking care of yourself. they just do the hard work for you. and people wonder why they gain the weight back so quickly. and don\\\'t even get me <b>started</b> on the atkins diet.</p>\r\n<p>um. okay. done.</p>',4,1940,0,0,'172.139.175.18','en'),(15996,2667,'2002-04-24 05:31:34',124,'gus','oh. you guys are talking different kinds of lists than I was thinking of. yah. I have a list of everyone I\\\'ve ever kissed. but it happened randomly. I think lists of things you HAVE done are different than lists of things you havent done or think you SHOULD do. yah. please, continue your lists. I make mine for the grocery store, you can make yours for living. what do I care!',0,380,0,0,'64.160.55.127','en'),(15997,2668,'2002-04-24 05:39:28',74,'jo','i\\\'ve always wanted to know how to do the writing that i associate with john irving and william vollmann where the reader is left really disturbed and cold at how graphic and realistic the brutality is.<BR>\r\nthis is an excellent example of what i most envy about that ability: feeling so grossed out at the glimpse inside the mind of someone so fucked up, that you completely forget that it\\\'s the author\\\'s fictional guess at the nonfictional acts of sick assholes.<BR>\r\ni didn\\\'t explain that well; it\\\'s late.',0,522,0,0,'172.139.175.18','en'),(15998,2668,'2002-04-24 05:39:31',124,'gus','art isnt always \\\"pretty\\\". it calls a response. maybe the response is \\\"oh look at that nice horsey\\\" or maybe the response is horror. This was well written, nice details, it implied things without saying them outright. it certainly had some impact, it had an effect, it didnt just sit there, it cant be ignored. You write for an audience, and while you dont want to piss off your readers, you cant fear the audience, you know? I think some people would prefer to think of nice horseys all the time. life isnt all nice horseys though. tuff shit! ',0,555,0,0,'64.160.55.127','en'),(15999,2670,'2002-04-24 12:08:53',155,'blue stocking','what a cool title! and a cool poem.',1,35,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(16000,2668,'2002-04-24 12:21:14',155,'blue stocking to everyone','you guys are amazing. seriously. i was wary of putting this on here, because, well, for many reasons. \r\n<br><br>\r\ni\\\'ve been active on internet writing forums for years, since what, 1996? 1997? and you know how sometimes your personality on the net becomes lopsided? i was always \\\"sweet\\\", that was almost like a prefix to my name. i had this stupid desire to have everyone like me. but of course that is a recipe for disaster. anyway, it was really hard for me to stop writing things that were pretty, or at least, to start sharing the things that were not pretty. \r\n<br><br>\r\nle, you put it so well. the darkness and beauty are inextricably linked. actually, i think the definition of \\\'beautiful\\\' has become limited. we use it to mean something which is nice to look at, but ugly things, grotesque things, true things, can be beautiful. i am going to go look up alan moore.\r\n<br><br>\r\nbtw, i did think twice about submitting this to swinney. i guess it is easier for me, le. i have less at stake. i am newer, here. maybe people have less expectations about/for me than they do for you. when you spend enough time in a forum, people form a picture of you through your words, don\\\'t they? the point is, of all the forums i participated in, i only posted this to one. the one that was the youngest, the one i had participated the least time in. you don\\\'t want to jerk-shock people. well, i don\\\'t/didn\\\'t. look, i\\\'m rambling too.\r\n<br><br>\r\njo, i still haven\\\'t read irving (i know! shame on me!) but william vollman is, is wow. i have \\\'the atlas\\\' and \\\'the rainbow stories\\\' and whenever i need a jolt of the ugly-gorgeous world, ugly-gorgeous mankind, in strangely incredible writing, that\\\'s what i reach for. i love how if you open the books at random, you often come across things written in very different styles, and things that (i) don\\\'t want to read, but can\\\'t stop reading, and can\\\'t stop admiring.\r\n<br><br>\r\ngus! you make me want to dig up my initial response to the backlash on the other forum. here\\\'s on of the responses i made:\r\n<br><br><i>\r\n\\\"there\\\'s no need for political correctness in art. and there will always be disagreements regarding matters of taste.\r\n<br><br>\r\nmy anger at [person who complained]\\\'s response was not because he obviously disliked my piece, which he and anybody else is welcome to do so and to express, but because he made it personal. instead of focusing on the writing, he chose to insult the writer. he coudl have said the piece was sensationalizing something, was crudely written, etc etc.\r\n<br><br>\r\ninstead, he appeared to call the writer a ghoul, and told the writer that he/she should grow up some. \r\n<br><br>\r\nhe should have told me what upset him about the piece.\r\n<br><br>\r\nno topic, properly done, should be off limits in writing.\r\n<br><br>\r\njust because something makes the reader uncomfortable, does not mean the writer should avoid it. art should be uncomfortable. art is sort of liek those canaries in the cages in the mines in the early nineteenth century. society looks to art to understand itself.\r\n<br><br>\r\nthat being said, a writer does have a responsibility to the reader.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni think [person who called me names] is living in a dreamworld if he thinks art should only be about mermaids and sweetness. that is a lopsided art. a weak art. art needs to be strong.\r\n<br><br>\r\n[person, name withheld], try to look at the piece, and see its strengths and weaknesses. hwo does it affect you. if it arouses negative emotions in you, why does this mean it is bad? isn\\\'t the invoking of strong feelings a measure of the power within a piece?\r\n<br><br>\r\njust my rushed blurtings...\r\n<br><br>\r\nperhaps more later, after i;ve thought it through.\\\"</i>\r\n',0,3775,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(16001,2668,'2002-04-24 12:57:56',75,'tyler','well. i dissent. what exactly is the point of this article? all i get from it is \\\"look at this horrible thing.\\\" i wish i hadn\\\'t read it, even if it\\\'s true. it\\\'s not beautiful.\r\n<p>\r\nwhy would somebody be \\\"annoyed\\\" about a tan?\r\n<p>\r\nthe last line...\\\"damn this paki bitch to hell?\\\" i don\\\'t get that either.\r\n<p>\r\ndo rapists lick toes? is this out in a parking lot? wouldn\\\'t they want to get her someplace else? where are the other students?',1,472,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(16002,2668,'2002-04-24 15:59:44',74,'jo','i don\\\'t want to speak for blue stocking as to the point because that\\\'s not for me to say, so i\\\'ll say this:<BR>\r\nit\\\'s easy, when faced with stories of gruesome behavior, particularly when it\\\'s a group of people performing a heinous act, to distance yourself from that act by thinking, \\\"that\\\'s <i>them</i>, they\\\'re evil, they\\\'re faceless, i could never be capable of anything like that.\\\"<BR>\r\nand this, and writing like this, is powerful because the reader can\\\'t do that. i think it\\\'s well-done because it\\\'s shocking and raw but still terse and careful.<BR>\r\n',3,597,75,0,'172.142.194.160','en'),(16003,2668,'2002-04-24 16:04:41',74,'jo','right and so now that i\\\'ve said the nice, thoughtful part:<BR>\r\njesus, tyler, read the fucking piece. \\\"damn this paki bitch\\\" refers to the fact that it was a few days after 9/11, so obviously this hate crime was instigated by these morons\\\' hatred for some unseen dark enemy. if you\\\'re going to criticize something, at least take the effort to carefully read it first.',6,382,75,0,'172.142.194.160','en'),(16004,2669,'2002-04-24 16:09:48',240,'xep','this made me feel guilty about the refined sugar i just put in my tea.',4,77,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(16005,2669,'2002-04-24 16:25:16',86,'egg','o fuk, i wanted to debate you.',0,30,65,0,'209.179.234.249','en'),(16006,2669,'2002-04-24 17:05:30',180,'auntie','since you brought it up here, this is probably a better place to talk about it.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni know all the things i should do, and i know that i have not been doing many of them while you are here.\r\n<br><br>\r\npart of growing up is itemizing the faults of your parents and determining not to be like them.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni don\\\'t WANT you to be like me, i want you to be better than me, smarter, make better choices, be happier, be more loved, and do more of the things you dream about.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni love you very much.  i won\\\'t let dad offer you any more bad food.  wake up and sew, please.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\n',1,604,65,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(16007,2669,'2002-04-24 17:12:28',124,'gus','I grew up on sugar cereal and spagettios and macncheese and bagel bite(tm)pizzas. My mom used to yell at us for not eating the cookies she bought, because she\\\'d end up eating them. She\\\'s diabetic now, just like her mom. I stopped drinking sodas, I cut down on the sugar in my tea, I eat rice instead of wheat whenever possible. I stick with meat and vegetables (the atkins diet doesnt really scare me, its the sugars that will kill my kind one day). People will consume anything and everything. not just food, but also anything NEW AND IMPROVED! I see commercials on tv for NEW AND IMPROVED products, and I know that the next time I go home, my mom will have bought them. She doesnt need them. <p> I feel guilty buying paper towels.',0,738,0,0,'64.160.55.127','en'),(16008,2669,'2002-04-24 17:14:38',180,'auntie','<b>Walmart</b>\r\n<br><br>\r\nthe one true thing about walmart is that it offers the things poor people desire to make them happy at a price they can afford.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni love walmart.  i can buy a cotton nightgown there for $15 that is quite nice and made in china.  the SAME cotton nightgown, equally as nice, and also made in china, is also available to me at the mall for $60.\r\n<br><br>\r\nwhy the disparity in pricing?\r\n<br><br>\r\nwhen local department stores stop building in a 150% markup on clothing, then walmart will fail.\r\n<br><br>\r\nwalmart works on grand volume, everything under one roof.  BUT - there are still plenty of people who prefer to shop elsewhere, enough people to support all manner of small business.\r\n<br><br>\r\nthe problem, the real problem, is that walmart doesn\\\'t pay the same TAXES that small business pays, and they have better funding (stock sales) to cover them when they make a bad product choice.\r\n<br><br>\r\nsmall business doesn\\\'t want to go public, so the funding problem can\\\'t be solved that way.  however, TAXES on small business COULD be solved.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni know whereof i speak.  i retired this year because i could not make a profit, and due to the tax laws, i could not take enough of a loss to keep me from having to pay tax on money i did not make.\r\n<br><br>\r\nwalmart, on the other hand, can take many deductions i am not allowed because i work in my house and do not own a million square acres of business related property to depreciate, etc.\r\n<br><br>\r\nin the meantime, i will continue to buy my cotton nightgowns at walmart.\r\n\r\n',5,1578,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(16009,2669,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i don\\\'t understand paper towels. why use em when you can have nice cotton ones instead? ',1,91,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(16010,2668,'2002-04-24 18:22:49',156,'blue stocking','thanks tyler, for responding. this happened to a pakistani (dark skinned) girl in her campus parking lot, late at night, a few weeks after sept 11, 2001, when anyone and everyone that looked Muslim/Arab/foreign was a target to some people.<br><br>\r\nwhy was the tan important? i won\\\'t spell it out to you. why did one of them lick her toes? who knows? i don\\\'t. where was everyone else? that\\\'s an excellent question? where the hell were they? this is just one incident. ic ould tell you of the muslim owned pizza parlour near my house that was burned down last october. i could tell you of my brother getting a back eye in high school. i could tell you of people, all of a sudden, in the space of a day, refusing to sit next to me on the bus, or anywhere near me, of spitting at me, of throwing rocks at us.<br><br>\r\nthis piece did have a warning. i\\\'m sorry you regret reading it, but reading it, (and reading it to the end) was your choice. and i can\\\'t help but be glad you did, because i wanted everyone to read it. i wanted everyone to stop and think. we\\\'ve heard a lot about the people in the world trade center, people who suffered unimaginable pain and tragedy. i ache for them, and did everything in my power to help.<br><br>\r\nbut you rarely hear now, six months later, about the other side. did you know that muslims were killed in the wtc? did you know that we suffer, as well. and that for us, the consequences were TERRIBLE! we\\\'re the enemy. to ignorant people, we\\\'re the enemy. i, *me* blue stocking, is the enemy.<br><br>\r\ni wanted this piece to make you think. i wanted to make you realise, hey, this is not a back and white issue, hey, we can\\\'t demonise an entire race/religion for the actions of an insane few. <br><br>\r\ni really hope you read my response, tyler. anyway, that\\\'s what i wrote it. to tell the truth.',1,1858,0,0,'206.191.1.88','en'),(16011,2668,'2002-04-24 18:26:01',156,'lotus','thank you very much. the thing is, i don\\\'t think the boys who did it were _evil_ ... i think they were ordinary regular guys in fact. thank you for your thoughtful comments.',1,176,74,0,'206.191.1.88','en'),(16012,2671,'2002-04-24 18:27:19',156,'lotus','this is very witty and clever and imaginative. :)',1,49,0,0,'206.191.1.88','en'),(16013,2668,'2002-04-24 19:12:14',124,'gus','Things like this happen everyday. No one likes to know that ugly things are going on all around them. Ugly things are going on all around you, all the time. \r\n<p>\r\nUnrelated story, same idea: A girl in line for the toilet at a bar saw a guy in a wheelchair, he\\\'d recently been in an accident. she said \\\"God! I wouldnt have the guts to go out if that happened to me!\\\" I said, \\\"You\\\'d get awfully bored at home.\\\" She said, \\\"But think of it! He was walking around last time I saw him!\\\" I looked at her like she was nuts and said, \\\"Dude! He is ALIVE!\\\" She was clearly unable to imagine something bad actually happening to her, causing her to have to face death. She still thinks she\\\'d choose death over a life of hardship. She is fucking stupid, dont you think? Wha she doesnt know: Bad things happen to everyone, even people like her. Bad things can happen to you too. ignore it for as long as you can, one day it\\\'ll bite you in the ass.',0,969,75,0,'64.160.55.127','en'),(16014,2668,'2002-04-24 19:26:43',75,'tyler','i did carefully read it. that\\\'s how i felt after reading it. just because i didn\\\'t agree with all of you...well, what\\\'s the point of discussion then?\r\n<p>\r\ni assume that most of the story, i.e. what is going through the rapists\\\' minds, is fiction. if it\\\'s fiction, it\\\'s art, and i am not criticising the pakistani girl or anything like that. \r\n<p>\r\nthe tan - the boy is annoyed by his own tan? i think that\\\'s a legitimate question.\r\n<p>\r\nhow does raping someone and the damnation of their immortal soul go together?  i think that also is a legitimate question.\r\n<p>\r\ni read it, and it was about a horrible subject that made me feel bad, so i poked my finger in some of the holes that i saw. sorry. i mean if you want to talk about hate crimes or rape, that\\\'s one thing, but why make up all those horrible (and the way they are written, improbable, to my mind) details?\r\n<p>\r\nso that response, along with my hawaiian shirt thing, are both my immediate responses. also gus, i know bad things happen all the time. i know this because sometimes they happen to me and people i love. it\\\'s a given, a fact of life. but it seems to me somehow that making up horrible details of something that, yes, was horrible, just adds to the misery in the world in some way. i\\\'m willing to accept that i could be wrong, but it made me feel so strongly that i had to say something. it wasn\\\'t easy. i knew people wouldn\\\'t like it.',1,1444,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(16015,2669,'2002-04-24 19:52:05',180,'auntie','paper towels are used for sanitary reasons.  cloth towels in the kitchen spread all sorts of nasty germs unless they are laundered between each use.  the electricity, water, etc. necessary to launder cloth towels effectively is probably equal or greater in cost than the cost of the paper towels.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nmy 70 year old mother shattered her knee,had 2 surgeries, 24 weeks of rehabilitation, took hundreds of pills, spent hundreds of hours in doctor\\\'s offices - because she used a cloth towel to clean up a water spill.  the cloth towel was not as absorbent as the paper towelling she should have used, it dripped on the tile floor, she slipped in the drip.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni have many reasons to prefer paper towels, not the least of which is hairballs.',1,756,57,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(16016,2669,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','weeb to auntie: hey, i am not trying to say everyone who uses a paper towel is evil. agree that washing the cloth towels take up resources. <p>but i think the resources i\'ve saved for not using a paper towel everytime i finish doing the dishes prolly outweighs the cost of buying paper towels. and disagree that towels don\'t soak up as much as paper towels. that\'s just silly! if they were that absorbant, we would be drying ourselves after showering with bounty. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni wash them when i do laundry, and i don\'t really have to worry about hygiene so much because i don\'t cook meat. they just dry my hands, dry dishes and the sponge wipes up food spills. i don\'t use them to mop the floor, i have a mop for that. i don\'t think towels can be blamed for your 70 year old mother falling on the floor when she\'s elderly. possibly that happened because... she\'s old? \r\n</p><p>\r\ni\'m just extolling the virtues of my fluffy towels. i know it\'s trivial at best. <i>best friendly</i></p>',1,594,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(16017,2668,'2002-04-24 20:09:13',155,'blue stocking','well, i\\\'m glad you did. you\\\'re right, i didn\\\'t know what was going through the minds of the boys who did this. i only know what they did (and to some degree, what they said) <shrug> one of them (with the tan) was angry that his skin colour looked similar to hers. when you commit a hate crime, i\\\'m guessing that you forget how similar all of us are...we\\\'re all human beings. it is easier to hate someone if you don\\\'t think of them as being like you. anyway, tyler, good for you for having the courage to write your comments, even if they are different from everyone else\\\'s opinion! i guess i\\\'ve been silenced all my life, and told to shut up about what happened to me, and to people i knew, and now i want to be heard. :) *best friendly*',1,761,75,0,'24.156.95.178','en'),(16018,2668,'2002-04-24 20:11:26',155,'blue stocking','p.s. i always try to add to the good to the world. i\\\'m sorry if you perceived this piece as adding misery to the world.',0,122,0,0,'24.156.95.178','en'),(16019,2668,'2002-04-24 20:14:21',155,'blue stocking','also, re: rape and hell. virginity is dangerously prized in eastern/muslim cultures. often when a girl is raped, people refuse to acknowledge that it isn\\\'t her fault (in my comunity anyway) and her reputation/life/honour/dignity  is ruined, and they believe the life hereafter is ruined for her too. i had trouble with the last line. i don\\\'t know. basically i wanted to invoke the thoughts above, and also, that when you hate someone, you do just want them to go to hell, you want them to just...blargh.',0,509,75,0,'24.156.95.178','en'),(16020,2669,'2002-04-24 20:39:48',129,'moebius','okay. i enjoyed this essay. there was a certain amount of truth in it. it reminded me very much of the sorts of things i wrote, oh, about ten years ago.\r\n<br><br>\r\nbut: guilt trips! they\\\'re everywhere! i\\\'ve had them handed to me all my life. and over time i\\\'ve decided that <i>there is nothing you can do</i> that probably won\\\'t have a negative effect somewhere, somehow. \r\n<br><br>\r\nand to make things worse, guilt follows trends. what\\\'s ok now wasn\\\'t ok a few years ago, and vice versa. it\\\'s hell to keep up with what you\\\'re not supposed to be doing and why.\r\n<br><br>\r\nconsider food. back in the 80s and early 90s you weren\\\'t supposed to eat grapes or other fruits picked in California. why? to show solidarity with the farmworker\\\'s union, who were striking for better wages. if you ate grapes, you were screwing the workers in favor of the farm bosses. bad.\r\n<br><br>\r\nand you weren\\\'t supposed to drink coke, or consume most fast food products, and a variety of other things. and why? not for dietary reasons. nope. it was because, ha, the companies that marketed such things held investments in apartheid South Africa. to consume such things meant you supported the apartheid system. and when you looked at the list of banned products it often seemed like there was nothing left to buy.\r\n<br><br>\r\nnowadays there are people who will make you feel guilty for the most elemental things. eating any sort of sugar at all. consuming cooked food. driving cars. watching tv. listening to pop music. wearing Gap t-shirts. only speaking one language. having vanilla sex. having kids. carrying on with traditional hetrosexual relationships. \r\n<br><br>\r\nthen, of course there\\\'s religious guilt. cultural guilt. and, overriding everything else, you have family guilt: the most basic source of guilt of all.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni say somewhere along the line you need to draw a line with the guilt. you can\\\'t go through life yoked to all the problems of the world. it\\\'s impossible to be a saint and it\\\'s impossible to expect others to be saints. all we can do is try to be good to each other and ourselves.\r\n<br><br>\r\nle\\\'s request that we think about what we are doing is sound. there\\\'s nothing wrong with being self-aware about the endless chain of cause and effect in which you take part. but at some point, at the end of the day, you have to let the guilt go. or it will drive you mad.',4,2422,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(16021,2668,'2002-04-24 20:40:47',75,'tyler','right on. i don\\\'t know about the rest of you, but i sort of feel that maybe hate crimes aren\\\'t really about whatever issue they seem to be about...like do you think that they raped her because they hated middle eastern people, or that that was just the excuse they made to themselves for doing it? like for example black lynchings in the south 65 years ago, did people do that because they hated blacks or because it gave them an excuse to act like savages? ',1,464,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(16022,2668,'2002-04-24 21:17:35',74,'jo','i think that some of the people involved hated blacks, some were just violent dicks, some were just weak-willed people incapable of stopping themselves. and, you know, maybe some were just <b>bored</b>.<BR>\r\nthat\\\'s exactly what i was trying to say before when i was talking about wanting to group people who commit violent acts into one slot, one mindset, \\\"they\\\" instead of \\\"him\\\" and \\\"him\\\" and \\\"him\\\" and \\\"her\\\" and \\\"him.\\\" it\\\'s upsetting how <b>banal</b> the reasons or impetus for heinous acts can be.<BR>\r\nthat\\\'s why, to me, writing like this is important and powerful.',0,614,75,0,'172.160.51.185','en'),(16023,2669,'2002-04-24 21:54:57',277,'slothrop','i don\\\'t know if walmart is evil, per se. it does bring jobs, reliable services, low prices, and centralized shopping to many communities that other merchants wouldn\\\'t touch with a ten-foot pole. the real problem is that people demand that its shelves get stocked with shit. Indeed, if people did think more about their choices, about what they consumed, we might effect some real change. Only 250 people to convince in this country!',3,438,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(16024,2669,'2002-04-24 22:15:41',277,'slothrop','250 MILLION people*',3,19,277,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(16025,2672,'2002-04-24 22:55:01',86,'egg','there were bedouins in the Scorpion King?',0,41,0,0,'216.244.18.23','en'),(16026,2672,'2002-04-24 23:15:16',82,'simon','the rock!',0,9,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16027,2669,'2002-04-24 23:45:23',168,'laney','<p>le, i like this post a lot, too. i just had my first experience with a certain psychadelic substance, and, while altered, was immediately struck by the sheer mass of consumption. not that i wasnâ€™t aware of it before, but it just became so dramatic and obvious. and youâ€™re right about the lack of thought that goes into it. random college girl goes out wearing her all-new wet seal outfit plus accessories, buys loads of overpriced drinks then stumbles around yelling about how she needs <i>food</i> like a hamburger and french fries. itâ€™s nothing to her--just the way she spends every saturday night. bleah.</p> \r\n    \r\n<p>on the walmart thing, da and i have talked a lot about this â€˜â€˜red americaâ€™â€™ phenomenon. as auntie says, walmart is there so poor people can buy things they can afford and feel happy. but that is PRECISELY THE PROBLEM. putting all this crap at their fingertips for \\\"the right price\\\" has created this sort of false sense of security. so when you ask these poor people--who are getting shafted by the inequities of the economy--about who the â€˜â€˜have-notsâ€™â€™ of society are, they donâ€™t consider themselves among them. this, my friends, is why dubya is our president right now.</p> ',0,1207,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(16028,2669,'2002-04-24 23:56:21',65,'le','yeah, it\\\'s the need to buy stuff to be happy that is the problem.',3,68,168,0,'168.191.184.197','en'),(16029,2669,'2002-04-25 00:22:25',186,'ook','<p>besides the default consumption and the production of things with fake value, this post makes me think about the question of being a mindful person and whether there is an residual impact when that person leaves the room.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>this is <A HREF=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0940795183/qid=1019693828/sr=1-1/ref=sr_1_1/102-1984023-4066555\\\">a book</A> about being mindful in ways that help other people to be mindful. it is intended for psychotherapists and healing professionals but the same practices (called the Hakomi method) get taught to laypeople too.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>as a person who tends to get way more into the \\\"be the change you want to see in the world\\\" end of things than the social activism scene, i find this really interesting and even, ahem, inspiring. as incredibly new agey and flaky as it sounds, it gives concrete ideas and ways to allow other people to find their own transformations. basically it is about being present, compassionate, and a good communicator.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i don\\\'t know. pretty much everything makes me think of hakomi right now because my special boy\\\'s mum is a hakomi counsellor, and she is one of the most impressively loving, aware, and healthy people that i know. she is going to die of cancer soon, so we have been spending a lot of time with people who all know her, and it is striking how everybody is so conscious.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>also, being with a wonderful person who has always done pretty much every healthy thing and loved it, and who is dying of cancer at 43 really emphasizes the way that being mindful is about the only thing that you get to keep out of your life. your health and your plans and your possessions can all fall apart, and your work in the world can go nowhere, but if you really live in your life, that\\\'s that. you\\\'ve lived.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>this is way more schmoopy and kumbaya than usually gets through my filter. please understand that i\\\'m in a pretty overwhelmed state :)</p>',3,1974,0,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(16030,2669,'2002-04-25 01:35:41',65,'le','my yoga teacher said something recently about mindfulness, it comes from buddhism i think. she was saying that all things are one thing, and because of that, if you make yourself good, you\\\'re making everything good.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni think it\\\'s an awesome sentiment but it\\\'s the sorta thing it\\\'s hard to see the effects of in a practical way. \r\n',2,352,186,0,'168.191.184.129','en'),(16031,2669,'2002-04-25 01:36:13',65,'le','i wonder if mercury is in scorpio.',0,34,0,0,'168.191.184.129','en'),(16032,2670,'2002-04-25 05:32:13',75,'tyler','thank you',1,9,0,0,'165.247.164.35','en'),(16033,2669,'2002-04-25 05:45:12',190,'the aforementioned yoga teacher yoga teacher, aka dahlia','<p>it isn\\\'t exactly a buddhist thought, though it might as well be. the basic tenets of buddhism and yoga are almost identical. that particular thought is one which was taught to me (the idea of doing one\\\'s part to better the universe by bettering the portion one has control over, oneself), viewed through the prism of  my conception of divinity: it\\\'s all one big thing, being is, and being an optimist these days i prefer to see that as divine.</P>\r\n\r\n<p> in my world divinity doesn\\\'t *do* anything. it just *is* everything. which of course means it does everything. and nothing. everything just happens.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>a few minutes ago in the car jim and i were admiring a beautiful red radiating across a beige wall. it was the glow from an office depot sign reflecting against the wall of the mall. it was breathtaking. jim said \\\"i think maybe now that everyone is taking pictures of how ugly consumerism is, the thing to do is take pictures of it being beautiful.\\\"</P>\r\n\r\n<p> as your yoga teacher, sweetheart, i would suggest that you look beneath the surface of your anger at \\\"the rest\\\". underneath anger is almost always sadness; anger is a defensive reaction to perceived injury. if you look hard at sadness that seems to be caused by external forces, you can often find a place to feel compassion. now we get to the buddhist thing. compassion. lovingkindness. that is a more inhabitable place than anger.</p>',1,1438,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(16034,2669,'2002-04-25 07:55:09',65,'le','hm, yes.\r\n<br><br>\r\nthe problem is just that it\\\'s not \\\"the rest,\\\" which is exactly why i\\\'m bothered. because i\\\'m involved somehow.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni should look at my anger in general, it\\\'s so foreign to me. i\\\'d like to own it and understand it. \r\n<br><br>\r\nthanks for appearing, dahlia. <b><tt>:) :)</tt></b>',1,325,190,0,'168.191.184.142','en'),(16035,2667,'2002-04-25 13:12:46',122,'hool','<p>i don\\\'t make lists to remember things.  usually, i want to most remember an idea i\\\'ve had when lying in bed before sleep comes.  i usually don\\\'t want to keep pulling out a notebook to write things down - if i did, i\\\'d never get to sleep.  so usually what i do is find something in reach of the bed - a cellphone, a pipe, a lighter, and then place it in an odd position/posture on my floor.  somewhere where i\\\'ll notice it in the morning, and remember that there\\\'s something i have to remember.  i\\\'m always able to remember from the night before what the idea was.  i remember things by setting triggers.</p>',0,631,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(16036,2667,'2002-04-25 13:18:18',66,'Peter','<br><tt>oh my god, i totally do that exact thing, too. although, if im especially sleepy, i can only remember that i needed to remember something. heh.</tt>',1,156,122,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16037,2669,'2002-04-25 15:17:49',190,'dahlia','yes, of course you are involved. the other interpretation of events, the one i almost mentioned but did not for fear of growing too verbose (who, me?), is the yogic view. multiple possibilities:\r\n\r\n<p>- if everything is part of the great divine whole, then crap at walmart is god. and cool ranch doritos are god. and your neato mom is god, and the lady smacking her kid in the checkout line at the supermarket is god. when i have a really hard time with someone, i find it useful to remind myself of this, and ask me what i have to learn from that hunk of the big throbbing being.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>- the self. ah, the self. in some ways, in reacting to anything, we are reacting to ourselves. to our own implication in the situation at hand, to what the thing shows us about ourselves that we do not want to see... and so forth.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>- if you love yourself good and hard, and you can\\\'t help but love everyone else. when we dislike others, there is some self-hatred in that. linked to the above.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and of course i showed up, dear. if you aren\\\'t going to journal, i have to look for you someplace! :) you are a very good teacher; i need the le food for my brain.</p>',0,1176,65,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(16038,2671,'2002-04-25 17:08:12',82,'simon','but where\\\'s photo?',0,21,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16039,2670,'2002-04-25 19:06:26',180,'auntie','i am there with you in my mind\\\'s eye. lovely!',1,48,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(16040,2668,'2002-04-25 19:58:34',278,'egenus','<p>It takes courage to face the darker side of humanity with honesty. No one wants to identify with such horror, so they overreact to distance themselves from appearing \\\"dark\\\". Anyone who can admit they have such potential in them will be better equipped to keep pandora\\\'s box closed.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>This was a disturbing story, but that would of course be the point. Kudos for being honest.</p>',2,399,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16041,2667,'2002-04-25 20:44:18',187,'kandrer','to Hool and Peter:<br>\r\ndamn! i used to do the same thing when i was a kid (except i usually threw a sock on the floor somewhere in the room). it\\\'s not that it\\\'s a so great idea, it\\\'s just fun to discover someone else doing it... nowadays, i tend to write things down instead; either my memory is falling apart, or i have \\\"weirder\\\" thoughts to remember. it happened many times that i look at some of these notes saying \\\"write about xxx\\\" and i can\\\'t figure out what the heck i wanted to write about it.</p><p>\r\nbtw, did some of you here played with Lego(R) ?\r\n</p>',1,587,0,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(16042,2667,'2002-04-25 21:07:48',187,'kandrer','yeah, <i>live your life</i> sounds great. but how would you answer to this question:<br>\r\nshould i:<br>\r\n- enjoy what i have, and not expect for better things (this is the path to stagnation and happiness) ?<br>\r\n- try to improve what i have, expect for better things (this is the path to improvement and frustration) ?<br>\r\ni guess the answer must be a mix between the two. how to determine that mix, then?<br>\r\nthanks',1,419,124,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(16043,2668,'2002-04-25 21:12:25',187,'kandrer','i just saw <i>Boys don\\\'t cry</i> this week-end, and i was not feeling like reading anything about rape and killing for a while... to late, but yes, i still loved your post. btw, thanks Swinney and everyone in so that the place to publish it exists :).',1,254,0,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(16044,2678,'2002-04-25 21:18:31',187,'kandrer','(the first part was written as a not-so-much-appropriate comment to one of  s5\\\'s posts)',1,90,0,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(16045,2667,'2002-04-25 21:47:51',66,'Peter','<br><tt>dude. i was the freakin <i>lego king</i> of my hometown! hahaha. cool.<tt>',1,82,187,0,'24.90.94.124','en'),(16046,2677,'2002-04-25 21:57:10',180,'auntie','oh my, i am truly touched.',1,26,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(16047,2667,'2002-04-26 02:31:58',180,'auntie','i no longer wait for things to happen. having a disease with a timeline til death causes you to either check off the things that you promised yourself you would do before you die, or don\\\'t make a list, just do stuff as it comes to you.  i chose the latter method.  some days i am content to sit about reading trashy romance novels and play the sims, other days i am madly creative with needle, paint, and words, and still other times i am suddenly panicked by liver function tests and i make trip plans to go places and by airplane tickets and pack and go and then i come home and begin again.',0,596,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(16048,2667,'2002-04-26 03:58:08',124,'gus','hm. I dont know, expectations can lead to disapointment. I dont like disapointment... so... often times I have very low expectations. I dont make lists, I dont make plans for the very distant future... and yet, I still manage to get to do amazing things. I let them take me by surprise. I guess it just depends on the person? ',0,326,187,0,'64.160.52.253','en'),(16049,2667,'2002-04-26 04:13:47',187,'kandrer','yeah, i use to let things come to me, in a way. but then, sometimes, i suddenly stop and ask myself: <i>damn, is it what i really wanted to do?!</i> the old doubt about plans that you never made, so they were never realized.<br>\r\nhey, Chuckspeed, what do you say about that?',1,274,124,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(16050,2667,'2002-04-26 04:16:24',187,'kandrer','i <i>knew</i> it! [i am sure this is more important than one think: i mean the fact that one did or did not play with Lego (R)]',1,127,66,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(16051,2679,'2002-04-26 07:52:32',65,'le','did i reply to that? i can\\\'t find a reply in my sent mail folders.',0,69,0,0,'168.191.184.169','en'),(16052,2679,'2002-04-26 09:36:28',278,'egenus','I didn\\\'t get a reply, so I would assume not. First e-mail I think you sent me was the other day.',1,99,65,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16053,2680,'2002-04-26 10:50:01',282,'paul','Interesting thought that - </p>\\\"Big things with Big people\\\".</p>\r\n\r\n</p>Making the big Splash seems to be the ultimate goal of many folks, and if not the goal then the measure of one\\\'s life.  The bigger the Splash the better the life. </p> \r\n\r\n</p>The Splash may be acquiring and wielding all the money, prestige, influence, power you can; or just commanding the attention and respect (or disrespect) of others.  The acquiring, wielding, commanding can be fun, and challenging, maybe even rewarding to a degree.  But we become preoccupied, even obsessed, with the game just like any other game with competition, rules, winners, losers, and quitters. </p> \r\n\r\n</p>To win the game we need to ensure that, to the greatest degree possible, we maximize the \\\"profitabilty\\\" of each of our interpersonal transactions, where \\\"profitabilty\\\" is a measure of how much that particular interaction/transaction increases our personal power/influence. And we equate the quality of our life and evaluate the the lives of others with success in the \\\"Splash game\\\".</p>\r\n\r\n</p>But Paul G.\\\'s story and phrase \\\"big things with big people\\\" reminds me that it\\\'s the \\\"little things we do with little people\\\" that really are a reflection of the quality of our life.  It\\\'s the way we are with people who cannot provide us with \\\"profitable transactions\\\".  Our kids, the weak, the needy.  How far out of our way do we go to help those who need it just because we Can help?  How much are we willing to help shoulder the burdens of others - and to help others grow when we see an opportunity to help. How much are we willing to really sacrifice for others?</p>\r\n</p>\r\nIn my mind, searching for the \\\"Big Splash\\\" doing big things with big people is a fool\\\'s errand that distracts, demoralizes, and often ultimately destroys people. The real measure of character is integrity, and the yardstick for the qualty of life is service - \\\"doing little things for little people\\\".  \r\n\r\n',2,2012,0,0,'4.46.170.69','en'),(16054,2681,'2002-04-26 11:32:50',66,'Peter','<br><tt>Modern science seems a bit confused about the role of gravity in nature, apparent by the fact that they\\\'ve been searching for a <i>particle</i> that \\\'carries\\\' gravity.  Particles or mass, or whatever you want to call them, can\\\'t effectively represent gravity. Yes, particles represent mass, and some might say even <i>space</i> and area.  I guess \\\'mass\\\' represents an identity for all things that exist whereas gravity represents the relationship between all of those masses that exist in our universe.  \r\n<br><br>\r\nBut to define the relationship of any two masses, gravity must somehow be shown to work between the masses.  Therefore, gravity is usually percieved as a series of bonds and connections that make up all real things; all bonds between all masses are graviational in nature. Every bond between every mass must be a connection of gravity; they call this a <i>gravion</i>.  \r\n<br><br>\r\nOnce you understands the role of gravity within/between units of mass, you can see that it rather sublimely represents the infinite connections that exist between every mass in our world. Throw in the law of <i>conservation of mass</i>, and you have a pretty good not-too-abstract model of string theory.<br><br>wow, i have really spun off on a tangent.</tt>\r\n\r\n',1,1286,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16055,2679,'2002-04-26 11:42:46',66,'Peter','<br><tt>hmm, <i>incoherent and patently bizarre emails to random aquaintences</i>; interesting. <br><br>for every one person that sends a genuinely communicative- albeit random or unsolicited- email like this (ive done it, so i cant be too judgmental here, heh), there\\\'s always a few that crop up out of no where and are all like <i>whassup, dude! so, you know, i was like reading your website (or lj, or digging through your photos, or... whatever), and i just wanted to say...</i> whereafter they proceed to tell you some random (and sometimes creepy) detail about their life, as if they\\\'ve known you for years. or even better, they criticize something about a relationship or breakup you wrote about a year ago, they make fun of your hometown, or they seize on some random detail and go off on some bizarre tangent about that, all the while forgetting you have no idea in the world who they are.<br><br>this is even better when they have some personal demon to expel, or some crazy thought-processes driving things. its interesting. ahh, the net...</tt>',1,1062,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16056,2678,'2002-04-26 11:46:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt>thats when you play by the <i>5 second rule</i>. as long as its been on the floor for fewer than 5 seconds, its safe to eat it, assuming you blow on it first ;)<Br><br>i once saw a guy drop a slice of pizza, face down, into the gutter of broadway; it was rainy and hepatitis-laden puddles were accumulating. he was inebriated. he picked the slice right up and continued to eat it.</tt>',1,393,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16057,2677,'2002-04-26 11:47:08',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this was just absolutely great...</tt>',1,46,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16058,2677,'2002-04-26 14:05:41',64,'Joanna','Our fridge is old and it leaks a lot and often I drool over sleek shiny stainless steel models in catelogues, but after reading this, I might look at it in a whole new light.  We\\\'ve had it for three years, purchased second (or third) hand for $500 and I\\\'ve worked my way up to owning 3/4 of it, so I guess it serves us well.  If nothing else, it gives me a place to put up my dirty word magnetic poetry from Good Vibrations and izone polaroid stickers.  Also, today I got a mark I was really proud of in school, and I was very tempted to put my paper up on the fridge, but I thought my flatmates might belittle me for that.  ',1,631,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(16059,2680,'2002-04-26 14:33:30',123,'dcc','i am glad that my parents never drove the idea that \\\"Big things with Big People\\\" was important. that being happy with who you are and what you do is. for some people money and material possessions is happiness. but for me, if i am not satisfied with everything else, what truely is the point in having that brand spanking new 50\\\" television?',0,350,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(16060,2667,'2002-04-26 14:55:20',265,'chuckspeed','<p>dichotomy.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>on one hand, it\\\'s important to keep your eyes on the horizon.  when making the transition from good skier to freaking gonzo skier, i found the further i looked ahead, the harder terrain i was able to tackle...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>life\\\'s not unlike that.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>on the other hand, it\\\'s vital to accept what comes.  the only thing we really have control over is how we respond to our \\\'world\\\' - i can\\\'t do anything about taxes, so there\\\'s no point in belaboring the point.  they get paid, and that\\\'s that.  if they really bother me, i could move to nevada and avoid the 4% income tax MI levies.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the trick, i believe, is to set a course and then accept the consequences of setting the course.  if you don\\\'t like the consequences - change the course.  don\\\'t get trapped in a life \\\'script\\\', and don\\\'t spend all your time in the \\\'now\\\'.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>on legos...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>sears sold this monster killer set back when i was a kid. saved up forever to get it - stored it in a tub the size of a kitchen wastebasket.  was one of life\\\'s great pleasures, and it was rewarding to be able to give the set to my daughters.  there\\\'s no waiting with legos -- you\\\'re in the now when you\\\'re sitting in the middle of the pile.  envision it, and make it real.</p>\r\n',0,1323,187,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(16061,2669,'2002-04-26 15:58:01',262,'szajic','\\\"Dr. Robert Atkins, who promotes a high-fat, low-carbohydrate diet, said he is recovering from cardiac arrest caused by a heart infection. The 71-year-old doctor said Thursday his illness \\\'is in no way related to diet.\\\'\\\"',2,232,74,0,'18.56.0.58','en'),(16062,2681,'2002-04-26 17:21:46',266,'spleenless','I heard a thing on the radio yesterday.  Based on the fact that some galaxies aren\\\'t where scientists think they ought to be, some fella who\\\'s name I missed believes that there is an \\\"anti-gravity\\\".  It\\\'s referred to as a dark force.  Spooky.',1,257,0,0,'66.92.184.86','en'),(16063,2681,'2002-04-26 18:53:35',174,'Mad Moll','I was born to conquer outer space.',6,34,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(16064,2676,'2002-04-26 19:08:17',83,'adamajah','<p>You gotta love Japan when PSOnes come standard in hotel rooms.  haha, those freaks.</p>',2,90,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(16065,2681,'2002-04-26 19:09:40',66,'Peter','<br><tt>you\\\'d be empress of a vast, endless and empty space full of stars!</tt>',1,82,174,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16066,2676,'2002-04-26 19:32:10',65,'le','<a href=\\\"http://links.net/vita/trip/japan/\\\"><img src=\\\"http://links.net/vita/trip/japan/lodging/lovehotel/panoreko/pix/justinbowl-sm.jpg\\\" width=\\\"200\\\" height=\\\"150\\\" border=\\\"1\\\"></a>',4,207,0,0,'63.178.200.171','en'),(16067,2681,'2002-04-26 19:34:47',65,'le','mad moll, beautiful space leader. long tresses floating in 0 gravity. pert breasts floating as well. yum.',4,105,0,0,'63.178.200.171','en'),(16068,2678,'2002-04-26 20:05:53',265,'chuckspeed','pocky.  i miss pocky.  taiwan beer too.',0,39,0,0,'208.5.114.92','en'),(16069,2679,'2002-04-26 20:29:46',195,'matt','is \\\"egenus\\\" pronounced anything like \\\"agnes\\\"?',1,57,0,0,'209.208.77.129','en'),(16070,2679,'2002-04-26 21:16:29',239,'Poonlarp','maybe if youre like irish or something',1,38,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16071,2682,'2002-04-26 21:19:33',168,'laney','i think the inability to discover--- to stumble upon once-hidden courtyards, gardens, exquisitely tiled fountains---is the exact reason i hate suburbs so much. you know exactly where the starbucks is, exactly where the vons is, and somehow, finding a new arco around the corner just doesn\\\'t do the trick. it just goes against all that is good. why, sprawl? why?!\r\n',0,367,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(16072,2682,'2002-04-26 21:27:45',66,'Peter','<br><tt>thats at least one thing thats nice about living in manhattan; its <i>finite</i>. its actually possible to traverse the entirity of the city\\\'s neighborhoods in a couple of years of adventuring, as it never grows outward. only upward...</tt>',1,251,168,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16073,2678,'2002-04-26 21:31:02',187,'kandrer','nice pizza story, yum, thanks Peter :].</p>\r\nnb: there is no such thing as the 5 seconds rule in france. it\\\'s the second time i hear about it. except last time it was 3 seconds... H doesn\\\'t believe that rule either; actually i am wondering what she will say if she knows i ate something <i>from the ground</i>. maybe she won\\\'t kiss me for a week? ;)',1,358,66,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(16074,2678,'2002-04-26 21:33:32',66,'Peter','<br><tt>on a somewhat relevant tangent, i <i>have</i> to post these:<br><br><img src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/images/koronets1.jpg\\\"><br><img src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/images/koronets3.jpg\\\"><br><br>story <a href=\\\"http://www.livejournal.com/talkread.bml?journal=rhodamine&itemid=39757\\\">here</a>.</tt>',1,321,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16075,2678,'2002-04-26 21:34:14',187,'kandrer','but you may find pocky here! we buy them in san mateo (lots of japanese shops) but i guess sf\\\'s \\\"japasen\\\" (japan town) must have them too. oh, but i forgot were you live... not in the bay area, right?',1,209,265,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(16076,2678,'2002-04-26 21:36:22',66,'Peter','<br><tt>any chinatown worth its salted squid ought to have every variety of pocky available... even the strawberry ones. mmm, pocky.</tt>',1,137,265,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16077,2676,'2002-04-26 22:24:25',83,'adamajah','yes, i want to go to japan.  i remember a high school chum telling me how there are sushi stands all over the place and you just eat sushi like fast food.  teka maki!  ',0,168,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(16078,2684,'2002-04-26 23:24:16',122,'hool','<p> as a sheer survival tactic in the spiritual vacuum that is the office environment, i constantly work toward perfecting doing nothing, and not minding doing nothing.  i sit, and stare at nothing with my eyes open, the nothing between me and the monitor.  i can do this for hours, though, i do need tiny breaks in between shift positions.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i remove or mask out all time measuring devices.  the lcd display on any nearby phones gets covered immediately with a post-it note on the day that i start the job and does not get removed until i leave the job for good.  if i\\\'m wearing a watch, it goes into my pocket every morning upon my arrival.  i opt off the system tray clock, remove timestamp formatting from any command line interface i may be using.  if i happen to be listening to a radio station on my walkman, i turn the volume off when i suspect that the dj is going to announce the time.  i avert my eyes from any other incidental time displays that may be about and attempting to ruin my goal of temporal agnosticism. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> i often think it quite amusing that i may in fact achieve enlightenment accidentally through my attempts to lose time at work by staring into nothingness for as long as possible. it makes me think of the path to achieving flight in the hitchhiker\\\'s guide to the galaxy: throwing yourself at the ground, and missing.</p>\r\n\r\n',0,1376,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(16079,2630,'2002-04-27 00:20:42',168,'laney','mmmm collecting scarves. i just bought a very happy, fuzzy one in multiple shades of green. ',0,92,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(16080,2680,'2002-04-27 00:33:15',176,'Scapegoat','I can remember the first time I was deemed not good enough or of the right caliber by a girlfriends parents. It was due to ethnic/religious background though instead of wealth/splash.<br><br>\r\nI\\\'d say that it was more unpleasant watching my girlfriend at the time squirm trying to figure out what to do then having a negative judgement passed on myself.  ',0,358,0,0,'64.229.163.177','en'),(16081,2681,'2002-04-27 00:41:53',176,'Scapegoat','<b>To The Stars!</b><br><br>\r\nLet me off this rock.<br>\r\n\r\n\r\n',0,61,0,0,'64.229.163.177','en'),(16082,2679,'2002-04-27 00:52:02',176,'Scapegoat','I wish more people were pushing the boundaries of their self image. Sometimes I am left feeling like the majority of people do not do this.',0,139,0,0,'64.229.163.177','en'),(16083,2678,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','or tomato flavor pocky, or green tea pocky...!<br>\r\n(^_^)',1,54,0,0,'63.165.31.7','en'),(16084,463,'2002-04-27 03:06:01',283,'mojo','Disco Fries sound suspiciously close to Poutine, a Quebecois delight that has made its way to the fast food restaurants of western Canada.  You can get it in Kentucky Fried Chicken in Vancouver!  \r\nThe original is made with cheese curd, but it\\\'s the same disgusting and delicious thing as Poutine.  Go Gravy!',1,311,0,0,'24.69.255.205','en'),(16085,463,'2002-04-27 03:38:05',74,'jo','okay i could see this if  were a cream-based gravy, because i have been known to dip my pizza in ranch dressing, and really that\\\'s not that far off, but brown gravy? that\\\'s fucking disgusting.',2,198,0,0,'172.172.186.160','en'),(16086,463,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i hate gravy, but this is a fine post.',6,38,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16087,2679,'2002-04-27 04:50:51',278,'egenus','think \\\"e-genus\\\"',3,21,195,0,'65.142.151.163','en'),(16088,2679,'2002-04-27 05:04:01',59,'s5','ever dabble in taxonomy?',6,24,278,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16089,2686,'2002-04-27 05:33:06',59,'s5','so, what is the current lie?',4,28,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16090,2676,'2002-04-27 06:18:16',64,'Joanna','If you have more time in Fukuoka, you should go to the Ocean Dome - a massively hugely ginormas indoor beach.  It\\\'s worth all those adjectives when you see it - that place is absolutely unreal.  ',0,198,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(16091,2682,'2002-04-27 06:20:20',174,'Mad Moll','The \\\'burbs are full of stuff. In my state there\\\'s an entire magazine devoted to the strange things people find in the sprawl.',6,131,168,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(16092,2679,'2002-04-27 06:26:18',278,'egenus','heh. no. The really sad thing is that I was forced to go to dictionary.com over that word. /me shakes his head',2,110,59,0,'65.138.43.101','en'),(16093,2679,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','don\\\'t worry, i\\\'m dumb too, and i have to live with him.',6,61,278,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16094,2684,'2002-04-27 08:34:00',187,'kandrer','beautiful! when i read your comment, i wish to be more bored. i realize my ridiculous half-bored condition. i am a worm. please lead me on the way to enborement.',1,161,122,0,'205.186.126.12','en'),(16095,2677,'2002-04-27 08:58:56',187,'kandrer','<u>to chuckspeed:</u><br>\r\nhey, keep up with poetry, common, give the needy family your new fridge and keep the pink one!<br>\r\nsorry, i have to learn how to be sarcastic in english too. was it ok? :}',1,199,0,0,'205.186.126.12','en'),(16096,2680,'2002-04-27 09:41:44',187,'kandrer','<u>to Paul G:</u> thank you for sharing. this is not a \\\"good story\\\"; it\\\'s a good piece of life. thanks.',1,112,0,0,'205.186.126.12','en'),(16097,2679,'2002-04-27 09:51:07',187,'kandrer','the majority of people are strugling to believe <i>themselves</i> in a positive image of their personality. that alone keeps them quite busy... ;) (i know u know)',1,162,176,0,'205.186.126.12','en'),(16098,2686,'2002-04-27 10:03:51',187,'kandrer','as i already said: <i>lie if you want, but lie as a pro</i>.<br>\r\nand yes, for sure, truth can be worse than lies in many cases. practically, you first need to get rid of the guilty feeling.',1,190,0,0,'205.186.126.12','en'),(16099,2686,'2002-04-27 10:31:42',187,'kandrer','ah! in the case you need an emergency advice for snowballs, here it is:<br>\r\n<i>you don\\\'t know</i><br>\r\nthat\\\'s the main trick for lies. if you don\\\'t want to tell the truth, or you did not decided to tell it yet, don\\\'t say <i>anything</i>. even stay silent is better than going where you don\\\'t wanna go. (wow, should i start a \\\"how to cheat for dummies\\\" ?)',1,376,0,0,'205.186.126.12','en'),(16100,2686,'2002-04-27 11:24:24',196,'jasonp','Telling lies is like writing with one of those erasable ballpoint pens that aren\\\'t really erasable. Each day is roll call. Recite the lines, trace the pattern, try and rub it out but you can\\\'t. Then the paper starts to rub away. You only get one test paper.<BR><BR>\nMaybe it\\\'s like driving out to the desert on a cold moonless night, driving up the gravel driveway, the lonely neon whorehouse. It could be the emptiness, or the grime, or just the stuffy cigarette air, fritos with hair in the bowl, cheez dip salsa.<BR>\nWhy does the frightened little chihuaha have to shiver and have that funny smell? It has knitted booties. Do you laugh? Do you feel pity? It has a small silver name tag on it. You look at it and see that it reads, \\\"fucking liar\\\". You realize the joke\\\'s on you and begin to sweat. It\\\'s sneering at you in the corner, growling. It is a frightening angry mouse dog. Do you make it scuttle away? Or just run out gasping sharp air? You\\\'re game is up either way.<BR><BR>\nPerhaps it\\\'s the tired desperate exhale, the aimless wandering, the dirty yearning faces. <BR>\nAnd then you exchange your lies, but there\\\'s really no point and it\\\'s to be expected anyway. Emptiness is the currency.<BR>\nIt\\\'s the damp rash driving home in your lap, longing for something that\\\'s gone. That\\\'s when the cold metalic lies clang around in your head like pots and pans put away in the bottom drawer. You haven\\\'t washed them. They\\\'re covered -- dried up food particles, grease, dog hair. Can you blindly hope it will not be noticed? Can you vainly imagine it doesn\\\'t matter? That it will just go away? What can you do?<BR><BR>\nA lie is a knife in the back. The truth is a razor at the throat.<BR>\n<BR>\nIt\\\'s like the earnest rodent on a fence nibbling on it\\\'s nuts. I keep telling the weasel to tell the truth, but it doesn\\\'t listen. It repeats its lies with rote indifference.<BR>\nKill with truth. Be clean. Be free.<BR>\nWe are free with truth -- to feel pain, life. Allow others the honor of their own pain, their own freedom.<BR>\nSlay willful idiot pretence defense. No honor in petty back knives; no fault in shining diamond destruction. The slaughter in your soul is clean forgiving, bright and alive; sprouting, unfolding life eternal.<BR><BR>\nMy assortment of lies press ten different ways, each making less of the whole. Familiar sapping, blinding.<BR>\nThey are ornamental ropes. I am slowly trudging along a mountain path. There is a huge round stone on my back. It is the world and my heart bound by my lies.<BR>\nI long for brightness, fresh streams, the farewell burnt ground horizon.<BR><BR>\n<I> * best friendly psychotic stream of conscious * </I><BR>',1,2716,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16101,2686,'2002-04-27 12:27:31',187,'kandrer','nice images... did not sound too psychotic to me, though: for a minute i even thought i was back in church.</p><p>\r\nobvious points:<br>\r\n- some people are not ready for the truth<br>\r\n- some people don\\\'t deserve it<br>\r\n- some people will give you lies in exchange of truth\r\n</p>\r\n<i>(damn, it seems it\\\'s time for me to sleep and lower my P level... good night :))</i>',3,374,196,0,'207.93.61.113','en'),(16102,2686,'2002-04-27 22:16:36',148,'jeeff','kandrer, i\\\'d say those three types of people are the ones who need to hear the truth most.  ',0,95,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(16103,2676,'2002-04-27 22:23:56',183,'lin','<p> I actually saw the Ocean Dome (or something exactly the same as it) in a documentary.  The funny thing was that it was pretty close to natural water, and they created false weather problems inside, for a more realistic feel. Ie: fake hurricane scares, and fake thunderstorms. Weird stuff. </p>',0,297,0,0,'24.42.108.124','en'),(16104,2677,'2002-04-27 22:33:24',265,'chuckspeed','no offense, but i don\\\'t understand.\r\n\r\nsometimes the intent of comments are lost in transmission.  besides, i\\\'m not a poetry geek - lyrical prose is more my style.',0,169,187,0,'205.188.199.178','en'),(16105,2677,'2002-04-27 23:02:48',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i was gonna commment exactly what kandrer said, earlier ;) keep the old fridge and give away the new one...</tt>',1,120,265,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16106,2688,'2002-04-28 00:29:48',278,'egenus','ooooh. i like.',1,14,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16107,2684,'2002-04-28 01:30:16',175,'Paul G','<p>Dear Austin,</p>\r\n<p>I am sorry that you are in Hell.  You are like some brightly colored butterfly.  And they keep you, my <a href=\\\"http://www.allwatchers.com/topics/info_4008.asp\\\">Papillon</a>, in a place that you do not belong.  It is the best story: a soul that struggles because it must be free.  Thank you for this place; you have made a difference.  You are a bigger man than they know.  Bastards.</p>\r\n<p>Paul.</p>\r\n',0,433,0,0,'67.24.118.12','en'),(16108,2688,'2002-04-28 02:56:33',265,'chuckspeed','very nice, lotus.',0,17,0,0,'152.163.213.51','en'),(16109,2677,'2002-04-28 03:03:51',265,'chuckspeed','<p>i like things that have earned the right to \\\'be\\\'.  new for the sake of new, well...it don\\\'t cut it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>at one point, we had three refrigerators - small, big, and pink.  i really liked the pink. sadly, pink was an energy pig, and it was a bad vibe having an appliance 30% more efficient jus gathering dust, so...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>off it went.  it was good for a few more years, so it serves someone in need.  we feel pretty good about that.</P>\r\n',0,457,66,0,'152.163.213.51','en'),(16110,2684,'2002-04-28 03:58:09',265,'chuckspeed','<p>austin-</p>\r\n\r\n<p>no offense, man, but your life is fucked up.  SoCal will do that to you.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>if you\\\'re aligned, it matters not what you do - you can live doing most anything.  adventureimports.com - the guy makes a living selling guatemalan hacky-sacks.  He travels the world when he feels like it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you\\\'ve been enslaved by the \\\'merican dream - bastards suck you in with it, then suck away life\\\'s energy.  vampires with counttry-club memberships.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>break free.  it\\\'s possible...I threw the keys to my SoCal home in a lake some eight years ago; been free ever since. i\\\'ll write about it sometime.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>What peter said.  bastards.  keep in mind you allow them to own you; that\\\'s the key.</p>\r\n\r\n',0,747,0,0,'172.137.70.203','en'),(16111,2687,'2002-04-28 04:12:15',196,'jasonp','Indifference from strangers is just what you should expect.<BR> Indifference can be good or bad. It\\\'s all context. To see someone bleeding in the gutter, crying for help and to be indifferent, unaffected, would be callous and self-centered. Being indifferent to those close to you could mean that the capacity for any kind of empathy or compassionate is not possible; someone with emotional problems, cold and distant.<BR>\nTo be blase and unaffected is a posture of indifference, i.e. being \\\"cool\\\". I think it\\\'s an avoidance of association, because of the fear of that thing being discredited leading to a loss in the cool equation. Also it\\\'s an avoidance of being genuine, since being genuine puts your real self on the line instead of a remote contrived pretense. It\\\'s not real indifference though in many cases, it\\\'s more a matter of pretending not to care.<BR>\nIndifference can be a matter of survival. It\\\'s difficult to feel for others if you yourself are always under attack or insecure. Not many warm fuzzies in the streets of a large city or a prison population. Callous cruelty breeds itself.<BR>\nIndifference is a matter of priorities. Whatever is most important is noted and reacted to carefully. We choose what should be reacted to, and what should not be regarded.<BR><BR>\nIndifference can be a good kind of thing, as in \\\"lack of attachment\\\". To be aloof towards one thing allows reverance of another. Affirming one by denying the other. To be indifferent to money, sex, power, fame, etc. for some higher principle could be considered virtuous. Lack of attachment is a main tenet of buddhist philosophy. Denying the ways of the world affirms the realm of the spirit.<BR>\n\n',2,1715,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16112,2687,'2002-04-28 04:30:50',176,'Scapegoat','I couldn\\\'t agree with the advice more but I\\\'m not sure if I see indifference as completely negative. If one doesn\\\'t have some amount indifference to others then one become a prisoner of community and unable to speak or be what one truly is. In short, I guess that indifference can be positive in my mind if it allows you to repel peer and social pressures. This may sound cold but I\\\'ve often felt constrained by inclusivity.<br><br>\r\nIndifference in my mind is just like so many other things it needs to be balanced.   ',0,531,0,0,'64.229.163.177','en'),(16113,2687,'2002-04-28 04:32:29',176,'Scapegoat','I forgot to mention I really like Desmond Morris \\\'The Human Zoo\\\' more then the \\\'Naked Ape\\\' though',0,109,0,0,'64.229.163.177','en'),(16114,2680,'2002-04-28 05:38:40',175,'Paul G','Thank you all very much for taking the time to read the piece and comment upon it.  The first comment, by paul, was especially meaningful and well-written; thank you again, sir.',0,177,0,0,'67.248.78.29','en'),(16115,2684,'2002-04-28 05:40:47',66,'Peter','<br><tt>um, i thinnk <i>paul</i> mentioned the bastards, by the way.</tt>',1,73,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16116,463,'2002-04-28 06:25:25',69,'starlajo','i believe canadians call the chip/cheese/gravy combo \\\"poutain\\\"\r\n\r\n',0,72,66,0,'210.86.87.110','en'),(16117,2689,'2002-04-28 06:55:39',175,'Paul G','Short List:\r\n<p>1984 Toyota Truck<br>\r\n1996 Ford F-Series Truck<br>\r\n1995 Honda Civic</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The Toyota was great, until I smashed it.  The Honda still rules.  The Ford sucked: After 13k it needed a new water pump, new fuel pump, and a new TRANSMISSION; it\\\'s seat broke, it\\\'s lights broke, it\\\'s windshield cracked.</p>',0,330,0,0,'67.248.78.29','en'),(16118,2689,'2002-04-28 07:00:04',66,'Peter','<br><tt>1987 toyota tercel (tree fell on it in tornado)<br>1971 volvo 240gl(broken drive shaft, cost too much to fix)<br>1984 honda civic hatchback (died at 245k)<br>1984 toyota corolla (still running well after an engine rebuild)<br><br>i have resigned myself to the fact that i am a <i>car-finisher</i>, you know, the guy who buys and drives people\'s old cars when they upgrade to nice new ones, heh.<br><br>my brother and father\'s list would be much cooler than mine, including a \'62 impala, a \'58 caddie, a few pickups, a corvette and a van or two; my mom, strictly toyota since a bad experience with her \'68 vw squareback.</tt>',1,411,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16119,2689,'2002-04-28 07:00:55',59,'s5','<br>\r\n1984 saab 900<br>\r\n1989 mazda 323<br>\r\n1989 honda prelude<br>\r\n1994 honda del sol<br>\r\n2000 VW jetta<br>\r\n1963 MGB<br>\r\n<br>\r\n(okay so the MGB is more of an object of lust than something i actually have, but it still gets to be in the list.)',0,247,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16120,2687,'2002-04-28 07:57:03',278,'egenus','I agree completely that one must be true to themselves and their opinions and voice those opinions without fear. Also I concur with your assesment that hysteria and paranoia are coping mechanisms, however I don\\\'t see the latter as a means to sugar-coat societal indifference. Most people, especially small-minded conservatives, feel that their ethics are not only superior, but also that their ethics unilaterally govern humankind (see Christianity). My theory is that hysteria is the common person\\\'s way of dealing with his/her own convoluted reasoning, and that paranoia is the literati\\\'s early warning system for the risks that coincide with a delusional society.',1,675,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16121,2689,'2002-04-28 07:58:44',240,'xep','<li>1975 honda cb350\r\n<li>1977 kawasaki kz400\r\n<li>1980/81 yamaha xs850\r\n<li>1978 suzuki gs550e\r\n<li>1987 honda vfr700\r\n<li>1990 bmw k75s\r\n<li>1978 bmw r100/7\r\n<li>1992 honda cbr600f2\r\n<li>1976 bmw r60/6\r\n<li>1990 honda hawk gt\r\n<li>1990 honda cb-1\r\n<li>1998 honda cbr400rr\r\n<p>\r\ni still have the r100/7, cb-1, and cbr400rr.\r\n<p>\r\ni\\\'ve never owned a car.\r\n',3,359,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(16122,2689,'2002-04-28 08:08:08',196,'jasonp','1969 VW Beetle - first car through high school. It was totally rusted out. You could put your hand through the glove department and wave at people through the hole in the hood.<BR>\n1982 VW Rabbit - Used this car for my Pizza Hut deliver job. Kind of a dud, even though I liked that it wasn\\\'t entirely rusted out. Finally ditched for next car after a lot of chronic electrical problems.<BR>\n1966 VW Beetle - This car only lasted a few days before it got stolen when I went up to visit my dad in LA.\n<BR>\n1989 Metro Geo - This was a loaner from the insurance from the stolen VW. Had this for a month while staying in Newport Beach for the summer.<BR>\n1964 Ford Falcon - First and only american car. Good car, the V8 was great. Kept this car through college until I left for Japan. Started out kind of cherry but then ended up kind of junked up after a freeway mishap and negligent use of fuel additive which screwed up the engine.<BR>\n19?? station wagon something - this was the very first car bought from returning from japan after not driving for 3 years. Got it for about $350. You get what you pay for. It lasted not even a single day. Had it salvaged for only $75.<BR>\n1988 VW Fox - Lasted about four years. Kind of embarrassing but it got me where I wanted to go.<BR>\n1999 VW Jetta VR6 - first new car, and loved it. Ended up totalling it after 9 months.<BR>\n2000 VW Jetta 1.8T - the first one was good enough so just got another. ',0,1438,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16123,2689,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>my favourite asides on the list are:</p>\r\n\r\n- marital aid<br>\r\n- the wife\\\'s new ride<br>\r\n\r\n<p>:D</p>',6,107,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16124,2676,'2002-04-28 08:45:41',93,'chiamattt','thats what you get when the rich dont feel like spending their HARD earned money on the poor. like we dont have enough real world weather problems to contend with. its motherfucking hot out here. why not dome the whole fucking city and add a bit of a breeze, block out the dust storms, and have some giant air freshners to mask the smell of fecal matter i have to deal with on the way down my street. god damn open sewers.',5,422,0,0,'211.32.132.228','en'),(16125,2689,'2002-04-28 09:17:48',65,'le','the only car worth having is a mid to late seventies el camino. ss.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni have a honda passport. it is not a car. but it has a basket. ',4,142,0,0,'172.160.133.54','en'),(16126,2689,'2002-04-28 09:21:49',65,'le','chuck, we had a red opel when i was a kid, in germany. the doors were rusting off, when you shut them, chunks would fall off. one day the opel didn\\\'t work because some brand of german rodent likes to chew on one type of fan belt inside the engine. i really really liked the opel tho, and was really sad when it had to go away. ',4,330,0,0,'172.160.133.54','en'),(16127,2689,'2002-04-28 09:56:27',218,'da','<p>\r\nthe only car of note i\\\'ve had was my first.  a 1977 celica, bought for one dollar in 1989 with no driver\\\'s side window - although it did feature an \\\"i brake for elvis\\\" bumpersticker.  traded up from that around the end of h.s. to jetta the first, an \\\'86 that - in a somewhat bizarre and probably subconsciously motivated act of cleansing - i ended up falling asleep in and dragging along the side of four or five innocent suburbanly parked vehicles.  luckily i was only going about 20, and it was really ok, this car seriously needed not to be in circulation.  the four grand worth of damages just about exactly matched it\\\'s presumed worth, so the glovebox was cleared out and it was summarily junked.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>the title for jetta the second - purchased a year or two after jetta the first\\\'s untimely demise, i drove a spare creaky volvo 240 in the interim - arrived in the mail just today.  it is paid off. (which would be swell, except that it\\\'s a pyrrhic victory in a frustratingly persistent financial muddle.)\r\n</p>',2,1050,0,0,'4.47.68.165','en'),(16128,2691,'2002-04-28 11:51:04',89,'sawks','i hope you are rocking much bulgogi',3,35,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(16129,2684,'2002-04-28 12:52:51',265,'chuckspeed','yet another stupid atack.  late night forays into e-lit should be banned',0,72,66,0,'205.188.200.163','en'),(16130,2689,'2002-04-28 13:00:47',265,'chuckspeed','<p>DAMN!</p>\r\n\r\ngot one of them \\\'ports too!  1982, dual baskets (added later). traded it for some russian motorcycle parts - have had it four years now.  been performance tuned so it goes 50 MPH on the flat.  got a race carb to put on it this year - hope to see 60 MPH when done. red/white - love grinding the pegs - it\\\'s like being in my own personal cartoon.',0,366,65,0,'205.188.200.163','en'),(16131,2689,'2002-04-28 13:06:51',265,'chuckspeed','<p>i\\\'ll do the bike list at a later time, but the \\\'Zook GS (RatZook) was near the top of the \\\'funride\\\' list.  GS850G - bought it for 50 bucks, rode it like a weapon, sold it for 500 and earned HUGE brownie points with the missus.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the /5 goes away this week<p>\r\n\r\n<p>snif<p>\r\n\r\n',0,299,240,0,'205.188.200.163','en'),(16132,2691,'2002-04-28 14:15:26',196,'jasonp','Hehe, good one. What did you think about the japanese/korean differences though? Weren\\\'t there more things that freaked you out or impressed over there?  You could be writing full length articles if you really went into detail.<BR>\nYeah public transportation is pretty amazing. When I first went I got a big kick out of the vending machines that sold beer and whiskey, or the lax liquor laws which allowed anyone to stroll down the street with a big ass kirin beer no problem. I think they considered that to be kind of barbaric. Who knows maybe it wasn\\\'t legal after all, but nobody stopped me. Normally I would be more discrete about public drinking though and just have a sake cup by the river with some dried squid and \\\"kakipi\\\" hot crackers. Then there are the gaijin bars, izakaya restaurants to hang out in.<BR>\nI was never bored there. Miserable many times for different reasons, but not in a boring kind of way. The place is just too hectic and wierd.<BR>\n',0,976,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16133,1248,'2002-04-28 17:29:55',67,'trouble','some things have leveled off, some things are still quite rough.  jealousy, loss of creativity and insomnia are still my biggest demons and i i still practice avoidance on a daily basis.  hence my disapearence on swinney.org - i only visit when i get my courage up these days.  so many creative people spilling so much creative thought... no place for the timid.',1,362,65,0,'68.47.202.98','en'),(16134,2689,'2002-04-28 17:33:37',82,'simon','1967 volvo, 1981 volvo, 1966 rambler american, two 1972 bmw 2002s and one 1975, 1987 el camino, and a 1986 volvo station wagon i bought and sold to raise a little money for fixing the 2002s. this is only just the beginning, check back with me in 10 years. hi.',0,259,0,0,'64.81.80.242','en'),(16135,2689,'2002-04-28 17:53:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt>rusty bug:<br><a href=\"http://www.citynoise.org\" border=\"0\"><img src=\"http://www.rhodamine.org/images/vw.jpg\"></a>',1,78,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16136,2690,'2002-04-29 01:59:24',17,'austin','having discussed this with my co-editors, i am going to post it.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nbut, FUCK you for this shit.  it really makes me mad.  you think you should post this on my website and not talk to me about it?<BR><BR>\r\nfuck you.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ngod damn it i am pissed.  you are going to hear about it  IN YOUR EARS.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nsure, im not supposed to talk on the internet, this permanent environment, when i am mad, but you did it first.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\noh you will say you were not mad, but how would you know, you were too drunk to know when to shut up and give it a rest.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n... and then you went and posted it on this website where in addition to total fucking strangers that read this shit, there are people who want to know who i am, and my parents, and my co workers.<BR><BR>\r\nhey all of you reading this out there in Web Land:<BR><BR>\r\n<FONT SIZE=7 COLOR=RED>FUCK YOU ASSHOLES, STAY OUT OF MY LIFE</FONT><BR><BR>\r\nyou are a real bitch sometimes.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nburn! burn!',6,970,0,1,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16137,2689,'2002-04-28 18:24:42',148,'jeeff','my list:\r\n\r\n<p>1988 mazda mpv minivan</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the end.</p>',0,60,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(16138,2690,'2002-04-29 01:59:24',17,'austin','<P>oooooohhhhhh kay, now i feel much better.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>hi, happy sunday morning!</P>',1,84,17,1,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16139,2669,'2002-04-28 18:37:16',285,'bdh','<p>Walmart - here in the middle of nowhere, where i ilve, it\\\'s the only thing open at 3am. I hate what it does and says about our culture, and I find my self shocked by the people I see there on saturday afternoons (I\\\'m nothing if not judgemental). \r\n<p>But at 3am on sunday, when you decide that you need to:\r\n\r\n<p>[cook|build|clean|create|distroy|art] \r\n\r\n<p>then walmart is there for you. And I have to have grudging(sp?) respect for anything that doesn\\\'t tie me to everyone else\\\'s sleep cycle. \r\n\r\n<p>\r\n[this all makes me miss the le rantings that use to be at elly.org - but that\\\'s neither here nor there.]',6,626,0,0,'129.79.118.143','en'),(16140,2669,'2002-04-28 18:43:41',285,'bdh','And, ok - \r\n\r\n<p> maybe that need for instant gratification is part of what you\\\'re saying is bad about our culture. And even if you\\\'re not, I\\\'d say that it is in some ways bad. (children need more patience. I need more.) \r\n\r\n<p> but to some extent, its not bad. Spontinaity(again, sp?) is a beautiful thing. I like things that support that, because some neat (as well as not so neat) things come out of that. \r\n\r\n<p>Where\\\'s the line? excessive-distructive-unreplenishable-consumption is bad, whining-squirming-impatient-ness is bad. But flexability != bad. freedom != bad. Is walmart that? Is some of modern culture, cellphones/internet/dvd/mailorder that? \r\n\r\n<p>I don\\\'t know. whatever. ',1,703,0,0,'129.79.118.143','en'),(16141,2689,'2002-04-28 20:32:00',285,'bdh','<p> >> i\\\'ve never owned a car. \r\n\r\n<p>It just rocks that there are people who can say that. \r\n<br>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>1990 Toy(ota) pickup truck. Small, cheap, fixable. (except for the auto-trans, which killed it).\r\n<li>2000 VW Passat. Expensive, paidf for, fun, stick. Happy. \r\n</ul>',4,279,0,0,'129.79.118.143','en'),(16142,2689,'2002-04-28 20:40:15',65,'jo','normally i would research this myself but it\\\'s sunday and i\\\'m lazy, so i need someone to verify this information (szajic, to the rescue?):<BR>\r\nrecently i heard that jettas (and possibly other volkswagons) have a chip that monitors the driver\\\'s driving style and adjusts itself to that style, i.e. fuel efficiency, and that this can cause serious problems if you buy a used jetta or even if more than one person drives the car, because once it has adjusted itself to the primary driver, it regulates itself to that person and thus <b>freaks out</b> and starts breaking down if the driver changes.<BR>\r\ni REALLY need to know why the manufacturers would ever think that this was smart technology.',0,703,0,0,'172.155.168.249','en'),(16143,2689,'2002-04-28 20:59:02',65,'jo','oh, also, my list:\r\n<ul><li><b>1983 pontiac parisienne</b>: my first car and if you\\\'re a parent and reading this, i need to recommend against buying your kid an old, enormous car as their first vehicle because it had a <B>huge</b> blind spot, shoddy brakes, and it was nearly impossible to come to quick stop, and on the busy streets of houston, this resulted in about 6 fender benders during my first year of ownership.</li>\r\n<li><b>1985 chrysler new yorker</b>: see complaints about the first car. however! i loved this car very much and drove it into the ground.<BR> <b>everything</b> was computerized, and it had a voice chip, so, for instance, when i was running low on fuel, a voice would say: \\\"your fuel. is low.\\\" it was a steel blue color and the seats were plush velour and resembled coffin cushions, <b>and</b> the car had been my great-uncle\\\'s and he died in the front seat of the car (a heart attack, not a car accident), thus i named the car \\\"the death star.\\\" (it sounds morbid, i know, but rather than being creeped out that my great-uncle died in the car, i sort of felt like it was a blessing and that he was watching over me to make sure nothing ever happened to me while i was driving his car.)</li>\r\n<li><b>2000 nissan frontier</b>: i hate this truck. hate. it runs okay but it has the worst karma. witness: since i started leasing this truck, the following things have happened: \r\n<ol><li>my friend stefan, a month after i get the truck, is driving our friend gregory home and hits another car. the entire front bumper and some internal parts have to be replaced.</li>\r\n<li>2 months later, my friend jesse is driving my truck and causes further damage to the as of yet unfixed bumper. before he\\\'d left the house, i said, \\\"jesse, be careful, especially to the front, because the bumper is already <b>this</b> close to falling off.\\\" after this, it is held on with a bungee pulled out of the leg of jesse\\\'s old navy cargo pants, which were popular that summer.</li>\r\n<li>2 months later, i come out to my truck one morning and there is a sizable dent all along one side of the truck. a hit and run.</li>\r\n<li>a year later, while in a bar with friends, someone is drunkenly pulling out next to me and leaves a sizable dent all along the OTHER side of the truck. when i leave the bar, a couple is standing, staring at my truck, horrified at the hit and run they just witnessed. however, they didn\\\'t manage to catch the license plate number of the offender.</li>\r\n<li>a month ago, my boyfriend and i are on our way to a record store when someone <b>slams</b> into me from behind. we stop and get out; she motions for us to pull into the parking lot so we aren\\\'t obstructing traffic. she then takes off. the third hit and run.</li></ol>\r\ni can\\\'t wait to get this piece of shit off my hands.</li>',0,2846,0,0,'172.155.168.249','en'),(16144,2689,'2002-04-28 21:16:56',240,'xep','my best friend in high school had a tiny\r\nlittle yellow opel.  it had a weird bump on the hood where some part of the engine slept.  i have many fond memories of cramming myself into the \\\"back seat\\\" on road trips between santa cruz and san francisco.  i went to see my first real concert in that car.  peter murphy at the warfield.  on the way home i fell asleep in a contorted position only to wake up to marti and stacia puking out either side of the front seat because of food poisoning they\\\'d gotten from some chinese resturant.  i was glad i hadn\\\'t eaten the pork.  marti eventually got rid of it when something broke and he called every mechanic in town and when they\\\'d ask what kind of car, and he\\\'d say opel, they\\\'d laugh and hang up.\r\nhe replaced it with a honda civic.\r\n',0,801,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(16145,2690,'2002-05-24 17:02:54',86,'egg','I would have been happy to trash this if I had known that it made austin this mad.  I thought it was a lighthearted and affectionate piece when I wrote it. I am the sort of person who jokes about things like butt sex because I think they are absurd. I think sex in general is funny. that is all.',0,295,0,1,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16146,2689,'2002-04-28 21:47:26',66,'Peter','<br><tt>overhead cams!<br>overhead cams!<br><br>my elementary school librarian had a blue opel kadet</tt>',1,105,240,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16147,2689,'2002-04-28 21:50:03',66,'Peter','<br><tt>your truck, however, has a good truckbed for riding around in and making photos from!</tt>',1,98,65,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16148,2690,'2002-04-29 01:59:24',17,'austin','here is an idea,  why dont we just have an open discussion on why it butt sex is such a touchy subject.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\npersonally, i know that there are people out there who have very strong negative views on the subject (hi dad) and i do not know who those people are.  therefore, i would appreciate not being implicated in any way in that sort of pigeon hole.  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nbut having discussed this post,  which regardless of what i thought about it for the first couple paragraphs, i came to think of as \\\"...austin...buttsex...\\\", at length with amy, and having read an email from jim that he can post if he wants, and having gotten permission from amy to take it down, and knowing full well that we here at swinney.org do not take things down just because people want us to,  i will leave it up and so will the other editors.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nbutt sex, what the hell?  it just feels good.   now please, if you are driving around town after the football game and you want to beat somebody up, dont pick me.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nconstructive.\r\n',3,1031,0,1,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16149,2689,'2002-04-28 22:08:17',67,'trouble','1979 pontiac grand prix - smashed it while skipping school<br>\r\n1988 honda CRX-Si - was rear-ended while at a dead stop, folded like a pop can and gave me the worst headache of my life\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.geocities.com/m_whyte/digital/leland/\\\">1973 VW Bus</a> - a work in progress<br>\r\n1990 mercury sable - piece of shit but was given to me so i don\\\'t complain too much',1,379,0,0,'68.47.202.98','en'),(16150,2690,'2002-04-29 01:59:24',65,'le','i dunno man, i\\\'m kind of too shocked by the unnatural public display of all things austin to move the conversation into a more abstract area of buttsex and why it\\\'s a taboo.\r\n<br><br>\r\nbasically i sorta think, austin, you shouldn\\\'t have posted this thing if you didn\\\'t want it posted. i mean, i have no problem with the level of personal exposure in this post. if you had been ok with it too, then it would have been good. but it was destined to be ugly because you just don\\\'t want this out there. so now it\\\'s just this negative thing sitting up here for everyone to read, the initial bumbling attempt at.. something, by egg, and your upset response. i read it and it makes me feel sad. tho before i read your response i didn\\\'t feel sad. i felt ok about it till i realized just how mad you really were. then i felt sad because i don\\\'t think egg was trying to make you mad, tho she may not be without fault, i don\\\'t think she meant to make you that mad. and then i felt empathy because i have often done stupid shit that made people mad when that wasn\\\'t what i meant to do.\r\n<br><br>\r\nANYWAY maybe it\\\'s good this is here so we can all explore our EMOTIONS surrounding this issue of public personal exposure.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni feel fine about buttsex, i just never think i can have it because i never know if i might have to poo.\r\n<br><br>\r\nseeya!\r\n\r\n',4,1377,0,1,'172.170.242.70','en'),(16151,2690,'2002-04-29 01:59:24',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i dont see what the big hoot and holler is all about... oh well.</tt>',1,77,0,1,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16152,2689,'2002-04-28 22:49:40',265,'chuckspeed','jo-\r\n\r\n<p>what you\\\'re talkng about is an adaptive map. it\\\'s only partially true - the computer stores *some* info, but that\\\'s usually related to error codes.  Don\\\'t worry \\\'bout it.  If you ever want to buy a used one, just disconnect the battery for a little while - five minutes is enough to clear codes.</p>  ',0,326,65,0,'205.188.200.38','en'),(16153,2689,'2002-04-28 22:58:29',265,'chuckspeed','<p>the \\\'bump\\\' of which you speak was the airhorn (air intake) for the Solex 2 bbl. progressive carburetor.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> I\\\'m the only guy in North America who knows how to make that carb work perfectly.  that\\\'s not a conceit - it\\\'s a fact. Maggie the Opel rests in Pop\\\'s barn, waiting for my eldest daughter to drive it - soon.  it can sit for a year or two, but fires immediately whenever I go to check on it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> being the North American Expert on a subject as arcane as a Solex progressive carb is as useful as tits on a boar hog.  Took me ten years to get good; now no one cares.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>being a car geek sux.  you don\\\'t meet people under the hood of a car.</p>  ',0,694,240,0,'205.188.200.38','en'),(16154,2690,'2002-04-29 01:59:24',20,'jim','<p>\r\nyou know, it completely failed to occur to me that it was the oblique mention of buttsex that was troubling you about this post.  never even crossed my mind.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI just kind of take it for granted that two people will do whatever they can think of to make each other feel good, as long as it doesn\'t damage themselves or others in some lasting way.  backrubs, badminton or buttsex...whatever makes you happy.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI liked Amy\'s post (and the process that led to Austin making it available here for you to read) a lot because it pokes at the depths of intimacy that exist between two people; how the things we aren\'t comfortable making available to each other, the things we need to hold close to ourselves, are so personal, so individual, that neither my culture, my lover, or myself can possibly predict or define what those things will be at any given moment.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nthe personal writing I find most interesting is all about digging around in that.  that\'s why I wanted this here.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',6,1054,0,1,'66.92.4.146','en'),(16155,2689,'2002-04-28 23:06:04',265,'chuckspeed','<p>the camaro had *the* worst karma.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>EVERY time i took it out, there was a near-miss accident.  \\\'splained this to Pop, but he didn\\\'t believe.  let him drive it to work for a while; had several near misses in the process.  decreed the car suffered from bad karma; i was allowed to sell it shortly thereafter.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>sold it to this hash bash disciple who promptly wrapped it around a tree. no remorse - the car wanted to die.</p>\r\n\r\n',0,451,65,0,'205.188.200.38','en'),(16156,2689,'2002-04-28 23:12:51',265,'chuckspeed','on the concert thing:\r\n\r\n<p>the opel gt was my first concert car, too.  kiss/cheap trick/new england; we had four stuffed into a two seat car. drank vodka from beer mugs; the animal passed out from a combination of player\\\'s navy cuts and vodka before kiss hit the stage.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>On the way back home, flashing lights in the rearview - \\\'bout peed my pants.  fortunately, it was just a police escort for the band - the first of many close calls.  but...that\\\'s for another list.</p>',0,490,240,0,'205.188.200.38','en'),(16157,2689,'2002-04-29 00:21:06',124,'gus','I listed the cars I\\\'ve dated, but not the cars I\\\'ve owned. \r\n<p>\r\n1968 VW Fastback - I drove it into the ground<br>\r\n1979 Porsche 924 - a dork of a porsche, one step up from a 914<br>\r\n1975 BMW 2002 - No e-brake, backfired EVERY time I shifted<br>\r\n1982 Honda Accord - only car I ever owned that had working defrosters. 180 degree spin out and full roll on 101 S just up the road from the Soledad exit. Some guy pounded out the windsheild frame, put a new one in, paid us 200 bucks and drove it away.\r\n<p>\r\nAnd now, there\\\'s no parking anywhere... so I take the bus.',0,574,0,0,'64.160.52.253','en'),(16158,2690,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>i dunno.  personal writing is all very well and good when it\'s about the author.  when it concerns other people it helps to take into account what you know they hold sacred.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i feel bad for austin because i know he\'s a private guy.  i feel bad for him because the pressure in the swinney circut for full disclosure is great.  i feel bad for him that i am contributing to a thread where other people are discussing how he should feel about details of his personal life being disclosed.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>that said, i think i can see everyone\'s viewpoint in this situation.  but the one i have most sympathy for is austin\'s.</p>',6,642,0,1,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16159,2534,'2002-04-29 00:50:36',286,'kalou','xx',0,2,0,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(16160,2534,'2002-04-29 00:59:08',286,'kalou','<p>Hi,</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We are two friends of Joannas who have just read you comments. \\\"Be sensible about it\\\"? How do you expect somebody to \\\"be sensible\\\" about something like that? Not to mention putting time limits on it - she probably never will get over it, we certainly wouldn\\\'t. And how can you possibly expect the anger she has towards you and him to be the same anger when you both held such different places in her heart and life. How can you expect her to be \\\"rational\\\" about something so emotional? Were you being rational when you did what you did?  You made the choice, now live with it. It\\\'s not Joanna\\\'s problem, its yours.</p><p> We couldn\\\'t help writing this as we read your comment and it really made us quite angry. Grrrr  </p>',0,769,69,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(16161,2686,'2002-04-29 01:05:21',187,'kandrer','sorry i have been ambiguous again <i>(not only ambiguous, i know)</i>; i just meant that there are some obvious cases where telling the truth is a bad idea (a harmful idea, for you or for your interlocutor). judeo-christian education doesn\\\'t mention these cases, that\\\'s too bad. i thought it could help the poster (<i>hi laney!</i>)',1,338,0,0,'205.184.183.22','en'),(16162,2687,'2002-04-29 01:29:49',187,'kandrer','thank you all for the comments. i will need some time to thing again about it and answer.<br>\r\nyou see, i did not categorize the post as \\\'spiritual\\\' myself (i wouldn\\\'t dare); there was not \\\'fantasy\\\' category so i left it uncategorized. i just found this fun (to me) way of making up a theory and that\\\'s why i wrote the post. there is nothing more than a few personal feelings here. but i will answer, i promise.',1,429,0,0,'205.184.183.22','en'),(16163,2686,'2002-04-29 02:17:35',196,'jasonp','To give any advice it depends on the actual situation and lie in question.<BR>\nI lie most frequently out of politeness or not wanting to hurt people\\\'s feelings. For example, last night my brother called up all excited about some reggae song he had made and he played it over the phone, and it totally sucked. If had been entirely brutally honest I would have said, \\\"Dude, that sucks. Learn how to sing man.\\\" But the kind of relationship I have with him is that he has a hard time dealing with criticism from his older brother so instead I say, \\\"Ohh.. Oh yeah, okay. Pretty good.\\\" <BR>\n\nLies to control or hurt are the worst. I\\\'ve never even been tempted to tell lies like that, but I have been the victim of them. I\\\'d consider that the one of the most wrong things you can do. That to me is why the epithet \\\"liar\\\" is so damning, when it applies to snakes that stab you in the back or try and use other people.<BR>\n\nThen there are the lies to avoid dealing with something or to get out of trouble. I\\\'m guessing this is what this situation is about. These are risky. For the most part I think these should be avoided also. It\\\'s better to pay everything up front rather than have it accumulate and snowball down the line. I think that develops integrity too, to just lay it on the line and take your lumps rather than try and weasel out of things. But then again when you get caught in a bind lying often seems like the best option. It can be a tough call, but I\\\'d err on the side of telling the truth. Take your lumps with dignity. <BR>\n\n',2,1572,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16164,2534,'2002-04-29 02:26:42',187,'kandrer','oh man, i wished i had friends like you... could you make a few burn-in-hell-forever wishes for a few people who did me wrong?',4,126,286,0,'205.184.183.22','en'),(16165,2689,'2002-04-29 02:48:18',188,'bark','I recently added a pickup for $100.  3/4 ton, Valient engine.  Good tires, runs nice, loose steering.  Speedometer, brake lights, signals and brights don\\\'t work.  It has a big tool box in back, but he kept the gun rack and radio.  Someday I will have had this truck.  \r\n<ul>\r\n<li>\\\'66 Ford pickup</li>\r\n</ul>',0,313,0,0,'12.110.65.233','en'),(16166,2691,'2002-04-29 02:48:45',187,'kandrer','nice report, thanks!<br>\r\ni like the drug dogs. they are always the nicer person in the room toward you. and you know they won\\\'t get mad if they find nothing. too bad they never let you pet them.',1,198,0,0,'205.184.183.22','en'),(16167,2688,'2002-04-29 02:54:16',187,'kandrer','<i>the trees never reach the sky</i><br>\r\n(share holders proverb, sorry Lotus ;))',1,81,0,0,'205.184.183.22','en'),(16168,2687,'2002-04-29 03:08:54',75,'tyler','&quot;paranoia is the literati\\\'s early warning system for the risks that coincide with a delusional society.&quot;\r\n<p>\r\nawesome.  ',1,134,0,0,'165.247.165.31','en'),(16169,2534,'2002-04-29 04:45:14',92,'kini','oh, please. what are we? fifteen?',0,33,286,0,'203.27.69.93','en'),(16170,2534,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>yeah really....</p>\r\n\r\n<p>settle down, eh?  there\\\'s only two people involved, not a whole entourage of gossping schoolgirls for christs sake.</p>',6,151,92,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16171,2534,'2002-04-29 06:48:33',92,'kini','was that comment supposed to be directed at me?\r\n\r\nI HATE YOU!! YOU\\\'RE TOTALLY DUMPED!!!\r\n\r\n:D',1,97,62,0,'203.27.69.91','en'),(16172,2534,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>OH MY GOD YOU BITCH!!!!!!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>that is it i am totally getting my ex-boyfriend to tell his girlfriend\\\'s sister that you fucked my science teacher\\\'s sisters son!!!!</p>',6,181,92,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16173,2534,'2002-04-29 07:10:05',59,'s5','*removes kini from \\\"friends\\\" list*',0,40,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16174,1532,'2002-04-29 07:29:53',196,'jasonp','This set of plane crash nightmares Peter had is kind of eerie post 9/11. Anyone notice this before?',0,99,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16175,2534,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<img src=\\\"http://saturn5.com/olivia/fighting_ladies.jpg\\\">',6,63,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16176,2534,'2002-04-29 08:19:34',92,'kini','dude, it was your science teacher\\\'s sister\\\'s husband!! as if i\\\'d even TOUCH her son!\r\n<p>also, s5 - you totally SUCK!!! down with you and your fascist ways.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>(okay, are we done now?)</p>',1,204,62,0,'203.27.69.91','en'),(16177,2695,'2002-04-29 09:48:46',196,'jasonp','The feeling of walking around post-processing events from 5 years ago, yeah I know what that\\\'s like. That\\\'s something of a pattern for me. A brief intense hectic period followed by a longer and much slower period of reflection of it all. Then while I\\\'m still hung up on the past my life slowly falls apart and reassembles itself again to a new time of intense activity to turn the cycle over again.\n',0,408,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16178,2534,'2002-04-29 09:54:36',69,'starlajo','+ kini\r\n\r\ni love you both. \r\nyou\\\'re hilarious.',1,49,62,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(16179,1532,'2002-04-29 10:07:41',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yep. <i>uncanny</i></tt>',1,32,196,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16180,2694,'2002-04-29 10:26:09',196,'jasonp','I think the fact that you\\\'ve kept a secret for so long gave it a much heavy influence.\nLooking at it objectively it doesn\\\'t seem that bad. Mabye the fact that it was something you choose that raises inner fears and doubts. You should realize you were just a kid. Probably holding this secret did a number on you.<BR>\nHave you ever checked out Howard Stern before? I think something like half the people on that show had some kind of molestation or early gay type experience. It\\\'s why they\\\'re obsessed with sex, and gays, and looking at women like pieces of meat all the time. Stern himself had an experience just like you describe with yourself. He talks about it on air. Maybe aspiring to be like Howard Stern is not the way to go, but the point is... keeping it secret lets it become in control of you and destroys your self-confidence. Being open about it and being able to take it lightly gets rid of it\\\'s power over you.<BR>',0,944,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16181,2695,'2002-04-29 11:46:20',66,'Peter','<br><tt>post-processing; the story of my life, lately...</tt>',1,61,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16182,2693,'2002-04-29 11:50:31',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i really <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1733.txt\\\">hate moving</a></tt>',1,107,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16183,2377,'2002-04-29 12:18:15',92,'kini','okay, so i\\\'m a little late, but i really like this.',1,54,0,0,'203.27.69.94','en'),(16184,2691,'2002-04-29 13:53:44',93,'chiamattt','i guess i could go into more detail about the differences in culture and people and all that but to be honest i dont really think i was in japan long enough, or in enough of japan to comment on how they differ , are the same. ill comment on how korea is different than canada or north american because ive been to a good part of each, but i mean....yeah i dont know. ',6,367,0,0,'61.37.53.26','en'),(16185,2694,'2002-04-29 14:09:25',265,'chuckspeed','<p>fuzzy</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it was molestation, and it was incest.  Most of these cases go unreported, on account of they are similar to what you\\\'ve experienced.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>IT\\\'S NOT YOUR FAULT.  TALK ABOUT IT.  the feeling of \\\'damaged goods\\\' is common to victims of molestation/incest, and keeping it inside makes it bigger over time.  it\\\'s not uncommon to wait several years for it to talk about it, but do it!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you were asked to perform a sex act before puberty - that is WRONG.  you are NOT culpable as you didn\\\'t know any better.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'ve spoken to many people about the incident with my daughter - it\\\'s amazing how many other folks have had similar experiences. in EVERY case, their feelings improved after talking aobut it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>made my day, actually - you\\\'re admission, that is.  keep talking.</p>  ',0,843,0,0,'208.5.114.108','en'),(16186,2639,'2002-04-29 14:14:22',265,'chuckspeed','an update...\r\n\r\nwith the whole church scandal going on, dear ol\\\' Father Ted\\\'s story hit the news locally over the weekend.  small world.',0,142,0,0,'208.5.114.108','en'),(16187,2694,'2002-04-29 15:26:46',75,'tyler','geez, chuck, it wasn\\\'t incest.\r\n<p>\r\ni agree that keeping it a secret probably has made it a bigger deal than it really is. he was a confused adolescent, and you were an sexually ignorant young child...it doesn\\\'t sound like you were intimidated or frightened...the guy definitely had to have known it was wrong to do what he did, but it sounds like nothing further happened and you were lucky. if it bothers you in your daily life, though, definitely see somebody. ',1,471,0,0,'165.247.165.31','en'),(16188,1512,'2002-04-29 15:32:32',283,'mojo','i just used to read and listen to personal ads, hoping i would find or hear one that was \\\'the one.\\\'  i never found it (besides the guy who wanted spankings, but that was different.)</p>\r\nbut i did find that there were a lot of people who sounded a lot like me, only more lonely.  it was sad.  when i finally did go out on a few dates with women (because i wasn\\\'t having any trouble finding acceptable men to sleep with,) i just went along for the date and said goodnight.  i was bored.  the only one who got me in bed was very pushy and got me really drunk.  ',4,568,0,0,'24.69.255.205','en'),(16189,2693,'2002-04-29 15:36:03',175,'Paul G','It\\\'s not about moving, heh; you\\\'ve seen some of my stuff.  But, thanks for commenting!',0,92,66,0,'67.248.78.105','en'),(16190,2693,'2002-04-29 15:41:03',66,'Peter','<br><tt>uh, call me crazy, but the tidbit about <i>\\\"Moving, as the word implies, is an unsettling activity: your private space and your private things are made public\\\"</i> and the title itself <i>on top of your car</i>, etc. makes a perfect parallel.</tt>',1,261,175,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16191,2696,'2002-04-29 16:03:22',66,'Peter','<br><tt>its september for me. sigh... <i>september</i>.</tt>',0,60,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16192,2693,'2002-04-29 16:19:31',175,'Paul G','(Crazy!)\r\n\r\n<p>Metaphor has an external form, and also a content.  You see the form: what a thing appears to be.  In this case, the form was the story about moving.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Well, it might be about moving; but it\\\'s not about moving in the physical sense only.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>People move from point A to point B in their lives.  Sometimes that means moving from Tennessee to NYC.  Other times that means moving from one place to another inside themselves: self-overcoming, or maturing, or whatever.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>1)Top of car = Any public space, even a website<br>\r\n2)Treasures of your boudoir = Personal details/Life-story<br>\r\n3)The mattress = Your sexual details in particular<br>\r\n4)Friend helping move  = Companion trusted with details</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\\\'ve been reading a lot about people struggling with public disclosure lately.  Anyone who has a \\\"web-presence\\\" seems to find, at one time or another, that the only thing that some people want is to fixate on peculiar details.  You see where this is going...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>At some point most of us understand that most of humanity is mostly the same.  From time to time we happen upon someone\\\'s stuff as they move it; no big deal.  The people who look for stains on other people\\\'s mattresses are the weird ones.</p> ',0,1286,0,0,'67.248.78.105','en'),(16193,2693,'2002-04-29 16:23:37',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i know, paulg. i just felt like sharing my stuff-on-car-roof story ;)</tt>',1,82,175,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16194,2693,'2002-04-29 16:25:59',175,'Paul G','<a href=\\\"http://www.citynoise.org/\\\"><b>Smart-Ass!</b></a>',0,63,66,0,'67.248.78.105','en'),(16195,2695,'2002-04-29 16:28:01',246,'erb','<p>on the bus this morning i was dozing...wavering between sleep and waking i could feel different strange sensations in my bodymind or subtle body.  a demon sitting on my left shoulder, nibbling at my neck where i told a lie.  the outside edges of my feet pressing into the floor of the bus,  drawing up breath.  struggling to keep my eyes open, i breathed emphatically into my solar plexus.  generating energy.  stoking the power furnace.  </p>\r\n<p>in the past 5 days or so i have also been having more deja vu than usual--recognizing the scenery so to speak, always from a dream.  when it happens you have to say it: \\\"you guys, this is so weird, i dreamed this once a long time ago, that we would be sitting here this way...\\\"  i wondered if it was the full moon, but reading this i wonder if it is something more pervasive.  lovely piece.</p>',2,851,0,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(16196,2696,'2002-04-29 17:02:52',65,'jo','it\\\'s wretched fucking february for me.',0,41,0,0,'172.164.20.93','en'),(16197,2694,'2002-04-29 17:04:22',246,'erb','<p>hi.  i think there are a lot of different ways to view what happened.  as an adult, you have the power to interpret it in a way that is conducive to being the person you want to be.  i hear what is being said about it being molestation, and that is one way to look at it; but it\\\'s important to remember that children are sexual beings, too, and they have natural sexual curiosity--and it isn\\\'t dirty or wrong for kids to explore their bodies and the bodies of others, as long as boundaries are respected and power is not taken by force.  these lines are so fine and difficult to discern, especially across the distance of many years.</p>\r\n<p>i have a memory from when i was about five years old of being under a bed with a boy named caribou.  there were dust bunnies on the wooden floor and we were cramped underneath the metal springs.  he was older, maybe 8 or 9, and wanted to touch me.  he took my underwear off.  there were adults in the other room, talking, and maybe my mom called me.  after some breathless groping, we hurriedly scrambled out from under the bed.  i remember this incident as exciting, but also scary and on the verge of violating.  it was so long ago that i can\\\'t really remember how i felt.  i can only guess.  at times i have wondered if this incident or similar ones i don\\\'t remember at all shaped my sexuality in some unhealthy way, made me afraid of men, and so on.  i think that, in some ways, just as sexual curiosity is innate in us, so is fear of the power of sexual union, and so sexual hang-ups are as natural as sexual desire.</p>\r\n<p>again, though, as adults we have the power to direct our consciousness where we choose, to interpret the events of the past to our own advantage.  maybe what happened to you did create a certain pattern in your relationships of distance or fear of intimacy.  can you think of anything it may have brought to you?  maybe it gave you a certain type of sensitivity or artistic ability--most likely whatever \\\"negative\\\" effects it had also came with a gift of some kind.  most survivors of sexual trauma feel that they emerge stronger people after healing.  it can take years, but human beings are amazingly resilient and able to take pain or shame and make something beautiful.  i don\\\'t say this to belittle the trauma; i just believe that you have the power to see the beauty in your life, whatever form it takes and even if it has been incredibly painful to you at times.</p>\r\n<p>i think seeing a therapist could be helpful, especially a therapist who is trained in somatics, using the memories and wisdom stored in the body to work through internal conflict.  art therapy might also be really helpful to you in expressing the feelings you have around the incident and your sexuality in general--sometimes words can\\\'t adequately sum up something and things come out black and white when they should be in color.  another thing that i would try (with or without the guidance of a professional) is really going back to the memory and talking in your best loving adult voice to yourself as a kid.  tell yourself in that moment that you have the power to say no if you\\\'re uncomfortable. tell yourself that you are loveable and cared for, and that you don\\\'t have to do anything you don\\\'t want to do.  really try to comfort yourself if you can, and see if your memory child-self has anything to say about what happened.  maybe this way you will gain some more clarity about why it happened then and how you can make peace with the event as an adult and see how it has shaped you into the beautiful human being that you are.</p>\r\n<p>finally, i would like to thank you for your openness and honesty.  there shouldn\\\'t be any shame in telling this (i was surprised at the shame i felt just typing the memory i had, which i hadn\\\'t thought about in years).  we can support each other here.  telling the secret is the first step in feeling released from its power.  right on.</p>',2,3978,0,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(16198,2691,'2002-04-29 17:28:28',83,'adamajah','<p>nice story.  the customs officers you encountered seem much nicer than the border patrol at the El Paso/Juarez border.  That is, unless they <i>did</i> grab your balls whilst searching you.</p>',3,196,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(16199,2686,'2002-04-29 18:01:59',246,'erb','god yes lying is horrible.  especially when you do it with \\\"good\\\" intentions: i.e., in order to not hurt someone\\\'s feelings, or to save your own ass from being loved less.  i vowed yesterday to try and be braver with the truth.  yes.',2,242,0,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(16200,2694,'2002-04-29 18:24:30',124,'gus','The other day I told someone one of my \\\"dark secrets\\\". something I never tell anyone, mostly because I dont think of it often. but it sits down there and affects my relationships with people. It pops into my mind when Im feeling vulnerable like a little blinking light that says, \\\"WARNING! WARNING! REMEMBER THAT? DONT GET SO CLOSE OR IT MIGHT HAPPEN AGAIN! WARNING! WARNING!\\\" \r\n<p>\r\nBut you get close to someone, start sharing the things that make you who you are, and you cant leave out the bad and only mention the good. But when you tell them, they validate you. They say they cant believe anyone would do such a thing. Or they grab you and hold you close. Sometimes the person you tell your secret to is even more upset about it than you are and you think, \\\"huh! I guess it was kind of a bad thing after all, I guess it wasnt my fault!\\\" Even when you know it wasnt. Or you just tell us here, and all of us pipe up and say, \\\"me too, me too\\\". \r\n<p>\r\nWhen you let one out, all of the rest lose a little power. ',0,1036,0,0,'64.169.160.194','en'),(16201,2695,'2002-04-29 19:30:40',124,'gus','hi yeti, this post reminded me of this thing I wrote once. \r\n<p>\r\n<b>Looking Down</b>\r\n<p> \r\nSitting, like the thinker,<br>\r\nlooking down.<br>\r\nWhat are they for?<br>\r\nLooking down,<br>\r\nand I don\'t wonder <br>\r\nwhy they spell<br>\r\nexcrement<br>\r\nin four letter words.\r\n<p>\r\nLooking down.\r\n<p>\r\n\"They\'re mine,\"<br>\r\nI think.<br>\r\nBut they\'re not.<br>\r\nThey\'re just something I thought <br>\r\nI had controll over once.<br>\r\nBut control is just something<br>\r\nyou think you have.\r\n<p>\r\nLooking down.\r\n<p>\r\nI wish I could look <br>\r\nforward,<br>\r\nand not spell anything<br>\r\nin four letter words,<br>\r\nbut that\'s all <br>\r\nthat comes to mind.<br>\r\nAll I have.\r\n<p>\r\nAnd not what I wanted at all.\r\n<p>\r\nSitting like the thinker<br>\r\nlooking down,<br>\r\nat the angles,  <br>\r\n\"What are they for?\"<br>\r\nI think,<br>\r\n\"If they dont take me anywhere?\"<br>\r\nand I wonder.\r\n<p>\r\nLooking down.\r\n<p>\r\nNot quite 45 degrees<br>\r\noff center; off the norm.<br>\r\nand I wonder,<br>\r\n\"If I\'m the only one that can see,<br>\r\ndoes it matter how many letters<br>\r\nit takes to spell it?\"<br>\r\nI don\'t wonder.<br>\r\nIt\'s just something <br>\r\nI thought I had control over.\r\n<p>\r\nSomething I see,<br>\r\nlooking down.\r\n<p>\r\n<i>1995</i>\r\n',0,1253,0,0,'64.169.160.194','en'),(16202,2696,'2002-04-29 19:42:48',59,'s5','i don\\\'t have bad months, but i do get whiny and cranky and irritable three days before the new moon of every month. olivia calls it my \\\"man period\\\" or something like that.',3,180,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16203,2689,'2002-04-29 19:46:54',266,'spleenless','1974 Oldsmobile Delta 88 - Huge\r\n<br>\r\n1970 Volvo 1800 with a 3.8 liter Buick V6 - Fun but a huge pain in the ass.  I still own it but it was decommisioned in 1997.  It sits in my dad\\\'s driveway.\r\n<br><br>\r\n1978 Kawasaki KZ650<br>\r\n1954 BSA M33 - made it run just long enough to trade it for...<br>\r\n1965 Ducati 250 single - Wrecked (goodbye spleen), rebuilt, raced<br>\r\n1989 Honda CR250<br>\r\n1975 BMW R90/6<br>\r\n1998 Honda VTR1000<br>\r\n1988 BMW R100GS - wrecked (broken back)<br>\r\n1989 BMW R100GS/PD - had to replace the wrecked one<br>\r\n1996 Honda XR400<br><br>\r\nSometimes I wish I\\\'d never started riding motorcycles.  Now, I can\\\'t stop.\r\n',0,650,0,0,'66.92.184.86','en'),(16204,2696,'2002-04-29 20:31:50',124,'gus','I dont have bad months really. I used to only ever get into relationships in september though. maybe there\\\'s something up around then, eh peter? though... that seems to have changed... and this april has been a piece of cake. with cherries on top.',0,250,0,0,'64.169.160.194','en'),(16205,2689,'2002-04-29 20:36:02',17,'austin','\\\'84 yamaha 150 scooter thing.  bought when i was 16 (\\\'92) sold it my sophmore year in college (\\\'97), i am not sure why.<BR><BR>\r\n\\\'86 Buick Century - thanks dad. got it my junior year in highschool.  i never got laid in this car.  totalled graduation weekend when i made a left turn from the right lane of a 3 lane one way street. HEAVY drinking was involved the night before.  i probably should have avoided driving the next day at all.<BR><BR>\r\n\\\'87 Isuzu Pup that was already totalled when i bought it for $1200.  this was my favorite vehicle of all time.  i wish i had not sold it but i was in college and broke. i couldnt afford insurance for it.  sold to my brother, who scrapped it eventually for an Xterra.<BR><BR>\r\n\\\'78 Honda Hawk.  orange.  there are bazillion of these around the united states.  they came in orange, or in blue.  bought for 500 dollars from a friend.  that friend didnt tell me he had bent the forks, and i didnt notice till i got it running a few weeks later.  he was not my friend anymore.  i had a salvage yard bend the forks \\\'strait\\\'.  this bike was left with the keys in the ignition at my old house at 31st and Speedway in Austin, TX.  it was removed when the house was remodelled.  I didnt care.<BR><BR>\r\n\\\'00 honda civic, 4dr.  most regretted purchase ever.  bought when i got out of college because i had no money, but good credit.  never buy a new car.  but if you are going to buy a new car, buy a civic.<BR><BR>\r\n\\\'72 bmw r75/5.  paid $900.  sadly broken in the garage right now.  all this beautiful weather too, a damn shame.',3,1591,0,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(16206,1512,'2002-04-29 21:20:53',74,'jo','hi. after posting the above comment about doubting whether true connections could be made via personal ads, i met my current boyfriend when he <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2217.txt\\\">responded to my personal ad</a>. so i\\\'m a big fucking hypocrite. rah!',1,285,0,0,'172.157.66.126','en'),(16207,2534,'2002-04-29 21:23:00',286,'kalou','if she wanted to keep it private she shouldn\\\'t have broadcast it on the net. \r\nanyway nice novel guys. \r\nOver it yet? Or will you stay up all night and keep going?',0,166,0,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(16208,2686,'2002-04-29 21:53:31',168,'laney','the lie [that i met this new person i am dating through my neighbor (the story was very elaborate, too) when i in fact met him through an internet dating service] was initially meant to protect myself from embarrassment (a notion that, now, seems ridiculous). but now that i\\\'ve told it, i want to keep it going not for me, but to protect the person i told it to (my best girlfriend). i\\\'m afraid she\\\'ll be really hurt i lied to her in the first place, making up an elaborate story when i should have just told her the simple truth. i guess i\\\'m also afraid she\\\'ll think less of me for it (i certainly think less of me for it!).\r\nthe stupidest part is it\\\'s not even a harmful lie--it\\\'s a silly made-up story, that is now causing me loads of grief. ugh. but thanks for all the comments guys. you\\\'re so nice.',0,827,196,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(16209,2534,'2002-04-29 22:27:41',69,'starlajo','That comment above that made you so angry was posted about 6 weeks ago and was my last effort. I\\\'ve given up. I don\\\'t care anymore.    \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nHer long-held grudge must be more stressful than trying to be civil.  It is hard to pretend someone doesn\\\'t exist when they are so obviously in the same circle.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nYou could let your friend fight her own battles.',3,376,286,0,'210.54.242.102','en'),(16210,2696,'2002-04-29 22:45:24',260,'Layla','Damn June.',0,10,0,0,'62.64.218.58','en'),(16211,2688,'2002-04-29 22:47:41',260,'Layla','Ah! To be tall! I wish to grow and be tall!',0,43,0,0,'62.64.218.58','en'),(16212,2696,'2002-04-30 00:21:14',206,'josta','bad things happen to me every may.',3,34,0,0,'205.188.199.186','en'),(16213,2534,'2002-04-30 01:03:54',65,'le','yo, take it to livejournal',4,26,0,0,'172.137.14.84','en'),(16214,2696,'2002-04-30 01:37:13',266,'spleenless','September \\\'92 - Crashed a Ducati, lost my spleen, colapsed my lung, got impaled a little.<br><br>\r\nSeptember \\\'99 - Crashed a BMW, broke my spine, ripped the sheath of my spinal cord, spent almost the entire month in two different hospitals, had to learn to walk again<br><br>\r\nSeptember \\\'02 - I\\\'m thinking of doing a triathalon just to show this stupid fucking month what\\\'s up.',0,392,0,0,'66.92.184.86','en'),(16215,2693,'2002-04-30 02:21:36',175,'Paul G','Oh, by the way, I really enjoyed that story that you linked to.  It is odd, but it [you moving story] actually reads like something that...well...your moving story is more polished than your writing is now, or something...',0,222,66,0,'67.195.61.68','en'),(16216,2534,'2002-04-30 02:55:32',187,'kandrer','oh, yes, this reminds me something i hate about me:<br>\r\nsometimes i can\\\'t stop arguing with unfair morons. i mean: it\\\'s obvious that they don\\\'t fight by the rules, and i still can\\\'t stop trying, while i should just smile and walk away... damn! what am i doing here?! why do i keep on writing comments? see, i did it again! i\\\'m so lame! shame on me. good day to you, kalou :)',4,390,0,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(16217,2697,'2002-04-30 04:06:38',168,'laney','<p>pretty, erb.</p>\r\n<p>maybe you can find a boy who will love you and let you keep kissing 21-year-olds you meet on the bus.</p>\r\n',0,131,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(16218,2694,'2002-04-30 04:20:51',196,'jasonp','Thought about this a little more. I agree with Chuck and the others that you were dvictimized. You shouldn\\\'t go around thinking that you were just going along with it. Being that young you couldn\\\'t know any better. Even if that guy wasn\\\'t an adult yet, he should have known better not to do that by the age of 15-17.<BR>\nMaybe it wouldn\\\'t help, but I\\\'d be pissed off about it. I think it would be better to see that guy as a total creep that wronged you rather than internalize it as being your fault.',2,516,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16219,2697,'2002-04-30 04:34:27',168,'laney','sorry, that didn\\\'t come out exactly the way i wanted it to. your piece, which was lovely, also evoked a lot of sadness for me. and it seems like you should be able to avoid that kind of sadness and frustration, maybe in ways that are <a href=\\\"http://www.lovemore.com/\\\">different</a> from how people have thought about love and relationships for so long.',0,362,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(16220,2694,'2002-04-30 04:52:23',70,'Fuzzy','Thanks.  All of you so far.<br><br>Erb, I like what you wrote.  And gus... same to you.  I agree with and understand your line: \\\"When you let one out, all of the rest lose a little power.\\\"<br><br>I don\\\'t think posting this was a bad idea.  If anything the positive and supportive comments from so far have inspired me to want to talk about it with close friends.    <br><br>I am still kind of running it over in my head and thinking about what I feel about the situation.  I can\\\'t settle on anything terribly concrete.  I\\\'m not sure what I was hoping to accomplish or what I may with this, but something is better than nothing I suppose.<br><br>Once again, thanks.',1,679,0,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(16221,2686,'2002-04-30 04:55:08',196,'jasonp','Oh that doesn\\\'t seem like such a big deal at all. You didn\\\'t want to tell your girl friend how you met some guy (right?). Girls are more sensitive about this kind of thing I guess.<BR>\nIf you feel confident that you do want to level then it\\\'s just a matter of doing it the right way.<BR>\nRemember that there always a lot of ways to look at anything, even if for yourself it\\\'s only been reduced to one. If you make a big deal about it and get upset it\\\'s more likely your friend will too.<BR>\nYou could see it as being kind of funny and ridiculous and tell her how she fell for a made up story you told her. You could just be very nonchalant and tell her out of the blue like it was no big deal -- which it actually wasn\\\'t (to me at least). You could carefully explain to her your motivations and your reasons for telling the story and then tell her how you feel bad about it, etc. <BR>\nThe last way seems most appropriate for you.',0,947,168,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16222,2696,'2002-04-30 08:31:55',234,'etienne','somehow here, in the southern hemisphere, it seems to be autumn for a lot of us - specifically march and april.',2,111,0,0,'210.55.126.62','en'),(16223,2599,'2002-04-30 08:35:56',234,'etienne','the thing i like best about machines and technology is their capacity to aid in the making of art, whether its art with pretense or art without. ',1,145,0,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(16224,2696,'2002-04-30 09:01:28',240,'xep','walking home today i said outloud \\\"it smells like christmas.\\\"  that is what happens when you grow up in a place with no seasons then move to a place with the opposite lack of seasonness\r\n<p>\r\nmy dad died in april.  ever since then april has been the month to think about that.\r\n\r\n',0,286,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(16225,2599,'2002-04-30 11:19:14',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>art machines!</i></tt>',1,33,234,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16226,2697,'2002-04-30 15:08:37',123,'dcc','i love your writing erb, in this piece, specifically this line:\r\n<p><i>\\\"... to keep it fresh and warm like bread under a napkin in a basket.\\\"</i>',1,151,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(16227,2698,'2002-04-30 15:10:31',82,'simon','hi dave. i\\\'m on my way to work right now but thanks for posting this. i have thoughts, perhaps i\\\'ll email you later. take care, old pal.',1,142,0,0,'12.236.164.112','en'),(16228,2698,'2002-04-30 15:13:44',148,'jeeff','many kudos on an interesting and thought-provoking read.  i can\\\'t quite put myself in your shoes, which makes it all the more fun to read.',0,141,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(16229,2599,'2002-04-30 17:08:19',82,'simon','hey, speaking of <i>art machines</i>, the last time i was in houston i kept coming across these old cigarette vending machines that had little cigarette package sized art objects. insert $5 in quarters, pull desired knob, and kachunk, out pops a mini art piece.',1,261,66,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16230,2698,'2002-04-30 17:39:37',83,'adamajah','<p>reminds me of memoirs of a geisha or something.  gees, sad.  thanks for the correspondence.</p>',2,98,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(16231,2698,'2002-04-30 17:56:17',82,'simon','it reminds me of <a href=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/tg/stores/detail/-/books/0802134009/glance/104-1320029-4513537\\\">butterfly stories</a> or <a href=\\\"http://www.salon.com/10/sneakpeeks/sneakpeeks1.html\\\">the atlas</a>.',1,237,83,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16232,2695,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','more, please. yeti, thank you for something indescribable and beautiful.',0,72,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(16233,2599,'2002-04-30 19:46:17',66,'Peter','<br><tt>thats just <i>very</i> cool... also: texas is always doing something to surprise me and show me what a neat place it is.</tt>',1,133,82,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16234,2695,'2002-04-30 19:58:29',180,'auntie','sometimes when i am on the edge of sleep, i suddenly become three years old again, it is so weird, my body shrinks to fit my little bed and i think i see the night light and want to have a conversation with my imaginary friend, Sally.',1,234,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(16235,2599,'2002-04-30 20:27:48',82,'simon','dude, i know! everyone thought i was nuts when i kept going to visit houston. i guess a lot of it was the same reason i stayed in LA almost a year longer than i had to. there are just so many of those weird little things, you don\\\'t want to give up looking in case you miss a bunch more.',0,289,66,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16236,2698,'2002-04-30 21:34:28',59,'s5','i love this story. it beats my sydney stripper story.',0,53,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16237,2611,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>i have had a migraine  / headache for the past two days :(</p>\r\n\r\n<p>damn smokers!</p>',6,89,0,0,'12.224.49.136','en'),(16238,2696,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','dude, once in a marathon my dad got hit by a car.  watch out, you\\\'re not safe!',6,81,266,0,'12.224.49.136','en'),(16239,2699,'2002-04-30 22:22:23',83,'adamajah','<p>\\\"Wow!  It\\\'s like a Koala crapped a rainbow in my brain!\\\"<br>\r\n<br>\r\n_Sea Lab</p>',4,92,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(16240,2599,'2002-04-30 23:00:22',129,'moebius','<a href=\\\"http://www.artnet.com/magazine/reviews/fiers/fiers1-9-01.asp\\\">art machine?</a> or <a href=\\\"http://www.observer.com/pages/story.asp?ID=5452\\\">poop?</a> ',3,171,66,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(16241,2687,'2002-05-01 01:13:32',187,'kandrer','<p>\r\nto jasonp and Scapegoat:<br>\r\nas jasonp pointed out <i>It\\\'s not real indifference though in many cases, it\\\'s more a matter of pretending not to care.</i>. i was just refering to that one, specifically toward people (not money etc). and only seen from the \\\'victim\\\' point of view. so no need to be balanced, i think: it\\\'s never a \\\"good\\\" thing (except maybe for celebrities who would apreciate more indifference, even fake, toward them in a few occasions).\r\n</p><p>\r\nto egenus:<br>\r\nstill, i see paranoia as a help. well, in fact, my mind can only work in P mode, so it\\\'s my favorite answer to anything. :)\r\n</p>\r\nthanks again for coming and commenting',1,678,0,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(16242,2699,'2002-05-01 01:15:23',75,'tyler','i like the scientific theory of \\\"unexplainable complexity,\\\" where some things are just too complex to have evolved over time; for example, the extremely complicated 12-step chemical reaction required for red blood cells to transfer oxygen.',1,245,0,0,'165.247.165.31','en'),(16243,2689,'2002-05-01 02:24:26',265,'chuckspeed','email me offlist and i\\\'ll try to figure out how to get you back up and running on the cheap.  you gotta ride - good for the soul.',0,132,17,0,'172.165.60.250','en'),(16244,2696,'2002-05-01 02:31:34',265,'chuckspeed','<p>you gotta stop highsiding, man.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n  ',0,48,266,0,'172.165.60.250','en'),(16245,2694,'2002-05-01 02:42:58',278,'egenus','There is nothing more admirable than uncut honesty. And there is nothing wrong with being young and unexperienced. If people were really honest about it, most everyone has deepset sexual insecurities that they would just rather not deal with it. It\\\'s refreshing to see someone handling it so openly and conciously. Kudos.',1,324,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16246,2698,'2002-05-01 02:54:35',278,'egenus','Wow. I love how the written word can persist a moment with all it\\\'s flavor and aroma. This is the kind of posting that I\\\'ll remember, relate and hell maybe even re-read. :-0',1,179,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16247,2698,'2002-05-01 05:09:16',196,'jasonp','Quite a night. Good story.<BR>\nI saw some poonanny stunts in thailand and it seemed really strange to me because when a woman smokes a cig with her cooch, or blows out birthday candles, or pulls out a string with razor blades, or whatever... is that supposed to be sexually attractive or something? Are people supposed to be amazed by it like a magic trick? What is up with that? I guess it\\\'s mostly the curiosity and titillation factor. After twenty minutes of it I had enough and left the infamous sleazy patpong area disappointed.<BR><BR>\nWhat I would do in Korea if I were you chia:<BR><UL>\n<LI>learn some language and use it\n<LI>find a all korean hangout\n<LI>go out with a korean girl\n</UL>\nI bet you\\\'ll get a lot more out of it with the above.<BR>\nIt seems like a hard drinking, smoke like a chimney kind of place like japan is. I ended up doing a lot of drinking over there. A lot of other ex-pats were like that too.<BR>\nBTW, what are you doing over there? Doing school, teaching english?',0,1002,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16248,2699,'2002-05-01 06:18:05',187,'the french dolphin','it\\\'s no fun playing with drowned humans',1,42,0,0,'204.33.141.14','en'),(16249,2699,'2002-05-01 06:22:33',187,'kandrer','unexplainable does not imply complexity, though. euh... and the contrary is also true. :]',1,89,0,0,'204.33.141.14','en'),(16250,2701,'2002-05-01 09:25:11',196,'jasonp','This rocked.\n<BR><BR>\nI could say something more\nbut orange relief, orange and beige<BR>\n\n\nI\\\'d prefer it silent<BR>\nwith black<BR>\nand blue<BR>\nsandy expanse shoreline darkness',0,179,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16251,2701,'2002-05-01 11:24:52',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i used to really hate teh colour orange. it was not anywhere near being on my list of favourite colours. now, the tables have turned, though, and im a big fan. its got all the oomph of yellow without being as annoying; it can also be calming. i painted my <A href=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/images/bathroom/bath1.jpg\\\">bathroom</a> orange when i had this revelation.</tt>',1,383,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16252,2599,'2002-05-01 11:26:36',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i like the <a href=\\\"http://www.ide-design.ch/engneer/smkmchn/smkpg1.html\\\">smoking machine</a></tt>',1,112,129,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16253,2599,'2002-05-01 11:28:20',66,'Peter','<br><tt>that same mindset is what\\\'s kept me content to roam the streets of nyc every weekend for the pst year or so, heh. theres just so much odd stuff going on! i feel less sheepish about my banal past-time after reading your comment; thanks, heh.</tt>',1,256,82,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16254,2698,'2002-05-01 14:09:12',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\nagreed. i drink a lot because i cant smoke the ganja here...\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\ni am learning some of the language. its kinda hard. ive found an all korean hangout. they serve great drinks. soju mixed with plum or pear juice. mmmmm \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni doubt ill get a korean girlfriend. if it happens it happens. if it doesnt...not much different than living in canada. not a big loss. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas for what im doing here. im teaching english. my students range from around 9-16 years old...and i have one adult class. id guess that a couple of them are moms, and a couple of them are a couple of years out of university. its fun. its easy. \r\n</p>\r\n',2,645,0,0,'211.32.132.224','en'),(16255,656,'2002-05-01 15:28:35',74,'jo','auntie, i have a red bra that i like quite a lot and seems to be lucky for me.<BR>\r\nunderwear is so good. i develop such an attachment to my underwear and get so sad when they start breaking down in the predictable ways, like underwires breaking or holes in underpants, etc.<BR>\r\ncome to think of it, i develop an attachment to all my clothing. now i have all these clothes that don\\\'t fit the same because i\\\'ve lost so much weight and although that should sort of excite me, it also makes me depressed because, yo, i worked really hard to make those pants as soft and worn as they are.',2,591,0,0,'172.166.135.220','en'),(16256,2701,'2002-05-01 15:31:38',278,'egenus','<p>Orange and I have mutual distance boundaries, but I can angstfully indentify with loathing that \\\"easy on the eyes, easy on the mind\\\" blue color. The only worse color in all of existence is what I term \\\"government blue\\\" (the color of carpet in government offices - especially the DMV).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I love to fly. From the time you arrive at the airport to the time you land at your destination there is endless entertainment in the form of your fellow passengers. Plus flying stoned is just good, clean fun.</p>',1,523,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16257,2701,'2002-05-01 15:56:01',265,'chuckspeed','<p>reliquishing control of my life to another so completely has always given me pause. that being said - once you\\\'re there...enjoy the ride.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i was in a 44 seat saab turboprop as it passed thru what was then the worst storm of the century.  the plane was buffeted like a leaf in a spring squall - many times did free fall visit us, and many times did my face contort from several g\\\'s of force. Passengers screamed repeatedly.  blood began to seep from nail gouges made in a husband\\\'s arm.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>after fear\\\'s first blush -- an epiphany. if the plane was to go down, well...so be it.  i could not prevent the event.  soo...i sat any enjoyed the very best rollercoaster ride of my life.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>pay your money and make your choice.',0,755,0,0,'208.5.114.114','en'),(16258,2700,'2002-05-01 16:56:02',82,'simon','i feel similarly.',1,17,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16259,2701,'2002-05-24 17:02:54',86,'egg','you voiced some of the exact thoughts i have sitting up in that shit. i always think of letters that i wrote to people and never sent, and really embarassing things Ive written in my diary as a kid, and hope that noone looks meticulously through my stuff (in the event of my uncontrolled death). I also feel really anxious about projects I havent finished and my will to live in order not to have died with a half-assed creative legacy is rekindled for a moment. and then I fall asleep.',1,486,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16260,2701,'2002-05-01 17:17:20',129,'moebius','<br>s.o. synchronicity: just yesterday afternoon i was reading a long <a href=\\\"http://www.demon.co.uk/momus/thought201201.html\\\">essay</a> by the british artist/musician momus wherein he laments the dull grey blue seats of london commuter trains:\r\n<br><br>\r\n<i>Question one, about the politics of colour, occurred to me sitting in a subway train in Tokyo. We\\\'d just left Daikanyama station. I noticed that the carriages were finished in moulded green plastic. Then I glimpsed something made of pink moulded plastic flashing by on the track outside. That was my \\\'photographer\\\'s eye\\\' noticing those things. The moment was gone before I could reach my digital camera, but my satisfaction at the bold colours was, I noticed, all tied up with a hatred of the textural conservatism of 1980s British subway trains with their mottled blue upholstery. Public transport in Amsterdam, Berlin, and Tokyo uses these bright, clean, optimistic colour schemes which have to be linked somehow with politics. There must be a politics of colour and texture! London\\\'s shabby seat covers are like that because Britain\\\'s public transport system has been underfunded for decades. Public transport is a guage of a nation\\\'s, or a government\\\'s, concern for civic man. It\\\'s a barometer of a society\\\'s humanism, community-mindedness, attitudes to technology, liberalism.</i>\r\n<br><br>so, i thought that was interesting. <br><br><b>question:</b> if airplanes had less \\\"textural conservatism,\\\" as momus puts it, would they be more welcoming to ride in? would their \\\"unnatural surroundings\\\" be less offputting?\r\n\r\n\r\n',1,1632,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(16261,2700,'2002-05-01 17:35:05',83,'adamajah','<p>This is so good.  This is my favorite thing you\\\'ve ever written.</p>',1,74,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(16262,2701,'2002-05-01 18:10:40',266,'spleenless','Having had both a near death experience and a near crippling experience I end up feeling like this much of the time.  It\\\'s actually kind of distracting.  How can I waste my time sitting in a chair working on a particularly useless website when I have the ability to walk?  I should be outside ambulating my happy ass all over god\\\'s green earth.  Fuck it, I\\\'m going to go take a walk.  Later on, after I have some coffee, I\\\'m going to exercise voluntary control of my bladder and bowels and I\\\'m going to be really happy about it.',0,543,0,0,'66.92.184.86','en'),(16263,2701,'2002-05-01 19:53:37',168,'laney','that is such a great essay; thanks for posting it. i love momus. and i think, yes, absolutely, if airplane decor was bright and cheery, they would be much more pleasant to fly in. like austin powers\\\' love jet.',0,212,129,0,'64.12.103.163','en'),(16264,2700,'2002-05-01 20:02:31',168,'laney','i love it, too',0,14,0,0,'64.12.103.163','en'),(16265,2701,'2002-05-01 21:05:08',174,'Mad Moll','<p>I usually think about death in bed. I think of going to sleep and not waking up.</p>\r\n<p>Everyone knows what it\\\'s like to be dead. We were all dead for billions of years before we were born.</p>\r\n<p>I don\\\'t like death (at the same time, I equate the resisting of death with death, too). The idea of experience ending is bad. What\\\'s worse is the idea of no one taking on my projects, of no one continuing where I left off, of my initiatives and all I value ending with me.</p>\r\n\r\n',6,491,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(16266,2695,'2002-05-01 21:11:05',174,'Mad Moll','excellence',6,10,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(16267,2701,'2002-05-01 21:31:02',129,'moebius','<br><br>last summer there was a period where some potential health complications had me worrying about death a lot. and the one absurd thing i kept finding myself fussing over was: <i>what will happen to my cd collection?</i><br><br> for some reason the thought of it all being thrown away or sold back to some store was immensely depressing. i started thinking of putting together a will expressely so i could ensure that, in the event of my death, the collection would be left to friends that i know would appreciate the music.',1,529,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(16268,2701,'2002-05-01 23:22:52',88,'deb','I will be flying in a little under two months. I haven\\\'t flown in years. Not because I am afraid of flying, but because I\\\'ve had nowhere to fly.  My passport photo makes me look like a terrorist, with my pale and pasty face in a gray shirt against an ugly gray background.  My shoes will probably be searched.',4,315,0,0,'198.139.250.89','en'),(16269,2700,'2002-05-01 23:27:08',88,'deb','I have things, and sometimes I can not figure out why I won\\\'t throw them away. I\\\'m afraid to throw away magazines, because I\\\'m afraid I\\\'ll need to refer back to an article someday (silly, because I could find it in a library).  I\\\'m afraid to give away books because I\\\'m afraid I\\\'ll want to read them again.  I\\\'m afraid to throw away clothes because I\\\'m afraid I\\\'ll want to wear them again, even though they don\\\'t fit and I never will.  Some things have a shred of sentimental value.  How do you know when to get rid of those things?  Half of my life is in boxes in storage, right now, and I can\\\'t help but think how much less I would be paying per month if I could just let go ...',4,716,0,0,'198.139.250.89','en'),(16270,2700,'2002-05-02 00:08:55',186,'ook','<p>this can happen in a similar but opposite way, too. i move all the time, but so does my family. i can\\\'t leave anything with them, so i have to keep it all with me. \r\n\r\n<p>it gets more and more disorganized and irretrievable as boxes get briefly sorted and re-amalgamated. there is no time to hi-grade and pack my treasures into a solid foundation for me in between unpacking daily items and repacking them to move again.\r\n\r\n<p>also, this post made me think of pirates :)',0,476,0,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(16271,2701,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>am i the only person who doesn\\\'t care what is left behind?  when i am in planes and there is turbulence enough to make me fear for my life all i can think of is s5\\\'s tear-stained face, which leads me to think of loss, which leads me to imagine what it would be like to lose him, which leads me to almost choke with sobs next to the aforementioned prada-shoed woman.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i don\\\'t believe there is any dishonour in death, amongst any kind of upholstry or fancily-shod people.  all material will fade to irrelevancy.  all i can see is beauty: the intensity,  the desperate and determined grab for life:  yes, this is what we are programmed for.  survive.  survive.  survive.</p>',6,692,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16272,2700,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>it made me think of pirates too.  ARRRRRRRRRt.</p>\r\n<p>i never miss the things i leave behind.</p>',6,101,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16273,2672,'2002-05-02 01:16:06',291,'da3bous','<p>ummm I hate to inform you my dear darling sister that the Scorpion King in fact is possibly the First of Egyptian Pharoahs.  They found while excavating his tomb that he possibly started many distinct features of Pharoanic burial.  He was the first with the sheperds crook, his tomb was a buried primitive step pyramid and much like other Pharoahs he was buried with his earthly possesions in anticipation of a boring after life. SO in that note NEINER NEINER<p/>',6,466,0,0,'63.230.172.204','en'),(16274,2700,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','\r\ni\\\'ve left lots of things behind. the only things i miss are the people, and sweaters. yep.',1,95,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(16275,2678,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','\r\nthe 5 second rule doesn\\\'t count in paris, land of too many dog poops on the ground.',0,88,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(16276,1028,'2002-05-02 04:59:57',65,'le invoking rollins','I poured salt on a large slug. The slug writhed and squirmed. The slug tried to escape me and my burning salt. The slug made no sound. I\\\'m sure if I was turned inside-out and dipped in salt, I would scream. I remember how the slug glistened and respirated until I put the salt on it. I remember how it tried to get away, secreting yellow-green mucus in great quantities that bubbled slightly. My fascination turned into revulsion as the slug writhed and tossed from side to side, secreting even more yellow-green mucus to try and beat the salt. It was a losing battle for the slug, because when the slug had succeeded in rubbing off some of the salt, I would simply turn the salt shaker over on the slug and the game would start again. Eventually I got bored and left the slug, still writhing, trying in vain to get free of the salt bath that would eventually suck the slug dry. Later I imagined that my whole body was a tongue, and I was dipped in salt.\r\n',4,959,0,0,'165.247.211.116','en'),(16277,111,'2002-05-02 05:08:23',65,'le','that wasn\\\'t really kibo was it?',6,34,0,0,'165.247.211.116','en'),(16278,2703,'2002-05-02 06:01:58',168,'laney','<img src=\\\"http://www.banchory.net/belleandsebastian/waking.html\\\" height=100>\r\n\r\nmmmmm.',0,92,0,0,'152.163.197.192','en'),(16279,2703,'2002-05-02 06:06:15',168,'laney','<img src=\\\"http://www.banchory.net/belleandsebastian/photos/waking3.jpg\\\" height=200> sorry for the fake-out!',0,113,0,0,'152.163.197.192','en'),(16280,2702,'2002-05-02 06:47:16',75,'tyler','well, i mean, what do you want to do in life? if your move will serve your goals, i say go for it. if your goals are undefined, why take the risk? it sounds like your true destination is (cliche)within yourself(/cliche), your direction in life.',1,244,0,0,'165.247.165.31','en'),(16281,2702,'2002-05-02 13:51:59',265,'chuckspeed','<p>the trick is to move to, rather than away, from an objective.  if you move away from NC simply to get away - you\\\'ll most likely find dissatisfaction.  i have relatives who have no idea what they want, yet are damned certain about what they don\\\'t want.  They\\\'re never happy.</p>\r\n\r\ntyler\\\'s right.  what you\\\'re looking for is inside.  a change of scenery might help get you there, but it\\\'s not the consumnate answer.\r\n',0,437,0,0,'208.5.114.117','en'),(16282,2704,'2002-05-02 14:38:16',66,'Peter','<br><tt>sometimes, all it takes is a short bridge to span a deep chasm...</tt>',1,78,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16283,2704,'2002-05-02 14:51:17',265,'chuckspeed','thanks.',0,7,66,0,'208.5.114.86','en'),(16284,2702,'2002-05-02 14:57:04',66,'Peter','<br><tt>you said: <i>\"the Internet itself is my residency and anywhere I \"live\" is just a place to hang my carcass\"</i><br><br>maybe you\'d be happier if you dug deeper into your physical existence some? just a thought. <i>best friendly</i>.</tt>',1,242,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16285,1028,'2002-05-24 17:02:54',86,'egg','finally, a comment.',0,19,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16286,2672,'2002-05-24 17:02:54',86,'egg','who are you talking to, my dear darling?',0,40,291,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16287,156,'2002-05-02 17:09:41',17,'austin','i still think this is one of the greatest posts of all time.  i wish i still knew adam lamas.  he is a funny guy.  <a href=\\\"http://riddleinthedark.com\\\">he has a website.</a>',1,179,0,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(16288,1099,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','our weather is better. plain and simple. there are lots of liberal crazies on the east coast. ever been to maine or vermont? but still, they seem a little more intense, a little more chapped than the rest of us. we loll around like fat grasshoppers, sneering at the hard working east coast ant. we eat fruit off the trees and oil our bodies. none of that old fangled new england conservatism, of making do or mending things. chopping wood in freezing weather. caning peaches, shovelling snow, stacking wood. living without moisturizer and in snoods.\r\n<br><br>\r\nfuck that. pass the avocado mango shake.',1,601,86,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(16289,156,'2002-05-02 18:26:55',82,'simon','fuck, yeah, i read this one every once and a while and crack up',0,63,17,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16290,2678,'2002-05-02 18:58:16',187,'kandrer','it\\\'s not only in paris, baby, it\\\'s in every fucking town where at least one person has a dog.<br>\r\ni went with a co-worker one day during lunch to take his dog out. the dog pooped an orange and green shit. the guy started laughing nervously and say: <i>since i feed her with these colored dog food, she always does that way... look around!</i> i looked around: the parking we were in was spotted with a dozen orange and green presents. the guy added: <i>i hope it will rain soon...</i></p><p>\r\ni have a bunch of other <i>crusty</i> dog shit stories, feel free to ask for more ;)...',1,587,57,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(16291,1099,'2002-05-02 19:25:05',66,'Peter','<br><tt>...and we all know that the west coast is flowing over with straight-laced conservatives ;)<br><br><i>[insert passionately defensive rant about the east coast here, etc etc, blah blah...]</i><br><br>weeb: actually, youve mentioned these supposed east coast types on here (or maybe somewhere else online? i forget) more than once before. did you have some specifically bad experience with this or something? ive yet to encounter these people around here... but maybe i need to get out of manhattan more!</tt>',1,270,57,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16292,2702,'2002-05-02 19:35:13',278,'egenus','I never said I had no direction. I just feel like I\\\'m taking the extremely long way to get where I want to go. This posting was more about situations getting beyond subjective control than anything else. And peter, as to my physical existence, let\\\'s just say the physical existence is the room where all the cereberal fun is had.',1,335,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16293,1099,'2002-05-02 20:23:54',74,'jo','how about neither coast? how about west coasters being too loopy from either too much sun or too much rain and east coasts being too snotty? how about that?<BR>\r\n[x] angry detractor, <b>so</b> fucking annoyed by this debate.',0,224,0,0,'172.150.204.4','en'),(16294,1099,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','wow. hi jo. and peter. i don\'t think any of us is taking this \"debate\" seriously. everyone chill, okay? i grew up in nyc and went to college in massachusetts and i loved it. i admire that stupid new england work ethic, okay? for goodness sakes, i thought this was all tongue in cheek. ',1,408,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(16295,1099,'2002-05-02 20:56:14',59,'s5','though it\\\'s pretty safe to say that manhattan is a completely different world than \\\"the east coast\\\". NOT THAT I\\\'M TAKING ANY SIDE IN THIS IMPORTANT DEBATE OR ANYTHING LIKE THAT.',6,189,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16296,1099,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','*drags s5 into the moshpit*',1,27,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(16297,1099,'2002-05-24 17:02:54',86,'egg','I think after all this we can all now agree that east coast people are just naturally SMARTER than west coast people. but east coast people who move to the west coast are just WICKEdly glamorous darling, to reject such a pedigree for sex games and quality marijuana.',0,266,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16298,1099,'2002-05-02 22:13:35',82,'simon','<p>\r\ndorks.<br>\r\nalso, 4-1-5 represent.',0,39,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16299,1099,'2002-05-02 22:15:29',74,'jo','by \\\"this debate,\\\" i didn\\\'t mean \\\"this one on swinney,\\\" i meant that i\\\'m really tired with hearing east coast vs. west coast being a subject of debate, period, ever. it\\\'s so tired. and ultimately? the reason that places are different is that <b>people are different</b> (SHOCK!) and shouldn\\\'t we just be grateful that there are perfect cities and towns for all of us and weeb will always be happy in sf and peter loves nyc and yippity-do?<BR>\r\ni\\\'m not really mad, i just find it hard to believe that people get worked up over something so arbitrary and subjective.',3,590,0,0,'172.162.237.80','en'),(16300,1099,'2002-05-02 22:23:53',66,'Peter','<br><tt>can i get a yes for big daddy kane? big daddy over nwa anyday, you wizZety west coast beeeeeeeeyatches!<br><br><i>[yes, tongue in cheek. btw: upstate new york is more redneck and backwards than alabammy, fyi. it is scaryville ;)]</i></tt>',1,246,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16301,1099,'2002-05-02 22:29:56',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i wasnt really serious; i hereby withdrawl from this, heh. i just get defensive cause my city has a notoriously bad name (for alot of valid reasons). it has such a sweet side, though, and it took me several years to discover this. i wish i could show it to each and every one of you. so i get all ruffled when peopel from far away say things that im either ignorant of or dont agree with (im stubborn like that).<br><br>i copped the same \\\'tude about my hometown when i lived in tennessee. and brooklyn. its all about having pride and stuff, no matter where you live. hell, id do it even if i lived in south dakota :D</tt>',1,632,74,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16302,2700,'2002-05-02 23:00:59',122,'hool','<p>i am in the process of moving.  i am tired. i am unshowered.  i moved from ottawa to toronto. i\\\'m sitting at my new roommate\\\'s computer.  i just finished setting up my computer in my room.  still much to do. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> i dumped a ton of stuff, without mercy, while packing up in ottawa.  i threw out so much.  i gave what i could to the salvation army.  then it came down to crunch time, and i had to keep throwing things out into garbage bag after garbage bag.  i felt shameful of the waste i created. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> i am now living in a very tiny room.  there isn\\\'t much space for superfluous possessions.  i am glad that this new living space will not allow for collection of more stuff.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> when i was packing up a few days ago in ottawa, i found in a hallway walk-in closet a little trap door with a hook latch behind a flattened cardboard box that was up against the wall.  i opened it.  i had totally forgot that it existed.  there was a bunch of pink insulation stuck on the back of the door.  i looked in, and saw that it lead to a bunch of empty space beneath the eaves of the roof (i lived on the top floor).  such sweet relief.  i saw that there was assorted junk in there from previous tenants who probably found themselves in the same position as me - only a few hours until moving time, things you can\\\'t take with you piling up - the types of things you can\\\'t really throw into a garbage bag.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> so it became my black hole, my everything bag. i remember reading \\\'choose your own adventure\\\' books when i was young, and one of the best items you could find during your adventures was the bottomless bag.  it served as a gateway to some type of infinite closet universe, so you could throw whatever you like in the bag, without limit.  you could also reach in and just think of the item, and voila, there it would appear in your hand as you withdrew it from the bag\\\'s mouth.  of course, the things i threw in through that trap door i won\\\'t ever retrieve, but i felt better throwing them in there , instead of into a finite garbage bag.  a slightly warped basketball and volleyball, some miscellaneous sweaters, some paint brushes, random cutlery, a few books, among many other things.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p> i threw them far inside the door, and couldn\\\'t see through the blackness beyond a few feet.  the space stretched far along either side.. i was able to throw things through the door to either side and hear only a distance-muffled thud.  i thought about the time that would come when someone would be cleaning all that stuff out, and what they would think, if anything, as they picked up each disowned object. <i> this used to belong to somebody</i>.</p>',0,2708,0,0,'64.229.167.114','en'),(16303,2701,'2002-05-03 02:03:00',124,'gus','I dont usually think about dying. Once in a while I get on a bus, or go for a walk, or get in a car, or on a plane and in transit, I cant picture the next day. I think, \\\"hm, maybe I wont be alive tomorrow.\\\" and then I get kinda nervous and wait anxiously to see if Im still alive when tomorrow comes. so far, I still am.\r\n<p>\r\nMore often my fears are like o\\\'s, I fear my house is burning down when Im not there, and my cats will not be saved. Or I fear that the screeching crash outside was a friend coming to meet me. I think that if someone doesnt call or show up when Im ready for them to, that maybe they\\\'ve been hurt or killed. \r\n<p>\r\nI can leave it all behind like I did when I was traveling, throw up my hands and leave it to fate -OR- I can grope for the lives I want around me and try to keep them all in my fists at the same time. I dont usually hang out in the middle ground.',0,898,0,0,'64.169.160.194','en'),(16304,2700,'2002-05-03 02:11:49',124,'gus','<i>Jesus at 19%</i>\r\n<p>\r\nMy mom wants me to clean my saved in boxes stuff and throw some away. I have given her clear orders that the legos and barbies are not to be trashed. When I am old, I will have lots of time to build lego towns and brush barbie hair.',0,258,0,0,'64.169.160.194','en'),(16305,2704,'2002-05-03 02:17:20',124,'gus','I like your stuff chuck.',0,24,0,0,'64.169.160.194','en'),(16306,2697,'2002-05-03 03:12:30',187,'kandrer','dear erb,<br>\r\nplease kiss anybody you feel like kissing, but quit mixing champagne and orange juice (except if the champagne is really really bad). :]',3,151,0,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(16307,2697,'2002-05-03 03:13:35',187,'kandrer','i loved the analogy with the restaurant',1,39,0,0,'208.223.155.32','en'),(16308,2708,'2002-05-03 03:32:13',174,'Mad Moll','<p><b><u>THIS IS THE STUPIDEST FUCKING PEOM I HAVE EVER SEEN IN MY ENTIRE LIFE!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!</p>\r\n<p>WHO THE FUCK USES DIAPHRAGM IN A POEM!@!!@!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!! ELLY, YOU TOTALLY SUCK MAN!!!!! GET A LIFE@!!!!!!!!!! HOLY FUCK!!!!!</p></u></b>',6,251,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(16309,2708,'2002-05-03 03:44:43',65,'le','<tt><b>:(</b></tt>',4,36,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(16310,2694,'2002-05-03 04:09:14',70,'Fuzzy','Interesting (and relevant) reading material: <a href=\\\"http://www.eye.net/eye/issue/issue_05.02.02/columns/feelings.html\\\">in this article</a>.',1,147,0,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(16311,2708,'2002-05-03 04:30:44',196,'jasonp','That wasn\\\'t very nice...<BR>\nDid the disclaimer somehow provoke that?<BR>\nOr was it just gettin all ironical and shit?<BR>\nPoems can be either well-crafted creations, abstract expressions of internal realities, or both if you\\\'re lucky or good.\n',0,250,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16312,2703,'2002-05-03 05:24:57',74,'jo','hey, laney, the b & s show that i saw tuesday night was one of the best shows i\\\'ve seen, maybe ever. they were so awe-inspiring. i\\\'m glad you had a good time at yours, too.',1,178,0,0,'172.157.32.194','en'),(16313,2709,'2002-05-03 06:23:01',17,'austin','<P>it is alright, go ahead, say it:</P>\r\n<P><B>\\\"ya\\\'ll\\\"</B></P>',3,71,0,0,'64.174.155.18','en'),(16314,2709,'2002-05-03 06:30:56',234,'etienne','i-i-i- just *couldn\\\'t*',1,25,17,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(16315,2708,'2002-05-03 08:17:33',174,'Mad Moll','<p>Or, in this case, <u><b><i>THEY CAN BE PURE HORSECRAP!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!! I WILL CALL YOU \\\"JAP SON\\\" FROM NOW ON BECAUSE YOU ARE SO FOOLISH!!!! IT IS AN ANAGRAM DO YOU KNOW WHAT THAT IS????????????????????? STFU!!!!</i></b></u>',6,233,196,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(16316,2708,'2002-05-03 08:23:22',175,'Paul G','At first I thought that this dealt with the idea of bringing a thing into being by naming it, e.g. \\\"God said let there be...\\\"  But after more thought it seems to be just the opposite.  That is to say that the name given to something - or perhaps here it is better to say: someone - defines and limits that thing, or person.  Words are, on some occasions, insufficient or inappropriate?\r\n\r\n<p>In short: Some things, or some people, are shown respect and care when they are not named?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Is this close, le?</p>',0,521,0,0,'67.248.113.236','en'),(16317,2704,'2002-05-03 13:31:57',265,'chuckspeed','<p>thanks.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\'raison\\\' is the result of exploring the backroads of OK, finishing up with a beautiful drive across the eastern part of OK\\\'s Route 66.  Had all the windows down and the sunroof open, listening to Natalie Cole reprise her father\\\'s songs.  Dawned on me that achivements occur primarly in retrospective, and that they can be generational. crossed a bridge in a little town, and the story dropped out.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>typically, i let the story rattle around in my head for a while - sometimes they pop out the same day; others take years to complete.  There are 3-4 stories in there right now - just not ready for the light of day.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the compliments mean quite a bit, actually - i\\\'m in awe of the level of talent residing here.  just finished reading heather\\\'s stuff (heathen) and all i could think was...i\\\'m not worthy - i\\\'m not worthy.  the same can be said for a dozen other regulars.</p>',0,935,124,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(16318,2708,'2002-05-03 13:34:00',65,'le','moll is from new jersey.\r\n<br><br>\r\nalso, art is a lie.\r\n',4,57,0,0,'209.86.5.114','en'),(16319,2708,'2002-05-03 13:42:22',265,'chuckspeed','hmm...\r\n\r\nthink this was pretty good on account of the work elicited its intended response.  the pre-dissing wasn\\\'t really needed, tho.',0,138,0,0,'208.5.114.108','en'),(16320,2708,'2002-05-03 14:17:42',66,'Peter','<br><tt>jersey rocks. this weekend, i will drive down rt. 22 and eat at the Lido Diner.<br><br><img src=\"http://www.rhodamine.org/futurecity/rt22.jpg\"></tt>',1,163,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16321,2708,'2002-05-03 14:50:29',265,'chuckspeed','jersey can be summed up via a single movie - \\\'clerks\\\'. ',0,61,0,0,'208.5.114.85','en'),(16322,2708,'2002-05-03 15:37:49',66,'Peter','<br><tt>oh but there is <i>so</i> much more to jersey; i was surprised myself when i discovered this. jersey is a uniquely wonderful place.</tt>',1,144,265,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16323,2708,'2002-05-03 15:40:25',17,'austin','Elizibutt Milkcan is a lie.',4,27,65,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(16324,2708,'2002-05-03 15:51:52',74,'jo','haha. milkcan. milkcan.',0,23,0,0,'172.150.25.176','en'),(16325,2708,'2002-05-24 17:02:54',86,'egg','i left my heart in jersey.',0,26,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16326,2708,'2002-05-03 16:06:59',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ever see that movie <i>Happiness</i> ? i love the tshirt that vlad has that reads: i [heart] NJ. hahahaha.</tt>',1,119,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16327,2708,'2002-05-24 17:02:54',86,'egg','and I dont see what all this weird negative joking is about. I think this is pretty. you know, maybe I should go to omega, because Im feeling like being optimistic and loving lately. call me a hippy. every couple of years my system gets filled up with irony, like a sink getting clogged with hair, and I have to open up the pipes and scrape all the hair out. or get some drano.',0,377,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16328,2704,'2002-05-03 16:10:02',17,'austin','<P>dude, heathen rules the school.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>so do you chuck.  this story sucked me in.  stories about architects capture the imagination.  the nature of the architect\\\'s life becomes permanent in the structures created by the person lending a timeless fashion like pyramids and public buildings and monuments.</P>',3,316,265,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(16329,2708,'2002-05-03 16:14:51',66,'Peter','<br><tt>omega folks: i know le is in, and if there are any others of you, do feel free to use my car and/or place to facilitate your nyc-airport-to-omega journey. yay for swinney adventures.</tt>',1,195,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16330,2704,'2002-05-03 16:52:35',265,'chuckspeed','<p>aww, shucks...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>*blush*</p>\r\n\r\n<p>now i can\\\'t leave my office on account of my head won\\\'t fit thru the door.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>seriously...you become what you do, whether you like it or not.  Best thing is to allow your avocation to drive your vocation.  when i was a kid, i wanted to become a professional speller.  later, i wanted to design either chassis or powertrains for ford or chrysler.  i\\\'m continuously driven by a need to create, albeit with dimensional tolerances - an artist trapped in an engineer\\\'s body, or vice versa.  i dunno which. time will tell.</p>  ',0,587,17,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(16331,2709,'2002-05-03 18:30:20',262,'szajic','Also we could play the Pulsars, and the Pixies.',3,47,0,0,'18.56.0.59','en'),(16332,2709,'2002-05-03 20:36:33',82,'simon','what about p. diddy and prince?',0,31,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16333,2709,'2002-05-03 21:29:22',129,'moebius','<br>i vote for inclusion of parliament/funkadelic.',2,50,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(16334,2703,'2002-05-03 22:19:18',168,'laney','yay!! i am so happy i finally got to see them, and you\\\'re right, awe-inspiring is the perfect word. watching them it seemed as though they just had it all figured out. like get together with a bunch of good people and make good music and happiness will come. :)',0,264,74,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(16335,2712,'2002-05-24 17:02:54',86,'egg','i at i am a mai tai  -jay',0,25,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16336,2712,'2003-03-05 23:09:34',129,'moebius','Di, Al, Togo, BÃ¶ll, Edna, Todd, Adolf, Sir Obadiah T