>',3,242,0,0,'210.86.106.107','en'),(16341,2712,'2002-05-04 07:57:50',175,'Paul G','I wonder why the guy who did the tile work quartered and nipped it around that thing in the floor.',0,98,0,0,'67.248.113.128','en'),(16342,2708,'2002-05-04 09:28:50',174,'Mad Moll','<p><b><i><u>WHAT DIDNER DID YOU GO TO DUDE????????? FELIX 9? RED TOWER III?? SUNSET?????? CRYSTAL QUEEN??????!!!!! I G0T LOST ON YM WAY TO SOMERVILLE OFF 22 2NIGHT!!!!!! IT ZUXED!!!! WE ERE LOOKING FOR A KAFE CALLED \\\"\\\"\\\"SANCTUARY\\\"\\\"\\\"</u></i></b></p>\r\n<p><b><i><u>P.S. ELLY ZUXXXXX!!!!!!!!!</u></i></b></p>',6,321,66,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(16343,2708,'2002-05-04 10:13:49',65,'le','i feel loving and optimistic too. i was feeling like a loser the other day because i haven\\\'t Accomplished anything Great, but then a dear friend reminded me the only thing i\\\'m supposed to be doing is making other people feel good. oh ok, i can do that. whew. i am happy a lot. tho slammed with all kinds of shit to do before i leave town.',1,344,17,0,'165.247.221.211','en'),(16344,2708,'2002-05-04 17:17:07',66,'Peter','<br><tt>the lido. i miss, however, the edison family restaurant on oaktree road. sigh!<br><br><img src=\"http://www.rhodamine.org/images/breakfast.jpg\"></tt>',1,91,174,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16345,2708,'2002-05-04 19:46:42',180,'auntie','new jersey sucks. many people from new jersey come to florida and bitch the whole time about how things aren\\\'t like they were at home and how they should be and i say, if new jersey was so fucking great, why the fuck aren\\\'t you still living there?',6,253,174,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(16346,2708,'2002-05-04 19:48:36',180,'auntie','without discussing the technique of poetry, about which i know absolutely nothing at all, i would say that i understand completely the idea behind your words. not naming those things that are most important may be genetic, but more likely, something that most people can relate to.',1,281,65,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(16347,2712,'2002-05-04 20:09:32',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\nso i says to he guys. \\\'gumo samga ka jusayoh\\\' and so like we go on our merry way riding around. he lets us out...like five minutes later. we get drinks. drinks cost lots. we walk maybe one block to find out that the taxi just drove us to the other side of where we were. that fucker. yeah latin this motherfuckers!!! thats all im sayin. you cant trust at least 4% of all taxi drivers in any country of the world. how i got 4% is none of your god damn business tho. beer costs lots when the bar looks like is from ikea....but we found this place with an egyptian theme...and it WAS over the top...but for seven of us....three pitchers of soju and one pitcher of beer only cost 5000won each.....at \\\'rock and roll\\\' bar one bottle of beer was 7000 won. fuckin rip...i mean it was four in the morning...and im pretty sure we got the \\\'white person\\\' menu....but yeah...taxi drivers cant be trusted. ',6,915,0,0,'61.37.109.20','en'),(16348,2708,'2002-05-04 20:16:09',93,'chiamattt','you remind me of a racist. someone who cant understand that people are different, and for hat reason you decide to just \\\'hate\\\' and \\\'poke fun\\\'. if there is a book that tells you what words dont belong in poetry, please footnote it. id be interested in reading it. however for the time being id like to say that you are perhaps the rudest person on swinney right now and ive bozo listed you. i like elly, and i like swinney.org, but like most good things its a minority of ignorant and selfish people that decide to ruin it for the collective. you represent why socialism will never work, and for this reason i hope you die pol pot style. a swift swing of a garden hoe to the back of the head. ',6,704,174,0,'61.37.109.20','en'),(16349,2712,'2002-05-05 01:29:36',153,'yhz','simon_fk I like this photograph a lot. The fallen polaroid in the corner makes the scene.',0,89,0,0,'64.115.25.31','en'),(16350,2708,'2002-05-05 04:57:52',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i had to giggle when you preached tolerance and then reccomended swift death in the same paragraph ;)</i></tt>',1,114,93,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16351,2708,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>seen on a bathroom wall in australia, related by s5, appropriate here:</p>\r\n\r\n<p><font face=\\\"20\\\" color=\\\"#FF0000\\\">SHOW SOME COMPASSION OR I\\\'LL KILL YOU</font></p>',6,179,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16352,2714,'2002-05-05 06:11:23',74,'jo','RIGHT. ON.',1,10,0,0,'172.139.108.61','en'),(16353,2714,'2002-05-05 06:14:57',59,'s5','i\\\'m one of those people who flash their brights in order to make you move to another lane. sorry about that.',4,111,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16354,2706,'2002-05-05 06:23:07',175,'Paul G','<p>1) Another great piece, Quinn;<br>\r\n2) Women are better company;<br>\r\n3) Life is about the small things;<br>\r\n4) Ouch.</p>',0,125,0,0,'67.24.10.44','en'),(16355,2714,'2002-05-05 06:50:47',72,'essemar','[<i>sigh....</i>] I used to be one of those people that are non-equiped with a filter between brain and mouth.  Then I read <i>How to Win Friends and Influence People</i> and learned to like the improved reactions I get when I know when to keep my mouth shut.  Pity, I miss the obnoxious asshole I once was.',4,307,0,0,'129.44.41.65','en'),(16356,2708,'2002-05-05 07:48:50',65,'le','moll actually was the inspiration for this piece, so there. that\\\'s the truth. he is beyond reproach.',1,103,0,0,'165.247.220.247','en'),(16357,2714,'2002-05-05 16:06:34',74,'jo','i read how to win friends and influence people when i was 13. the only thing i remember from it was to use people\\\'s names as often as possible when speaking to them, because there is no sweeter sound to anyone\\\'s ears than his own name. they did all this research and found out that people were so much more receptive to ideas if you used their name: \\\"it\\\'s so good to see you, <i>joanna</i>,\\\" vs. \\\"it\\\'s so good to see you.\\\" i don\\\'t know how true that is, but i still use first names a lot when talking with people.<BR>\r\nalso, i call people by their full given names a lot when i\\\'m 1) frustrated with them or 2) want their attention, because i think it might call up memories of their parents. tricky.<BR>\r\nalso, that spider-man movie was great.',1,773,0,0,'172.153.39.174','en'),(16358,2707,'2002-05-05 16:09:15',74,'jo','kandrer, why do you translate the letter \\\"j\\\" as \\\"t\\\" twice here?',2,75,0,0,'172.153.39.174','en'),(16359,2706,'2002-05-05 16:25:20',124,'gus','being a woman, I\\\'ve always been more entertained on the men\\\'s side of the room. Of course... I\\\'ve always thought I\\\'d make a great Lady Macbeth... ',0,158,0,0,'64.169.160.194','en'),(16360,2714,'2002-05-05 18:34:54',66,'Peter','<br><tt>im a light-flasher too, and proud. i think that the people who drive slowly in the passing lane are the real assholes. these types usually think the rules dont apply to them, and thus they deserve the flash ;)</tt>',1,218,59,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16361,2714,'2002-05-05 19:55:16',59,'s5','i\\\'ve never read \\\'how to win friends ...\\\' but it strikes me as an instruction manual for how to manipulate your friends and get away with it. or is it a well intentioned guide to \\\'being nice\\\', with only a side order of machiavelli?',4,245,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16362,2714,'2002-05-05 20:11:13',74,'jo','it\\\'s a well-intentioned guide. i wouldn\\\'t even say that there\\\'s a macchiavelli nature. <u>the prince</u> addresses the fact that people in power are very often weak and insecure and easy to take advantage of; carnegie addresses that people want to be <i>listened to</i>, are instinctively suspicious of being manipulated, and respect intelligence and sensitivity over brute power. one might even say that it\\\'s anti-machiavellian: the message is how to fulfill people\\\'s (particularly clients\\\') needs also getting what you want, not just tricking them into sacrificing their needs for yours.<BR>\r\ni think. i haven\\\'t read either in ten years so i\\\'m relying on my 14-year-old understanding of the texts.',3,723,59,0,'172.146.53.66','en'),(16363,2707,'2002-05-05 22:13:35',187,'kandrer','t stands for toy; i am not sure i should have given the french version here, except for it was what i wrote first. to tell u the truth, i am still working on what comes next after that and it seems long and boring. so i can\\\'t say what was a good idea in all this series... :/',1,278,74,0,'205.184.183.134','en'),(16364,2708,'2002-05-05 22:41:18',187,'kandrer','<u>Peter/chiamattt:</u> i think it perfectly makes sense. i was thinking recently about writing an excuse letter that would finish with threats. letters are sometimes too slow for communication, so you might want to anticipate the possible reader\\\'s reactions and answer to some of them. or maybe we lost too much of our ancesters spontaneity: nothing\\\'s better than a good face-to-face talk with a knife in your hand. he he he.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<u>o/s5:</u> this demonstrates again the buddhist philosophy; bathroom are for sure a part of the big truth.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<u>le/all:</u> without the <<ironical>> <</ironical>> tag, i am unable to be sure of who\\\'s saying what. usually, i don\\\'t comment in these cases, to hide my stupidity. but here i liked the poem. thanks.\r\n</p>',1,775,0,0,'205.184.183.134','en'),(16365,2708,'2002-05-05 22:43:38',187,'kandrer','i meant ironical and /ironical tags; how do one insert this damn inferior/superior characters in html? i try backslash here: \\\\<',1,130,0,0,'205.184.183.134','en'),(16366,2708,'2002-05-06 03:07:39',196,'jasonp','Don\\\'t be misled by my use \\\"ironical\\\", it\\\'s either \\\"irony\\\", \\\"ironic\\\" or \\\"ironically\\\". Same goes for the sentence ender \\\"and shit\\\". Using \\\"and shit\\\" along with some other filler words, \\\"like\\\" and \\\"all\\\" can make your english sound more natural to tenth-graders, but is not proper.<BR>\nFor example, <BR>\nNo: \\\"I\\\'m like, all hungry and shit\\\"<BR>\nYes: \\\"I\\\'m hungry\\\"<BR>\nAnother thing, the symbols \\\'&lt;\\\' and \\\'&gt;\\\' are referred to as \\\'greater than\\\' and \\\'lesser than\\\'.<BR>\nJust some friendly english help.',0,592,187,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16367,2716,'2002-05-06 03:24:55',174,'Mad Moll','What do you make?',6,17,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(16368,2714,'2002-05-06 03:41:24',196,'jasonp','s5 and Peter, I can\\\'t believe you guys are admitting to being highbeam flashers. I never would have thought. I won\\\'t hold it against you though. Just don\\\'t do it to me.<BR>\nI find that, along with tailgating to be really infuriating. That\\\'s mostly because I don\\\'t drive slowly in the fast lane. When I drive in the fast lane it\\\'s normally 80mph, 15mph over the posted speed limit, and at least fast enough for the \\\"real-world\\\" fast lane speed I\\\'d say. If someone wants to be Mario Andretti and go 90+, fine no problem, they can just change lanes around me then. That\\\'s what I would do if I want to go fast.<BR>\nIf someone flashes his beams to get me to move I do what I always do in that situation... nothing at all.  I don\\\'t speed up or change lanes. If they continue to flash I\\\'ll slow down. If they yell shit or flip me off as they pass me I\\\'ll respond with the same.  That is my numero uno driving pet peeve. It is so rude. Would you walk down the sidewalk poking people in the back to get them out of your way? Why is this acceptable on the freeway then?<BR>\nThis doesn\\\'t happen that often though since I\\\'m not a slow driver. There is an exception also. On two-lane freeways you should stay out of the left side unless you\\\'re passing.\n',0,1288,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16369,2716,'2002-05-06 04:31:49',295,'karenlee','<p>I write, I hammer, I chisel, I file, I sew, I drill, I measure, I saw, I draw, I look, I cook, I burn. I make many things. You have many aliases...do you make enemies?</p>\r\n',1,176,0,0,'203.221.9.244','en'),(16370,2714,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','s5 only flashes if they\\\'re meant to be on the left.  he is a rad driver.  if he ever gets into some kind of situation with another driver, he puts on a clown nose and stares them down.',6,187,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16371,2710,'2002-05-06 05:16:26',75,'tyler','you are not a bad person. it sounds to me like you are a good person. roll with the punches.',1,92,0,0,'165.247.164.62','en'),(16372,2714,'2002-05-06 05:35:18',175,'Paul G','<p>Machiavelli: \\\"For men, universally, judge more by the eyes than by the hands, because it is given to everyone to see, but to few that they can touch\\\" (<i>Prince</i>, Chapter XVIII, paragraph 7).<br>\r\n\r\nXenophon: \\\"Now tyranny displays openly, evident for all to see, the possessions which are held to be of much value\\\" (<i>Hiero</i>, 2.3-4).</p>',0,359,0,0,'67.248.113.243','en'),(16373,2714,'2002-05-06 06:13:47',59,'s5','i don\\\'t understand what those quotes are supposed to mean. also i don\\\'t understand why it\\\'s always \\\"men this\\\" and \\\"men that\\\".',5,146,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16374,2714,'2002-05-06 06:40:21',175,'Paul G','It seemed wrong to say too much in a post that was not mine, or about theory.  But it also seemed wrong to say nothing.  And so I hoped that the quotes alone would suffice.  If Machiavelli taught something about the utility of maintaining a particular appearance, then he also, in so doing, taught his readers to be suspicious of appearances.  And the more suspicious people/men/etc are of appearances, the less useful those appearances are to maintain.    ',0,457,0,0,'67.248.113.243','en'),(16375,2716,'2002-05-06 06:42:57',174,'Quong Moll','I make only graves for the unrighteous.',6,39,295,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(16376,2714,'2002-05-06 11:16:38',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i never was a light-flasher until i made the 900+-miles-each-way round-trip drive between nyc and my hometown something like 34 times; as my travels often take me through virginia (and also due to my dad getting an overnight stay in a cell-block in that state due to his radar detector) i have avoided that guilty pleasure and opted instead for light-flashing. i dont do it often, and just to the people that insist on driving 45 mph in the far left lane of a 4 lane hwy. yuck. i grew up in a state that had \\\"assume a responsible speed\\\" speed limit signs, thus the logic for driving so slow in the passing lane baffles me. so i flash. <i>flash flash</i>.',1,668,196,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16377,2714,'2002-05-06 11:18:34',66,'Peter','<br><tt>although, i am now quite tempted to don a clown nose. to date, my best way to intimidate annoying drivers in traffic stare-downs is via gratuitously dramatic nose-picking; this is especially effective if done with both hands at the same time, while squinting.</tt>',1,272,62,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16378,2714,'2002-05-06 11:21:30',66,'Peter','<br><tt>oh, and also: i must add that i live by an immense double-standard here. i <b>hate</b> being flashed on the hwy. my favourite thing to do, when people flash me, is to tap my brake lightly several times and flash my brake-lights back at them. i swear i am not half as much of an asshole as i sound like, heh.</tt>',1,320,196,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16379,2140,'2002-05-06 11:50:29',298,'lurkinginsagas','i am the queen of bad posture.  will you still be my friend?<br>\r\nright, so this really had no point . . .',1,106,0,0,'207.218.223.216','en'),(16380,2710,'2002-05-06 11:55:11',298,'lurkinginsagas','\\\"am i a bad person?\\\" seems to be a staple question from me.  this may or may not put me in a position to judge, but i\\\'d say that from what i read you aren\\\'t bad.  furthermore, moving is a pain in my arse.  i hope to never do it again (except that i have to if i\\\'m ever going to get out of the suburbs).',2,317,0,0,'207.218.223.216','en'),(16381,2716,'2002-05-06 13:17:41',295,'karenlee','That\\\'s nice dear, it\\\'s good to have a job that will take you into the future. Plenty of work in your line, I expect.',1,122,174,0,'203.194.17.210','en'),(16382,2646,'2002-05-06 13:21:10',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i love the line that says: <i>\\\"when you get down to it, my fingernail and my hot dogs and my ramen noodles are all made of stars and asteroids\\\"</i></tt>',1,166,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16383,2714,'2002-05-06 15:30:56',180,'auntie','so much discussion about light flashing. auntie will now speak on the subject.<br><br>\r\nit may be frustrating to be driving behind a slow person in the \\\'fast lane\\\' - but you must know that persons who are dim enough to drive slow in the fast lane are also probably too dim to be enlightened by your flashing.<br><br>\r\n\r\nfurthermore, in france and germany, where this practice originated, it is now illegal.  for a reason.  it is dangerous.  the person you flash may not be the only one who is made temporary blind by the flashes and accidents may ensue.<br><br>\r\n\r\nhow terrible to be responsible for the death or injury of another person because you couldn\\\'t wait for a chance to pass!  the proper thing to do when faced with slowness in the left lane is to slow down a bit, move to the right lane, then wait for an opportunity to pass the slow person on the left.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthank you for your attention to this important auntie service bulletin.<br><br>\r\n',4,966,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(16384,205,'2002-05-06 15:34:41',278,'egenus','I love the hidden secrets of our imagination, only revealed under the influence of something. Who knew that it could be so cool to hear light?',1,142,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16385,2714,'2002-05-06 15:44:16',66,'Peter','<br><tt>in that 15th hour of a 20 hour drive, my goal honestly isnt <i>enlightenment</i>; i just want them to move. thankfully, as i generally drive during the day, i can rationalize that my oh-so-brief flashes arent all that blinding, although i guess you could argue otherwise. and anyway, im not one of those immense assholes that really lay on the brights. <i>[side note: people that are that easily blinded should probably reconsider driving on the highway at night anyway; i know i have night-vision problems, so i avoid it when possible]</i>. yes; back in my days as a 2002 fan, i vividly recall the tradition of bmw drivers to flash their headlights to other bmw drivers as a greeting. curiously, this practise seemed to be encouraged, even.</tt>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',1,764,180,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16386,205,'2002-05-06 15:48:22',66,'Peter','<br><tt>the most profoundly beautiful metamorphosis occurs, though, when you familiarize yourself with this altered topology enough that it finally becomes accessable without the chemicals...</tt>\r\n',1,198,278,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16387,2712,'2002-05-06 16:58:44',82,'simon','thanks, i rather like this one myself.',0,38,153,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16388,2714,'2002-05-06 17:32:07',265,'chuckspeed','<p>re: light flashing-</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i used to flash.  built a special car to flash properly and often.  it had two alternators and a bank of headlights down low where the bumper was. dunno how many million candlepower was on tap, but i could make out traffic signs two miles in advance.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>said lightsabers were used with joyous abandon until the day i flashed a cop.  i could tell he was a cop from the hat perched above his very disgruntled expression - readable over his very own high beams used to flash me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>an expensive lesson, but such is the cost of wisdom.</p>\r\n\r\n',0,586,0,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(16389,2706,'2002-05-06 17:42:20',265,'chuckspeed','<p>it\\\'s a penis thing.  We\\\'re not allowed to do the instantaneous measurement check our ancestors did in the back of the cave to determine who was the biggest dick - now we have to compete for the honor in virtually every social situation.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s the only rational explanation for suv\\\'s...</p>',0,313,0,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(16390,2710,'2002-05-06 18:08:29',265,'chuckspeed','<p>there is the friend who made it his vocation to run from his life.  i ran into him a few years back - it was disconcerting - disconcerting to see a guy in his 30\\\'s attempt to look and act 19.  he looked very sad - trapped, so to speak  i have the urge to look him up now and then, but i\\\'ve seen enough of what he\\\'s chosen not to become.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ess - may i call you ess? - you seem to be pretty tightly packed in as much as seen in your writing.  the real question is whether or not this is the person the \\\'rest\\\' of the world sees.  there seems to be a fair number of folks willing to \\\'drop the mask\\\' in an electronic forum who are unable to do so in real time.  i\\\'m not saying you do that - it just painful to hear you\r\nyearn for stability.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>keep going, girl - there\\\'s a person and a place waiting just for you.</p>\r\n\r\n \r\n',0,864,0,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(16391,2714,'2002-05-06 18:34:47',129,'moebius','<br>the topic of bright-flashing on the freeway is a hot button for me, so wtf, i\\\'ll add my two cents.<br><br>\r\ni\\\'ve been dealing with a workday commute from SF to its suburbs and back ever since 1995 or so, and during that time i\\\'ve grown to really hate pesky impatient tailgating drivers.<br><br>from \\\'95 to 2000 i was communting to Pleasanton, which meant a bay bridge crossing twice a day, and that was when things were at their worst. mornings were generally fine, but every evening involved not just one, but a succession of jacked-up SUV maniacs on my tail, flashing brights, driving right up against my back bumper, and otherwise going out of my way to be agressive. and i\\\'d be going 80+ mph, in pea-soup thick traffic!\r\n<br><br>since then i\\\'ve been commuting to redwood city, and this line is a lot quieter. the freeway nutcase quotient seems to be much smaller. i\\\'m not sure if the route is the change, though. i think it\\\'s the fact that most of the worst SUV-driving nutjobs were taken out by the dot.com.bomb.<br><br>something else about SUVs and brights: those cars are jacked up just high enough so that their lights DO blind you, even when they\\\'re <i>not</i> flashing brights. many times on the drive home i\\\'ve had to flip my rearview mirror down to protect myself from the glare. whew. nuff said. ',3,1343,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(16392,1287,'2002-05-06 18:43:03',298,'lurkinginsagas','all the days tend to run together for me.  i often walk upstairs & forget what i was going to get, or turn on my computer & can\\\'t remember why.  i think it\\\'s what happens when life is tedious & repetitive.  things have to change some time, right?',2,252,0,0,'198.216.248.96','en'),(16393,1107,'2002-05-06 18:48:59',298,'lurkinginsagas','ally sheedy is a goddess.\r\nthat is all.',2,39,0,0,'198.216.248.96','en'),(16394,2708,'2002-05-06 20:31:59',187,'kandrer','i was about to write \\\"ironic\\\" (french: ironique) when i reread your comment. but i then checked in the dictionary and ironical looked valid as well.',1,154,196,0,'208.223.155.199','en'),(16395,2714,'2002-05-06 20:35:21',129,'moebius','relevant link, hot off the presses: telemarketers (and their managers) <a href=\\\"http://slate.msn.com/?id=2065321&entry=2065396\\\">are people too.</a>',3,153,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(16396,2706,'2002-05-06 20:37:42',187,'kandrer','suv? i saw many women driving that as well... ',3,46,265,0,'208.223.155.199','en'),(16397,2714,'2002-05-06 20:51:43',187,'kandrer','in france, we flash AND WE HONK, at 100mph MINIMUM. you guys are wussies. :o)',4,77,0,0,'208.223.155.199','en'),(16398,2714,'2002-05-06 22:55:25',17,'austin','imagining s5 with a clown nose in a traffic terror situation is too funny.  seeing that i have had to deal with several psychos in rush hour traffic at speeds of up to 90 miles per hour (on 280), i think it is abolutely necessary that i procure a clown nose as soon as possible, or stop cutting people off.',3,306,59,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(16399,2706,'2002-05-07 01:08:38',168,'laney','this was great, thank you. it was like a glimpse of future 30-something married dinner parties i know i will attend. and made me glad i am a woman. :)',0,150,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(16400,2706,'2002-05-07 02:28:03',265,'chuckspeed','penis envy.',0,11,187,0,'172.130.106.32','en'),(16401,2706,'2002-05-07 02:28:40',265,'chuckspeed','consider it a four-wheeled strap-on.',0,36,187,0,'172.130.106.32','en'),(16402,2714,'2002-05-07 02:42:13',66,'Peter','<br><tt>my grandpa had a citroen; it went maybe 50mph, TOPS ;) it was a pretty car though!</tt>',1,95,187,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16403,1714,'2002-05-07 02:49:29',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this is odd.</tt>',1,25,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16404,1359,'2002-05-07 03:33:43',295,'karenlee','I think it\\\'s clear from  Peter\\\'s dream that you both need to be wormed.',1,77,0,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(16405,2714,'2002-05-07 03:39:53',187,'kandrer','a DS, you mean? too cool! my grandpa had one two :) i saw some in SF not in a too bad shape; in france they are rather rare now.',1,128,66,0,'208.223.155.199','en'),(16406,2723,'2002-05-07 09:13:57',89,'sawks','hi<br>\r\n<br>\r\nyou should invest in a double boiler!<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni can also highly recommend making thin ginger-snap style biscuits and glazing them with more of the butter - 1 of those will cause an average human to freak right out :)',3,233,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(16407,2710,'2002-05-07 10:52:11',72,'essemar','girl?  When did I become one of those?<p>heh... I feel like I\\\'m hurtling into a life similar to your 30-year-old-19-acting friend.  In my history, I\\\'ve been an aspiring physicist, aspiring opera singer, aspiring philospher, aspiring start-up founder, actual pizza delivery guy, ballroom dance instructor, and currently an aspiring dancer/actor.  Way too much ADDness happening in my life.  Anyways, finished moving 90% of my stuff, and in 12 hours I\\\'ll be standing in the middle of a baron room filled with boxes.  fun...</p>',0,534,265,0,'64.12.107.177','en'),(16408,2710,'2002-05-07 13:12:52',265,'chuckspeed','<p>if not a girl, then what? mayhap i have confused you with someone else, yes?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>my friend sought fame and fortune.  sadly, these are empty pursuits - an effect rather than a cause.  at one point, he\\\'d convinced himself that three weeks (!) was all it took to become a rock superstar - one week to get a band together, one week to practice and make a demo tape, and one week of dropping it off at radio stations to get big airplay and the killer contract.  never mind that he didn\\\'t know how to play - he had a nice early strat, so he *looked* the part.  now i\\\'\\\'m not saying you\\\'re doing that - it\\\'s just silly in hindsight.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>concentrate on the little things - concentrate on the things you do well.  focus entirely on a singular pursuit - the rest will come.</p>',0,799,0,0,'208.5.114.88','en'),(16409,2719,'2002-05-07 13:17:47',265,'chuckspeed','<p>the closest thing to \\\'letting go\\\' in a relationship is a trust fall.  that\\\'s where you climb atop something high, fall back and let 8 of your close friends catch you...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>releasing one\\\'s self to another is truly the most terrifying thing i\\\'ve ever done - and the most important.</p>',0,308,0,0,'208.5.114.88','en'),(16410,2723,'2002-05-07 13:22:11',265,'chuckspeed','mortar and pestle\r\n\r\ngrind into powder\r\n\r\nmicrowave peanut butter\r\n\r\nmix powder into p-nut butter\r\n\r\nstore jar in school locker\r\n\r\nmake sandwiches when bored\r\n\r\nclassroom goes two dimensional',0,191,0,0,'208.5.114.88','en'),(16411,2706,'2002-05-07 16:05:21',273,'Quinn','When you hit 30 you start to believe that your life has found it\\\'s track.  You\\\'re locked in, for all intents and purposes.  But the fact is that life seldom locks you in.  You choose your tracks, you jump from one to another.  You see the same kinds of people that you saw when you were 25, but you begin to realize that they are still 25.  For a minute or two you begin to think that you are old, that suddenly your gripped by the fist of years and your are OLD.  But then you realize that FUCK you\\\'re 32 and damn proud of it.  You\\\'re 32 and you ACT like your 32...it means you LEARNED something when you were 25, 26, 27, etc.  I mean [goddamit!]when you see people aging into parenthood and still acting like a teenager you kind of feel sorry for them as they cling, white knuckled, to their youth.  Getting older is good.  Even as young as 32 you have more perspective than someone at 28.  Treasure your years and don\\\'t envy youth. ',0,950,0,0,'165.247.6.11','en'),(16412,2712,'2002-05-07 17:46:55',195,'matt','i feel like graffiti-ing these on random walls around my town.',1,62,0,0,'209.208.109.3','en'),(16413,2723,'2002-05-07 17:52:13',195,'matt','buncha damn degenerates on here;)',1,33,0,0,'209.208.109.3','en'),(16414,2712,'2002-05-07 18:11:50',82,'simon','cool',0,4,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16415,2723,'2002-05-07 19:21:27',278,'egenus','<p>The miracle herb can be worked into just about any kind of dish. Personally, my favorite as of yet is still brownies. 3 brownies, made with a 1/2 oz == nirvana.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Oh, and there are lotsa reefer recipies at <a href=\\\"http://www.medicalmarijuana.org\\\">MedicalMarijuana.org</a>.',1,290,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16416,2723,'2002-05-07 20:11:39',75,'tyler','it probably would be easier with a double boiler, but waiting to scoop it off the top of the water is more fun. i get that \\\'kindergartner doing a science project\\\' feeling.',1,177,0,0,'165.247.164.62','en'),(16417,2724,'2002-05-08 00:30:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt><b>a fragmented comment:</b><br><br>i grew up near athens. pylon rocks. <br><br>also: good luck with everything, especially the newfound spirituality ;) <br><br>my flatmate works as a ranger in central park; almost nightly, he finds the remains of santeria get-togethers. these usually consist of saucers of blood and entrails left as offerings and eviscerated animals nailed in circles on trees. he generally has trouble sleeping.</tt>',1,444,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16418,2724,'2002-05-08 03:38:35',196,'jasonp','I\\\'d recommend keeping clean for a while longer. Not sure your motivation for rehab, but going back to pot right away seems to be counter-productive. Yeah, not as bad as the harder stuff but being sober is underrated. If you went back to pot right away it\\\'s like rehab was holding your breath, like \\\"substances\\\" is oxygen. It\\\'s not. Staying clean on your own volition for about six months will give you another view of things. Maybe even more different than the one you have now after rehab only.<BR>\nI stopped drinking for about 21months. The original goal was only for 1 year. It was hardest refraining the first few weeks or so. Then the next six months or so certain situations would trigger desire. After a year the desire to drink wasn\\\'t there at all so I continued to keep off it. I finally had a beer just because I didn\\\'t think I wanted the mental pressure of *never* drinking again. That was a couple of months ago. I\\\'ll mostly just stop at a beer or two now. After 21 months my brain and body was as clean as a whistle. One single heineken and I was buzzed as hell.<BR>\nNever getting out of control drunk seems absolutely fine to me. I still haven\\\'t had enough to have a hangover and that\\\'s how I\\\'ll keep it. After a few months of minor splurging I might stop again just because that might be better.<BR>\nTry and savor your sobriety more. There\\\'s a lot more to life you can appreciate without diluting or polluting your body and mind. Waking up clear and awake, no hangover, alcohol smell, or guilt/dread about the night before. No out of control behavior, running around like a criminal or deliquent, hiding, etc. Addiction is bondage. If there\\\'s anything you\\\'re serious about getting done in your life it\\\'s going to be ten times easier being clear and present.<BR>\nGet into health addiction. Healthy foods, supplements, exercise, yoga, etc. if you want to be addicted to something.',0,1938,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16419,2724,'2002-05-08 03:53:59',180,'auntie','one of the basic tenets of any 12 step program is to acknowledge a higher power.  it doesn\\\'t have to be god, it can be mother earth, but you have to acknowledge that there is a greater force than YOU in the world.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni\\\'m an absolute christian, i firmly believe in the power of prayer, and i have never had a prayer go unanswered - maybe not the answer i wished for, but always answered.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni don\\\'t go to church.  i don\\\'t enjoy canned religion unless it is very formal - catholic, epizcopal - lots of pomp and no altar calls.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\nyou can\\\'t just smoke a little pot, and you know that, and you also know that you NEED a higher power to help you remember that.  god really does care what happens to you, or he wouldn\\\'t be trying to pull you into his arms. don\\\'t try to question it, just accept it, and the comfort it can bring you.<br><br>\r\n\r\nand.. having said that... all too often recovering addicts will think they have to go way far in the other direction, to become as fanatically straight as they were drunks to be saved from their addictions, and when they can\\\'t toe the line they have drawn of a perfect christian, they fall into the abyss of despair and right back into the addiction, only worse.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmoderation in all things.  find out where the AA meets, and GO TO MEETINGS! every day, twice a day, as often as you need to go to beat the monkey.  get a sponsor.  you will be amazed at how \\\"regular\\\" the other attendees can be.  you will not be alone.  i will be praying for you.<br><br>\r\n\r\noh, and flush that coke down the sink, please.  it will grow in your mind like the eye of sauron, seeking you until you tumble.',2,1686,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(16420,2724,'2002-05-08 04:34:44',175,'Paul G','<br>\r\n\\\"i think it\\\'ll be alright as long as i stick to...\\\"<br>\r\nIt\\\'s just so easy to think about not doing the second thing until you\\\'ve first thing.<br>\r\nBe careful, matt - day by day.  And good luck.',0,215,0,0,'67.248.78.159','en'),(16421,2724,'2002-05-08 07:00:10',74,'jo','dear auntie:<BR>\r\nthank you for saying \\\"like the eye of sauron,\\\" as that was awesomely nerdy of you.',0,106,0,0,'172.163.140.54','en'),(16422,2719,'2002-05-08 10:26:10',295,'karenlee','...sounds like you are wearing all the blame for the ruin of all your relationships in the last 4 years. Is that an ego trip - were you actually that bad a partner? Or do you not see that it couldn\\\'t possibly have been ALL your fault? Gosh.',3,243,0,0,'203.194.16.21','en'),(16423,1858,'2002-05-08 11:19:27',295,'karenlee','I lived in a suburb once that had a Returned Services club that sported on its front lawn a.....DONER KEBAB FOUNTAIN...yes, that\\\'s right, the little slaty stones were all stacked up on each other, forming a wonderful meaty tower, over which the water flowed, giving a delightful likeness of the real thing. We went down there one morning with a big carving knife to take photos but the nice man shooed us away.',3,413,0,0,'203.194.16.21','en'),(16424,1858,'2002-05-08 11:48:21',66,'Peter','<br><tt>alas, almost 8 months later, i am no longer a partaker of the kebabs; and after spending a few months avoiding big slabs of meat (and generally living healthier) ive found that im much happier and such; however, there is and will always be a doner kebab shaped void in my heart.</tt> ',1,292,295,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16425,2724,'2002-05-08 17:41:20',195,'matt','<BR><BR>thanks for the good advice. i really appreciate it.<BR><BR>as far as santeria goes, i\\\'m not down with the animal sacrifice. there are plenty of ways to go about worshipping the orishas that don\\\'t involve animals.',1,226,0,0,'209.208.109.25','en'),(16426,1858,'2002-05-08 17:44:16',74,'jo','peter, i hope that your hiatus from meat won\\\'t stop you from taking terrifying pictures of yourself and food items, as those are my favorite swinney contributions of yours. the pizza was a <b>good step</b>; perhaps there is in the future a picture of <b>PETER + FALAFEL</B>? ',1,278,66,0,'172.132.207.127','en'),(16427,1858,'2002-05-08 17:48:28',74,'jo','oh god i can\\\'t believe i just mentioned falafel and now have a huge falafel craving and did in fact JUST walk from spider house, which is across the fucking street from tom\\\'s tabooley which has my favorite falafel in town. and here i am. home. falafel-less. watching snl reruns on tv, slurping my smoothie, oh so desirous of FALAFEL, HOLY HOLY FALAFEL.',1,358,0,0,'172.132.207.127','en'),(16428,1858,'2002-05-08 19:46:32',17,'austin','i find myself here again, commenting, \\\"i am hungry.\\\"',4,58,17,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(16429,1858,'2002-05-08 20:54:32',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ohhh!!! now it is settled: i <i>must</i> walk across broadway to <i>jerusalem shaworma</i> for supper tonight and get a falafel sandwich. i havent had one in months. heres hoping the man recognizes me and gives me the usual extra tahini!</tt>',1,250,74,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16430,2710,'2002-05-08 21:23:31',17,'austin','i used to want the talents of others, and i have a guitar to prove it.  i try really hard not to be that way now.  however, it is hard to test the reality of a possibility without stepping out on a limb and buying the guitar, the motorcycle, the domain name.',3,258,265,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(16431,2715,'2002-05-08 22:36:27',295,'karenlee','I wrote this for myself to recite in the mirror every once in a while.',3,70,0,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(16432,2725,'2002-05-08 22:38:04',295,'karenlee','Who says all naked women on beds are un-edited?',3,47,0,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(16433,2725,'2002-05-08 23:02:35',82,'simon','she\\\'s got a point there. i\\\'ve airbrushed my share of tits and ass, not to mention chins, armpits and elbows.',0,114,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16434,2725,'2002-05-08 23:54:05',88,'deb','I like this.  It alsmost seems a little arrogant, but at the same time it seems a little innocent.\r\n',3,100,0,0,'65.223.130.93','en'),(16435,2724,'2002-05-08 23:55:19',218,'da','AA might work for you, but you might want to look into options like\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.rationalrecovery.org/\\\">Rational Recovery</a>, too.  Do a web search on \\\"AA alternatives\\\" or something like that for more.  RR replaces the disease model of addiction with something they call a\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.rationalrecovery.org/Structure.html\\\">structural model</a>.  This model may not be perfect, but it jives a <i>lot</i> better with my experiences and some of the other stuff I\\\'ve read on this topic than the AA information I\\\'ve been exposed to.  Good luck.',1,580,0,0,'4.47.68.165','en'),(16436,2724,'2002-05-08 23:58:43',88,'deb','If you check yourself in, are you allowed to check yourself out?\r\n\r\nAs far as religion is concerned, if you find yourself doubting things at any point in time, just remember that you do not NEED religion or a higher power to be decent and have a few morals. \r\n\r\nI think you did the right thing for yourself. Good luck.',2,318,0,0,'65.223.130.93','en'),(16437,2722,'2002-05-09 00:00:26',88,'deb','this is different. i like it, for the most part, but i\\\'m not sure about the part about sex before breakfast and then the cream cheese with eggs...the lines didn\\\'t sit well with my imagination?',4,198,0,0,'65.223.130.93','en'),(16438,2724,'2002-05-09 00:02:02',59,'s5','that site kicks ass, da. thanks for posting it here.',2,52,218,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16439,2718,'2002-05-09 00:05:00',88,'deb','all dads would like to believe that they can fix things. sometimes they can. sometimes they can\\\'t. sometimes it just doesn\\\'t matter.',0,138,0,0,'65.223.130.93','en'),(16440,2717,'2002-05-09 00:07:37',88,'deb','why?',6,4,0,0,'65.223.130.93','en'),(16441,2718,'2002-05-09 00:08:57',295,'karenlee','yes, he was a fix it person (and so am I). Only 3 wks before he died he fixed the garage door, which was a pretty good effort.',3,126,88,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(16442,2718,'2002-05-09 00:31:11',82,'simon','i like fix it people. my dad\\\'s a fix it guy. and i try my best. i don\\\'t have much of an attention span but i like to tinker around with this and that.',0,156,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16443,2719,'2002-05-09 02:01:15',297,'kelly','<p>yeah - i didnt really mean it to sound like that. the thing is, i haven\\\'t actually gotten to the point of even having real relationships. after one that totally failed, and no, not all my fault of course. i\\\'m sure had i actually been able to get close enough to establish some real, deep, close relationships.. sure, they might not have worked out in the end. no one\\\'s fault. but i never found out cos i never get to that point. </p>',1,445,0,0,'24.185.90.139','en'),(16444,1858,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<i>Add some salad, add a chili.<br>\r\nWash your hands, Don\'t you touch your willy.</i><br> oh the splender of your prose, Peter.',1,126,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(16445,2719,'2002-05-09 04:15:36',295,'karenlee','The good one will come along and then there will be big times i\\\'m sure.',3,74,0,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(16446,666,'2002-05-09 04:19:33',295,'karenlee','Hey do you know the lyrics to Saving the Best \\\'til Last by Vanessa W? Now that\\\'s a top tune.',3,98,0,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(16447,666,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','sometimes the very thing you\\\'re looking for is the one thing you can\\\'t see...',6,83,295,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16448,2725,'2002-05-09 06:13:10',115,'lulu','you\\\'re not as lucky as you think you are.',2,44,0,0,'209.179.232.154','en'),(16449,666,'2002-05-09 07:29:17',92,'kini','there goes your street cred.',1,28,62,0,'152.91.9.43','en'),(16450,666,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','a-ha, but my street cred is for knowing all the lyrics to every song ever written! :D:D:D:D!  I WIN!!!',6,102,92,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16451,1858,'2002-05-09 08:53:13',295,'karenlee','Hey don\\\'t stop at the falafel...look at that monumental rotating juicy oily fly catcher and tell me you don\\\'t want some!....BIG YEEROS YEAH.\r\n',2,148,66,0,'203.194.21.89','en'),(16452,910,'2002-05-09 09:03:10',295,'karenlee','\\\"The general technique of deer hunting applies throughout the world. Judgment of the range for a shot and shot placement are vital. Good deer hunters bring deer down almost every time they squeeze the trigger. They do it by placing the bullet in the lung area. You can\\\'t afford to miss. There are no second chances.\\\"',2,325,0,0,'203.194.21.89','en'),(16453,910,'2002-05-09 09:06:16',295,'karenlee','...whoops I meant to add that I found that passage in The Deerhunter\\\'s Handbook, a shocking volume that I found recently in a secondhand shop.',3,145,0,0,'203.194.21.89','en'),(16454,666,'2002-05-09 09:10:29',295,'karenlee','Sometimes the snow comes down in June, sometimes the sun goes round the moon.',3,77,62,0,'203.194.21.89','en'),(16455,1858,'2002-05-09 11:46:23',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>Of course, there\\\'s\r\nsalad in there for the ladies too</i>. i like that line, for some reason. kebabs, kebabs for everyone!</tt>',1,141,57,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16456,2719,'2002-05-09 17:33:23',297,'kelly','yeah - that\\\'s one thing i haven\\\'t lost is my sincere belief that one day everyone will meet someone and if it\\\'s meant to be.. nothing can stand in your way. ',2,166,0,0,'166.77.209.191','en'),(16457,666,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','all of the nights you came to me<br>\r\nwhen some silly girl had set you free<br>\r\nyou wondered how you\\\'d make it through<br>\r\ni wondered what was wrong with you<br>\r\nhow could you give your love to someone else<br>\r\nand share your dreams with me<br>\r\nsometimes the very thing you\\\'re looking for is the one thing you can\\\'t see<br>\r\n',6,339,295,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16458,2723,'2002-05-09 19:26:57',218,'da','when i lived right in the haight, my roommate/landlord was this nice old hippie woman who made insane loaded ghee.  i have no idea how she did it, but she made me a plate of brownies made with this stuff to take to work.  i vaguely recall eating one sometime after lunch, working for a while, then things are blurry, and then i\\\'m in jim\\\'s truck heading west through soma to one of the cp quarterly party/meetings.  it was sunny and hot and everything was funny and i felt really, really good.  god that was a nice day.',1,524,0,0,'4.47.68.165','en'),(16459,2717,'2002-05-09 20:26:42',298,'lurkinginsagas','because sometimes fear can immobilize you.',1,42,88,0,'207.218.203.209','en'),(16460,2723,'2002-05-09 20:58:54',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ahh, the wonders of highly-lipid-soluable psychoactives!</tt>',1,69,218,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16461,2722,'2002-05-09 21:41:52',168,'laney','yes, in hindsight, i believe i should have tried a little harder to separate those images...but i hope in your critique you did not lose the point about cream cheese in the eggs. it rules.',0,188,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(16462,2722,'2002-05-09 21:49:44',66,'Peter','<br><tt>...i just thought it was some sort of naughty metaphor</tt>',1,67,168,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16463,2722,'2002-05-09 22:22:36',168,'laney','eeewwww!! i wish i was that naughty. truthfully, i really didn\\\'t see the humor there until it was pointed out. i thought the lines looked a little funny together, but now...duh! :)',0,183,66,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(16464,666,'2002-05-09 22:56:01',17,'austin','stop defacing the alter of sabath with teenie pop!\r\n',6,52,0,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(16465,2586,'2002-05-09 23:08:40',297,'kelly','i lived in a small town in upper michigan until i was 19 in the same house. my whole family still lives in that same town. since then i\\\'ve lived in 2 cities in colorado, pennsylvania, massachusetts, and now new york. i\\\'ve been in new york for a year now and i\\\'m having that same \\\"need to move\\\" feeling. i need constant change. the only thing stopping me is i love the job i have, and  i think a person definitely needs more than a year to experience everything new york has to offer. ',0,499,0,0,'24.185.90.139','en'),(16466,666,'2002-05-09 23:10:33',82,'simon','can\\\' t you re-write it as Saving the BEAST \\\'Til Last?',0,59,295,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16467,666,'2002-05-09 23:10:46',82,'simon','at least stick with the theme, eh',0,33,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16468,666,'2002-05-10 02:07:58',295,'karenlee','goddam! you aced me. you are the master..... And Now We\\\'re Standing Face To Face...Isn\\\'t This World A Crazy Place .....Just When I Thought Our Chance Had Passed............. You Go And Save The Best For Last.',3,214,82,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(16469,666,'2002-05-10 06:54:54',295,'karenlee','..whoops I meant to say Beast for Last, but now the moment is gone.',3,67,82,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(16470,666,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i think sacraficing vanessa williams on the alter of heavy metal is totally appropriate.',6,88,17,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16471,2227,'2002-05-10 08:59:16',86,'egg','i hate fish balls. you know that song, \\\"i dont like mondays\\\"? I used to walk through bangkok singing the tune to that song with the words, \\\"i dont like fish balls (tell me why?) i dont like fishballs.\\\"',0,213,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16472,2118,'2002-05-10 09:06:38',86,'egg','I saw \\\"kiss me deadly\\\" tonight at the parkway. they did that awful thing where they ask trivia questions at the beginning and then give away prizes. I knew two out of three answers (\\\"besides pulp fiction what 1980s film pays tribute to the glowing suitcase motif? <i>Repo MAn</i>....which MARY TYLER MOORE actress made her screen debut in this film? <i> Cloris Leachman</i> but that bitch tiki goddess didnt call on me. I raised my hand silently. you know, the teacher always says, \\\"dont call out\\\", but then she doesnt notice you unless you make a lot of noise. thats the bitch of this world, this world of nuclear detonation and haircuts that require you sleep in hot rollers. I ve had it. I dont want to be a woman anymore. good night.',0,752,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16473,2118,'2002-05-10 09:08:52',86,'egg','also, \\\"kiss me deadly\\\" is a fantastic movie. I suggest you all rent it this weekend - all you two people who are reading my comment. It is filled with snicker-worhty sexual innuendo and post WW2 paranoia. and for all you fabulous feminists out there, it\\\'s worth about 100 pages of cultural research on the \\\"femme fatale\\\". really the whole movie is about Mike Hammer and why all the chicks want to kiss up on him, and why he\\\'s too much of a man to want it back. which just makes you want it more!!! who cares about the murder mystery, where did you get that car???',0,581,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16474,2729,'2002-05-10 12:23:47',303,'charlotte','this connects so deeply to my innermost thoughts. i\\\'ve been trying to forgive for 2 years now. it hasn\\\'t happened. i\\\'m trapped and will be for my whole life.',2,166,0,0,'203.56.239.111','en'),(16475,2729,'2002-05-10 12:32:27',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>My secrets speak a silent rhyme</i>',1,46,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16476,2729,'2002-05-10 15:08:39',265,'chuckspeed','you gotta give it up.  the memory \\\'owns\\\' you - you be come a prisoner of the transgression.  let go - breathe - you\\\'re still alive after exhaling.',0,155,303,0,'208.5.114.92','en'),(16477,2729,'2002-05-10 15:53:10',295,'karenlee','Hi charlotte, I\\\'m glad there was something in this for you, but remember, sometimes, forgiveness is for assholes. Despite the tone of this piece, it\\\'s really just a bunch of words that rhyme.\r\n',3,199,303,0,'203.194.7.24','en'),(16478,2729,'2002-05-10 15:54:52',295,'karenlee','No damn you....MY secrets speak a silent rhyme!\r\n',3,49,66,0,'203.194.7.24','en'),(16479,2731,'2002-05-10 17:51:52',82,'simon','for some reason the only think i could think of to say is \\\"best friendly\\\" but it\\\'s early and i\\\'m a little wonky this morning.',0,137,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16480,2729,'2002-05-10 18:34:42',175,'Paul G','As far as I can tell, Peter seems to be one of the brightest and most creative people around.  And what\\\'s more, I think that he\\\'s a good man too.  It\\\'s high praise for him to pay attention to your piece.  Just saying...<br>\r\n[Best Friendly]',1,249,295,0,'67.248.86.25','en'),(16481,2729,'2002-05-10 20:21:50',187,'kandrer','<i>\r\n...and abroad i became like a shy girly\r\nkept my mouth shut all day so i don\\\'t look dummy\r\n</i>\r\n</p><p>OK, this time i try to ask (please help your little puzzled frenchie!). \r\nplease tell me, anyone, if what i understood is correct:<br>\r\n- Peter quoted the line he really liked<br>\r\n- kerenlee made a joke about that (no, not your secret, but mine)<br>\r\n- Paul G... mmm, here i don\\\'t get it. Paul G, my dear, were you serious or kidding?\r\n</p>\r\n',1,458,0,0,'205.186.126.226','en'),(16482,2729,'2002-05-10 20:30:18',187,'kandrer','damn! i <u>am</u> dummy!</p>\r\n\r\n<i>\r\nit is no use to try to make alexandrines<br>\r\nif one forgets the break at the end of the line! \r\n</i>\r\n\r\n</p><p>\r\nand, yeah, i know it was karenlee; this one was a real typo. this one-chance comment editing thing kills me.\r\n</p>',1,265,0,0,'205.186.126.226','en'),(16483,2729,'2002-05-10 20:59:38',175,'Paul G','Your question about a specific comment points towards a larger issue concerning comments as a whole.  There is interaction here; and there is interaction in places other than here.  It is difficult to know who is who, and what the relationship between each party really is.  Familiar remarks are made publicly.  And sometimes they engender a response other than that which was intended.  \r\n\r\n<p>It can be a difficult thing: There is no sign on the door explaining the happenings inside.  There is a core and periphery here.  There are personal and also purely intellectual engagements.  Sometimes the lines between the two are not clear.  I am writing this from an \\\"outside\\\" perspective.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Having said that, you should understand that I have had a sustained discourse with Peter.  And you should understand that I like him, and support what he is about.  And those things that I said [that he was bright and creative] were meant to be taken very seriously.  I may from time to time appear difficult - but as a rule I try to engage ideas and hierarchies rather than individuals.  If I felt otherwise about Peter, I would say nothing at all.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It can be awkward to say things.  But it is usually beneficial, in the long run, to break the guilty silence and be free.</p> \r\n',0,1292,0,0,'67.248.52.247','en'),(16484,2728,'2002-05-10 22:37:24',278,'egenus','I wish that situations like this were uncommon. Unfortunately my best friend is living with a similiar burden. Baseball bats and blood I say.',1,141,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16485,2392,'2002-05-10 22:49:35',278,'egenus','<p>I agree completely with the posting. Our reality is merely a gradient of different shades of gray. Each of our opinions equals exactly jackshit in the objective scope of the universe. So in the example of cats and dogs, the value one person, or a billion people for that matter, associates with the object is entirely subjective.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Diversity is paramount; without our differences we would have weak genes and simple minds. Evolution and adaptation in general (a very necessary ability) require that we be open to differences. To have a personal belief is harmless and even positive, but to assume that this \\\"truth\\\" (as you view it) is the only truth is arrogance.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Truth, like beauty, is in the eye of the beholder.</p>',1,746,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16486,2599,'2002-05-10 22:52:13',129,'moebius\\\' dad','My son turned me on to this article.  Very neat stuff.  It resonates with what I do for a living.  I\\\'m in the mining industry, and boy, if you like large mechanical stuff, this is the field for you.  Trucks that haul 300 tonnes, with tires 12 feet in diameter.  Shovels that pick up 55 cubic yards (enough to bury your suburban garage) in a single scoop.  Crushers that can chew up Volkswagen-sized rocks in a mater of seconds.\r\n\r\nI grew up with Tonka toys.  This is merely it\\\'s logical extreme.\r\n\r\nOur cars, our cell phones, our computers and our food.  It\\\'s all either grown or dug up out of the earth.',2,613,0,0,'64.175.150.66','en'),(16487,2728,'2002-05-10 22:59:25',298,'lurkinginsagas','ha.  if only i was the violent type.',1,36,278,0,'207.218.212.143','en'),(16488,2729,'2002-05-11 00:42:01',295,'karenlee','Hey PG, brakes on the seriousness!! Peter and I have had a few \\\"conversations\\\" - mostly humorous (like the one above - note Peter\\\'s original comment) and I feel pretty sure he knows there is no ill intent in any of my comments. But thanks for saying what you wanted to say anyway.',3,289,175,0,'203.194.10.242','en'),(16489,2729,'2002-05-11 02:03:42',175,'Paul G','My own intent has been called into question on a number of occasions.  And so this time it seemed prudent to offer as thorough an explanation as possible to the person posting as Kandrer.\r\n\r\n<p>I seem to know very little about the things that are happening on-line - and the good is suffering along with the bad because of it.</p> ',0,331,295,0,'67.248.52.89','en'),(16490,2433,'2002-05-11 02:13:07',295,'karenlee','I am with Peter on the no comments comment. Even if no-one had commented on your words above, there is no doubt it is a wonderfully told story and even less doubt that it would resonate for many people. I also sat beside my dad a few hours before he died and I never said the things I\\\'d wanted to. It didn\\\'t matter too much..I knew he loved me and he always knew I loved my dad....still, 6 years down the track I am still sitting here a little teary eyed after reading this. :)\r\n',1,485,175,0,'203.194.10.242','en'),(16491,2729,'2002-05-11 02:16:12',59,'s5','sigh',6,4,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16492,2729,'2002-05-11 02:17:50',295,'karenlee','Don\\\'t sweat it PG, just always know that there is no malicious or destructive motive in my comments. :)\r\n',1,108,175,0,'203.194.10.242','en'),(16493,2729,'2002-05-11 03:37:38',66,'Peter','<br><tt>huh?</tt>',1,17,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16494,2730,'2002-05-11 04:24:18',75,'tyler','and then you pick up the phone, hesitate, and dial my number. i answer. you hear marvin gaye in the background. you\\\'re on your way.',1,134,0,0,'165.247.164.62','en'),(16495,2729,'2002-05-11 04:37:55',295,'karenlee','Oh hi Peter, I hope you realise my responses to your articles/comments are always in good humour, never nasty.',3,110,66,0,'203.194.14.245','en'),(16496,2729,'2002-05-11 04:50:05',66,'Peter','<br><tt>of course! its the rest of this thread that i dont get, ha.</tt>',1,72,187,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16497,2733,'2002-05-11 05:22:33',124,'gus','my dad is turning 70 this year... he looks about 20 years younger than this old guy. I wonder if this old guy loved too much, or too little...',0,142,0,0,'64.172.58.210','en'),(16498,2733,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>did you tell him that if he really wants love, perhaps he should stop laughing at the fashions of today and rather invest in some?  i mean really, those glasses.</p>',6,168,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16499,2729,'2002-05-11 09:42:12',187,'kandrer','ok, then it seems i understood everything right!!! (you can\\\'t know how happy i am; my most used word in the US is \\\"what?\\\")<br>\r\nPaulG, i knew you were serious about what you think of Peter. it\\\'s just... you know, many times here i see people writing weird stuff and i think <i>ah... they must be friends, that must be a private joke</i>. usually, i get the confirmation by reading what comes after. in fact, i didn\\\'t plan to answer anything, but then i got these bad verses in my mind about my habit to shut up, so i made the comment you read. that reminds me about a recent thread with mad moll involved. i couldn\\\'t figure out who was sarcastic or not.</p>\r\n<p>and yeah, Peter is cool! and i like you too, Paul. well, i love you all. alleluyah. :)</p>',1,770,0,0,'207.93.61.165','en'),(16500,2733,'2002-05-11 10:10:01',174,'Mad Moll','Very cool',6,9,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(16501,2729,'2002-05-11 10:27:42',295,'karenlee','YAY! Everybody loves everybody!',3,31,0,0,'203.221.80.97','en'),(16502,2733,'2002-05-11 11:40:46',187,'kandrer','great post, thanks!</p><p>\r\n\r\n<i>state is the coldest of the cold monsters</i><br>\r\nwas it from Nietsche? how was it said? anyway you can\\\'t expect love from the governement, and that can only be a good thing, right? this guy wanted a Ministry of Love a la 1984... a man now hoping to receive love from a heartless organisation to be protected from women... i\\\'m supposing that you selected with care the quotes in all that he said to you, no?',1,447,0,0,'207.93.61.165','en'),(16503,2731,'2002-05-11 14:15:53',295,'karenlee','what a sweet little thing you have written.\r\n',1,45,0,0,'203.194.21.102','en'),(16504,2733,'2002-05-11 14:23:23',122,'hool','<p> i was selective with my quotes, yes, but only omitted some personal details he gave me. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>i got to thinking about what it\\\'d be like to live in a country where love was considered a basic human right, so the government would set up social assistance programs for those in short supply of it.  how would they implement it?  would there be love stamps?  what size would be the unit ration of love be? would the people be happier?</p>',0,446,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(16505,2729,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','no, everyone does not love everybody. my bozolist button is flaming hot, i better lay off it for a while.',1,106,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(16506,2729,'2002-05-11 14:55:48',295,'karenlee','Weeb!...I love you!...you have enriched my life...but my dearest darling whom I have never more loved, please pay more attention to the thread before you stick the needle in.\r\n',3,176,57,0,'203.194.21.102','en'),(16507,2729,'2002-05-11 15:13:27',65,'le','i don\\\'t really understand what\\\'s going on in this thread, but everyone really doesn\\\'t love everybody. at least on this plane.',4,134,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16508,2729,'2002-05-11 20:47:50',59,'s5','i blame anyone who has ever written \\\"swinney.org\\\" on a bathroom wall for this thread.',6,91,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16509,2733,'2002-05-11 21:21:40',187,'kandrer','yeah, cool idea! and there will be people never satisfied, always wanting for more, cheating to get some more illegaly. the government would fight back: they would create a special service to investigate about love frauds. also, the people that have enough love would trade their love tickets... :)',1,298,122,0,'205.184.181.22','en'),(16510,2733,'2002-05-12 02:24:37',278,'egenus','Interesting. I can identify with that kind of disillusionment a little. Don\\\'t know if I would be down with the whole LoveCare plan though. Thanks for the post.',1,162,0,0,'24.25.107.161','en'),(16511,2733,'2002-05-12 02:59:17',196,'jasonp','Another quality submission hool.<BR>\nIt makes me want to get and use a digital camera even though I doubt I\\\'d have the time or motivation to actually do anything if I got one.',0,178,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16512,2729,'2002-05-12 03:14:44',196,'jasonp','<B>Okay, but who could ever forgive the Nazis?<BR>\n<Font size=\\\"-1\\\"><I>\nThat was the time for someone to mention nazis so this thread can end.<BR>\n<A href=\\\"http://www.killfile.org/~tskirvin/faqs/godwin.faq\\\">\nper Godwin\\\'s Law</A>\n</I>\n</font>',0,255,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16513,2728,'2002-05-12 03:20:23',180,'auntie','everyone has a stalker story by the time they hit forty.  which is too bad. but the reality is that some people just don\\\'t know how to end things, whether it is a relationship, a job, or a place in time.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe stalkers of this world are sometimes dangerous, and sometimes not, but they are always scary to the stalkee, and there really isn\\\'t much you can do, for restraining orders only do so much.  if the stalker has any possibility of cure, a restraining order might actually stop the stalking.... if not... well, it could make things worse - but you should keep a log and save the letters, because you might need them for proof someday.<br><br>\r\n ',1,666,298,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(16514,2735,'2002-05-12 06:54:07',74,'jo','wow. some of this rang painfully true.',1,38,0,0,'12.162.144.138','en'),(16515,2728,'2002-05-12 14:52:08',298,'lurkinginsagas','that\\\'s a tad creepy, but i see where you\\\'re coming from.  the day i broke up with him i wrote down the conversation when he was threatening to show up & my friend said to keep it.  shudder.',1,195,180,0,'207.218.212.172','en'),(16516,2737,'2002-05-12 20:46:28',278,'egenus','Your luck is disturbing. Watch for falling meteors.',1,51,0,0,'24.25.107.161','en'),(16517,476,'2002-05-12 20:52:32',300,'ken bit me','ahhh-memoirs of a geisha. i rememmber that one.  \r\nThe introduction where he\\\'s describing how the woman runs the teahouse in new york and chooses him to chronical her life... it took me a minute to understand that it was apart of the story and not a real introduction.\r\n\r\nyey.',1,279,0,0,'206.217.148.31','en'),(16518,2735,'2002-05-12 20:57:49',187,'kandrer','this is not a true story, i mean not the <i>complete</i> story. but the separate pieces of it are things i knew. i started to write this when i realized that my email forwarding was broken these last days. i could not send an email to these people i would be sad to miss a message from. <i>\\\"hey, did you email me recently? because it would have been lost.\\\"</i> this would have meant <i>i have been hoping you write to me</i>. i think i can call this the telephone complex. waiting for calls. i started the post to write ten lines (they could have been ambiguous), and i got a bit more expansive...',1,603,0,0,'205.184.181.226','en'),(16519,2729,'2002-05-12 22:00:43',187,'kandrer','thanks, and this is good: <a href=\\\"http://www.wired.com/wired/archive/2.10/godwin.if_pr.html\\\">counter-MEME</a>',1,116,196,0,'205.184.181.226','en'),(16520,2737,'2002-05-12 23:07:57',128,'Javorai','please don\\\'t break and divide atoms.  you could destroy the universe.',0,72,0,0,'129.130.38.124','en'),(16521,2632,'2002-05-12 23:12:46',128,'Javorai',':)',0,2,0,0,'129.130.38.124','en'),(16522,2736,'2002-05-12 23:27:35',66,'Peter','<br><tt>enjoy it; your first few times are unbeatable :)</tt>',1,61,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16523,2736,'2002-05-13 02:01:31',278,'egenus','Wow I was so fucked up last night I couldn\\\'t spell. Hehe...I\\\'ll call it poetic license and say its for effect.',1,116,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16524,2736,'2002-05-13 04:59:43',75,'tyler','strip club on E, huh...right on. that really is fun.\r\n<p>\r\ni quit doing E because it makes me another person, like some alcoholics claim alcohol does to them. the person it makes me is much cooler and more confident, but my mojo comes from not being so somehow. ',1,262,0,0,'165.247.164.62','en'),(16525,2736,'2002-05-13 05:33:09',278,'egenus','Not quite a strip club, but you couldn\\\'t tell for the girl.',1,62,75,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16526,2468,'2002-05-13 15:21:01',278,'egenus','Very cool. I like the existentialist flavour a lot.',1,51,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16527,111,'2002-05-13 15:24:06',278,'egenus','Yes, write in the print world...just sell some copies online. :)',1,64,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16528,2736,'2002-05-13 15:53:50',89,'sawks','<a href=\\\"http://www.f3fs.com/parents/E.mp3\\\">E</a>',5,55,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(16529,2734,'2002-05-13 16:58:03',82,'simon','hi al. this reminds me of someone.',0,34,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16530,2737,'2002-05-13 17:18:09',83,'adamajah','<p>you are never visiting me</p>',0,32,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(16531,2738,'2002-05-13 17:25:52',278,'egenus','Unfortunately most people will go to just about any length to avoid confrontation, even if the confrontation is in their best interest. So the reality is that it takes courage to be honest to yourself, because you have to rely on your own sense of ethics instead of using the opinions of society at large as the rule book for life.',1,331,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16532,2735,'2002-05-13 17:50:40',82,'simon','i like this. it reminds me of the few weeks before i left new york and moved to la.',0,83,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16533,2729,'2002-05-14 01:03:10',218,'da','*goes back through and erases all the swinney.org\\\'s he\\\'s incribed in UCSB bathrooms*',1,90,0,0,'4.47.68.165','en'),(16534,2736,'2002-05-14 02:15:47',148,'jeeff','good timing, i can really relate to this.',0,41,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(16535,109,'2002-05-14 04:37:00',168,'laney','this old one popped up and it was more fun reading it now that i have had the pleasure of meeting the real austin swinney, in the flesh. and come to think of it, he was wearing black and red. hi austin!',0,202,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(16536,1404,'2002-05-14 06:33:36',86,'egg','I saw the ghost of fernando in the rose garden today. I am filled with remorse. ',0,80,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16537,2737,'2002-05-14 06:41:22',148,'jeeff','i feel for you, i have a similar problem.  i have a technology curse.  if i touch anything with moving parts, it will break.  i can\\\'t begin to tell you how many things are wrong with this computer.  all you can do is get used to it, and get a warrantee.',0,256,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(16538,2738,'2002-05-14 09:54:49',295,'karenlee','***I don\\\'t think getting along at any price is a desirable notion. Where do people draw the line? Do people still draw the line? If they draw the line, how many times is it redrawn to avoid contentious issues?*** This is an excellent thing to ponder....really, how CAN you keep your old selves from affecting your present thinking? My answer/idea is that you can\\\'t, but you can be aware of it and prevent it from letting you make destructive choices.\r\n\r\n\r\n',3,462,0,0,'203.194.21.192','en'),(16539,2741,'2002-05-14 12:27:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i totally <i>had</i> to activate this one...</tt>',1,57,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16540,2739,'2002-05-14 14:31:42',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this piece really ranks high on the quality and creativity scales; i like it alot and plan on reading it several times :D</tt>',1,134,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16541,2739,'2002-05-14 14:57:09',278,'egenus','Very interesting. The illustrations were a nifty addition.',1,58,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16542,2741,'2002-05-14 14:59:15',87,'bmg','i also would like a personality test like that..',1,48,0,0,'17.104.194.17','en'),(16543,2741,'2002-05-14 15:00:22',278,'egenus','So called patriots with flags on their cars and \\\"Power of Pride\\\" stickers don\\\'t even realize that a patriot is most often one fighting against the status quo...not for it\\\'s perpetuation. Besides, how patriotic can all these people be if the voter turnout at each election is still less than 20%?',2,307,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16544,2741,'2002-05-14 15:02:46',86,'egg','this is my favorite post by green.',0,34,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16545,2741,'2002-05-14 15:19:18',66,'Peter','<br><tt>also: lots of nyc cab drivers have the following flag sticker on their cabs...<br><br> <img src=\"http://flagsandpennants.safeshopper.com/images/bb0h2ezf.gif\" height=\"50\" width=\"100\"><br><br>flag-waving people remind me of the sort of people that wear metallica t-shirts to a metallica concert. theyre revelling in the fandom of their collective situation.</tt>',1,209,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16546,2739,'2002-05-14 15:47:19',74,'jo','wow. yay.',1,9,0,0,'172.130.97.36','en'),(16547,618,'2002-05-14 15:56:39',86,'egg','I went a resort in scottsdale for my ex-husband\\\'s stepfather\\\'s 70th birthday. we went horseback riding in the desert. those cactuses that only grow an inch every ten years, that kills me. the desert is deep.',0,213,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16548,2739,'2002-05-14 16:50:20',75,'tyler','outstanding.',1,12,0,0,'165.247.165.58','en'),(16549,618,'2002-05-14 17:23:13',17,'austin','i didnt know you were married!  this is a stunning revalation.',3,62,86,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(16550,2743,'2002-05-14 17:39:22',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\\\"walking in downtown seoul is like walking in a rave</i>\\\"<br><br>i love that image...</tt>',1,107,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16551,2739,'2002-05-14 17:42:52',129,'moebius','<br>i love this piece as well. about time some good cartoon-type drawings showed up here.<br><br>also: after reading this i\\\'ve decided to call off that offworld trip to the asteroid belt i had planned for, um, most of next year. sounds like i\\\'d be better off on a remote desert island. :D',1,294,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(16552,618,'2002-05-14 17:43:12',86,'egg','something new every day.',0,24,17,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16553,2741,'2002-05-14 17:55:16',129,'moebius','<br>a couple of weeks ago molls and i found ourselves driving behind a car that was rolling <i>real slow</i>through SF\\\'s south of market. the car\\\'s passengers were waving flags on long wooden poles through each of the car\\\'s windows (including the sunroof). we didn\\\'t have any idea what nationality the flags belonged to. it was all quite mysterious. and then we watched the news that night, and everything became clear.<br><br>they were palestinian flags, of course. <br><br>',3,487,0,0,'148.87.1.165','en'),(16554,618,'2002-05-14 18:12:52',83,'adamajah','scottsdale is a joke.  <br>\r\n<br>\r\n==\\\\/\\\\/3ST S1D3==',0,57,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(16555,618,'2002-05-14 18:19:58',82,'simon','swinney, did we stop in scottsdale for a bagel that one time?',0,61,17,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16556,2739,'2002-05-14 18:21:49',17,'austin','<P>i am wiped out with astonishment.  the way a typical really long one-way plane flight is extrapolated into space migration is fantastic.  there is no doubt in my mind that i would feel the same way on this ship as you represent.  these pictures.  these pictures.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>fantastic (<TT>*****</TT> out of 5)</P>',1,315,0,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(16557,618,'2002-05-14 18:23:32',17,'austin','we got lost and dipped in and out of scottsdale.  i think the bagel was in the city.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nsimon is related to a pack of wolves in scottsdale i think.\r\n',1,157,82,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(16558,618,'2002-05-14 18:25:43',82,'simon','probably. and my (now ex)  friend jessica is related to a married, rich, mega-bitch sorority girl from scottsdale. perhaps therein lies the source of our differences.',0,166,17,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16559,2743,'2002-05-14 19:04:53',278,'egenus','Well damn. I can\\\'t even think of anything to add to that, so I\\\'ll just say thanks and kudos.',1,98,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16560,618,'2002-05-14 19:17:40',66,'Peter','<br><tt>they have bagels in arizona? <i>gasp</i>. my neighbor used to have a chevy pickup; i believe its model was <i>scottsdale</i>, incidentally.</tt>',1,152,82,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16561,2684,'2002-05-14 19:45:20',278,'egenus','<p>to austin: Great posting. Eloquence is the last desperate attempt at dignity for the damned. I can relate.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>to hool: Brilliant. I may have to try that.</p>',1,167,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16562,2739,'2002-05-14 19:49:06',266,'spleenless','\r\nI liked it and I know that fiction\\\'s not supposed to be factual but... from my understanding, there\\\'d be no turbulence in space.  I don\\\'t think the engines would be running all the time either.  There\\\'s no drag, so engines are only needed to accelerate or change course.  o-kay, I\\\'m done now.',0,309,0,0,'66.92.184.86','en'),(16563,2739,'2002-05-14 20:00:18',66,'Peter','<br><tt>forget turbulence; were i flying in space, id be afraid of hitting meteors.</tt>',1,88,266,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16564,2739,'2002-05-14 20:06:50',307,'jeeff&hool','<p>you\\\'re assuming that the \\\'engine\\\' is one similar to an engine that would be used today.  i didn\\\'t imagine it that way.  and it seems you\\\'re assuming that the turbulence spoken of is equivalent to hitting air pockets in an airplane. i didn\\\'t imagine turbulence in that kind of way.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>your comments are valid, and appreciated.  no need to get into techincal validity discussions. i figured there\\\'d be at least one Comic Book Guy response, and any are welcomed. </p>',0,497,266,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(16565,2676,'2002-05-14 20:08:26',300,'ken bit me','onigiri in the subway. yum.\r\ngreat picture of justin in whatever he\\\'s in. o bebe.',0,84,83,0,'205.186.169.207','en'),(16566,2739,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','yay for nice thoughtful swinney content.  this is one of those one-liner pat on the back posts that everyone hates so much.',6,123,307,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16567,2739,'2002-05-14 20:23:08',59,'s5','articles like this are the reason i still read swinney. if there\\\'s ever a \\\"best of,\\\" this piece should definitely be included.',1,135,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16568,2735,'2002-05-14 21:41:03',234,'etienne','i like it a lot.',1,16,187,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(16569,2738,'2002-05-14 21:46:55',234,'etienne','everyone\\\'s conceptions of where the lines should be or are drawn are different anyway.  they redraw it to suit the situation, and most often themselves.',2,155,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(16570,2739,'2002-05-14 21:58:59',234,'etienne','the writing is great; the illustrations add the perfect level.  all too often illustrations or pictures are token & sometimes even only related tangentially, these totally helped set the mood/tone of the writing.  i like this alot.',1,231,307,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(16571,2741,'2002-05-14 22:00:28',234,'etienne','flags are such a strange way of showing your collective belonging.  flags themselves i find strange.',2,100,66,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(16572,2733,'2002-05-14 22:15:42',300,'ken bit me','about people with digital cameras.... do you just carry it around with you when you go out?\r\nJust curious.  I\\\'m saving up for a good $700-1000 camera.\r\n\r\nbeautiful post.',1,172,0,0,'205.186.169.208','en'),(16573,2735,'2002-05-14 22:17:13',300,'ken bit me','that reminds me.. I should start studying for my french satIIs.',0,63,0,0,'205.186.169.208','en'),(16574,2739,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i smell a cartoon series. ROCK.',0,31,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(16575,2733,'2002-05-14 23:25:00',307,'hool','<p>my camera comes with me pretty much everywhere i go, unless i know that i\\\'m heading out to get extremely intoxicated.</p>',0,127,300,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(16576,2739,'2002-05-14 23:26:52',66,'Peter','<br><tt>huh. i smell pea soup, actually. wait, thats my dinner.</tt>',1,68,57,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16577,2738,'2002-05-14 23:42:11',176,'Scapegoat','I don\\\'t think that I am completely isolated from my older self. The problem to other people seems to lie in that I feel a certain degree of shame at the old self. When they feel memories of it should create doubt of my current position. ',0,240,295,0,'64.229.164.160','en'),(16578,2738,'2002-05-15 00:12:09',196,'jasonp','Sometimes people like to keep you pigeonholed from how you were at a previous time out of insecurity.<BR>\nThey might not like it when you\\\'re doing well since it brings out their own feelings of inadequancy. Or they don\\\'t like it when, in their eyes, you aren\\\'t doing that well, as if that deficiency will rub off on them, or drag them back.<BR>\nOr actually in most cases people just change so much that the common bonds that once kept them together just aren\\\'t there anymore.<BR>\n',0,492,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16579,2745,'2002-05-15 00:18:37',196,'jasonp','There\\\'s a buddhist meditation where as usual you focus on your breathing. When any thoughts come up they are categorized as either being based in the future or the past. If it is future then the right index finger is raised, if it\\\'s past the left finger.<BR>\nIt\\\'s interesting to see how most of the time our heads are either scheming and dreaming about the future or recollecting and responding to things from the past.<BR> This detached observation makes it easier to be objective of the thoughts and feelings which can be let go of to again focus on the breathing in the present.<BR>',0,594,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16580,2741,'2002-05-15 00:57:58',168,'laney','i got really fed up with seeing all the american flags around on SUVs, and debated going around with a black permanent marker to vandalize them (like writing \\\"SOLD\\\" across them or something). but not wanting to get myself killed, i opted for slapping a UN flag sticker on my car. got one for da too. it was the best thing we could think of to illustrate where we stand on the whole thing. weâ€™re trying to start an anti-jingoism trend. but we are pretty sure no one even gets it. *sigh*',0,491,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(16581,2746,'2002-05-15 01:43:51',187,'kandrer','a lot can be said; i will just comment your last sentence (it\\\'s a kind of segregation too: whatever you said, only the first and the last sentence are gonna reach people\\\'s brain, ha ha ;))<br>\r\ni don\\\'t think there exists any kind of \\\"pure\\\" behavior of children towards skin color difference (or any other difference, btw).<br>\r\nin an international school, kids must get used to difference, so they don\\\'t pay attention to it anymore. now if there is a big majority of [blue/pink/purple/whatever], even kids, and maybe <i>specially</i> kids, are gonna notice it.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthanks to jasonp, i know now about Godwin\\\'s law, so i am afraid this thread won\\\'t go very far... let\\\'s see! :)\r\n</p><p>\r\nmmm... ken... i didn\\\'t notice you were brown. thanks for telling me. :o)\r\n</p><p>\r\n[x] full of hope\r\n</p>\r\n',1,829,0,0,'208.223.155.199','en'),(16582,2735,'2002-05-15 01:47:34',187,'kandrer','thanks a lot :)',1,15,234,0,'208.223.155.199','en'),(16583,2741,'2002-05-15 02:18:38',187,'kandrer','i speak only for myself but it seems common to me: in france, it\\\'s really rare to have someone proudly displaying a french flag without this someone being an asshole. i\\\'m not sure, though, since i never had anybody i talk to who was displaying a french flag... <br>\r\nso i was quite surprised of this flag-everywhere party in the US. i thought all these people were going to war or something. and that\\\'s maybe what they felt like doing. amazing...',1,455,0,0,'208.223.155.199','en'),(16584,2731,'2002-05-15 06:46:50',312,'samiz','perhaps one of the \\\"children\\\" will be so kind as to spit in your food and provide you with some of that flavor you\\\'re missing.',3,135,0,0,'24.136.83.21','en'),(16585,2747,'2002-05-15 07:34:40',307,'hool','i really like the flow of the last line, it\\\'s really good, except for the last few words.  the sentence doesn\\\'t finish with the flow.  i think something like \\\'still stuck in one from long ago\\\' would work much better.  it really struck me, nonetheless, this line.  ',0,276,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(16586,2740,'2002-05-15 09:47:59',64,'Joanna','3 simple songs that make my body relax:\r\n<LI> \\\'Fade Into You\\\' - Mazzy Star\r\n<LI> \\\'No Suprises\\\' - Radiohead\r\n<LI> \\\'Burger Queen\\\' - Placebo',0,155,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(16587,2747,'2002-05-15 11:06:17',295,'karenlee','Etienne, I prefer the last line how you have it...it flows, the rhythm seems to match what you are saying, and \\\"Many many...etc.\\\" for me reinforces/ expresses the \\\"when did it really all start to be like this\\\" idea. (Whereas \\\"all...long ago\\\" is a more generic statement). :)\r\n',3,294,0,0,'203.194.14.18','en'),(16588,2740,'2002-05-15 12:58:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt><UL TYPE=\\\"square\\\">\r\n<LI>kosheen: \\\'hungry\\\'\r\n<LI>harold melvin & the blue notes: \\\'dont leave me this way\\\'\r\n<LI>paul kalkbrenner: \\\'zeit\\\'\r\n<LI>aretha franklin: \\\'bridge over troubled water\\\'\r\n<LI>white stripes: \\\'hotel yorba\\\'\r\n</UL></tt>',1,274,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16589,2749,'2002-05-15 13:09:58',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i used to have some nice velcro \\\'roos. i remember that they had a zipper pouch i carried found pennies in. they also had one giant velcro closure; it looked like two, but was connected in the middle. i recall being in third grade and cutting the middle part out so i could criss-cross the velcro tabs. seems that that was en vogue at the moment...</tt>',1,363,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16590,2739,'2002-05-15 14:57:00',148,'jeeff','thanks for the kind comments all.  etienne especially, i put a lot of thought into the illustrations beyond just what the pictures would look like.  \r\n\r\n<p>ps. this was a lot of fun, maybe there <i>will</i> be a series.</p>',0,223,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(16591,2751,'2002-05-15 17:18:04',82,'simon','<p>\r\nmy house smells like faint cigarette smoke, WD-40, flowers and damp dirt (from all the plants in the back room off the kitchen).\r\n</p>',0,139,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16592,2740,'2002-05-15 19:03:20',129,'moebius','<br>so here\\\'s what\\\'s is musically happymaking for me today:\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>gang of four-->to hell with poverty\r\n<li>the seconds-->oh no it aint so\r\n<li>life without buildings-->the leanover\r\n<li>wilco-->jesus,etc.\r\n<li>mates of state-->girls singing\r\n<li>louis prima-->beep! beep!\r\n</ul>\r\n<br>\r\ntomorrow, of course, i\\\'ll be into something completely different. <tt>:D</tt>',1,377,0,0,'148.87.1.165','en'),(16593,2739,'2002-05-15 19:50:01',124,'gus','there HAS to be a series! !!',0,28,148,0,'64.172.58.63','en'),(16594,2746,'2002-05-15 19:52:04',300,'ken bit me','ah well- it was my first post.  I gave it a shot. T_T\r\n\r\nby the way, my real name isn\\\'t ken (oii, I\\\'m a girl).. seems to confuse people.',0,142,0,0,'207.93.59.130','en'),(16595,2749,'2002-05-15 19:57:19',300,'ken bit me','ah- &how fast the mode changes.  4th or 5th grade I believe, velcro went out of fashion.  tisk tisk',0,99,0,0,'207.93.59.130','en'),(16596,2746,'2002-05-15 20:00:12',124,'gus','girls with boy names are #1 !! \r\n<p>\r\nhi.',0,41,0,0,'64.172.58.63','en'),(16597,2746,'2002-05-15 20:21:32',82,'simon','yeah, i suppose they\\\'re alright.',0,35,124,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16598,2750,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','aw, this is really good!  YAY HEP!',6,34,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16599,2747,'2002-05-15 22:09:36',234,'etienne','thanks hool and karenlee, its interesting to pull apart the words like that.  i think i do like \\\"many many ago\\\" because it emphasises the time ago, like putting a whole lot of single days next to each other and there are many many of them; whereas long ago makes it seem like it really was far in the past.  maybe?  ',1,322,0,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(16600,2749,'2002-05-15 23:39:32',148,'jeeff','velcro out of fashion?!  <i>never!</i>',0,38,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(16601,2749,'2002-05-15 23:55:11',186,'ook','good grief, i had purple velcro sneakers last year, but no peer group to remind me about all the stylin ways to wear them. thanks, peter!',0,137,0,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(16602,2750,'2002-05-16 00:03:38',186,'ook','<p>double yay!\r\n\r\n<p>one of the things i like best about hanging out with kids is how they are not small adults AT ALL. they are an entirely different kind of being, which to the adult experience can often be intensely weird.',0,225,0,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(16603,2739,'2002-05-16 00:09:52',266,'spleenless','Comic Book Guy?  As in, \\\"Worst episode ever\\\"?  Man, that hurts.',0,69,307,0,'66.92.184.86','en'),(16604,2672,'2002-05-16 01:54:52',291,'da3bous','chi chi the amazing chia sister',1,31,17,0,'63.230.175.201','en'),(16605,2740,'2002-05-16 03:57:54',69,'starlajo','5 songs that have cheered me up recently:\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n<LI>Birthday - The Sugar Cubes<BR>\r\n<LI>Bam Bam - Sister Nancy<BR>\r\n<LI>Super Sharp Shooter - Ganja Kru<BR>\r\n<LI>A Life Less Ordinary - Ash<BR>\r\n<LI>Cannonball - The Breeders',0,227,0,0,'210.54.242.102','en'),(16606,2749,'2002-05-16 04:19:59',234,'etienne','hell yes, the crisscross styles were the way you had to do it!',1,62,0,0,'210.55.125.2','en'),(16607,2750,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','kids are aliens!',6,16,186,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16608,2740,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>songs that make me boing:</p>\r\n<p>\r\nletter from an occupant [the new pornographers]<br />\r\nnew object [adult]<br />\r\nhotel yorba [white stripes]<br />\r\ndwarf invasion [reggie and the full effect]<br />\r\ngo [apples in stereo]<br />\r\nass n\' titties [dj assault]<br />\r\nhalf shark alligator half man [dr. octagon]<br />\r\ntear me down [hedwig & the angry inch]<br />\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>also, i wrote this without reading the article and have since done so and realised that laney has lette from an occupant listed too!  right on!</p>',6,310,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16609,2740,'2002-05-16 12:13:02',234,'etienne','these make me feel rad:\r\n\r\nlast night, the strokes\r\nmaria, blondie\r\nsci-fi wasabi, cibo matto\r\nwe float, pj harvey\r\nthis is hardcore, pulp',2,138,0,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(16610,2753,'2002-05-16 13:43:05',265,'chuckspeed','every generation since the dawn of time has echoed this sentiment.  reality is what you make of it; not what your parents did/did not do.',0,137,0,0,'208.5.114.113','en'),(16611,2748,'2002-05-16 14:32:34',75,'tyler','yes that\\\'s pretty sad.',1,25,0,0,'165.247.165.58','en'),(16612,2750,'2002-05-16 16:55:24',186,'ook','<p>but in a good way... i really do try to let kids know i think they are neat and not freaks. but yes, the larval stage is a weird one to meet.',0,144,62,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(16613,2755,'2002-05-16 17:13:57',246,'erb','maybe you can smile with your eyes.  it worked for lady brett in the sun also rises.  she got all kinds of play by just crinkling up the corners of her eyes.  ',1,159,0,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(16614,2753,'2002-05-16 17:39:29',278,'egenus','<p><i>\\\"Art? Oh yes, I remember. See, I made those pictures before I met daddy, now I donÂ´t have time. Time, mommy, whatÂ´s that? ThatÂ´s when you grow up, and everything you hoped for in life suddenly gets over taken by the monsters of the world, the people that want your money, the people that want to steal that little person inside of you...\\\"</i> This unforunately describes my mother; repressing everything unique and individualistic about herself, always trying to martyr herself into heaven.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Great posting. While the responsibility to do something productive with our lives lies in our hands, our parents and society haven\\\'t left us much to work with.',1,675,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16615,2754,'2002-05-16 18:01:30',17,'austin','i am fascinated by these new york city posts.  i have been there, but i think i missed these fabric stitchings.  i live here in santa cruz and maybe i am missing them, though i do go to the beaches and watch surfers at dusk.  that is kind of the same sort of culture worship.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni will move to new york for a little while some day.  maybe when i am a retired old man.  maybe in five years.',2,397,0,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(16616,2754,'2002-05-16 18:17:15',185,'orion','You never know.  Buy the right lotto ticket, put $1000 into the right stock, save some wealthy old lady\\\'s poodle FiFi that wanders onto the freeway, invent a new flavor of slurpee, and you could be retired in five years.  Anything\\\'s possible. :-)',1,252,17,0,'216.201.248.18','en'),(16617,2754,'2002-05-16 18:17:40',82,'simon','i love them too. when i lived in new york i had a column in a little magazine and tried to write about things like this. my favorite was a little lean-to built on s 1st and union streets in williamsburg. someone had lugged out a couch and hung up a hose with a sprinkler attachment (it was a real hot summer) and there was a shrine next to it for one of the unknown bikers (peurto rican bike gang in the neighborhood). i hung out by that corner for a bit (drinking a tallboy and smoking) every night for almost two months.',0,522,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16618,2755,'2002-05-16 18:52:45',67,'trouble','tyler, while most will disagree tis alright not to smile.  i\\\'m not much of a smiler myself, i\\\'m cool with that and amused sometimes because (and i\\\'m sure you have seen this) <i>not smiling</i> tends to make others around you <i>mad nervous</i>.  most times people can\\\'t understand that not smiling doesn\\\'t always mean unhappiness, doesn\\\'t always mean ugliness and doesn\\\'t always mean you don\\\'t have a sense of humor.  i prefer to look at the whole issue of \\\'why don\\\'t you smile?\\\' as nothing more than people that don\\\'t smile all the time are perhaps more content with themselves and their surroundings.  while this applies to me sometimes i will admit... i\\\'m a neurotic fuck sometimes.  *best friendly*',1,741,0,0,'139.76.65.132','en'),(16619,2754,'2002-05-16 19:11:18',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i met a guy who lives in his car on 5th street, between avenues a and b. he survives on enough cash for rolling papers and a bite to eat now and then, it seems. so, instead of winning the lottery, one could ebay some of their surplus tech gear, tired cd\'s, non-working vehicles, etc. and come on an extended budget visit. basically, thats what i did, and i ended up here forever. also: having friends here gives you places to crash for free ;) i have been spoiled, what with all the s.o friends and others stopping by as of late; my wish is that i could show tons of good people all over this city which i am hopelessly in love with.</tt>',1,459,17,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16620,2753,'2002-05-16 20:19:53',65,'le','hello eve. i live with you. i want to read this but i also want to check my email before the line of SPIRITUALLY ORIENTED COMPUTER VULTURES behind me gets antsy. love.',4,167,0,1,'208.239.147.211','en'),(16621,2753,'2002-05-16 20:26:14',86,'egg','uh, I dont think it\'s the eve you think it is.',0,49,0,1,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16622,2750,'2002-05-16 20:29:31',187,'kandrer','right! and women are aliens too! :D',1,35,62,0,'208.223.155.89','en'),(16623,2753,'2002-05-16 21:02:04',86,'egg','my parents tried really hard. it\\\'s me who\\\'s the jerk.',0,59,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16624,2757,'2002-05-16 21:06:51',86,'egg','are you growing a beard?',0,24,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16625,2757,'2002-05-16 21:43:49',17,'austin','MY GOD I CANT CONCENTRATE, WHY IS THAT PILLOW OVER THERE ASKEW!!!???????\r\n<BR><BR>\r\noh, hi.  if you need me to send you some rice, let me know.  or doritos.  or pizza boats packed in dry ice.',3,191,0,0,'205.179.19.89','en'),(16626,2753,'2002-05-16 22:01:22',311,'ntl','in addition to the denial many parents participate in.  we are all fighting against the images thrown at us daily.  it\\\'s not only that the older generations are in denial about the lives of today\\\'s \\\"youth,\\\" but they are also ignoring the a major force which influences (despite the fact that we all wish it didn\\\'t) our behavior.  that being the media.  ',0,368,0,0,'144.92.164.199','en'),(16627,2739,'2002-05-16 22:24:33',206,'josta','this is fantastic. a series would be great.',1,43,0,0,'64.12.106.32','en'),(16628,2739,'2002-05-16 22:25:18',206,'josta','this is fantastic. a series would be great.\r\nalso, this \\\"best of\\\" swinney.org thing; is that actually in the works? i\\\'d buy a hardback compilation in a second.',1,168,0,0,'64.12.106.32','en'),(16629,2757,'2002-05-16 22:38:23',265,'chuckspeed','gee le - it sounds cool.  stay away from the cheese, tho - it\\\'s government surplus...',0,88,0,0,'172.160.83.35','en'),(16630,2753,'2002-05-16 22:44:51',265,'chuckspeed','that\\\'s a great comment, man - really.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>in an age of seemingly limitless choices, we\\\'ve managed to \\\'pop 40\\\' our lives.  same shit all over the place - uniformity rules.  Nose rings, green hair and labrets?  Standard issue non-conformist uniform - same wherever you go.  hard to just \\\'be\\\'...',0,316,278,0,'172.160.83.35','en'),(16631,2757,'2002-05-16 23:14:36',66,'Peter','<br><tt>they let you drive the golf cart!</tt>',1,46,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16632,2753,'2002-05-16 23:15:04',234,'etienne','i think some of what my parents do/think i don\\\'t want to know; they\\\'re older than most of the people my age [i\\\'m 21, my father is 70, my mother 57] & it shows in some of their attitudes.  they don\\\'t like me to drink beer, or wear sneakers.  they make racist generalisation and are homophobic.  <br>despite this, my siblings & i are not and we try to educate them back.  it has to go both ways sometimes.',1,415,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(16633,2754,'2002-05-16 23:16:29',234,'etienne','yes, your writing about the city makes me long to visit it even more.  ',1,71,66,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(16634,2757,'2002-05-16 23:19:08',234,'etienne','my stepfather used to have a golf cart.  once my brother & i stole it & rode around the neighbourhood on it.  that thing was pretty fast when you got it going.  its just a pity that we side-swiped the letterbox with it when returning it.',1,237,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(16635,2754,'2002-05-16 23:35:38',66,'Peter','<br><tt>then you should! :D</tt>',1,32,234,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16636,2757,'2002-05-17 00:11:17',231,'alana','leatherman, maglight, aw yeah.\r\n\r\nlady elly, i wrote you a letter.\r\nexpect it.',2,78,0,0,'203.22.202.131','en'),(16637,2754,'2002-05-17 00:11:38',129,'moebius','<br><a href=\\\"http://www.npr.org/programs/lnfsound/stories/990723.stories.html\\\">this</a> is one of my favorite non-Peter-related NYC stories. sit down & listen to it, if you have the time. it always makes me smile.<br><br>my molls has been handling some projects for an NYC-based firm for awhile now. if things go well with that, there\\\'s a good possibility they\\\'ll be wanting her over there in the head office. and if she\\\'s going over there, i\\\'m going over there too. i\\\'m no fool. (i\\\'m not promisin\\\' nothin\\\', peter, but it\\\'s possible...!)',1,570,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(16638,2755,'2002-05-17 00:47:28',295,'karenlee','I will smile a lot for you if you explain your comment on my article!!',3,70,0,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(16639,2748,'2002-05-17 00:48:17',295,'karenlee','Huh? I wrote this because I had reached a happy conclusion, not a sad one. :)',3,77,75,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(16640,2757,'2002-05-17 00:49:44',278,'egenus','<p>To le: Perfectionism can be a focusing method I suppose. Sounds like your kind of fun. :)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>To austin: hehe</p>',1,122,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16641,2757,'2002-05-17 00:54:38',64,'Joanna','What\\\'s a pizza boat?',1,23,17,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(16642,2753,'2002-05-17 01:39:48',176,'Scapegoat','<b>Media Smedia....</b><br><br>\r\nThis argument lives up to my name. Although I often feel as a generalization older folks are a little more regressive then youth. Yes, thats a little ageist but if I\\\'m going to talk in broad sweeping strokes.<br><br>\r\nI don\\\'t think that it gets worse  generation to generation though. I  imagine that this generational blame placing is just a way for people to blow off steam and shift responsiblity off themselves.<br><br>\r\nIf anything I think that given the increase in levels of social entropy we have at least stayed even, if not progressed. I\\\'d even go so far as to say that most frustration of this kind isn\\\'t really due to degredation but rather anger at the slow pace of <i>progress</i>.  ',1,742,311,0,'64.229.164.160','en'),(16643,2753,'2002-05-17 01:52:04',314,'eve','IÂ´d like to add that I think this writing may have been taken to the wrong effect. This was not directed at general society/parents/generations persay, just merely the cynical ramblings  of someone sleep deprived at 4 a.m. 50% biographical, 25% cynical, 25% over dramatic.',0,272,0,0,'217.228.165.56','en'),(16644,2757,'2002-05-17 02:15:55',89,'sawks','i find the concept of le regularly getting up  at 5:30am very endearing :)',1,74,0,0,'144.137.0.110','en'),(16645,2754,'2002-05-17 02:57:56',234,'etienne','maybe i will!  then you\\\'ll be sorry!',1,39,66,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(16646,2754,'2002-05-17 03:27:16',66,'Peter','<br><tt>sorry? <i>pshaw!</i></tt>',1,33,234,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16647,2755,'2002-05-17 03:39:00',75,'tyler','well karen, you talk about thinking that everyone around you has a better life than yours, which is untrue and sad. you mention an undefined sadness that just is, which is called depression. also sad. then you say that you will develop obsessions which is unhealthy and sad. what will these obsessions be?\r\n<p>\r\nwhat is making you sad? do people around you <i>really</i> have better lives than yours? when i feel that way all i have to do is go shopping at wal-mart and see the people there and i am reassured. you are obviously a bright person, to be writing and using a computer and the internet.',1,598,295,0,'165.247.165.58','en'),(16648,2757,'2002-05-17 04:05:21',124,'gus','I could symetrically stack pillows for hours. and arrange chairs. mmm!',0,70,0,0,'64.163.215.108','en'),(16649,2757,'2002-05-17 04:21:44',66,'Peter','<br><tt>one of my flatmates is a recovering obsessive/compulsive; she used to spend hours and hours straightening the fringe on the rug. since we got a fringeless rug to combat her bad habit, she now yells at us 24/7 about weird things like making the stairway squeak, opening the blind too high or too low, or touching the bike rack.</tt>',1,339,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16650,2757,'2002-05-17 05:07:34',17,'austin','<P>a pizza boat is one of these:</P>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.stouffers.com/images/pizza3.jpg\">\r\n<P>there is no better frozen pizza device in existence.  no better.</P>',3,170,64,0,'64.174.155.58','en'),(16651,2755,'2002-05-17 09:48:07',295,'karenlee','well, tyler, hi again, it seems I thought everyone would undestand that what I was expressing was a frame of mind, not a personal belief system of mine. I think it would be fairly preposterous if all humans denied they ever had feelings of insecurity, etc. SO, what I was expressing was a fleeting mood, which was alleviated by realising that all I needed to do was find things to obsess myself. By obsessions, I do not mean bad things like stalking people or repeatedly counting the pegs on the clothesline. I mean good things, things that enliven me, things that make me smile when I wake up. As for Wal-mart.....it is when I go to such places for consolation that I really start to worry!!!!! I hope you understand now.. Have a great day.\r\n',3,743,75,0,'203.194.10.74','en'),(16652,2748,'2002-05-17 09:50:54',295,'karenlee','See reply under your article.',3,29,75,0,'203.194.10.74','en'),(16653,2748,'2002-05-17 09:58:23',295,'karenlee','well, tyler, hi again, it seems I thought everyone would undestand that what I was expressing was a frame of mind, not a personal belief system of mine. I think it would be fairly preposterous if all humans denied they ever had feelings of insecurity, etc. SO, what I was expressing was a fleeting mood, which was alleviated by realising that all I needed to do was find things to obsess myself. By obsessions, I do not mean bad things like stalking people or repeatedly counting the pegs on the clothesline. I mean good things, things that enliven me, things that make me smile when I wake up. As for Wal-mart.....it is when I go to such places for consolation that I really start to worry!!!!! I hope you understand now.. Have a great day.\r\n',3,743,0,0,'203.194.10.74','en'),(16654,2754,'2002-05-17 12:59:16',174,'Mad Moll','<p>I still haven\\\'t been able to get back into NYC.</p>\r\n<p>For a year or so I used to spend almost every weekend in either brooklyn or the e. village. I keep meaning to go back, but I can hear Gotham whispering, \\\"I <i>will</i> stress you out.\\\"</p>',0,256,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(16655,2754,'2002-05-17 13:06:44',66,'Peter','<br><tt>I still haven\'t been able to get back into Jersey.<br><br>\r\n\r\nFor a year or so I used to spend almost every weekend in either scotch plains or south plainfield. I keep meaning to go back, but I can hear the pineys whispering, \"you <i>will</i> fear the mullet.\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>heh</b><br><br>\r\nreally, though; you should come back sometime. things are very nice here now, and the springtime weather is quite auspicious.\r\n</tt>\r\n',1,276,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16656,2755,'2002-05-17 13:10:52',174,'Aq\\\'chk\\\'uu\\\'ng Moll','<p>Hmm. That\\\'s pretty rough. I\\\'m kind of in the same boat -- I can\\\'t make the physical movements to express emotion very easily. I\\\'ve actually lost friendships because, even though I\\\'m showing interest in a person and making an effort to hang out with them, I\\\'m not giving off emotional body-language and it kreeps them out.<p>',6,345,75,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(16657,2758,'2002-05-17 13:14:19',278,'egenus','Sartre suggested that our hell is other people in \\\"No Exit\\\", using the example of people stuck in a room with an unlocked door. They stayed because without each other they couldn\\\'t tell what was real. Self-deception keeps us where we are when the logical decision is to move.',1,284,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16658,2422,'2002-05-17 13:18:51',278,'egenus','Goddamnit I want some fucking acid. It has ceased to exist here. And all I wanted was a little enlightenment...',1,111,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16659,2757,'2002-05-17 13:59:39',74,'jo','i once help my ex set up chairs at his synagogue for the services that night and i agree that it\\\'s meditative to devote yourself to a task that creates order for spiritual gatherings. ',1,187,0,0,'172.135.95.213','en'),(16660,2757,'2002-05-17 16:10:04',74,'jo','i say bring back the rug with fringe. ocd people need their little compulsions or they\\\'re a pain in everyone\\\'s ass.',1,121,66,0,'172.153.219.151','en'),(16661,2757,'2002-05-17 16:18:25',186,'ook','this sounds a lot like being a summer camp counsellor, depending on what activities you find it rewarding to help people with. spiritual growth vs. crafts and outdoors skills. setting up circles vs. aligning lean-tos to the trees, wind and sun. outdoor communities + people = good times.',0,287,0,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(16662,2757,'2002-05-17 16:25:03',66,'Peter','<br><tt>omega has neat meditative stacks of stones everywhere; i want to bring lots of stones into my apartment and stack them, meditatively.</tt>',1,146,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16663,2754,'2002-05-17 16:45:34',82,'simon','i will be there on tuesday.',0,27,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16664,2753,'2002-05-17 17:20:56',311,'ntl','for one example: http://www.about-face.org/index.html',0,53,176,0,'144.92.164.199','en'),(16665,2665,'2002-05-17 18:10:08',187,'kandrer','hey lotus, how are u doing? how is the groom???',1,47,156,0,'205.184.181.94','en'),(16666,2757,'2002-05-17 18:30:56',187,'kandrer','H took a few classes for japanese tea ceremony. (damn, i just asked her, and \\\"a few\\\" was actually once a week for... a year.) anyway they taught her to count a specific number of \\\"rice straw\\\" on the tatami to know where to place each object.<br>\r\ni never thought yoga people would pay attention to the alignment of cushions, but that makes sense. i think about the last part of the movie Kundun when the monks make this big \\\"drawing\\\" with colored sand.<br>\r\nring the bell, elly! :)',1,499,0,0,'205.184.181.94','en'),(16667,2755,'2002-05-17 18:56:04',187,'kandrer','i have been angry at you since your first comment at one of my post. you wrote <i>\\\"you should say thank you and mean it\\\"</i>. i was not very angry, but since then i thought \\\"tyler can be a perfect moron\\\". facts are that i can be a perfect moron too, and that you often sound much nicer than some of your short comments. so here is my moron\\\'s theory about you: people don\\\'t go away from you because a lack of smile on your face but because of a few misunderstood comments. what do you think?<br>\r\n[x] not angry anymore but rather provocative',1,558,75,0,'205.184.181.94','en'),(16668,2708,'2002-05-17 19:58:19',315,'theprophet','elly millican (milkcan) is a total fucking tool and a slutty bitch. \r\n\r\nhave a nice life!',1,89,0,0,'64.175.80.68','en'),(16669,2757,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i do that with books... they are stacked everywhere!',1,52,66,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(16670,2753,'2002-05-18 01:05:06',176,'Scapegoat','I may seem callous but I have worked in media, including media awareness, the source of the problems come from within the individual not the media.<br><br>\r\nWith the huge spectrum of shapes and sizes we humans come in it seems  telling to me that the <i>negative</i> or so called images that we put in our media are the same ones that ensure our viewing.<br><br>\r\nThere will always be an ideal and dominant image of the human body. No matter what the image is, there will always be people that are affected negatively.<br><br>\r\nbest friendly',0,541,311,0,'64.229.164.160','en'),(16671,2708,'2002-05-18 01:05:53',278,'egenus','<p>Moll: Die, you\\\'re littering swinney.org.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>le: :)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>w00t for chiamattt.</p>',0,97,0,0,'24.25.107.161','en'),(16672,2757,'2002-05-18 01:29:23',240,'xep','one of my favorite things about rock climbing was wandering through the woods looking for the route and finding the little cairns of stones that people had neatly stacked to show the way.  tiny signs of like minded people in the middle of the wilderness.\r\n',0,256,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(16673,2759,'2002-05-18 20:58:14',187,'kandrer','nice poem. i\\\'m not sure about the colon thing, though. but i like the poem.',1,78,0,0,'205.184.181.106','en'),(16674,2759,'2002-05-18 21:42:10',66,'Peter','<br><tt>colons get a bad rap; but not as badly as my man the <i>semi-colon</i>;<br><br>a wise man once said:<br><br><i><a href=\\\"http://www.antrestica.org/2002_04_07_archive.htm\\\">semi-colons</a> are like the rolling grassy hills in an otherwise concrete hell of abrupt punctuation.</i></tt>\r\n',1,297,187,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16675,2759,'2002-05-18 22:31:24',187,'kandrer','antrestica! i am both confused and ready for devotion toward antrestica. that\\\'s your baby, peter?',1,100,66,0,'205.184.181.106','en'),(16676,2743,'2002-05-18 22:59:04',187,'kandrer','it think that our societies have here reached a kind of balance. unfortunately, a balanced state doesn\\\'t have to be good: it just needs to be stable. there might be other balanced states that we could have ended up in, but from where we are now, they are out of reach.<br>\r\ni am afraid that the only way to get out of our current balanced state would be to destroy it completely. this would lead to a huge mess, followed by a new balanced state, maybe different from the current one and maybe similar.</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni am dreaming of a new movement, a kind of religion maybe, that would slowly but inexorably replace people. look at the scifi movies: the only one where the aliens succeed to conquer the earth is \\\"body snatchers\\\". i trust scifi more than history. scifi was sometimes right, while history did it all wrong.</p>',1,830,0,0,'205.184.181.106','en'),(16677,2759,'2002-05-19 00:16:07',295,'karenlee','Perhaps PG intended the pause of a colon here, like an introduction to a scene, rather than a flowing sentence. Maybe. That\\\'s how I read it.',3,143,0,0,'203.221.83.10','en'),(16678,2757,'2002-09-05 18:40:19',18,'ix','yeah i think the 5.30 thing is a lie.',0,37,0,0,'63.194.210.116','en'),(16679,2757,'2002-05-19 04:45:10',65,'le','mmm pizza boats would hit the spot right now',0,44,0,0,'208.239.147.211','en'),(16680,2757,'2002-05-19 05:32:29',65,'le','well, i haven\\\'t had a 6am shift yet, so yeah. but i just got off work at midnight so. i told them i\\\'m better with being 3 to midnight instead of 6am to 10am, or whatever. we\\\'ll see. why does everyone have to always point out that i can\\\'t get up early? ',4,264,16,0,'208.239.147.211','en'),(16681,2757,'2002-05-19 05:41:38',66,'Peter','<br><tt>getting up early is overrated, anyway...</tt>',1,53,65,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16682,2759,'2002-05-19 06:10:07',175,'Paul G','There is a rather lame academic joke to be made about a Dr. of English and inflamed <a href=\\\"http://www.fas.harvard.edu/~wricntr/semi.html\\\">semi-colons</a> and <a href=\\\"http://www.nutsandboltsguide.com/punctuation.html#colons\\\">colons</a>; in any case, I do have something else on my mind: Hong Kong.\r\n\r\n<p>Thank you for commenting.',0,343,0,0,'67.248.52.230','en'),(16683,2759,'2002-05-19 06:51:05',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>between independent clauses as an alternative to using a comma and a conjunction!</i></tt>',1,101,175,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16684,2760,'2002-05-19 07:21:32',66,'Peter','<br><tt><UL TYPE=\"square\">\r\n<li>scootching metal-footed chairs across terazzo floors\r\n<li>salsa music\r\n<li>my alarm clock\r\n<li>my flatmate\'s moose-like, loud, grating nervous laughter\r\n<li>the computerized voice on the new subway cars\r\n<li>scratched records\r\n<li>tv commercials that have phones ringing in them\r\n</UL>\r\n<br><br>also: i am guilty of neck-popping; so gross, yet feels so good!</tt>',1,401,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16685,2759,'2002-05-19 10:18:12',234,'etienne','devotion to antrestica is a must!',1,33,187,0,'210.86.44.125','en'),(16686,1140,'2002-05-19 13:33:43',317,'grace','mix-tapes are something i confess to indulging in quite regularly. i find that making them clears my mind in a way nothing else can. i am so consumed with the organising of the songs that i am transported into a secret little world for a few hours. it is pure bliss when i realise that i have created something that captures my mood for that day and will live on for years to come.',0,381,0,0,'203.56.239.116','en'),(16687,2760,'2002-05-19 15:01:34',307,'hool','i fucking hate whistling.  there is no more self indulgent sound to make than whistling.  i can handle whistling if it\\\'s of the really skilled melodious variety, but usually, it isn\\\'t.  usually, it\\\'s off key in both pitch and cheer.  (even worse, i hate people <i>with</i> whistles at any sort of musical event, but that\\\'s another story).  there\\\'s something about the higher pitches that really poke in my ears, and the assault from a self indulgence act only makes it worse.  \r\n\r\ni think of chief wiggum starving the attack dogs and whipping them into a vicious frenzy by singing off key on purpose.  that\\\'s what whistling does to me.\r\n\r\ni fucking hate whistling.',0,682,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(16688,2751,'2002-05-19 18:02:22',176,'Scapegoat','This made me sad a little but in good way, if that makes any sense.',0,67,0,0,'64.229.162.222','en'),(16689,2760,'2002-05-19 18:17:21',176,'Scapegoat','<li>loud crying\r\n<li>pretentious tone of voice (think stereotypical)\r\n<li>Car Horns, especially living downtown after a sporting event\r\n<li>Cars or motorbikes with bad or no mufflers\r\n<li>crickets\r\n<li>Construction outside my window in the summer, specifically the reverse beep of heavy machinery<br><br>\r\n<b>Classic Pick</b><br><br>\r\n<li>Nails on a blackboard\r\n',0,362,0,0,'64.229.162.222','en'),(16690,2760,'2002-05-19 19:08:30',74,'jo','wow. i love the sounds of both whistling <b>and</b> crickets. in fact, my roommate and i were both just talking yesterday about how much we love it when people whistle loudly and unselfconsciously. ',1,198,0,0,'172.158.98.224','en'),(16691,2760,'2002-05-19 21:16:26',240,'xep','<li> people doing bad fake latin american accents\r\n<li> babies crying\r\n<li> adults doing baby talk at anything/anyone\r\n<li> harley davidsons\r\n<li> whining dogs\r\n<li> people fighting with people they know (as opposed to random people yelling at eachother on the street)\r\n<li> the noise of the motor in my video camera when it\\\'s about to eject a tape, rewind, or fast forward\r\n<li> the breaking of silence unless well framed\r\n<li> loud noise in general\r\n<li> talk radio\r\n',0,472,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(16692,2755,'2002-05-19 21:28:09',75,'tyler','karen, i see what you mean. i disagree that for your life to have meaning you must be obsessed by the things you enjoy; you and i might have a different definition of the word \\\'obsession\\\'...which means to be involved with something to the exclusion of everything else. to do that is to become less than human, to become a machine focused on one task or set of tasks. what it implies is that you want to avoid life by reducing your worldview to a few projects, petty or otherwise. and i think that to do that would be, in fact, as i said, sad. \r\n<p>\r\nbut- like i said, i think we are using different definitions for \\\'obsession\\\'.',1,639,295,0,'165.247.164.202','en'),(16693,2755,'2002-05-19 21:41:02',75,'tyler','i guess something about a sarcastic thanks to God or whoever you believe in for the gift of life rubs me the wrong way. so yeah, i think you should say thank you and mean it or go ahead and donate your organs. as far as people &quot;going away from me&quot, i didn\\\'t say that people did, i just said that it was more difficult for me to express my true feelings through my facial expression and that this is a social handicap that, as i explained in the post, i have found ways to overcome. also i was trying to imply that my life has been the richer for it, and to take it one step further, for that i thank god.',1,616,187,0,'165.247.164.202','en'),(16694,2760,'2002-05-19 23:01:51',59,'s5','i was going to say \\\"babies crying\\\" too. and the sound of the phone ringing when i\\\'m sleeping. :( :( :(',6,111,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16695,2760,'2002-05-19 23:28:50',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i do the tooth-whistle as opposed to the lip-whistle. youd be surprised how many people absolutely HATE it; and im quite good at it too. oh well. better i had spent my time studying calculus or something than perfecting the tooth whistle. it appears to be so socially repugnant that i can only get away with it in private, or while driving or otherwise doing something blanketed with lots of ambient white noise.</tt>',1,425,74,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16696,2760,'2002-05-19 23:30:17',66,'Peter','<br><tt>speaking of... i love when loud sirens or subway trains pass. it allows me the opportunity to scream excessively loud [or whistle, for that matter] in a public place without arousing too much suspicion. you have to time it jsut right, though, or you embarass yourself.</tt>',1,282,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16697,2760,'2002-05-19 23:31:54',195,'matt','<BR><BR>*when people clear their throat only halfway then talk with that phlegm-laden voice. my sister calls it the \\\"creamy\\\" voice.<BR>*a transmission grinding<BR>*chihuahuas or other similar yapping little dogs<BR>*doors slamming<BR>*certain northern accents<BR>*some cell phone rings/tones<BR>*when people on mass transit with you or in line with you let loud, ripping farts',1,382,0,0,'209.208.109.163','en'),(16698,2760,'2002-05-20 00:01:15',187,'kandrer','damn right! i usually yell with sirens, bark with dogs and cry loud with babies. this helps me a lot. if my timing is wrong, that\\\'s true that people stare at me, but i think this is more by envy. they wish they could shout too. :)',1,233,66,0,'205.184.183.60','en'),(16699,2760,'2002-05-20 00:10:01',187,'kandrer','having reviewed your list, it seems that you are rather upseted by the <i>meaning</i> of these sounds. mmm... anyway i think it\\\'s the case for everybody. let\\\'s have a minute of pity for these old people who are upseted by sounds of kids playing in a backyard (<i>arghhh! some kids are happy over there... i can\\\'t stand this!</i>)',1,338,74,0,'205.184.183.60','en'),(16700,2759,'2002-05-20 00:31:22',187,'kandrer','in fact, i <i>wanted</i> to make a criticism, and this is the only thing i found. i will make a short post to explain that...',1,125,0,0,'205.184.183.60','en'),(16701,2759,'2002-05-20 01:12:01',187,'kandrer','<a ref=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2763\\\">there</a> it is. :)',1,84,0,0,'205.184.183.60','en'),(16702,2755,'2002-05-20 01:42:31',187,'kandrer','<p>i don\\\'t think one can be sarcasticaly talking to god (or thanking). i mean if one believes in Him. obviously, i don\\\'t. so let\\\'s say my post was in a way addressed to believers, and the sarcasm was that i didn\\\'t find (at that very moment) that life is a thing we can say thanks for.<br>\r\nso excuse me, but you just make no point, as a believer, to comment that you don\\\'t agree. that\\\'s why i couldn\\\'t understand your comment\\\'s meaning. arguing about ideas and beliefs is always fine, but you are not a world-reference for people to be interested by a one-sentence sharp comment expressing if you agree or not. right or wrong?\r\n</p><p>\r\nadded to that, my post is in a short series titled \\\"the happy complainer\\\" and i have no other goal in there than to complain, should i be wrong. i beg you and everyone to suspect 2nd degree in each word i can write and say.\r\n</p>',3,896,75,0,'205.184.183.60','en'),(16703,2757,'2002-05-20 01:54:18',187,'kandrer','there is a war between the ones that wake up early and the ones that can\\\'t. it is purely unfair. there is obviously a physical difference between them, but some pretend it\\\'s laziness - usually they are the ones who can\\\'t keep their eyes open after 10pm.<br>\r\ni say this is segregation. (guess in which camp i stand)',1,324,0,0,'205.184.183.60','en'),(16704,2757,'2002-05-20 02:10:04',66,'Peter','<br><tt>dont forget the ones that get up insanely early and go to bed too late :D</tt>',1,86,187,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16705,2762,'2002-05-20 02:12:56',187,'kandrer','except the first photo, i liked it all. i guess you could not take one of the passengers\\\' faces, uh? ;) see also: the tunnel in the movie \\\"Yume\\\" (dreams) from Kurosawa.',1,177,0,0,'205.184.183.60','en'),(16706,2760,'2002-05-20 02:37:27',74,'jo','you\\\'re partly right, but a lot of the sounds which annoy people classically boil down to <i>friction between uncomplementary materials</i>, i.e. plastic w/ styrofoam, flesh w/alumninum, nails w/ chalkboard, chair casters on the floor, etc. etc. i am <b>especially</b> annoyed when those sounds coincide with obnoxious behavior. incidentally, i also hate long fingernails being rapped rhythmically on a hard surface as a manifestation of impatience. this happened in the waiting room of a planned parenthood a few weeks ago and it took everything in my power not to walk over to the girl and SLAP her hand.<BR>\r\nalso, i don\\\'t think that i dislike the sound of babies crying for the same reason that most people dislike it: it\\\'s not the sound itself that bothers me but feeling the parents\\\' embarassment and hopelessness at their child\\\'s crying. i want badly to help in some way but know that the polite thing is to pretend that i don\\\'t hear it / it doesn\\\'t bother me.',1,987,187,0,'172.174.42.30','en'),(16707,2757,'2002-05-20 02:40:29',59,'s5','<a href=\\\"http://www.stanford.edu/~dement/delayed.html\\\">an article about night owls</a>, by stanford\\\'s own dr. william dement.',0,134,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16708,2760,'2002-05-20 02:44:30',59,'s5','agreed, it\\\'s not about the sound of a baby crying, but knowing the parent has put their child in a situation that is inappropriate / boring / stressful. the sounds of poor parenting are toxic to my ears. also, i reserve the right to eat my words if or when i become a parent.',0,278,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16709,2760,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>i think humans are pre-programmed to respond on a very deep level to crying babies.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i never think that the parents are embarrased.  i think they probably just hate the crying as much as i do and are resiting the urge to smother the baby as much as i am ;)</p>',6,272,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16710,2753,'2002-05-20 03:04:30',187,'kandrer','i am sorry i didn\\\'t read carefully enough what you answered to, Scapegoat, but i am quite sure you are reducing a bit when you say:<br>\r\n1- <i>the source of the problems come from within the individual not the media.</i>\r\n<br>and<br>\r\n2- <i>There will always be an ideal and dominant image of the human body.</i><br>\r\nsituation can only be more complex. and, please, why not wait to be an old fart before saying <i>always</i> again? ;)<br>\r\n[x] not minding my own business',3,475,176,0,'205.184.183.60','en'),(16711,2762,'2002-05-20 03:08:51',66,'Peter','<br><tt>the first one is merely for context... no streaky lights in the station.</tt>',1,85,187,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16712,2757,'2002-05-20 03:17:59',187,'kandrer','damn, it\\\'s so well describing me... H even got the idea of what they call Chronotherapy; <i>if we cannot go to bed earlier, why not continue to delay forward until we reach the other end?</i>',1,194,59,0,'205.184.183.60','en'),(16713,2762,'2002-05-20 03:27:33',66,'Peter','<img src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/images/72_1.jpg\\\"><br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/futurecity/path.jpg\\\"><br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/images/train.jpg\\\"<br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/futurecity/train2.jpg\\\"><br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/futurecity/newyorkpenn.jpg\\\"><br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/futurecity/newarksub.jpg\\\"><br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/futurecity/subway.jpg\\\"><br>',1,482,187,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16714,2757,'2002-05-20 03:34:53',124,'gus','huh. that describes me as an adolescent pretty well. I would lie in bed each night forever and not fall asleep. I\\\'d sleep till late afternoon on weekends. boy did my mom hate it.',0,181,0,0,'64.163.213.99','en'),(16715,2762,'2002-05-20 03:37:19',187,'kandrer','4,7,1',1,5,66,0,'205.184.183.60','en'),(16716,2757,'2002-05-20 03:39:37',187,'kandrer','yeah, parents are so quick to think you\\\'re gonna do like that you\\\'re whole life... :)',1,91,124,0,'205.184.183.60','en'),(16717,2762,'2002-05-20 04:24:34',66,'Peter','<br><tt>?</tt>',1,14,187,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16718,2760,'2002-05-20 06:01:02',74,'jo','poor parenting usually has little to do with why small children cry in public places. it\\\'s a combination of crowds and their being too small to understand why they can\\\'t have their way about something: i.e. why they eat <i>right that instant</i>, why they can\\\'t have the toy that is locked inside the car miles away. if public transit of any kind, particularly planes, factors in, it\\\'s a whole other ballgame, for a variety of factors not limited to being in a cramped, loud, moving vehicle.<BR>\r\ni mean, if a parent is YELLING at their child, that\\\'s a different story altogether, but.',3,600,59,0,'172.137.250.204','en'),(16719,2760,'2002-05-20 06:37:23',196,'jasonp','There aren\\\'t that many sounds that really bother me except for ingrained ones like metal screeching sounds, chewing on tinfoil, loud alarms, baby crying, etc.<BR><BR>\r\nCome to think of it I\\\'m only sensitive to sounds when I\\\'m at work. In fact after thinking about it some more I realize that the majority of the sounds that bugged me at work came from one person -- the guy in the cubicle next to me. My cubicle neighbor was a middle-aged russian programmer guy with a lot of really annoying habits. He was kind of amusing and friendly in some ways but actually mostly just belligerent and obnoxious. After writing totally crap code and alienating everyone with his obstinate, belligerent personality he was finally laid off.<BR>\r\nThis specialized list is of sounds that my ex-cubicle-neighbor Val the russian programmer would make that I really did not like at all:<BR>\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI> <B>teeth sucking </B> - this is one of the most constant and also extremely annoying. It was especially bad after his lunch. It wasn\\\'t like little furtive attempts at dislodging food in his mouth. It was more like long leisurely smacks of the lips and sucking of the teeth that would go on for half an hour or more.\r\n\r\n<LI> <B>noodle slurping </B> - I have no idea what this guy put in his lunch, but all I know is that he brought it in, ate at his desk and it smelled terrible. Something very pungent that nearly made me nauseous. I\\\'m assuming it was noodles by the sounds of loud slurping. The smell was bad enough when the the slurping began sometimes I would just have to leave for lunch to not deal with it. \r\n\r\n<LI> <B>playing of terrible russian pop music at \\\'10\\\' on the PC CD player</B> - \r\nI didn\\\'t recognize anything familiar of the music he played but the warbling tones and beats were still annoyingly audible. It sounded kind of like bad 80\\\'s new wave played in a russian piano bar on acid. This was one thing I could stop because it wouldn\\\'t be too unreasonable to have him stop playing it (as opposed telling him to stop clearing his through with a russian accent which would have been futile)\r\n\r\n<LI> <B>long, loud and incoherent arguing</B> - In meetings what he lacked in sound reasoning he made up for in volume and persistence. His tactic was to just wear anyone out that disagreed with him even if what he said made no sense. Even if he wasn\\\'t saying anything he maintained a continual drone of opposition to logic by filling in his words with \\\"ehh... ehhr.. ehm\\\" So it would be, \\\"No, no, no. Is not so. This.. ehh.. ehhr... you cannot do. Does not make sense. Ah, uhhh.. ehhr... Whats the big deal?! No problem.\\\"\r\n\r\n<LI> <B>snickering with a russian accent</B> - not that I occasionally goof off (actually I\\\'m pretty diligent) but when I do I don\\\'t make it totally obvious by snickering at every web site that tickles my funny bone. In Val\\\'s case I think the root of the problem was just that he was just hard of hearing and unaware of what was audible. Sometimes I\\\'d peek over the cubicle wall and see some kind of russian language web site then he\\\'d flash around with a panicked look on his face and make his classic \\\"err, ehr.. ehm...\\\" sound. \r\n\r\n</UL>',0,3226,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16720,2760,'2002-05-20 06:38:59',59,'s5','well, i guess it depends on what your definition of \\\"good parenting\\\" is. for example, when i hear a child crying endlessly in a mall or a restaurant, i wonder why the child was brought there. the child had little choice about its situation, has no desire to be there, yet the parent does nothing to improve matters. often this is as simple as bringing an activity (like a drawing pad or a toy), or leaving the child at home.\r\n<br><br>\r\nperhaps i am ignoring some gross complexity about parenting, but i feel that people need to be more creative. too much suffering is caused when people forget to be creative. that\\\'s all i have to say.',0,644,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16721,2760,'2002-05-20 06:39:24',59,'s5','(that was meant to be \\\"to jo\\\")',0,36,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16722,2760,'2002-05-20 06:53:36',255,'qq','I don\\\'t really find any particular noises that annoying. Actually I find being peevishly annoyed by specific noises more annoying than any specific noises. I do have sorta weird hearing and so I find really loud noises painful, especially really loud continuous background noise like bars and parties and things. But that\\\'s probably more physiological than anything else. \r\n',0,380,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(16723,2760,'2002-05-20 07:22:55',124,'gus','peter: you hate salsa music, have you read the fan man? that guy cant stand puerto rican music. <p>\r\nI cant stand the sound of my former college room mate (coincidentally also named peter) eating. <br>\r\nor the sound of my father taking out his teeth and sucking the stuck food out<br>\r\nor when anyone says \\\"uh\\\" or \\\"um\\\" more than a couple of times<br>\r\nor the sound of a shopping cart full of cans and bottles trundling by outside my window<br>\r\n\r\n',0,459,0,0,'64.163.213.99','en'),(16724,2757,'2002-09-05 18:40:19',18,'ix','i think you\\\'ve made the right choice pal. you\\\'re definitely more of a 3 to midnight kinda girl, if you know what i mean.',0,126,65,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(16725,2766,'2002-05-20 08:06:12',175,'Paul G','I was a T.A. for an American government class this past semester.  25% of the students in that class had no idea who Abraham Lincoln was.  The class was offered at a public university in Illinois - the \\\"Land of Lincoln\\\".  The buildings on campus have names like Lincoln, Grant, and Douglas.\r\n\r\n<p>The degree of ignorance and apathy amongst our youth is terrifying.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Corn syrup, ground beef, MTV, and the herd is all too happy to fatten - body, mind, and soul.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Moo.</p>',0,493,0,0,'67.248.78.67','en'),(16726,2760,'2002-05-20 11:43:42',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ooh, ive been meaning to score that title since you posted <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2158#12204\\\">this</a>. i have a big, fat $15 b&n card thats burning a hole in my pocket, so maybe ill finally get around to it...<br><br>coincidentally, i finished this tremendously long-winded book this morning, and have been thinking of what to read next... looks like you just answered that question for me, so thanks.</tt>',1,452,124,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16727,2766,'2002-05-20 11:47:16',66,'Peter','<br><tt>one that ranks at the top of my list of pet-peeves: when youre forced to sit next to excessively large people on public trans, and even though their thighs are practically lapping 6 inches into your seat and over your legs, the scowl and growl at you as if its <i>you</i> who are impolitely sitting on <i>them</i>. or maybe its that they think i lack tact for not surrendering two whole rush-hour seats to them, i dont know.</tt> ',1,311,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16728,2766,'2002-05-20 12:38:54',64,'Joanna','Hi. I\\\'m fat, like really fat.  I am also extremely healthy.  I have perfect blood sugar levels, low cholestrol and good blood pressure.  I don\\\'t take enough exercise, admittedly, but generally I eat a healthy diet, including extended periods of veganism.  I am highly educated and considerate, and I crave vegetables on a daily basis.  That\\\'s all.  ',1,358,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(16729,2760,'2002-05-20 12:49:24',64,'Joanna','the sound of my parents eating breakfast.  Lunch and dinner don\\\'t bother me, nor does the sound of anyone else eating breakfast.  It\\\'s just a particular combination of toast crunching and tea slurping I guess.  Also currently really annoying sound is my flatmate and her friends doing kung fu in the lounge.  They\\\'re all really cool kids, but it\\\'s just tiring to listen to them for some reason.  ',1,408,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(16730,2766,'2002-05-20 16:57:36',124,'gus','peter: actually when the girl next to me scuttled into me at one point, she politely excused herself.\r\n<p>\r\njoanna: I think you might be in a minority when it comes to health and larger sized people. The girls next to me may be in fair general health, I dont know. But the morbidly obsese girl, I kept thinking about her poor heart pumping its guts out just to walk her around. And the work her ankles and knees must have to do. \r\n<p>\r\nAlso, I dont necessarily think a vegan diet is healthy. It certainly isnt for everyone! (For me it\\\'s those nasty carbs!) heh.\r\n<p>\r\nand paul G, I have saved the stupidity of america for another missive. I have found that stupidity has very little to do with weight. However, it might have something to do with childhood nutritian.',0,769,0,0,'64.163.213.99','en'),(16731,2760,'2002-05-20 17:25:36',168,'laney','okay i came onto this one late, and since all of the sounds i hate have been mentioned, i will go with top 5 sounds i love:\r\n* the splatter of the rain on the roof of my outdoor kitchen\r\n* peanut shells cracking\r\n* the moan of my neighbor\\\'s yellow lab when she wants you to pet her\r\n* chatter of foreign accents on a busy street\r\n* clothes tumbling in a dryer\r\n',0,364,0,0,'198.81.16.174','en'),(16732,2766,'2002-05-20 17:27:02',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ahh, alas, polite 1.5-seaters are few and far between in nyc, i guess. most are rather gruff, honestly. i have empathy for them, though, as it must be really tough to be obese in a city thats so fast-paced and aggressively synchronized.<br><br>on the health side of things:<br><br>\r\nknee and ankle wear in obese individuals: interestingly, here at the hospital i work at, theyre doing a long-term study regarding the wear on articular cartilage (the slick padding in your joints that let them move smoothly) in obese individuals; this particular problem is rather profound, as such cartilage breaks down really quickly under un-natural loads (i,e: atheletes, weight-lifters, the obese), and is one of the few tissues that can\\\'t naturally regenerate. so, if any of you would budding bioengineers would like to make a quick hundred million bucks, come up with a synthetic replacement for knee cartilage!<br><br>\r\nas for a vegan diet, its supposedly quite healthy, actually, assuming you do it right.<br><br>\r\nmy two cents, of course...</tt>\r\n',1,1051,124,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16733,2760,'2002-05-20 17:28:29',66,'Peter','<br><tt>im fond of the tinkling bells of the ice-cream truck; they sound like windchimes, but with doppler shifts.</tt>',1,119,168,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16734,2766,'2002-05-20 18:14:58',176,'Scapegoat','If people are healthy - that is what matters the most. There is also the issue of genetic predisposition/body type.<br><br>\r\nI do think that the issues around health need to be addressed more openly. I have family that falls into the health risk category due to weight issues. I find it hard to grapple with.<br><br>\r\nAs a side note it isn\\\'t strictly an American thing, it is bad north of the border too.   ',0,410,0,0,'64.229.160.154','en'),(16735,2760,'2002-05-20 18:23:12',176,'Scapegoat','On the sounds that make me happy front I\\\'d have to choose the sound of a fire. ',0,82,0,0,'64.229.160.154','en'),(16736,2762,'2002-05-20 19:59:29',187,'kandrer','sorry, it wasn\\\'t any secret code, i was just giving my choice among the 7 photos you added. :)',1,97,66,0,'208.223.155.221','en'),(16737,2762,'2002-05-20 20:18:48',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ahh :D</tt>',1,19,187,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16738,2762,'2002-05-20 20:40:55',278,'egenus','Nifty idea.',0,11,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16739,2760,'2002-05-20 21:04:30',83,'adamajah','i <b>hate</b> salsa music :)',0,28,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(16740,2753,'2002-05-20 22:44:42',176,'Scapegoat','You are absolutely right that I am reducing Kandrer, but how does one talk about any complex social issue with out reducing? It is a question of what information you decide to leave out when you reduce something to a level which allows for dialogue, no?<br><br>\r\nThe first point I made is due to my overwhelming personal bias of putting the responsibility on the individual. In this case media consumption is a choice with the exception of billboards in public places. As an individual you can opt out of the images on cable and magazines if they are damaging to you (I did for several years). It seems to me much like a person who always burns their hand on the stove top, making sure they put their hand down on the stove everytime they walk by.<br><br>\r\nThe second point you make pins down my unclear writing. There is a difference between the ideal image and the dominant one, as the ideal is the personal highest conception and the dominant is simply market demand.<br><br>\r\nAs for saying <b>always</b>, at the ripe age of a quarter century perhaps I am too cynical. But as a younger person I find a lot of strength in manuveuring through life can be gained from such a perspective.<br><br>\r\nYoung and Obstinate    ',0,1220,187,0,'64.229.160.154','en'),(16741,2766,'2002-05-21 00:15:54',180,'auntie','i had a discussion about this with a doctor in the houston airport waiting room a few months ago.<br><br>\r\n\r\nhe said that he thought it was a combination of junk food and sitting in front of the teevee.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni said - cure what puts children at mickey d\\\'s for dinner and behind a locked door on the sofa, and you cure the obesity problem.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhen we were young, we came home from school and shed our \\\"good\\\" clothes for play clothes and dashed out the door to play tag with our neighbors while mom cooked dinner we would all eat around the table together.<br><br>\r\n\r\ntoday\\\'s child may not even KNOW the other kids in the neighborhood.  if they do, they may not see them.<br><br>\r\n\r\nkids who have two working parents are locked behind a door from the time they come home from school until they have dinner for safety.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthose who are not sitting in front of the teevee eating microwave popcorn are probably being ferried to lessons or sports in the afternoons and on the weekends.<br><br>\r\n\r\nhome cooking is expensive.  fast food is often cheaper than home cooked, and certainly faster.  the 9-5 worker of the sixties had a more liesurely workplace, without the stringent performance expectations of today - many jobs are no longer paid hourly, but by a salary that is connected to work that takes 60 hours a week to complete.<br><br>\r\n\r\nparents are performing jobs that were once performed by two or three people out of necessity.  they come home exhausted.<br><br>\r\n\r\ntired parents equal teevee watching, nintendo playing children who eat happy meals which means too many carbs and fats.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe are a nation of exhausted people with neglected children, but i don\\\'t see it changing until we stop needing so much fucking STUFF to feel good about ourselves.<br><br>',3,1806,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(16742,2766,'2002-05-21 02:31:11',59,'s5','although fast food is cheaper per pound of food, it costs the individual more over a period of time, if you add up health care and other hidden costs, like the opportunity cost of a life spent in front of a tv. not to mention the cost to society to maintain a large population of sickly couch potatoes. or the cost of topsoil degradation (from unsustainable and factory farming) and pollution (wasteful packaging). but also, there\\\'s an article in some newspaper today about oakland schools removing soda and chip vending machines because their kids eat nothing but junk food all day long. imagine that.',3,605,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16743,2762,'2002-05-21 04:01:19',174,'Mad Moll','PATH :D',3,7,66,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(16744,2766,'2002-05-21 05:00:43',180,'auntie','yes - but what is the answer?  how do we, as citizens of this country safeguard our futures - the mind and health of our young persons?\r\n\r\nit is the responsibility of each adult to see to the welfare and education of those who come after us, whether or not they are the spawn of our own loins.\r\n\r\nyet we refuse to protect our futures, and theirs, with adequate educations. most americans wail at the taxes required to pay for education, and worse, fail to make themselves aware of what their own children are being taught in our school systems - never mind those who do not have children in school - those childless persons wring their hands and claim they should not have to pay ANYTHING for education - and most certainly do not care if the children cannot read and write or do simple figures upon graduation - until they are accosted and robbed by the illiterate and unwashed result of their neglect.\r\n\r\nwhat is the answer?  how will we fix the problem without the interest of all of those with the power to change things?',3,1025,59,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(16745,2766,'2002-05-21 06:06:45',74,'jo','<p>fast food is cheaper than cooking at home? since when? for the same five dollars as a combo meal, i can make enough pasta, veggies, and soup to feed three people.<BR>\r\nokay so while we\\\'re on this subject, i know that sonic food chains are a strictly southern novelty, so let me tell you about this television ad they\\\'re running right now:</p>\r\n<p>wife brings home sonic meal, in paper bags, to husband who is watching tv. she says, \\\"honey, i brought home wraps for dinner.\\\"<BR>\r\nwe hear the husband \\\"think\\\": \\\"wraps? what\\\'s next? exercise? jogging? yoga?\\\"<BR>\r\nthen the wife says, \\\"oh and i got sundaes, too,\\\" and takes ice cream sundaes out of the bag. flash to man\\\'s face, grinning ear to ear.</p>\r\n<p>um, since when are fast food wraps, particularly ones with fried chicken strips, healthy? there is probably as much fat in that wrap as there is in the average burger, especially since it\\\'s probably drenched in ranch dressing.<BR>\r\nwhich doesn\\\'t even address how insane the <b>rest</b> of the commercial is.</p>\r\n',2,1061,0,0,'172.165.22.110','en'),(16746,2766,'2002-05-21 06:23:46',74,'jo','<p>also, on the topic of how families used to eat and exercise vs. how families eat and excercise now: i think there have been as many steps forward as there have been back and that it\\\'s a little too simple to generalize about \\\"now\\\" v. \\\"then.\\\" sure, the average kid got more exercise then. they also ate eggs, bacon, pancakes, buttered toast, and a glass of milk before even leaving the house.</p>\r\n<p>i mean, can we take a closer look at the \\\"good old days\\\" and realize that all the statistics about heart disease, diabetes, liver failure, etc. come from  middle-aged people who are feeling the effects now of a lifetime of too much everything, too much red meat, too much cholesterol, too much dairy, too much fat? bad habits that started with those home-cooked family meals?</p>\r\n<p>and sorry to be all soap-boxy but <b>jesus</b>, it can get so fucking depressing on swinney in these debates. <b>THERE IS HOPE, YO!</b>',1,942,0,0,'172.165.22.110','en'),(16747,2766,'2002-05-21 07:25:59',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\nwhen they put new seats in redskins stadium, they took out 4000 of them to make the rest of the seats bigger. the reason: the average redskin fan had grown in width.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas a side note. im in korea...the land of short skinny men and women. i think junk food and candy and tv and pc games are a newer thing here then i think they are...but id be interested in seeing the state of this country in twenty years. the ammount of junk my kids eat is considerably higher then what i can remember consuming. its almost like haloween everyday here. mcdonalds, kfc, popeyes chicken, pizza hut, and burger king, are very popular here. more so with the 20ish crowd then they are in n.america. its a bit of a status symbol to be able to consume the expensive fast food. like what s5 said, while fast food is cheaper per pound than conventional foodstuffs, over time it still costs more in secondary ways, as well as in the individual pocketbooks. however, what i have noticed is that inside grocery stores there is an almost welcomed lack of microwavable foods. so for the time being, when koreans eat at home, what they eat is prepared.\r\n</p>\r\n',1,1145,0,0,'211.32.132.229','en'),(16748,2760,'2002-05-21 07:28:51',93,'chiamattt','too much hate in this world. how about dislike? and how about just listening to a walkman and freeing yourself from all that agony you seemly have on a day to day basis being in public. ',3,186,0,0,'211.32.132.229','en'),(16749,2766,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>joanna sweetie, you are so far from morbidly obese that i could call the gap the pacific ocean.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>my dad always told mum she was too fat, but she eats better than any of us, exersizes and is as strong as an ox and twice as healthy, according to the doc.  he\\\'s the one that had a heart attack last year, despite being skinny his entire life...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>lumping all fat people in together is pretty harsh (not that i think your article does that, gus).  some people just have built-in padding.  whatever.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i think part of this problem of \\\"fat people in america\\\" that y\\\'all are so fond of going on about is the general distaste for fat.  the more fat you have, the more depressed you are about it, therefore less likely to have necessary motivation to stop being unhealthy, if you are.  the more fat you have, the less likely you are to get the job to make the money to eat better.  vicious cycle? i dunno.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>maybe we should all stop being so concerned about other people\\\'s \\\"problems\\\" and work on ourselves....</p>\r\n\r\n<p>n.b this comment not marked constructive.</p>',6,1113,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16750,2766,'2002-05-21 11:33:15',175,'Paul G','I think that there is some relationship between democracy and market economies and obesity.\r\n\r\n<p>Democracy is the regime type in which individual desires are pursued.  That might be good or bad, depending upon the nature of the individual\\\'s desire.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>People who lack education are unable to discern what is good from what seems to be good.  And they are therefore as likely to pursue bad desires as good desires.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Advertising, among other things, creates within people desires for bad things.  It\\\'s rather heartbreaking to watch children especially as they clamor for whatever piece of crap they happen to have seen on television.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>As I read the story by Gus, my own thoughts were directed towards the patterns of consumption in our society, and \\\"Western\\\" culture as a whole.  The young woman on the bus was a gross [triple word play] manifestation of the unthinking pursuit of individual desire that is the dark shadow of the brilliance made possible by our liberal democracy.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I think that\\\'s right.  I think that the story is not an attack upon large people, but rather an exposition of the state of affairs.  There are large people who are healthy; but there are also many people who are larger than they should be.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And there does seem to be more money to be made selling people unhealthy food and treatments for the diseases and disfigurations that such food produces, than there is to be made through selling them healthy food.</p>       \r\n',0,1506,0,0,'67.248.52.103','en'),(16751,2766,'2002-05-21 14:00:53',265,'chuckspeed','<p>IMHO, there is a high degree of correlation between eating disorders and dual-income/single parent families.  Follow a fat kid home...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>aww, hell, what do I know?  Praise the fryer and pass the shaq pack!</p>\r\n\r\n',0,224,0,0,'208.5.114.56','en'),(16752,2760,'2002-05-21 14:10:34',265,'chuckspeed','<p>man, youse guys got some serious sound issues...</p>\r\n\r\nOne person\\\'s noise is another\\\'s music. On summer nights, the windows are thrown open to catch postmodern industrial noise - big block chevys, blown hemis, and straight pipe harleys conduct a symphony for this slightly deaf motorhead\\\'s ears.',0,308,0,0,'208.5.114.56','en'),(16753,2669,'2002-05-21 14:29:41',297,'kelly','i love this entry. everything said here completely sums up how i feel when i leave new york to visit my hometown in upper michigan. ',0,132,0,0,'166.77.209.190','en'),(16754,2669,'2002-05-21 14:45:48',67,'trouble','upper michigan?  where in upper michigan?!',1,42,297,0,'139.76.65.132','en'),(16755,2760,'2002-05-21 15:05:23',74,'jo','agony? never. i\\\'m a happy-go-lucky person, likely to be one of the more consistently joyful people that you\\\'ll ever know. there are just some sounds, sounds i hear once or twice a month, that really disturb me, often because they\\\'re interrupting my enjoyment of another sound. i.e.: happy chatter at a coffeehouse, conversations at the counter, the album that they\\\'re playing in the background, <i>ahhhhhh</i> and then, a table away: <b>slurrrrrrrrp</b>. nerves grating.',1,482,0,0,'172.166.192.47','en'),(16756,2768,'2002-05-21 15:54:47',66,'Peter','<br><tt>these thoughts are a common issue for lots of us, for sure; here\'s to finding <a href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1958.txt\">our own places</a> within our cities.</a></tt>',1,204,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16757,2760,'2002-05-21 16:28:58',186,'ook','<p>annoying sound no one seems to have mentioned: a person vaguely singing along to a song in a breathy monotone and getting the words wrong. happens a lot in grocery stores that play soft rock over the speakers, but is worse with good songs, especially when stuck in a car with the wimpy singer.\r\n\r\n<p>controversially annoying sound: mosques when you aren\\\'t praying at them. my parents can count 16 mosques in the view out their window. the calls to prayer aren\\\'t quite coordinated between them all, so the 5-times-daily blends in to a near-constant carpet until after dark. i kind of like it, but they get all aggro. (\\\"they turned the speaker up again! i know they did! call the building manager!\\\")',0,712,0,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(16758,2757,'2002-05-21 17:59:01',195,'matt','they say the night people are after the day people.',1,51,0,0,'150.176.174.11','en'),(16759,2769,'2002-05-21 19:29:24',17,'austin','i go through the same thing.  once i talked to one of them, an attractive college student.  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni  said, \\\"all your clothes are dirty, huh?\\\"  it sounds like a dumb question, but she was wearing the most aweful maternity looking tarp.  it was comical in its unattractive drapery.  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nshe laughed and said, \\\"oh yes, this is my house frau outfit that it all comes down to at the end of the laundry cycle.\\\"\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nwe chatted some more.  it was nice.',0,477,0,0,'205.179.19.111','en'),(16760,2766,'2002-05-21 19:45:40',124,'gus','Yah dude, in thailand the only fat people I saw were kids. Why was that? McDonalds and Mister Donut I bet!! not to mention riding moto\\\'s instead of riding bikes. ahhh, the great western influence. Manifest destiny is all about happy meals and motor cars.',0,257,93,0,'64.163.213.99','en'),(16761,2766,'2002-05-21 19:47:37',124,'gus','<b>P.S.</b><br>\r\nI REALLY want a mcdonalds cheeseburger after reading this thread. Thanks guys!!!\r\n<p>\r\nBut instead I will go to boogaloos and have stuff that I pretend is good for me. like sausage and eggs and baked beans and coffee. \r\n<p>\r\nbye!',0,246,0,0,'64.163.213.99','en'),(16762,2753,'2002-05-21 20:00:28',187,'kandrer','- true, one has to analyze and synthesize to talk about social issues (they are always complex, it seems, btw).<br>\r\n- true, this will distort information <br>\r\n- when i said \\\"reduce\\\" i meant you did it too much<br>\r\n- i changed my mind (or my mood) since the other day, and i now agree with you: we need to reduce, we <i>want</i> to reduce<br>\r\n- my writing was more unclear than yours. i meant that: you reduce in 1 & 2, situation 1 is more complex, and let\\\'s not say always in 2.<br>\r\n- as i am the old fart, and i want to reduce, i will say that i consider the media responsible of all the shit it puts into people\\\'s mind. i don\\\'t give a damn if people ask for it. i want the media to feel responsible in any case of what happens because of the media. i want atomic scientists to feel responsible of hiroshima and nagasaki. i want gwb to feel responsible for the killing of innocent afghans. i want priests to feel responsible for the ideas they put into weak people\\\'s mind. i want to feel responsible for i did nothing in my life to change the rotten world we live in.<br>\r\n- and once everyone is truly feeling responsible for everything, i want everyone to feel in peace and to forgive eachother for our tinyness and our futility. amen. :)\r\n',1,1265,176,0,'208.223.155.221','en'),(16763,2766,'2002-05-21 20:14:41',174,'Qi\\\'ng Moll','<p>In my country, eat fish. Caribou. Seaweeds. No TV. Kids run around. Kids like small animal in my country. Make noise fight in streets.</p>\r\n<p>When child, go in woods kill small  boar. Make fire you eat it. Is not so bad?</p>\r\n<p>Here, food like air -- eat and still hungry. No taste. This why so bad.</p>',6,308,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(16764,2766,'2002-05-21 20:17:14',17,'austin','that made me snort my tea.',1,26,174,0,'205.179.19.111','en'),(16765,2753,'2002-05-21 21:30:45',311,'ntl','i think it\\\'s unrealistic to say that one can isolate themselves from the media if they wish to escape it\\\'s  unhealthy messages.  i too attempted to remove media influence from my life.  i stopped watching television, going to movies, reading magazines.  but i can\\\'t stop the people around me from absorbing media.  it bleeds out into society.  i can\\\'t expect to stay a white sock when i am in a world of red clothing.  each person must take responsibility for themselves.  but I refuse to accept that we should \\\"forgive\\\" (or it seems excuse) media for the way it is and not fight back.  taking responsibility for oneself doesn\\\'t always mean absolving others...',0,681,0,0,'144.92.164.199','en'),(16766,2769,'2002-05-21 22:37:24',196,'jasonp','Yeah, there\\\'s something about the laundromat that really promotes attraction. I guess it\\\'s the solitary nature of the task and the silent waiting, each persons personal garmets, bed sheets, underwear  brought out in the open.<BR><BR>\nHere\\\'s an interesting laundromat story:<BR>\nI was doing laundry and saw a pretty good looking woman, late 20 to early 30\\\'s. At first it was like the regular laundromat attraction and subtle flirting. She seemed more interested than usual so I dared start with some innocuous line about the dryer or something. A few minutes later out of the blue she asks me \\\"Do you like porn?\\\" I was totally flabbergasted. Finally I just responded with, \\\"Well, not really. Why?\\\" What else could I have said? \\\"Yeah baby, I love the porn. Give me porn\\\". She said she had a \\\"HomeGrown Video\\\" T-shirt that she didn\\\'t want. It\\\'s a local amateur porn video company.<BR>\nThis had gotten me totally intrigued so after some more ackward chit-chat a progression of revelations followed. First she had only been to an adult video convention, then she was somehow marginally involved, then she\\\'s actually a video \\\"producer\\\" herself. I suspected she was more than just a producer from the way it kept unfolding. I was totally confused as to what her motive was all about when she mentions her husband several times.<BR>\nI was all mixed up -- really nervous, excited and confused. If anything I figured that she was on the lookout for \\\"amateurs\\\" for the videos. I wasn\\\'t interested in that. After a while it seemed just too wierd to get into any further so I just left after my laundry was done. I still have the T-shirt she gave me though.\n',0,1705,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16767,1958,'2002-05-21 22:41:26',234,'etienne','yes yes and yes.  the finding of passion in what might otherwise seem devoid of it is part of citylove true and pure.',1,117,66,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(16768,2766,'2002-05-21 22:44:09',175,'Paul G','Great article.',1,14,124,0,'67.248.52.246','en'),(16769,2769,'2002-05-21 22:49:18',234,'etienne','i got this on sunday after visiting the local laundromat for the first time.  this isn\\\'t your ordinary laundromat, its in an increasingly gentrified suburb where the hippies and hipsters wait in line for the same machines.  <br>i had two crushes by the time i left, & winter doesn\\\'t seem so bad if it means that i have to visit the laundromat of love every tiem i do a load of washing.',1,391,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(16770,2768,'2002-05-21 23:01:55',295,'karenlee','Parallels I love \\\'em! I wrote this thing in 1996 after 12 years in Sydney...........ha ha I come from a 6-digit town!.',3,121,66,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(16771,1958,'2002-05-22 00:14:39',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yes, sometimes the truest passions lurk vaguely in some sort of longing... that concept is part of why i like where i live; if you live here right, theres an endless search for stisfaction amist the millions that is, in itself, satisfying.</tt>',1,252,234,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16772,2768,'2002-05-22 00:15:33',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i wrote that this year, after 8 years of getting to know my city :D</tt>',1,80,66,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16773,2766,'2002-05-22 00:50:50',124,'gus','Coincidentally, there was a summit on obesity today in this here country, America. 27% of adults here are obese (meaning: severely overweight) as are 15% of children. Obesity related ailments will soon be placing a larger strain on American healthcare than any other disease. That is all.\r\n<p>\r\nsorry I couldnt find a url for this, I saw it on the TV while I was sitting around on my ass like a good American.',0,409,0,0,'64.163.213.99','en'),(16774,2766,'2002-05-22 01:43:58',59,'s5','and then today, i read this article, called <a href=\\\"http://www.nzherald.co.nz/storydisplay.cfm?storyID=2042311&thesection=news&thesubsection=world\\\">wave of extinctions sweeping the planet</a>. it makes sense, really. all that fat has to come from somewhere. (and by \\\"fat\\\" i don\\\'t necessarily mean lipid tissue.)',0,327,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16775,2769,'2002-05-22 04:11:41',307,'hool','<p>felicity: reading this made me feel comfortable and soothed like only the rhythm of asychronous clothes dropping and redropping in rows of dryers can do.  it made me actually pine for the laundromat - i\\\'d never realized the heightened sense of attraction in there - but you related it in a way that was immediately familiar to me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>also, it\\\'s good to know that girls feel the same things in those situations (from the watchful boy perspective).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>my first kiss was from a girl beside a laundromat.  she was much taller than me and i ran home afterward.</p>',0,582,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(16776,2769,'2002-05-22 04:23:35',66,'Peter','<br><tt>anyone can fall in love when surrounded by dryerwhitenoise.</tt>',1,72,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16777,2769,'2002-05-22 06:32:46',124,'gus','I really need to do a load of laundry right now. Im out of panties. ',0,68,0,0,'64.163.213.99','en'),(16778,2769,'2002-05-22 14:35:59',174,'Quong Moll','like this good',6,14,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(16779,2769,'2002-05-22 14:47:05',265,'chuckspeed','i\\\'m not allowed in laundromats on account of i take the machines apart and clean out the coin in the bottom of the tub.  There\\\'s usually a couple of bucks\\\' worth of coin under the agitator, which pops off after you unscrew the retaining cap. if the attendant is asleep at the switch, you can clear 15-20 bucks in coin in the average-sized laundromat.  keeping one\\\'s head in a machine precludes the possiblity of chance encounters...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>of course, your mileage my vary.</p>',0,491,0,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(16780,2769,'2002-05-22 15:55:22',74,'jo','hi, i like this very much.',1,26,0,0,'172.164.95.81','en'),(16781,2760,'2002-05-22 16:09:29',74,'jo','that reminds me of a pleasant sound: living near enough to a church / temple with a bell tower that rings twice a day at the 12s. but oy. 7 mosques, 5 times a day. ',0,164,186,0,'172.164.95.81','en'),(16784,2769,'2002-05-22 18:23:49',66,'Peter','<br><tt>im going to go do laundry now; maybe ill get lucky... theres a certain washing machine ive had my eye on for some time now.</tt>',1,136,81,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16785,2770,'2002-05-22 18:55:57',86,'egg','I think every girl on earth has experienced this trauma, not to trivialize your irritation. I read in the paper this morning that more species are becoming extinct at a faster rate than ever before in earth\\\'s history, that the planet risks becoming \\\"oversimplified\\\". We have to remember to fight homogenized standards of beauty in ourselves. when i lived in new york, i hung around places where there were lots of funny looking and non-skinny people; it\\\'s possible that long island is just boring. funny looking is where it\\\'s at. I have this vague memory of being in a social circle where being conventionally attractive was actually a drawback. fashion is always moving.',0,686,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16786,2769,'2002-05-22 18:59:49',86,'egg','how many sitcoms and romantic comedies have we seen in which someone tries or is picked up at the laundromat?',0,109,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16787,2770,'2002-05-22 19:39:37',265,'chuckspeed','you\\\'re on the merry-go-round, and all you see are the tails of the fillies in front of you...Look behind; you\\\'ll see you\\\'re really at the head of the pack.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>where in mighty michigan, kelly?',0,207,0,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(16788,2770,'2002-05-22 19:55:48',67,'trouble','yes, where in michigan?',1,23,0,0,'139.76.65.132','en'),(16789,2766,'2002-05-22 21:23:53',124,'gus','not the world, just america, and yes, obesity related problems are sometimes concurrent with geriatric problems. Diabetes, heart problems, etc... Anyway, Im not old (yet) or fat (yet). Take your argument up with the guys in the news, not me... ok!',0,247,123,0,'64.172.56.92','en'),(16790,2770,'2002-05-22 21:45:19',265,'chuckspeed','FWIW, the epicenter of the center of the universe is at 13 mile and woodward in royal oak, mi. now if trouble and kelly can relate...',0,133,0,0,'172.150.38.226','en'),(16791,2770,'2002-05-22 21:52:31',65,'le','i was feeling this when i was in nyc a little over a week ago. there\\\'s definitely some tidy grooming happening there, and i\\\'m a total hairy hippie. also sometimes i smell. and i don\\\'t wash my hair. i was feeling a little weird. i think you just have to remind yourself that those women have different priorities than you and that\\\'s ok. be thankful you\\\'re not spending hours a day preening and grooming and stuff. ',4,428,0,0,'208.239.147.211','en'),(16792,2770,'2002-05-22 22:05:05',67,'trouble','i can relate, does Noir Leather still do fetish shows?  snootch.',1,64,265,0,'68.47.202.98','en'),(16793,2770,'2002-05-22 22:28:44',59,'s5','it is hard to live in the castro. castro boys have bigger muscles and nicer clothing than i do. better haircuts, too.',4,117,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16794,111,'2002-05-22 22:53:58',17,'austin','ELIZIBUTT MILKCAN',1,17,0,0,'205.179.19.111','en'),(16795,2770,'2002-05-23 00:16:21',86,'egg','when was the last time you knew a girl to go for <i>that</i>?',0,61,59,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16796,2770,'2002-05-23 01:39:55',59,'s5','i can\\\'t really say, in general, what women go for, but living in a neighborhood populated predominantly by \\\"perfect\\\" men can sometimes be intimidating. so i posted a \\\"me too\\\" to kelly\\\'s article.',5,212,86,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16797,2770,'2002-05-23 03:58:32',297,'kelly','i grew up in menominee, michigan.. it\\\'s at the southern tip of the upper penninsula. in the past couple years i\\\'ve started saying wisconsin instead, since i was about 2 minutes from the border, and all the closest actual cities are in wisconsin. whenever i say michigan people think of the detroit area which was actually over 8 hours away. ',0,347,0,0,'24.185.90.139','en'),(16798,111,'2002-05-23 04:52:34',76,'chi chi','ooh boy i love you so, never ever ever gonna let you go, once i get my hands on you- ooh',0,88,0,0,'64.168.23.22','en'),(16799,2770,'2002-05-23 06:01:14',314,'eve','I grew up in Jackson, MI, some people said it was a little Detroit at times. After that, I moved to a smaller town in the southwestern region, in Brooklyn, MI. Now, THAT was a small town. I used to laugh at the girls there that spent all their time doing their hair, but when I came to Heidelberg, Germany, where I live now, I was faced with the same scenario. Of course there are normal people, but itÂ´s hard to weed them out from the hoardes of anoreics on diets, the flashing red and purple rows of hair immaculately done up, with faces and clothes that designers themselves could not present better. And I came here with my joggin shoes and sweatshirts. I still feel like a hill billy, and I get made fun of for my jeans. I guess a lot has to do with wanting to fit in, but like the other comment, I am glad I dont spend those hours wasted on preening and primping. I stopped reading nutrition labels a looong time ago =)',0,925,0,0,'80.132.27.40','en'),(16800,2753,'2002-05-23 06:11:22',314,'eve','Responsibility for oneÂ´s actions..or lack thereof. (Nodding head in approval.) Just seems like no one cares. Is it because no one really fights back anymore? Were my parents back in the 60s protesting for things of the moment? I donÂ´t believe that, I think they wanted the world to take responsibility for what it had done and was doing.. but the President, the first person who should have listened from the \\\"Democracy\\\" shoved them back down to the bowels of the earth and into the pigs hands.. because they stood up and tried to fight. Somehow that just drifted totally off of what the main point was. IÂ´m getting off my rant and going to bed. ItÂ´s been a long day. ',0,674,187,0,'80.132.27.40','en'),(16801,2752,'2002-05-23 06:29:43',187,'kandrer','another dream one is happy to wake up from...<br> i often had this same scenario, that a guy wants to attack me while i have a gun or a knife. i try to intimidate him but nothing would do. i shoot once, but he keeps on attacking. it ends up in a bloody mess like yours, except i never carried the body yet.<br>\r\nmaybe the solution is to get ourselves a more respectable weapon, like a missile-launcher from unreal tournament. at least there won\\\'t be anything left to clean...',1,478,0,0,'207.93.61.44','en'),(16802,2753,'2002-05-23 06:41:54',187,'kandrer','<p>\r\n<u>ntl</u>: mmm... you got it, right, that i agree with you?<br>\r\n<u>scapegoat</u>: it\\\'s nice talking with you; so let\\\'s keep on this way: you argue for the media, and we argue against them (it?), he he he.<br>\r\n<u>eve</u>: don\\\'t worry. i don\\\'t even know what\\\'s taking me these days: i never thought i cared for all this \\\"political bullshit\\\". let\\\'s just keep on talking since it seems this is the only possible thing to do...\r\n</p>',1,460,0,0,'207.93.61.44','en'),(16803,2752,'2002-05-23 07:09:14',187,'kandrer','btw Austin, how come your post didn\\\'t appear in the main page? i had to go in the article list to see it...',1,110,0,0,'207.93.61.44','en'),(16804,2760,'2002-05-23 07:19:42',75,'tyler','1. People rustling paper, closing books, and most importantly unzipping bookbags before the professor says ok time to go. Like they are trying to signal to him that it\\\'s over. He\\\'ll let you know when it\\\'s over.\r\n<p>\r\n2. Another college one: people who come in late to class, and then unzip their bookbags. Take the books and notebook out of the damn bag <i>before</i> coming in the room and have them in your hand.\r\n<p>\r\n3. Forced laughter of any kind be it polite or attention-getting.\r\n<p>\r\nOn the other hand, I like hearing babies crying, because man, they fucking CRY when it\\\'s time to cry, don\\\'t they? There\\\'s a daycare across the parking lot from my apartment, pre-k, and they raise hell sometimes. But I like it.',1,737,0,0,'165.247.165.22','en'),(16805,2770,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<p>this reminds me of a picture that for some reason struck me so much that i stopped and stared at it for a while, trying to understand the mental behavior behind it.\r\n</p><p>\r\nit was a picture in kathy lee gifford\'s autobiography, and she is sitting on the floor next to her 3 month old toddler, with her legs out in front of her, in a bathrobe. she looks completely exhausted. her eyes have some makeup melting off in black flakes. the kid is on it\'s stomach, on a blanket asleep. SHE\'S DOING HER NAILS. they are huge long pink talons, and she is carefully painting them when she should be 1) asleep 2) cuddling her baby and i was shuddering, imagining that poor boy growing up being fondled and poked by long magenta claws. and expecting all girls to devote half their day to looking perfectly painted and powdered and puffed like his mother.\r\n</p><p>\r\nhow fucked up IS that. i mean, really. moms should smell like moms and food, not makeup and acetone. blegh. i was horrified at my june cleaver response: \"oh but she should be doing that someplace away from that baby!\" but who knows. the kid\'s prolly used to it.\r\n</p><p>\r\nbut man. she looked so fucking tired. \"oh my god, i have  chipped nail!\" instead of \"i should go take a nap!\"</p><p>\r\nsoap. and water. soap. and water. that\'s all you need! (uhm and also a nice lavendar lotion maybe)</p>',1,956,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(16806,2772,'2002-05-23 08:50:26',86,'egg','some of us call it \\\"dildo\\\" or \\\"vibrator\\\"',0,52,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16807,2772,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','the idea of a detachable penis is really gross to me, especially seeing this fucked up japanese movie where a girl bites it off her lover that she just strangled.  ix, you remember that?',6,186,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16808,2772,'2002-05-23 12:47:51',74,'jo','or \\\"best buddy\\\" or \\\"lifesaver\\\" or, according to the box, \\\"mr. smooth flexible.\\\"<BR>\r\nah, so much nicer when not complicated by, you know, feelings and insecurities.',1,182,0,0,'172.131.92.44','en'),(16809,2772,'2002-05-23 15:11:06',265,'chuckspeed','girls get multiple orgasms while guys get to pee standing up - it\\\'s not fair.',0,80,0,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(16810,2770,'2002-05-23 15:15:31',265,'chuckspeed','re: noir leather-</p>\r\n\r\nyah.  they\\\'ve expanded them into royal oak music theatre - they got busted for porn last time - bummer.</p>\r\n\r\nFWIW, demf starts @romt this friday with a kickoff rave - i\\\'ll be at the theatre a bloc away doing a musical - double bummer. \r\n\r\n',0,272,67,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(16811,2770,'2002-05-23 15:26:43',265,'chuckspeed','menominee is as far from NYC as you can possibly go - demographically, that is.</p>\r\n\r\nyou\\\'ve sunk lower dan da trolls down below.</p>\r\n\r\nplay to your strengths - you\\\'re real.',0,181,297,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(16812,2772,'2002-05-23 15:30:58',75,'tyler','i\\\'ll take pee standing up as an attribute vs. the multiple orgasm. one does me jusssssst finnnnne.',1,101,0,0,'165.247.165.22','en'),(16813,2772,'2002-05-23 15:51:02',265,'chuckspeed','i dunno \\\'bout that - i\\\'ve been an active participant in mo events, and i get real envious around the seventh wave.  it\\\'s nice to know she enjoys it that much, but it would be even nicer to actively participate.  can\\\'t seem to knock off any more than two at a time...',0,278,75,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(16814,2772,'2002-05-23 16:26:23',186,'ook','<p>1. men can have multiple orgasms. it is easiest if <a href=\\\"http://www.luckymojo.com/tknorthaustin.html\\\">ejaculation and orgasm are separated</a>.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>2. women can pee <a href=\\\"http://www.restrooms.org/standing.html\\\">standing up</a>. it is easiest with practise.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>that is all.',0,306,0,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(16815,2772,'2002-05-23 18:34:53',175,'Paul G','I said last night: \\\"You do understand that in my mind this is not a \\\'real\\\' detachable penis, but rather a sort of fashion accessory - like earings or a purse. You put it on; you take it off; you get a new one when the old one goes out of style.  And it\\\'s probably brightly colored.\\\"<br>  \r\nSo, ok, maybe Egg was right; Karen Lee said the same thing; Jo seems to agree too.  It just never occurred to me, go figure...<br>\r\nI did think about the <a href=\\\"http://www.art-pacific.com/artifacts/nuguinea/bilas/phalloco.htm\\\">gourds</a> though:\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.art-pacific.com/images/penijmen.gif\\\">',0,624,0,0,'67.248.113.35','en'),(16816,2775,'2002-05-23 19:43:57',83,'adamajah','i read this',0,11,0,0,'68.65.228.29','en'),(16817,2775,'2002-05-23 20:00:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt><b>and from the still-sore-wound files:</b> <i>\\\"but he organized and helped pay for the wtc attacks... yeah, bill clinton helped organize the attacks against a pharmaceuticals plant in sudan, resulting in the deaths of about 100,000 sudanese because of the resulting lack of affordable medicine. you dont call him a terrorist...\\\"</i><br><br>maybe its just all the wtc fumes that ive been breathing for the past 8 months, but i think comparing <t>wtc attacks</i> to <i>death via lack of affordable medicine</i> is somewhat apples-and-oranges. no offense, but really. <br><br>Chia, i always <i>really</i> like reading your pieces, and i mean that. they generally have some wonderful observations and are some of the more interesting and pleasant ones on here; they often make me smile. but ive noticed that they sometimes often contain some <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2708#16348\\\">tangential statements</a> that kind of distract from the messages youre clearly very dedicated to. i mean no offense, of course, but im sure i might speak for others too when i say that what you write might be taken a little more seriously (and perhaps get more of the attention it deserves?) if you gave a slightly more thoughtful spin to some of your interesting commentaries/observations/rants/sermons, etc...<br><br>just my unsolicited two cents, of course :) <i>\\\'best friendly\\\'</i></tt>',1,1422,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16818,2777,'2002-05-23 20:43:39',74,'jo','<p>i had a fancy panda bear shirt. the panda bear was sliding down a rainbow. i loved it.</p>\r\n<p>i also relate to this a lot: my mom and i did weight watchers together when i was 11, and looking back on that, while i\\\'m grateful that my mom cared enough to help me find a healthy solution to a problem that was worrying me, i <b>can\\\'t believe</b> that i wasn\\\'t just playing with dolls or something. 11. jesus christ.</p>\r\n<p>when i was a senior in high school, i became massively depressed and lost 30 lbs at the drop of a hat doing something that i jokingly called the \\\"1-2-3 diet\\\": 1. lunch was 2 dr. peppers. dr pepper is made of prune juice, and is a diuretic. 2. one meal when i came home from school, usually scrambled eggs or ramen. 3. spent all night at a coffeehouse, drinking 6-10 cups of coffee. see: diuretic. you might as well add a #4: self-loathing, because that a big contributor. i sure did lose weight. i was also malnourished, and when i started college, i put that weight back on. and more. of course.</p>\r\n<p>i am actually losing weight right now, successfully for the first time in my life, which can be owed about 1/4 to weight watchers online and 3/4 to the fact that i\\\'m <b>happy</b> and walking everywhere and not stuck behind a computer terminal for 8 hours a day. i don\\\'t think i would have been able to do this a few years ago; i didn\\\'t like myself enough, i didn\\\'t think it, or i, was worth the effort. it seemed easier to eat.</p>\r\n<p><b>man</b>, i am just all about long comments lately.</p>\r\n<p>this was a good piece. i\\\'m glad that you wrote it. it takes guts to write about this. i tried to write about it and failed: people assumed i was losing weight because i\\\'d caved, because i needed to conform, because i hated myself, and i was criticized, as if i had depledged from the chubby girl sorority. which is funny because being overweight my whole life and feeling constantly judged has only made me feel alone.</p>',2,1983,0,0,'172.136.149.225','en'),(16819,2777,'2002-05-23 20:50:29',66,'Peter','<br><tt><a href=\\\"http://www.snopes2.com/business/secret/drpepper.htm\\\">snopes strikes again</a>...<br><br>i love dr. pepper though. i am happy that theyre now selling it at more than 5 places in manhattan! :D</tt>',1,218,0,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16820,2775,'2002-05-23 20:58:36',278,'egenus','The wtc attacks were horrible, but they didn\\\'t originate from as far away as we would like to believe. I agree with chiamattt that we must deal with the governmental/corporate terrorism that our country generates. It sickens me that we blame and lay down merciless judgement on everyone but ourselves, and we are some of the worst.',3,334,66,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16821,2777,'2002-05-23 20:59:20',196,'jasonp','But it wasn\\\'t that when you were 10 you cared about your body so much. You cared about the opinion of someone you admired.<BR><BR>\nBody image and weight issues aren\\\'t really guy things to worry about (there are things that plague us). I was able to lose 15 lbs almost fairly easily because I wasn\\\'t emotionally hung up on it. My goal was to be \\\"in-shape\\\". My goal wasn\\\'t to lose weight it was to be able to run a certain distance, lift a certain amount, etc. When you start working out for the purposee of losing weight it all of a sudden becomes much more burdensome.<BR><BR>\nIt\\\'s best when you can find friends that don\\\'t make that a big issue against you. A girlfriend (now wife) of a friend of mine was anorexic/bulemic, unnaturally skinny. It was all in her circle of friends.<BR>\nI think body image and all that is just a focal point for insecurity to come out as. That\\\'s why the anorexics are never satisfied with how they look. ',0,963,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16822,2775,'2002-05-23 21:12:59',66,'Peter','<br><tt>im all about being non-judgmental and fair, as well as admitting to and accepting blame. we\'re indeed all guilty.<br><br>my point was simply that maybe that exact point could be raised a little more effectively than giving a witty retort comparing clinton and bin laden; i dont know about you, but bomb-wounded people dying from lack of medicine (as chia says) is terrible but perhaps not directly comparible to toppling the trade center. i mean, there were many people hit by wtc schrapnel that died after the fact or were maimed because there was no immediate treatment available due to the panic, so whatever. the two are only finitely comparable.<br><br>\r\noh well, this sort of coment is bound to get me flamed, so ill just end it here and claim its just my opinion. i dont know why i posted this comment in the first place, i guess its just my bickering manhattanite nature coming up for a gasp of fresh air. :D</tt>',1,577,278,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16823,2775,'2002-05-23 21:44:21',196,'jasonp','Hmm, it\\\'s been awhile since 9/11 because this doesn\\\'t enrage me as much as it once would have. Now its only slightly irritating.<BR>\nI\\\'ll leave it at that since Peter has enough composure and charity to not let it bother him.<BR>\n',0,239,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16824,2777,'2002-05-23 22:40:22',74,'jo','oh wow. thanks for debunking the myth. i think that part of the reason it\\\'s succeeded for so long was their \\\"10, 2, 4\\\" logo, which people took to mean: \\\"drinking a dr. pepper at 10, 2, & 4 will keep you regular.\\\" <BR><a href=\\\"http://www.mmmbed.com/cam/pepper.jpg\\\">i\\\'m a pepper</a>, too, peter.',1,317,66,0,'172.169.90.12','en'),(16825,2775,'2002-05-23 22:53:05',66,'Peter','<br><tt><a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2576.txt\\\">some memories</a> we\\\'d like to fade quicker than others. im definitely over-sensitive still, as i live in the city and work within the wtc radius. were i to have witnessed it from a far away place, through the tv as opposed to through my nose and hands and eyes, i\\\'d probably wholeheartedly agree with your sentiment, though.</tt>',1,418,196,0,'24.90.92.20','en'),(16826,2772,'2002-05-23 23:26:46',176,'Scapegoat','I have to second what <b>ook</b> pointed out.<br><br>I would also like to state there have been so many times I wish I could take mine off and put it away. I often find my \\\'unit\\\', as I like to call it, can be both a benevolent and cruel dictator - but always a dictator. ',0,277,0,0,'64.229.168.12','en'),(16827,2775,'2002-05-24 00:29:42',176,'Scapegoat','Random thoughts floating in my mind related to the WTC attacks.<br><br>\r\n<li>You support your military regardless of what they are deployed for - they do a job its the politicians that decide what that job is. If you are angry the anger is for the politicians not the military<br><br>\r\n<li>What is being done elsewhere may not be motivated by my reasoning but it is still achieving things I support in Afghanistan<br><br>\r\n<li>The rest of the developed world should be a little less critical of the US globally, especially Europe. No other global player is willing to take the lead very often in anything vital that requires force.<br><br>\r\nThese are just MHO though. American foreign policy has not been a shinning beacon of morality but that doesn\\\'t change how I feel about the above. The stuff like the Osama key chain is kinda like those Serial killer cards on steriods, where there is money to be made... <br><br>\r\nFor the record, I\\\'m a Canadian.       ',0,964,0,0,'64.229.168.12','en'),(16828,2770,'2002-05-24 00:32:12',168,'laney','i have to chime in here, if only to let kelly know that egg is right -- every girl on earth has felt this. i feel it all the time living around god forsaken los angeles. and don\\\'t even get me started on santa barbara. i used to think i could never compete with any of those women, being short and slightly chubby and somewhat scruffy (as in not put-together) and it made me so depressed all the time. but i think i realized i don\\\'t want what they are all competing for. \r\nand now i feel much happier with myself, and am so at one with my body that i am about to prance around the woods naked. woo!',0,603,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(16829,2769,'2002-05-24 01:06:29',168,'laney','yes, tons, because it\\\'s the standard loney-single-person dating fantasy. but kinda like the grocery store, i don\\\'t believe it really happens much in real life. when i was single and washer/dryer-less i definitely had fantasies about meeting someone in the laundromat. but i lived in a mountain town and unfortunately all the guys were toothless or had gun racks.',0,368,86,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(16830,2753,'2002-05-24 01:11:39',176,'Scapegoat','<b>ntl:</b> I understand the issue of the white sock in the world of red. When confronted with media images (either from the media or other people) that I myself have issues with I react by negatively judging other peoples taste instead of internalizing, I suppose. Does this help me make more sense to you?<br><br>\r\n<b>Kandrer:</b> I agree with the idea of people feeling responsible. I\\\'d say though that the media probably does feel somewhat responsible for the stuff it puts in peoples heads but it has no real collective desire to change. I also agree with the tinyness but I\\\'m still too young perhaps to agree with the futility. Give me another 2 or 3 years.',0,669,0,0,'64.229.168.12','en'),(16831,2773,'2002-05-24 01:30:31',234,'etienne','that is creepy.',0,15,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(16832,2770,'2002-05-24 01:46:55',297,'kelly','thanks - what you said is stuff i really needed to hear. i know i\\\'m not the only one who feels like this but at times here it\\\'s hard to remember that. i\\\'ve heard similar things about l.a. as well. i\\\'m glad i didnt grow up in places like this though. when i told my friend here how things were in my hometown she became instantly jealous and said it must be so nice to go somewhere and not have the pressure to look thin and perfect constantly. ',0,456,168,0,'24.185.90.139','en'),(16833,1624,'2002-05-24 01:50:25',234,'etienne','meet you on the stoop after work, then we will sit outside all night long.  smoke cigarettes & talk to the people in your neigbourhood.  we\\\'ll run down teh road and take photos, sing silly improv\\\'d songs about people we see, swing around in the swirling colours of the city.',1,280,66,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(16834,1624,'2002-05-24 02:13:45',66,'Peter','<br><tt>saint etienne</tt>',1,26,234,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(16835,2779,'2002-05-24 02:22:35',65,'le','<b>confidential to cyan if she ever reads this</b>:<br>\r\ni washed my hand after peeing on it. ',4,94,0,0,'208.239.14