,'en'),(16843,2779,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i love the astrology talk. it\\\'s so soothing; it carries me along like a catchy song with lyrics in another language. i don\\\'t understand, but i think i understand. it\\\'s nice.',1,182,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(16844,2779,'2002-05-24 04:36:15',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i used to think that martha stewart was the #1 vocal track for napping to. now, i want to take a nap and listen to le\\\'s astrology teacher\\\'s tapes. very soothing. talk of houses and moons and cusps; very pretty.</tt>',1,229,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(16845,2779,'2002-05-24 04:40:16',59,'s5','when i had lessons from jamie, i learned quickly that listening to her tapes before bed was a losing proposition. i would fall asleep after 10 minutes of listening every night.',0,176,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16846,2779,'2002-05-24 05:13:38',93,'chiamattt','peeing on your feet is also good for athletes foot. just dont eat the yellow snow, that DOES NOT get rid of heartburn.',2,118,0,0,'211.32.132.206','en'),(16847,2779,'2002-05-24 05:40:53',168,'laney','more astrology talk, le! and it\\\'s not just because i\\\'m an aquarian...',0,75,0,0,'198.81.17.162','en'),(16848,2779,'2002-05-24 17:04:39',17,'austin','<P>you had a bumper sticker.  that means i can have one.</P>',4,60,86,0,'64.174.154.174','en'),(16849,2779,'2002-05-24 05:42:13',17,'austin','get up on your period?',3,22,65,0,'64.174.154.174','en'),(16850,2777,'2002-05-24 06:11:17',168,'laney','ntl: you feel that way because you have been programmed, against your will, to think that you are somehow less of a person because you don\\\'t live up to the advertising industry\\\'s idea of what a perfect body is.</p>\r\n<p>there have been so many posts lately, it seems, about body image, and it\\\'s really pissing me off. not because i don\\\'t get it, but because i TOTALLY get it. for a long time it\\\'s something i dealt with all the time. every day. and it sucks so badly. i felt like if i were 25 or 30 pounds skinnier, i would actually be <i>completely happy</i>. i know it\\\'s not true, but i couldn\\\'t shake the feeling, because everywhere there are images and messages telling me it is true. have you heard those radio commercials about \\\"body solution\\\" or some shit that they say you drink at night before you go to bed and in a few months you will be skinny? i was driving in my car and feeling so shitty about myself that i called the fucking phone number and ordered the stuff. it came, and i felt like the biggest asshole. it made me so mad that i actually fell for the stupid scam, i never tried it. it\\\'s still sitting somewhere in my bathroom.</p>\r\n<p>anyway, i am acting like i\\\'m over the whole thing, because i\\\'m trying to make myself think i am over it (i have not lost weight, just tried very hard to accept my body love it). but it\\\'s so so difficult, i know. *sigh*</p>',0,1415,0,0,'198.81.16.156','en'),(16851,2772,'2002-05-24 06:23:29',168,'laney','rock on. i am intimately familiar with the man having multiple orgasms thing, but i have to admit i did *not* know it was so easy for women to pee standing up. i am going to try it now. feeling very empowered, thanks.',0,217,186,0,'198.81.16.156','en'),(16852,2779,'2002-05-24 06:38:12',74,'jo','ME, TOO! BECAUSE. FULL MOON! MUAHAHAHAHA I\\\'VE RUINED YOU!',1,60,65,0,'172.169.26.250','en'),(16853,2777,'2002-05-24 08:46:49',59,'s5','you should find the body solution stuff and set it on fire. if it\\\'s flammable. otherwise use it as fertilizer.',2,113,168,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16854,2779,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>me and aquarians are weird.  it\\\'s a very definite trend.  they want varying degrees of intensity and commitment from me.  usually i can\\\'t return it. frustrating for them. frustrating for me, because mostly the aquarians i meet are really nice, and really the only reason i can\\\'t return their feelings is laziness.  oh well.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>poison ivy sucks.  le, when i got my clit hood pierced, i was told to pee on it if i could manage it.  i managed it!</p>',6,466,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16855,2779,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>maybe jamie\\\'s tapes are the cure to your intermittent insomnia?</p>',6,73,59,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16856,2770,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>yeah kelly.  i felt like that when i lived in sydney where not only the gorgeous skinny pristine australian girls but also the buff pristine fags made me feel like a hefalump.  and the two guys in sydney that aren\\\'t fags are after the skinny pristine women.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i recently married a greek-italian boy who loves my body so much that i have basically forgotten about the body issues i used to have.  i wonder if i would still be irritated by the skinny pristine girls if it wasn\\\'t for him.</p>',6,506,297,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16857,2770,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','not that being greek-italian has anything to do with it.  i just like mentioning it.',6,84,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16858,2779,'2002-05-24 09:13:27',86,'egg','I would say that for me the core manifestation of moon in aquarius is a strong feeling of kinship towards anyone regardless of their institutional, biological, or sexual relationship to you. when i was young I saw the french movie <i>Diva</i> and there was a brief moment that made a strong impression on me: the disgruntled mail delivery boy whose moped has been lost visits his married couple friends at their home, and is so distraught that he lays in their bed with his helmet on. the wife embraces him and lays down with him, and then the husband joins. just like that, they all lay down in bed together, without any sort of sexual pretense or self-consciousness. moon in aquarius is a feeling of general humanitarian goodwill, about wanting to include everyone you know in the flow of love, even the garbage collector you dont know yet and your best friend\\\'s other best friend whom youve just met. everyone, family, in love, and free from posession. ',0,959,65,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16859,2779,'2002-05-24 09:19:13',86,'egg','wow i just wrote that comment without realizing that was my brief experience of omega. most everyone I met was quite warm, and not in a forced or showy way, just welcoming and relaxed. lazy shirtless summer smiles. ',0,215,65,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16860,2779,'2002-05-24 09:20:56',59,'s5','the only thing i remember about the movie \\\'diva\\\' is a scene involving an ice pick.',4,88,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16861,2777,'2002-05-24 14:01:04',168,'laney','good suggestion, s5. and i did want to add that i think the reason i feel so much better lately is because i have this ver nice boy who is constantly telling me how much he loves my body the way it is. i know this doesn\\\'t help you, ntl, right now--if you don\\\'t have anyone-- but just know it\\\'s out there.',0,313,0,0,'198.81.17.184','en'),(16862,2773,'2002-05-24 14:43:42',265,'chuckspeed','s\\\'what happens when you crash a high school cheerleader party - beauty on the outside/ugly on the inside.  kanderer\\\'s got x-ray vision.',0,141,0,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(16863,2779,'2002-05-24 15:39:17',278,'egenus','<p>I am also curious to know how much of the uber-feminisim is real and how much helps sell tickets/books/etc... I\\\'d love to hear your thoughts.</p>',1,151,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16864,2773,'2002-05-24 16:17:36',186,'ook','<p>i have a friend who does this to most songs when she sings along - she makes them all about her instead of all about the mysterious \\\"you\\\" that the singer sings to.',0,172,0,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(16865,2779,'2002-05-24 16:20:21',266,'spleenless','Once again, I\\\'m picking up on the least profound part of the discussion...\r\n\r\nI once save a very stupid 100lb doberman from sliding off a bit of a cliff.  Unfortunately, part of the rescue involed grabbing onto the root of a poison oak plant.  I went home and took a shower and washed with lots of soap.  Unfortunately, that only spread the oil all over me.  Including my private parts.  I had a swollen pustulating penis for about a week.  I didn\\\'t go to work.  I didn\\\'t do anything.  It sucked.  It was all over my arm and hand too.  Then in got onto my face.  ughhhhhh...  I had managed to spread the oil onto sheets and clothing even after I showered.\r\n\r\nThe moral of the story is that if you think you\\\'ve come in contact with the stuff, you\\\'ve got to wash your skin with something that will remove the oil.  Rubbing alcohol works.  Gasoline works.  I don\\\'t know about pee. \r\n\r\nNote:  sometimes soap and water will work but not when you\\\'ve been holding onto the root of the plant while supporting a 100lb dog.',0,1034,0,0,'66.92.184.86','en'),(16866,2779,'2002-05-24 17:03:40',86,'egg','you\\\'re <i> so specific</i> with this question that it naturally opens an intelligent discussion. how much of this spiderman is real and how much of it is just to sell books/tickets?  and that stephen hawking guy, was he a REAL physicist or was he just trying to make some money? and those guys on Iron Chef, are they really chefs? does anyone ever question the motives of Michael Moore or Studs Terkel? I\\\'ve never heard that. Self-help is definitely a marketing scheme to some extent, but noone ever questions of Thich Nath Han is <i>actually</i> a monk or asks to see Deepak Chopra\\\'s <i>medical license</i>. excuse my Salinger-esque italics.',0,651,278,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16867,2778,'2002-05-24 17:57:42',186,'ook','<p>this reminded me of the rainforest not too far from my house, so thanks. wet, green, cold, enormous.\r\n\r\n<p>it also invoked visions of that scene from <i>Legend</i> where young Tom Cruise is holding a wild fox. double thanks!',0,227,0,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(16868,2773,'2002-05-24 18:51:12',187,'kandrer','i wanted to write something on my own sounding like this (i don\\\'t know why; a sort of \\\"thought of that day\\\" thing), and the radiohead song came by itself.<br>\r\nyeah... i wonder how it must be to think that way during \\\"normal life time\\\". for most of us it would be an experience a la \\\"being john malkovitch\\\", i guess.<br>\r\n<u>ook:</u> wow, she must be <i>interesting</i> whether it\\\'s first or second degree. i have a french friend who sings english songs translating them to french. that instantly gives them a terrible cheesy style :).',1,559,0,0,'207.93.61.92','en'),(16869,2763,'2002-05-24 19:12:37',307,'hool','<p>appreciated. negative criticism is good, and all too rare.</p>',0,65,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(16870,2773,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>i replace \\\'you\\\' with \\\'shoes\\\'.  also \\\'love\\\' with \\\'lunch\\\'.  it makes most songs much more interesting.  also recently i have been subbing \\\'me\\\' with \\\'steve\\\'.  regard:</p>\r\n<p>\\\"i wanna know what lunch is, i want shoes to show steve!\\\"</p>',6,278,186,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16871,2686,'2002-05-24 20:31:56',200,'chiclet','your imagery knocked me on my ass and made me giggle like a schoolgirl.',0,71,196,0,'12.233.47.46','en'),(16872,2773,'2002-05-24 21:08:05',124,'gus','hahaha',0,6,62,0,'64.172.56.92','en'),(16873,2777,'2002-05-24 21:16:24',311,'ntl','i do have someone and they care for me very much.  and when i\\\'m with them, i feel beautiful.  unfortunately, i\\\'ve had such a terrible image of myself for so long, that the second i walk away from the \\\"positive reinforcement,\\\" i forgot it all together.   there are many habits that are hard to break - self loathing (for any reason) is definitely one of them.',0,370,0,0,'144.92.164.199','en'),(16874,2753,'2002-05-24 21:21:11',311,'ntl','kandrer:  i did understand that you were in agreement with me...   \r\n\r\nscapegoat:  your explanation does make your stance more clear.  i only wish that i had the ability to dismiss negative images instead of becoming addicted to them...',0,236,0,0,'144.92.164.199','en'),(16875,2777,'2002-05-24 21:56:02',124,'gus','I once heard naomi wolfe speak, she\\\'s a feminist and all that, but that\\\'s not important. She talked about a study that was done. they took random college students and put them all on various diets for a couple of months. Diets from magazines and books, all the popular types. At the end of the study, all of the college students had one or more signs of an eating disorder. They were all male.\r\n<p>\r\nIt does happen with boys, we just dont hear about it as often. boys dont sit around complaining about their thighs like us girls do.\r\n<p>\r\nI consider myself lucky, maybe it\\\'s because I was really active as a kid, or because I didnt have many friends so didnt notice what people thought about me, or the people who didnt like me were all lame ass \\\'tards so I didnt care what they thought so much? i dunno. but as a dancer it is pretty rare that I didnt suffer some sort of body image diet problems early on. I have some minor food issues these days, and worry about about my belly poking out, but well, Im over 30 now and it IS poking out a little. I know Im very lucky in this regard. ',0,1097,196,0,'64.172.56.92','en'),(16876,2777,'2002-05-24 22:22:20',59,'s5','personally i find a bit of belly on a girl to be insanely cute.',2,63,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16877,2779,'2002-05-24 23:41:07',74,'jo','i heart aquarians. there\\\'s always some subject about which an aquarian is exceptionally knowledgable and can make interesting to anyone. aquarians would make great professors.<BR>\r\nhard to go to for advice, though, because they\\\'ll tell you <b>exactly</b> what they think, tactlessly, and repeatedly.',1,305,0,0,'172.164.1.149','en'),(16878,2779,'2002-05-25 01:24:45',120,'joyrider','poison ivy aside (or included, depending on how you come at it), it sounds like omega is a good place so far.  i remember tent dreams from summer camp:  they are visceral.',0,171,65,0,'24.205.201.2','en'),(16879,2779,'2002-05-25 02:59:15',170,'xipibi','Stephen Hawking is most definitely a real physicist who is unfortunately cursed with a body that has left him unable to communicate \\\'cept thru electronics \\\'n\\\' an assistant who can interpret for him.',1,207,86,0,'209.39.34.8','en'),(16880,2775,'2002-05-25 04:20:47',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\ntake the lead?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe u.s presence in afghanistan is a response...not a good will mission of love. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlittle has changed in much of afghanistan. sure the taliban are out of power, but so what? its not like they had a strong central government to begin with. their power was a coalition between a bunch of warlords, who for the most part are still in power. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe press might talk about women being able to walk the streets, but in most pashtun areas, the situation regarding women hasnt changed much. women are free to roam in areas patrolled by coalition forces, but in areas under local militia control, fear is still present. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nafghanistan is a very poor nation. no marshall plan in sight. there are cell phones, but no schools. there are soldiers but not hospitals. there is talk, but no change. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nits a deeply fragmented society, and for this reason nothing military can change what is going on there. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nperhaps you feel there is something positive happening because your dad helped drop a few tonnes of food...or perhaps youre just glad to see the taliban gone. regardless of whose in power, change will not occur at the end of a bayonet. change will occur when people have a future; a future no afghani can realistically see right now. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nas for the keychain. lighten up. if i told all of you a diana joke when diana died most of you would have snickered because diana isnt close to you, but when those jokes hit home, everyone is supposed to take a sympathetic line. no one ever told me to \\\"feel\\\" for the iraqis who died when all those gulf war jokes were going around. blah. people are fickle and one sided. \r\n</p>\r\n',3,1709,176,0,'211.32.132.230','en'),(16881,2775,'2002-05-25 04:24:18',93,'chiamattt','oh and by the way. this is the SECOND war on terrorism. reagan began one in the eighties that sorta just fizzled. no one brings up that these days do they? just like reagan, bush will fail. violence doesn not cure violence. ',0,224,0,0,'211.32.132.230','en'),(16882,2779,'2002-05-25 05:34:37',17,'austin','after the divorce, he married his assistant.  was it becuase he was in love with her, or because she spoon fed him?',3,115,170,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(16883,2777,'2002-05-25 05:52:49',124,'gus','heh, I was just watching 48 Hours, that news show, and they were doing a special report on men with eating disorders. apparently 1 in 6 people with eating disorders are men these days (as opposed to 1 in 10 however many years ago). I am just full of statistics lately. Im not backing them up, Im just mindlessly passing on what I hear on the tv. ok.',0,349,0,0,'64.172.56.92','en'),(16884,2779,'2002-05-25 06:42:14',177,'pony','hi! i\\\'m a cancer. with aquarius in my moon. hee hee. i use to work with anita hill. i never thought she was a hardcore feminist. she just seemed like a really nice lady who belived in standing up for herself. also, i liked egg\\\'s description of the aquarian thing. it explains why im always wanting to nuzzle people ive just met. and before i forget i have to say that law professors tend to be the biggest prima donnas. but anita hill was always nice. ok. i think that\\\'s it for now. bye bye.',1,500,65,0,'209.239.204.137','en'),(16885,2773,'2002-05-25 10:03:22',187,'kandrer','lunch, exciting and new<br>\r\ncome aboard,<br>\r\nwe\\\'re expecting shoes\r\n:>\r\n',1,77,62,0,'205.184.158.187','en'),(16886,2779,'2002-05-25 10:25:42',187,'kandrer','if i were brave, i would suggest that you come back. hist, i mean shit, le, why don\\\'t you come back?',1,103,65,0,'205.184.158.187','en'),(16887,2775,'2002-05-25 10:47:50',187,'kandrer','i agree. btw, i can\\\'t remember a time i thought you weren\\\'t right.',1,72,93,0,'205.184.158.187','en'),(16888,2768,'2002-05-25 11:23:24',187,'kandrer','Peter, i have been wondering: i can\\\'t feel a tenth of what you feel for a city. a city is no one. a city is a wave on the ocean. drops who compose the wave don\\\'t know what they\\\'re doing. the wave don\\\'t know about the drops. the ocean doesn\\\'t know about the wave. and the wave is melted into another one in a few seconds.<br>\r\n(where are the men?)',1,361,66,0,'205.184.158.187','en'),(16889,2775,'2002-05-25 11:29:35',196,'jasonp','I agree with most of the political points you\\\'re saying. What initially bugged me is what seems to be a kind of amused and flippant attitude, especially after looking up your article on 9/11/2001 those were some pretty low, cheap political points to be made.<BR><BR>\nHere\\\'s your hypocrisy I see:<BR>\nYou are so \\\"sensitive\\\" to the downtrodden around the world who suffer under US oppression, but people right here on this site that were directly affected you could care less about. <BR>\nWhere\\\'s your righteousness when it comes to Islamic fascist fundamentalists and what they\\\'ve done? If you want to rail against injustice or oppression why does it start and stop at what the US/west/capitalism has done? What about the parts of the world where women are treated like dirt, where people are executed for speaking their mind?<BR>\n<HR>\nA journalist/commentator I think is cool as hell is Christopher Hitchens, (a socialist BTW) who takes on the predictable reaction of the left.<BR>\nHere\\\'s a from a\n<A href=\\\"http://www.thenation.com/doc.mhtml?i=20011008&s=hitchens\\\">\n10/22 column</A>:\n\n<I><BLOCKQUOTE><FONT size=\\\"-2\\\">\n...the bombers of Manhattan represent fascism with an Islamic face, and there\\\'s no point in any euphemism about it. What they abominate about \\\"the West,\\\" to put it in a phrase, is not what Western liberals don\\\'t like and can\\\'t defend about their own system, but what they do like about it and must defend: its emancipated women, its scientific inquiry, its separation of religion from the state. Loose talk about chickens coming home to roost is the moral equivalent of the hateful garbage emitted by Falwell and Robertson, and exhibits about the same intellectual content.\n</font></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>\nThen a little\n<A href=\\\"http://www.thenation.com/special/20010911debate.mhtml\\\"> skirmish with him and Chomsky</A> followed.<BR><BR>\nOn Osama from\n<A HREF=\\\"http://www.thenation.com/doc.mhtml?i=20011022&s=hitchens\\\">\n10/22 Blaming bin Laden First</A>\n:<BR>\n<BLOCKQUOTE><FONT size=\\\"-2\\\">\nHe is the spoiled-brat son of one of our preferred despotisms and the proud beneficiary of the export of violence. Why, then, do so many fools consider him as the interpreter of their \\\"concerns,\\\" let alone seek to appoint their ignorant selves as the medium for his?\n</font></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>\n',0,2361,93,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16890,2773,'2002-05-25 13:49:45',240,'xep','When the earth was still flat,<br>\r\n And the clouds made of fire,<br>\r\n And mountains stretched up to the sky,<br>\r\n  Sometimes higher,<br>\r\n Folks roamed the earth<br>\r\nLike big rolling kegs.<br>\r\nThey had two sets of arms.<br>\r\nThey had two sets of legs.<br>\r\nThey had two faces peering<br>\r\nOut of one giant head<br>\r\nSo they could watch all around them<br>\r\nAs they talked; while they read.<br>\r\nAnd they never knew nothing of lunch<br>\r\n It was before the origin of lunch<br>',0,480,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(16891,2768,'2002-05-25 15:31:38',74,'jo','cities are most definitely more than just cities. you might be interested in the <a href=\\\"http://www.citystories.com\\\">citystories</a> website.',0,148,187,0,'172.136.206.33','en'),(16892,2775,'2002-05-25 17:14:25',278,'egenus','<p>I know. The only comments I can usually make on his articles are \\\"Hell yeah\\\" and \\\"I should have thought of that\\\".</p>\r\n\r\n<p>to all: I can\\\'t resist but throw my 2 cents in on the on-going terrorism flame war. I think most people don\\\'t realize how aggressively and consistently the United States engages in acts of terrorism. We\\\'re not merely protecting our own interests, we\\\'re trying to shape the politics and land boundaries of the middle east for our own <b>financial</b> gain. Not to mention that the example that we\\\'re setting is one of violence and intolerance. Sharon is merely copying after the actions of the US following 9/11, and we\\\'re acting surprised at the level of violence they\\\'re using and the trouble they\\\'re starting. Running tanks through the streets of Pakistani cities is extreme and intrusive, but not anywhere close to the extremism of the US in regards to Afghanistan. Before we can spout off about the evils of Islamic extremists, I think we should take a very close look at who\\\'s really responsible for all this trouble. bin Laden was a CIA operative for those who didn\\\'t know...</p>',1,1154,187,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16893,2775,'2002-05-25 21:34:35',196,'jasonp','Just a few clarifications, before this turns into a politico-flamo-fest.<BR>\nI <B>agree</B> with chia and egenus on the main points of what they\\\'re saying. The US has acted out of political and economic self-interest before in overthrowing governments, propping up other governments, causing the deaths around the world, etc.  That\\\'s not any surprise to me.<BR>\n<BR> \nBeing able to reduce 9/11 to some abstract political event on the day it happened is the cause of my emotional reaction. <BR>\nThe implied message seems to be, \\\"Well it\\\'s about time the US got it\\\'s ass kicked.\\\" or \\\"We had it coming\\\". That what\\\'s pisses me off. It\\\'s wrong on a factual level because it\\\'s not a fight, it was a sneak attack mass murder. There was no valid grievance that would justify mass-murder, and that wasn\\\'t their motivation anyway. They represent no valid cause. All of the US atrocities don\\\'t excuse or justify what happened.\n<BR>\nI do see a big difference between, the actions of a terrorist and the US government. The US government kills out of <I><B>greed, power and callous efficiency</B></I>. The Islamic terrorists are motivated by <I><B>hatred, fanaticism, envy, ignorance and intolerance</B></I>. \nNeither side is too good. I\\\'ll side with the first since it\\\'s at least rational. But to think that the terrorists of the world are somehow justified or fighting some noble cause is ridiculous.\n<BR><BR>\nAnd I\\\'ll end that with a big blast of my fire extinguisher and say, \\\"let\\\'s leave it at that.\\\" I don\\\'t want to get anyone riled up any more than necessary. Sex and religion are fair game here, but Swinney is not a good place for politics IMHO.',0,1700,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16894,2775,'2002-05-25 22:18:14',196,'jasonp','A few corrections before I drop this for good.<BR>\nI wrote I\\\'d side with US foreign oppression over Islamic terrorism. That was flip comment. No, actually I side with <B>neither</B>. I won\\\'t define myelf by a reaction.<BR>\nI\\\'m against the killing of innocent people -- actually why is the word \\\"innocent\\\" even necessary? I\\\'m against violence and killing, period. In all cases, except true self defense. I don\\\'t accept the loss of life as either \\\"casualties\\\", \\\"infidels\\\", \\\"capitalist pigs\\\", \\\"gooks\\\", or any other dehumanizing term that reduces anyone to a political, national or racial label.<BR>\nI\\\'m down with the Dalai Lhama. That\\\'s my political hero. The politics of awareness, compassion, and conscience. He doesn\\\'t get angry, yell or point fingers, even though as a truly oppressed person he would certainly seem entitled to. I\\\'ll try and do the same; get real, chill out and lay off the self-righteous rhetoric.\n',0,974,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16895,2773,'2002-05-26 00:59:59',187,'kandrer','don\\\'t know the song but it\\\'s inspired by Platon\\\'s \\\"About Lunch\\\"... hi hi hi. thanks, o, for this good trick. :)',1,126,240,0,'205.184.181.200','en'),(16896,2775,'2002-05-26 01:25:08',187,'kandrer','<i>\\\"Sex and religion are fair game here, but Swinney is not a good place for politics IMHO\\\"</i><br>\r\nwe might have hard times trying to talk about sex, religion, (and other important themes frequent here, like death, racism, education, violence...) <i>without</i> getting politics, right?<br>\r\ni think swinney is one of the rare places where we can have rather constructive discussions. i would regret that some subjects become taboo here too.</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni never thought chia was unfair. does that mean i am unfair myself?\r\n</p>',1,533,196,0,'205.184.181.200','en'),(16897,2768,'2002-05-26 01:36:18',187,'kandrer','i am wondering how one can love a city, but i don\\\'t deny such love is reasonable. i was just expressing my feeling. depending of the moment, i can speak like that about body cells, countries or families. you guess i can\\\'t be 100% serious about this way of viewing things.',1,277,74,0,'205.184.181.200','en'),(16898,2775,'2002-05-26 02:22:41',196,'jasonp','Nothing that you said I\\\'d consider unfair just by agreeing with chia who I largely agree with anyway.<BR><BR>\nI wouldn\\\'t saying politics should be <I>taboo</I>, just that it tends to lead to endless bickering, which is either aggravating or boring depending on if you\\\'re involved in it or not.<BR>\nMuch better to keep it creative and personal here in swinneyland.\n\n',0,374,187,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16899,2773,'2002-05-26 02:36:49',240,'xep','yes indeedy.  <a href=\\\"http://logy.org/~xep/cracklywords/symposium5.txt\\\">here</a> it is.  also, <a href=\\\"http://www.hedwiginabox.com/origin_of_love.htm\\\">here</a> are the rest of the song lyrics.',0,206,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(16900,2377,'2002-05-26 04:30:23',295,'karenlee','Paul thankyou, you have made me think of my wonderful great grandmother. Wilhelmine was her name....she lived a block away from us and we would go over there all the time and make mud pies under her house and hang out in her kitchen. It was always clear she was happy to see us. She lived until she was 95. I keep a picture of her in my room. *nice memories* \r\n',1,361,175,0,'203.194.7.55','en'),(16901,2779,'2002-05-26 05:00:23',65,'le','but i like it here!',0,19,187,0,'208.239.147.211','en'),(16902,2775,'2002-05-26 07:34:13',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\nlets be clear. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe various uses of tomahawk cruise missiles on targets around the world were sneak attacks. the geneva convention, of which the unites states is a signatory, maintains that any direct attack against another state sould be declared and made clear prior to attack. this rarely happens anymore, by anyone, though some \\\'terrorist\\\' groups still notify, through the media, that bombings will take place. this is a somewhat classic tactic used by groups to lessen the loss of life while making their point clear with a bomb blast. shatter windows not bodies. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe u.s uses the talibans record of abuses against women all the time but rarely criticizes the saudi government(the key arab ally in the middle east) for their barbaric treatment of women. Oh yeah, and now the pakistan, an islamic fundamentalist state who DOES support terrorism(in kashmir for instance) is now a friend of the united states for the sole reason that it provides space for the u.s war machine inside its borders for attacks against afghanistan. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i><b>\r\nYou are so \\\"sensitive\\\" to the downtrodden around the world who suffer under US oppression, but people right here on this site that were directly affected you could care less about.\r\n</i></b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthis is not true. to think about any attack on the individual level is less than realistic. i understand that thousands of people were directly and indirectly affected by the wtc attacks, but to include their suffering into the causes and roots of the attack are in my opinion not relevant. to compare a situation like this by observing the horrors of one or two people i think singles them out and somehow tries to connect their individual feelings with the feelings of everyone affected.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i><b>\r\nIf you want to rail against injustice or oppression why does it start and stop at what the US/west/capitalism has done? What about the parts of the world where women are treated like dirt, where people are executed for speaking their mind?\r\n</i></b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni speak out against injustice towards women and those who are executed for speaking their mind in all areas of the world, but because i live in a society so economically linked with the united states i cant help but rail. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit is a fact that as standards of living, education, medical attention go up, crime goes down. religious activity disipates and tolerance expands. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe west is the richest entity on the face of this planet, yet it(capitalism) cannot invision a world where wealth is shared because greed is so entrenched in the ways we think. beyond anything else, profit is how you move ahead these days rather then invoation, cooperation, and tolerance. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i><b>\r\nThe US government kills out of greed, power and callous efficiency. The Islamic terrorists are motivated by hatred, fanaticism, envy, ignorance and intolerance.\r\n</i></b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni dont agree with this one bit. would you not agree that from a terrorist perspective the wtc attack was callously effient? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndid the u.s and soviet stances towards each other not stem from their HATRED of each others ideologies? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ngreed is greed, but on each side that greed is different in how each side defines power. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfanatic - patriot - jingoist\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\none in the same. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nignorance and intolerance. this stems from the fact that areas of the world most affected by violent crime, terrorism, and war have for the most part crumbling or non existent education and medical facilities, low standards of living and little or no hope for a positive future. you dont have to look far from home to see what im talking about, though the further from home you go, the more clear it becomes. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i><b>\r\nI wouldn\\\'t saying politics should be taboo, just that it tends to lead to endless bickering, which is either aggravating or boring depending on if you\\\'re involved in it or not.\r\nMuch better to keep it creative and personal here in swinneyland. \r\n</i></b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhaha\r\n</p> ',1,4099,0,0,'61.37.53.25','en'),(16903,2782,'2002-05-26 07:54:36',93,'chiamattt','ignorance is bliss, until you get genital herpes. ',6,50,0,0,'61.37.53.25','en'),(16904,2782,'2002-05-26 12:06:37',89,'sawks','you\\\'re young and stupid, but at least you didn\\\'t catch hiv and kill anyone...',5,83,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(16905,2783,'2002-05-26 20:51:34',323,'outlandishjosh','<p>Lovely lovely urban potics, whistful and woeful and piercing. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Can I use it for a performance?</p>',1,111,0,0,'66.114.70.38','en'),(16906,2783,'2002-05-26 23:08:39',295,'karenlee','Sure can. Where is your performance?',2,36,323,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(16907,2782,'2002-05-27 01:48:46',186,'ook','the thing is that anyone who may have caught this from you is equally stupid. ',0,78,0,0,'209.148.183.179','en'),(16908,2785,'2002-05-27 04:33:07',127,'pokey','The first rule of fight club is you do not talk about fight club.\r\n\r\nHonestly... what were you thinking?\r\n\r\n',1,108,0,0,'63.191.128.6','en'),(16909,2785,'2002-05-27 04:34:36',127,'pokey','oops.  i forgot the </sarcasm> tag.\r\n\r\n',0,39,0,0,'63.191.128.6','en'),(16910,2785,'2002-05-27 04:36:12',127,'pokey','end sarcasm\r\n',0,13,0,0,'63.191.128.6','en'),(16911,2785,'2002-05-27 04:47:35',75,'tyler','cool',1,4,0,0,'165.247.164.55','en'),(16912,2785,'2002-05-27 10:16:56',174,'Mad Moll','<p>What? Stifle progress in the name of <i>THE QUAINT?</i> Is that all we have left to live for? Cash in our chips and just CHILL OUT? Sacrifice the problems of civlization <i>for what</i>? For the sake of what? Some non-confrontational, \\\"everything is like, relative, dewd\\\" culture of mediocrity? Will there be a special army to viciously stomp out anyone or anything that is not sufficiently CHILLED OUT? Why don\\\'t we just cut to the chase and shoot ourselves right here? Reincarnate into beavers and squirrels and call it even? For fuck\\\'s sake. You have to be kidding me with this shit. I don\\\'t even <i>like</i> The System all that much, but even this corporate psycho bullshit is better than trying to turn the world into some contrived playschool where everyone agrees with everyone else and no one commits to anything and no one has any real convictions about, where no one dares to take risks.</p>\r\n<p>THAT WHICH IS GIVEN FREELY HAS NO VALUE!</p>\r\n<p>LONG LIVE THE EMPIRE!</p>',0,998,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(16913,2785,'2002-05-27 11:14:47',196,'jasonp','I imagined the voice as just like that hippy teacher on beavis and butthead.<BR>\nThis was cool because it was ambiguously absurd and/or utopian.<BR><BR>\nMoll, how would you like a world where you could be tied up in leather bondage gear, pumped-up full of steroids and be allowed to box kangaroos in public to relieve all that hostility?',0,337,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16914,2784,'2002-05-27 11:26:51',196,'jasonp','descriptive and depressing',0,26,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16915,2783,'2002-05-27 11:27:56',196,'jasonp','I like the neurotic, antibiotic, etc. line.<BR>\nthis could make a good song lyrics',0,82,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16916,2786,'2002-05-27 11:58:19',196,'jasonp','I got drunk last night and sat in front of my monitor for almost an hour trying to conjure some vague story idea I had about being drunk in a bar in Fairbanks, Alaska. The image I had was of staring at a wooden brown bar counter with water rings in it from where glasses without coasters were placed. It would be very cold outside but stuffy and stale inside, with the smell of cigarretes, stale beer and fish. There would be an impassive, mute, middle-aged eskimo woman behind the counter and a broken black-white TV sitting on a folding chair showing white static.',0,566,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16917,2785,'2002-05-27 12:25:27',295,'karenlee','ha I\\\'m laughing at (I like) the bit about the black market ha .\r\n',2,68,0,0,'203.194.19.142','en'),(16918,2785,'2002-05-27 18:05:51',278,'egenus','<p>Very cool.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Moll: Stop being repressed.</p>',1,55,0,0,'24.25.107.161','en'),(16919,2785,'2002-05-27 18:44:28',323,'outlandishjosh','<p>Mad Moll:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Well played. Context: I was trying to approxomate something along the lines of Stepen Gaskin\\\'s \\\"Monday Night Class\\\" in an attempt to come to terms with my flagratly hippy liniage. It is a bit beyond the realm of pragmatism. I sincerely hope you enjoy and profit by your zero-sum theories. They tend to be higly effective. I for one believe that people can win w/o other people having to loose. That\\\'s probably why I\\\'m poor.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>LONG LIVE THE REBELLION</p>',1,499,0,0,'66.114.70.38','en'),(16920,1242,'2002-05-27 19:39:20',196,'jasonp','Canadian jingoist... Chairman mao does not approve.',0,51,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16921,2735,'2002-05-27 21:30:23',200,'chiclet','i really like the caption.  poignant.',0,37,0,0,'12.233.47.46','en'),(16922,2782,'2002-05-27 21:47:12',187,'kandrer','today, i don\\\'t find this so stupid. being careful when you\\\'re about to have sex is a highly unnatural behavior. now, society declares that this is highly stupid. i can see why but i don\\\'t agree.',1,203,0,0,'204.33.141.66','en'),(16923,2713,'2002-05-28 00:38:00',200,'chiclet','it\\\'s neat to see how this evolved from your notes.  also i really like it; it reminds me of la cite des enfants perdus.',0,122,0,0,'12.233.47.46','en'),(16924,2785,'2002-05-28 04:26:18',176,'Scapegoat','I have to say that I\\\'m with <b>Mad Moll</b> on this one. I might not be invecting as much though.<br><br>\r\nEMPIRE. ',0,118,0,0,'64.229.162.191','en'),(16925,2779,'2002-05-28 04:28:52',185,'orion','I\\\'m an aquarius.  feb 1, 1978, 8:52pm houston, tx.  Virgo ascendent.  It seems like all my friends end up being either virgos or geminis, not that I use it as a criterion or litmus or something, just that\\\'s how it seems to shake out.  My ex is a gemini, as is my best friend from high school, my ex\\\'s best friend, and my mom.  My other best friend from high school is an aquarius, born one year and one day after me.  Not that this information has any real point, just tossing more data into the aquarius thread. ;-)  maybe i should set up viewing hours at my apartment like a zoo.  \\\"Come see the wild aquarius in it\\\'s native habitat!  (\\\"ook! ook! goddamned sendmail!\\\")\\\" lol...',3,701,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(16926,2785,'2002-05-28 04:53:13',65,'le','squirrels are cute. i\\\'d rather be a chipmunk though. i\\\'m not even touching the beaver reference. \r\n<br><br>\r\nwhere is moll. down by the tracks?',4,149,0,0,'208.239.147.211','en'),(16927,2785,'2002-05-28 04:58:54',74,'jo','man. i hate hippies.',1,20,0,0,'172.137.20.170','en'),(16928,2785,'2002-05-28 05:36:24',17,'austin','<P>i am a hippie.</P>',4,21,74,0,'64.174.154.202','en'),(16929,2785,'2002-05-28 06:36:17',59,'s5','i pictured george carlin.',4,25,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16930,2785,'2002-05-28 12:25:30',64,'Joanna','I think there should be more pirates on television.  That\\\'s all. ',1,68,0,0,'203.167.247.214','en'),(16931,2783,'2002-05-28 12:29:33',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i heard a guy doing some amazing urban spoken-word in detroit this past weekend; this reminded me of it all over again.</tt>',1,132,295,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16932,2783,'2002-05-28 16:28:09',265,'chuckspeed','sorry i missed you man - text message came thru but i didnt get it till late monday.  a lost weekend; a story for another time.',0,127,66,0,'208.5.114.89','en'),(16933,2783,'2002-05-28 16:30:22',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ahh, next time, then. i can assure you that after this past weekend, i will definitely and repeatedly be returning to detroit!</tt>',1,139,265,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16934,2783,'2002-05-28 16:34:33',265,'chuckspeed','center of the universe, man.',0,28,0,0,'208.5.114.89','en'),(16935,2783,'2002-05-28 16:46:07',66,'Peter','<br><tt>before last friday night, i had my doubts; now i know for sure that youre absolutely <a href=\"http://www.citynoise.org/index.php?article=353\">right</a>, and on many levels.</tt>',1,187,265,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16936,2787,'2002-05-28 17:32:50',180,'auntie','i am totally confused.  and alarmed.',4,36,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(16937,2787,'2002-05-28 17:46:47',66,'Peter','<br><tt>sorry, but the words \\\"lotto chat forum\\\" make me crack up...</tt>',1,78,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16938,2787,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','dude.  australians are dumb.',6,28,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16939,2779,'2002-05-28 19:23:26',86,'egg','you\\\'re a genius.',0,19,170,0,'216.175.88.18','en'),(16940,2790,'2002-05-28 22:34:10',128,'Javorai','no just a high art, but a way of life.  cheers!',1,47,0,0,'63.39.68.29','en'),(16941,2791,'2002-05-28 22:41:56',278,'egenus','<p>My wants are very simple. I want to be able to freely express myself all the time, and to surround myself with people freely expressing themselves. I want to be able to live life as I see fit, as long as it doesn\\\'t hurt anyone else. Just peace, love, unity and respect.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>That, and I want to run a rave club in Amsterdam and have greenhouses full of pot....</p>',1,375,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(16942,2775,'2002-05-29 00:24:51',176,'Scapegoat','I have to ask if you think the fact that there are many areas still under local militia control is a good thing? The presence in Afghanistan is in response but in the context of response the US lead forces are the only ones with a mandate outside of the Capital of Kabul.<br><br>\r\nThe international community is unwilling to commit the man/woman power to fully secure the country for the period of time it needs to establish its own security infrastructure. The US will shy away from any sort of nation building due to backlash from other because of their dominant stance already.<br><br>\r\nAs a another side note the majority of the warlords still in government come from non-Pashtun ethnicities ala Northern Alliance.<br><br>\r\nI see the failure of things like this as failures of the global community and not something to be soley placed on the U.S. We like to put the majority of responsibility on them because we feel they have all the power. Perhaps, I sound like an apologist but I am tiried of watching only <i>Band Aid</i> solutions being used because <i>Band Aids</i> are all that people will really support or put up with.<br><br>\r\nAs for my Dad\\\'s involvement he and I both think it is pointless what we/the west are doing over there. It won\\\'t change a thing unless we really commit the aproppriate resources. It just bothers me that we aren\\\'t willing to do that be those resources enough military to provide security for the fledgling gov\\\'t and women or simply enough food so people aren\\\'t starving.<br><br>\r\nI could get into more detail and go on and on about this sort of stuff but I think this illustrates the points I was trying to make.\r\n',0,1668,93,0,'64.229.162.191','en'),(16943,2790,'2002-05-29 02:08:08',206,'josta','i am reading this as i put off studying for finals...',2,53,0,0,'152.163.206.202','en'),(16944,2785,'2002-05-29 02:44:07',130,'treetop','<p>I don\\\'t really understand this article.  I am sort of a hippie /neo hippie.  I have actually been to Gaskin\\\'s <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.thefarm.org/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">farm</a> in Tennessee.  One of the most astonishing things about that place is \r\nthat their midwifes have a higher successful birthrate than the nearest hospital.  They have solar powerd showers, organic \r\nfarming, and re-use / recycle everything possible.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But wood burning stoves?  Ummmm, I think your \\\"dewd\\\" man, is a little confused about evolving.  <br>What about \r\nconserving our natural resources and building energy efficient homes and transportation?  We have the technology and the \r\ndesire.  Maybe big oil is just suppressing the demand?  Your \\\"neo hippie\\\" in this article seems to be an idiot who would rather \r\ngo back in time than go <a href=\\\"http://www.gaia.org/about/guide-ev-start.asp\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">forward</a>.<br>\r\n{destabilizing}</p>\r\n',4,974,323,0,'172.170.205.102','en'),(16945,2790,'2002-05-29 03:03:31',124,'gus','Im putting off writing my next swinney by reading this. DAMN YOU!\r\n<P>\r\nI hate lists. I try not to make them unless they are lists of what I want for my birthday or for xmas. then I make them REALLLL Loooong.',0,208,0,0,'64.163.214.225','en'),(16946,2791,'2002-05-29 03:13:31',130,'treetop','<b>to egenus:</b>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>I like the Amsterdam idea!  Got mad skills, need any help?',1,87,0,0,'172.170.205.102','en'),(16947,2790,'2002-05-29 04:25:29',93,'chiamattt','i find that cleaning the bathroom is usually a way of procrastinating. productive procrastination is the key. putting off something crap by doing a lot of other small things that make a difference in the short/medium term. ie house work. ',6,238,0,0,'211.32.132.205','en'),(16948,2790,'2002-05-29 05:21:27',307,'jeeff&hool','<p>i never make lists.  random access is the way to go.</p>',0,59,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(16949,2790,'2002-05-29 05:22:11',307,'hool','<p>fuck. i posted with the wrong name. i\\\'m too lazy to go edit it. this will do. resume. </p>',0,96,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(16950,2790,'2002-05-29 05:33:33',17,'austin','im a list maker.  i like to get things done.  that is the best place for me.  the doing place.  i spend most of my time aimlessly doing.  on the way to finishing a list, i may get side tracked for a month, but when i get finished with the side track, i have my list to keep me from VERTIGO.  back to the list.',3,309,0,0,'64.174.154.108','en'),(16951,2790,'2002-05-29 05:56:00',59,'s5','okay, so you put off the laundry. but how many times have you done the laundry instead of doing some other thing you were meant to be doing? it all balances out. the more you put off, the more you get done.',4,206,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16952,2785,'2002-05-29 06:30:43',174,'Vong Moll','<p>Through his prejudices, through his obsession with \\\'harmony\\\', his enmity towards real difference (it\\\'s \\\"okay\\\" to have different views, so long as you don\\\'t act on them, so long as you don\\\'t \\\"cause trouble\\\") -- the hippie reveals his true face: an good-natured authoritarian in sheep\\\'s clothing.</p>',6,331,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(16953,2791,'2002-05-29 06:41:02',174,'Mad Moll','<p>My wants are also simple. I want to be Emperor. That is all. I want to be able to look out the window on a nice day and know that I can go for a walk down the street, surrounded by my musicians, courtesans, translators, soldiers, incense-holders, crystal-voiced eunuchs, saints, painters, biographers and a retinue of peons who would sweep and polish the pavement -- and if they had time, maybe plant some flowers -- in the general direction I am walking.</p>\r\n<p>That\\\'s all I want: a proper walk down the avenue.</p>',6,523,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(16954,2791,'2002-05-29 14:02:24',180,'auntie','i want to not be exhausted and in pain anymore.  i want to live to see my grandchildren grow up.  i want a doctor who will try to find out what is wrong with me.',4,161,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(16955,2791,'2002-05-29 14:14:39',66,'Peter','<br><tt>to be happy</tt>',1,24,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16956,2791,'2002-05-29 15:40:45',246,'erb','you seem plenty happy to me.  what would make you happier?\r\n:)',2,62,66,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(16957,2791,'2002-05-29 15:42:28',66,'Peter','<br><tt>oh im happy now; i just hope to perpetuate the happiness all around me! having good people around would help. after travelling around some, im starting to realize what asses alot of nyc folks are.</tt>',1,84,246,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16958,2791,'2002-05-29 15:59:41',246,'erb','at least new yorkers are unapologetic about their cigarette smokin\\\' coffee sluggin\\\' subway shovin\\\' selves.  better to live among jerks than phony niceguys.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nlooking back at this post, i think i should move to santa cruz after grad school.  ',2,259,0,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(16959,2790,'2002-05-29 16:11:12',81,'felicity','WOW!  You guys are all incredibly productive procrastinators.  When I don\\\'t want to do something I either take a nap or masturbate to porn, neither of which do either me or society much good in the long run.',1,210,0,0,'216.42.123.44','en'),(16960,2785,'2002-05-29 16:23:19',75,'tyler','i think it\\\'s great and you should book a venue and perform it. leave out the part, \\\"I\\\'m sort of playing a character here,\\\" because of course you are, and the hippy references too. this should be a universal message. dress like an old country boy with a straw hat. be whittling something. and when you get to the \\\"expanding your conciousness\\\" line, take out a joint and sort of roll it in your fingers. and don\\\'t make eye contact with the audience. man that would be awesome.',1,495,0,0,'165.247.164.238','en'),(16961,2791,'2002-05-29 17:20:05',124,'gus','to be content and secure. which means that right now, I want a job that pays my rent. Outside of that, I want time to sit quietly.',0,130,0,0,'64.163.214.225','en'),(16962,2782,'2002-05-29 18:43:23',128,'Javorai','yes',0,3,187,0,'63.39.68.155','en'),(16963,426,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>hm.  i wanted to re-activate this article and make a comment about how happiness is having one thing that you can do with all of your being, and having that thing be fulfilling.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i found out that happiness isn\\\'t fake, unattainable, or minute to minute.</p>',6,271,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(16964,426,'2002-05-29 21:02:32',187,'kandrer','you made me think about the dual nature of emotions.<br>\r\nin physics, they describe the dual nature of light: as a wave and as a bunch of particles.<br>\r\nthoughts are like that too. i think i remember they discovered something like a brain pulse. it\\\'s opposing to how we use to think of our consciousness, as a continued wave - just a bit different while we sleep.<br>\r\nthe hapiness you described in your post looked rather like a particle. the one you talk about in your comment of today must be a wave. :)\r\n',1,512,62,0,'208.223.155.158','en'),(16965,2791,'2002-05-29 21:22:16',240,'xep','santa cruz is the land of phoney nice guys.  it\\\'s not politically correct to be rude.  unless you\\\'re in a health food store, then it\\\'s a requirement to be solemn.',0,171,246,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(16966,2791,'2002-05-29 21:35:12',246,'erb','oh, my god, xep. you\\\'re right.  where is there an ocean town not teeming with phoney niceguys?  i was thinking more of l.a. when i wrote that to peter, but yes.  college town new agers.  having grown up in humboldt, i had my fill of those dudes, and i honestly never want to see a v.w. bus, a tiedye or a phish sticker again.  well, i could go for a v.w. bus for some crosscountry roadtrippin if it didn\\\'t have any phish stickers.  ',2,438,0,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(16967,2791,'2002-05-29 22:31:08',266,'spleenless','I want a job in deep sea exploration and I want to be the first to observe the giant squid in it\\\'s natural habitat.  If there\\\'s something down there even cooler than Archituethus Dux (is that possible?), I\\\'d like to find that too.\r\n<br><br>\r\nIf that all falls through, I\\\'d like to have a really good sandwich.',0,321,0,0,'66.92.184.86','en'),(16968,782,'2002-05-29 22:44:37',17,'yoko','mew. mims not fat, mims big boned. mew.',2,39,0,0,'205.179.19.111','en'),(16969,2791,'2002-05-29 22:45:54',66,'Peter','<tt>my great grandfather was a sponge fisherman; im not sure it was as high-adventure a job as undersea exploring, but he had a mint-green coloured boat.</tt>',1,158,266,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(16970,2791,'2002-05-29 22:47:23',196,'jasonp','I lived in or visited quite a few ocean towns (in fact I can see a sliver of the ocean from my window right now in Ocean Beach, San Diego).<BR>\nLA is definitely the epicenter of phony. The flaky/trendy/vapid factor is just too high.<BR>\nDown here in San Diego it has some of that quality, but much less. Noone comes here to be a \\\"star\\\" or be seen with one, but the constant sunshine tends to dull people minds nevertheless.<BR>\nThere are communities up the coast from here that have their own unique personality: Solana Beach is the upscale new-ager little nook by the sea. Encinitas is similar but it also a strong surfer element too. <BR>\nI used to live in San Clemente. There in the high school instead of the popular kids being football players they were all surfers. The surfers aren\\\'t all phoney, or nice, either. Imagine just as much testosterone as a jock but channeled into hanging out at the beach, smoking weed and banging all the hot chicks that flocked to them. There is a definitely an element of relaxation which isn\\\'t forced or a put-on though which comes from interacting with nature so often. That\\\'s what the surf lifestyle is all about. If you get up at sunrise and float around in the ocean for a few hours it will change your disposition.<BR>\nI don\\\'t know about Santa Cruz. My dad and brother lived there and I\\\'ve visited. I totally get the phish/VW van/earnest vegetarian vibe entirely. Actually OB has similar atmosphere but less PC and more harleys and locally owned businesses. There was a whole movement here here to stop starbucks from coming in. That would be unheard of anywhere else in SD (or LA)<BR>\nActually I think the phoniness factor mostly comes from people who aren\\\'t native. People looking to \\\"fit in\\\", but there\\\'s not enough of any kind of substantial tradition to really follow or adapt to.',0,1863,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(16971,2791,'2002-05-29 22:51:43',17,'yoko','<P>m3w.  all mew need is a lap to sit on.  m3w.  perhaps some balls to warm. m3w.</P>\r\n<P><img src=\\\"http://www.saturn5.com/steve/photos/lfdir/series/cats/display/yoko.jpg\\\"></P>',0,182,246,0,'205.179.19.111','en'),(16972,2791,'2002-05-29 23:17:13',176,'Scapegoat','I\\\'d like to be able to get into & support myself as a carpenter. Say good bye to offices for good. A motorbike would be nice for transportation - and an environmentally sustainable, stand alone, home would be nice.  ',0,219,0,0,'64.229.168.61','en'),(16973,2791,'2002-05-29 23:33:47',246,'erb','you are so cute! so cuddly and warm and alive  i forgot to add to my list, a cat! <b> i want a cat! </b> why are all my roommates allergic to the point of death?  <br>\r\n<br>\r\nsome balls to warm wouldn\\\'t be half bad, either.',2,226,17,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(16974,2793,'2002-05-30 07:19:49',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\nmake a rob puppet and slow dance with it to a classic rock ballad. being drunk helps with this. A LOT!\r\n</p>\r\n',1,115,0,0,'211.32.132.212','en'),(16975,2792,'2002-05-30 07:55:28',174,'Mad Moll','This is really nice. I like it better than the first one. It\\\'s more vague, mysterious.',6,89,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(16976,2793,'2002-05-30 12:05:45',295,'karenlee','kelly: If you think this guy knows how you feel, then you must also consider the strong possibility that he is a complete ass. But you won\\\'t know unless you talk to him!!! \r\n',1,177,0,0,'203.221.82.247','en'),(16977,2793,'2002-05-30 12:24:36',295,'karenlee','...or maybe he is not a complete ass, maybe he is just shy about the situation.\r\n',1,81,0,0,'203.221.82.247','en'),(16978,2793,'2002-05-30 13:05:25',307,'hool','<p>skinny != anorexic.  and if you don\\\'t tell him soberly and completely how you feel, then you deserve the frustration.</p>',0,127,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(16979,2792,'2002-05-30 14:31:10',74,'jo','dude. i am so in appreciate awe to you guys from bringing something pretty and thoughtful and strange into my morning.',1,118,0,0,'172.157.186.179','en'),(16980,2791,'2002-05-30 14:40:43',74,'jo','<p>hi, so. i\\\'ve been thinking about this a lot lately because i\\\'m unemployed, thus i have nothing better to do with my time than walk to coffeehouses and read 900+ page novels and do yoga and wonder what i\\\'m going to do with my life. here are some thoughts:</p>\r\n<p>+ work in a nonprofit childcare facility.<BR>\r\n+ go to grad school for english.<BR>\r\n+ stay in austin for another year or so.<BR>\r\n+ then maybe chapel hill? portland? olympia? seattle? most likely chapel hill.<BR>\r\n+ have kids. possibly enlist patrick on this one.</p>\r\n<p>that\\\'s just the next five years. i have trouble visualizing past that when i really have no idea what might happen to me tomorrow, so.</p>',1,689,0,0,'172.157.186.179','en'),(16981,2785,'2002-05-30 15:55:41',323,'outlandishjosh','treetop:\r\n\r\nThanks for pointing out my shortcomings. I\\\'m making an emotional appeal partially based on nostalgia and the things that make me feel good. So wood-burning stoves (or pellet stoves to be more efficient and precise) are a part of the equation. It\\\'s not an artice, but actually text meant for performance. Theater, if you will. I also live in New York City and don\\\'t have many friends who know much about the hippy way, so it\\\'s meant to incite the interest of the uninitiated. Thanks for the tips though. Always good to hear from an elder.\r\n\r\ntyler:\r\n\r\nI actually have performed this once. It went off pretty well. Appreciate the comments though. Funny how things kind of loose something in translation form performed text to written text. When dealing with written material you seem to either be decidedly serious or decidely sarcastic/comic, whereas with my performance I was able to convey the sense that I\\\'m not really serious about the plan (or lack thereof) proposed, but that I am serious about the feelings I\\\'m attempting to communicate.\r\n\r\nAll that being said, I\\\'m actually travelling in the Netherlands at the moment, and it seems a lot like what I imagine my world would be like. Everyone is riding bikes and even though it\\\'s a small densely populated country they grow all the food they need (actually exporting agricultural products). Of course there\\\'s all the nice progressive laws, but there\\\'s also the fact that people don\\\'t watch a lot of TV and they are comfortable striking up a conversation with (egad!) strangers on the train. It just seems to make more sense.\r\n\r\nAnyway, I have more text to post when I get back to the US, some performative and some more logical/rhetorical/expository. I\\\'m glad I got so many comments! ',0,1787,0,0,'137.120.142.23','en'),(16982,2785,'2002-05-30 15:57:39',323,'outlandishjosh','<p>treetop:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Thanks for pointing out my shortcomings. I\\\'m making an emotional appeal partially based on nostalgia and the things that make me feel good. So wood-burning stoves (or pellet stoves to be more efficient and precise) are a part of the equation. It\\\'s not an artice, but actually text meant for performance. Theater, if you will. I also live in New York City and don\\\'t have many friends who know much about the hippy way, so it\\\'s meant to incite the interest of the uninitiated. Thanks for the tips though. Always good to hear from an elder.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>tyler:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I actually have performed this once. It went off pretty well. Appreciate the comments though. Funny how things kind of loose something in translation form performed text to written text. When dealing with written material you seem to either be decidedly serious or decidely sarcastic/comic, whereas with my performance I was able to convey the sense that I\\\'m not really serious about the plan (or lack thereof) proposed, but that I am serious about the feelings I\\\'m attempting to communicate.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>All that being said, I\\\'m actually travelling in the Netherlands at the moment, and it seems a lot like what I imagine my world would be like. Everyone is riding bikes and even though it\\\'s a small densely populated country they grow all the food they need (actually exporting agricultural products). Of course there\\\'s all the nice progressive laws, but there\\\'s also the fact that people don\\\'t watch a lot of TV and they are comfortable striking up a conversation with (egad!) strangers on the train. It just seems to make more sense.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Anyway, I have more text to post when I get back to the US, some performative and some more logical/rhetorical/expository. I\\\'m glad I got so many comments! Makes me feel kinds special (awwwwww...). Seriously, no sarcasm intended. Thanks for reading and caring enough to ping me back.</p>',0,1956,0,0,'137.120.142.23','en'),(16983,2785,'2002-05-30 16:02:27',66,'Peter','<br><tt>thats cause there are no hippies in nyc...</tt>',1,55,323,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16984,2793,'2002-05-30 16:41:34',82,'simon','what\\\'s a NOC?',0,16,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16985,2791,'2002-05-30 16:43:53',246,'erb','i\\\'m very inspired by all of you.  i like the carpenter idea.  why not?  seattle.  maybe seattle is the place.  i hear they hate californians, though.  perhaps i will masquerade as an oregonian.  something else occurred to me last night and now i can\\\'t remember.  well, the obvious things that i didn\\\'t put on my list.  health, and connections with people.  big strong vibrating connections.<br>\r\n<br>i notice for a saggitarius i have remarkably little inclination to travel.  must be my moon in cancer, makes me want to stay at home and be cozy.  however, i will add travel to my list.  cozy travel, maybe to vermont or barcelona.  <br>\r\n<br>and a boat! i want a small graceful sailboat, which i can live on if i feel like being alone.  ',2,746,0,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(16986,2793,'2002-05-30 17:14:52',89,'sawks','<a href=\\\"http://puck.nether.net/netops/\\\">it\\\'s kind of like an air traffic control tower, for nerds</a>',3,111,82,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(16987,2791,'2002-05-30 17:20:44',82,'simon','at the moment, i want to go back to bed where someone i like being around is still asleep. yawn. but also i want to take pictures all day instead of working on this magazine. and if i could have any food right now, i\\\'d be at this fish taco stand in this town just past ensenada or at kate\\\'s in new york.',0,309,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16988,2791,'2002-05-30 17:34:13',66,'Peter','<br><tt>mmm, kates joint. they sling a mean fake-steak; i might have to go by there for supper tonight now.</tt>',1,112,82,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(16989,2793,'2002-05-30 17:38:43',297,'kelly','the NOC is mtv networks, it stands for network operations center. and i dont think he\\\'s a complete ass, because we\\\'ve hung out with the same group of good friends for well over a year, and i do know him pretty well. i think he is just shy, because before he knew this he was a normal friend to me, now he\\\'s got this act on, or is constantly being a pain in the ass in a teasing way, and i dont know if he\\\'s just shy or just doesn\\\'t know how to handle it. when i get up the courage to flirt, he flirts back tho.  ',0,527,0,0,'166.77.209.191','en'),(16990,2793,'2002-05-30 17:38:50',246,'erb','girl, i am feeling your pain.  awkward work relations are the worst.  i\\\'m glad no hot guys work at my office. <br>\r\n<br>\r\ni always think infatuation is a facsinating process.  i\\\'m kind of getting over a crush right now.  withdrawing the shimmering mirage of projection to see the *real person*! underneath it.  the cool thing about the infatuation is that whatever you are seeing in the crush is something you created for yourself.  your very own hero.  i like it. sometimes, though, as you have noticed, it can really suck.  remember that what you see in this guy is something precious of your own essence.  yum essence.  ',2,629,0,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(16991,2792,'2002-05-30 18:10:49',123,'dcc','you two have such magic with words and images. thank you, once again.',1,69,0,0,'66.207.199.132','en'),(16992,2793,'2002-05-30 18:12:09',246,'erb','also, kelly, it kinda sounds like he likes you.  i think you should lay a big kiss on him in the breakroom and see what happens.',1,128,297,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(16993,2791,'2002-05-30 19:41:43',82,'simon','dude, i had TWO unturkey clubs last week. that shit is the shit, yo.',1,68,66,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16994,2793,'2002-05-30 19:43:19',82,'simon','or cop a fast feel ',0,19,297,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(16995,2787,'2002-05-30 20:16:59',120,'joyrider','having lived in reno, i know that gambling people can get a little, um, obsessive and weird.  however, this person sort of takes it to a whole new level, you know?  jeez.',0,170,0,0,'12.224.184.3','en'),(16996,2793,'2002-05-30 20:19:08',195,'matt','si\\\'s right - pinch you a little ass',1,38,0,0,'150.176.174.11','en'),(16997,2791,'2002-05-30 20:23:32',74,'jo','i\\\'m sun in cancer, moon in sagittarius! so i relate! i wanna stay home! i wanna travel! i love it here! i can\\\'t wait to leave! tug tug tug tug!',1,149,246,0,'172.169.181.41','en'),(16998,2792,'2002-05-30 22:02:29',83,'adamajah','nice illustrations :)',2,21,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(16999,2795,'2002-05-30 22:13:15',82,'simon','<p>\r\ntwo tangentially related items:<br>\r\n<br>\r\n1. i\\\'ve always wanted to live on the corner of pink and pearl streets in san francisco.\r\n<br>\r\n2. someone once left me a torn photograph of a lovely flapper girl with the words \\\"yours, lu\\\" on the back in perfect penmanship.\r\n</p>',1,286,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17000,2795,'2002-05-30 22:20:52',195,'matt','this is strange because i\\\'ve been thinking about this subject all day - the interconnectedness of things, how everything is part of the greater whole, the universe indivisable',1,178,0,0,'209.208.109.70','en'),(17001,938,'2002-05-30 22:33:54',187,'kandrer','<u>to hedwig:</u> my god, you\\\'re so...heu... catholic.<br>\r\nbut i forgive you your lack of many things.<br>\r\nnothing quite like unsolicited forgiveness from a stranger...',4,173,0,0,'208.223.155.158','en'),(17002,2791,'2002-05-30 23:01:03',196,'jasonp','As far as <I>wanting</I> goes I feel kind of disconnected and suspicious of it.<BR>\nI tend to immediately see how \\\"realistic\\\" anything is before taking it up as a real desire. And I\\\'m not that materialistic to begin with, so I\\\'ve already become fairly content. I\\\'ve turned into something of a nihilistic hermit.<BR>\nThere are dozens of daydream type desires I can think up, but I see them as that so I don\\\'t take them seriously.<BR>\nIf I were to be preachy I could say \\\"I am wanting to not want.\\\" Take that one level further and just say \\\"I am\\\". Then finally, \\\"am\\\".<BR>\nThat\\\'s a mental and theoretical understanding of buddhism, but it\\\'s not where I actually am or even aspire to be.<BR>\nActually, I do have desires but there are conflicted between other desires and obligations and perceived limitations that cancel each other out. It\\\'s a long story.\n',0,897,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(17003,2791,'2002-05-30 23:02:42',196,'jasonp','Hey, you have the same sun/moon combo as Jimi Hendrix!',0,54,246,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(17004,2793,'2002-05-30 23:21:51',196,'jasonp','Hmm...  here\\\'s my take.<BR>\nEither he is playing with your mind, not that interested, or is interested. Consider the conclusion of each situation: being played by a total ass, falling for someone who isn\\\'t into you, or getting involved in an office romance.<BR>\nNone of those sound very good. The only reason my assessment is so negative is because if it were the beginning of something good or real there wouldn\\\'t be this much confusion and obsession already. You\\\'d be much more grounded and sure about what was going on. Again, just IMHO.<BR>\nIn any case I\\\'d say play it safe since this could end up seriously screwing with your emotions and livelyhood, which would be no fun for someone trying to survive in NYC on their own.<BR><BR>\nBTW, working at MTV seems both very interesting, and horribly polluted by drama and politics.',0,845,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(17005,2795,'2002-05-30 23:47:37',196,'jasonp','I think this is the next wave of religious and spiritual belief. The common acknoledgement that whatever images, icons, beliefs, stories, etc. each religion has, there\\\'s an acknowledgement that they shouldn\\\'t be taken to literaly and an acknowledgement that many may point to the same kind of underlying truth.<BR>\nIt\\\'s these \\\"new-age\\\" (for lack of a better term) groups that both borrow and adapt from traditional beliefs, while also establishing new ideas. It\\\'s hard to say how much of this new stuff is bogus or real. I\\\'d guess 80%/20% mostly bogus, but that still leaves something that\\\'s valid.\n<BR><BR>\nI just thought of a great theological research idea. Research as many new-age/psychic groups and communities as possible. Then gather and compile all of the beliefs of all and try to come up with a master report of what they commonly agree on and how they differ. Then compare this with the more traditional religions for the original sources, similarities and differences, etc.<BR>\nI find this interesting since there seems to be a common groundwork but I\\\'ve never seen it clearly laid out. For ex., \\\"crossing over\\\" when you die in some kind of limbo, ghosts, purgatory, premonitions, revelations, etc. Also astrology, auras, chakras, prayer, magic, etc.<BR><BR>\nBTW, heh.. I like the term \\\"blocking one\\\'s head from the angels\\\" as a term for getting drunk. \\\"Hey dude, let\\\'s get some beer and block our heads from the angels!\\\"\n\n',0,1487,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(17006,2795,'2002-05-31 00:40:45',295,'karenlee','I love the old-photo-thing, especially ones with scenes that Ican picture myself in. In a buckled, twisty, roundy-bouty way, it seems that everything is connected.',1,163,0,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(17007,2796,'2002-05-31 03:15:41',196,'jasonp','This was a nice little snippet. <BR>\nHave you seen the movie <I>Y Tu Mama Tambien</I>? I saw it a few days ago. Pretty good.<BR>\nThe Mexican setting here reminded of it, even though it\\\'s not similar in any other way.<BR>\nThis whole thing seems vaguely resembles a fantasy of mine where I\\\'m a middle-aged guy living in a boat or near the sea somewhere in a foreign tropical setting. Yeah that\\\'s kind of Hemmingway-esque I guess.',0,436,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(17008,2795,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i found a website a while ago which was old postcards that someone had found or bought.  they\\\'d scanned them and painstakingly typed out each message for the eyes of anonymous internet viewers.  i wish i knew what to search for to link it here.',6,247,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17009,2793,'2002-05-31 05:10:14',297,'kelly','here\\\'s an update-we made out tonight behind a semi truck and in his car and talked for about an hour. ',0,105,0,0,'24.185.90.139','en'),(17010,2795,'2002-05-31 05:30:35',295,'karenlee','Here\\\'s one, but there\\\'s not  many on it..... <a href=\\\"http://www.geocities.com/iamamysterywriter/\\\">old postcards</a> (hope I got the link right).',1,157,62,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(17011,2793,'2002-05-31 05:32:22',295,'karenlee','YAY !!!!',2,8,297,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(17012,2793,'2002-05-31 05:55:10',295,'karenlee','(I think).',3,10,297,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(17013,2793,'2002-05-31 12:04:10',66,'Peter','<br><tt>making out behind semi trucks #1.</tt> ',1,47,297,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17014,2257,'2002-05-31 12:09:18',66,'Peter','<br><tt>almost 5 months later, and my penny stacks have yet to be stolen. this makes me trust humanity a litte more, if only by 20 cents.</tt>',1,142,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17015,2793,'2002-05-31 15:01:52',74,'jo','i might need to shorten that to: making out #1.',0,47,0,0,'172.158.53.206','en'),(17016,2793,'2002-05-31 15:42:28',66,'Peter','<br><tt>good point. making out <i>is</i> the best. but semi-trucks pretty much make everything that much better.</tt>',1,117,74,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17017,2792,'2002-05-31 17:17:50',17,'austin','what is it saying? talking slugs!',4,33,0,0,'205.179.19.111','en'),(17018,2792,'2002-05-31 17:24:29',93,'chiamattt','saso drunkl.. dont get it atsall. fuck!',6,39,0,0,'211.32.132.205','en'),(17019,2792,'2002-05-31 17:25:22',93,'chiamattt','DAEATH CFRFAEEW POSSSSE AUNITED! BOMEGA, VAN, KLOOREA!!!!!!!!!',6,62,0,0,'211.32.132.205','en'),(17020,2796,'2002-05-31 17:45:30',17,'austin','<P>i thought this was going to be about colonias, the border towns outside any city grid or utilities:  outhouses,  streetside sewage, open style.  houses constructed of cardboard boxes flattened out, scrap plywood, layered over a frame of wooden pallets, cinderblocks.  dogs running around, gaurding their yards.</P>\r\n\r\n<P><a href=\\\"http://www.sos.state.tx.us/border/colonias/faqs.shtml\\\">faq</a></P>',3,405,0,0,'205.179.19.111','en'),(17021,2795,'2002-05-31 18:13:35',65,'le','this is a great swinney. i like your writing so much egg. i like \\\"i blocked my head from the angels\\\"',0,106,0,0,'208.239.147.248','en'),(17022,2793,'2002-05-31 18:28:45',297,'kelly','it was actually funny because he kept saying it was really weird that we were behind a semi. i also wanted to point out my reason for writing this making me sound very obsessive and issue-ridden was on purpose- i wanted to poke fun at myself and my silly notions. \r\n',0,266,0,0,'24.185.90.139','en'),(17023,2785,'2002-05-31 20:08:00',130,'treetop','<p>Sorry to use the word <i>idiot</i> in my comment.  That word is a little harsh.  Old hippie views vs. new hippie views?  Some labels or genralizations can be taken out of context.  Maybe an article about a yuppie\\\'s SUV that never leaves the city would be a good counter piece?  Nah, thats ok.  Here is an article about how Iceland is trying to move forward by switching from oil to <a href=\\\"http://www.siliconvalley.com/mld/siliconvalley/news/3361602.htm\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">hydrogen</a>.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So what happened to all of the <a href=\\\"http://www.hippy.com/hippyway.htm\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">hippies</a> in NY?</p>',1,639,323,0,'172.167.62.13','en'),(17024,2763,'2002-05-31 22:10:18',187,'kandrer','i forgot to say how i hate it when people pick a word in one\\\'s post and start an irrelevant comment about it.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i>well, since you mentionned \\\'sex\\\', man, i\\\'ve got this great thing to say: blah blah...</i>\r\n</p>',4,230,0,0,'208.223.155.158','en'),(17025,2793,'2002-06-01 00:18:22',124,'gus','making out is #1, but semi\\\'s and frieght elevators, and other large equipment is also #1 ',0,92,0,0,'64.163.214.225','en'),(17026,2795,'2002-06-01 00:32:54',124,'gus','eggs and angels and bourbon',0,27,0,0,'64.163.214.225','en'),(17027,2796,'2002-06-01 02:37:02',195,'matt','<BR><BR>there seems to be something missing in this article. is it boring? or, should i say, not <I>compelling</I>? does it need a little more juice, or is it just fine?<BR><BR>i was consciously trying to imitate hemingway, to see if i could take his style and do my own thing with it.',1,285,0,0,'209.208.110.16','en'),(17028,2796,'2002-06-01 03:57:29',196,'jasonp','The line about driving drunk was amusing. The descriptions of the location and characters seemed like a good start for something, but nothing really happens. There wasn\\\'t much of any point or conclusion.<BR>\nWere you going to continue or expand on what\\\'s here already? Or was it like a one-off thing?<BR><BR>\nI\\\'ve done a couple of short thingies: \n<A HREF=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2638.txt\\\">\ntide</A>\n<A HREF=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2636.txt\\\">\nfluffy</A>\n<BR> They have 0 comments.\nDoes anyone have any comments for either one? I\\\'d be curious to know what anyone thought of them, what they were trying to say if anything, or any kind of feelings or ideas evoked, etc.\n<BR>\n',0,748,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(17029,2793,'2002-06-01 12:31:50',180,'auntie','i am reminded of brenda in six feet under with all this making out behind trucks and freight elevator stuff.  i prefer to make out in places that are not smelling of grease and oil. but then again, so long as you get some touch, who cares?',1,239,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(17030,2793,'2002-06-01 16:55:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt>the freight elevator is a nice idea; my favourite places so far have been a phone booth, in the back of taxis, and on the dancefloor.</tt>',1,146,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17031,2800,'2002-06-01 18:16:21',87,'bmg','<p>for a long time I collected barbie dolls, and barbie things... one year I had a barbie style birthday party... that era is over now, but I still have fond memories of lifting barbie\\\'s dress to check whats under there...',3,225,0,0,'17.104.195.95','en'),(17032,2800,'2002-06-01 20:34:46',59,'s5','hm. i want to go to this.',4,25,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17033,2793,'2002-06-01 20:38:18',174,'Mad Moll','ah in country we all make kissing in back of whale boat next to blubber good cushon yes? ha ha ha. ',6,99,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(17034,2798,'2002-06-01 22:38:08',67,'trouble','a while back i started to play with my doses as well.  i was saving the good stuff for weekends out and the not so good stuff for long weekdays spent at work.  i still don\\\'t follow the directions most times but i\\\'m slowly learning that there is indeed a need for them.  good piece, i could totally relate.',1,311,0,0,'68.47.202.98','en'),(17035,2800,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>response:</p>\r\n\r\n<li>it makes me really uncomfortable  to hear people claim near-poverty in one breath and then declare they\\\'re going to do x  insanely expensive thing anyway in the second;</li>\r\n\r\n<li>first it was stated that men will attend, but then we are told that the event is about women (or do you mean the little plastic women?);</li>\r\n\r\n<li>i have been \\\"people collected\\\" before.  it is infintely upsetting when you thought you were friends with someone, only to find you have been \\\"collected\\\" and are ultimately in line to be popped off the end of the stack when the next vaguely interesting person comes along.  this is probably not the kind of people collecting auntie is talking about tho, right auntie?</li>',6,740,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17036,2800,'2002-06-02 03:25:42',240,'xep','i find the above comment really shocking for a couple reasons.  just because something is about women doesn\\\'t mean men can\\\'t attend.  what is wrong with spending your last dollars on something that makes you very happy?  having been collected as you spoke of, in the past , myself, i do not think that is what auntie spoke of at all and i wonder why that was the assumption that was made.  the collecting she speaks of seems to be a collection of like minds that enjoy eachothers company when it is time to be near one another.  the one you speak of seems to be a collection of people whose emotions the collector controls.  the closeness of group of people that see eachother once a year is unlike other groups of people.  there is a certain openness about it because they know they can leave when the week is over and return to their daily grind.  the stories and experiences they shared with the people of the convention will stay there, tucked away like seasonal decorations, until next year when they are brought out again and admired anew.  \r\n<br>\r\nmostly i just don\\\'t understand the purely negative reaction to this seemingly innocuous post.',4,1157,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(17037,2790,'2002-06-02 03:36:37',240,'xep','i have an anatomy test on tuesday.  i don\\\'t know the material at all.  today i made a list of all the things i can do to proscranstinate studying.  on the top of the list was cleaning the kitchen but when i got home from dropping eric off at the airport doug, the flat mate who also has said anatomy test on tuesday, had already cleaned it.  we\\\'re stuck in a vicious cycle of dueling procrastination.  doomed.',0,415,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(17038,2800,'2002-06-02 05:51:57',295,'karenlee','I was never allowed to have a Barbie when I was a girl, but later on I developed an attraction to Barbies. For my 31st birthday, some friends gave me a mermaid Barbie and a Cool Shavin\\\' Ken (ken\\\'s beard could be \\\"shaved\\\" with warm water). Every now and then I walk down the toystore aisles, admiring all the Barbies with their beautiful fake mindless smiles and tiny little feet and moulded-in underpants. \r\n',3,420,0,0,'203.221.9.114','en'),(17039,2800,'2002-06-02 07:30:41',180,'auntie','1 - have you never purchased (or perhaps had someone purchase for you as a gift) - a plane ticket you could not really afford  in order to be with people who made you feel it was okay to be you?<br><br>\r\n\r\n2 - the event is about women, there are men who attend, most of them are artists or super collectors who \\\"do\\\" barbie as a profession, rather than as recreational therapy.<br><br>\r\n\r\n3- your assumption was wrong.  xep got it. perhaps reading <a href=\\\"http://www.dollzine.com/barbiecon2002.asp\\\">the entire piece, edited for the public</a> will bring more understanding.<br><br>\r\n\r\n',3,597,62,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(17040,2800,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>hi</p>\r\n<p>i didn\\\'t actually assume that the collecting i spoke of was what auntie spoke of.  you could just read the last sentance of my post if you want verification.</p>\r\n<p>oh, i\\\'m sorry, aren\\\'t i allowed to feel a certain way about a situation i see a lot?  my mistake.  um.</p>\r\n<p>i fail to see how the event is \\\"about women\\\", bar the little plastic ones, when both men and women play with, collect, idolise and fetishise barbie.  if it was, say, an IUD conference, i wouldn\\\'t have commented.</p>\r\n',6,526,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17041,2798,'2002-06-02 16:55:06',75,'tyler','cut the pills in half?',1,22,0,0,'165.247.165.85','en'),(17042,2800,'2002-06-02 18:31:02',180,'auntie','one of the more interesting facts about the barbie doll convention is this... it is probably the single largest non-political gathering of women in the world.<br><br>\r\n\r\nevery other large gathering of women i know about has a political agenda of some sort, whether it is sexual orientation, political, or religious.<br><br>',4,323,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(17043,2799,'2002-06-02 21:21:10',180,'auntie','if you want to change how the world treats children, the best place to start is to campaign for a better education for the children who will grow up to be parents of tomorrow.  go into teaching as a career.  make a difference one child at a time.  my daughter is a teacher, for many of the children in her classes, she is the ONLY adult those children will see on a daily basis who will listen to what they have to say.',3,419,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(17044,2800,'2002-06-02 21:44:49',240,'xep','you are allowed to feel however you want about anything you want.  \r\n<br>\r\nand yes, you did say that\\\'s not the sort of people collecting that was mentioned but i fail to see the relevence of bringing up the other sort if it had no connection to what you were saying.  maybe it was just the phrase \\\"people collecting\\\" that you object to?\r\n',0,347,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(17045,2798,'2002-06-02 21:45:24',278,'egenus','I don\\\'t trust pharamaceutical drugs. Smoke weed and relax.',3,61,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(17046,2792,'2002-06-02 22:59:40',307,'hool','chia needs an internet breathalyzer.',1,36,0,0,'24.114.101.198','en'),(17047,2800,'2002-06-02 23:51:38',180,'auntie','perhaps \\\"people collecting\\\" is a worrisome phrase, a poor choice for description.  set alone by itself, it does bring to mind a trophy wall with many personages of all sizes hanging via a loop on their shirts, kicking and waving arms.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthen again, that sort of people collecting might be fun, too, if it weren\\\'t so gosh darn illegal.<br><br>',0,360,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(17048,2800,'2002-09-05 18:40:19',18,'ix','i hang people on meat hooks',0,27,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(17049,2800,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','oh really?  you\\\'re into the piercing community, then ix?',6,59,16,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17050,2802,'2002-06-03 04:50:57',180,'auntie','i met a woman in the drugstore the other day.  she was looking at canes.  did you know they come in all colors and styles these days?  we were both waiting on prescriptions, and she had a friendly face, so we got to talking.  she is about my age - late forties.  she was job hunting.  she is raising her grandchildren, so she needs the work.  she told me she was thinking about buying a cane to use on a job interview the following week.  she thought that being disabled would give her a better chance in the selection process.  she says she doesn\\\'t need a cane, but she does have arthritis, so it wouldn\\\'t really be lying...<br><br>\r\n\r\ni dunno.  i thought we were done with affirmative action programs and quota hiring.  i thought that we were hiring based on merit these days.  i guess i was wrong, huh?',2,811,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(17051,2802,'2002-06-03 05:20:16',176,'Scapegoat','Actually, In the case of the US I think it is determined by state - isn\\\'t it? Up here however (Canada) we still do this sort of thing. I have heard that California is actually considering legislation that would take certain identity criteria (the main one being race) off government forms. The reason being cited is that the increasing numbers of people with mixed lineage are making that sort of information useless.<br><br>\r\nI hope the women from the drugstore gets the job with or without the cane. ',0,505,180,0,'64.229.166.236','en'),(17052,2803,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','stoo, this is really beautiful and depressing.',6,46,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17053,2799,'2002-06-03 16:30:58',298,'lurkinginsagas','i have considered teaching.  i\\\'m not sure if i\\\'m cut out for it.  i suppose it\\\'s an option i\\\'ll keep open.',2,118,0,0,'207.218.215.74','en'),(17054,2800,'2002-06-03 17:06:10',186,'ook','<p>i think that the relationships developed with people that you only see for brief events are interesting. sometimes the instant \\\"we\\\'re in a club together!\\\" sensation makes me feel indulgent and lazy, but there are ways to make it more creative. at the very least i like having to invent a new kind of friendship - we\\\'re not making everyday friends, we\\\'re making barbie convention week friends.\r\n\r\n<p>this post reminded me of things like band field trips, or drama festival week. maybe events are never really about the scheduled activities.\r\n\r\n<p>also, please don\\\'t forget about the girl guides and girl scouts. largest women\\\'s organization in the world, not about politics, not about religion, and as an added bonus, not about consumerism. instead, all about skillz. (now let\\\'s play spot the 13 year veteran girl guide)',0,846,0,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(17055,2800,'2002-09-05 18:40:19',18,'ix','meat hooks',0,10,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(17056,2803,'2002-06-03 17:49:06',75,'tyler','<pre>\r\nlife could be a dream/\r\nif i could take you up to paradise, up above/\r\nif you would tell me i\\\'m the only one that you love/\r\nlife could be a dream, sweetheart/\r\nsh-boom hello hello again/\r\nsh-boom here\\\'s hoping we meet again\r\n\r\noh, life could be a dream/\r\nif only all my precious plans would come true/\r\nif you would let me spend my whole life lovin\\\' you/\r\nlife could be a dream/\r\nsweetheaaaaaart.\r\n\r\n&quot;Shboom&quot;---download it\r\n</pre>\r\n',1,459,0,0,'165.247.164.122','en'),(17057,2802,'2002-06-03 18:18:09',168,'laney','this is a very tricky topic, but my feeling is until our country (the US) fixes itself so that everyone really does have an equal chance, these programs are valid and important. in some parts of the country, a black man won\\\'t get an apartment or a job just because he is black; no matter how qualified. sometimes it\\\'s hard to see these programs working because inidividual cases (as the one described in this post) can be really fucked up. but they are there for good reason. at least i think so. ',0,503,0,0,'198.81.16.58','en'),(17058,2800,'2002-06-03 18:20:37',168,'laney','i am highly troubled by barbie dolls. just saying. but i thought this was a nicely written post, auntie.',0,104,0,0,'198.81.16.58','en'),(17059,2791,'2002-06-03 19:06:38',246,'erb','maybe i could be in your entourage.  sounds like a job that would satisfy my need for glamour, physical motion, creativity, and expressive licence.  i\\\'ll go see if i can find some eunuchs on craigslist.',1,205,174,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(17060,2802,'2002-06-03 19:07:02',174,'Mad Moll','<p>This certainly sucks, but it\\\'s the necessary price we pay for prejudice and descrimination. If society isn\\\'t mature enough to give people who are different (but just as capable) a fair chance, then it must be forced to.</p>\r\n<p>It\\\'s not always pretty, and it\\\'s not always fair -- and it\\\'s not supposed to be. But don\\\'t blame the programs. Blame the people who make them necessary.</p>\r\n',6,407,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(17061,2800,'2002-06-03 19:11:44',17,'Elvis Costello','<PRE>\r\n...\r\nand it\\\'s the damage that we do\r\nyou\\\'ll never know\r\nit\\\'s the words that we don\\\'t say\r\nthat scare me so\r\nthere are so many people to see\r\nso many people you can check upon\r\n<B>and add to your collection</B>\r\nbut they keep you hanging on\r\nuntil you\\\'re well hung\r\nyour mouth is made up\r\nbut your mind is undone\r\n...\r\n</PRE>',4,335,0,0,'205.179.19.111','en'),(17062,2799,'2002-06-03 19:20:29',174,'Mad Moll','<p>Maybe (hopefully) it is not true for you, but I find that the people who want to save the world the most also tend to be the ones least capable of saving themselves and being good to the people immediatly around them. I\\\'ve met too many \\\'revolutionaries\\\' and humanitarians who don\\\'t know what to do with themselves and treat the people they\\\'re close to like shit. You don\\\'t owe the world anything. Take care of yourself and be good to your own. The others will figure themselves out.</p>\r\n<p>Good luck anyway, though.</p>\r\n\r\n',6,545,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(17063,2804,'2002-06-03 21:34:21',86,'egg','superheroes live with the same paradox. and you can too.',0,56,0,0,'216.175.88.18','en'),(17064,2805,'2002-06-03 21:41:30',186,'icky','if it isn\\\'t things or stuff getting you down, maybe it is you. left with nothing else to focus on, maybe the state that you yourself are in needs to be examined. just sayin. best of luck.',0,190,0,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(17065,2804,'2002-06-03 21:50:12',82,'super simon','i know i do.',0,12,86,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17066,2802,'2002-06-03 23:54:09',129,'moebius','<br>whenever this topic comes up i\\\'m always reminded of my failed attempts to become a dj at my university radio station.<br><br>my university, like many liberal arts educational institutions at the time, was obsessed with political correctness to a fault. the new station manager was determined to have her staff represent the entire rainbow of marginalized peoples of the world, but in my opinion she went a bit overboard. we had djs with speech impedements stuttering their way through folk music programs. elderly black women running afternoon gospel shows. and so forth. it was kind of clear that WASP heterosexual males need not apply but i did my best.<br><br>i was told that if i did volunteer work around the station that would help, so i put in a month of collating papers, running copies, and taking out the garbage. i also networked with my dj friends and had them put in good words for me. then the interview came, and it went fine until they asked the question \\\"what are your feelings about affirmative action?\\\" my heart sunk. i did my best to be chipper about it but i lost the job anyway. i was so dispirited i gave up on the dream.<br><br>while this was going on a friend of mine interviewed with the station twice without luck. on his third try, he checked \\\"gay\\\" on the application. and <i>voila</i>, he was hired. i suppose they hadn\\\'t quite filled their quota of gay male radio personalities yet. if only i\\\'d been audacious enough to try that....',3,1487,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(17067,2802,'2002-06-04 00:03:13',129,'moebius','and hey, just to ward off potential flames: i\\\'m not exactly against the concept of affirmative action in general, though i do think it\\\'s a simplistic and dangerous solution in some circumstances.<br><br>i suppose i could rationalize my experience by saying that i just went through something that\\\'s a crappy fact of life for a lot of other people in the US. i just wish it hadn\\\'t been applied then, at that time. i woulda loved being a college radio dj. \\\'nuff said.',0,480,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(17068,2804,'2002-06-04 02:21:45',266,'spleenless','Traipsing and whatnot isn\\\'t really sustainable as a full time occupation.  I sort of tried for a while.  A whole bunch of my friends from back then tried longer and harder.  I\\\'ve never really seen it work.  The far more real reality has a nasty habit of forcing it\\\'s way into happy land and kicking your arse if you try to ignore it.\r\n<br><br>\r\nThat\\\'s the bad news.  The good news is that the real reality is really big.  If you don\\\'t like the part of it you\\\'re in right now, move on.  There\\\'s got to be something out there that\\\'s both sustainable and satisfing for you.\r\n<br><br>\r\nA lot of people will tell you to just find something you love and make that your life.  I won\\\'t really tell you that.  Instead, I\\\'d tell you to find something that you love and that other people are willing to pay you to do.  They don\\\'t have to be willing to pay you a lot but they have to be willing to pay enough to support yourself.  For instance, if getting loaded and dancing is what you love to do, you may have trouble finding someone to pay you to do that.  If you expect to be fed without providing any service to anyone but yourself, I\\\'d consider you a parasite.  Someone has to work to provide you with food and shelter.  If you\\\'re doing nothing to repay their effort, well that\\\'s just rude.',0,1326,0,0,'66.92.184.86','en'),(17069,2804,'2002-06-04 02:26:55',20,'jim','<p>\r\nis the problem that you\'re living a double life, or is it just that one of those lives has stopped bringing you joy?\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nyou can commit yourself to just one way of being, but I\'m not convinced that making that choice comes with any greater guarantee of happiness.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<i>\"Specialization is for insects.\"</i> - Robert Heinlein\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI\'m a firm believer in <b>diversified living</b>. I find I\'m happiest when I\'m living lots of lives, not just one or two.  I see no sense in forbidding myself any possible experience by defining my existence too narrowly.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nwhen one of those lives is broken in some way, it\'s easy to blame it on diversified living.  for me, that convenient scapegoat never stands up to scrutiny.  it always turns out that some specific aspect of myself needs a little (or a lot) of work.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nwould you be a happier journalist if you were writing about things that hit you closer to home than City Hall? I used to design interfaces for profit-obsessed corporations, and now I design interfaces for non-profit organizations.  that little adjustment to one aspect of myself changed the balance of the whole: the part of me that wants to leave the world a little better than I found it is   happier; the part of me that wants to sell all my stuff, live in a bus, and travel from party to party is nonplussed; the part of me that likes antique Mercedes-Benz roadsters, flying business class to Europe, and staying in resort hotels is cranky.  I\'ll make some more little adjustments.  I\'ll keep making little adjustments until I die.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nyou said:<br>\r\n<i>Why should i dress in sarongs and fishnets and dance like i am on the moon when, hours later, i have to give that up for the ugly reality that is on the other side of the door?</i>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nyou should because it feels good.  the thing you give it up for at the end of the weekend, that reality on the other side of the door, that can make you feel good too.  living is the challenge of figuring out how, and then helping other people figure out how.\r\n</p>',6,2118,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(17070,2802,'2002-06-04 02:32:29',59,'s5','lucky for you, you can still keep the dream alive at one of the many local college radio stations in the bay area that are open to the community: KALX, KZSU, or KUSF, for example. i\\\'m pretty sure accept white men. but you never really know. after all, this is california. tread lightly.',3,289,129,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17071,2805,'2002-06-04 02:35:20',168,'laney','i can relate with this feeling. i think a lot of people can. i don\\\'t know if this is the case for you, but for me sometimes i think it\\\'s the general weight of the world that does it. even if everything in your own life is going great, there is so much that is going to shit in the world that you have no control over, it\\\'s hard to ever be totally happy sometimes. i don\\\'t know, maybe it\\\'s even something that seaps into your subconscious, which is why you can never figure out why you\\\'re not happy.\r\n',0,518,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(17072,2804,'2002-06-04 03:31:04',168,'laney','i guess the problem is that the two lives are so incongruent the way they are now.\r\nitâ€™s not that being a journalist doesnâ€™t give me joy; it does, in fact, a lot. but for some reason I feel a bit icky doing both when they are so in conflict with each other. like writing stories about the dangers of teenage drug use and then taking those drugs myself. it seems dishonest and hypocritical.\r\nwhen i was writing this i was thinking, in order to make these two (or more) lives fit in better with each other, i would have to do something really radical and throw everything i have done so far completely off course, which is partly why itâ€™s so scary to me. but what you said about the adjusting really resonates. i have been suspecting for awhile that my calling may be more on the alternative-media tip. and in that, i know i will have to sacrifice the side of me that wants to spend $70 on a facial and stay in fancy hotel rooms. but i think that will be just fine, at least until i adjust again. thanks jim. this helps.',0,1018,20,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(17073,2804,'2002-06-04 06:25:58',74,'jo','i think i understand what you mean. you feel like work laney and party laney wouldn\\\'t think highly of one another should the two distinct halves meet at a cocktail party. you feel like you\\\'re constantly being disloyal to yourself, no matter which life you chose.<BR>\r\ngood luck reconciling this to your happiness. i tend to feel like jim, that all sides of ourselves are valuable and equally \\\"us.\\\" \r\n',1,412,168,0,'172.158.115.187','en'),(17074,2802,'2002-06-04 07:05:24',176,'Scapegoat','I\\\'m going to go out on a limb here which is probably implied in my post, but I think that quota programs reinforce racial identities and promote a passive racism.<br><br>\r\nI\\\'m curious who the <i>we</i> is that pays for the prejudice and discrimination. I also think it is important to distinguish these two concepts. Prejudice is an attitude - discrimination is action based. I\\\'ve already been labelled (erroneously i.m.o.)prejudice and now I\\\'m forced to discriminate as part of my job.<br><br>\r\nIMHO my situation makes a farce out of the ideal of equality. I can decide which is more perverse - a blantant expression against equality or a more subversive tactic, such as this.<br><br>\r\n ',0,700,0,0,'64.229.166.236','en'),(17075,2805,'2002-06-04 12:40:22',295,'karenlee','just be easy on yourself :)',1,27,0,0,'203.221.80.88','en'),(17076,2805,'2002-06-04 13:10:56',123,'dcc','interesting to think of ... and i suppose it could be a good explaination. I just find it so much easier to feel comfortable with the world around me when i can rationalize what i feel.',0,185,168,0,'66.207.199.132','en'),(17077,2802,'2002-06-04 14:55:59',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\nJust remember corey, you are still the guy hiring someone. just be glad its not the other way around. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas for affirmative action and quota programs, they have a good and bad side, and its often the \\\'white\\\' side that feels discrimated against. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe fact remains that for the most part western society as a entire being see\\\'s \\\'the other\\\' as a threat and for this reason see\\\'s these programs as a threat as well. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe fact that we have these programs indicates that there is a much larger problem on the other side of the fence, and while i cant and wont try to understand the problems seen by that side of the fence i understand that it must be hard sometimes. \r\n</p>',6,724,176,0,'61.37.53.55','en'),(17078,2799,'2002-06-04 15:13:19',298,'lurkinginsagas','\\\"cause we owe the world nothing / but we owe each other the world.\\\" - ani difranco\r\ni certainly wouldn\\\'t sabotage my own relationships in favor of some people i might not even meet.  i\\\'d just like to reach a little further is all.',2,242,174,0,'207.218.203.18','en'),(17079,2804,'2002-06-04 16:10:08',265,'chuckspeed','reconciliation...\r\n\r\nit\\\'s actually a growing-up thing.  the need to \\\'go wild\\\' subsides somewhat as you get older - just hang onto some of the wildness for balance.<br>\r\n\r\nin my case, motorcycles kept me balanced.  i\\\'d be corporate 80% of the time and caution-to-the-wind the other 20%.  the 20% kept the 80% focused, and the 80% made the 20% all the more enjoyable.<br>\r\n\r\nit\\\'s a yin-yang thing...you appreciate one beacuse of the other.  too much of either and you risk going postal.',0,499,168,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(17080,109,'2002-06-04 17:37:07',86,'egg','austin, you say that you hate everything pastel, but I would like to think that since then I have helped you to appreciate pink. ',0,129,0,0,'216.175.88.18','en'),(17081,2801,'2002-06-04 18:10:42',195,'matt','tyler, you\\\'re a poet and you know it:)',1,41,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(17082,109,'2002-06-04 18:19:23',82,'MAC make-up','thanks for re-thinking pink.',0,28,86,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17083,2796,'2002-06-04 20:33:19',195,'matt','on second thought, this is juuuuuuuust right.',1,45,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(17084,2804,'2002-06-04 20:41:51',190,'dahlia','laney, one of the tricks to making this work is living from within yourself. if you look from the outside and try to match up the labels on the tags that the various aspects of your life seem to wear, it isn\\\'t going to work. a fulfilling life isn\\\'t a garanimals outfit, it\\\'s a serious of carefully knit paradoxes.\r\n<p>look inside. know who you are and what you want and why you want to do all the things you do. the thing that unifies the journalist and the raver is *you*.  that cop isn\\\'t living your life. neither is your mom, or your boyfriend, or me.</p>\r\n<p>as jim said, when you find parts that aren\\\'t resonating properly, you shift things until those parts are gone, or until they fit right.</p>\r\n<p>it\\\'s worth the effort. this is your life. if that isn\\\'t worth effort to make all the things you love fit in, what is?</p>\r\n<p> good luck. be well.</p>',0,878,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(17085,2800,'2002-06-04 23:06:22',124,'gus','I know some people who hang eachother with meat hooks. and very large fish hooks. they are collectors of sorts I guess. ',0,120,0,0,'64.160.52.142','en'),(17086,2805,'2002-06-04 23:13:03',278,'egenus','A job and a girlfriend != life. There\\\'s plenty left to be concerned about. Maybe your discontentment comes from a desire to expand your horizons.',3,148,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(17087,2802,'2002-06-04 23:16:43',176,'Scapegoat','Being thankful for being employed doesn\\\'t have any bearing on the fact that this is still discrimination anyway you slice it.<br><br>\r\nIf you say it is justified in one circumstance seems to me your on a slippery slope opening the doors to other circumstance where the same can and will be said. I wouldn\\\'t take that chance.<br><br>\r\nOn the issue of the west fearing the <i>other</i>, my notion of the west isn\\\'t race based, it is based on values and includes anyone that shares those values, regardless of race.<br><br>\r\nMy biggest problem with all this is personal. I am being forced due to economic dependency and legally binding contract to act in ways that are in direct conflict with my beliefs. It would be a non-issue if I could walk away and leave it to other good intentioned people to discriminate. The fault is my own I suppose for not realizing that I was getting involved in an organization that has questionable ethics. The moral - no matter how indebt and in need of a job  you are always read the fine print.',0,1034,0,0,'64.229.166.236','en'),(17088,2804,'2002-06-04 23:21:40',278,'egenus','<p>First of all...don\\\'t write articles about the dangers of teenage drug use, or at least be fair to the drug culture by focusing on some of the good parts as well. Mainstream media has lost my respect because they are very, very one-sided.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Otherwise, I would also agree that there is nothing wrong with being diverse. However, I personally can\\\'t handle not being able to fully express myself, even at work.</p>',1,427,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(17089,2803,'2002-06-04 23:23:51',278,'egenus','\\\"Without death we\\\'d have no reason to live at all.\\\"',1,60,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(17090,2802,'2002-06-04 23:40:58',278,'egenus','<p>Affirmative action is a bad idea. Whatever happened to hiring someone based on their qualifications. My own job hunting experiences have taught me a few rather disturbing lessons. First, discrimination goes beyond race. White/black/whatever, just try and get a corporate job with red hair and piercings; or worse, try and get one under the age of 21. Second, qualifications don\\\'t matter. Employers don\\\'t really read your resume to learn what skills you have (or at least say you have), they read it to learn something about your personality. Companies have a template that each of their employees must fit, rather rigidly, or else will not be hired. Third, token employees hardly escape discrimination. Other employees, burned by affirmative discrimination, will likely make life harder on them anyway. Employers forced to hire a less qualified person will probably react similarly.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The real issue is that people have stopped paying attention to a person\\\'s ability to work and have (as most humans inevitably do) focused entirely on aesthetics. Pretty women still get hired before the less traditionally attractive women, and young workers still get treated like shit. I say we should handle equality problems in courts and schools. Forcing a quick fix, especially this one, will likely widen the social tear.</p>',1,1335,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(17091,2805,'2002-06-05 03:44:38',70,'Fuzzy','No goals equals death.<br><br>You\\\'re happy because you have the basic fulfillments covered but if you can\\\'t think of anything else to aspire towards or some clear goals to accomplish then you\\\'ve stalled and would therefore be unhappy.  Maybe somewhere along the way towards happiness you lied to yourself and said what you had was what you really wanted when, in fact, it was anything but.  Maybe a <i>New Car Smell</i> air freshener would help restore the feeling of newness in your life.<br><br>On a dissimilar note: I think I just ate some baaaaaaaaad tuna.',1,569,0,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(17092,2800,'2002-06-05 08:14:06',240,'xep','i know a boy and a girl who do doubles trapeze by him hanging from hooks hanging from the trapeze and her hanging from him.  i was helping them practice one day and she said, in the sweetest voice ever, \\\"honey, i\\\'m coming to accept the fact that you might vomit on me during the performance.\\\"',0,301,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(17093,2808,'2002-06-05 18:05:26',83,'adamajah','catch shellie dancing on stage with the White Stripes on the mtv movie awards on thursday.  they had to redo their performance like 4 times.  are they good? ',0,157,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(17094,2808,'2002-06-05 18:37:19',86,'egg','why did I always think those awards shows were live? and why is shellie dancing onstage with the white stripes? was it a choreographed number? like, do they do the roger rabbit and the running man? ',0,198,0,0,'216.175.88.18','en'),(17095,2808,'2002-06-05 19:11:46',17,'austin','she is a go go dancer.<BR><BR>i think if the mtv producer saw you do the roger rabbit, you would definately be on the stage too.',4,128,86,0,'205.179.19.111','en'),(17096,2808,'2002-06-05 19:13:12',83,'adamajah','they made her do the robot. she rebelled.  yeah, all those awards show are waaay fake.  they pre-film them and do several takes of all the acts.  i feel sorry for the audience.  the crowd BOO\\\'d Kelly Osbourne\\\'s performance and yelled \\\"THIEF!\\\" at Winona.  All that will be edited out with applause...  HOLLYWOOD IS A LIE ;p',0,334,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(17097,2808,'2002-06-05 19:41:41',17,'austin','<P>no no no, it cant be true.  it cant be true.  you are not my father!</P>\r\n\r\n<P>are the oscars live?  those actors know how to act like they behave.</P>',4,154,83,0,'205.179.19.111','en'),(17098,2808,'2002-06-05 20:35:44',86,'egg','my friend keith told me that tom waits was at the white stripes show with his wife and kids last night. I know this has nothing to do with anything, but we had just been talking about him at that bar beforehand, remember.',0,221,0,0,'216.175.88.18','en'),(17099,2809,'2002-06-05 20:50:54',174,'Mad Moll','<p>I liked this piece a lot. It was very thoughtful, and I think the writing itself was very good too. And I definitely agree with what youâ€™re saying. I believe pretty firmly that the temporary nature of our lives, far from being what makes them â€œsmallâ€ or â€œinsignificantâ€, in fact gives life value. I also agree strongly with your ideas about drug use.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But youâ€™re also dead wrong (HA HA) about a lot of things. For one, <i>a lot</i> of stuff has been going on since the Victorian era, and the fields of human knowledge have been anything but stagnant. Psychology, anthropology, cognitive science, sociology, linguistics and many other fields barely existed 100 years ago. Also, a lot of big things have happened in the traditional fields of philosophy, physics, art and literature since then. In fact, I canâ€™t really think of a time in which thereâ€™s been so much change condensed into such a short period.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Also, a lot of what youâ€™re talking about â€“ about how people are complacent, about how living to survive isnâ€™t enough, about how machines do most of the heavy labor today â€“ all of this is only true for a small part of the human population. Itâ€™s only true for the aristocrats of the world. You may not consider yourself one, but you are. Most labor isnâ€™t done by machines. Itâ€™s done by dark-skinned people who live far away. Survival may not seem like a big deal to us, but it is for most people, who actually do have to worry about where the next meal is going to come from or what disease is going to get them or how much rain is going to fall next season. Most people canâ€™t afford to be bored enough to even consider â€œsqueezingâ€ more out of life.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>To the priveledged few who can afford it, however, what youâ€™re saying does make a lot of sense.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You say that repression slows down creativity, discovery, progress, and things like that. Thatâ€™s actually not true at all, because repression is generally followed by <i>sublimation</i>. Psychic energy doesnâ€™t just vanish â€“ if you have a desire or a drive for something, and repress it, that energy is channeled somewhere else â€“ often into the arts and sciences (and sometimes into anxiety, violence and other bad things). Some of the most restrictive and â€˜repressedâ€™ societies in history have ironically been the most scientifically and culturally progressive.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I hope you donâ€™t mind the criticism â€“I really did like this post a lot.</p>\r\n',3,2440,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(17100,2808,'2002-06-05 21:06:38',174,'Aqua Moll','his new albums blow :~{ what happened to his voice?\r\n\r\n',6,55,86,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(17101,2809,'2002-06-05 21:19:43',278,'egenus','I agree that this peice was directed at a western audience. You are also very correct that the less fortunate members of our global society end up doing all our labor. I will clarify by saying that my culture is repressed by it\\\'s own creations, whether mechanical or tryannical in nature. Our [again my culture\\\'s] obsession with materialism is the source of this tyranny and our suppliers, from the oil barons to Nike and Kathie Lee, feed our addiction relentlessly. But it gets us nowhere, and its really fucking up everything.',0,534,174,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(17102,2809,'2002-06-06 01:00:45',176,'Scapegoat','I really like this and agree with it for the most part.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>Mad Moll</b> brought up the things that I had a few problems with, this being mainly the comment related to intellectual territory and the Victorian era.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI think that what is happening now is that the intellectual leaps and bounds that are made don\\\'t really impact general societies ways of thought.<br><br>\r\n\r\nOn the issue of materialism... until we bite the big one I don\\\'t think we can get away from this but our consumption is driven by comfort (at the expense of others) instead of freedom. I think we suffer culturally from a general malaise and have lost core values (In my moralizing I\\\'m not really talking about family one\\\'s in this case either -not to demonise family though)<br><br>\r\n\r\nYour take on the role of death in life is perhaps one of healthiest I\\\'ve heard. Reading this actually lifted my spirits.<br><br>',0,921,0,0,'64.229.160.150','en'),(17103,2807,'2002-06-06 01:09:08',176,'Scapegoat','I\\\'d eat Honeycombs by the box if I got to have dreams like this. Very intriguing.',0,84,0,0,'64.229.160.150','en'),(17104,2808,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','bittersweet drostes are filling my stomach. end transmission.',1,61,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(17105,2809,'2002-06-06 02:39:20',59,'s5','<p>\r\ni disagree with the notion of exploration slowing to a halt. space exploration, both by probes and telescopes here on earth, continues to expand every year. also, explorers continue to learn new things about antarctica, amazonia, the ocean floor, and other remote parts of this planet that exist somewhere beyond where the freeway ends. finally, you admit to using psychedelics and posting on the internet. well, the exploration of the psyche through text and chemicals are also two highly active areas of exploration.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni also disagree with the notion that \\\"we\\\" (who is this \\\"we\\\"? do you mean \\\"i\\\"? do you mean \\\"people i know/know about\\\"?) have no purpose or goal. yes, there is no inherent purpose to anyone\\\'s life. but to say that you, me, anyone else, or humanity as a collective has no goal or purpose to rally around is a hastily formed assertion.\r\n</p><p>\r\nit\\\'s very easy to say \\\"everything used to be better/more interesting back in the old days when there were fewer people and fewer problems\\\", but i think that\\\'s bullshit. it\\\'s only just beginning now.\r\n</p>',6,1122,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17106,2810,'2002-06-06 05:07:52',174,'Quinong Moll','<p>I listen to the evangelist station every morning at work. My favorite program is \\\"Insight for Living\\\" with Chuck Swindoll.</p>\r\n<p>I\\\'m not a Christian myself, and in the home country we do not have things like preachers. Our religions are mostly private, and so to hear this man on the radio is always interesting for me. This Chuck, I have to say, is very skilled in what he does. It is really a pleasure to listen to him even if you don\\\'t believe in the details of what he\\\'s saying.</p>\r\n',6,508,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(17107,2810,'2002-06-06 05:09:37',120,'joyrider','\\\"eternal salvation! act now - seating is limited!\\\"',0,56,0,0,'12.224.184.3','en'),(17108,2810,'2002-06-06 06:53:51',298,'lurkinginsagas','\\\"eternal salvation . . . for only fifty dollars!\\\"',2,55,120,0,'207.218.233.180','en'),(17109,2810,'2002-06-06 08:14:06',59,'s5','this would be good as an mp3.',0,29,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17110,2801,'2002-06-06 13:26:17',317,'grace','dude, this is a really good poem. actually, would you mind if i used it in an english assignment of mine? we just have to find some poems and write about their form and content. hope you don\\\'t mind.',2,201,0,0,'203.56.239.111','en'),(17111,2809,'2002-06-06 15:25:17',278,'egenus','I wouldn\\\'t say that \\\"things were better before\\\". In fact, humans have been fucking up the planet and each other for an exceedingly long time; and it must stop. The \\\"we\\\" I refer to is my culture, and while certain individuals may still try to express themselves, society as a whole is becoming complacent, like ignorance is something to aspire to.',3,361,59,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(17112,2808,'2002-06-06 15:26:18',79,'yeti','Oh no no no, yoko ono! I am really surprised that you think they blow. What other albums did you like better? Personally, I thought they were musically and lyrically excellent. I guess if you were really really into something like Closing Time or The Heart of Saturday Night or the One From The Heart soundtrack, you\\\'d not like them. But I think that it\\\'s a logical and good musical progression for the albums to take. What turned you off to them?',1,453,174,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(17113,2808,'2002-06-06 15:27:10',79,'yeti','I could hear that rain beating down on the tent. It sounded beautiful. Big drops. Wish I could have been there!',1,111,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(17114,2805,'2002-06-06 16:38:17',183,'lin','<p>I feel this same way alot. The harder things push down on me, school, work, social upheaval, the world, knowledge, everything.. the more I rise up to meet the challenge. But when things start to go my way, up creep the butterflies in my stomach at nights, or the weighty feelings at work.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> I get stressed to a point of wanting to mash things up, but often there is a sense of release for me through creating. That\\\'s my suggestion. When you laze about on a couch being comfortable all afternoon, you feel slothy.. you\\\'re enjoying it, but it\\\'s like you\\\'re storing up all the energy you\\\'re so used to spending. Your body/mind has to spend it somehow, so you maybe feel uneasy, or depressed, or just bad.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> Maybe get something you can play with.. something stimulating (clay? paint? sandbox? chiapet?). I\\\'m not saying to ignore the world, but maybe you use what\\\'s in your life (good and bad) to make things. That\\\'s what I think art is made from. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I also think making your environment less electronic, and more organic will probably help with uneasiness. You\\\'d be suprised how a few plants can make you feel so much different.</p>\r\n\r\n',2,1188,123,0,'137.122.14.20','en'),(17115,2801,'2002-06-06 16:46:41',75,'tyler','that woud be fine. Tyler Lee',1,28,0,0,'165.247.164.72','en'),(17116,2801,'2002-06-06 16:46:51',75,'tyler','that would be fine. Tyler Lee',1,29,0,0,'165.247.164.72','en'),(17117,2810,'2002-06-06 16:49:37',298,'lurkinginsagas','if only i had some means of recording her half of the conversation.',1,67,59,0,'207.218.209.206','en'),(17118,2805,'2002-06-06 19:12:29',123,'dcc','you\\\'ve wonderful suggestions, but oddly enough, i\\\'ve already followed some of them to an extent.\r\n<p>i\\\'ve found the most effective creative outlet i have is writing. i do it quite a bit, more than i post on swinney,  here.\r\n<p>plants, i have many, and keep wanting more. unfortunately my place is too small to hold as many as i\\\'d like.\r\n<p>i\\\'ve even taken up more physical activities. i ride my bike to work instead of packing myself into a streetcar. i took up aikido to train both my mind and my body.<i>(i highly recommend aikido to anyone, it\\\'s fun)</i>\r\n<p>i think laney was also right, just the weight of the world and it\\\'s bad-ness just has been getting to me.',2,688,183,0,'66.207.199.132','en'),(17119,2805,'2002-06-06 19:12:29',123,'dcc','you\\\'ve wonderful suggestions, but oddly enough, i\\\'ve already followed some of them to an extent.\r\n<p>i\\\'ve found the most effective creative outlet i have is writing. i do it quite a bit, more than i post on swinney,  here.\r\n<p>plants, i have many, and keep wanting more. unfortunately my place is too small to hold as many as i\\\'d like.\r\n<p>i\\\'ve even taken up more physical activities. i ride my bike to work instead of packing myself into a streetcar. i took up aikido to train both my mind and my body.<i>(i highly recommend aikido to anyone, it\\\'s fun)</i>\r\n<p>i think laney was also right, just the weight of the world and it\\\'s bad-ness just has been getting to me.',2,688,183,0,'66.207.199.132','en'),(17120,2810,'2002-06-06 19:30:15',124,'gus','my aunt, uncle and cousins made a video of some fake commercials for their church potlucks or whatever. in them they pretend to be italians and try to do italian accents and stuff like that. one of my cousins wife was really good, she kept in character, but every 5 seconds my aunt would break character to recite some bible verses or talk about getting jesus into your heart. they showed them to us recently. Im sure you all would have died laughing. I however, had to wait until they left.\r\n<p>\r\nThere are plenty of christians who arent insane though. You just dont know it because well, they arent insane.',0,608,0,0,'64.160.55.92','en'),(17121,2810,'2002-06-06 19:33:09',59,'s5','you want <a href=\\\"http://www.radioshack.com/product.asp?catalog%5Fname=CTLG&category%5Fname=CTLG%5F001%5F001%5F013%5F010&product%5Fid=43%2D228\\\">one of these</a>.',3,167,298,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17122,2814,'2002-06-06 19:33:59',124,'gus','You dont need to try to get her to talk. Just make sure she knows she can talk to you if she wants to or needs to. Make sure she knows you will love her either way, and that you are honored that she would share with you. And when you share your secrets, it builds more trust between you and makes it easier for you both. maybe.',0,327,0,0,'64.160.55.92','en'),(17123,2812,'2002-06-06 20:51:40',74,'jo','this is beautiful.',1,18,0,0,'172.148.52.39','en'),(17124,2788,'2002-06-07 04:21:20',174,'Mad Moll','<p>your friends are morons, yo. sorry.</p>\r\n<p>you tell a good story.</p>',6,73,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(17125,2812,'2002-06-07 05:50:14',120,'joyrider','thanks, jo.  i\\\'m glad you liked it.',0,38,74,0,'12.224.184.3','en'),(17126,2812,'2002-06-07 09:18:58',295,'karenlee','<i>...the paradise of a heartbeat</i>....what a wonderful description. \r\n \r\n',1,76,120,0,'203.194.3.62','en'),(17127,2810,'2002-06-07 09:28:01',295,'karenlee','<a href=\\\"http://www.jesusdressup.com\\\">Jesus loves you, so dress him up!!</a> \r\n\r\n',1,87,0,0,'203.194.3.62','en'),(17128,2576,'2002-06-07 13:28:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this skyline once filled with towers:<br><br><A href=\"http://www.citynoise.org/index.php?article=373\"><img border=\"0\" src=\"http://www.rhodamine.org/images/lomo/liberty2.jpg\"></tt>',1,125,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17129,2809,'2002-06-07 16:14:02',307,'hool','<p>agree with s5, re bullshit.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>egenus said: \\\"society as a whole is becoming complacent, like ignorance is something to aspire to.\\\"  this claim is ignorant in itself.  complacency has always been the rule, not the exception.  it serves no purpose except self indulgence to believe that \\\'society as a whole is becoming complacent\\\'.  complacency is an inherent social trait.  organisms that form social networks do so to make things easier on themselves individually. </p>\r\n\r\n',3,495,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17130,2788,'2002-06-07 16:28:09',307,'jeeff&hool','<p>kandrer, agreed, this was well told with dialogue.  are you misunderstood often?  i always refuse to apologize when i\\\'ve done nothing wrong.  i resent being held accountable for other people\\\'s excessively sensitivity.</p>',0,230,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17131,2576,'2002-06-07 16:47:28',265,'chuckspeed','peter\\\'s stuff is tight - really tight.  this makes commenting difficult; while it usually elicits a tacit response, the answer contained within the missive is usually better than anything we could conjure independently.',0,222,0,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(17132,2810,'2002-06-07 17:10:07',298,'lurkinginsagas','i\\\'ve been a fan of jesus dress up for quite some time.  once again: i\\\'m going to hell.',1,92,295,0,'207.218.213.190','en'),(17133,2813,'2002-06-07 17:12:08',298,'lurkinginsagas','\\\"And an oily film casts flat rainbows across its surface.\\\"\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.aboutme.com/dishabille/eyes/rainbowwater.jpg\\\" alt=\\\"oily\\\">',1,156,0,0,'207.218.213.190','en'),(17134,2805,'2002-06-07 19:07:28',183,'lin','<p>I suppose I just don\\\'t view the world as such. Even with all the rot that goes on, it still holds so much potential. From and discipline, any standpoint, there are so many things that we can channel our collective energy into.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Understanding that most people just don\\\'t seem to be that forward thinking is something hard to grasp for me sometimes. I remember occasionally and let it get me down a bit, but if I allow myself to let my image of the \\\"world\\\" be based on all the terrible things, it makes my reality pretty unbearable.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> I came to a decision that my reality is not anyone else\\\'s and likewise for all. No matter how close we get, there are still separate little bits in each of us that allow the way we see everything to be our own. I think once we find our niche in that place, accept that we create the reality we live in as much as it exists outside of us, and begin to take a sense of responsibility for the purpose of ourselves and our life .. well.. That\\\'s a goal anyway. I don\\\'t know what will happen then. Maybe we\\\'ll all just hold doors open for stangers. </p>',2,1125,123,0,'137.122.14.20','en'),(17135,2816,'2002-06-07 23:22:15',190,'dahlia','*applause*',0,10,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(17136,2816,'2002-06-07 23:52:18',17,'austin','i agree.',1,8,190,0,'205.179.19.111','en'),(17137,2818,'2002-06-07 23:54:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt><img src=\\\"http://i.walmart.com/i/p/00/03/94/05/55/0003940555312_150X150.jpg\\\"></tt>',1,96,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17138,2818,'2002-06-08 00:49:35',295,'karenlee','To lin: take your dreams [a little bit] seriously. in real life, you may have subconsciously \\\'noticed\\\' someone hanging around your house. of course there may be some metaphoric/symbolic rationale, but still always check on the real/tangible possibilities. that sounds paranoid, but it\\\'s not really. just boringly practical. my dreams have told me heaps of stuff. \r\n\r\n\r\n',1,378,0,0,'203.194.8.175','en'),(17139,2599,'2002-06-08 13:44:15',295,'karenlee','yay! the Luddites were wrong!! \r\n',1,33,0,0,'203.194.9.82','en'),(17140,2599,'2002-06-08 15:09:06',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\\\"It\\\'s all either grown or dug up out of the earth. \\\"</i> nice!</tt>',1,87,129,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17141,2813,'2002-06-08 20:02:03',175,'Paul G','Thank you for the picture; it\\\'s nice.',0,40,0,0,'67.195.73.82','en'),(17142,2788,'2002-06-08 22:42:46',187,'kandrer','thanks :). please read the next (and last) part, i explain a bit about this too.',1,80,174,0,'205.186.126.167','en'),(17143,2788,'2002-06-08 23:01:19',187,'kandrer','thanks, i first wrote it without dialogues, and it was nearly as boring as the real event... so i rewrote it this way. i took me much more time but this story was on my mind enough to keep me working on it ;).<br>\r\ni am often misunderstood, but it rarely gets that bad since i try to be careful (i\\\'m getting old now). also, i try to apologize as much as i can, even if i don\\\'t think i\\\'m wrong. it\\\'s good for me (note that i wrote \\\"i try\\\", it doesn\\\'t mean i apologize often...). in this story i fucked up, but i know it\\\'s not a big deal.',1,560,307,0,'205.186.126.167','en'),(17144,2788,'2002-06-08 23:02:39',187,'kandrer','i wrote \\\"partly fictionalized\\\" because i changed the names, and also because i can\\\'t pretend to describe exactly how it happened.',1,138,0,0,'205.186.126.167','en'),(17145,2809,'2002-06-08 23:22:26',278,'egenus','It would be folly I suppose to expect a sentient being to supercede their primal instincts with conscious thought.',4,114,307,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(17146,2776,'2002-06-08 23:37:59',75,'tyler','or maybe i won\\\'t. the play\\\'s were great-- everything else was close but ultimately disappointing. ',1,104,0,0,'165.247.164.72','en'),(17147,2813,'2002-06-08 23:58:46',187,'kandrer','i don\\\'t know how to express it. maybe \\\"if you publish a book i\\\'ll be one of your readers\\\" could make it. thanks for the post.',1,137,175,0,'205.186.126.167','en'),(17148,2809,'2002-06-09 09:09:15',187,'kandrer','to me, <i>\\\"that\\\'s bullshit. it\\\'s only just beginning now\\\"</i> sounds rather close to <i>\\\"that\\\'s bullshit. it was better in the old days\\\"</i>... ;)<br>\r\nseriously, s5, i can\\\'t see any positive point in how the world is going. and please don\\\'t tell me \\\"science is progressing\\\" because you know science doesn\\\'t help anybody here.</P>\r\n<p>\r\neugenus, i liked your stuff, and yeah, you\\\'ve got some naive ideas on many things. but aren\\\'t we all naive writing comments here?\r\n</p>\r\n',1,516,0,0,'207.93.61.74','en'),(17149,2822,'2002-06-09 09:25:13',187,'kandrer','i don\\\'t know what you refer to by \\\"dream analysis\\\" but the classic psycho-analysis one supposes that you decrypt the dream using the personal material of the dreamer. so this is conform to what you say; the analysis of the dream is actually made by the dreamer himself. that\\\'s why it can work.',1,305,0,0,'207.93.61.74','en'),(17150,2788,'2002-06-09 16:49:13',307,'hool','just for reference, the comment by jeeff&hool was by me alone (forgot to change the nick when i commented).',0,107,0,0,'24.42.106.104','en'),(17151,2823,'2002-06-09 21:48:02',295,'karenlee','dreamy-delicious \r\n',2,19,0,0,'203.194.16.246','en'),(17152,2813,'2002-06-10 03:11:33',295,'karenlee','The big blob in that picture looks like a map of Australia. :) \r\n',2,65,298,0,'203.194.8.29','en'),(17153,2813,'2002-06-10 05:43:17',175,'Paul G','I love you.',0,11,187,0,'64.156.107.212','en'),(17154,2809,'2002-06-10 07:36:04',59,'s5','sure kandrer, but there are two sides to everything. when someone tells me how disappointed they are with civilization, i tell them, go move to the middle of nowhere, recycle all your toilet water, stop adding to all the waste and filth produced by eating packaged food and typing on a computer powered by a fossil fuel generator. go take all your morning shits in a hole you dig in the ground.\r\n<br><br>\r\nanyone who types on their computer to post to this website enjoys the benefits of the western civilization, regardless of how cool and post-modern it is to be contrary. there\\\'s two sides to everything. science makes everything better and science makes everything worse.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\\\"the world is going to shit\\\" is a centuries old observation, and i don\\\'t admire those who write about it tirelessly. THIS TIME, REALLY, HUMANITY\\\'S PROBLEMS ARE REALLY REALLY BAD. oh goodness me, remind me to pack away an extra sweater for the bomb shelter.\r\n',6,960,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17155,2809,'2002-06-10 16:31:08',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\"science doesn\'t help anybody here\"</i>...<br><br>well, i for one disagree. science helps me. on an abstract level, it has helped keep me alive at times, and at the least, often allows me to live better than i would be living without it. on a more base level, it gives me a job.<br><br>i think anyone alive in this day and age would sound a bit hypocritical when saying \"science doesnt help anybody\"; i could go on and on about this, but ill leave it at that. <i>\"best friendly\"</i></tt>',1,482,187,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17156,2162,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','lulu is brilliant.',0,18,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(17157,2824,'2002-06-10 19:50:53',124,'gus','who\\\'s the \\\"we\\\" in this? Im missing something.',0,54,0,0,'64.163.213.110','en'),(17158,1858,'2002-06-10 20:18:07',17,'austin','that looks like a plate of idigestion.',6,38,66,0,'205.179.19.111','en'),(17159,1858,'2002-06-10 21:12:23',66,'Peter','<br><tt>the pepper garnish was the best part.</tt>',1,50,17,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17160,2824,'2002-06-10 21:36:08',75,'tyler','who\\\'s the &quot;we&quot; in <i>The Love Song of J. Alfred Prufrock</i>?',1,74,0,0,'165.247.164.71','en'),(17161,2824,'2002-06-10 21:41:01',66,'Peter','<br><tt><img src=\\\"http://www2.twcnyc.com/imgs/logos/we.gif\\\"></tt>',1,71,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17162,2816,'2002-06-10 23:58:34',83,'adamajah','yes, this is really great',1,25,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(17163,2824,'2002-06-11 04:09:22',174,'Mad Moll','<p>Maybe the \\\"we\\\" is the \\\'generation\\\'.</p>\r\n<p>I\\\'ve never understood the \\\'generation\\\' thing. I can see it abstractly but I don\\\'t understand identifying with it.</p>\r\n<p>Which is not to say that this poem necessarily is either.</p>',6,254,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(17164,2826,'2002-06-11 04:12:46',174,'Mad Moll','This was really nice, especially towards the end. Your stuff has been getting mad diverse lately, Mr G. Kudos.',6,110,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(17165,2824,'2002-06-11 04:23:11',124,'gus','yah, if it\\\'s a generation, its not one Im aware of. my question remains. I guess I\\\'ll continue to be unenlightened.',0,121,0,0,'64.163.213.110','en'),(17166,2821,'2002-06-11 04:32:16',174,'Quong Moll','<p>Do you really think you did anything wrong? Even if you <i>did</i> have some \\\'incorrect\\\' views about China, so what? What do they care? It seems more like a political thing. I think a lot of people encounter similar situations with the Jew/Israel thing. Being critical of the latter doesn\\\'t necessarily mean being biased against the former. But who knows, maybe this doesn\\\'t apply. Maybe you are biased against the chinese <b>race</b>, whatever that means.</p>\r\n<p>Not that I am really a good spokesperson for this. I was raised in a place where everyone is taught that people who aren\\\'t one of us or people who don\\\'t speak our language are sub-human, that they are literally only a small step above wild animals. This dissolves once you get to know foreigners personally, but it\\\'s still a challenge to see strangers as individuals rather than homogenous herds of Other.</p>  \r\n',6,902,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(17167,2821,'2002-06-11 04:38:57',66,'Peter','<br><tt>moll, sometimes you are very eloquent and profound.</tt>',1,64,174,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17168,2826,'2002-06-11 04:56:25',176,'Scapegoat','I\\\'ll be honest and say that I may not be picking up on the meaning behind all the phrases employed here but it still sounds beatiful as I read it aloud in my head.',0,166,0,0,'64.229.167.115','en'),(17169,2826,'2002-06-11 04:58:25',176,'Scapegoat','Beautiful. not beatiful',0,23,0,0,'64.229.167.115','en'),(17170,2809,'2002-06-11 05:15:24',176,'Scapegoat','<b>s5:</b> I just wanted to say that postmodernism doesn\\\'t necessarily relate to bemoaning the benefits of western civilization. It can also be employed in support of it. If I were to think of my own personal ontology and it marries love of many things western and postmodernism. I\\\'m sensitive about postmodernism.<br><br>\r\nTo add to Peter\\\'s comments about science, I would be lost without it. It provides the majority of structure for my metaphysical viewpoint and the underlying infrastructure for the culture and society in which I live. It often is what provides those special moments in my life when I am happy about being human.<br><br> ',0,652,0,0,'64.229.167.115','en'),(17171,2824,'2002-06-11 05:33:10',75,'tyler','maybe it\\\'s the royal we. maybe it\\\'s me and you. maybe it\\\'s not me and someone else, or me and god, or you and god, or the entire race of men. what difference does it make? i would have specified who it was if it were important to the meaning of the poem. the question is, do you like it or not, and why?',1,312,0,0,'165.247.164.28','en'),(17172,2824,'2002-06-11 05:41:04',75,'tyler','ok: the idea here is, by writing about a generation, you create it--not the other way around. that is to say it is created by the artists for the people who then in biological imperative mass frenzy descend upon it and <i>identify</i>. if it were already a generation, i wouldn\\\'t be an artist--i would be a descender. proof that i have achieved my goal (to a very small extent, in swinney experimental space) is gus\\\'s remark that &quot;if it\\\'s a generation it\\\'s not one [gus] recognize[s].&quot; if it was...',1,520,0,0,'165.247.164.28','en'),(17173,2824,'2002-06-11 05:55:15',75,'tyler','ok, i\\\'m going for three. of course i am talking about my generation--the references at the beginning to Lost, Beat--obvious. too obvious? that\\\'s for you to decide. part of what i am getting at is that obviousness and complaining are aspects of my generation. gus\\\'s statement, \\\'it\\\'s not one i\\\'m aware of,\\\' i take to mean \\\'it\\\'s not one i identify with or i am part of.\\\' what is a generation? we talk about the Beat generation, the Lost generation...who was a member and who was not? did those terms mean the same thing to everyone who identified himself or herself as a member? this is the generation i feel i belong to--by writing this poem i define what it is for me. and it\\\'s definition for me is--drumroll please--it\\\'s exactly the same as all the others, in that identifying with a generation is a search for meaning, which is a common trait of all societies far-removed enough from the mechanics of living ie plowing the fields to contemplate life in the abstract.\r\n<p>\r\nbut did you like it? did you think the words were pretty and did you like the way the rhymes matched up? was it logical? did it build to a climax? are you glad you read it or sad? do you think it\\\'s stupid? do you think it\\\'s original? did it make you think? ',1,1273,0,0,'165.247.164.28','en'),(17174,632,'2002-06-11 08:33:47',86,'huevita','wait, what is the russian officer?',0,34,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17175,1858,'2002-06-11 09:45:25',295,'karenlee','The first picture is the best one. For sure. \r\n',1,47,0,0,'203.194.5.124','en'),(17176,1858,'2002-06-11 11:16:29',67,'trouble','i want to watch thursays Wings game at the golden fleece.',0,57,0,0,'68.47.202.98','en'),(17177,632,'2002-06-11 11:26:46',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ive been waiting like a year for an answer to that one, too.</tt>',1,73,86,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17178,1858,'2002-06-11 11:27:33',66,'Peter','<br><tt>oh man, greasy goodness! also: <i>flaming cheese</i>!</tt>',1,66,67,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17179,2809,'2002-06-11 14:46:58',278,'egenus','<p>While science makes our lives easier, in that we do not have to physically perform many of the tasks necessary for survival, it does not make our lives \\\"better\\\". This kind of qualitative judgement is left to the beholder, and I would venture to say that most people do not feel happier as a result of science. Science can become almost a religion in itself, governed by the nebulous \\\"them\\\", i.e. \\\"They say that world is flat.\\\", \\\"They say that our universe is composed of matter.\\\", etc... Many other theories exist, but are rarely argued outside the academic community; as a result of the society-wide complacency I mentioned. People that actively pursue knowledge are the exception and not the rule, and thus I say that \\\"we\\\" (western society in general) are disinterested and becoming rapidly stagnant.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Maybe my perception of our philsophical progression is tainted by my environment, but in the philosophy section of any given bookstore, I am unable to find any radically new thoughts since the latter existentialists.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>New and refreshing perspectives have been conceived, written and shared, but only in small forums (i.e. swinney, blogs, etc...). Our social repression is an intellectual one more than a physical one; read <u>1984</u> if you doubt me. My comments were made as broad generalizations about my culture, and I feel that one can only accurately judge a culture\\\'s progression by it\\\'s weakest points. Consider that all of our greatest scientific acheivements have come with terrible costs and have usually ended up having as much negative effect as they do a positive effect. The \\\"Power of Pride\\\" is a dangerous substance.</p>',3,1703,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(17180,2812,'2002-06-11 14:57:17',278,'egenus','Hauntingly beautiful, and well said.',1,36,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(17181,2809,'2002-06-11 15:32:07',66,'Peter','<br><tt>complacent is right; in my opinion, the only people who could possibly view science as <i>governed by the nebulous \"them\"</i> are the uneducated or otherwise ignorant.<br><br>\"science\" doesnt explain everything, nor is it a perfect all-encompassing theory; but i know that my life is in fact better with it; i dont have to worry about polio and countless other maladays. i can get fresh, healthy veggies year round. i can buy exquisitely pure mind-expanding chemicals. i have a car specifically enginneered to not kill me in the event of an accident. i have uv protection in my contacts and light in my apartment and an internet to play on. yes, life is indeed better thanks to science. no melodramatic \"atomic genie\", no cliche \"better living through chemistry\", but downright higherer quality living, and i dare you to argue against this, heh.<br><br>\r\nin regard to finding radically new thought in book-stores: perhaps try an academic journal. sometimes, current progressive thought takes a while to filter down to consumer level, kind of like most of those great underground tracks you hear in the club dont make it to Tower Records right off... they spend a while going through their respective peer-reviews, you know, being field-tested and justified by the pros before being passed out for widespread consumption. the progressive intellectuals get their hands on them first, andproceed to break them down into tidbits that the status-quo can understand; this is probably where most of the frustration you speak of is birthed, but i digress. <br><br>\r\nnew and refreshing perspectives only shared in small communities? how about the bazillion insurgent, contercultural, or other wise revolutionary repositories of info on the net alone? how about widespread artistic trends and musical developments? <br><br>and intellectual social repression? i think that these two things in particular are following trends exactly opposite what you describe!<br><br>let me generalize by saying that generalizations can be dangerous. judging cultural progression by its weak-points is a tremendously flawed method. statistically speaking, while \"our\" culture might have as many weak-points (or more) as it did 50 years ago, there are exponentially more and more high-points year after year, and the weak-points, no matter how persistant or profound, become less and less signitifant. thus is the law of science; means and deviations, bell curves, logic and averages.<br><br>\r\n<i>\"all of our greatest scientific acheivements have come with terrible costs\"</i>... like? who do you mean by \"our\"?<br><br>\r\njust some friendly banter here, but really- if you make more effort to embrace scientific/cultural/technological progress, you might see that its not so bad, especially in the perspective of the past 100 years or so. odds are rather high that you might not be sitting right there breathing right now were it not for the past 100 years of science. tell me that isnt an improvement...</tt>\r\n',1,2742,278,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17182,2826,'2002-06-11 18:54:05',278,'egenus','<p>Damn skippy. This is good, very good. I especially liked:</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>\\\"It is, I suppose, like a pagan incarnation: The word becomes flesh. But the word is eros â€“ and that makes it a heresy.\\\"</i>.</p>',1,209,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(17183,2827,'2002-06-11 19:00:04',278,'egenus','I\\\'d hate to speak judgement, karma can be a bitch. Truth is that everyone runs a high risk living an everyday normal life; being a statistic is all too easy.',2,160,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(17184,2809,'2002-06-11 20:28:00',295,'karenlee','Oh, phew, mein gott thank goodness I am uneducated...otherwise I would be ignorant!!!!! I amm glad I doo not hav pole eo.\r\n*best unedumacated friendly* \r\n',1,154,66,0,'203.194.15.140','en'),(17185,2826,'2002-06-11 21:02:16',82,'simon','i miss my girlfriend. :(<br>\r\nalso, this was real nice. i liked it.',0,67,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17186,2809,'2002-06-11 21:06:56',278,'egenus','<p>1. Please do not think I am suggesting that the nebulous \\\"them\\\" is to be heeded. That is in fact my entire issue. I believe that the majority of people have ceased to think for themselves.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>2. I have run out of faith in humanity, at least as they are now. What could be and what is are two entirely different matters.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>3. The pursuit of knowledge in the form of science is benefitial to us, if we are willing to embrace new realms of thinking. But how willing are we to change (keep in mind that we are only as fast as the slowest learner)?</p>',2,571,66,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(17187,2809,'2002-06-11 21:19:32',66,'Peter','<br>i dunno. i guess i just dont buy it on the cliche level. that <i>\\\"only as fast as the slowest learner\\\"</i> stuff and all.<br><br>i know that im smart, and i know that society and technology and innovation and progress run hugely far ahead of me... so someone\\\'s doing it somewhere, and it aint the slow guys.<br><br>again, averages.</tt>',1,349,278,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17188,2827,'2002-06-11 21:40:31',168,'laney','someone really needs to figure out a way to test for it in the early stages, or when someone is just a carrier. even the most meticulously careful person i know now suffers the same fate. it totally sucks. *sympathies*\r\n',0,220,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(17189,2809,'2002-06-11 22:27:30',59,'s5','so, egenus, can you please describe a time in history in which the majority of people *did* think for themselves? and if you could name such a time, would you have more or less faith in humanity than you do now? i\\\'d also like to remind you that your presence or lack of faith in humanity doesn\\\'t prove that humanity is good or bad, in the same way that someone\\\'s faith or lack of faith in god doesn\\\'t prove or disprove the existence of god.\r\n<br><br>also, this is the last thing i\\\'m going to post on this thread. this is coming down to faith and belief and opinion, all of which are irrelevant to the way things are.',6,631,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17190,2552,'2002-06-11 22:59:34',17,'austin','i had to clean out my car for my parents visit.  i left the trunk intacked.  but i threw out the water bottles, junk food, wrappers, coffee cups, and washed the clothing.',3,170,0,0,'205.179.19.111','en'),(17191,2552,'2002-06-11 23:05:32',82,'simon','heh. when jessica\\\'s grandma visited, she had to grandma-proof the apartment which meant all the porn and a few drug related items were exiled to the trunk of her car and then forgotten until we went to napa and discovered a variety of dirty mags and comics to go along with our picnic.',0,288,17,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17192,2826,'2002-06-12 00:13:45',120,'joyrider','this has got some really good passages, paul.  you turn a wicked phrase.',2,72,0,0,'12.224.184.3','en'),(17193,2812,'2002-06-12 00:17:24',120,'joyrider','i just wish it was made up.  i\\\'d be happier telling lies.',0,60,0,0,'12.224.184.3','en'),(17194,2824,'2002-06-12 13:43:56',174,'Vuolongkathdewage Moll','<p>Hmm. Well, it\\\'s certainly made us think, but perhaps not in the way you intended. My feelings on the writing itself are mixed.</p>\r\n<p>The \\\"give them back\\\" part is very good, I think, and so is buildup at the end. In general I think your style and use of words are both pretty strong. On the other hand, some phrases struck me as overdramatic (\\\"What have you done to us? What have we done to ourselves?\\\"), but this is of course coming from someone who doesn\\\'t feel strongly about the topic, so make of it what you will.</p>\r\n',6,546,75,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(17195,2809,'2002-06-12 15:00:49',74,'jo','keep arguing, this is interesting. especially peter, whooo doggie, peter you\\\'re a good debater.<BR>\r\n[x rabble rouser]',2,121,0,0,'172.143.190.95','en'),(17196,2824,'2002-06-12 15:10:46',74,'jo','i have a longstanding personal rule that if a poem has to be explained or defended before people like and/or understand it, it needs more work.<BR>\r\ntyler, i, too have issues with the nebulous \\\"we,\\\" <b>especially</b> if it means \\\"our generation.\\\" why don\\\'t you try writing about yourself or about a friend of yours and see if you can evoke the same message through a portrait of that person? see allen ginsburg\\\'s \\\"<a href=\\\"http://www.english.upenn.edu/~afilreis/88/america.html\\\">america</a>.\\\"<BR>\r\nbut you asked if, aside from the generation stuff, we liked the words and flow, and i\\\'d say that at this point? not really. i don\\\'t like poetry that generalizes. it doesn\\\'t challenge you or the reader. ',3,739,75,0,'172.143.190.95','en'),(17197,157,'2002-06-12 17:30:32',82,'simon','dude, what ever happened to this guy, he was the shit.',0,54,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17198,2826,'2002-06-12 17:34:44',175,'Paul G','Thank you, thank you, thank you all so much for taking the time to read and comment - it means the world to me.',0,111,0,0,'67.24.8.41','en'),(17199,2824,'2002-06-12 18:33:42',75,'tyler','see allan ginsburg\\\'s &quot;howl&quot;.',1,41,0,0,'165.247.165.177','en'),(17200,2824,'2002-06-12 18:59:42',74,'jo','i have. it\\\'s an incredibly specific poem. there\\\'s a subtle but critical difference between \\\"my generation\\\" and \\\"the best minds of my generation,\\\" and ginsberg goes on to carefully paint a picture of his subjects. if what you\\\'re saying is that you were going for a \\\"howl\\\" feel with this poem, um. i see very few similarities.',0,351,0,0,'172.138.133.38','en'),(17201,2829,'2002-06-12 19:28:56',86,'egg','paging dr. freud.',0,17,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17202,2829,'2002-06-12 19:42:52',86,'egg','i would title this \\\"italian boys so close to bedtime\\\".',0,60,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17203,2830,'2002-06-12 20:32:41',66,'Peter','<br><tt>43 \\\'fuck\\\'s in one piece. nice.</tt>',1,49,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17204,2829,'2002-06-12 20:58:45',295,'karenlee','\\\"Steel Belt <i>adventure studs</i>\\\"',1,41,0,0,'203.194.19.99','en'),(17205,2824,'2002-06-12 21:39:19',59,'s5','i\\\'m with jo - \\\"we\\\" is a lazy \\\"i\\\". i\\\'d rather read about the author\\\'s experience, rather than the author telling me what my experience should be. a well-written story about a single phone conversation or a walk through the park can do more to define a generation than a million lines of generalizations.',3,323,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17206,2824,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i just think most poetry is really dum.',6,39,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17207,2829,'2002-06-12 21:55:56',82,'simon','<i>Adventure Studs 4: Rock Boys</i>',1,35,295,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17208,2830,'2002-06-12 21:58:15',82,'simon','i like sex and crack. i say this having only read that one paragraph. i should comment again after i\\\'ve read the whole thing.',0,128,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17209,2824,'2002-06-12 22:03:18',82,'poetry','well, i think you smell funny! heehee!',0,38,62,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17210,2830,'2002-06-12 22:14:03',83,'adamajah','If sex is man-made, then sex is crack.',2,38,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(17211,2818,'2002-06-12 23:25:36',183,'lin','<p>I started putting weight on my dreams a little while back. They always revolve around things going on in my life. In this case though, I don\\\'t think I\\\'m in danger. It\\\'s more like I have a mountain of stress that is starting to supress my ability to function..</p> ',0,276,0,0,'137.122.14.20','en'),(17212,2830,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>such an angry young man!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>*pets chia*</p>',6,53,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17213,2824,'2002-06-13 01:47:22',20,'jim','<p>\r\nthis poem (and most every other attempt I\'m aware of to synthesize a movement or concoct a label for the spirit of our time) doesn\'t work for me for the simple reason that it mistakes the void our generation\'s unique character would evolve out of if we weren\'t so lazy/anesthetized/bought off for the unique character itself.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nso many of the individuals we have identified as the creators of our culture have been pointing at that void for over a decade, insisting that it defines us as a generation, along with a predetermined enslavement to the hollow legacy of our parents.  that\'s exactly what a movement isn\'t.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nthe Losts evolved at the end of World War I, when a generation of people couldn\'t imagine being the soldiers or businessmen their parents had been and made life about something else together. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nthe Beats evolved at the end of World War II, when a generation of people couldn\'t imagine being the soldiers or businessmen their parents had been and made life about something else together. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nthe hippies evolved in the midst of Vietnam, when a generation of people couldn\'t imagine being the soldiers or businessmen their parents had been and made life about something else together. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nmaybe war make it easier for a generation to find it\'s voice and the relative peace our generation has known that has made it harder to find ours.  \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nmaybe corporate domination and arbitration of the creation of culture is an even bigger obstacle.  \r\n.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nit seems pretty likely to me that the upheaval ahead will make it easier for us to find something more compelling to define ourselves by than the vacuousness we\'ve clung to so far.\r\n</p>',6,1767,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(17214,2830,'2002-06-13 02:26:39',174,'Lalakodagh Moll','<p>Yo chia, guess what bro?</p>\r\n<p><b><u><i>YER WRONG. DEAD WRONG BROTHER. NICE TRY!!!!</i></u></b></p>',6,104,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(17215,2826,'2002-06-13 02:27:05',124,'gus','Just to be pedantic \\\"The word was made flesh\\\" is not a pagan anything. the word = the spirit of God, made flesh = jesus as God on earth. Not that anyone cares, I just had to take a class once about this specific particular issue. blahblahblah.',0,249,0,0,'64.172.57.166','en'),(17216,2826,'2002-06-13 02:27:45',124,'gus','or maybe that\\\'s what you were saying, and Im just tired and reading wrong. hi!',0,81,0,0,'64.172.57.166','en'),(17217,2829,'2002-06-13 02:29:17',124,'gus','somehow I keep finding myself with an italian boy close to bed time... ',0,71,0,0,'64.172.57.166','en'),(17218,2830,'2002-06-13 02:33:47',176,'Scapegoat','I admire your tenacity lets say that first. Perhaps though your hate of desire, <i>\\\"i hate lust. i hate \\\'want\\\'. i hate \\\'cravings\\\'. i hate \\\'not being able to\\\'.\\\"</i> suggests a bias towards taoism. From what I understand one of its goals is to become desireless.<br><br>\r\nIf I\\\'m being an ass tell me to fuck off and I\\\'ll drop it but...<br><br>\r\nI\\\'m curious about something though - If you hate want, cravings, etc. Why did you bother to steal the coolers? Or is it something that you feel in yourself and thats why you loathe it? Hate is a rather strong behavior modifier I\\\'d think it would stop such things.<br><br>\r\nAs for the scapegoating of whites as the killers of all things good. You know better - its one thing to blow off steam but switch that to some other identity group and then read it back to yourself and think what judgements would be going through your head if you read it. Maybe I\\\'m being nitpicky, just my opinion.<br><br>\r\nOn a totally different note if you come back for a quick cuddle with your cat, give a call. I miss the beers and hashin out ideas with ya.<br><br>\r\nBest Friendly\r\n    ',0,1147,0,0,'64.229.168.43','en'),(17219,2824,'2002-06-13 03:07:31',75,'tyler','so this is the last comment i\\\'ll ever make about something i write here. so don\\\'t expect this to go on all the time. remember, it\\\'s stuff like this that makes the community of minds that gathers here grow and interact in new ways.\r\n<p>\r\nmaybe it\\\'s made it hard for you (and by you i mean all of you or maybe one or maybe this is self-referential) to find <i>your</i> voice, except to blanket criticize my poem only after being MADLY provoked, but <i>my</i> arrogant boastful ridiculous vocal apparatus seems to be working just fine. the fact is, i\\\'m filling that vacuum you (and i) refer to by writing this poem. that\\\'s the point--i identify it, then search for resolution. i say we because my ego is too big for just one person. \r\n<p>\r\nlook at it this way--i know what the perfect swinney article looks like, ok? i can do that. it\\\'s tempting to do it and come back and read all the oohs and ahhs, but instead i try to add to the community by writing something challenging, or outlandish, or whatever--and no one even bothers to comment, really. i could write some nonsense sentances with big words scattered around like <i>hesitation brings me to the brink, my glorious mind waits for the touch of your crooked elbow, inevitable</i> etc etc etc and then everybody would think i\\\'m a genius and the new jim morrison, but i\\\'m not. i am just me(we) trying to construct a poem that takes a lot of thought and has structure and logical progression and took several drafts. what else could anyone who reads this site possibly want? why all the negativity? you look at it and say <i>that\\\'s arrogant of you to presume how i feel</i>--and you\\\'re right. but you can\\\'t do it without being arrogant, even if it means you are not so popular. i mean, to live is to be arrogant. babies don\\\'t give a fuck if they keep you up all night crying, do they?\r\n<p>\r\nso in conclusion, let me end by saying, as we bring the program to a close, now that we\\\'re all out of time, uh-oh the stage manager is signaling me, you\\\'ve been a great crowd, that this is a defense of my aesthetic and the last one i\\\'ll probably ever do. it\\\'s more for me than it is for you. ',1,2185,0,0,'165.247.165.177','en'),(17220,410,'2002-06-13 03:15:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>nice</tt>',1,17,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17221,2830,'2002-06-13 05:09:09',196,'jasonp','Word up chia. You are good at ranting. Take it easy though nonetheless.<BR>\n',0,76,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(17222,2824,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<p>tyler, some thoughts:\r\n</p><p>\r\nmaybe you\'re confusing the fact that some of your good stuff was written in a moment of inspiration (and thus seemed easier to write) in comparison to the stuff that you consciously sat down to craft and put together. things like this poem.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwe\'re not saying you need to stop trying to make poems consciously. the mark of a great artist is someone that can craft and hone their art AND fly by the seat of their pants. the best stuff is a little of both. in fact, we\'re cheering you on to keep on challenging yourself, to keep making poems that need polishing and work. \r\n</p><p>\r\nkeep working at it, tyler. i\'m sure your ego wouldn\'t let you do otherwise. but do listen when other people actually give you honest feedback. it\'s the only way you\'ll ever grow.\r\n</p><p>\r\nfor the record, i didn\'t like the poem. not because it didn\'t have promise, but it felt it needed more meat and content and work. it\'s not really enough to throw a bunch of strong statements together without a lot of structure. it really throws the weight of the poem in the content, and the content struck me as a little trite.\r\n</p><p>\r\nanyway. step back. ego can be a boost when you\'re down, or it can make you deaf to things that could help you grow as an artist. be a baby, and learn what the universe has to show you. it\'s really immature to just stomp off and say \"you guys should appreciate this poem simply because i worked hard on it!\" and \"you liked my other stuff and that was baby stuff!\" because in the end, that doesn\'t matter. i could spend 10 years molding my mucus into the perfect buttplug, but no one\'s gonna buy it.</p>',1,1430,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(17223,2830,'2002-06-13 05:41:53',66,'Peter','<br><tt>chia, are you there to \\\'turn it all into a comodoty\\\'? are you white?<br><br>im sure there are many like you, in that way, there; you should seek them out and see what theyre in it for.</tt>',1,203,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17224,2826,'2002-06-13 05:55:12',175,'Paul G','Right, right, right: You\\\'re tired; I\\\'m drunk.  But, both of your comments were good.  It is pagan, in this instance, because the \\\"word\\\" isn\\\'t Jesus, but rather \\\"eros\\\".  Thanks for drawing attention to the source though, and reading, and thinking, and blah, blah, blah.\r\n\r\n<p>Also: Though you are one of my favorite writers here I really came across like a terrible dick when I tried to interact with you at first.  So, as I see it, you should feel free to rip me without remorse.  Oh well.</p>',0,514,124,0,'67.24.9.134','en'),(17225,2824,'2002-06-13 05:59:48',59,'s5','(i would buy it.)',0,17,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17226,2809,'2002-06-13 07:03:22',176,'Scapegoat','<b>egenus</b> Have you lost true faith in humanity or your version of humanities collective potential? Have you ever considered that the issue is your expectations for all of humanity and not its true potential? Think of  humanities collective resentment towards having so many expectations placed on it. I know I\\\'d be slow to meet such expectations, just out spite because I\\\'m immature that way. Reason with me and I\\\'ll change a hell of a lot faster.<br><br>\r\n<b>s5 said:</b> <i>\\\"this is coming down to faith and belief and opinion, all of which are irrelevant to the way things are.\\\"</i> I agree with the first clause of this sentence but think the second is too much. Pain in the ass & runnning around in circles yes but, irrelevant to the way things are - hardly. ',0,783,278,0,'64.229.162.207','en'),(17227,2824,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>wow, this poem was so challenging.  it\\\'s so outlandish.  come on tyler.  give me a break.  you like your poem, well great for you.  other people think it\\\'s dum.  someone somewhere probably thinks it\\\'s a work of genius.  big fucking deal.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>furthermore i challenge you to write the perfect swinney article.  please label it as such so i may ooh and ahh appropriately.  *rolls eyes*</p>',6,404,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17228,2824,'2002-06-13 07:41:04',74,'jo','<p><i>maybe it\\\'s made it hard for you (and by you i mean all of you or maybe one or maybe this is self-referential) to find your voice, except to blanket criticize my poem only after being MADLY provoked,</i></p>\r\n<p>you assume that we (or maybe me or maybe you) would choose to express our \\\"voice\\\" on swinney, that it is our only outlet, that we are all either inclined to or obligated somehow to express ourselves via this medium. at least for me, that\\\'s far from the case.<BR>\r\npersonally, tyler, i gave feedback because it was being demanded. i\\\'m sorry you didn\\\'t like the feedback, but the feedback was not my <i>voice</i>, for fuck\\\'s sake, it was just an opinion on some writing you did.<BR>\r\ni guess what i want to say here is something to the equivalent of heat and getting out of kitchens but blah, this is such a tired argument.</p>',0,863,0,0,'172.129.185.197','en'),(17229,2824,'2002-06-13 07:52:52',175,'Paul G','Gang-bang.',0,10,0,0,'67.24.9.134','en'),(17230,2824,'2002-06-13 07:56:37',59,'s5','is a gang-bang anything like a circle-jerk?',4,43,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17231,2824,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','houston <strike>500</strike> 620',6,32,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17232,2824,'2002-06-13 11:38:20',75,'tyler','if i couldn\\\'t take the heat (or the gang-bang) i would have bowed out of this right at the beginning. this isn\\\'t my only means of communication or of being read. but...well, it\\\'s not really about this poem in particular. \r\n<p>\r\ni mean yeah, it\\\'s mediocre. come on. but everything\\\'s an experiment.\r\n<p>\r\nthis is more about defending something i have done just for the sake of defending it. at first i was mad at the vagueness of the comments, the snide tone, \\\'if this is a generation it\\\'s not one <i>i\\\'m</i> familiar with\\\' etc.--it was like, \\\'this doesn\\\'t even deserve criticism.\\\' then after i became un-mad again, i wrote the follow-up. the criticism that came after that was also kind of vague. so now we have the real meat of it after the second one--the comment about the perfect swinney article was, in my humble opinion, the perfect finger in the eye.',1,892,0,0,'165.247.165.177','en'),(17233,2829,'2002-06-13 12:08:31',295,'karenlee','<i>Adventure Studs 5: The Big Guy Wants His Belt Back</i> \r\n',1,60,82,0,'203.194.11.43','en'),(17234,2829,'2002-06-13 17:00:11',168,'laney','mmmm italian boys at bedtime',0,28,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(17235,2830,'2002-06-13 18:19:49',180,'auntie','there are no colors, no religion, no ethnicity, no sexual identities.  there are only people.  human personages in many sizes and shapes and colors.  <br><br>\r\ngeneralizations are dangerous, i know, but sometimes, to make the world a better place, they must apply.\r\n<br><br>\r\nidentifying a group of \\\"whites\\\" as behaving badly, even when you are a member of that self-identified group, is just as bad, in my mind, as identifying a group of blacks or asians or whatever.\r\n<br><br>\r\nyou should read up on culture shock. i think you\\\'ve been there just long enough to have begun the first phase, in which all things native are good and right.  in a couple of months, the reverse will be true.  it takes a year to even things out.\r\n<br><br>\r\nwith great love and affection, your auntie\r\n',1,789,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(17236,2830,'2002-06-13 18:36:36',86,'egg','with all due respect, I disagree. while I believe that people are capable of behaving as individuals, they sadly do not always do so. people from similar social, economic, and racial backgrounds, in my experience, often behave similarly, because the developmental practices of their ilk nurtured them that way. It is not the over-riding rule that white people in asia \\\"behave badly\\\", but it is common enough to note as a trend. that said, I dont fucking hate white people, even though, being culturally western and racially half-white, I experience pangs of guilt at benefitting from the oppressive effects of globalization, which has benefitted American consumers over Asian workers, and the many complicated aspects of colonialism which undeniably rested on ideas of racial superiority. it is hard to know from chiamatt\\\'s rant just what <i>exactly</i> he is identifying as hateful in white people, but again, I dont believe that it is erroneous to refer to people by race, when they are behaving as a mindless member of one behavoiral pattern. also, asian people cant drive!!!! it\\\'s the truth.',0,1107,180,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17237,2829,'2002-06-13 18:50:28',82,'simon','you guys are funny',0,18,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17238,1717,'2002-06-13 21:29:38',295,'karenlee','<i>1:1 map</i>. I like that.',1,28,65,0,'203.194.6.206','en'),(17239,2830,'2002-06-13 21:30:26',180,'auntie','but, if you believe, really believe in your heart, that we are all equally human persons, and you relay that belief in your daily life, pretty soon people who know and love you will also begin to believe, and as they do, the world becomes a better place for all of us human personages, no matter the size or shape or color.\r\n<br><br>\r\nps. i can\\\'t drive either. in fact, i drive so poorly that i have only ever passed the written driving test, never the road one, and i am 48 years old with a restricted drivers license. ',1,523,86,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(17240,2830,'2002-06-13 22:54:22',333,'just-sade','I\\\'d have to agree with aunt bou the generalizations, but the comments on sec, i aggree.',2,90,0,0,'152.163.201.178','en'),(17241,2824,'2002-06-13 23:46:03',124,'gus','ok, did I like it? well, not really, because I want to be able to identify what it\\\'s about. and I couldnt do that because it isnt clear (to me) in the piece, and you wont tell me. Is it your generation? what generation alive today has no wars to speak of? I cant think of any. So it confused me. My comment asking what it was about wasnt meant to be snide or whatever, I was really asking because I really wanted to know, and you wouldnt tell me, which was frustrating.\r\n<p>\r\nFor me, if something seems to have some actual meaning, then the meaning itself helps to determine whether or not I like it. For me, your words and how they were put together werent enough on their own to make me throw out my question of what you were going for. When you wouldnt tell me what to look for, you prevented me from experiencing what you wanted me to experience. \r\n<p>\r\nI think I like most of your other stuff, so I guess I was trying to figure this one out so I could like it more? anyway. you asked if we liked it or not, asked people to tell you what they thought, and now that they have, you\\\'re faulting them for it. some people are snitty, you wont get them to unsnit just to suit you. when you put yourself out there, sometimes you get shit thrown at your head. \r\n',0,1264,0,0,'64.172.57.166','en'),(17242,2824,'2002-06-13 23:55:16',124,'gus','oh, and I dont have any problem with people who do write <b>\\\"the perfect swinney article\\\"</b>. \r\n<p>\r\nWhen I write it\\\'s to please an audience, to get a reaction. Maybe to offend and audience and get a reaction. but I write for an audience, and I want them to understand what Im saying, to identify with a character or a feeling. It\\\'s also to test my skill at putting together words in ways that other people like as much as I do. I dont think its bad try for that.\r\n<p>\r\nI dont think its bad to try to experiment either, but if your goal is the anti \\\"perfect swinney article\\\" and you\\\'re not going for ooh\\\'s and aah\\\'s, then uh, didnt you just hit your nail on the head with this one? When you do something different, you might make people uncomfortable. and I\\\'ll say again, when you do that, and you ASK for us to say what we think, and we TELL YOU, and you dont like it, that is YOUR tuff ca-ca.\r\n<p>\r\n<i> smoochie</i>',0,948,0,0,'64.172.57.166','en'),(17243,2832,'2002-06-14 00:00:27',82,'simon','i\\\'m sorry i didn\\\'t have a chance to read this very thoroughly today. but at a glance, it gave me the inspiration to write a swinney article entitled \\\"the wisdom of scary mustache,\\\" about a sleazy guy i know with a big huge \\\'stash. ',0,246,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17244,2811,'2002-06-14 02:12:11',74,'jo','austin swinney, why aren\\\'t you putting your own articles on the front page anymore? i enjoyed this a lot. i think it deserves to <b>repruhzent</b>.',1,150,0,0,'172.134.219.118','en'),(17245,2811,'2002-06-14 02:13:11',74,'jo','(also for some reason this reminded me of zadie smith\\\'s white teeth. or tom robbins\\\' skinny legs and all. both, maybe. the end.)',0,134,0,0,'172.134.219.118','en'),(17246,2830,'2002-06-14 02:35:29',174,'Licorice Moll','<p>I\\\'m wit egg on this. There are ethnicities. There are religions. There are sexual identities. Newsflash, dudes, human beings are <i>cultural, social</i> organisms, and this is pretty much what it means to be human. The cultureless individual is -- what? An unsensing, unfeeling, uncomprehending seer of nothing? Even the concepts we use to manage and make sense of experience are the inherited byproducts of a specific culture (or hodgepodgy mishmash of them) at a specific time. <i>Even the idea of the \\\'individual\\\'</i> took thousands of years to develop into what we understand it as today.</p>\r\n<p>Yeah man, \\\"generalizations suck\\\" and everything. Unfortunately, you can hardly say you\\\'re honestly interested in \\\"understanding the individual\\\" without also using generalizations about the world they\\\'re a part of.</p>\r\n<p>Holy Jesus. This means we might just have to commit to actually saying something that can be disagreed with when trying to understand something or someone! It means we <i>might be wrong</i>! It means we might have <i>views</i>. I know -- they are dangerous things, but you\\\'re going to have them and you\\\'re going to generalize no matter how hard you try not to. Might as well fess up to the fact and try to be good at it, no?</p>\r\n\r\n',6,1291,0,0,'24.191.177.245','en'),(17247,2811,'2002-06-14 03:09:32',295,'karenlee','I love all the characters in this account, especially Evelyn Rosenthrall.\r\n',1,75,0,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(17248,2832,'2002-06-14 03:14:58',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i have a neighbor with the <i>mo\\\'stache</i>, or molestor-stache if you will; i should write up something about him, perhaps. we could have us a kickass series on \\\'staches!</tt>',1,190,82,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17249,2811,'2002-06-14 04:21:57',124,'gus','yes, I like the characters a lot. I want to see what they do next. ',0,67,0,0,'64.172.57.166','en'),(17250,2811,'2002-06-14 04:55:42',64,'Joanna','I like this so much more than almost anything I have read lately.  I especially like the bit that reads like a complicated maths question.',0,138,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(17251,2824,'2002-06-14 06:24:50',75,'tyler','ha ha ha. yeah i guess i did hit the nail on the head. i don\\\'t know, i think it\\\'s good to work these things out all the way. i tried to say what i really felt. plus i just quit smoking and i am easily irritated. my Mr. Show dvd\\\'s came today, though, so i am much happier.',1,280,0,0,'165.247.165.173','en'),(17252,2811,'2002-06-14 06:36:52',295,'karenlee','666!!!',1,6,0,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(17253,2811,'2002-06-14 06:58:00',86,'egg','is evelyn rosenthall post- , pre-, or trans?',0,44,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17254,2821,'2002-06-14 06:59:12',187,'kandrer','<i>\\\"Maybe you are biased against the chinese race, whatever that means\\\"</i><br>\r\nwhat do you mean with your \\\"maybe\\\"? is it a question? did i write anything that made you believe that i think anything wrong about something called \\\"chinese race\\\"? maybe i should feel offensed by your comment.',5,308,0,0,'80.13.233.88','en'),(17255,2813,'2002-06-14 07:10:09',187,'kandrer',':}',1,2,175,0,'80.13.233.88','en'),(17256,2809,'2002-06-14 07:42:03',187,'kandrer','i said: \\\"science doesn\\\'t help anybody here\\\"<br>\r\nsorry, i was drunk. i meant partially what egenus already said now, that people are not trully happier, and partially that science is not doing it for making people happier.<br> \r\nscience is helping the good and the bad in the same ways. better medecine, better weapons. science is funded by money, that\\\'s how its goals of better life can be reached only indirectly.<br>\r\n</p><p>\r\nto s5 and peter:<br>\r\nyou guys are not really friendly anymore if science is criticized, are you? s5, you advised me not to use toilets anymore. peter best friendly said that i sound hypocritical. thanks a lot.\r\n</p>',3,658,0,0,'80.13.233.88','en'),(17257,2809,'2002-06-14 08:45:09',59,'s5','oh kandrer. i\\\'m not defending science. i\\\'m defending the notion of looking at both sides of things. i disliked this article because it lacks perspective and history, yet makes definitive and authoritative sounding statements.\r\n<br><br>\r\n(oh shit, and now i\\\'m posting again when i promised i wouldn\\\'t.)',5,313,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17258,2811,'2002-06-14 11:15:53',295,'karenlee','Also, will Appendorf be flogging off said \\\"few thousand dollars worth of computer equipment\\\"? \r\n',1,102,0,0,'203.194.6.216','en'),(17259,2809,'2002-06-14 12:58:33',66,'Peter','<br><tt>my apologies if a single honest comment about something i feel strongly about made me seem unfriendly or hurt you. much like you claim of science, my goal isnt always to make people happy; we\'re all creatures of opinion, some of which are held very strongly and preached very ardently. i happen have a certain bias towards the analytical and natural sciences; this bias isnt of personal concern to you so much as it is seeking to challenge your preconceptions of what science is. so no offense, im just responding to some of your observations that i disagree with, regardless of the fact that i think youre a neat person!<br><br>i dont know what i think about science helping the good and the bad; i agree that money (and more likely, human\'s ulterior motives) run the show as far as this is concerned, but maybe thats naive of me.<br><br>is the atom to blame for horrific radioactive wastes? is it to blame for the mass-slaughter possible through fission reactions? is it to thank for radiological technology or the ability to kill a malignant brain tumor? none of the above. the atom is just a mindless bundle of particles orbiting within specifically charged fields. the atom couldnt give two fucks about mankind, life, death or money. it is just a ball of energy.<br><br>\r\nscience is rather abstract like that; its merely a framework of processes, a substrate on which humans can construct both amazing feats and dastardly evils. so, id blame mankind\'s nature, rather than science. science is but one of countless specific means to a huge end, a tool if you will.<br><br> as robert oppenheimer said <i><b>\"science underlies every single thing in existence, but it is not everything. it is, however, very beautiful, and powerful.\"</i></b> i agree. its not science\'s fault that its used to kill; neither is it to science\'s credit that it does good things. it is, however, immensely and undeniably wonderful in that it provides one of the most trustworthy and believable, proven frameworks for human thought, reference for trends and progress, and concrete evidence of the properties of the world around us, which is basically more than any religious/social/political/philosophical/intellectual/artistic trend in history can claim thus far.<br><br>so thats why i like science, and why i felt compelled to defend it.<br><Br> <i>best friendly</i>, again.</tt>',1,1249,187,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17260,2832,'2002-06-14 17:07:30',82,'simon','i think this is a great idea. i\\\'ll have my story in by monday, sir.',1,70,66,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17261,2835,'2002-06-14 17:19:51',195,'matt','tyler, this is the perfect swinney article.',1,43,0,0,'209.208.108.140','en'),(17262,2831,'2002-06-14 19:04:25',83,'adamajah','i like \\\"grey and greyer.\\\"  \r\n\r\n(is jim carey in that?)',0,60,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(17263,2835,'2002-06-14 19:43:58',124,'gus','and now I am making the perfect swinney comment.',0,48,0,0,'64.172.57.166','en'),(17264,2831,'2002-06-14 20:52:43',295,'karenlee','No! But if George C walked on set I wouldn\\\'t mind a bit!',1,59,83,0,'203.194.5.213','en'),(17265,2097,'2002-06-14 21:04:29',295,'karenlee','I don\\\'t have any tvp recipes, however I am just pointing out retrospectively that this article was posted on my birthday. Also, I like it. It pretty much sums up some arthaus experiences of mine. Also I very much like the word <i>eschew</i>. \r\n\r\n\r\n',1,251,0,0,'203.194.5.213','en'),(17266,2834,'2002-06-14 22:05:56',17,'austin','im reading this.  it is going to take me a little while, but i am reading it.',3,77,0,0,'205.179.19.111','en'),(17267,2835,'2002-06-14 22:40:32',59,'s5','ooohh, ahhh.',6,12,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17268,2835,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','swinney.org has become something i don\\\'t recognise.',6,54,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17269,2830,'2002-06-14 22:42:01',186,'ook','<p>generalizations and culture can exist without a person having to be a generalization herself. there is a difference between a population and an individual.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>a person can have traits that are drawn from a larger context, or that are common in a larger group, and still be an individual. that is because each individual can have any combination of traits drawn from any number of groups. all those traits come from somewhere, but the person is unique.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>for instance, in general women are 15% smaller than men, but any random man might be smaller than any random woman, or not. maybe most asians can\\\'t drive, but any individual asian person might be fine at it (just as any individual auntie might not be ;)  ).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>thus, ook concludes that it can be useful to discuss the nature and habits of <em>groups</em> in society, but that this does not apply directly to individuals. still, i think individuals need to recognize the context within which they are acting and be sensitive to it, especially if it is a visible group.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>sorry to get all boring and statistical. this is pretty much straight from stats for geneticists/paleontologists.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>also, good rant, chia.</p>',0,1210,174,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(17270,2830,'2002-06-14 23:05:28',176,'Scapegoat','I agree with the need to recognize the context within which they act but to what level does one need to be sensitive? If you are part of a dominant <i>context</i> when does one know to hold back in the name of sensitivity? - or when one is allowed to be assertive.<br><br>\r\nAs a member of several dominant <i>contexts</i> I can empathize with struggles but have serious issues with pity that masquerades as sensitivity.  ',0,421,186,0,'64.229.162.207','en'),(17271,2835,'2002-06-14 23:28:50',82,'simon','what do you mean? it doesn\\\'t seem all that different.',1,56,62,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17272,2835,'2002-06-14 23:28:54',124,'gus','lucky for you it says \\\"swinney.org\\\" right at the top to help you with that.',0,81,62,0,'64.172.57.166','en'),(17273,2830,'2002-06-15 00:11:08',186,'ook','<p>i just meant sensitive as in recognizing how you might appear to someone, not as in trying to be sympathetic or babying the person. replace senstive with \\\"aware\\\" at will :) cheers.</p>',0,193,176,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(17274,2835,'2002-06-15 00:28:21',122,'hool','what makes this the perfect swinney article?  i don\\\'t feel that i have any idea as to what charateristics comprise perfection in the swinney context, so i\\\'m curious.',0,171,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17275,2830,'2002-06-15 00:32:34',295,'karenlee','actions might quite often not speak louder than words, but they  mean more to me. \r\n\r\n\r\n',1,88,0,0,'203.194.4.157','en'),(17276,2835,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','bitch!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!',6,25,124,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17277,2836,'2002-06-15 06:29:54',295,'karenlee','<i>i just didnt want to be there. i didnt want to be around my friends. i wonder if this is normal. sometimes i feel like i have to drop out of society for a day or two and ignore phone calls and hibernate in my dumpy apartment and brood (which is what im currently doing)</i>\r\n<p>Sounds like a fantastic and wonderful thing to do kelly if everything feels too big. Surely your dumpy apartment has some redeeming qualities though?</p>\r\n',1,436,0,0,'203.194.16.146','en'),(17278,2837,'2002-06-15 06:35:24',295,'karenlee','Both these scenes sound good to me. The first one sounds really good [the way you wrote it....<i>as sensation runs rapidly through a station in search of the next train to jump</i>]. Yes. \r\n\r\n',1,192,0,0,'203.194.16.146','en'),(17279,2837,'2002-06-15 10:15:52',59,'s5','what about the scene where she hates him so she fucks him one more time simply to humiliate him in front of his family?',4,119,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17280,2835,'2002-06-15 16:02:10',278,'egenus','This story reads like a david lynch screenplay.',0,47,0,0,'24.25.107.161','en'),(17281,2837,'2002-06-15 16:06:14',278,'egenus','ummm... angsty much?',0,20,59,0,'24.25.107.161','en'),(17282,2835,'2002-06-15 19:00:52',124,'gus','its like a joke hool, tyler is subtly making fun of people who write to please the swinney audience. I think he thinks some of us are formulaic amature hacks (or whatever) who are just out to stroke our own egos by posting only in front of our pals who\\\'ll like whatever we do anyway. while he himself is much more experimental and Im sure, heaps more intelligent and talented and therefore often posts crap no one likes at all. funny huh!?\r\n<p>\r\nplease to see the comment area of tyler\\\'s water wings post for background.\r\n<p>\r\nIm sure tyler will now tell me Im all wrong, but this is my take on it just the same.\r\n<p>\r\nSmoochies!',0,635,0,0,'64.169.160.102','en'),(17283,2835,'2002-06-15 19:23:37',90,'leonard','i\\\'m afraid no one knows how to take a joke anymore. i\\\'m not really afraid though.',1,87,0,0,'66.32.149.188','en'),(17284,2835,'2002-06-15 20:22:16',122,'hool','whether it\\\'s a joke or not, my question still stands.  \r\n\r\ni try to read each entry here individually.  good is good, crap is crap.',0,134,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17285,2835,'2002-06-15 21:29:23',124,'gus','Im with you hool. I was being snarky. if you ask me, there is no \\\"perfect swinney post\\\" there is just writing. some of it\\\'s good, some of it\\\'s bad, and most of it\\\'s mediocre. just like anything else.',0,214,122,0,'64.169.160.102','en'),(17286,2835,'2002-06-15 21:39:10',180,'auntie','actually, there IS a perfect swinney article.  sorta like the perfect country song, i will write it someday for fun and entertainment value.  maybe even today, depending on whether or not my nap works out.',4,205,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(17287,2830,'2002-06-15 23:21:59',92,'kini','seoul was the first asian city i ever visited. i was sick before i arrived, and even sicker when i left. but i think i\\\'d like to go back.',1,140,0,0,'202.62.120.76','en'),(17288,2837,'2002-06-15 23:40:44',298,'lurkinginsagas','i think i really prefer the first followed by the second.',1,57,295,0,'207.218.209.247','en'),(17289,2837,'2002-06-15 23:41:55',298,'lurkinginsagas','i\\\'m not familiar with that one, as i\\\'m not a vengeful bitch. :)',3,69,59,0,'207.218.209.247','en'),(17290,2837,'2002-06-16 01:18:17',240,'xep','what about the one that goes \\\"mmm ooh ow\\\" \\\"ow?\\\" \\\"you\\\'re on my hair\\\" \\\"oh, sorry\\\" mmgmgmgpgph \\\"wait, hold on, there\\\'s hair in my mouth\\\" *waits patiently* \\\"ok come here and kiss me\\\" *kiss kiss* \\\"er, hold on a second, can you scratch my back?\\\"  *scratch scratch*\r\n*smooch smooch* \\\"auugh leg cramp!\\\" etc',0,352,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(17291,2837,'2002-06-16 01:35:53',295,'karenlee','those there are scenes-within-the-scene.',1,40,240,0,'203.194.8.11','en'),(17292,2837,'2002-06-16 01:42:21',295,'karenlee','OR maybe yes, those are in fact fully-fledged free-standing scenes, but they have been craftily omitted from the above account. \r\n\r\n',1,132,240,0,'203.194.8.11','en'),(17293,2835,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','actually, the really fucking scary thing is, it\'s not a joke. if you don\'t think this is the ultimate swinney article EVER, oh my god, i shit me pantaloons, tyler\'s ego will come and beat you up after school. be afraid.',6,310,90,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(17294,564,'2002-06-16 04:31:29',295,'karenlee','I used to have a recruiter guy who called me up every few months when he had a crappy contract for me to do, writing shitty boring horrible did I say shitty boring horrible user manuals for the most shitty boring horrible software packages you ever saw. I always believed I was on the bottom of his list because of this...his \\\"hmmmm who might be sucker enough to do this job\\\" list.\r\n<p>Whenever he sent me to interviews, he would prompt me with things I wasn\\\'t allowed to say. Then later, he would ring me up at home and grill me about what I said/they said etc. The last interview I had through him, he did the same thing, telling me what-not-to-say, so I met the person, discovered the job was another shitty boring horrible one, and proceeded to say all the things I wasn\\\'t supposed to.</p>\r\n<p>As per usual, he called me up later that night and asked how it went and if I thought I\\\'d said anything that might have gone against me [snicker]. I very sweetly said oh no, it went great. Thank goodness, it did the trick and I have never heard from him again.</p>\r\n\r\n',1,1081,0,0,'203.194.10.210','en'),(17295,2694,'2002-06-16 04:34:31',147,'eclipse','<p>Fuzzy, such things happen sometimes that, when we look back, we blame ourselves for past incidences. I do not want to tell you what <I>I</I> think what it is that happened to you as a child. What is important is what <I>you</I> think now, about yourself. I can tell you that I understand on a very similar level, and, having told only one person in confidence - well heavily drunk - I have realized that my problems thus far seem to be because I blame myself. Which seem crazy - why should I blame myself, wasn\\\'t I innocent? </p>\r\n<p>We blame ourselves for so many things. We \\\"are capable of such beautiful dreams, and such horrible nightmares,\\\" that sometimes, we force ourselves to dream with worst. </p>\r\n<p>I cannot tell you that what you feel will go away. Perhaps I feel pessimistic about it. (I do believe that it will one day fade, I hope it will, lest we go on feeling traped in such occasions.) But I will tell you, that your friends - here or otherwise - will not judge you on such things the way you may have judged yourself. Do not feel you have to be truly alone on this. I know I hope that I am not.</p>\r\n',2,1132,70,0,'65.93.104.16','en'),(17296,2694,'2002-06-16 04:46:19',295,'karenlee','I disagree with you about this having been molestation. The Paul guy was only 15-17. He was learning about his sexual place in the world. Fuzzy just happened to be present for some of it. <b>Fuzzy:</b> these experiences are only damaging if you don\\\'t change your perspective on them. Change your perspective on them. \r\n\r\n\r\n',1,326,265,0,'203.194.10.210','en'),(17297,2838,'2002-06-16 05:26:38',64,'Joanna','One of my friends recently moved to Korea to teach English, and we all thought it would be really good for him, because he was fucked up and doing too many drugs and just so obviously needed a change of scene.  Now I\\\'m scared for him.  ',1,239,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(17298,2740,'2002-06-16 07:37:11',231,'alana','. rocksteady by aretha franklin <br>\r\n. song for sharon by joni mitchell <br>\r\n. this charming man by the smiths <br>\r\n. theme for a nude beach by the b52s <br>\r\n. lucystoners by amy ray <br>\r\n. dyslexic heart by paul westerberg <br>\r\n. anything by mark mothersbaugh <br>',3,271,0,0,'203.221.30.117','en'),(17299,2826,'2002-06-16 09:22:14',187,'kandrer','beatiful (fr beatitude, n m)',1,28,176,0,'80.13.233.26','en'),(17300,2832,'2002-06-16 09:29:25',187,'kandrer','Paul G, take care, OK?',1,22,0,0,'80.13.233.26','en'),(17301,2838,'2002-06-16 11:23:43',196,'jasonp','Well, chia it\\\'s like what they say, \\\"No matter where you go... there you are.\\\"<BR><BR>\nI certainly can relate to all that you\\\'re saying. Like I\\\'ve mentioned before I spent a couple of years in Japan teaching English right out of college. I went there mainly because of my ex-girlfriend who I met when she came to the US as a foreign exchange student.<BR>\nThat whole living in Japan experience was intense, too intense maybe. It took a long time to get all over it. It was largely equal parts positive, negative and just plain \\\'out there\\\'. Overall though it was good. I got a lot out of it.<BR>\nThere\\\'s probably some similarity between the two countries, but from what you\\\'ve described Seoul is kind of a dump. I was in Hiroshima which is a smaller sized (by Japan standards) fairly nice city. My whole time in Japan was totally entwined with my relationship. The good and best times were when I first came there just to visit. Those first trips were great. Later it was constant drama and arguing, then finally a break up after being there after a year. It was a real rollercoaster. I came over with no job when I first got there. The first few months were just living week to week. A lot of drama, a lot over-stimulation from the physical environment, excitement, joy, anxiety, and despair. Actually for a long time I missed that kind of stimulation. My life is infinitely more boring now and that is good/bad.<BR>\nAnyway, the culture over there is really into drinking, and I did my fair share. A lot of foreigners I saw drank a lot too, partly because of the culture that totally encourages it, partly because of the stress, and partly since it\\\'s the only social outlet for many.<BR>\nIt was a very strange existence there. The stress I had was not from work, but from the country itself and my relationship.  In between classes I\\\'d bide my time in a very mundane yet exotic kind of way. A day might be to teach class for an hour, an hour or more of travel on trains or a bus getting there, and then time wasted haning out in cafes, walking around aimlessly, hanging out in my tiny apartment, trying to decipher manga, studying japanese, etc.\nI realized that I\\\'ve always had kind of an \\\"outsider\\\" type feeling about life and here I was living the life of a \\\"gai-jin\\\" (outside person) foreigner.<BR>\nI got a lot out of it though. The best thing being there was the sense of adventure and freedom. The funny thing is that when I felt free I felt like drinking. Either setting out on a Friday night to hit all the gaijin (foreigner) bars, or just eating a meal and drinking some beer at the local izakaya restaurant/bar where I was the token foreigner who got plenty of attention. Total strangers would get drunk then spew out all their sordid personal troubles. It was amazing. That\\\'s a japanese thing to blow off steam and release tension when drunk, plus also my status as gaijin allowed and encouraged that too. Often I\\\'d stop by a local convenience store get a couple cups of sake and hang out by the river on a bench just contempolating. Even after a year that sense of \\\"wow, I\\\'m in Japan\\\" was still there.<BR>\nMaybe a big difference from korea is that in a lot of ways I found japan to be better or more advanced than the states in some ways. There\\\'s plenty that\\\'s not as good though also. Overall just *different*. That\\\'s the best way to put it. It\\\'s an amazing society and country actually. Being judgemental will spoil it for you. There were plenty of foreigners that hated japan and loved complaining. Then there a few that loved it and saw it as an adventure and special opportunity as a foreigner. I was more in the latter group. I found the culture and language and a lot of the everyday mundanity very interesting, so in that sense I was never bored. Miserable, yes, definitely. But not in a boring way.<BR>\nThe worst part was the sense of isolation and confinement from being around so many people, no car, etc. Also for me it really sucked because when I broke up finally it really made it ten times worse and it was pretty heavy.<BR>\nAnyway this comment is already too long. I\\\'ll save it for a real article and spare everyone.',0,4221,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(17302,2838,'2002-06-16 15:04:23',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\njasonp. thanks for the comment. it was nice. dont get me wrong. i think every minute of the day is an adventure, but adventures can get boring at points. i love korea. i love being here, i just find that there is SO much to look at that it just gets to me sometimes. seoul isnt a dump. its actually a pretty good city. the area i live in is a dump. it wont get better until they finally decide to bury the open sewers. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nim having a great time, and living with shane is a lot of fun. the guy is so funny and always has things to say. i dont know that id be having as good a time here if he wasnt here, because for hte most part im not a \\\'go out and explore\\\' type person. he\\\'s so much more open and up for confrontation with the various people he meets on the streets. if it werent for him, id have no social life, wouldnt have met the girl i like, and wouldnt know about all the different cool spots to hang out in. it would have taken me a lot longer to get out and do stuff, but thanks to him ive seen and done a lot. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nteaching is good and i think id do it again somewhere else. i dont know that ill stay in korea for more than a year, but right now who knows. once again, thanks for the comment. i appreciated it. \r\n</p>\r\n',1,1262,0,0,'61.37.53.38','en'),(17303,2835,'2002-06-16 15:06:34',93,'chiamattt','im perfect',0,10,0,0,'61.37.53.38','en'),(17304,2835,'2002-06-16 17:32:49',86,'egg','where you been.',0,15,90,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17305,1111,'2002-06-16 17:34:32',86,'egg','matt: I just scrolled down to make the same comment.<br><br>\r\nhorses horses horses horses\r\n',0,91,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17306,2837,'2002-06-16 19:12:02',124,'gus','also omitted are the scenes with a lot of laughing, the ones that involve a chase around the house, the ones where you try to watch tv over your partners shoulder, and the ones where you\\\'ve got 5 minutes, snarl a few times, and jump in the shower.',0,250,0,0,'64.172.56.241','en'),(17307,2835,'2002-06-16 21:41:40',75,'tyler','oh, settle down. it\\\'s just a joke.',1,37,0,0,'165.247.165.125','en'),(17308,2832,'2002-06-16 22:01:11',175,'Paul G','Tu t\\\'en vas?\r\nQu\\\'est-ce qui c\\\'est passe?\r\nAdieu mon ami et bonne chance.',0,81,0,0,'67.25.196.220','en'),(17309,2840,'2002-06-16 22:03:44',59,'s5','i have a similar relationship with a particular billie holiday album.',2,69,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17310,2832,'2002-06-16 22:19:35',66,'Peter','<br><tt><b>my lovely mo\'stache:</b><br><br><img src=\"http://www.rhodamine.org/images/mo-stache.jpg\"></tt>',1,108,82,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17311,2841,'2002-06-16 22:40:46',278,'egenus','Everywhere I look I see my peers wasting their potential because they can\\\'t decide how to apply it. Even if there is no way to solve our species\\\' problems, it is the journey, not the destination that makes a trip worthwhile.',3,230,0,0,'24.25.107.161','en'),(17312,2834,'2002-06-16 23:22:43',122,'hool','i read it from start to finish with no pauses.  it was the detail that interested me.  such fine detail of such a foreign place.  have you studied it much?  it was well written.  i tend to write long entries, so i make an effort to read the long entries posted by others.',0,271,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17313,1922,'2002-06-17 00:05:25',338,'shirtless guy','yellow number five yellow number five five five yellow number five yellow number five five five yellow number five yellow number five five five yellow number five yellow number five',1,181,0,0,'64.230.125.111','en'),(17314,1922,'2002-06-17 00:33:47',295,'karenlee','I hate having to yell out \\\"Back Door!!\\\" It always comes out wrong and the wrongness is magnified if <i>The Driver</i> doesn\\\'t hear you for 6 attempts. \r\n',1,162,0,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(17315,1922,'2002-06-17 00:55:15',66,'Peter','<br><tt>and its just dumb... at least here in nyc; the bus has a green light that lights in the back door <i>whenever</i> the bus is stopped. if you press thte button o nthe doors when this light is lit, the doors open; this concept is explained in countless informative sickers on the doors, in two languages and also in universal pictograms- this is quite possibly th easiest concept to understand- when green light is lit, press button to open doors- rear doors not operated by driver.<br><br>\r\nhowever, a zillion times, ive seen green-light bathed riders staring at the door and shoulting \\\"BACK DOOR!\\\"; i just dont get it. do these people have brains?</tt>',1,666,295,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17316,1922,'2002-06-17 01:02:36',295,'karenlee','Gosh! We have no such magical buttons here. Maybe we once did, but in recent times there were a few incidents of people getting dragged along by buses, so now <i>The Driver</i> is in full door control. \r\n\r\n',1,206,0,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(17317,1922,'2002-06-17 01:04:13',295,'karenlee','For a while after the unfortunate people got dragged by buses, the back doors were not even used!! \r\n',1,101,66,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(17318,2832,'2002-06-17 02:22:50',175,'Paul G','This is, indeed, greatly removed from the story - which really had nothing to do with moustaches at all - but, the best moustache belonged to the most beautiful man: Dali.  The pictures do not do him justice; I say this without any lust or jealousy.\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.the-artfile.com/gallery/artists/dali/dalienvaderT.jpg\\\">\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.the-artfile.com/gallery/artists/dali/facezijT.jpg\\\">\r\n',0,415,0,0,'67.24.117.49','en'),(17319,2832,'2002-06-17 02:43:22',295,'karenlee','Let\\\'s not forget <a href=\\\"http://www.vu.union.edu/~ciolinoa/\\\">Freda\\\'s</a> fine stache. \r\n\r\n\r\n',1,105,175,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(17320,2835,'2002-06-17 02:52:05',65,'le','this isn\\\'t porny enough to be the perfect swinney article',0,60,0,0,'208.239.147.211','en'),(17321,2832,'2002-06-17 04:15:49',66,'Peter','<br><tt>but we know the truest beauty of s.o comments is in the crazy tangents that they take :D</tt>',1,101,175,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17322,2832,'2002-06-17 04:17:21',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ms. kahlo also often sports a nice <a href=\\\"http://www.monobrow.com/\\\">monobrow</a>!</tt>',1,102,295,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17323,2835,'2002-06-17 05:38:57',175,'Paul G','<a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=935.txt\\\"><p>\\\"but once a community becomes more open and more visible, a crisis point is inevitably reached. members who identify themselves as \\\'the core\\\' will say things like, \\\'what happened?\\\', \\\'i don\\\'t like the site anymore, who are all these people?\\\', or \\\'these new people just don\\\'t get it.\\\'\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"the irony of \\\'getting it\\\' is that online communities rarely begin with a charter. rather, the group mind attracts and shapes the style and content of communications. so new members arrive, and attempt their best guess of what is needed/expected. often times, even well-intentioned contributions result in tedious flame wars.\\\"</p> </a>\r\n\r\n<p>At least two positive ways of effecting change come to mind: 1) Actually produce more writing, personally, that is \\\"appropriate\\\" and thereby change tone that exists here; 2) Find some simple, clear, direct way to communicate to people who you see struggling - perhaps not so well - to fit in.</p>',0,1058,0,0,'64.156.104.212','en'),(17324,2835,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','YOU ARE ALL DICKS',6,17,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17325,2835,'2002-06-17 06:36:49',59,'s5','or if you\\\'re like me, (3) start posting somewhere else. :)',0,61,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17326,2832,'2002-06-17 08:39:20',187,'kandrer','i\\\'m already gone in a way (from the US); but i might come again on swinney. except my life will be too busy for a while for that.<br>\r\ni meant: take care for your liver, from the fear of the empty bed, and from fighting this fear by inviting vampire women to share your dark and shiny blood.<br>\r\ni wish to see you around for much much longer...',1,348,175,0,'80.13.233.26','en'),(17327,2752,'2002-06-17 11:33:02',295,'karenlee','austin: I like the way it was a dream, but you were still worrying about getting in trouble. \r\n\r\n',1,97,0,0,'203.194.7.129','en'),(17328,2752,'2002-06-17 11:33:48',295,'karenlee','...for making a mess, that is.\r\n',1,32,0,0,'203.194.7.129','en'),(17329,2835,'2002-06-17 13:10:08',74,'jo','i was always fond of 4) wonder why you actually care about a fucking website.',1,77,0,0,'172.149.232.130','en'),(17330,2832,'2002-06-17 14:14:47',175,'Paul G','I understand that you spoke from a position of sympathy for me and lack of knowledge of any particular woman in my life.  However, without being rude, I hope, I would ask as a gentleman that whatever fault there is in my relationships be laid at my own feet, please.  Small words can sometimes cause great harm to those who deserve least to be hurt.  I do not want that to happen here and now.  I apologize if I have offended you.  But this is important to write for the sake of defending the honor and good character of someone else who should in no way be faulted.  My problems are the result of my own faults, and not anyone else\\\'s.  I think that you will understand.  Thank you again, Kandrer.  ',0,702,187,0,'67.25.195.13','en'),(17331,2842,'2002-06-17 14:16:34',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i grew up with a half-brother who my father had had in a previous marriage; upon my parent\\\'s divorce, my mom raised both my step-brother and i alone.<br><br>\r\nseveral hundred times, growing up, when people learned that my mom had an adopted son, they always assumed it was me due to the fact that her un-biologically-related son, my older half-brother, looked astonishingly like her, whereas i look exactly like my father. i always got an immense kick out of this, and it seemed to make my half-brother feel much more secure at times.</tt>',1,550,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17332,2842,'2002-06-17 14:36:38',295,'karenlee','My next oldest sister used to tell people she was adopted. I used to tell people I was astro boy. \r\n',1,100,66,0,'203.194.17.8','en'),(17333,2835,'2002-06-17 15:48:10',175,'Paul G','I don\\\'t mean to be rude, and I really don\\\'t have time for too much of this, but to, \\\"wonder why you actually care about a fucking website,\\\" is no different than wondering why anyone would care about books, or paintings, or music.\r\n\r\n<p>A website is a means by which ideas are shared.  Ideas are important.  The ability to share ideas freely is important.  Demonstrating some respect for the ideas that other people share is important too.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\\\'s a little act of bravery to sing a song where people can hear it, or to hang a painting where people can see it, or to write a story where people can read it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Let\\\'s be honest: It\\\'s a far easier thing to criticize what other people do than it is to do things yourself.  And the most negative way to define your group is to mock/humiliate/insult those who are different from you.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>A website is different than a book, or painting, or song in at least one respect: things on the web are linked together.  To deliberately connect things and then express shock when they are discovered - together - is not logical.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\\\'m sorry if this seemed hurtful - I don\\\'t intend it to be.  But I\\\'ve seen this cycle repeat here and in other places.  Oh well.</p> ',0,1254,0,0,'67.25.195.13','en'),(17334,2835,'2002-06-17 15:54:47',124,'gus','some of you guys take yourselves so seriously. you should stop that, you might get an ulcer.',0,92,0,0,'64.172.56.241','en'),(17335,2835,'2002-06-17 16:02:55',175,'Paul G','Agreed.<br>\r\n<i>*best smoochies*</i>',0,36,0,0,'67.25.195.13','en'),(17336,2835,'2002-06-17 17:38:17',74,'jo','i agree that a website can be a work of art, or at the very least self-expression, which is important. i never said that there weren\\\'t beautiful, inspiring, well-written, insightful pieces that are written on this website. i respect everyone\\\'s right to voice their opinions. i don\\\'t even have a bozo list, because i would rather read the thoughts of people who irritate me than not get the full range of opinions expressed.<BR>\r\ni don\\\'t think my comment necessitated that little lecture.',0,499,0,0,'172.154.39.40','en'),(17337,2835,'2002-06-17 18:38:40',175,'Paul G','I\\\'m sorry for any offense that I gave.<br>\r\nI tried to communicate in as positive a manner as possible.<br>\r\nThank you for sharing.<br>\r\nHave a good day.<br>\r\nPaul.</p>\r\n',0,173,74,0,'67.195.73.166','en'),(17338,2843,'2002-06-17 20:22:37',295,'karenlee','Dear shirtless-guy-who-would formulate-a-new-god-for-us : here is some <a href=\\\"http://www.stainblue.com/cubensis.html\\\">information</a> on which you might base some of your research. Please hurry up because we sinners are impatient for a new saviour. \r\n',1,259,0,0,'203.194.20.41','en'),(17339,2843,'2002-06-17 21:19:06',338,'shirtless guy','<p>PS .:. <b>God\\\'s thought process</b> - it\\\'s hard to believe that this phrase exists, and isn\\\'t yet the name of some dark ambient jazz entourage.</p>',3,159,0,0,'64.230.121.133','en'),(17340,2835,'2002-06-17 22:10:00',90,'leonard','i\\\'ve been taking the trash out.',1,34,86,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(17341,2835,'2002-06-17 22:10:43',90,'leonard','HAHA HAHAHA HAHAHAHAHAHA  HAHAH AHAHAH AHAHAHA HAHAHAHAHAH AHAHAHAHAHA. HA.',1,67,75,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(17342,2835,'2002-06-18 01:53:27',59,'s5','everything is so meta and ironic!!!!!!!',6,39,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17343,2837,'2002-06-18 04:04:48',298,'lurkinginsagas','i did leave those out.  i think i was going for the big picture.  furthermore, i don\\\'t know if the hair stuff would make for a good story, although i do have a few stories i could tell.',1,188,240,0,'207.218.214.184','en'),(17344,1678,'2002-06-18 04:09:22',298,'lurkinginsagas','doo doo doo doo doo doo\r\n(okay, you can\\\'t hear the tune in text)',1,67,0,0,'207.218.214.184','en'),(17345,1593,'2002-06-18 04:10:51',298,'lurkinginsagas','gabrielle is gorgeous, by the way.',1,34,0,0,'207.218.214.184','en'),(17346,1103,'2002-06-18 04:19:09',218,'da','hm, funny coda to this piece:\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.georgecarlin.com/georgecarlin/home/dontblame.html\\\">\r\ngeorge carlin didn\\\'t write it.\r\n</a>',1,149,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(17347,632,'2002-06-18 04:41:58',168,'laney','<a href=\\\"http://store.yahoo.com/sexualpositions/pilwitnos.html\\\">this one</a>. p.s. what\\\'s a russian officer?',0,117,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(17348,1216,'2002-06-18 04:44:23',298,'lurkinginsagas','\\\"there\\\'s a reason for everything.\\\"\r\n\\\"oh really?  then explain the holocaust to me.  i\\\'ve been wondering about that one for a while now.\\\"\r\n- dream for an insomniac',3,180,65,0,'207.218.214.184','en'),(17349,2843,'2002-06-18 06:21:38',223,'shanE','What you gonna do, brother, when Hulkamania runs wild on you?',4,61,0,0,'61.37.53.46','en'),(17350,2843,'2002-06-18 06:38:02',74,'jo','there\\\'s a book -- maybe someone here will know what i\\\'m talking about -- that suggests that every religious figure (but most notably jesus) was actually just this one immortal guy who lived throughout history and kept doing amazing stuff that people confused for \\\"miracles,\\\" kind of like monty python\\\'s \\\"what about brian?\\\" but a much more solid plot. and, well, not meant to be funny as much as meant to be scary as shit. i read it when i was very young (maybe 12?) and it totally shaped some of my thoughts. maybe it can help you?',1,552,338,0,'172.155.199.192','en'),(17351,2843,'2002-06-18 06:42:56',74,'jo','i went to see if i could find the book and had no luck, but i found <a href=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0785265678/\\\">what would jesus eat?</a> which made me laugh laugh laugh.',0,193,0,0,'172.155.199.192','en'),(17352,2843,'2002-06-18 06:55:56',59,'s5','have you ever read <a href=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0425074811/\\\">chariots of the gods?</a> it\\\'s about how much of the bible is actually an account of alien visitation to the earth. i read it when i was about 12, and it also shaped much of how i view ancient history.',2,290,74,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17353,2843,'2002-06-18 12:09:00',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\\\"If you truly want to follow Jesus in every area of your life, you cannot ignore your eating habits\\\"</i>, says that link.</tt> ',1,144,74,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17354,785,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','WEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEB',6,19,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17355,2843,'2002-06-18 12:30:52',295,'karenlee','The 16 bucks for that book is worth it just for the recipe where Jeez turns the water into grog. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',1,105,74,0,'203.194.7.218','en'),(17356,2212,'2002-06-18 14:02:21',295,'commuter','It was a shameless plug that brought this article to my attention. It is unfortunate that at this moment I cannot utilise the information contained within it. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',2,169,0,0,'203.194.4.64','en'),(17357,2843,'2002-06-18 14:09:14',338,'shirtless guy','funny ... dearly departed terence mckenna wrote a book called <a href=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0553371304/qid=1024408850/sr=2-1/ref=sr_2_1/002-2694072-9034454\\\"><i>food of the gods</i></a> .... can you guess what it was about? i\\\'m definitely interested in the book that you mentioned earlier though, if you uncover anything more in the future.',1,366,74,0,'64.230.121.133','en'),(17358,2846,'2002-06-18 14:33:34',338,'shirtless guy','like at the intermission for a david lynch stage-play; like she kissed me quickly then ran inside; like anyone who saw \\\'fellowship of the ring\\\' last december: i\\\'m waiting anxiously but patiently for <i>partie deux</i>.',1,227,0,0,'64.230.121.133','en'),(17359,2832,'2002-06-18 15:17:03',187,'kandrer','gasp! no no no, Paul, i didn\\\'t speak about any woman in particular. i\\\'m sorry if i made you think i did. you tagged your last posts with \\\"non-fiction\\\", so i supposed that at least some of your words were close to your feelings of these days. but i know nothing and noone in your private life, and i didn\\\'t want to talk about your private life either.<br>\r\nmy comment was rather based on personnal memories; how i sometimes ended up with people just to avoid to be alone. the vampire/blood image was just for some theatrical fun.<br>\r\nby the way, i hope that i was (finally) clearer in this comment, and that you understand you own my complete respect.',1,666,175,0,'80.14.219.20','en'),(17360,2846,'2002-06-18 16:49:14',86,'egg','Before I went to bed I read about <a href=\\\"http://photoarts.com/gallery/harper/intro.html\\\">sharon harper\\\'s pictures</a>, some text referring to the \\\"smudge\\\" of memory. and now i wake up to the \\\"smoosh\\\" of creation. It feels ok to wake up to the beginning of the world. ',0,290,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17361,2843,'2002-06-18 17:54:24',74,'jo','steve -- no. i\\\'ll wishlist it, tho.<BR>\r\nshirtless guy -- maybe my mom will remember what it was called, cause she read it, too. i searched amazon for about 1/2 hour last night with all different strings and couldn\\\'t find it.',1,231,0,0,'172.170.180.142','en'),(17362,2832,'2002-06-18 18:51:21',175,'Paul G','Thank you, K.<br>\r\nI respect you too.<br>\r\nYou are a gentleman.<br>\r\nAgain: <i>Bonne chance, mon ami.</i>',0,105,187,0,'67.25.199.141','en'),(17363,2435,'2002-06-18 19:02:05',17,'austin','<P>i have new jeans on.  they are blue levis 501s.  i dont really like blue  \r\njeans.  i dont really like these blue jeans.  i dont like levis.\r\n</P><P>\r\nWHY THEN did i buy these blue jeans?\r\n</P><P>\r\ni bought them because oof threw a fit the other day because i always wear the same pair of black wranglers.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni would have bought some more black wranglers, but i dont really shop.  i go and i buy.  i went to sears.  they didnt have wranglers.  what they did have other than levis were not my size, and i didnt like them.\r\n</P><P>\r\nso now i have blue levis that are my size and i dont like them.  i wish i had bought black ones because these blue ones faded immediately.  i only bought them because the dark denim blue with the reddish orange stitching was acceptable.  now that is gone and i feel like it is the first day of grade school and little austin has some new little jeans on.\r\n</P><P>\r\nburn! burn!\r\n</P>',6,924,0,0,'205.179.19.139','en'),(17364,2435,'2002-06-18 19:37:11',82,'simon','i am wearing some hip pair of deisel jeans that my roommate got me for free so i couldn\\\'t pass them up. i guess they are my size but they seem weird and not really pleasant. i want my old levi\\\'s back! they have disintegrated, pretty much.<br><br>\r\nalso, burn burn!',0,270,17,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17365,2834,'2002-06-18 19:45:08',333,'just-sade','i studied the scribes of eygpt for a while, and it interested me how much stress and pressure was put upon the little boys from the age of 6, it tended to remind me of, for some reason, the old english school system. thank you',1,226,0,0,'152.163.207.82','en'),(17366,2435,'2002-06-18 19:53:09',86,'egg','<p>I have a pair of sparkley snake-green plastic pants-jeans that I had hemmed with duct tape. *SOMEBODY* put them in the dryer and now i have gnarled hemlines. </p>',0,165,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17367,2435,'2002-06-18 20:04:26',17,'austin','obviously that *somebody* was not you.',4,38,86,0,'205.179.19.139','en'),(17368,2435,'2002-06-18 20:12:22',86,'egg','YOU',0,3,17,0,'64.168.21.40','en'),(17369,2846,'2002-06-19 02:30:25',69,'starlajo','O: Nga mihi aroha ki a koe.\r\n\r\n\r\n:)',1,35,0,0,'210.54.242.102','en'),(17370,2435,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i can\'t stop wearing this one damn pair of orange dickies. they\'re really comfy. they\'re the only pair of pants i have that i can kneel in the dirt in, and after a full meal. they\'ve been so dirty they\'re a dirty mango color instead of safety orange. i really just need another pair in a more camoflaugey color so people won\'t know i wear the same damn pair of pants everyday. damn. send help!!! also, my trash really stinks. whew.',1,398,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(17371,785,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i\\\'ll prolly have had a zillion jobs by the time i\\\'m done with this life. maybe this is my calling. some people are artists, some are poets, i am the chronically changing career girl. hmm. \r\n<br><br>\r\nOOO-ooo-OOOOOooooOOOOOOOOlivia!@! (screamed to the tune of \\\"Staying Alive\\\" hehehe)',1,294,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(17372,2848,'2002-06-19 07:31:51',122,'hool','<i>Naturally, this means that at this momement in Idaho there is a pig farmer wishing he could be a programmer in Texas. <i>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nthat\\\'s a classic line.  \\\"i laughed out loud\\\" - hool.\r\n\r\nas for what to do - be creative.  not fluffy-happy-daily-affirmation creative, but creative.  make something that you can lose yourself in even if it sucks as a finished project.  then do it again.  i was in a similar situation, and that\\\'s when i started really producing things, and i haven\\\'t stopped since.  it shifted the balance.  \r\n\r\ngetting out of high tech for a while will help too, after you\\\'ve paid off your debts and can coast for a while, even for a few months.  that\\\'s what i\\\'m doing right now, and i\\\'m fucking loving it.\r\n\r\nto talk, feel free to <a href=\\\"mailto:inconduit@inconduit.com\\\">email me.</a> ',0,838,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17373,2848,'2002-06-19 07:33:49',122,'hool','fuck.  </i>.  can someone fix that one the quote and then get rid of this message?  thanks',0,90,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17374,2848,'2002-06-19 07:36:52',122,'hool','fuck, again. see, in that last comment, i tried to say that i forgot a closing italics tag in the first message.  but it got swallowed up by the parser.  so now i have to write a third comment because i\\\'m stupid.  that second one didn\\\'t really seem to make much sense anyway.  i blame it on the pipe.  and i bailed on my bike today at an intersection.  but it was fun, and i was glad that there were witnesses.  i always laugh my ass off when i see people wipe out in the city, so i can take my lumps.  ',0,509,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17375,2848,'2002-06-19 08:17:57',20,'jim','I was going to fix this, but now it\\\'s too amusing.\r\n\r\n',6,57,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(17376,2848,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','poor orion.',6,11,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17377,2848,'2002-06-19 08:38:09',59,'s5','orion, it\\\'s time to get a cat or a dog.',2,42,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17378,2848,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','you need to sign her up to <a href=\\\"http://www.heartless-bitches.com/\\\">heartless bitches international</a>',6,112,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17379,2848,'2002-06-19 10:56:25',120,'joyrider','damn, orion, that\\\'s rough.  after my girl and i ended it, i was pretty much adrift. i picked up everything in my life and examined and re-examined.  realized why things went bad between us, and finally realized why it was going to be a good split after all.  tech hasn\\\'t treated me too kindly, and i think i\\\'m ready to get out of it now.  maybe you should do the same - like hool said.  good luck with it.',2,414,0,0,'12.224.184.3','en'),(17380,2846,'2002-06-19 11:16:40',120,'joyrider','nothing beats recreating the universe.',2,38,0,0,'12.224.184.3','en'),(17381,2842,'2002-06-19 15:17:49',180,'auntie','pets are the children of the new order.  taking on the responsibility of caring for a dog, particularly, it much like adopting a child, for they are totally dependent on their owner in ways that cats are not.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni have two cats, molly and sophie. for a short time, they were in florida and i was in texas.  until they arrived, it didn\\\'t feel much like home.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni used to have a cat named clara tomato kitty the speedy wonder cat. le found her by a dumpster in germany during an ice storm.  mark and i loved that cat with a fierceness it is hard to describe.  she ruled our home.  she had dreadful runaway adventures that required sleepless nights and long trips to save her from herself.\r\n<br><br>\r\nthe same week i had my liver biopsy and my gallbladder removed and mark left for texas to take the new job, clara went missing.  she never came home again.  i grieve for her, two years later, and i am sure i will do so forever.\r\n<br><br>\r\nhaving said that, a cat is not a child, nor is a dog.  a child is a child, whether they are the spawn of your loins or the product of endless paperwork required by adoption or custody by divorce.\r\n<br><br>\r\nmotherhood (or fatherhood) is unexplainable to anyone who has not experienced it.  the bonds go beyond childhood, beyond time.\r\n<br><br>\r\nbecoming a parent, both times, was absolutely amazingly brilliant of me. but not nearly so brilliant as the children i bore.\r\n<br><br>\r\nlove your pet, but, if you have a chance to share a child with another person, you will come back to this piece and be amazed at yourself.\r\n<br><br>\r\nbest friendly!',1,1602,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(17382,2842,'2002-06-19 15:56:13',295,'karenlee','<i>...if you have a chance to share a child with another person...</i>\r\n<p>I do understand your point of view.... I have 5 siblings and 27 nieces and nephews and I see a whole lotta love (yes i am the youngest!!). Thanks very much for your comment. :)</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n',1,261,180,0,'203.194.16.42','en'),(17383,2848,'2002-06-19 17:07:55',17,'austin','<P>you should join the army concentrating on liberating lulu\\\'s dog salami from her oppressive clutches.</P>\r\n\r\n',3,114,0,0,'205.179.19.139','en'),(17384,2842,'2002-06-19 17:09:48',265,'chuckspeed','two thoughts spring to mind...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>first, you need a license to drive a car/own a gun/go fishing, but any damned fool with functional reproductive organs is allowed to mate.  problem is, the intelligent folk of the earth now often choose to parent non-humans, or nothing at all.  This leaves the denizens of Jerry Springer to populate the earth - somewhere in a mass of paperback sci-fi is a short story entitled \\\'The Marching Morons\\\' which sums up our current predicament rather nicely.  The stupid shall indeed inherit the earth.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Second, cats are allowed in our house ONLY if the children accept FULL responsibility for their care.  The reality check is that a child may care for a cat and the cat will thrive - this is not the case when a child cares for a child. Chevrolet (our youngest cat) is pretty self sufficient, but Boo (our youngest child) suffers when left in the grips of her elder sister Eesh.   ',0,933,295,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(17385,2848,'2002-06-19 17:52:04',246,'erb','awwww.  breakups suck and they are also LIBERATING!!!!  find a cute girl on the internet and get her to go on dates with you.  i think dates are the best cure for the breakup blues.  you get to see yourself in a different light.  also, working hard.  for money, a better body, enlightenment, self-improvement.  read some great books.  schopenhauers \\\"world as will and idea\\\" is fun.  anything philosophical.  when my heart is broken open, i seem to be a lot better at digesting new ideas.  good luck.   ',1,508,0,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(17386,2848,'2002-06-19 18:47:10',74,'jo','i\\\'m always a promoter of <i>post-breakup body modification</i>. dramatically cut your hair. pierce your eyebrow. or tongue. something that makes you feel like you\\\'re symbolically shedding your old skin. even if it\\\'s just a silly little adornment.<BR>\r\ni got my nose pierced after my last bad break-up, particularly helpful for grieving because he thought that nose rings were Really hot. so every time i looked in the mirror, i had a little reminder.',1,459,0,0,'172.165.22.178','en'),(17387,2848,'2002-06-19 18:53:45',17,'austin','he should do well with those new glistening pecks and abs of steel, fresh from the gym.',2,87,246,0,'205.179.19.139','en'),(17388,2841,'2002-06-19 19:41:33',75,'tyler','well...there\\\'s only one way off the bus.',1,43,278,0,'165.247.165.125','en'),(17389,2842,'2002-06-19 20:36:16',295,'karenlee','I am not keen to bring more people into the tv studio either. However, I am happy for people to just do whatever they like. Sometimes though, it just seems really hard to get acknowledgement for my non-reproductive perspective. \r\n\r\n',1,232,265,0,'203.194.6.58','en'),(17390,2847,'2002-06-20 00:26:48',338,'shirtless guy','but life is a stage, right? it\\\'s all about acting in the different roles, experiencing the different emotions, and getting the best seat when a genius performer is on. and continuous comedy would suck, wouldn\\\'t it?<br>\r\npersonally, i\\\'m a fan of tragedy. at least, that\\\'s my excuse.',3,293,0,0,'64.230.121.133','en'),(17391,2846,'2002-06-20 00:46:38',234,'etienne','i love maori mythology.  awww yeah.',1,35,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(17392,2749,'2002-06-20 00:58:21',295,'karenlee','In my book there will always be a page for velcro. I have never heard of the kangaroo shoes though. Where was I?\r\n\r\n',1,116,0,0,'203.221.9.166','en'),(17393,2848,'2002-06-20 01:30:27',82,'simon','swine-y, who is oppressing salami?',0,34,17,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17394,2849,'2002-06-20 11:37:38',75,'tyler','ummm...it\\\'s <i>odyssey</i>.\r\n<p>\r\nbut now you can say for sure that you learned something!',1,93,0,0,'165.247.165.125','en'),(17395,2849,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','hahahahaahhahah ahahahahahahah ahahahahahahah ahahahahahahaha hahahahahha',6,69,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17396,2848,'2002-06-20 12:38:46',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ive been whiling away the past year or so since i went thorugh my breakup; a dog in a hat would have definitely shortened this to at least a month or two! :D</tt>',1,170,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17397,2849,'2002-06-20 12:40:41',66,'Peter','<br><tt>hay, us s.o edaters be a hard werking bunch, fer shure!</tt>',1,59,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17398,2847,'2002-06-20 12:42:38',66,'Peter','<br><tt>continuous tragedy is as bad as continuous comedy, for sure...</tt>',1,74,338,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17399,2669,'2002-06-20 14:04:17',338,'shirtless guy','you\\\'re arguing that since you (and I and everyone else in the west) do so many bad things that you shouldn\\\'t have to feel guilty for all of them. I don\\\'t really follow the logic.\r\n\r\nin my opinion, if you want to eat meat and drink milk, if you want to wear gap t-shirts and nike sneaks, if you want to fire cannisters of tear gas on a group of open-minded kids, do so by dealing with the consequences. if it\\\'s a bad thing, i don\\\'t see why people shouldn\\\'t feel bad for doing it.',5,496,129,0,'64.230.121.133','en'),(17400,2669,'2002-06-20 14:10:09',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i think moe\'s point was that its tedious and dumb to let universal guilt influence you; as in, changing your living patterns based on guilt that you have nothing to do with... quite annoying; people often use this much more passive sort of thing as an alternative to actually taking action or enacting some truly useful change. like \"if i stop drinking coke, ill be doing my part to end apartheid!\"... yeah right.</tt>',1,191,338,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17401,2849,'2002-06-20 18:35:22',17,'austin','h8r',4,3,62,0,'205.179.19.139','en'),(17402,2849,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','u no it bizzach',6,15,17,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17403,2848,'2002-06-20 23:58:57',186,'ook','<p>i\\\'m with hool. when not making a relationship, a good way to get to know yourself again is to create a project of some kind. not a purging or self-defining project necessarily, just like... a large craft. i usually have my best inspirations when i am wallowing with myself anyway.</p>',0,290,0,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(17404,2851,'2002-06-21 00:27:13',82,'simon','thing one: \\\" a fourty in one sweaty palm and my ass in another\\\"<br>\r\nthing two: were you perchance in the mission, dining beside THE old red man?<br>\r\nthing three: ..it happened so naturally, didn\\\'t i know it was love. the next thing i felt was you, holding me close, what was i gonna do, i let myself go. now we fly through the stars, i hope this night will last forever, oh oh oh OH, ain\\\'t nobody does me better than you, makes me happy, makes me feel this way, ain\\\'t nobody....',1,495,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17405,2851,'2002-06-21 00:30:18',82,'simon','...i\\\'ve been waiting for you, it\\\'s been so long. i knew just what i would do when i heard your song...filled my heart with your bliss, gave me freedom you knew i could not resist, i needed someone...',0,205,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17406,2851,'2002-06-21 00:56:50',86,'egg','i am obsessed. ',0,15,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17407,2852,'2002-06-21 02:11:58',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\\\"songs segue into each other cleanly, barely broken even by introduction of the next\\\"</i>...<br><br>i really love how music can do that.</tt>',1,158,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17408,1912,'2002-06-21 13:43:12',295,'karenlee','oh hello, I was hsamelessly plugged here, but as it utrns out, today someone sent me an article from a small town in tasmania, wher eexactly the same thing is goign on. no-one wants the halfway houses. (is that what is going on here?) \r\n',1,237,66,0,'203.194.20.64','en'),(17409,2843,'2002-06-22 02:39:21',124,'gus','for me, a more likely hypothosis is not that mushrooms or some other plant based thing is God incarnate, but that these substances open an immediate pathway of communication between our little minds and the mind of God, that would otherwise take years of meditation or study or blahblah to achieve.. or something like that. <p>\r\nIm not saying I believe that. but I really dont go for say \\\"word made mushroom\\\" so much. it just doesnt make any sense... unless you\\\'re high. ',0,480,0,0,'64.172.56.181','en'),(17410,2843,'2002-06-22 06:31:07',66,'Peter','<br><tt>it makes perfect sense if youve ever <i>been</i> high.</tt>',1,67,124,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17411,1559,'2002-06-22 07:05:01',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>...months later</i>...<br><br>i think this is one of my favourite swintries.</tt>',1,92,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17412,2843,'2002-06-22 16:11:09',338,'shirtless guy','*raises glass to Peter (as usual)\r\n<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\r\nI had a tough time last night explaining the mushroom experience to an inexperienced friend. We were playing guitar, and I tried to exemplify the different way of looking at things: \\\"every musical note, every chord and progression carries an interesting meaning. when we don\\\'t know how to make a guitar say what we want it to say, we stumble around and accidentally hit stories that we don\\\'t realise we\\\'re playing.\\\" He looked at me like one would look at a homeless guy telling a bullshit story. \\\"Oh yeah? I guess that\\\'s interesting.\\\" Guess I just can\\\'t find the right words to convey the nature of the experience.\r\n<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\r\nThough I find myself stumbling over sentences while under the influence, I find that the English language (and its intense, seemingly random grammar & punctuation rules) seem to make much more sense. It was either Leary or McKenna that put forth the notion of language as a lucid conept, which seems perfectly plausible to me. Wouldn\\\'t you agree that most of the people around us would probably prefer to communicate through grunts or barks?',1,1182,0,0,'64.230.122.244','en'),(17413,2843,'2002-06-22 17:30:12',124,'gus','Maybe I had too many theories before I ever got high. I dont know. Maybe Im resistant because I think if you really want to meet God(s) you can do it sober. I still like my theory better... Maybe it\'s because mushrooms make me barf. (Though, I have to say, last time I puked - in the gutter on the corner of haight and divis - I DID feel like I was expelling the devil from my being). ahem. Anyway, what Im saying is, I understand, I dont have any trouble seeing where this is coming from, I\'m just saying that my ideas on this form of the infinate differ.\r\n<p>\r\n<b>to shirtless guy</b>: I would definitely pick language over grunts and barks. I wouldnt give up the pleasure of forming intricate strands of words to convey meaning, and to subtly overlay and include subtext.. Its like magic. However, I might pick movement(dance) and color over language if I really had to choose some favorite and effective non-word form of communication. Luckily I dont.',0,902,0,0,'64.172.56.181','en'),(17414,2843,'2002-06-22 18:11:18',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i think music is a good language for such states. i will save you my digression about the wonder of electronic pulses and midified analog grunts now, however, heh.</tt>',1,176,124,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17415,2843,'2002-06-22 18:46:07',124,'gus','hee. ok. and I\\\'ll spare you my whole longwinded diatribe on dance/movement in modern times vs primitive cultures and why I think it currently revolves around the use of intoxicants. ;)',0,187,66,0,'64.172.56.181','en'),(17416,2854,'2002-06-22 18:48:31',124,'gus','I like this.. but I am itching to edit it and remove the little unnecessary words. heh. Im a freak.',0,99,0,0,'64.172.56.181','en'),(17417,1559,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<br><img src=\"http://www.weeb.com/pics/souldujour.jpg\">',0,88,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(17418,2843,'2002-06-22 20:25:22',66,'Peter','<br><tt>um, it <i>does</i> revolve around the use of key substances, for sure. or it at least caters to such use, at best. actually, id be very interested to hear your diatribe :D</tt>',1,184,124,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17419,2843,'2002-06-22 21:26:45',124,'gus','Yah, it does, my diatribe is about WHY it does and why it has to. I dont think dance scenes cater to drug use, I think there are some reasons why intoxicant use is necessary (for some) for dance type movement to occurr in the first place in modern times. \r\n<p>\r\nMaybe I\\\'ll edit my old diatribe, and put it up for ya! ',0,320,0,0,'64.172.56.181','en'),(17420,2858,'2002-06-22 23:38:56',65,'le','three nights ago, i had a dream that a man from the dominican republic worked in an apartment building that i used to live in. i was visiting the building in my dream, after moving out. the man from the dominican republic showed me two cards, one was the eight of spades, one had some writing on it i cannot remember. the cards were supposed to somehow explain or reflect why i broke up with a boyfriend, i don\\\'t know what boyfriend. <br><br>there\\\'s a magician here at omega this week, teaching a workshop. his name is jeff mcbride. yesterday as we were setting up a room for him, he did magic tricks for us. at one point, he handed me and my friend chris each a card. chris\\\'s card, the eight of diamonds, my card, the ace of spades.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni have no idea what\\\'s going on. it\\\'s like i\\\'m a shoddy psychic over here. \r\n<br><br> \r\nyour super should have blown out the candle the other day.\r\n ',4,911,0,0,'208.239.147.211','en'),(17421,2858,'2002-06-22 23:50:49',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yes, he could have done quite a number on it with his hose via our open front window, as he has twice this week already! but, viva clean kitchen floors.</tt>',1,165,65,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17422,2843,'2002-06-23 01:14:33',70,'Fuzzy','I think that most people require intoxication in order to get around insecurities when it comes to dancing.  I know when I stopped doing E it took me longer to actually get into a <b>right</b> mood to dance but I would still dance for six hours solid, only stopping to refill my water bottle.  It was still as good, if not better, without the drugs.<br><br>Personally, I haven\\\'t done a crazy night of dancing \\\'till it hurts in a long time.  I miss it and want to have it again.  But I still know that self-consciousness plays a big part for me  and whether I\\\'m going to be dancing or not.  It\\\'s a shame, really.',1,623,0,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(17423,2853,'2002-06-23 01:55:37',122,'hool','note: mouseover the words',0,25,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17424,2853,'2002-06-23 01:58:20',66,'Peter','<br><tt>the touch, the feel of minimal</tt>',1,43,122,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17425,2858,'2002-06-23 03:06:55',59,'s5','a fine story.',1,13,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17426,2853,'2002-06-23 03:30:37',17,'austin','heh',1,3,66,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(17427,1188,'2002-06-23 11:21:21',261,'sine','\r\ni loved that man so much. he\\\'s gone.',0,41,0,0,'216.102.198.168','en'),(17428,2862,'2002-06-23 18:29:40',323,'outlandishjosh','Well done! Quite eviscerating in its content postmodernism. Not the angsty \\\"look at me, I\\\'m tortured and sensitive,\\\" cry of the emotionally stunted attention junkie, but the simple meditation on getting by. Enjoyed it.',1,227,0,0,'216.165.3.117','en'),(17429,2862,'2002-06-23 18:41:14',66,'Peter','<br><tt>uh oh, you said <i>postmodernism</i>...</tt>',1,52,323,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17430,2861,'2002-06-23 18:43:20',66,'Peter','<br><tt>amen...</tt>',1,20,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17431,2858,'2002-06-23 18:44:26',323,'outlandishjosh','<p>Bully for the tales of real people! Huzzah! More of this, please!</p>',1,72,0,0,'216.165.3.117','en'),(17432,2859,'2002-06-23 20:01:06',338,'shirtless guy','This is the first description of such a state -- something that we probably all hit, to some degree -- that hasn\\\'t been riddled with blame. You know? \\\"Because of my parents,\\\" ... \\\"In this Communist state,\\\" ... \\\"If I had it <i>my</i> way,\\\" ....\r\n<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\r\nYour beautifully phrased view (because \\\'beautiful\\\' doesn\\\'t necessarily mean \\\'happy\\\') I think parallels my own, prior to running away to University. Which was a silly thing to do -- all I\\\'ve done up to this point is skip class and get introduced to drugs. Which is no ladder out of the downward spiral, I\\\'m sure you\\\'ll agree. But on the other hand, cautious, moderate, knowledgeful use of certain intoxicants has led to some irreplaceable insight. Recently, my good friend Psilocybin influenced me to write this fragment of a poem:\r\n<br><br>\r\n<font face=\\\"courier new\\\" size=-1>\r\nloneliness leads to depression<br>\r\ndepression leads to abuse<br><br>\r\ni miss you<br>\r\ni miss someone, that is<br>\r\nbut i\\\'m not sure we\\\'ve been properly introduced</font>',1,1079,0,0,'64.230.122.244','en'),(17433,2859,'2002-06-23 20:10:36',338,'shirtless guy','Oops, wait -- I\\\'m not advocating drug use. Obviously, there must be a reason that Schedule I substances are so heavily restricted, right? I was originally trying to say that I put the blame on my state of mind, before I learned that I could change that state of mind with certain intoxicants. I then put all the weight on drugs, making them responsible for my wellbeing. Which didn\\\'t help a fucking thing.\r\n<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\r\nTo shorten the story, it\\\'s a bad idea to put blame on anything. And I just want to applaud <b>blue</b> for not blaming. We\\\'re all responsible for our own states of mind, right? And as long as we know that ultimately we are in control -- we decide why to participate in what, when to administer what, who we interact with and how we interact with them -- then we\\\'ve won.\r\n<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\r\nI\\\'m not sure what the point of this life is, why this reality exists, but I\\\'m sure as shit not going to be its victim.',1,977,0,0,'64.230.122.244','en'),(17434,2862,'2002-06-23 20:19:44',295,'karenlee','The feeling i had when I scribbled this into a little book was certainly not tortured. I felt lucky that I could get thrills out of little things to get by while my friend had to take a concoction of mind stabilising drugs to get by. I was feeling lucky. The pills in this picture are actually paracetamol that I coloured in with a texta. heh. \r\n\r\n',1,348,323,0,'203.194.7.53','en'),(17435,2859,'2002-06-23 23:27:07',124,'gus','love wont come flying in through the window and wash your face and pull off your food stained jammies and pull a fresh tshirt over your head and sit on your lap and tell you you\\\'re beautiful.\r\n<p>\r\nIf you dont want to change, you wont. If you do want to change, you will.\r\n<p>\r\nI used to sit around feeling sorry for myself about this love stuff. First other people got sick of my whining, then I got sick of my whining. \r\n<p>\r\nFunny, when I stopped being such a pain in the ass and whining and feeling sorry for myself, I found a lot more love out there was willing to come inside, dirty jammies or not.\r\n<p>\r\n<i>destabilizing</i>',4,634,0,0,'64.172.56.181','en'),(17436,2859,'2002-06-24 00:13:23',66,'Peter','<br><tt>on a lighter note, your comment made me think of <a href=\\\"http://www.brunching.com/features/morepathetic.html\\\">this</a>.</tt>',1,139,124,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17437,2861,'2002-06-24 06:21:43',86,'egg','hi sine. I have often admired/identified with your submissions but never have a specific comment. ',0,98,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17438,2863,'2002-06-24 16:39:44',338,'shirtless guy','What\\\'s up with submissions not making it to the \\\"Recent\\\" list on the front page? Is there a standard that I\\\'m just not hitting?',4,139,0,1,'64.230.122.244','en'),(17439,2835,'2002-06-24 17:19:20',75,'tyler','ahhh.',1,5,0,0,'165.247.165.70','en'),(17440,2863,'2002-06-24 18:45:52',346,'eno','So, you know what\\\'s wrong with the system. The challenge is knowing what to replace it with... any ideas?',2,108,0,1,'64.229.193.228','en'),(17441,2861,'2002-06-25 00:40:06',17,'austin','this nature oriented piece of yours, sine, reminds me of the time we watched the humming birds play above the creek fall in the japanese garden.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni need to find these magical tide pools you are talking about.  egg and i tried to find the sand bridges once but didnt see them.',1,285,0,0,'205.179.19.150','en'),(17442,2861,'2002-06-25 04:12:51',240,'xep','tide pools and natural bridges: do you know where west cliff drive is?  that is\r\nthe road you need to find.  and you need\r\nto find the north most end of it where it intersects with swanton boulevard. there you will find the entrance to natural bridges state park.  there is actually only one bridge now.  one of them fell down.  it should be natural bridge state park or lone natural bridge state park or maybe the solitary natural bridge state park.  park on swanton blvd so you don\\\'t have to pay to use the parking lot.  then walk down the stairs or the cliff trail to the beach.  on the beach you will be able to see the natural bridge.  walk across the beach to where there is a sort of shelf of rock above the beach, sticking out into the ocean.  climb onto this shelf.  walk along it and there you will find magical tide pools.',0,836,17,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(17443,2861,'2002-06-25 05:38:36',122,'hool','i think it\\\'s cool that you carved yourself your own pen.  i wish i could carve myself a new digital camera.  write a lot of stuff and post it here.',0,150,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17444,2853,'2002-06-25 06:11:54',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\ndoesnt work here in korea. i think they banned mouseovers here because the north has been using mouseovers for years. ',2,123,0,0,'211.32.132.223','en'),(17445,2856,'2002-06-25 06:13:47',93,'chiamattt','the reason you dont know anything about toronto night life is you dont try to know anything about toronto night life. god im in a bad mood. sorry.',0,146,0,0,'211.32.132.223','en'),(17446,2861,'2002-06-25 06:27:21',124,'gus','my grandparents used to live in a mobile home park that was just above natural bridges. they never had to pay for parking, and neither did we when we went to visit.\r\n<p>\r\nthere were a lot of geese near there, and they poop\\\'d a lot.',0,234,0,0,'64.172.56.181','en'),(17447,2861,'2002-06-25 07:53:32',261,'sine','\r\nwestern drive yes...down to natural bridges state park. right down the hill from dvb\\\'s house. when you get to the beach, head to the right, across some tidepools there\\\'s a little inlet. above this inlet is where the retirement home is that gus spoke of i think? you will see there on the right, next to a big log, the stand of bamboo i wrote about. if you go up to the ridge on the right, there\\\'s a little path up to nowhere and a wooden sign. maybe you can see something written on that sign? I wonder who put that there!?',1,534,0,0,'216.102.198.168','en'),(17448,2862,'2002-06-25 09:41:10',187,'kandrer','nice :)',1,7,0,0,'80.15.167.43','en'),(17449,2859,'2002-06-25 11:34:27',75,'tyler','how do you know it\\\'s the first?',1,34,338,0,'165.247.165.70','en'),(17450,2856,'2002-06-25 15:45:59',176,'Scapegoat','To be honest complete lack of knowledge of Toronto nightlife was an exaggeration I suppose. I have been out to the Bovine, Lee\\\'s, Green Room, and even hit up the Matador afterhours. Just compared to my knowledge of what was going on in Ottawa it feels like I know nothing about what is happening here.',0,304,93,0,'204.19.79.66','en'),(17451,1104,'2002-06-25 23:04:31',17,'austin','what a bunch of crap!',4,21,0,0,'205.179.19.150','en'),(17452,2864,'2002-06-25 23:20:01',74,'jo','have you read rick moody\\\'s <a href=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0316578991/\\\">the black veil</a>? also, can i ask if you medicate? if you really spend your life consumed with senseless fear and paranoia, you are probably chemically imbalanced and there could be some nice drug with your name on it that would help these thoughts out a <b>lot</a>.',1,365,0,0,'172.170.83.253','en'),(17453,2864,'2002-06-26 00:19:07',75,'tyler','might i suggest the album <i>Everyday</i> by Widespread Panic?',1,62,0,0,'165.247.165.70','en'),(17454,2864,'2002-06-26 00:41:10',124,'gus','I fear polka dots, which are similar to jelly beans in a way. interesting.',0,74,0,0,'64.172.56.181','en'),(17455,2865,'2002-06-26 02:38:47',59,'s5','well if you begin from the premise that all sentient beings are equally free to live without suffering, then this is not such a good job. or, if you believe that humans have special rights in this area, then you might feel better about what you\\\'re doing. regardless, the unshakable sensation you feel when slicing rats open might be trying to tell you something ...',5,368,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17456,2865,'2002-06-26 02:42:32',59,'s5','ps, it\\\'s interesting that you use the word \\\"sacrifice\\\", as this is the jargon that animal experimenters use instead of \\\"kill\\\" or \\\"euthanize\\\". i\\\'m vegan so you know where i stand, but it\\\'s interesting how often word choice reflects people\\\'s outlook.',4,278,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17457,2866,'2002-06-26 03:11:48',180,'auntie','i made le some oatmeal and raisin cookies and mailed them to new york.  cookies should taste decadent, have real butter and cause feelings that are a mixture of guilt, pleasure, and a degree of rebellion.',1,204,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(17458,2865,'2002-06-26 03:12:40',66,'Peter','<br><tt>the people in the hospital i work in that do such things use \"sacrifice\" too; blech. it is a topic of much discussion on the internal ethical review board. it seems everyone is at a perpetual odds about the act, which i think is a pretty solid sign. if the ethical board cant agree, then, well...</tt>',1,311,59,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17459,2865,'2002-06-26 03:15:46',66,'Peter','<br><tt><b>also:</b> consciences speak strongly; i guess you have to decide if its worth selling your soul for 8$/hour. here, you can sack groceries at the independent market for something like 11$/hr.</tt>',1,195,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17460,2865,'2002-06-26 03:17:17',75,'tyler','&quot;sacrifice&quot; is in fact the term used by the doctors in the lab. i agree with them that it is probably actually the best possible word to use, the concept being that we sacrifice them to advance ourselves. that\\\'s a true statement i think.\r\n<p>\r\nthe question is, are they sentient?\r\n<p> \r\nwe are only shortening lives with no further possibility for advancement along lifestyle lines if you know what i mean. but then, you could argue that the reason that is so is because of us...\r\n<p>\r\ni had been having thoughts lately before this became a problem for me that maybe nothing humans do can be considered wrong from a truly <i>objective</i> point of view, because we and all life are a product of matter---no process has objective priority over another, whether it occurs as a result of pollution by humans or as a result of something as random as two atoms colliding in a field of gas. \r\n<p>\r\nso my thinking is, if there is infinite matter in space, and infinite time, and infinite energy, then everything has already happened before and will happen exactly the same way one day again in the infinite future. there is only matter, and non-matter to deliniate the bouindaries of reality. therefore, nothing is of any consequence, which is another way of saying everything is of the same consequence, and you as a sentient being have the privilege and inclination to decide for yourself what it\\\'s value is. this is reflected in your physical actions, which have no value except what you place on them yourself, because the true memory of them will die with you.\r\n<p>\r\nso there is no right answer. it hurts me to hurt the rats, but hurting them might help people with epilepsy be able to walk down main street without having the fear that they will fall down in the street and have violent convulsions in front of everybody. i don\\\'t know which is more important, and God won\\\'t tell me, so I will accept my pain as payment for the lives of the rats. but i will at least admit it.',1,1996,0,0,'165.247.165.70','en'),(17461,2865,'2002-06-26 03:26:51',75,'tyler','wow. this is such a mind-boggling issue. is animal experimentation the noblest thing one can do when one feels empathy for the rats but presses on for his fellow man, or the absolute lowest thing one can do because one is causing pain in another organism? this one\\\'s got zing, baby. &quot;Am I A Man Or Am I A Monster? An Animal Experimenter Speaks Out.&quot;',1,362,0,0,'165.247.165.70','en'),(17462,2865,'2002-06-26 03:32:33',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yeah, youre bound to have like 500 comments here. people want new highways but they dont want to pay the taxes; people want scientific progress, but they dont always want to face the costs. alot of the same groups that are trying to help ban animal testing have also helped put major restrictions on engineered-tissues testing. its all so mind boggling. im glad i can be involved in medicine and only talk to people, microscopes and computers.</tt> ',1,457,75,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17463,2864,'2002-06-26 03:47:25',123,'dcc','is it the colours? i think it\\\'s the colours of jellybeans that get me. the problem is, they\\\'re so tasty.\r\n<p>how can i be so scared, yet so in love with them?\r\n<p>have you ever had the buttered popcorn jelly-belly ones? creepy',4,232,124,0,'24.101.118.76','en'),(17464,2866,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','cookies should be decadent and rich, but not contain any animal products',6,72,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17465,2865,'2002-06-26 04:37:31',59,'s5','i read a really cool article in a recent issue of discover magazine. it was about epilepsy, and how a bunch of scientists were using chaos theory to analyze seizure data. from the data, they could predict seizures over 90% of the time. the theory here is that the detection software could go on a chip, along with a tiny medicine dispenser that would be implanted inside the human skull. when the chip detected an impending seizure, the medicine would spray out and prevent anything bad from happening.\r\n<br><br>\r\nso the point here is that there is always another way. the experiment i described above involves the suffering of no one, human or animal, yet provides great insight and a potential cure.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni believe whenever researchers are willing to use a different perspective to understand how bodies function, they can achieve similar results without resorting to unnecessary cruelty.\r\n',3,898,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17466,2853,'2002-06-26 05:34:42',122,'hool','it\\\'s not a mouseover like a javascript event.  in a lot of browsers, when you hold the cursor over an image, the \\\'alt=\\\' part of the image tag pops up. ',0,160,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17467,2866,'2002-06-26 06:14:22',17,'austin','<P>come on olive, call it like it is supposed to be.</P>\r\n<P>a cookie should be <B><I>MOIST</I></B>.',4,100,62,0,'64.174.154.149','en'),(17468,2866,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','or at least <big><b>TASTY</b></big>',6,35,17,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17469,2866,'2002-06-26 06:28:22',59,'s5','mm, i want cookies now.',4,23,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17470,2866,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','mmm, i want s5 now.  *pounce*',6,29,59,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17471,2866,'2002-06-26 11:56:41',65,'le','i am looking forward to receiving these butter laden cookies of which you speak, mom. \r\n<br><br>\r\nthey make tasty vegan desserts here at omega, about three times a week. when there\\\'s no dessert, i eat bread and butter with honey. ',4,233,0,0,'208.239.147.211','en'),(17472,2867,'2002-06-26 14:55:35',66,'Peter','<br><tt><img src=\\\"http://www.greensmiths.com/images/ticks1a.jpg\\\"></tt>',1,76,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17473,2867,'2002-06-26 15:59:25',65,'le','peter, that was wrong.',0,22,0,0,'208.239.147.211','en'),(17474,2867,'2002-06-26 16:49:59',180,'auntie','if i were a mean mom, i would tell dad about your tick and he would obsess over lyme disease for the rest of your natural life and call you on the phone asking if you had any rashes and badger me endlessly about whether i have questioned you thoroughly enough about the rashes, and it is funny as shit that you girls always thought your MOM was the over-protective smothering worrier.  ROTFLMAO!',4,395,65,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(17475,2867,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','hahhahaa. Peter. that was wrong but made me laugh lots. <br>\r\n\r\nle! you should find a good tick removal/inspection partner. i\'m serious. lyme\'s is rare on the west coast, but on the east coast, it\'s much more serious. it\'s not good (like in 50% of all ticks). it\'s even possible that a good aura won\'t protect you. bah! no jinx, no jinx, -mommy weeb.',1,364,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(17476,2867,'2002-06-26 17:06:23',17,'austin','<P>in california, it is the little ones that carry lyme disease, not the big ones.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>**informative**</P>',3,112,65,0,'205.179.19.150','en'),(17477,2866,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<p>yesterday, i had 3/4ths of a really really strong (and <strong>moist</strong>) pot brownie. i remember kinda wrigging back and forth in my seat, and then i was lying on the carpet, and then a curious cat tried to eat my head. i dreamed about red and purple cats falling over a cliff like a waterfall. jeff told me stories about pink orangutans in pacific union trains. and then i fell asleep on bart and woke up in bed. \r\n</p><p>\r\nwhat a day!\r\n</p><p>\r\nalso, it had butter in it, and i ate it anyway. jeff gave me kisses anyway. he\\\'s my man. my MAN. i am gushing.</p>',1,573,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(17478,2866,'2002-06-26 17:31:05',82,'simon','hi',0,2,0,0,'12.235.220.66','en'),(17479,2868,'2002-06-26 18:02:03',83,'adamajah','riding my bike along the greenbelt to BookPeople, cutting through the summer humidity, coffee grounds under my fingernails, getting a ride home from a girl, my window a/c unit that sounded like a harley davidson...<br>\r\n<br>i feel guilty because i can\\\'t remember so many street names anymore. ',0,296,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(17480,2867,'2002-06-26 18:04:54',65,'le','i\\\'m actually trying to start some kind of like, timed staff naked tick check. maybe every other day at a set time, mass tick check.',4,134,0,0,'208.239.147.211','en'),(17481,2868,'2002-06-26 18:17:12',17,'austin','<P>egg beater bikes.</P>\r\n<P>welfare tacos.</P>\r\n<P>beer stocked convenience stores.</P>\r\n<P>smelly dumpsters.</P>\r\n<P>ex-girfriends.</P>',4,137,83,0,'205.179.19.150','en'),(17482,2867,'2002-06-26 18:17:48',66,'Peter','<br><tt>sorry! couldnt resist, heh.</tt>',0,40,65,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17483,2867,'2002-06-26 18:46:53',74,'jo','hey! this is unrelated, but if i have a texas license for massage therapy by next summer, do you think they\\\'d let me do massages at omega? or do you think i\\\'d have to get the new york state license, which is the hardest and most rigorous in the country?',1,259,65,0,'172.171.112.190','en'),(17484,2866,'2002-06-26 18:58:39',86,'egg','<p>i just had agitated dreams about my ex-boyfriend eating lots of different kinds of pork out to breakfast with me. I kept thinking, \\\"so much pork!\\\" and jay and ashley were there and jay ripped his bread and sopped up some of my ex\\\'s fried eggs. I thought, \\\"that\\\'s intimate\\\".</p>\r\n<p>I think it\\\'s because all these people are talking about \\\"high fidelity\\\" and \\\"annie hall\\\" on the maz film list. It is making me have dreams of the movie \\\"The Amy-Bill Story\\\". or pork.</p>\r\n<p>pork. sugar. boys, sugar. pork.</p>',0,550,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17485,2867,'2002-06-26 22:14:24',66,'Peter','<br><tt>reprhazent! keepin\\\' it locked-down hardcore on massage therapists an\\\' shit since 1995; we\\\'re tuff like that...</tt>',1,132,74,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17486,2867,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>in vanuatu my dog and i did tick checks every day.  first i would pull them off her, ranging from 20 to 100, and then i would pull them off me, usually none, sometimes one in the crease of my inner elbow or behind my ear.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the ticks there were harmless but numerous, and i took great joy in smooshing them under my shoe on the driveway and watching the blood spread out.</p>',6,386,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17487,2866,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i think steve would refuse to kiss me for a week if i let butter enter my body willingly',6,88,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17488,2865,'2002-06-27 01:03:55',124,'gus','yah, you\\\'ll have 500 comments, but mine will be the only one like this.\r\n<p>\r\nOhhhh. you\\\'re an english major. now I get it.',0,129,0,0,'64.172.56.181','en'),(17489,2866,'2002-06-27 01:40:14',124,'gus','im a big fan of pork. the only thing my nice boyfriend wont kiss me after i eat, is salmon. he\\\'s allergic. makes him puff up and stuff.',0,138,0,0,'64.172.56.181','en'),(17490,2866,'2002-06-27 01:45:24',86,'egg','you have to come see <a href=\\\"http://www.sex-n-death.com/gsprout.htm\\\">G-Sprout!</a> at ladyfest.',0,102,62,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17491,2867,'2002-06-27 01:48:31',124,'gus','jo, you\'d have to check up on NY\'s required certificates and things. I have also heard that ny was one of the more strict places, requiring many more hours than whimpy california where Im certified... I think if I went to NY, i\'d have to take approximately 350 more hours of study to legally practice.\r\n<p>\r\nIt\'s regulated by state, and some states, like CA, it\'s regulated by county. so I can work as a massage therapist in SF, but if I went to LA, I would probably have to get a liscense. (sf has no \"liscense\" unless you are working under specific conditions that require it).\r\n<p>\r\nthere\'s that one national certification that is pretty widely recognized, its like 500 or 600 hours of schooling. of course, I cant afford that much schooling now, so. la la la.',0,647,74,0,'64.172.56.181','en'),(17492,2865,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','this is going to be really random comment: it\\\'s so weird, to be picturing you holding a struggling mousey, preparing to cut, fold, and perforate it, and it made me think of reading foxfire before bed the other night and wondering if the whole world went kerfucking-blooey, whether i would be able to live up in the mountains, and eat veggies and not kill animals. because it takes about 1500 feet of intense growing to sustain one person for one year, much less trying to find good tomato seeds when the world is radioactive soup. and then i wondered whether i could skin a pig like in the book. hook it up by its tendons between branches. i think i used to be able to. i was a pretty pragmatic kid. i think i might be able to now, because the same switch that let me watch the instructional first aid video (bones, blood, childbirth and 3rd degree burns) on sunday may let me slaughter and death. humans and their thinking brain blobs are. so. weird.\r\n\r\n',3,958,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(17493,2867,'2002-06-27 02:49:53',231,'alana','a few months ago my blood was sent to america to be tested for lyme disease. i felt a bit special. i had a big phat foot that the doctor could not explain, and i had been doing a lot of wandering beside rivers in georgia and so he tested me. i wonder how many other australians get their blood sent to another country. ps. i do not have lyme disease. i merely have arthritis. this is better than having lyme disease. okay bye.',3,426,0,0,'203.220.82.240','en'),(17494,2867,'2002-06-27 03:10:18',66,'Peter','<br><tt>the place i work gets skin samples from the uk lots</tt>',1,64,231,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17495,2868,'2002-06-27 03:46:24',86,'egg','this evening on my walk I contemplated this post. My whole routine seems to revolve around the rose garden, which is a bowl of slopes and pools nestled into a hill. I ran my legs over the edges of the slopes like a gigantic thumb smoothing the inside of a vessel, and up to the tops where I observed the geometric chaos of boxy houses pushing their heads out from the bowl. The sweetest-smelling rose was named Chrysler Imperial. Now how did it get a name like that?\r\n<p>whenver we walk to the hebba hobba, you stop and smell the neighbor\\\'s roses. That is one of the many reasons why you are dear.</p>',0,604,17,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17496,2865,'2002-06-27 04:17:57',75,'tyler','another day at the lab today. the anesthetics are not working very well on the current group of rats. imagine what <i>that\\\'s</i> like. \r\n<p>\r\ni can\\\'t say i\\\'m any closer to a resolution. i don\\\'t understand the ethics of what i\\\'m doing well enough to fully commit one way or the other. i will carry on, and if later i decide that this is really  wrong, at least i\\\'ll have some consolation in remembering that i took it seriously and worried about doing the right thing. \r\n<p>\r\nsometimes it feels like i run my soul like a crooked CEO, hiding losses, inflating gains. hedging my bets. looking for &quot;plausible deniability.&quot;',1,646,0,0,'165.247.165.70','en'),(17497,2865,'2002-06-27 07:58:48',59,'s5','i was going to repost the lyrics to testure by skinny puppy, but you can search on the web yourself.',5,100,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17498,2865,'2002-06-27 08:03:03',59,'s5','tyler, can i ask you why you\\\'re working there in the first place? is it because epilepsy research is particularly interesting to you? is it because you feel driven to serve humanity? or is it because you just need a job?',5,223,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17499,1220,'2002-06-27 08:55:02',240,'xep','there is no more rowan.  good bye rowan.\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.logy.org/~xep/neef/rowan.gif\\\">\r\n\r\n',0,108,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(17500,1220,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','????????  no more rowan ????????',6,32,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17501,1220,'2002-06-27 09:34:12',240,'xep','rowan was put to sleep earlier this week.  i don\\\'t know which day.  danny tried to call me but the time zones kept getting mixed up.  he woke up at 2am this morning so he could call me at 7pm.\r\n<p>\r\nrowan was old.  he couldn\\\'t walk very well anymore.  he fell over a lot and had a hard time breathing.  i don\\\'t know exactly how old.  when we got him from the shelter they thought he might have been 6.  that was in 1997. \r\n<p>\r\nrip, dorko.',0,448,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(17502,1220,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','rip, big n stinky nice friendly doggie of xep',6,45,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17503,2865,'2002-06-27 11:39:13',75,'tyler','i just needed a work-study job for the summer. but--i thought it would be interesting and also an opportunity to contribute to science/medicine.',1,144,0,0,'165.247.165.70','en'),(17504,2869,'2002-06-27 11:49:52',295,'karenlee','Fantastic! You have been threatened by the SELLOTAPE Â® gang! Stick it to \\\'em!!',1,81,0,0,'203.194.17.2','en'),(17505,2869,'2002-06-27 11:54:42',89,'sawks','this is as funny as it is fucked up\r\n\r\nbut now give them a call and abuse them whenever you need a pick me up :)',1,112,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(17506,2869,'2002-06-27 12:52:34',66,'Peter','<br><tt>youd think they had lots better things to do than dig through your 2 year old journal entries, but then again, youd think alot of things about lawyers.</tt>',1,164,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17507,2867,'2002-06-27 14:33:56',74,'jo','yeah, texas and california (and florida, i think) have the easiest programs apparently, or at least require the least amount of classroom hours. <BR>\r\ni\\\'m not just looking at omega but at all spiritual centers that hire summer help, just for a month to 6 weeks next summer.<BR>\r\nhowever, if i get into unc next fall, i might go to north carolina at the beginning of the summer and start paying for the additional 200 hours of massage classes so i could be licensed <b>there</b>.<BR>\r\nanyway, it\\\'s way too early to figure any of this out right now. i\\\'m just going to hang out with your daughter.',0,603,124,0,'172.152.125.189','en'),(17508,2867,'2002-06-27 14:35:29',74,'jo','(p.s. can i mention how much i like the idea of paying for grad school with massages? so much. that will be the most relaxed english department in the country, i tell you.)',1,172,0,0,'172.152.125.189','en'),(17509,2869,'2002-06-27 14:39:46',74,'jo','they probably have employees whose express job is to search for uses of the sellotape brand. this reminds me of when my brother was a bartender and he met a guy who worked for coca-cola and this guy\\\'s <b>entire job</b> involved going into bars and asking for a coke and seeing how the bartender responded. i.e.: \\\"we don\\\'t have coke, would you like a pepsi?\\\" etc. etc.',1,379,66,0,'172.152.125.189','en'),(17510,2869,'2002-06-27 14:42:26',74,'jo','okay but wait. i\\\'m confused. i thought that companies were supposed to be HAPPY when people used their brand name to refer to the generic product. like kleenex or hoover or band-aid. isn\\\'t it supposed to be a positive thing? will someone more business-minded explain this to me?',1,284,0,0,'172.152.125.189','en'),(17511,2869,'2002-06-27 14:48:02',66,'Peter','<tt>well, firstly, they probably dont want their name associated with inferior quality products. also, they probably dont want to lose sales.<br><br>i know that when i lived in tennessee, they called soda \"coke\" in a generic fashion... so, like, youd walk into a restaurant and ask for a coke, then specify what you wanted: <i>\"gimme a burger and a large coke... ill have dr. pepper\"</i> or something like that. they probably dont want people going into places, asking for their product and having something that theyre not going to profit on acceptingly sold in its place...</tt>',1,588,74,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17512,1220,'2002-06-27 16:49:15',86,'egg','meh. sad.',0,9,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17513,2869,'2002-06-27 16:57:42',17,'austin','cellotape is cellotape is cellotape.  i dont see what the big fucking deal is.  Cellotape &reg and cellotape both keep my balls from getting in the way every day.',3,162,0,0,'205.179.19.150','en'),(17514,2868,'2002-06-27 17:13:11',83,'adamajah','thinking about my old egg beater, the green machine, practically brings a tear to my eye.  it\\\'s probably stripped and half buried in a field somewhere on the outskirts of austin.',0,181,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(17515,2869,'2002-06-27 18:31:29',83,'adamajah','<p>\\\"The information contained in this e-mail message is intended only for the CONFIDENTIAL use of the designated addressee named above.  The information transmitted is subject to the attorney-client privilege and/or represents confidential attorney work product.  Recipients should not file copies of\r\nthis email with publicly accessible records.  If YOU are not the\r\ndesignated addressee named above or the authorized agent responsible for\r\ndelivering it to the designated addressee, you received this document\r\nthrough inadvertent error and any further review, dissemination,\r\ndistribution or copying of this communication by you or anyone else is\r\nstrictly prohibited.  IF YOU RECEIVED THIS COMMUNICATION IN ERROR, PLEASE\r\nNOTIFY US IMMEDIATELY BY TELEPHONING THE SENDER NAMED ABOVE AT (xxx)\r\nxxx-xxxx.  Thank you.</p>',0,824,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(17516,2858,'2002-06-27 18:37:37',195,'matt','yo, i could go for summa that.',1,30,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(17517,1220,'2002-06-27 20:37:42',17,'austin','<P>ole rowan.</P>\r\n<P>**cracks egg on dogfood**</P>\r\n<P>**pours it on curb**</P>',4,80,0,0,'205.179.19.150','en'),(17518,1025,'2002-06-27 20:56:41',17,'austin','<P>just an update here.</P>\r\n<P>my car now has 53k miles.</P>\r\n<P>there is no more booming system.</P>\r\n<P>i have an old bmw motorbike that i broke.</P>\r\n<P>things dont change, they just get older.</P>',4,201,17,0,'205.179.19.150','en'),(17519,2468,'2002-06-27 20:58:31',17,'austin','puce never came back.  one of the big disappointments for 2002.',4,63,0,0,'205.179.19.150','en'),(17520,2468,'2002-06-27 21:26:23',295,'karenlee','\\\"Puce\\\" is an excellent name.',1,34,0,0,'203.194.8.248','en'),(17521,2869,'2002-06-27 21:34:01',195,'matt','advise sellotape to jam it up their ass.',1,40,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(17522,2858,'2002-06-27 21:37:20',195,'matt','summa them $20 brown grams. i could tear that shit up.',1,54,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(17523,2868,'2002-06-27 22:22:47',234,'etienne','curved, ergonomic style buildings really annoy me.  its not like they\\\'re any less obstrusive.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<tt>fuck you landscape, i will not blend ! </tt>',1,156,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(17524,2867,'2002-06-27 22:32:56',261,'sine','\r\n\r\nwhoa this is crazy. I just had an awful nightmare (night before last) about being attacked by ticks. a thick swarm. all over my legs. and they were mean and nasty, burrowing under my skin. I woke up and there was an ant crawling on my arm. i have only slept about 3 hours since then. eek!',1,292,0,0,'216.102.198.168','en'),(17525,2868,'2002-06-27 23:33:58',66,'Peter','<tt>hmph. you beat me to the <i>fuck you landscape, i will not blend</i>...<br><br>in tennessee, there was this community of people- and i kid you not- that lived in circular/raised flying-saucer-esque houses. they were the only houses on stilts that ive ever seen. they had drop-down staircases and wedge-shaped rooms centered around a big circular fireplace. the one and only tiem i was ever in one of these houses, i thought it was going to be livechangingly cool; but i ended up almost getting sick due to the curvy walls in every room.<br><br>i still maintain an ongoing lust for retractable staircases and house-stilts though.</tt>',1,637,234,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17526,2868,'2002-06-28 00:02:11',234,'etienne','i knew you\\\'d appreciate that.<br><br>yes, i must admit, living in a house on stilts is one of the better things i can claim to my fame, such as it is a claim.  who has a retractable staircase?<br><br>i want a murphy bed!',1,223,66,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(17527,2869,'2002-06-28 00:42:43',64,'Joanna','This was a PAPER letter, without the word confidential anywhere on it.  Hence why I have no qualms in putting it up.  ',1,118,83,0,'130.216.131.85','en'),(17528,2869,'2002-06-28 01:12:31',83,'adamajah','oops, you missed the boat.',0,26,64,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(17529,2860,'2002-06-28 01:22:08',333,'just-sade','^_^',2,3,0,0,'64.12.107.181','en'),(17530,2869,'2002-06-28 02:40:28',75,'tyler','i would LOVE to see them try to take you to court over that. i say leave it just like it was. there\\\'s a free-speech lawsuit in there somewhere.',1,146,0,0,'165.247.165.70','en'),(17531,2860,'2002-06-28 04:24:23',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i never really understood what that was supposed to mean</tt>',1,69,333,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17532,2868,'2002-06-28 05:08:14',175,'Paul G','\\\"...from swerve of shore to bend of bay, brings us by a commodius vicus of recirculation back to...\\\"  (Joyce)',0,115,0,0,'65.144.161.18','en'),(17533,2869,'2002-06-28 05:40:24',59,'s5','i guess they figure that some percentage of people are stupid enough to fall for it.',0,84,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17534,2870,'2002-06-28 08:13:02',295,'karenlee','<i>....the hot girl with the clear braces....</i>\r\n\r\n<p>There was always one.</p>\r\n',1,83,0,0,'203.221.83.138','en'),(17535,2873,'2002-06-28 11:00:15',120,'joyrider','we are in the kali yuga, and it is a good time to write poems.  \r\n\r\n<p>elly\\\'s poems rock.</p> ',1,97,0,0,'12.224.184.3','en'),(17536,2874,'2002-06-28 14:25:12',187,'kandrer','liked it, thanks',1,16,0,0,'213.36.107.192','en'),(17537,2870,'2002-06-28 14:50:22',67,'trouble','austin, check out kevin seconds\\\' side projects, some of the early <i>drop acid</i> stuff was a pretty good as i remember.  you\\\'re right, bands like 7 seconds and Minor Threat do bring back the memories.',1,208,0,0,'139.76.65.132','en'),(17538,2735,'2002-06-28 14:56:52',187,'kandrer','the caption is not from me... it\\\'s inspired by a french song from Michel Jonasz (lyrics <a href=\\\"http://site.ifrance.com/leparolier/textes/jevoulaistedirequejetattends.htm\\\">here</a>).',1,192,200,0,'213.36.107.192','en'),(17539,2713,'2002-06-28 15:00:07',187,'kandrer','it\\\'s a great visual movie. you\\\'re too nice :).',1,52,200,0,'213.36.107.192','en'),(17540,2865,'2002-06-28 15:18:33',176,'Scapegoat','If <b>Tyler</b> is a monster, Hmmmm....<br><br>\r\nI know alot of people around swinney.org are Vegetarians or Vegans. That being said I\\\'m going to ask the others why they would distinguish between the aforementioned rats and any other livestock raised for meat. Vegetarians and Vegans I can understand objections from, others not so much. Just curious.   ',0,357,0,0,'64.229.160.15','en'),(17541,2868,'2002-06-28 15:33:17',74,'jo','being that i am the kind of person who regularly goes between two extremes of aching wanderlust and smug complacent home-worshipping (some would say sagittarius moon duking it out with cancer sun), i appreciate the times when you and your austin peeps get moony-eyed about my city. i went for a walk yesterday, winding through the streets of hyde park from 38th & guadalupe to quack\\\'s at 45th & duval, and i appreciated it a lot more than i normally do. thanks.',1,464,17,0,'172.142.83.60','en'),(17542,2870,'2002-06-28 16:37:53',82,'7 seconds','<b>WALK TOGETHER, ROCK TOGETHER</b>',1,35,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17543,2868,'2002-06-28 16:46:08',82,'simon','come to california to see lots of houses on stilts.',0,51,66,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17544,2870,'2002-06-28 17:38:31',66,'Peter','<br><tt>minor threat! 7th grade all over again when being straightedge was cool.</tt>',1,85,67,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17545,2868,'2002-06-28 17:40:06',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i really wanted to tighhtropewalk across that pipe in the frisbee-golf ravine place, but was too cool to request a chance.</tt>',1,135,74,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17546,2868,'2002-06-28 17:40:46',66,'Peter','<br><tt>my current subterrainean living layout is starting to get to me. stilts!</tt>',1,85,82,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17547,2868,'2002-06-28 18:11:28',82,'simon','i have this hilarious book published by california magazine in 1984 entitled \\\"How to California\\\" which details everything from giving your dealer a pass to the studio executive parking lot to which tennis shoes and flip-flops to wear during which season. It also talks a lot about the stages of how californian you\\\'ve become in relation to your feelings about earthquakes and houses on stilts and how evolved your espresso-drink preferences have become.',0,462,66,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17548,2868,'2002-06-28 18:34:22',66,'Peter','<br><tt>heh! sounds alot like <i>less than zero</i></tt>',1,56,82,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17549,2875,'2002-06-28 18:47:03',66,'Peter','<br><tt>people on st. marks <i>work</i>?!</tt>',1,46,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17550,1152,'2002-06-28 23:50:06',82,'simon','heehee',0,6,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17551,2873,'2002-09-05 18:40:19',18,'ix','le, do any of your new friends read this site?',0,46,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(17552,2873,'2002-06-29 03:21:19',278,'egenus','God have I missed your daily insights le. Reading this made me smile.',1,69,0,0,'66.56.126.164','en'),(17553,2875,'2002-06-29 04:12:04',261,'sine','<img src=\\\"http://www.fonem.org/photo/nyc/bluehi.jpg\\\"><br>\r\nst. marks, july 1999. hot person, cool building.',1,113,0,0,'216.102.198.168','en'),(17554,2868,'2002-06-29 04:17:29',74,'jo','awwww, you should have spoken up! we would have stopped.<BR>\r\ndo you know what there is now, peter, off of lamar right across the street from the creek? <b>A BIG PILE OF GRAVEL.</B> just sitting there. unattended. it begs to be played in.',1,238,66,0,'172.170.98.13','en'),(17555,2873,'2002-06-29 04:57:45',65,'le','ix, none of my new friends read this site. i tell them about how i post writing on the internet but they don\\\'t seem compelled to check it out. heh.',4,150,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17556,2873,'2002-06-29 15:36:20',17,'new friends','what is the internet?  you mean like hot mail?  you write in hotmail?',3,69,65,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17557,2875,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','st. marks is where i got the best haircut in the world, AND found the coolest little basement bookstore ever. that was a pretty good day...',1,139,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(17558,2873,'2002-06-29 19:03:34',195,'matt','this reminds me of that country song, something about all these people praying and \\\"one voice was heard.\\\"<BR><BR>the rainbow goes back through the prism and comes out as light....',1,185,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(17559,2865,'2002-06-29 19:47:32',75,'tyler','i have found a solution to my problem...i am using the skills i learned in the military to <i>pretend</i> to be taking part in the experiment, while actually accomplishing no work at all. it\\\'s amazing what you can get away with in this world.',1,245,0,0,'165.247.165.70','en'),(17560,2875,'2002-06-29 20:05:50',82,'simon','hello, i do believe i know that person. how odd.',0,48,261,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17561,2875,'2002-06-29 21:14:02',66,'Peter','<br><tt>why do so many neat bklyn/st. marks-ish people live elsewhere?</tt>',1,75,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17562,1671,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','there are a lot more definitions of tribe than the american indian one.',6,71,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17563,2875,'2002-06-29 22:30:45',82,'simon','i don\\\'t know. but i, for one, am returning, like moving back. in december. brr.',0,82,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17564,2865,'2002-06-30 04:02:53',187,'kandrer','sorry, but somehow i kinda like it to think about you opening rats. $8.00 per hour for rats. how much for cats?<br>\r\n[x] drunk and mean',4,135,75,0,'80.15.164.49','en'),(17565,2870,'2002-06-30 04:08:54',187,'kandrer','cf <i>ring</i>. satako keeps her head tilted since she \\\"fell\\\" in the well. one eye always hidden by her long dark hair. (unrelated comment)',1,145,0,0,'80.15.164.49','en'),(17566,2875,'2002-06-30 08:05:49',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ooh. chalk another one up for the n-y-c :D</tt>',1,55,82,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17567,2879,'2002-06-30 12:01:04',295,'karenlee','go back to the dream and sue their pants off.',3,45,0,0,'203.194.11.66','en'),(17568,2879,'2002-06-30 17:24:55',17,'austin','i remarked yesterday that i have not been to either ikea or costco.  people think i should keep it that way, but the buildings are so huge.  i am curious what is inside.  certainly if there are nude people posed as furniture, i want to see them.',3,245,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17569,2879,'2002-07-01 02:24:22',74,'jo','oh, no, i disagree, you should definitely go to ikea. it\\\'s like a sam\\\'s club, except that instead of 300-packs of ramen, it\\\'s byooootiful furniture and home furnishings, oh my gosh i\\\'m getting excited just THINKING about ikea. and it\\\'s all cheap! so cheap!',0,271,17,0,'172.144.248.124','en'),(17570,2879,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','wow, someone that likes the ikea furniture.',6,43,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17571,2878,'2002-07-01 03:54:59',353,'arabella','<p>The New York Times magazine published an article in mid-may about the nuclear threat.  I can\\\'t remember the name of the writer, but he made the same case about the reality of the \\\"dirty bomb.\\\"  I believe this particular writer was more concerned about the thereat of chemical and biological terror than the nuclear threat... although he did go into great detail about the potential for radioactive warfare - that someone could leave some radioactive material in the middle of say, Union Square, and unless the city had detection devices in place, nobody would know about it until people started showing up at the hospital days later.  A sobering thought.</p>',3,670,0,0,'66.68.45.204','en'),(17572,2878,'2002-07-01 13:08:51',66,'Peter','<br><tt>\r\nfortunately, thanks not only to the <a href=\"http://www.nyc.gov/html/doh/html/radioh/radioh.html\">NYC Bureau of Radiological Health</a> but to the several <a href=\"http://www.cscap.nuctrans.org/Nuc_Trans/links/newnet/newnet1.htm\">organizations</a> currently <a href=\"http://www.cscap.nuctrans.org/Nuc_Trans/links/radiation-air/radiation-air.htm\">monitoring</a> the <A href=\"http://www.jaeri.go.jp/~speedi/whatis.html\">real-time</a> levels of <a href=\"http://newnet2.lanl.gov/concept.asp\">atmospheric</a>/ground-level radiation levels of about every square inch of the world (via about every method possible, including atmospheric, satellite and ground-level detection,  especially around major metropolitan areas) a major nuclear incident (at least of the sort that would cause the slow, undetected horror, as you describe) is statistically nil. <br><Br>sure, there would be exposure, but a situation where people appeared at hospitals for some mystery exposure, days later, sounds like fodder for paranoia. having seen how quickly (and obscenely seriously) both the NYC and Federal authorities have responded to even minor non-threatening radiation exposures where I work has convinced me to rather trust the powers that be, at least in this regard.<br><br>An instantaneous nuclear detonation of some sort, however, is another story completely, but this is another matter beyond small-scale chemical/biological warfare...</tt>',1,916,353,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17573,2878,'2002-07-01 13:44:04',66,'Peter','<br><tt>regarding the <i>biologically engineered fungi</i>... <A href=\"http://www.mapinc.org/drugnews/v98/n495/a03.html\">this</a> is a good link. the statement that caught my eye was <i>\"While killer fungi and many poisonous herbicides are not approved for use in the US, people in developing countries often have no say in what toxins are released in their communities\"</i>...<br><br><A href=\"http://www.drugwarfacts.org/\">this</a> is also a good link.</tt>\r\n',1,396,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17574,2873,'2002-07-01 17:35:02',124,'gus','some people just dont get the internet. some people wanted to see some of my writing, but they just wont click on the link. what can you do. too bad for them.',0,158,0,0,'64.172.56.181','en'),(17575,2879,'2002-07-01 17:50:27',124,'gus','i dont have any problems with ikea, except that if I go buy a sofa there, I will worry that everyone else has the exact same sofa. Im not convinced of the quality of some of their items. but yah, it seems to be somewhat affordable. i\\\'ve never had any dreams about it though.',0,277,0,0,'64.172.56.181','en'),(17576,2879,'2002-07-01 18:04:43',353,'arabella','i have no explanation for the IKEA dream.  I don\\\'t own anything from IKEA except a cheap, metal picture frame.',1,113,0,0,'66.68.45.204','en'),(17577,2879,'2002-07-01 18:15:38',354,'martha','www.fantasticteam.com/ikea\r\n\r\nsounds like you were hanging out with us :)',2,73,0,0,'207.87.107.10','en'),(17578,2878,'2002-07-01 20:43:07',295,'karenlee','I am afraid of people who get on the bus with a strange, empty looking bag and shifty demeanor. I think this is relevant to this article. \r\n',1,140,0,0,'203.194.7.160','en'),(17579,2878,'2002-07-01 22:05:55',66,'Peter','<br><tt>maybe i jsut live in a crappy neighborhood, but that sounds like every work-bound weirdo that gets on my bus every morning, heh</tt>',1,140,295,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17580,2865,'2002-07-02 01:35:42',75,'tyler','do you drink turpentine or something before writing?',1,52,187,0,'165.247.164.53','en'),(17581,2878,'2002-07-02 02:52:30',75,'tyler','it\\\'s all a fucking conspiracy, man.',1,38,0,0,'165.247.164.53','en'),(17582,2881,'2002-07-02 04:15:12',339,'blue','I\\\'m spending the week with my mom in Brooklyn. She grew up here millions and millions of years ago. But then she left her mom and her brother for the southwest and raised a family and divorced and now her two children are out on their own on the East coast and her brother and her mother died within six weeks of each other so she is back for good. Back to Brooklyn, in the same apartment she left 30-some-odd years ago. She\\\'s engaged, too.</p><P>\r\nBut before I tell you my whole life story, I\\\'d like to add a comment to your swinney.</p><p>\r\nYesterday night we were covered in sweat, sitting in front of a 13\\\" television, cooling off by the aid of an $8.99 fan purchased at K-mart, watching the News. Apparently, Mayor Blumberg <i>did</i> raise some taxes-- cigarette taxes. Now, a pack will cost your flatmate, who is quitting smoking so he can marry his fiance, up to $7.00. That\\\'s almost as much as our store-bought fan. </p><p>\r\nPerhaps the monetary incentive will be more than the love incentive to keep your flatmate\\\'s life in tact.',1,1057,0,0,'152.163.204.74','en'),(17583,2881,'2002-07-02 04:28:05',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yep, the tax goes into effect on midnight tonight. i neglected to mention that that was also part of why we went around the block; so he could rant his way to the bodega to get a carton before it went into effect. hes a cheap bastard sometimes, but i love him.<br><br>\r\ni installed a ceiling fan last week, and its helped alot. i do myss my place in brooklyn, though, with its 8 windows and harbor breeze. hope your mom has good luck here.</tt> ',1,453,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17584,2881,'2002-07-02 05:47:35',17,'austin','<P>i like how cigarettes are treated.  need money?  tax cigarettes.  aparently they are not that important to people, not important enough to thow a shitfit.  why not raise the taxes on alchohol too?  imagine the taxes that could be levied on weed!  if cigarettes are 7 dollars a pack, and 6 dollars of that is taxes, then on pot, you could easily get 20 dollars.  a pack of pot for 21.00.  that is allot cheaper than it is now for the equivalent of a pack, isnt it?  what if cigarette manufacturers started focusing on potency (far more than they already do) and you could get for 7 dollars SuperSmoker3000\\\'s.  people sitting around at bus stops passing the cigarette from hand to hand.  \\\"dont bogart the cigarette!\\\"  or maybe smokers would just bogart them happily.  sit up on their roof puffing away at their $.25 cigarette, not wanting to share.  </P><P>while we are at fighting terrorism, we should give some serious thought to that old email tax bouncer.  the government should take over the telecommuncations infrastructure, like when mexico took over the railroads in 1937.  of course we would be nationalizing our own private businesses, not foriegn owned ones, as in the case of mexico, but it is to fight terrorism!  it really needs to be fought.  we cant have terror.</P><P>gas should definately have the shit taxed out of it.  if a ticket on the bus 80 miles is 20 dollars, and you can fit 70 people on a bus, then gas should be like 20 dollars a gallon because that bus is charging 1400 all together.  the amount that bus charges all together should be the base price for a gallon of gas, raise the taxes on gas 18 dollars please, to fight terrorism!</P>',3,1677,0,0,'64.174.155.117','en'),(17585,2879,'2002-09-05 18:40:19',18,'ix','it\\\'s that great commercial that runs before movies at the cinema. the one with the two girls driving on a sunny afternoon.',0,125,353,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(17586,2873,'2002-09-05 18:40:19',18,'ix','the tires are the things on your car that make contact with the road.',0,69,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(17587,2881,'2002-07-02 12:40:21',66,'Peter','<tt>yeah, its really weird. i think that the state thinks that no one will complain about a cigarette-tax... and no one is, because no one wants the stigma of \\\"defending big tobacco\\\". oh well, it doesnt really effect me much, so i dont have much of an opinion. better tobacco than food or something.<br><br>but im kind of pissed. in the boom economy, they axed the commuter tax. now, literally billions a year in nyc dollars flood into jersey and connecticut, untaxed. thats where i would have started.\r\n<br><br>ive been hearing tons of whining, though, from my halfway house neighbors. listening to their hour-long ranty complaints through my window every night has been somewhat enlightening, if not entertaining.<br><br>and its uncanny how many people you see rolling their own now, as loose tobacco doesnt seem to be taxed, or at least not as much. so now the tokers are out as well, banking on being less-conspicuous thanks to all the legit homemade smokes going around.</tt>\r\n\r\n',1,990,17,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17588,2881,'2002-07-02 15:34:00',86,'egg','it is a bad business, quitting smoking for the spouse.',0,54,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17589,2881,'2002-07-02 17:30:44',265,'chuckspeed','no - not bad juju to give up a vice for a loved one; \\\'tis bad juju to *say* you\\\'ve given it up only to continue in a clandestine fashion.  if this simple committment cannot be held, then what about the big one?\r\n\r\n',0,220,86,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(17590,2881,'2002-07-02 17:33:45',180,'auntie','they don\\\'t tax alcohol because all the politicians drink so much of it.<br><br>\r\n\r\neven the healh-nut pols have a nice glass of wine and they don\\\'t want to pay extra for it.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni have been trying to stop smoking for so long, i just don\\\'t know if i will ever be able to do it, no matter how high the price of cigarettes goes.  it\\\'s not about the money, it\\\'s about addiction.<br><br>',3,404,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(17591,2881,'2002-07-02 17:37:51',66,'Peter','<tt>what amuses me is that shes dated/lived with him for like 4 years and his smoking wasnt ever an issue until he asked her to marry him.</tt>',1,143,265,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17592,2881,'2002-07-02 18:48:55',86,'egg','I just dont think it works if you do it expressly for someone else, as a condition of the relationship. and then it gets all freakily tied up into relationship control issues and chunks of couple splatter everywhere.',0,216,265,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17593,2881,'2002-07-02 20:52:44',74,'jo','yeah i\\\'m with egg wholeheartedly. it\\\'s too hard of a habit to kick unless you are personally convicted to do so.',0,118,0,0,'172.143.169.231','en'),(17594,2881,'2002-07-02 21:14:11',295,'karenlee','i have quit smoking recently and i miss it like hell, but in some freakish accident of nature, i am able this time to ignore all cravings. the best part is i did it quite by myself, no insisting/controlling partner, which was the case when my previous quitting attempts failed. \r\n\r\n\r\n',1,284,0,0,'203.194.6.84','en'),(17595,2873,'2002-07-02 21:22:59',130,'treetoph','the car is the thing on the road that takes you back to your abode',0,66,0,0,'172.141.56.42','en'),(17596,2881,'2002-07-02 22:38:52',295,'karenlee','Also, PaulG inadvertently gave me an excellent mental slogan. He said.....<i>do stop smoking</i>, which has been much more effective than the ones I have used in the past, such as \\\"don\\\'t smoke, it\\\'s making your lungs bleed\\\".\r\n',1,238,0,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(17597,1250,'2002-07-02 22:44:05',86,'egg','<p>I saw this just now<br>\r\nA year later, nothing\\\'s changed<br>\r\nmusic and staring</p>\r\n',0,91,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17598,1250,'2002-07-02 22:56:09',295,'karenlee','please, do not be fooled\r\nby summer\\\'s white burning rays\r\nyour skin will shrivel.',1,84,0,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(17599,1250,'2002-07-02 22:58:18',295,'karenlee','<small>goddam</small>\r\n<p>please, do not be fooled<br>\r\nby summer\\\'s white burning rays<br>\r\nyour skin will shrivel.<br></p>\r\n\r\n\r\n',1,132,0,0,'203.20.98.67','en'),(17600,1420,'2002-07-03 00:46:13',17,'austin','ha ha, what the fuck is this?',4,29,0,0,'205.179.19.127','en'),(17601,1250,'2002-07-03 05:49:10',66,'Peter','<br><tt>\r\nhot in the city<br>\r\nhot in the city, tonight<br>\r\nsang billy idol<br></tt>',1,85,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17602,2883,'2002-07-03 12:43:36',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i havent spent much time in brighton beach, but coney island... man, that place is a trip. its got a vacant seedy/creepy vibe that sticks to it almost year-round (minus maybe a month or two in the summer); getting off the F train and walking up surf avenue in the early spring or autumn, seeing the giant vacant station waiting to accomodate the summer throngs who stopped coming 25 years ago... with all the boarded up freakshows and old folks sitting on the sidewalks in lawn chairs...</tt>',1,500,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17603,2869,'2002-07-03 13:37:17',76,'chi chi','does NZ have free speech?',0,25,0,0,'194.79.106.233','en'),(17604,2881,'2002-07-03 14:30:26',265,'chuckspeed','it\\\'s funny...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>most women i\\\'ve encountered have this secret marriage script.  when they find *the* one, they whip out the script and start a-readin\\\' it - problem is, they rarely share the script with their SO.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I love him so, but...</p>\r\n<p>He needs to...</p>\r\n<p>I don\\\'t know what he did b4 me...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>toss the scripts, ladies.  we\\\'re gonna scratch our balls, fart in bed, and belch in public whether you like it or not.  if you don\\\'t like the guy AS-IS...go find another one, dammit.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>while we\\\'re on this rant, howzabout handing over a copy of the script after, say, the first 2 dates?  We\\\'d sure like to know in advance what we\\\'re signing up for - if we don\\\'t run screaming from the bistro, then there\\\'s a good chance we\\\'re meant for each other.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>much pain,heartache, and half finished meals would be avoided in the process...\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',0,914,66,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(17605,2881,'2002-07-03 16:27:01',195,'matt','i think sometimes people use these shortcomings in their significant others as reasons to avoid deeper commitment.',1,114,0,0,'66.192.26.250','en'),(17606,2883,'2002-07-03 16:33:31',195,'matt','coney island reminds me of \\\"requiem for a dream.\\\" seediness.',1,66,0,0,'66.192.26.250','en'),(17607,2883,'2002-07-03 17:02:48',82,'simon','i spent as much time as possible in coney island and brighton beach when i lived in brooklyn. such nice walks, nice times.',0,122,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17608,2878,'2002-07-03 17:03:33',180,'auntie','yep, it\\\'s all a conspiracy.  if you look hard enough, you can find a conspiracy in all facets of your life.  you have to blow it all off and concentrate on the things you DO have power over.  the only way to change the world is to do it one person at a time, one day at a time. ',4,281,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(17609,2883,'2002-07-03 17:13:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt>and the <a href=\\\"http://www.coneyisland.com/mermaid.shtml\\\">mermaid parade</a> was last weekend! fun indeed... coney island in the hizzy.</tt>',1,155,82,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17610,2883,'2002-07-03 17:56:14',82,'simon','ah yes, the mermaid parade. i heard they are charging now? and it\\\'s a bit different than it once was. a few years back, my friends and i won \\\"best float\\\" and we got a big trophy with a dolphin on it.',0,208,66,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17611,2883,'2002-07-03 18:44:56',86,'egg','at one of those beaches this kid dared me to get naked and GO IN THE WATER, and promised me that he would if I did first. so I did, and eve got nervous that I was going to get arrested for indecent exposure, and that raver kid never did take his drawers off. I think I found a heroine needle in my pubic hair later. the mermaid parade is a torture. chute called me yesterday to tell me about the locations of all her zits and the grease on her pillow from the heat in brooklyn.',0,477,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17612,2883,'2002-07-03 18:54:44',66,'Peter','<br><tt>its scary that coney island has been outmoded by the jersey shore.</tt>',1,79,86,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17613,2105,'2002-07-03 20:39:20',75,'tyler','well...it turns out that there are stories by piers anthony about a planet or something called <i>Xanth</i>, unbeknownst to me. even better, there are stories by another writer whose name i can\\\'t recall about a mysterious bottle full of a mysterious magical substance...oh yeah, <i>Jitterbug Perfume</i> by Tom Robbins. i just want the record to show that i didn\\\'t know about either of these books when i wrote this. thank you.',1,433,0,0,'165.247.164.53','en'),(17614,2865,'2002-07-04 00:27:50',185,'orion','Just consider it payback for the Black Plague.  One dead human = one dead rat, and you\\\'ve got allowance there for several hojillion rats.  (Not to make light of your ethical quandry.  If it really bugs you, quit.)',1,216,75,0,'216.201.248.18','en'),(17615,2883,'2002-07-04 00:40:53',339,'blue','There was a newspaper article about the mermaid parade when I first got here earlier this week, it was in the local-free-Brooklyn-newspaper-something-or-other. I didn\\\'t read the article, just looked at the pictures. One of \\\'em featured a girl completely naked save sequins pasted on her nipples. The caption read, \\\"One beautiful lady shows off her fancy mermaid costume.\\\" Or something clever like that. I may have made up those words, but I really wasn\\\'t aware that mermaids wore sequins!</p><p>\r\nAlso, more on the New York front. 42nd street subway station, waiting for the N. I was right near a door so I stood to the side of it while three people exited the train. A lady all dressed up with a big Burberry bag pushed me. I\\\'m a pretty easy going gal, so I just moved aside. She pushed me again so I looked up to see what was going on. She barked at me, \\\"Move aside so that the people can exit the train.\\\" And then she ran inside before me.</p><p>\r\nMy mom and I very calmly exchanged glances and sat down. As the train started moving, I saw she was sitting across from me, staring at me. I gave her a look and then said to my mom in a pretty audible voice, \\\"It\\\'s too bad that lady has had such a rotten day!\\\" My mom told me to shush and explained, \\\"She\\\'s a New Yorker.\\\" The lady interjected, \\\"MY DAY WAS NOT ROTTEN AT ALL.\\\" I could tell by her defensiveness that she had indeed had a bad day. She was just too stubborn to admit to it. My mom again mentioned, \\\"She\\\'s just a New Yorker.\\\" The lady said, \\\"That\\\'s right, I am a New Yorker.\\\" I leaned over to my mom, because I didn\\\'t want to cause a scene, and whispered in her ear, \\\"Coming from New York is not an excuse to be rude.\\\" My mom nodded in agreement, I read my book. The entire time I was reading I could feel the lady\\\'s eyes peircing through my skin--I think she really just wanted to tell somebody about her rotten day. </p><p>\r\nAlso, I don\\\'t believe she was a real New Yorker; I think she\\\'s from Long Island.',1,2053,0,0,'64.12.107.24','en'),(17616,2881,'2002-07-04 00:43:37',339,'blue','Perhaps a new commonly accepted cliche in place of the old \\\"I\\\'ll marry you when pigs fly out of my asshole!\\\" will be, \\\"I\\\'ll marry you when you stop smoking.\\\" ',3,176,0,0,'64.12.107.24','en'),(17617,2881,'2002-07-04 01:58:45',295,'karenlee','hahaha the secret marriage script thing is funny. Of course you cannot deny that some blokes also carry one of those around in their tackle box. My firsthand pre-marriage-script experience was being asked if I would marry him (the hook), a little while later saying yes (the sinker), then a little while later he said \\\"oh, but you are not the person who I required you to be\\\" (the line), then a little (but still too long) while later I jumped ship. Now he calls me twice a week to tell me what is in the tv guide. Go figure. Oh well, at least I can always find out what\\\'s on tv (with commentary). \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',1,619,265,0,'203.194.13.171','en'),(17618,2881,'2002-07-04 04:29:19',59,'s5','sounds like you should just stop dating blokes.',6,47,295,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17619,2878,'2002-07-04 04:40:19',176,'Scapegoat','<a href=\\\"http://www.aftermathpictures.com/\\\">Aftermath: the remnants of war</a> is a good documentary that touches on the dioxin deal in Vietnam. Also the fact that there are still areas of France being demined from WWII, the tresure hunting industry in Russian (around Stalingrad - don\\\'t know the post soviet name) related to WWII memorabilia dug up from old battlefields, and rebuilding attempt in post war Bosnia.\r\n\r\n',0,428,0,0,'64.229.160.13','en'),(17620,2883,'2002-07-04 04:43:21',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yeah, cause most <i>real</i> new yorkers are nice...</tt>',1,65,339,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17621,2881,'2002-07-04 07:41:47',295,'karenlee','huh? but that would be half the fun gone!! Also, <i>perhaps you should just stop dating blokes</i> sounds like as much of a generalisation as \\\"most women I\\\'ve encountered\\\". Oh, I see, perhaps chucksp has only encountered a small number of women...\r\n\r\n\r\n',1,262,59,0,'203.194.5.216','en'),(17622,2881,'2002-07-04 07:46:55',59,'s5','i wasn\\\'t making any generalizations, though ...',6,50,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17623,2881,'2002-07-04 08:40:50',295,'karenlee','oh, ok, i guess i don\\\'t understand your original comment then. i like blokes, so i will keep dating them....i certainly don\\\'t like to use the actions of one to represent the rest. my example was simply to balance chucksp\\\'s comment about women having marriage scripts. perhaps it was irrelevant.... \r\n \r\n\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',1,332,59,0,'203.221.83.237','en'),(17624,2881,'2002-07-04 11:24:48',265,'chuckspeed','pah!</p>\r\n\r\nphooey!</p>\r\n\r\nWe all do carry scripts of sorts, leftover snippets of our parents lives thru children\\\'s eyes interspersed with idealistic \\\'this-would-be-perfect-if\\\' fantasies.</p>\r\n\r\nthe trick is to \\\'share\\\' expectations with your SO, and be aware you both have a script.  Guys *usually* are more direct about such things; women tend to *sneak* them out as the relationship progresses - as if WE woudn\\\'t notice/mind a new restriction beng imposed on us.\r\n\r\nIf you choose to give up a vice for a partner - cool.  I tossed out 3 or 4 for my wife; pretty sure this is why i\\\'m still alive.\r\n\r\n',0,621,295,0,'64.12.107.159','en'),(17625,2881,'2002-07-04 13:06:17',295,'karenlee','yes! i quite agree with you! although I am confused about the pah! and phooey!! my comments re quitting smoking were not really related to my comments re being dicked around by a partner with a <u>hidden</u> script (as opposed to openly sharing expectations). ok that\\\'s it from me. : )\r\n\r\n\r\n',1,294,265,0,'203.194.18.215','en'),(17626,2881,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<p>chuckspeed, thank you for your advice to all women. i mean, all women are the same right? like all men? and we\\\'re all passive aggressive information with-holding bitches? thanks for showing me the light. oh, and by the way, you sound like a catch.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<strong>BURN! BURN!</strong></p>',6,296,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(17627,2881,'2002-07-04 17:29:39',124,'gus','has no one considered the fact that if she is planning to marry this guy, a \\\"lifetime commitment\\\", that she would prefer it if he lived a nice long and productive life? No one wants to have the thought of caring for a person dying of cancer or emphysema in the back of their mind. I dont think its sneaky, I think its practical. <p>And why would she worry about it pre engagement? There was no promise of a \\\"lifetime commitment\\\" then. <p> Not to mention, they could send their kids to college on the money he\\\'s going to spend smoking in the next 15 years. <p> and yah, I know most marriages arent lifetime long anymore, but that is totally beside the point. ',0,673,0,0,'64.160.55.53','en'),(17628,2885,'2002-07-04 18:56:11',17,'austin','you could put a black zorro bar over your eyes.  that would be cool.',3,68,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17629,2883,'2002-07-04 19:37:20',156,'lotus','the back of my shirt is still damp from the sweat during my evening walk, over the bridge, under a half-hidden moon, the sun absent but still felt. i\\\'d like to say i was with someone, but i wasn\\\'t. but that was good, strangely. i wanted the heat to burn off my skin, i was feeling raw and crazy. <br><br>\r\ni considered walking in the middle of the road, the little girl on my right shoulder coyly dared me too, but there were too many cars, and anyway, i\\\'ve given up on those urges months ago. sometimes i think, if i could just step off the roof, just one step, it wouldn\\\'t be much, i could prove something. <br><br>\r\nmy sister said the government had declared a state of heat emergency. i don\\\'t remember ottawa ever being this hot. it is the humidity. it creates edges for the air, edges that can slam into your face the moment you step outside. i danced on the bridge, bathed in heat and warmth and the night was like a swish of material on my skin.',1,967,339,0,'64.230.113.90','en'),(17630,2885,'2002-07-04 20:04:23',65,'le','hello lotus. i am glad you still exist.',4,39,0,0,'208.239.147.211','en'),(17631,2873,'2002-07-04 20:10:22',156,'lotus','rememeber when you were ten years old? <br>\r\nstanding in front of the french windows <br>\r\nyour face half in sun, half shadow<br>\r\nspinning spinning spinning<br>\r\naround the room, your feet lifting<br>\r\njust above your mother\\\'s cream carpet<br>\r\nyour body catching<br>\r\nyour mind already up there<br>\r\ndizzy, floating, dancing in mid-air<br>',0,344,0,0,'64.230.113.90','en'),(17632,2882,'2002-07-04 20:12:26',156,'lotus','matt, you rock. i am jealous of your range. i was reading this play and trying out different voices and i could _see it_ and it was weird. and cool. i want to write something *different.*',1,187,0,0,'64.230.113.90','en'),(17633,2881,'2002-07-04 20:14:15',156,'lotus','i did nothing but sit and feel the sun take over my body last night.',0,68,66,0,'64.230.113.90','en'),(17634,2879,'2002-07-04 20:23:44',156,'lotus','i admit it, i like wandering around ikea, though so far all i\\\'ve bought are the packages of art post cards. people here go to hang out there on saturdays, actually plan it, like going out for bowling and stuff. i\\\'ve been invited to a couple of such things. weirdness.',0,273,0,0,'64.230.113.90','en'),(17635,2878,'2002-07-04 20:25:08',156,'lotus','really? chapters does that? i want to know more.\r\n',0,50,0,0,'64.230.113.90','en'),(17636,2875,'2002-07-04 20:27:47',156,'lotus','i never know how to react when a stranger joins into a conversation on pubic transport. but they often say interesting things. ',0,127,0,0,'64.230.113.90','en'),(17637,2885,'2002-07-04 23:11:14',187,'kandrer','actually your post takes me my only motivation to post something... i was thinking about a fiction showing how islam and your (supposed) authoritarian husband kept you away from internet...</p><p>\r\nseriously, i\\\'m very happy to see you around (without photo is fine too) :)</p>',1,279,0,0,'80.13.151.241','en'),(17638,2885,'2002-07-04 23:50:49',122,'hool','lotus: i saw your face before i saw your words.  when i saw your words, they identified your face in a way that a photograph alone could not depict.\r\n\r\nwelcome back. it\\\'s good to see you again. now get back to writing stuff so that i have something good to read when i roll out of bed just before noon each day.',0,314,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17639,2883,'2002-07-05 02:34:59',353,'arabella','I think you only have to pay for the Mermaid parade if you want to sit in the stadium for the judging and ceremony.  I believe they still parade through the streets.. you know... before they all march into the baseball park with no clothes on, and plant their bums in the plastic seats where Brooklyn Cyclones fans will sit the very next night...',1,346,82,0,'66.68.45.204','en'),(17640,2882,'2002-07-05 02:55:52',353,'arabella','interesting... i enjoyed this, thanks.',2,38,0,0,'66.68.45.204','en'),(17641,2886,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','hey pal!  at least you got invited eh!!!',0,40,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17642,2886,'2002-07-05 05:45:01',86,'egg','oh my god! we tried to call you a million times today, and some weird noise came on and a recording.',0,100,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17643,2886,'2002-07-05 05:45:29',59,'s5','you should have come over!!!!!',0,30,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17644,2886,'2002-09-05 18:40:19',18,'ix','all\\\'s well that ends well!',0,29,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(17645,2664,'2002-09-05 18:40:19',18,'ix','update: the house was finally sold for something like $25k.',0,59,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(17646,2886,'2002-07-05 11:20:00',64,'Joanna','What\\\'s an oil can? Also, I think everyone should invite ix to their parties - I still remember being in Melbourne and being all apologetic for getting fucked up on 50% vodka and making him update my livejournal for me and then not remembering it in the mornign and all he said to me was \\\"Hey, I used to live in a fraternity, it\\\'s okay\\\". ',1,349,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(17647,2886,'2002-09-05 18:40:19',18,'ix','it\\\'s a 32oz can of foster\\\'s. my usual byo, a tradition that started in college for no particular reason.',0,110,64,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(17648,2881,'2002-07-05 13:38:44',265,'chuckspeed','weeb-</p>\r\n\r\nplease bush up on your reading skills.  The response did not say \\\'all\\\'; it said \\\'most.\\\'</p>\r\n\r\nsheesh.  whatsamatter - did it hit too close to home?  fear and loathing directed at latent passive/agressive tendencies, perhaps?</p>\r\n\r\ndon\\\'t quite get the catch comment, either. i\\\'m married - 16 years now - to a woman who asked me to share her life. we discuss subjects as this all the time; she is studying for her masters in social work.</p>\r\n\r\nmy first burn! burn! - thanks.\r\n',3,508,57,0,'208.5.114.92','en'),(17649,2881,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i\\\'m sure it\\\'s not your first.',0,35,265,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(17650,2881,'2002-07-05 19:26:08',74,'jo','i agree with gus that you have different concerns when thinking about spending your life with someone than you do when you\\\'re just dating that person, but it\\\'s not as if all women are thinking, \\\"well this behavior is just fine now, but if he wants to marry me, he\\\'ll have to <b>change, change, change!</b>\\\" and i\\\'m with weeb that making it seem as if all (or most) women have that agenda is erroneous and offensive.',1,433,0,0,'172.158.243.164','en'),(17651,2881,'2002-07-05 19:30:13',74,'jo','also, i don\\\'t know anything about you, but being married for (x number) of years doesn\\\'t your generalizations any more true or give you special insight into women. i know way more married clueless assholes than single ones.<BR>\r\nwhich isn\\\'t to say you\\\'re a clueless asshole, but i think the, \\\"well i\\\'m married, so i know!\\\" doesn\\\'t fly, yo.',0,363,265,0,'172.158.243.164','en'),(17652,2881,'2002-07-05 21:41:21',17,'austin','<P>my script involves me and the sort of love of my life moving to one of those inner suburbs, the ones that were far away from down town 100 years ago but now are pretty close in, and buying an old but quaint house, with a dog, and two (2) volvo station wagons.  we both are very liberal and have stickers validating this on our volvos, one each of the following on each volvo: local low end dial member radio station, local zoo, presidential race stickers from election more than 4 years ago for the liberal and possibly obscure democrat or \\\'green party\\\' candidate lost.  gore lieberman! we also have a mutt dog that we got from the local not-for-profit no-kill shelter, and another from a rescue club, something big like a bull mastif.  we have two kids, they are in school at the neigborhood accredited co-op of home schoolers.  the wife, she teaches there 2 days aweek.  i teach  there 1 day a week.  on the weekends, the whole family volunteers at the place of their choice, the kids were not happy with this at first but they got into it.  compulsory volunteer service.  we are pillars of the community. ',2,1117,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17653,2881,'2002-07-05 21:47:11',74,'jo','you should host the \\\"world music\\\" show on said low-end of the dial volunteer radio station and your wife should wear j. crew yoga pants but never do yoga.',3,160,17,0,'172.131.55.122','en'),(17654,2881,'2002-07-06 00:54:17',75,'tyler','but luckily, just by doing yoga every once in a while, she would validate your whole existance...now <i>there\\\'s</i> a motivation to exercise!',1,144,0,0,'165.247.164.234','en'),(17655,2848,'2002-07-06 02:50:30',170,'xipibi','hang in there... [sh]it happens...  your plans are good re exercise & new beginnings...  work can be therapy...  keep in touch with those who do love you... call your mom\\\'n\\\'dad, family will always be there... her loss is your gain...  breakups are liberating... look forward always...',1,290,0,0,'209.39.34.189','en'),(17656,2881,'2002-07-06 04:44:11',59,'s5','you left out ikea, shnookums.',0,29,17,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17657,2887,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','cyoot, love is grand and so is head on chest time.',6,50,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17658,2887,'2002-07-06 06:46:40',66,'Peter','<tt>my name is peter carroll. i am a sleep-talker. sleeptalking can be a dangerous thing</tt>',1,93,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17659,2878,'2002-07-06 07:19:57',223,'shanE','The USA also used chemical warfare when they were giving blankets laced with smallpox to the Natives during colonial days, my korean confidente!\r\n\r\nAnd, here in Geumo, Seoul South Korea, the regulars are lacing our allyways and washroom with a stench that the North Koreans wouldn t be able to bear! How could you forget to mention this?!',3,338,0,0,'211.32.132.229','en'),(17660,2887,'2002-07-06 17:18:12',180,'auntie','when we were still dating, back in 1972, mark got out of bed walked in circles saying over and over \\\" the officers are coming\\\".  a few years later he did it again.  he has never had an explanation for this, he has no memory of it.  i think it had something to do with a protest march he was in way back in the days of protest marches, when we all made big circles and waited to be dragged away by \\\"the officers.\\\"\r\n\r\n',0,428,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(17661,2885,'2002-07-06 17:19:02',124,'gus','why not post some pictures you\\\'re not in? give us a window.',0,62,0,0,'64.160.55.53','en'),(17662,2887,'2002-07-06 20:31:21',65,'le','i love sleeptalkers. i feel like people who talk in their sleep are telling me secrets. some sleeptalking incidents:\r\n<br><br>\r\n- sleeping lover grinds teeth in sleep. i gently touched his face and whispered <i>you\\\'re grinding your teeth</i> and he sat up halfway, and barked at me, saying: NONSENSE! then rolled over. he was quite adamant, tho he\\\'s rarely that adamant in waking life. \r\n<br><br>\r\n- recently i have been having sleepovers with an israeli gentleman who talks in his sleep in half hebrew and half english. also, the other night he said MY GOD! in his sleep and the next morning when i asked him about he remembered exactly what he was dreaming at the time. he encouraged me to wake him up after he\\\'s done dreaming if he sleeptalks, and ask him what he was dreaming about. \r\n<br><br>\r\n- drunken roomate stumbles out of room, wearing one sock and a jacket and nothing else. he raises one finger at the occupants of the living room, and declares: IT MUST BE THE BETAMAX. then he started yelling random numbers and went back to bed. next morning, no recollection.\r\n<br><br>\r\n- yeti sleeptalks pretty regular. it\\\'s usually a lot of mumbling, but sometimes you catch words. the words always make you wish you knew the whole sentence. i have caught him sleeptalking about masturbation, macintoshes, and also muttering things like \\\"this\\\'ll fix it!\\\"\r\n<br><br>\r\ni do not sleeptalk much that i know of. jo seems like a prime candidate for sleeptalking, though i can\\\'t explain why i think that. mmm, bed.',4,1531,0,0,'208.239.147.211','en'),(17663,2887,'2002-07-06 22:26:14',74,'jo','i knew that i sleeptalked as a child. at slumber parties, if i fell asleep while friends were still awake talking, i would frequently chime in on the subject (while sleeping) several minutes after the converssation was over, because i was still having the conversation in my dream.<BR>\r\ni didn\\\'t know that i still did it, though. the other night, about five minutes after i fell asleep, i rolled over and said, \\\"mike, what time is everyone else getting to the theater?\\\" and he started laughing, which woke me up.',1,521,0,0,'172.153.186.205','en'),(17664,2889,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i puke on EC.  luckily s5 and i seem to have the whole rhythm/withdrawl down (it\\\'s been working for like 10 months so far).  i was going to try the IUD but the more i think about it the more scared i get.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nthere has got to be a better way.',6,252,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17665,2889,'2002-07-07 11:28:56',89,'sawks','anal? :P',1,8,62,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(17666,2889,'2002-07-07 11:52:13',64,'Joanna','This is good, like not the having to get the EC bit (although it\\\'s really well written), but the way that it\\\'s so obvious that you guys care about each other, and that you could communicate that, and everything.\r\n\r\nWhen I had to take the EC, the guy offered to come with me, but i said \\\"no, it\\\'s no big deal\\\" although I was freaking, and then when I went to see him afterwards, he was with his girlfriend, and that more than anything made me feel like a fallen woman (and this is where you can all feel free to say that well maybe I am). ',1,553,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(17667,2889,'2002-07-07 15:17:16',74,'jo','<p>there has <b>got</b> to be a better way.<BR>\r\ni loathe birth control. i was on it for six months a few years ago and it made me an emotional wreck. it\\\'s ironic to me that i would want to take birth control the most when in an intimate relationship, but that the pill would make me such a spaz, which is the last thing i want when in an intimate relationship. if i could just take the pill when i was single and be bitchy to my cats rather than my boyfriend.</p>\r\n',1,469,0,0,'172.158.68.119','en'),(17668,2889,'2002-07-07 21:22:24',295,'karenlee','Ditto on the pill - nausea, headaches, bad moods etc. etc. My sister tried an IUD but had nothing but pain and trouble. So that\\\'s out. Of course, condoms are necessary for the single gal but in fully tested relationship, my best option so far has been rhythm/withdrawal, which is a bit scary. \r\n\r\n\r\n',1,302,0,0,'203.194.15.244','en'),(17669,2821,'2002-07-07 21:35:18',187,'kandrer','i just updated the blurb. i noticed how (unnecessarily) upset i have been about Moll\\\'s comment. i need to be clear:<br>\r\nthe fact that i feel scared to discuss with chinese people before actually knowing if they support their government is a racist behavior.<br>\r\nthough, i have nothing against chinese people. nothing. i don\\\'t believe in any \\\"race\\\" criterium as a way to evaluate people. ',1,401,0,0,'80.13.233.40','en'),(17670,2889,'2002-07-07 23:20:51',124,'gus','yah, the pill made me nuts. what a relief to go off it and realize I was not nuts! am still undecided on the iud, someone I know just had theirs removed because they just couldnt take the pain. (And they\\\'d had a kid in the past, which is supposed to make it easier, so). going to stick with foam for now.',0,307,0,0,'64.160.55.53','en'),(17671,2889,'2002-07-08 01:45:34',59,'s5','the IUD seems like one of those things where it\'s a coin toss for whether it\'s going to be bliss or agony. :(',0,114,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17672,2889,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i went off the pill and ChaCHing!@ i rediscovered my sex drive and realized i was crazygirl on the pill. too late to save that particular relationship, but hey. this one is pill-free and going strong! condoms are nice, but i think i\\\'m going to give the cervical cap and the iud a try. *hugs of commiseration* i know, jo. it sucks. i\\\'m on an all female mailing list, and this topic comes up every few months, to the obvious conclusion of it all being a crapshoot as to what will work for you. grr.',0,502,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(17673,2889,'2002-07-08 04:17:11',64,'Joanna','When I was on Femulen, I was psychocrazygirl with no sex drive, so I stopped taking that. Now, 2 years later, I\\\'m taking estelle35 for non contraceptive purposes, and I don\\\'t think it\\\'s changing me, although it might be to blame for a huge evil migraine that I had.  I know lots of people who\\\'ve had bad experiances on one kind of pill and then a great time on another, so if you really feel like the pill would be the best kind of birth control for you, then maybe you could try out a couple of different kinds? ',1,525,74,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(17674,2878,'2002-07-08 04:20:38',334,'gone','<p>it\\\'s funny when you realize that the classical mustard gas (a di(ethylchloro) methyl amine) developed way way back is still used as an anti-cancer agent today.  sometimes i get so buried in azirdines and nitrosoureas and trazines and tying them to HIV-1 Tat peptides and more science mumbo-jumbo that i forget where it all came from.</p><p>people wanted to kill each other.  now we use it to slowly kill ourselves.  funny how that works.</p>',2,447,0,0,'64.108.128.15','en'),(17675,2878,'2002-07-08 04:43:36',66,'Peter','<br><tt>amines are something beautiful</tt>',1,43,334,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17676,2889,'2002-07-08 05:25:01',89,'sawks','the man pill needs to happen<br>\r\n<br>\r\nwe\\\'d forget to take it of course, so i suggest that it should be added to beer<br><br>\r\n\\\"honey, can you grab a sixer of jizz wrangler on your way home?\\\"<br><br>\r\neveryones a winner! :)',3,233,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(17677,2889,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','what about suppositories delivered via strap on?\r\n\r\nBEND OVER BOYFRIEND',6,71,89,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17678,555,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','please never do this again. - the management',6,44,59,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17679,2889,'2002-07-08 12:39:24',66,'Peter','<Br><tt><A href=\"http://www.vasectomy.cc/\">vasectomy</a> is a good answer for guys that want to do their part, heh. thanks to non-invasive microsurgery, its supposedly relatively cheap and painless. and its (most of the time) reversable in case you get second thoughts years later. i, myself, am the product of a done/undone/done/re-undone vasectomy, (and that was 25 years ago, in lotech times). so yeah. although most people save such a proceedure until theyre in a permanent relationship, it is relatively foolproof and hassle-free.<br><br>so go ahead guys, cringe and freak about someone snipping away at your testes... but it might, at least at some point, be better than the alternatives that the girls have to go through. at least nutsnipping doesnt make you go crazy or perforate your uterus or take you on emotional rollercoaster rides... i once lived with a girl who was a slave to ortho tri-cyclen\'s whims- no one deserves the effects of that shit.</tt>',1,340,89,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17680,2889,'2002-07-08 14:57:18',67,'trouble','i have thought about this.  moreso because i don\\\'t think i will ever desire children.  with all the effort put into contraceptives it would be a nice thought not to have to worry about such things.  <p>my birthday is in a couple of weeks, maybe that will be my present to myself.  </p><p><i>snippity snip</i>',1,311,66,0,'68.47.202.98','en'),(17681,2889,'2002-07-08 16:07:48',74,'jo','ortho is the pill that i was on. jesus christ, it was awful. but like joanna said, it came highly recommended from a friend who\\\'d said she never experienced side effects with it, so pills can really vary from person to person.<BR>\r\nand IUDs are out for me because i plan on having kids within the next five years, or i would at least like the option.<BR>\r\nreproduction just convinces me that there must be a higher power. \\\"okay, human offspring are an incredible pain in the ass, a huge sacrifice, so how can i ensure their procreation? i know, i\\\'ll make reproduction <b>the best thing ever</b>, so good that the humans crave it constantly, and i\\\'ll make contraceptives ridiculously complicated and risky! THERE WILL BE BABIES! MANY MANY BABIES! MUAHHAHAHAHA.\\\"<BR>\r\nor something.',1,794,0,0,'172.147.171.85','en'),(17682,2889,'2002-07-08 17:24:43',81,'felicity','<p>I\\\'m a big Depo fan.  One shot every 3 months.  No daily regimen.  No \\\"monthly visitor.\\\"  I\\\'m sure I save at least as much as the shot costs by not having to buy feminie hygiene products.  The only time I ever went off it, I got pregnant due to condom slippage (If it hadn\\\'t been for my cervix, I think the damn thing would have found it\\\'s way to my spleen.)</p>\r\n<p>Pills were always too tricky (ok....I\\\'m lazy) and side effect ridden for me.  I\\\'m still a big fan of the condom, but I like the piece of mind that Depo offers without having to think about birth control on a daily basis.</p>',1,617,0,0,'216.42.123.44','en'),(17683,2889,'2002-07-08 18:35:06',186,'ook','<p>a testament to how much contraceptive technology sucks: i rank different methods almost entirely on how easy it is to back out of them when they start sucking.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>if depo sucks, it sucks for THREE MONTHS plus.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>if the pill makes you bonky, you can quit or switch brands that very day (except that doctors seem to like you to \\\"see if it settles\\\" for 3-5 mo).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>etc.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>may the force be with you! waiting to find out about babies/no babies is surreal.</p>',0,493,0,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(17684,2889,'2002-07-08 18:48:52',265,'chuckspeed','all methods of birth control suck.</p>\r\n\r\nmy wife\\\'s latest thought on the subject (and a good one) is to disable the male reproductive system at puberty, and enable it again at such time that one actually WANTS chilren.  eliminates the ten-story stomach drop at the thought of an unwanted pregnancy, and simplifies situations like this.</p>\r\n\r\ngood luck, girl...hope it all turns out the way you want it to.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',0,420,0,0,'208.5.114.49','en'),(17685,2885,'2002-07-08 19:31:48',156,'lotus','le. i must confess i have snuck onto swin to read you and gus and a few other people. but heat and marriage have drained away my own capacity to write. actually, i\\\'m terrified i will never write again. <br>\r\naustin and gus: good suggestions. i\\\'m considering them, because i do want to show you guys the wedding. <br>\r\nkandrer. sweet kandrer. do not worry.<br>\r\nhool. i bumped into a mutual acquaintance on canada day, who said he saw you downtown in <i>ottawa</i>? if you were here, you are so so dead for not letting me know! btw, what is your (new?) email?<br>\r\n-lotus',0,576,0,0,'64.230.113.218','en'),(17686,2889,'2002-07-08 19:33:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt>only problem with tri-cyclen is that, metabolically speaking, it activates changes in hormone levels that stick with you for a while. once youve been taking it for a few weeks (the necessary time for it to reach effectiveness and also let you know if its right for you in teh first place- noe one really only takes it for a few days), simply <i>switching brands that very day</i> isn\\\'t as good a fix as you might think. firstly, it can take weeks to months for things to realign in your body. secondly, no competent doctor is going to throw more chemicals into your endocrine system the day after you stop one pill. its a no win situation. its basically frustrating and alienating to those unfortunate enough to have to do it, and to those that care about the people having to endure the weirdness in their own bodies.<br><br>sorry, had to free my inner doctor there. i still say vasectomy. accessable, cheap, easy, almost infallably reliable (with proper sperm-count testing for the first month or so after the surgery), available on an outpatient basis, and doesnt require fucking with your body/brain/metabolic/hormonal chemistry.</tt>',1,1149,186,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17687,2889,'2002-07-08 19:33:42',156,'lotus','i have to say things here but i can\\\'t say them. i\\\'m too embarrassed and overwhelmed. this comment will have to serve as a place holder/trigger for when i\\\'m ready.',0,171,0,0,'64.230.113.218','en'),(17688,2889,'2002-07-08 22:28:00',186,'ook','<p>hey good points peter. i didn\\\'t mean to imply that pills go away instantly (it takes what, three months on average before your cycle steadies at all after you quit ocp?). i was just saying that with pills at least you can decide what day you start recovering (or what day you start waiting for a new cycle to try again with something else), whereas with depo you have to wait 3 months for the dose to wear off and then start recovering.</p>',0,446,0,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(17689,2885,'2002-07-08 23:17:39',187,'kandrer','*blushes* ok! :}',1,16,156,0,'80.13.151.57','en'),(17690,2878,'2002-07-08 23:21:38',334,'gone','<p>the small ones are stinky; TEA, DIPEA, and pyridine are kind of repulsive but kind of nice at the same time.  maybe i should keep my head out of my hood.</p>',2,160,66,0,'144.92.164.199','en'),(17691,2878,'2002-07-09 11:26:42',66,'Peter','<br><tt>you could make a poem: triethylamine, diisopropylethylamine, diethylhydroxylamine, la la la.<br><br>but its the good ones like n-methyldiethanolamine and straight-up methylamine that are in high demand around here. i have plasticizing agents in my hood; i think the fumes are currently making me crazy.</tt>',1,315,334,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17692,2878,'2002-07-09 14:23:09',93,'chiamattt','i have gangsta rap in my hood. HOOTY HOO',0,40,0,0,'61.37.53.97','en'),(17693,2878,'2002-07-09 15:07:12',334,'gone','<p>totally!  a poem of amines.  do natural alkaloids count?  because those have some pretty kickin\\\' names.</p>',2,113,66,0,'64.108.128.15','en'),(17694,2878,'2002-07-09 15:35:19',66,'Peter','<br><tt>cyclic organic alkaloids do have names with mass appeal: particularly <i>nicotine</i>, <i>cocaine</i> and <i>morphine</i>. well, and <i>quinine</i>, for all the health nuts. <br><br>if <A href=\"http://www.cognitiveliberty.com/shulgin/adsarchive/archiveindex.htm\">dr. shulgin</a> can write a <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0963009605/qid=1026228633/sr=1-1/ref=sr_1_1/002-7048388-5620823\">love story</a> for phenethylamines, i guess i can do poems about chemicals :D</tt>',1,505,334,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17695,2881,'2002-07-09 17:17:53',66,'Peter','<br><tt>change it to an audi wagon, and that dude lives across the street from me</tt>',1,88,17,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17696,2893,'2002-07-09 17:41:20',83,'adamajah','this made me laugh',2,18,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(17697,2893,'2002-07-09 17:59:30',17,'austin','i laughed allot too.',3,20,0,0,'205.179.19.127','en'),(17698,2887,'2002-07-09 18:09:31',129,'moebius','<br>i sleeptalk pretty regularly. the things i\\\'m told i talk about have changed over the years. back in my early twenties i apparently had long arguments with either my sister or mother: \\\"mom, i can\\\'t, i\\\'m late.\\\" \\\"i\\\'ll take care of it later.\\\" \\\"mom! give me a break!\\\" and so on.<br><br>then later it was work. the stillness of the night would be broken by lectures on how this bit or that aspect of some mundane piece of hr enterprise software worked. sometimes i recited text word-for-word from the manuals i was writing during the day. later, when during my brief shot as a manager, i held staff meetings at 3 am, with the groggy cats at the foot of the bed as my audience.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nbut i\\\'m always worried about the sleeptalking, because i never know what i say. once a girl i was dating broke up with me because i said something insulting about her in my sleep. it was something i couldn\\\'t imagine ever saying in the waking world. i don\\\'t know where it came from. but she decided that i was probably being more truthful about my feelings when i was asleep than when i was awake and talking to her. so she left.',1,1155,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(17699,2893,'2002-07-09 18:13:00',129,'moebius','hilarious. :D',1,13,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(17700,2893,'2002-07-09 18:42:15',176,'Scapegoat','beauty',0,6,0,0,'204.19.79.66','en'),(17701,2893,'2002-07-09 19:11:24',187,'kandrer','shit, i\\\'m afraid i said again recently that americans had no sense of humor. *i* am the jerk.',1,96,0,0,'80.13.150.228','en'),(17702,2893,'2002-07-09 20:05:11',67,'trouble','outstanding.  <p>thanks austin for bringing everything back.  i regret quoting Nietzsche but it seemed only fitting that i add to the drama. ;)',1,143,0,0,'68.47.202.98','en'),(17703,2893,'2002-07-09 20:48:38',83,'adamajah','well, actually he\\\'s canadian.  and we all know how funny canadians are.',0,74,187,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(17704,2893,'2002-07-09 21:05:20',180,'auntie','does this mean i am not gonna get my dividends?',6,47,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(17705,2878,'2002-07-09 21:10:48',334,'gone','<p>\\\"That said, if you wouldn\\\'t know a carbon atom from a Honda Civic...\\\"</p><p>it could be a whole song even.  do you think it should be to-the-tune-of, or an original composition?</p>',0,193,66,0,'144.92.164.199','en'),(17706,2893,'2002-07-09 23:29:07',120,'joyrider','i hope SWIN does not get delisted!',3,34,0,0,'12.224.184.3','en'),(17707,2584,'2002-07-10 00:00:19',83,'adamajah','that has got to be the best goddamn swinney title ever',0,54,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(17708,2894,'2002-07-10 01:48:17',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i have said it before, and i shall say it again: moerex kicks a mean lyric.</tt>',1,88,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17709,2894,'2002-07-10 02:21:46',67,'trouble','i see this as first in a really good series moebius.  a <i>really good</i> series.  ',1,84,0,0,'68.47.202.98','en'),(17710,2821,'2002-07-10 02:53:23',176,'Scapegoat','My questions are this - If one has negative views of certain groups based on the clash between ones own values and theirs is one then a racist or prejudice? How does one stand for something with out being against its opposite - If the opposite of something one stands for or believes in is a core characteristic of a given other group is one then prejudice with all its negative connotations? Is there ever a time when prejudicial treatment is desirable?<br><br>\r\nIf one beleives in equality of people, does one not hold all humans behaviour up for scrutiny against an your own individual value system? or do we beleive in the equality of cultures and values systems before people?<br><br>\r\n\r\nBest friendly - you always get me thinking Kandrer   ',1,746,0,0,'64.229.162.41','en'),(17711,2892,'2002-07-10 05:43:50',175,'Paul G','Hey, K: My heart is with you.',0,29,0,0,'65.144.160.252','en'),(17712,2893,'2002-07-10 09:43:02',295,'karenlee','nice detail: i like the way he pleaded the fifth again before running away. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',1,84,0,0,'203.221.80.21','en'),(17713,2821,'2002-07-10 10:07:13',187,'kandrer','thank you :). do you think i can answer it all with this:<br>\r\nif one believes that such thing as core characteristics of a given group exists, then one has prejudice.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<u>a naive example:</u><br>\r\nthe french group is defined (let\\\'s say) by people who have the french nationality.<br>\r\nit\\\'s OK to say: \\\"more red wine per inhabitant is drunk in france than in the US\\\".<br>\r\nit should not be OK to say: \\\"french drink more red wine than american\\\".<br>\r\nif you say so, you try to define a core characteristic of the \\\"french nationality\\\" group.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni think this is where it all starts, if one pushes the \\\"prejudice\\\" notice completely. people belong to an infinite number of groups. the groups of \\\"race\\\" and \\\"citizenship\\\" might exist, but they cannot gather more than one core characteristic each.<br>\r\nfor example, i can decide to call a race the people who have green eyes, and this won\\\'t be a prejudice until i try to associate other characteristics to my green eyes race. if i say \\\"green eyes run faster\\\", there i go...</p><p>\r\nstatistics have been used to trick us in a wrong direction. publishing that \\\"65% of people in jail in Mozambic have green eyes\\\" is tricking people to get bias. this is dangerously efficient on us because our brain also works by making generalizations. that\\\'s why humans are racist by nature.\r\n</p><p>\r\n(i did not think too much about this; this is an open discussion, feel free to correct me)\r\n</p>',1,1499,176,0,'80.14.219.18','en'),(17714,2821,'2002-07-10 10:10:51',187,'kandrer','(sorry, i wrote <i>notice</i> instead of <i>notion</i>)',1,55,0,0,'80.14.219.18','en'),(17715,2892,'2002-07-10 10:24:12',187,'kandrer','thank you',1,9,0,0,'80.14.219.18','en'),(17716,2892,'2002-07-10 15:01:12',74,'jo','this is a beautiful piece and i\\\'m very sorry for your loss.',1,62,0,0,'172.171.215.168','en'),(17717,2893,'2002-07-10 15:15:45',278,'egenus','tee hee',1,7,0,0,'66.141.181.43','en'),(17718,2791,'2002-07-10 15:53:58',246,'erb','jo, i just went and looked at your website for the first time, and you are amazing. so witty! so cute! so knowledgable about indie rock!\r\n\r\nmaybe i will send you fanmail later.',1,176,74,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(17719,1102,'2002-07-10 16:06:16',246,'erb','<p>running away from denial seems like a bad idea. however, i commend you and hope that now, more than a year later, you are happy.</p>\r\n<p>what i like most about this piece is the idea of <i>movement</i>.  i have also been of the mind lately that you just have to keep on moving, keep movement happening, keep going into the fire and burning up.  redefining yourself/your reality.  health is a function of balanced movement.</p>',1,429,0,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(17720,2821,'2002-07-10 16:55:34',176,'Scapegoat','Ok, I agree with you on the whole defining core characteristics thing and how that relates to one having prejudice.<br><br>\r\nI\\\'m interested though in how you\\\'d relate this strain of thought to the following.<br><br>\r\nConflict on a large social scale (idealogical debate all the way to outright violence). How does one determine if the conflict is based on a clash of values or issues of racial or national prejudice? When I asked if you ever felt that there might be a time when prejudicial treatment was justified. What I was driving at is that I think idealogical/value prejudices are necessary (how would one make any judgements otherwise) and I think that sometimes people don\\\'t distinguish the basis of certain prejudicial action. Your thoughts on  this? ',1,769,0,0,'204.19.79.66','en'),(17721,2889,'2002-07-10 17:13:11',81,'felicity','<p>Did I actually type \\\"piece of mind?\\\"  (With my current cellulite status, I would much prefer \\\"peace of ass.\\\")  And I thought that Depo didn\\\'t cause any side-effects for me.  Apparently it has caused some sort of homonym transposition disorder.  Dam...too more months bee four eye start two right write again.</p>\r\n<p>Terrible puns aside, good point about the waiting period.  I never even took into consideration that I might want off and not be able to do anything about it.</p>',1,497,0,0,'216.42.123.44','en'),(17722,2895,'2002-07-10 21:02:36',82,'simon','that\\\'s fuckin right!',0,23,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17723,2896,'2002-07-10 21:47:00',295,'karenlee','We evolved? heh! A good read was had here.',1,42,0,0,'203.194.12.214','en'),(17724,2892,'2002-07-10 22:41:30',234,'etienne','its funny the things that we say in an effort to console when often the best thing is just a companionable silence.  when my stepfather died i didn\\\'t want anyone to say anything, i just wanted to know that they were there in case i needed someone to say something.',1,267,187,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(17725,2887,'2002-07-10 22:45:58',58,'chaya','i giggle in my sleep.  sometimes i wake myself up laughing really hard.  i find this pleasing, other people have been known to find it disturbing.',1,146,0,0,'64.168.29.42','en'),(17726,2791,'2002-07-10 23:07:49',74,'jo','well, uh, i\\\'m blushing furiously. thanks.',1,44,246,0,'172.163.156.53','en'),(17727,2892,'2002-07-11 02:11:41',170,'xipibi','Thanks.  My Dad is at that point now.  Every time the phone rings, I expect it to be \\\"The Call.\\\"  Birth and death...the defining points of a life...',1,154,0,0,'209.39.34.112','en'),(17728,2896,'2002-07-11 03:10:56',66,'Peter','<br><tt>were you stoned when you wrote this? it seems like it would be uberprofound were i stoned right now. <i>best friendly!</i></tt>',1,135,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17729,2893,'2002-07-11 04:00:34',353,'arabella','Fabulous. Have you thought about writing for the Onion?',2,55,0,0,'66.68.45.204','en'),(17730,2899,'2002-07-11 05:54:26',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this is great...</tt>',1,29,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17731,2899,'2002-07-11 06:58:15',175,'Paul G','Well, it is hard to find anything evil in <a href=\\\"http://artscenecal.com/ArticlesFile/Archive/Articles2000/Articles0600/TJankay2.html\\\">this.</a>',0,151,0,0,'65.144.128.219','en'),(17732,2896,'2002-07-11 15:36:17',175,'Paul G','It is one thing to break yourself on <a href=\\\"http://www.blupete.com/Literature/Poetry/Wreck.htm\\\">on the reef of Norman\\\'s Woe</a>, and it\\\'s another thing to break someone else along with you - especially if you refuse to tell them where you\\\'re headed.',0,266,0,0,'65.145.10.98','en'),(17733,2898,'2002-07-11 15:48:15',122,'hool','i really liked the undulations of both the description and the feelings it imparts.  it\\\'s close writing, and it\\\'s really good.  \r\nfinishing off with \\\"bangin\\\'\\\" felt a bit awkward - although you started off with it, there was too much flow and substance in between, so it made me forget that you started off with the same word you finished with.  i think that\\\'s why it felt awkward when i saw it again - but it\\\'s not a major issue.',0,450,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17734,2899,'2002-07-11 17:00:26',86,'egg','dude thats tight. also, waterpig is a fantastic nick.',0,53,0,0,'216.175.89.40','en'),(17735,2899,'2002-07-11 18:44:11',87,'bmg','after reading this, I thought about sending this to atleast five tormented artist friends of mine for review... but then, how would that change their point of view... would they become tormented because of their lack of self? So I didn\\\'t send it to them thinking it would be too harsh of a critisism for them to handle. I never knew this about Tibor Jankay, nor can I really say that I know much about art in general; but this was informative. Thanks, great writing too.',1,473,0,0,'17.104.194.79','en'),(17736,2898,'2002-07-11 20:11:35',175,'Paul G','Thanks hool, a lot, for the critique and praise.  I like the things that you make, and so this carries some weight.  I\\\'m struggling with writing, among other things.',0,168,0,0,'65.144.129.28','en'),(17737,2893,'2002-07-11 22:04:11',183,'lin','<p>The word \\\"shmocks\\\" just made my vocabulary. Nicely done.</p>',0,69,0,0,'137.122.14.20','en'),(17738,2894,'2002-07-12 00:15:44',234,'etienne','have i mentioned that you rock?',1,31,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(17739,2899,'2002-07-12 02:37:55',75,'tyler','i liked this an awful lot with the exception of the first sentence--nanotechnology and ethnic cleansing? but yeah, really good article. i have to say, there are lots of good happy artists out there making happy art and i think that\\\'s cool. it\\\'s rare that i find someone who agrees. even darker artists often use pain as more of a spice than the main course.\r\n<p>\r\nps- by &quot;Heavy&quot; do you mean heroin? i appreciate personal oblivion but maybe you should stick to nitrous. ',1,485,0,0,'165.247.164.196','en'),(17740,2898,'2002-07-12 02:43:31',75,'tyler','i liked it very much too. there\\\'s some cool experimentation going on, the word in quotes especially. really cool. keep your head up. you are obviously a bright and sensitive guy, &quot;and that ain\\\'t half bad.&quot;',1,221,0,0,'165.247.164.196','en'),(17741,2865,'2002-07-12 02:45:45',75,'tyler','that\\\'s hilarious. i think i\\\'ve gotten over it--i toook a lot of E that weekend (bonnaroo!), so maybe my empathy meter was just too high to cut into living flesh without feeling it myself. but i bet that would make an AWESOME psychology experiment with MDMA...',1,265,0,0,'165.247.164.196','en'),(17742,2899,'2002-07-12 03:09:36',358,'waterpig','i hear you on that opening line. duly noted. heavy\\\'s just a potent scottish beer. i could have written irn-bru there, but i fear the effect (and the truth) might have suffered. ',0,180,75,0,'208.155.246.243','en'),(17743,2899,'2002-07-12 03:11:50',358,'waterpig','thanks for the comment, and i\\\'m glad you like wp.  swamp mote dubbed me thus.',0,80,86,0,'208.155.246.243','en'),(17744,2899,'2002-07-12 03:29:09',358,'waterpig','much obliged. it was one of those rare moments where i actually had a point.',0,76,66,0,'208.155.246.243','en'),(17745,2899,'2002-07-12 03:38:24',358,'waterpig','i\\\'m not sure that the truly agonised artist would care much about jankay (or perhaps they would)...it\\\'s more just a pleasant reminder that being exceptional and creative don\\\'t have to rely on being tormented. thanks for the positive feedback by the way.  it cut my agony of not having any Heavy in half. ',0,313,87,0,'208.155.246.243','en'),(17746,2658,'2002-07-12 03:47:17',74,'jo','i had a blackened yellowfin tuna sandwich today at hyde park grill and it was really tasty. mmmm, remoulade sauce. ALL HAIL TUNA.',1,129,0,0,'172.172.200.153','en'),(17747,2896,'2002-07-12 03:47:41',358,'waterpig','point taken. and my opposable thumbs are overrated anyway.',0,58,295,0,'208.155.246.243','en'),(17748,2896,'2002-07-12 03:49:21',358,'waterpig','actually, the more stoned you get, the meager profundity that\\\'s actually there diffuses. go figure.',0,102,66,0,'208.155.246.243','en'),(17749,2896,'2002-07-12 03:51:45',358,'waterpig','amen.  and the link is greatly appreciated.',0,43,175,0,'208.155.246.243','en'),(17750,2658,'2002-07-12 12:10:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i saw them dicing whole fatty tuna the other day on some lurid rerun of <i>iron chef</i>. the first thing that popped into my mind was this article, followed closely by a curiosity regarding exactly <i>why</i> the chef was saving the entrails.</tt>',1,256,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17751,2899,'2002-07-12 16:59:53',129,'moebius','<br>this reminds me of a weird conversation i had with a political science professor back when i was a university student. i\\\'d originally gone to see him to apply for a paid position as his research assistant. <br><br>but when he found out i was a creative writing major, he changed the subject to this weird paper he\\\'d written in his spare time. its point was that anecdotal evidence indicated that writers had the most dangerous of all artistic professions. why? because of all the different types of artists they were the most prone to suicide.<br><br>i think he was trying to warn me away from becoming a writer, but i figured i\\\'d be more likely to off myself if i couldn\\\'t write, so here i am, working as a writer and then writing even more in my spare time. (and i\\\'m only \\\"tortured\\\" and \\\"filled with angst\\\" on sunday afternoons and alternate tuesdays. no suicide plans as of yet.)',2,913,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(17752,2025,'2002-07-12 18:00:07',86,'egg','I must come back to this now because Im getting a rash again!! its the same rash!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!! I never figured out what it was before and all I can do now is try not to scratch. I have discovered the cause of my symptom: <b> the result of stressing while simultaneously denying oneself the sense of entitlement to stress. </b> we must  now create a sniglet for this sickness.',0,377,0,0,'216.175.89.40','en'),(17753,2899,'2002-07-12 18:03:09',124,'gus','I\\\'ve met a few holocaust survivors and once wrote some papers on the artists of the ghettos for a class called Faith in the Holocaust. The people were/are often unbelievably calm and gentle and forgiving of their situations. dont get me wrong, I\\\'ve never met any that excused what happened, there is just something about the way the people I\\\'ve met deal with it. There are beautiful poems by 10 year olds about the wonder of butterflys. Nevermind that they were starving and living 12 to a room and being ravaged by diptheria when they wrote it. \r\n<p>\r\nI am sure that having escaped certain death, and then even finding his lost wife (!) your man Jankay knew he was a lucky bastard. ',0,692,0,0,'64.169.160.49','en'),(17754,2889,'2002-07-12 18:10:24',183,'lin','I had trouble with tri-cyclen for months when I was on it. To the point where I couldn\\\'t handle eating food in regular-meal-sized portions. Even worse that I was smoking at the time too, nausea city.<br>\r\nI switched to a brand called Alesse, which is supposed to be the weakest sort of bc, but I find it screws around more with my emotions than it does with my stomach. <br> There\\\'s a tonne of different pills out there, problem being that it becomes really trial and error, which just isn\\\'t all fun and games for those first 3-5 months they ask you to wait until things maybe don\\\'t settle down. <br> \r\nI agree totally with Peter on this one, when guys have that option, it should be considered.. I wonder if that sort of things is covered by OHIP up here... ',0,771,0,0,'137.122.14.20','en'),(17755,2025,'2002-07-12 19:36:35',82,'simon','when i do that i get a stomach ache that lasts at least a week. i have one that\\\'s going on almost two weeks now. ow.',0,119,86,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17756,2894,'2002-07-12 20:18:26',129,'moebius','not in my (admittedly faulty) recent memory...thanks! :D',0,56,234,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(17757,2901,'2002-07-12 21:43:32',124,'gus','As no one else is commenting, I feel obliged to post one myself! \r\n<p>\r\nIm reading this very interesting book right now \\\"The Spirit Catches You and You Fall Down\\\"  bu Anne Fadiman about a Hmong family (tribe that lives/lived in northern Laos and emigrated to the California central valley in the 80s) who\\\'s infant daughter has severe epilepsy. The book is basically about the difference between the way the disease is viewed by the western doctors and by the Hmong parents. A great look at how war affected this group of people too. \r\n<p>\r\nIm about halfway through, but Im already willing to recommened it.',0,615,0,0,'64.169.160.49','en'),(17758,2889,'2002-07-12 23:59:25',168,'laney','this was really good, jo. i could totally relate to everything, especially the bit about being in the planned parenthood office. in my case, i was surrounded by all of these 15-year-old hoochies, freaking out on my lunch hour when i had to cover something in court that afternoon. the guy was so NOT supportive.\r\nnow i have a very nice boy and i was supposed to go on the pill a couple of months ago, but conveniently forgot to ever pick it up from the drug store. there *has* to be a better way.',0,496,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(17759,2900,'2002-07-13 01:05:30',295,'karenlee','all those crazy person things in the last paragraph sound pretty good.\r\n<p>also: <i>I Don\\\'t Have That</i> is an excellent slogan. \r\n\r\n',1,137,0,0,'203.221.83.68','en'),(17760,2901,'2002-07-13 06:05:07',75,'tyler','i\\\'m commenting.',1,18,0,0,'165.247.164.117','en'),(17761,2892,'2002-07-13 12:45:32',187,'kandrer','etienne: well, there is no easy answer to the question \\\"what to say?\\\". some want to be talked to, some don\\\'t. i had a phone call from a friend who asked when the funeral was. since he was sort of hesitating, i guessed: i told him it\\\'s OK not to come, and he was relieved. people are afraid to do the wrong thing.<br>\r\nfor me the problem is kinda reversed: i observe them and listen to them to try to figure out what they expect from me. my relationship with my father is miles away from what is happening here around me now. i just need to have calm and a lot of time to think about it.\r\n</p><p>\r\nxipibi: they called me. the guy said \\\"sorry to tell it to you on the phone.\\\" i said \\\"no problem\\\" and he sounded relieved. i think i was relieved too, you know.\r\n</p><p>\r\njo: thank you.\r\n</p><p>\r\nall: i just needed to write this down. i wasn\\\'t looking for any condolence comment here. i didn\\\'t check the \\\"non-fiction\\\" on purpose too. it could have been a fiction, right? anyway, i am glad swinney exists, with all of you guys around.',1,1065,0,0,'80.15.167.236','en'),(17762,2821,'2002-07-13 15:39:02',187,'kandrer','i am not sure i understand, specially the part \\\"don\\\'t distinguish the basis of certain prejudicial action\\\". but i can say at least that the concept of \\\"value\\\" is suspect to me. is a value something you decided not to discuss anymore?<br>\r\nsome time ago, there has been a clash of values in france about the muslim veil:<br>\r\n- muslim girls needed to go to school wearing a veil (to respect their religious beliefs)<br>\r\n- teachers needed the kids not to show any religious sign (to respect the religious neutrality of schools)<br>\r\nmaybe you can give me an example of what you\\\'re thinking about.<br>\r\nalso: if a value is \\\"as right as it pretends to be\\\", then it can be discussed. if it\\\'s right, it will remain. unfortunately, it seems that people often refuse to rediscuss their values.\r\n',1,815,176,0,'80.15.167.236','en'),(17763,2025,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','osento? massage oil? more sleep??? let us now all soak together...',1,66,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(17764,2025,'2002-07-13 20:07:52',59,'s5','maybe a week of fasting would help.',3,35,86,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17765,2025,'2002-07-13 23:04:20',74,'jo','so, wait. have you ruled out food allergies? because it could be something really simple that you only very occasionally eat...',1,127,86,0,'172.132.9.169','en'),(17766,2902,'2002-07-14 03:00:28',59,'s5','what\\\'s a troilus?',0,20,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17767,2902,'2002-07-14 03:04:54',86,'egg','was this swinney composed of m-w.com\\\'s \\\"word of the day\\\"s?',0,67,0,0,'216.175.89.40','en'),(17768,2902,'2002-07-14 04:48:07',295,'karenlee','it\\\'s a real bugger when squinting doesn\\\'t work. also: how can you perpetrate the fraud of a combover when everyone knows it\\\'s a combover? \r\n',1,149,0,0,'203.194.11.166','en'),(17769,2902,'2002-07-14 05:39:51',66,'Peter','<br><tt>im still trying to figure out what <A href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2896.txt\\\">slainte</a> is...</tt>',1,135,59,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17770,2903,'2002-07-14 08:36:07',196,'jasonp','I know what you\\\'re talking about chia. It\\\'s a wierd mind trip to be a foreign whitey in an asian country.\nThe thing I found kind of annoying was people with a stranded-at-sea attitude. They\\\'d kind of assume you are as desperate as them for some kind of reassurance or something.\nI don\\\'t think it\\\'s worth getting that freaked out about it though. It\\\'s just people you\\\'d probably have no relations with that are now in a forced situation where they have to try and talk to you.\nI had a real similiar feeling when the people I worked with were all intent on trying to hang out together all the time. They had some kind of halloween party or something that I didn\\\'t feel like going to. I saw it as people I work with, even if it is a foreign country, so I wasn\\\'t obliged to go to their thing. The next day they were, \\\"Where were you? Why didn\\\'t you go?\\\" Okay, man calm down. The english speaking teacher-manager guy was kind of cool but there was always this insinuation you had to go to his own little \\\"whitey\\\" get-togethers. I distinctly remembering thinking to myself, \\\"Normally I wouldn\\\'t want to hang out with these people. It\\\'s only because we\\\'re all foreigners here and they\\\'re desperate to be with people of their own kind.\\\" It\\\'s these same people that were the ones that liked to complain the most also.\n\nAnyway, just do whatever you want to do and take it easy. It\\\'s not that big a deal.\n',0,1460,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(17771,2903,'2002-07-14 13:01:38',89,'sawks','kiwis are famous for this sort of thing, so many of us leave the country for a few years to do our \\\'big oe\\\' (overseas experience) in europe - and end up living, loving and drinking with a bunch of other kiwis (and aussie + s.a. folk) in the same suburbs of london<br>\r\n<br>\r\ncloser to home, i\\\'ve been to plenty of parties here in australia where everyone in attendance was an ex-pat nz\\\'er<br>\r\n<br>\r\neven being totally aware and cautious of this, i\\\'m living with 2 americans and my closest friends in melbourne are mostly from nz or england (the 2 countries where i hold a passport)<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni\\\'d like to think that i\\\'m pretty well balanced and together about cultural/racial integration etc - so perhaps its not so much this (innate?) tendancy to hang with people from home thats getting on your tits, as it is most humans being insecure fucktards that need constant reinforcement of their social standing - something thats easier to notice when they are in the minority<br>\r\n<br>',2,1003,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(17772,2903,'2002-07-14 16:39:48',74,'jo','i remember being embarassed to be an american in france because big groups of american tourists are ghastly. even in places that -- i feel -- command reverence, like the mont saint-michel or the sacre coeur, there was bound to be a loud american family expressing their uncultured opinions at unnecessary volumes and it made me want to crawl into my skin in shame.',1,364,0,0,'172.168.6.117','en'),(17773,2902,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','huh, how can you bring a person (cressida) to another person (troilus)? i\'m a little confused.(assuming you mean cressida and troilus from shakespeare?)',1,194,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(17774,2905,'2002-07-14 18:14:52',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i went to school with a girl who used to walk from the main campus at 116th and b\\\'way up to the music studios at 125th and b\\\'way <i>barefooted</i>. every day on the 1 train, theres a barefooted homeless guy.<br><br>what i love about new york city is that you can, i nfact, find all those disparate things in the same stretch of street sometimes.</tt>',1,364,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17775,2905,'2002-07-14 22:44:44',74,'jo','wow. this is the grossest thing ever.<BR>\r\nwhen i get a cut on my foot and i have to wear a band-aid, i\\\'m made aware of how dirty my feet get in 24 hours when i peel off that band-aid and it\\\'s lily-white surrounded by foot grime.<BR>\r\nmy ex goes barefoot a lot. a <b>lot</b>. i call him my hobbit. his footskin is much tougher than mine as a result of this, much less sensitive, and he\\\'s intensely proud of the hardening of his soles. one day, i will smear his feet with vaseline and put plastic bags over them and then socks on top of that and he\\\'ll wake up with baby\\\'s-butt-smooth feet and probably hate me forever.',1,632,0,0,'172.158.28.91','en'),(17776,2905,'2002-07-15 01:30:22',65,'le','there is much barefoot action at omega. sometimes i imagine being one of those people who go everywhere barefoot. there are some here. i do a lot of stuff barefoot but i don\\\'t want to step on a bee. ',4,202,0,0,'208.239.147.211','en'),(17777,2903,'2002-07-15 02:22:24',75,'tyler','wow. you type really well drunk--i can\\\'t type for shit when i\\\'m drunk, which is why you all have been spared (mostly) my most incoherant ramblings.',1,153,0,0,'165.247.165.5','en'),(17778,2902,'2002-07-15 02:51:06',358,'waterpig','it\\\'s a twelfth night reference. skip it.',0,43,57,0,'208.155.247.159','en'),(17779,2902,'2002-07-15 03:00:34',358,'waterpig','it\\\'s just one of those handy gaelic words. don\\\'t let it (or that accompanying stream of consciousness shite) distress you.',0,128,66,0,'208.155.247.159','en'),(17780,2905,'2002-07-15 03:26:22',234,'etienne','i always used to go barefoot or at most wear sandals.  i\\\'d cut the bottoms of my feet and only notice when there was blood marking up some surface.  in about the last year i have been wearing shoes more often, and for the first time i don\\\'t have calloused feet.',1,267,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(17781,2902,'2002-07-15 06:17:21',175,'Paul G','<p>Upon raising the glass: \\\"SlÃ¡inte!\\\" (To Your Health!) - an Irish Gaelic toast.</p>',0,90,66,0,'65.145.10.251','en'),(17782,2905,'2002-07-15 08:55:41',240,'xep','if we all walked on our hands, would we wear gloves?',0,52,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(17783,2905,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>i like things to be soft.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>my friend kyla goes barefoot places and it pretty much terrifies me because i don\\\'t want her to cut herself up.  she is very sweet and i would be sad to see her in pain.  i hope she doesn\\\'t try to go barefoot when she visits here next month.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>is this poetry?  because i really liked it, but i don\\\'t usually like poetry.</p>',6,380,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17784,2905,'2002-07-15 10:31:52',59,'s5','i liked it too. i always notice the gross things on the sidewalk when i\\\'m walking around the mission. i love shoes.',0,118,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17785,2908,'2002-07-15 12:52:59',75,'tyler','??',1,2,0,0,'165.247.164.208','en'),(17786,2905,'2002-07-15 13:06:33',295,'karenlee','barefoot is mostly a no-no for me. although i do like having my toenails showing. there are so many funky shoes to be worn which can protect your feet from urine-puddled metrocards.  \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',1,191,0,0,'203.194.20.90','en'),(17787,2908,'2002-07-15 14:15:12',123,'dcc','i like the sense of isolation in this piece. it creates a hollow atmosphere, yet leaves you wondering how alone the narrator is.\r\n<p>it makes me want to know what the decision was, and why it was made.',1,201,0,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(17788,2905,'2002-07-15 14:59:25',86,'egg','for some reason, I feel like my hands have eyes.',0,48,240,0,'216.175.89.40','en'),(17789,2905,'2002-07-15 20:08:26',65,'le','<a href=\\\"http://www.sacredmirrors.org/html/mirrors.cgi?m=18\\\">avalokitesvara</a> has a thousand hands, and an eye in each hand. \r\n',0,135,86,0,'208.239.147.211','en'),(17790,2905,'2002-07-15 20:15:06',240,'xep','blindfolded hands can\\\'t look both ways before they cross the street.',0,71,86,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(17791,2905,'2002-07-15 21:48:49',82,'simon','walking anywhere barefooted creeps me out.',0,42,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17792,2902,'2002-07-16 00:23:05',358,'waterpig','that\\\'s it exactly.  cheers.',0,30,175,0,'63.172.169.16','en'),(17793,2898,'2002-07-16 02:45:51',175,'Paul G','Thank you tyler!',0,16,0,0,'65.144.130.68','en'),(17794,2909,'2002-07-16 11:48:18',66,'Peter','<br><tt>thanks paul. this was really interesting to read. i like how the river runs right through chicago. the idea of a city being dissected by water intrigues me much mroe than the parallel-framed waterways around my city.</tt>',1,229,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17795,2905,'2002-07-16 11:50:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt>well, one good sort of barefootedness is when a lighter someone does that thing where they pad around on your back without shoes, to help pop your back. thats nice sometimes.<br><Br>driving barefooted in the summertime- preferably to or from the shore- is also nice. i like hanging my toes over the top of the clutch like nubby talons.</tt>',1,343,82,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(17796,2908,'2002-07-16 14:28:20',70,'Fuzzy','The decision is in there.<br><br>This one started with me being extremely tired one day and thinking about all the people I have known and all the new people in my life and becoming really happy and satisfied.<br><br>Not sure how it ended up the way it did, though.',1,265,123,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(17797,2905,'2002-07-16 17:25:02',82,'simon','you are right. i drove barefoot to san diego last weekend.',0,58,66,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17798,2912,'2002-07-16 19:59:38',223,'shanE','And he had the gall to tell me that I survived the dreams SOME of the time... and that I was helping SOME of the time. What a fucker this guy is... I can t believe our boss tho',6,176,0,0,'61.37.53.98','en'),(17799,2912,'2002-07-16 20:13:04',83,'adamajah','your boss is totally embezzling all that money that should be your overtime, and the maintenance funds too.  it\\\'s so obvious.  he knows you can\\\'t do shit about it.  you guys should play some major pranks on him.',0,217,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(17800,2905,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>nubby talons!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>driving barefoot is joyful, but also illegal where i\\\'m from.  not sure why.</p>',6,109,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17801,2912,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','does your boss have a boss?  time to go higher up in the chain...',6,65,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17802,2912,'2002-07-16 22:25:19',124,'gus','i have some boss issues lately. At one of my jobs, I cant get the boss to return my phone calls. this is bad because my phone calls are to ask him if he has received my freaking timesheet because I havent been paid. (for work I did in may) I havent even tried to schedule more work with them since the rare times I do get him on the phone, he hangs up as fast as he possibly can. I dont think I work there anymore, but I havent quit and he hasnt fired me. Im going to send him a nasty note AFTER I get all the money Im owed. (I got someone else to give me the payroll person\\\'s email address, lo and behold she had not seen my timesheet even though the boss assured me yesterday in a rare and brief telephone interlude that he gave it to her).\r\n<p>\r\nAt my other joblet, I am always paid on time, however, I was told not to bother coming in today or tomorrow, since there\\\'s not enough room in the office for me to use a computer. and of course they dont want me to work from home. so, i wont be getting paid anytime soon for the work I\\\'ve ALREADY done, and dont have any new work to do. too bad rent I cant say to my landlord, oh huh, im sure rent was sent, I will check! and hang up like bossman at job #1.\r\n<p>\r\nAnd thank god I have an interview for a real job tomorrow.',0,1279,0,0,'64.160.54.112','en'),(17803,2912,'2002-07-16 23:14:00',59,'s5','i thought contracts were legally binding documents. can\\\'t you take people to small claims court if they don\\\'t pay you?',0,124,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17804,2909,'2002-07-17 04:56:06',175,'Paul G','Thank you Peter.  I\\\'m really glad that you read the piece.  Chicago can be a handsome city.  \r\n\r\n<p>This is beer-drinking weather.  And I\\\'m in the mood to drink beer.  \\\"SlÃ¡inte!\\\"</p>',0,194,66,0,'65.145.10.145','en'),(17805,2901,'2002-07-17 05:03:41',175,'Paul G','Your stuff is great.',0,20,124,0,'65.145.10.145','en'),(17806,2912,'2002-07-17 11:07:10',93,'chiamattt','o: boss owns the school.\r\n\r\ns5: i got paid my overtime the day after i spoke ot you on icb about this whole thing. the numbers he gave me for time worked was lower than what i had thought, but it was only two hours less...so i didnt make a big stink. \r\n\r\nadamajah: he literally isnt smart enough to embezel. he\\\'s a really dim person. i just cant see him crunching numbers and fixing the books. ',3,397,0,0,'61.37.53.97','en'),(17807,2908,'2002-07-17 18:20:43',123,'dcc','\\\"I may not have gone where I intended to go, but I think I have ended up where I intended to be.\\\"\r\n<p><i>Douglas Adams</i>',1,128,70,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(17808,2700,'2002-07-17 21:18:58',362,'Austin\\\'s Dad','I think the name of your great, great, grandfather on your mother\\\'s side was Johann Sebastain Fahl.  As I recall, your Uncle Doug has a copy of the book that document your family tree on your mother\\\'s side back to the 1700\\\'s or 1800\\\'s.  You might want to get a copy from him.\r\n\r\nMy Dad was a salesman and Penecostal Preacher.  Not bad genetics for a career in marketing.  Love Dad',3,392,0,0,'208.189.162.130','en'),(17809,2912,'2002-07-18 18:25:35',17,'austin','hi, i really like chia, but this is just a test.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nthis is a test of the swinney commenting system.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nthis is only a test.',1,140,0,0,'205.179.19.127','en'),(17810,2912,'2002-07-18 18:57:38',90,'leonard','um, please don\\\'t get me started. i think you, actually everyone, should act like ted nugent at the workplace. tomorrow, i\\\'m hunting for my lunch. fuck \\\'em.',1,164,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(17811,2918,'2002-07-19 02:54:07',124,'gus','\r\nunfortunately, being an international traveller has no bearing whatsoever on getting jobs. though it does help to explain why I was unemployed for so damn long. you however will not even get that much, having left only for 3 weeks. \r\n<p>you really should use a title. and it would have been more interesting to know where you were going. asia? europe? south america? it\\\'s on a train, and you\\\'re only giving yourself 3 weeks, so I\\\'ll assume europe. for my own enjoyment, I will also assume you\\\'re going to the netherlands and germany, by way of london. \r\n',0,568,0,0,'64.160.54.112','en'),(17812,2919,'2002-07-19 09:17:21',154,'ryan','i like the rubberband series a lot.',1,35,0,0,'66.217.129.174','en'),(17813,2918,'2002-07-19 09:26:32',154,'ryan','if you actually drive from sf down to mexico, way south mexico, and actually speak spanish or german you will realize you are in another country.  maybe go as far as honduras, then head back north. that\\\'s international.  but you have to drive -- fuck the planes.',0,265,0,0,'66.217.129.174','en'),(17814,2917,'2002-07-19 16:10:10',122,'hool','<p>although i\\\'ve never tasted gasoline, i\\\'ve smelled it, and it smells just like mescal.  when i had a bottle of it, i\\\'d take the cap off and watch the fumes wave up and out just like out of a car\\\'s gas port.  and never eat a chocolate bar while drinking mescal. i barfed.</p>',0,288,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17815,2905,'2002-07-19 16:44:54',124,'gus','you\\\'re not supposed to drive barefoot here either. I think it is illegal. I remember hearing that anyway. I think it\\\'s because if your feet are unprotected, you might say, step on a rock or piece of trash on your car floor, hurt your foot, then crash when you are unable to slam on the brakes or whatever.\r\n<p>\r\nDid you also know that it is illegal to wear super skinny spike heels? yah. not like these laws are ever enforced purposefully. Apparently those skinny spike heels can sink into asphalt, causing women trying to cross the street to get stuck and run over by semis or what have you.',0,598,62,0,'64.160.54.112','en'),(17816,2905,'2002-07-19 18:03:11',17,'austin','<P>maybe spike heels are illegal as a way of making the prostitutes illegal?  sounds like a law with a hidden motivation.</P>',4,125,124,0,'205.179.19.127','en'),(17817,2918,'2002-07-19 19:08:20',83,'adamajah','who the heck would take a laptop along on vacation?  \r\n\r\nwell, actually i guess a lot of you would.',0,99,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(17818,2912,'2002-07-19 19:43:14',278,'egenus','burn burn!!!!',1,13,0,0,'66.141.181.43','en'),(17819,2917,'2002-07-20 00:45:53',255,'qq','I drank some gasoline by accident a week or so ago. The scooter\\\'s gas valve started leaking very badly so I had to empty the tank out quickly. Rather than doing anything sensible, I siphoned it out with a hose. As you may suspect, initiating the siphoning process acquainted me rather suddenly with the delicate flavor of chevron\\\'s finest. There isn\\\'t much worse than gasoline burps. \r\n',0,395,0,0,'64.81.241.248','en'),(17820,2917,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','gasoline pie. gasoline noodles. gasoline souffle soda. i always think the sun should always go down in a wave of fumes, like a ball of fire. i guess i need to move to the southwest. gasoline jello.',0,197,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(17821,2920,'2002-07-20 04:23:20',75,'tyler','i\\\'d catapult downtown, to seek the audience of my favorite fiancee in a lavender gown. but i\\\'m hooked up to a machine--it performs my daily functions, through a tube in my ween. so there ain\\\'t gonna be no wedding, no love affair, no art to which none can compare.',1,272,0,0,'165.247.164.208','en'),(17822,2921,'2002-07-20 06:02:37',295,'karenlee','I have not read this story you mention by nikolai gogol, about the nose wearing the overcoat, but it sounds like some stories a friend wrote about the adventures of a coathanger that escapes from the wardrobe for a night-on-the-town. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',1,244,0,0,'203.194.10.205','en'),(17823,2920,'2002-07-20 07:22:49',195,'matt','tyler, you\\\'re gettin\\\' good.',1,33,0,0,'209.208.108.123','en'),(17824,2917,'2002-07-20 16:27:58',93,'chiamattt','EGG should have admin',0,21,0,0,'61.37.53.96','en'),(17825,2922,'2002-07-20 20:03:37',59,'s5','chia my sad friend. the answer is sitting in front of you.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"i didnt even ask for sex.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"she said one night that i was too nice.\\\"<br>',1,160,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17826,2922,'2002-07-20 20:08:28',122,'hool','<p>i wrote you a long email. i sent it to you twice to be sure that you got it and you still didn\\\'t reply. </p>',0,114,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17827,2922,'2002-07-20 20:11:46',122,'hool','<p>i retract the above comment.  on a hunch, i checked my old email address (hool@home.com) and i see an email from you there.  i don\\\'t use that address anymore - i use inconduit@inconduit.com . now i will read your email and reply to it. </p>',0,246,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17828,2922,'2002-07-20 22:56:57',65,'le','you always say that you think other people don\\\'t care about you, but i think it\\\'s because they don\\\'t show care the same way you do.',4,140,0,0,'208.239.147.211','en'),(17829,2897,'2002-07-21 12:27:17',295,'karenlee','<i>maps folded instead of rolled.........as they blow out the window</i>\r\n\r\n<p>I really like that map analogy section</p>\r\n',1,123,0,0,'203.194.21.114','en'),(17830,2922,'2002-07-21 17:57:06',176,'Scapegoat','I think I\\\'m one of the people you hate. Actually I\\\'m pretty sure of it. I share a lot of the same critical perspectives that you embody but I regulate them to just that critical perspectives and not the dominant ones.<br><br>\r\nIt has been my experience that you aren\\\'t far off with your points about what most women look for. My own tact has simply been to be as Neanderthal as I can get away with, well doing my best not to be to disrespectful.<br><br>\r\nI think that your moving away from communism might facilitate a change away from being a misanthrope, just don\\\'t lose the value of community.<br><br>\r\n ',1,619,0,0,'64.229.161.34','en'),(17831,2922,'2002-07-21 18:28:00',175,'Paul G','Hey Chia -<br>\r\nThere\\\'s a lot of truth in what you wrote.  People are funny: men and women.  When I was a bad boy I had a much more active social life.  And the felons in jail always had much better looking women visit them than did the misdemeanants - go figure.  But, are the women who are attracted to violent men the ones that you want to be with?  You know that they\\\'re not.  Something went horribly wrong in the lives of those women, and they\\\'re damaged - just like the men who are violent.  It\\\'s a vicious circle.  They both belong in therapy.<br>\r\nThe \\\"forceful, demanding men, who expect things\\\" attract a certain type of woman.<br>\r\nBeing a man who is polite, and respectful, and a good listener - as you said that you are - is a great thing: you\\\'ll attract a certain type of person too.<br>\r\nTry to remember that what counts are your actions - the things that you can control: if you\\\'re doing what you know to be right, then take satisfaction in that.<br>\r\nMaybe the person that you gave your attention to only needed it for a while - until she felt strong enough to move on.  No, that\\\'s not fair to you.<br>\r\nAllowing her to use you, leave, and return isn\\\'t healthy for you or her.<br>\r\nLove and friendship are two-way streets.  It takes two people to make it work or fail.<br>\r\nBreak bad cycles.  But don\\\'t give up.<br>\r\nIt\\\'s a big world: give what you\\\'ve got to someone who appreciates it - she\\\'s out there.',1,1463,0,0,'65.145.10.39','en'),(17832,2922,'2002-07-21 19:10:38',363,'monkey3','some girls are too \\\"nice\\\" and we just say a guy is \\\"nice\\\" cuz we dont want to hurt his feelings. its so stupid i know it is. but we dont like them in any other sort of way then just a friend, but yet we\\\'ll lead them on to believe there may be something more, b/c we dont have the heart to say otherwise. follow my advice. the next time a girl says ur \\\"nice\\\", move along. we may return your affection, we may lead u to believe there is something more, but as soon as we say \\\"NICE.\\\" its all over. \r\n',2,524,0,0,'24.112.144.37','en'),(17833,2921,'2002-07-21 21:01:50',124,'gus','it is the mundane things that you never think about that make your life comfortable and happy. like... these delicious leftovers I only grudgingly took home from my mom yesterday. yep.',0,184,0,0,'64.160.53.114','en'),(17834,2922,'2002-07-21 21:09:46',124,'gus','Aw chia. It does suck sometimes. But if you keep being nice, someone WILL appreciate it someday, and it will all be worth it. \r\n<p>\r\nSometimes you have to build up a few scars and when you do, you\\\'ll put yourself first for a while, but one day, someone will want you to be too nice, and they\\\'ll love you for it.\r\n<p>\r\nOnce an ex of mine, we dated for 3 years, he was the first love type guy, he went camping for a week and was gone on new years eve without even telling me. It is no fun to feel unimportant. But, I finally realized, it happens when you let it.',0,566,0,0,'64.160.53.114','en'),(17835,2922,'2002-07-21 21:33:35',168,'laney','mmmm alcohol...the cause of, and solution to, all of life\\\'s problems!<br>\r\nbut seriously, chia, i and i think a lot of people are familiar with the mental state you seemed to be in at the time of this post. it sucks ass, but being nice (more importantly, being yourself) really is the best way. if this girl isn\\\'t considerate or strong enough to face you and say goodbye, it has nothing to do with you. don\\\'t internalize it. just vent your anger and frustration (clearly you\\\'re on the right track there) and move on. and i am with s5, any girl who says you are \\\"too nice\\\" isn\\\'t worth what you can offer her. hang in there. if i could send you some pot, i would.\r\n',0,684,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(17836,2922,'2002-07-21 23:40:22',195,'matt','girls going for assholes? is it biological? maybe they have a higher sperm count and the girls can tell somehow?<BR><BR>anyway, nice girls like nice boys.',1,154,0,0,'209.208.108.43','en'),(17837,2921,'2002-07-22 00:27:32',66,'Peter','<br><tt>the mundane things <i>rock</i>.</tt>',1,44,124,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17838,2923,'2002-07-22 03:32:28',75,'tyler','this is really cheezy, but...i thought it would never happen too.',1,65,0,0,'165.247.164.208','en'),(17839,2923,'2002-07-22 03:55:16',358,'waterpig','<p>it\\\'s actually more processed cheese food.  you\\\'re rather generous to elevate this brainless pule to an actual dairy product.<\\\\p> ',0,141,75,0,'208.155.246.108','en'),(17840,2923,'2002-07-22 03:56:33',358,'waterpig','oh for..',0,8,0,0,'208.155.246.108','en'),(17841,2921,'2002-07-22 04:30:55',358,'waterpig','karenlee: the nose actually does some other amusing things as well, but the coat hanger escapades sound equally engaging.\r\n\r\ngus: scientists have proved mom atoms make food better.\r\n\r\npeter: i agree...although there are some doubleplusunmundane things that rock too... like peter.',1,280,0,0,'208.155.246.108','en'),(17842,2922,'2002-07-22 06:22:46',186,'ook','<p>aggression is not the only kind of force. a lot of people can\\\'t tell what kind of power someone else has, and just appreciate any old display of power. some people are more discerning. i vote for forces of creativity and love.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>also, i was reading miss manners at random today, and she made the point that many social tensions are a result of differing standards of ettiquette. in almost any situation, one person will feel the other \\\"has no manners at all\\\" and the other will feel that the first person is either \\\"too nice\\\" or \\\"too snobby.\\\" it can be a difference in expressive style rather than a difference in underlying values.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>yep. good luck. hating people is lonely work.</p>',0,724,0,0,'209.148.183.183','en'),(17843,2920,'2002-07-22 06:40:18',130,'phishie','<p>A little scoop of plaster mix<br>\r\nsome coffee grounds and mud<br>\r\nand then she stirred it with the ladle<br>\r\nthat her Grandmother had bought her\r\n\r\n<p>threw in a pot of melted wax<br>\r\na forefoot and a hoof<br>\r\napple core, worms galore<br>\r\nand a can of some corrosive<br>\r\n\r\n<p>coconuts and chloroform<br>\r\nsome wicker and some cork<br>\r\nToxic waste, some purple paste<br>\r\nshe hoped was not explosive</p>\r\n',0,415,0,0,'172.157.77.179','en'),(17844,2925,'2002-07-22 13:56:12',123,'dcc','what\\\'s really creepy about these images, is that it\\\'s kind of how i see people on the street too. faces that aren\\\'t really even there. bodies moving; almost just obstacles in my path to work, to home, to wherever i am going.',1,233,0,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(17845,2925,'2002-07-22 14:41:59',93,'chiamattt','you pay attention to the faces, but not the bodies, yet you take the faces off. the pictures might have been better if the heads were floating. i dont know. ',6,157,0,0,'61.37.53.96','en'),(17846,2925,'2002-07-22 15:51:17',122,'hool','i never remember individual faces.  that\\\'s not to say i remember individual bodies, i just remember general presence. and besides, removing the bodies is too much work and i\\\'m lazy.',0,187,93,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17847,2923,'2002-07-22 17:58:12',75,'tyler','take \\\'er easy, dude.',1,23,0,0,'129.252.4.103','en'),(17848,2922,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>it seems to me that you are only acting nice, but in your thoughts you are actually not a very nice person.  you say as much in this post.  you look down on people.  you\\\'re thinking one thing and saying something completely different.  i\\\'ve got no advice for you.</p>',6,276,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17849,2925,'2002-07-22 23:42:24',154,'ryan','music has the right to children',0,31,0,0,'66.217.130.41','en'),(17850,2925,'2002-07-23 01:52:09',234,'etienne','the pictures creep me out but i can\\\'t stop looking at them.  ',1,64,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(17851,2925,'2002-07-23 04:37:51',196,'jasonp','\r\nVery Piscean.\r\n',0,17,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(17852,2925,'2002-07-23 06:38:08',74,'jo','summer is great for coffeehouses and bars with outdoor patios or -- even better -- big windows for people-watching from inside. i like to play the game where i try to imagine what every stranger would be like as a lover. i like to think that they would surprise me. that the guy behind the counter in the wife beater with the tattoos and spikey hair is probably a spooner and a slow, tender lover.',0,397,0,0,'172.168.251.4','en'),(17853,2922,'2002-07-23 07:51:33',196,'jasonp','<p>\r\nI have to agree with O.\r\nChia, you\'re leaking way too much anger and hostility all over the place for your \"niceness\" to be genuine. Niceness is not the same as spinelessness.<br>\r\nAnd since when is not trying to get some action from someone you know and like \"nice\"? It should be more of a mutually enjoyable thing, not like she\'s doing you a favor.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI don\'t really know you chia. Mostly you seem like an allright guy. I feel like I can relate since it seems like we might have had similiar personal kind of problems.<br>\r\nHere follows some \"tough love\" counsel, or at least \"angry advice\". Take it any which way you feel like.\r\n</p><p>\r\n\r\nOn that girl that ditched you, it\'s one of three things:<br>\r\nShe just couldn\'t deal with you for whatever reason.<br>\r\nOR<br>\r\nShe just doesn\'t like you.<br>\r\nOR<br>\r\nShe\'s very inconsiderate and flaky.\r\n</p><p>\r\nIn any case, what does crying like a baby do for you? Grow up. Is really that bad or do you just get off on creating drama for you to stew about when you\'re loaded?\r\n</p><p>\r\nWhat you should do is start spewing that bile where it belongs -- that would be this girl in particular, not here at swinney, and don\'t blame the US government or the \"white people\". Try and be more articulate and rational if possible and tell her off and ask for an explanation. Maybe she won\'t respond, maybe she won\'t \"like\" you anymore. Fine, at least it\'s off your chest and your self-esteem might rise an inch or two.\r\nOr then again maybe there is some legitimate reason for what happened, or she\'s sorry. She might apologize and it\'ll work out... or maybe not, whatever. It\'s not the end of the world in any case. \r\n</p><p>\r\nDon\'t cave in like a loser. If you act in a way that screams you have zero self-respect and self-control she\'ll dump on you that much more. And that would be YOUR fault not hers. Don\'t blame her or anyone else.\r\n</p><p>\r\nIt\'s not that women only like forceful jerks. A few troubled ones do, most don\'t. In general people admire others with self-respect and self-assurance.<br>\r\nActing like a doormat in some kind of delusional \"trade-off\" the \"I\'ll repress and lower myself to try and obtain you. Now you are obliged to follow my rules\" kind of thing doesn\'t work.\r\n</p><p>\r\nIt\'s just like the belief that you can repress individuals ambition and free will for some kind of idealistic, non-existent utopia bullshit. Yeah, I didn\'t want to go there, but I feel like the communism angle seems to be either a cause or effect for a lot of your problems. You are delusional dude. We\'re all delusional in some way, but being able to acknowledge it disperses it at least. Holding onto all that dogma doesn\'t help anything.\r\n</p><p>\r\nMore advice:\r\n</p><p>\r\nDon\'t be a lameass. Don\'t be a victim. Take responsiblity for your life and your mental and emotional state. Forget about the \"outside world\", which in your case seems more like an enormous projection of your own problems. Focus on what *you* can do in *your life*, which is all you can ever do anyway.\r\n</p><p>\r\nStop polluting your brain with alcohol. It makes you stupid.<br>\r\nBe honest with yourself and other people.\r\n</p><p>\r\nDeal with it.<br>\r\n\"It\" being \"you\" and your own head that you have to live with. Pointing fingers and ranting about the outside world doesn\'t do you or the external problems that you go on and on and on about any good. It\'s a cop-out. You\'re projecting your own problems on the outside world and then you cry about how you can\'t do anything about it.<br>\r\nThat\'s your problem in a nutshell.<br>\r\nYes people want to do whatever they want to do it\'s called free-will. People have the ability to screw you over, and likewise you have the ability to prevent it or do something about it. Don\'t just sit there crying that the world isn\'t fair. That\'s fucking lame and really annoying! Make it a fair place, for you at least, or if you really want to do a good job make it fair for others. You seem to be doing nothing except getting upset all the time and complaining all the time.\r\n</p><p>\r\nIf you *really* care so much about all these political problems of the oppressed people or whatever then go and do something about it for real. Volunteer. Join the peace corps or something.\r\n</p><p>\r\nOtherwise it\'s just bullshit. All it does is reinforce your own identity as a helpless, powerless failure. And it probably alienates and irritates anyone who might want to get to know you better.\r\n</p>\r\n',0,4478,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(17854,2922,'2002-07-23 14:17:12',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>thats</i> a long comment</tt>',1,40,196,0,'204.168.247.124','en'),(17855,2920,'2002-07-23 21:30:43',75,'tyler','reba dip a ladle<br>\r\nfor a taste of her creation, and<br>\r\nshe knew that what she made<br>\r\nwould be the finest in the nation.',1,127,0,0,'165.247.164.208','en'),(17856,2922,'2002-07-23 22:33:28',59,'s5','do you think he Z-snapped at the end?',0,37,66,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17857,2821,'2002-07-23 22:35:20',176,'Scapegoat','I think your example with the school and veils is a good one. Am I prejudice or racist because I agree on the seperation of church and state and the resulting need to have religiously neutral schools (assuming this was a public school), over religious freedom? It doesn\\\'t matter to me what group it comes from veil, crucifix, etc. Are you asking if I question my own values that would make me put the church and state seperation above religious freedom? Of course I question those values but I still take a stance and fight for what I think is best for my communtity and myself. I hope you actually check back on this thread.   ',0,631,187,0,'64.229.163.128','en'),(17858,2926,'2002-07-23 22:51:24',88,'deb','Anything that is meant to be understood, will be understood. It just takes time.',4,80,0,0,'63.17.43.54','en'),(17859,2922,'2002-07-23 23:21:09',196,'jasonp','Peter: Yup.\r\n<BR>\r\ns5: That\\\'s where I intended it to end. I\\\'m assuming Z-snapped is when the edit box or DB automatically truncates it.\r\n<BR>\r\nAlso, forgot to mark as \\\"best friendly\\\".',0,195,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(17860,2922,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','no a z-snap is where you\\\'re enraged and/or giving advice and you end it with \\\"GIRLFRIEND!!\\\" and make the tri-finger-click affectionately known as the z-snap, possibly made widely famous by the riki lake show.',6,217,196,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17861,2922,'2002-07-24 03:34:21',66,'Peter','<br><tt>nb: it is also sometimes punctuated with phrases such as <i>\\\"ima cutchoo ass, biatch!\\\"</i> or <i>\\\"uh uh uh baby, girlfriend dont play dat!\\\"</i></tt>',1,168,196,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17862,2922,'2002-07-24 03:43:44',278,'egenus','Amen chia! Yet there is hope, if only because hope gives us a reason to live, and we really don\\\'t have anything better to do.',1,128,0,0,'66.141.181.43','en'),(17863,2922,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','or my favourite \\\"NO YOU DI\\\'NT!\\\"',6,40,66,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17864,2922,'2002-07-24 06:06:02',196,'jasonp','<BR>\r\nHehehe. I have to keep up on the riki show.<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nI came back to apologize about how my rant-on-a-rant was too heavy-handed, but I guess actually it came off as too ghetto bitchy.<BR><BR>\r\nHey chia avoid high buildings for me would you. Please don\\\'t take offense.<BR><BR>\r\nActually, if you were to hear me as I wrote that it would be, \\\"clackety-clack, know what\\\'m sayin? clackety-clackety, know what\\\'m saying? clacky-clack-clack, Yeaaah dat\\\'s right, yeah...clackety-clackety, fur real yo.\\\"\r\n',0,520,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(17865,2922,'2002-07-24 08:31:42',255,'qq','One time on market street a bum asked me for spare change and i said \\\"sorry\\\" and walked on. and he said, \\\"WELL, WELL, SORRY DON\\\'T BUY ME NO BEER!\\\"\r\n',0,163,0,0,'64.81.241.248','en'),(17866,2922,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','this guy on 24th street once asked me and s5 to buy him a hamburger.  i said, \\\"what about a vegeburger?  i\\\'m vegan, so i can\\\'t buy you meat.\\\" he said, \\\"how about you just give me 5 bucks and i won\\\'t tell you what i buy.\\\"  he got his $5.',6,257,255,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17867,2927,'2002-07-24 14:48:18',196,'jasonp','<BR>And taken before bed Gingko Biloba can turbo charge your dreams too...\n<BR>',0,79,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(17868,2927,'2002-07-24 15:28:51',130,'treetop','Yes!  Ginkgo!  Im college I usto put a mL of extract in my expresso before leaving for class.  The stuff works great for concentration and also for hangovers.  I dont know if I would have made it without it.  Yay for herbs!',0,223,0,0,'172.145.205.196','en'),(17869,2926,'2002-07-24 18:31:30',90,'leonard','great picture. i can\\\'t really see his feet too well, but right now it makes me smile to think that he\\\'s on his tippy toes trying to look out.',1,147,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(17870,2928,'2002-07-24 23:28:33',17,'austin','were the batteries for the item, \\\"Great for couples\\\"?\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nwas it great?',3,84,0,0,'205.179.19.127','en'),(17871,2928,'2002-07-25 00:07:58',90,'leonard','all i have to say is \\\"jackhammer\\\". no really, don\\\'t ever go by yourself to buy one of these. you\\\'ll feel so dirty.',1,126,17,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(17872,2924,'2002-07-25 03:52:51',297,'kelly','this reminded me of my many bus experiences i had when i lived in denver. i never had any super gorgeous guy encounters though, but one time a really creepy drunk guy and his friend were riding downtown with me and he kept telling me i was the \\\"finest girl he\\\'d ever seen\\\" the entire 20 minute ride. i would just say thank you and then he\\\'d turn around and say it again. ',0,383,0,0,'24.208.33.174','en'),(17873,2927,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','there was a gingko tree that was over 100 years old outside the greenhouse at college. it was beautiful. unbelievably symmetrical and perfect, it had found a perfect spot and flourished there. did you know gingkos hibernate? well, the tree version of hibernate. and on the first day of its winter rest, it sheds all its leaves in one day. you get to work, it\'s full and green, the next day, you unlock the greenhouse backdoor, and the ground is a yellow circle made of leaves and the branches are bare, free, and moonishly silver in the sunlight.',1,491,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(17874,2927,'2002-07-25 12:02:46',66,'Peter','<br><tt>gingkoes hibernate! wow. when i used to live near it, i would go to the bklyn botanical garden and frolic in their big stand of gingkoes there; i remember reading on a plaque there that gingkoes are one of the most reproductively active phyla in the botanical world- and sure enough, during parts of the year, certain species produce/slough off/regenerate big seed pods like clockwork, probably very much tied to their hibernation cycles. they even had some odd species there with bluish-gray leaves- those trees were supposedly almost 300 years old.<br><br>\r\ngingkoes are beautiful trees.<br><br>\r\ni also liked that you said <i>moonishly silver</i>. thats a descriptive phrase ill file away for future use, for sure.</tt>',1,730,57,0,'204.168.247.124','en'),(17875,2927,'2002-07-25 12:20:56',295,'karenlee','weeb and Peter: yes! <i>moonishly silver</i> is much preferable to the term <i>glaucous</i>, which is used by botanists to describe bluish-grey foliage........just for fun I will sneak a random <i>moonishly silver</i> into a specimen record in the bot. gardens database here in sydney... ha! (if there are any of the bluey gingko plants in there, I will put it on one of those). \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',1,393,0,0,'203.221.82.208','en'),(17876,2929,'2002-07-25 17:36:44',17,'austin','<P>somebody was walking through the mission with me last week saying they were reading a book by writers about writing, picked up from a second hand store.  it struck me as the type of book that one finds in second hand stores.  books that never made it onto any best seller list.  books that never made it to anyone\\\'s book shelf, except a junk person, who sent the book to a junk store.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"one of them said, \\\'there are writers that live, i am one of them.  who go out and do crazy things so they have something to write about.\\\'\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\ni dont do crazy things that often.  the last crazy thing i did was a motorcycle trip in thailand, or trying to poop on the bus, also thailand.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthere has to be crazy things to do around here.  i am not looking at the terrain with my crazy eyes.  we have buses here with toilets.  we have mountainous areas.  i even have a motorcycle.\r\n</P><P>look with the crazy eyes.</P>',3,943,0,0,'205.179.19.127','en'),(17877,2927,'2002-07-25 18:08:43',195,'matt','<B><I>cigarrettes and coffee</I></B>.  wasn\\\'t that a series of shorts that jim jarmusch did with actor-y types discussing various subjects in a diner? almost like totally improved? i think one had tom waits and iggy pop in it.',1,229,0,0,'209.208.110.59','en'),(17878,2927,'2002-07-25 18:10:15',195,'matt','that would be \\\"totally improved\\\" as in \\\"totally improvised.\\\"',1,72,0,0,'209.208.110.59','en'),(17879,2928,'2002-07-25 23:44:40',90,'leonard','oh, i recommend \\\"my first vibe\\\" by hustler.',1,49,17,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(17880,2929,'2002-07-26 05:49:11',339,'blue','Reading this article, about hanging pictures on a wall, I was prompted to tell you to read the book <u>A Widow for One Year</u> by John Irving. <br><br>\r\nAnd then I read Austin\\\'s comment, which also prompted me to mention the exact same book. <br><br>\r\nJust call me Rabbit.',1,276,0,0,'172.172.239.152','en'),(17881,2929,'2002-07-26 06:00:36',17,'austin','i just read Cider House Rules by john irving, and let me just say, i found it lacking of vigor or force.  im not planning on picking up anymore of his books anytime soon.',4,170,339,0,'64.174.155.16','en'),(17882,2932,'2002-07-26 08:37:39',59,'s5','well, at least you can check \\\"born again\\\" off your checklist of life experiences.',0,87,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17883,2932,'2002-07-26 12:19:30',66,'Peter','<br><tt>christianity has such a stigma; but an awareness of what they call <i>christ</i> is surely a good thing.</tt>',1,117,0,0,'204.168.247.124','en'),(17884,2932,'2002-07-26 14:19:09',74,'jo','don\\\'t you think that it might be easier to find a denomination more suitable to your ideals and lifestyle than to try and change the baptist church? that\\\'s a lofty goal, yo.<BR>\r\ni suggest swinging to the other end of the christian spectrum and trying out a presbyterian or methodist church where no one would bat an eye at your incorporation of other faith systems. or fuckitall and GO UNITARIAN! those kids rule.<BR>\r\nanyway, just speaking from experience that it was a lot more fulfilling for me to worship with like-minded people than hold my tongue in a maelstrom of judgmental condemnation. doesn\\\'t \\\"we disapprove of everything!\\\" as a mantra get a little tiresome?',1,685,0,0,'172.174.91.121','en'),(17885,2933,'2002-07-26 14:55:05',74,'jo','i liked this a lot. yay for simple, clear, moving writing styles.',0,65,0,0,'172.174.91.121','en'),(17886,2933,'2002-07-26 18:40:43',175,'Paul G','Thanks jo!<br>',0,56,0,0,'67.248.86.139','en'),(17887,2929,'2002-07-26 19:01:37',339,'blue','But it\\\'s about prostitutes and Mrs. Robinson-esque sex! (I never read the Cider House Rules so I can\\\'t compare the two)',1,125,17,0,'172.131.169.102','en'),(17888,2932,'2002-07-26 21:22:42',195,'matt','you\\\'re right - it would be easier to change churches than change the Church. i have some methodist friends, so i might check them out. if a church doesn\\\'t feed me spiritually, i reserve the right to look elsewhere. thanks.',1,228,74,0,'209.208.109.92','en'),(17889,2895,'2002-07-26 21:44:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>hmm. my original image got replaced with a stylish shot of the ole man!</tt>',1,84,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17890,2895,'2002-07-26 22:08:21',17,'austin','OOPS',3,4,0,0,'205.179.19.127','en'),(17891,2895,'2002-07-26 22:09:00',17,'austin','peter, please report to the front desk.',4,39,66,0,'205.179.19.127','en'),(17892,2933,'2002-07-26 23:46:17',17,'austin','i dig the shit out of this piece!',1,33,175,0,'205.179.19.127','en'),(17893,2932,'2002-07-27 00:38:08',124,'gus','having grown up in a methodist church, full of old folks and young mothers, I really dont think they\\\'d embrace anyone thinking christ was an avatar of krishna. Doesnt bother me, but it might bother them. \r\n<p>\r\nThe protestant denominations arent really all that different from eachother. The baptists\\\' songs seem more dum\\\'d down to me, and is it them and those episcopalians who speak in tongues? that stuff is wackjob. But lutheran, prespyterian, methodist, baptist, all the same basic deal. Especially here in california. No one seems to pay much mind to the flavor.',0,577,0,0,'64.160.53.114','en'),(17894,2932,'2002-07-27 03:55:54',74,'jo','wow, i couldn\\\'t disagree more.<BR>\r\ni guess if you\\\'re talking about the basic church service, you\\\'re right, there\\\'s not a significant difference among protestant denominations. but if you want to actually join the church, become an active member, the denominations have more differences than they have in common. and baptists, particularly southern baptists, resemble other prostestant denominations less than catholics do.',1,435,124,0,'172.131.231.41','en'),(17895,2932,'2002-07-27 04:02:35',59,'s5','christian churches all seem to love bake sales. they can\\\'t be all that bad. who doesn\\\'t love cookies?',6,107,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17896,2932,'2002-07-27 05:04:44',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yeah, jo\\\'s kind of right...</tt>',1,43,124,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17897,2932,'2002-07-27 07:54:24',59,'s5','it sounds like one of those things where if you\\\'re involved in it, it\\\'s easy to spot all the differences that blend together if you\\\'re an outsider.',0,156,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17898,2932,'2002-07-27 19:11:31',65,'le','there were some hare krishnas here last week. they were beautiful. they had robes and facial tattoos, they were like, reformed punk hare krishnas. i talked to one of them and asked him if they would be staying for long. the five of them travel together in a band of hare. he said \\\"we are controlled by love. we can only go where the love is. if there is enough love here, we will stay.\\\" and then he spoke to me at length about the gopi, who only exist to give pleasure to krishna, and i became somewhat entranced and started wondering if that meant i was supposed to give pleasure to him as a human incarnation of krishna. i managed to shake it off. they left two days ago, i\\\'m not surprised. there\\\'s not as much love here as you might think.',4,754,0,0,'208.239.147.211','en'),(17899,2926,'2002-07-28 03:08:02',75,'tyler','awesome',1,7,0,0,'165.247.164.208','en'),(17900,2937,'2002-07-28 06:45:22',17,'yoko','mew  *shred* mew *shred*',2,24,0,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(17901,2926,'2002-07-28 21:02:56',81,'felicity','Thanks all.  I do have plenty of other reasons to love him, and looking at this has helped me with my somewhat short temper lately.  The new baby, an impending major career change, and a non-existant love life have left me a little lacking in my good graces lately.  Whenever I get ready to yell at him about whatever his latest toddler mishap happens to be, I try to think of all of the cool things that he does.  That helps me keep his \\\"youthful indescretions\\\" in perspective.',1,484,0,0,'216.42.123.44','en'),(17902,2936,'2002-07-28 21:52:45',366,'eunoia','Chia, that was great undrunk stuff. I initially thought about posting to your drunk and unloved and anti-moral piece, but I decided against sounding preachy. This time I\\\'ll try supportive. I think it\\\'s weird that you hang out with kids, well at least I used to think it was weird until I read your story. I don\\\'t know why you didn\\\'t get in the pit though. Fucking mudslinging is what everybody should be about, especially guys with poli-sci degrees. ',2,462,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(17903,2937,'2002-07-28 23:12:20',82,'simon','swinney. hi homes. i\\\'m thinking nice thoughts about you right now. what up? hi.',0,82,0,0,'12.236.164.112','en'),(17904,2937,'2002-07-28 23:59:27',65,'le','the sweat lodge was extremely intense. i wish i could explain it, all i can say is that it was one of the most humbling and grounding things i\\\'ve ever been through, and i feel so close to all the people who were in the lodge with me. i am no longer the coyote trickster. i thought of you saying that, austin swinney, you calling me the coyote trickster, during that year when people kept blaming me for their broken hearts, hearts they broke themselves, hearts they broke using me as a scapegoat, and i was so proud you\\\'d called me the coyote trickster. what a thing to say. but i realized in the dark lodge while staring at the glowing hot rocks in the center that i am no longer the coyote trickster. i have been reborn into something else, what, i do not know.\r\n<br><br>\r\npeople at omega worship yeti shamelessly, and they only met him for a few days. they talk about how fast he washed dishes, and ask me when he\\\'s coming back.',4,940,0,0,'208.239.147.205','en'),(17905,2937,'2002-07-29 00:32:19',195,'matt','maybe you are the bear now. if so, you will be the leader of a big ol\\\' band. (or a big ol\\\' something) an animal should resonate with you soon. that will be your totem.',1,173,65,0,'209.208.111.143','en'),(17906,2937,'2002-07-29 00:52:08',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i vote <i>happily clicking parakeet</i>. also: yeti rocks.</tt>',1,71,65,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17907,2937,'2002-07-29 01:18:03',65,'le','ooh, i wish there was a <i>happily clicking parakeet</i> category i could check next to my comment.',0,99,0,0,'208.239.147.211','en'),(17908,2937,'2002-07-29 02:13:52',66,'Peter','<br><tt>add one!</tt>',1,21,65,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17909,2937,'2002-07-29 04:18:39',75,'tyler','i had a humbling and grounding experience today, when i &quot;did myself&quot; in the shower. no kidding.',1,105,0,0,'165.247.164.208','en'),(17910,2937,'2002-07-29 06:30:49',86,'egg','here is an example. I said, \\\"ive got to go puke,\\\" and yeti wanted me to wait so that he could clean out the toilet for me first. Thankfully I upchucked on 18th in the gutter 