el like touchin you...',0,137,66,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17920,2941,'2002-07-29 19:52:58',122,'hool','<p><i>And so I sit upon my shelf and dream of the day when I can find a way down.</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>stop dreaming and jump.</p>\r\n \r\n',0,128,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17921,2939,'2002-07-30 04:11:55',75,'tyler','dude. whoa.',1,11,0,0,'165.247.164.208','en'),(17922,2943,'2002-07-30 06:06:28',93,'chiamattt','your new song sucks. \\\'its getting hot in here lets take off our clothes?\\\' wtf is wrong with you man? and what the fuck is that bandaid on your face for? ',6,159,82,0,'61.37.53.96','en'),(17923,2943,'2002-07-30 06:31:01',59,'s5','that song was playing at ladyfest, on egg\\\'s film day.',0,56,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17924,2943,'2002-07-30 07:10:14',86,'egg','oh yeah, that was our intermission music. someone put on magnetic fields and I said, \\\"I am not a splendid butterfly or else I\\\'ll rip your nipples off.\\\"',0,160,59,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17925,2937,'2002-07-30 07:21:55',93,'chiamattt','well well well. you beat me to 200! i was sure back when i had reached 100 that i was going to beat you to it, but i guess not. congrats my friend. *muttering the whole time*',1,174,17,0,'61.37.53.96','en'),(17926,2945,'2002-07-30 07:58:19',59,'s5','i didn\\\'t know the koreans and the japanese didn\\\'t get along. any idea why?',0,80,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17927,2945,'2002-07-30 08:40:08',154,'ryan','perhaps it\\\'s japan\\\'s colonial domination of eastern and southeastern aisa during wwii?',0,92,0,0,'66.217.130.97','en'),(17928,2945,'2002-07-30 08:53:40',175,'Paul G','<a href=\\\"http://online.sfsu.edu/~soh/cw-links.htm\\\">Jerry D. Boucher and Chunghee Sarah Soh</a>',0,100,0,0,'67.248.113.154','en'),(17929,2936,'2002-07-30 09:27:10',196,'jasonp','That\\\'s too much. I went on a \\\"camping\\\" trip that sounds eerily similiar. It was for a english school and a bunch of kids too. Momonokikoen \\\"pine tree park\\\". Really just an excuse to get away for a while. No real camping activity. Just doing stuff in the gymnasium because... it was raining. Hiked all day, had a beer that night and then puked after sitting in the hot bath for too long. Near fall when the leaves were changing.',0,442,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(17930,2945,'2002-07-30 09:30:41',196,'jasonp','Hmm... wow, didn\\\'t know it was a korean girl. That\\\'s a whole nother story then.\r\n<BR>\r\nI can\\\'t say anything really, but there is a whole lot of \\\"stuff\\\" between asians and westerners that is very hard to figure out. They view the world and life really differently. They\\\'re all individuals of course but their context and upbring is totally different.<BR>\r\nMaybe this was a long term decision beforehand and then you came around?\r\n<BR>If possible why don\\\'t you just go ahead and send it?\r\n\r\n',0,510,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(17931,2945,'2002-07-30 09:56:39',93,'chiamattt','im not sending it because i dont feel like making things worse than they are. if she doesnt want to talk, then thats her decision. it wouldnt be the first westerner she\\\'d have dated. thats why i dont think its anything all that cultural. i just needed to get those thoughts out and swinney is a good place to do that. ',1,321,0,0,'218.145.63.36','en'),(17932,2464,'2002-07-30 14:49:59',368,'haecceity','<p>Your \\\"naivete\\\" regarding the situation just means you haven\\\'t been wounded.  Your friends are trying to stop that from happening.  There are certain things I believe can be accepted about people; however, no matter how much potential, how much promise you see amongst the dross, you cannot change somebody else.  It\\\'s true change /can/ happen, but that\\\'s up to the person himself to effect it.  Even those of us who are supposedly not \\\"naive\\\" and who supposedly know what we are doing in this relationship thing fall for the \\\"I can change him/her\\\" myth.</p>As another person commenting here pointed out, even if you could save someone, savior-salvaged is not any kind of a basis for a romantic relationship, and that\\\'s a hard lesson for the altruist to learn.  IF this guy has a grumpy facade, that\\\'s one thing; IF you are actually being abused physically, mentally, and/or emotionally, you need to get out.  The longer you wait, the more invested you will get in the relationship, the more you will be able to intellectualize and rationalize what\\\'s going on, the more mired and entrenched you will become, until you begin to buy into the idea that either you can\\\'t live without this person, in spite of the abuse, or you can\\\'t abandon them because he needs your help.</p><p>The easiest person to lie to is yourself, and that Hawkwind quote is absolutely true: There is no shame, no crime in looking out for yourself.  At the end of the day, the only person who can reliably take care of you is you.',3,1544,114,0,'12.250.43.198','en'),(17933,2941,'2002-07-30 15:04:03',368,'haecceity','Recently, I have been edging ever closer to the ledge.  Trying to take steps so that I don\\\'t crash and burn when I do.  I think it\\\'s only a matter of time now.',1,165,0,0,'12.250.43.198','en'),(17934,2945,'2002-07-30 17:24:49',83,'adamajah','i think you\\\'re fine chia.  some day some girl is going to appreciate you for the wacko you are.  one thing i believe though, is that you won\\\'t <i>really</i> know what your true opinion about sex is until you\\\'ve done with a girl or two.  the idea of it builds up, then you do it, then you realize that it is what it is.  then you can move on.  you don\\\'t even know what it is yet :)',0,392,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(17935,2943,'2002-07-30 17:31:22',82,'simon','i like that song',0,16,93,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17936,2937,'2002-07-30 19:32:14',17,'austin','i had not even realized that i hit 200.  that is how genuine i am in my swinney writing.<BR><BR>\r\nsucker!',1,105,93,0,'205.179.19.127','en'),(17937,2806,'2002-07-30 21:33:05',366,'eunoia','This dream reminds me of the primordal ooze that we all come from. It is a shame that our dream life become our sole connection with that realm of de-evolution. The siren in your psalm is leading you not into death in the physical sense, but a death, a tear, a revulsion of your appollonian self. She is mother nature, claiming you, like she claims all of man\\\'s creations in time. It is a violent death, like sparagmos, she partakes of your flesh. It is a sacrificial connection she is making. You are the source of the transubstantiation. I\\\'d say its your calling towards love and envitable destruction and death. Life is a cycle, like the phases of the womb. Are you passionate about something that needs letting go? Kill it, beat it, start over again.',4,760,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(17938,2941,'2002-07-30 21:53:19',366,'eunoia','<p>He doesn\\\'t seem to sound consistently progressive. \\\"Eschewing the status quo\\\" and diving into social contracts like marriage are in paradox to each other.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Yours, is a relationship in which two people are just discovering who they are. Well at least you are discovering who he is and who you are,and maybe he isn\\\'t living up to your own fixed ideal.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>The most disturbing thing about this piece is that you only judge yourself in relation to him, instead asserting your own needs. May be this was a conscious choice on your part, your own creative perspective, but you sound pretty broken up inside, and until you define who you are as an individual, the relationship will be facistic in nature.</p> ',5,734,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(17939,2941,'2002-07-30 22:03:30',175,'Paul G','\\\"Rituals are important. Nowadays it\\\'s not hip to be married.  I\\\'m not interested in being hip.\\\" (John Lennon)',0,121,366,0,'67.248.86.44','en'),(17940,2605,'2002-07-30 23:56:04',366,'eunoia','A serious query: do ducks make different noises when strangled at a muddy river bank than say when they are strangled on a busy city street, or near a babbling brook? I hate the bloody creatures, their meat is gammy and they always lunge at me in public. ',4,255,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(17941,2947,'2002-07-30 23:57:27',123,'dcc','isn\\\'t it strange how sometimes it\\\'s so easy to look back and focus on only the bad things that happened to you in any given period of time?\r\n<p>i know personally i sometimes have a challenging time searching my memories for truely happy/good events. i guess it\\\'s all a matter of perspective, as always.',2,311,0,0,'24.101.118.76','en'),(17942,2945,'2002-07-31 00:08:41',366,'eunoia','<p>I wish I had your attitude towards sex. I\\\'m supposed to be the most-christian of all my friends, and I do have a passive and polite nature like you, but when it comes to women I\\\'m all eros. I also like performance art, so I feel like sex is very expressive and speaks volumes more than getting to know a person solely through talking.</P>\r\n\r\n<p> The only thing I have to say is that you can\\\'t mystify the female, or anybody for that matter. Life is about fucking, we are beasts. If you choose to transcend this, that\\\'s heroic to me. Just don\\\'t wait or lead a solitary existence because you don\\\'t feel worthy of that type of human connection, that is my recurring mistake</P>',3,695,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(17943,2945,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','god, if you have to trancend fucking to be anything but a beast, i\\\'ll be a beast for life.\r\n\r\nfucking #1',6,107,366,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17944,2945,'2002-07-31 00:35:55',66,'Peter','<br><tt>human connection: #1; solid connection with a human through gratuitously sexing them nasty: #0</tt>',1,69,366,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17945,2945,'2002-07-31 00:42:11',59,'s5','the tantrics say that sex is how humans look into the infinite.',5,63,366,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17946,2945,'2002-07-31 01:35:10',17,'austin','<B>Great Olivia Quotes</B>',3,26,62,0,'64.174.154.13','en'),(17947,2945,'2002-07-31 02:26:48',124,'gus','i dont think chia wants to \\\"transend fucking\\\" I think chia wants fucking to be more than an animal act, because chia wants to be more than an animal. \r\n<p>\r\nand that doesnt seem so wrong.',0,193,0,0,'64.160.53.114','en'),(17948,2937,'2002-07-31 02:36:49',124,'gus','you are funny. lets go to some indie rock shows soon! ',0,54,79,0,'64.160.53.114','en'),(17949,2945,'2002-07-31 02:58:14',366,'eunoia','<p>and become what? a rational passive human being? NO. The closer that we get to our true intentions and expressing feelings and committing action, then we are good beasts.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Good cannot be superimposed on us, without leading to repression, which I think is what bothers Chia. I think Chia\\\'s expression is suffocated in the real world and surfaces on this site in a safe environment with beautiful loving friends. This site is like self-help program for a lot of people, it seems.</p> \r\n\r\n<p> You know it\\\'s damn fine to be supportive, but Chia, you need to just tell her how you feel and see if things fall apart. I hate the fear that underlies politeness.</p>',3,676,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(17950,2945,'2002-07-31 05:30:01',124,'gus','ah ha. i\\\'ve figured out what bugs me about your comments. its YOUR WAY or the highway with all your comments. Too bad one answer doesnt work for everyone huh? ',0,162,366,0,'64.160.53.114','en'),(17951,2945,'2002-07-31 06:40:08',59,'s5','totally.',0,8,124,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17952,2945,'2002-07-31 10:17:48',65,'le','i think i agree with adamajah on this one. i think that sometimes you just need to take risks so that you can have the experience to draw on. i don\\\'t think having sex with someone early in the relationship means that you are \\\"putting sex first\\\" or being flippant about your intent to love the person for the person. the two aren\\\'t mutually exclusive. chia, i think you should trust that you can have sex with a woman earlier than you think, and still be a man of honor. i think you can. i think it could be really amazing for you. and educational.\r\n<br><br>\r\nalso, this is a great line: \\\"<i>I love humanity, the world, and cats.</i>\\\" ',4,652,0,0,'208.239.147.211','en'),(17953,2945,'2002-07-31 10:55:56',175,'Paul G','<p>I\\\'ve argued with you here before, because my values are not the same as your values.<br>\r\nHaving said that, I admire you: you\\\'ve spent time thinking about things; you\\\'ve determined a number of principles according to which you feel right in acting; and you\\\'ve then actually lived in accord with those principles despite the consequences.<br>\r\nThat is really fucking manly, Chia.<br>\r\nThat is anything but fearful.<br>\r\nYour \\\"expression is <b>not</b> suffocated in the real world.\\\"<br>\r\nYou are \\\"expressing <b>your</b> feelings and committing <b>your</b> action.\\\"<br>\r\nI think that it\\\'s important to place your original post in context: you were heartbroken and frustrated.  Who wouldn\\\'t be?<br>\r\nBringing the feelings that you had, at that moment, into a piece of writing was a still a positive act - even if the piece was ugly.</p>\r\n \r\n<p>I dunno, defining man as: \\\"the animal that fucks,\\\" seems radically incomplete - to me.  Embracing the human experience in its totality can be a daunting task.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>[noble]</i></p>',0,1066,93,0,'67.248.113.106','en'),(17954,2948,'2002-07-31 12:44:28',66,'Peter','<br><tt>sometimes, the secret ingredient in these sorts of dishes is... sitting at home eating them. i appreciated this story because ive had this same experience; at least for me, the dish just never tastes the same as it did, ten years ago, sitting at home with the family, heh.</tt>',1,285,0,0,'204.168.247.124','en'),(17955,2947,'2002-07-31 12:46:18',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this is, definitely, my favorite thing youve written on here. it reminds me of the summer of 1987 when i was swimming in a lake in rural northwest georgia, climbing up the homemade welded-pipe ladder onto the rotting dock, being nibbled on by tiny mayfly-hungry fish...</tt>',1,282,0,0,'204.168.247.124','en'),(17956,2944,'2002-07-31 12:49:37',66,'Peter','<br><tt>detroit is fucking magnetic... please be sure to snag a <i>coney island</i> for me and hit the basement of saint andrew\'s or something. im <i>so</i> going to be in detroit this summer.</tt>',1,201,0,0,'204.168.247.124','en'),(17957,2945,'2002-07-31 17:38:55',366,'eunoia','<p>Thanks for calling me on my absolutism.</p> ',0,47,124,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(17958,2927,'2002-07-31 18:58:43',66,'Peter','<br><img src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/images/gingko.jpg\\\">',1,64,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17959,2945,'2002-07-31 20:27:21',175,'Paul G','There is some irony in faulting chia for writing here instead of speaking directly to a woman who has chosen to end communication with him - even as you write, here, under an alias, yourself.  Yes, that is ironic...\r\n\r\n<p>Curious: Why not fault her for ending the dialogue without explanation?</p>  ',0,299,366,0,'65.144.161.174','en'),(17960,2946,'2002-07-31 20:41:59',195,'matt','my hometown\\\'s like that, but with people. virtually nobody i graduated with lives here anymore. it doesn\\\'t seem to be a place any young folks see themselves having a future in. so every year, kids graduate and never come back. kind of sad, but everything always changes, i guess.',1,285,0,0,'209.208.110.8','en'),(17961,2948,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','cooking is magic :D',6,19,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17962,2948,'2002-07-31 21:33:28',90,'leonard','i\\\'ve tried that easy mac shiat, but it doesn\\\'t compare to what mama used to make.',2,87,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(17963,2945,'2002-07-31 21:41:52',122,'hool','<p>i\\\'d like to briefly interject here for informative purposes.  eunoia knows chiamattt personally and has for a while just as i\\\'ve known them both; we\\\'re part of the same close circle of friends.  this is probably why i don\\\'t see eunoia\\\'s comments as placing fault in any way.  i see them as supportive and constructive. </p>',0,341,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17964,2949,'2002-07-31 22:37:49',86,'egg','it really doesnt sound like a horrible place to work. at least everyone is INTERESTING enough to warrant funny nicknames. at least you have cute little ex-dancer tarts running around, something pleasant to look at throughout the day, yknow? all ive got is Laura Ashley and some guys with ponytails.',0,298,0,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(17965,2949,'2002-07-31 22:40:22',82,'simon','Well, the dirty underhanded sabotaging two-faced stuff i\\\'ve left out because i prefer comedy and i don\\\'t want to whine and bitch. i do enough of that elsewhere. i will say though that when sparrowe worked here for two months, she left saying, this is the most demoralizing place i\\\'ve ever worked. if i were you, i\\\'d get out now. and she is the most friendly, diplomatic person i\\\'ve ever worked with. people here really are insane. i swear.',0,454,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17966,2945,'2002-07-31 23:07:16',175,'Paul G','Thanks hool!',0,12,122,0,'65.144.160.93','en'),(17967,2948,'2002-07-31 23:56:11',329,'stoo','for reals! the secret ingredient is love. moms beat powdered cheese hands down.',1,79,0,0,'12.146.203.237','en'),(17968,2945,'2002-08-01 00:04:49',366,'eunoia','the cavalry has arrived, and my cover is blown. Thanks hool.',1,60,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(17969,2947,'2002-08-01 01:20:43',75,'tyler','what\\\'s a &quot;kilometer&quot;?',1,34,0,0,'129.252.163.113','en'),(17970,2941,'2002-08-01 03:50:55',368,'haecceity','<p>Yes, it is my own creative perspective.  Personal details aside, I was going for ambience, here...distilling the experience in an attempt to convey the disil(de?)lusion, the sense of slow, cool betrayal, the surrealness, the senselessness...the hurt one feels at having one\\\'s emotions slowly deconstructed through the use, by an external source, of \\\'logic,\\\' and the sense of being objectified.</p><p>And as far as being \\\'broken up inside\\\' as you put it...yes.',3,477,366,0,'12.250.43.198','en'),(17971,2941,'2002-08-01 03:54:43',368,'haecceity','Oh, and another thing...I wasn\\\'t judging myself in relation to him...I was showing you his rather detached point of view by couching it that way.  There\\\'s a lot of irony to this piece, and it was intentional.  Does it show?',3,229,366,0,'12.250.43.198','en'),(17972,2949,'2002-08-01 06:27:56',185,'orion','Heh.  I hear you.  I can\\\'t wait for the economy to turn around so that cool workplaces blossom again, people are happy, budgets aren\\\'t tight, and if a job sux you can just ... get a new one, with no hunting for weeks or months.  ',1,235,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(17973,2949,'2002-08-01 07:02:02',93,'chiamattt','hook me up with some of those cute interns. any of them moving to seoul to teach english? har har. ',1,99,0,0,'211.32.132.224','en'),(17974,2945,'2002-08-01 07:06:05',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\nthe reason i dont send this letter is because i dont really think its worth it. i think i made the effort in past conversations with her. this letter was just somthing i wanted to write. something i wanted to post. something i wanted people i know to read. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsomething funny that ive noticed about some people on swinney is that they expect every article to involve them in some way. I find that some of you think articles dont belong because you see them as, for instance me, whining about life. yeah i whine. yeah i complain. yeah i get angry and drunk. so what? bozo list me. ',1,595,0,0,'211.32.132.224','en'),(17975,2945,'2002-08-01 07:08:16',93,'chiamattt','also...i have no idea who you are. ',0,35,366,0,'211.32.132.224','en'),(17976,2937,'2002-08-01 07:17:17',93,'chiamattt','<P>\r\n<pre>\r\nDate: Thu, 27 Jun 2002 19:20:29 -0700\r\nFrom: Austin Swinney\r\nTo: Chia Mattt\r\nSubject: eat a dong\r\n\r\n\r\naustin (198)\r\n...\r\nchiamattt (126)\r\n\r\ni am going to beat you to 200!\r\n</pre>\r\n</p>',6,196,17,0,'211.32.132.224','en'),(17977,2945,'2002-08-01 19:55:39',122,'hool','<p>by posting an article to a community like this, you are by definition involving its members.  from reading the comments on your entries, aside from the random anomaly, i don\\\'t see anyone saying that your articles don\\\'t belong.  if you don\\\'t want responses to what you write, then just post your writing on your site where there there is no comment system.  if you do want responses, then you have to take the good with the bad.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>if you don\\\'t like someone\\\'s comments, and they say that your article doesn\\\'t belong, so what? practice what you preach and bozo list <i>them</i>.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>also, eunoia = bark bananas = the brown recluse = mark</p>',0,673,93,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17978,2949,'2002-08-01 20:07:29',90,'leonard','fuck, don\\\'t even get me started. i like stories about the workplace because that\\\'s where i spend 30% of my fucking day. any stories about a face smashed into a pillow? i need to take a nap.',1,195,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(17979,2949,'2002-08-01 20:11:24',90,'leonard','i also think you should actually \\\"do\\\" Who Knew? magazine. i mean, who knew you were going to do this?',1,107,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(17980,2949,'2002-08-01 21:40:58',82,'simon','fuck, dude, who knew!?',0,22,90,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17981,2949,'2002-08-01 21:59:16',81,'felicity','Nice series.  I just handed in my letter of resignation at my publishing job (newspaper) yesterday.  I\\\'m about to make a huge career change (graphic/web designer to radio morning show host), which is a little scary.  But it certainly beats dealing with the \\\"chest-pounding\\\" style of management that has left me less than happy here.\r\n<p>Having the weight taken off my shoulders has been incredibly liberating.  I say get the heck out of there if you\\\'re not happy.  You never know what else may come your way.</p>',1,524,90,0,'216.42.123.44','en'),(17982,2949,'2002-08-01 22:10:19',90,'leonard','easy for you to say... you had your own TV show! never cut your hair again, mmmkay?',1,83,81,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(17983,2949,'2002-08-01 22:33:45',81,'felicity','Boy...for an intelligent girl, I can really be dense sometimes.  I just spent a full minute trying to figure out how you knew I just cut my hair.  (I\\\'ve never seen the show, so it didn\\\'t dawn on me.  I\\\'ve had that nickname for 7 years now, and almost changed it when the show came out.)\r\n<p>Still, best of luck with whatever you choose to do.</p>',0,355,90,0,'216.42.123.44','en'),(17984,2942,'2002-08-02 03:11:39',368,'haecceity','I had a similar experience last November when I left the City of Big Shoulders to spend a weekend in the Hudson Valley.  Coming back up the Tri-State, it was just so good to be back where I belong.',1,197,0,0,'12.250.43.198','en'),(17985,2950,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','whoo :D',6,7,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(17986,2949,'2002-08-02 06:18:53',86,'egg','also, I love \\\"TK\\\". fyi. I mean can you just clear that up for all of us right now what that means....i always read it \\\"to come\\\" but why is it K? 2 KUM. my name is egg I dont spell it  \\\"joe\\\" .',0,209,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(17987,2945,'2002-08-02 13:28:19',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\nOh i love comments. I just felt like saying that. \r\n</p>',0,61,0,0,'61.37.53.62','en'),(17988,2950,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','oh my god. i am restraining myself from jumping on the first plane to aukland so i can have a hut of my own. oh god! that would be so wonderful... sigh. it\\\'s too bad jeff would never want to live someplace like that. consternation!',1,234,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(17989,2950,'2002-08-02 16:08:38',66,'Peter','<br><tt>three words: <i>houses on stilts!</i></tt>',1,50,57,0,'204.168.247.124','en'),(17990,2949,'2002-08-02 16:51:19',82,'simon','a lot of place-markers are like that. purposely misspelled so you don\\\'t accidentally think they\\\'re supposed to be printed. like TK and HEDs and DEKs.',0,155,86,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(17991,2950,'2002-08-02 17:12:30',175,'Paul G','So cool.',0,8,0,0,'67.248.113.249','en'),(17992,2947,'2002-08-02 17:15:30',122,'hool','<p>1 kilometer == 0.6 miles</p>\r\n<p><i>.. who keeps the metric system down? we doo... we dooo </i></p>',0,102,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(17993,2947,'2002-08-02 17:23:41',66,'Peter','<br><tt>back in the late 70s, when the metric system was almost on the verge of being chic, my hometown in tennessee switched all their signs to metric, anticipating that they would be ahead of the curve that never came... and they still remain, despite the fact that no one knows what those damn pesky <i>killy-meters</i> things are. in typical ignorant local fashion, the signs say oddly abbreviated things like \"knox: <i>40 KIL.\"</i></tt>',1,443,122,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17994,2952,'2002-08-02 17:52:51',66,'Peter','<br><tt>make a b-line for your local news-stand and pick up the august \\\'02 issue of the <a href=\\\"http://www.jbmr-online.org/\\\"><i>journal of bone mineral research</i></a> where you can read my first science-geek publication in all its glossy-glory.</tt>',1,261,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17995,2948,'2002-08-02 18:03:01',124,'gus','dairy makes me sick now, but I have always always preferred the mac n cheese from a box over home made. who knows why. these days I am really into the annies mac and cheese boxes, and then there\\\'s the one I can actually eat, amy\\\'s mac and soy. its not as bad as it sounds.',0,278,0,0,'64.160.53.114','en'),(17996,2952,'2002-08-02 18:03:31',75,'tyler','are you a grad student, or a Ph.D?',1,34,0,0,'129.252.163.113','en'),(17997,2952,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i used FTIR the summer of my junior year in college to research block co-polymers and something something. that was a long time ago. what i do remember, is cleaning all. that. fricking. glassware. in acetone, and drying them in special oven so that they don\\\'t get any water vapor from the atmosphere on special quartz square tubes, because water fucks the hell out of your measurements. i had dry crackly skin that summer. whee!',1,431,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(17998,2952,'2002-08-02 18:18:41',66,'Peter','<br><tt>hahaha! the water-background from atmospheric humidity! yeah, we equalize for that via spectral subtraction of a pure water-vapor spectrum. the plus side is that the room i work in is 50 degrees and < 10% humidity (to keep the machines calibrated); its <i>so nice</i> during the steamy nyc summers, but in the winter... gasp.<br><br>but yeah, my samples were embedded in methacrylate polymer, which is much less susceptible to water interference than BaF3 windows or what have you.<br><br>the acetone cleanup is a bitch, though. my hands dont function well when constantly bathed in acetone and denatured alcohol.<br><br>the FTIRI machine is pretty cool, as it produces graphical images of chemical content, as opposed to single spectra. the machine detects and array of 64x64 spectra and compiles them into a single-plane image, color-coding each probed peak\'s intensity and integral to produce a visual image. actually, that was my part of all of this; i didnt really participate in the actual scientific study so much as i did the technical side of the digital aquisition and array modelling.<br><br><a href=\"http://www.hss.edu/htdoc/research/IR%20Imaging%20Web%20Page-HSS/IR%20Imaging%20web%20page%20HSS_new.asp\">here\'s info</a> about my machine/job, oh oh.  gasp, i am a dork. apologies for that...</tt>',1,1159,57,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(17999,2952,'2002-08-02 18:37:55',59,'s5','the phrase \\\"fourier transform\\\" gives me warm fuzzies. :)',1,62,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18000,2952,'2002-08-02 18:45:23',17,'austin','i am astonished.',3,16,0,0,'205.179.19.163','en'),(18001,2952,'2002-08-02 18:51:14',66,'Peter','<br><tt>me too; im also fond of <i>partial differential equations</i> and <i>boundary analysis</i> and how they all tie in to the <i>FT</i>. yay math!</tt>',1,155,59,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18002,2952,'2002-08-02 19:04:58',82,'simon','we once built a motel room set for a movie and made walls, plastered them, painted them and then, in order to age and crack and chip the paint realistically, i put denatured alcohol in a hudson sprayer and sprayed the fuckers down. it got all over me, made me nauseous and burned my skin. ow. but hey, it looked hot as fuck.',0,324,66,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18003,2952,'2002-08-02 19:09:47',334,'gone','denatured means that they put benzene and other nasties in it.  absolute ethanol just tastes bitter as hell...it doesn\\\'t usually burn.',1,137,82,0,'144.92.164.199','en'),(18004,2952,'2002-08-02 19:14:08',206,'josta','this is truly cool! i work at nasa grc and the only spectrometers (ametek dycor stuff) i get to use are as old as me.\r\n\r\nacetone sucks ass, as does hexane.',2,155,0,0,'128.156.10.12','en'),(18005,2952,'2002-08-02 19:18:28',66,'Peter','<br><tt>but dude, that stuff dehydrates yout skin <i>so fast</i> that it feels like fire! ouch! and my boss is too cheap to splurge on luxuries like neoprene gloves, too, heh...</tt>',1,182,334,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18006,2952,'2002-08-02 19:23:10',66,'Peter','<br><tt>dycor is some oldskool stuff indeed... we have an old rusty ametek residual oxygen analyzer that- i swear to god- runs on vacuum tubes and makes <i>eniac-esque</i> clicks and whirrs. since a new one costs some obscenely large amount of cash, they just keep repairing the old stuff and recalibrating it like every month, heh. we have some stone-age mattson spectral analyzers, too, that have these crazy 60\\\'s sci-fi analog light-up panels that make them look like they fell out of star-trek.</tt>',1,506,206,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18007,2952,'2002-08-02 21:05:29',124,'gus','you are all very smarty pants. except for the fact that you wont buy your own damn neoprene gloves.  But then my dad used to stick his hands in a tank of sulfuric acid for a few seconds each night to \\\"keep them nice and soft!\\\" so you know.',0,245,0,0,'66.32.178.106','en'),(18008,2945,'2002-08-02 21:31:11',86,'egg','hi chiamatt. you said you liked comments a second ago so I clicked on it. you are a rare and beautiful creature. dont succumb to peer pressure!',0,143,0,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(18009,2894,'2002-08-02 21:59:54',82,'simon','i just re-read this and want to say, i effin love it. just fyi.',0,63,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18010,2866,'2002-08-02 22:13:44',59,'s5','denouement: G-Sprout! was awesome.',0,34,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18011,2952,'2002-08-03 01:02:17',122,'hool','<p>fourier transforms are cool shit. it\\\'s cool that they\\\'re used across so many different domains.  i studied fast fourier transforms in an upper year math course.  i had no idea (until now, did a bit of google searching on them in general) that they were applied across multiple domains.  they seem like a physical phenomenon.  cool. </p>',0,345,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(18012,2952,'2002-08-03 01:44:24',59,'s5','one of my old professors, dr. cooley, developed the fast fourier transform algorithm. i felt like an EE fanboy at the time.',0,123,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18013,2952,'2002-08-03 02:54:15',65,'le','one time i asked peter if he was a scientist and he was forced to say yes.',4,74,0,0,'208.239.147.211','en'),(18014,2806,'2002-08-03 03:16:29',130,'treetop','hi<p>This dream might have been a foreshadowing of the relationship I was in at the time.  weird relationship = weird dream.  She was taking my energy.  I am much better off being away that person.\r\n\r\n<p>The other day I had another dream that was weird.  I was in a car accident and my penis was cut off somehow.  I carried it with me to the emergency room to have it sewn back on.  But, the ER staff kept doing other things.\r\n\r\n<p>One nurse started beating a snare drum with sticks until she broke through the head.  I kept asking them to fix my penis and they kept ignoring me.  My thinking was, the faster they put this thing back on the better the success rate.\r\n\r\n<p>Eventually I told the doctor that I was going to kill myself if they didn\\\'t sew it back on immediately.  He said, OK.  Then I woke up in a pool of sweat.  Now that was a frustrating dream!</p>',3,867,0,0,'172.166.79.196','en'),(18015,2945,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','s/rare and beautiful creature/splendid butterfly/',6,49,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18016,2945,'2002-08-03 15:13:19',86,'egg','it is his wings that make him beautiful. ',0,41,62,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(18017,2952,'2002-08-03 21:59:58',358,'waterpig','your posts never cease to amaze.  perusing this actually made me miss my old days at UTHC mucking about with gel electrophoresis and HPLC. ',0,139,66,0,'63.172.168.174','en'),(18018,2952,'2002-08-04 03:15:31',151,'stella','I\\\'m always so impressed by people who are insanely artist, and also are very inclined mathematically and or scientifically. I suck at math/science and therefore am in awe of the brain that can encompass such realms all at once. You rock, Peter.',1,247,0,0,'168.100.203.247','en'),(18019,2950,'2002-08-04 10:27:38',234,'etienne','houses on stilts are in papua new guinea, not auckland...',1,57,66,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(18020,2952,'2002-08-04 10:30:10',234,'etienne','*speechless*',1,12,66,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(18021,2944,'2002-08-04 22:18:06',195,'matt','<I><B>\\\"in a way \\\'i am you\\\'\\\"</B></I><BR><BR>yes. so divine, to get that flash that we are all part of a greater whole, working, in all our differing ways, for the ultimate good of the whole.<BR><BR>this feeling, when i get it, makes me want to try ecstasy. i want to amplify it.',1,289,0,0,'209.208.109.226','en'),(18022,2952,'2002-08-05 02:59:02',148,'jeeff','brain... hurting...\r\n\r\now.',1,26,0,0,'24.43.239.77','en'),(18023,2956,'2002-08-05 06:43:33',17,'austin','i am eating bland cream of wheat.',3,33,0,0,'64.174.155.111','en'),(18024,2956,'2002-08-05 09:51:12',96,'dj1luna','  \\\' in august and everything after, i come after everything \\\' ',2,68,0,0,'24.27.26.240','en'),(18025,2956,'2002-08-05 11:51:24',59,'s5','this article reminded me of that dunkin donuts ad from a long time ago where the donut guy gets out of bed, half-asleep, scratches himself, and says \"time to make the donuts\" over and over again on his way to the shower in that grumbly donut man voice. i guess you had to be there.',0,286,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18026,2956,'2002-08-05 12:47:29',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this article made me think of the commercial i hear or see about 50 times a day... <i>\"a sunday just isnt a sunday without the new york times\"</i>; but then again, everything that mentions \"sunday\" makes me think of that. guess their psychology worked. damn sundays.</tt>',1,287,0,0,'204.168.247.124','en'),(18027,2956,'2002-08-05 17:55:36',82,'simon','i love sundays. they\\\'re my favorite day. especially when boozey brunch is involved.',0,86,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18028,2956,'2002-08-05 18:16:26',17,'austin','sims, you totally stood me up for the boozy sunday brunch.  totally. stood. me. up.',6,83,82,0,'205.179.19.163','en'),(18029,2956,'2002-08-05 19:14:36',82,'simon','i know, i\\\'m sorry. instead of enjoying a boozy brunch with you, i was dealing with the aftermath of a really scary car accident that happened in front of my house late the night before.',0,188,17,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18030,2956,'2002-08-05 19:37:55',90,'leonard','i had bland oatmeal.',1,20,17,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(18031,2951,'2002-08-05 20:25:27',90,'leonard','Young Spindlechair replied, \\\"It\\\'s a good thing I don\\\'t have any feelings. A damn good thing.\\\"',1,105,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(18032,2956,'2002-08-05 21:52:45',234,'etienne','i hate sundays.  but i like this.',1,33,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(18033,2948,'2002-08-06 01:20:22',188,'bark','the little seasonal stream has dried up and the berries are starting to fade.  so she strolled down into the draw today and gathered the last of the black berries for cobbler.  it is in the oven.  actually, i\\\'ve proabbly eaten it by now. it was probably very good.  i can\\\'t remember yet. \r\n ',0,297,0,0,'12.110.65.175','en'),(18034,2925,'2002-08-06 02:26:46',366,'eunoia','<p>I can tell that you haven\\\'t been sleeping so well again. Your eyes look a little bit puffy, and I can see that you are getting the shakes from overworking yourself. But, you are the first one to say that you don\\\'t work hard enough, and I respect that attitude.</p>\r\n<p> Maybe you say that because it\\\'s a defensive reaction on your part because your parents and friends are quick to persecute you, for nothing but living, a little self-indulgence here and there, and who doesn\\\'t do that when other people aren\\\'t around its easy to slip up. Who asked you to carry those burdens for others when you have so many of your own? If you just shift yourself out of the equation, everything else becomes easier to solve: objectivity, they called it once.</P>\r\n<p>I just worry that you are burning yourself out. For what? Is it love again? You know you have the biggest heart, so big that you could get a world inside of it, and you certainly try to and no one asked you even, but you want to hold on so dearly to all of humanity, but you have to please yourself sometime, because the weight of the world is not for you to carry alone and you\\\'re allowed to enjoy yourself on this earth if there is only one lifetime.</p>\r\n<p> Well you may reoccur again, or at least have a tendency to appear, if superstring theory is correct. You know I don\\\'t even know if you exist sometimes because its like you are an extension of me, not completely me, but I think like you, or least I want to think like you, that\\\'s why I listen so deeply and drink in every story you tell me. It never had to be truthful because I do it myself. We all lie in public, our parents, our friends, our jobs, our resume is an example of who we want to see when we wake up each morning.</P>\r\n<p> Those eyes just look heavy with sleep everytime, puffy, without rest and dreaming through the waking life without realization that we don\\\'t travel in linear fashion, as there are a million deaths piled up on eachother: we rode our bikes and got our degrees and off we pedalled into our mid twenties looking for love or respect or some quotient that seems reasonable enough to keep us together. Is it marriage or drugs or religion that\\\'s the big fix? or is it that we can\\\'t come to terms with falling apart, not just hopes, but reality dissolves into the sea of faces that passes in the street reminding us that we are too faceless to other people too.</P>\r\n<p> Why were we so self-centred together. When I have kids I won\\\'t be so fixated on me, the paradigm will shift, it has to okay. Even as I write this I feel myself removed, and you? You probably don\\\'t think this is about you at all and you probably don\\\'t think about me at all. And who are you to think you exist when I\\\'m convinced you are only a figment that has a tendency to occur. But, still I have to write you to make sure I\\\'m together again because in the silence I\\\'m not at home.</P>\r\n',1,2955,122,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(18035,2952,'2002-08-06 17:21:46',195,'matt','we\\\'re all polymaths on here:)',1,32,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(18036,2952,'2002-08-06 17:23:02',195,'matt','fourier serieses are also very important in economics.',1,54,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(18037,2953,'2002-08-06 17:30:40',366,'eunoia','Mike and Ikes are sick candy. I had one friend who always used to have them up for trade at lunch time, which no one but the homely girl would trade for. \r\n\r\nLike you, I have a bit of a candy fetish as well. I grew up eating some black liquorice things called nibs at the time. I found out that way back in the seventies they were called \\\"nigger babies.\\\" That\\\'s pretty offensive isn\\\'t it. \r\n\r\nI hate anything that tastes like that racist candy now.',4,460,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(18038,2957,'2002-08-06 17:57:06',82,'simon','i was watching desperately seeking susan the other day and someone remarked, you know, it\\\'s very \\\'80s to have amnesia. and i agreed.',0,138,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18039,2957,'2002-08-06 18:08:47',66,'Peter','<br><tt>also very 80\\\'s... <i>bad perms</i>:<br><br><img src=\\\"http://www.beardburn.com/popit/madonna3.jpg\\\"></tt>',1,120,82,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18040,2957,'2002-08-06 18:38:28',82,'simon','i wish you had the shot a few seconds after this where she\\\'s airing her armpit on the hand dryer and you see her black bra under the pink mesh coca cola tshirt. oooh so hot.',0,176,66,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18041,2958,'2002-08-06 18:58:31',66,'Peter','<br><tt>every day last week, i had an immense craving for tomatoes; perhaps my body needed its one micromole of molybdenum or something. i bought dozens of big, plump vine-ripened suckers from the fruit vendor on my street, diced them up, made them into soups and stews, sliced them for sandwiches, simmered them into sauce.<br><br>this week, im craving spinach. i know its tangential, but thinking <i>tomatoes!</i> made me remember how rewarding its been, this past week or so, to eat strictly according to what my body tells me it wants.</tt>',1,544,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18042,2957,'2002-08-06 19:23:50',175,'Paul G','Sigh: <a href=\\\"http://www.madonnashots.com/1985.html\\\">1985</a>.',0,69,82,0,'67.248.52.154','en'),(18043,2958,'2002-08-06 19:32:41',124,'gus','there were vine ripened orange and yellow tomatos with the red when I went to the store this morning. i got a few of each. I will make my famous tomato salad. do you want to make it too? ok!\r\n<p>\r\n<b>Gus\\\' Tomato Salad</b>\r\nIngredients!<br>\r\ntomatos (just red, or make it fancy with yellow and/or orange thrown in)<br>\r\nred onion<br>\r\nfresh basil<br>\r\nbalsamic vinegar<br>\r\nvirgin olive oil<br>\r\nsalt and pepper\r\n<p>\r\nslice the tomatos in thin slices. slice onions real thin, chop up the basil real nice. dump enough balsamic to cover them all well, dump in enough olive oil to coat them too. put in lots of salt so the onions wont be too sharp. then some pepper. let is marinate for a half hour if you want or just eat it. if you let it sit a day it only gets better as the onions mellow out and it soaks up the basil and vinegar. \r\n\r\n',0,838,0,0,'64.160.53.114','en'),(18044,2957,'2002-08-06 19:38:44',175,'Paul G','Also: <p><img src=\\\"http://www.madonnashots.com/suscap06.jpg\\\"></p>',0,71,0,0,'67.248.52.154','en'),(18045,2957,'2002-08-06 19:44:11',82,'simon','the mid 80s were a great time for madonna\\\'s boobs, don\\\'t you think?',0,73,175,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18046,2957,'2002-08-06 20:32:53',371,'Kyla','Oh dear gods!  How did this happen?  :P',6,39,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18047,2957,'2002-08-06 20:37:56',175,'Paul G','Yes!  I seriously liked her style: it got real cool again to have a full body and curves.  It swings back and forth, I guess.  My ex looked like her - after a fashion.  <b>Sigh.</b>  For me - I think because I was in high school and college in the 80\\\'s - this [<I>Desperately Seeking Susan</I> look]  is still the pinnacle of sexuality. ',0,340,82,0,'67.248.86.32','en'),(18048,2958,'2002-08-06 20:56:04',175,'Paul G','<p><b>Tomato Basil Pasta</b><br>\r\n1) Soak tomatoes, basil, and garlic in cold water<br>\r\n2) Fill a stock-pot with water for pasta, and heat<br>\r\n3) Slice tomatoes and place on a plate<br>\r\n4) Crush garlic and place next to tomatoes<br>\r\n5) Coarsely cut basil and place next to garlic<br>\r\n6) Heat a large frying pan<br>\r\n7) Add enough olive oil to pan to coat it<br>\r\n8) Add garlic to oiled pan, cook, do not let it brown<br>\r\n9) Add tomatoes to garlic and oil<br>\r\n10)Cook tomatoes and garlic till tomatoes melt<br>\r\n11)Add Basil to tomato-garlic-oil mixture<br>\r\n12)Add salt to taste<br>\r\n13)During this process cook favorite pasta and drain<br>\r\n14)Serve tomato mixture over pasta<br>\r\n<b>Ingredients</b>: <br>\r\nRipe Tomatoes- about 3 per person<br>\r\nGarlic- about 1 to 3 cloves per person, depending<br>\r\nBasil- 1 sprig (6 leaves) per person<br>\r\nOlive Oil- extra virgin first cold pressing<br>\r\nPasta- favorite shape<br>\r\n<b>Optiona</b>l: <br>\r\nParmesan/Romano grated cheese added after cooking<br>\r\nRed Pepper added along with garlic, amount to taste<br>\r\n<b>Serve with</b>: <br>\r\nRed Wine (Chianti, Zinfandel, Merlot, Shiraz) <br>\r\nGreen Salad</p>',0,1154,124,0,'67.248.86.32','en'),(18049,2957,'2002-08-06 21:15:41',234,'etienne','oh that is the best site ever.  i have been having massive madonna nostalgia this week because starlajo & thomas gave me a double dvd set of madonna videos.  and i say, ROCK.<br><br>\r\nthe other thing that rocks is our band kyles!!',1,230,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(18050,2957,'2002-08-06 21:27:19',234,'etienne','i also now have <a href=\\\"http://w1.112.telia.com/%7Eu11207498/wall/wallp10.jpg\\\">this</a> as my desktop.  yeah.',1,116,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(18051,2958,'2002-08-06 21:32:09',75,'tyler','wow. it seems like almost everybody here is 25 and unemployes or nearly so.',1,75,0,0,'165.247.190.67','en'),(18052,2957,'2002-08-06 21:37:35',82,'simon','i\\\'m afraid it was my fault. oops!',0,36,371,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18053,2957,'2002-08-06 22:58:32',371,'Kyla','That\\\'s okay.  The Madonna pictures were a nice distraction.  I spent too long looking at that damn website :>',6,112,82,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18054,2957,'2002-08-06 22:59:44',371,'Kyla','Yes, we do rock.  We\\\'ll be as rich and famous as Madonna, it\\\'s all good.',6,78,234,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18055,2958,'2002-08-07 00:17:25',124,'gus','I am not 25. ',0,13,0,0,'64.160.53.114','en'),(18056,2958,'2002-08-07 01:10:53',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i am 25... but oh wait, i have a job.</tt>',1,50,75,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18057,2957,'2002-08-07 02:32:34',234,'etienne','even just that famous would be good!  pity i don\\\'t look anything like her.  nor will i wear tops which make my