 sailing through the sky. above, within the cloud strata. enveloped by the phasing whitenoise of twin-engines. hiss of cabin pressure. the roundels of sunset shadow arcing through tempered glass window portals. turbulent rattle of red-colored emergency handles. whining scream of climbing engines. thin air. cirrus condensation on chromed aluminum exoskeleton. wings of <a href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1532\">death</a>, wings of <a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1510.txt\">life</a>, zoom lift soar glide roar boom hiss hush. touchdown... <i>we\'re flying</i>.</tt> ',1,603,0,0,'204.168.247.124','en'),(18070,2929,'2002-08-07 16:24:35',86,'egg','this is brilliant. you should write a movie script.',0,51,0,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(18071,2957,'2002-08-07 17:11:57',82,'simon','i\\\'ll do it if you do it.',0,27,86,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18072,2956,'2002-08-07 18:01:48',74,'jo','i hate that commercial.',1,23,66,0,'172.130.57.93','en'),(18073,2958,'2002-08-07 19:31:30',176,'Scapegoat','I\\\'m 25 and will be unemployed in just under a month and a half.',0,66,0,0,'204.19.79.66','en'),(18074,2958,'2002-08-07 20:31:17',82,'simon','i just turned 23 and i have a few jobs',0,38,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18075,2950,'2002-08-07 20:43:24',371,'Kyla','Yaaay!',6,6,57,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18076,2959,'2002-08-07 21:02:49',72,'essemar','<p>I know a guy who is kicking himself for not having had a child when he was 18.  The fantasy goes like this:  Having a child at 18 means that kid will be 18 (read <i>leaving the house</i>) when you are 36.  Boom, you\\\'re done with the whole child rearing thing in your mid-thirties and can go on with your life occasionally musing to the missus, \\\"Wonder what the kids are up to these days.\\\"</p><P>Anyways, he\\\'s currently pushing the big three-O and didn\\\'t have any kids in his teens.  He did the \\\'responsible\\\' thing going to college, fucking around, taking drugs, and partying instead of what his friend did having kids at 17, going to college, getting a lucrative job at Microsoft, and driving his Porche to work every day from his beautiful house in the suburbs.</p>',4,790,0,0,'64.12.96.202','en'),(18077,2958,'2002-08-07 22:35:48',17,'austin','i hate this commercial.',6,23,0,0,'205.179.19.163','en'),(18078,2963,'2002-08-07 23:47:07',17,'austin','dammit.  this is DIRTY!  nobody under 17 read this.  i\\\'ll throw you in jail.  this is great!\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni need a cigarette.',1,95,0,0,'205.179.19.163','en'),(18079,2963,'2002-08-07 23:53:25',82,'simon','sean-eh. ',0,9,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18080,2963,'2002-08-08 00:06:13',90,'leonard','i just called my wife. we\\\'re going home for a little bit, or however long it takes.',1,86,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(18081,2963,'2002-08-08 00:08:01',377,'dammit','it was hard to to type all that with one hand : (',0,49,0,0,'66.122.107.46','en'),(18082,2957,'2002-08-08 01:21:32',234,'etienne','no, no its not. i\\\'m all for showing my breasts off, but i dont\\\' think that\\\'s the best way to do it.',1,108,371,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(18083,2963,'2002-08-08 02:09:22',86,'egg','dammit. and to think I thought you were a nice Catholic boy from Tewsbury. or is it Tyngsboro. Im ripping you off my to-do list.',0,128,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(18084,2961,'2002-08-08 05:16:21',234,'etienne','i know who you are.  ',0,21,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(18085,2963,'2002-08-08 06:11:37',65,'le','fuck that was fucking AWESOME.',4,30,0,0,'208.239.147.211','en'),(18086,2963,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>oh</p>\r\n\r\n<p>my</p>\r\n\r\n<p>GOD</p>',6,36,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18087,2963,'2002-08-08 07:34:10',120,'joyrider','in one hour i\\\'m picking up a friend for some fun.  if either of us die tonight, dammit, it\\\'s on your head.',0,112,0,0,'12.224.184.3','en'),(18088,2963,'2002-08-08 07:39:14',59,'s5','holy fuck.',0,10,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18089,2963,'2002-08-08 18:15:44',176,'Scapegoat','That was damn good. Shouldn\\\'t have read it at work though, I think I need to leave early today.  ',0,100,0,0,'204.19.79.66','en'),(18090,2957,'2002-08-08 21:30:12',371,'Kyla','*grin*',6,6,234,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18091,2961,'2002-08-08 23:20:07',366,'eunoia','<p>I think that I have to offer little solid advice in this world that is why I am often a great listener, since I\\\'m waiting for those gems of wisdom to be bestowed upon me.</P>\r\n\r\n<p> However, when it comes to writing and men who claim to be writers I feel like I am not too out of my skin. I give you this caveat: never trust a man who is writing a novel and talks about it -- he\\\'s broke and he\\\'s dreaming aloud trying to make you fall in love with the idea of him being something more than a public transport rider, and at 2:30 am you\\\'ve got to believe the stakes are high that he has alterior motives.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> Still you should fall in love at least thrice daily -- that is my greated advice, fool proof.</P> ',1,727,0,0,'24.156.7.170','en'),(18092,2961,'2002-08-08 23:23:07',366,'eunoia','sercretly you\\\'re envious of him. You always want to talk to people on the subway in Toronto. The girls just look too bitchy, and they all look like their boyfriend is within two feet.',4,186,366,0,'24.156.7.170','en'),(18093,2963,'2002-08-08 23:24:51',124,'gus','look at all the ego stroking you\\\'re getting! why, it\\\'s getting almost as much strocking as your cock! oh wait... that\\\'s probably not possible.',0,151,377,0,'64.160.53.114','en'),(18094,2963,'2002-08-08 23:57:46',168,'laney','holy fucking fuck. i\\\'m surprised i got this through the porn filters! nice job, dude.',0,88,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(18095,2963,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','what porn filters?',0,18,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(18096,2963,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','oh, you mean the PORN FILTERS. the horde of sweaty child goblin labor we keep in the machine to collect every last shred of precious porn from the database for its preservation and distribution. yes.',0,199,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(18097,2963,'2002-08-09 02:28:29',261,'sine','\r\n\r\ngoblins?\r\n\r\nhi dammit, I thought you were dead. just kidding.',1,65,0,0,'216.102.198.168','en'),(18098,2963,'2002-08-09 02:56:44',334,'gone','well, i\\\'m depressed.',1,23,0,0,'64.108.128.56','en'),(18099,2963,'2002-08-09 03:50:02',20,'jim','*re-bookmarks Swinney.org*',6,26,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(18100,2963,'2002-08-09 03:54:08',20,'jim','also, Tsing-Tao is a really good stripper name.',6,47,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(18101,2961,'2002-08-09 04:36:39',156,'lotus','i\\\'m not sure what this is: coincidences? meaning of life?\r\n<br><br>\r\ni am feeling very lonely and depressed, and i want to call someone, but it is late and i will disturb the people i love. so i went to email, and found a letter from my friend hool, and after reading it i felt like dancing, and also was reminded of swinney, and i read this artlicle first, and umm, four hours ago i just finished the unknown errors of our lives? what is going on. this is so weird i\\\'m not sure if in my sleep-deprived state i am making this up.\r\n<br><br>\r\np.s. which were your favourite stories in it?',1,592,0,0,'64.230.106.251','en'),(18102,2952,'2002-08-09 05:02:46',261,'sine','\r\n\r\nmy man be smart!!',1,21,0,0,'216.102.198.168','en'),(18103,2961,'2002-08-09 05:03:59',175,'Paul G','<br>\r\nDear lotus,<br>\r\nPlease write, here, again.<br>\r\nPaul.<br>\r\n[best friendly ]',1,82,156,0,'65.144.129.18','en'),(18104,2861,'2002-08-09 06:32:51',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i have the bamboo pen now, and it is indeed magical, somehow, and covered with tiny inky magical sine fingerprints.</tt>',1,74,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18105,2963,'2002-08-09 17:02:42',17,'austin','a fine beer too!',2,16,20,0,'205.179.19.163','en'),(18106,2964,'2002-08-09 17:34:36',75,'tyler','i don\\\'t think being a &quot;gemini&quot; has anything to do with daydreaming at a sucky boring job.',1,102,0,0,'165.247.179.9','en'),(18107,2963,'2002-08-09 17:44:12',75,'tyler','&quot;it was exactly what I wanted, and the worst possible way to get it.&quot;\r\n--awesome',1,90,0,0,'165.247.179.9','en'),(18108,2958,'2002-08-09 20:11:05',370,'cumshot','you say tomato, i say tomato. wait, that\\\'s not funny.',0,56,0,0,'67.68.64.233','en'),(18109,2961,'2002-08-09 23:31:35',376,'incognito','<p><3</p>',1,9,234,0,'12.88.161.34','en'),(18110,2961,'2002-08-09 23:37:46',376,'incognito','yes, you are probably right about alterior motives where would-be novelists are concerned.<br>\r\nsomewhere there is a trick to unthreatening conversations with strangers on public transport. this guy was definitely entertaining me, but sometimes i am just bothered and a little scared. the night after this incident, a man kept standing directly in front of me on the train and just looking at me, i think attempting to make eye contact before moving on to some kind of <i>hey cutie</i> sort of line. but whenever i\\\'d look back at him, i would give him the evil eye. and he didn\\\'t just back off, he moved clear into the next car.',1,634,366,0,'12.88.161.34','en'),(18111,2961,'2002-08-09 23:40:52',376,'incognito','oh, lotus. that is just the kind of synchronicity that keeps me going most days. i wish i knew what it meant, though i might enjoy such moments less if there was concrete meaning behind them.</p>\r\n<p>as for the book: i liked \\\"mrs dutta writes a letter\\\" because it reminded me of this idea that had been kicking about around in conversations the time that i read it: \\\"it\\\'s not the trauma, it\\\'s the repression of the trauma.\\\" i like when themes get reinforced like that. i was impressed by the pacing in \\\"what the body knows,\\\" which is perhaps a dorky response to have to a story. but it moved along so evenly. there were just these perfect moments throughout the book that i loved; like:</p>\r\n<p><i>There are fireflies everywhere tonight, pinpoints of light blurring into a luminous ooze. Perhaps to disappear is the next best thing to being forgotten.</i></p>\r\n<p>what did you like in it?</p>',1,916,156,0,'12.88.161.34','en'),(18112,2960,'2002-08-09 23:52:33',376,'incognito','amen.',2,5,0,0,'12.88.161.34','en'),(18113,2960,'2002-08-10 05:56:16',59,'s5','i like doing nothing.',0,21,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18114,2960,'2002-08-10 07:14:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i <i>do nothing</i> for a living; someone actually pays me for it too. yay.</tt>',1,88,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18115,2960,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','it has been said that out of hardship comes the best art.',6,57,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18116,2965,'2002-08-10 23:03:24',124,'gus','except for all the times you\\\'ve published your own stuff on the interwebs...',0,79,0,0,'64.169.160.225','en'),(18117,1672,'2002-08-11 00:35:50',66,'Peter','<br><tt>and exactly one year later, things make a complete circle; some vague cycles heal things, set new things in motion, and last year provides a launchpad for this one. life is so good to me sometimes in these current moments, especially through its being bad in past experiences.<br><br>god, i am such a better person this year, and in such a better place. i am happy. <i>yay</i> being happy.</tt>',1,210,59,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18118,2927,'2002-08-11 02:10:50',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this piece wasn\\\'t about a tree, by the way.</tt>',1,59,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18119,2964,'2002-08-11 20:43:39',371,'Kyla','I was more referring there to my double life.. reality and the world in my head, haha ;)',1,88,75,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18120,2960,'2002-08-11 22:28:30',234,'etienne','but does it have to be that way?',1,32,62,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(18121,2956,'2002-08-12 00:00:51',297,'kelly','i\\\'ve hated sundays for about a year now. that shawn mullins lyric that wss quoted was always one of my favorites because i understood it so well. ',0,149,0,0,'24.185.90.90','en'),(18122,2960,'2002-08-12 02:05:42',195,'matt','yeah. stay hungry.',1,18,0,0,'209.208.108.126','en'),(18123,2812,'2002-08-12 03:10:50',376,'incognito','i\\\'m sure it\\\'s of little consolation to you that your thoughts here come out so achingly fantastic, but partially it\\\'s so great to me because i know those hurts, that sharp inbetween while at the edge of the world. it\\\'s almost comforting for me to remember it\\\'s not a solitary feeling. that\\\'s easy to forget when such times cloud the mind.',2,356,120,0,'12.88.160.230','en'),(18124,2927,'2002-08-12 03:24:47',234,'etienne','is it ever?',1,11,66,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(18125,2960,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','why not?  artists are just people, not superhero deities.  they\\\'re well equipt to look after themselves.',6,107,234,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18126,2964,'2002-08-12 20:06:09',176,'Scapegoat','I can\\\'t say that being a gemini has a casual relationship with the dual life you are talking about but as fellow gemini that suffers from a similar split I defintely think that there is a corelation. ',0,203,371,0,'204.19.79.66','en'),(18127,2453,'2002-08-12 21:32:16',251,'Jamie','heh i hope that wasn\\\'t my crappy code that got u fired dude ;)',1,65,66,0,'212.159.23.145','en'),(18128,2970,'2002-08-13 00:16:33',90,'leonard','life is very long when you\\\'re lonely...',1,42,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(18129,2968,'2002-08-13 07:05:57',93,'chiamattt','ottawa shmottawa',6,16,0,0,'211.32.132.226','en'),(18130,2971,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','this is a fine, fine entry.  porny entries  on swinney #1.',6,58,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18131,2971,'2002-08-13 09:02:38',59,'s5','and here i thought you were a nice christian boy.',0,49,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18132,2971,'2002-08-13 12:52:44',66,'Peter','<br><tt>kind of gives an all new meaning to <i>turn the other cheek</i>, huh?</tt>',1,82,59,0,'204.168.247.124','en'),(18133,2968,'2002-08-13 13:11:14',93,'chiamattt','i cant help but think the only reason you want to move to ottawa again is because you had better luck with women there. heh. i laughed when i thought about that in class today. laughing in class doesnt really make my kids flinch anymore, but they still start laughing with me. it was nice to imagine them knowing exactly what i was laughing at and laughing along with me. instead of at me. 60% of the jr hotties were gone today. ',6,429,0,0,'61.37.53.25','en'),(18134,2968,'2002-08-13 14:18:37',176,'Scapegoat','I\\\'m interested in moving back for a variety of reasons actually. First being that I can get a joe job in Ottawa a hell of a lot faster then in Smogtown. Second I have expotentially more contacts in that town both male and female. Third over half of my family is there. Fourth all that Toronto has done for me is make me a more agressive person, I wanted to kill a homeless guy last night. I hate what this city is turning me into.<br><br>\r\nAs for the ladies Dave the only reason that I have a better track record in Ottawa is because I was there for 5 years versus the one here in Toronto. There has also been a priority shift in what I\\\'m looking for in a partner.<br><br>\r\nI also figure if I actually have a chance to leave this country I may as well spend the last bit of my time in the place of my birth and the capital. Not to mention that the downtown Toronto streets aren\\\'t board friendly. If I had $$$ then this city might be a bit more interesting but guess what...      ',0,988,93,0,'204.19.79.66','en'),(18135,2972,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<p>hello oof, you are great. come away with me. <br>\r\n</p><p>\r\nyour number one fan, <br>\r\nweeb</p>',1,98,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(18136,2972,'2002-08-13 16:21:25',17,'austin','ahem, sorry weeb.  you are actually his number <B>two</B> fan.',3,62,57,0,'205.179.19.163','en'),(18137,2968,'2002-08-13 17:36:44',123,'dcc','Hogtown; Smogtown. heh. nice play on words with that one.\r\n<p>\r\nI think I\\\'m totally on your side for getting out of this town. the excitement and pulse of the culture is what keeps me here. i guess it\\\'s different types of oppourtunities that draw you back to the capitol.',3,277,176,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(18138,2972,'2002-08-13 20:27:57',86,'egg','yeah, gotta keep a rhythm.\r\n<p>\r\nThis shaking keeps me steady. I should know.<br>\r\nWhat falls away is always. And is near.<br>\r\nI wake to sleep, and take my waking slow.<br>\r\nI learn by going where I have to go.<br>-Theodore Roethke</p>\r\n',0,238,0,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(18139,2971,'2002-08-13 20:34:20',75,'tyler','dang',1,4,0,0,'165.247.184.2','en'),(18140,2971,'2002-08-13 21:00:08',86,'egg','romance indeed.',0,15,0,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(18141,2971,'2002-08-13 21:37:32',83,'adamajah','that was extremely entertaining.  ',0,34,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(18142,2605,'2002-08-13 21:58:20',251,'Jamie','true. the last time i ate duck i almost vomited.',0,48,366,0,'62.252.192.7','en'),(18143,2595,'2002-08-13 22:04:08',251,'Jamie','it is... much to my discontent, though the feeling has somewhat subsided with the cessation of the dreams. though this may be temporary',0,135,260,0,'62.252.192.7','en'),(18144,2971,'2002-08-13 22:16:53',251,'Jamie','I don\\\'t have much to say on this piece other than i REALLY LIKE IT and i thought i should just make that point. good stuff boyo',0,130,0,0,'62.252.192.7','en'),(18145,2970,'2002-08-14 00:54:55',180,'auntie','life can seem very short when you have too many people calling on your time, too.  somewhere there is a happy medium.',0,117,90,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(18146,2964,'2002-08-14 00:57:04',180,'auntie','having dreams to succor you through mundane existence is a gift from god.',1,73,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(18147,2972,'2002-08-14 00:58:46',234,'etienne','<i>the trick is to keep breathing</i>',1,37,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(18148,2963,'2002-08-14 01:03:31',180,'auntie','i always wondered what a lapdance was like. not what i expected.  i do have a question, though ... is it actually possible for the male personal part to break in half?',4,167,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(18149,2960,'2002-08-14 02:15:37',234,'etienne','the difference being that art doesn\\\'t always get paid for at a regular hourly rate.  artists have to work other jobs to support themselves, which often takes away energy needed to create said art.  its a vicious cycle.  i never said they were superhuman deities, its just that considering what could be created if some individuals had the headspace, time and money its sad that they can\\\'t.  ',1,397,62,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(18150,2972,'2002-08-14 03:56:38',65,'le','i miss you.',0,11,0,0,'24.193.96.177','en'),(18151,2963,'2002-08-14 13:31:12',123,'dcc','someone remind me not to read swinney first thing in the morning. i think i need to leave now. sexy.',1,100,377,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(18152,2958,'2002-08-14 13:50:59',123,'dcc','<b>dcc\\\'s fresh salsa:</b><br>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>3 almost over-ripe BIG tomatoes\r\n<li>1/2 red onion\r\n<li>1 bunch fresh cilantro\r\n<li>1 bunch fresh MINT\r\n<li>2 cloves garlic\r\n<li>1 lime\r\n<li>1/2 teaspoon cayanne pepper to taste\r\n</ul>\r\n<p>\r\nchop chop chop up the tomatoes, onion and cilantro and mint and garlic. combine in a bowl. the fun part is mixing it all up with your hands.\r\n<p>\r\nsqueeeeze the juice out of the whole lime over the chopped up pile of veggies and spice with the cayanne to your preference. i say the hotter the better!',2,535,0,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(18153,2968,'2002-08-14 16:33:56',156,'lotus','come to ottawa.',0,15,0,0,'64.230.105.107','en'),(18154,2958,'2002-08-14 18:08:27',17,'austin','im going to try this salsa of yours. :)',3,39,123,0,'205.179.19.163','en'),(18155,2975,'2002-08-14 18:08:53',180,'auntie','despair is a terrible thing. it paralyzes you, making it impossible to change the condition that brought on the despair in the first place.  i have suffered from this many times in my life. the only thing i have ever found that will help it is to find someone who has an even more desperate situation than my own, and help them in some tangible way.  i learned from experience that you have to give the help anonymously as possible, or their despair will make your own even worse, sucking you dry. i eill pray for you.',1,518,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(18156,2976,'2002-08-15 00:31:33',371,'Kyla','<p>\r\nI understand 100% of what you are saying.  I broke up with my boyfriend (and I almost said recently, but that\\\'s such a lie) a while ago, but it ended up carrying on for a couple of years.  In limbo for all that time.  It nearly destroyed me!  I hated him and loved him all at the same time.. and I think in a way I still feel like that.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI wrote a song about it, called \\\'This reminds me of you\\\'.\r\n</p>\r\n',1,428,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18157,2976,'2002-08-15 00:49:51',66,'Peter','<br><tt>love. its the best.</tt>',1,32,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18158,2976,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','\r\nhating who you are. lying to yourself about the future. trying to change. succeeding. failing. seeing your partner in a new light- unsavory, then good. seeing him as others see him. seeing him as you see him. placing the responsibility foryour happiness squarely on someone else\\\'s shoulder. being completely independant of all expectations. falling out of love when you realize you will never be that original vision of yourself with this person. falling in love with your own imperfect self. falling out of love with the person because they weren\\\'t what you imagined them to be. working on the relationship once the initial euphoria wears off. being cynical. being consumed with joy. being thankful.\r\n\r\nyeah love.',0,722,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(18159,2976,'2002-08-15 02:10:35',82,'simon','i haven\\\'t seen my girlfriend for 3 months. and i\\\'ll be seeing her on friday.',0,82,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18160,2976,'2002-08-15 02:20:57',66,'Peter','<tt>odd. i havent ever seen my girlfriend. but ill be seeing her on saturday night.</tt>',1,88,82,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18161,2976,'2002-08-15 02:59:45',82,'simon','i\\\'m not sure how that works. but that\\\'s cool!',0,51,66,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18162,2963,'2002-08-15 03:03:36',82,'simon','heh, you said personal part! tee hee!',0,37,180,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18163,2976,'2002-08-15 03:03:44',175,'Paul G','Wow - I think that you \\\"showed\\\" more of your current self [assuming that this is non-fiction] in this piece than in anything before.  And both your vocabulary and also your content have changed.  It is good to read your thoughtful and well-crafted words again.  Thank you for writing.\r\n\r\n<p>I found myself stuck on this line in the first paragraph: \\\"i always feel funny when a man cries.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I suppose that it is pertinent to my own life, and what I am going through now; but more than that, it made me think a good deal about the way in which men tend to express themselves.  Your [or your fictitious self\\\'s] reaction to his tears was interesting.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The piece made me wonder, too, about when things between people really end: the two of you still desire things from each other, and still exchange things with each other.  What is absent seems to be physical contact.  That in turn begs the question: When do things really begin?  And how well is it possible to know someone, and care for them, through their words alone?</p>  ',0,1057,0,0,'67.248.139.224','en'),(18164,2976,'2002-08-15 03:10:51',175,'Paul G','[Also: Good luck simon/Peter.]',0,30,0,0,'67.248.139.224','en'),(18165,2976,'2002-08-15 03:16:52',66,'Peter','<br><tt>me either. such is life!</tt>',1,37,82,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18166,2976,'2002-08-15 03:47:09',234,'etienne','i have been in love and i have been loved.  i used to be scared that no one would ever love me again but then i realised that that was stupid, & i\\\'m glad i came to that realisation because thinking that was making me unhappy.',1,228,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(18167,2976,'2002-08-15 04:54:41',176,'Scapegoat','Being friends after a relationship is dependent on the individuals involved and circumstances that end the relationship in my experience. Until recently I always tried to maintain some sort of friendship bond with past girlfriends. Sometimes it is a little too difficult though as I get snagged up and can\\\'t move on with out more space.<br><br>\r\nAs for the desire to break <i>the rules</i> sometimes, I\\\'m with you on that one. I hate <i>the rules</i> alot of the time.<br><br>\r\nOn the soul thing Holly sounds cool couldn\\\'t agree more with that way of looking at things.<br><br>     ',0,591,0,0,'65.95.143.4','en'),(18168,2976,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','lotus, i swoon over your writing.',6,33,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18169,2976,'2002-08-15 09:14:03',86,'egg','I cant recall having been anything but grateful when a man cried.',0,65,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(18170,2968,'2002-08-15 12:38:50',93,'chiamattt','i like toronto. also, havnt you ever wanted to kill any homeless people in ottawa? i sure have. that guy \\\"if you cant spare any change. how about a smile!\\\" oh man. i had to walk past that guy for almost four years. whats worse is he knew i didnt like him. he\\\'d pipe down when i was close to him. i had an encounter with him on elgin. he was working the sidewalk outside of the bell building. he said something to me, and i was in a bad mood and said something to him. then he said something like \\\"just trying to get by\\\" and i turned around while still walking towards seans place and said \\\"so am i, im going to school, not panhandling\\\". its not that im against panhandling. i think its a fine way to make cash i guess if thats what you want. i know of at least a few people that make really good money panhandling or squeegying. they are the minority, but regardless, if i dont want to give them money then i dont think i should be given some cliche guilt trip phase. i dont know. im off topic. i just dont like ottawa. perhaps its because of my last four shitty months there, but i just didnt really like it the whole five years. its fucking cold and there isnt much i like to do there, other then eat out and drink at the duke. i loved the duke. i had great times there. i had great times in ottawa, its just not a city i can see myself settling down in. im finding that seoul is a GIANT ottawa. not much else to do here other than eat out and drink. as for the money part...yer livin pretty large down town with the condo. there are much cheaper parts of town where you can live. in he end i know its upto you, i used to really hate toronto, but when i moved back after school it really grew on me. good friendship network, lots of things i liked to do, and a great place to take pictures, something seoul lacks. i see a lot of cool things here, but they just dont strike me as being something \\\'ID\\\' like to take a picture of. be cool brother. ',6,1973,0,0,'61.37.53.25','en'),(18171,2976,'2002-08-15 13:28:10',153,'yhz','\\\"cities at night I feel contain men who cry in their sleep and say Nothing.\\\" ',0,83,0,0,'64.115.5.82','en'),(18172,2974,'2002-08-15 15:08:43',156,'lotus','damn copy and paste. this isn\\\'t supposed to have those weird line breaks. meh.',0,81,0,0,'64.230.106.161','en'),(18173,2968,'2002-08-15 15:11:45',156,'blue stocking','i never wanted to kill that panhandler, but i always try to walk very quickly past him. he stresses me out. he seems so strident. and then i feel guilty. i mentioned him once, in a <a href=http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2346.txt>prose piece</a>i wrote here.',1,271,93,0,'64.230.106.161','en'),(18174,2976,'2002-08-15 15:25:02',156,'lotus','\r\n<br><br>\r\nthank you to everyone for responding. *happy*\r\n<br><br>\r\nweeb! i read your comment and got to the end and went right back to the beginning and read it through again. so, parts of love are universal, then?\r\n<br><br>\r\nPaul G, you give such encouraging feedback! thank you. i haven\\\'t been able to write anything in months and months, and then yesterday the floodgates crashed open.\r\n<br><br>\r\nKyla. i have a terrible feeling my limbo-land relationship will go on for several years, like yours.\r\n<br><br>\r\npeter...i\\\'m intrigued. did you meet her online?\r\n<br><br>\r\nsimon - you must be so happy and/or excited?\r\n<br><br>\r\netienne. this is weird. a.t. and i had this exact same discussion two nights ago. that is how he felt, and still feels, and i can\\\'t convince him otherwise. self-perceptions are so weird. gah.\r\n<br><br>\r\no! you are always so sweet. i\\\'ve missed you!\r\n<br><br>\r\nscapegoat, do you have any tips? i can\\\'t, i just can\\\'t, cut him out of my life. but keeping him in it is making me crazy. \r\n<br>\r\nrules. i\\\'ve been meditating on rules.\r\n<br><br>\r\negg...gratitude, every time? wow. i don\\\'t know. i am so ambivalent, and i realise that this is irrational. i mean, why shouldn\\\'t men cry just as much as women?\r\n<br><br>\r\nyhz...who wrote that? it is moving. reads like the first line of a novel or something?',1,1351,0,0,'64.230.106.161','en'),(18175,2971,'2002-08-15 15:28:41',156,'lotus','dear matt,<br><br>\r\ni read your articles with a sort of delirious joy. you write incredibly well. i often go back and reread and wonder how you make your pieces seem so organic, how you play with form and make it work. i think it must come naturally to you. whenever i try anything like that, it ends up being too gimmicky. this was a great article.',1,349,0,0,'64.230.106.161','en'),(18176,2977,'2002-08-15 16:23:53',251,'Jamie','hey, at least she\\\'s not a picasso',4,36,0,0,'217.159.50.164','en'),(18177,2976,'2002-08-15 18:06:46',123,'dcc','there\\\'s no need to completely and permanently severe ties.\r\n<p>personally, i\\\'ve gotten really far in \\\'getting over it\\\' just by losing contact for a period of time. months or so. just a small reminder, quick phonecall, once in awhile keeps at least the idea of friendship alive. after, it can change into something brand new and exciting.',2,349,156,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(18178,2978,'2002-08-15 18:17:40',195,'matt','some people in my church think a unified europe is one more sign that the apocalypse is on its way. pretty funny.',1,113,0,0,'209.208.109.59','en'),(18179,2977,'2002-08-15 18:46:51',387,'tempest','what a pleasing collection of words. every girl should be lucky enough to have a boy feel that way about her.',0,109,0,0,'63.88.160.101','en'),(18180,2976,'2002-08-15 20:18:52',153,'yhz','Martin Amis. First line of <a href=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/tg/stores/detail/-/books/0679735739/reader/3/102-3028341-7832927#reader-link\\\">The Information</a>. I\\\'m slightly surprised I quoted it almost right, sight unseen, but then as you said, it is a good line. ',0,282,156,0,'64.115.5.82','en'),(18181,2978,'2002-08-15 20:26:51',251,'Jamie','i would hasten to agree. let\\\'s cast our minds back a way. does anyone remember who originally though up this whole unified europe scenario? clue: his surname rhymes with titler...',4,182,195,0,'195.92.168.163','en'),(18182,2976,'2002-08-15 21:42:54',234,'etienne','the thing is, i think he\\\'ll have to come to that realisation himself, because apart from loving him the way he wants to be loved [a tough ask at the best of times] you can\\\'t do shit about it.',1,197,156,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(18183,2974,'2002-08-15 21:44:28',234,'etienne','<p>it doesn\\\'t matter, its lovely.</p>\r\n<p><i>your love is the colour of evening</i></p>',1,90,156,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(18184,2970,'2002-08-15 22:33:39',175,'Paul G','It\\\'s terrifying, really.  Everything else in the world has disappeared.  Only the familiar rhythm remains.\r\n\r\n<p>What happened, or didn\\\'t happen, that made him go away...</p>',0,180,0,0,'67.248.78.59','en'),(18185,2978,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i am very excited about one day using euros.  they seem cool.',6,61,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18186,2974,'2002-08-16 12:34:31',156,'lotus','thank you!',1,10,234,0,'64.230.106.161','en'),(18187,2976,'2002-08-16 17:14:41',389,'Anlogo','Night Train?',0,12,153,0,'159.134.245.169','en'),(18188,2976,'2002-08-16 17:17:49',389,'Anlogo','\r\nA Martian Sends a Postcard Home  \r\nby Craig Raine \r\n\r\nCaxtons are mechanical birds with many wings \r\nand some are treasured for their markings-- \r\n\r\nthey cause the eyes to melt \r\nor the body to shriek without pain. \r\n\r\nI have never seen one fly, but \r\nsometimes they perch on the hand. \r\n\r\nMist is when the sky is tired of flight \r\nand rests its soft machine on the ground: \r\n\r\nthen the world is dim and bookish \r\nlike engravings under tissue paper. \r\n\r\nRain is when the earth is television. \r\nIt has the property of making colours darker. \r\n\r\nModel T is a room with the lock inside-- \r\na key is turned to free the world \r\n\r\nfor movement, so quick there is a film \r\nto watch for anything missed. \r\n\r\nBut time is tied to the wrist \r\nor kept in a box, ticking with impatience. \r\n\r\nIn homes, a haunted apparatus sleeps, \r\nthat snores when you pick it up. \r\n\r\nIf the ghost cries, they carry it \r\nto their lips and sooth it to sleep \r\n\r\nwith sounds. And yet, they wake it up \r\ndeliberately, by tickling with a finger. \r\n\r\nOnly the young are allowed to suffer \r\nopenly. Adults go to a punishment room \r\n\r\nwith water but nothing to eat. \r\nThey lock the door and suffer the noises \r\n\r\nalone. No one is exempt \r\nand everyone\\\'s pain has a different smell. \r\n\r\nAt night, when all the colours die, \r\nthey hide in pairs \r\n\r\nand read about themselves-- \r\nin colour, with the eyelids shut. \r\n',1,1382,156,0,'159.134.245.169','en'),(18189,2971,'2002-08-16 20:36:04',195,'matt','thank you all for such kind words.  and thank you to austin swinney for providing this forum for our expression.',1,112,0,0,'209.208.109.81','en'),(18190,2975,'2002-08-16 21:54:48',232,'necklace','<p>dear s.,</p> <p>your wedding dress was beautiful, but on the picture you were looking away. you know that this is not the kind of life that you should be living. you know there\\\'s more waiting for you around the corner, there\\\'s always more for people like you with talent, so much talent, my dear. you can leave. yes, you can. you can weave your own life with your magical threads of words. yes, you can. now, please do. please dare.</p>',3,445,0,0,'193.249.235.144','en'),(18191,2980,'2002-08-16 23:56:09',180,'auntie','i believe in stress.  i have diabetes and i know that any time i am unhappy, worried, or even bored, my sugar goes up like 75-100 points.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhen i am in my house in florida, my sugar drops 100 points from the time i arrive, no matter what i am eating.  as soon as i get back to texas, it goes right back up and i have to jump through hoops, take twice as much insulin, and do 7-10 miles on the exercise bike daily just to keep it from going into comatose levels.',1,471,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(18192,2977,'2002-08-17 02:16:07',358,'waterpig','she is a bit...but only during the summer he spent in gosol...something distinctly and classically mediteranean about her.\r\n\r\n',0,126,251,0,'208.155.247.194','en'),(18193,2977,'2002-08-17 02:26:01',358,'waterpig','pleased you liked it.  she puts the axons in my absinthe.  always will.  i am truly a fortunate bastard. ',0,105,387,0,'208.155.247.194','en'),(18194,2984,'2002-08-18 02:02:50',390,'saboteur','<i long for the slow friendly pace of ireland. just the thought of it has brought a smile to my face. thanks!/> ',0,112,0,0,'64.229.187.178','en'),(18195,2984,'2002-08-18 05:02:33',390,'saboteur','oops... i\\\'m new to all this *spins around on the swinney page* \r\ni long for the easy friendly ways of ireland. reading this brought a smile. thanks. ',0,152,0,0,'64.229.187.178','en'),(18196,2968,'2002-08-18 06:28:54',366,'eunoia','<p>I fell in love that last time I was in Ottawa. it\\\'s a place with many charms and fat government jobs. But do you have such short memory when thinking of the place and your last stay there.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>The last time I made Ottawa home I left to escape a woman and bilingualism.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Toronto, polluting unilinguals vs. Ottawa, bureaucratic-run smalltown bringtown (a trag\\\' hip song!).</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Tough choice, so I go with where there is fire and passion over the nurturing bossum of twattawa.</p>',3,509,176,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(18197,2980,'2002-08-18 11:17:12',64,'Joanna','I also throw up if I\\\'m very stressed.  There have been super fun periods of a couple of weeks when if I ate anything at all it would come right back.  It\\\'s not very healthy and I get terrified that it\\\'s going to reoccur sometimes.   ',1,242,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18198,2980,'2002-08-18 14:57:43',251,'Jamie','I think fear, from a strictly scientific point of view is a natural reaction of most animals to adversity. The <i>fight or flight syndrome</i> if you will. Though perhaps us humans with all our <i>intelligence</i> are a little less equipped to deal with it.',4,257,0,0,'195.92.194.17','en'),(18199,2984,'2002-08-18 16:30:43',180,'auntie','living in a small town anywhere is like that.  it can be a good thing or a bad thing.  if you forget to be careful about revealing yourself to people you meet in a small town, you might accidentally say something that colors the entire rest of your stay.',1,254,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(18200,2968,'2002-08-18 21:10:41',176,'Scapegoat','Fire and passion that made me laugh! There is no fire and passion here, that was one of the reasons I originally wanted to come here cuz compared to Ottz, Toronto has less of the old fire and passion. I needed a break from the overflowing passion. As for the language bit, anything \\\'bi\\\' is good to me.  ',0,309,366,0,'65.95.143.4','en'),(18201,2988,'2002-08-19 04:01:56',175,'Paul G','Wow, this was a really nice piece of writing: it held together well and maintained my interest despite its length; it made me curious.<br>  \r\nIt is also interesting to compare this to an  <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2698.txt\\\">earlier piece of yours</a>, from Asia, in which you described an encounter with a female prostitute/performer: the tone of the \\\"grass\\\" piece is darker - more shadowy - and yet you seem more comfortable in it.<br>\r\nIt is very intriguing to read your accounts of the \\\"dark underbelly\\\" of the culture that you are in.  Thanks for sharing. P.\r\n\r\n\r\n',0,618,0,0,'67.248.86.41','en'),(18202,2990,'2002-08-19 19:17:19',123,'dcc','wow, that was so worth reading. long, but at the same time it drew me further and further along.\r\n<p>wonderful work. please write more!!!',1,137,0,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(18203,2990,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i am a regular dammit fan.  it is nice that you can write so sweetly in addition to the nastyness of the last piece.  also i think i am hot for tsing tao.',6,154,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18204,2988,'2002-08-19 23:52:24',366,'eunoia','<p>You know that was a great story despite the name mr mysteria which I couldn\\\'t completely buy into, but you are a snarky kind of guy, so I kept thinking about him anway. In my mind\\\'s eye, he kind of looked like a mexican wrestler, with the crazy mask bue with gold sequince trim and dark chest hair. Turf could have been his tag team partner. Do you think I have been watching too much wwe smackdown? I do.</p> \r\n<p>You know what impressed me most was your composure in all of this. The chia that I know is a paranoid mofo, but that was when he was on the pot. Glad to hear that you are fearless in Seoul.</p>',0,617,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(18205,2979,'2002-08-20 00:06:37',366,'eunoia','<p>I\\\'m trying to be constructive, which rarely happens around here. It seems like mum\\\'s the word when people dislike something.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\\\'m not sure about the secret splinters thing. It just seems to cryptic for music. Are the splinters like thorns, like lies or sins? With thorns you can have a biblical tie in, but splinters get no respect. What kind of music do you do? or is it poetry? I say that the song needs work when I see a verse like this:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"and in the night, they all ignite,\r\nAnd pierce me once again.\r\nSometimes the white, it takes delight\r\nIn knowing it will remain.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It is a cryptic metaphor to begin with, and then that verse is just plain confusing. Are the splinters on fire? and who or what is the \\\"white\\\"? Jeez I didn\\\'t want to sound harsh.</P>\r\n\r\n',3,814,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(18206,2990,'2002-08-20 03:14:18',86,'egg','If you want to express a genuine mood of tenderness, you might try naming the female character something other than \\\"Tsing Tao\\\". it was an appropriate gag for the porno story but not for a character that is more or less supposed to feel like a real person. every time i read it i just think its a fetish or a joke.',0,320,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(18207,2988,'2002-08-20 03:34:04',75,'tyler','how can I be an English teacher in Korea?',1,41,0,0,'165.247.169.195','en'),(18208,2990,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','but isn\\\'t Tsing Tao the same Tsing Tao from \\\"Lapdance\\\"?  i feel like this is rewound a few years before she got into exotic dancing, when they were together.  i feel a series coming on. also i think Tsing Tao is a pretty cool name.  am i missing something?  does it mean \\\"big fat cunt\\\" or something like that?',6,324,86,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18209,2990,'2002-08-20 04:41:47',124,'gus','yah. our little dammit hass only showed us a couple of chapters in one longer story. <p>\r\nI agree that the name isnt the best, and exact addresses shouldnt be used and blahblah. He assures me it\\\'ll all be addressed in the first revision. ta da.',0,247,0,0,'64.172.58.39','en'),(18210,2965,'2002-08-20 05:24:28',366,'eunoia','Sugar, it\\\'s too bad your ass wasn\\\'t in the pic. It\\\'s one sweet badd ass cuz I n\\\'ver done seen another white boys ass like you. ',0,139,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(18211,2980,'2002-08-20 05:32:36',124,'gus','yah. stress is a natural reaction to environmental factors. a bear jumps out at the caveman and tries to eat him, he freaks and runs away. if that action of the bear didnt trigger that fight or flight stress response, the caveman gets eaten and his dna ceases to continue to exist. but the caveman runs back home in the bushes as fast as he can and sweats and pumps his muscles and flushes those stress chemicals and hormones out of his system.\r\n<p>\r\nwhen we\\\'re hit with a fight or flight response, we usually continue to sit in front of a computer for 8 hours. after a while there\\\'s a big build up and no release. so you drink excessively or punch some guy out, or burst into tears when the restaurant doesnt have the right kind of salad dressing. ',0,755,0,0,'64.172.58.39','en'),(18212,2979,'2002-08-20 05:43:01',124,'gus','I guess I agree it needs work, mostly cause rhyming is just hard to stomach sometimes, and especially so with lyrics if you cant hear the tune. but..\r\n<p>\r\nIm going to hazzard a guess that the splinters are like, splinters left over of the guy (or whoever) in the heart of the speaker person. you know, little bits of pain hiding out and then stabbing the speaker when they thought they had gotten the person all out of their system. you know. same story you\\\'ve heard a million times.\r\n<p>\r\nBecause it\\\'s easily seen as cryptic (i mean maybe Im wrong and it IS cryptic) and because if Im not way off, it\\\'s a story everyone\\\'s heard and told themselves a million times, the melody and delivery probably are HUGE in determining whether or not the song is good or crap. i cant hear the melody from here.',0,810,0,0,'64.172.58.39','en'),(18213,2990,'2002-08-20 05:54:01',377,'dammit','yeah. these are actually chapters from something that apparently wants to be a novel. no one in the writing has a real name except the narrator. it\\\'s the influence of gustav hasford\\\'s black comedy about vietnam, \\\"the short timers.\\\" let\\\'s see. so far, there\\\'s marshall, tsing tao, the destroyer, la chupacabra, hondo, bianca, kid abramovitz, the huffer, lil pete, methuselah, etc etc. although when one character refers to another by name they use a proper name: the destroyer is \\\"claire\\\" and la chupacabra is \\\"ines.\\\" do you care? no ; )\r\n<p>\r\noh yeah. tsing tao\\\'s back story is that she is the child of an alcholic american diplomat and a chinese academic. so he named her after his favorite beer. and yes, that back story is stolen from not one but <i>two</i> of my friends. ',0,809,0,0,'192.174.2.78','en'),(18214,2988,'2002-08-20 06:50:08',196,'jasonp','Yeah I read this all the way through.<BR>\r\nI liked this had a feeling of both extra-real and unreal at the same time.<BR>\r\nThat\\\'s the way it really is after all.<BR>\r\nLayers of projection between how it is and how it could be and how it will be wrapped up in an enigma sitting next to a bagel.<BR>\r\nMeanwhile people sweat and think fervent thoughts. One person get\\\'s drunk, another person thinks about getting drunk and yet another person is making an art project with little pieces of macaroni pasted on construction paper -- a child... that will one day get drunk, and then think about that day they made macaroni art project.<BR>\r\nSee it all comes together after all.<BR>\r\nAnd then finally someone starts thinking about a plate of shrimp, and everyone starts thinking about the plate of shrimp. It\\\'s inevitable. It\\\'s all about the plate of shrimp, but then it keeps changing.<BR>\r\n<BR>Threads.\r\n',0,910,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(18215,2988,'2002-08-20 06:54:51',93,'chiamattt','there are a few pages on the net that post job offers from schools, though im really not too sure what those sites are right now. i can give you this information though, you need a B.A. thats it. i dont even think they check for criminal records. dont know though. anyways, if you want to teach here. id recommend vietnam instead of s.korea. ',6,342,75,0,'211.32.132.203','en'),(18216,2989,'2002-08-20 12:02:42',240,'xep','not knowing what to do at the right time is one of my biggest fears.  i\\\'ve taken all sorts of first aid courses in my life and technically, i know what to do.  however, the last time i was at the scene of an accident (a motorcycle was hit a by a car right in front of me) i froze.  it was exactly what i thought i would do.  my fears were realized and went unconquered.  ',0,374,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(18217,2990,'2002-09-05 18:40:19',18,'ix','what\\\'s wrong with exact addresses? makes it seem more real. verisimilitude.\r\n',0,80,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(18218,2990,'2002-08-20 16:48:50',377,'dammit','i love that word.',0,17,0,0,'66.122.107.46','en'),(18219,2990,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i like exact addresses too.  michelle tea all but gives them in valencia.  it did mean nothing to me when i first read it though, because i didn\\\'t live in sf.<p>so tsing tao is a beer make eh?',6,195,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18220,2990,'2002-09-05 18:40:19',18,'ix','playing the valencia card so soon?',0,34,62,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(18221,2990,'2002-08-20 20:02:47',124,'gus','first: <a href=\\\"http://www.tsingtaobeer.com/tsingtao/welcome.htm\\\">Tsing Tao</a>\r\n<p>\r\nsecond, well, before you use an exact address, make sure no one you like actually lives there. especially if you\\\'re writing about horny half asian strippers. If you ever do get it published, you dont want some poor woman living at 442 cap to get hassled or raped by some shitfucker fan of yours. thats all!',0,401,0,0,'64.172.58.39','en'),(18222,2990,'2002-08-20 20:29:54',377,'dammit','good point gus. i have enough crimes to answer for already.',0,59,0,0,'66.122.107.46','en'),(18223,2990,'2002-08-20 20:48:03',129,'moebius','regarding exact addresses: the easiest thing to do is provide an address that doesn\\\'t really exist. let the mooks go looking for their fantasy stripper dreamgirl and be stumped by an empty lot full of weeds.',0,210,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(18224,2990,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i didn\\\'t know valencia was a card to play!<p>yeah i can see that giving someone elses address out would be bad.  but just the streets, and the actual names of bars and cafes, that\\\'s nice :)',6,195,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18225,2990,'2002-09-05 18:40:19',18,'ix','the main character of don bajema\\\'s \\\"boy in the air\\\" (or maybe another one of his books) lived in an upstairs flat at 16th & valencia. when i lived in that neighborhood i used to walk by and imagine the guy looking out of his window at me.',0,247,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(18226,2990,'2002-08-20 22:11:20',377,'dammit','ah, that\\\'s a great book. i used to see don bajema all the time at the bitterroot and the 500 club. he married a woman name carmen, a waitress at bitteroot, who was only about 4 years older than me but was everybody\\\'s mom. they had a beautiful baby named epiphany.\r\n<p>\r\ndon was in his early 50s when his life took that happy turn. doesn\\\'t that give you hope for the future?\r\n<p>\r\nme neither ; )',0,403,0,0,'66.122.107.46','en'),(18227,2979,'2002-08-20 23:50:21',371,'Kyla','<p>\r\nYou guys are right, the rhyming looks bad when it\\\'s right infront of you.  As a songwriter, I like lyrics to rhyme.  However, the song itself is much different, it\\\'s a shame you can\\\'t hear it while you read the lyrics.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAs for the topic, it\\\'s supposed to be cryptic :>  Just something a bit different I thought.  If you really want to know what it\\\'s about... an ex boyfriend of mine once threw a Buddah statue against a wall in a rage and it shattered everywhere.  I swept it all up, but two years later (and after he broke my heart), I still find tiny fragments of poor Buddah in the cracks of the floorboards.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOnce I get some mp3\\\'s uploaded you can hear the song, and <i>then</i> tell me what you think! :>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThanks for the input though, much valued.\r\n</p>',1,814,0,0,'203.109.254.57','en'),(18228,2979,'2002-08-21 03:24:52',175,'Paul G','Now that\\\'s an interesting story: a passive image of non-violence becomes an active instrument of violence; Buddha broke, and so did your heart; bits of Buddha and the relationship linger.',0,190,371,0,'67.248.113.50','en'),(18229,2993,'2002-08-21 05:00:29',93,'chiamattt','well tyler, since you are going into your last year of college, you should ask around campus if you are interested in teaching in s.korea or anywhere else for that matter. i seem to remember a lot of u.s yootz saying that they found information on teaching here while they were in their final year of college. ',3,310,0,0,'211.32.132.227','en'),(18230,2990,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<p>i\\\'m pretty impressed that your character went weightlifting after riding all day. i used to just sit on the couch and breathe and drink water for an hour until i was fit to deal with real life and a burrito.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni met the nicest boys while riding. i really miss them. the gentlest, nicest humans with eyes like babies. looking like death but willing to lend me their last dollar. bringing tofu to their backyard bbq\\\'s so i could have something to eat. i\\\'ve certainly known sweet nice boys since then, but not so many, en masse, dressed in rags, all hanging out together, 20, 30, 40 at a time. </p>',1,615,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(18231,2979,'2002-08-21 19:42:05',366,'eunoia','not so cyptic anymore. I like the song more now, but then again I like things to be more literal as of late, so you\\\'re explanation shed some light. PLease PLease Mp3s!',0,170,371,0,'24.156.7.170','en'),(18232,2389,'2002-08-22 01:56:49',66,'Peter','<br><tt><b>update:</b> the watch, given to me on a blazingly sunny manhattan day several years ago, has just- rather ironically- died; it has simply <i>stopped ticking</i>. today, walking through a wintery-feeling, breezy sundown afternoon in berkeley, i suddenly felt compelled to check the time, where i witnessed its last 6 or 7 seconds of its life before it seized at 6:12:21 pm.<br><br>profundity abounds; the watch, thankfully never engraved, given to me by an old love... dying on the wrist who\\\'s hand was holding the hand of a new, final love in a different timezone, a different place, an unimaginably different scenario.<br><Br>it was about time. the watch, its once-gleaming crystal bezel now succumbed to sweaty corrosion, its steel band now scratched, bent and loosely fitting, shall be aptly arced into the san francisco bay with a subtle overhanded pitch. time for a new watch to tick off the new seconds of a wonderfully new, well-timed part of my life... sigh. yay.</tt>',1,990,0,0,'216.102.198.168','en'),(18233,2995,'2002-08-22 06:49:12',93,'chiamattt','ive been waiting for the piece on 210 gloucester. where is it? this piece made me feel sad. my dad has only hit me once and the day after he did he felt so bad he actually spent money on me. he got me a nintendo 16 or something. the one with duck hunt. he hit me in florida after driving for something like 14 hours. he spend over 200 american on that damn game thing and im sure he never hit me again because he didnt want to spend the money. haha. i laugh at that. ',1,467,0,0,'211.32.132.205','en'),(18234,2988,'2002-08-22 06:50:52',93,'chiamattt','ever see that show on TLC          called  \\\'connections\\\'? what a great show. ',1,83,196,0,'211.32.132.205','en'),(18235,2988,'2002-08-22 06:53:54',93,'chiamattt','you know. its hard not to keep your composure in korea. its so chill being a white dude here. there is more to say about this article, but it will have to be said when i am stationed back in canada. \\\'station\\\' is a cool word. i explained all its uses in my jr hotties class on tuesday. they couldnt quite grasp its broad uses. ',1,332,366,0,'211.32.132.205','en'),(18236,2972,'2002-08-22 08:31:23',189,'cyantist','stop time and you\\\'ll never forget the smell.',1,47,0,0,'24.221.170.119','en'),(18237,2994,'2002-08-22 14:47:03',180,'auntie','in this story, it sounds like the woman was/is so angry about the circumstances of the relationship she intentionally causes situations that will justify your anger to you.<br><br>\r\n\r\ngoading the other party into acts that can be wrapped into a neat package of unacceptable behaviour justifies the anger.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe problem is, at some point, this woman is going to push this man over the edge, and he will not change direction.  when that happens, everyone loses.  they both lose respect for themselves and each other.<br><br>\r\n\r\nanger cannot change the situation that caused it. compromise and communication could possibly make things better.  posssibly not.  depends on from where the anger stems.<br><br>\r\n\r\nat the very least, this relationship is unhealthy.  at the worst, it may destroy someone\\\'s soul.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe question is always, in the famous words of ann landers - \\\"would my life be better with or without him/her.\\\"  answering the question is important. acting on the answer is very, very hard.<br><br>\r\n\r\nyou are responsible for the actions of your own anger - not his.  but don\\\'t wake a sleeping lion, please.<br><br>\r\n',1,1153,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(18238,2994,'2002-09-05 18:40:19',18,'ix','<a href=\\\"http://www.lyricsstyle.com/h/hole/hehitme.html\\\">he hit me and it felt like a kiss</a>',0,100,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(18239,2912,'2002-08-22 16:53:36',17,'austin','<FONT SIZE=1>\r\n<PRE>\r\n[=Arrive=] chiam (chiam@still.hungry.com) entered group\r\nhi there austin. \r\nyou arent here right now so i think ill tell you a story. \r\na story of a man named bomont\r\nbomont was a kid \r\nhe sat at his desk and tried so hard to understand page 50 of\r\nSBS book one. \r\nthe vocab on the page were words like \\\"dress, necklace,\r\nbracelet, and pocketbook\\\". oh how his pronunciation was\r\nterrible. \r\ni tickled bomont until he said pocketbook. then he started\r\ncrying and i tickled him somemore. then he started laughing. \r\nhow fickle these kids are.\r\nthat is all. \r\nhis name wasnt bomont. but what a nice name. his name was jin gue. \r\n</PRE></FONT>',1,879,0,0,'205.179.19.163','en'),(18240,2996,'2002-08-22 18:18:24',195,'matt','for one thing, very few street level dealers carry guns.',1,56,0,0,'209.208.110.133','en'),(18241,2996,'2002-08-22 20:50:36',151,'stella','That\\\'s the second reference to Amherst/Umass. Is that random, or did you go there?  ',0,87,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(18242,2996,'2002-08-22 20:58:20',377,'dammit','<p>the characters in these stories met at UMASS in the early nineties and relocated to san francisco after graduation.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nby coincidence, i\\\'m a UMASS alumnus--class of \\\'93.\r\n</p>',0,193,0,0,'66.122.107.46','en'),(18243,2996,'2002-08-22 21:00:39',151,'stella','gotcha! I asked because my boyfriend and all of his buddies went to Umass. But if was there in \\\'93, he was a freshman',0,120,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(18244,2996,'2002-08-23 00:03:47',65,'le','i\\\'m really compelled by this story and looking forward to more. i think the memory of the first time marshall saw tsing naked is very well placed here - i think you could go even further into it without frustrating the reader too much. it really made me feel the time that had passed between these two.',4,305,0,0,'208.239.147.205','en'),(18245,2994,'2002-08-23 01:45:32',75,'tyler','yeah, it sounds like you like it. good luck with that.',1,54,0,0,'165.247.184.197','en'),(18246,2979,'2002-08-23 02:11:43',371,'Kyla','Exactly!  I thought it was worthy of a song :)',6,46,175,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18247,2979,'2002-08-23 02:12:25',371,'Kyla','Soon :)  ',6,9,366,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18248,2998,'2002-08-23 07:56:59',175,'Paul G','Your writing has been so passionate, so intense, and so good: it almost begs to be interacted with.  But, not knowing where the line between reality and artistry lies, maybe it is best to leave a simple comment just to affirm that you are being read, and thought about.  ',0,271,0,0,'67.248.78.33','en'),(18249,2995,'2002-08-23 08:55:32',339,'blue','My dad also had a big red monster face.\r\n\r\nHe made me eat a tomato once when I was 6 or 7. I told him I\\\'d vomit and my sister told him I\\\'d vomit but my mom wasn\\\'t home to tell him to leave me alone so he made me eat the tomato. And then I went to the toilet and vomitted and he turned big and ugly and red. \r\n\r\n',2,320,0,0,'152.163.201.40','en'),(18250,2998,'2002-08-23 17:09:29',156,'lotus','the line between realtiy and fiction often blurs in my writing, because i strive for \\\"emotional truth\\\" more than keeping to the facts or to <i>what really happened</i>...often i try harder to capture the texture of the experience itself than naything else.<Br><br>\r\nthank you very much for reading, and for letting me know.',0,329,0,0,'64.230.107.41','en'),(18251,2988,'2002-08-23 21:14:32',370,'cumshot','the quiet american taught us the effectiveness of bike bombs. bombs bombs bombs.',0,80,0,0,'24.68.117.127','en'),(18252,2998,'2002-08-23 22:15:48',175,'Paul G','<br>\r\nP1 - \\\"<b>horses</b> whinny\\\"<br>\r\nP3 - \\\"<b>hoarse</b> word\\\"<br>\r\nP4 - \\\"the moon is a misplaced <b>seahorse</b>\\\"<br>\r\nIntentional?  It\\\'s cool, either way.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nOn a lighter note: the use of the word \\\"fur\\\" to describe the hair on a horse made me chuckle.',0,290,156,0,'67.248.139.23','en'),(18253,3000,'2002-08-23 22:31:10',195,'matt','here we all are: living in the same day, bookended by sleep and dreams, the big pomegranate moon oscillating, seasons in sequence.',1,130,0,0,'209.208.109.188','en'),(18254,2994,'2002-08-24 01:55:36',124,'gus','it doesnt sound like she likes it. abusive relationships are never simple like that. too bad. if they were, some of us might have gotten out of them sooner. oh well!',0,165,0,0,'64.172.58.39','en'),(18255,3000,'2002-08-24 02:18:12',240,'xep','monterey market is the first place i ever smelled a durian.  i love it there.',0,77,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(18256,2998,'2002-08-24 02:44:51',124,'gus','I lost my voice for like 4 days recently. one of the days I had to throw my mom\\\'s 60th birthday party complete with bazillions of her old pals and our relatives. it is not easy to throw a big party when you cant talk. \r\n<p>\r\nThe weird thing, is that people will often say more than they normally would if you\\\'re mute. they were all dishing out too much information.',0,371,0,0,'64.172.58.39','en'),(18257,2971,'2002-08-24 02:49:51',124,'gus','i finally read this. You are a dirty little monkey!',0,51,0,0,'64.172.58.39','en'),(18258,3000,'2002-08-24 09:09:10',251,'Jamie','I\\\'m glad you\\\'re happy, Peter. I\\\'m happy for you. I was there once and it\\\'s the best place on earth.',1,111,0,0,'195.92.168.173','en'),(18259,2995,'2002-08-24 09:17:31',251,'Jamie','...like the tomato',2,18,339,0,'195.92.168.173','en'),(18260,2889,'2002-08-24 19:10:29',251,'Jamie','The pill is evil. My girlfriend is on the pill and she is as mad as a badger. That\\\'s when she\\\'s not asleep or suffeing from a migraine attack. Now i\\\'m no doctor but is it a good idea to stop taking one brand and start a new brand the very next day? Methinks Irish doctors are less than competent.',4,305,0,0,'195.92.194.15','en'),(18261,2887,'2002-08-24 19:19:32',251,'Jamie','Sometimes Caroline will wake up; normally after falling asleep on the sofa, and be in a semi-awake state. I\\\'ve never seen anything like ti before and it can be somewhat unsettling. Sometimes she\\\'ll appear at forst to be acting normally, talking and answering questions until you notice a lack of coherence and a somewhat angry, agitated demeanour. She will argue about a non-sensical subject for minutes at a time until either falling back to sleep or waking up completely. Sometimes she switches all the lights on and then goes back to sleep.',0,549,0,0,'195.92.194.15','en'),(18262,2993,'2002-08-25 00:58:30',320,'slushie','**nodding and sighing in unfortunate agreement** \r\n-- a \\\"true student\\\" from UNC',2,85,0,0,'12.77.29.38','en'),(18263,3002,'2002-08-25 03:42:50',17,'austin','hi, if you are anonymous, send me an email: austin@swinney.org.',3,63,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18264,3001,'2002-08-25 07:20:26',334,'gone','aka Why I\\\'m So Unhappy.',1,26,0,0,'64.108.128.30','en'),(18265,3001,'2002-08-25 09:36:43',339,'blue','When you chose another path it all eventually leads to the same ending point. So maybe you would have had different experiences, but you would have no doubt ended up with the same emotional mindset as you\\\'ve got now. Remember, the only thing for certain is that we\\\'re all going to die anyhow. <br>Man, I love this optimistic view on life I\\\'ve got going on.',1,365,0,0,'152.163.204.141','en'),(18266,3002,'2002-08-25 12:28:37',81,'felicity','The written word does not usually give me chills.....THIS gave me chills.\r\n<p>Thank You</p>',1,91,0,0,'216.42.123.44','en'),(18267,3000,'2002-08-25 16:37:15',86,'egg','in my old age I have grown to love mornings. today I walk up for a paper and come back for coffee. maybe I will doze again and then stare out the window. you and sine look radiant. fills me up with breath.',0,205,0,0,'216.175.88.131','en'),(18268,3000,'2002-08-25 20:23:55',251,'Jamie','It\\\'s funny how you don\\\'t notice the beauty of a morning when you\\\'re busy travelling to work. This past few saturdays i\\\'ve been getting up early, like 08:30 to take Caroline into work. The deadness, the silence of Enniskillen that early in the morning is refreshing. With no people, little traffic and almost utter silence i was acutely aware the cool fresh air coming in off the lake. I felt chilled to my bones, an exhilirating shiver I haven\\\'t felt since the first time i visited here nearly two years ago. I noticed the the sound of silence. No sirens. No horns. No nothing. Just the wind. A mini urban environment all to myself. My own mini city. I ambled idly across town, without the determined pace i usually adopt, but with a rumble in my stomach. It occured to me that I hadn\\\'t eaten since lunch yesterday. McDonalds was empty, and with just enough loose change I ordered a Sausage and Egg McMuffin and a coffee, a double. I think, for the first time in my life i am appreciating serenity and natural beauty, perhaps i am growing up, perhaps this is ok...',1,1082,0,0,'62.252.192.8','en'),(18269,3000,'2002-10-06 17:40:35',261,'sine','\r\ni am absolutely in love and happier than i could imagine. \r\n\r\nhow could i not be? he bought me afro-ken knee socks. ',1,118,0,0,'216.102.198.168','en'),(18270,3000,'2002-08-26 09:17:44',66,'Peter','<br><tt>never have afro-ken knee-socks seen such a lovely lady; <i>sigh</i>. i, also, am in love. this here is a good thing.</tt>',1,129,0,0,'216.102.198.168','en'),(18271,3001,'2002-08-26 11:14:08',174,'Mad Moll','We all die. What really matters is Winning before that time comes. One should be able to say, \\\"my work is done here. time to get high and take naps until i croak.\\\" yeahh.',6,176,339,0,'24.189.13.22','en'),(18272,3004,'2002-08-26 16:01:41',371,'Kyla','So pretty :)',1,12,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18273,3000,'2002-08-26 17:26:46',82,'simon','me too. shh..',0,13,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18274,2996,'2002-08-26 18:47:22',124,'gus','they\\\'d never cut out some lines of powder on a mirror with a razor and roll up a dollar bill in a moving car. Thats for at home on the coffee table. When they\\\'re on the move they\\\'d probably take a key, dip the end into the stuff, and snort it off the key end. also good for doorways and dance floors. not that I would know.',0,332,377,0,'64.163.214.152','en'),(18275,2996,'2002-08-26 19:05:15',17,'austin','is hondo like <a href=\\\"http://members.aol.com/gallery7v/punch5.jpg\\\">Piston Honda</a> from mike tyson\\\'s punch out?',3,122,0,0,'205.179.19.163','en'),(18276,2996,'2002-08-26 19:16:28',377,'dammit','no, hondo was a cheap make of electric guitar in the 1980s.',0,59,0,0,'66.122.107.46','en'),(18277,3001,'2002-08-26 23:04:35',82,'simon','word',0,4,174,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18278,3005,'2002-08-27 23:09:09',82,'simon','hi',0,2,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18279,2999,'2002-08-27 23:31:42',90,'leonard','What the frick is a \\\"Slapper\\\"? Dudes that high five each other or simply slap each other on their bottoms? I am thinking that you have coined this phrase. More details please. I WANT TO KNOW WHAT A SLAPPER IS OR I\\\'LL DIE.',1,230,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(18280,2999,'2002-08-27 23:34:18',90,'leonard','Never mind, I found <a href=\\\"http://www.1stlinesecurity.com/leatslap.html\\\">it</a>. I think.',1,97,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(18281,3000,'2002-08-28 00:50:22',17,'austin','<P>This is article 3000.</P>\r\n<P>This is article <a href=\\\"article.phtml?id=2000\\\">2000</a>.</P>\r\n<P>This is article <a href=\\\"article.phtml?id=1000\\\">1000</a>.</P>\r\n<P>And, this is article <a href=\\\"article.phtml?id=666\\\">666</a>.</P>',3,247,0,0,'64.174.155.250','en'),(18282,3002,'2002-08-28 01:16:35',183,'lin','I\\\'m glad to see you found what you seemed to need. Your words are harsh, but I think also beautiful, and that in them you are saying something important, even if only to you. ',0,178,0,0,'64.230.57.142','en'),(18283,1000,'2002-08-29 04:52:21',86,'egg','I was just talking last night to a family friend who lives in Santa Cruz and house manages the El Rio about Joan Jett. you know, that was ON. remember when she sang \\\"i wanna be your dog\\\"? I projected back to that moment once I found out Austin was moving there. ',0,268,0,0,'216.175.88.131','en'),(18284,3007,'2002-08-29 06:26:24',93,'chiamattt','i really didnt understand this at all. but what i do want to know is what corey meant by \r\n<P>\r\n\\\"Fuck the Law, Fuck community. People fucked with me I don\\\'t give a shit anymore. If I catch the fuck that lifted what little shit I got I\\\'m going to fucking make a nice artistic fucking flesh sculpture.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n',6,320,0,0,'211.32.132.229','en'),(18285,2999,'2002-08-29 14:57:54',86,'egg','an australian person would have to answer this.',0,47,90,0,'216.175.88.131','en'),(18286,3005,'2002-08-29 17:32:33',122,'hool','<p>hi dammit</p>\r\n\r\n<p>this had a good flow to it up to candle being knocked over.  although the entire story felt rather similar to your others, i still enjoyed reading it. the dialogue is neatly economic, something i really appreciate in writing.  the ending felt contrived to me, or at least out of place - it felt more like a slapstick comedy which didn\\\'t match the first 3/4 of the story.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>when the girl read her poems, i laughed.  well written (by you, not her poems), especially your diplomatic responses.  it reminded me of about a million goth girl weblog poems i\\\'ve read.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i think that there is a lack of response to this post because it\\\'s similar to the others, and because it\\\'s longer than the others.  i\\\'m not one to complain about length; i post my share of lengthy entries - but i think a lot of the readers here get tired after a page or two.  but don\\\'t quote me on that, it\\\'s an educated guess.</p>',0,954,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(18287,3005,'2002-08-29 18:14:40',377,'dammit','hi hool. thanks for your thoughtful comments. i\\\'m hoping that any similarities being the things i have been posting is due to the fact that they\\\'re all part of a larger work, and a lot of it concerns the misadventures people have when they are depressed or under the influence of substances or both. i\\\'m a little iffy on the attempt at a humorous ending, too, but so many other chapters are so depressing i had to try to due something to leaven the mood. and it sets up some real problems for marshall later on. thanks for reading to the end, though.',0,559,122,0,'66.122.107.46','en'),(18288,3007,'2002-08-29 19:27:39',122,'hool','thanks for the insight and additional relevant commentary, dave.',0,64,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(18289,3005,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','it\\\'s good but-- would c\\\'s  mom really grab him and started bawling about how her daughter getting married la la la... to some guy she doesn\\\'t even know? some guy that came bounding out of her daughter\\\'s room holding onto his pants? both parents sobbing with joy? while their house burned down. hmm. it\\\'s only because some of your characters seems so real and _believable_ that less well written stuff kinda sticks out.\r\n\r\ni like your writing when it\\\'s no holds barred. i say go for the storyline that feels right, even if it\\\'s less \\\"happy\\\".',1,567,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(18290,2999,'2002-08-29 22:11:57',90,'leonard','Oh, now I get it. I feel like I should say something else, but I know exactly what you mean now. So, no.',1,104,86,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(18291,3007,'2002-08-30 07:24:35',366,'eunoia','This is a mystical and poetic understanding of electronic communication, that I have mixed feelings about. You know I think that the screen in front of me is a membrane sometimes, like I can feel a person right through it if I put my finger up to it. Other times I feel like I am writing a diary. A very personal journal for all the world to see. It\\\'s not like I don\\\'t feel attached to my emotions, it just feels like my words (really soembody elses) translate to numbers(0000110) that go through networks of computers and arrive in a kind of no-space. It feels like so many letter unsent. Harmless and unread. ',4,617,122,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(18292,3006,'2002-08-30 08:30:13',175,'Paul G','Things like this hurt real bad.  Suicide is contagious.  When people close to you die in this way, you are more likely to die in this way too.  Everyone has questions about the meaning and purpose of life.  Sometimes the answers to those questions seem inadequate.  But, if we stay connected, the people around us help us through those moments of doubt and despair - in ways they don\\\'t even know.  To die that way is to say not only is there no point to life, but also that there is no one who is worth living for.  For family, and friends, and lovers, that is a devastating thing.  I hope that my comment is appropriate; it is what this article brought to my mind. ',0,669,0,0,'67.248.86.45','en'),(18293,3008,'2002-08-31 04:35:15',17,'austin','i know it is stupid but once when i was a kid i did stick my tongue to a block of ice in the minnesota winter.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nand another time as a child in minnesota i went to daluth with my mom.  while i was there i heard about a large boat that was dragging anchor on a windy day through the harbor.  when they brought the anchor up there was a car from twenty or thrity years ago on the end of the anchor.  two perfectly frozen people were inside it.  the bottom of the harbor in daluth is super cold.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni have never zipped myself up in my pants.',2,555,0,0,'64.174.154.13','en'),(18294,3007,'2002-08-31 05:35:41',17,'austin','i am trying to think of the last time i felt this way.  i think it was with my first girlfriend.  ine was her name.  christine without the christ.  just \\\'een\\\'.  but that was just the wanting.  not the approval rating.  for that i probably look to my dad.  i really want him to approve of what i do.  he usually does not, unless i am doing what he wants me to do.  it is true, there is what i want to do and dont do, and there is what i dont want to do but do it because he wants me to.  computers are not involved in any of this.',3,535,0,0,'64.174.154.13','en'),(18295,3006,'2002-08-31 05:40:59',17,'austin','i have never known anyone very well that died.  i say that and i think it.  but i do know some people that have died.  but the people that i really know, close friends, my brother, people like that, none of them have died.  so  when confronted with others who have loved ones who died i dont know how to act, like i am on stage and i forgot my lines.  the most i can muster is a sort of halmark card reaction.  all this will change some day.  im not looking forward to that of course.  part of me thinks that i might be the one to die.  the first one to go.  always one step ahead, somebody said about me once.  that person didn\\\'t know me very well, but well enough to think they could say that.  and i think what the really meant was always too impatient to deal with restrictions.  or to put it even worse, always to impatient to deal with the preparation it takes meet requirements.  the ramones are on ini the next room as part of the Royal Tennenbaums, \\\"I said die, oh yea... I said die, oh yea...\\\"  the big requirement.',3,906,0,0,'64.174.154.13','en'),(18296,3001,'2002-08-31 07:04:56',17,'austin','<P>for me, the most crushing aspect of the big What If is that i cannot pick a point.  by my own standards i consider myself a failure in terms of my goals.  anything that i really really want to do is restricted to me.  cows, grass.  what decision did i make that started it all?  i dont think it was my decision to make.  i try as hard as i am comfortable with.  that is pretty good for some things like this website and not good enough for other things like doing well at school or at work.<BR><BR>\r\ni did read a poem the other day that haunted me for several hours afterwards.  since i have it right here in front of me, i am going to TRAMPLE ALL OVER COPYRIGHT LAWS and quote it.  you only live once right?\r\n<BLOCKQUOTE>\r\n<B>inference</B><BR><BR>\r\nwhen you hear a list of the things\r\n<BR>\r\nmost people wish they could do\r\n<BR>\r\nbefore they die,\r\n<BR>\r\nyou will really hear a list\r\n<BR>\r\nof the things they will most\r\n<BR>\r\nregret missing when \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nthey actually do.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n<sup>from <i>every day we humans</i> by christopher cunningham, <a href=\\\"http://showerhead.org\\\">showerhead press</a>, 2002</sup>\r\n</BLOCKQUOTE>\r\n<P>that poem has me recognizing that i am about to pass up opportunities when i get layed off here in a month.  i dont have a big purse or anything, but i will be holding it down financially enough to not take the first job i get offered just because it pays money.  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nif i play my cards right, i have a chance to cross off one or two of the things i regret not having done yet.</P>',3,1548,0,0,'64.174.154.13','en'),(18297,3007,'2002-08-31 09:32:57',370,'cumshot','insane in the ol membrane. i am trying to give up on technology. i believe it to be the destruction of humanity. we have created our own suicide machine. all in the name of comfort and ease. we live in a entirely different universe now. we are turning on machines while we turn off our brains. we can kill ourselves from far away. just one push of the button. just one push of the button and its gone. thanks for coming out. the sad part is that we have already lost. this is supposed to coincide with your story hool, but fucked if i know what\\\'s what. i dig those crazy pics too. good job. keep it up you dead dead dead man.',0,628,0,0,'24.68.117.127','en'),(18298,3002,'2002-08-31 14:43:27',122,'hool','utmost respect. these words chase me like a dead ambulance across the plains of saskatchewan in the middle of the night.',0,120,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(18299,3006,'2002-08-31 17:12:26',170,'xipibi','think of it this way:\r\n\r\nA suicide is a person who has answered the following three questions wrong.\r\n\r\n1.  Am I searching for a permanent solution to all my temporary problems?\r\n\r\n2.  Do I want to do the worst thing I can to my family and friends?\r\n\r\n3.  Do I not have any interest in what happens next?\r\n\r\n',1,308,0,0,'209.39.34.35','en'),(18300,3007,'2002-08-31 18:00:35',17,'nuttloaf','lately i have been looking to extreme prodestant teachings from the 19th century.  from this i have come up with the affirmation, \\\"All technology is evil (in the eyes of God), some is just less evil.\\\"',3,206,370,0,'64.174.154.86','en'),(18301,3007,'2002-08-31 20:25:43',122,'hool','i agree that technology will destroy humanity.  i believe that we will use technology to become the gods that we worship and fear.  and so yes, in the eyes of the current reigning god, technology is evil, for it spells the beginning of His end.',0,244,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(18302,3007,'2002-09-01 01:12:38',206,'josta','<a href=\\\"http://www.biota.org/people/douglasadams/\\\">i don\\\'t really believe this but i\\\'m still naive.<a>\r\n\r\ngreat article as ever, hool. i\\\'m thinking about sending it to a friend+ who i won\\\'t see except via the internet for the next year and a half.',2,266,0,0,'152.163.189.132','en'),(18303,3009,'2002-09-01 02:43:06',75,'tyler','every place looks good, from the ghetto.',1,40,0,0,'165.247.165.92','en'),(18304,3010,'2002-09-01 04:18:09',251,'Jamie','editor please scrub this post, damn speech recogtion messed up and i didnt check before posting!',0,96,0,0,'195.92.168.174','en'),(18305,3008,'2002-09-01 04:29:58',251,'Jamie','you should read <i>The Hollow Earth Enigma</i>',4,46,0,0,'195.92.168.174','en'),(18306,3007,'2002-09-01 04:38:22',251,'Jamie','Whether we like it or not we\\\'ve become almost inextricably reliant on technology. I say this because it\\\'s true. I realised this about a year ago while working as a web developer. The electricity went off... and i couldn\\\'t think of anything non-electronic i could do until it came back on.',4,297,0,0,'195.92.168.174','en'),(18307,3007,'2002-09-01 05:41:58',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\ni like technology. i like it a lot. im not afraid of it. i dont hold it higher than i do a living person. i understand that it can be used for all the wrong reasons, but i also think it can be used for all the right ones. giving into that fear lets those who use it for the wrong reasons use it more. how can we say that technology will be the end of humanity. if anything technology has helped humanity grow. it has helped us come closer together. it has helped us do a lot of things our parents never would have dreamt of. we\\\'d like to think that life was easier and \\\'better\\\' before technology, but during every generation of human existance there has been technological advances. from the wheel to the use of copper, bronze, and steel. humans have manufactured tools for good and bad since before written and oral history.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwithout technology, hool would have never taken pictures, cumshot and eunoia would have never had a radio show, and i would have never made music. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI can only see one problem. i can only see people looking towards the bad things. reporting on the problems and on the fear created by the prospects of dirty bombs and genetically modified microwavable plastic vegtables. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ntechnology is everything. not everything is evil. \r\n</p>\r\n',4,1304,0,0,'61.37.53.102','en'),(18308,3010,'2002-09-01 05:50:38',17,'austin','edit it in your settings area...\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nsettings, edit article.',4,67,0,0,'64.174.154.75','en'),(18309,2699,'2002-09-01 06:23:48',251,'Jamie','It seems the human-dolphin relationship goes back millenia. A dolphins sonar enables them to actually see into our bodies to the extent that most dolphins will display a curiousity toards people with artifical bod parts, metal plates, pins, pacemakers etc. It has also been noted that dolphins display an unusual interest in disabled children, with prolonged contact actually impoving conditions such as autism and downs syndrome. It is now understood that dolphin sonar when focused at full power is powerfull enough to re-arrange matter at a moleclar level. I\\\'m not making this up honest.',4,593,0,0,'195.92.194.17','en'),(18310,3007,'2002-09-01 06:29:27',175,'Paul G','<p>\r\nWe have had tyrants; we always will have tyrants.<br>\r\nThe degree to which any particular tyranny has been able to extend itself has been limited by the technology at the disposal of the tyrant.<br>\r\nAs technology improves, so will tyranny.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nScience itself is, per se, neutral.<br>\r\nHowever, choosing to study a thing, and choosing to study it in a particular mode, involves valuation.<br>\r\nValuation is theoretic, not scientific.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nPeople who hold political power desire to hold political power.<br>\r\nPeople who desire to hold political power are not wise.<br>\r\nPeople who hold political power are not wise.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe prospects for sensible use of our evolving technology seem low.<br>\r\nThe prospects for a new tyranny seem high.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBut, hey, I hope I\\\'m wrong.</p>',0,801,0,0,'67.248.113.158','en'),(18311,3007,'2002-09-01 08:56:18',394,'ho3','protect me from technology. protect me from myself. watch as i plunge into the downward spiral of self indulgence followed by self loathing. free music for me as i destroy my work ethic and sabotage the indusrty that i at one time believed i had a future in. watch as i become more selective of the pornography i wish to view and more dissatisfied with my own sex life. change is confusing. join me if you wish. join me or seek refuge in the real world where you will feed yourself enough lies and guidelines to trick yourself into staying away. i will act like a virus trying to suck you back in and infect you with my own deficiencies due to my own loneliness. everyone is so unrelatable.',6,690,0,0,'24.68.117.127','en'),(18312,3007,'2002-09-01 08:59:00',394,'ho3','in the end my only satisfaction could be destroying you.',0,56,0,0,'24.68.117.127','en'),(18313,3002,'2002-09-01 11:07:41',93,'chiamattt','back in nam. did two tours there. it was a nice place after they bombed the living shit out of that shit they call jungle. got fucked standing up twice. short time five dollars. long time ten. ',6,193,0,0,'61.37.53.102','en'),(18314,3007,'2002-09-01 11:11:30',93,'chiamattt','yeah and its technology that helps us fight tyranny. remember that. ',0,68,0,0,'61.37.53.102','en'),(18315,3007,'2002-09-01 15:14:44',122,'hool','when i speak of technology destroying humanity, i think of it in positive terms.  yes, technology is a tool that can be wielded for good or evil.  i have faith that in the end, we will positively integrate technology so much into our beings (lifetimes beyond our current integration) that we\\\'ll no longer be recognizable as human.  hence the \\\'destruction\\\' of humanity.  so i see it as a good thing.  i love technology.  i feel it is our ultimate spiritual expression.',0,476,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(18316,3007,'2002-09-01 22:38:06',206,'josta','a la \\\"2001\\\"?',2,18,122,0,'205.188.208.166','en'),(18317,3007,'2002-09-02 06:48:29',176,'Scapegoat','I\\\'m with Hool on this one. Technology will destroy humanity but in a positive way. And it seems that those that think of it as evil are the ones obsessed with its dark applications.<br><br> \r\n<b>\\\"I hope to god that Technology mutates humanity beyond recognition, because its the only hope I have.\\\"</b><br><br>\r\nFighting against technology seems like an unwillingness to accept responsibility good, bad or otherwise. Its like we all the spoiled children of God that want all the fruits the universe has to bare but only if we can experience them in a naive childlike state. We won\\\'t collectively grow up on our own so I feel that the possible  harm in pushing things upon us that force us too a little is totally outweigh by the prospective benefits.   \r\n  ',0,768,0,0,'65.95.141.94','en'),(18318,3002,'2002-09-02 06:58:21',176,'Cerebral Poison','Running straight into insanity<br>\r\nFuck my humanity<br>\r\nI\\\'m taking the next level<br>\r\nProvin\\\' that I\\\'m mental<br>\r\nRunning down the ghosts<br>\r\nFrom the past of all my hosts<br>\r\nLike a parasite <br>\r\nI blow up when I light<br>\r\nI like what ya write<br>\r\nNo slight<br>',0,280,0,0,'65.95.141.94','en'),(18319,3007,'2002-09-02 19:40:34',394,'cumshot','slaves. technology annoys me because it enslaves me. periodically i attempt to return to my luddite roots and exist without any assistance from technology. i take stairs, i eat my own feces, i use rocks to light my angel dust joints. but it is impossible. i cannot escape. living in urban centres makes it even more implausible. i appreciate and revel in many of technology\\\'s assets. if technology is the only way to improve humaity than that is also an escape. an excuse to refuse responsiblity.',0,499,0,0,'24.68.117.127','en'),(18320,3007,'2002-09-02 22:44:04',86,'egg','why does your name have to be cumshot?',0,38,0,0,'216.175.88.131','en'),(18321,2790,'2002-09-03 02:25:17',170,'xipibi','This is \\\'coffee-cup wisdom\\\' [formerly known as \\\'bumper-sticker wisdom\\\']:\r\n\r\n\r\nIf it wern\\\'t for the last minute,\r\nSome things would never get done.',1,161,0,0,'209.39.34.137','en'),(18322,3017,'2002-09-03 20:21:40',82,'simon','chia, you are a stand-up guy.',0,29,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18323,2892,'2002-09-04 02:44:00',170,'xipibi','Well, \\\"The Call\\\" came.  My sister called me.  Dad while Mom held his hand as the Hospice chaplain said \\\'Amen\\\' after a prayer.  He had been \\\'asleep\\\' for about 3 days.  I\\\'m so glad it was peaceful and that Mom didn\\\'t have to see him suffer at the end.  Six weeks later and I\\\'m still working it out.  I guess I will for a long while.  The life he led is still leaving ripples in the lives of many others.  I hope the ripples last a long time...',1,468,187,0,'209.39.34.71','en'),(18324,3017,'2002-09-04 04:02:19',92,'kini','<p>third paragraph from the bottom: yes, agreed.</p>\r\n<p>i go home next week. i have mixed feelings about this.</p>',1,115,0,0,'202.62.122.53','en'),(18325,3017,'2002-09-04 05:01:11',366,'eunoia','I hear since the rains that are you drowning in soya sauce over there? \r\n\r\nI hear it rained cats and dogs, so who\\\'s cooking dinner?\r\n\r\nWell that\\\'s my racey humour bit. The natural disaster that has hit Korea probably is not the best thing to be joking on about but I am secretly scared and thought that may be a couple jokes could make an easy transition into asking:\r\n\r\n\\\"Are you fucking okay? there was a typhoon!!\\\"',1,428,0,0,'24.156.7.170','en'),(18326,3007,'2002-09-04 05:03:57',366,'eunoia','Probably to fertilize you egg. Which came first the cumshot or the egg?',6,71,0,0,'24.156.7.170','en'),(18327,3017,'2002-09-04 07:00:59',93,'chiamattt','yeah im ok. the typhoon hit hard way south of me. the only real thing in seoul was some hard rain. i dont even think anyone even died in seoul. well at least me and shane didnt. the only thing that really happens in seoul is nothing. no trees to fall down really. im sure they all fell down years ago due to lack of clean air. when it rains hard people die from light posts not being grounded. people in their sandles walking into pools of electrified water. scary thought. this is perhaps one reason i dont wear sandels. well that and all that yellow brown mould on my toenails. i really should do something about that. got a grinder handy? one of those dremel multi tools would work. diamond dust bit with some goggles should make it safe. ',4,742,366,0,'218.145.63.36','en'),(18328,3007,'2002-09-04 15:53:39',86,'egg','ew.',0,3,366,0,'64.165.109.140','en'),(18329,1515,'2002-09-04 23:19:49',86,'egg','i had forgotten that my labia were named Marge.',0,47,0,0,'64.165.109.140','en'),(18330,3009,'2002-09-05 01:15:05',124,'gus','except where you are.',0,21,75,0,'64.172.58.184','en'),(18331,3007,'2002-09-05 03:10:01',75,'tyler','&quot;People who hold political power desire to hold political power.\r\nPeople who desire to hold political power are not wise.\r\nPeople who hold political power are not wise&quot;\r\n<p>\r\n--i disagree. there are wise people who desire and in fact hold political power. i, for example, desire political power because i know that i am a smart and compassionate person and that it is therefore my <b>duty</b> to influence the world and the people in it as much as possible, in as good a way as possible. i could make ten times as much just staying in private practice rather than running for a judgeship or the legislature, and plenty of current politicians also have given up extremely lucrative careers to hold office. ',1,715,0,0,'165.247.164.240','en'),(18332,3017,'2002-09-05 05:15:58',366,'eunoia','why not just use some oil of olay on those dainty feet of yours?\r\n\r\nDamn it Chia I was hoping you\\\'d have some live disaster pictures complete with looting and machete men. But no, chia had to live in a safe place, didn\\\'t he?',6,230,93,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(18333,3017,'2002-09-05 06:32:41',93,'chiamattt','heh. ms fitzhenry? buggsy brown! ',6,33,0,0,'211.32.132.216','en'),(18334,3017,'2002-09-05 07:45:34',17,'austin','i could use a turkey dinner in a can right now.  all i have is potato chips and bushmills, a famine of irish proportions.  arrrrrrrr.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nare you going to be chiam the industrialist, importing canned goods to the united states of america?\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nyou should teach your kids as much slang as possible.  that kind of stuff they wont be able to get from real teachers when you are gone.',3,393,0,0,'64.174.154.139','en'),(18335,3017,'2002-09-05 13:28:42',93,'chiamattt','my kids dont know from joke!',0,28,17,0,'61.37.53.60','en'),(18336,3006,'2002-09-05 13:29:33',392,'p.','i think with his death i realized what a suicide does to a family, the confusion that it causes and the pain that becomes infused into a person.  i agree that the idea of suicide is contagious, but there also seems to be a flipside to it, that it can prevent another\\\'s death.  had i not seen firsthand what family and friends go through i\\\'m not so sure that i wouldn\\\'t have after a few years of therapy hadn\\\'t made my mind any clearer as to why i chose to live.  after his death i stopped thinking about what my own emotions did to me and more about how i chose to express those emotions affected the people around me.  i\\\'m fairly certain it changed the way i lived my own life.',0,693,0,0,'12.13.167.78','en'),(18337,3019,'2002-09-05 17:45:32',195,'matt','plumpy. how funny. self-deprecation is usually amusing.',1,55,0,0,'209.208.108.183','en'),(18338,3020,'2002-09-05 19:44:23',66,'Peter','<br><tt>mmmmm; <a href=\\\"http://www.dictionary.com/search?q=thermocline\\\"><i>thermocline</i></a>...</tt>',1,108,0,0,'216.102.198.168','en'),(18339,3015,'2002-09-05 19:47:21',66,'Peter','<br><tt>im going to be in chicago in a few days... that photo makes my travelling all the more compelling.</tt>',1,111,0,0,'216.102.198.168','en'),(18340,3014,'2002-09-05 19:49:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt>\\\'...<i>Rich people. Successful people. Beautiful people. Gay or straight people. The worms will still eat your face...\\\'</i></tt>',1,142,0,0,'216.102.198.168','en'),(18341,3019,'2002-09-05 20:15:31',83,'adamajah','for the first two paragraphs I thought you were talking about whores',0,68,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(18342,3015,'2002-09-05 23:51:01',175,'Paul G','Hey Peter.  My auto and I will be at your disposal, in Chicago.  Of course, if you think that I\\\'m scary I\\\'d understand that too.  But in either case, there\\\'s a bakery on Grand Ave. at May St. - D\\\'Amato\\\'s - that has good \\\"walking around looking at architecture food.\\\"  You can pick up a slice, or a focaccio, or cookies in a brown paper bag; it\\\'s on the near-west side, in a safe neighborhood not too far from the Loop.  Also, concerning the Chicago River: there is a \\\"boat-taxi\\\" service that will take you down the river, to the lakefront, from the train station at Madison and Canal [$2 I think?]  Bon voyage.',1,640,66,0,'67.195.57.187','en'),(18343,3007,'2002-09-06 07:24:35',59,'s5','but, everything i read in the news that pisses me off the most was done in the interest of \\\"the public good\\\". what makes you any different or better?',0,155,75,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18344,3007,'2002-09-06 08:32:57',196,'jasonp','ho3: interesting comments<BR><BR>\r\nTechnology and politics are both exercises in grasping at objects. Technology could not take away from humanity what it chooses to forfeit.<BR><BR>\r\nBy the way these drawings are intriguing.',0,225,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(18345,3021,'2002-09-06 08:40:18',196,'jasonp','This be getting all visual on my ass.<BR><BR>\r\nIt\\\'s like what they say about decay as an inherent property of life and shit.<BR>\r\n\r\n',0,135,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(18346,3020,'2002-09-06 08:47:18',196,'jasonp','thermocline made me think of <I>\\\"barco lounger\\\"</I> for some reason.<BR><BR>\r\nThe ocean is awesome/scary/womblike.',0,120,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(18347,3008,'2002-09-06 09:00:38',196,'jasonp','I\\\'ve had dreams where I thought I realize I\\\'m really bald and they\\\'re always strangely unsettling. They\\\'re kind of related to the ones where your stuck in public in your underwear and kind of like a recurring dream of mine where I suddenly realize I\\\'ve been supposed to be going to a class but I\\\'ve missed every one and I have to find where the class is but I can\\\'t.',0,387,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(18348,3011,'2002-09-06 09:10:44',196,'jasonp','Hordes of teenage subterraneans...<BR>\r\nthe <B>beer</B> is everpresent.<BR><BR>\r\nA dumb blind beast with an interesting story and a temporarily good mood had to butt in.<BR>\r\nAnd that was me and this is now, and I\\\'m sweaty drunk grasping at something.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n<FONT SIZE=\\\"-2\\\"><TT>(this needed a comment)</TT></FONT>\r\n',0,329,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(18349,2792,'2002-09-06 09:20:20',196,'jasonp','reading this now my own meta-spin on this is that it\\\'s a progressive cartoon broadcast into the brains of everyone who then has a unique and sometimes allergic reaction to it.',0,178,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(18350,3006,'2002-09-06 09:38:47',196,'jasonp','I think suicide is what people think of when things seem totaly overwhelmed and hopeless. I have sometimes have glimpes of seeing things that way but it doesn\\\'t last.<BR> From how that person was described he probably had a problem with putting things in perspective also.<BR>',0,279,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(18351,3019,'2002-09-06 09:44:51',196,'jasonp','More stories about the the Korean pussies...<BR><BR>\r\nI think the first two paragraphs of every story is about whores.',0,118,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(18352,2990,'2002-09-06 09:56:28',196,'jasonp','tsing tao is a chinese beer or maybe everyone already knows that',0,64,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(18353,2846,'2002-09-06 10:10:55',196,'jasonp','I like legends explaining existence.<BR>\r\nIt sounds similiar to the zoroaster explanation which itself seems strangely similiar to the Judeo-Christion creation story.<BR><TT>basically: there was nothing/everything/goodness/light, there was nothing/badness/darkness, the nothing/everything/good/lightness gets mixed up with the nothing/badness/darkness gets all mixed up and now we\\\'re having a heck of time with trying to sort it out.</TT>',0,441,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(18354,2468,'2002-09-06 10:59:05',196,'jasonp','This is not any kind of argument. Arguments are boring and annoying. This is mind exposition.<BR><TT><FONT SIZE=\\\"-2\\\">\r\nAnd so it rests on a goodie by puce.\r\n7 the number of heaven and of luck;\r\n777 octal for all permissions set.</font>\r\n',0,243,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(18355,3021,'2002-09-06 12:51:15',156,'lotus','this is very cool.',1,18,0,0,'64.230.111.130','en'),(18356,3021,'2002-09-06 16:53:00',392,'p.','*sigh* the complete and utter joy of vivid imagery',0,50,0,0,'12.13.167.78','en'),(18357,3019,'2002-09-06 22:18:38',90,'leonard','i love the title. fracking great.',1,33,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(18358,3021,'2002-09-06 23:52:55',90,'leonard','Staring at that pic makes me feel loco.',1,39,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(18359,3021,'2002-09-07 06:35:50',17,'austin','\\\"Neo! That is LOCO!\\\"<BR><BR>thank you, Tank',3,49,0,0,'64.174.154.91','en'),(18360,2468,'2002-09-07 23:50:00',215,'puce','hi!! i\\\'m happy to see this here. :)',1,38,0,0,'66.171.52.131','en'),(18361,3021,'2002-09-08 07:43:34',175,'Paul G','Thanks!<br>\r\n:D<br>\r\n[James Garner?]',0,36,0,0,'67.248.52.56','en'),(18362,2468,'2002-09-08 09:05:38',196,'jasonp','It\\\'s about time puce finally returned! :D<BR>\r\n',0,50,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(18363,3007,'2002-09-08 19:08:49',154,'ryan','to tyler:<p>\r\ni hope you never hold any office.',6,47,0,0,'66.217.129.149','en'),(18364,3020,'2002-09-09 03:10:56',175,'Paul G','This was rich.  The piece is good after several readings: it\\\'s a very nurturing space to exist in - psychically.  Sometimes, someone will invoke a word like <i>massage</i> and simply hearing it is relaxing.  So too your description of water had that effect.  Thanks.',0,269,0,0,'67.195.110.115','en'),(18365,3007,'2002-09-09 17:06:40',176,'Scapegoat','Everything that politicians do will be described as in the public interest good, bad, or otherwise. As someone that works in government I can tell you it doesn\\\'t metter what you do there will be people that it pisses off. You just have to get used to the idea that you are never going to be seen as the good guy. That\\\'s were that sense of duty comes in otherwise only madmen and masochists would get involved in governance of any form. Don\\\'t get me wrong there are a lot of jacked people in governance but the only reason we are still around is due to the few good ones.   ',0,582,0,0,'204.19.79.66','en'),(18366,3023,'2002-09-09 17:23:10',82,'simon','i like this. thanks, man.',0,25,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18367,3023,'2002-09-10 04:53:03',175,'Paul G','Your prose is really tight, matt.',0,33,0,0,'67.248.78.191','en'),(18368,3025,'2002-09-10 12:29:43',392,'p.','breaking emotionally under any influence produces the most thought-provoking cries you\\\'ll ever have.  or rather, that i\\\'ve ever had.  despite the fuzziness between your mind and your body your emotions are never quite so frank or honest or clouded by better judgement or logic.   however, i\\\'ve never been able to recall such an episode to document it so accurately as you have.',0,386,0,0,'12.13.167.78','en'),(18369,3025,'2002-09-10 18:36:59',122,'hool','what did you consume?',0,21,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(18370,3025,'2002-09-10 23:43:21',371,'Kyla','Couldn\\\'t you tell? :>  I didn\\\'t want to specify but because you asked, it was 5-MeO-DMT.',6,94,122,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18371,3023,'2002-09-11 01:45:07',195,'matt','just like my choad. hahahahaha.<BR><BR>seriously, thanks for the compliment.',1,76,0,0,'209.208.108.251','en'),(18372,3025,'2002-09-11 02:03:33',66,'Peter','<br><tt>our pipe is still covered with your dmt residue, heh...</tt>',1,68,371,0,'216.102.198.168','en'),(18373,3027,'2002-09-11 20:13:01',90,'leonard','This makes me want to be nicer to street people. But, due to current fashion trends, it\\\'s getting harder and harder to tell a \\\"street person\\\" from a \\\"regular person\\\". Fuck, who am I kidding? I just don\\\'t like being nice to people at all, and it\\\'s even harder to be nice to someone that pays over $200 for a pair of jeans that look like they\\\'ve been worn by a homeless track team. \r\n\r\nI really like this entry a lot and not because you mentioned my name. Last stop: Marfa.',1,495,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(18374,3027,'2002-09-11 20:50:59',180,'auntie','street-personhood becomes a lifestyle, just like everything else, after a time, if it\\\'s too easy.\r\n<br><br>\r\nthere was a time when people who loitered about were arrested with vagrancy.  there was a reason for that. cities and towns felt like anyone who was healthy should be working.  i think i agree with that.\r\n<br><br>\r\npeople who are not healthy should be getting treatment.  if they refuse treatment, they should be in jail.\r\n<br><br>\r\ntreatment should be paid for with those tax dollars we are now using to police the crimes committed by street people.\r\n<br><br>\r\ndon\\\'t want to pay for the treatment?  well, when the street people move into your front yard, i bet you change your mind.',4,698,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(18375,3027,'2002-09-11 21:00:59',17,'Jamie Gumb','\\\"It puts the lotion in the basket!!!\\\"',5,43,180,0,'205.179.19.163','en'),(18376,3027,'2002-09-12 01:51:55',196,'jasonp','there was a time when I liked to have little \\\"street\\\" adventures.\r\n\r\nGet a bottle of nighttrain or OE and just chill with the street people. Another variation was to find the seediest bars possible and soak in the atmosphere while swilling down cheap drinks. \r\n\r\nThere was place here in downtown SD before they cleaned it up called \\\"fun land\\\" which was a really filthy and sleazy arcade/pool hall a few blocks down from the YMCA and greyhound bus station. It had mostly homeless, runaways, street people, etc. Sometimes we would do speed and hang out there at 3am playing pool. I remember once there was a vagrant/junkie looking janitor guy there who kept nodding off as he tried to mop up the floor.\r\n\r\n',0,716,0,0,'207.158.12.162','en'),(18377,3027,'2002-09-12 02:55:46',175,'Paul G','<p>Sometimes - in the winter when everything here is covered in a gray ash from the road salt and pollution, or when I sit in traffic surrounded by huge concrete road works - I get a certain feeling: it is as though everything is simply repetition - without color.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I sort of lose my direction, or at least the understanding of why any particular direction is better than another; \\\"Just spinning slowly,\\\" is a good way to say it.  \\\"I don\\\'t know what to do with myself,\\\" and I doubt everything.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>This year I got that feeling in the month of May; it still hasn\\\'t totally left me.  Last night though, someone shared pictures of people and a place that were important.  And I received an, \\\"I love you, Paul,\\\" from someone who meant it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And in spite of whatever else is happening in the world, today was a better day for me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I enjoyed this story because it feels like a place that I know [squirrel kicks it.]</p>',0,969,0,0,'67.248.78.85','en'),(18378,3027,'2002-09-12 03:13:01',17,'austin','Paul G, whussup.  karate was good tonight.  i have a warsteiner that i opened right when i walked in the door.  it makes me wonder if i should be drinking at all if that is the first thing i want but, yo, GOD DAMN IT TASTES GOOD.',1,229,175,0,'64.174.155.111','en'),(18379,3027,'2002-09-12 03:22:07',17,'austin','<P>excuse that gumb thing.  that is the first thing i thought of when i read your comment.  i, like jasonp, have been putting myself in a position to commune with the homeless youth lately.  i chill with them.  give them a buck or two.  talk shop.  some of them are brain dead.  some are pretty nice talking.  better talking than, oh, say, my manager at work.   they have nice stories to tell.  i dont think locking them up if they feel like hanging out for a while is in order, even if they are the future of no business homeless people.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni do think though that if they want a place to stay and for some value of \\\'call their own\\\' they should have it.  in santa cruz, there is city land that they are allowed to camp on.  i guess it does not bother the home owners.  and i would support providing a housing system for them too similiar to the one in the movie <i>Elling</i>.  it is pretty clear to me that many of the more interesting ones could not hold my job down.  i dont know why, and i dont think it is my place to judge why.  if they commit some insane acts of violence, then sure, put them away where they cant hurt anyone.  but if they are unstable in a social conversation-on-the-park-bench way, GREAT.  i am down with that.\r\n</P>',1,1256,180,0,'64.174.155.111','en'),(18380,3027,'2002-09-12 03:23:25',17,'austin','oh and gumb, i was just thinking of the question \\\'who is the insane one here\\\' when people get locked up because it makes others feel better about themselves.',2,163,180,0,'64.174.155.111','en'),(18381,3027,'2002-09-12 03:24:07',17,'austin','christ, i am becoming a liberal?\r\n<BR>\r\n<B>*destabilizing*</b>',4,62,17,0,'64.174.155.111','en'),(18382,3027,'2002-09-12 03:36:51',75,'tyler','ok. i\\\'m really really going to mail that book tomorrow.',1,58,0,0,'165.247.165.251','en'),(18383,3027,'2002-09-12 03:52:51',180,'auntie','i think that i prolly was not as clear as i might have been, i was looking for brevity.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhat makes people homeless?\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n<ol>\r\n<li>lack of employable skills.\r\n<li>mental instability.\r\n<li>addiction to whatever.\r\n<li>sometimes, it\\\'s a conscious choice.\r\n\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\nwhat will solve these problems? education, therapy, rehabilitation.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nperhaps i have a different take on this because i have BEEN homeless several times in my life.  i lived in a car for months - more than once - with my 5 siblings, aged 6 weeks to 13 years. it may sound like an adventure in some ways... cooking our meals in tin foil packets placed on the manifold of the engine as we drove down the road may sound cool to some people.\r\n\r\na ten year old child vomiting up the residue when siphoning gas out of cars in parking lots in not cool.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nit\\\'s not a lack of compassion, nor is it an \\\"anti-liberal\\\" comment.  what i propose is something i have proposed many times in other articles and comments here...\r\n<br><br>\r\nit is the responsibility of society to offer solutions that protect society from homelessness and hopelessness.\r\n<br><br>\r\nthat means that we, as citizens, must make the education of ALL children our first priority.\r\n<br><br>\r\nwe must NOT abandon the mentally ill to bedrools in the gutters. just because someone is mentally ill does not mean they cannot be treated, not cured perhaps, but treated in such a way that they will be able to function within some sort of mainstream way that gives them the ability to work if they can, have safe shelter, food, clothing, and medical care.\r\n<br><br>\r\nthere\\\'s a big difference between a \\\"young street person\\\" and a sixty year old junkie that would kill his neighbor for his shoes.\r\n<br><br>\r\nthere have always been drifters, people who prefer not to be tied down to an address.  this will never change.  but...\r\n<br><br>\r\ni do not believe that any mentally stable person would PREFER to live on the street for much longer than a temporary adventure.\r\n<br><br>\r\n',4,2056,17,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(18384,3027,'2002-09-12 05:22:52',93,'chiamattt','one thing ive noticed about seoul is that there are very few homeless people. ive heard the numbers as low as 200. in a city of over 18 million this seems a bit weird to me, but apparently homelessness just doesnt really occur here as much as in the west. perhaps it has to do with the korean \\\'family\\\' structures. ',0,320,0,0,'211.32.132.205','en'),(18385,3027,'2002-09-12 12:17:20',64,'Joanna','I guess it\\\'s kinda like that6 in Japan. Officially, there are no homeless people. Sure it\\\'s a different story if you go out on \\\"Rice Patrol\\\" at your economically socially environmentlaly concious American High School, but yeah, offfically, there are no homeless people in Japan. What you see at Shinjuku station isn\\\'t real.',1,338,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18386,3020,'2002-09-12 13:37:09',392,'p.','completely what i felt: comfort.  i\\\'m glad i could convey it as such.  ',0,74,175,0,'12.13.167.78','en'),(18387,3027,'2002-09-13 00:59:53',175,'Paul G','<br><a href=\\\"http://www.warsteiner.de/de/homepage/index_home.asp?mscssid=\\\">Glug, glug, glug.</a><br>  Chop, chop, chop.<br>  Beer and karate.<br>\r\nAss-kicking machine.\r\n\r\n<p>I put a new battery in Mom\\\'s car today.</p>  ',0,228,17,0,'63.10.197.205','en'),(18388,3028,'2002-09-13 01:23:53',175,'Paul G','Wow - great story.<br>  \r\nI wonder about education as a remedy, though.  Some people that I know received technical educations: they studied systems: medical, legal, mechanical, computer, musical, etc.  They are, for the most part, well-employed.  They have things.  And their contentment is derived from the things that they have.<br>\r\nIn a sense: they didn\\\'t really receive educations at all, but rather training to provide a service of some kind - a service that was deemed to be valuable.<br>\r\nOther people in the Arts - of all sorts - learned to do things that were not deemed to be very valuable at all; and what was worse, they were taught to question the process of valuation itself, so that even knowing what ought to be done became a difficult thing.<br>\r\nBut, I guess it\\\'s not just about education: it\\\'s about stopping, and thinking, and asking questions.  And plenty of people in all fields do that.<br>\r\nLosing purpose is a dangerous thing; but it\\\'s probably inevitable, from time to time.  And some types of education seem to foster it - or at least promote a lot of questioning.<br>\r\nYou told the story very well, and I think that you are wise.<br>\r\nPoor Homer.',0,1188,0,0,'63.10.197.205','en'),(18389,3027,'2002-09-13 03:37:45',17,'austin','i think we should all go to germany in march, be homeless in auntie\\\'s yard, and drink warsteiner.  ya, das ist gut!',1,118,175,0,'64.174.155.115','en'),(18390,3027,'2002-09-13 05:29:00',180,'auntie','lol!  you better ask dad if he\\\'s gonna share the beer before you buy the plane ticket.',1,89,17,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(18391,3027,'2002-09-13 05:36:28',175,'Paul G','<br>\r\nWith a bold vision of the future such as this, you should be in politics.<br>\r\nPerhaps the admixture of beer makes all ideas seem reasonable.<br>\r\n<b>I like your plans.</b><br>\r\nIf the President shared his plans over beer, I would like them too.  He seems like a beer kinda guy - but more like a BudLight kinda guy and not at all like a Warsteiner kinda guy.<br>\r\nI bet if he had Warsteiner he would get all crazy-weird.<br>\r\nSo, if we drink with him, he does not get behind the wheel - ever.<br>\r\nIt\\\'s probably not something to worry about though.<br>\r\nYou know, just saying...',0,587,17,0,'67.248.86.139','en'),(18392,3027,'2002-09-13 06:34:09',17,'austin','<P>i\\\'ve thrown back a few beers with ole\\\' GW in the past.  back when he was governor of texas.  i\\\'m speaking from experience when i say that he can put the bud lights away.  and when he gets to wobbling around too much, i just give him a couple lines of coke.  that usually straitens him out just fine.',2,311,175,0,'64.174.155.2','en'),(18393,3029,'2002-09-13 21:15:14',122,'hool','<p><i>any genetic abnormailities have been cured in the lab. persons who were gay in their lifetimes are no longer gay.</i></p>\r\n<p>homosexuality is a disease that needs to be cured?</p>',5,186,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(18394,3029,'2002-09-13 21:16:49',122,'hool','addendum to above: <b>[X] angry detractor</b>',0,45,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(18395,3029,'2002-09-13 21:56:18',396,'gaylord','everyone in my ideal city would be a big gay intern! of course.',0,63,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18396,3029,'2002-09-13 22:38:20',180,'auntie','nope, you can have a perfect city where everyone is gay, but for the purposes of my particular fantasy, since i am female, i want the gay celebs to be straight so i can date them in my pretend town.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe best thing about this insomnia game is that everyone has a different idea of utopia.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nand... i don\\\'t always play the same game, either.  sometimes the place is a large city and i\\\'ve filtered in all these people who nobody paid attention to when they were alive, and now they are frigging rich, famous, and ruling the world, politically and financially.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\none of my best ones along those lines is that i am the assistant to nero wolfe and archie and we take an assignment on behalf of the federal reserve and it\\\'s run by someone totally never thought of as a financial wizard, like marilyn monroe or peewee herman...\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nit\\\'s a game! lighten up!',1,912,122,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(18397,3029,'2002-09-13 23:32:23',396,'gaylord','heehee!',0,7,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18398,3029,'2002-09-13 23:36:00',122,'hool','it was the way that you worded it, and not the game.  i understand your point about it being your fantasy, and wanting straight men in it so that they\\\'re available to you.',0,174,180,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(18399,3030,'2002-09-14 20:10:22',17,'austin','I wouldn\\\'t believe people dry clothes on trash cans were it not for this picture.  That is funky, completely funky.  This is a nice retrospective though.  Mine would talk about water skiing and dropping cherry bombs of the dock recreating a sense of Allied bombers over Berlin.',3,280,0,0,'64.174.155.150','en'),(18400,3025,'2002-09-15 03:25:42',371,'Kyla','Ooops.  Sorry about that :>',6,27,66,0,'203.96.98.132','en'),(18401,3019,'2002-09-16 00:13:43',17,'austin','geumho pussy crew <B>OR</B> The REAL Aristocats: What Disney Never Told You',3,75,0,0,'64.174.154.220','en'),(18402,3030,'2002-09-16 07:23:09',175,'Paul G','I\\\'m not sure which is worse:<br>\r\n1) the parents putting their children\\\'s underwear on the filth, or<br>\r\n2) everyone else failing to recognize that something is wrong with the parents putting their children\\\'s underwear on the filth.<br>\r\nThe people in the picture literally <i>live in garbage</i>.<br>\r\nI hear people say: \\\"It\\\'s their culture,\\\" or, \\\"It\\\'s good enough for them,\\\" and it pisses me off.<br>\r\nSomething is really fucked up here if this kind of stuff is ok.  3 of my 4 grandparents were immigrants, and 3 of the 4 didn\\\'t speak English in their homes - but they knew that underwear + garbage = infection.<br>\r\nI\\\'m on a rant; but St. Pauli Girl was on sale - you understand.<br>\r\nWaterskiing and cherry bombs sound pretty good. ',0,770,17,0,'67.248.86.53','en'),(18403,3029,'2002-09-17 00:55:03',129,'moebius','<br>hmm. this reads like a really strange and potentially popular Sims add-on.... :)',2,84,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(18404,3016,'2002-09-17 01:25:28',124,'gus','this would be more interesting if you addressed some of these questions for us...\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"i sometimes ponder who i am now, who i\\\'ve become, and who i will be this time next year. but what about looking back to see how i got here? and what has made me this way?\\\"\r\n',0,271,0,0,'165.121.193.177','en'),(18405,3020,'2002-09-17 01:34:57',124,'gus','1. There\\\'s this lake I\\\'ve heard a talk about, its a mile deep under the ocean (have I mentioned this before?) it is one of the most amazing things ever. its a lake under the ocean. the water in the lake is sulferous and many times denser than the regular ocean water. there are waves on this lake deep in the ocean. \r\n<p>\r\n2. there are these worms that live by the heat vents in the deep ocean (same talk) one end of the worm is in water around boiling temp the other end of the worm is in water that\\\'s near freezing.\r\n<p>\r\nthose are the cool things I know about water at the moment.',0,592,0,0,'165.121.193.177','en'),(18406,3031,'2002-09-17 05:03:27',17,'austin','<P>the only thing worse than the Hitler card...</P>\r\n<P>...is the wet dead kitten card.</P>\r\n<P>i am destabilized.</P>',4,118,0,0,'64.174.155.115','en'),(18407,3029,'2002-09-17 05:43:27',180,'auntie','well, i am totally addicted to the sims at times, so maybe that is why.  i\\\'ve played this game in some form or the other for years.\r\n<br><br>\r\nmaybe those guys at maxis were reading my brain waves?  maybe i better start wearing a tin foil night cap...',4,254,129,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(18408,3031,'2002-09-17 05:44:27',180,'auntie','oh my god',0,9,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(18409,3031,'2002-09-17 11:03:11',64,'Joanna','Oh my god',1,9,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18410,3031,'2002-09-17 13:47:21',66,'Peter','<br><tt>one mf my budgies died while i was in sf this past month; my flatmate informed me that he \\\"somehow got loose while i wqs feeding him and drowned in the toilet\\\". this troubled me alot. the continuity with your drowned pet also troubles me. yuck. down with losing pets to irresponsible flatmates.<br><br>also: euphemisms are key when breaking the passing of ones pet to the uninformed pet owner. i discovered this the hard way.</tt>',1,444,0,0,'204.168.247.128','en'),(18411,3031,'2002-09-17 14:34:10',67,'trouble','drowning (albeit by innocent mistake) a cat?  fear karma.  <p>\r\n\r\nlet me share a somewhat positive <i>pro-kitty life</i> story to perk you up.  back in \\\'88 or so my aunt and uncle were starting to tear down a rather large barn at the back of their property.  in the dead of winter while trying to asses what the next step might be they found a small litter of kittens in a corner of one of the stalls previously used to house cattle, the mother was nowhere to be found.  3 of the 5 kittens were dead.  malnurished and frozen.  the remaining two were going fast, they were nuzzled up next to each other assumably for warmth but still they were freezing before my aunts eyes so she scooped them up and took them into the house.  priority number one was to get them warm so in her infinate wisdom she pre-heated the oven and placed them on two small, clay plates and put them in the oven and raised the door up - note:  she did not close the door - and proceeded to carefully watch them for any sign of movement.  after a couple of minutes the small of the two started to twitch and she removed him immediately and wrapped him in a dish towel.  minutes later the other started to twitch and she pulled him out as well and wrapped him in another towel.  long (and sappy) story short, the two lived long lives.  my dad took in one and it stayed with him for years.  curiously enough it never grew more than 10\\\" long and was the absolute most psychotic little ball of fur ever seen in person but maybe i should save some of his exploits for a post of my own.',1,1558,0,0,'63.70.119.94','en'),(18412,3031,'2002-09-17 14:53:29',86,'egg','holy shit.',0,10,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(18413,3031,'2002-09-17 17:06:16',17,'austin','one of your <i>whats?</I>',3,25,66,0,'205.179.19.163','en'),(18414,3031,'2002-09-17 17:53:58',175,'Paul G','<p><i>\\\"somewhat positive pro-kitty life story\\\"</i><br>\r\nYeah, so, my uncle used a bar mower that hung off the side of the tractor: it looked like an 8 foot hedgeclipper.<br>\r\nIn the summer, he would cut the brush that the cows didn\\\'t keep in check.<br>\r\nOn time, he wasn\\\'t paying too much attention - and neither was one of the cats.<br>\r\nSo, it wasn\\\'t a clean cut; my aunt came running - with her sewing kit - and finished off the leg with her scissors and then closed everything up.<br>\r\nThe cat lived - and she had kittens.<br>\r\nBy all accounts, she was a very good cat.<br>\r\nAnd it was the damnedest thing to to watch a three-legged cat running around the farm.<br>\r\nMy uncle got rid of the bar mower after that.</p>',0,735,67,0,'67.248.86.140','en'),(18415,3031,'2002-09-17 18:06:41',175,'Paul G','<p>Peter - so sad.  My sincere condolences on your loss.<br>\r\nThis: \\\"somehow got loose while i wqs feeding him and drowned in the toilet,\\\" is harsh.<br> \r\n[It sounds a lot like the defense given by a low-grade member of a street crew in Chicago: \\\"Somehow, your honor, he ran into the river and drowned.\\\"]</p>',0,320,66,0,'67.248.86.140','en'),(18416,3031,'2002-09-17 18:09:52',17,'anonymous','<P>my grandmother was known for her \\\'prairie justice\\\' birth control methods.  if her cats had unwanted kittens, she took them by the hind quarters and gave them one good hit up against the fence post.  we are talking oklahoma, a long time ago.</P>',3,253,0,0,'205.179.19.163','en'),(18417,3031,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','thank you austin for warning me that this was about dead kittens.',6,65,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18418,3033,'2002-09-17 19:09:51',17,'austin','this is so good.  i am sending out links like a ho.',1,51,0,0,'205.179.19.163','en'),(18419,3033,'2002-09-17 19:45:03',123,'dcc','wow. amazing, once again.\r\n<p>i\\\'m liking the progression through each stage of this story. the details resolve in both words and images the further we go. fantastic.',0,168,0,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(18420,3031,'2002-09-17 20:42:07',170,'xipibi','<p>Thanks to all for your comforting comments...</p>\r\n<p>Apologies to those to whom this brought up bad memories...</p>\r\n<p>I still miss the \\\'thruuuuuummmmm\\\'...</p>',1,170,0,0,'209.39.34.142','en'),(18421,3031,'2002-09-17 23:44:17',148,'jeeff','i\\\'m so so sorry.  you\\\'re good to forgive.  it is tough.',0,61,0,0,'24.43.239.60','en'),(18422,3033,'2002-09-18 05:05:28',93,'chiamattt','is this story about the kurds in northern iraq? i feel their pain. i like how the u.s protects them there with a nofly zone, but wont lobby turkey into treating them better. the middle east is a mess, invading iraq would make it even more unstable. ',1,249,0,0,'211.32.132.206','en'),(18423,3033,'2002-09-18 05:39:07',176,'Scapegoat','My favourite so far, keeps getting better and better. Hungry for more. Solid.',0,77,0,0,'64.231.194.145','en'),(18424,3033,'2002-09-18 05:50:36',17,'austin','<P>why is it you are not a white house correspondent?  you need to be one of those angry detractors on the television giving both sides of the story in an informative fashion.</P>\r\n<P>meet me in vegas.</P>',4,205,93,0,'64.174.154.251','en'),(18425,3033,'2002-09-18 14:28:55',392,'p.','holy schnikes, so being new to swinney, i just ran through the entire series.  i\\\'m a little more than floored.  something like right through the concrete and into the offices downstairs.  oh, jeeff and hool, would that i not be such a slack ass due to your wonderful work.\r\n\r\nbut, yum guys.  this is awesome.',0,311,0,0,'12.13.167.78','en'),(18426,3031,'2002-09-18 18:54:00',251,'Jamie','how does a budgie drown in a toilet?',1,36,66,0,'195.92.168.170','en'),(18427,3031,'2002-09-18 19:20:44',17,'austin to peter, jaime','WHAT IS A BUDGIE?<BR><BR>i am dying to know what it is that died in your toilet bowl!',6,85,0,0,'205.179.19.163','en'),(18428,3031,'2002-09-18 20:53:46',154,'ryan','isn\\\'t it a bird?',0,19,17,0,'66.217.129.201','en'),(18429,3031,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','a budgie is a bird.',6,19,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18430,3031,'2002-09-19 01:35:41',129,'moebius','good lord. auugh. how heartbreaking.<br><br>this piece reminded me of all the crap i went through with roommates in this city, all of them thinking they knew what was best for my cats, or behaving irresponsibly with them. like the creepy assbite kids i lived with briefly who insisted on letting my cat out whenever i was away from the house: \\\"all cats are outdoor cats. it\\\'s cruel to keep them inside.\\\" yeah, sure, buddy, until they come back with parasites, abscesses from bites, feline leukemia, broken legs, or not at all. and when that happens you\\\'ll inevitably just sigh and say: \\\"oh, wow, tough luck.\\\"<br><br>things like that eventually provoked me to move into a studio and live alone with my critters for five years. of course, they got on my nerves too, sometimes. but they were easier to forgive, being just cats and all.',0,850,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(18431,3034,'2002-09-19 07:17:55',175,'Paul G','<p>Regardless of whether it\\\'s true, it usually isn\\\'t very satisfying to be told that we are predisposed towards certain ways of being.<br>\r\nSomehow, to be human - in the fullest sense - is to be capable of choosing, and defining, and creating.<br>\r\nI find a very deep sort of concord between your words and my own life experience.<br>\r\nSometimes, living a life in opposition to something can be every bit as limiting as simply accepting circumstances.  Or, at least, that has been my own experience - learned too late.<br>\r\nI enjoyed the imagery of literal self-examination via the mirror: so much of the better personal content of the web seems to be just that: an effort to know and be known through such processes.<br>\r\nThanks, Matt.</p>',0,746,0,0,'66.19.52.163','en'),(18432,3033,'2002-09-19 16:49:13',90,'leonard','I love the third image. It looks like a sardine can going nowhere fast. The poor people. ',1,89,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(18433,3030,'2002-09-19 20:07:05',124,'gus','in other countries people often dry their clothes places you wouldnt. Often people who are poor are mistakenly thought to \\\"live in filth\\\" when really, they might be drying their clothes on a garbage can that is clean enough to eat out of. (Not that these cans were that clean, but you know what I mean). \r\n<p>\r\nWho are we to tell them what to do? Are we going to supply them with public drying racks? If not, we best keep our mouths shut and stay out of their business dont you think? \r\n<p>\r\nThere is nothing worse than telling someone they\\\'re doing it all wrong while giving them no other option. Telling someone they\\\'ve got no choice but to live wrong probably doesn\\\'t make anyone feel too great.',0,713,0,0,'64.172.58.184','en'),(18434,3030,'2002-09-19 23:50:21',82,'simon','i actually didn\\\'t give the clothes drying on the trash can a second thought. mostly i was just like what a nice summer day picture. i wish i were there.',0,155,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18435,3030,'2002-09-20 00:25:39',17,'austin','now i feel like a big jerk.  next time i am swimming at the park, i\\\'ll remember where to go to dry my trunks.',3,112,0,0,'64.174.154.170','en'),(18436,3030,'2002-09-20 03:22:27',175,'Paul G','<p>Thanks gus; you\\\'re right.<br>\r\nI wasn\\\'t too happy with this piece: I liked the photo, but felt that the prose was caught in an awkward place between my poetic and short story styles.<br> \r\nMaybe the comment made me seem even more preachy; I don\\\'t know.<br>\r\nI\\\'ve learned a lot, here; I\\\'m trying to learn more.<br>\r\nI understand your point about other countries.<br>\r\nBut, the people in the picture live in the same city and country as I do; we\\\'re a part of the same community.<br>\r\nAnd they had money for the dolphin balloon, and string to hang it with: why not hang the clothing, like the balloon?<br>\r\nThe things that I do affect them; the things that they do affect me.<br>\r\nOn the one hand, a self-interested argument can be made: if they are willing to live and work in less healthy conditions, then I will have to lower my own expectations for what I can enjoy in life.<br>\r\nBut, in a more important way - in my opinion - every kid deserves a chance to be healthy and clean.<br>\r\nThere\\\'s a certain \\\"oneness\\\" with the urban environment that\\\'s just not cool - because there\\\'s a lot of stuff in it that is really nasty.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\\\'m pretty weird about the garbage cans, because I worked for a park district one year.  At the end of the summer I had to drive a truck around and collect literally hundreds of 55 gallon drums.  They were rank: full of dog crap, diapers, needles, vomit, and such.  And I got real close to each one as I lifted it onto the back of the F-350 flatbed.<br>\r\nI usually don\\\'t say anything to people in public anymore either - and maybe I\\\'m most upset with myself.<br>\r\nI\\\'ll still get involved on the bus if someone is acting up, but my grandfather used to talk to everyone.<br>\r\nIt was good - better - when people were more involved with each other and cared more.<br>\r\nI think that sense of being a part of something - together - is fading/has faded.</p>',0,1931,124,0,'66.19.53.94','en'),(18437,3030,'2002-09-20 03:28:13',175,'Paul G','<p>It was a good day, simon.  The most important thing, probably, was that these people were there together: as a family.  I was struck by the ironic juxtaposition of the dolphin balloon and the underwear/trash.  It is easy to condition yourself to ignore a certain amount of urban white noise, maybe?  I dunno.  You are cool; thanks for this and your previous comments.  Madonna and El Caminos - yeah!</p>',0,406,82,0,'66.19.53.94','en'),(18438,3030,'2002-09-20 03:30:02',175,'Paul G','Heh-heh.',0,8,17,0,'66.19.53.94','en'),(18439,3035,'2002-09-20 05:10:40',234,'etienne','i like.',1,7,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(18440,3030,'2002-09-20 08:30:39',17,'austin','i met simon at the john spencer blues explosion in santa cruz tonight.  her friends from nyc were opening up, but i missed them.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nsanta cruz is a college town, a vaction town, a beach town.  i have ignored the college thing.  i dont see it much.  but tonight it was in full force.  i kept thinking i saw old friends from college.  the styles have not changed, apparently.  skinny boys or beefy red-neck chic boys.  girls, girls, girls.  what is the girls\\\' steeze?  about everything from 1965 on was represented in vivid clarity with a bit of fusion here and there.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni am wondering how many of the people there could be summed up by Madonna and El Caminos?',3,678,0,0,'64.174.154.103','en'),(18441,3033,'2002-09-20 15:10:13',148,'jeeff','thanks!  leonard, originally it looked like a vcr. ;)',0,53,0,0,'24.43.239.60','en'),(18442,3036,'2002-09-20 18:09:07',17,'austin','<P>\r\nhi Sarah, welcome to the site.  my little sister is named sarah.  for a panicked moment i thought maybe she had found me.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni enjoyed reading this.  it made me feel a little sappy.  i think i read it in the context of the childrens\\\' perspective.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni have not seen my mom in a dog\\\'s years.\r\n</P>',3,321,0,0,'205.179.19.163','en'),(18443,3036,'2002-09-20 21:00:20',180,'auntie','the thing about motherhood is that it is so hard to separate what other people think you should to do \\\"raise your kids right,\\\" and what your heart tells you is the right thing.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\ni think that all the times i went with my heart, it ended up being the \\\"right\\\" thing for that child, and almost every time i went with what the books said, or the school, or my parents or his parents, or my friends.... it was a disaster.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\nmandy and elly are 28 and 24 now.  i wish i had spent less time worrying about what other people thought about me, my house, my stuff, etc. and more time thinking about what would make my kids smile.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\nthe worst thing i ever did to mandy was to not let her help take care of her baby sister.\r\n<br><br>\r\nthe worst thing i ever did to elly was to send her to school before she was 7 years old.\r\n<br><br>\r\nevery kid is different.  unconditional love is great.',1,922,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(18444,3036,'2002-09-20 21:11:42',402,'Sarah','Thank you for this, I\\\'m glad you saw it from the childrens perspective, I was thinking of their feelings when I wrote it. Funny, I often get comments from people on subjects because of my name, so many Sarahs out there!',0,222,17,0,'203.96.111.237','en'),(18445,3036,'2002-09-20 21:16:46',402,'Sarah','I know what you mean about the \\\'right\\\' way to do things. I have had to stop reading parenting books and what not, they\\\'re just so depressing!\r\nThanks for your input!\r\n',0,176,180,0,'203.96.111.237','en'),(18446,3037,'2002-09-21 12:49:45',64,'Joanna','Please don\\\'t die of West Nile disease before you have made and sent me your zine.  Thank you. ',1,97,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18447,3037,'2002-09-21 14:09:03',57,'weeb','is there a zine ring happenin here?! count me in! ',0,50,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(18448,3031,'2002-09-22 02:11:05',402,'Sarah','oh...oh....I know how you feel, my kitty died last year, my children named a star after her. Actually it\\\'s Venus but I didn\\\'t want to tell them...',0,152,170,0,'210.54.236.8','en'),(18449,3036,'2002-09-22 04:07:08',170,'xipibi','<p>Hi, Sarah</p>\r\nPresuming you are of the prevailing Western European cultural background, you will understand this reference.<p/>\r\n<p>Eve had to see one of hers off the other one... How\\\'s that for a complex?  Anyway, all you can do is the best you can.  I understand to the quick your comment of them hurting you with love and you hurting them with anger...</p>\r\n<p> Hang in there, at least you, being the adult in the equation, remember your responsibility and recognize your limits in doing the daily miracle we call \\\'life\\\'...</p>\r\n<p>Keep on keeping on...</p>',1,573,0,0,'209.39.34.166','en'),(18450,3037,'2002-09-22 04:49:10',64,'Joanna','My zine is called <A HREF=\\\"http://hubris.co.nz/zine.html\\\"> BOYS BOYS BOYS BOYS BOYS.</A>  Apparently all the staff at New Zealand\\\'s top advertising agency love it. So there you go. ',1,190,57,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18451,3037,'2002-09-22 06:13:44',175,'Paul G','<p>Last week I wore sandals to the bar.  A guy walked in, stood next to me, and did his thing.  I felt the mist of his overspray on the tops of my feet.  It was really gross.  I am sick now.  Maybe it was caused by the mist.  Or, maybe it\\\'s West Nile Virus - I actually considered that.  In either case, I am going to wear <b>boots</b> when I know that I\\\'ll be going to the tavern, from now on.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The stainless steel trough is one of the most dehumanizing things in existence.  To make matters worse, sometimes the sinks are stainless steel troughs too.  And most guys who have had 6-12 don\\\'t need too much encouragement to piss in the sink to begin with.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The descriptive prose was great.  The piece feels like 90% of the public restrooms in such establishments: stickers - yes, and things written in marker.<br>\r\nThe most popular bathroom band name must be:<br>\r\n1) AC/DC, followed by<br>\r\n2) Metallica or Slayer.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Yes: the noise and the light are so surreal.  It is odd that in a way we pay more for the privilege of not really seeing the place that we are.</p>',1,1099,0,0,'66.19.124.141','en'),(18452,3035,'2002-09-22 06:32:24',175,'Paul G','<p>Rock on Chia - you are stretching your style.  The form of the piece was fresh and it worked for me.  I hit one sort of intellectual snag when I read it, though: \r\n<br>given democracy - that is to say the right to choose - most people seem to choose the very life of materialistic satisfaction that you rail against.  \r\n<br>It seems to be the case that through the low politics of economics most of the world is being transformed - I don\\\'t say for the better or worse, only that it\\\'s through an appeal to their greed/desire, and that democracy is somehow very much connected to wanting.\r\n<br>So, yes: this made me think; even if my thoughts are crap.\r\n<br>Thanks.</p> ',2,677,0,0,'66.19.124.141','en'),(18453,3037,'2002-09-22 08:40:55',17,'austin','<P>there were quite a few sandles at the show.  i didn\\\'t understand why anyone would wear open toe shoes to a concert.  i didnt specifically think of  wading the soup in the men\\\'s room.  that makes it worse.  i dont know what i am talking about.  i am really tired.  suicide girls girls are on the tele.  they are all sitting down next to courtney.',3,354,175,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18454,3035,'2002-09-22 09:22:49',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\nok. lets think about this. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n1. Government by the people; a form of government in which the supreme power is retained and directly exercised by the people.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<b>Webster\\\'s Revised Unabridged Dictionary, &copy; 1996, 1998 MICRA, Inc.</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI dont agree that people choose the life of \\\"materialistic satisfaction\\\". I agree that there is a great deal of greed and desire in this world, but we must also agree that the majority of the world lives below the standard we live in n.america. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\npeople have a low self esteem in the world today. they would rather say things like \\\'my thoughts are crap\\\', hide behind a wall of manufactured goods, and shrug off any chance at making a stance. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nthe middle and working class in n.america are too afraid to organize. they believe they are meek, and as individuals they are, yet organized they are strong.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nperhaps the only problem i can see with a consumer goods world, is that consumer goods have become extensions of the individual, rather than extensions of the community. what do i mean? i just feel that we are encouraged to be individualistic in so many ways, that we tend to let our obligations to the community suffer. it isnt enough that we have an unimaginable number of choices in products and services, but we are bombarded with advertising telling us to \\\'buy now\\\'. we live in a hurry hurry world.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nbeing in korea, i have seen the frustration of the average korean. i see it more clearly because i havnt grown up in the same social framework they have. i didnt grow up seeing what im seeing everyday; letting the true nature of each act blur and fade into a \\\'thats just the way it is\\\' cliche shrugoff tactic. Im sure that if i were to live in any major city in n.america with the unclouded eyes i have here, id see much the same. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyou made me think; even though my thoughts are crap. thanks. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n',1,1967,175,0,'61.37.53.58','en'),(18455,3036,'2002-09-22 20:04:05',251,'Jamie','you mean your family haven\\\'t come across this site yet? My mum found my website... and now she thinks i\\\'m nuts.',4,117,17,0,'195.92.168.163','en'),(18456,3037,'2002-09-22 20:11:24',251,'Jamie','<img src=\\\"http://www.jamazon.co.uk/photos/51donotpiss.jpg\\\">',4,65,0,0,'195.92.168.163','en'),(18457,3039,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','buddha reprezent',6,16,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18458,3039,'2002-09-23 17:56:42',123,'dcc','wonderful piece. the serenity it illustrates is fantastic. the progression from work to home and day to night is really well done.\r\n\r\n<p>i love hearing of the magic of places, i just wish there was more magic near where i live. maybe i\\\'m just not looking hard enough.',1,270,0,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(18459,3039,'2002-09-24 04:50:26',175,'Paul G','It\\\'s interesting when things connect in such a way that the author\\\'s perspective, or \\\"voice,\\\" begins to make sense: comparing this house piece to this <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2950.txt\\\">house piece</a> that begins to happen.<br>\r\nSometimes the text here [swinney] works like a photo album of some kind, in which a collection of sorts helps to communicate something of import to its creator.<br>\r\nFor whatever reason, these do more for me than the lyrics. ',0,502,0,0,'66.19.126.162','en'),(18460,3039,'2002-09-24 13:39:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>its like a photo album, but with cross-referenced with little pieces of text you can click. ahh, the INTORWEB; what will they think of next?!</tt>\r\n',1,156,0,0,'204.168.247.128','en'),(18461,1834,'2002-09-24 15:45:19',66,'Peter','<br><tt> exactly one day short of being one year later, my first waking moments no longer suck. YAY. now therye infinities better than i ever thought they could ever be.</tt>',1,174,66,0,'204.168.247.128','en'),(18462,1834,'2002-09-25 00:53:44',86,'egg','the same thing happened to me a little while ago. A friend told me that the painful waking feeling is some absence of the hormone oxytocin that causes this feeling and that if I rode it out for 2-3 weeks she promised relief would come. It did! hurray for predictable physiology. It makes me wonder, though, what other urges and desires can be mastered with a little patience, diet and exercise. and why choose to embrace them at all? Like that song that pj harvey covers: \\\"when you went away, I thought I would die. but then when I didnt die, I thought, is that all there is?\\\" Im paraphrasing here.',0,604,66,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(18463,3035,'2002-09-25 04:35:57',404,'saldaern','just do it.',2,11,0,0,'12.216.70.107','en'),(18464,3037,'2002-09-25 04:37:07',404,'saldaern','this urinal intentionally left blank',2,36,0,0,'12.216.70.107','en'),(18465,2996,'2002-09-25 05:36:18',405,'violet','If a street level dealer were going to carry a gun, because maybe he\\\'s into more than dealing or whatever, I can\\\'t imagine that it would be a revolver. While revolvers are more reliable, they don\\\'t have the gangsta cred that a semi-automatic handgun has. I\\\'d make it, say, either a 9mm (which might be a bit overplayed these days) or a .38 special.',3,360,0,0,'63.201.75.58','en'),(18466,3045,'2002-09-25 15:17:54',123,'dcc','how can you be so sentimental and yet not tacky? fantastic.',0,59,0,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(18467,2996,'2002-09-25 15:18:37',66,'Peter','<br><tt>guns kill, maim and destroy people and animals equally, regardless of type.</tt>',1,88,405,0,'204.168.247.128','en'),(18468,3044,'2002-09-25 17:06:00',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i keep my work id clipped to my bag... on the train, the people across from me are always staring at it intently, then looking up at my face... then back to the id, face, etc... as if theyre trying to see if its really me.</tt>',1,235,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18469,3045,'2002-09-25 17:57:24',176,'Scapegoat','An interesting, fresh, and touching angle. ',0,43,0,0,'204.19.79.66','en'),(18470,3045,'2002-09-25 17:58:51',404,'saldaern','in these days since the day i\\\'ve often tried to think of the word that might best describe the visceral reaction i felt each time i had seen them myself...  today i know the word is bludgeoned.  thank you friend.',2,215,0,0,'12.216.70.107','en'),(18471,3045,'2002-09-25 19:51:24',175,'Paul G','<p>This is a very good collection: the whole communicates something more than any individual part.  Maybe some stories are too big for any one person to tell.</p>\r\n<p>Sometimes it\\\'s good to look at an album of sorts after an anniversary, and to remember how things have changed.  Holidays can be like that too: a time to survey the faces of the family that remain, and to recall those no longer present, and maybe most importantly to be thankful for the new ones that are in the pictures now.</p>',0,499,0,0,'66.19.48.91','en'),(18472,3044,'2002-09-25 19:55:48',175,'Paul G','I am intrigued.  And my interest was generated by a well-crafted storyline, not an image or graphic textual content.  Well played.',0,130,0,0,'66.19.48.91','en'),(18473,3045,'2002-09-25 22:28:13',406,'don','This is good stuff.',1,19,0,0,'63.110.151.2','en'),(18474,3045,'2002-09-25 23:08:20',66,'Peter','<br><tt>thanks for all the positive comments; some of you might be interested in reading some of my other thoughts about these buildings <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2576\\\">here</a><br><br><b>dcc:</b> the loss of these buildings is something thats troubled me rather substantially, on different levels than what the news has overkilled; certainly any manhattanites out there know exactly what im talking about: <i>the landmarks- the <b>only</b> way to tell north from south when youre stumbling out of a club in the east village at 4am; the summer concert series in the plaza; the techno battles in wtc 1\\\'s messenger depot, etc etc etc</i><br><br>\r\ni could blabber on and on, but suffice to say, after observing the event mere feet away and living in a city that is still quite tangibly reeling from it, the last thing i could (or would) be is tacky... thanks again for your comment.</tt>\r\n\r\n',1,929,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18475,2980,'2002-09-26 00:36:06',402,'Sarah','I believe in stress, help, if stress isn\\\'t for real, then I\\\'ve got issues I never knew I had! Good luck with all the stress in your life, it certainly sounds difficult right now.',1,184,0,0,'203.96.111.237','en'),(18476,376,'2002-09-26 09:46:12',89,'sawks','hmmmmm\r\n\r\n<hr>\r\n<a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/info_user.phtml?uid=240\\\">xep</a> : fefs!\r\n',4,101,0,0,'144.132.117.240','en'),(18477,2996,'2002-09-26 17:13:09',405,'violet','Indeed. I own two of them, and I\\\'ve grown up around a pretty intensive gun community. My dad is legislative liaison for the Maryland State Rifle and Pistol association. I\\\'m just adding a little opinion here. Revolvers have a sort of stigma where I come from. -- That said, my  handgun is a .38 special five-shot revolver. But mine was purchased for defense, not to carry around on the streets and look tough.',0,414,66,0,'63.201.75.58','en'),(18478,3044,'2002-09-26 23:54:21',142,'oof','<i>His right shoelace is untied and has settled across her left sneaker.</i>\r\n\r\ngood work gus.',2,94,0,0,'128.148.188.186','en'),(18479,3047,'2002-09-27 11:15:44',180,'auntie','i am disturbed by this',4,22,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(18480,3047,'2002-09-27 16:30:24',404,'saldaern','disturbed good, or disturbed bad?',0,33,180,0,'12.216.70.107','en'),(18481,3047,'2002-09-27 17:02:51',180,'auntie','i\\\'m thinking about that. i felt the feelings you meant me to feel, i think, regret, despair, fear, confusion. i prefer not to have those feelings, perhaps.',3,158,404,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(18482,3048,'2002-09-28 05:47:17',92,'kini','isa, fiji.',0,10,0,0,'203.27.69.112','en'),(18483,3045,'2002-09-28 10:14:45',234,'etienne','puts a new slant on <i>fuck you landscape, i will not blend</i>, doesn\\\'t it.',1,79,66,0,'203.96.111.237','en'),(18484,3048,'2002-09-28 17:48:47',195,'matt','\\\"the years will run like rabbits.\\\"',1,40,0,0,'209.208.108.191','en'),(18485,3045,'2002-09-29 05:04:07',66,'Peter','<br><tt>we just watched <a href=\\\"http://us.imdb.com/Title?0150230\\\">the cruise</a> for the second time since last night; in my basement, my little screen flashed black and white documentary images of the wtc over and over and over and <i>over</i>, and it was spinning and spinning and maybe even appeared to fall in the post-spinning dizziness. i miss the wtc towers. alot.the skyline now all blends, from the arms of uptown to the lowlands of south ferry and battery park.</tt>',1,483,234,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18486,3049,'2002-09-29 18:45:05',124,'gus','I wanted to say that I like this. I like it\\\'s style.',0,55,0,0,'64.160.55.202','en'),(18487,3030,'2002-09-30 15:46:08',124,'gus','Oh I am with you on the disgustingness of the garbage cans. dont you worry none. And while the people live here and \\\"should do what we do\\\" or whatever, they arent FROM here and its hard to change someone who\\\'s learned another way.\r\n<p>\r\nalso I dont think its fair to say they can afford a balloon and therefore should not live like trash monkeys. Even trash monkeys want to treat their kids to something fun and bright and happy once in a while. And I think they should spend their money on what makes them happy. Heck, maybe drying their clothes on the trash cans makes them feel great, maybe they like making people squirm. I dunno !',0,644,175,0,'64.160.55.202','en'),(18488,3049,'2002-09-30 17:14:37',82,'simon','yeah so did i.',0,14,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18489,3051,'2002-09-30 17:24:02',82,'simon','al, this stuff is great. i\\\'m a fan.',1,38,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18490,3051,'2002-09-30 17:59:47',195,'matt','this is good. i like the undercurrent of menace that builds up all through it and is released at the end.',1,105,0,0,'209.208.110.152','en'),(18491,3048,'2002-09-30 19:24:00',123,'dcc','the wonderful smell of fallen leaves is coming.',0,47,0,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(18492,3030,'2002-10-01 05:37:24',175,'Paul G','<p>\r\n<i>\\\"something fun and bright and happy once in a while\\\"</i><br>\r\nYep.  Without that, life isn\\\'t worth too much.<br>\r\nMonotony sucks.<br>\r\nLong hallways made of cinder block, and artificial light depress me.<br>\r\nCan I share a secret, and mock myself?<br>  \r\nGood.<br>\r\nI had hoped that the \\\"fun and bright and happy\\\" stuff in life would also be the family/relationship stuff.  In my little fantasy world, people are more important than things.  And there is nothing brighter or happier - to me - than seeing the people that I care for, smiling.  To the extent that the dolphin balloon makes the kids smile - it\\\'s great.<br>\r\nThe kids are worth a lot; the dolphin balloon is a piece of plastic crap - the balloon doesn\\\'t matter, one way or the other.  And yet the parents put the dolphin balloon on a line in the trees, and the kids underwear in the trash; they should be in opposite places - in my fantasy world.<br>\r\nOh well.  I think that you\\\'re right in what you\\\'ve written; I just struggle with a lot of the things that the scene made me think of.  Thanks for pulling all of this out; it should have been [bad me] in the piece to begin with, I suppose...</p>',1,1194,124,0,'66.19.127.33','en'),(18493,3051,'2002-10-01 05:46:00',175,'Paul G','I didn\\\'t get a sense of any menace building: in the first three paragraphs some form of the word \\\"laugh\\\" was used.  It was the last paragraph that introduced tension - as I read it.  Damn the tension; I want them to fall in love.',1,238,195,0,'66.19.127.33','en'),(18494,3049,'2002-10-01 06:04:12',175,'Paul G','<a href=\\\"http://www.hti.umich.edu/cgi/k/kjv/kjv-idx?type=DIV1&byte=2546945\\\">Yep: vanity.</a>',0,98,0,0,'66.19.127.33','en'),(18495,3050,'2002-10-01 06:51:34',17,'Austin','Only a red commie bastard would write something like this and not use capital letters.',1,86,0,0,'64.174.154.187','en'),(18496,3050,'2002-10-01 06:55:27',93,'chiamattt','well my red commie ass also loves the idea of full employment. dont you swinney? FULL EMPLOYMENT! dont you see you lost your job because some fucker in a leather chair a few floors above you was juggling stock options for his own personal gain rather than doing his job and trying to secure long term job prospects for his rank and file? ',6,338,0,0,'61.37.53.25','en'),(18497,3050,'2002-10-01 07:00:55',93,'chiamattt','AND! capital letters are CAPITALIST. why should the first letter in a sentence get the added status? arent all letters equally important in life? no one letter is more important than the next. poor x and z. neglected like kurds and native indians. i feel their pain. ',0,267,0,0,'61.37.53.25','en'),(18498,3050,'2002-10-01 07:03:10',17,'austin','you slack unpatriotic deserter, throw all the stones you like but dont expect any pats on the back from swinney.org.  your oppinions are irrelavent.  you dont vote.  you live in korea. if it were not for the US, canada would be trading in maple leaves and beaver skins.  have a little respect for the decisions that give your parents jobs, even if you dont have enough respect for yourself or your country to hold down a job in your homeland and make some kind of contribution.',5,477,0,0,'64.174.154.187','en'),(18499,3050,'2002-10-01 07:04:42',17,'austin','oh, and notice at the bottom there are no canadian cities.  there is a reason for that!',6,87,93,0,'64.174.154.187','en'),(18500,3050,'2002-10-01 07:10:08',93,'chiamattt','i once met a CAPITALIST who said that it was upto the worker to gain the skills necessary to get a job once a job was lost. he said that it wasnt the responsibility of the employer to give the worker the means to move up in life. he was so fond of the individualistic life. he was fond of man vs man and dog eat dog. Ive eaten dog, and i know that leaving canada to find employment has nothing to do with how patriotic i am. no matter how much i love my country i will not close my eyes to the injustice i see. imperialism isnt what makes the average american proud. the average american is proud to be able to take a shit in any state he wants to. to be able to buy not only playstation 2, but also xbox. to walk into a supermaket and not have to buy the ingredients to make a meal, but to buy the whole god damn meal at once and just throw it into the microwave. this is why you close your eyes. this is why you are afraid to say anything to those who need to be told what they are doing is wrong. YOU DIDNT EVEN VOTE FOR YOUR PRESIDENT, but you will let him gain the necessary congressional approval to commit to a war the population hasnt even been given an opportunity to vote on. in a true democracy, the order to goto war should rest in the public, for its the public that dies during war! the politician never smells the trench. the politician never sees the blood and for this reason you will get drafted because of your unemployed uneducated ass and you will be the one at the end of a bayonnet. YOU WILL DIE because of your PRIDE. ',6,1542,0,0,'61.37.53.25','en'),(18501,3050,'2002-10-01 07:24:15',17,'austin','first of all, God Bless George W. Bush.  i voted for him and i know everyone else at swinney.org that is a red blooded american voted for him too.  he was the obvious choice.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nyou know, a funny thing, i never hear anything about international politics and canada.  why is that?  because canadians dont understand international politics.  they are too busy doping up on legalized marijuana and drinking piss beer for 12 dollars a six pack, and taking sedatives subscribed by third rate government doctors.  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni support the succession of Quebec.  if i lived in canada, i would want to leave as well.  a more mismanaged and clunker of a country ever to cling to its neighbor--there never was.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nwhat canada needs is a military regime to set the basis for some real economic growth to get them out of imperial bitch mode that some how they got stuck in.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nwhen we get done with sadam, i think we should turn our focus to canada.\r\n',6,964,93,0,'64.174.154.187','en'),(18502,3050,'2002-10-01 13:19:17',93,'chiamattt','YOU WANT SOME SUGAR SWINDAWG? COME GET SOME!',0,44,17,0,'61.37.53.25','en'),(18503,3050,'2002-10-01 14:41:05',66,'Peter','<br><tt>\r\n<a href=\"http://us.imdb.com/Title?0109370\">canadian bacon</a><br><br>\r\n<b>Genre:</b> Comedy / War<br>  \r\n<b>Tagline:</b> Surrender pronto, or we\'ll level Toronto!<br> \r\n<b>Plot Outline:</b> The U.S. President, low in the opinion polls, gets talked into raising his popularity by trying to start a cold war against Canada.</tt>',1,345,17,0,'204.168.247.128','en'),(18504,3051,'2002-10-01 15:20:25',124,'gus','Yah, but its deceptive tension in this section. shes tense because she just realized she missed her stop, you know how you freak out when you realize you didnt get off the bus 4 stops ago like you were supposed to? she isnt tense at all about the guy. I mean thats why she misses the stop in the first place, she\'s so comfy with him. Or maybe that\'s really obvious. there\'s more tention in the first section I guess, which is still the same story, so it counts.',0,320,0,0,'64.160.55.202','en'),(18505,3050,'2002-10-01 17:11:20',176,'Scapegoat','The following outlines my feelings on the issue in regards to the whole debate domestically in Canada. As for the comments a la Quebec seperatism I\\\'m pushing for Alberta to seperate first myself.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>Anti-Americanism trumps humanitarianism in Canada</b><br><br>\r\n\r\nOne can hardly blame a person for fighting against war, in this case a coming war in Iraq, but there comes a time when arguments must be weighed. It is disturbing that in this country, which likes to hold itself up as softer more humanitarian member of the international community, that we have lost this focus in both our media and political debate on the issue of a new war in Iraq. So blinded are we with our insecuirties about our neighbor to the south that the real humanitarian perspective has received little or no attention.<br><br> \r\n\r\nSo busy have we been demonising the intentions of George W. Bush and his adminastration that we have failed to look at the larger picture regarding Iraq. Speculation on the true intentions behind the American push for war becomes irrelevant when one starts to look at certain numbers. <br><br>\r\n\r\nWhen the first Gulf War ended a set of sanctions where put in place as part of the agreement that ended the fighting. Since 1991, these sanctions have been often quoted as the cause of death for 1.5 million Iraqis by various humanitarian groups most recently by a group of high profile Canadians stating their opposition to war. When compared to some of the higher casualty estimates of the first Gulf War of approximately 300,000 (mostly Iraqis) the human life cost of the first Gulf War pales when compared to the burden of suffering and death that has been placed on the backs of regular Iraqis.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe humanitarian argument for the saving of the largest amount of human life then becomes clear. The end goal of the new war by America is regime change if successful the west would have no more basis to continue to uphold sanctions. Sanctions that have by far killed more Iraqis then any direct military conflict with the west not to mention provided cheap oil through the UN food for oil program off which Europe and the rest of us profit. Why is Europe so opposed to the war? - for the same reason they claim the US thinks it necessary. The death toll from the sanctions continues to climb as we bicker about motivations. Must be nice to live in a part of the world where you can so easily talk & debate about these things while regular people die due to our unwillingness to act.<br><br>\r\n\r\nEven if casualty were three times as high as this time around it would still be less then the number of Iraqi civilians, women, & children that have suffered and died under sanctions. It would seem however that  Canadians are more concerned with questioning the American motive then in sticking with a more humanitarian world view, under which we would be concerned about the Iraqi people dying under sanctions and the fastest way to remove those sanctions safely.<br><br>\r\n\r\nJust another example of how Canada and many European nations are quickly becoming countries with a  unwillingness to get their hands dirty or a bit of a bloody nose to do what is right for a segment of humanity outside its borders. I wash my hands of the blood of the Iraqis killed by these sanctions and place it on the hands of those of us in the west that are complacent in the face of what needs to be done.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThere is some rhetoric for ya, Chia.',0,3460,0,0,'64.231.193.201','en'),(18506,3030,'2002-10-02 01:24:53',124,'gus','yah, its the \\\"SHOULD\\\"s that get me. and yah, its your fantasy where things are switched so that the clothes are on the trees, but you dont get to tell anyone what they SHOULD do, and you\\\'ll just go crazy if you dont accept them how they are. right? right. they\\\'re happy and their kids are happy with the balloon and its a nice day in the park. la la la.',0,365,0,0,'64.160.55.202','en'),(18507,3030,'2002-10-02 05:17:44',175,'Paul G','<p>I\\\'m just going to accept what you\\\'ve said.<br>\r\nBut, I\\\'m afraid now that, in the next episode, you\\\'re going to have the bus in <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=3051\\\">Standing Up 2</a> crash, and the people on it get killed or something, just to prove a point.<br>\r\nOh well.<br>\r\nI don\\\'t have kids, anyway.<br>\r\nI do have underwear; I like the boxer/brief kind.<br>\r\nAt my mom\\\'s place there\\\'s a clothesline that runs between a tree and the garage.  I dry my underwear there, when I can.  But, the squirrels chew on the clothesline and it breaks a lot.  If I was a squirrel, I wouldn\\\'t chew on the clothesline; I\\\'d climb to the highest spot that I could.  And I\\\'d sit there and gaze out upon Creation - contemplating it.  Of course, then a big crow or something might catch me and carry me off.  But if I could just relax, and become one with the experience, I bet that looking down from the talons of the bird would be really cool.  Well, it would be cool until the bird got back to its nest or whatever.</p> ',1,1068,0,0,'66.19.39.70','en'),(18508,3052,'2002-10-03 06:37:58',175,'Paul G','<p><i>\\\"...looking for work that would whore out my body more than my mind...\\\"</i><br>\r\nThat\\\'s a rough one.  It seems like either you\\\'re the type of person who asks questions, or you\\\'re not.  You know?  Sitting at a desk, or standing at a bench, some people ask, \\\"Why?\\\" and others don\\\'t.  If you figure it out, please share the answer.<br>\r\nYou tackle a lot of big stuff in your writing; and it\\\'s neat to see you and Chia approach it differently.</p>  ',0,478,0,0,'67.195.73.178','en'),(18509,3053,'2002-10-03 11:25:44',93,'chiamattt','he\\\'s holding the paddle backwards. \r\n',6,40,0,0,'211.32.132.242','en'),(18510,3054,'2002-10-03 14:08:38',66,'Peter','<br><tt>as an aesthetic, its nice, but as a way of life? not very fulfilling.</tt>',1,82,0,0,'204.168.247.128','en'),(18511,3052,'2002-10-03 14:15:29',123,'dcc','i think we can change the world. we just have to do it one person at a time. once it has momentum, there\\\'s nothing gonna stop it, baby.',0,138,0,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(18512,3053,'2002-10-03 14:21:36',93,'chiamattt','i hope to god christ never held the cross upside down. too much of the sacreligious wine or something. ',2,103,0,0,'61.37.53.25','en'),(18513,3054,'2002-10-03 14:22:18',123,'dcc','yet, isn\\\'t it incredibly ironic that computer savy people more frequently anthropomorphize the hardware and software they use?\r\n<p>i know i do so on an almost daily basis. and for some reason, it makes dealing with a crash or an unexpected behavior (see, there it is!) in a more understandable way! I\\\'m not crazy, <a href=\\\"http://www.tuxedo.org/~esr/jargon/html/Anthropomorphization.html\\\">other people</a> do this all of the time!',2,442,0,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(18514,3054,'2002-10-03 17:07:49',122,'hool','a computer is a mirror that doesn\\\'t reflect lonliness.',0,57,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(18515,3054,'2002-10-03 18:30:50',66,'Peter','<br><tt>when are we all gonna have our own robots?!</tt>',1,56,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18516,3045,'2002-10-03 18:42:11',195,'matt','<BR><BR><tt><I><B>I placed two tiny kisses on my left index and middle fingers and set them adrift on the evening riverbreeze</I></B><BR><BR>that\\\'s a nice line. i can picture you doing that</tt>',1,197,0,0,'209.208.109.13','en'),(18517,3045,'2002-10-03 18:48:23',66,'Peter','<br><tt>its true; i also kiss the envelopes of love-letters before sending them.</tt>',1,85,195,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18518,3052,'2002-10-03 20:07:45',183,'lin','There is this gerenal frustration with society that I hold aswell, and like dcc, I used to think that one person at a time would be sufficient to starting to change things, but I\\\'m pretty convinced that we\\\'re all pretty doomed. Ok, that was a little harsh, but some things about financial inequities are starting to irk me in serious ways, and I\\\'m part of the priviledged Canadian group... \r\n\r\nI don\\\'t have any clue how we\\\'re ever going to relate to each other though, because it seems like all everyone wants to do anymore is push the rest of the world away and treat themselves like the \\\"special case\\\".\r\n\r\nAs if someone who is lower on the economic scale is less important, less human.. ',3,710,0,0,'66.46.197.102','en'),(18519,3055,'2002-10-03 21:18:43',175,'Paul G','<p>Your writing makes me remember things.<br> \r\nSometimes, those things make me sad.<br>  \r\nBut, I like your writing a lot.<br>\r\nCertain places seem to collect memories: the beach, shops and garages, etc.<br>\r\nThe stuff that you\\\'ve written takes me there, while I\\\'m stuck at a desk.<br>\r\nThanks.</p>\r\n  ',0,309,0,0,'66.19.50.167','en'),(18520,3057,'2002-10-03 23:55:42',371,'Kyla','Awwe Peter :) :)  You guys are so sweet, and so perfect for each other.  You have given me new faith in love and romance and all that squishy stuff.  \r\n\r\nxxx',6,157,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18521,3057,'2002-10-04 00:55:57',17,'austin','<BR>\r\ni know she is the<BR>\r\nright one because when i\\\'m gone<BR>\r\nshe vacuumes the house.',1,92,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18522,3057,'2002-10-04 01:06:13',195,'matt','<BR><BR>and don\\\'t be mackin\\\'<BR>on all the girlies while yo<BR>luvah is away',1,82,0,0,'209.208.108.42','en'),(18523,3057,'2002-10-04 03:27:57',66,'Peter','<br><tt>she be the only girl i mack, yo.</tt>',1,45,195,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18524,3054,'2002-10-04 03:51:28',409,'rachel','i was promised a flying car!',2,28,0,0,'144.132.117.240','en'),(18525,3054,'2002-10-04 04:19:01',57,'weeb','<p>i\\\'m waiting for the haircut preserving nanobot in spray form. when you get the perfect haircut, you spray nanos on your hair and they keep it at that exact length until you spray it with deactivating hairbot spray.\r\n</p><p>\r\nyou could go around in high school spraying people with deactivating sprays. the industry would then go ape over hairbots with key signatures. you wouldn\\\'t need hairdressers because you could buy preprogrammed hairbots in the can. never to make small talk with a hair stylist again!!!\r\n</p><p>\r\nfuture\\\'s so bright... i gotta wear shades\r\n</p>',1,579,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(18526,3053,'2002-10-04 04:44:16',176,'Scapegoat','<b>Chia</b> - I am not holding the paddle backwards I am about to stroke on my left side.<br><br>\r\n<b>Hool</b> - I\\\'m coming home baby and I\\\'ll have to thank you for this wonderful photo. I hope you checked the bagel for mold I\\\'ve been neglecting my personal hygiene lately.<br><br>',0,290,0,0,'129.37.148.33','en'),(18527,3052,'2002-10-04 04:58:58',176,'Scapegoat','I think that Chia and I are both a little obsessed with the big stuff, sometimes to our own detriment. The common thread between the two of us from my perspective is that we both seem to yearn for some form of justice, we just have slightly different ideas of what is just.<br><br>\r\nWhen I went to school in Ottawa with Chia, him and I were a lot closer ideologically. Different experiences have steered us in slightly different ways though. I miss him and look forward to him being around during the holidays, hopefully we will get a chance to talk things over a bit. He is one of a few of my friends that are good in discussions dealing with some of the bigger stuff.<br>  ',0,675,175,0,'129.37.148.33','en'),(18528,3052,'2002-10-04 05:29:18',176,'Scapegoat','I\\\'m curious a bit about your issue with financial inequities and the statement \\\"<i>As if someone who is lower on the economic scale is less important, less human..</i>\\\" I\\\'m trying to figure out if you equate being important with being human. <br><br>Importance is something that is relative, being classified as human is something that one hopes is no longer relative. I do have to mention that there are many places in the world were certain identity groups of human beings aren\\\'t treated like human beings, but I know you know this. For the most part these groupings of humanity are treated differently due to things like race, gender, etc.<br><br>\r\nYou mentioned that everyone wants to treat themselves as a special case pushing the rest of the world aside, then you go on to mention the issue of economic scale. If everyone was indeed treating themselves as a special case wouldn\\\'t they also be treating the ones above as less then themselves as well?<br><br>\r\nAn equal existence for everyone is not attainable, only an equal opportunity even this is really difficult, rarely attained, and when it is only in small pockets. I\\\'ve grappled with the issue of financial inequities myself and been really irked and like you said we both come from a priviledged group. I guess what I\\\'m trying to say is that we will relate to each other as vital human beings but not as human beings of equal importance. Even great concepts such as equality have their limits or so it seems to me.<br><br>\r\nMan that sounds rather defeating but I still think it is important to fight for whatever you feel equality is. I think the main problem with changing things is that the people that want change all want different kinds of change.<br><br>    ',0,1752,183,0,'129.37.148.162','en'),(18529,3053,'2002-10-04 07:13:02',17,'austin','is that a camo fishing vest?',2,28,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18530,3054,'2002-10-04 07:23:28',174,'Resplendent Paralysis','I don\\\'t get it. Mechanical and biological systems are both systems, both have limits, are both understandable (to a degree) and both break down. I\\\'m sure there are good reasons to prefer one over the other, depending on the context, but you don\\\'t point out any significant differences. ',6,295,0,0,'24.189.13.22','en'),(18531,3053,'2002-10-04 07:40:35',175,'Paul G','<i>\\\"...I am about to stroke on my left side.\\\"</i><br>\r\nOh yeah.',0,69,93,0,'67.195.79.214','en'),(18532,3053,'2002-10-04 16:31:38',122,'hool','that is indeed a camo fishing vest / life jacket. ',0,50,17,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(18533,3053,'2002-10-04 17:02:24',123,'dcc','but, what if scapegoat wants to GO backwards. ever think of THAT, huh?! HUH?!',3,77,93,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(18534,3053,'2002-10-04 18:57:55',65,'le','hehe, stroke',0,12,0,0,'24.193.96.217','en'),(18535,3052,'2002-10-04 20:49:01',370,'seanthecon','most people share the same views of what they would like the world to look like. you\\\'d be hard pressed to find anyone voice an opinion against individual freedoms and equality. however, everyone tends to have entirely different perspectives on how they wish these goals to be reached. the rights and freedoms of the individual amongst the collective with always clash. if two different roads lead to the same place, then is there a problem? however, the problem i see with indiviualism is that the individual refuses to recognizes this same inherent right in others. democracy does not work in large numbers. there will always be losers. to impose a single inevitably results in conflict. ',0,692,0,0,'209.139.209.202','en'),(18536,3052,'2002-10-04 20:54:44',370,'seanthecon','glad to see you free from your burden. hopefully some time away will help sober you up and i hope you have better luck when you enter your next portal. i also hope you stole a great deal of office supplies before you left, maybe even a few movies. right before i left iraq as chief weapons inspector in 1998 i managed to sneak out my share of weapons of mass destruction and some iraqi porno mags. lots of ankle to ankle pics. the mags now sit ontop of scud missle that i use as a coffee table in my living room.',0,512,176,0,'209.139.209.202','en'),(18537,3053,'2002-10-04 21:00:50',370,'seanthecon','i think this photo pretty much sums corey up. ',0,46,0,0,'209.139.209.202','en'),(18538,3056,'2002-10-04 21:08:22',370,'seanthecon','funny, i believe i am a walking corpse. alive in my mind, but dead to the world. you should have thrown around a few tabernacs and zut alors.',0,141,0,0,'209.139.209.202','en'),(18539,3053,'2002-10-04 21:52:02',66,'Peter','<br><tt>word. the junglist life-vest.</tt>',1,42,122,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18540,3053,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','hool is a woman!?',6,17,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18541,3053,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','or is this about manlove?',6,25,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18542,3024,'2002-10-04 22:44:32',411,'ZorK','You need a vacation!!',0,21,0,0,'66.178.34.163','en'),(18543,3053,'2002-10-04 23:05:38',122,'hool','i am a manboy, fourth dan.  this was more about deity worship to thank the roommate-god for providing a bountiful harvest.',0,122,62,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(18544,3053,'2002-10-04 23:32:33',366,'eunoia','Great. Another white devil I have to pray to. Whatever happened to great coloured gods like James Brown,Gaundhi Mohammed Ali, Magic Johnson, and Richard Pryor?\r\n\r\nIf they were in a canoe that was sinkin\\\' who would get the only camo lifevest?\r\n',0,246,0,0,'193.167.219.100','en'),(18545,3053,'2002-10-04 23:39:06',366,'eunoia','<p>Bring back Aresenio Hall, put him in a blowup raft against this white kid, and I bet he\\\'d beat the white kid to the luke warm kraft dinner at the edge of the lawn.</p> <p>I think he\\\'d win because boxcut always beats crew cut 9X out of 10.</p>',0,251,0,0,'193.167.219.100','en'),(18546,3053,'2002-10-04 23:58:10',370,'seanthecon','great point my brother. white people smell like sour milk. is that why you returned to the motherland of finland? i don\\\'t know if you can buy any outs, but eunoia, you certinaly are good at selling it. \r\n\r\n\\\"yo kish, you go to africa? naw, japan.\\\"',0,255,366,0,'209.139.209.202','en'),(18547,3053,'2002-10-05 00:09:06',366,'eunoia','<p>now my brother, I would like to clarify your posudinous mistake. I be livin in Finnland, not Finland, which used to be called Zaire no Congo no Zaire by the white man. As the new ruler in this democratic republic autonomously controlled by my oil and diamond conglomerate, backed my US arms support, I do say I keep it real --as real as the ivory false tooth I capped in gold with a diamond tip that sits in my beautifu pink gums. You know why they pink. Because I floss. Do you floss my brother?</p>',0,503,0,0,'193.167.219.100','en'),(18548,3052,'2002-10-05 00:39:30',366,'eunoia','<p>What you need to do is run a low density cooperative commune where you live work and be free as the white anglo saxon male that you are (armed to the tits if you like).</p> \r\n<p>\r\nI think it is sad to hear about a person who spent thousands of dollars on a social sciences degree and finds that his own critical thinking has backfired on him and he shuns his pluralist education.</p> \r\n<p>\r\nI suggest that thinking about work and its implications on your own critical mind can be sustituted by simply towing the party line and taking more value in community. My brother has a big money, cushy private sector job as a  financial officer for a successful hitech firm. But, when I talk to him we never talk about business unless I instigate. He talks about his kids,\\\"the wife,\\\" community soccer he coaches and his other dreams to be a better Catholic and maybe even volunteer doing social work.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>His work is not his life. I think us kids who freshly turned into adults still believe in this idea of work being a vocation, something that will represent us and our values. Really, you can only hope your company isn\\\'t setting up a military dictatorship in Africa, or has some pay equity mandate. I propose that instead of integrating work with quasi spiritual sense of community, we devalue work\\\'s place in our hearts and look towards our soon to be born children and our communities. We need more civic action and less armchair politiking.</p>    ',3,1467,0,0,'193.167.219.100','en'),(18549,3050,'2002-10-05 01:11:15',366,'eunoia','<p>Hey hey say what you will about Canada\\\'s foreign policy, but don\\\'t fuck with our economy. We still have one percent growth to our GDP minus social services. America\\\'s economy is in negative growth stage and risking Japanese style deflation. We have a low dollar which makes Americans eat up all our exports. America is now trying to focus in on a war, as true red-blooded americans and try not to think about all the shit they buy on credit. Americans have no foresight when it comes to their appetite for spending. Bush made a fucking tax cut which nearly half of you fuckers voted him in for, which will soon be repealed. Yeah war is great thing to think about when your savings plans are in the shitter and your interest rates are going up. Mortage reevaluation is around the corner, have fun digging in your pockets broke ass imperialist scum.</p> ',6,864,0,0,'193.167.219.100','en'),(18550,3050,'2002-10-05 02:50:01',66,'Peter','<br><tt>generalizations <i>rule</i>.</tt>',1,41,366,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18551,3050,'2002-10-05 03:47:58',175,'Paul G','<p>\\\"appetite for spending\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"<b>shit</b> on credit\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"Americans <i>eat</i> exports\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"in the <i>shit</i>ter\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"digging in pockets\\\"<br>\r\nAmericans stop <b>eating</b>.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>Canada</i> has surplus.<br>\r\nLabor is cut.<br>\r\n<b>Canadians</b> stop eating.</p>',0,311,366,0,'66.19.49.58','en'),(18552,3050,'2002-10-05 15:38:44',366,'eunoia','tabarnac, nous canadienes need no-thing from you americans. we live on poutine, ces\\\'t ca. and dans Quebec we import no cheez kurds we drive to work in yellow school bus if we half to and if we\\\'re desperate we will sell much water power et celine dion re-kurds to greedy new yorkurs, pourquoi pas? nous are all integrated, wit eachodder.',0,342,0,0,'193.167.219.100','en'),(18553,3053,'2002-10-05 17:26:20',175,'Paul G','This is, actually, what all Canadians look like prior to undergoing the metamorphosis from the pupal to the adult form.<br>\r\nSoon, Scapegoat will shed his canoe, and then crawl about looking for moose meat, rye, and canola oil.',0,227,0,0,'66.19.124.138','en'),(18554,3058,'2002-10-05 22:09:50',66,'Peter','<br><tt>hi. did you know you live in my neighborhood?</tt>',1,58,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18555,3058,'2002-10-05 22:30:32',210,'may','no! i live next to the project on 63rd.',0,39,66,0,'12.88.86.159','en'),(18556,3058,'2002-10-06 00:38:42',66,'Peter','<br><tt>and pretty near lincoln center ;)</tt>',1,47,210,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18557,3058,'2002-10-06 03:32:55',17,'austin','when harry met sally is on the tv.  the panorama of central park covered in snow makes me want to move to nyc.',3,110,0,0,'64.174.155.195','en'),(18558,3058,'2002-10-06 03:51:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>please do, man. you can live in me and sine\'s basement and eat doughnuts with us.</tt>',1,91,17,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18559,3053,'2002-10-06 04:16:14',176,'Scapegoat','I would like to state for the record that I have already reached the rye stage of my development and I usually like it mixed 50-50 with ginger ale. ',0,148,0,0,'65.95.140.65','en'),(18560,3050,'2002-10-06 04:21:40',176,'Scapegoat','Out of curiosity if one of you Americans were asked to describe Canadians to a third party what would you say?',0,110,0,0,'65.95.140.65','en'),(18561,3058,'2002-10-06 04:43:48',17,'austin','i <i>am</i> a basement dweller.',0,31,0,0,'64.174.154.182','en'),(18562,3058,'2002-10-06 06:12:06',66,'Peter','<br><tt>great. ill bring the doughnuts.</tt>',1,44,17,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18563,3061,'2002-10-06 07:15:23',176,'Scapegoat','They were the first live show I saw upon moving to Toronto. Damn sweet. ',0,72,0,0,'65.95.140.118','en'),(18564,3053,'2002-10-06 10:46:19',93,'chiamattt','I dont believe in a god. shiticus, errr rather shanicus believes in the god of dick and pussaaaay!\r\n<br>\r\nI on he other hand believe in no god. together we are one. alone youve just won(\\\\).\r\n<br>\r\nand to be serious. that picture of corey is recent. to the untrained eye it looks like the face of a baby, but to the professional corey watcher you\\\'d know this fine specimine was at least 24 years of age. \r\n<br>\r\nshane just said he thought canada should build \\\"just two aircraft carriers, a support fleet, and some nuclear submarines. send them somewhere no one cares about and start some shit\\\"\r\ncrazy nutter\r\n<br>\r\nsaw goldie at ritz carleton hotel. he got into a scuffle with a GI. it was classic. more later. ',1,722,0,0,'61.37.53.25','en'),(18565,3050,'2002-10-06 11:10:44',93,'chiamattt','a few americans like to always talk about how \\\'negative\\\' canadians are towards america. its because they only really know of the negatives spouted by canadians because they rarely want to know about the other things canadians think about. canadians know a lot about america because there is a lot to know. its everywhere. a canadian when asked of something in africa would probably generalize as much as americans do about canadians. RED WHITE AND when the war starts BLUE!!! ',2,482,176,0,'61.37.53.25','en'),(18566,3058,'2002-10-06 12:24:03',240,'xep','i bought a plane ticket to new york today.  this is the furthest in advance i\\\'ve ever bought one.  can we meet at new years, peter and sine?',0,143,0,0,'64.81.241.111','en'),(18567,3061,'2002-10-06 15:41:31',122,'hool','<p>i saw them in ottawa and they were terrible.  i walked out halfway through the show.  i found the vocals and lyrics particularly awful and contrived.</p>\r\n<p><i>\\\"music made by and for angry people who also like paintings and flowers and strolls in the park\\\"</i></p>\r\n<p>this can be done very well, or so over indulgently that i grate my teeth and shudder at every strong note.  i experienced the latter, as did my companion for the show.  we were both relieved to leave the club. i\\\'d give them a second chance, but only if it were free.</p>\r\n',0,554,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(18568,3058,'2002-10-06 15:55:20',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yes please.</tt>',1,24,240,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18569,3058,'2002-10-06 17:54:43',210,'may','i wish my apartment wasn\\\'t so expensive. and small. but new york city is definitely still worth it.',0,102,66,0,'12.88.85.55','en'),(18570,3053,'2002-10-06 18:23:15',366,'eunoia','Goldie?! What\\\'s he doing there and what are you doing going to his show. If he is who I think he is then he is all pussy jungle and should be stopped by all law enforcement.',0,176,93,0,'193.167.219.100','en'),(18571,3054,'2002-10-06 19:22:02',176,'Scapegoat','I agree with resplendent.',0,25,0,0,'65.95.140.118','en'),(18572,3056,'2002-10-07 07:33:07',409,'rachel','i\\\'ve heard battle royale is great; let me know what you think.',1,65,0,0,'207.214.90.66','en'),(18573,3058,'2002-10-07 14:45:09',17,'austin','<P>this is a nice peice.  my mom is a teacher, and my roomate is a teacher, all his friends are teachers.  i understand it is an involving profession.  my mom does speach therapy, and i remember when she was working in dallas she would have the most funny, and most sad stories to tell.  suicide in the girls gym shower.  three hundred pound transvestite with unintelligible lisp.  truck stolen while loading files.  she had a real hard time there.  i have no idea what my reaction would be.  a person i met that taught in east austin had a gun put to his head when he asked a person what they were doing in the hall way of the school.  all this reminds me how difficult education is as an experience for the kids, for that matter.</P><P><i>nice supportive</i></P>\r\n',2,766,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18574,3058,'2002-10-07 22:04:20',210,'may','thank you for the kind comment. i love my job.\r\n\r\nbut it\\\'s 6 pm, i\\\'ve been out of the apartment for over 12 hours and i have 110 papers to grade tonight.\r\n\r\nat least i wasn\\\'t the teacher who got spat upon today.\r\n\r\n',0,224,17,0,'12.88.87.55','en'),(18575,3058,'2002-10-07 22:34:02',17,'austin','<BR><TT>:(</TT><BR>\r\nif you ever want a sweet teaching job in lovely santa cruz at a state funded charter school for intelligent kids, let me know.  i could probably hook you up.  the weather is nice.\r\n</P>',2,206,210,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18576,3058,'2002-10-07 22:47:14',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i gotta vouch for austin. i just got back from a month in cali, and the weather here in manhattan is despicable, comparitively. especially today. gross and gray. no bike ride for me, thanks manhattan.</tt>',1,213,210,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18577,3058,'2002-10-07 22:57:59',210,'may','santa cruz sounds lovely. i am sick of the blah.\r\n\r\nin the meantime, i\\\'m looking for a coffee partner. caffeine is definitely necessary to grade these papers and write a 5-pager of my own for linguistics class tomorrow. \r\n\r\nhope everyone else\\\'s night is more relaxing than mine.',0,284,0,0,'12.88.87.55','en'),(18578,3064,'2002-10-08 01:20:52',17,'Leonard DiCaprio','I just missed you.',3,10,0,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(18579,3058,'2002-10-08 01:46:53',17,'austin','down with blah.',3,15,210,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18580,1653,'2002-10-08 01:55:35',17,'austin','Just a final note about that toothbrush.  It is still where it was at the time of this writing.  Now it is something of an attraction.  People come to San Francisco, and want to use my toothbrush.  That toothbrush is now encrusted in a cake of toothpaste.  Apparently the last person to use it (kyles?) was one of the kind of people that do not wash the toothbrush when they are done.  That grosses me out, and consequently I will never use it again.',6,450,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18581,1653,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','ew, that\\\'s gross.\r\n\r\nfor the record, i am a rinser.  i am sure austie thoroughly checked out my toof brush to make sure i wasn\\\'t the culprit :)',6,149,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18582,1653,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','also i have my own toothbrush now.',6,34,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18583,1653,'2002-10-08 14:10:31',66,'Peter','<br><tt>he gots toothbrushes in different area codez!<br><br>im a fan of the <i>sonicare</i> lately. so nice.</tt>',1,114,0,0,'204.168.247.128','en'),(18584,3065,'2002-10-08 21:02:31',195,'matt','we cry for ourselves, mostly',1,28,0,0,'209.208.108.4','en'),(18585,1653,'2002-10-09 00:57:05',371,'Kyla','Excuuuuse me, but I brought my own toothbrush to SF.  Why would I want to use Austin\\\'s toothpaste encrusted one?  Ew!',6,120,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18586,1653,'2002-10-09 01:05:35',17,'austin','SOMEBODY encrusted my toothbrush and it wasnt me.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nwho was it?!  fess up!',4,83,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18587,1653,'2002-10-09 01:34:44',66,'Peter','<br><tt>if youre the one that framed me and sine for leaving a tank of pee on the playa, its karma getting you back, muahahaha!<br><br>someone in our camp actually left tons of piss behind. erk.</tt>',1,199,371,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18588,1653,'2002-10-09 10:35:09',59,'s5','oh fuck, it might have been me.',4,31,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18589,3063,'2002-10-10 00:44:36',174,'Gestating Enterprise','I usually don\\\'t read the longer swinney pieces, but this here is a fine story, son. Good flow, good description, good choice of character and setting, and it gives some interesting concepts to think about. I only have one suggestion: don\\\'t really say how the father died. The description of him on the porch is excellent, but it\\\'s sort of ruined by explaining what happened. Just describe and leave it at that, I say. Oh yeah, and is the Barrington/Harrington thing intentional?',1,487,0,0,'24.189.13.22','en'),(18590,3063,'2002-10-10 02:01:46',17,'austin','i\\\'m saving this one for sunday morning.',2,42,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18591,3066,'2002-10-10 04:14:48',123,'dcc','wonderful use of style. poetic prose is something i greatly admire and have been challenged by.\r\n<p><i>gruesome grime<i></p>\r\n<p>love it!!!',2,139,0,0,'24.101.118.76','en'),(18592,3066,'2002-10-10 04:16:44',175,'Paul G','This has the rhythm, or cadence, of a piece of poetry delivered orally - at a slam, or something.  The imagery is the same type that I would expect to encounter in that setting too.  The whole thing is raw, and strong; and I like it.  It doesn\\\'t lead from point A to point B; it isn\\\'t bound by continuity.  And that\\\'s ok.',1,330,0,0,'67.195.182.197','en'),(18593,3058,'2002-10-10 18:28:04',370,'seanthecon','the bridge is over.',0,19,0,0,'209.52.190.49','en'),(18594,3058,'2002-10-10 18:49:21',66,'Peter','<br><Tt>im just tryna find the bridge; has anyone seen the bridge? wheres that confounded bridge.</tt>',1,102,370,0,'204.168.247.128','en'),(18595,3063,'2002-10-10 21:57:20',377,'dammit','dude. that is a good suggestion. i shall implement it.',0,54,174,0,'66.122.107.46','en'),(18596,1653,'2002-10-10 22:27:21',261,'sine','CRUST PUNX~!!!',1,14,0,0,'152.163.189.132','en'),(18597,3066,'2002-10-11 15:21:02',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\n2 is a number common in the life of seanthecon.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI lived with seanthecon for two years. from heaven to hell. each year contrasted the other. if i hadnt left when i did, id have knife fucked him to bits and scattered his remains in the ottawa river\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen me and sean toured the west coast spreading the socialist word, we fucked behind the volkswagon minibus thing and he only lasted two minutes. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nregardless of his endurance, i was glad he didnt last longer. his dick is only two inches...from the ground. *ouch*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthough i often stood over sean when he was asleep with a kitchen knife just to see how long i could do it before paranoia of him waking up overtook anger, i still love the kid.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\noh yeah. i met a girl.\r\n</p>',6,781,0,0,'61.37.53.25','en'),(18598,3071,'2002-10-11 22:56:48',66,'Peter','<br><tt>heh. excellent.</tt>',1,28,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18599,3071,'2002-10-12 02:22:34',57,'weeb','\r\ni. fucking. hate. fleet. week.\r\n\r\n<i>burn! burn!</i>',6,54,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(18600,3071,'2002-10-12 03:08:04',59,'s5','i hate fleet week. i feel like we\\\'re under attack. maybe the bullhorn will come in handy.',0,92,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18601,3071,'2002-10-12 04:29:02',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>nice art car...</i></tt>',1,35,59,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18602,3071,'2003-05-10 16:09:14',79,'yeti','Fleet week is fun. The idea that there are these men in the pinnacle of Avation technology (circa 1979?) hauling motherfucking ass above my head is awesome. I wish I could be up there with them. I wish we could replace all motorcycles that wake me up at night with year \\\'round F-16\\\'s overhead during the day. Tear my ass up, you big blue bastards, I love every minute! Imagine!',1,383,0,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(18603,3071,'2002-10-12 07:04:17',93,'chiamattt','id have to say that those men are at the pinncacle of killing technology. Its not really about flight anymore. war war war. ',0,124,17,0,'61.37.53.56','en'),(18604,3071,'2002-10-12 08:02:58',17,'austin','really only one thing occurs to me when i watch them flying around the city.  this is what it is like to live in afganistan.',6,124,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18605,3070,'2002-10-12 09:41:33',409,'rachel','i very much like the last paragraph. i sometimes feel like that too, especially as the number of people i can handle in one place at one time dwindles. it makes me feel as though my senses are becoming both hypersensitive and deadened at the same time as i age.',2,261,0,0,'207.214.90.66','en'),(18606,3072,'2002-10-12 16:58:00',82,'simon','hi gus. i love the romance.',0,27,0,0,'216.175.90.224','en'),(18607,3071,'2003-05-10 16:09:14',79,'yeti','I sort of agree with you, chia. But the airplane is only as deadly as what\\\'s attached to it. Passenger jets deliver people, crop dusters deliver pesticides, etc. The Blue Angels are using jets designed for the military, true, but they arent equipped to fuck shit up as they fly over San Francisco. \r\n\r\nI recently read a coffee table book called Robosapiens. It had page after page of interviews with various designers and creators of robots from around the world. Many of them were working under DoD, DoE, Navy, Air Force, etc. contracts or funding. Those guys wanted to use the technology for surveillance, finding mines, and a plethora of other things. What stuck out to me was that the researchers were working on some amazing technology, and most of them were okay with working for the government, as long as they could go public with their work. They were clearly in it for the technology. Many people were concerned that their devices would be used for harm, but were emphasizing that the devices could also be used for the good of the human race. I think the exception to that was the company who had made a robotic, remotely controlled rifle. \r\n\r\nAnyhow, I\\\'m rambling. Good morning! The point I was making is that most technology can be used for harm. Most of that technology can be used for good things too.\r\n\r\nBest friendly! (And hellbent on breaking the \\\"It will be a cold day in hell when I mix with you people.\\\" barrier!)',1,1446,93,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(18608,3066,'2002-10-12 19:11:37',370,'seanthecon','i have no idea who you are. please stop emailing me pictures of you blowing goats.',0,82,93,0,'209.52.190.36','en'),(18609,3066,'2002-10-12 19:12:30',370,'seanthecon','wait a minute. son?',0,19,93,0,'209.52.190.36','en'),(18610,3072,'2002-10-12 19:51:45',175,'Paul G','Yeah, this is beautiful writing: subtle, delicate, well-crafted.  And it compliments the romantic action of the characters.  So good. ',1,134,0,0,'66.19.125.144','en'),(18611,3071,'2002-10-12 23:00:25',57,'weeb','yeah but useful technology can still be annoying as heck.\r\n<br><br>\r\nand if you came over to hang out in a fighter plane, you better give me a ride in it, yo.',1,158,17,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(18612,3071,'2002-10-13 03:42:52',65,'le','i thoroughly enjoyed fleet week. i used to hate it but i hate little these days, and i always secretly liked it anyway. ',4,120,0,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(18613,3073,'2002-10-13 07:39:31',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i drove through nebraska twice last month. this story really reminds me of alot of the folks i met in nebraska, and their landscape, for some reason.</tt>',1,162,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18614,3049,'2002-10-13 21:40:56',195,'matt','this would have been better as prose, maybe.<BR><BR>i forgot to tell you about paige and i playing thumb war. there\\\'s a song that goes with it that must be relatively new: \\\"one, two, three, four. i declare thumb war. five, six, seven, eight. try to keep your thumb straight.\\\"',1,284,0,0,'209.208.108.172','en'),(18615,3023,'2002-10-13 21:42:20',195,'matt','channelling hemingway, again.',1,29,0,0,'209.208.108.172','en'),(18616,2971,'2002-10-13 21:44:15',195,'matt','this one was really well-received. i always wonder what makes some articles garner more and better comments while others don\\\'t. one of the great mysteries of our time, i guess.',1,179,0,0,'209.208.108.172','en'),(18617,2956,'2002-10-13 21:48:12',195,'matt','the book decided it wanted to be a screenplay, which it started life as. i was having problems working out the plot, so i started writing it as a novel. as soon as i untangled it, it started coming out in screenplay format, which is a lot easier than novel-ing.<BR><BR>also, johnny says, \\\"shawn mullins, my ass.\\\"',1,318,0,0,'209.208.108.172','en'),(18618,2932,'2002-10-13 21:50:22',195,'matt','the love affair with christianity has cooled considerably. i found the baptists kind of crazy. started going to the methodist church. haven\\\'t been in over a month.',1,166,0,0,'209.208.108.172','en'),(18619,3071,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i hate the jet planes roaring overhead.  it must cost a considerable amount and have a considerable environmental impact to be burning so much jet fuel. not to mention when it wakes me up i feel like the sky is falling.  they are beautiful, and it is cool that they can fly in formation, but kites can to that too and they\\\'re much less terrifying.',6,350,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18620,3071,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','god i really am turning into an old married woman.  KVETCH!!',6,60,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18621,3071,'2002-10-14 02:43:17',59,'s5','<p>\r\nthe dalai lama says:\r\n</p><p>\r\n\\\"The unfortunate truth is that we are conditioned to regard warfare as something exciting and even glamorous: the soldiers in smart uniforms (so attractive to children) with their military bands playing alongside them. We see murder as dreadful, but there is no association of war with criminality. On the contrary, it is seen as an opportunity for people to prove their competence and courage. We speak of the heros it produces, almost as if the greater the number killed, the more heroic the individual.\r\n</p><p>\r\nAnd we talk about this or that weapon as a marvelous piece of technology, forgetting that when it is used it will actually maim and murder living people. Your friend, my friend, our mothers, our fathers, our sisters and brothers, you and me.\\\"\r\n</p>',5,806,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18622,3071,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i want to fuck the dalai lama.\r\n\r\nKIDDING!',6,42,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18623,3071,'2002-10-14 05:28:12',176,'Scapegoat','All technology is simply instruments used to carry out human will.<br><br>\r\nI\\\'d like to bring the dalai lama to a Remembrance Day (or Veteran\\\'s Day in the US - I think) Ceremony with me an see if he still thinks that we view warfare as glamourous. They have always been very solemn and depressing events for me. The reason heros are created out of war is because to be able to deal with the existence and fallout of war any sane person needs to find something redeeming in it. For some of us it would be too harsh a reality to deal with, otherwise. Maybe war is different were the dalai lama comes from.<br><br>\r\nI think the heaviness of war is that we don\\\'t forget that when we use certain marvelous pieces of technology that human lives are lost. Isn\\\'t this why war sucks and we aren\\\'t always trying to start it? <br><br>\r\nThe technology is also used in attempts to make sure fathers, sisters, mothers, and brothers might come back to you.<br><br>\r\nI know I like seeing this technology and feeling secure that it functions because when my Father is sent out in his unarmed transport to drop off humanitarian aid. I like to know that some of this \\\'killing technology\\\' has been used ahead of time to up the chances that he comes back and doesn\\\'t run into any problems. Selfish of me maybe but I won\\\'t apologize for it.<br><br>\r\nI also imagine that enduring an air show is probably a long way from living in a country that has endured war for the last decade or two.<br><br>\r\nDon\\\'t mean to be overly sensitive about this, just expressing my perspective.<br><br>\r\n ',0,1593,0,0,'129.37.148.36','en'),(18624,3052,'2002-10-14 05:33:00',176,'Scapegoat','I don\\\'t shun pluralism.',0,26,0,0,'129.37.148.36','en'),(18625,3071,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','the dalai lama travels a lot.  i am sure he has seen many types of war and many instances of war remembrance in his time.\r\n\r\nthe fact that anyone actually wants to remember war on one day every single year validates the dalai lama\\\'s point in my opinion.',6,256,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18626,3071,'2002-10-14 06:42:26',59,'s5','<p>\r\nscapegoat, i agree that enduring an airshow is a long way from what others have endured for decades.\r\n</p><p>\r\nand on the same week that pretty blue jet fighters flew over my city, while onlookers marveled over their destructive potential as \\\"beautiful\\\", congress voted on and approved war plans, to send more of the same jets, to fly over another city, much further away from san francisco. where they will spread fear and suffering. this irony was not lost on me.\r\n</p><p>\r\nyet through all of this, there was not one protest of war. simply smiling, drooling fans, oohing and ahhing, waving on the machines of killing, as they forced their celebration on those, like me, who are deeply disturbed by their use and what they represent.\r\n</p><p>\r\nif there are to be air shows, i see no reason why they should disrupt an entire city for a week, and distress those who so adamently oppose war and killing.\r\n</p><p>\r\nso, fuck fleet week.\r\n</p>',6,949,176,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18627,3071,'2002-10-14 07:26:28',176,'Scapegoat','<b>To o</b> the idea is not to remember war but to remember those lost and sacrifices made by people involved.<br><br>\r\n<b>To s5</b> ok fuck fleet week I\\\'m not in San Fran so I can\\\'t really say. I\\\'m glad that you oppose war and killing - I agree with you 99% of the time. Not being an American maybe I\\\'m not as distressed by the recent congressional development. What I see though is a desire to get rid of a regime that the West uses as justification for sanctions, that have killed four times as many (some estimates say as many as 2 million) Iraqi people then the initial Gulf War (and that\\\'s using Iraqi casualty stats for the war approx. 300,000). If the war plans inflict more casualties then the sanctions then the blood will be on the minds and hands of people like myself who hope for some sort of change. A new regime would leave no moral grounds for sanctions that everyday continue to kill innocent Iraqi civilians. It becomes a question of what you deplore more war or starvation both cause death.<br><br>\r\nI could give a shit about what the American administrations motivations are be they personal, security, or oil. If the regime is changed and the sanctions can be dropped then perhaps there might be some hope for stopping the Iraqi civilian death toll.<br><br>\r\nI don\\\'t want to be an ass or incite anger or negative feelings and it is still cold to look at this from a numbers perspective but when those numbers are human lives... <br><br>      ',0,1482,0,0,'129.37.148.47','en'),(18628,3071,'2002-10-14 07:48:30',59,'s5','it\\\'s okay, i just feel on edge after a week of jet fighters swooping over my house and looking like they\\\'re going to plow into downtown. :( :( i don\\\'t think you were inciting anything. after further thought, maybe it\\\'s good to have fleet week. otherwise the military is something you only see on the news. at least if it\\\'s out in the open, everyone\\\'s true feelings about war come to the surface.',4,413,176,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18629,3071,'2002-10-14 07:56:41',176,'Scapegoat','I agree that it would be better if this stuff was talked about a little more. In the current situation and just in general I think it is hard to talk about though cause it is such upsetting stuff. ',0,197,59,0,'129.37.148.47','en'),(18630,3071,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','homos.',6,6,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18631,3058,'2002-10-14 13:18:44',67,'trouble','being a HUGE old school hip-hop & BDP  fan in general, allow me to shed a little light on this.\r\n\r\n<p>actually \\\'the bridge\\\' refers to a track that MC Shan did.  MC Shan was from Queens and after his track \\\'the bridge\\\' blew up everybody was up on Queens as ground zero for hip-hop.  the mighty KRS-1 and Scott LaRock being from the South Bronx didn\\\'t take kindly to this and came out with \\\'the bridge is over\\\' to diss.  some say it was the first great hip-hop fued.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>this concludes the lesson for today.',1,534,66,0,'63.70.119.94','en'),(18632,3058,'2002-10-14 15:16:44',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yeah. i was just quoting zeppelin.</tt>',1,47,67,0,'204.168.247.128','en'),(18633,1946,'2002-10-14 18:08:37',86,'egg','Ive just been reading over this and it strikes me as really silly. I never applied to grad school last year. But Im leading my life by the question: \\\"is this any better than grad school?\\\" it is a trial.',0,208,0,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(18634,2972,'2002-10-14 18:40:55',195,'matt','remember to breathe',1,19,0,0,'209.208.110.31','en'),(18635,3076,'2002-10-15 03:50:38',67,'trouble','for whatever reason i see this opportunity as an exciting one.  best of luck to you, i\\\'ll be looking forward to the next class.',1,130,0,0,'67.243.188.127','en'),(18636,3076,'2002-10-15 04:25:52',176,'Scapegoat','I agree with trouble this sounds like it would be an interesting and exciting path. I just imagine all the wacked out weird things that you might see. It\\\'ll make amazing stories, my friend Mohammed drives a taxi and whenever I run into him he always has a bunch of them. Taxi driving & bartending are both good professions for this sort of thing. ',0,350,0,0,'65.95.157.46','en'),(18637,3074,'2002-10-15 04:45:58',176,'Scapegoat','Make sure you call me when you are back this xmas, I want to go for a beer. I feel like all my swinney comments lately are related to Iraq, etc.<br><br>\r\nGun control arguments are different in that the right of an individual to security or arms (pick your side) is distinctly different from the rights of the state.<br><br>\r\nInternational law is trampled on by everyone because law is something that is made by the state and <i>enforced</i> by the state\\\'s use of <i>force</i>. Last time I checked we didn\\\'t have a global state and the UN is a far cry from it they have now physical force with which to enforce things. Thus International Law is still dependent on everyones good will the only area of International curiously enough that tends to have some sort of weight behind it is economic, not human rights (because human rights are so often cosidered culturally relative in the international community) To propose human rights as non relative is labelled as imperialistic.<br><br>\r\nAs for the biblicization of the American Constitution last I checked the majority of people hadn\\\'t biblicized it. Isn\\\'t this why there has been so much opposition to the US in the UN lately?<br><br>\r\nThe funny thing about rights is that historically they usually aren\\\'t something that is just granted they are usually taken or fought for sometimes politically sometimes by force. So, as far as one regime having the right to change another...<br><br>',0,1451,0,0,'65.95.157.46','en'),(18638,3073,'2002-10-15 08:32:16',175,'Paul G','<p>I liked the story a lot: it was really tight, and I laughed in a way that made me wonder if I ought to be laughing at all.</p>\r\n<p>It reminded me of a good, Irish short story that approached life and death in a way both comical and touching.</p>\r\n<p>I thought of <a href=\\\"http://bedfordbooks.com/litlinks/fiction/froconnor.htm\\\">Frank</a> <a href=\\\"http://members.aol.com/litpage/frankpref.html\\\">O\\\'Connor</a>.</p>',0,429,0,0,'66.19.39.154','en'),(18639,3074,'2002-10-15 12:28:57',65,'le','is this article about the girl, or about war? or are these things somehow related?',4,82,0,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(18640,3074,'2002-10-15 13:43:48',93,'chiamattt','just things on my mind. ',0,24,65,0,'61.37.53.25','en'),(18641,3077,'2002-10-15 17:04:38',82,'simon','hmm',0,3,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18642,3071,'2002-10-15 19:00:15',17,'GI Jane','<img src=\\\"http://www.trashy.com/store2/halloween/images/1999/P05FN.jpg\\\">',6,78,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18643,3077,'2002-10-15 19:40:47',123,'dcc','<p><i>\\\"the coconut trees, the tropical breeze\\\"</i>\r\n<p>i\\\'m going to be saying that in my head over and over again all day. it just rolls off the err ... tongue... mind tongue? *ick*',3,190,0,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(18644,3076,'2002-10-16 00:21:18',370,'seanthecon','interesting and disturbing predicament. returning to where your grandparents started isn\\\'t exactly inpirational, but it does seem that you are somewhere else. it is more important to have self respect and integrity than to simply conform. it is also couragous to reject what could come easily. i hope you find what you are looking for. scapegoat is right, this should expose you to a new side of humanity. maybe you could be like michael moore and refuse to pick up white people.',0,482,0,0,'209.53.180.72','en'),(18645,3072,'2002-10-16 15:56:27',17,'austin','mushy! what a cute  bit.',1,24,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18646,1076,'2002-10-16 18:08:18',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i really like this post; i was happy to find and read it again. it reminds me of what seems like a <i>really</i> long time ago.</tt>',1,140,0,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(18647,3080,'2002-10-16 19:23:42',148,'jeeff','that was an excellent read.',0,27,0,0,'24.43.239.60','en'),(18648,3080,'2002-10-16 19:47:48',123,'dcc','amazing, as always. i loved the flashback memory use. simply fantastic. it left me feeling hollow inside, wishing for things that could be, but never will. a snippet of a greater story i feel i can relate to.',2,208,0,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(18649,927,'2002-10-16 19:48:22',74,'jo','i came back to this thread because i\\\'m looking for a specific interview with dave eggers and i was seeing if elly had linked to it from swinney, but she doesn\\\'t seem to have, but LISTEN: i need to know why people think that zorba the greek is a good book. my best friend just finished it after someone had enthusiastically recommended it to her and she <b>loathed</b> it. so what\\\'s the deal? discuss.',0,409,0,0,'146.6.172.96','en'),(18650,927,'2002-10-16 20:01:25',74,'jo','elly did link, but the link is now broken because the harvard advocate is undergoing revisions. here is an active link to the <a href=\\\"http://www.armchairnews.com/freelance/eggers.html\\\">same interview</a> if anyone is interested.',0,235,0,0,'146.6.172.96','en'),(18651,3082,'2002-10-16 20:14:17',195,'matt','you\\\'d be obese, with 10 kids, and the proprietor of Suck Creek Bar-B-Cue.',1,76,0,0,'209.208.108.209','en'),(18652,3082,'2002-10-17 02:05:26',175,'Paul G','<p><i>\\\"time passes fast nowadays\\\"</i><br>\r\nYeah, that was my thought: maybe the manner in which time had passed/was passing would be the chief difference.  In NY you are in flux.  In TN things seem very much more static; the rate of change is different.</p>  \r\n<p>The story that you told, here, was itself a nice change of pace: it was slower, and had a gentler feel to it - like TN, I guess.  It was refreshing.</p>',0,422,0,0,'66.19.50.68','en'),(18653,3082,'2002-10-17 02:55:39',66,'Peter','<br><tt>for the past couple of years, ive been preoccupid with my cosmopolitan (hah) life in the city; im thinking i might like to write a few pieces about what it was like living in the middle of nowhere; there are some truly odd stories, indeed.</tt>',1,252,175,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18654,3082,'2002-10-17 03:00:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>i just discovered this, taken of me on the block behind the <i>golden gallon</i> a few years back:</i><br><br><img src=\\\"http://saturn5.com/~peter/album/banal/dada.jpg\\\"></tt>',1,190,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18655,3077,'2002-10-17 08:47:05',414,'zarathustra','<p>ok so the best part about your being a limbless potato is that you could get people to pay you to let them roll you down hills and yell a lot.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>hm. maybe not. but damn.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>two of my good friends leave this weekend to spend a month in bali. i mean, no, lightning doesn\\\'t normally strike twice, but come on. i try and say \\\'go somewhere else!\\\' and they say \\\'but we already have the tickets!\\\'. crazy kids.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ps gold blazer-chains rule and eddie hirsch can tongue my nutz.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>lurve,</p> \r\nthe clandestine norwegian.',1,559,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(18656,3077,'2002-10-17 15:24:36',86,'egg','clandestine my ass. \r\n',0,22,414,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(18657,3077,'2002-10-17 18:01:36',414,'zarathustra','well yeah, not even norwegian really. but \\\'clandestine norwegian\\\', it\\\'s got some sort of ring to it? possibly possible. hammer time.',1,141,86,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(18658,3071,'2002-10-17 22:35:25',129,'moebius','well for what it\\\'s worth, my primary thought whilst watching the jet fighter fun & games last weekend was: <i>if we lived in a magical world where all jet fighters were for was putting on fancy entertainments like this...i\\\'d be down with that.</i><br><br>also, what we saw was nothing like what folks saw in, say, Afghanistan or, say, Serbia. because there, if you heard the sonic boom of jet planes, it generally meant they were already on their merry way out of the area, and you had better brace yourself for the explosion of whatever bombs they might\\\'ve launched as they passed by. ',3,595,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(18659,3081,'2002-10-17 22:42:50',75,'tyler','the coffee is hotter, causing the instantly melted sugar to seperate into its componets, some of which rise to the top . this process lowers the density of the coffee-sugar solution, making the sound of the spoon scraping the sides rise in pitch.',1,246,0,0,'165.247.164.127','en'),(18660,3084,'2002-10-18 01:42:15',65,'le','hey so was that postcard i got from spain from you?',0,51,0,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(18661,3071,'2002-10-18 05:09:18',17,'austin','<P>\r\nreally!  it brings it all home to think of the perspective of civilians in countries that are currently the target of ariel attacks.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nafter s5, i am all for the ability to allocate my tax dollars:\r\n</P>\r\n<PRE>\r\n[X] I want none of my dollars to go to\r\n    foriegn conflict, bombs, or war\r\n    machines.  I want those dollars \r\n    reallocated to emergency\r\n    preparedness and education.\r\n</PRE>\r\n<P>\r\nwe don\\\'t have that yet though.  but hey, this is california, cant we get a referendum or something?\r\n</P>',3,525,129,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18662,3082,'2002-10-18 08:44:24',82,'simon','once my friend cindy from asheville, nc drove me to johnson city, tn and we saw a band play in the drummer\\\'s mom\\\'s basement and danced and jumped around and someone broke a bottle in the bass player\\\'s eye but he was a good sport about it. we went to the emergency room. then we went out driving. it was nice.',0,317,0,0,'216.175.99.139','en'),(18663,3076,'2002-10-18 14:27:49',418,'Sp','<p>I live in Chicago.  And hoping that you make it past chapter nine, maybe one day I\\\'ll have a chat with you in your cab.</P>',1,129,0,0,'12.106.111.10','en'),(18664,3084,'2002-10-18 14:35:43',75,'tyler','no, sorry.',1,10,0,0,'165.247.164.127','en');
INSERT INTO `comments` VALUES (18665,3082,'2002-10-18 15:48:29',392,'p.','<p>when i was little, mom used to get gas at one of the three golden gallons of calhoun.  if i was with her i was guaranteed a coke or cherry coke slushie.  being ten in a small town with nothing to do, slushies with every fillup made me anticipate the car rides home from school when the needle came close enough to \\\'e.\\\' i\\\'d watch the gas gauge meaningfully while mom asked me how my day was and i\\\'d absent-mindedly say \\\"fine.\\\"  i\\\'d sit up so that my head rested on the passenger seat headrest and say, \\\"mom, it looks like we need gas.\\\"  she\\\'s smile at me with her knowing mom smile and nod.</p>\r\n<p>after i moved to georgia tech, coke slushies were impossible to find until krystal started carrying them.  with a krystal within walking distance of my west-campus dorm, i had that slushie machine at my disposal 24 hours a day, 7 days a week.  granted, the only time i really went to krystal was at 3 am, fairly intoxicated by one substance or another.  it was the substance that determined whether i was in the mood for a slushie or just a sackful.</p>\r\n<p>now, working and going to school in the vicinity of calhoun, the coke slushie machines are never working in any given golden gallon.  there is no krystal on my commmute to school or work, or in between the two.  i have resigned myself to the fruit-flavored slush puppie found at the favorite market between two carpet plants.</p>',0,1418,0,0,'12.13.167.78','en'),(18666,3082,'2002-10-18 22:15:40',66,'Peter','<br><tt>krystal! favorite market! golden gallon! heh. good memories. hey, if youre still around gt, at least you have <i>the varsity</i> no? go go frozen oranges!</tt>',1,167,392,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18667,3081,'2002-10-19 08:54:25',174,'Mad Moll','I wouldn\\\'t be worried about why the sound of the spoon changes pitch. I\\\'d be much more worried about WHY SOMEONE WOULD DRINK MICROWAVED DAY-OLD COFFEE. HARDFUCKINGCORE.',6,174,0,0,'24.189.13.22','en'),(18668,3085,'2002-10-19 13:55:47',66,'Peter','<br><tt>are you kidding? autumn in manhattan is simply beautiful. the city is full of golden trees and oange colors; at least in my neighborhood.</tt>',1,150,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18669,3085,'2002-10-19 16:51:37',210,'may','ok, phew. \r\ni\\\'ve yet to see it, but hopefully soon.',0,54,0,0,'12.88.88.141','en'),(18670,3085,'2002-10-19 16:51:41',210,'may','ok, phew. \r\ni\\\'ve yet to see it, but hopefully soon.',0,54,66,0,'12.88.88.141','en'),(18671,3087,'2002-10-19 17:48:34',65,'le','this is the best swinney i\\\'ve read in some time. thanks sims. i like drunken honesty',4,87,0,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(18672,3087,'2002-10-19 22:49:48',175,'Paul G','This was a good read.  After the first sentence, I was afraid that you were going to write about <a href=\\\"http://www.sfmoma.org/info/press/press_richter_07_05_02.html\\\">Gerhard</a> <a href=\\\"http://www.artcyclopedia.com/artists/richter_gerhard.html\\\">Richter</a> - but you didn\\\'t.  It was also good to see the word, \\\"love,\\\" being used [Yay for passion.]<br>\r\nYou have an interesting life; please write more. ',0,426,0,0,'66.19.37.186','en'),(18673,3087,'2002-10-20 00:07:06',57,'weeb','oh i see. so you didn\\\'t come and get some soup because you were TOO BUSY with your carousing and pillaging, too busy running around getting drunk and kissing girls. sniff!\r\n<br><br>\r\nyou are like living the movie star lifestyle. it\\\'s quite amazing, even from afar.',1,270,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(18674,3085,'2002-10-20 03:22:47',66,'Peter','<br><tt>word. i was down at the 14th street flea market today, and the place was swarmed with golden gingko leaves. so pretty.</tt>',1,131,210,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18675,3087,'2002-10-20 05:32:18',82,'simon','heh',1,3,57,0,'216.175.93.76','en'),(18676,3087,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','cute :D',6,7,82,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18677,3083,'2002-10-21 00:36:40',371,'Kyla','I wish it would snow in Auckland.  Instead, it\\\'s just grey and cold.\r\n\r\nI say if you\\\'re going to have winter, do it right!  Do it with snow, go the whole way.\r\n\r\nI am envious :)',6,183,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18678,3083,'2002-10-21 00:42:04',17,'austin','what is the best time of year to visit new zealand?',1,51,371,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18679,3086,'2002-10-21 06:43:59',175,'Paul G','I was too hard on Mr. N.  \r\nHe has his own horrible life story. \r\nHe does care; but he cares in a warped sort of way: like someone who was never cared for, and therefore doesn\\\'t really understand what caring is.  \r\nI guess that many people - especially those who were raised in the urban centers of the States - have had terrible things happen to them.\r\nTalk about free will and the soul isn\\\'t very popular with my colleagues in the social sciences.\r\nEveryone handles such events differently though; I have yet to read a satisfactory explanation why.\r\nWhy is Mrs. T. so resilient?\r\nWhy is Mr. N. so aggressive?\r\nI don\\\'t know.\r\nPaying attention to the biographies of the individuals that make up the city can be overwhelming.\r\nSometimes it is all too much.',0,764,0,0,'66.19.39.74','en'),(18680,3083,'2002-10-21 10:08:33',64,'Joanna','Any time is a good time to visit New Zealand - we\\\'re pretty mild here so it just depends if you want to go skiing or do summer outdoory type things, then obviously you pick winter or summer, but if you just want to come and experience our culture adn wonderful people and lifestyle then come right now. ',1,306,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18681,3082,'2002-10-21 11:38:30',392,'p.','guh, the varsity.  long are the hours spent in the bathroom after frequenting such a place.  we had it.  went there a total of once.',0,132,66,0,'12.13.167.78','en'),(18682,2873,'2002-10-21 14:12:47',93,'chiamattt','sometimes i just need to read le.',1,33,0,0,'211.32.132.229','en'),(18683,3082,'2002-10-21 14:35:19',66,'Peter','<br><tt>heh. in my pre-veggie days, i experienced very similar symptoms after dining at <i>the varsity</i>, heh. yet somehow, i kept going back each time i rolled into ATL. its an undeniable institution.</tt>',1,208,392,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(18684,3087,'2002-10-21 14:36:52',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i have the urge to call you <i>simon easton ellis</i> after reading this, heh.</tt>',1,91,82,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(18685,3083,'2002-10-21 14:56:02',57,'weeb','damn you austie. you better take me with you!!!!!!',0,50,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(18686,3087,'2002-10-21 16:06:03',86,'egg','simon, I love your way of tenderness. ',0,38,0,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(18687,3087,'2002-10-21 18:28:27',83,'adamajah','I liked what you wrote, but wouldn\\\'t have minded if you wrote about Gerhard.  I need to get up there and see that show. ',2,123,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(18688,3091,'2002-10-21 19:42:49',17,'austin','your failed relationships are not because of your parents divorce.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nrelationships are not failures.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nare you trying to say you are a failure?  if you have had a relationship at all, any kind of relationship, then you have not failed as a living creature.  even if you were a rock, you would have relationship to other rocks, dirt, and animals, and you would not be a failure.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nif you were the only molecule in existance, in a plain of nothingness, then i might accept the arguement of failure.',2,523,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18689,3083,'2002-10-21 19:52:51',17,'austin','oh sure, let me just peel a few hundreds out of my wallet and buy you a ticket too.',3,83,57,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18690,3087,'2002-10-21 19:59:51',82,'simon','actually, mang, i was going to write about the show. because i like richter and would be interested in writing about it. however, as i was getting started, i realized i spent more time at the opening concentrating on the above than the art. more later when i go back alone.',0,273,83,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18691,3091,'2002-10-21 20:36:23',371,'Kyla','Perhaps I haven\\\'t failed at having relationships, but I\\\'ve definitely failed at having happy ones.  Romantic ones anyway.  I wasn\\\'t suggesting that this makes me a failure, just\r\nwondering if I\\\'ll ever experience true love, companionship.\r\n\r\nFingers crossed!',6,270,17,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18692,3087,'2002-10-21 22:27:10',86,'egg','I have recently begun to feel, \\\"when I die, I wouldnt be proud to have had many parties. I want to leave behind a good dropping of work.\\\" then there is Paul Tsongas, who chose not to run for senate due to a cancer condition: \\\"Noone ever said, I wish I spent more time at the office.\\\" He refers, I guess, to the time you waste at work, without love.  why does death come up so often? It instructs the present. This is why afterlife is so important? It tells us how to live now. I just burped loudly in my open office. ',0,529,0,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(18693,3091,'2002-10-21 22:52:12',17,'austin','i think so.',3,11,371,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18694,3091,'2002-10-21 23:33:42',86,'egg','I definitely consider my parents\\\' divorces whenever I have relationship trouble (which seems like, always). It feels to a child of divorce, that you are always waiting for the end, for the fights, for the warnings, for the loneliness, and somehow self-destruct? it is still better to get in the ring and fight though - youre gonna die anyway! might as well kick and scratch - and handcuff and spank. and uh. the belt. and then there is the tenderness that comes sometimes without effort. so far its all worth it. and I think my parents would say the same thing.',0,564,371,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(18695,3087,'2002-10-21 23:55:30',82,'simon','i assume you want to leave behind work made out of love and not a whole lot of html coding for people to remember you by. i just accidentally sang along to the song on my headphones in my open office: I Get Around by Tupac. oops.',0,229,86,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18696,3087,'2002-10-22 01:21:47',86,'egg','i guess i dont wanna leave behind a bag o shit.',0,47,82,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(18697,3091,'2002-10-22 03:53:48',66,'Peter','<br><tt>my parents had about as nasty a divorce as any two people could ever have had; as soon as i grew up enough to gain perspective (and lose alot of insecurities), i was reassured that it wasnt my fault at all. since, ive found myself in the most lovely relationship, and i know that without a doubt, my life will never be complicated with the sorts of issues that set my parents against each other.<br><br>it is indeed a lovely and liberating feeling. dont worry kyla, it will come naturally and very subtlely; i know for a fact that youre a good person, so i have no doubts about this at all.</tt>',1,603,371,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18698,3081,'2002-10-22 07:18:06',409,'rachel','<p>\r\nwell, if you turn off the coffeepot burner shortly after you make the coffee and remove the filter full of grounds so that it doesn\\\'t drip bitter over-extracted coffee into the pot, day-old microwaved coffee tastes just fine. \r\n</p>\r\n',3,242,0,0,'207.214.90.66','en'),(18699,3091,'2002-10-22 08:18:44',17,'austin','i was talking to my step dad the other day.  i hope he never reads this, but whatever...  he said my mom used to give him shit constantly, after my little sister was born. then when my mom was probably in her mid to late forties, she got a hysterectomy.  after that, she didn\\\'t give him shit anymore.  but then my sister became a teenager and took up the torch.',3,364,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18700,3093,'2002-10-22 16:43:42',86,'egg','i drank one a few weeks ago. it was good. but why would you (even cleverly) want to advertise for pepsi?  ps: poonlarp sounds like a thai name.',0,143,0,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(18701,3093,'2002-10-22 16:56:24',66,'Peter','<Br><tt>pepsi blue tastes like sweet-tarts, but with caffeine. yuck.</tt>',1,73,0,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(18702,3087,'2002-10-22 17:06:04',82,'simon','not even if you could light it on fire and ring the doorbell and run first?',0,75,86,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18703,3093,'2002-10-22 17:30:38',90,'leonard','Pepsi Blue made me brown in the pants. Catch my drift?',1,54,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(18704,3087,'2002-10-22 17:36:33',83,'adamajah','<p>one of bubba\\\'s ex-roomies once argued that her morbid skeletal blobs of bronze were more valid pieces of art than my paintings because they would survive a nuclear holocaust.</p>\r\n\r\n',3,188,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(18705,3093,'2002-10-22 18:21:42',83,'adamajah','\\\"fecund tincture\\\" haha',0,28,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(18706,3087,'2002-10-22 18:26:18',82,'simon','did you ask her then who might care about her blobs after a nuclear holocaust?',0,78,83,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(18707,3087,'2002-10-22 18:42:22',66,'Peter','<br><tt>is it still art if no one is around to see it?</tt>',1,59,82,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(18708,3093,'2002-10-22 19:05:35',17,'austin','im going to run out and buy one!\r\n',0,34,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18709,3093,'2002-10-22 21:41:13',86,'egg','smartass.',0,9,17,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(18710,3093,'2002-10-23 00:06:05',17,'austin','that\\\'s SMARTASSTIN to you!',4,29,86,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18711,3087,'2002-10-23 00:42:32',83,'adamajah','yeah, as long as it\\\'s not interactive art.  heh',0,50,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(18712,3093,'2002-10-23 02:39:20',59,'s5','post-modernism.',6,15,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18713,3093,'2002-10-23 02:39:34',59,'s5','there, i said it.',6,17,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18714,3093,'2002-10-23 03:09:57',66,'Peter','<br><tt>*sighs*; *flutters eyelashes*</tt>',1,42,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18715,3093,'2002-10-23 03:11:14',65,'le','i began to sneeze uncontrollably while reading this.',0,52,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18716,3093,'2002-10-23 03:21:38',239,'Poonlarp','im fucking brilliant. i will rule the galaxies someday.',1,55,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18717,3080,'2002-10-23 04:07:15',314,'eve','Very creative imagery.',0,22,0,0,'80.139.136.213','en'),(18718,3094,'2002-10-23 06:36:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt>sounds like nyc in the 70s</tt>',1,39,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18719,3093,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','it doesn\\\'t make you less retarded to use big words.',6,54,239,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18720,3096,'2002-10-23 22:09:21',65,'le','this is awesome. where did you do your research about the santeria stuff? i love it. also excellent use of the word \\\'cockblock\\\'',4,133,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18721,3096,'2002-10-23 22:10:54',377,'dammit','i went to the santeria store on valencia and 20th  or 21st. there was a big statue of san la muerte. i did a little bit of internet research, and made up the rest :)',0,165,65,0,'66.122.107.46','en'),(18722,3093,'2002-10-23 22:27:46',65,'le','i drank a dr. pepper today',4,26,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18723,3093,'2002-10-23 22:34:21',17,'austin','my dad drinks dr. peppers with peanuts rolling around in the bottom of the can. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\npersonally i dont like them.  i think dr. pepper is slimey.',3,151,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18724,3093,'2002-10-23 22:40:58',74,'jo','i broke a pretty strong addiction to dr. pepper that involved the consumption of (occassionally) 44 oz. of it at a time, which i know doesn\\\'t seem like a lot to some people because, hey, it\\\'s just a big gulp, until you realize that it\\\'s like 200 gm of sugar. yeah, anyway, i haven\\\'t had dr. pepper in 10 months and i feel a hell of a lot better.',0,357,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(18725,3093,'2002-10-23 23:22:05',66,'Peter','<br><tt>before someone else mentions it, let me just say that dr. pepper does not contain <a href=\\\"http://www.snopes.com/business/secret/drpepper.htm\\\">prune juice</a>.</tt>',1,178,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18726,1600,'2002-10-23 23:46:03',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this <i>totally</i> made me crack up for some reason.</tt>',1,66,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18727,3097,'2002-10-23 23:59:37',195,'matt','doooooood, you are coming through in waaaaaves.<BR><BR>also, the red and blue are turned up so magnifique in that photo.',1,120,0,0,'209.208.109.54','en'),(18728,3091,'2002-10-24 02:18:06',124,'gus','my parents are still married, something like 37 years. You dont think the fact that they didnt get divorced doesnt cause all sorts of problems in my relationships? well it has. Your parents yelled and fought and then split up so they wouldnt do it anymore. mine just kept on doing it until I moved out and couldnt hear them anymore. <p>\r\nif 50% of marriages end in divorce, you\\\'re not a minority, if you think about it, you\\\'re normal for having gone through a parents divorce. this makes your relationships and your relationship problems average and normal. ',0,564,0,0,'64.160.53.99','en'),(18729,3093,'2002-10-24 02:21:37',124,'gus','drinking 40 oz of soda in a day is horrifying to me. not just the sugar, but the phosphorus in carbonated drinks leeches the calcium out of your bones. \r\n<p>\r\nI think dr pepper is gross too.\r\n<p>\r\nI dont know about pepsi blue, is it blue colored? if it is, I only want to drink it out of a clear container.',0,306,0,0,'64.160.53.99','en'),(18730,3093,'2002-10-24 03:03:40',66,'Peter','<br><tt>sorry, my inner scientist is screaming... in normal healthy people without bone degenerative disorders, drinking soda has no effect on bone modelling. just thought id add my two cents for you paranoid folks out there ;) <i>best friendly!</i></tt>',1,254,124,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18731,3099,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','it\\\'s the basement, it\\\'s soul-sucking.',6,43,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18732,3099,'2002-10-24 06:19:04',65,'le','normally i am like \\\"leaving won\\\'t solve anything!\\\" but i left for a while and it solved a lot. i think if you feel trapped and freaked, going somewhere to cool out is a good idea. find somewhere cheap where you can work for your duckets and just. go. there. if you don\\\'t like it, you can always come back. maybe you should <a href=\\\"wwoof.org\\\">WWOOF</a> or something along those lines. \r\n<br><br>\r\ndon\\\'t feel bad for oscillating around trying to figure out what to do - we all do it. \r\n<br><br>\r\n<tt><3</tt>',4,527,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18733,3099,'2002-10-24 07:23:00',93,'chiamattt','to quote billy bragg. \\\"im not looking for a new england, just looking for another girl\\\"',0,93,0,0,'211.32.132.228','en'),(18734,3099,'2002-10-24 16:14:49',86,'egg','perhaps some Crumb comics will bust your nads into the good-humored OMS we love. <br><br> -gang phet ped yaang. ',0,112,0,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(18735,279,'2002-10-24 17:49:32',66,'Peter','<br><tt><a href=\\\"http://saturn5.com/~peter/music/le_tigre-cars_that_go_boom.mp3\\\">cars that go boom</a>',1,108,17,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(18736,279,'2002-10-24 19:52:02',86,'egg','actually, the song is by \\\"L\\\'Trimm\\\", an old school rap duo, and not \\\"le tigre\\\", that tragically hip electropop trio.',0,130,66,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(18737,279,'2002-10-24 20:48:31',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yeah, thats just whatever it was titled when i downloaded it; most likely, whoever uploaded it was confused by the fact that l\'trimm was aka \'tigre & bunny\'.<br><img src=\"http://www.itslikebuttababy.com/images/ltrimm.gif\"></tt>',1,96,86,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18738,3091,'2002-10-24 21:08:03',376,'incognito','<p>divorce is such a different experience for everyone. even my sister and i have slightly different perspectives on my parents\\\' divorce. like a friend of mine thinks that when parents separate later in life when the children are adults, it\\\'s easier. in her experience, her parents were going to get divorced when she was younger, and she basically ordered them not to. so they stayed together until she was out of college. that\\\'s a pretty exceptional experience. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>my parents divorced when i was the same age as she had been, and i\\\'m still having a hard time getting through it and feeling confident that i could even attempt to have a romantic-type relationship with someone. jeez, i don\\\'t even try at this point, i just avoid it. i don\\\'t want to be rejected now and i certainly don\\\'t want it to suddenly happen 25 years from now.</p>\r\n\r\n</p>these things can be hard to forget.</p>',3,913,124,0,'206.208.110.111','en'),(18739,3099,'2002-10-24 21:11:29',142,'oof','<p>wait it is not. the basement rules. i love the basement.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i would just lay there in the bed with the windows open listening to the hill and thinking about nothing and everything and being so content to just be alive.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i haven\\\'t been that satisfied to be somewhere in a long time. usually it\\\'s all discomfort and anxiety and what\\\'s going on what am i doing what should i be doing i need to go need to do need to need to need to.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>but in the basement i was happy enough to just lay there and be.</p>',1,537,62,0,'128.148.188.186','en'),(18740,3099,'2002-10-24 21:17:16',17,'austin','i should just say that so far you are all wrong.',4,48,0,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(18741,3099,'2002-10-24 21:29:56',57,'weeb','what you need, austin, is a good spanking and lots of beer time at the zeitgeist. also, lots more thai food, we need more thai food. oh god, foooood.\r\n\r\ncan we organize a little field trip food night again? can we go on a little camping trip? in your backyard? we could huddle under the hot tub tarp and tell scary stories. yeah.',0,329,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(18742,3099,'2002-10-24 22:11:29',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i had my worst night\\\'s sleep <i>ever</i> in that basement (not hte basement\\\'s fault). to top it off, i awoke to insanely loud radiohead cacophony form upstairs. ha.</tt>',1,183,142,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18743,3101,'2002-10-24 23:34:27',17,'austin','<P>i really feel like a bastard every time i make a one line comment, so i am going to ramble a bit.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nramble\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nramble\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nramble\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nthis is really superb.\r\n</P>',2,194,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18744,3101,'2002-10-24 23:40:11',90,'leonard','There\\\'s nothing wrong with a one line comment and/or a non sequitur now and then.',6,84,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(18745,3099,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','aww!!! come back to the basement anytime!\r\n\r\nright now tho, the furnace is borken and it\\\'s freezing down there and everywhere.  miserable.',6,141,142,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18746,3101,'2002-10-25 00:31:34',65,'le','is this supposed to be funny or sad or both? is this a true story? ',4,67,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18747,3101,'2002-10-25 01:06:21',90,'leonard','It\\\'s quite sad actually.',1,27,0,0,'216.249.108.191','en'),(18748,3093,'2002-10-25 02:56:24',124,'gus','really? ok well a lot of people say it leeches shit out of your bones. so what do I know! nothing I guess. it still isnt very good for you to drink lots of soda. smooooch.',0,171,66,0,'64.160.53.99','en'),(18749,3093,'2002-10-25 03:16:36',66,'Peter','<br><tt>nah, in a nutshell, thats like saying that drinking a glass of water would dehydrate you. not really, but sort of. think phosphates and phosphatases and phosphorylation and polarity and ions and blood/metabolic energy levels. im not arguing, i just happen to work on a bone biomechanics laboratory and happen to have discussed this alot with paranoid moms and patients. yeah.</tt>',1,388,124,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18750,3101,'2002-10-25 03:30:17',57,'weeb','i think it\'s hilarious- but where\'s the cuddling part? the whole point of make believe fighting is the making snugglys afterwards.<br><br>\r\n\r\nno snugglies is kinda sad.',0,184,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(18751,3101,'2002-10-25 04:49:54',17,'austin','perhaps it was a real fight?  oh shiza, SEX IN THE CITY IS ON SOME MORE!',3,72,57,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18752,3099,'2002-10-25 05:43:04',196,'jasonp','The sullen groove grinds and grinds. The mashing and scraping, the numb cold feet. A hammer on the thumb. Another cold beer and it\\\'s another Tuesday afternoon.\n\nWhere is the neatly packaged, color coordinated life changing moment to be had? They used to hand them out free, but now they\\\'ve closed up shop.\nRumor has it the new place across town has it.\n\nWherever I go will I be there, or no?\nDoes the brand new extra large convenience store really sell convenience? It\\\'s a sham man... It\\\'s just a bunch of Fritos, antacid and bad coffee and bullshit like that.\n\nI need:\nan instruction booklet\na new pair of eyeballs\na shot in the ass and slap in the face\na ticket to parts unknown\n\n---\n\nToo many pointless hours compulsively spent on the internet?\nThat\\\'s something of a problem for me.\n\nMy thing is to store up several years of mild dissatisfaction and restlessness for one severe yet undeterminted life changing moment.\nAnd don\\\'t worry it doesn\\\'t involve a gun or anything...\n\n',0,999,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(18753,3099,'2002-10-25 06:05:26',196,'jasonp','BTW, the above is *best friendly*.\n<BR>Kind of hard to tell from lack of formatting.<BR>\n\n',1,90,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(18754,3094,'2002-10-25 06:46:42',416,'Taxman','Wow.  Excellent imagery...and I liked how it didn\\\'t seem all \\\"bright and cheery\\\".  I felt like I was in Amsterdam when I read it....it\\\'s too bad I\\\'m not.  Heh.  Anyway, excellent work.',2,199,0,0,'12.216.21.139','en'),(18755,3099,'2002-10-25 06:54:02',416,'Taxman','I\\\'ve been feelin what you\\\'re feelin almost my whole life.  It does help to drive and to go somewhere to cool out.  But don\\\'t try to escape your blues- nobody can outrun the blues.',2,188,0,0,'12.216.21.139','en'),(18756,3099,'2002-10-25 07:00:09',17,'austin','i really think getting rid debt would further my cause and not make me feel so attached to it all.',3,98,196,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18757,3102,'2002-10-25 07:01:48',416,'Taxman','Aw, come on now, don\\\'t you remember when school was fun? Yeah, me neither.  You captured the frustration I feel every day I have another class to attend.  I liked your poetic style...simply because the emotional trauma resulting from the elusive lie that is higher education can only be captured with such words.',2,315,0,0,'12.216.21.139','en'),(18758,3098,'2002-10-25 07:12:13',416,'Taxman','Holy shit.  One of the most TWISTED, DARK, and generally SCREWED UP articles I\\\'ve seen.  I loved it. ',1,104,0,0,'12.216.21.139','en'),(18759,3099,'2002-10-25 07:23:32',196,'jasonp','Yeah, it\\\'s a common predicament.<BR>\nMaybe you need a \\\"master plan\\\".<BR>\nMy prob is I\\\'ll have all kinds of ideas but in the end it\\\'s only the routine crap that I ever end up doing.<BR>\nFor me, taking care of the materialistic demands is a lot easier than taking care of the personal kind of stuff. Just the opposite of how it used to be. So it\\\'s always something.<BR>\n',0,386,17,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(18760,3095,'2002-10-25 07:49:26',196,'jasonp','I\\\'ll have to check out some Krishnamurti books now. <BR><BR>\nWhat puts people off to \\\"preaching\\\" is that it is egotistically based. The person preaching is always right, is superior, is such a righteous person, etc.<BR><BR>\nYou could say that life is essentially trivial and internal. Trivial in the sense that any sort of external situation or achievement really doesn\\\'t matter it\\\'s what\\\'s in your head and heart that matters. \n\n',0,448,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(18761,3095,'2002-10-25 08:02:16',17,'austin','my step mom was really into this Krishnamurti and his books for a while.  then i think she lost all faith.  oh well.  i don\\\'t know anything about him.  he probably isn\\\'t into faith?  probably into thinking.',3,212,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18762,3095,'2002-10-25 08:43:04',196,'jasonp','You could say it\\\'s about \\\"being\\\" or \\\"knowing\\\" than thinking. Sitting around thinking too much just gives you a headache. Meditation is basically an effort to concentrate and rid your mind of distractions -- the opposite of regular conjecture, chatter, theorizing, worrying, etc. of thinking. That\\\'s why for zen the emphasis is on non-verbal, non-rational.<BR>\nThere\\\'s no effort to force a belief. It\\\'s more like, \\\"Here it is. See for yourself\\\". So it\\\'s hard to really \\\"lose faith\\\" compared to something like christianity which can tend to be like, \\\"You believe it right?!? You believe it right?!? Say it! You\\\'re going to hell if you don\\\'t say this!\\\"\n',0,701,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(18763,3095,'2002-10-25 09:22:00',17,'austin','i have difficulty fostering an interest in what people think i should think, and for that matter, be or know.  i understand that some of these illuminaries have nice ideas and have things reduced to their root problems in human understanding, but that does not change things fast enough to create a Bill & Ted\\\'s Enlightenment.  as far as i know, mass enlightenment leads to more and different bad things.  no matter what, there will be more people until the end.  when the end starts, game over.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni hate talking in unsubstanciated touchy feely apocalypse talk.<BR><BR>\r\nbad austin',4,593,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18764,3095,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i recommend sri chinmoy.  he\\\'s nice, and can lift weights, and fetishizes ladie di.',6,86,17,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18765,3099,'2002-10-25 12:32:29',67,'trouble','i\\\'m right where you are now <i>sort of</i>.  if you go to canada stop by detroit for a while and i\\\'ll take you out for a beer or two or seven or fifteen.',1,159,17,0,'63.70.119.94','en'),(18766,3098,'2002-10-25 12:37:02',67,'trouble','yeah, i agree with Taxman this was nice and dark.  i like to think of vegas as somewhat slimy and not the theme park that it\\\'s turned out to be these days.  i want to hear about binges, losing everything and then winning enough for breakfast after an all night bender, hookers with lipstick smears and liquor... oh the liquor.\r\n\r\n*best friendly*',1,348,0,0,'63.70.119.94','en'),(18767,3098,'2002-10-25 13:23:54',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ive never been to vegas, but i was in reno last month; its seedy and beautiful. ive never been as drunk as i was in reno, and ever eaten so many shrimp. black jacuzzis, champaigne room-service, drop-dead gorgeous girl in heels and cashmere kangol. unbridled sex after too many complimentary stoli ohranj cosmos. untactful blackjack dealer hinting that we should leave the $35 minimum table, guffawing, falling over, hands full of countless nickles spewing from the video poker machine, filthy lucre.</tt>',1,512,0,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(18768,3102,'2002-10-25 14:21:55',57,'weeb','oof, you are a beautiful person. we miss you in sf. sigh!\r\n\r\n',0,61,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(18769,3102,'2002-10-25 15:22:27',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>I\\\'d rather have you in my arms than the world at my fingertips</i> is a beautiful line.<br><br>this is a very profound piece; very different from the jolly, drunken oof i talked to in sf this summer.</tt>',1,218,0,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(18770,3099,'2002-10-25 15:39:05',122,'hool','<p>i have found when i\\\'m in a mental environment that you have described, moderation of computer use is a good starting point.  yesterday i spent <i>far</i> more time away from my computer than i did in front of it.  and i did a variety of things in analog space, walked places, and had satisfaction.</p>\r\n<p>context switch.  give yourself a context switch.  travelling for me is not running away from my problems - it is giving myself a different environment in which to consider them, a different arrangement to try to parse them.</p>\r\n<p>if you come to canada, we will do an austin swinney fashion shoot.  and at busy intersections here in toronto, we will build anatomically correct snowmen and snowomen in compromising positions.</p>',0,741,17,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(18771,3101,'2002-10-25 16:11:51',90,'leonard','After three days and a few sweaty sessions on the treadmill, she has finally stopped ignoring me. We celebrated last night with a snuggle fest and double A batteries. It was brilliant.',1,184,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(18772,3099,'2002-10-25 16:14:30',17,'austin','is there already snow in trana?',3,31,122,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18773,3101,'2002-10-25 17:10:14',83,'adamajah','a similar thing happened to me just last week.  except we\\\'re not married yet...',0,82,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(18774,3101,'2002-10-25 17:16:58',156,'lotus','oh this is so brilliant and true. pretty much the exact same thing happened to me on monday. freaky. ',1,101,0,0,'64.230.114.72','en'),(18775,2929,'2002-10-25 17:20:31',156,'lotus','i am going to keep reading this series. <br><br> we have still not hung our pictures on the wall. they are propped behind one of the sofas. tomorrow will be our five month anniversary.',1,184,0,0,'64.230.114.72','en'),(18776,3101,'2002-10-25 17:46:14',17,'austin','which side of this were you on, lotus?  you thought it was sex night and he didnt know, or you thought it was time to go to sleep and he was like, \\\"i\\\'m lying here with a pole, what what what?\\\"',3,199,156,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18777,3099,'2002-10-25 18:01:49',122,'hool','not yet, but soon.  light snow is forecast for november 1st.  i will pee on it to voice my golden contempt.',0,107,17,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(18778,3036,'2002-10-25 21:51:08',420,'thanatos','beautiful disclosure !  i can relate.\r\n\r\n11 years ago, at an early 20something age, fresh from gradschool - i was pregnant and became a single mom.  my parents supported me financially along with a nanny to help bring him up so i could still maintain my career.... when i was pregnant, i decided, i was going to be \\\"selfish\\\", in an \\\"Ayn Rand\\\" sort of way.... i was to attend to my needs and be happy, and carefree and just me... this would allow me to project this loving disposition into my little one.... he grew into a beautiful boy, i still get compliments on how well-mannered he is etc... but i also do not filter the bad_side in me, yes, i can\\\'t help feeling guilty ... but, we are all human, all2human, ... a happy mom = a happy son...\r\n\r\nlove, respect, capacity to self-reflect and analyze - i think those are basic ingredients . . . i would not want to be too perfect or a goody2shoe, that would be too boring for my taste ... ;-)',2,955,0,0,'128.242.125.65','en'),(18779,3103,'2002-10-26 01:48:14',66,'Peter','<br><tt><img src=\\\"http://gs.cdnow.com/graphics/COVERART/local/L/30/23/00003023.jpg\\\"></tt>',1,95,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18780,3095,'2002-10-26 03:30:50',176,'Scapegoat','I met Sri Chimnoy in person two years ago in Ottawa. Interesting man, he has a restaurant in Ottawa that sells really good veg. food and has tons of pictures of him and signifigant world figures - a disportionate amount of them are him and Lady Di.',0,248,62,0,'65.95.143.82','en'),(18781,3103,'2002-10-26 04:28:31',176,'Albino Sasquatch','Bring on the future, if for nothing more then my insatiable desire for entertainment. May it be a world in which everyone is brave.',0,131,0,0,'65.95.143.82','en'),(18782,3103,'2002-10-26 04:36:39',239,'Poonlarp','no, the future is a world in which everyone will be <i>dead</i>... eventually',6,77,0,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18783,3103,'2002-10-26 04:44:59',176,'Albino Sasquatch','Maybe some of us can be brave when we face death? I wouldn\\\'t mind being one of the ones around to see the last chapter. I have to say that I\\\'m insanely curious about what will eventually take us all out. I sometimes imagine the end with humanity coming to terms with itself. There was an interesting movie made called <i>Last Night</i> that this reminded me of.  ',0,369,239,0,'65.95.143.82','en'),(18784,3095,'2002-10-26 06:25:00',59,'Sri Chinmoy','hey, and you should check out my original art. goes well with the samosa, don\\\'t you think?',4,93,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18785,3095,'2002-10-26 06:25:34',59,'Sri Chinmoy','hi, i\\\'m Sri Chinmoy.',4,23,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18786,3095,'2002-10-26 06:25:57',59,'Sri Chinmoy','Lady Di is SUPER HOTT XXX LUCKY OKAY!!!!!!11111',6,47,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18787,3095,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','omg i spelt it ladie di.  i am a supermoron.',6,44,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18788,3095,'2002-10-26 06:31:52',59,'Sri Chinmoy','i can totally kick the Supreme Master Ching Hai\\\'s ass at samosas.',6,68,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18789,3095,'2002-10-26 06:49:45',59,'Sri Chinmoy','who is this \\\"Bill & Ted\\\" anyway? do they own a restaurant?',5,64,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18790,3108,'2002-10-26 07:34:18',421,'chimeres','(hehehehe...) I love the short travel snippets!  Makes me want to travel with pen and book and very open eyes.  A fresh little escape.  :) ',1,139,0,0,'209.232.55.9','en'),(18791,3102,'2002-10-26 11:53:39',65,'le','*snuffle*<br>\r\n*sniffsniffsniff*<br>\r\n',4,38,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18792,3101,'2002-10-26 13:48:39',57,'weeb','<i>i am lying here with a pole, what what what?</i>\r\n\r\nfucking brilliant.',1,73,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(18793,3095,'2002-10-26 13:55:26',69,'starlajo','ladie dadie, we like to party.',0,30,0,0,'203.96.111.237','en'),(18794,3095,'2002-10-26 22:54:08',74,'jo','weird. i started reading a krishnamurti book a week ago.',0,56,0,0,'172.131.135.170','en'),(18795,3097,'2002-10-27 00:07:44',251,'Jamie','this looks like an album cover',6,30,0,0,'195.92.194.18','en'),(18796,3108,'2002-10-27 01:11:25',420,'thanatos','well hello, chimeres... ;-)  i travel with an open heart and a hungry mind... nice to see u here.',1,97,0,0,'63.193.245.138','en'),(18797,2996,'2002-10-27 07:58:05',421,'chimeres','Not to destroy your 22-year-old-virgin-naivete, but cum is spelled cum, not come.  Nice how that one scene brings such depth and life in.  I\\\'m curious why Marshall bought the painting, but i kinda like the wondering. ',2,220,0,0,'66.123.206.229','en'),(18798,3111,'2002-10-27 23:48:29',17,'austin','i looked up travail, is that a typo for travel?  the definition i read was something about pain of child birth, a great pain.\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni think ix would like this piece.  it sounds kind of like they are going to slay the dragon.',1,231,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18799,3114,'2002-10-28 00:41:06',65,'le','it takes taurus longer to come to decisions and emotional understanding than other signs. they really have to decide on their own time how they feel about things.\r\n<br><br>\r\nit\\\'s funny, i used to be very in love with sf, and i still love it, but i have begun to feel as if my time here is very limited, and i feel good about that. \r\n<br><br>\r\nbut i\\\'ll miss the huge billowing waves of fog pouring over twin peaks in the evenings.',4,435,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18800,3114,'2002-10-28 00:45:22',17,'austin','you are so disagreeable these days!\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nwhat did they do to you up in that hippy camp!  i want the old band wagon joining milkcan back!',3,142,65,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18801,3114,'2002-10-28 01:33:41',66,'Peter','<br><tt>sometimes, i muse that the northeast fates have seized le in my place. i have this vague feeling that im going to end up gladly growing old in the bay area, even though 6 months ago, youd have had to pry nyc from my cold, dead fingers. its odd, actually.</tt>',1,267,17,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18802,3114,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','cyantist:  agree :D',6,19,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18803,3114,'2002-10-28 03:31:13',59,'s5','what i love most about san francisco is that it doesn\\\'t have weather or seasons, it has moods.',1,97,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18804,3114,'2002-10-28 03:38:56',59,'s5','ps, the front page article of the most recent sf weekly made me love this city one more notch.',0,94,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18805,3114,'2002-10-28 04:57:06',65,'le','<B><FONT SIZE=\\\"+10\\\" COLOR=\\\"RED\\\">MONKEY KNIFE FIGHT</FONT></B>',5,73,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18806,3114,'2002-10-28 06:07:29',176,'Scapegoat','I read so much about San Fransisco & New York here on Swinney I am going to have to come see them for myself sometime.<br><br>\r\nMy city <b>Toronto</b> is Huron for <i>meeting place</i> and it lives up to its name. According to many studies the most multicultural or culturally diverse city in the world, takes up approximately 5,902 square kilometers situated at 43:40:12 N & 79:22:12 W. <br><br>\r\n2,276 miles or 3,664 kilometers from San Fransisco.<br><br>\r\n347 miles or 558 kilometers from New York.<br><br>\r\nNo romantic fogs here though, main event weather wise is a winter that if your lucky isn\\\'t too slushy. Its original name before incorporation as a city was York, not Toronto. There you are more then you ever wanted to know about Toronto. Wish I could say I felt some connection to it but I think it is just a temporary stop for me. I\\\'m envious of people that feel connected to the city they live. ',0,914,0,0,'65.95.142.125','en'),(18807,3114,'2002-10-28 06:14:54',17,'austin','i have been thinking of visiting and i looked up those figures on yahoo! maps the other day.  it would be a long drive.  i would have to buy tire chains.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nlast time i was in texas when it froze, i drove my 87 buick century onto ice patches and hit the brakes, for fun.  i got too flats and put in a side panel.  it was really fun.',3,340,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18808,3114,'2002-10-28 06:15:52',17,'austin','<font size=7 color=\\\"RED\\\">ELIZIBUTT MILKCAN BITES</FONT>',4,61,65,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18809,723,'2002-10-28 06:20:41',17,'austin','I would like to update this by saying that as far as Yeti is concerned, I am now \\\"Swinnolito\\\" and he is \\\"Radavasquez\\\" which is short for The Baron Von Madacascar Radavasquez III.  In related news, I finally watched The Count of Monte Cristo at H2.',3,259,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18810,723,'2002-10-28 06:22:24',65,'le','<b>radulovasquez</b>',6,20,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18811,3114,'2002-10-28 06:23:12',65,'le','<tt><font size=\\\"+4\\\">:(  :(  :(</font></tt>\r\n<br><br>\r\ni am nice!',0,70,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18812,3114,'2002-10-28 09:12:13',59,'Sri Chinmoy','i\\\'m just here for the samosas.',4,33,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18813,2866,'2002-10-28 11:00:50',65,'le','for some reason i feel the need to update this with the following information: i stopped eating butter at omega when a nice capricorn (capricorns seem to be the key to veganism for many) forced me to try EARTH BALANCE brand buttery spread. that shit is THE shit. it\\\'s all non gmo expeller pressed oils and holy schneikies it is good. i should go mention this on vegan challenge too.',4,385,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18814,2866,'2002-10-28 14:39:39',66,'Peter','<br><tt>one word: <i>tofutti</i></tt>',1,37,65,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(18815,3117,'2002-10-28 15:34:03',17,'austin','this is really fantastic.  sometimes i laugh to myself because working out is so gross, but at the same time sexy.',3,114,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18816,3114,'2002-10-28 17:00:12',156,'lotus','<a href=\\\"http://www.msnbc.com/news/818149.asp\\\"> Letters to the Editor: New York and Toronto go head to head again</a> ',1,124,176,0,'64.230.108.179','en'),(18817,3117,'2002-10-28 19:08:14',124,'gus','I miss boxing. getting so disgustingly sweaty and tired. then afterward, being so goofed up on endorphins. I miss the jumping rope too. ',0,136,0,0,'64.160.53.99','en'),(18818,3117,'2002-10-28 19:45:49',17,'austin','i think there are quite a few on this site that enjoy the ass whipping arts.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nit is not clear to me if this piece is fiction, slightly non-fiction, or completely non-fiction.  not that i think that distinction needs to be made,  but for the purpose of determining how many people exactly enjoy the butt kicking arts it would be nice to know.',3,351,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18819,3117,'2002-10-28 19:46:15',17,'austin','and the ending sentence is great.',3,33,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18820,3100,'2002-10-28 20:03:21',370,'seanthecon','to me, it seems instinctual to snap and crash every once in awhile. life is too fucked up to think about all the time. sometimes it is better just let things happen and react accordingly. ',0,188,0,0,'216.21.133.195','en'),(18821,3117,'2002-10-28 20:11:47',415,'lydia','yes, non-fiction. which is also why i\\\'ve switched over from muay thai to boxing. <i>NEVER</i> sleep with your trainer. ',0,122,17,0,'172.195.71.8','en'),(18822,2866,'2002-10-28 20:17:26',74,'jo','i have been such a model vegan the past few months, but just in the past week, my body has really been craving rich buttery soft cookies and cakes. constantly, in perpetuity. i have only given in a couple of times, but last night i decided to flip through vegan cookbooks and find cookie recipes. tonight i will try a banana-oatmeal cookie recipe in the \\\"how it all vegan\\\" book. maybe i will use this delicious spread of which you speak, elly. (p.s. if anyone has already made said recipe and has comments or would like to steer me towards another, better recipe in the same book, <a href=\\\"mailto:joanna@unpopular.com\\\">let me know</a>.)',2,648,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(18823,3114,'2002-10-28 20:27:57',74,'jo','my relationship with my city changes: sometimes i want to scream from rooftops about my city and my passionate love for her; other times when people bring her up, i\\\'m immediately self-conscious about her faults and i want to change the subject as quickly as possible and hide her from scrutiny; sometimes i start looking around at other cities, comparing the two side by side, thinking that the other is who i should choose. but most of the time i have a new-friend crush on her, where i want to exclaim, \\\"LOOK AT MY RAD FRIEND!\\\" to everyone i meet, where every new discovery about her is exciting.',1,607,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(18824,3117,'2002-10-28 21:00:22',90,'leonard','The other day I was at the gym and this guy comes up to me while I was using the dip machine and asks, \\\"Are you taking anything?\\\" And I replied, \\\"Yeah, a lot of shit from my wife.\\\"',1,192,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(18825,3117,'2002-10-28 21:34:01',124,'gus','I switched from muay thai because when I used my arms AND legs I couldnt breathe. when I just used my arms, it was a little easier. (I slept with a trainer too, but with no ill effects on my workout schedule).',0,209,415,0,'64.160.53.99','en'),(18826,3114,'2002-10-28 22:17:47',176,'Scapegoat','to <b>austin</b> a visit would be exciting. It would be nice to meet some of the people on here from the other cities. You are joking about the tire chain, right? I don\\\'t think I have ever seen a vehicle with tire chains on the move, not even in my memories of northern Alberta. Aren\\\'t they illegal?<br><br>\r\nto <b>lotus</b> I think it is a hard comparison to make between NYC and Tdot. NYC has a lot more history and if there was ever an unofficial global capital NYC is it. Toronto is cool but in its own distinct way but I\\\'ve also never been to NYC so I\\\'m talking based on readings not first hand experience.<br><br>\r\n',0,633,0,0,'65.95.159.4','en'),(18827,3114,'2002-10-28 22:24:16',86,'egg','san francisco makes me ill. literally. one week here and i have a cold. but thankfully, i also have a roommate who stocks up on vitamins and tea. IT\\\'S COLD UP IN THIS BIYATCH. ',0,179,0,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(18828,3117,'2002-10-29 00:48:28',82,'simon','heehee!',0,7,124,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(18829,3114,'2002-10-29 03:31:55',124,'gus','Maybe in Northern Alberta they all have snow tires? Around here chains are sometimes required as opposed to illegal. Like on the way to Tahoe if it\\\'s snowing or over the grapevine (hwy 5) to LA in winter storms.\r\n\r\n',0,218,176,0,'64.160.53.99','en'),(18830,3111,'2002-10-29 03:48:15',314,'eve','*coughs* Prevail, not travail.',0,30,17,0,'80.139.148.26','en'),(18831,3118,'2002-10-29 03:50:19',314,'eve','Late nights tend to have that effect on me as well. I sympathise.',0,65,0,0,'80.139.148.26','en'),(18832,3117,'2002-10-29 03:55:44',314,'eve','That was an excellant piece of non-fiction, humor, and information in one little bottle. And it was worth a good laugh. ;)\r\n\r\n',0,126,0,0,'80.139.148.26','en'),(18833,3116,'2002-10-29 03:58:53',314,'eve','Growing up in a small town, I think that one misses out on the ability to label him or herself to a larger community, and still remain anonymous - but, accepted as well. One time in my life, I would like to experience that.',0,223,0,0,'80.139.148.26','en'),(18834,3114,'2002-10-29 04:04:04',314,'eve','Tire chains. Those two words brought back a lot of childhood memories not worth mentioning - just along the lines of our garage (ie my playhouse and hideout.) I\\\'d love to debate tire chains, but my car no longer exists and I already have one bike chain, and that\\\'s quite enough.',0,284,0,0,'80.139.148.26','en'),(18835,3101,'2002-10-29 04:15:44',314,'eve','I just added 2 more points to the reasons I should be terrified of marriage. Two words, \\\"Sex Night.\\\" The thought of scheduling sex is far, far beyond me. Great post. ',0,172,0,0,'80.139.148.26','en'),(18836,3099,'2002-10-29 04:59:50',314,'eve','<i>\\\"I have nothing to say. It is a chronic problem at this point. I am furniture. I need to get out of town and cool out. It is too much soul sucking internet that doesn\\\'t matter. I have become indifferent.\\\"</i>  - was like rereading my words from the last several months. When you figure out a solution, clue me in. Thus far, traveling short distance, walking, getting out of town, hasn\\\'t helped much. If you don\\\'t change on the way while you\\\'re out, you just come back to the same routine. Dead end cycle.\r\nMaybe it just takes a little more energy (mentally more than anything) to really STAND UP and change something. Hopefully, the Turkish Riviera in November holds some inspiration, time to think (beach and free drinks(!!!) *cough*, and some real space away from <i>this</i> space that will let me loose.',0,828,17,0,'80.139.183.106','en'),(18837,3099,'2002-10-29 05:28:35',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i took a month-long vacation in berkeley and sf. that did the trick for me; seeing the country, driving 3000 miles, and everything else...</tt>',1,151,314,0,'66.108.186.54','en'),(18838,3114,'2002-10-29 05:39:06',176,'Scapegoat','Definitely have to use snow tires in northern Alberta.<br><br>\r\nto <b>eve</b> definitely prefer bike chains to tire chains but a little dangerous in the winter, no?',0,164,124,0,'65.95.143.75','en'),(18839,3114,'2002-10-29 06:54:24',17,'austin','<a href=\\\"http://www.freenet.edmonton.ab.ca/ebc/winter.htm\\\">bike spikes, Do-it-yourself</a><BR><BR>\r\n\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.peterwhitecycles.com/studdedtires.asp\\\">or buy them online</a>',3,195,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18840,3016,'2002-10-29 07:34:37',421,'chimeres','I agree.  We encounter these questions every day as the past haunts us, or as we haunt it.  Sometimes I think i\\\'ve changed until some buried artifact surfaces and reminds me how static my self really is.  But sometimes the light suddenly reflects off a relic and as it blinds me i realize how oddly entwined i was in another world.  It\\\'s frightening why we ask these questions, why it matters whether or how we change.  Why do we define ourselves like that?  Perhaps it\\\'s the only way we can define ourselves and conceptualize relationships.  I don\\\'t know.  I wonder how others answer these questions, and why all ask them...',0,637,0,0,'207.214.222.102','en'),(18841,3072,'2002-10-29 07:58:44',421,'chimeres','I love the newspaper coin slot bit!  Quirky details of human nature... adds emotion and depth and a bitter spice.  ',0,115,0,0,'207.214.222.102','en'),(18842,3102,'2002-10-29 13:12:50',264,'samizzz','in your arms a love will land, as the high school banner sailed back into that bitch-ass, security guard\\\'s hands.  \r\n\r\nfuck, i owe you many letters.  keep an eye out for kitten explosions and sexy, latina maids.',2,214,0,0,'24.171.133.250','en'),(18843,3114,'2002-10-29 15:01:00',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i love how that article makes references to toronto being \\\"safer\\\" than nyc. about 25 years have passed since the days of fiscal crisis here in nyc, and passing with those years was most of the stereotypical roughness, danger and risk that became a hallmark of the city.<br><br>ive lived here for 8 years and have basically had <i>no</i> trouble, never felt unsafe, and never been attacked, robbed or otherwise molested. i suppose old legends die hard.<br><br>especially in this post-sept-11th nyc, things have reached an exceptionally pastoral pace- people are nice, cops smile and life is good. everyone should visit nyc and see what its all about, themselves.</tt>  ',1,682,156,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(18844,3116,'2002-10-29 15:06:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt>then you definitely need to read <a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1958\"><b>this article</b></a>... it makes a lot of sense in respect to what you mentioned; a set of feelings ive very much felt myself.</tt>',1,290,314,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(18845,3116,'2002-10-29 16:22:34',57,'weeb','i have a picture of the fall foliage in vermont on my desktop. it makes me miss the east coast in a stabbingly sweet way. old relationships, old friends, old places. \r\n<br><br>\r\nit\\\'s the best part of leaving. only remembering the good times. memories so sweet you want to cry.',1,279,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(18846,3114,'2002-10-29 17:47:23',156,'lotus','i like visiting toronto. i want to start visiting n.y. i\\\'ve only been there once...when i was ten.',1,101,0,0,'64.230.108.179','en'),(18847,3114,'2002-10-29 17:49:14',65,'le','new york city is the greatest. the pearl of the civilized world. everyone is really friendly. when i went there i ended up drinking with people i just met, got invited to random parties, all kinds of things! it\\\'s a fun place.',4,228,0,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(18848,3096,'2002-10-29 18:02:16',314,'eve','That was ... WOW. It was like reading the second chapter of an amazingly crafted book. The imagery, the characters, the details, everything fit together perfectly and flowed. That would be a book I would buy.',0,208,0,0,'80.139.143.79','en'),(18849,3114,'2002-10-29 18:08:34',226,'superbacana','nyc reprazent',2,13,0,0,'64.236.233.243','en'),(18850,3114,'2002-10-29 18:44:30',17,'austin','that is because you have orange dreadlocks and walk around in underpants.',2,73,65,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18851,3114,'2002-10-29 19:22:35',189,'cyantist','i\\\'ve never actually thought of san francisco in that regard. (re: moods).  my perception of the weather here is forever changed!',1,131,59,0,'216.39.112.17','en'),(18852,3117,'2002-10-29 20:10:55',175,'Paul G','I used to box with a bunch of guys; one of those guys was named Art.  Art was a really big Polish boy; he was a type common to Chicago.  \r\n\r\n<p>Anyway, one day Art was at work in the lab.  He claimed - quite suddenly - that the screen on his computer had turned in to a tunnel.  Art said that Doberman pinschers ran out of the screen/tunnel and into a hole in the air in another part of the lab.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>After he started taking meds, Art seemed to be better.  I even felt comfortable enough to lace up with him again.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But, when we had the gloves on, Art proceeded to tell me that he had a new understanding of human consciousness: \\\"The brain,\\\" he said, \\\"is like a walnut.\\\"  \\\"And if you crack the shell, it\\\'s better.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Art\\\'s eyes got real big.  He then started to try to crack my shell/skull.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And that was the last time that I boxed with Art.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The end.</p>',0,919,0,0,'67.195.182.94','en'),(18853,3096,'2002-10-29 20:11:05',421,'chimeres','I\\\'m hooked deep.  You\\\'ve really pulled me hard into this world of yours with this series.  I want more.  You going to let us know when we can get the book, or are you going to disappear without a word as if we don\\\'t deserve a final farewell? ',2,251,0,0,'207.113.160.173','en'),(18854,3099,'2002-10-29 21:18:02',74,'jo','peter, no disrespect because i\\\'m sure that nyc IS much safer than most people make it out to be, but you\\\'re a 6\\\' + white male. that  makes you safer than most people. plus, safety is relative: i define safe as someplace where i feel perfectly fine walking home alone after midnight. but i realize that i can\\\'t do that in providence, ri, let alone nyc, so.',1,367,66,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(18855,3117,'2002-10-29 21:56:40',129,'moebius','every time i take a break from swinney i return to a surprise. rock on lydia! i knew your stuff would work well here.',1,117,415,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(18856,3114,'2003-03-05 23:09:34',129,'moebius','<br>i was in love with san francisco from the moment i moved here back in \\\'91. but around \\\'95 i let my career start to pull me away from the city; after five years it seemed like i wasn\\\'t living here at all. i was work-focused, cubicle minded, living only in brief stolen moments.<br><br>coming back to the city after the crash was one of the best things that\\\'s happened to me in the past couple of years. i\\\'ve managed to reaquaint myself with its fogbound avenues and crooked alleys, learn new secrets from once familiar neighborhoods, and get my hearbeat back in time with the wavebreaker foghorn pulse of the barbary coast once again. it\\\'s been like waking from a dream...or perhaps leaping back into one.<br><br>sooner or later i will probably leave sf. but no matter where i go next sf will always be home.',0,829,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(18857,3114,'2002-10-29 22:22:57',129,'moebius','also: i lived in toronto long before i had a chance to visit nyc. and i\\\'ve always thought toronto was a kick-ass town. every kind of diversity and difference you could want, but perhaps without as much of the razors edge that you get down in the states. also: people there are snazzy dressers. or at least that\\\'s the way it seemed back in \\\'89.',0,352,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(18858,3117,'2002-10-29 22:38:52',415,'lydia','that\\\'s so fucking hilarious',0,30,175,0,'172.193.49.14','en'),(18859,3117,'2002-10-29 22:39:19',415,'lydia','lydia loves moebius!',0,20,129,0,'172.193.49.14','en'),(18860,3121,'2002-10-30 01:44:37',66,'Peter','<br><tt>so, whats the recipe?</tt>',1,34,0,0,'66.108.186.66','en'),(18861,3121,'2002-10-30 01:54:44',90,'leonard','How much did my wife pay you to say that?',1,41,66,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(18862,3121,'2002-10-30 01:57:36',66,'Peter','<br><tt>im just in it for a slice of the pie.</tt>',1,50,0,0,'66.108.186.66','en'),(18863,3121,'2002-10-30 07:19:29',124,'gus','In the covers one I liked your wife, even with the drama, it was twisted but somehow sweet. In this one, I want to know how you kept yourself from slamming her head in the fridge door.',0,184,90,0,'64.169.160.111','en'),(18864,3121,'2002-10-30 08:02:56',17,'austin','or like that scene in Lock Stock and Two Smoking Barrels where the hit man takes that dude\\\'s head and slams it in the car door repeatedly... only with the fridge door.',4,170,124,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18865,3121,'2002-10-30 15:06:39',195,'matt','<I><B>I\\\'ll suck your dick for a piece of that pie</I></B><BR><BR>so funny',1,76,0,0,'209.208.108.6','en'),(18866,3125,'2002-10-30 17:35:58',90,'leonard','This make me think of how I never ride my bike anymore.',1,55,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(18867,3096,'2002-10-30 18:20:42',377,'dammit','haha. do i look like the kind of guy that would do something like that? wait-- don\\\'t answer. i\\\'m slaving away on it every night. i\\\'ll keep posting chapters as long as you promise to still buy a copy when it\\\'s done. kinkos here i come!',0,246,421,0,'66.122.107.46','en'),(18868,3122,'2002-10-30 18:23:23',124,'gus','when I read this I want a little more meat. I mean I guess I know what you\\\'re talking about and its good not to hit anyone over the head, but there\\\'s almost nothing here for me to grasp. ',0,193,0,0,'64.169.160.111','en'),(18869,3123,'2002-10-30 18:25:59',175,'Paul G','Oh man.  Tension building.',0,26,0,0,'67.195.65.167','en'),(18870,3121,'2002-10-30 18:28:41',124,'gus','Yah, I havent seen that, but I can picture it. Only he wouldnt slam her head so hard, and the edge of the fridge has that rubberly stuff to make the cold seal inside that would only leave a dull ache and not a deep gash.',0,220,17,0,'64.169.160.111','en'),(18871,3121,'2002-10-30 18:53:28',123,'dcc','it\\\'s a high claim that your mom\\\'s recipe is the best, when, clearly, my mom\\\'s recipe is the best. and it does, like any *proper* banana bread recipe, include banana\\\'s that are almost completely rotten.',4,213,0,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(18872,3121,'2002-10-30 18:58:09',66,'Peter','<br><tt>im still wondering when were going to see some of these fabled recipes...</tt>',1,86,123,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(18873,3121,'2002-10-30 19:29:49',129,'moebius','slamming your lover\\\'s head in the fridge door! well, there\\\'s an argument resolution strategy i\\\'ve not yet seen discussed on Dr Phil....',0,144,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(18874,3121,'2002-10-30 20:25:34',90,'leonard','As if!',1,6,123,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(18875,3125,'2002-10-30 22:43:01',421,'chimeres','Ahh... I needed a reminder.  and Zelda kicked ass.',2,50,0,0,'207.113.163.18','en'),(18876,3121,'2002-10-30 23:02:31',57,'weeb','actually, *I* have the best banana bread recipe of all time. it involves dates and spectrum spread. oh lordy.',0,109,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(18877,3121,'2002-10-30 23:06:19',74,'jo','oh my god. banana bread. oh good lord.\r\nwe had this recipe when i was younger called \\\"aloha bread\\\" which was banana bread with pineapple juice and coconut and it was the BEST BANANA BREAD EVER. that bread was better with grossly ripe bananas, too.',0,253,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(18878,3121,'2002-10-30 23:07:07',74,'jo','you know what i love? when the crust of zucchini bread is almost bitter. and then the inside is all sweet. drooooool. okay i have to go home and make some damned bread.',0,168,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(18879,3121,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i thought this swinney was great.',6,33,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18880,3121,'2002-10-30 23:11:43',124,'gus','I dont even like gross fruit and vegge breads, and you guys are making me hungry.',0,81,0,0,'64.169.160.111','en'),(18881,3121,'2002-10-30 23:21:59',66,'Peter','<br><tt>once again, i wont believe any of you until i see a <i>recipe</i>...</tt>',1,81,0,0,'66.108.186.66','en'),(18882,3121,'2002-10-31 00:00:15',129,'moebius','i just want the recipe so i can make it for molls and tell her it was <i>my</i> mom\\\'s invention. <br><br>of course, she\\\'ll just be thrilled to see me cooking. if she doesn\\\'t throw out the banannas.',0,206,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(18883,3121,'2002-10-31 00:07:12',83,'adamajah','good swintry, chap',2,18,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(18884,3124,'2002-10-31 05:52:42',93,'chiamattt','dude. FUCK THE MILITARY. ugh. you didnt like the shit you had to put up with at the film board,,,but you are willing to put up with the shit in the military. yer dreaming if you think its any better. ',3,200,0,0,'211.32.132.227','en'),(18885,3124,'2002-10-31 06:58:05',175,'Paul G','Herman Melville is supposed to have written the best American book: <i><a href=\\\"http://www.princeton.edu/~batke/moby/moby_001.html\\\">Moby Dick</a></i>.\r\n\r\n<p>Oh, people can argue about the word \\\"best,\\\" and the worth of something that was first published in 1851 after being written by a European male.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But, Corey, my dear Albino Sasquatch, if you consider the <i>The Whale</i> - especially the first paragraph - I think that you may find something akin between Ishmael and yourself.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>If the military is your Ocean, then go.</i>  Working as a medic might be a good way to transition to a municipal Fire Dept.  Be creative.  And, good luck.</p>',0,675,0,0,'67.195.65.111','en'),(18886,3123,'2002-10-31 20:18:56',82,'simon','thing one: gus, i love when you keep things building with each installment. good job.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nthing two: this makes me think of the cute 19 year old girl named felicia who tried to pick me up on the bus last year. i wanted to call her. i think her number might even still be on my cell phone.',1,295,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(18887,2843,'2002-10-31 22:17:30',195,'matt','yeah, miles is the shit, man.',1,29,0,0,'209.208.110.111','en'),(18888,3122,'2002-10-31 22:32:32',371,'Kyla','Aheheheh.  It\\\'s CHANGED!  Now your comment looks morbid.  :)',6,63,124,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18889,3124,'2002-11-01 00:16:31',176,'Scapegoat','Thank you for your words. I\\\'m leaning more in the direction of the military. The largest stubbling block for me has been the way most people view the military and those in it. High time at the age of 25 that I start making decisions based on my own values though. I might even be able to get the Can. military to pay for me to go to law school which could translate into a nice civilian career after 5-10 years service.<br><br>\r\n \r\n',0,435,175,0,'65.93.220.247','en'),(18890,3124,'2002-11-01 00:18:49',176,'Scapegoat','May my dreams be realized.',0,26,93,0,'65.93.220.247','en'),(18891,3126,'2002-11-01 01:43:10',57,'weeb','<p>i just got home. i was tired and life wasn\\\'t very good. then i read this, and life got very good again.\r\n</p><p>\r\nholy shit dammit, this is really good. all the chapters are really good. i think you should polish it all up and really publish it. it\\\'s got everything, pirates of penzance, love, vomit, beer, the uptown (oops sorry, the midtown) and well i don\\\'t know what else to say that wouldn\\\'t turn your head and stop you from editing the last 5% of this excellent material.\r\n</p><p>\r\nhey, you don\\\'t pour beer into the trash! <- my only feeble constructive crit\r\n</p><p>\r\nhot damn! thank you for writing here.</p>',1,634,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(18892,3126,'2003-05-10 16:09:14',79,'yeti','Nice, dammit! I enjoyed reading this. It made me happy to see Raymond and Peter ressurected in this with a twist. Good stuff!',1,125,0,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(18893,3126,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','oh dammit.  this is so good.',6,28,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18894,3126,'2002-11-01 05:25:40',65,'le','poor marshall.\r\n',4,16,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18895,3126,'2002-11-01 07:03:50',421,'chimeres','<p>Oooooh! I promise I\\\'ll buy it... keep writing, keep posting!  Marshall is so sad, so sweet and lost.</p>  <p>Haha...Reminds me of an old old ex-boyfriend who dumped me for his first love, then had a semi-psychotic episode crying about how his cat was the only one who loved him, and then shacked up with some chubby short girl who ironed for him. teehee...  maybe it was the drugs... Can\\\'t wait to see what happens here!</p>',1,433,0,0,'66.123.206.106','en'),(18896,3126,'2002-11-01 19:56:45',129,'moebius','no swinny writer has made me more jealous of their storytelling talents than dammit. my only question: why hasn\\\'t he set up his stories as a series so i can easily read them in the correct order?',1,198,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(18897,3126,'2002-11-01 20:29:07',377,'dammit','the rewriting process began with \\\"your father\\\'s funeral\\\" and continues from there. from that post on, the chapters are in their correct sequence--although there may, in the future, be a chapter about marshall\\\'s workplace inserted between \\\"hand of glory\\\" and \\\"ghosts of the midtown.\\\" \r\n<p>\r\ni just wanted to thank you all for your very kind words. i probably wouldn\\\'t have persisted this far without your encouragement. only twenty-two more chapters to rewrite ;)',0,489,129,0,'66.122.107.46','en'),(18898,3126,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i was going to make a series for dammit but then i thought that was kinda weird.',6,80,129,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18899,3130,'2002-11-02 06:28:15',17,'austin','oh fuck',3,7,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18900,3130,'2002-11-02 06:29:31',17,'austin','i have killed animals that i found hurt badly.  i never really thought much of it.  i\\\'m talking about wounded birds.  once i thought about killing a cat that had been mauled by dogs.  i didnt though.  ',4,204,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18901,3130,'2002-11-02 06:58:30',154,'ryan','i was with some friends once and we hit a deer.  the deer ended up in a ditch, screaming.  deer are not supposed to scream and people are not supposed to hear deer scream.  eric got out of the truck, went down into the ditch, and shot it in the head.  it was, to him, an automatic response.  it still sits heavy with me, but you are right, what can one do?<p>another time, my first cat was hit by a car while i was out in my parent\\\'s yard mowing the lawn.  the person just drove off.  i went down to see the cat, she was still alive, with one eye hanging out and a large bruise on the top of her head.  all i could do then, was pet it and talk to it until it died, making sure i was touching it the entire time.<p>chances are the dog was in shock, so perhaps it was beyond pain at that point.',2,795,0,0,'65.58.157.147','en'),(18902,3130,'2002-11-02 16:45:10',90,'leonard','This dog was just another obstacle in your life and you ran over it just fine. Next time, ask your mom to wait at the end of the driveway while you get your truck.',1,163,0,0,'216.249.107.74','en'),(18903,3130,'2002-11-02 20:48:14',176,'Scapegoat','It has always amazed me that in the case of animals when things are beyond the point of repair, we will often do something to shorten the length of suffering. With other humans though we like to watch them to the bitter end. I don\\\'t think I will ever understand why this is.',1,277,0,0,'65.95.141.32','en'),(18904,3126,'2002-11-02 20:51:51',124,'gus','oh. hi, I was supposed to write a faq about making a series. maybe I\\\'ll do that now, and send it to dammit. heh. oopsie.\r\n\r\n',0,127,0,0,'64.163.214.13','en'),(18905,3130,'2002-11-02 21:27:03',124,'gus','I read this and then all I can think is how you ended up with such a lack of compassion? not that you have to have compassion for a dog you hit if you dont want to, but coupled with the mom stuff, it shines through. ',0,216,0,0,'64.163.214.13','en'),(18906,3130,'2002-11-02 21:35:38',65,'le','this is fiction.',4,16,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18907,3124,'2002-11-02 21:42:29',75,'tyler','i feel obligated to say that the military sucks ass. in too many ways to describe well.',1,87,0,0,'165.247.164.216','en'),(18908,3116,'2002-11-02 21:47:02',75,'tyler','ny, ny, ny, ny.',1,15,0,0,'165.247.164.216','en'),(18909,3130,'2002-11-02 22:13:34',154,'ryan','who the fuck is mad moll?',6,25,0,0,'65.58.2.184','en'),(18910,3130,'2002-11-02 22:27:04',124,'gus','heh. yah? well good! and I believed it, so thats good too. heh.',0,63,65,0,'64.163.214.13','en'),(18911,3122,'2002-11-02 22:29:04',124,'gus','hurrah! Im morbid!',0,18,371,0,'64.163.214.13','en'),(18912,3130,'2002-11-02 22:34:16',314,'eve','I also hit a dog once. I was driving before I had a license at night on a country road. It didn\\\'t run across the road. The only thing I saw as I turned off my brights for an oncoming car were two white spotlights staring me in the face and a large black figure sprawled across my entire lane. \r\n\r\nWhen I hit this already hit dog, it thwacked my car and I felt my feet jump as my tires went over it\\\'s head. I was terrified and I felt horrible. Later I thought about the dog, the fact that it was already hit, and felt maybe I had put it out of it\\\'s misery. I thought a lot about the fact that maybe that was someone\\\'s dog, and it could have been mine. I refused to go back down the road with the thought that I might see it crawling across the road. I got lost on the way home.',0,788,0,0,'80.139.158.166','en'),(18913,3130,'2002-11-02 22:40:44',82,'simon','who the fuck is ryan?',0,21,154,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(18914,3130,'2002-11-02 23:31:44',65,'le','i mean, i am pretty sure it is fiction.<br><br>\r\nmad moll and ryan are both my friends. they should hang out. and chop wood together.',4,133,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18915,3130,'2002-11-02 23:37:46',421,'chimeres','When my pet rat Lenny started to bleed from the eyes (cancer i was told), my dad guillotine\\\'d him with a chinese butcher knife.  It\\\'s still his favorite knife.  ',0,167,0,0,'209.232.55.150','en'),(18916,3130,'2002-11-03 00:39:26',240,'xep','the only time i\\\'ve ever tried driving a dog ran in front of the car and i swerved to avoid it and nearly went off the road into ditch.  i decided then that i shouldn\\\'t drive.',0,180,0,0,'144.132.117.240','en'),(18917,3130,'2002-11-03 05:20:42',17,'austin','you should not drive because you wont run over dogs?',4,52,240,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18918,3114,'2002-11-03 05:22:10',18,'bcm','lately i feel like i\\\'ve forgotten everything about this town. i haven\\\'t been to a film in weeks, taken a long walk in months. my new job and all the other crazy things i\\\'m busy with have kept me on the move. i think maybe it\\\'s time to slow down again.\r\n',0,265,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(18919,3111,'2002-11-03 05:26:31',18,'bcm','but first they have to find the overgrown temple and deal with the monkey demons that infest the ruins.\r\n\r\none thing they know for sure, having been warned by every ostler, stableboy and merchant within two dozen leagues: DON\\\'T GO DOWN THE WELL!\r\n',0,250,17,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(18920,3111,'2002-11-03 05:27:15',18,'bcm','soundtrack: \\\"these dreams\\\" by heart',0,41,17,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(18921,3130,'2002-11-03 05:32:32',18,'bcm','just outside the montgomery bart station, some crazy guy hauled off and kicked a pigeon. there was a loud thump, not unlike the sound a soccer ball makes when you kick it, and feathers flew everywhere. i think he must have just clipped it, because i watched it fly off across market street. i asked him \\\"what the fuck is your problem?\\\" he didn\\\'t answer. it ocurred to me about a block up second street that i should have followed up with a kick of my own.\r\n',0,466,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(18922,2457,'2002-11-03 05:41:06',18,'bcm','for what it\\\'s worth, i did show up in ithaca this summer. i even brought elly along so she could see the campus, my frat house and all the spots where i was arrested and where i booted.\r\n',0,190,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(18923,3130,'2002-11-03 08:17:30',174,'Mad Moll','chimeres: your dad is fucking cool.',6,35,421,0,'24.189.13.22','en'),(18924,3124,'2002-11-03 10:42:16',93,'chiamattt','may you get everything you want. ',0,33,176,0,'61.37.53.107','en'),(18925,3130,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','but then, who is worse?  a guy kicking a pidgeon or a guy kicking a guy?',6,72,18,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18926,3130,'2002-11-04 12:07:14',17,'austin','i am thinking, the guy has probably been kicked quite a bit already.\r\n',2,70,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18927,3130,'2002-11-04 13:06:25',240,'xep','i shouldn\\\'t drive because i\\\'ll risk my own life before hitting a dog.  on the bike it\\\'s easier to avoid things.',0,120,17,0,'144.132.122.131','en'),(18928,3130,'2002-11-04 14:44:20',123,'dcc','i\\\'ve wondered the same thing for a long time. what is the selfishness in holding on to a dying loved one?',0,108,176,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(18929,3130,'2002-11-04 15:49:38',18,'bcm','aw, maybe i should\\\'ve handed him a dollar and sympathized with his oppressed condition!<br><br>\r\nhe actually looked like a normal guy, except for the blank expression.\r\n',0,172,17,0,'64.84.39.162','en'),(18930,3130,'2002-11-04 16:41:24',124,'gus','a blank expression is normal too. ',0,34,18,0,'64.169.160.49','en'),(18931,2858,'2002-11-04 16:42:12',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i posted this about 5 months ago. this past month, my landlady discovered roberto\\\'s presence, and has decided to evict him. she also informed me that he was tapped into my electricity... well, i guess that explains the $100/month electricity bill. also, i\\\'ll inherit his space. admittedly, though, it\\\'ll be odd not having him around...</tt>',1,357,0,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(18932,2762,'2002-11-04 16:59:07',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i used to ride the path twice a week. now, my job no longer takes me to newark, and i miss it. scenes of urban wastelands and jersey sprawl. sigh.</tt>',1,159,174,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(18933,2970,'2002-11-04 17:48:40',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>minature steam powered automaton blacksmith</i> makes for some great imagery.</tt>',1,88,0,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(18934,3135,'2002-11-04 19:35:39',371,'Kyla','Sigh.  This makes me want to write about my own love story.. I wish I could write as eloquently as this though :)',6,113,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18935,3130,'2002-11-04 21:48:19',83,'adamajah','if my Bridget got run over, i would probably need therapy',0,57,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(18936,3134,'2002-11-04 21:50:53',83,'adamajah','you put a lot into everything you write on swinney.  what motivates your creativity?',0,84,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(18937,3134,'2002-11-05 01:17:01',122,'hool','unemployment! ha. actually, it\\\'s loneliness.  when i\\\'m making things, writing or otherwise, it\\\'s like i\\\'m talking to friends, even though i might not know them just yet.  i see a lot of beauty and i feel very lonely if i\\\'m the only witness.',0,255,83,0,'66.185.85.80','en'),(18938,3130,'2002-11-05 04:12:39',175,'Paul G','OK.  But, if you did throw a kick, what kind of dice would you have rolled for damage?',0,86,18,0,'67.195.31.151','en'),(18939,3130,'2002-11-05 04:41:36',17,'austin','i was implying that i recognise some people are insane for reasons.  it does not make me like them, or even give a shit about them.  in fact, i don\\\'t care about the pigeon either  i am a nihilist, i believe in nothing.\r\n',0,223,18,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18940,3130,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','WHATEVER AUSTIE!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!',6,31,17,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18941,3134,'2002-11-05 08:23:30',370,'seanthecon','sean the con likes this very much. sean the con was stung by the words.of hool. much like the sting when you realize you\\\'re out of colt 45. sean the con knows hool very well so these words are very powerful. sean the con refers to himself in the third person because the po-po keep interrogating and cross examinating sean the con. sean the con will not be broken.',0,367,122,0,'216.129.68.81','en'),(18942,3134,'2002-11-09 06:34:51',93,'chiamattt','chiamattt has fucked sean the con in the ass with a butter knife. sean the con was asleep but sean the con had a terribly eerie grin on his face. \r\n',0,148,370,0,'61.37.53.25','en'),(18943,1442,'2002-11-05 21:12:15',86,'egg','hi. I feel so different now than I felt during this post. 2003 is the year of gut-wrenching emotion. that is my fashion prediction for now. we are gut-wrenching it up for winter, ladies.',0,186,0,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(18944,3135,'2002-11-05 21:33:14',86,'egg','yum',0,3,0,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(18945,3121,'2002-11-05 21:35:19',86,'egg','why you gotta be my hero',0,24,0,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(18946,3130,'2002-11-06 14:45:39',366,'eggers','<p>A guy that kicks a pigeon might be doing it a favor I think. Those damn birds are fat and lazy, living off the excess of humans.<b> \r\n\r\nThey rarely fly away from death.<b> \r\n\r\nThe birds just fall over on the sidewalk do to their own inertia.<b> \r\n\r\nBut the dog may have some will to survive, only its silent.<b> \r\n\r\nSilence breeds contempt.<b> \r\n\r\nOnly those with a voice can have rights.</p>',0,395,0,0,'193.167.219.184','en'),(18947,2958,'2002-11-06 15:13:12',57,'weeb','eunoia, i really like your writing. whooo hooot! ',1,49,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(18948,3130,'2002-11-06 17:33:01',17,'austin','well put, dave!',1,15,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(18949,1442,'2002-11-06 18:45:35',82,'simon','yeah, that seems about right. we did gay it up for fall, now let\\\'s gutwretch it up for winter! i just bought some great shoes to go with that.',1,145,86,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(18950,3138,'2002-11-06 19:40:57',74,'jo','also, people who don\\\'t vote can suck it. seriously. if you don\\\'t vote, you fucking suck.',0,94,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(18951,1442,'2002-11-06 19:45:42',195,'matt','whatever happened to kat?',1,25,0,0,'209.208.110.56','en'),(18952,3138,'2002-11-06 20:28:27',154,'ryan','fascist.  ',0,10,74,0,'65.58.158.203','en'),(18953,3138,'2002-11-06 20:36:17',79,'yeti','If you didnt vote, you shouldnt complain about politics. Either take part in it or dont, but dont complain about something you wont take part in. It is your right and duty to do your part to steer the direction of this country, and this includes voting and jury duty. Non-participation does not mean protest, it means you\\\'re sitting on your ass.\r\n\r\nBest friendly!',1,366,0,0,'206.111.131.88','en'),(18954,3138,'2002-11-06 20:40:00',74,'jo','it\\\'s <a href=\\\"http://www.dictionary.com/search?q=fascism\\\">fascist</a> to say that people who don\\\'t exercise their right to take part in a democratic government suck. hmm. interesting.',0,195,154,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(18955,3138,'2002-11-06 20:40:45',74,'jo','i heart you, all the time.',0,26,79,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(18956,3138,'2002-11-06 20:42:20',66,'Peter','<br><tt>no, i think its the folks that dont vote and <i>then</i> bitch and whine about the government that suck. if you dont whine, its ok if you dont vote, heh. [note: i voted]</tt>',1,182,74,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(18957,3138,'2002-11-06 20:48:52',74,'jo','granted, but can you think of a single person that you know who doesn\\\'t whine about politics <b>ever</b>, even if it\\\'s just some snide comment about how we\\\'re (most likely) going to war soon? i can\\\'t. did all of those people vote? not likely. if they\\\'d voted, is it possible that we wouldn\\\'t have a republican-controlled house and senate now, a legislation that will, without doubt, push us closer to war? pretty likely. so i return to the \\\"if you didn\\\'t vote, you suck\\\" stance.',1,505,66,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(18958,3138,'2002-11-06 21:18:11',262,'szajic','To quote ryan on <a href=\\\"http://nedia.net/\\\">nedia.net</a>:  \r\n\r\nFuck you if you didn\\\'t vote.  Fuck you.',4,113,0,0,'18.56.1.250','en'),(18959,3134,'2002-11-09 06:34:51',370,'seanthecon','again. i have no idea who you are and please stop calling me on the phone late and night with requests for me to shit on your face. if this continues i will be forced to give you a cleveland steamer.',0,199,93,0,'216.129.68.83','en'),(18960,3138,'2002-11-06 22:28:10',20,'jim','you know, I used to be really tolerant of people not voting, but two years of a Bush presidency that could have been avoided if all the smart, progressive and lazy people between the ages of 18 and 35 hadn\'t believed the lie that their vote didn\'t matter has changed all that for me.\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\njo said it best.  if you didn\'t vote, you fucking suck.  I don\'t see how it\'s fascist to suggest that a citizen of a free nation who takes advantage of that hard-won freedom to avoid spending an hour a year going to the polls fucking sucks.  in a nation poised on the brink of war, run by a controversially narrowly elected president, staying home on voting day is inexcusable.\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nthat doesn\'t keep otherwise intelligent people from trying.  \"I don\'t know enough about the issues to vote\" is the hip excuse this year, replacing last year\'s favorite, \"my vote doesn\'t count\" which sounds too ridiculous even to non-voters after Bush was elected by the number of people who read this website.  I don\'t see how that\'s any different from saying \"I\"m too important to spend a few hours forming opinions about what life in this city, this state, this nation should be like, as will be defined by the people and the propositions on this ballot.\"  you couldn\'t even pick just two or three issues and go vote on those?  you couldn\'t read the ONE SENTENCE LONG Proposition S encouraging better systems for distributing medical marijuana to cancer patients and go vote on that? that\'s the kind of self-centered apathy that allows power-hungry dictators to take control of nations.  it fucking sucks, and I\'m not going to pretend I find it excusable anymore.',6,1707,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(18961,3138,'2002-11-06 22:30:00',90,'leonard','Do you think fascists get facials?',1,34,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(18962,3138,'2002-11-06 22:45:34',371,'Kyla','I see these discussions every time a nation has an election.\r\n\r\nI\\\'m amazed that no one has learned by now that these arguments are pointless, as are <i>any</i> arguments to do with personal choice.\r\n\r\nLeave it up to the individual, that\\\'s what I say.  I\\\'m pro-choice!\r\n',6,278,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18963,3138,'2002-11-06 22:55:41',20,'jim','you\\\'re right.  people never change their minds based on what other people they know think.  whatever was I thinking?\r\n\r\n',6,123,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(18964,3138,'2002-11-06 22:57:09',20,'jim','you\\\'d think the advertising industry would have learned that by now, too!  those silly people, thinking personal choice is susceptible to any kind of influence!  you could save them all that money.',6,200,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(18965,3138,'2002-11-06 23:09:12',20,'jim','wait, why am I bothering to write this at all?  you\\\'ve already made up your mind about this, and as you said, it\\\'s impervious to change!',0,142,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(18966,3138,'2002-11-06 23:48:21',371,'Kyla','Uhhh... alrighty!',6,17,20,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18967,3138,'2002-11-07 00:00:46',83,'adamajah','i think maybe if i was unemployed i would have voted.',0,53,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(18968,3138,'2002-11-07 00:05:37',371,'Kyla','<p>\r\nCrikey, didn\\\'t think my small comment about not arguing would start an argument.  You either didn\\\'t understand my point or you\\\'re reaching pretty far for argument\\\'s sake..\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAnd no, I don\\\'t think people should changed their minds based on what other people think.  That\\\'s dumb - not to mention a bloody scary thought.  As for your advertising(??) comments, totally irrelevant.  Bizarre.\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nAlso, I don\\\'t remember saying anywhere that my mind is impervious to change.  All I said was that I\\\'m pro-choice, and that individuals should be able to do what they want.  Including the right to not vote, or vote, as they choose, and not be critized by anyone else.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNext time read carefully!\r\n</p>',6,748,20,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18969,3138,'2002-11-07 01:19:51',371,'Kyla','I just read that over and it sounds bitchier than what I meant, but I still mean what I said!',6,93,20,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(18970,3138,'2002-11-07 02:15:39',189,'cyantist','every year the same topic comes up, i agree, however if i wasn\\\'t pushed by all of my peers to get over all of my laziness, i wouldn\\\'t have cared.  now, i care.  thank you everyone!',1,186,0,0,'216.39.112.17','en'),(18971,3138,'2002-11-07 03:48:54',17,'austin','i am with jim.  i think it is my right to bug jim to vote, or challenge him to form ideas about anything else.  if during and after my pestering, he still does not vote, i\\\'ll bug him afterwards!  persistence! why?  because it is important to me. this is the foundation of the democratic system, arguing and pestering.  if there is not arguing and pestering, what is there?\r\n<BR><BR>\r\na nation of people not arguing or pestering, taking whatever comes down the hierarchy for them to suck on.',3,484,371,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(18972,3138,'2002-11-07 03:51:54',17,'austin','RE: this happens every year...\r\n<BR><BR>\r\none of my favorite memories from the study of athens was an archaeological find of a heap of clay ballots tossed into the dump.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nsome think it is evidence of ballot fraud, dumping the votes.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nLONG LIVE DEMOCRACY!',3,274,189,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(18973,3138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','if you voted, you have no right to whine about the government.  by voting, you are inferring your support of the current electoral process.  you agree to whatever outcome happens.<br />\r\n<br />\r\nif you didn\\\'t vote, you have every right to complain that the government and electoral process is still not functioning in a way you can support.<br />\r\n<br />\r\nwhatever.  fuck you to everyone who hates me because i don\\\'t vote.  fuck you for thinking you are better than me.  fuck you for making assumptions about my reasons.  fuck you for thinking that i can\\\'t voice my opinions because i don\\\'t agree with you and do something you wanted me to do.  fuck you, fuck you, and fuck you.<br />\r\n<br />\r\nthat said, let\\\'s talk about it.  let\\\'s try and convince each other.  let\\\'s be passionate and share ideas.  let\\\'s exersize tolerance and compassion.  let\\\'s promote understanding instead of blind hate.  let\\\'s recognise higher goals.  let\\\'s be friends.\r\n\r\n',6,980,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18974,3138,'2002-11-07 04:25:55',20,'jim','how will not voting change the political process?',6,49,62,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(18975,3138,'2002-11-07 04:30:41',20,'jim','exactly.  I see the challenging of personal assumptions one of the chief responsibilities of a community.\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nKyla said, \\\"As for your advertising(??) comments, totally irrelevant.\\\"\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nKyla also said, \\\" I\\\'m amazed that no one has learned by now that these arguments are pointless, as are any arguments to do with personal choice.\\\"\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nmy point is that advertising is an entire industry built around the simple fact that people\\\'s personal choices (what products to buy, what media to listen to, where to live, what to eat) are easily influenced by new information, and that arguments that present new information are far from pointless. \r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nKyla said, \\\"And no, I don\\\'t think people should changed their minds based on what other people think. That\\\'s dumb - not to mention a bloody scary thought.\\\"\r\n\r\nit\\\'s a bloody scary thought that  what other people think will change the way you think?  really?  what do you see as the purpose of media if not to effect the way other people think?  what\\\'s the purpose of education, if not to effect the way other people think?  I can\\\'t imagine what the world would be like if I were scared by the idea that other people might do or say something that would change the way I think.  \r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nKyla said, \\\"All I said was that I\\\'m pro-choice, and that individuals should be able to do what they want. Including the right to not vote, or vote, as they choose, and not be critized by anyone else.\\\"\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\ncritical discussion allows people to see issues from perspectives they wouldn\\\'t have considered on their own.  I don\\\'t find that scary, I find that to be an essential aspect of being a thinking person.\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\none perspective on not voting is that people can do whatever they want, and that\\\'s great.\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nanother perspective on not voting is that one of the greatest problems we have to face as an nation is that more than half of our citizens have become so apathetic that they are willing to allow the choices of a minority make the rules they will live by, and that real social progress is impossible until that changes in a real and meaningful way.\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nhow is trying to understand the reasoning behind those perspectives pointless?  how else can a community understand each other?  that\\\'s a perspective I don\\\'t understand at all.',6,2436,17,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(18976,3138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i think assuming that most people don\\\'t vote out of apathy is a large one to make.  there have to many and varied reasons.  i guess if you really want to you can write those reasons off as apathy too, but you can write a lot of things off as things they\\\'re not.<br />\r\n<br />\r\nvoting for people you don\\\'t agree with to be in charge of you just doesn\\\'t go down well with some people.  refusal to vote for the \\\"lesser of evils\\\" is a form of protest, so sorry if you don\\\'t recognise it as such, but let\\\'s give a little weight to intent shall we?  you may see this as having no impact, having no result apart from allowing mean-spirited evil people to rule over you, but voting still works the same whether 100% of people vote or 25% do - the holder of the majority vote wins.  that\\\'s it!<br />\r\n<br />\r\npeople are concered at the statistics of non-voters in the the usa.  where will this concern take us?  investigation into why people feel the need to boycott voting?  maybe a little progress?  maybe at least a little thought on how perhaps the governmental structure and voting procedure is just not happening for a very, very large chunk of the population?  not voting does make a difference.  just like voting does.  the value of each decision depends on what side of the fence you\\\'re on.',6,1320,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18977,3138,'2002-11-07 04:55:37',20,'jim','you said \"voting still works the same whether 100% of people vote or 25% do - the holder of the majority vote wins. that\'s it!\"\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nthe problem is that the 25% of the people who do vote tend to be white, upper class, and over 35. \r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nwhat specific positive changes do you see coming out of not voting?   given that the people who are now running the country are more likely to stay in power if people like you don\'t vote,  why do you believe that they will change the way they are governing based on your choice to not vote?  if they want to stay in power, doesn\'t it make more sense that they will do everything possible to not make the kinds of changes you believe not voting will bring about?  and since they are in power, what\'s to stop them?',6,763,62,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(18978,3138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','do you think that forcing people who are not white, upper class and over 35 to vote for people who ARE white, upper class and over 35 to make decisions on their behalf is a good idea?<br />\r\n<br />\r\nnot voting can make a big change, just maybe not the kind that you are thinking of.  not voting can remind you that you, as a human, are able to take an extreme viewpoint on national matters, rather than conceeding and voting for someone who won\\\'t necessarily come to your door and kill you but will probably poison your drinking water anyway.  not voting shows no confidence in the voting system for your country.  not voting can be protest!  what it does help is show that many, many people of voting age do not agree with the current system and are more willing to boycott than offer their implicit approval.<br />',6,819,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18979,3138,'2002-11-07 06:13:44',65,'le','but o, you shouldn\\\'t feel like anyone is attacking you personally over not voting, because you\\\'re not even allowed to vote, are you? i mean, you not voting isn\\\'t a statement of any sort, it\\\'s just happenstance based on your citizenship status. right? or are you just defending people\\\'s right to not vote?',4,319,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18980,3138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i am a citizen of new zealand.  i get to vote there, if i want to.',6,66,65,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18981,3138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','also i wasn\\\'t responding to a personal attack, i was responding in the same vein as jo and yeti and cyan and jim.  they\\\'re all very angry at non-voters in general, so i was turning the tables.',6,198,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18982,3138,'2002-11-07 06:28:47',65,'le','whether or not voting actually \\\"makes a difference,\\\" it is easy, and it can\\\'t hurt. i think voting makes more of a difference at a local level, so getting out to vote on props is important. it really will affect you that crime goes up in the next year because we passed the prop that takes cash payments away from people on welfare and tries to replace them with food/housing vouchers. just for an example. i can\\\'t ever pull back far enough to really grok what\\\'s going on in washingtion, but i really get with the local proposition action. and i\\\'m not ashamed to go vote on some stuff i know about and leave the rest of the ballot blank. i mean, it\\\'s just too easy to vote and possibly be doing some good. it\\\'s silly not to. \r\n<br><br>\r\ni mean are people actually trying to make a statement by not voting? not voting is not like not eating meat or not buying leather. it doesn\\\'t affect the supply and demand of voting. it doesn\\\'t keep you from getting a stomach ache. do people really feel GOOD about not voting? i don\\\'t think so. i think if anything it\\\'s just as much of a compromise as anything else, and if you\\\'re going to compromise, you should compromise in the direction of doing a thing, not the other way around. \r\n<br><br>\r\nbut i don\\\'t feel anger or contempt for people who don\\\'t vote, and i don\\\'t like all the FUCKYOUS in this thread. and all the \\\"poeple who do X suck and people who don\\\'t do X suck.\\\" i don\\\'t think anyone is trying to feel superior to anyone, though, i think people just get heated and forget that they can be articulate without being so adversarial. as with anything else, just continue to pester your friends, make your point, and do what you can. stop telling each other to fuck off tho, no one makes a point like that.\r\n',4,1812,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18983,3138,'2002-11-07 06:32:47',18,'bcm','it is my right to not really care about others\\\' right to take a morally absolute position on my right to make value choices (such as choosing to prepare for my upcoming d&d game instead of educating myself on ballot measures).\r\n<br><br>\r\nsome day you fucking assholes are going to learn that calling people fucking assholes is not likely to increase interest in your causes.\r\n',0,379,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(18984,3138,'2002-11-07 06:33:56',65,'le','<i>some day you fucking assholes are going to learn that calling people fucking assholes is not likely to increase interest in your causes. </i><br><br>amen!',4,157,18,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18985,3138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','well, actually not getting pissed off with people is the point that i was trying to make in my FUCK YOU post.  i was trying to make that clear in the last paragraph.  this explains why i never won any awards in debate.<br />\r\n<br />\r\ni absolutely believe that giving people a hard time about something they have thought through and don\\\'t want to do is not going to do anything but make them more resolved to do what they were going to do anyway.  talking and not judging or jumping to conclusions goes a long way, tho.<br />\r\n<br />\r\nif you are not going to believe me that some people are trying to make a statement by not voting, i guess my posts above and anything i will say will not convince you.<br />\r\n<br />\r\nif you also believe that reducing your own demand for leather and meat does not reduce a global demand for leather and meat, i can\\\'t convince you otherwise either.  but i wonder what good you think your one vote is going to do if you can\\\'t believe that not buying one pair of leather shoes reduces demand for leather.  i also wonder how supply and demand works if it doesn\\\'t come down to individual consumers.<br />\r\n<br />\r\nthe tao says:  do not doing.  so according to that, do i vote or not?  i\\\'m confused ;)<br />\r\n<br />',6,1257,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18986,3138,'2002-11-07 07:06:08',65,'le','well, i don\\\'t think anyone disbelieves that some people are trying to make a statement by not voting, but i really do think the vast majority of people who don\\\'t vote just don\\\'t care, which is really bad. ',0,214,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18987,3138,'2002-11-07 07:22:25',79,'yeti','<p>Your silence is just that. Silence. It\\\'s not protest. I do not see any person I know who does not vote actively involved in anything having to do with the future of this country.</p>\r\n<p>The simple fact of the matter is, we are locked in to a political system that is not likely to change any time soon, and even less likely to change when people claim to not be voting because of some greater political ideals. If you want to bring about positive political change, you will have to do it within the constraints of the political system as it stands now. I think our nation is making steps in the right direction by thinking about campaign reform and about reworking the balloting process.</p>\r\n<p>If you don\\\'t like the evil bastards running our country now, you have a few choices. Get out and vote for someone less evil. People not voting brought us Asscroft and the Bush dynasty.</p>\r\n<p>You are allowed by law to write in candidates for office, and if you don\\\'t like the people printed on the ballot, write someone in. Don\\\'t stay home. Tom Ammiano ended up in a last minute write in campaign against the current mayor in this town, and executed it smashingly, considering his funding and time constraints.</p>\r\n<p>Twenty five percent voter turnout is pretty low, but numbers aside, Republicans in this country have always had better voter turnout, and that is unlikely to change.</p>\r\n<p>I dislike the political climate of this country, and I expect I will continue to do so for quite some time, but I\\\'m not willing to go out and pick up a gun and march on Washington DC, and I\\\'m not willing to sit on my ass thinking that my non-participation is going to change a damn thing. I\\\'d be a fool to think it would. This leaves me with participation as my only option. If I don\\\'t like it now, I\\\'ll try to change it in the future. That is how this country works. What happened to the federalists, or the whigs? Both were powerful political parties here once. They\\\'ve gone, replaced by new ones. What is happening with the Libertarians, the Greens, or the Natural Law party? How about the American Independents or the Socialists? More and more these parties are being noticed, they have thousands more votes than they used to. Things are changing. Why are unions so powerful politically here? Because their members vote. People do count.</p>\r\n<p>This country has not been inclined to look at voter numbers as anything but \\\"Gosh, why doesn\\\'t anyone get out and vote?\\\", if you expect some great political god to suddenly stoop down and realize that the current system is flawed and call you on the telephone asking you for your personal opinion on how the country should be run, think again. It won\\\'t happen.</p>\r\n<p>This country is supposed to be a place where you can be free to make choices for yourself, and if you choose not to vote, I understand that, but are you really willing to not vote when the people we have in office now can detain you as a non-citizen for an unspecified length of time for any reason they feel like? That scares me. It\\\'s things like that that keep me coming to the polls. How about funding cuts for HIV research? Check. How about refusing to fund family planning clinics that offer abortion-related counseling or services? Check. Drilling the Alaskan wilderness for oil? Check. Loosening environmental restrictions for large companies? Check. Bombing the country of Iraq back to the dark ages becoming a family tradition? Check. Loosening logging restrictions on public lands to \\\"avoid forest fires\\\"? Check. At this point, I don\\\'t see how anyone can afford not to vote if they don\\\'t like things how they are now.</p>\r\n<p>Political change is not brought about overnight. It takes patience, participation, and preservation. Voting is your right. Paying taxes, being drafted, serving on a jury, and abiding by the laws that voters establish are not something you have a say in if you choose not to vote.</p>\r\n<p>On the other hand, you could just do nothing and hope it all goes away. It *is* your choice.</p>\r\n',2,4092,0,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(18988,3138,'2002-11-07 07:23:58',59,'s5','i think many people who are not voting out of apathy are more frustrated than apathetic. the difference between the two major US parties is rapidly approaching zero. (don\\\'t forget how many bloodthirsty democrats in congress were willing to vote in favor of war.) even our own governor davis, a democrat, has been widely criticized as supporting big corporations, while turning his back on the working people of california. he\\\'s lucky to have won the election.\r\n',6,467,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18989,3138,'2002-11-07 07:27:35',59,'s5','i think it\\\'s also important to consider that voting isn\\\'t the only way to bring about political change. it\\\'s not even the best way. it is simply the easiest way. i believe that if the enough citizens are frustrated enough with the ruling government, and the elections aren\\\'t changing anything, the result will be revolution.',6,336,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18990,3138,'2002-11-07 07:28:25',59,'s5','disclaimer: i voted, like i always do, for the green party.',0,59,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18991,3138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','yeti, i am surprised that you know what people in governments think and what will change them.  i am surprised to hear that you know exactly what will change your country and what will not.  i am surprised that you think that people who are active non voters spend the rest of their time sitting on their ass and complaining.  it\\\'s a very narrow view to take on a very large amount of people.',6,395,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18992,3138,'2002-11-07 07:31:17',65,'le','how many nonvoters outside of your immediate social group have you spoken to about their choices? i really think if you talk to some people who are in different parts of the country, or are in different  age or class groups than yourself, you\\\'ll find that they really just don\\\'t care, or don\\\'t feel like voting \\\"makes a difference.\\\" i don\\\'t think there\\\'s an overwhelming active frustration or anger with the voting system in america, i think there\\\'s an overwhelming apathy. if there was active frustration, i think there\\\'d be more activism in the direction of changing the political system, rather than just.. not.. voting...',4,652,59,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18993,3138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','but....there is a lot of activism directed towards changing politcal systems.  maybe just not within your own social circle, if we\\\'re resorting to playing that card.',6,168,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18994,3138,'2002-11-07 07:34:30',65,'le','ooh, good point about write-ins, yeti. ',0,39,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18995,3138,'2002-11-07 07:38:03',59,'s5','sure, but that apathy didn\\\'t come out of nowhere. younger people feel no connection to politicians because politicians aren\\\'t campaigning to younger people, (there was a washington post article about this recently.) and instead, focusing on issues like the price of pharmaceuticals, for example.',0,301,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18996,3138,'2002-11-07 07:38:21',65,'le','there is, but i think if every person who wasn\\\'t voting was also supporting a political activist group, there would be a very large nation wide movement against the current political climate. i don\\\'t see that happening tho, therefore i\\\'m inclined to believe that nonvoters don\\\'t actually care too much about what\\\'s going on. ',0,340,62,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(18997,3138,'2002-11-07 07:47:12',59,'s5','i still think that if politicians gave non-voters a reason to care, they would care, maybe a little bit more. but if there are only two choices with any chance of getting in office, and if both choices are equally bland, why would the majority of people be excited about voting? i realize it\\\'s circular reasoning, but unless there are exciting choices, no one is going to care about choosing.\r\n<br><br>\r\nit would be like if i gave you the choice between a lifetime of eating nothing but plain oatmeal, or nothing but raw celery.',0,531,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18998,3138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','you could adjust that to say  \\\"therefore i\\\'m inclined to believe that *some* nonvoters don\\\'t actually care too much about what\\\'s going on.\\\" and then you wouldn\\\'t be lumping well intentioned people in with people who don\\\'t care.  but i guess that is the tone of the thread and what people actually believe.',6,326,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(18999,3138,'2002-11-07 07:53:45',65,'le','is the whole point you are trying to make that some nonvoters are trying to make a point by not voting? i don\\\'t deny that at all, i don\\\'t think anyone denies that. i just think that it\\\'s not a large enough group of people to be making a difference right now, and that not-doing, when not combined with other forms of activism, cannot get you too far. also i think the type of nonvoting people are angry about here is apathetic or disempowered nonvoting.  don\\\'t you agree that apathy and a feeling of disempowerment is sad and should be avoided? in any context, not just in the political system?\r\n<br><br>i mean, i understand being irritated that people use language like \\\"everyone\\\" or \\\"always\\\" or \\\"never,\\\" but when having debates like this, you have to sort of take use of that type of language as a given, and recognize that it usually means \\\"some\\\" or \\\"mostly.\\\"',4,904,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(19000,3138,'2002-11-07 07:54:34',79,'yeti','<p>I know that voting has a better chance of changing my country than doing nothing. I have heard all of the non-voters I know complain about this country at one time or another, and while I dont think that this is all they do, it\\\'s still too much for someone who is unwilling to participate in making this country what it is, or what it could be. As for the narrow view, please inform me as to how, by your non-voting stance, you intend to change the country in the short or long term.</p> \r\n<p>Finally, I never did say anything more than \\\"by not voting, you have no say in what happens politically in our country.\\\" I did not claim to know what was right for it. I dont think I even implied it. If you choose not to vote, that is fine, but you still have to accept the rest of the political process that comes with that decision.</p>  ',3,845,66,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(19001,3138,'2002-11-07 07:56:09',79,'yeti','whups. That\\\'s to O, not to Peter. Sorry!',1,43,0,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(19002,3138,'2002-11-07 07:57:44',17,'austin','fuck you!',6,9,62,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19003,3138,'2002-11-07 07:57:52',79,'yeti','This is *not* about a two party system. This is about your say, no matter how small. Write in, write in, write in!',1,114,0,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(19004,3138,'2002-11-07 07:58:07',59,'s5','<i>how many nonvoters outside of your immediate social group have you spoken to about their choices? i really think if you talk to some people who are in different parts of the country, or are in different age or class groups than yourself, you\\\'ll find that they really just don\\\'t care, or don\\\'t feel like voting \\\"makes a difference.\\\"</i>\r\n<br><br>\r\ni realize this isn\\\'t necessarily the best sample, but i do talk to people in other parts of the country on the suicide girls message board. the majority of those people aren\\\'t the punk and emo scensters that you\\\'d expect from a cyber-hip website like SG. rather, they are your average, gun toting, meat-eating citizens of middle america. many of the people on that site are openly anti-abortion, openly racist, or openly pro-war. in short, they are exactly who you night expect your average porn obsessed person between the age of 18-40 to be.\r\n<br><br>\r\na lot of people don\\\'t give a shit. a lot of people are frustrated. a lot of people are annoyed by the mudslinging. a lot of people are happy with how things are. a lot of people are well-off and don\\\'t care about what government does to anyone else because they can download all the porn and video games they can get. a lot of people wish things could be better, but they only see things getting worse. a lot of people don\\\'t even know who their representatives are. a lot of people have anarchist ideals about voting. a lot of people keep guns around in case they need to open fire at the white house. i\\\'ve read a variety of opinions and non-opinions. i feel so informed about what people in the rest of the US think, that it actually makes me sick to my stomach sometimes.\r\n',0,1715,65,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19005,3138,'2002-11-07 08:05:59',65,'le','yah. but of all those people, do you get a general overall feeling that the people who aren\\\'t voting are doing it as an active protest? i\\\'ve been talking to some older folks about not voting, and they seem to just not care. ',4,230,59,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(19006,3138,'2002-11-07 08:06:33',65,'le','yah. but of all those people, do you get a general overall feeling that the people who aren\\\'t voting are doing it as an active protest? i\\\'ve been talking to some older folks about not voting, and they seem to just not care. ',4,230,59,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(19007,3138,'2002-11-07 08:07:04',65,'le','whoops sorry. router goof over here.',0,36,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(19008,3138,'2002-11-07 08:10:16',17,'austin','oh, sorry, i am coming in late here.  i see your real message there.  it was not fuck you at all!',6,97,62,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19009,3138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','yeti:  i have no intention to force change on any country, this one or the one i can vote in.  my choice is to be true to myself and to not accept even a tiny amount of compromise on my standards and view of how things should be. attack that as you will, it is the only option i see open that allows me to respect myself.<br />\r\n<br />\r\nyou say that nonvoters are \\\"unwilling to participate in making this country what it is\\\" but there are many ways of participating in community than just not voting.  what do you do, other than voting?  probably the same things that loads of non-voters do.  it\\\'s not that we\\\'re all completely uninterested, you see. some of us are just not happy to vote.<br />\r\n<br />\r\nso let\\\'s take me.  i can\\\'t vote here, even if i wanted to.  do i have no influence over the future of the city i live in, over this country?  one might say i have a small chance at influence.  one might say if i campaigned and organised and protested that i would be contributing to change.  does that make me less valuable than you, as a voter, if you just sat on your ass looking at a computer for 364 days of the year, and got up to vote on election day?<br />\r\n<br />\r\nif you choose to vote, that\\\'s fine.  i don\\\'t even care if you whine to me about how the government you support sucks.  you see, whining is our natural right, and yours is to plug your ears if you don\\\'t agree.',6,1413,79,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19010,3138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','oh austin, in your case i really meant fuck you :)',6,50,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19011,3138,'2002-11-07 08:16:03',17,'austin','really <TT>:D</TT>, will s5 be joining us?',4,42,62,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19012,3138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o',':(:(:(:(:(:(:(:(:(:(:(',6,22,17,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19013,3138,'2002-11-07 08:21:40',59,'s5','btw, there are ways other than voting to affect real change. and that is: consumer choices. as a vegan, for example, i save over one million gallons of water each year. so, i could either wait around and hope that my vote will get someone in office who supports water conservation, or i could actually conserve water through my actions. in my opinion, the people who are voting but not living the change they would like to see in the world are the people who really shouldn\\\'t complain. it\\\'s irrational to vote for one set of ideals, but through your actions and your consumer choices, support everything you are against.\r\n',0,628,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19014,3138,'2002-11-07 08:24:59',59,'s5','oh and ps. as i\\\'ve been writing my last post, the power in my neighborhood has gone out 7 times, yet i voted for public power yesterday.',0,139,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19015,3138,'2002-11-07 08:38:00',176,'Scapegoat','I wish I could vote in American elections - cause American elected officials and their policies end up having a huge influence on Canadian policy. Some here even think you could easier influence our government through an American election then one of our own. ',0,260,0,0,'65.95.140.17','en'),(19016,3138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','it would actually be really cool if everyone did vote one year.  then we could all sit around and smoke a bong and see if things got better or if we should have been thankful that all those voices remained silent :)',6,215,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19017,3138,'2002-11-07 08:48:52',65,'le','<a href=\\\"http://still.hungry.com/~le/fighting.050.gif\\\"><img src=\\\"http://still.hungry.com/~le/fighting.050.gif\\\" width=\\\"200\\\" border=\\\"1\\\"></A>',6,117,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(19018,3138,'2002-11-07 09:02:02',17,'austin','<P>\r\ni am really not \\\'fuck you\\\' angry about anything.  republicans have\r\nswept DC.  all the rings are aligned.  i wish everyone had voted,\r\nsure, but that is just a personal wish.  i really think starting wars\r\nand killing people is something this country needs to move on from.  i\r\nwrite letters to GW every week about that.  i don\\\'t think he pays much\r\nattention though.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthe thing about democracy that i see getting lost in the murk is\r\ndemocracy was never intended to be what anyone person wanted.  the\r\npoliticians are not representing just one of their constituents.  they\r\nare supposed to speak for the majority of people and for the common\r\ngood of The People.  the way it is designed, voters give voice to a\r\nmost fitting politician.  in some cases, organizations of voters can\r\nhave sway over what a politician will work for.  union voters,\r\nneighborhood associations, secret fraternal orders, and extremist\r\ngroups.  i think if no one on the ballot is acceptable even after\r\nconsidering the various compromises that can be made through\r\ncommunication with that politician, then it is time to hit the\r\ncampaign trail with yourself on the hay bail because there are probably\r\nothers like you that feel the same way.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthis system of representative democracy works out to a degree that is\r\nsatisfactory, and we do not have s5\\\'s revolution.  i think something\r\non the magnitude of Hitler\\\'s Oven\\\'s is required to cause a revolution\r\nin this fat country, bombing innocent people is not going to make me\r\npick up the pitch fork.  though, i am the first to admit there is\r\nnothing like a money grubbing militarist dictatorship to cause\r\npolitical unrest in the little people.  lucky for bush, America has a\r\nHUGE, ENORMOUS even, suckling middle class.  s5\\\'s politically radical\r\ndisenfranchised fringe elite may yet surface to lead us into a new\r\nage, but they will have to lose some weight first and get off the\r\nteet.\r\n</P>\r\n\r\n<PRE>\r\nYou say you want a revolution\r\nWell you know\r\nwe all want to change the world\r\nYou tell me that it\\\'s evolution\r\nWell you know\r\nWe all want to change the world\r\nBut when you talk about destruction\r\nDon\\\'t you know you can count me out (in)\r\nDon\\\'t you know it\\\'s gonna be alright\r\nAlright   Alright\r\nYou say you got a real solution\r\nWell you know\r\nwe\\\'d all love to see the plan\r\nYou ask me for a contribution\r\nWell you know\r\nWe\\\'re doing what we can\r\nBut when you want money for people with minds that hate\r\nAll I can tell you is brother you have to wait\r\nDon\\\'t you know it\\\'s gonna be alright\r\nAlright   Alright\r\n\r\nYou say you\\\'ll change the constitution\r\nWell you know\r\nwe all want to change your head\r\nYou tell me it\\\'s the institution\r\nWell you know\r\nYou better free your mind instead\r\nBut if you go carrying pictures of Chairman Mao\r\nYou ain\\\'t going to make it with anyone anyhow\r\nDon\\\'t you know know it\\\'s gonna be alright\r\nAlright   Alright\r\n</PRE>\r\n<P>i\\\'ll be running for president in 2004, btw.\r\n</P>',3,3019,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19019,3138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i\\\'ll vote for you, austie<br />\r\n<br />\r\nyou know, i actually wish i could vote on local issues.',6,99,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19020,3138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','ps: welcome to gemini',6,21,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19021,3138,'2002-11-07 11:27:02',65,'le','i thought about your gemini-ness about 10 comments back. ha ha.',0,63,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(19022,3138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','SCORPIO',6,7,65,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19023,3138,'2002-11-07 11:57:24',65,'le','i love being the scariest sign of the zodiac.',0,45,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(19024,3138,'2002-11-07 15:31:10',57,'weeb','they aren\'t all scary in their own special way? \r\n...\r\n<p>\r\ni really love it when people really really care about issues, but when it spills into \"you don\'t care as much as i do, therefor You Suck/Fuck You\" is really not the way to go. but, we gotta make allowances for panic-y and angsty responses to the current election results into account. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni still think the only way we can kick the republicans out of office is to just make ONE party against them: \"anti-right christian coalition\" party. we can all join hands and sing anti-kumbaya songs. it\'s too bad we all think for ourselves and have to be part of independant political parties and rip ourselves to pieces in the meantime. oh well.\r\n</p><p>\r\nhey. it\'s going to be okay. take a deep breath. gentle ocean waves still make sand out of rocks over time. \r\n</p>\r\n',1,880,65,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(19025,3138,'2002-11-07 18:38:53',154,'ryan','to clear up the fascist comment:  it was used in a semi-sarcastic way without much attention paid to the strict definition of the word, but more the idea.  think moral absolutism.\r\n<p>\r\nsecondly, i wonder how many people that bitch about people that bitch about politics yet abstain from voting do anything but bitch about all the bitchy people.<p>\r\nit seems that if one thought of voting as such an important institution they would become somewhat proactive.  say go door to door the day before and the day of election day and explain to people in a nonpolitical way how important it is to vote.  perhaps make the message a personal one so that it rises above all the public service announcements and rock the vote (yo) ads.  explain why <i>you think</i> it is important.<p>\r\nor you could just ask them if they voted and if they replied with a negative you could call them big greasy donkey dongs and piss on their door.',0,921,0,0,'65.58.153.132','en'),(19026,3138,'2002-11-07 20:16:23',74,'jo','elly was right that i had better, more articulate things to say than \\\"you suck\\\" or \\\"fuck you,\\\" but i had just spent an hour listening to NPR and feeling dejected and beaten down and generally disenchanted. luckily in the time between when i wrote that yesterday and when i checked it today, jim and yeti said all the smart, well-thought-out things that i would have said, anyway.<BR>\r\nsorry for lashing out. not sorry for the passion behind the lashing out, but sorry for the immaturity to not express myself better. i wasn\\\'t going for eloquent, i was going for blunt; all of you who pointed out that bluntness never won were right.',0,647,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(19027,3138,'2002-11-07 20:25:21',74,'jo','also, you\\\'re right. although i\\\'m well-informed and i care a lot, i could have done much more to be a more active participant in my government. i wondered yesterday if things would have been different if my friends and i had actually pitched in on the <a href=\\\"http://www.ronkirk.com/\\\">ron kirk</a>, <a href=\\\"http://www.johnsharp.com/\\\">john sharp</a>, <a href=\\\"http://kirkwatson.org/\\\">kirk watson</a>, or <a href=\\\"http://www.annkitchen.com/\\\">ann kitchen</a> campaigns. so, that\\\'s something towards which to work.',1,544,154,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(19028,3138,'2002-11-07 20:35:32',371,'Kyla','And I still love and respect everyone.. voters and non-voters alike!  Peace!',6,76,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(19029,3130,'2002-11-07 20:43:02',189,'cyantist','<p>this reminds me of a very sad moment in my life.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni was walking home from work with a friend, when we spotted two kittens on the side of the road.  carefully, i approached them meowing.  the two kittens looked at me and started mewing back, but one of the kittens darted out into the street and was run over by a car.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ncompletely horrified, i went out to gather the kitten and the poor thing was smooshed.  the kitten was crying.  very alive.  very aware of me.  i brought the cat to the side of the road, because it was obvious she was going to die, but i didn\\\'t know when.  the other kitten circled around my feet and started mewing, eventually coming over to her/his sibling and licking their face, no longer scared of me.\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\ni didn\\\'t know what to do.  my friend offered to snap the neck of the kitten, but i just couldn\\\'t deal with it.  eventually, i ran home and grabbed my boyfriend and he took the kitten to the emergency animal clinic where they put \\\"her\\\" under. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>i don\\\'t know if you\\\'ve ever seen the max, but i had dreams at night of that kitten, much like the girl in the max heard the rabbit scratching in the box.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe lesson i learned from that whole experience, is that animals have emotions.\r\n</p>',1,1290,0,0,'216.39.112.17','en'),(19030,3138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','scorpios aren\\\'t scary!',6,25,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19031,3138,'2002-11-07 23:10:52',17,'austin','lets all vote now on what the best sign is?\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nwhat is GW\\\'s sign anyway?',6,83,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19032,3140,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i always thank the muni driver when i get off.  usually they respond with something nice.  i think they should teach muni drivers about proper use of the brake pedal!!',6,167,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19033,3138,'2002-11-07 23:22:54',59,'s5','GW is a cancer.',0,15,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19034,3138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','the best sign is:  STOP',6,23,17,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19035,3140,'2002-11-07 23:44:32',189,'cyantist','you are sweet!  have you been on the bus when they are training a new driver?  now, that\\\'s an adventure!',1,107,62,0,'216.39.112.17','en'),(19036,3140,'2002-11-08 00:12:52',234,'etienne','i like it when people thank bus drivers.  once i heard some americans talking on the bus about how crazy it is that new zealanders thank the bus driver and i felt bad for the driver because if i\\\'m the geek up the front cringing, then the driver must have heard too.',1,268,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(19037,3092,'2002-11-08 04:27:47',370,'seanthecon','my good man. if you\\\'re not the one for her, then she\\\'s not the one for you. i\\\'ve had a few crushes where i have gone \\\"insane\\\" over a woman. meaning that i was convinced that she was everything i was looking for. but nothing materialized. for each case, in retrospect i realized that they were not the person i had made them to be. not that there was anything wrong with them, but i had imposed my own fantasies onto them. in the end, i am glad nothing happened, although i wouldn\\\'t have said it at the time. it is allright to dream and wish, but if things don\\\'t work out, don\\\'t interpet that as a reflection that something is wrong with you. be true my good man and things will work out. and remember to send more money to the seanthecon fund for retarded monkeys.',0,788,0,0,'209.139.209.206','en'),(19038,3140,'2002-11-08 04:44:55',240,'xep','when i was a whee geek thing i used to take the bus to ucsc every day so i could use their computer lab.  it was about an hour ride from my house (though a 10 minute drive.  go figure.)  i had the same driver all the time and i was often the last person on the bus so i\\\'d sit up front and we\\\'d chat.  i\\\'d ask him about his day and he\\\'d tell me wacky bus driver stories.  it was excellent.',0,400,0,0,'144.132.122.131','en'),(19039,3134,'2002-11-09 06:34:51',366,'eunoia','Personally I like my cleveland steamers with some mustard and sauerkraut. Me and your mom enjoy them when we go to those late night Indians games while you are fast alseep after we drugged you with daterape drugs.\r\n\r\nHool, I will send you some personal comments aboiut your piece via email.',0,290,370,0,'193.167.219.184','en'),(19040,3140,'2002-11-08 14:55:22',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i always thank the bus driver...</tt>',1,45,234,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(19041,3138,'2002-11-08 14:57:54',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i like the <i>crossing</i> signs that say <i>\\\"x-ing\\\"</i>. whenever i see one, i go <b>\\\"ZING!\\\"</b>. also: cancer poster-boy that i am, i hereby denounce gw as one of us. so there.</tt>',1,203,0,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(19042,3138,'2002-11-08 15:46:51',75,'tyler','hardly any of the people my age that i know voted. hardly any of them are even aware of &quot;the issues&quot; enough to vote intelligently. most have a bunch of half-formed opinions on this and that. their conversations on political subjects are filled with urban legend, and when pressed on just about anything with a couple facts and truths, they fold instantly. i hate them for that. i think that they are insufferably self-centered and bovine, especially to think that their vote affects them only! you vote for yourself, yes, but also on behalf of others who are not intelligent enough to do so. when you do not vote, conversely, you are letting others down as well--assuming there are issues which affect the upward mobility of the lower classes, for example, or, gee, i don\\\'t know, babies\\\' fucking right to grow up without leukemia from toxic runoff! the right to breath clean air! now that the republicans are in charge, you can fucking bet that the wheels are ALREADY IN MOTION to sacrifice the future health and well-being of all mankind to benefit a few individuals in the short-term. so if you didn\\\'t vote, it\\\'s your fault, it\\\'s your fucking fault, but if you are high enough in society to be able to be reading this, luckily for you it might not have such an impact on <i>your</i> life.',1,1315,0,0,'165.247.164.216','en'),(19043,3138,'2002-11-08 16:17:45',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i think that the problem for most people that dont vote is that they think <i>\\\"gee, there will still be toxic runoff and death, pestilence and republicans whether i vote or not. so, i might as well spend the time i would be spending voting eating a big mac and watching tv\\\"</i>.</tt>',1,297,75,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(19044,3135,'2002-11-08 19:01:02',82,'simon','gus, you are making me feel gushy inside. i\\\'m not sure how i feel about this. where is my heart of stone?',0,108,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19045,3135,'2002-11-08 21:15:13',175,'Paul G','Sometimes it\\\'s hard not to skip ahead to the ending - racing over the text in the middle - because you get involved with the characters, and lost in the story.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nAnd that\\\'s what this was like.',0,209,0,0,'67.195.182.246','en'),(19046,3134,'2002-11-09 06:34:51',122,'hool','please stop the unrelated commentary.',0,37,0,0,'66.185.85.80','en'),(19049,3134,'2002-11-09 06:58:08',93,'chiamattt','dont tell us what to do!',6,24,0,0,'61.37.53.62','en'),(19047,3134,'2002-11-09 04:43:04',93,'chiamattt','OH MY GOD!!!!!!!!!!!!!!! OH MY GOD!!!!!!!!!!!!! OH MY GOD!!!!!!!!!!!!! when in the hell did swinney.org employ the tactic of deleting comments? i am at a loss for words regarding this kind of censorship. FOR SHAME!!!!! FOR SHAME!!!!!',6,234,0,1,'211.32.132.229','en'),(19048,3140,'2002-11-09 05:48:51',174,'Mad Moll','<p>In Newark, when they still used the old trolley cars from the 30\\\'s, there was this subway driver who would always do this long porkey-pig announcement when we arrived at Penn Station. \\\"La-la-la-la-laaaaaaast top, Peeeeeeen Stay-shon.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>It was nice thing to hear at the end of the day, like, <i>Yes, it\\\'s good to be going home.</i></p>',6,356,0,0,'24.189.13.22','en'),(19050,3138,'2002-11-09 07:27:04',189,'cyantist','<p><i>most have a bunch of half-formed opinions on this and that. their conversations on political subjects are filled with urban legend, and when pressed on just about anything with a couple facts and truths, they fold instantly</i></p>\r\n<p>this reminds me of \\\"brave new world\\\" for some reason</p>',1,304,75,0,'24.221.170.119','en'),(19051,3139,'2002-11-09 10:31:20',174,'Mad Moll','Where the hell do you live?',1,27,0,0,'24.189.13.22','en'),(19052,3140,'2002-11-09 10:41:48',65,'le','last time i took the train into new york city, the driver announced: WELCOME TO NEW YORK CITY, LADIES AND GENTLEMAN, NEW YORK\\\'S PENN STATION, THAT\\\'S RIGHT, NEW YORK CITY, THE PEARL OF THE CIVILIZED WORLD. i couldn\\\'t tell if he was being sarcastic. i think he was and wasn\\\'t.<br><br>\r\nonce i was getting on a bus in SF, and as i got on the bus i said \\\"what\\\'s up?\\\" to  the bus driver, and he said, \\\"i\\\'m still living in this overpriced rathole of a town, that\\\'s what\\\'s up\\\" in this really growly voice. ',4,535,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(19053,3140,'2002-11-09 11:04:30',59,'s5','that\\\'s because new yorkers are post-grumpy. we\\\'re still pessimism newbies in sf.',0,86,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19054,3134,'2002-11-09 20:56:36',122,'hool','i will tell you what to do when you are being a hypocrite.  many, many times i have read and heard personally your complaints about unrelated comments on your entries.  i don\\\'t really mind unrelated commentary, but i do mind when you trivialize something i so obviously put a lot of care and emotion into writing with unrelated, unfunny sub-gradeschool \\\'humor\\\'.  ',0,372,93,0,'66.185.85.80','en'),(19055,3140,'2002-11-10 00:31:28',240,'xep','my very favorite public transportation experience was the time on bart when there were two trains going along side by side and they kept passing eachother then being passed and the driver was giving a howard cosell type horse race commentary.  when we pulled up the station he said \\\"next stop... CABO SAN LUCAS!!!\\\"',0,320,0,0,'144.132.122.131','en'),(19056,3139,'2002-11-10 20:38:49',371,'Kyla','Kare Kare, Waitakere City, New Zealand :)',6,41,174,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(19057,3140,'2002-11-11 01:26:32',124,'gus','xep that is hilarious.\r\n<p>\r\nI usually thank the drivers unless they are jerks. \r\nOnce I was on the 45 in the rain in SOMA, a few stops from the caltrain stop. the bus stopped and a woman standing on the curb in the rain asked if he went to the caltrain station. Instead of saying \\\"yes\\\" He said \\\"do you want to take a bus, a cab, or walk?\\\" In a sarcastic tone. The woman kept asking, \\\"do you go to the caltrain station?\\\" and he kept saying that same line over and over again. when she said didnt understand and he said it AGAIN. Finally she sounded like she was going to cry and said, \\\"Why are you being a jerk?\\\" and he shouted \\\"YOU\\\'RE THE JERK!\\\" and slamed the door in her face and drove off. all the passengers jaws dropped. I wrote down his bus number and made a formal complaint about him. What a fucking asshole. ',0,851,0,0,'64.172.58.52','en'),(19058,3140,'2002-11-11 03:27:51',154,'ryan','bus riding is good for stories.  there are the fights, the drunks, the guy with one arm huffing gas, and of course coming down at 7am in the bright sunshine riding home in night clothes while everyone else is going to work.<p>the portland busses do not compare to muni however -- only one story so far.<p>i miss muni a lot.',1,323,0,0,'65.58.157.56','en'),(19059,3140,'2002-11-11 03:39:39',59,'s5','i have a muni story involving a pregnant prostitute who was too high to stay in one place for more than two seconds, but i forget exactly what happened. i wish i had a video camera at all times. i knew she was a prostitute because she was wearing an easy-off dress, no underwear, and was waving a large stack of money in everyone\\\'s faces while yelling at them incoherently about her babies. she fell on me at least once. eventually the driver pulled over and the police took over.',0,483,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19060,3140,'2002-11-11 03:47:29',59,'s5','ps, i think it\\\'s time for another printing of the \\\'MUNI WANTS YOU DEAD\\\' stickers.',0,90,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19061,3140,'2002-11-11 11:41:14',240,'xep','i gave some of those to some folks in melbs and they just didn\\\'t get it.',0,75,59,0,'144.132.122.131','en'),(19062,3134,'2002-11-11 14:38:18',183,'lin','I like the picture you included with this piece. It has something of a profile in the face, as well as being a straight on shot. The heavy contrasts really work with the story, and the mental image you must have. When I think of memory sequence (perhaps because of tv?)I usually think of a sort of washed out faded scenario, but it\\\'s equally true that your memories can turn up as brightly as any real scene experienced today. <br>\r\nYour story gave me mental pictures. As with most of your writing,  and most good writing in general, I find that a story unfolds in a visual sense as you read through, and a logical progression occurs upstairs, in my brain\\\'s nether regions. Sometimes more vividly felt than others. This was vivid. Thanks for a vivid moment. ',0,764,122,0,'64.230.144.62','en'),(19063,3089,'2002-11-11 14:50:28',183,'lin','It sometimes seems like you care too much about what others (they)are saying, but I understand the desire, and a need to have someone else who can share things. <br> \r\nYou seem sometimes not to understand that life requires so much patience. I can even hear you thinking right now, tha you\\\'ve been waiting oh so long that your patience is worn not thin but off. I suppose my desire to preach friendly advice sometimes overshadows the situation that others are actually experiencing, but I will say, that when you finally find someone, some them, worth waiting for. Someone whose presence in life fills you with a sense of your own worth, and whose absence in your temporal reality can still elicit beauty, just in thought, you will perhaps understand what I mean by patience. <br>\r\n<br>\r\nI say, don\\\'t just force forward a link that isn\\\'t to advance, ie, don\\\'t run off to a find a them who isn\\\'t your mathc, but is close enough. Too much happiness is spoiled in that way. <br>\r\n\r\nAs for major life altering decisions based around joining with certain organizations; it would be nice to have a conversation about this sometime. I will call you when I return from New York. ',0,1186,0,0,'64.230.144.62','en'),(19064,3134,'2002-11-11 15:16:29',366,'eunoia','<p> Sorry about callous comments before.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Corrections:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I think there is a struggle inside you for purpose and finding the meaning of being here. You really push yourself, your mind to its very frontiers.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>While I can\\\'t get you any closer to the meaning of your life, I can see hope for you with some clarity through my own life situation. At present, I am overwelmed by the feling of being cool. I see it in Nordic functionalism, through my endless recoiliation of christianity and buddhism, and through many other new age tricks I use to quiet down my soul and keep things calm, and under our control.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>I worry that I am beginning to root myself in non-feeling, where sex becomes recreational, where love is an impossiblity. My last relationship ended in such hurt, she had been abused before she was with me, and this really dictated how she interrelated with me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There are a list of a million things that are out there to keep us cool, and cool in the sense of Marshall McLuhan\\\'s cool medium, where there is little two-way interraction, where we become passive in the face of life\\\'s challenge. This is no revelation, but it is an emerging symptom of my own humanity freezing up.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I think when I look at you, I see this person in the grips of many emotions, even though you claim yourself as feeling inertia. You haven\\\'t given up on feeling. Death of parents, abuse, fear of intimacy can all be interrelated, as my previous relationship showed to me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My cousin Anna once told me before her wedding day, that she was getting married out of convience, that she could now have kids, and her fiance Ryan, was a good person and persued the same line of work. I thought at the time, how sad, that she doesn\\\'t seek romance. But, I never thought until now that some people are maybe incapable of such a feeling as love. I don\\\'t think it\\\'s rooted in selfishness, or because there are weird hormones in the water, I just think that some people have forgotten this essential piece of human nature over time.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You may hurt in your love, you may feel absolutely fucking miserably about life, but you can feel, and this is a very great step. I see you in an art-making process to heal yourself, this is how you repair the damage, by making something beautiful.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Doris Lessing once wrote: art is the mirror of our betrayed ideals.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Obviusly you are making some stark realizations about life right now, and you are at a crossroads. It\\\'s beatuful and scary to witness, but I respect the fact that there is so much beauty and creativity in destruction. You must realize that you are working in that process as well. Your pain is nothing to be feared, because inertia is the end of fear, it is a challenge of the spirit, one which I know you are a match for in everyway. Sure you might bitch that you are tired, but I know you won\\\'t give in so easily, because you are aware of the alternative, which is being void of emotion and void of life (death). You struggle too much to give up on hope my friend. You have a bright future.</p> ',1,3139,122,0,'193.167.219.184','en'),(19065,3134,'2002-11-11 18:10:41',93,'chiamattt','the ottz crew are the kings of gradeschool humour. relax man!',0,61,0,0,'61.37.53.25','en'),(19066,3143,'2002-11-11 18:27:10',83,'adamajah','My family used to go to Ernie\\\'s for mexican food when I was a kid.  It was a cool place in many ways.  The walls were were coated with faded murals of Mexican desert landscapes.  It was the only place I\\\'ve ever known to serve sarsparilla soda.  If I was a good kid, my parents would buy me a little peppermint stick dipped in chocolate that they had behind the counter.  Ernie\\\'s was demolished and made way for a French Connection I think.  I liked Old Town Pasadena more ten years ago, while it was still a recovering red light district. ',0,548,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(19067,3143,'2002-11-11 19:11:06',74,'jo','11/11 is kind of a big deal in america if you\\\'re a veteran.',0,62,0,0,'172.134.213.57','en'),(19068,3143,'2002-11-11 19:11:49',74,'jo','also, wtf is pancake day? did you make that up? you made that up, right?',0,72,0,0,'172.134.213.57','en'),(19069,3143,'2002-11-11 19:20:50',66,'Peter','<br><tt>august 3rd is <a href=\\\"http://www.mustardweb.com/news&events/day/\\\">national mustard day</a>... i suppose anything is possible!</tt>',1,145,0,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(19070,3143,'2002-11-11 20:30:12',175,'Paul G','Poki?  Pokki?  Poke?  What are they?  The sticks: chocolate covered cookie-like sticks.  I need to go back to Chinatown...',0,122,0,0,'66.19.39.57','en'),(19071,3143,'2002-11-11 22:22:03',231,'alana','11/11 is rememberance day in australia, but i forgot about it. everyone is meant to be silent for a few minutes at eleven o\\\'clock. i think i was on a tram on elizabeth street, with the beginnings of a migraine, and not able to see. i asked the old lady opposite me to tell me when to get off the tram. she kept praying for me. she kept telling me to pray. i\\\'m not really into prayer.\r\n\r\ni think that was really nice of her.',2,429,0,0,'203.221.32.202','en'),(19072,3143,'2002-11-11 22:32:11',124,'gus','<a href=\\\"http://www.alde.com/anime/pocky1.html\\\">Pocky</a>.',0,64,175,0,'64.172.58.52','en'),(19073,3143,'2002-11-11 23:03:23',176,'Scapegoat','11/11 is the most important day for me in my relationship with my Dad. I can forget to call him on b-days, anniversaries, etc. but 11/11 is the one day I always need to remember to call. I get to listen to my Dad talk about history, global politics, and the importance of defending your values. He always mentions my grandfather, a veteran who passed away about 7 years ago two days before 11/11, and how they were going to officially participate in a military remembrance ceremony together that year. It is the one day a year that I know my Dad will cry.br><br>\r\nPepero sounds tasty, though. A little more light hearted and fun too.\r\n     ',0,640,0,0,'65.93.221.137','en'),(19074,3143,'2002-11-12 00:45:22',65,'le','pancake day sounds pretty cool.',0,31,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(19075,3143,'2002-11-12 02:26:16',195,'matt','no pocky for kitty',1,18,0,0,'209.208.109.202','en'),(19076,3143,'2002-11-12 02:57:05',174,'Mad Moll','kookies in korea -- what\\\'ll they think of next?',6,50,0,0,'24.189.13.22','en'),(19077,3143,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i want to celebrate pepero day.  and pancake day.  pepero pancakes!!!',6,69,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19078,3143,'2002-11-12 03:37:48',370,'seanthecon','do the beer shaped ones have alcohol in them? that would be tony the tiger great.\r\n\r\nyes, shame on you chiamattt for not remembering veterans day. this is my first 11/11 without my grandfather, a WWII air force radar vet. radar operators have an interesting history in the war. their technology played a crucial part in giving the allies a major advantage in air battles. however, the canadian air force has done a disgraceful job in recognizing thier achievement and has very little history on them. \r\n\r\nmy grandfather was a cold bastard, but a good man. we got along very well though, but conversation never got much beyond baseball and politics. the last christmas before he got his voice box and a few more cancers i lucked out by finding this obscure book written about the history of the canadian radar operators in WWII. it was printed and published without any help from a company or the government. no one would touch it. so the writer, a vet himself, just printed about 50 copies himself. it turned out to be the best gift my grandfather had ever recieved and we spent hours going through the book, him telling me and my sister all sorts of stories about dumping tea off a boat and nearly hitting the queen and saving another soldiers life in a vicious storm. i had never been prouder of him. it was the last time we were able to talk. i was very lucky to have gotten that oppurtunity. ',0,1396,0,0,'216.129.68.90','en'),(19079,3143,'2002-11-12 03:44:46',370,'seanthecon','<p>pancake day is shrove tuesday, a christian celebration. it is the day before ash wednesday and is supposed to mark the beginning of lent (which is when christians are supposed to spend 40 days fasting to commemerate jc\\\'s 40 days of being hungry).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i used to eat an enormous amount of pancakes on shrove tuesday. i think my record was when i was 7 or 8 and i consumed something like 30. i was not a big child. that was back when me and my family used to believe in god. how foolish we all were. hahahahaha(laugher like bad english dub in japanese martial arts movie).</p>\r\n',0,587,74,0,'216.129.68.90','en'),(19080,3143,'2002-11-12 03:46:34',370,'seanthecon','hey you. i just saw your name in this now. do you still have my grandfather\\\'s knife? i hope you were able to cut up some motherfuckers with it. death crew posse represent!',0,174,65,0,'216.129.68.90','en'),(19081,3145,'2002-11-12 06:02:03',176,'Scapegoat','I agree with the statement about the interaction between youth and the elderly. I picked up the 150th anniversary edition of Moby Dick after your last comment to me. I\\\'m still looking for my equivalent to the sea, and hope to find it soon.   ',0,245,0,0,'65.93.221.26','en'),(19082,3143,'2002-11-12 06:31:46',93,'chiamattt','shame on me for not remembering veterans day. you are right. Ive given up on history. im sorry. ',1,96,370,0,'211.32.132.222','en'),(19083,3143,'2002-11-12 06:33:07',93,'chiamattt','<a href=\\\"http://www.asiamex.com/tayabas/chocbissticp.html\\\">pepero</a>',1,75,0,0,'211.32.132.222','en'),(19084,3143,'2002-11-12 06:55:25',93,'chiamattt','and lets not forget. veterans day ISNT that big a deal in canada anymore. It might be to the random few, but to most its just another day of the week. Sure the government and schools have a moment of silence for the soldiers that died in the world wars, but to be honest, its been losing its importance for years. Is this wrong? im not sure it is. We have memorials. We learn about it in school. The end of its importance came when big business refused to stop working for that \\\'minute\\\' because it lost too much money. ie. assembly lines and manufacturing plants. If it meant anything, the canadian government should have cracked down on it. I guess in the end money talks and dead soldiers rot. ',1,702,0,0,'211.32.132.222','en'),(19085,3143,'2002-11-12 19:27:00',74,'jo','it isn\\\'t that big of a deal here, either. only government employees get the day off. otherwise, it\\\'s mostly forgotten. it also happens to be one of my brothers\\\' birthdays, so that\\\'s why i usually remember it.',0,220,93,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(19086,3143,'2002-11-12 19:59:53',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i love bank holidays that come near the middle of the month; it gives my rent check one extra day to clear ;)</tt>',1,122,74,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(19087,3143,'2002-11-13 05:38:41',74,'jo','i hear that. except! when you\\\'re waiting for a paycheck to come in the mail so you can deposit it so your rent check will clear. doh! i check banking.wellsfargo.com about 5 times a day. special torture.',1,205,66,0,'172.166.236.107','en'),(19088,3146,'2002-11-13 08:29:12',17,'austin','gus\\\' characters are hip shakers.',3,35,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19089,3145,'2002-11-13 09:39:17',175,'Paul G','Hey Corey, that is so cool; I still don\\\'t own a paper copy.  You know the way that advice works: I say what I want to hear, and you hear what you want to be said.  <i>Moby Dick</i> has a lot of good stuff in it though.  I never used the text at Uni, and I never read an analysis of it.  It took me a long time to figure out that it might have something to offer me; but I can be slow.  With a name like Queequeg I should have known that it would be good.   ',0,460,0,0,'67.195.73.185','en'),(19090,3146,'2002-11-13 19:05:41',82,'simon','i like gus\\\' characters a lot. they remind me sometimes of certain photographs i\\\'ve seen.',0,94,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19091,3146,'2002-11-13 19:08:16',82,'simon','<a href=\\\"http://www.mopa.org/NYNYgoinghom.jpg\\\">like this one!</a>',1,71,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19092,3143,'2002-11-14 08:38:26',366,'eunoia','I eat pancakes and pocky any goddamn day I please. Except since I moved here I only get to eat pancakes that are the size of large pizzas and they are called pannekoke. As fara as treats, all I can get is salmiaki, salty-sweet liqourice, which is not my favorite treat. \r\n',0,272,0,0,'193.167.219.184','en'),(19093,3146,'2002-11-15 01:54:37',195,'matt','hey, that\\\'s my grandpa! what\\\'s was he doing on the subway with that woman?! i\\\'ve never seen her before.',1,112,0,0,'209.208.109.59','en'),(19094,2943,'2002-1