ecame a sort of adjunct member of the gang of goofs that i ran with at the time.<br><br>dille was one of my favorite people back then--i could tell lots of stories about him. he did indeed have an obsession with white; he was like a reverse-negative goth character. he dressed in white lacy King Louis XIV finery (that became increasingly ragged and soiled over the years), had long white dreadlocks, drove a white bread delivery truck, and only ate white food (mostly bread, which he made at home, and things like cauliflower and marshmallows).<br><br>dille spoke with a fake french accent (he was born and rased in the San Jose area), but i don\\\'t think he ever spoke French. at one point travelled to France to hook up with a group of creative-anachronist-type kids who apparently all dressed and acted as if they were nobles in pre-French Revolution times. but all of his money was stolen, and he returned home. there\\\'s more, if you\\\'re interested.',1,1104,240,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(19213,3162,'2002-12-06 20:35:27',82,'simon','dude. tell more. i love this guy already!',1,41,129,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19214,3162,'2002-12-07 00:16:39',129,'moebius','okay, i just created a post with all sorts of entertaining facts. it\\\'s heavily augmented with the fond reminisences of my old college pal Jonathan, who knew Dille much better than I did....',0,192,82,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(19215,3174,'2002-12-07 01:13:22',65,'le','i hope he does a websearch on his name and finds himself mythologized.',6,70,0,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(19216,3174,'2002-12-07 01:46:39',82,'simon','thanks',0,6,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19217,3173,'2002-12-07 01:57:58',57,'weeb','winona ryder.',1,13,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(19218,3173,'2002-12-07 02:26:00',17,'austin','a girl i dated told me various stories of when she began to notice men staring at her.  he did not like it at all and it troubled her deeply.',4,141,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19219,3174,'2002-12-07 04:48:09',67,'trouble','\\\"This was strange because to my eyes Dille had all the sexual charisma of a prepubescent eunich.\\\"\r\n\r\noh wow, my sides hurt.  i loved this piece.',1,150,0,0,'67.243.206.109','en'),(19220,2599,'2002-12-07 05:52:22',234,'etienne','art machines of doom !  (even)  we are lovers of the modern world.',1,66,66,0,'203.96.111.237','en'),(19221,3173,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i think people can be more sympathetic to the plight of beautiful creatures simply because they are beautiful.  you can also be hardened to someones plight because of percieved ugliness:  i.e. homeless people, etc.  so it\\\'s not that their plight is worse because they\\\'re beautiful, you just care more. ',6,308,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19222,3173,'2002-12-07 09:53:57',152,'nadnad_nixie_nox','<p>well, truth be told, it was web porn that got me thinking about this.  obviously, the market for beauty is only increasing.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni can\\\'t help but wonder, what is life like for the latest \\\'black on blondes\\\' starlet.  what about those girls in japan who get gang raped?  can you just pick up your paycheck, and be content after going through something like that?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nif you aren\\\'t quite so attractive, maybe it\\\'s an advantage.  an advantage that people don\\\'t fantasize about you.  that you will never be subjected to all of these fringe benefits of looks.\r\n</p>  ',3,597,0,0,'24.78.54.203','en'),(19223,3173,'2002-12-07 13:49:08',64,'Joanna','Hi - bad shit happens to unattractive people too.  Thank you. ',1,62,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(19224,3174,'2002-12-07 14:22:17',240,'xep','thank you for this, moebius.\r\n<br>\r\nit didn\\\'t occur to me until very recently that is was probably dille who read me poetry on the 1 bus heading into town.  he walked up and asked \\\"would you like to hear a love poem?\\\" and i said yes please.  and he read it then walked away. i smiled quietly in that \\\"the universe is inexplicably fabulous\\\" way.',0,359,0,0,'144.132.122.179','en'),(19225,3173,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','the grass is greener, etc.<br />\r\n<br />\r\nattractive people wish they didn\\\'t have so much attention<br />\r\n<br />\r\nunattactive people wish they had attention<br />\r\n<br />\r\npeople with straight hair want curly hair<br />\r\n<br />\r\netc',6,236,152,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19226,3173,'2002-12-08 03:11:15',240,'xep','... when you live in melbourne you wish you lived in san francisco ... when you live in san francisco you wish you lived in melbourne.',0,134,0,0,'144.132.122.179','en'),(19227,3170,'2002-12-08 03:23:32',180,'auntie','excuse me?  i\\\'m gonna tell dad you said that. if i ever have a torrid affair, you better believe it\\\'s not gonna be with another fifty year old person, (unless it\\\'s david letterman), it\\\'s gonna be with some totally hot young babe with a perversion for chubby old ladies.',6,281,65,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(19228,3170,'2002-12-08 06:43:42',17,'austin','do you check out hot young guys?  sideways glances in public?  lurid stares?',2,76,180,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19229,3173,'2002-12-08 06:56:48',152,'nadnad_nixie_nox','<p>i have given my initial argument some thought, and think that i must revise my previous assertion slightly.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere are 4 different classifications that i can broadly place teenagers into:\r\n</p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>female, beautiful</li>\r\n<li>female, un-beautiful</li>\r\n<li>male, beautiful</li>\r\n<li>male, un-beautiful</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p>\r\nwhile my brush is still course, i would like to refine it slightly.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni think that the beautiful male can not be grouped with the beautiful female.  infact, the beautiful male, will likely develop significant advantages, from an early age, as the result of his appearance.  the beautiful female, however, is still destined to a painful, lonlely life, of disappointments, disillusion, and raunchy gang-bangs with unknown partners.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni think that the difference is highlighted by the standards by which teens of either sex are measured.  with females, appearance is primary.  for males, it is likely first academic or atheletic excellence.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhile attractive males may gain some extra attention as a result of their appearance, this attention will not typically be of a predatory nature.  Further, they will not be conditioned to rely on this appearance in gaining rewards.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe advantages of being attractive could potentially open doors for males.  these include earlier chances for dating and leadership positions.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso, ranked in order of \\\'attractiveness\\\', if given a concious choice while still in my momma\\\'s belly, i would choose first to be an attractive male.  in descending order, the best options are:\r\n</p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>male, beautiful</li>\r\n<li>male, un-beautiful</li>\r\n<li>female, un-beautiful</li>\r\n<li>female, beautiful</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n ',3,1746,0,0,'24.78.54.203','en'),(19230,3173,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i think you\\\'re on crack; everyone\\\'s problems seem really really fucking bad to them, it\\\'s impossible to judge who has it worst, if there is such a thing',6,161,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19231,3173,'2002-12-08 10:02:53',176,'Scapegoat','I have to disagree with your argument. I had written a comment but realized that it was way too long so I\\\'m just going on record saying interesting perspective but I disagree. It may be a cope out but I think attractiveness is relative and can\\\'t be so easily pegged as you suggest. ',0,288,0,0,'65.95.140.253','en'),(19232,3173,'2002-12-08 14:06:58',240,'xep','i agree with o and scapegoat.  perhaps that is the universe you live in and if that\\\'s the case, then i would put \\\"nadnad_nixie_nox\\\'s universe\\\" below \\\"female, beautiful\\\" on your list.  also, have you stated what the actual benefits and disadvantages you\\\'re talking about are?  fulfilling one\\\'s dreams?  accomplishing life goals?  or are we talking about who gets picked up first at the pub for a cheap shag or gets to be captain of the football team?',0,473,152,0,'144.132.122.179','en'),(19233,3173,'2002-12-08 14:21:08',57,'weeb','i think people mature out of this mindset once they\\\'re out of high school. thank god. \r\n\r\n',1,93,152,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(19234,3170,'2002-12-08 17:55:04',180,'auntie','hmmm, no, not really. i look around, but have never seen anyone, young or old, that was worth dropping my drawers for since i was very, very young, younger than le.\r\n<br><br>\r\neven then, lust is a transient thing.  marriage to someone who worships you is much more valuable that scratching a momentary itch.  that\\\'s what masturbation is for...\r\n<br><br>\r\nsport fucking is something i\\\'ve been meaning to write about on swinney.  perhaps i will when i have a little time this evening.\r\n\r\n',3,492,17,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(19235,3173,'2002-12-08 20:25:51',152,'nadnad_nixie_nox','<p>\r\nit\\\'s a hard cause to win support for.  this i am sure of.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe thing is that i have so much empirical evidence to support this.  i have met a number of girls, who, as a result of being beautiful, have become targets.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen you are young, it\\\'s hard to understand so much of the world around you.  it\\\'s hard to make rational, competent decisions.  it\\\'s hard to understand the motives of people around you.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy point is that beautiful young girls must have a great deal of pressure on them.  pressure that comes in addition to all the other pressures of growing up.  unbeautiful girls, are relatively free from predation. free to develop interesting personalities, interesting hobbies, and such.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit\\\'s a very nebulous argument.  i am sure that there has never been research done on this, for just the same reasons that people have stated in reply to my writing.  reasons like:\r\n</p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>what is beauty?  it\\\'s hard to define.  almost a moving target</li>\r\n<li>beauty, on the surface, is considered to be an advantage.  these don\\\'t need our sympathy</li>\r\n<li>surely, there are many more worthy causes than the beautiful.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p>\r\nthis is not something that keeps me up at night.  but, it is real.  there is a certain class of \\\'beautiful\\\' women in this world who are quite tragic.  when i become a father, this is something that will weigh heavily on my mind.\r\n</p>',3,1452,0,0,'24.78.54.203','en'),(19236,3175,'2002-12-08 22:29:03',66,'Peter','<br><tt>oh my god, im <i>totally</i> an 11:11 person; i was actually talking to sine about that the other night. how odd. im a fan of this time for a variety of reasons, all too odd and long-winded to explain here.<br><br>im also a fan of 12:34 and 8:08.</tt>',1,259,0,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19237,3175,'2002-12-08 22:55:58',371,'Kyla','<p>\r\nI am also an 11:11 person! \r\n</P><p>\r\nI\\\'m a 3:33 person aswell!   You know what they say about time-synchronicity right?  That you\\\'re on the right path :)\r\n</P>',6,171,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(19238,3175,'2002-12-08 23:02:12',148,'jeeff','i am an 11:11 person as well.  i got an email at 11:11am yesterday and took special note of that fact.  how odd that you post this the very next day.',0,149,0,0,'24.43.239.60','en'),(19239,3175,'2002-12-08 23:18:19',17,'austin','i constantly notice whenever it is 11:11 or 1:11.  often i happen to check the time and it is all 1.',3,100,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19240,3173,'2002-12-08 23:35:51',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i have been both <i>beautiful</i> and <i>not beautiful</i>. both have their caveats, apparently.</tt>',1,109,0,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19241,3174,'2002-12-08 23:41:23',66,'Peter','<br><tt>wow, i really like pieces about real people, such as this. nice!</tt>',1,77,0,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19242,3176,'2002-12-08 23:51:41',65,'le','i want to do that. where will you go? today i am considering a life of moving from <a href=\\\"http://eomega.org\\\">community</a> to <a href=\\\"http://www.esalen.org\\\">community</a> to <a href=\\\"http://www.lostvalley.org\\\">community</a>.',2,245,0,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(19243,3176,'2002-12-09 00:43:14',371,'Kyla','<p>\r\nI wish I could come to SF!  But silly America won\\\'t let me.\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nMmm, you should come and stay with me in <a href=\\\"http://www.wellpark.co.nz/accomm.htm#Meditation\\\">my community</a> !!  You\\\'d love it :)  The most beautiful views in the world.\r\n</P>',6,271,65,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(19244,3175,'2002-12-09 01:02:34',240,'xep','11:11, 12:34, 7:47.  my locker combination at school is 808.  yay.',0,66,0,0,'144.132.122.179','en'),(19245,3176,'2002-12-09 03:20:48',59,'s5','http://www.greencardlottery.com/',0,32,371,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19246,3175,'2002-12-09 03:36:36',59,'s5','*raids xep\\\'s locker*',0,23,240,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19247,3176,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','kyles why don\\\'t you work at wellpark?<br />\r\n<br />\r\nthey have a <a href=\\\"http://www.wellpark.co.nz/Admin_manager.htm\\\">job opening</a>',6,143,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19248,3175,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','yeah.  i used to look at my alarm clock at night in high school and think \\\"this is MY minute!!!\\\" and then i realised that everyone does that.  so i am actually kind of embittered towards 11:11, that slutty arse minute.',6,224,371,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19249,3173,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','what is worse:  extreme pressure, or  being constantly ignored?<br />\r\n<br />\r\nyou\\\'re still on crack.',6,104,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19250,3175,'2002-12-09 06:08:55',122,'hool','4:20 is the only significant time in my day.',0,44,0,0,'66.185.85.80','en'),(19251,3175,'2002-12-09 06:39:59',65,'le','i am definitely an 11:11 person, since i was a little kid and my dad said \\\"look kid, it\\\'s 11:11. one one one one. that\\\'s the only time the clock ever has four of the same numbers on it.\\\"\r\n\r\n',4,202,0,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(19252,3175,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','unless it\\\'s a 24 hour clock, that is.',6,40,65,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19253,3175,'2002-12-09 07:37:51',152,'nadnad_nixie_nox','<p>on a 24h clock, 23:45 always makes me look twice.  00:00 as well.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere is a sense of balance in the time 1:59.\r\n</p>',3,132,0,0,'24.78.54.203','en'),(19254,3173,'2002-12-09 08:08:14',152,'nadnad_nixie_nox','<p>ok, i will let it rest.  but just remember this thread the next time you are in a bar, and you see a really beautiful woman\r\n</p>\r\n<p>go up to her, and let her know, you feel her pain.  tell her, that you realize it must be very difficult being so beautiful.  that it must be a burden to attract countless glances whenever she turns.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nslowly, with the help of a few compliments, you will win her confidence.  she will tell you her lifes story.  about growing up in the backwoods of rural alabama.  about her father, the travelling salesman who was never home.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nshe obviously didn\\\'t understand your initial intent.  at this point, it\\\'s better to change the subject.  if her stories continue any longer, the place will be swimming in tears.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbuy her a glass of wine.  lighten things up a little bit.  tell her a funny story, and watch her face light up.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlook at her face.  it really is cute.  and she has a very tight fitting blouse on too!  you think... \\\'i want to suck on her nipples\\\'.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nher glass of wine was emptied pretty quickly.  subtly, you motion for the bartender to fill it up again.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nshe\\\'s a tad tipsy now, and is turning into a wild woman.  her stories are animated, her face is flush.  you whisper \\\'come home with me\\\'.  she smiles.\r\n</p>\r\n<hr>\r\n<p>\r\nthe next day, you\\\'ll wonder, how did this all start?  was it worth it?  she wasn\\\'t so good in bed.  besides, you were a bit too drunk to enjoy it.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nshe is obviously uncomfortable when she leaves in the morning.  \\\'want some eggs?\\\'  \\\'no thanks\\\'.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nafter the door closes, you feel relieved.  perhaps you could call her again, but you really doubt it.\r\n</p>\r\n<hr>\r\n<p>\r\ni think to be ignored is better.  under constant pressure, you can rarely think for yourself.\r\n</p>',4,1856,0,0,'24.78.54.203','en'),(19255,3176,'2002-12-09 08:22:46',371,'Kyla','You are sweet :)  Worth a try!',6,30,59,0,'203.96.111.237','en'),(19256,3176,'2002-12-09 08:23:17',371,'Kyla','*wink*',6,6,62,0,'210.54.79.99','en'),(19257,3176,'2002-12-09 09:27:50',234,'etienne','yup.  that\\\'s what i think as well.  fuck the career + making \\\"good choices\\\" + having \\\"security.  i wanna make theatre and write.  so there.',1,151,371,0,'203.96.111.237','en'),(19258,3176,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','it\\\'s hard to see people look good jobs in the face when so many people i know here are jobless and desperate....',6,115,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19259,3173,'2002-12-09 09:56:35',59,'s5','ah, so the truth comes out: \\\"RESPECT THE COCK! AND TAME THE CUNT!\\\"',5,72,152,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19260,3175,'2002-12-09 18:30:13',124,'gus','My birthdate: 7/7/71 @ 11:11\r\n<p>\r\nI am your leader. Someone bring me a sandwhich please. thanks.',0,97,0,0,'64.163.212.122','en'),(19261,3175,'2002-12-09 18:46:24',129,'moebius','this discussion reminds me of when i was a wee little boy and my family lived in Inspiration, a parochial copper mining town that was kind of off by itself in the southwestern corner of Arizona.<br><br>one July evening during dinner, my father called for pause in the conversation and had us look at the wall clock in the kitchen, illuminated then by the dusky golden sunset light that poured through the picture windows.<br><br><i>look</i>, he said. <i>it\\\'s seven oh seven. do you know what that means?</i><br><br><i>no</i>, i replied.<br><br><i>it\\\'s seven oh seven on seven - seven - seventyseven. it won\\\'t be this time and that date again for another one hundred years.</i> i was stunned. once again my father had revealed himself to be nothing short of a wizard, with powers of mystical knowledge beyond my comprehension. we all waited and watched the clock in spellbound silence until the minute hand leapt forward to eight. then, the trance broken, we resumed our meal and the mundane world took over again.<br><br>sadly, i failed to memorialize similar moments in \\\'88 and \\\'99. but i\\\'ll always remember that summer evening when time stopped for the number seven.',2,1186,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(19262,3175,'2002-12-09 18:56:04',66,'Peter','<br><tt>that was exactly 6 days after i was born. yay!</tt>',1,59,129,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(19263,3176,'2002-12-09 20:09:13',371,'Kyla','True I guess.. but wouldn\\\'t you rather everyone does what makes them happy?',6,78,62,0,'203.96.111.237','en'),(19264,3175,'2002-12-09 21:25:32',142,'oof','you\\\'re all wrong. 2:42 is <i>the</i> time.',6,45,0,0,'128.148.188.186','en'),(19265,3175,'2002-12-09 21:41:02',129,'moebius','i\\\'m thinking that we should start planning now for a huge blowout swinney.org (re)union festival that will reach a peak of excitment/love transfer/\\\"novelty\\\"/level of inebreation at 11:11pm on November 11, 2011. consider it a warmup for the koionea hysteria of the following year if you want.\r\n\r\n',0,304,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(19266,3175,'2002-12-09 23:20:15',82,'simon','oh my god, gus, my ex girlfriend\\\'s birthday is 11/11/71. you guys are like cult leaders. cool.',1,97,124,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19267,3175,'2002-12-10 00:01:25',83,'adamajah','i noticed the clock when it was 11:11am, on 11/11/02.  meaning in the meaningless?',0,82,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(19268,3173,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i\\\'m not really into picking up models at bars with cheesy lines.  you\\\'re on crack.',6,88,152,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19269,3176,'2002-12-10 00:38:07',124,'gus','what would make me happy is to be able to pay my rent and bills. To be able to afford to go out to eat and a movie once a week. I\\\'ve discovered that liking your job is a luxury, and something you have try hard to find. So sure, I\\\'d like to do what made me happy, if you sent me $500 a month, Im sure I could manage to be happy. I could write and do massage and still work enough to be able to afford to pay my rent. Please contact me for my address.\r\n<p>\r\nuntil your checks start arriving, I\\\'ll continue to apply for jobs I once said I\\\'d never do again, and when I get one? I\\\'ll work hard and do my best until years go by and forget and take it all for granted once again. Enjoy it while you can, and if you never have to do something that doesnt make you happy, that\\\'s swell, use your good fortune to help those who dont get that luxury.\r\n<p>\r\nIts cold outside, I hope youre thinking about the people who are unable to do what makes them happy, who are unable even to be warm and fed. I hope you\\\'re using your good fortune to help them to increase their chances at being happy too. unless you meant \\\"wouldn\\\'t you rather I do what makes me happy?\\\" \r\n<p>\r\nhi. i have pms. also, I cant afford to \\\"do what makes me happy\\\" anymore.',0,1263,0,0,'64.163.212.122','en'),(19270,3175,'2002-12-10 02:19:43',69,'starlajo','I always seem to look at the clock when it\\\'s 11:11, too. \r\n\r\nI no longer feel special!\r\n\r\n',2,93,0,0,'203.96.111.237','en'),(19271,3173,'2002-12-10 02:27:48',64,'Joanna','But what about when you picked up ME, o? ',1,41,62,0,'130.216.131.85','en'),(19272,3173,'2002-12-10 02:31:18',176,'Scapegoat','So you think that beautiful women often have tragic stories, then you mention not calling them after a one night stand because they seemed uncomfortable? Did I miss something? It would seem that the discomfort would be on your side of things, no? Why else wouldn\\\'t you call? I must have missed something.',0,307,0,0,'64.231.192.64','en'),(19273,3177,'2002-12-10 04:21:38',370,'seanthecon','keep sending these postcards hoolio. maybe i\\\'ll catch you one day.',0,69,0,0,'209.139.252.5','en'),(19274,3180,'2002-12-10 14:51:19',66,'Peter','<br><tt>I love how the seasons change, year after year.</tt>',1,60,0,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(19275,3176,'2002-12-10 18:23:36',371,'Kyla','Although I feel deeply for homeless people and unemployed people (I have been there myself), and think that the problem is one of the most awful in modern society, I don\\\'t think someone should have to take a job because this problem exists.  Thank you.',6,255,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(19276,3176,'2002-12-10 19:48:57',129,'moebius','hey, if you don\\\'t mind the relative poverty and instability and fears about rent payments and access to decent healthcare and so on and so forth, more power to you, i guess. just don\\\'t burn bridges with family and friends, because there will be times when a safety net will come in handy.<br><br>i say: you\\\'re young, you\\\'re (hopefully) not tied down by debts and responsibilities, so why not wander the world and have adventures? it sucks to look back at your twenties and realize they went by in the blur of a 9 to 5 job. if you can handle the hardships of the gypsy lifestyle, go for it. you can always seek stability later, if/when uncertainty becomes less of an adventure and more of a burden. ',1,710,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(19277,3176,'2002-12-10 21:16:14',371,'Kyla','My thoughts exactly.  Thanks moebius.  We\\\'ll see.. perhaps I can accept a job involving travel ;)',6,100,129,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(19278,3176,'2002-12-10 21:40:59',129,'moebius','my friend liz is sort of doing the same thing, except in reverse order, which is a lot harder: after a dozen years of building and running a successful graphic design business, she\\\'s (temporarily?) abandoning everything to spend one or two years <a href=\\\"http://www.whereisliz.com\\\">traveling around the world</a> on her own. she\\\'s been preparing for the trip for almost a year now; apparently her departure date is coming up soon.',1,442,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(19279,3181,'2002-12-10 21:52:20',90,'leonard','Wonderful. My head is spinning.',1,31,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(19280,3175,'2002-12-10 23:53:37',186,'ook','<p>when my age was measured in single digits, i had a force field around my bed to keep murderers and corpses away, which i could activate only during significant minutes on my bedside clock. 11:11, 12:34, 4:44, 7:47 and all that. when desperate to reactivate the shield times which were symmetrical, even rotationally, were allowed. 5:12, 9:06, etc.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>eventually i started to need to reactivate the shield whenever the time had any fancy features. then i had to wean myself because i wasn\\\'t getting to sleep on time.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>11:11 is good for wishing on.</p>',2,572,0,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(19281,3175,'2002-12-10 23:56:27',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\"kiss the clock\"...</i> some people would say, while making a wish at 11:11.</tt>',1,82,186,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19282,2656,'2002-12-11 04:26:30',183,'lin','<p>I\\\'ve read this article/account over a few times, and every time it present accurate pictures of a reality in my mind. I believe in the presence of this account, and possibly even moreseo as I accept the visuals it creates. It\\\'s an emotional thing for me even, because I know some of the characters in the story so well, and because I know they way you think, and your though processes related to situations, although not as well as I used to think I did. \r\n<br><br>\r\nIt\\\'s a revelation to me every time I read something you write, a new little peek into your way of being, which sounds like a lot of weirdness, but it\\\'s something I find really fascinating. I only have this to offer right now: <br> <br>\r\n\r\nEveryone\\\'s lives are different, and we rationalize them against one another, against the family unit, and the societal constructions in which we create communities. We all talk and think about our relationships with people and somehow our own past and present become all mixed up in our identity, and this all relates to our parents, or lack thereof. Knowing you as a friend, I can honestly say that you\\\'re brave, and strong, and that I\\\'m proud to know you, and to know you can deal with such pain through the release of writing, and that you have the restraint to keep in those things that are so often just spilled out to all. I like that you think, and I think it\\\'s just fine to be a person who can have enemies and know that it\\\'s not just all drama. Your Dad was a scuker because he never knew you, but his ignorance and viciousness is in some random ways responsible for you, in my opinion, if only by the circumstrance of your creation. As for your mom, she\\\'s a good woman, and deserves respect. Sometimes I think you\\\'re too hard on her. We\\\'re all a little damaged in some ways, and its those we love, and who love us who have the luxury of touching our lives enough to expose those tender nerves, and to prod them and make them hurt. But we hurt each other, I suppose, with the best intentions.\r\n</p>',0,2052,132,0,'64.230.168.111','en'),(19283,3176,'2002-12-11 04:49:43',124,'gus','no one should have to take a job just because some people dont get the choice of turning down a job offer. but it\\\'s a little simplistic and unrealistic to say \\\"everyone should do what makes them happy!\\\" and leave it at that. It\\\'s a very affluent western point of view.',0,280,0,0,'64.172.57.180','en'),(19284,3176,'2002-12-11 05:13:41',371,'Kyla','<p>\r\nLook, I\\\'m not completely naive.  When I made the happy statement I was mainly referring to myself.  But I still believe there is happiness to be found everywhere, anywhere.  I want everyone to be happy!  I did\\\'t really expect to get flamed for it.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI know this world is a shit place, and a hard place to survive in.  You can either be cynical about it, or live with hope and also acceptance and learn to see the beauty around you - and try to make a difference.  I\\\'m not wealthy, or priveleged in any way, I haven\\\'t had an education, I don\\\'t own anything of value.  Hell, I don\\\'t even have electricity at my house!  I live differently than most people but I appreciate my experiences, I learn, and I give what I can.  I say all this because you speak to me as though I\\\'m totally ignorant.  I\\\'m simple, a little mad, a total hippie, but definitely not ignorant.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAnd I won\\\'t let things get me down, I reserve the right to seek my own happiness, and I know that everyone else can do the same.  It comes from within.\r\n</p>',6,1073,124,0,'210.86.43.150','en'),(19285,3175,'2002-12-11 06:07:22',255,'qq','All one! All one!\r\n\r\ndon\\\'t drink soap.. .etc...\r\n',0,52,0,0,'64.81.241.248','en'),(19286,3182,'2002-12-11 14:57:00',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i started at the end, and read this backwards, paragraph by paragraph for some reason. it was suspenseful.</tt>',1,119,0,0,'204.168.247.130','en'),(19287,3177,'2002-12-11 15:26:26',366,'eunoia','I like short sentences too...\r\nBut, i think if you match the writing to a style I would says its ambiguity. When i read this I want the images to be so rich, as they rightfully are. The writing does tend to be a bit dry for me, a bit disconnected from a narrative, more speculative like retelling a dream. I guess this is the careful tension you are working with, but it has yet to lead me anywhere but to the images. The images are quite a story in and of themselves. I think what could be interesting is to have the text shortened and the images tell the story, then flip it around to having the story told only in words. I guess i am not a big fan of serials. This type of writing makes me think of sitcom writers... ',1,720,0,0,'193.167.219.184','en'),(19288,3176,'2002-12-11 17:40:55',124,'gus','Aw now, I wasnt flaming you. I was just pointing out that your comment was too simple. People should do whatever they want, whatever makes them happy, at every opportunity. The thing is, SOME people dont always get that opportunity, and its important that we realize that some people do not always have a choice.<p>\r\nYou ARE wealthy and privilaged when it comes to \\\"this shit world\\\" if you\\\'re living in north america, I dont care if you choose to have electricity or not. heh. And so am I, and everyone else here with us. We dont even know how lucky we are.',0,566,371,0,'64.172.56.64','en'),(19289,3176,'2002-12-11 18:45:28',371,'Kyla','Oh, I live in New Zealand - and believe me, I know exactly how lucky I am :)',6,76,124,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(19290,3176,'2002-12-11 18:52:20',371,'Kyla','Also, it\\\'s not my choice re the electricity, I live in a very rural place and it\\\'s off the grid, I have a potbelly stove and a gas shower, I don\\\'t use a fridge and I have a flashlight as my bedroom light.  It\\\'s a very simple way to live, and I have a bit of a love/hate relationship with it.  Mostly I love it :)',6,324,124,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(19291,3182,'2002-12-11 19:20:03',82,'simon','at 714 we didn\\\'t have a phone or a working toilet. we used the ones at the chevron station down the street. if  someone needed to get in touch, they\\\'d say, be at the payphone at 7 on wednesday night and we\\\'d go down there, eat a chic-o-stick and wait for the call.',1,273,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19292,3182,'2002-12-11 20:38:53',234,'etienne','it was kind of weird how it started off being \\\'they\\\' and changed to \\\'we\\\'.  i liked it, i wanted more.',1,113,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(19293,3182,'2002-12-11 20:55:21',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yeah, i really hope you continue this story, if theres more to continue with.</tt>',1,90,0,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19294,3180,'2002-12-11 21:55:09',129,'moebius','there\\\'s a nice eliptic arc to this piece. i like how it\\\'s about the beach without actually being <i>at</i> the beach, at least until it makes that surprise appearance at the end (which may or may not mean that it is unnecessary).<br><br>i understand that this was to be a longer assignment, which is a shame, because it seems pretty airtight at the moment. i say either stand your ground or take on the challenge of possibly reinventing the entire piece.<br><br>one thing it reminded me of is the way an extended beach visit totally changes my mood and sense of self in an undefinable way. the sun, air, and water work together to charge my body with a strangely contemplative energy that continues to roil long after the sun\\\'s gone down and the tide\\\'s gone out.',1,774,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(19295,3180,'2002-12-11 22:03:32',234,'etienne','i think that\\\'s basically what i was getting at; the beach changes you and it makes you go back for more...',1,109,129,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(19296,3180,'2002-12-11 22:10:21',234,'etienne','<p>you would have loved james\\\' play \\\"turn\\\" - its exactly about that.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>\\\"Here we are â€“ at the dawn of an age.  Everythingâ€™s new, everythingâ€™s fresh.  Iâ€™ve got a great feeling about this evening.  Anything could happen â€“ the possibilities are limitless â€“ or nearly.  This is a chance to begin again, to wipe the slate clean, a chance for rebirth!\\\"</i></p>',1,379,66,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(19297,3182,'2002-12-12 00:15:12',124,'gus','<i>We\\\'d make love with the windows open, listening for the landlady and the neighbors. We learned the varieties of quiet. We\\\'d lie in bed and listen to the outside noises, traffic and frogs and the winter rain falling, and life felt underlined with import and bound in death.</i>\r\n<p>\r\na little depth in the lives of the potheads. It reads like this is their turning point. Like after this, something bad happens that leads to worse. But I like that the voice is coming from much later, like he\\\'s back on his feet after whatever life hit him in the face with.',0,568,0,0,'64.172.56.64','en'),(19298,3177,'2002-12-12 04:11:38',122,'hool','this is good feedback. thank you.  it\\\'s been tough to write this way and maintain an overt narrative course.   but it is actually going somewhere (finally) in the next installment.  i\\\'ve felt too (as has jeeff) the build has been a bit too long.  i would disagree that this is like sitcom writing because it follows a direct story line from installment to installment whereas sitcoms generally don\\\'t.   but speaking of comedy - i was thinking of this character winding up getting a telemarketing job on this planet making calls to earth to sell settlements - not even by leaving earth can you escape shitty jobs.  i won\\\'t be going that route, though.\r\n\r\n',0,666,366,0,'66.185.85.80','en'),(19299,3177,'2002-12-12 05:28:10',93,'chiamattt','is the climax them just the people ordering shwarma\\\'s at la roche on bank street? if so thats totally dejavu',0,111,0,0,'61.37.53.41','en'),(19300,3176,'2002-12-12 05:59:12',59,'s5','ATTENTION INTERNET. NOT EVERYONE LIVES IN AMERICA. THANK YOU.',0,61,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19301,3176,'2002-12-12 06:21:01',66,'Peter','<br><tt>no doubt. i live in a third world country far removed from america.</tt>',1,80,59,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19302,3175,'2002-12-12 07:10:26',93,'chiamattt','ITS ALL ABOUT THE MOTHERFUCKING 4:20!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!! \r\n\r\n<p>\r\n\r\ni am also an 11:11 person. arent you supposed to make a wish if you look at the clock when its 11:11? \r\n\r\n<p>\r\n\r\ni also like the number 808 and 909. aciddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddd',0,300,0,0,'61.37.53.41','en'),(19303,3176,'2002-12-12 13:35:27',240,'xep','i am thinking about my step-newphew that was born 5 months ago addicted to methadone and taken away from his mother and put into foster care where he\\\'ll probably stay until he\\\'s 18 because she refuses to let him be adopted because she wants to \\\"take care of her baby\\\" even though she has three other children age 5 to 13 she doesn\\\'t take care of who are being raised by their 76 year old great-grandmother and i do not think he will grow up knowing how lucky he is just because he lives in north america. ',0,520,0,0,'144.132.122.179','en'),(19304,3180,'2002-12-12 15:01:04',67,'trouble','this was great, it opened the floodgates of emotion and memories of many summers spent north.  summer has a way of automatically changing what would be your normal routine and/or thought-pattern.  you walk a little more where you might otherwise drive/ride, you take special notice of sensory things like the feel of sun on your skin or beads of water on blades of grass under your feet.  <br<br>for those of us with seasons this is even more true, each summer brings you a sensation that while you might have experienced the same things last summer this time is different somehow.',1,581,0,0,'63.70.119.94','en'),(19305,3176,'2002-12-12 20:06:45',371,'Kyla','Oh xep, such a sad story, it\\\'s a shit world - I don\\\'t really know what to say.\r\n\r\nSo instead, I\\\'ll just say:  COME AND STAY WITH ME IN NZ!!!!  Okay good :)',6,164,240,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(19306,3176,'2002-12-12 22:05:11',124,'gus','sometimes kids in those situations come out of it and turn out great. one of my favorite people came from a crappy situation like that one. ',0,140,240,0,'64.160.52.151','en'),(19307,3177,'2002-12-12 22:21:51',370,'seanthecon','i think i did that telemarketing job once. except it was more of a gay sex line. i love the story and the images, but i would have to agree with ennoying, that there has been a lot of buildup. i\\\'ll tell you a story about seanthecon, i have never lasted more than 10 seconds in the sack. so let spiderman\\\'s sweet white fluid flow. let it flow, let it flow. other than that, i\\\'ve been jizzing all over this project. keep em cumming.',0,439,122,0,'209.53.180.49','en'),(19308,3176,'2002-12-13 00:13:29',240,'xep','ok kyla if i stay in .au i will come to .nz next year.  it\\\'s a plan.',0,71,371,0,'144.132.122.179','en'),(19309,3176,'2002-12-13 05:12:12',174,'Qluth Deishi Moll','How old are you? (if you don\\\'t mind divulging)\r\n',1,51,371,0,'24.189.13.22','en'),(19310,3173,'2002-12-13 16:23:13',195,'matt','\\\'i want to suck on her nipples.\\\'',1,38,0,0,'24.129.129.219','en'),(19311,3176,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','kyles is 1,233,567 years old next may.',6,38,174,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19312,3183,'2002-12-14 09:06:10',17,'austin','i would be way more impressed if you kissed neal pollack.',2,57,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19313,3183,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','the mack is back!',6,17,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19314,3183,'2002-12-14 09:46:52',59,'s5','who is neal pollack?',0,20,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19315,3186,'2002-12-14 09:50:59',59,'s5','all you whiteys look the same anyway.',0,37,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19316,3183,'2002-12-14 19:37:34',17,'neal pollack','<img src=\\\"http://store6.yimg.com/I/mcsweeneysbooks_1699_17348\\\">',3,69,59,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19317,3183,'2002-12-14 19:43:01',195,'matt','that man ain\\\'t got no clothes on!',1,36,0,0,'209.208.108.194','en'),(19318,3176,'2002-12-14 22:05:36',371,'Kyla','Yay, yes, good!  You can\\\'t be in Oz and not come to NZ, it\\\'s so close :)\r\n\r\nLove!',6,87,240,0,'203.96.111.237','en'),(19319,3185,'2002-12-14 23:47:00',195,'matt','is there a moratorium on tyler articles or something?<BR><BR><I><B>best friendly</B></I>',1,88,0,0,'209.208.108.238','en'),(19320,3183,'2002-12-15 00:22:52',74,'jo','ha, so since i wrote this? said boy emailed me one morning after a particularly nice night we\\\'d spent together and was like, \\\"i don\\\'t think we\\\'re going to work out...\\\"<BR>\r\n<b>we</b>? i\\\'d known him four days. there\\\'s no <b>we</b>. i\\\'ve had zits longer than that.<BR>\r\nso, anyway, now we\\\'re just hanging out. which is good, because what i really need in my life right now is yet another boy with whom i love spending time, to whom i\\\'m attracted (and vice versa), but with whom i\\\'m not having sex. all the attraction, none of the payoff! special torture!<BR>\r\nthe mack is gone, o, the mack is gone.',1,629,0,0,'172.159.131.125','en'),(19321,3183,'2002-12-15 00:24:00',74,'jo','i\\\'m wondering why this was posted without the links?',0,55,0,0,'172.159.131.125','en'),(19322,3183,'2002-12-15 00:35:50',59,'s5','what\\\'s happening to that poor cat?',5,37,17,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19323,3183,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','neal, dude, that\\\'s not okay.',6,31,17,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19324,3186,'2002-12-15 01:29:54',394,'ho3','and you dont see a similar attitude in Canada? i\\\'ve never been to korea, but i imagine we buy far more American products here, not to mention the fact that we totally leech off their economy. yet, Canadians for some reason tend to have very strong anti-american sentiments and seem to assume the rest of the world feels the same way. maybe it\\\'s just jealousy.   ',0,368,0,0,'216.113.217.17','en'),(19325,3186,'2002-12-15 01:38:35',66,'sine','\r\n\r\nI would like to give the Koreans the benefit of the doubt and assume they do understand that the american military does not equal american culture. or american people in general. a great majority of americans do not even remotely identifiy with the politicians or the military of this regime. bush is a horrendous president, a frighteningly ignorant warmonger surrounded by reagan-era hawks. dissent grows louder by the day here in nyc and elsewhere as the total information awareness project comes underway and we are constantly patrolled overhead by fighter jets.\r\n\r\nan interesting <a href=\\\"http://www.villagevoice.com/issues/0250/robbins.php\\\">article</a> in this week\\\'s voice makes plain subtle dissent even in the rank and file of the hallowed military institute, west point. even if bush has no conscience, a growing number of americans are voicing theirs. here\\\'s hoping.',2,892,0,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19326,3183,'2002-12-15 02:04:50',74,'jo','wait, also? there\\\'s a big chunk of this article missing. like the beginning of the neal pollack conversation, which doesn\\\'t make sense without the part which i had written.<BR>\r\nif you\\\'re not going to publish stuff without editing it first, could you at least have asked me to do the editing? or just not post it at all? since when does posting stuff on swinney give the permission for someone to go in and tinker with the content?',6,440,0,0,'172.149.169.20','en'),(19327,3183,'2002-12-15 03:12:54',17,'austin','jo: that was your bad html tags!  fixed.',2,38,74,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19328,3183,'2002-12-15 03:51:30',74,'jo','oh thank god. visions of content nazis were dancing in my head. thanks for the fix, el jefe.',1,92,17,0,'172.139.49.100','en'),(19329,3183,'2002-12-15 03:52:01',17,'neal pollack to o, s5','what?  i like pussy.',3,20,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19330,3183,'2002-12-15 03:52:56',17,'neal pollack','<UL>\r\n<LI>pussy\r\n<LI>scotch\r\n<LI>typewriters\r\n<LI>leather couches\r\n</UL>',1,72,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19331,3183,'2002-12-15 04:44:47',59,'s5','ah! now that whole conversation makes much more sense!',0,54,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19332,3188,'2002-12-16 06:45:01',175,'Paul G','<p>So, I read the piece twice - maybe more.  The first time through it, I wondered a lot about the narrative.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You have a real talent for descriptive prose; that much is certain. But there is something different about your current series: in the last group of articles that you put together there was a real sense of movement - each element of the series inclined towards the next.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I think that simon\\\'s comment upon the last article in the current series was very insightful: these pieces are very much like still photographs.  Whereas the last series was dynamic, the current one is static.  The fact that the characters here are old men - as opposed to the young young lovers in the previous series - is nicely complimented by the (perceived) change in the writing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\\\'s classic American Realism - like the painting of Edward Hopper; I really enjoy it.</p>',1,893,0,0,'67.195.73.79','en'),(19333,3189,'2002-12-16 18:31:14',195,'matt','Here in my car<BR>I feel safest of all<BR>I can lock all my doors<BR>It\\\'s the only way to live<BR>In cars\r\n\r\n',1,112,0,0,'209.208.109.144','en'),(19334,3188,'2002-12-16 18:32:55',195,'matt','this is good.  i know guys like slim.',1,37,0,0,'209.208.109.144','en'),(19335,3189,'2002-12-16 18:42:39',371,'Kyla','Driving paranoid<br>\r\nThey will drive me off the road<br>\r\nIndicate, fucker<br>',6,67,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(19336,3189,'2002-12-16 19:32:59',124,'gus','Life is a highway,<br>\r\nI want to ride it all night long.',0,57,0,0,'64.172.57.166','en'),(19337,3189,'2002-12-16 20:39:49',17,'austin','\r\nBuzzbomb buzzbomb macho-mobile<BR>\r\nThe road\\\'s my slave, that\\\'s how I feel<BR>\r\nI cruise alone, I cruise real far<BR>\r\nShoo young punk! I love my car\r\n',3,158,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19338,3190,'2002-12-17 00:06:37',122,'hool','<p>i say i\\\'ll give feedback and then i never do.  but now i will.   the details of the high tech environment are solid and believable thus far.  i don\\\'t really have too many suggestions about the tech environment just yet, only because the story hasn\\\'t gone too deep into it just yet.</p>\r\n<p>the whole piece is a smooth read and the transitions between past and present are clear and intuitive.  the dialogue is economical and i like that.</p>\r\n<p><i>In fact, his airway had tightened up so much that the level of oxygen reaching the Hufferâ€™s brain was roughly equivalent to that found at the summit of one of the junior peaks in the Himalayas. In the Captainâ€™s presence, the Huffer managed to contract severe altitude sickness while still at sea level.</i><br><br>\r\nThe first sentence here is excessive and a bit awkward.  The analogy is fitting and I like it, but the first sentence is too long and clumsy to be finished well by the second sentence (which is well worded).\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe huffer is the only character you\\\'ve written that i don\\\'t quite get a substantial picture of, but i can\\\'t figure out why just yet.  the other characters all feel very real to me.</p>\r\n<p><i>not particularly emotional or action packed, but necessary.</i> - nuts to disclaimers and people that will only give their attention to action packed emotional writing.  this was well written just like your other entries so you don\\\'t need to offer excuses.</p>',0,1462,0,0,'66.185.85.80','en'),(19339,3189,'2002-12-17 01:56:50',66,'Peter','<br><tt>It was me and the posse with Bunny D<br>\r\nWe were cruisinâ€™ in the Jags or the Lambroghinis<br>\r\nWhen low and behold there appeared a mirage<br>\r\nHe was hooking up a car in his is daddyâ€™s garage<br>\r\nWe stopped short, did a double take<br>\r\nHe was looking so fly, I thought I wasnâ€™t awake<br>\r\nHe was obviously hooking up bass I assume<br>\r\nBut then he turned a little button and the car went <b>boom</b>... </tt>',1,407,0,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19340,3189,'2002-12-17 02:32:09',83,'adamajah','<p>\r\nred white rumble strip<br>\r\nthe apex is the sweet spot<br>\r\nHoosiers all around<br>\r\n</p>\r\n',3,96,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(19341,3189,'2002-12-17 03:12:18',129,'roger miller','<br>Trailer for sale or rent<br>\r\nRooms to let...fifty cents.<br>\r\nNo phone, no pool, no pets<br>\r\nI ain\\\'t got no cigarettes<br>\r\nAh, but..two hours of pushin\\\' broom<br>\r\nBuys an eight by twelve four-bit room<br>\r\nI\\\'m a man of means by no means<br>\r\n<b>King of the road.</b><br>\r\n',0,289,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(19342,3189,'2002-12-17 04:20:57',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>in a fast german car,<br>\r\nim amazed that i survived.<br>\r\nan airbag saved my life.</i></tt>',1,103,0,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19343,3189,'2002-12-17 04:26:39',239,'Poonlarp','One foot on the break and one on the gas<br>\r\nWell there\\\'s too much traffic I can\\\'t pass, no<Br>\r\nWhat used to take three hours, now takes all 