>\r\n<br />\r\nSomething in the way she moves <br />\r\nAttracts me like no other lover <br />\r\nSomething in the way she moves me<br />\r\nI don\'t want to leave her now <br />\r\nYou know I believe and how <br />\r\n<br />\r\nYou\'re asking me will our love grow<br />\r\nI don\'t know, I don\'t know <br />\r\nYou stick around and it may show <br />\r\nI don\'t know, I don\'t know <br />\r\n<br />\r\nSomething in the way she moves<br />\r\nAll I gotta do is think of her <br />\r\nSomething in her style that shows me<br />\r\nI don\'t want to leave her now <br />\r\nYou know I believe and how<br />',6,963,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19474,3211,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','way to hoist the blame off yourselves.  god isn\\\'t bumbling, you are.',6,71,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19475,3211,'2003-01-17 23:07:55',82,'simon','actually, i blame you!',1,22,62,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19476,3211,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','that\\\'s okay.  i blame austin.',6,32,82,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19477,3211,'2003-01-18 00:32:51',195,'matt','it was synchronous today when i came across a book in the pastor\\\'s office when i was vacuuming (and, yes, i wiped it off afterward) called <U>facing an abusive god</U>.  it posited that god is loving but also occaisonally abusive in many ways.  it gave holocausts and child abuse as examples.  to posit a god who is, at times, at odds with us and unjust is, i think, a more even-handed approach when considering a deity\\\'s motives.  (i think the pastor is a closet liberal.)',1,479,0,0,'209.208.110.66','en'),(19478,3217,'2003-01-18 01:16:37',17,'austin','can it, ho.',3,11,62,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19479,3211,'2003-01-18 12:47:56',187,'kandrer','like orphans wish parents, i often surprise myself wishing God could be mean, but here.<br>\r\nsee also: \\\"Les grenouilles demandaient un roi\\\" by Lafontaine; oh, it seems it is also in the Bible (First book of Samuel, 8, 1-19)',1,229,62,0,'211.4.130.8','en'),(19480,3211,'2003-01-18 12:50:08',187,'kandrer','bad title, sorry; here is the ref: <a href=\\\"http://www.lafontaine.net/fables/3grenroi.php\\\">Les Grenouilles qui demandent un Roi</a>\r\n',1,139,0,0,'211.4.130.8','en'),(19481,3211,'2003-01-19 03:32:03',195,'matt','and what the hell does that say?  the grenouilles that demanded a king?  i don\\\'t speak french.  i wish i did.\r\n\r\n<I><B>best friendly</I></B>',1,143,0,0,'209.208.108.12','en'),(19482,3211,'2003-01-19 05:30:32',187,'kandrer','i could not find it in english; only a few fables <a href=\\\"http://www.pacificnet.net/~johnr/aesop/aesopjdlf.html\\\">there</a>. and i am sure this is not easy stuff for translation.<br>\r\nah, but i found Aesop\\\'s original piece <a href=\\\"http://www.pagebypagebooks.com/Aesop/Aesops_Fables/The_Frogs_Desiring_a_King_p1.html\\\">here</a>.<br>\r\nbut La Fontaine\\\'s ending is a bit better, i think...<br>\r\nlearning french (at least understand it) from english should not be that hard, from what i heard.',1,506,195,0,'211.134.105.119','en'),(19483,3007,'2003-01-19 05:30:37',370,'seanthecon','i am reading this again and i think i only understand now. shit man. ',0,69,0,0,'216.113.217.177','en'),(19484,3007,'2003-01-19 09:33:34',187,'kandrer','i agree with ryan, and earlier with Paul G: i never thought of you as someone wise. smart, maybe; the word seems to have so many meanings according to my dictionary...',4,167,75,0,'210.198.200.37','en'),(19485,3007,'2003-01-20 07:56:18',187,'kandrer','and, hool: yes, some of us are \\\'there\\\', please keep on posting.',1,69,122,0,'210.239.112.88','en'),(19486,2794,'2003-01-20 08:10:24',187,'kandrer','<p>well, in case you come here again, my old friend, i can only repeat: i\\\'d like to read more from you.\r\n</p><p>\r\nrem: how come, in the very short list (less than 10) of people that i appreciate, at least 3 are colorblind?<br>\r\nfound on the web: <i>one out of twelve men and one out of two hundred women</i> are colorblind.<br>\r\ni know no colorblind woman; this is perfectly normal.<br>\r\ni should have 36 men in my favorites list... see? there is something!<br>\r\nmaybe it\\\'s just because any kind of difference (specially a hidden one) helps to understand the world?\r\n</p>',1,577,0,0,'210.239.112.88','en'),(19487,3215,'2003-01-20 18:35:14',251,'Jamie','damn you. damn you all for writing comments longer and more beautful than my actual article. damn you!',6,102,0,0,'172.179.92.179','en'),(19488,3223,'2003-01-21 00:43:45',240,'xep','go go gadget dissertation generator',0,35,0,0,'64.81.241.248','en'),(19489,3222,'2003-01-21 00:57:08',175,'Paul G','<p>This was - for me - 90% \\\"Fuck yeah!\\\" and 10% \\\"Fuck you!\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ok.  I love the structure of most of the things that I\\\'ve seen you write, here.  My last comment to you made reference to the, \\\"cadence,\\\" of your prose; I think that previous comment holds.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I really dig most of the imagery that you employ.  When I read something like, <i>\\\"The mermaids cry,\\\"</i> I\\\'m hooked.  Also, the transition back and forth from the ship and its crew to the family and its members is a nice one.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It seems to be the case that there are certain symbols, or types, that speak directly to the unconscious; there is also a surreal mode of artistry that seems to parallel the working of the unconscious: oddly juxtaposing things and events - in a manner that appears to lack sense, but which may - in fact - speak more sensibly or truthfully than \\\"better\\\" ordered communication.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But there are also nasty things hidden down there in the basement of the mind.  I haven\\\'t read much Freud.  But in Plato at least, these (nasty)things are said to be expressed in our dreams.  Plato wrote about the desire of a man to have intercourse with his mother - and that same event appears in other Greek works.  You took me - in first person - to a place in which horrific violence was done to a partner.  And that was someplace that I didn\\\'t want to go - even in text.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>More mermaids - less skull-smashing, please.  90/10.  Cheers.</p>',3,1488,0,0,'67.195.182.212','en'),(19490,3226,'2003-01-21 03:18:19',57,'weeb','you. did. not. vomit into your motorcycle helmut.\r\n<br><br>\r\noh lordy shiznitz, this is the funniest swinney entry evereverEVER!!!!!! how can i tell you how much this has made my week???????? gasping.',1,200,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(19491,3226,'2003-01-21 03:31:19',64,'Joanna','Drink driving is not cool. ',1,27,0,0,'156.62.3.21','en'),(19492,3226,'2003-01-21 03:33:15',17,'austin','I am listening to triumphant Tchaikovsky music.',1,47,57,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19493,3226,'2003-01-21 03:40:03',17,'austin','Yea, don\\\'t drink and drive. ',5,31,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19494,3226,'2003-01-21 03:41:31',66,'Peter','<br><tt>shiza! damn, this was a <i>fine</i> piece, if i do say so. im cracking up!</tt>',1,87,17,0,'66.108.188.76','en'),(19495,3226,'2003-01-21 05:26:33',65,'le','i am crying',4,11,0,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(19496,3226,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','oh my god.<br />\r\n<br />\r\noh my god.<br />\r\n<br />\r\nyou are my vomiting hero.',6,77,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19497,3226,'2003-01-21 13:40:55',59,'s5','!!!!',0,4,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19498,3224,'2003-01-21 15:17:00',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ok, now youve got <i>\\\"something in the way she moves\\\"</i> stuck on repeat in my head...</tt>',1,106,0,0,'204.168.247.129','en'),(19499,3226,'2003-01-21 20:18:08',83,'adamajah','never drink after driving one beer',3,34,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(19500,3226,'2003-01-21 20:31:19',175,'Paul G','<i>\\\"I wish I was a warrior king inscrutable benign<br>\r\nwith a faceless charging power always at my command...\\\"</i>',1,121,0,0,'67.195.79.181','en'),(19501,3226,'2003-01-21 21:56:57',17,'austin','<a href=\\\"http://www.dispatchcomm.com/media/burp.wav\\\">BURP!</A>',2,68,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19502,3224,'2003-01-22 07:32:37',175,'Paul G','<p>\r\n<i>\r\n\\\"I love her skinny hips.\\\"\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.twin-music.com/azlyrics/s_file/sir.html\\\">Down with skinny hips!</a>\r\n</p>',0,159,0,0,'67.195.31.66','en'),(19503,3224,'2003-01-22 15:10:02',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i used to be a hater, but now ive gotta admit, <i>skinny hips #1</i>, especially when they packs much back. damn!</tt>',1,82,175,0,'204.168.247.129','en'),(19504,3224,'2003-01-22 19:31:03',175,'Paul G','Oh, it doesn\\\'t really matter; all hips are good.  I was just so much happier when I understood the music.  Now, when I ask the kids what they want on the radio, they say, \\\"Bling the bling,\\\" or something like that.  And I don\\\'t know what a bling is.  At night, I put on 92.3 or 96.3 FM in Chicago; and that seems to make them happy.  But, they bob and rock to the bass in a way that frightens me.  And I just feel very old. ',0,435,66,0,'67.195.79.95','en'),(19505,3224,'2003-01-22 19:36:36',66,'Peter','<br><tt>chi-town\\\'s 88.9 has a good chicago-house show on weekend evenings. tune in to it while driving through the southside. thats where the people really know how to boogie.</tt>',1,183,175,0,'204.168.247.129','en'),(19506,3224,'2003-01-22 19:59:34',175,'Paul G','Verily, I say unto you, it is a bit ironic that some of the most \\\"street\\\" or \\\"underground\\\" radio in town is produced by exclusive academic instituions: <a href=\\\"http://www.iit.edu/~wiit/\\\">88.9 FM</a> and <a href=\\\"http://www.wnur.org/\\\">89.3 FM</a>.  I have better luck getting 89.3 in the car - and I like their Reggae and Punk.  Wee!\r\n\r\n<p>Perhaps, this is all a discreet appeal to Jamie to set his lyrical prose in song?  Hmmm...</p>',0,458,66,0,'67.195.79.95','en'),(19507,3224,'2003-01-22 20:03:27',66,'Peter','<br><tt>its the same in nyc. academic stations are less constrained by having to please a largely commercial audience, ergo, they can play a wider variety of the sort of music people actually want to listen to, or eclectic/discombobulated music, without having to alienate their for-profit sources of funding.</tt> ',1,315,175,0,'204.168.247.129','en'),(19508,3224,'2003-01-22 20:07:21',175,'Paul G','Word, my brother.  Haha.\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n:D\r\n</p>',0,41,66,0,'67.195.79.95','en'),(19509,3226,'2003-01-23 11:59:45',231,'alana','nice, austin.',2,13,0,0,'203.221.33.121','en'),(19510,3222,'2003-01-23 19:02:18',122,'hool','aye captain my captain i\\\'ll follow to stay dead.\r\n\r\nchange nothing, the hammer is perfect punctuation, i admire your violence.',0,129,0,0,'66.185.85.80','en'),(19511,3226,'2003-01-24 07:28:50',449,'s','<p>\r\nDamn. That\\\'s just cool. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI mean, y\\\'know...for vomiting and all.\r\n</p>',2,92,0,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(19512,3230,'2003-01-24 12:07:38',82,'simon','two things:<br>\n1. i once left a condom on the floor next to my roommate\\\'s bed when my <a href=\\\"http://www.rs-erections.com/keough11.html\\\">future ex-husband</a> was in town. oops!<br>\n2. also, a guy i knew in la went to tijuana to get a hooker and he ran into another professed feminist, anti-hooker getting guy we knew from la getting TWO HOOKERS for himself. my friend katie said, upon finding out, that guy has a SECRET hooker habit, at least nate is upfront about it. i thought the idea of having a secret hooker habit was sort of gross and funny. that\\\'s all.',1,575,0,0,'69.3.26.85','en'),(19513,3226,'2003-01-24 12:11:11',82,'simon','i was talking to my sis who had the stomach flu for a few days. she told me, in detail, the contents, quantity and duration of every barf. then she also told me that the ballet master at the ballet company where she dances axed her  how she got so thin so fast and she was impressed. gross, huh..',1,296,17,0,'69.3.26.85','en'),(19514,3226,'2003-01-24 12:12:05',82,'simon','the last time i barfed it was all southern comfort and i was lying on the sidewalk in a sailor outfit. fyi.',1,107,0,0,'69.3.26.85','en'),(19515,3230,'2003-01-24 23:55:18',74,'jo','i want to start referring to my boyfriends as my future ex-husbands. ',1,69,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(19516,3230,'2003-01-24 23:55:54',74,'jo','nevermind: i want to start a band called \\\"your future ex-girlfriend.\\\"',0,75,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(19517,3229,'2003-01-25 09:02:50',82,'simon','ha! sean, this is great. i laughed my ass off. also, hi.',0,56,0,0,'69.3.136.102','en'),(19518,3226,'2003-01-26 03:20:44',255,'qq','Yucky.\r\n\r\n\r\ndo you want a new gas tank that has working reserve valves?\r\n',1,73,0,0,'64.81.241.248','en'),(19519,3226,'2003-01-26 09:21:39',17,'austin','no, i want <a href=\\\"http://www.beemergarage.com/Tanks/gm.jpg\\\">an eleven gallon gas tank</a>.',2,98,255,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19520,3226,'2003-01-26 17:30:24',17,'Austin\\\'s Dad','Rule number one for we motorcycle riders:  Always fill the tank after a long ride and before you park it.  Otherwise, you forget how much gas is in the tank.  Enjoyed reading your website...Love Dad',0,198,0,0,'24.175.65.27','en'),(19521,3226,'2003-01-26 17:33:46',240,'xep','i read your comment as a \\\"new gas mask\\\"\r\ninstead of gas tank then started picturing some weird device you fit over your face like der schnuffelpenis that would allow you to puke while wearing a helmet and it wouldn\\\'t get all over you or the bike.  you could have the output hose go right next to the carburetor overflows.\r\n',0,332,255,0,'64.81.241.248','en'),(19522,3227,'2003-01-27 05:24:37',93,'chiamattt','fuck you. im sick of being archived. ',4,37,0,0,'218.145.25.79','en'),(19523,3233,'2003-01-27 07:03:44',65,'le','ladies and gentleman. my mother. she is talking about the fact that my 50 year old civilian father is going to kuwait out of a sense of duty - he\\\'s a government contractor for the army.\r\n<br><br>\r\nshe thinks i\\\'m foolish and idealistic to want peace - to march for it. and she believes this war is truly about keeping america \\\"safe\\\" from those bad iraqis.\r\n<br><br>\r\npersonally i don\\\'t see why my father\\\'s imminent danger isn\\\'t a good enough reason for her to rethink her stance, but hey, what do i know, i\\\'m just a freak with dreads and piercings. ',6,572,0,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(19524,3233,'2003-01-27 07:24:45',449,'s','<p>Ah yes. My ex-boyfriend/kindabestfriend got shipped out a few weeks ago from North Carolina. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHis mother said \\\"It\\\'s God\\\'s will\\\".\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'m just pretty damn pissed.\r\n</p>',2,205,0,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(19525,3233,'2003-01-27 08:54:37',59,'s5','other countries have protests and free speech, but they don\\\'t drop bombs on people or send their families into war.',6,118,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19526,3233,'2003-01-27 09:07:52',75,'tyler','yes they do.',1,12,0,0,'165.247.165.115','en'),(19527,3233,'2003-01-27 09:14:44',234,'etienne','this made me cry.',1,17,0,0,'210.86.57.176','en'),(19528,3233,'2003-01-27 09:24:20',75,'tyler','i went over there in the summer of 1996. i was scared, tired, and very young, and i didn\\\'t like it. we shot missles at iraq that summer, from my ship among others, and i didn\\\'t like that either, even though i was mostly scared of there being some kind of accident. i had a lot of late nights that summer.<br><br>\r\nas far as whether or not war is the right thing to do, well...i don\\\'t know, and i doubt very seriously whether anyone knows. is bush corrupt? maybe. if he is, and this is a totally unjust war, then he has managed to elude all of the checks and balances that we have built into our system to stop that sort of thing from happening. i\\\'d like to have a little more faith in our government. even nixon couldn\\\'t keep the balls in the air <i>that</i> well, or that long.<br><br>\r\ni don\\\'t know if we should go to war with iraq, and neither do you! how can you know? even the government doesn\\\'t seem to know. in some cases, not going is worse than going--that\\\'s a sad fact of the world. this is a complicated situation with a lot of factors, and to form a valid opinion on it, you would need to be very educated. \\\'gut feeling\\\' isn\\\'t quite going to cut it when the stakes are as high as they are. <br><br>\r\nso it goes...',1,1258,0,0,'165.247.165.115','en'),(19529,3233,'2003-01-27 10:18:55',154,'ryan','i should get cable soon.',0,24,0,0,'65.58.0.176','en'),(19530,3233,'2003-01-27 11:04:14',65,'le','he\\\'s a civilian. he can quit whenever he wants. ',0,51,0,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(19531,3233,'2003-01-27 11:05:39',59,'s5','\\\"not going is worse than going--that\\\'s a sad fact of the world.\\\"\r\n<br><br>\r\nthe only sad fact of the world is that people still believe in the false necessity of killing.\r\n<br><br>\r\nalso, plenty of nations with free speech and protests do not go to war. you are sadly misinformed. some of those same nations don\\\'t even have a military. they find freedom by not turning the world against them.',6,404,75,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19532,3233,'2003-01-27 16:17:05',67,'trouble','this sucks.  i have nothing new to say with regards to the whole situation other than \\\'this sucks\\\'.  all the rhetoric now sounds tired, this whole thing is a monumental mistake and *we* will pay dearly.  none of the marching we did, none of the posters we made will make any difference.  my heart goes out to you and your husband.',1,336,0,0,'63.70.119.94','en'),(19533,3233,'2003-01-27 16:43:16',180,'auntie','the bottom line for me is that mark is intelligent, not easily duped by government spin or propaganda, and a pacifist.  if his heart tells him that he needs to continue in his capacity as an army civilian, even if it means going to a war zone, i have to trust that he has knowledge and information that i do not have, that he knows things he cannot share with me.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmost soldiers are pacifists, believe it or not.  this is because they know what war IS.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhen we were young, we both marched against the vietnam war.  enough times that we made it into fbi databases.<br><br>\r\n\r\nit doesn\\\'t really matter, on a personal level, whether or not the war is justified or not.  what matters is that he is going to go there, and it is my job to make sure he leaves here feeling as secure as he can that the girls and i will be safe in his absence.<br><br>\r\n\r\nhe is going because three other people, in three other places, came up with medical reasons why THEY could not go. they all signed the same piece of paper agreeing to mobilize when requested, they all draw the same paycheck, but some people understand honour, and others do not.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmarch against the war all day long, but marching is not going to change the situation.<br><br>\r\n\r\nif you are serious about your political beliefs, you will do whatever it takes to make the people who can change things listen to what you have to say in a forum that will listen to it without blowing you off as hippie whackos. this is a lesson we learned very well during the vietnam war.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe had to cut our hair and trade our sandals and bell bottoms for \\\"establishment\\\" attire.  when we did that, they listened.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n',0,1703,0,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(19534,3233,'2003-01-27 18:29:25',195,'matt','we should have lunch sometime while you\\\'re down here.  i think that would be fun.  let me know.',1,98,0,0,'209.208.110.242','en'),(19535,3233,'2003-01-27 21:30:38',82,'simon','hi auntie. your article was pretty intense and, of course, there\\\'s way more to it than this. but i just wanted to make an observation. what struck me about the protest in san francisco, which i attended with my entire family, was the amazing diversity of the crowd. perhaps moreso than any other i\\\'ve seen. aside from hippy wackos, i saw vietman vets (including my uncles), WWII vets (including my grandfather), raider fans, zen monks, jocks, business people, rich ladies in prada boots, college students, sushi chefs, homeless people, etc.<br>\r\nsincerely, simon',1,568,180,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19536,3233,'2003-01-28 04:16:49',180,'auntie','of course it was diverse!  nobody wants a war, especially the soldiers and civilians who are liable to be blown to smithereens if they have one. - but which of those protesters do you think has a chance of being taken seriously by a mainstream american voting populace?<br><br>\r\n\r\nyou gotta consider who the majority of the voters are, who gives the majority of the money to political candidates, and who the candidates actually take time to listen to seriously.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthen, mold your outward appearance, speech patterns, and manerisms into something that will appeal to all or some of those groups.  be prepared, be succinct, and go where those people hang out, talk to them, one at a time.  if that means the local baptist wednesday night potluck supper, or the monster truck rally, or a long line at the bank, there is where you will make your case, one person at a time.<br><br>\r\n\r\nremember, you can\\\'t change the world overnight, and you can only do it one person at a time.  the chances of anyone with a firm belief in opposition to yours giving a rats ass for a crowd of demonstrators is nil and none.<br><br>\r\n\r\non the other hand, you make yourself a face with a name and a family and a personality, a person who does not frighten, put off, or disturb, then you have a chance of getting a person to listen - and ponder - what you have to say.<br><br>',0,1365,82,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(19537,3233,'2003-01-28 04:32:20',59,'s5','i guess i don\\\'t understand what your point is. no one wants war, but it\\\'s okay to go work for war? how does that make you a pacifist? and, do you honestly think the anti-war movement is just a bunch of granola hippies? the world has changed quite a bit since the vietnam war.',6,281,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19538,3233,'2003-01-28 05:01:15',82,'simon','i think the mainstream american voting populous is pretty diverse, isn\\\'t it?',1,79,180,0,'67.121.84.106','en'),(19539,3233,'2003-01-28 05:08:38',82,'simon','also, <a href=\\\"http://www.moveon.org/nowar/\\\">these people</a> seem pretty mainstream.',1,91,0,0,'67.121.84.106','en'),(19540,3233,'2003-01-28 05:23:13',59,'s5','no, only white middle aged christian men who wear suits are allowed to vote.',0,76,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19541,3233,'2003-01-28 05:31:12',175,'Paul G','How about this idea:\r\n<p>\r\nIn the interest of preserving a certain place, and/or way of living - a \\\"mode of being,\\\" if you will - some people are willing to band together and struggle against the \\\"enemies\\\" who threaten them.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nVarious groups, and individuals, would argue about the definitions of words like: \\\"struggle,\\\" and \\\"enemy.\\\"  And yet, the preceding paragraph explains the motivations of the soldiers, the hippies, and the militant followers of Islam.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAnd they\\\'re all right - from their own perspective:\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe soldiers are good and loyal citizens of their State, who are willing to give life and limb in order to defend their families, and values, and way of living.  They have a sense of honor; they tend to be very pragmatic.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe hippies understand that the State is as great a threat - if not a greater threat - to the personal freedom that is at the heart of the appeal of democracy and the \\\"Western\\\" way of life, than is any outside group, or ideology.  They tend to be more sensitive than the soldiers; they empathize with all living things; they want to avoid any suffering - at almost any cost.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe militant followers of Islam understand that along with Western democratic economic systems comes an attachment to possessions that is corrupting of the soul.  They too are pragmatic: they understand that even as the West exports its culture, so too it extends its political hegemony.  And - just like any democrat - they desire some measure of self-determination, as opposed to being forced to accept another\\\'s will.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAnd so, like-minded individuals wear their uniforms: flattops and polyester, or dreadlocks and cotton - it doesn\\\'t really matter - and they band together to wage their struggle in their own way.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMaybe - just maybe - freedom depends upon each of them doing what they are doing in the way that they are doing it.  Maybe, real freedom requires some sort of balancing of opposing forces - as opposed to any one becoming too strong.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nLife has gotten really stinking complicated.  If I had a daughter (she would be 18 now) I would tell her to worry about having people in her life who cared for her - the person that she was - and not to worry about the other stuff.  Because the rest of it - the clothes, and big ideas, and all of that stuff - don\\\'t mean anything in the end.\r\n</p>',0,2441,0,0,'67.195.31.55','en'),(19542,3233,'2003-01-28 05:57:57',65,'le','when i was younger, you used to support my right to be a freethinker. you encouraged it. now you are telling me that i am supposed to conform? what\\\'s going on. ',6,163,0,0,'216.27.183.162','en'),(19543,3233,'2003-01-28 06:51:37',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\nHow much does a missile cost? Even one of the cheap ones.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHow much does it cost the U.S tax payer to have hundreds of thousands of U.S army troops stationed and training in the middle east?\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nHow much does it cost? \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nI work with a dude who went to Beirut in the early 80\\\'s. He brags about blowing up goats and kicking down doors looking for \\\'sand niggers\\\'. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMost soldiers are pacifists. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHow much does it cost to train a pacifist to kill? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHow much would it costs to airdrop every consumer product from every walmart in america. Drop toasters and kitchen appliances. Drop freeze dried coffee, and poptarts. Drop \\\'I love NY\\\" keychains and tampons. Drop it all. Unload all the public storage facilities of America and drop all that old furnature on saddam. Drop what the people want. Drop what saddam wont give them. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe keep hearing that saddam has been such a bad guy; bombing his own people, gassing his own people, and directing little of the nations wealth at the iraqi people. We want saddam to comply. We want saddam to retire. We want saddam to stop being bad. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe want to get saddam out by bombing his people. We want to get saddam out by killing hundreds of thousands of iraqi\\\'s by way of mass air command bombing. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhy are we different then saddam? why do we think we are protecting anything? are you protecting me? Isnt saddam protecting himself by bombing and killing his own? why are we going to do his job for him? money. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIf we drop something an iraqi wants to protect, we can then expect and admire the iraqi people. We can then watch them on cnn and not some general in a sterile beifing room. we can watch those new iraqis fight for something they want to protect. People become so much more patriotic when a symbol of their statehood is being threatened by an outside actor. what we need is for the iraqi people to want to change things. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\ncome home johnny and billy. sally and sarah. station paul and smith in the walmart. have them load c130\\\'s. have them pack all those drones with nailpolish and cheap chinese shoes. walmart can save the planet. \r\n</p>\r\n  ',1,2231,0,0,'218.145.25.15','en'),(19544,3233,'2003-01-28 09:11:06',187,'kandrer','hey all, this is the mean frenchie speaking :) i saw \\\"black hawk down\\\" recently. good movie, i think. and yeah, i am glad not to pay tax anymore in the US. still, i regret i missed the march in SF.<br>\r\nand auntie: i think i learned that when some people can\\\'t listen to you because of the way you dress, they won\\\'t understand you better if you dress \\\"correctly\\\". i don\\\'t desagree with you. what you say about honor and many things is true...<br>\r\ntoo many things to say anyway; it is known that the american governement + TV + etc is lying, has been lying. the bombing of Afghanistan *was* a shame. how this can happen again right after is in deed a mystery. let\\\'s hope that with some chance bin laden will be captured in a cave of Bagdad?<br>\r\n[x] ignore me, i am french',1,796,0,0,'211.4.130.252','en'),(19545,3233,'2003-01-28 11:36:36',20,'jim','<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nI desperately wish I believed that this war was about protecting life and safeguarding human freedom.  in reality, I think it has very little to do with that.  at best, George Bush is one of the worst diplomats in US presidential history<sup>1</sup>, who sees every international conflict as part of an epic struggle against good and evil<sup>2</sup>, and sees himself as the arbiter of God\'s will in that fight<sup>3</sup>.  at worst, he\'s a man who recognizes that the coming oil shortage will change the global economy in unimaginable ways, believes that he has a mandate from God to establish the United States as the undisputed empire of the world before this happens, and is not just willing but eager to shed blood to realize this goal, regardless of international outcry.  in either case, his reasoning for this war is not sound.  never before has the United States moved so quickly into a conflict without the support of it\'s citizens or allies around the world.  I absolutely recognize that the power of the United States Armed Forces makes it possible for me to live in freedom.  this fact does not give legitimacy to abuse of that power.  \r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nI desperately wish I believed that the anti-war demonstrations and propaganda were nothing but pointless, naive insolence.  in reality, I believe a cohesive and ubiquitous protest movement is one of the few chances the people of this great nation have of preventing the United States from becoming the sort of government we\'ve spent the last 227 years evolving away from.  from this movement, I believe leaders will emerge, willing to devote their lives and careers to a political system they know to be corrupt, holding on to the remote hope that they might have a chance to change it from within.  at the very least, the movement may prevent a majority of Americans from turning their backs on democracy in November.  that alone could effect dramatic change on our foreign policy.\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<font size=\"-1\">1: in his own words: \"If this were a dictatorship, it\'d be a heck of a lot easier, just so long as I\'m the dictator.\" \"You\'re either with us, or you\'re with the terrorists.\"\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n2: \"I know we can overcome evil with greater good.\"\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n3: \"This world He created is of moral design.\" \"Our responsibility to history is already clear: to rid the world of evil.\"</font>\r\n\r\n',6,2426,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(19546,3233,'2003-01-28 14:32:36',180,'auntie','i\\\'m not taking away my support for you to be a free thinker.  i love you too much to debate this in public. i wrote this swinney before we discussed the situation.<br><br> \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',0,185,65,0,'24.162.106.240','en'),(19547,3233,'2003-01-28 15:19:38',66,'Peter','<br><tt>footnotes in a comment. you have now officially made my day.</tt>',1,73,20,0,'204.168.247.129','en'),(19548,3233,'2003-01-28 17:35:26',74,'jo','clapclapclapclapclap. yes, well said, annotations and all.',2,58,20,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(19549,2511,'2003-01-29 02:15:58',17,'austin','fucktard.\r\n',0,11,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19550,3233,'2003-01-29 03:02:12',59,'s5','i can\\\'t post this in enough places: if you really want to know the attitudes and agenda of the bush administration, you can read it <a href=\\\"http://www.newamericancentury.org/statementofprinciples.htm\\\">in their own words</a>, or summarized for you in <a href=\\\"http://www.ccmep.org/2002_articles/Iraq/121202_america.htm\\\">this handy article</a>.',6,358,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19551,2511,'2003-01-29 03:46:59',66,'Peter','<br><tt>fucktong?</tt>',1,22,0,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19552,3233,'2003-01-29 08:35:55',154,'ryan','a possible outcome: <a href=\\\"http://www.idleworm.com/nws/2002/11/iraq2.shtml\\\">Gulf War II</a> (requires flash)',0,116,0,0,'65.58.7.192','en'),(19553,2511,'2003-01-29 21:25:03',86,'egg','I thought you weren\\\'t cussin\\\'.',0,36,17,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(19554,3233,'2003-01-30 02:34:10',74,'jo','<p>i got the following email from my representative, lloyd doggett, today:</p>\r\n<p>Dear Ms. Vaught:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>As the Administration draws ever closer to launching a ground\r\ninvasion of Iraq, I continue to work to slow the rush to war and to\r\nsupport effective international inspections.  Today, I joined Reps.\r\nDennis Kucinich, Sheila Jackson-Lee and some of our other\r\ncolleagues at a national press conference in the Capitol to respond\r\nto the President\\\'s State of the Union address.  It is deeply troubling\r\nthat yet another opportunity was missed to present solid evidence,\r\nnot just more repackaged rhetoric.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Indeed, the President\\\'s speech contained as much evidence of the\r\nimmediacy of Iraq\\\'s threat as it had mentions of Osama bin Laden.\r\nThe President\\\'s talk of peace set against the continued rush to war\r\nreminds me of one of those old Marx Brothers movies when\r\nGroucho was caught in a compromising situation -- \\\"you can either\r\nbelieve me or believe your eyes.\\\"  The growing number of troops\r\nbeing moved to the Persian Gulf region belies the talk of peace.\r\nThe case has simply not been made for launching a near unilateral\r\ninvasion that would deplete our treasury, endanger our security,\r\nand diminish our moral authority.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>As I and a number of my House colleagues recently stated in a\r\nletter to President Bush, \\\"the U.S. should make every attempt to\r\nachieve Iraq\\\'s disarmament through diplomatic means and with the\r\nfull support of our allies, in accordance with the process articulated\r\nin UN Security Council resolution 1441.\\\"\r\n</p><p>\r\nI welcome your continued counsel on this and other federal\r\nmatters.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Lloyd Doggett<BR>\r\n201 Cannon House Office Building<BR>\r\nWashington, D.C. 20515<BR>\r\n(202) 225-4865, (512) 916-5921<BR>\r\nwww.house.gov/doggett</p>\r\n\r\n<P>needless to say, he is a rockstar, and the reason that i continue to believe that good things <b>can</b> happen and there are still some good guys in power.</p>\r\n',1,2000,0,0,'172.159.220.202','en'),(19555,3237,'2003-01-30 07:02:12',124,'gus','Im still liking the series. ',0,28,0,0,'64.163.213.118','en'),(19556,3237,'2003-01-30 07:11:03',66,'Peter','<Br><tt>when i was in college, my partial diff. eq. professor always called series <i>serie</i>. it really bugged me out. now, every time i hear the word <i>series</i> i have bad memories of professor shou wu zheng.<Br><Br>infinte serie!</tt>',1,242,0,0,'66.108.188.76','en'),(19557,3233,'2003-01-30 13:51:36',67,'trouble','that was fantastic jo.  that gave me a glimmer of hope that when it comes to government it\\\'s not exactly all rich, old, stuffy white men hell-bent on killing large percentages of mostly innocent men, women and children with errant bombs so that we may have more cheap oil, something to watch on CNN @ night and a stronger foothold in a land where we don\\\'t respect the culture or history.  go america! *best friendly*',1,422,74,0,'63.70.119.94','en'),(19558,3240,'2003-01-30 18:23:48',195,'matt','it feels like a hot mama is about to make the scene.',1,52,0,0,'209.208.109.180','en'),(19559,3233,'2003-01-30 19:09:33',129,'moebius','weird. last night i was carrying on with my usual innocuous, simple, mind-distracting and solitary pursuits: playing the ps2, watching a French musical on dvd, checking the web, reading comics--when i had this sudden moment of...what? clarity? lucidity? i\\\'m not sure.<br><br>i just looked up at the grey ceiling of my apartment and said out loud: \\\"holy shit. war. we\\\'re going to war in Iraq.\\\"<br><br>i think part of the problem is i keep immersing myself in so many mediated and false realities--be it books, music, vid games, movies, \\\"reality\\\" tv shows or whatever--that on one level this whole situation in Iraq just feels like one more simulation. it was an awful thing to realize the brutal (sur)reality of the impending event: <i>we are actually going to war. in god for fucking sake Iraq of all places. how did this happen? what the fuck is going on? pull the plug on this production now!</i><br><br>I kept looking up at the ceiling for a long while. Then I went onto the roof and just stared out into space.<br><br>and now it\\\'s a new morning and a new day, and to be brutally honest, that moment has ended. the sensation that this situation is somehow a fiction, a distressing entertainment, has returned. and to be honest, i\\\'m not sure if i want to experience the cold hand of reality again. will i be able to avoid it in the days to come?',1,1371,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(19560,3240,'2003-01-30 19:39:09',82,'simon','yeah it does.',1,13,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19561,3233,'2003-01-30 20:17:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i look for answers on the ceiling too; sometimes theyre there if you look hard/long enough.</tT>',1,104,129,0,'204.168.247.124','en'),(19562,3237,'2003-01-31 00:15:15',195,'matt','thank you for the positive reinforcement.  it means a lot.  i really appreciate it.  take care.',1,95,0,0,'209.208.111.135','en'),(19563,3237,'2003-01-31 00:17:36',82,'simon','i\\\'ve been looking forward to each installment of this, dude. when it comes time to comment, it\\\'s hard to sum up or explain sometimes.',1,139,195,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19564,3237,'2003-01-31 00:25:23',195,'matt','i can\\\'t wait for the time that this is finished and all posted.  this in between time feels like something incomplete, like a bastardized version of something.  when it\\\'s done, i\\\'ll be able to shop it around.  i just hope somebody wants to publish it.',1,260,0,0,'209.208.111.135','en'),(19565,3233,'2003-01-31 16:49:59',17,'austin','<P>\r\nthings fall apart.\r\n</P><P>\r\nin a sense, auntie, you are a microcosm of the universe, or the planet, or anything that was ever on the upswing of life, or atomic matter.\r\n</P><P>\r\nanother one bites the dust.\r\n</P><P>\r\nan astronaut in his fifties now said that if there were extraterrestrial, he thought they were probably watching us to see if we were going to reach the consciousness of stewardship of our own planet. they won\\\'t want us on their planet, or in the universe, if we cannot take care of ourselves and our one single puny planet.\r\n</P><P>\r\nSinging on and a on and on on and on\r\n</P><P>\r\ni much prefer popping and locking to bomb dropping. i understand the rhetoric of sacrifice. i put myself through painful things so that i feel better about myself and my time on this planet.\r\n</P><P>\r\nwar, what is it good for?\r\n</P><P>\r\nperhaps it will maybe be me. i don\\\'t have a comprehensive understanding of the politics of war, but my feelings are that when it is time to start thinking about sacrificing ourselves before others, there will be little argument needed.\r\n</P><P>\r\nwhen the sucker mcs try to prove a point\r\n</P><P>\r\nalong the same lines, the decision is best made on one\\\'s own, what they want to do about their own personal problems. so many dumb things have happened in my little microcosm of the universe because i did what other people thought was the right thing to do. advise is always appreciated though.</P>',6,1433,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19566,3237,'2003-01-31 17:16:52',124,'gus','yah dude, we need some rich swinney lover to make us a publishing company, or fund our crap so we can make it into books. ',0,122,195,0,'64.172.57.227','en'),(19567,3239,'2003-02-01 03:26:38',17,'austin','huh.',4,4,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19568,3239,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','crikey!',6,7,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19569,3242,'2003-02-01 21:47:22',195,'matt','<BR><BR>revision notes:<BR><BR>anna calling howard at this juncture means that matt would know she\\\'s been deported when he got out of jail, not two days later.<BR><BR>secondly, why didn\\\'t anna leave him some money before she was deported?  she asks if she could, and they tell her they don\\\'t let inmates transfer money from their account to that of a fellow inmate because \\\"that\\\'s how they pay for drugs and sex.\\\"',1,431,0,0,'209.208.108.250','en'),(19570,3242,'2003-02-02 00:31:38',195,'matt','<BR><BR>also:<BR><BR>matt tries to call her after howard gives him the number, but she is not at per\\\'s anymore.  per doesn\\\'t know where she is.',1,149,0,0,'209.208.110.249','en'),(19571,3233,'2003-02-02 11:39:06',187,'kandrer','i remember one thing that shocked me in the US. one day the anthem went on, and some folks around did that thing with their hand: maybe put it on their heart, or up straight in the air like former reich soldiers, i forgot exactly how, i was too surprised.<br>\r\ndisbelief is maybe a thing that america needs. follow the good example of france, my great country, where the president is a liar too, but nobody believes him.\r\n',1,422,0,0,'210.239.96.39','en'),(19572,3239,'2003-02-02 20:58:52',124,'gus','dammit, i just realized what you should do. you should rewrite all the parables as kung fu stories.',0,99,0,0,'64.172.56.246','en'),(19573,3239,'2003-02-02 23:58:48',377,'dammit','nm already did that, far better than i ever could. he called it \\\"kung fu jesus.\\\" i will try to find a copy of it--it was priceless.',0,137,0,0,'209.246.65.144','en'),(19574,3239,'2003-02-02 23:59:40',377,'dammit','http://nickm.com/writing/fiction/kung_fu_christ.html',0,52,0,0,'209.246.65.144','en'),(19575,3239,'2003-02-03 00:00:48',66,'Peter','<br><tt>or hiphop street-cred lyrics.</tt>',1,42,124,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19576,3239,'2003-02-03 01:17:40',124,'gus','wow, already been done, who knew. ok nevermind!',0,47,0,0,'64.172.56.246','en'),(19577,3243,'2003-02-03 01:23:56',66,'Peter','<br><tt>sigh. i hear ya.</tt>',1,29,0,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19578,3246,'2003-02-03 18:32:37',195,'matt','me and simon were wrong about the hot mama.',1,43,0,0,'209.208.109.158','en'),(19579,3242,'2003-02-03 18:40:34',195,'matt','<BR><BR>and <B><I>also:</B></I><BR><BR>am having intense waves of self-doubt about this project recently.  it has a sort of glacial, detached tone (somewhat intended, somewhat serendipitous) that i think is a product of writing it right before i go to bed.  if i wake up around four in the morning and write, it will be more fitzgerald, less hemingway.  do i want to do that?  is it good enough for publication as it is, or should i go for improved quality?  have i read books comparable in style to this?  is it good enough?<BR><BR><B><I>and:</B></I><BR><BR>am thinking about publishing it under a pen name.  that way, i can say whatever i want in it and still maintain my privacy.  is that copping out?  is it a fancy way of staying in the closet if i include gay stuff in it?',1,778,0,0,'209.208.109.158','en'),(19580,3242,'2003-02-03 18:46:27',82,'simon','i have thoughts, can i email you?',1,33,195,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19581,3242,'2003-02-03 19:29:15',195,'matt','email is sixessevensandnines@yahoo.com',1,38,0,0,'24.129.129.219','en'),(19582,3242,'2003-02-03 22:17:59',239,'Poonlarp','dude, its all about the pseudonym.',1,34,0,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19583,3247,'2003-02-04 03:02:01',66,'Peter','<Br><tt>hi. this is my <a href=\"http://saturn5.com/~peter/album/polaroid/pol3.jpg\">housemate</a>. he is one brilliant muthafucka.</tt>',1,125,0,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19584,3246,'2003-02-04 03:41:56',124,'gus','Just because you cant see her, doesnt mean she isnt there....',0,61,195,0,'64.172.59.24','en'),(19585,3246,'2003-02-04 03:59:35',175,'Paul G','Deep.',0,5,0,0,'67.195.63.187','en'),(19586,3243,'2003-02-04 04:33:32',175,'Paul G','Yeah - Germany is supposed to have one of the highest rates of urbanization in the world, with a population equal to roughly 1/3 of the U.S.A. living in a territory the size of the single State of New Mexico - and 98% of those people living in cities. \r\n\r\n<p>Compare that to New Zealand, with a population 1/80 of the U.S.A. living in a territory that is also roughly the size of New Mexico.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>(But I learned that a while ago, and I am too dour this eve to check my facts - <i>as if there were such things</i>.)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Anyway, it has seemed to me, in most respects, to be much worse to be without money in the city, than it is to be without money in some rural setting.  I think.  Concrete is hard.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Nice piece of writing, eve.</p>',0,751,0,0,'67.195.63.187','en'),(19587,3247,'2003-02-04 06:29:10',93,'chiamattt','i don\\\'t find this funny. i just dont get it. what do you mean?',6,65,0,0,'218.145.25.47','en'),(19588,3247,'2003-02-04 07:18:45',17,'austin','you wouldn\\\'t find this funny, chiam.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni thought it was hysterical from my ivory tower.  i\\\'m not sure that i would have activated it though.  probably not.\r\n',1,172,93,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19589,3248,'2003-02-04 19:25:44',74,'jo','i liked this a lot.',0,19,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(19590,3248,'2003-02-04 20:36:29',175,'Paul G','Powerful.  What a radical shift in perspective: you\\\'re a literary Picasso, dammit.',0,85,0,0,'67.195.57.80','en'),(19591,3241,'2003-02-05 00:46:53',370,'seanthecon','i see this as a perfect analogy for north and south korea relations. the fat and rich veteran, the south, versus the poor rookie, the north. but before shit can settle itself, here comes the americans with their big shoes. damn fine metaphor chia.\r\n\r\nor maybe its a story about the inner struggle of one mr. chiamattt. the chia versus the matt. who controls who? which one is real? i have never been able to figure it out. and i have never seen the two of them in the same room at the same time. you figure it out.\r\n\r\nor maybe its a story about a young traveller adapting to his adopted home. \r\n\r\nor maybe its about two cats getting the shit kicked out of them. personally, i am in a very heated war with a black cat right now. he has laid claim on my woman and we have fierce battles for her nipples.',6,801,0,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(19592,3249,'2003-02-05 02:27:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yay. i really dug this.</tt>',1,36,0,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19593,3248,'2003-02-05 04:07:35',75,'tyler','so the kid died again? i don\\\'t get it. i like it though.',1,59,0,0,'165.247.164.5','en'),(19594,3249,'2003-02-05 04:41:06',228,'belly','<p>instructions for next time you eat pocky:\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n1. put pocky in mouth.<br>\r\n2. remove hands from pocky. <br>\r\n3. suck chocolate/yoghurty coating from pocky. manipulate pocky using tongue and lips to remove all coating. do not touch pocky with hands. <br>\r\n4. once pocky is just pocky stick, without pocky coating, break pocky in half with tongue. do not touch pocky with hands.<br>\r\n5. using deft tongue, lip and jaw manipulation, stick half broken pocky stick up one nostril, so that nose and mouth are connected via pocky. (bite pocky stick to accomodate individual variation in mouth/nose distance if necessary.) do not touch pocky with hands.<br>\r\n6. stick other half up other nostril.<br>\r\n7. experts may request \\\"right or left?\\\" and stick both halves up selected nostril in one movement.<br>\r\n</p><p>\r\na buddhist monk i like to call pocky-sensie taught me this trick in the wee hours of new year\\\'s day, at a temple on a mountain surrounded by feet of snow. i can get to step five. \r\n</p><p>\r\nhi, i\\\'m annabel. i live in japan. \r\n</p>\r\n',0,1061,0,0,'210.224.189.253','en'),(19595,3137,'2003-02-05 05:30:59',228,'belly','<p>i am reading <i>my ishmael</i> right now, which is the sequel to <i>ishmael</i>, but not really a sequel in that i don\\\'t think you need to read them in order. i didn\\\'t. <i>my ishmael</i> is a good book, about learning from our history and living tribally. i don\\\'t agree with all of it, it seems a bit naive at times and the writing can get a little clumsy and contrived, but it has made me want to read <i>ishmael</i>, and also talk about this book a lot. \r\n</p>',0,474,0,0,'210.224.189.253','en'),(19596,3249,'2003-02-05 05:35:04',17,'austin','hi belly, are you really living in japan?\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ndid you link in from justin hall\\\'s live from japan show?',1,112,228,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19597,3249,'2003-02-05 05:38:04',17,'austin','the other day i saw a chiropractor office of a man named POOPAK.',4,64,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19598,3249,'2003-02-05 06:07:47',228,'belly','<p>yup, i\\\'ve been living in japan for exactly six months and one day. and nope, what is justin hall\\\'s live in japan show?</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni am a longtime swinney lurker. i decided to start posting (or commenting, at least) because i am coming to visit sf in six months :-D and thought i should introduce myself. i feel like i know everyone here; i keep forgetting that you don\\\'t know me.</p>\r\n\r\n',0,398,17,0,'210.224.189.253','en'),(19599,3249,'2003-02-05 08:25:11',449,'s','Thanks, Peter.\r\n\r\nHa, POOPAK. I don\\\'t want that guy touching my back.\r\n\r\nBelly has enlightened me. Does it work with Almond Crunch pocky? It\\\'s more jagged and...nutty.\r\n\r\nI got to step two.',4,195,0,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(19600,3247,'2003-02-05 08:29:06',449,'s','I can just imagine this being screamed out over some angry punk rocker guitar-ing.',1,82,0,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(19601,3245,'2003-02-05 08:30:40',449,'s','This is pretty. It really is.',2,29,0,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(19602,3248,'2003-02-05 08:33:50',449,'s','Wow, that\\\'s good. I liked it a lot. It\\\'s a unique, and odd perspective. But damn, it worked.',1,98,0,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(19603,3249,'2003-02-05 16:06:01',185,'orion','hmm, i\\\'m not so sure about this\r\npocky/nose connection. seems like it might be a little scratchy.  (and welcome to swinney, btw.  best friendly, but on principle i have to mark this destabilizing because i can\\\'t think anything else would be appropriate for a comment concerning the intersection of sinus cavities and confections)',4,335,228,0,'64.244.122.155','en'),(19604,3248,'2003-02-05 17:10:26',377,'dammit','this was a writing exercise. we were supposed to rewrite this raymond carver short story:\r\n\r\nhttp://www.mexsa.com/arboltsef/cibernauta/archivos/000305.php\r\n\r\nfrom another POV in 500 or words or less.',0,199,0,0,'209.244.183.110','en'),(19605,3249,'2003-02-05 18:49:02',82,'simon','<p>\r\ncool. i used to live in downtown LA and i liked it quite a bit. i too bought the gross but cool looking marble soda thingy. i liked shopping at the big japanese grocery store a lot. they had everything from hello kitty seaweed to prada scarves to cheap sashimi. <a href=\\\"http://www.angelfire.com/ca/X8/Amho.html\\\">this is where i lived.</a>\r\n</p>',1,356,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19606,3248,'2003-02-05 19:48:08',74,'jo','oh my god, i like it EVEN MORE now.  ',1,37,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(19607,3249,'2003-02-05 19:51:25',74,'jo','i like that i don\\\'t know the genders of over 1/2 the people who post on swinney and that this piece, among others, changes drastically when you imagine the writer as having a different gender than what you originally assumed when you read it.',1,245,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(19608,3137,'2003-02-06 03:51:54',228,'belly','i just finished <i>my ishmael</i>, and a page at the end mentioned the ishmael.com website. it is in desperate need of a <a href=\\\"http://www.ishmael.com/ToDo/websiteredesign.cfm\\\">redesign</a>, if you are looking for something to do to help...',0,248,0,0,'61.201.235.126','en'),(19609,3228,'2003-02-06 14:02:29',180,'auntie','i have been meaning to comment on this story since i first read it, but the words never seem to come out right.  this is a powerfully frightening bit of literature.',1,164,0,0,'24.92.161.252','en'),(19610,3251,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i hate you, poonlarp.',6,21,0,1,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19611,3251,'2003-02-06 16:20:37',17,'austin','never trust a man with two first names, especially if they are the same!\r\n',3,174,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19613,3251,'2003-02-06 17:51:30',66,'Peter','<br><tt>my goal for today is to use <i>\"cherried-out\"</i> to describe something...</tt>',1,90,0,0,'204.168.247.129','en'),(19612,3251,'2003-02-06 16:21:12',17,'austin','that is a sweet rig, too.',3,25,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19614,3249,'2003-02-06 19:25:03',234,'etienne','everyone should adore belly.  she is greatness personified.\r\n\r\nis there suju in japan?',1,86,0,0,'203.96.111.237','en'),(19615,2597,'2003-02-06 19:59:25',57,'weeb','<p>\r\ni reread this swinney with a little bit of embarassment. you don\\\'t add red wrigglers to the compost heap, they die because composts get too hot, up to 160 degrees Fahrenheit! oops. they go in worm bins, silly weeb. there have been happy worm wriggling at the casa de s5 and o for many a moon.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni also feel like i should go over there and do more gardeny things. perhaps next thursday. mmm yay for surrogate gardens! it needs help... i was talking to davel today and we both agreed that i need a truck... mmm trucks...</p>',1,538,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(19616,3249,'2003-02-07 01:48:14',228,'belly','ahhhaaa, etienne is YOU! *blush*',0,32,0,0,'210.155.207.129','en'),(19617,3249,'2003-02-07 01:51:29',228,'belly','i\\\'ve never tried it with almond crunch pocky. i just can\\\'t go past the regular old milk or white chocolate stuff. i imagine that if the suction was sufficient you would be able to remove all of the almond crunchs. \r\n\r\nand nope, no suju in japan. you can get vege juice in a 200ml steel can from vending machines, though.   ',0,329,0,0,'210.155.207.129','en'),(19618,2954,'2003-02-07 08:15:06',175,'Paul G','<p>So, in the dream, all the, \\\"men who write on swinney.org,\\\" are in a cheap motel room with 50\\\'s style furniture.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And everyone is wearing a fluffy pink robe, and fluffy pink slippers.  There\\\'s a black & white documentary about the \\\"Desert Fox\\\" Irwin Rommel playing on the tv: Nordic-looking men are posing against big machines, and wearing black leather - in the hot sun of North Africa.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Some people have their hair up in curlers; others have their heads wrapped in towels.  There\\\'s a little gossip about the girls, and a lot of serious talk about cars, and cameras, and computers.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Two guys are pouting; one runs into the bathroom, crying, and locks the door.  His friend goes to help him, casting a dirty look over his shoulder.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Someone else is doing his toenails, oblivious to the situation; it\\\'s probably Austin.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ice is thrown from a drink - the person that it hits yells: \\\"Bitch!\\\" in a sassy way.  And then everyone laughs.  Sigh.</p>',1,1019,0,0,'205.184.176.249','en'),(19619,2954,'2003-02-07 09:54:02',195,'matt','hell, baby, that\\\'s no dream - that\\\'s every saturday night.',1,64,0,0,'209.208.108.69','en'),(19620,2954,'2003-02-07 15:30:49',17,'austin','that is pretty funny, paul.\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\np.s., i need to do my bike up like that one.',2,85,175,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19621,2954,'2003-02-07 17:15:44',82,'simon','hahaha. me and swinney do our toenails and talk about cars and cameras all the time.',1,84,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19622,2954,'2003-02-07 17:59:48',66,'Peter','<br><tt>although, i cant really imagine anyone on here being geeky enough to talk about <i>computers</i>, of all things...</tt>',1,127,0,0,'204.168.247.129','en'),(19623,3247,'2003-02-07 18:22:40',186,'ook','in your town do people alter all the \\\"class war now!\\\" graffiti to say \\\"ass war now!\\\"? i liked this because it reminded me of the ass war, which brings me joy.',2,170,0,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(19624,3247,'2003-02-07 18:33:41',82,'simon','hehe, ass.',0,10,186,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19625,2954,'2003-02-07 21:26:18',17,'austin','s5 would be in the bathroom applying product to his hair, dont forget that.',1,75,82,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19626,2954,'2003-02-07 22:28:01',175,'Paul G','<p>\r\nYour bike is like one of those people that I have encountered on-line, and think that I would like \\\"in real life,\\\" but whom I have never really met.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\np.s. I think that simon might have girl germs.\r\n</p>',0,224,17,0,'207.95.52.135','en'),(19627,3248,'2003-02-08 01:21:33',124,'gus','good going dammit, you\\\'re in a writing class?',0,48,0,0,'207.20.18.18','en'),(19628,3253,'2003-02-08 01:24:16',124,'gus','this one is really rambling compared to the others. I couldnt figure out exactly how it relates. Maybe I should read it again.',0,126,0,0,'207.20.18.18','en'),(19629,3253,'2003-02-08 17:46:18',195,'matt','you\\\'re right, gus:  this one\\\'s ripe for revision.  maybe i\\\'ll rewrite it as matt\\\'s first day on the job at the church.  i\\\'d eliminate all the asides in his walk to town.  i guess the change in style and plotting is jarring.',1,238,0,0,'209.208.108.96','en'),(19630,3248,'2003-02-09 04:34:43',66,'~','\r\n\r\nhoning the edge.',1,20,0,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19631,3253,'2003-02-09 11:23:07',89,'sawks','millican?!',4,10,0,0,'61.9.128.173','en'),(19632,3253,'2003-02-09 17:14:30',195,'matt','yeah.  that\\\'s her name.  i found out her first name is lynn and the people across the road\\\'s last name is dale.<BR><BR>her husband might have been related to elly somehow.  it\\\'s not an uncommon name, i guess.',1,217,89,0,'209.208.108.65','en'),(19633,1064,'2003-02-09 22:28:47',66,'Peter','<br><tt>a year-and-a-half later, im reading hakim bey and come across this rant... <i>\\\"MUZAK is designed to hypnotize & control- its machinery can be smashed... malign ideas can be assaulted through the artifacts they generate.\\\"</i><br><br>the death of muzak at the hands of muzak itself. hmm.</tt>',1,304,0,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19634,3249,'2003-02-10 02:39:12',234,'etienne','i don\\\'t like bus transfers either but i would like to go to la.  i have north america-itis or something.',1,107,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(19635,406,'2003-02-10 02:43:17',234,'mandapanda','panda bears can do all sorts of things.',1,39,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(19636,3253,'2003-02-10 05:57:17',175,'Paul G','You\\\'re a good writer, with an interesting bag of life experience.  Who cares about anything else.  Take care of yourself, matt; finish this thing.',0,149,195,0,'67.195.73.240','en'),(19637,3228,'2003-02-11 06:32:36',421,'chimeres','ditto.  haunting.  ...feels like i\\\'ve been hit with a serious chill of icy reverse dejavu.  so unreal it feels real and frighteningly intimate.',0,146,0,0,'64.156.230.9','en'),(19638,3249,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>ps:  annabel/belly is one of the NEW ZEALANDERS!!!!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>god defend new zealand... (we can\\\'t do it, we disbanded the air force!)</p>',6,143,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19639,3253,'2003-02-12 01:13:23',195,'matt','i tell you paul, my man: i\\\'ve got my heart set on finishing it, but it\\\'s going to take a while.',1,101,0,0,'209.208.110.81','en'),(19640,3256,'2003-02-13 16:36:56',66,'Peter','<br><tt>bravo belgium, indeed. lots of good beers come from there.</tt>',1,71,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19641,3256,'2003-02-13 19:07:26',87,'bmg','<p>I have noticed that the press word of the week is \\\"hawk.\\\" BBC, CNN, NYTimes, Asia times and even salon have used this word to describe America, Iraq, and Bush. Why is it that this word is so popular?</p><p>I have lots of respect for Germany right now... standing strong</p>',3,282,0,0,'147.80.216.86','en'),(19642,3256,'2003-02-13 20:00:23',129,'moebius','ha, i just used \\\"hawk\\\" and \\\"dove\\\" in swinchat a few seconds ago, before reading this.<br><br>i don\\\'t know how long this \\\"hawk\\\" and \\\"dove\\\" stuff has been going on but I bet it dates back at least to WWII. the association of doves with peace goes way back, and i guess at some point people started thinking of \\\"hawks\\\" as the antithesis of doves. and so there you go.<br><br>i first learned of the hawk & dove thing as a kid, reading back issue reprints of <i>Mad</i> magazine. during the Vietnam era, they had a strip called \\\"Sergeant Hawk and Private Dove\\\" where Dove was this hippie soldier who was always finding creative ways to decorate his army base (and even Hawk, his commanding officer) with enormous peace signs.',0,759,87,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(19643,3256,'2003-02-13 22:28:12',175,'Paul G','<i>\\\"during the Vietnam era...\\\"</i>\r\n<p>\r\nI remember the end of the Vietnam Era, too.  I\\\'ve been thinking about it a lot lately: it seems like the mood in the country, now, isn\\\'t too much different than it was, then.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSomeone who is older than I am will remember this better, and correct me.  But, wow, one of the things that I recall from that time was the frequency with which we encountered someone who was missing a leg, or arm, or hand.  It wasn\\\'t only the younger guys who were back from \\\'Nam, there were still many survivors of the Korean War and even WWII walking around, too.  The real costs of what we were doing were apparent to everyone - at least in my social class.  The sight of hooks and wheelchairs really frightened me, as a child.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMy cousin Mike died this past year; he was in his 50\\\'s.  He did things in the Army, in Vietnam; and he was different afterwards - even though he wasn\\\'t missing anything that anyone could see.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe 1980\\\'s did feel a lot like the 1950\\\'s were supposed to have felt: Ronald Reagan painted lovely pictures in words - pictures that made almost everyone swell with pride.  And now doubts, and suspicion, color those pictures; and it feels like the 1970\\\'s, again.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis is an interactive, storytelling environment; that\\\'s a beautiful thing.  And I truly admire, and am thankful for, the bravery of the people in their teens and twenties who are willing to expose themselves to public ridicule and government harassment in order to voice their opposition to what is happening now.   But I find myself wishing, more and more, that there was someone older, someone who would sort of say, \\\"Everything will be ok.\\\"  That\\\'s missing - in my life, anyway.  My grandfathers, father, uncles, and older cousins are gone.  And I have the distinct impression that the people who are in elected office, now, are nervous.\r\n</p>',1,1943,129,0,'67.195.182.60','en'),(19644,3256,'2003-02-14 04:34:13',93,'chiamattt','I have faith in humanity. I just dont trust people who say \\\"whyyyyy?\\\" when you tell them you have a cat. ',6,111,0,0,'218.145.25.79','en'),(19645,3256,'2003-02-14 05:34:17',241,'linus van pelt','<I was reading a recent copy of Harper\\\'s Index which stated that the two countries in violation of the most human rights are Turkey and Israel, ranking first and second, respectively. \\\"I\\\'ve got friends in low places\\\"> ',2,230,0,0,'64.230.169.17','en'),(19646,3256,'2003-02-14 05:37:26',241,'linus van pelt','<p> I screwed up. I meant to say that I was reading a recent edition of Harper\\\'s Index which stated that the two countries that are in violation of the most human rights are Turkey and Israel, ranking one and two, respectively. \\\"I\\\'ve got friends in low places\\\" </p>',2,277,0,0,'64.230.169.17','en'),(19647,3256,'2003-02-14 11:19:05',314,'eve','I was on the tram on the way home and traffic was stopped by the police because of all the US Army trucks pulling across the intersection. I haven\\\'t heard any news, but I think it might have been the US\\\'s threat to pull their troops out of here (Germany.) If so, then so be it. *shrugs* I don\\\'t see why the US occupies us still, half a century after the war. \r\n\r\nPeter: Belgium beer is horrible, what are you talking about? :D\r\n\r\nIt\\\'s kind of strange to think of Turkey being drawn into the whole main field of the war. I was just there a couple months ago on vacation. The Kurds are a prevelant people, and some of the strangest bit of the gene pool I\\\'ve seen. They were friendly though. Turkey seemed all too innocent in a way, the people I spoke too only devoted to the value and enjoyment of life... to switch face and pick up arms, not something I can imagine. But reality has a way of doing that, surprises are in store, I\\\'m sure.\r\n\r\nEither way, I don\\\'t want to see another war. And I think Germany, France and Belgium made the right decision. ',0,1071,0,0,'80.139.143.78','en'),(19648,3256,'2003-02-14 18:45:19',75,'tyler','i wonder if the militant muslim people see all these protestors, and that\\\'s why they haven\\\'t blown anything up yet? that would be pretty cool. i think if i were to march around with a sign, it would say \\\'PLEASE DON\\\'T KILL ME.\\\'',1,241,0,0,'165.247.166.188','en'),(19649,3256,'2003-02-14 18:47:38',75,'tyler','but by the way---maybe our great buddy and apparent moral superior Germany has a LOT to do with why we\\\'re \\\'hawks\\\' now. ',1,128,0,0,'165.247.166.188','en'),(19650,3253,'2003-02-16 06:56:38',234,'etienne','i always wonder when i\\\'m reading and the characters are borrowing/reading books if the author planned the books that the character has for a specific reason or if they\\\'re just random.',1,189,0,0,'210.86.59.67','en'),(19651,3256,'2003-02-16 20:41:13',251,'Jamie','I am drinking Belgian beer. If i had to choose between drinking beer or killing Iraqis i would choose the beer. Yes.',6,116,0,0,'172.181.148.163','en'),(19652,3233,'2003-02-17 05:27:31',176,'Scapegoat','When I was growing up my Mother was always very careful to ensure <i>the rules</i> where followed whenever my Father was heading off somewhere dangerous. When I was younger I didn\\\'t understand the importance of it all but now I\\\'m incredibly thankful she did this with my sister and I.<br><br>\r\nAs I got older my Father started to tell me about some of the close calls that he had. All I could think about during his stories was what we had said to him before he had left on said trip.<br><br>\r\nI guess I just want to express appreciation for your part. I think <i>the rules</i> make things a little more bearable for them. Following them is no small task.<br>  ',1,667,180,0,'66.185.85.80','en'),(19653,3256,'2003-02-17 05:41:06',176,'Scapegoat','There a few logic flaws in your position as stated but I\\\'m sure your aware of that. So I\\\'m left with the impression that you would like to see Turkey somehow suffer for what it has done to the Kurds. That is after all your logic for not defending them. Doesn\\\'t this go against your general stance against war?<br><br>\r\nIf Turkey wasn\\\'t a muslim country would we be so quick to dismiss its defence? Replace Turkey with any other christian Nato memeber and there wouldn\\\'t be the same opposition to its defence. Is it any suprise that the two main offenders the French and Germans are two European countries that have been known to have some contentious domestic issues with their Muslim immigrants in the case of Germany, Turks in particular. Peaceniks or rascists is what I\\\'m left wondering.<br><br>',0,816,93,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(19654,3256,'2003-02-17 09:58:35',93,'chiamattt','You can say what you will. Turkey has been closer to its european neighbours than it has ever been to other non-secular muslim nations.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhat i am worried about is that we are now giving the turks weapons and military logistics to \\\"defend itself\\\". we did this during the 1980\\\'s with Iraq. They are also a secular nation. I dont really think we need another \\\"IRAQ\\\".\r\n<br><br>\r\nIn regards to France and Germany and their treatment of middle eastern and north african immigrants. The problems we are seeing with regards to these people is not because of a state sponsored aggression. we are seeing the presence of a formerly less vocal right wing MINORITY causing problems...If the aggression towards the kurds in Turkey wasnt state sponsored, im sure my outlook would be different. \r\n<br><br>',1,815,176,0,'218.145.25.15','en'),(19655,3256,'2003-02-17 16:44:51',180,'auntie','the problem of the kurds is one that needs to be dealt with.  their traditional homeland is located inside the turkish borders.<br><br>\r\n\r\njust like the palestinians, they need a place to call their own.<br><br>\r\n\r\n',0,215,0,0,'24.92.161.252','en'),(19656,3256,'2003-02-17 16:49:29',180,'auntie','oh, i think that the americans should definitely pull out of germany completely.<br><br>\r\n\r\none thing i learned when living there for twelve years was that our presence was not appreciated by anyone except those who stood to make a buck on account of it.<br><br>\r\n\r\nalthough it is also true that in some areas, when the amis pulled out, the local economy really tanked.  electric and water bills went up thirty percent in gelnhausen when we left, for example. which is strange, because the base there was so small, how could the utility payments have accounted for thirty percent of the total revenue?',0,601,314,0,'24.92.161.252','en'),(19657,3260,'2003-02-17 22:30:36',75,'tyler','fucking right on.',1,17,0,0,'165.247.166.188','en'),(19658,3260,'2003-02-17 22:56:57',66,'Peter','<br><tt>thank you for taking the time to write this. for real.</tt>',1,67,0,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19659,3179,'2003-02-18 02:49:42',120,'cosmos','<img src=\\\"http://www.nasa.gov/images/content/find/Full_m.jpg\\\">\r\n\r\nazul, yes.',2,82,0,0,'12.252.221.216','en'),(19660,3260,'2003-02-18 04:38:36',175,'Paul G','<p>\r\nThis was written <b>one year ago</b>, on today\\\'s date:\r\n<br>\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.observer.co.uk/Print/0,3858,4357739,00.html\\\">Will Hutton<br>Sunday February 17, 2002<br>The Observer</a>\r\n<br>\r\n<i>\\\"The most obvious flashpoint is the weight of evidence that after Afghanistan George Bush intends a massive military intervention to topple Iraq\\\'s Saddam Hussein.\\\"</i>\r\n<br>\r\n<i>\\\"The most important political story of our time is the rise of the American Right and the near collapse of American liberalism.\\\"</i>\r\n</p>',0,542,0,0,'206.215.75.240','en'),(19661,3260,'2003-02-18 04:55:03',180,'auntie','very well said, dear. i keep thinking that somewhere out there is a voice that will unite all americans, regardless of income, religion, race, or ethnicity.<br><br>\r\n\r\nbut we have to remember that all men are human, and that a real hero is hard to find. those who reach a position of public power generally don\\\'t get there without some serious baggage - i can think of few presidents in the last hundred years who were not totally morally bankrupt in some serious way.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe need election reform, we need americans to register and vote and make their opinions heard, and we need to graduate informed citizens from our high schools and universities.  so many problems, so few problem solvers...<br><br>',2,712,0,0,'24.92.161.252','en'),(19662,3260,'2003-02-18 06:12:57',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\nI think what you had to say was pretty much the truth, but I do think there are elements of the left that are genuine in their desire to propose creative and realistic solutions to the problems that have been created over the years by government and society. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nYou do not need to align with the left or the right. You do not need to stand in the center. You can focus. You can get involved with what you believe. You must not make the \\\'fractured\\\' and very \\\'random\\\' left your scapegoat. \r\n</p>',1,520,0,0,'61.37.53.41','en'),(19663,3261,'2003-02-18 14:11:59',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this was great. i just got into work after braving nyc\\\'s worst blizzard in 100 years and found this. a nice way to start the day indeed.</tt>',1,152,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19664,3261,'2003-02-18 16:42:45',122,'hool','highest compliments paul.  selfishly i am very glad you got a job as a cab driver because it means i get to read great stories like these.  thanks for writing them.  they\\\'re very warm, the kind of thing you like to read more than a few times with the same size smile.  what did you do with the thong?',0,303,0,0,'66.185.85.80','en'),(19665,3261,'2003-02-18 18:53:41',17,'austin','i really enjoyed reading this and didnt get bored or decide to stop reading it once.  a couple questions though,  what happened on the corner that no cabs would pick up the white guy?  who is the amazing blackstone and what is his significance here?\r\n<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\n*constructive*',3,277,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19666,3261,'2003-02-18 18:55:10',17,'austin','also my leg is alseep now!',0,26,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19667,3261,'2003-02-18 19:48:39',175,'Paul G','<p>\r\nFor your viewing pleasure - <a href=\\\"http://monograph.org/taxi/pics/valsday.html\\\">the objects mentioned in the story...</a>',0,134,122,0,'207.220.172.166','en'),(19668,3261,'2003-02-18 20:15:55',175,'Paul G','Several hours apart from one another, during the same day, I saw two different Black men standing at Halsted and Madison.  Neither of them was able to get another taxi driver to stop.\r\n<p>\r\nThe first guy wanted the car for himself; the second guy wanted the taxi for the older lady.  She would have had better luck if she had tried, herself: he was young, and large, in addition to being a Black male.  Every Black guy who has gotten in the car has talked about how hard it is to get a taxi driver to stop for them.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHalsted is sort of the dividing line between, \\\"Downtown,\\\" and, \\\"The West Side.\\\"  It\\\'s changing/has changed.  But, bad things can still happen - quickly.  Is that what you meant?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<a href=\\\"http://trendy.org/magic/blackstone/upi.html\\\">Blackstone</a>=Kreskin=Psychic\r\n<br>\r\nAfter my ex and I broke it off, she started dating a guy who looked like <A href=\\\"http://www.amdest.com/stars/harryb.gif\\\">Harry Blackstone</a>, and so he\\\'s hard for me to forget - haha.\r\n</p>',0,1031,17,0,'207.220.172.166','en'),(19669,3261,'2003-02-18 20:19:32',175,'Paul G','Thank you so much, Peter!',0,25,66,0,'207.220.172.166','en'),(19670,3261,'2003-02-18 22:16:35',122,'hool','<p>thanks for the photos, i was going to ask..  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>but what\\\'s goin on with that thong? it looks really dirty, and i don\\\'t mean in the sexy dirty kind of way.  the mystery of soiled thong..</p>',0,206,0,0,'66.185.85.80','en'),(19671,3262,'2003-02-18 23:46:09',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this sounds good if you read it in a voice like the \"choose life\" spiel in trainspotting.</tt>',1,103,0,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19672,2489,'2003-02-19 00:16:18',82,'simon','<p>\r\ndo you remember straw hat pizza? there was one in palo alto on san antonio i think. and one in millbrae. and i\\\'m sure plenty of others. they also had those arcades in them. my grandparents would take me there and give me quarters to play. i liked my pizza with mushrooms, partially because it was good but mostly because my sister hated them.\r\n</p>\r\n',0,358,124,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19673,3262,'2003-02-19 00:17:03',82,'simon','ok i just tried that. it was fun, thanks.',1,41,66,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19674,3262,'2003-02-19 00:30:31',66,'Peter','<br><tt>everybody should do a fake british accent at least once a day. its good for the aftest.</tt>',1,101,82,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19675,3261,'2003-02-19 01:47:11',175,'Paul G','<p>\r\nhool: dude, floral print!\r\n<br>\r\nit\\\'s not like a man had them on...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\naustin: the guy with the beard at montrose?\r\n<br>\r\nhe wasn\\\'t waiting long at all; he was just in a big hurry to get a hold of his sausage.\r\n</p>\r\n',0,237,0,0,'205.186.223.102','en'),(19676,2848,'2003-02-19 02:11:17',228,'belly','japan + relationships = complete and total disaster.\r\n\r\ni moved to japan with my long-term \\\"partner\\\" six months ago. he broke up with me after six weeks. i had just signed a year-long contract. woohoo!\r\n\r\ni\\\'m still alive, though, and i\\\'m still in godforesaken japan. i would love to be a programmer in texas! ',0,321,0,0,'61.201.234.51','en'),(19677,2848,'2003-02-19 07:50:03',17,'austin','godforsaken?  so you are not into it?\r\n<BR><BR>\r\njustin seemed to do well moving there within six months of meeting his significant other.  no doubt it is a hard land for white man to make a living, as are all foreign lands.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni\\\'m preparing to die a terrible death upon a japanese GO server.  earlier this evening i died a most terrible death upon the battle field at the SF Go Club.  i am all about death today.',3,424,228,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19678,3260,'2003-02-20 00:25:58',129,'moebius','Yeah, this was a pretty good essay. Most of your commentary on the march pretty much echoed what I felt about, well, all of the marches I\\\'ve attended, including the ones I attended for the first Gulf War when I was still in college. At the end of the day they seemed to be token, useless activities that did little to change the status quoe.<br><br>But I do have to say that the tide in that respect does seem to be turning somewhat. It seemed like last weekend\\\'s set of marches <i>finally</i> got the respect from the media. And I think this is because the repetition of the marches and their exponentially increasing size and prevalence is making it clear that these things aren\\\'t the product of a bunch of \\\"whacko\\\" crypto-marxist dolphin-safe eco-friendly mumia-loving anarchist kids with dreadlocks and forehead bindis, but rather actually so-called \\\"ordinary\\\" people: grandparents, children, executives, gov\\\'t workers, farmers, regular joe shmoes. Ordinary people all over the world who are fucking pissed off about the direction in which things are going.<br><br>I find a bit of hope in that. Perhaps the marches are starting to be less about the \\\"left\\\" and more about \\\"we the people.\\\" I think Bush has been looking nervous in his latest set of appearances. I think he knows his mandate is running out.',1,1344,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(19679,3262,'2003-02-20 00:33:46',251,'Jamie','i used to do a fake british accent ever day when i lived in ireland',4,67,66,0,'172.181.7.189','en'),(19680,3262,'2003-02-20 00:39:46',251,'Jamie','oh.. don\\\'t EVER let a Scottish person hear you calling them British by the way! LMAO :o)',4,91,66,0,'172.181.7.189','en'),(19681,3261,'2003-02-20 01:44:53',449,'s','That thong is not terribly sexy.\r\n\r\nWonderful story, I loved it.',1,64,0,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(19682,3256,'2003-02-20 01:59:08',314,'eve','Unfortunately, that is all too true. Depending on who you ask and how strong they connect politics to real people, generally the presence of Americans in Germany is ... well, anything but appreciated. I guess it doesn\\\'t help that the majority of what they see are the young and loud exchange students with little respect for the culture (read: they\\\'re here for the beer.) or on the other hand, the less-than-always-educated soldier. The Ami soldiers are a breed all on their own. I have yet to meet one that gave a rat\\\'s ass about learning the language or being considerate of the cultural differences while looking for a \\\'hot european girlfriend.\\\' I wish there was a better way to put it really, but this is how Americans are seen in Germany. \r\n\r\nAnd like you said, if there\\\'s a dollar to be made, the American will be King for the moment, but otherwise just the fool.',0,887,180,0,'80.139.189.24','en'),(19683,3262,'2003-02-20 04:54:18',66,'Peter','<br><tt>why, theyre as british as sean connery!</tt>',1,52,251,0,'66.108.190.99','en'),(19684,3256,'2003-02-20 05:17:41',93,'chiamattt','the kurd lands cover areas within about five countries. Turkey, and Iraq are giving the kurds the most problems. One is our ally. One is our enemy. ',2,148,180,0,'218.145.25.15','en'),(19685,3230,'2003-02-20 05:35:27',175,'Paul G','The two of you make a very attractive couple.  It looks like you both have such similar, and well-formed, lips and eyes - in this pic.  But, wow, there\\\'s a lot of tension in the small space of that image: like something is happening, or has happened.\r\n<p>\r\nOne of my sisters was on my mind, tonight; you look kinda like her.  Sometimes it\\\'s easier to like or dislike someone, because they look like someone else.  Of course, people told her that she and her ex made an attractive couple, and they\\\'re not together now, either.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIf I ever get serious with anyone again, maybe it should be with someone who I don\\\'t look like I belong with; and then no one will say the words: \\\"attractive couple,\\\" and then everything will be ok.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSorry if this seemed weird.  But I got my permanent license from the City this afternoon; and then I got German beer, to celebrate.  I wouldn\\\'t operate a motorized vehicle now; but the mouse is another story.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOk, I said it.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i>best friendly</i>\r\n<br>\r\nnot, <i>weird scary</i>, please.\r\n</p>',1,1084,82,0,'67.195.57.5','en'),(19686,3260,'2003-02-20 07:42:49',99,'wb','The difficult situation Bush and the country is in is that public opinion has clearly shifted since the time of the election. People got tired of the story looming. The left woke up. The few that were truly angry have inspired the many that were apathetic. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nMoving troops and setting up for a war is not something that can move as fast as opinion polls and anti-war rallies. Once the process started, around the time of elections, Bush had to move the troops in to establish that he was serious (and not Bill Clinton).\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nIt\\\'s a mess because the issue was decided around November, when the elections happened and Congress authorized action. Opinion is certainly part of democracy, but elections are where action talks, complaining, protesting, letter writing, etc walks. Let\\\'s face it: Many people who are now going to rallies were either not aware, or not that interested in the subject months ago. They certainly weren\\\'t voting.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nBeing against the war, I do realize this fact: If Bush removes troops now, if he accepts a compromise with Saddam, anything less than the removal of the government or letting an American force inspect weapons, it reduces the influence and power of the United States in the region. And it will destroy Bush\\\'s presidency.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI want Bush to be a one term president. But even if I\\\'m not for him nor for the war, for the timing, for the power play in the first place, I realize that American power and influence in the region is important. The left lies when it tries to deny that: Power matters in the region. Old school power. Many of us are against a war in Iraq, but this would be a far more just war, a far less deadly war than say Pakistan slipping out of control. A loss of American clout reduces the chance of a solution in the Palestinian situation. The list goes on. It\\\'s a tough situation.  ',3,1884,129,0,'63.207.101.34','en'),(19687,3260,'2003-02-20 08:01:48',59,'s5','<p>\r\nladies and gentlemen, the libertarian party has just claimed another soul.\r\n</p><p>\r\nhere wayne, this is for you:\r\n</p><p>\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.lp.org/lpnews/0303/Iraq.html\\\">10 reasons why the USA should not attack Iraq</a>\r\n</p>',0,240,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19688,3260,'2003-02-20 08:25:38',59,'s5','<p>\r\n<i>I realize that American power and influence in the region is important. The left lies when it tries to deny that: Power matters in the region.</i>\r\n</p><p>\r\nactually, wayne, it is absolutely reasonable to argue that the desire for power in that region is the direct cause of the mess we\\\'re in.\r\n</p>',5,310,99,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19689,3260,'2003-02-20 08:45:17',99,'wb','We are a powerful country. It\\\'s not a \\\'desire\\\' for power. We already have it. How much do we exert in the region to most benefit the United States. Do we pretend we aren\\\'t the most powerful nation? Do we pretend we don\\\'t exert influence? It is a lie to pretend we are aren\\\'t powerful and just there \\\"to do business.\\\" In the past it was a lie that worked, but it\\\'s clear things have changed. The left says Bush is shooting for \\\"an empire,\\\" but nobody in his right mind would do that, because it would mean managing the region. The real moral injustice of a war with Iraq is not the war, but toppling the nation and then leaving it as a mess. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI disagree with many of the points this LP page states. Iraq is a threat to the United States economically. Saddam has not aided terrorists in the past, but anyone can imagine him doing so. The worst case scenario that the Bush administration keeps going on about is plausible. ',0,964,59,0,'63.207.101.34','en'),(19690,3260,'2003-02-20 09:03:12',59,'s5','<p>\r\ni know the US is a powerful country. however, you seem to believe that maintaining that power (especially in that region) is good and necessary, and i believe it\\\'s bad and dangerous. we have different values, you and i.\r\n</p><p>\r\nalso, i don\\\'t see any public debate, major media outlet, or discussion in congress that has given equal time to pacifism and non-violence as an alternative to war. and you wonder why anti-war protestors flounder. it\\\'s because there\\\'s little to no discussion (other than on internet message boards) that addresses other value systems, that could prevent crises such as this from happening in the first place. \r\n</p><p>\r\ninstead, we have the usual cycle: government incites fear to sway public opinion. \\\"the left\\\" cries out \\\"no war!\\\" but doesn\\\'t know why. \\\"the right\\\" reminds \\\"the left\\\" how stupid they are. media and public debate frames all discussion of war in terms of two sides: either you are in favor of bombing foreign countries, or you are in favor of allowing evil brown tyrants to destroy america. there is never any discussion of how to defuse evil tyrants. instead we are forced to accept that the only reality is one where we have to repeatedly chop off the head of evil, and hope another doesn\\\'t sprout up again in its place.\r\n</p>',5,1321,99,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19691,3260,'2003-02-20 09:07:30',99,'wb','Bush has been clear about his intentions for a long time. It is the public\\\'s fault. That people finally woke up, after the election, a few weeks before the war is the reason we are in this mess. At the rally speakers were congratulating the crowd for protesting before the war. That pretty much sums it up: self-congratulation and bad timing.',0,345,59,0,'63.207.101.34','en'),(19692,3260,'2003-02-20 09:18:32',99,'wb','Power (not just military power) in the region is in important as long as we intend to get oil from it. They aren\\\'t democratic, they aren\\\'t capitalist. They have older value systems which recognize power. Maybe it is \\\"bad and dangerous\\\" to deal with them at all? It\\\'s easy to say it\\\'s \\\"bad and dangerous,\\\" but most things in our lives are connected to the product, so it\\\'s not realistic to pretend we can slip some money under the door, take our milk and leave. And even if it was, I\\\'m not sure that would improve their lives and stop violence there. ',0,580,59,0,'63.207.101.34','en'),(19693,3260,'2003-02-20 10:09:39',129,'moebius','<br>wb, i don\\\'t know if i would lay all the blame for the current situation at the feet of the american public.<br><br>sure, there\\\'s a fair amount of ignorance and slothfulness out there, but it\\\'s not as if everyone in the heartland has been pulling a rip van winkle act and suddenly started awake at the realization that Bush Is Evil and War Is Bad.<br><br>i think you need to take into account the fact that people are not so much asleep as incredibly confused, frightened, and not quite sure what to believe. consider: we\\\'ve got a president who wasn\\\'t quite elected fair and square, whose administration is perhaps one of the most secretive in american history. they don\\\'t tell us much about what they\\\'re doing or planning, and have found ways to heavily discourage the press from trying to find out.<br><br>and speaking of the american press, it\\\'s pretty much compromised from top to bottom, for a whole variety of reasons. what\\\'s truth and what\\\'s propaganda? how is the average person supposed to know?<br><br>then there\\\'s 9/11, the subsequent warnings of further attacks, and the \\\"war on terror\\\" itself, all of which have americans whipped up into a frenzy of fear, grief, and blind \\\"these colors don\\\'t run\\\" patriotism.<br><br>and on top of <i>all that</i>, there\\\'s this economic slump we\\\'re in. there are a great many people out there who have most of their attention focused on finding work and keeping their families fed and sheltered. you don\\\'t have too tell them that things are falling apart. it\\\'s just that a war in iraq is a fairly abstract concept for a lot of them. and they\\\'ve been slow to realize that this war will likely make things worse for them, not better.<br><br>so in the final assesment, i bet a lot of folks in this country haven\\\'t been sleeping much at all.<br><br>finally: it <i>is</i> an accomplishment to have mass antiwar protests of this size in advance of the war itself. it took <i>years</i> for the Vietnam antiwar protests to get to this size, and by that time thousands of American soldiers (not to mention hundreds of thousands of Vietnamese) had already been killed.',1,2173,0,0,'63.203.71.23','en'),(19694,3260,'2003-02-20 10:36:17',154,'ryan','<p>\r\ni agree with wb in that we (the US) need to be over there in some fashion (not necessarily in the form of a military presence) because our current survival depends on sucking the oil out of the ground in the region.  a stable oil producing region is good for us.  we need to get away from oil in the long wrong and i believe we will. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe thing that really rubs me the wrong way is the way the current administration is selling this.  they are scaring people while exaggerating the iraqi threat. they are attempting to connect iraq to terrorism and bin laden. they are floating lofty goals about how we are going to liberate the iraqi people and encourage an arab democracy that will spread throughout the region. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthey have offered no proof of any terrorist connection besides a couple fuzzy photographs of trucks moving about and a tape that they claim was recorded by bin laden.  the rainbow of doom is elevated and we are ordered to buy duct tape and make safe rooms.  you want to bet that the rainbow of doom is downgraded once we take iraq and the situation stabilizes?  manipulation.  the last point the administration makes about liberating the iraqi people and encouraging a democracy is transparent.  we will install a friendly puppet just like we did in afghanistan and have done throughout our history.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni guess, to get to the end of this, i find all of the above rather insulting and i wish people in power would just call a spade a spade and come clean with the actual motivations for going on this foreign adventure.\r\n</p>',0,1577,0,0,'216.99.204.7','en'),(19695,3260,'2003-02-20 11:06:00',59,'s5','i would just as soon disentangle ourselves from the middle east, and watch the price of oil skyrocket. for better or worse, the devestating impact that would have on the economy would motivate policy makers to push for other sources of fuel. maybe we would finally start covering the state of montana with windmills. maybe we\\\'d see hydrogen cars on the market or biodiesel at every pump much sooner than we would have otherwise. the problem with being \\\"realistic\\\" is that sometimes it does little else but to maintain the shitty status quo. true realism would be accepting the nature of the crisis, and acting on it, rather than sitting on our hands until 2050.',4,670,99,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19696,3260,'2003-02-20 12:16:27',154,'ryan','\\\"long wrong\\\" is supposed to = \\\"long run\\\".  hah.\r\n<p>\r\nmy cat is totally against the war.',0,100,0,0,'216.99.204.113','en'),(19697,3261,'2003-02-20 13:27:26',377,'dammit','i dug it the most. tell me this: how do you keep your details straight? do you take a moment after ever drop off to jot down some notes?',0,136,0,0,'64.152.164.157','en'),(19698,3230,'2003-02-20 17:23:52',82,'simon','heh. not weird and scary at all. the funny thing about me and that guy is that i guess we are kind of exes now but we\\\'ve always joked  that one day we\\\'d get married and divorced. maybe we still will.',1,205,175,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19699,3260,'2003-02-20 17:47:34',57,'weeb','i heart s5.',1,11,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(19700,3260,'2003-02-20 17:49:43',99,'wb','I agree it\\\'s time to take the next step to the future. It\\\'s really in the hands of voters and consumers.<BR><BR> \r\n\r\nBut even if corporations and the government got on board and said, \\\"we\\\'re outta there,\\\" I suspect it would take until 2050 to actually do the untangling. It\\\'s not just cars and solar power, it is many other industries and businesses. And then it would mean having much of the industrial world agree to the shift. So even if the detangling started, there would be at the very least 20 years of it, in which the same foreign policy issues (and worse) would emerge. ',0,598,59,0,'63.207.101.34','en'),(19701,3260,'2003-02-20 18:00:18',99,'wb','I agree that people are scared, busy, poor, etc, etc. But public apathy and the decline the percentage of voters has been going on long before 9/11 and this situation. And half of the people who did vote in the 2000 election voted for \\\"George Bush\\\" a second time. <BR><BR>\r\nRecall that Vietnam ended when the VC surrounded Saigon and we fled. Richard Nixon was elected a second time. The problem is in the back of our minds we think that protests \\\"helped end\\\" Vietnam, but that wasn\\\'t the case. Voting, voting, voting kept Henry Kissinger in power.',0,563,129,0,'63.207.101.34','en'),(19702,3262,'2003-02-20 20:30:47',251,'Jamie','Who lives in Spain',4,18,66,0,'172.183.178.114','en'),(19703,2848,'2003-02-21 01:15:21',228,'belly','i do actually quite like japan. it\\\'s an incredibly interesting place that is completely different from anything i have ever known. i tend to refer to it as \\\"godforesaken motherfucking japan\\\" out of a smart-ass habit i formed when i realised i was kind of stuck here. \r\n<br><br>\r\ni must have written this comment on one of my bad-japan days - days when nothing seems to make sense or fit into my way of seeing/doing; days when i feel indescribably \\\"foreign\\\" and alone; days when i hate my job and the weather and the food and i just want to understand what is going on around me. \r\n<br><br>\r\nit\\\'s a really hard place to live, but i don\\\'t hate it here and i\\\'ll miss it when i go. on the other hand, i can\\\'t wait to leave. its contradictory nature must be rubbing off on me...\r\n<br><br>\r\nalso, who is this justin fellow and what is his url? he seems to be ungoogleable.\r\n',0,895,17,0,'61.201.234.98','en'),(19704,2848,'2003-02-21 01:31:13',82,'simon','<a href=\\\"http://www.links.net\\\">the internet\\\'s justin hall</a>',0,70,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19705,2848,'2003-02-21 01:51:40',66,'Peter','<br><img src=\"http://saturn5.com/~peter/b3ta/google.jpg\">',1,59,228,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19706,2848,'2003-02-21 03:12:36',175,'Paul G','<p>\r\n<i>\r\n\\\"...relationships = complete and total disaster\\\"\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI have this idea in my head, and maybe it\\\'s completely false.  But, I think that you played a major role in something, in my, \\\"real life.\\\"  It\\\'s far too late to change anything.  Even so, maybe I\\\'m wrong, and you\\\'d care to say something to me, directly:\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\npaul@monograph.org\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThank you for your consideration.\r\n</p>',0,438,228,0,'67.195.182.111','en'),(19707,3261,'2003-02-21 04:05:19',175,'Paul G','Hey, in reference to the details: nope, no notes.  Maybe what follows will, \\\"ring true,\\\" in your mind: It is difficult to know when certain things that you\\\'ve learned to do in life will suddenly become useful to you.  Possessing a variety of, \\\"life-skills,\\\" can be a wonderful thing - even if you\\\'re not always certain exactly what you\\\'re being prepared for.\r\n<p>\r\nWorking as a Deputy Sheriff, I had to attend a, \\\"report writing,\\\" class.  A certain style was emphasized - as well as an attention to detail and chronology, on the part of the observer.  Those lessons were reinforced when I worked as a 911 Operator.  Everything always began the same way: \\\"On the above listed date and time, R/D observed...\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI kept a log of the locations and fare/tip totals one night; but the results were so depressing that I discontinued the practice.  I\\\'ve found that the best thing to do is to look for the individual stories, and also to try to see things with the camera.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI don\\\'t know.  The business of imposing some order or value upon a seemingly disconnected series of events can be difficult; but that holds equally true for all of life, doesn\\\'t it...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe only real danger with the taxi stories is that there are so many that one will cover/bury/obscure the one before it; it makes it hard to know what to keep in, and what to toss out too...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe writing that you have done about your, \\\"real life,\\\" resonates with me.  Yes it does...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere was a passage in <b>Ray Bradbury\\\'s</b> <i>Fahrenheit 451</i> that always stuck with me, and seemed to describe - so well - the feeling I have had, and that I think, maybe, you have felt too: The protagonist: Montag, is in the firehouse, or approaching it, and he notices a difference in the Mechanical Hound\\\'s relationship with himself.  Montag looks like a fireman; Montag wears a fireman\\\'s uniform; but after his contact with Clarisse, Montag isn\\\'t a fireman, anymore.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThat\\\'s a deeply meaningful thing for me, and it could easily consume an article or book if I could force myself to explain it properly.  But, not tonight.\r\n</p>\r\n<p> \r\nWe have this choice, I think, my friend, between making visible - articulating - the internal dialogue: the doubts, fears, and questions, in an honest way; or holding back, and releasing things in a more careful and contrived way: worrying over their appearance, and how they might affect our position in the rank of some order that is the product of a will alien to our own.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOh yeah: hi.\r\n</p>',1,2613,377,0,'67.195.182.111','en'),(19708,3263,'2003-02-21 06:35:16',231,'alana','totally.',2,8,0,0,'203.220.14.241','en'),(19709,2848,'2003-02-21 07:21:17',228,'belly','oh my god, did that ungoogleable poster already exist? that\\\'s weird. ',0,72,66,0,'210.155.207.21','en'),(19710,3263,'2003-02-21 15:12:38',66,'Peter','<br><tt>perfer et obdura; dolor hic tibi proderit olim...</tt>',1,62,0,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19711,2848,'2003-02-21 22:06:54',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yeah, that film was a pretty big hit last year...</tt>',1,63,228,0,'66.108.188.37','en'),(19712,2848,'2003-02-22 21:48:52',17,'austin','one thing justin is:\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.google.com/search?q=justin+japan\\\">http://www.google.com/search?q=justin+japan</a>',1,129,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19713,3264,'2003-02-24 18:43:17',66,'Peter','<br><tt>although only vaguely related to your article, <a href=\"http://www.amnesty.org/ailib/aireport/ar99/asa24.htm\">human rights violations</a> are probably a big issue standing in the way of north korea\'s progress (well, and scores of other countries, the US included, but...)<br><br>on a vaguely related note:<br>\r\n<a href=\"http://english.pravda.ru/world/2003/02/24/43665.html\">some reading from pravda</a><br>\r\n<a href=\"http://monthly.chosun.com/html/200201/200201280001_1.html\">some s. korean perspective</a><br>\r\n<A href=\"http://www.hankooki.com/kt_op/200004/e20000419161706481176.htm\">more</a><br><br></tt>',1,581,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19714,3261,'2003-02-25 03:16:32',175,'Paul G','Hey - thanks.<br>\r\nNext time I\\\'ll ask for something a bit more sexy...<br>\r\nHa.',0,82,449,0,'67.195.182.179','en'),(19715,3264,'2003-02-25 04:04:29',75,'tyler','\\\'Fearing a possible \\\"socialist utopia\\\", the United States began a massive arms buildup in the South. In response the North retooled its economy for war.\\\'\r\n--oh my god, that\\\'s such <i>bullshit</i>. a \\\'socialist utopia\\\'? if that were true, it would be the first. the us began the buildup to keep the ussr/china/n korea/whoever from taking s. korea and enslaving its people. ',1,393,0,0,'165.247.166.59','en'),(19716,2952,'2003-02-25 05:43:45',66,'Peter','<br><tt>update... all this bizness finally got <A href=\\\"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/query.fcgi?cmd=Retrieve&db=PubMed&list_uids=12574874&dopt=Abstract\\\">published</a>... hooray.</tt>',1,194,0,0,'66.108.190.99','en'),(19717,3259,'2003-02-25 05:44:47',175,'Paul G','<p>\r\n<i>\r\n\\\"cold wetness everywhere. \r\nour heat the only thing between us.\\\"\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe day we spent together at summer\\\'s end.<br>\r\ncold cloth clinging to taut skin.<br>\r\nfingers chilled and to the bone - thin.<br>\r\nfingers raking hair, combing strands: feeling sand.<br>\r\nand wet hair hanging, pendulous in the wind.<br>\r\nthe water crashed.<br>\r\nthe sun set.<br>\r\nand i knew you better.\r\n</p>',1,411,0,0,'67.195.182.179','en'),(19718,3264,'2003-02-25 06:07:40',66,'Peter','<br><tt><a href=\\\"http://www.usspueblo.org/v2f/incident/incidentframe.html\\\">uss pueblo</a>. related? probably not, but interesting, nevertheless.</tt>',1,155,0,0,'66.108.190.99','en'),(19719,3264,'2003-02-25 07:08:21',175,'Paul G','Hey Peter: I read a few of those articles; and then I had to stop.  Wow, the human suffering associated with this situation is overwhelming.  \r\n<p>\r\nA number of the guys that I grew up with were from Korea.  I think it\\\'s safe to say that they\\\'re pretty intense people.  Maybe it has something to do with sort of being caught - as a group - between the hammer of Japan, and the anvil of China.  I dunno.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThey seemed to feel that they were the \\\"parent\\\" culture in East Asia, from whom others had taken without acknowledging the debt.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere seems to be this weird sort of racism involved too: the Korean guys referred to the Japanese, Koreans, and people of northern China (Manchuria) as being true Asians; the people from Southeast Asia they called \\\"Black.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhenever I read or hear about South Korea and relations with other states in the area, I think about <a href=\\\"http://www.inq7.net/wnw/2001/aug/14/wnw_3-1.htm\\\">this incident</a>.  I don\\\'t think that I\\\'ve ever encountered anything similar in the good ol\\\' U.S.A.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFrom page 36 of Hunter S. Thompson\\\'s <i>Curse of Lono</i>:\\\"...they [Koreans] can kick the shit out of anything that walks on two legs, except maybe a Samoan.\\\"\r\n</p>',1,1272,66,0,'67.195.182.179','en'),(19720,3266,'2003-02-25 14:06:02',75,'tyler','but there will be time...time for a hundred visions and revisions...before the taking of a toast and tea.',1,105,0,0,'165.247.166.59','en'),(19721,3266,'2003-02-25 22:31:17',175,'Paul G','Curious: It\\\'s a brief of the <a href=\\\"http://www.prufrock.org/poem/fulltext.html\\\">original</a>, to which you refer in the title; Michaelangelo, <a href=\\\"http://www.bard.org/SectionEducate/charactershamlet.html\\\">Polonius</a>, Lazarus, et al, are there.  If Eliot\\\'s original was an indictment of a class, or generation, is this poem of yours meant to express a similar, bitter, sentiment - but in regard to a particular individual?  Or is this meant to be a satire of Eliot, and the notion of, \\\"real manliness,\\\" that one might easily assume he longs for, in his poem...\r\n<p>\r\nJust thinking...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i>\r\n*constructive*\r\n</i>\r\n</p>',3,658,0,0,'207.220.178.215','en'),(19722,3266,'2003-02-26 03:50:50',449,'s','<p>It\\\'s a response to Prufrock himself - the embodiment of the second guessing, insecure, socially impotent man(no, it is not directed to anyone I know).\r\nI suppose is a personal poem  - something capturing the tone that I can imagine the newly liberated women of his time spouting out in the Vagina Monologues of our time(kindasorta). I\\\'ve never been one to examine my \\\"poetry\\\", never having been much of a poet.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOn a less literary note - my modern american poetry teacher has been making us all write response poetry. This was inspired by an <a href=\\\"http://www.robertsward.com/rsward_tseliot.html\\\">article</a> written by one of our visiting poets, Robert Sward.\r\n</p>\r\n',3,702,175,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(19723,3265,'2003-02-26 03:56:41',449,'s','Interesting; I like the third person viewpoint. The sporadic rhyme is odd, but seems to work anyhow.',3,100,0,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(19724,3266,'2003-02-26 15:48:11',75,'tyler','it\\\'s really a bizrre coincidence--i was inspired to write my last poem on here, right under yours, by <i>Prufrock.</i> when i saw your article thing right on top of it, i thought maybe you had picked up on that. but i guess from your comments that that\\\'s not the case. wow, then. i was like, whoa.',1,303,0,0,'165.247.166.59','en'),(19725,2333,'2003-02-26 18:55:52',66,'Peter','<br><tt>im rereading some of my old entries today; this one truly made me pause and contemplate the question of <i>whats next</i>.<br><br>maybe i should add to this; the past few months have had me thinking that maye enough time has passed that i might have worthwhile developments to add as footnotes.</tt>',1,307,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19726,3265,'2003-02-26 19:55:26',449,'s','<p>\r\nWow, I read on your comments on my poem(right above yours) that you were inspired by Prufrock with this one. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhoa...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI didn\\\'t notice it earlier, but damn, it works really well.\r\n</p>',0,215,0,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(19727,3264,'2003-02-26 22:58:48',370,'seanthecon','great facts and analysis chiamattt. especially liked the carter facts. that man is in no way deserving of a nobel peace prize. let\\\'s also not forget about how clinton and the united states failed to live up to its end of the bargain with n. korea after the 94 agreement. while oil did flow in, the money and assistance to build nucular infrastructure that was promised did not. n. korea has a very legitimate reason to be pissed off with u.s. delays. however, what\\\'s up with that crazy motherfucker kim jong-il and his wacked out hairstyle. he\\\'s no fucking good. there has been ample evidence (not just from the US, but from NGOs) that he has interfered with desperately needed food aid. he continues to live an extravagant and luxerious life while his people starve. ',6,777,0,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(19728,3267,'2003-02-27 06:14:46',185,'orion','You\\\'re such a good writer.  I\\\'ve\r\nreally enjoyed reading your posts \r\nhere.  It\\\'s rare to find such\r\ngood literature on the web... Thank you for choosing to share your talent(s) with us. :-)',1,199,0,0,'66.68.96.2','en'),(19729,3264,'2003-02-27 06:25:33',187,'kandrer','great article!<br>\r\ni still know quite nothing about Korean people, to my regret, even though the japanese TV/newspapers keep on giving (long) news and reports about anything happening in N or S Korea (political event or not).<br>\r\nabout your link, Paul, i have been unable to understand why the jp prime minister visited that damn shrine. all the japanese people i know found it stupid; but Koizumi is not known for being stupid. or is he? did he do that to \\\"seduce\\\" some old farts voters of extreme right wing? a few weeks just before that, he was visiting N.Korea to \\\"tighten relationships\\\"... i would be happy to know what you guys think about it.\r\n',1,665,0,0,'210.239.99.51','en'),(19730,3267,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','jesus bloody crikey!  amazing!',6,30,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19731,3268,'2003-02-28 14:50:18',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this was really interesting and astonishingly well organized, btw. cool.</tt>',1,85,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19732,3268,'2003-02-28 17:06:05',75,'tyler','it\'s either a postmodern statement on the detailed, yet soulless and frustrating triviality of a life spent serving a bovine, confused, low-income public--or else...the author is simply somehow disturbed, to be moved to write this here.',1,257,0,0,'165.247.166.59','en'),(19733,3264,'2003-02-28 17:08:14',75,'tyler','how can you know it\\\'s a great article without knowing anything about Korea? this is representative of our whole problem right now.',1,133,187,0,'165.247.166.59','en'),(19734,3264,'2003-02-28 17:12:36',75,'tyler','by the way--would you rather N. Korea had \\\'first-strike dominance\\\' the whole time? because <b>one of us was going to</b>. do you really think that if we had not protected south Korea as we did, north Korea would have left it alone? <i>hell</i> no! they would have taken all their property and enslaved their people just like all communist leaders have done throughout history. this article still pisses me off.',1,416,0,0,'165.247.166.59','en'),(19735,3266,'2003-03-03 02:11:20',17,'austin','hi',1,2,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19736,3270,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','fuckin\\\' aye!',6,15,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19737,3266,'2003-03-03 04:07:01',449,'s','Hello.',1,6,17,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(19738,3268,'2003-03-03 04:15:07',449,'s','This made for some very interesting reading.',2,44,0,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(19739,3264,'2003-03-03 06:43:43',187,'kandrer','my dear, I meant (and wrote) Korean <i>people</i>. There is a huge difference between a country\\\'s culture (in a broad meaning)  and how the same country\\\'s government acts. That\\\'s how I can still love America and Americans while I wait for next election to kick out the tiny George from a responsability much too big for his pea-sized brains. That\\\'s also why I can still accept to be french even though I am ashamed of the governement we french have.</p><p>\r\nalso, this article is great to me because it is clear, well organized, and helped me understand better the Korean people. Chiamattt being living over there, his opinion is great to me, as it should be to you. does the fact that N Korea would have probably \\\"swallowed\\\" the south change that much to the quality of the article?',1,801,75,0,'211.134.136.14','en'),(19740,3264,'2003-03-03 06:52:56',187,'kandrer','Koizumi news: this is the answer to my own previous question; Japan prime minister Koizumi visited the \\\"war-criminals\\\" shrine to \\\"honor\\\" former elections promises toward \\\"pro war\\\" voters. well, it\\\'s just the opinion of my (japanese) father in law.<br>\r\nNB: in the Japan Times of today, nice picture of the same Koizumi with Fidel Castro... Castro proposed his help to deal with N. Korea... In the same time, Japan is supposed to give money to US as a participation in Iraq war efforts. Is the world becoming <i>that</i> small?',1,547,0,0,'211.134.136.14','en'),(19741,3264,'2003-03-03 06:59:41',187,'kandrer','<b>to chiamatt</b>: (sorry to fill up the comments column) just a question, do you think there is a chance of unification with such a crazy fellow running N.Korea? This guy is an emperor; I doubt he can give up power. And he is so moody that he could ruin easily any kind of negotiations. Well, I only know this by Japanese TV... :}',1,332,0,0,'211.134.136.14','en'),(19742,3264,'2003-03-03 07:58:51',175,'Paul G','My dearest K,<br>\r\nI am sorry if I seemes to ignore you.<br>\r\n1) I have little/no meaningful knowledge about the PM; my insights into his behavior would be meaningless;<br>\r\n2) I suspect that I already write too much, here.\r\n<p>\r\nFor my part, I am content to second your opinions in this matter.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI appreciate your interest in the character/culture of the leader/people when examining political behavior, e.g. very few people who understood Korea would have been surprised that Korean soldiers developed a reputation for behaving poorly during the conflict in Vietnam.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAnd - even as you referred to the type of soul possessed by the leader of North Korea, I would say that the most dangerous thing about the Chief Executive in the U.S.A. is his character - and specifically: his relationship with power.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe greatness that was to be found in people like Washington or Lincoln was evidenced most by what they did not do - even though the opportunity presented itself to them.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nBut, I\\\'m saying too much...\r\n</p>',1,1059,187,0,'207.220.155.196','en'),(19743,3270,'2003-03-04 05:15:31',228,'belly','choice, bro!',0,12,0,0,'61.201.234.143','en'),(19744,3268,'2003-03-04 18:33:19',122,'hool','<p>why would someone have to be disturbed to write this here? you don\\\'t need to dig very deep into the swinney archives to find precedent</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i liked reading this, even though i don\\\'t live in the states. i don\\\'t wear glasses, so i don\\\'t know much about the procedures in canada.  it was an interesting read, properly reserved - something like this could\\\'ve easily turned into a self indulgent rant, but it didn\\\'t.  and yes, i agree with peter, very well organized.  thanks for the post</p>',0,513,0,0,'66.185.85.79','en'),(19745,3268,'2003-03-04 19:00:59',239,'Poonlarp','this is the best thing ive read here since my last post.',1,56,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19746,3268,'2003-03-04 22:30:17',75,'tyler','whatever, this was ridiculous. who cares about buying glasses with health insurance? i mean i\\\'m sorry your job sucks ass and you have to deal with this kind of thing, but that doesn\\\'t make it interesting. go back to school.',1,229,0,0,'165.247.166.251','en'),(19747,3268,'2003-03-05 00:07:00',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i thought it was interesting; i guess that makes me rediculous too, huh?</tt>',1,85,75,1,'66.108.185.72','en'),(19748,3249,'2003-03-05 02:42:33',228,'belly','<a href=\\\"http://metropolis.japantoday.com/biginjapanarchive299/259/biginjapaninc.htm\\\" target=\\\"blank\\\">all about pocky</a>',0,132,0,0,'61.201.235.114','en'),(19749,3268,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','uh, who cares?<br />\r\n<br />\r\nthis is the most boring piece of drivel i\\\'ve read here since poonlarp\\\'s last post.<br />\r\n<br />\r\nBO-RING',6,141,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19750,3217,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','it\\\'s me or poonlarp',6,22,17,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19751,3272,'2003-03-05 09:40:25',69,'starlajo','that was really insightful, dude!',0,33,0,0,'203.96.111.237','en'),(19752,3268,'2003-03-05 17:53:24',82,'simon','i didnt think it was boring',0,27,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19753,3268,'2003-03-05 19:25:45',239,'tubcat','i care!',1,7,62,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19754,3268,'2003-03-05 19:33:45',17,'austin','obviously something is up your ass.  since you don\\\'t write here, i suggest you just leave.  i found this piece mildly amusing and certainly different from anything else here.  i would encourage its writer to come back.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n*nice supportive*',2,250,62,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19755,3272,'2003-03-05 19:36:26',17,'austin','that was a test of the emergency broadcasting system, only a test. :)',1,69,69,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19756,3268,'2003-03-05 22:46:05',463,'timethian','<p>1) Was there anything actually contructive in your comment, or do you simply like to project your own particular feelings of uselessness on others? :)</p><p>2) An article is an article, and this particular \\\'rant\\\' as it were was aimed at a certain audience. If you didn\\\'t fit within that audence, kudos... is there any particular reason you feel my posting it entitles you to fling feces at me without any particular reasoning?</p><p>3) My job certainly doesn\\\'t \\\"suck ass\\\"... it\\\'s more the uninformed, unwashed masses that tend to frequent us who do. Most people don\\\'t bother to think about the human factor inside a medical facility.',1,660,75,0,'172.193.221.119','en'),(19757,2610,'2003-03-06 03:00:41',370,'seanthecon','<p>this was paralysingly beatuiful. is paralysingly a word? fuck it. it is now.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>a wall of no-sound that drowns out her next words. I have offered nothing in reply, If only her words could stay.</p>\r\n\r\nmy god man. wordsworth has struck me dead. i thought you a formidable writer, but a poet too? now everything has come into place.',6,341,0,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(19758,3268,'2003-03-06 05:55:35',75,'tyler','well, since you asked...<br><br>\r\n1) i had a strong reaction to your \\\"article\\\" and responded accordingly. i think it was dumb, regardless of what anyone else thinks, for you to write what i assume was your first article here about the ins and outs of medical customer service--just about the most banal subject there is. it seemed like you just wanted to rant about the frustrations of your job, which i can\\\'t stand people doing, when they could just get another damn job if it\\\'s so bad that yopu feel moved to share the details with strangers. of course i could be wrong.<br><br>\r\n2)your posting it here, with a \\\'comments\\\' section attached, does in fact afford me the right to \\\'fling feces\\\' as you put it at your article. if you wanted to exclude that possiblilty, you should have tried to get it published in a magazine, or maybe written it on a piece of paper and torn it up--that\\\'s what a psychologist might have advised you to do, to release your work-related stress.<br><br\r\n3) i assume that your job forces you into contact with said unwashed masses? that the massess in question therefore are a <i>part</i> of your job? and they suck ass? see where i\\\'m going with this?\r\n',1,1209,463,0,'165.247.166.251','en'),(19759,3268,'2003-03-06 05:59:08',75,'tyler','and finally--what in GODs name makes you think an internet literary website would be read by the unwashed 50-dollar arguing peasantry? it\\\'s the very last place any of the people you are ostensibly writing to would ever, ever, ever see it. ever. ever ever. if you can explain THAT, i definitely apologize.',1,307,0,0,'165.247.166.251','en'),(19760,3268,'2003-03-06 06:17:53',59,'s5','while the subject matter of this article wasn\\\'t interesting to me, i\\\'m not sure why it elicited angry responses. thoughts?',0,128,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19761,3268,'2003-03-06 06:23:59',59,'s5','tyler, you\\\'re nuts. who is this \\\"peasantry\\\" you\\\'re ranting about?',0,77,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19762,3268,'2003-03-06 06:38:42',17,'austin','Tyler, do you wear glasses?\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThis thread really makes me sick.  It is one thing to nit pick a\r\nphilosophical or political item and extrapolate as happens here often\r\nand seems to be the most fruitful discussion, but to just blanketly\r\nsay something sucks or somebody sucks, especially a new person to the\r\nsite, or their work, chaps my ass (you can quote me on that).\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThere are a handful of people who really do post to this website that\r\nhave never posted anything bad.  They also only post about once or\r\ntwice a year.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nPersonally, I know I have some low points on the quality of my work,\r\nbut the important thing is to try.  Let me pull down my worn copy of\r\n\\\"Everything I Ever Needed To Know I Learned in the Third Grade\\\".  The\r\neffort of posting something in a site like this, for the first time,\r\nfor the 150th time, is fantastic.  This is an intimidating place.  I\r\nam always glad when someone new shows up. I will almost always publish\r\ntheir first piece if it is at all readable.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI have worn glasses my whole life.  I found this amusing because I\r\nknow what a pit of hell getting new glasses can be regarding\r\ninsurance, and costs, etc.  I\\\'m not saying you should find it amusing\r\neven if you do wear glasses.  I am saying it may be for a target\r\naudience, and I may or may not be in that audience, but I read\r\nwhatever I want on this site.   I can usually tell in the first 3 to 4\r\nseconds if I want to read something at all, at a glance.  If I don\\\'t,\r\nI move on.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nAnd Tyler, I have written plenty of stuff that I didn\\\'t feel was up to\r\nthe nebulous standards of this public venue.\r\n',6,1660,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19763,3250,'2003-03-06 07:14:24',154,'ryan','this thing fucking sucks big donkey dong.',0,41,0,0,'216.99.204.83','en'),(19764,3268,'2003-03-06 07:32:52',231,'alana','<p>tyler. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>are you wondering if povvos read swinney? you\\\'re saying probably not.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>but yes dear, they do.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>they is me. living on $300AUD a fortnight my friend. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>reading is free. unless you can\\\'t afford to buy glasses if you need them, which means you can\\\'t read unless you have money to buy glasses.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>(i have no idea if this is relevant. ok.)</p>',3,401,75,0,'131.170.189.250','en'),(19765,3268,'2003-03-06 13:48:37',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\\\"Most people don\\\'t bother to think about the human factor inside a medical facility\\\"</i><br><br>you said it. that very thought is a rather consistent theme where i work, also.</tt>',1,200,463,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19766,3268,'2003-03-06 17:34:34',17,'austin','a couple more notes on pop culture items that have to do with suck jobs and people love to read them:\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI>Dilbert\r\n<LI>Office Space\r\n<LI>9 to 5\r\n</UL>',3,157,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19767,3268,'2003-03-06 19:21:12',195,'matt','9 to 5: that was some funny shit.',1,33,17,0,'24.129.129.219','en'),(19768,3268,'2003-03-06 20:04:07',240,'xep','i don\\\'t understand why either.  i don\\\'t understand why people spend so much energy to say such mean things to people who haven\\\'t done anything to them.  ',0,162,59,0,'64.81.241.248','en'),(19769,3268,'2003-03-06 20:12:41',239,'Poonlarp','i agree. i was caught a bit off guard by the mean comments here, especially after reading what i thought was a pleasantly/intentionally banal story. oh well.',1,157,240,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19770,3269,'2003-03-06 20:20:40',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\\\"If you don\\\'t find me a bathroom soon, you\\\'re going to be swimming in illusion.\\\"</i><br><br>heh...</tt>\r\n\r\n',1,130,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19771,3268,'2003-03-06 20:27:06',59,'s5','<p>\r\ni\\\'m rereading it now - maybe it\\\'s because the tone of the article came off as nitpicky and grumbly to some people. sometimes it\\\'s funny to read that kind of thing (like dilbert or office space), and sometimes it\\\'s really negative. maybe the article put some people off? i know that, personally, when i go to the doctor\\\'s office, i\\\'m often really confused and bewildered about what i\\\'m supposed to do, and the last thing i want to know is that the person behind the desk is sneering at me under their breath.\r\n</p><p>\r\nin any case, this article is hardly out of place here, though i think i can understand how it would irritate someone to read it.\r\n</p><p>\r\nnice, i just clicked \\\"nice supportive\\\" for the first time ever.\r\n</p>',2,758,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19772,3268,'2003-03-06 20:48:16',240,'xep','i actually found the article enlightening.  i have been guilty of showing up for a dentist appointment with no insurance card and being confused that they couldn\\\'t just get the info from previous appointments.  \r\n\r\nalso, how is it really any different than those of us who have worked in tech support whining about all the clueless users we have/had to support?  there are entire usenet groups dedicated to the topic.  just because fewer people who read this forum are interested in the optical industry than the computer industry doesn\\\'t make it any less valid.  just because *you\\\'re* not interested in a topic doesn\\\'t make it banal to everyone else.    ',0,667,0,0,'64.81.241.248','en'),(19773,3268,'2003-03-06 20:55:31',59,'s5','of course, i don\\\'t think it\\\'s any different, like you said. it wasn\\\'t interesting to me, but i don\\\'t think it\\\'s out of place here. also, i don\\\'t expect to brought to orgasm by every single post here, and it\\\'s nice to see that austin continues to keep an open door to new writers.',0,300,240,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19774,3268,'2003-03-06 21:30:24',195,'matt','could we please get a list of some articles that might bring us to orgasm?',1,74,59,0,'209.208.108.196','en'),(19775,3268,'2003-03-06 21:31:22',463,'timethian','<p>Feh... I\\\'m just going to bozo the guy... I read several other comments of his... and unless he supports the particular issue at hand, he trolls it. Oh well. On to better things. :) I do appreciate the constructive comments. While I know the article in question is a bit constrictive in its audience, it at least informs -- which was the point.</p>\r\n<p>I wasn\\\'t writing to be humorous, or make social commentary... or any of that rot. I saw an issue, and figured someone, anyone, out there might actually get some info out of it... perhaps even an explanation for something in even another field that might have confused them.</P>\r\n<p>If that happened, then I did my job.</p>',0,683,0,0,'172.191.221.43','en'),(19776,3268,'2003-03-06 22:39:43',82,'simon','it was helpful to me as i\\\'m a big spazz when it comes to paperwork and insurance and the like. thanks, man.',0,110,463,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19777,2818,'2003-03-06 22:46:46',370,'seanthecon','what the hell happened to all your other stories? they got me high like i was huffing gas. now i got tha shakes. oh please oh please oh please, just give me just one more hit.',6,175,0,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(19778,3268,'2003-03-06 23:40:05',59,'s5','Your Orgasm May Vary',0,20,195,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19779,3268,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','okay just noted request to leave.  bye.',6,39,62,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19780,3268,'2003-03-06 23:57:38',64,'Joanna','Try Dammit\\\'s first piece about the strip club. That certainly seemed to get a few people off. ',1,97,195,0,'156.62.3.21','en'),(19781,3268,'2003-03-07 00:19:33',75,'tyler','ok, ok, gee whiz. sorry timethian. i\\\'m an overanalyzing bastard. ',1,68,0,0,'165.247.166.251','en'),(19782,3268,'2003-03-07 01:59:55',17,'austin','alright, tyler apologized, EOT.',1,31,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19783,3264,'2003-03-07 13:37:32',93,'chiamattt','Oh I think unification is closer than most people think. I think the South and North Koreans are much closer than you think. To be honest, a good number of the newer generations of Korean citizens are getting rather annoyed at the presence of American soldiers in South Korea. This is the biggest stumbling block over unification. \r\n\r\nI think that when Kim Jong ill dies or gives power to his son (supposedly he is really stupid) things will change even more. \r\n\r\nWhat worries me is the ammount of rhetoric coming out of the western press on or about n.korea and its leader. \r\n\r\nI DO think they will unite....but as you say...that guy up north is a real nut job...so who knows. ',0,678,187,0,'61.37.109.32','en'),(19784,3264,'2003-03-07 21:36:38',17,'austin','test.',3,5,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19785,3264,'2003-03-08 15:25:48',75,'tyler','don\\\'t you think maybe they are annoyed at the US soldiers because they don\\\'t understand the situation? because, in fact, as you say, they are younger?',1,156,93,0,'165.247.166.251','en'),(19786,3264,'2003-03-09 00:17:17',82,'simon','possibly because US soldiers can be total creeps.',0,49,0,0,'12.236.164.112','en'),(19787,3260,'2003-03-09 00:48:10',168,'laney','two things.\r\n\r\n1) one of the biggest problems i have with the current protest movement is that it is not connected to voting, or the system at all. that\\\'s one of the reasons da and i started this <a href=\\\"http://www.usprogressives.org\\\">new coalition</a>\r\n\r\n2) there is someone running for the democratic ticket who really gets this stuff. at least from what i\\\'ve seen so far. i was pretty knocked out by his latest <a href=\\\"http://www.deanforamerica.com/dean.cfm?section=about&page=speeches&drill=021703\\\">foreign policy speech</a> everyone going to these protests should drop the \\\"no blood for oil\\\" crap and start carrying a \\\"Dean for President\\\" sign.',1,681,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(19788,3260,'2003-03-09 03:04:48',154,'ryan','the problem with dean is that his intellectualism is largely targeted towards the people on either coast.  if he cannot get middle america to buy in or \\\"get\\\" his message he has no hope at all.',1,198,168,0,'216.99.204.125','en'),(19789,3264,'2003-03-09 03:58:50',187,'kandrer','from what i saw (on japanese TV, sorry again) i got that feeling: yes, they seem to want to be a single country. i regret i was too young to pay attention to what happened in germany before the wall fell. but for sure, if they still consider themselves to be *one* people, then they will be one someday (and even despite the crazy guy on command in the N at the moment).</p>\r\n<u>tyler:</u> even you can understand that any country cannot feel \\\"good\\\" with foreign troops inside its border, even if they was/is/might be a reason for these troops to be useful. i guess you are bad at putting yourself in someone else\\\'s place; if you can, think how you would feel to live in an hypothetic area of the US under the protection of another country\\\'s army (pick your choice: french, korean, chinese, english, etc). you wouldn\\\'t need to be dumb to find yourself upset sometimes, no?',1,887,93,0,'210.198.205.42','en'),(19790,3264,'2003-03-09 04:14:34',154,'ryan','<a href=\\\"http://www.dailykos.com/archives/001920.html\\\">http://www.dailykos.com/archives/001920.html</a><p>\r\n\r\na good commentary on the situation from an anti-anti commentary site.  apparently we are seriously considering a pullback.<p>\r\nthe US troops over there are basically a human tripwire and would be slaughtered in any conflict that involved the north pressing south.<p>\r\n\r\nanyway.',0,393,0,0,'216.99.204.110','en'),(19791,3260,'2003-03-09 06:10:41',59,'s5','personally, i think america has been ruled by the farm states for too long.',0,75,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19792,3264,'2003-03-09 23:19:51',75,'tyler','i wrote a whole bunch of shit here, and then i was like--what\\\'s the point? just thought i\\\'d share that.',1,109,187,0,'165.247.166.251','en'),(19793,3278,'2003-03-10 16:48:28',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i liked this installment better than most of the others. it has nice details and really paints a vivid picture in my mind.<br><Br>i like where you said <i>\\\"the sidewalks roll up at five o\\\'clock...\\\"</i></tt>',1,223,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19794,3275,'2003-03-11 06:00:22',93,'chiamattt','holy fuck! did you get someone pregnant?',1,40,0,0,'218.145.25.79','en'),(19795,3152,'2003-03-11 06:36:33',175,'Paul G','I feel bad.',1,11,0,0,'205.186.208.237','en'),(19796,2274,'2003-03-11 09:35:48',154,'ryan','i can\\\'t believe you were at a house party that had craig david on the decks.  yo.',1,84,93,0,'216.99.204.47','en'),(19797,3275,'2003-03-11 09:42:20',370,'seanthecon','nope. just caught in a perpetual state of dismotion. i think its effected his brain...if he has one. a tale of lives and lies. a present for another. ',6,150,0,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(19798,3275,'2003-03-11 10:21:59',370,'seanthecon','a poem for my lover.',6,20,0,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(19799,3275,'2003-03-11 17:53:50',241,'linus van pelt','<p>you need to buy new shoes.</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p>i called in sick to work today and then i read this. i realized that i\\\'m not sick, our relationships are. also, i\\\'m not really sick, i just didn\\\'t want to work.</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p>nice piece.</p> ',0,250,0,0,'64.230.96.81','en'),(19800,3281,'2003-03-11 22:33:00',195,'matt','that\\\'s the day i turn 33.',1,28,0,0,'209.208.109.109','en'),(19801,3280,'2003-03-11 22:40:45',195,'matt','i can relate exactly to you wanting to run away from everything.  i don\\\'t wanna go to rehab, i don\\\'t wanna live with mom and dad.',1,135,0,0,'209.208.109.109','en'),(19802,3281,'2003-03-11 23:50:40',129,'moebius','ha. i can beat that hand: that\\\'s the day i turn 34. (and my given name is also matt.) ',0,89,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(19803,3281,'2003-03-12 00:04:09',195,'matt','so we\\\'re birthday buddies.  that\\\'s something.',1,51,0,0,'24.129.129.219','en'),(19804,3281,'2003-03-12 00:32:32',83,'adamajah','When it rains in Beijing, children chase the dragonflies with little nets.  ',2,76,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(19805,3281,'2003-03-12 02:50:28',17,'austin','fantastic writing, dave.  that part about conflict playing itself out is something that is on my mind.  when i\\\'m asked what i think of america\\\'s foreign interventions i feel that sentiment, interupted conflict not allowed to play itself out.  i feel that america interupts the development of other countries.  i\\\'m not an isolationist, but i am definately not an interventionist.  ',2,389,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19806,3280,'2003-03-12 03:12:57',66,'Peter','<br><tt>life is better with <a href=\\\"http://saturn5.com/~peter/ljphoto/lionelsm.jpg\\\">lionel richie</a>.</tt>',1,114,0,0,'66.108.184.119','en'),(19807,3280,'2003-03-12 05:21:18',175,'Paul G','It seems that you drive and think in circles, while the world around you moves in a linear fashion towards an undesirable end.  There are tangents; but there isn\\\'t any meaningful connection.  You\\\'re left wondering: Why?  Yeah, that resonates...',0,250,0,0,'206.215.77.9','en'),(19808,3281,'2003-03-12 05:55:22',82,'simon','dave, i wish i could come by and visit before you leave. but i\\\'ll be in touch along the way, i\\\'m sure.',0,108,0,0,'67.121.84.106','en'),(19809,3281,'2003-03-12 05:57:00',175,'Paul G','This was a really good piece of writing, Dave; but then again, I\\\'ve enjoyed everything that you\\\'ve written while in Korea.\r\n<p>\r\nI think though, that while civil war is an old idea/process, the weapons with which war is fought are now so powerful that the consequences of fighting are different than they have been in the past.  It\\\'s too easy, to kill too many people, too quickly.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe corollary - that you\\\'ve hinted at - is that sometimes the horror that the tought of conflict induces is sufficient to cause people to accept a \\\"peace\\\" that is in many ways worse than the conflict itself would have been.  That\\\'s <i>1984</i> eh?  And maybe <i>Cyropaedia</i> too.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI am less certain about what is right, now, than I have ever been before - so, trust me, I\\\'m not trying to argue for a certain thing.  There are many difficult issues wrapped up together in this, and whether I agree with you or not, I respect you willingness to state your convictions plainly; that seems to be the only way that it\\\'s possible to conduct meaningful dialogue, and reach some sort of imperfect understanding with each other.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI hope that you\\\'ll be open to other voices, and allow for the fact that many of the things that you think are true, now, won\\\'t seem quite so certain, in time...\r\n</p>  ',1,1340,0,0,'206.215.77.9','en'),(19810,3280,'2003-03-12 07:37:53',75,'tyler','i agree about school. like i really want to learn everything there is to know about the law. i see it as a means to security, and then i can pursue my interests. ',1,162,0,0,'165.247.166.251','en'),(19811,3283,'2003-03-12 08:27:26',59,'s5','i hear that there are recipes for margarine too, but it depends on which kind. someone should find one and put it in <a href=\\\"http://veganchallenge.org\\\">veganchallenge</a>.',0,178,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19812,3281,'2003-03-12 11:02:19',92,'kini','i like.',1,7,0,0,'203.27.69.124','en'),(19813,3281,'2003-03-12 17:41:13',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\nI do not think the North Koreans would use nuclear weapons in a war with South Korea. I just think the U.S needs to step back over here and let things play their course. Whose to say PyongYang doesnt warm upto the South when there isn\\\'t 37,000 U.S troops standing between them. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIs it not fact that the U.S has enough fire power aboard any of the many ships \\\"protecting\\\" Taiwan and Japan to blow North Korea back into the Chosun Dynasty? I just think the buffer isnt really necessary. You dont need two brake pads per wheel do you? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*friendly*\r\n</p>',2,587,175,0,'61.40.66.81','en'),(19814,3281,'2003-03-12 18:50:35',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\"I do not think the North Koreans would use nuclear weapons in a war with South Korea\"</i>\r\n<br><br>I, for one, think that nuclear weapons are the <i>least</i> of s. korea\'s wories...<br><Br><i>\"South Korea is loath to drive North Korea into a corner because Pyongyang has deployed the bulk of its 1.1-million-strong army, the world`s fifth largest, just across their common border. South Korea`s capital Seoul lies within range of the North`s artillery\"</i> [article <A href=\"http://archives.econ.utah.edu/archives/marxism/2002w52/msg00134.htm\">here</a>, from the <i>Marxism Mailing-list</i>]<br><br>that stat creates an odd perspective for your statement <i>\"Whose to say PyongYang doesnt warm upto the South when there isn\'t 37,000 U.S troops standing between them\"</i>, as I think most would agree that its silly to assume that such deployment is strictly a product of US presence there...</tt>',1,923,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19815,3281,'2003-03-12 18:54:50',239,'Poonlarp','whos to say Seoul doesnt warm up to the North when there isn\\\'t ~1.1 mil North Korean troops (plus artillery) standing between them?',1,134,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19816,3283,'2003-03-12 19:19:39',66,'Peter','<br><tt>\r\n<a href=\"http://www.erowid.org/plants/cannabis/cannabis_faq_eat.shtml\">1</a>, <a href=\"http://www.erowid.org/plants/cannabis/cannabis_recipe2.shtml\">2</a>, <A href=\"http://www.erowid.org/plants/cannabis/cannabis_recipe1.shtml\">3</a>, <a href=\"http://www.erowid.org/plants/cannabis/cannabis_recipe4.shtml\">4</a>, <A href=\"http://www.erowid.org/plants/cannabis/cannabis_recipe3.shtml\">5</a>, <A href=\"http://www.erowid.org/plants/cannabis/cannabis_recipe5.shtml\">6</a>, and a very interesting <A href=\"http://www.erowid.org/plants/cannabis/cannabis_recipe6.shtml\">7</a>. fun with oil/alcohol soluable <A href=\"http://www.erowid.org/plants/cannabis/references/journal/1975_munson_nci_1/1975_munson_nci_1_text.shtml\">cannabinoids</a>...</tt>',1,180,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19817,3192,'2003-03-13 02:22:32',241,'linus van pelt','<p>this is the best piece i\\\'ve ever read by you. blew me away.</p>',0,69,0,0,'64.230.96.81','en'),(19818,3192,'2003-03-13 02:43:16',66,'Peter','<br><tt>sex is the color of being in love</tt>',1,46,0,0,'66.108.185.72','en'),(19819,3282,'2003-03-13 03:58:58',370,'seanthecon','this was fucking incredible and left me wanting more. not to take anything away from the short but strong impact of this, but i think this definantly could be turned into something bigger. good to see you back linus.',6,216,0,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(19820,3282,'2003-03-13 04:00:13',370,'charlie braunvuesne','stop diddling my sister herr linus or i will be forced to sick the peppernator on you.',6,86,0,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(19821,3281,'2003-03-13 10:59:14',370,'seanthecon','i don\\\'t think there\\\'s anyway n.korea would attack s. korean. any s.korean/u.s. alliance in a war against n. korea would end in ultimate destruction for n.korea. they wouldn\\\'t stand a chance. and you can bet for sure that china wouldn\\\'t be coming in to save their asses like the first korean war. russia would dwiddle its thumbs while japan would probably get in there and fuck around too. n. korea at best could fuck up s.korea, but its not worth the devestating consequences. the country is so damn broke that there is no way it could stand an intensified military campaign. no, n. korea isn\\\'t that dumb. in fact, i think that maniac kim knows exactly what he\\\'s doing. just stirring the pot of chickenheads. the rooster says cock-a-doddle-do.',6,761,0,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(19822,3281,'2003-03-13 11:03:29',370,'seanthecon','a great piece dave. and it is a excellecnt extro to your year. if anything, i find it most amazing how alcohol fueld and crime free the place is. fucked up. but i think you\\\'re right dave. the u.s. needs to get out. it definantly isn\\\'t helping. but i think it\\\'s too scared that japan or china will move in and become the dominant power in the area without a strong u.s. presence. i think you were right a few years back when you said that china will be the next superpower threat. they are growing with imense speed.',6,524,0,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(19823,3281,'2003-03-13 16:49:56',66,'Peter','<br><tt><A href=\\\"http://www.socialistworker.org/2003-1/444/444_07_NKorea.shtml\\\">more reading...</a></tt>',1,110,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19824,3284,'2003-03-14 01:31:18',195,'matt','is there a boot camp, chapter one?',1,34,0,0,'209.208.110.55','en'),(19825,3284,'2003-03-14 03:44:32',75,'tyler','yep. maybe somebody who knows how could put them in a series--i don\\\'t know how to do it exactly. ',1,100,0,0,'165.247.165.68','en'),(19826,3284,'2003-03-14 14:10:52',66,'Peter','<br><tt>done...</tt>',1,20,75,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19827,3260,'2003-03-14 21:03:17',175,'Paul G','There will be mistakes, this needs to be written quickly, but:<br>\r\nIf the Right of the political spectrum - in contemporary American politics - can be said to place more value on <b>social order</b>; and the Left - in contemporary American politics - can be said to place more value upon <b>social freedom</b>; then it ought not to be surprising that the \\\"Left\\\" is less well organized than the Right.\r\n<p>\r\nTo the degree that the Left becomes a highly ordered, hierarchic, monolithic institution, it resembles the Right.  And yet, as wb indicated, lacking those qualities its political progress is frustrated.  It\\\'s like a paradox.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhat do members of labor unions have in common with people in graduate school?  What do people who identify with the \\\"Old South\\\" have in common with young, urban, homosexuals?  Well, they\\\'re all important elements of the \\\"Big Tent\\\" coalition: the Democratic Party.  Imagine those 4 people arguing over who\\\'s going to drive 1 car.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe dialogue that preceded this comment was among the most edifying things here; it was really a pleasure to read; I\\\'d just push for the windmills to be placed in the area from Kansas to South Dakota - not Montana.\r\n</p> ',1,1238,0,0,'207.221.71.254','en'),(19828,3286,'2003-03-14 23:14:23',86,'egg','\\\"girls in white dresses with blue satin sashes/snowflakes that stay on my nose and eyelashes\\\". the way that \\\"sashes\\\" and \\\"eyelashes\\\" rhyme, the way that show tunes give me ironic and sincere glee, the way that I like to eat something sweet in the afternoon, the way i like to videotape dusky elk in the fog while im asleep, yeah. thats how I like this piece.',0,376,0,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(19829,3286,'2003-03-15 00:27:53',195,'matt','you have a remarkable dream-memory to remember all those details.  i usually forget my dreams five minutes after i wake up.',1,123,0,0,'209.208.111.1','en'),(19830,3286,'2003-03-15 00:33:33',82,'simon','it happens rather rarely but when it does...hoo boy. plus i\\\'m good with the details. especially when fashions and food are involved.',0,135,195,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19831,3276,'2003-03-15 01:37:07',370,'seanthecon','this was very strange and i\\\'m not sure if this is real, but i liked it very much anyways. is this real? if not, it\\\'s not far fetched to beleive that someone would write this type of letter from any country or any situation. it\\\'s funny how often atheists or non-belivers turn to god when they are desperate. i also thought it was an interesting contradiction that this was done for money, a whole shit load of money, but still for an honourable reason. thanks for the interesting piece.',6,494,0,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(19832,3286,'2003-03-15 04:58:21',175,'Paul G','Your style is open and friendly - anyone reading this would feel as though they knew you.  But which do you really prefer: dome or towers?',1,138,0,0,'67.195.73.219','en'),(19833,3276,'2003-03-15 05:07:01',239,'Poonlarp','of course this is real. i need someone to help me move my shitload of cash out of nigeria so that i can carry on my late husband\'s vaguely pointless wishes. im glad you found it interesting. your entertainment is, indeed, why i posted this here!',1,105,370,0,'66.108.185.72','en'),(19834,3275,'2003-03-15 16:45:30',183,'lin','<p>This is somewhat off topic, but I recently learned the tale of Lilith, (which explains lilith fair..) and I was wondering if you know it?<br>\r\nFirst wife of Adam, a defiant sort of firebrand who leaves when Adam won\\\'t let her be on top, or so it was explained to me.. just seemed pertinent in relation to your title. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> Nice piece. </p> ',0,352,370,0,'64.230.96.81','en'),(19835,3275,'2003-03-15 16:57:57',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\naccording to my roommate john. Lotusland is a kiddy porn site on the net. how he knows this i have no idea. He is a bit twisted. He talks about banging 15year olds in Cambodia. And to think. Im just going there to blow up a cow. Is it the land of OZ? \r\n</p>\r\n',1,264,0,0,'61.40.66.77','en'),(19836,3286,'2003-03-15 23:44:19',65,'le','i love dreams. i love simon. best combo!',4,40,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(19837,3284,'2003-03-16 19:05:32',195,'matt','peter, you\\\'re the editor\\\'s editor. :D',1,43,0,0,'209.208.109.138','en'),(19838,2965,'2003-03-16 20:01:44',148,'jeeff','simon: 2\r\norganized religion: 0\r\n\r\n(why 2 you ask?  don\\\'t forget about the humping of the virgin mary)',4,105,0,0,'24.156.25.230','en'),(19839,3286,'2003-03-16 21:51:17',142,'oof','this was great. i want to see movies of your dreams.',1,52,0,0,'128.148.188.50','en'),(19840,3286,'2003-03-17 01:30:05',82,'simon','the possible meanings of the above question have me smiling. i guess i prefer domes mostly. towers sometimes. there\\\'s something very inviting about a dome.',1,158,175,0,'12.236.164.112','en'),(19841,3287,'2003-03-17 01:33:39',82,'simon','today was a beautiful day in san francisco. i walked up and down polk street and thought about girls. i have one of those cameras too. i love it. they can be a little light-leaky but that\\\'s nothing a bit of tape wont fix.',1,224,0,0,'12.236.164.112','en'),(19842,2965,'2003-03-17 01:34:56',82,'simon','wait what now?',0,14,0,0,'12.236.164.112','en'),(19843,3287,'2003-03-17 04:58:42',66,'Peter','<br><tt>mmm, quadcam love. sources say springtime is beginning beautifully on the east coast, also.</tt>',1,104,82,0,'66.108.185.72','en'),(19844,3287,'2003-03-17 06:58:10',93,'chiamattt','I like to sing when i wake up. I sing songs I have no idea why I know. You know its strange when you wake up singing o/~ you picked a fine time to leave me lucelle. four hungry children and a crop in the field o/~',1,213,0,1,'218.145.25.111','en'),(19845,3287,'2003-03-17 06:58:10',93,'chiamattt','I like to sing when i wake up. I sing songs I have no idea why I know. You know its strange when you wake up singing o/~ you picked a fine time to leave me lucelle. four hungry children and a crop in the field o/~',1,213,0,1,'218.145.25.111','en'),(19846,3287,'2003-03-17 06:58:11',93,'chiamattt','I like to sing when i wake up. I sing songs I have no idea why I know. You know its strange when you wake up singing o/~ you picked a fine time to leave me lucelle. four hungry children and a crop in the field o/~',1,213,0,0,'218.145.25.47','en'),(19847,3275,'2003-03-17 07:50:11',370,'seanthecon','word on the street is that after leaving adam, she became a baby-killing demoness who seduced sleeping men. by street i mean ancient hebrew mythology. the street i hang out on has quite a number of synagogues.',6,209,183,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(19848,3275,'2003-03-17 07:53:40',370,'seanthecon','vancouver is often called lotusland. in greek mythology, the fruit from the lotus tree would make strangers forget their native land or lose all desire to return to it once eaten. but i guess the greeks were also into banging boys, so maybe your roommate is onto something.',6,273,93,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(19849,3268,'2003-03-17 15:13:09',156,'lotus','maybe because i wear glasses and have dealt with weird insurance stuff...i liked this article.',2,94,0,0,'64.230.108.44','en'),(19850,3288,'2003-03-17 16:24:06',93,'chiamattt','dont forget the cries of those seperatist snail munchers in quebec my dear st sean. ',6,84,0,0,'61.40.66.78','en'),(19851,3268,'2003-03-17 17:26:19',66,'Peter','<Br><tt>im going in to get my eyes checked and pick up some stylin glasses in about two weeks. i will now be certain that all my insurance shiz is in order...</tt>',1,163,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19852,3288,'2003-03-17 19:40:17',370,'jeanlecon','\r\nmais oui mon frere. i did not forget. i mention right hafter koreans. vive le quebec libre.',6,93,93,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(19853,3287,'2003-03-17 19:49:13',370,'seanthecon','for some reason this reminded me of a necro song. or at least the beginning of one. it\\\'s called std. anyway, the beginning necro croons something like, \\\'i was walking, in a park, it was beautiful, so very beautiful\\\' and then shit hits the fan as necro snaps and loses it on this std by taking a wrench to its head. its a fucking good song.',6,348,0,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(19854,1005,'2003-03-18 00:08:30',472,'GildtheLily','Does anyone know Levi Glassrock? If so please email me at hotmail.com I really need to speak to him, thank you!!!',0,126,0,0,'163.179.150.143','en'),(19855,1005,'2003-03-18 01:56:13',239,'Poonlarp','',6,0,472,1,'128.59.236.182','en'),(19856,1005,'2003-03-18 01:56:28',239,'Poonlarp','yeah, we all know him. sure, we\\\'ll all email you.',0,52,472,0,'128.59.236.182','en'),(19857,1005,'2003-03-18 02:10:47',17,'austin','haha',2,4,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19858,3285,'2003-03-18 07:57:07',370,'seanthecon','i think of adam myself quite a bit these days. he is a constant inspiration in my life. i think about his wonderful photos. i think about the last time he tried to take photos of me and how my body refused its reception due to the self-loathing i felt. ',6,253,0,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(19859,3288,'2003-03-18 11:06:25',370,'jeanlecon','i spent the day pretending i was jeanlecon. a famous parisian con artist who pick pocketed tourist under the eiffel tower and made passionate love to women under the stars. ',6,173,0,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(19860,1005,'2003-03-18 16:33:30',74,'jo','i haven\\\'t seen him on the drag for almost a year, yo. i actually wondered if he even lived in austin anymore. maybe he ran through all the people who would possibly buy his stuff. good luck.',1,193,472,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(19861,1005,'2003-03-18 16:37:11',74,'jo','OH MY GOD. i can\\\'t believe that standing.ontheoutside.com now redirects to a CAMGIRL SITE. jesus!',4,100,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(19862,3289,'2003-03-18 17:57:08',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i have a job that finds me at one of the interfaces of biology and technology; my work really fulfills my tech/geek/scientist side, yet lately ive been feeling profoundly unfulfilled concerning the creative side of things. such analytic work can force you to use your left-brain more than you might like, so keep that in mind, too.</tt>',1,319,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19863,3291,'2003-03-19 02:57:41',74,'jo','i really liked this. really a lot. it reminds me of graham greene\\\'s writing for some reason.',1,95,0,0,'64.217.80.227','en'),(19864,3285,'2003-03-19 05:06:57',123,'dcc','<p>the streetcar is an unusual place sometimes. the one i take has distressing regularity in it\\\'s passengers. i miss the queen car now, it was always more interesting.\n</p>\n<p>i, like you, do a lot of back-of-the-car watching and i do wonder what it would be like to ride the rail from start to finish for several runs.</p>',2,326,0,0,'64.26.176.107','en'),(19865,3289,'2003-03-19 16:39:52',122,'hool','<p>dcc send</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i did the comp sci degree. i liked it, but i also felt the lack of human touch later in the later years of the degree.  that\\\'s why i chose to write my undergrad thesis with the HCI (human computer interaction) department - it was run under the Psych department.  I researched and created an experiment, ran it with subjects and watched them perform the tasks on a computer.  i interviewed them afterward, talked to them about their interaction.  everyone\\\'s approach to solving a simple task with a simple toolset was so different. it was very satisfying to try to make an intuitive tool that people could learn and adapt to their own method easily. far more satisfying than the almost 3 years of abstract tech work i\\\'d already done up to that point, and also more satisfying than any of the straight comp sci things i\\\'d learned.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>U of T has a really good HCI program, you should look into it. i\\\'ve considered doing a masters there, but i\\\'ve still had enough of school for now.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>p.s. i don\\\'t think anyone really loves databases.  the only thing i find more distasteful than databases is testing frameworks, or office small talk.</p>',0,1192,0,0,'66.185.85.80','en'),(19866,3289,'2003-03-19 20:59:57',183,'lin','<p> Being in a visual arts program, I can represent the creative aspects, I suppose, with some inside knowledge about what goes on. I warn you, there can be a lot of crap to deal with on either side of the line, if there is a line really..<br> Often, during my last three years I have missed the depths to which I could have gone learning fascinating things about technology, but one thing is becoming increasingly clear. School is a resource like any other, a means of inputting things into your mind. <br> I made a conscious choice to steer clear of technology while I\\\'m in university. No0t for lack of interest, but because the atmosphere I create work in leave me feeling that I can be more productive in learning with a more hands on approach, to art creation and to various other things. So I\\\'m not in intermedia, I\\\'m in drawing. <p/> \r\n<p>',0,855,0,0,'64.230.96.81','en'),(19867,3289,'2003-03-19 21:02:08',183,'lin','<p> Before you head back to school, maybe you could benefit from reading a bit about semiotics. It can be a really interesting way to approach the questions you\\\'ve reaised. Also, good for you. </p>',0,200,0,0,'64.230.96.81','en'),(19868,3289,'2003-03-20 04:35:59',123,'dcc','wow, fantatic folks! all insight that i find very wonderful. strangely enough, i am planning to go into cognitive science, as the title talks to. it is, from what i can tell, a lot of \\\'well-rounded\\\' learning. HCI is also an area i\\\'m interested in, so i\\\'ll study that too. semiotics, well, that\\\'s one of the core subjects to learn in what i want to begin to cram my brain full of soon. perception is an amazing thing, especially how we percieve language and communicate ideas. and that\\\'s just the thing, we do so in such creative ways. expression in any form, visual, audial(?!), textual ...',0,608,0,0,'64.26.176.107','en'),(19869,3290,'2003-03-20 06:02:25',93,'chiamattt','lets just hope George Sr doesnt have any other sons. Perhaps Politicians should only be alowed to have daughters. ',1,114,0,0,'218.145.25.47','en'),(19870,3294,'2003-03-20 16:25:55',180,'auntie','mark phoned this morning from the northern kuwaiti border.  said he looked up and saw a SCUD missile over his head.  was confused, because hans blix said they don\\\'t have any..',0,178,0,0,'24.92.161.252','en'),(19871,3294,'2003-03-20 17:17:28',93,'chiamattt','hans blix never said they dont have any scud missiles. Iraq was destroying them just days ago. what hans blix said was that iraqi leader saddam probably would not use chemical or wmd systems to retaliate a coalition attack. Im not sure I agree with good ol hanz. Idealism can only go so far these days. especially within the United Nations. ',1,341,0,0,'61.40.66.67','en'),(19872,3294,'2003-03-20 17:23:31',93,'chiamattt','OH YEAH! \\\"OPERATION IRAQ FREEDOM\\\"?????\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nwhat the hell does that mean? it is lame. it is so dubya! I\\\'ll bet he had a temper tantrom to get that operation name. fucker nuts. \r\n<br> ',5,200,0,0,'61.40.66.67','en'),(19873,3294,'2003-03-20 17:44:39',87,'bmg','<p>Operation Infinite Justice, Enduring Freedom... It is as if they just go off of this list of <a href=\"http://www.geocities.com/tufelhundin/ldrtraits.htm\">Leadership Traits</a>.</p>\r\n<p>I noticed that the reports from CNN seemed to be full of fluff, circumstance and speculation as the events unfolded. Kinda like in middle school when rumors and gossip float around, gets bigger and bigger each time it is passed around. I must admit, I was quite impressed with the theme music so far. Seems as though this is much better prepared, having the reporters right there with the troops (sitting in lawn chairs!). I can\'t wait to see the General Franks War Reporting room! Too bad Patton isn\'t around to give Franks a good ol\' slap - show him how a general is supposed to act...</p',0,808,0,0,'147.80.216.86','en'),(19874,3294,'2003-03-20 18:21:53',74,'jo','chia, i\\\'m a big fan of your spastic outburst ranty posts.',1,60,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(19875,3293,'2003-03-20 18:24:54',74,'jo','DUDE. these are so good.',1,24,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(19876,3294,'2003-03-20 19:52:16',175,'Paul G','<i>\\\"Go on America, go forth and free them from their chains!!! God Blell you all.\\\"</i>\r\n<p>\r\nGod Blell you too, young chia.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBut Lo!  Now taketh unto thyself a wife, or husband, and cleave unto their domes and towers.\r\n</p>\r\n',1,242,93,0,'205.185.5.145','en'),(19877,3294,'2003-03-21 01:02:01',17,'austin','OPERATION CHIA\\\'S PHATPANTS',3,29,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19878,3294,'2003-03-21 01:05:32',17,'austin','i have contemplated the audibility of the C in Darth\\\'s CNN on many occasions.  did they just cut it off because it took to long?  did he really decide the pronounciation sounded better without the C?  <i>no comprende</I>.  ',4,226,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19879,3289,'2003-03-21 02:54:27',156,'lotus','hello. who are you? i am going back to school this september after realising i cannot spend another year, let alone possibly the rest of my life, programming. i am taking HCI.',1,175,0,0,'64.230.106.183','en'),(19880,3294,'2003-03-21 03:43:13',123,'dcc','operation iraqi french fries!\n\nas much as i hate this war, i\\\'m more scared of the media power than the war itself. warping the minds of millions is like shooting fish in a barrel these days, it seems. dub-yuh\\\'s popularity rating has skyrocketed in the past 2 days!',2,270,93,0,'64.26.176.107','en'),(19881,3294,'2003-03-21 08:25:08',65,'le','<br><font size=\\\"+10\\\">&hearts;</font>',4,42,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(19882,3294,'2003-03-21 14:01:34',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i hate not being able to see cartoons or mindless sitcoms on tv when <i>every fucking station</i> is playing the same rehash of the same vague views about this war</i>. i mean, i see more than enough about it already in the hours i spend reading the news online; im already tired of seeing it everywhere i go. surely im not the only one that wants a break.<br><Br>curiously, at least here in nyc, upn and fox are broadcasting the <i>exact</i> same broadcast, four stations apart. and they keep showing the same clips. i saw night-vision shots of a missle barrage at 8, and at midnight, they were still on. its frustrating, among other things...</tt>',1,572,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19883,3294,'2003-03-21 17:56:21',96,'dj1luna','NBC had friends one and their usual line up.',1,44,0,0,'207.8.101.10','en'),(19884,3294,'2003-03-21 18:26:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i think that, here in nyc, were getting alot of pre-emptive news riding over most shows due to the general tendency for new yorkers to embrace post WTC paranoia. ack.</tt> ',1,180,96,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19885,3294,'2003-03-22 02:57:01',59,'s5','all the military rhetoric reminds of me of china\\\'s \\\"people liberation army\\\" that marched into tibet and \\\"liberated\\\" the tibetans.',0,144,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19886,3294,'2003-03-22 02:59:28',59,'s5','also, a rant: right now, i am completely sick of the news helicopters that have been circling overhead for the last two days, day in and day out. they fly in so low that the windows rattle, and they don\\\'t stop, not even in the middle of the night. AARRRGGHHHHH!!!! attention news: go fuck yourself. !!!!!! okay i\\\'m done.',0,326,0,0,'64.81.243.3','en'),(19887,3296,'2003-03-22 08:00:27',75,'tyler','so who is the jerk? and which of you is sweaty?',1,47,0,0,'165.247.166.13','en'),(19888,3294,'2003-03-23 02:24:28',240,'xep','are you sure they\\\'re news helicopters?  the ones rattling my windows say CHP on them.',0,88,59,0,'64.81.241.248','en'),(19889,3294,'2003-03-23 04:12:55',66,'Peter','<br><tt>the aircraft rattling my windows are moving too fast to really see; but, if i could see them, they\\\'d say <i>USAF</i>. its scary. last night, we were listening to the pretty thunder form the spring rains interspersed with the booming roar of the passing jets, scrambled to protect our city from \\\"evil\\\" and \\\"terrorism\\\" and \\\"war\\\". i found myself wondering if theyre the same sorts of planes that are dropping bombs on iraq.<br><br>i just got back from biking around my city; im sitting here in my basement listening to the jets. i wonder if there is a 25 year old iraqi guy, sitting in his flat with his girlfriend, after biking around his city, listening to the roar of the same planes. i offer infinite prayers for this guy.</tt>',1,757,240,0,'66.108.184.158','en'),(19890,3152,'2003-03-23 18:09:47',195,'matt','was there something else in the body of this post originally? i think there was. something about \\\"a david lynch moment\\\" or something.',1,139,0,0,'209.208.109.98','en'),(19891,3289,'2003-03-24 07:01:43',123,'dcc','i\\\'m just a toronto boy realizing that the career i chose a few years back is slowly driving me completely and utterly insane. i remember a time when i loved programming. those days seem ever so far away now though. alas, i shall make the best of it. utoronto will soon be my second home, rather than my desk in the corner of the office i work in.',0,349,156,0,'64.26.176.107','en'),(19892,3299,'2003-03-25 17:36:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt>time is magic; just wait, and the epiphany will eventually reveal itself.</tt>',1,86,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19893,3298,'2003-03-25 18:14:32',74,'jo','stefan, you rule. i\\\'ve been so frustrated lately because the liberals and protesters as represented by the media are all like, \\\"um, <i>fumble</i>, NO DEATH!\\\" and while that\\\'s <b>valid</b>, no one wants innocent people to die, there are some other points to consider here&#151;not just the repurcussions of war, but why we\\\'re really there in the first place. also, i\\\'d like to take a bat to the skulls of protesters who\\\'re looting or destroying things during protests. the end.',1,497,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(19894,3299,'2003-03-25 20:47:25',124,'gus','sometimes you have to stop the eternal soul wrestling and just enjoy the present.',0,81,0,0,'65.84.1.77','en'),(19895,3299,'2003-03-25 20:56:40',175,'Paul G','Hey Peter!<br>\r\nIt\\\'s all about the <a href=\\\"http://www.madonnashots.com/81c12.jpg\\\">eternal succubus</a>: coffee.<br>\r\nOne of my sisters gave me a <a href=\\\"http://www.bialetti.it/caffe_eng.html\\\">Bialetti</a>, and it changed my life.<br>\r\nOne pot isn\\\'t enough.',1,276,66,0,'207.221.71.111','en'),(19896,3299,'2003-03-25 21:47:34',175,'Paul G','Yep.  You too - as well as I can pretend to know - understand the feeling of the years passing, while existing in an increasingly large, and increasingly desperate, population of working people in America.\r\n<p>\r\nI don\\\'t need to type out all the implications of the changes that are taking place in the world, now.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI miss your writing, and your presence here.  So, whether you meant to offer support, or a critique, thanks.  Take care of yourself, Paul.\r\n</p>',1,473,124,0,'206.217.112.163','en'),(19897,3297,'2003-03-25 23:03:28',82,'simon','i know the feeling, dave. it sucks. ',1,36,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19898,3299,'2003-03-26 00:09:29',239,'Poonlarp','no, its all about the <i>crack cocaine!</i>',6,43,175,0,'66.108.184.158','en'),(19899,3297,'2003-03-26 00:16:26',370,'seanthecon','shitty state. not much else feels worse. stay cool brother, you got many in your corner.',6,88,0,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(19900,3297,'2003-03-26 01:39:37',241,'linus van pelt','<p>awful feeling. eventually the pain will go away but then you\\\'ll realize that that\\\'s an awful feeling as well. stay cool.</p>  ',0,135,0,0,'64.230.98.204','en'),(19901,3299,'2003-03-26 04:24:34',75,'tyler','i feel the same way sometimes. i have been wrestling with some of the same questions lately, about death and what it means. a lot of art is about death, especially the stuff i\\\'m reading in my english classes right now. it makes me almost have panic attacks sometimes. the real kicker is when you really realize that all art and writing is done by people in the same position as you--there are no messages, as far as i can tell, from on high. some music seems transcendent, but we can see it that way only because it is in fact ephemeral. sometimes i think that having children is the ultimate act of evil, to tie another sentient creature onto this whirling machine only to be crushed at the end...still...life is good and the sun is hot, according to virginia woolf. there\\\'s always heroin if it just gets too bad. i personally plan to make a whole bunch of money, buy a boat, and sail around the world until something kills me. ',1,935,0,0,'165.247.166.13','en'),(19902,3299,'2003-03-26 04:31:37',239,'Poonlarp','thats basically what l. ron hubbard did, you know.',6,50,75,0,'66.108.184.158','en'),(19903,3298,'2003-03-26 04:33:42',75,'tyler','so if there\\\'s no good reason, why are we having the war? why would one of the two political parties in the US risk permanent damage to its viability in the future for, as you say, a negligable amount of oil? i\\\'m not agreeing or disagreeing with you, just adding a question. how did they manage to convince both houses of Congress to fund it, if it\\\'s so obviously wrong? they aren\\\'t stupid people, nor powerless--this is not a dictatorship we are living in. these are the questions that prevent me from taking a strong anti-war stance.',1,546,0,0,'165.247.166.13','en'),(19904,3298,'2003-03-26 04:34:27',75,'tyler','\\\'they\\\' meaning the members of Congress.',1,45,0,0,'165.247.166.13','en'),(19905,3299,'2003-03-26 05:10:43',75,'tyler','oh wow! i\\\'m fascinated by LRH and Scientology. funny you bring it up. not fascinated like i believe it, of course. i read all about his little navy and everything. amazing! that isn\\\'t quite what i have in mind-- I want to sail to the polar regions, and around Cape Horn, and across the Pacific, etc. just to see it all. ',0,326,239,0,'165.247.166.13','en'),(19906,3297,'2003-03-26 17:03:28',75,'tyler','time to get out the old snoop dogg cds.',1,39,0,0,'165.247.165.60','en'),(19907,3299,'2003-03-26 18:03:16',122,'hool','<p>we build houses to insulate ourselves from the physical nature we cannot control.  we accumulate worldly things as an act of insulation against the cold void of our mental nature, which we also cannot control.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>don\\\'t weather the storm; embrace it. embrace the overt pointlessness of life. i do. i think it\\\'s great now that i can hug it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>rhetorical: what if you were told what the meaning actually was, knew it to be true, and you didn\\\'t like it?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>why should sadness always be a cause for distress? it seems that if we are at any point unhappy or unsure in life, then we are failing, we are doing something wrong.  i disagree.  i don\\\'t mind being sad or unsure; i realize it means that i am living a full life.  now that i\\\'ve graduated from university, i am (as i\\\'m sure the majority of people in my position are) more unsure than ever.  but i came to see that that was the point of school, not so much to grow yourself, but to exponentially grow your perception of the world, to feel ever shrinking, to rediscover the world as an infinitely large expanse.  it\\\'s exactly like becoming a child again, if you dry your eyes and look at it thoughtfully</p>\r\n\r\n<p>paul, you\\\'re headed in the right direction.  you\\\'ve got a camera now, and you\\\'ve always had the eyes.  i don\\\'t look for meaning with my camera, i make it with each new photo.  the meaning dies after i\\\'m done making the photo, the meaning is in the process and not the product.  so i walk out the door again, and make another meaning, out of nothing, living as a microcosmic god.</p>',0,1607,175,0,'66.185.85.80','en'),(19908,3299,'2003-03-26 18:58:15',67,'trouble','i can\\\'t believe <i>me of all people</i> is saying this but - \\\'keep the faith\\\' paul. *nice supportive*',1,110,175,0,'66.32.73.111','en'),(19909,3289,'2003-03-27 04:07:40',122,'hool','lotus! stop disappearing on me! send me an email - let\\\'s talk about HCI - hool @ inconduit.com',0,97,156,0,'66.185.85.80','en'),(19910,3298,'2003-03-27 15:34:44',262,'szajic','<p>Well, if we\\\'ve ruled out all of the good reasons for this war, then we must be fighting it for bad ones. I have no actual knowledge of the motives of the current U.S. administration, but I\\\'d speculate that the real reasons for war include:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>1. Politics.  In a time of war, the public (in particular, a public informed solely by the pro-war media) will rally around its leader.  You ask why the ruling party would risk its reputation with war - clearly, it doesn\\\'t think it\\\'s risking it.  In fact, it probably thinks its bolstering its support among Americans, and it might be right.  All of this is helped by the post-9/11 mindset of fear that the administration has done its best to instill in the populace.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>2. Personal/corporate gain.  Whenever there\\\'s money at play, look out.  The U.S. Army has already awarded contracts in post-war Iraq to a Halliburton subsidiary (remember, until 2000, Dick Cheney ran Halliburton).  Those contracts were  awarded <em>without bidding</em>.  On a global scale, the amount of money and oil to be extracted from a conquered Iraq isn\\\'t much.  But on an individual scale, it\\\'s enough to make those in favor with the administration very, very rich.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>3.  Obsession?  Fanaticism?  Twisted Freudian Oedipal-related compulsion?  I don\\\'t know the root cause, but President Bush has been deadset on attacking Iraq - by no means any real threat to our country - even since he got into office, even before Sept. 11, 2001.</p>\r\n\r\nI wish I knew the real reaons, but I can only guess.  I still don\\\'t think there are any <em>good reasons</em> - I think American troops and Iraqi civilians are dying without good cause.',3,1698,75,0,'18.56.1.250','en'),(19911,3299,'2003-03-27 23:11:32',234,'etienne','paul, as much as committing suicide of the e-self may seem a good idea at times, i urge you - and anyone reading this - not to.  an old friend committed suicide last week.  i\\\'ve spent the days since questioning why, both to myself and mutual friends.  the grief has been overwhelming, and it is only today as i return to my normal routine and workplace that i can start to try and put into motion some of the things i have realised and thought about as a result of this.  an end to any (or all) parts of yourself affects more than you might realise.  stacy is gone, but its what we do with the knowledge of this grief and her life that makes the difference now.',1,664,175,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(19912,3299,'2003-03-28 00:27:40',175,'Paul G','The theme of distress - because of the apparent randomness of being - is a familiar one: it\\\'s in Melville, and Shakespeare, and Nietzsche, and the writings of the faiths of Abraham, and it\\\'s in the teachings of the Buddha, and in many other places as well.\r\n<p>\r\nThere are some longstanding questions in human discourse.  And the biggest one seems to be: \\\"WTF?\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe ideas about the proper response to the, \\\"WTF?\\\" problem are numerous.  And people are as open to discussion about their own chosen response as they are open to discussion about the current war, or abortion.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBut I\\\'m under the impression that the schism within the U.S.A. over the current war is in some way related to my own personal struggle, and the struggle of other people who are similar to myself.  If this bit of writing had simply been a, \\\"Boo,hoo,hoo - poor me!\\\" expression, I never would have submitted it here.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI have been made extremely uncomfortable by the President\\\'s use of God in his political rhetoric.  I mean: I want to vomit.  And, again, it points towards the tension between teachings of the world\\\'s major faiths and philosophies, and the economic system that underpins our regime.  What does an ambitious millionaire who sends a nation to war have to do with Jesus?  I would be hard pressed to imagine two characters more contrary to one another.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'m a big boy!  I\\\'m not a starry-eyed child.  I\\\'m prepared to discuss necessity and expediency.  Yep, I know, \\\"how the world works.\\\"  It just seems to be becoming more and more difficult to do what needs to be done without compromising a set of values that the great bulk of humanity purports to hold dear.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAnd so I\\\'m frustrated, and confused.  No, I don\\\'t feel that this is unprecedented: I remember Hamlet and the others.  And I know that I\\\'m not alone: many other people are struggling with these things - especially now.  Writing seems better than mortified silence.  It\\\'s a good first step towards finding a better way to be - finding a new epiphany.   \r\n</p>',0,2118,0,0,'207.221.71.113','en'),(19913,3299,'2003-03-28 00:43:31',175,'Paul G','I am sorry.  I have been where you are, and the feeling was not good.  I am at a loss for any real understanding about why some things happen; sometimes they just do.  But I am all the more aware of how fragile and precious life is.  I do not meant to despair, or to cause you to despair.  Communicating the issues in a thoughtful way seems to be a far better thing than internalizing the suffering - and thereby also causing people with similar feelings to remain alone - isolated.  Much of what was written above about hardships simply being a part of the human experience rings true; but that doesn\\\'t stop any of it from hurting any less.  Feel free to write.  And as heavy as things seem now, in time they will distant.',1,726,234,0,'207.221.71.113','en'),(19914,3298,'2003-03-29 00:02:52',175,'Paul G','<i>\\\"this is not a dictatorship we are living in\\\"</i>\r\n<p>\r\nIn theory, we live in a republic.  We are supposed to have a government that is limited.  The powers of our government are meant to be defined by our Constitution.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe President dervies his powers from Article II of the Constitution.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBut lately he\\\'s just been making shit up.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAt a press conference this week, he described his, \\\"most important job,\\\" as, \\\"providing for the security of the American people.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNow that\\\'s sort of \\\"Constitutional sounding,\\\" inasmuch as he used the word, \\\"providing.\\\"  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBut if you whip out your copy of the Constitution, you\\\'ll see nothing like that in Article II, or anywhere else.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe President also described this war of his as a, \\\"global war,\\\" this week.  Well, what\\\'s up with that?  I don\\\'t remember Congress declaring war on the world.  Already, today, numerous references to Syria have been made in regard to their suspected role in, \\\"terror.\\\"  All appearances are that they will be next on the list, after Iraq.  And where after Syria?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe logic is as follows:\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>something blew up in NY\r\n<li>Saddam has things that blow up\r\n<li>Saddam blows up the things in NY\r\n</ul>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>wood floats\r\n<li>ducks float\r\n<li>ducks are made of wood\r\n</ul>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFor some reason, we\\\'re willing to mobilize hundreds of thousands of men and women, and spend tens of billions of dollars, to go abroad and crush/kill/destroy in the name of God and the the Constitution, while tolerating poverty, crime, and disease at home.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn an economic sense, things are not good here.  And they are very likely to get worse.  It is difficult to believe that alienating the major players in the international community - outside of the Commonwealth - will not affect our trade.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt all confuses the Hell out me.  \r\n</p>',4,1963,75,0,'207.220.190.140','en'),(19915,3298,'2003-03-29 04:26:49',427,'hopper','<p>Notice the vote by congress to authorize force in Iraq was called shortly before the election (one with major stakes in both houses of congress) and that while the term \\\"the third rail of politics\\\" is much overused, voting against such a bill creates the opportunity for some very distorted and very nasty 30 second attack ads (etc), so it just might qualify. No one wants to touch this.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>While congress is meant to act as a check on the executive branch, in the present political climate (read: \\\"Post 9/11\\\") it has been seen as a politically dangerous act to do anything that could stand in the way of homeland security - no matter how questionable that thing may be.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Does the war stop in Iraq, or is this train just getting started? Iran? Syria? Who\\\'s next? Some people are already counting on the \\\"next war\\\" - the question is whether or not the sane majority [will||can] speak loudly enough to stop them.</p>\r\n',3,956,75,0,'12.222.65.69','en'),(19916,3298,'2003-03-29 05:31:43',75,'tyler','well, it seems like a gimme that Bush and co. will be ousted next election...if all the people demonstrating can manage not to damage their cause too much and get out the vote. i mean i\\\'m not a bush fan. i can\\\'t believe such an obvious retard got elected. i just hope all this dissent can be channeled and focused against the administration. god damn, i dont think i could handle it if...i cant even say it.',1,413,0,0,'66.245.48.238','en'),(19917,3302,'2003-03-29 23:03:01',195,'matt','so funny.',1,9,0,0,'209.208.108.32','en'),(19918,3297,'2003-03-30 08:33:34',185,'orion','kinda know how you feel, chiamatt...\r\nhang in there man, time heals all.\r\ni went through the worst breakup\r\nof my life (to date) last may; \r\nlong about december i was pretty\r\nmuch over it.  this spring i think\r\ni\\\'ve even thought about my ex two or three times in three months.\r\n\r\n/me lifts a beer in toast to rememberance of days past and the hope of days to come.',1,367,0,0,'66.68.96.2','en'),(19919,3298,'2003-03-30 18:32:13',262,'szajic','<p><em>well, it seems like a gimme that Bush and co. will be ousted next election</em></p>\r\n<p>\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.foxnews.com/story/0,2933,82005,00.html\\\">FOXNews.com</a>: \\\"About seven in 10 people said they support the war.\\\"  <a href=\\\"http://www.msnbc.com/news/892568.asp\\\">Newsweek</a>: \\\"As the war in Iraq enters its second week, President George W. Bushâ€™s approval rating rose to 68 percent.\\\"</p>',3,425,75,0,'63.214.123.217','en'),(19920,3299,'2003-03-30 18:57:30',187,'kandrer','these days i feel like making a kid; i feel like it\\\'s a good thing to do for my wife and me - maybe not for the world, maybe not for our kid himself. our kid, if he comes to life, will have to deal with the questions you guys mentionned, and this used to scare me enough to postpone until i get a reasonable answer (that will never come).<br>\r\nTyler made a good comment about making a kid; i think i have a partial answer, and it relates to other points in the above comments:<br>\r\nour questions, our suffering about the lack of meaning of life is due to the lack of coherence (not to say more) of the moral rules we are given as we grow up. these rules contain mistakes and lies, since they were designed mostly to make us behave in our society.<br>\r\nanyone clever enough (and openminded enough) will naturaly come to questionning these rules. of course, the mistakes and lies will easily be discovered. but since it\\\'s very hard to unlearn what we\\\'ve been told since we exist, this discovery will only give us pain; replace a lie by a lack.<br>\r\ni don\\\'t think i will be able to discover other rules for myself; rules that could give an answer or a meaning to everything around me. i gave up resisting, maybe. not making a kid was also a way to fight. i am giving up progressively, and it feels so good.\r\n(it seems that Gus and Hool gave similar ideas)<br>\r\ni felt bad so many times thinking about the lack of meaning of life. however, i don\\\'t give a damn about it when i am surrounded by loving friends, loving wife, and, shit, there is much enough space and love left for a loving kid! :) ',1,1606,175,0,'80.15.167.235','en'),(19921,3274,'2003-03-30 19:11:43',187,'kandrer','(test comment)',1,14,0,0,'80.15.167.235','en'),(19922,3274,'2003-03-30 19:13:25',187,'kandrer','commentaire en franÃ§ais (Ã Ã©Ã¯Ã´Ã¹)',1,31,0,0,'80.15.167.235','fr'),(19923,3298,'2003-03-31 01:06:51',75,'tyler','7/10 <i>voters</i>, though?',1,27,0,0,'165.247.165.58','en'),(19924,3298,'2003-03-31 02:40:11',20,'jim','probably closer to 7.5 in 10 voters.  conservatives vote in greater numbers than progressives.    ',6,98,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(19925,3302,'2003-03-31 05:36:46',449,'s','This is wonderful.',1,18,0,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(19926,3308,'2003-03-31 16:08:04',17,'austin','i\\\'ve been saying \\\'dude\\\' quite a bit instead of articulating my feelings for things.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nDUDE',2,108,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19927,3308,'2003-03-31 17:40:58',82,'simon','oh....my....god. DUDE',0,21,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19928,3274,'2003-03-31 18:52:30',187,'kandrer','&#12486;&#12473;&#12488;&#12290;&#35501;&#12415;&#12414;&#12377;&#12363;',1,72,0,0,'80.13.233.62','jp'),(19929,3308,'2003-03-31 19:29:54',234,'etienne','at least you got an ice-cream missy! :P \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',1,48,0,0,'203.96.111.237','en'),(19930,2770,'2003-04-01 02:02:10',228,'belly','dude. for pure hephalumpitis, try being a 5 foot 10 inch-tall woman living in japan! crikey, i\\\'m a freak here.',0,113,0,0,'210.224.190.60','en'),(19931,3307,'2003-04-01 02:40:26',83,'adamajah','Pasadena in tha HOUSE',1,21,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(19932,3307,'2003-04-01 04:27:34',241,'linus van pelt','<p>I\\\'d like to hear more about Carrie Fisher making out with Leonard Nimoy.</p> ',0,83,0,0,'64.230.174.24','en'),(19933,3307,'2003-04-01 05:22:32',449,'s','Shatner and Nimoy were on stage talking - they were doing some sort of quasi stand up routine. At some point, they said something about Carrie Fisher who was in a nearby room signing autographs. She decided to come on stage and say hi. She gave Billy a hug, and after he was kind enough to introduce Nimoy - \\\"Have you met my friend Leonard?\\\".... Commence nice long smooch, to the cheers and claps of delighted fanboys and girls. \r\n\r\nIt wasn\\\'t so much \\\"making out\\\" as \\\"fan pleasing smoochiness\\\".\r\n\r\n',2,519,241,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(19934,3298,'2003-04-01 09:44:11',477,'jehannum','firstly let me say how refreshing and encouraging it is to hear americans speaking out against george w bush and the war.\r\n\r\ni think michael moore said it best at the academy awards press conference, that the u.s. armed forces need to end their occupation of news rooms across america.\r\n\r\nto listen to cbs or nbc news half way across the world in new zealand, one gets the impression that all of america are salivating, war-loving bush-ites.\r\n\r\nbut we know different. :)\r\n\r\nas for bush\\\'s motivations? american politicians never feel safer than when the american people have a \\\"boggey-man\\\" to fear, so that they can \\\"save\\\" them and provide \\\"national security\\\".\r\n\r\ncommunism, al-qaeda, bin-laden, hussein, noriega, khruschev... who gives a damn as long as people stop asking questions about the economy, enron, iran contra, rigged elections and everything that actually matters to the lives of american citizens.',2,931,0,0,'203.109.254.59','en'),(19935,3307,'2003-04-01 10:28:25',189,'cyantist','i\\\'m jealous! ;-)',1,19,0,0,'24.221.170.119','en'),(19936,3307,'2003-04-01 21:19:32',175,'Paul G','The \\\"bad kids\\\" used to congregate around a set defunct railroad tracks.  The area with the rails was a wasteland of cinder and broken glass, ringed by box elders and trees of heaven.  We had bmx bikes and small motorcycles, and spent countless hours building ramps and trails for them.  Our work incorporated creosote soaked old ties, hunks of rusted steel drums, and other such toxic goodies.\r\n<p>\r\nFor whatever reason, there were also massive stashes of porn magazines in the area too.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOne day we found a magazine, and in it we saw - much to our horror - a man who was supposed to be William Shatner with a woman who was supposed to be Angie Dickinson.  They were doing stuff; and it was like seeing your parents.  I think that it had something to do with <a href=\\\"http://www.johnsolie.com/images/onesheets/bigbadmama.jpg\\\">this</a>.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nLoving your writing - please bring back the old captain, and a vision of a future with sideburns and Elvis boots.\r\n</p>',1,990,449,0,'205.185.3.3','en'),(19937,3300,'2003-04-01 21:28:59',75,'tyler','i think i\\\'m going to take a break from the boot camp story for a little while. i\\\'m not sure i\\\'m getting it across the way i want to. i\\\'m afraid that i might be portraying it too realistically, too much like a travelouge...the point is supposed to be how shocking it was, to someone who grew up with the ordinary notions about these things. how can i describe what it was like to lose all power in the world? to find yourself in such a trap? anyway, if any of you want to read more like this, let me know--otherwise i think i\\\'ll put it away on the hard rive until i feel better about it, or start over.',1,616,0,0,'165.247.165.58','en'),(19938,3300,'2003-04-01 21:47:15',195,'matt','the challenge of writing is to find ways to describe the indescribable.  there\\\'s a way, believe it or not.  you just have to keep trying.  i\\\'ve really enjoyed this series.  please keep writing.',1,199,0,0,'209.208.110.252','en'),(19939,3304,'2003-04-01 22:04:26',273,'Quinn','The kids here are probably to young to identify with someone who entered jr. high in 1983...a quick poll would likely reveal that most were around toddlers around that time.  Am I right?  But take heart; I\\\'m older than you.',1,226,0,0,'63.150.109.234','en'),(19940,3304,'2003-04-01 23:57:47',195,'matt','me, too.  i went into jr. high in \\\'82.  moebius is a year older than me.',1,75,0,0,'24.129.129.219','en'),(19941,3299,'2003-04-02 01:04:07',421,'chimeres','Ah, this is (these feelings and discussions) ARE so much like the war and the *schism* it creates!  As much as they hurt, they give sadistic and guilty pleasure to others, but i don\\\'t feel guilty now.\r\n\r\nRule 1: life has no inherent meaning\r\nRule 2: life is chaos grande\r\nRule 3: Never stop constructing\r\nRule 4: *you/self* is such a constained perspective to get stuck in\r\nRule 5: Never stop experimenting (play games with human nature just to see how bizarre it is)\r\nRule 6: whatever... (everyone is full of it, but the best ones don\\\'t give a damn)\r\n\r\nAnyways, maybe these will help.  Do you/anyone think age is a factor to feeling this way - to needing a new epiphany?  \r\n\r\n\r\n ',0,686,0,0,'208.27.203.128','en'),(19942,3309,'2003-04-02 02:14:06',177,'pony','i haven\\\'t seen or talked to my brother in so many years i\\\'m not sure how long it\\\'s been. maybe five. or six. or seven i guess. unfortunately i remember all too clearly what he looks like. sometimes he\\\'s even in my dreams. go away bad dreams. go away. families are made up of people. and sometimes people can do mean shitty things. especially when you\\\'re just a defenseless kid. especially when they\\\'re older and angry at the world. \r\n\r\n\r\ni don\\\'t miss him at all. i don\\\'t know him. and he doesn\\\'t know me. such is life sometimes. but yea...i\\\'m glad to be alive too. i\\\'m always up for seeing what the next chapter might bring.\r\n\r\n',4,661,0,0,'12.233.252.245','en'),(19943,3309,'2003-04-02 02:25:21',449,'s','A piece called Catharsis that is actually cathartic. I liked it a lot. You have a simple, enjoyable way of writing.  ',2,117,0,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(19944,3309,'2003-04-02 03:55:57',234,'etienne','those kind of meetings are always so emotionally loaded too.  i\\\'m glad it went well..xx',1,90,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(19945,2770,'2003-04-02 04:01:41',234,'etienne','<3 heffalumps',1,13,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(19946,3309,'2003-04-02 04:36:55',75,'tyler','i had a band called Catharsis in high school.',1,45,0,0,'165.247.165.58','en'),(19947,3309,'2003-04-02 07:23:47',17,'austin','let\\\'s everybody thank tyler for that insightful comment.\r\n<HR>\r\ni would like to have seen some secret private investigator photos of the meeting.  some very candid shots.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni think you are really great, Peter.  i am glad you post to the site.',3,254,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19948,3294,'2003-04-02 07:24:19',477,'jehannum','pop quiz...\r\n\r\n\\\"we must defeat this great evil and remove the dictator, a vicious leader that was never democratically elected by his people. a man who has weapons of mass detruction and defies the united nations. god is on our side, and we are truly righteous and justified.\\\"\r\n\r\nnow is this george talking about saddam, or saddam talking about george?\r\n\r\njust a thought...',2,379,0,0,'203.109.147.165','en'),(19949,3309,'2003-04-02 14:50:37',66,'Peter','<br><tt>thanks again austin; im glad i post here too. youve made a good site.<br><Br>candid photos would have been nice. i had the urge to lug along the old polaroid and shoot some intimidatingly head-on <a href=\"http://www.richardavedon.com/\">avedon</a>-esque shots of my dad for my personal collection (i have no photos of him), but i think he would have thought it strange. he was already pretty weirded out by the fact that i didn\'t own a car, that i was paying over $300/month for rent (ha), and that i was wearing lots of black clothing. he\'s a comparatively conservative southern man.</tt>',1,608,17,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(19950,2784,'2003-04-02 15:44:28',298,'lurkinginsagas','i love the first line of this.',1,30,0,0,'198.216.248.250','en'),(19951,3309,'2003-04-02 20:33:26',17,'austin','on the other hand, you could make an argument for not caring what he thought. :)\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nnot taking into account, i\\\'m sure it was a grave situation in many respects.\r\n',4,173,66,0,'64.209.168.14','en'),(19952,3268,'2003-04-02 20:37:34',57,'weeb','i\\\'m heading over to the optometrist today, and wanted to reread this article because i liked it. \r\n\r\nthanks for the thoughts and tips, because this is such a pain in the ass for most optometrists, the one i go to doesn\\\'t even accept insurance, i just bring a copy to work to get reimbursed. it seems easier for all that way.\r\n\r\nmmmMMm new glasses (new lenses for old comfy glasses) and contacts!! i\\\'m so excited!!! little things like this make me very happy. it makes me feel bionic and improved.',1,505,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(19953,3137,'2003-04-02 23:25:39',120,'joyrider','you might want to check out a book called \\\"the chalice and the blade.\\\"',3,76,0,0,'12.252.221.216','en'),(19954,3309,'2003-04-03 00:08:29',66,'Peter','<br><tt>truth be told, i wish i had made photos simply so that i could post them here with my story. oh well. maybe next time?</tt>',1,131,17,0,'66.108.184.212','en'),(19955,3268,'2003-04-03 00:56:06',66,'Peter','<br><tt>everyone should get sexy glasses. <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/1160_2.jpg\\\">yeah</a>...</tt>',1,120,57,0,'66.108.184.212','en'),(19956,3137,'2003-04-03 01:05:07',189,'cyantist','thank you! i\\\'ll add that to my list.   i\\\'m currently reading tipping point and then i plan on reading \\\"my ishmael\\\". have you read either?',1,149,120,0,'216.39.112.17','en'),(19957,3268,'2003-04-03 03:12:16',234,'etienne','i love that feeling, especially when i am wearing contacts.  i feel like mentioning it all the time, and exclaiming.  i feel the happiest and most-bionic when i am swimming and can see clearly :D',1,195,57,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(19958,3268,'2003-04-03 22:00:04',74,'jo','are you talking about sine\\\'s glasses or elly\\\'s goggles? mmm, goggles.',1,75,66,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(19959,3268,'2003-04-03 22:02:15',74,'jo','anyway what i wanted to say was that almost exactly a year ago, i was standing in the kitchen of my parents\\\' house, talking to my mom about something, and cleaning my glasses with the underside of my shirt, when of the hinges broke off neatly. i called about getting it soldered but was told that it would be ~$30 and would probably only hold up for a month or so and it would make more sense to go get new frames. i was recently unemployed; i haven\\\'t had optical insurance in over a year now. i haven\\\'t worn glasses since. i miss them a LOT.',1,551,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(19960,3268,'2003-04-03 22:03:37',74,'jo','i wish that szajic would now get onboard and provide a link to that story about the guy who is only attracted to girls who wear glasses.',1,136,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(19961,3268,'2003-04-03 23:02:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt>sine in glasses. yum. although, admittedly the goggles are cool, yet they might be hard to catch on as a fashion statement.</tt>',1,136,74,0,'66.108.184.212','en'),(19962,3268,'2003-04-04 00:07:51',82,'simon','as far as i recall, their fashion statement has come and gone.',0,62,66,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(19963,3268,'2003-04-04 03:06:03',66,'Peter','<br><tt>perhaps i should clarify that the goggles i was referring to were of the <i>arabian</i> variety.</tt>',1,109,82,0,'66.108.184.212','en'),(19964,3312,'2003-04-04 10:56:24',65,'le','definitely.',4,11,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(19965,3309,'2003-04-05 07:52:47',75,'tyler','oops, sorry. guess i didn\\\'t take everyone\\\'s <i>feelings</i> into <i>consideration.</i><br><hr>\r\nit amazes me that at 25 my dad can still have such a strong influence on me sometimes. i only saw him one weekend a month, two weeks a year for most of my childhood. just like a national guardsman. he was a \\\'citizen dad,\\\' i guess you might say. anyway everything he ever said to me was kind of like the gospel in my mind until i was 22 or so. i always figured that since he was a pretty successful and well-respected guy, he probably had it all figured out, right? nope. for a long time, i was thinking \\\'am i really that messed up a person, or is dad just a total fucking asshole?\\\' and then it gradually became clear that he was just a dick, and i was cool. what do you know.<br><br>\r\nps--fucking Catharsis <i>rocked!</i>',1,835,0,0,'165.247.165.58','en'),(19966,3309,'2003-04-05 09:22:25',17,'austin','shit tyler, if your dad is only 25 that would make you AT MOST between 11 and 13.',3,81,75,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19967,3309,'2003-04-05 10:33:44',187,'kandrer','right on! i often had the feeling that tyler is a 12 year-old genius.<br>\r\n[x] kidding, not trying to be mean</p><p>\r\nvery nice post. it made me think that in movies, girls often run after the first kiss (or just before it). maybe because speed allows to disperse away overwhelming feelings.\r\none day, i drove my motorbike at full speed after a revealing fight with my father.<br>\r\nthanks, Peter.',1,396,0,0,'210.239.96.105','en'),(19968,3309,'2003-04-05 16:39:57',195,'matt','this is a very thought-provoking post.  it makes me think of a recurring vision i have of leaving my family for ten years or so then coming back and seeing how they\\\'ve changed.<BR><BR>faulkner wrote a lot about \\\"the human heart in conflict with itself,\\\" how sometimes it hurts too much to see the ones you love, or to decide if indeed you really love them.  some of the hardest questions we face....',1,408,0,0,'209.208.108.215','en'),(19969,3309,'2003-04-05 22:33:37',66,'Peter','<br><tt>im 25. i was just trying to imagien what it would be like to have a 13 year old kid. <i>yikes</i>...</tt>',1,113,17,0,'66.108.184.233','en'),(19970,2770,'2003-04-06 08:35:27',477,'jehannum','for the record, i am yet to meet a woman that has ever been happy with the way she looks. tall, short, toned or full-figured - there is always something that simply does not measure against their perceptions of \\\"acceptable\\\".\r\n\r\nyes, even the \\\"one piece of celery a day\\\" women have these issues. i work in film and television and actors are about the most insecure and obsessive group people i have ever met. if anyone thinks that the tanned and toned, gym and diet crowd aren\\\'t insecure, they are very very wrong.\r\n\r\noh and just for the record (in case i sound too zen and settled), i absolutely hate my body. it\\\'s skinny where it should be muscular and flabby where it should be, well, muscular.\r\n\r\nscrew the perfect people.',2,743,0,0,'130.123.128.24','en'),(19971,3260,'2003-04-06 09:03:29',477,'jehannum','<p>first and foremost i have to say that it is disheartening to read comments from people who still believe that america has any right whatsoever to be in iraq.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>nobody would ever dispute that america is powerful, or that it can overthrow saddam and control iraq if it so wishes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>but since when was the ability to invade and control another country justification for doing so?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>people are dying every day over there at the hands of americans and britons. people who have suffered under hussein for years. people who want nothing more than to get up and go to work so they can feed their families.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> for sure getting rid of saddam will make iraq a better place. but it\\\'s a question of method. so far, bush has managed to rally support around saddam from elements within his own country, and arab nations in the region. bush has cost the lives of iraqis, americans and britons.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the ramifications of this war will be felt in international relations for years to come, and may very well destabilise the united nations to a point where it may no longer be of any significance.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>what saddens me most is that everybody seems to be missing the point; that this war is the best distraction bush could have ever hoped for back home. everyone sees hussein, bin laden, omar and every other demon dangled before them in the press. they forget all about enron, and budget cuts and rigged elections.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>who will the next demon nation be? north korea? pakistan? jordan? syria? what happens when the war is over and bush is in a tight spot again?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it seems all bush has to do is wave the american flag and he can do whatever he likes to fuck over americans and get away with it. it\\\'s a shame.</p>',4,1757,0,0,'130.123.128.24','en'),(19972,3317,'2003-04-06 20:57:35',17,'austin','i\\\'m not to the point in my life when anyone would take me seriously if i asked to test drive a car worth more than 30k.  i like corvettes.  i really don\\\'t think there is anything to dislike about them.  one might perfer something else, but not fault the vette.  bmg\\\'s cousin, i think she might be in her early twenties, married a pro golfer and he bought her a corvette.  stories like that make me simultaneously lose faith in the world and find a way to pull in 160k a year.',6,484,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(19973,3317,'2003-04-06 21:25:57',66,'Peter','<br><tt><img src=\"http://www.canadiandriver.com/articles/jk/images/03z06_3-1.jpg\"></tt>',1,80,0,0,'66.108.184.233','en'),(19974,3302,'2003-04-07 02:18:18',128,'Javorai',':P hehe',0,7,0,0,'200.85.34.114','en'),(19975,3317,'2003-04-07 07:44:03',265,'chuckspeed','honestly i think vettes suck.</p>\r\n\r\nthey\\\'re for fat balding guys in their 50\\\'s who need something to attract chix.  haven\\\'t been a vette fan since pop bought one about 15 years back - it logged 500 miles total before he sold it.</p>\r\n\r\nthe z06 is an entirely different anmial, tho.  hp-to-weight rules.',0,312,0,0,'152.163.189.202','en'),(19976,3318,'2003-04-07 11:19:28',187,'kandrer','i like this.',1,12,0,0,'210.239.98.238','en'),(19977,3314,'2003-04-07 11:41:24',187,'kandrer','when GWB was elected, i was working in SF, and there was exactly 0 (zero) of my american co-workers who voted for him. i had to be told that the \\\"average GWB voter\\\" is often quite different from my until-then idea of the \\\"average american\\\".<br>\r\nanyway, very nice post, Tyler, it made me feel like offering you a few beers :). oh, and if you guys find a way to get rid of a powerful moron, please share it, since we have one in france too.',1,451,0,0,'210.239.98.238','en'),(19978,3317,'2003-04-07 16:41:16',17,'austin','i like them!  i promise i would not get chicks with one, i would BURN ASS!',3,74,265,0,'64.209.168.14','en'),(19979,3318,'2003-04-07 23:25:30',234,'etienne','ohhh, me too.',1,13,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(19980,3245,'2003-04-08 01:33:16',477,'jehannum','that is gorgeous etienne. and from a fellow kiwi too. :)',1,56,0,0,'203.109.147.165','en'),(19981,3007,'2003-04-08 10:39:36',370,'seanthecon','i go back and read this every so often. it\\\'s good to know that others want just as desperatly and passionatly as i do. i wish you\\\'d write more.',6,149,0,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(19982,3320,'2003-04-08 11:21:17',175,'Paul G','For whatever it\\\'s worth, the claim that \\\"rubber bullets\\\" and other similar projectiles are non-lethal, is <a href=\\\"http://www.cbsnews.com/stories/2002/05/24/health/main510084.shtml\\\">false.</a>\r\n<p>\r\nAny such object that strikes the face, neck, or groin, has the potential to either kill or permanently disable.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nDeadly force is to be used only to pretect one\\\'s own life, or the life of another, or to prevent the commission of a forcible felony - end of story.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhoever gave the order to fire, and whoever fired on the crowd, is guilty of not only a criminal offense, but also of violating the oath that they swore to uphold the State and Federal Constitutions.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBut if the President can violate his oath, then why can\\\'t everyone else?  Let\\\'s all just wipe our butts with the Constitution and be done with it.\r\n</p>',0,872,0,0,'205.186.223.126','en'),(19983,3320,'2003-04-08 11:46:00',65,'le','thanks for posting this. i have been linking to it from other places, fyi. great firsthand account.',4,99,0,0,'216.27.177.123',''),(19984,3320,'2003-04-08 22:59:38',154,'ryan','in case anybody missed in in the swinchat, here\\\'s some photos (and an article) that <a href=\\\"http://www.commondreams.org/headlines03/0407-06.htm\\\">clearly illustrate</a> what rubber bullets can do.\r\n<p>\r\nin portland they have just been using stun grenades and good old-fashioned clubs so far.',0,300,0,0,'216.99.204.39','en'),(19985,2959,'2003-04-08 23:25:43',195,'matt','whatever happened to green? shit, whatever happened to essemar?  who else is missing?  kat.  darwin.  other people.  that dude who was really into kicking ass.  jesus, i\\\'m high.  then there\\\'s the lurkers.  what do the stats say?',1,234,0,0,'24.129.129.219','en'),(19986,2959,'2003-04-09 02:16:20',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i run into essemar now and again; he lives in my hood.</tt>',1,67,195,0,'66.108.184.212','en'),(19987,3324,'2003-04-09 05:56:55',477,'jehannum','for what it\\\'s worth, you\\\'re not the only one who has suffered through the cringe-fest that is working for pornslingers. my own personal set of nightmares are derived from the eighteen months i worked as a freelance video editor and one of my clients was an adult distributor. their television commercials were fine to do but the censor cutting... well i learned two things; firstly that i will forever be afraid of german people of both sexes and, second that i am, despite what many people have told me, remarkably normal (at least when compared to the things i witnessed back then). good luck sleeping well. :)',1,618,0,0,'203.109.147.165','en'),(19988,3320,'2003-04-09 08:47:21',187,'kandrer','in The Japan Times, April 9, source AFP-Jiji:<br>\r\n<i>But protesters, city officials and port workers at the scene said the demonstration was entirely peaceful.<br>\r\nCity council aide Joel Tena, an observer of the protest, said he saw no provocation by the picketers. \\\"It was scary. They were shooting indiscriminately,\\\" he said.</i>',1,339,0,0,'210.198.200.125','en'),(19989,3313,'2003-04-09 14:48:00',240,'xep','i\\\'m glad you\\\'re ok.  you are now a survivor.\r\n<p>\r\nout of curiosity, did they diagnose you with polycystic ovarian syndrome before they found the cancerous growths?  ',0,172,0,0,'64.81.241.248','en'),(19990,3299,'2003-04-09 15:14:56',124,'gus','hi, i was too busy and freaked out by my own lame life to write. Put the worldly angst on top of it... I wanted to write but well. yah. I was too busy with the eternal soul wrestling. heh.\r\n<p>\r\nChimeres, do you really think the best ones dont give a damn? Wouldnt you say the best ones just know WHEN to give a damn - and when not to?\r\n<p>\r\nAge may play a roll in epiphanies, but you arent limited to one per lifetime or one per year, so Im not sure what the roll would be.',0,474,0,0,'64.160.54.180','en'),(19991,3324,'2003-04-09 17:30:57',195,'matt','every swinneyite should have a porn name.',1,41,0,0,'65.144.79.54','en'),(19992,3322,'2003-04-09 19:08:48',421,'chimeres','That second to last paragraph reminds me of the movie \\\"Freeway\\\" (Reese Witherspoon in a not so very Reese-role and Keifer Southerland), which is very worth seeing.  On another note, detachment is wonderfully soothing in that melancholy way, for a little while at least.  I think i should try to approach my approaching lunch hour in a suave-cabbie sort of way.',0,366,0,0,'208.27.203.128','en'),(19993,3299,'2003-04-09 19:32:11',421,'chimeres','<p>About age, it seems it plays a role in us needing ephiphanies.  Maybe cuz we stop seeing things fresh, i\\\'m not sure, but it seems as you get older you start feeling shallower.</p> <p>about not giving a damn... i meant OF the ones who are full of it (we all are i think - it\\\'s just a matter of justification - of constructed rationale/logic mixed with rhetoric -some just have better justifications for their beliefs/selfs than others), the best ones know deep down that they are, and they don\\\'t take themselves so seriously that they forget how to see the world outside of themselves... ...or something like that.</p>',0,629,0,0,'208.27.203.128','en'),(19994,3322,'2003-04-09 22:02:17',449,'s','I love your taxi-driver-snippets. Please, keep on writing them.\r\n\r\nThis one was sad, in that melancholy kind of way. I loved it. ',1,129,0,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(19995,3317,'2003-04-10 00:31:11',265,'chuckspeed','damned thing is like crack.  can\\\'t get it outta my mind.  If I get a balloon loan, I can get the payment down to proletarian levels.,</p>\r\n\r\nbad chuck.',0,154,0,0,'205.188.209.48','en'),(19996,3322,'2003-04-10 12:45:38',175,'Paul G','So much is so dark.  Tonight it was a homeless, pregnant woman who wanted to trade sex for food.  Her face was so innocent, and simple.  More than one thing went wrong in her life; and it was beyond my power to make things right for her.\r\n<p>\r\nI don\\\'t know what to say.  Just thanks for reading and understanding.\r\n</p>',0,322,449,0,'205.186.223.213','en'),(19997,3314,'2003-04-10 18:12:06',74,'jo','okay, here\\\'s a way to help: research NOW of the possible democratic candidates which one best represents your needs and wishes and start campaigning for the candidate. encourage your friends to do the same. do not let anyone convince you that there is no clear winner. if you\\\'re feeling overwhelmed, rent \\\"the war room\\\" to see how, in 1992, people were so unhappy with bush sr. and wanted him out of there badly but there didn\\\'t appear to be a good contender who could beat him and clinton came out of NOWHERE and DESTROYED him in one of the biggest voting margins in presidential history. ',2,605,0,0,'66.25.138.204','en'),(19998,3314,'2003-04-10 18:16:04',74,'jo','(oh, personally? i am <a href=\\\"http://www.deanforamerica.com/\\\">behind dean</a>.)',1,86,0,0,'66.25.138.204','en'),(19999,3317,'2003-04-11 19:24:59',83,'adamajah','there\\\'s a programmer here who would buy a vette today if he could find one with a manual.  not a dealership in all of southern cali with anything but automatics for the old men who buy them.  i personally would take either.\r\n\r\nanother thing - no dealership allows test drives.  Apparently people don\\\'t buy vettes with more than zero mileage.  eh?',3,352,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(20000,3298,'2003-04-11 20:15:54',421,'chimeres','A little late comment to add...:  I\\\'ll tell you why we are going to war (or is \\\"\\\'went\\\' to war\\\" yet?, since the militarians now tell us the regime is dead).  Its the same overarching reason everyone has always went to war - - for self glorification, profit and power.  We chose Iraq because there were enough reasons to convince enough people (barely, enough people, though i think) to support it and feel good about it.  Saddam is sick and many under his rule hate him, blah blah blah.  Whatever that Bush administration says about \\\"protecting\\\" ourselves and \\\"helping\\\" others is just propaganda for their libidous urges to conquer and be cowboys.  Not that I think there\\\'s anything wrong with that in principle, it\\\'s the story of life and maybe even the necessary, forever, root of life to exert power and try to get bigger and stronger, even if it\\\'s a mistake all in all.  What sucks is that the Bush administration is so freaking stupid and narrow minded that they can\\\'t do it with more subtle style, at least.',0,1051,0,0,'208.27.203.128','en'),(20001,3298,'2003-04-11 20:19:45',421,'chimeres','- and most of all because they were an easy target, or so Bush thought.',0,71,0,0,'208.27.203.128','en'),(20002,3322,'2003-04-11 22:13:39',449,'s','Once when I was about 12, the guy in the ice cream truck gave me a popsicle even though I was ten cents short. I thought that was the coolest thing, and I still do. It\\\'s incredible to see people do things for others; there is so much apathy nowadays - it just makes me happy to see people <i>care</i>. I think that if there were more people like you in the world, it would be a little better. ',0,396,175,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(20003,3322,'2003-04-12 06:43:00',187,'kandrer','when i was about 10, the owner of the shop made me go back home to get 10 centimes (=0.1 french franc; that\\\'s less than 2 cents) to buy a box of plastic pearls (yeah, i was playing with the girls in my building and that\\\'s the kind of things they used to play with).<br>\r\ni never forgot this shop owner and this story. not because he must have been really greedy but rather because i keep on feeling ashamed like hell if i cannot pay for something i just took. i am ashamed if people might think i am trying to get more than i deserve, maybe. well, enough about my self analysis.<br>\r\nanyway, i am sure i would never have forgotten the guy if he had let me have the box. just like i never forgot this SF tramway driver who let me in with my out-of-validity transfer. of course i could have paid the dollar. it\\\'s just that look the guy gave me. a bit like a father smilling at his son being drunk for the first time, or something like that.</p><p>\r\n\r\nand, S, if there were more people like Paul G, it would not be a little better : it would be definitively good. but, yeah, we won\\\'t see this happen. maybe a few dozens generations from us ? one day, people will actually believe the message that has been already printed and told nearly everywhere, and act accordingly. (fuck, i am in such a good mood again! :) )',1,1323,0,0,'210.198.234.104','en'),(20004,3298,'2003-04-12 07:01:27',187,'kandrer','i take the occasion of being maybe the only french around here to let you know that:<br>\r\n- i was surprised to follow the war news and to think manytimes something like \\\"this is a good thing that Americains kick Saddam out of there\\\".<br>\r\n- i have real doubts that the countries opposing to the war (including france) did it only for humanitarian reasons.<br>\r\nwell, i might be wrong but i think the average french people might think a bit like me now: since you guys started the war, we hope it will be fast and \\\"successful\\\" (as successful as a war can be, of course). only a few motherfuckers idiots are happy when US or UK soldiers get killed there. if some of these people are french, they are not human in my opinion.<br>\r\ni am worried now about how Irak can be turned into a democracy. in my typically french style pessimism, i am afraid it won\\\'t work. but few things can stop America as history showed...',1,926,0,0,'210.198.234.104','en'),(20005,3322,'2003-04-12 19:06:23',17,'austin','when i was ten i was arrested for stealing candy from the Tom Thumb store.  sort of arrested.  nabbed is better.  the manager was pissed.  actually i was not ten, i think i was 6 maybe.',3,185,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20006,3327,'2003-04-13 23:22:53',17,'austin','hm.  can\\\'t you make the end into something where you bump into him in the mall.  you both are unhappily married with shitty little shitbottomed kids so you divorce your spouces and run off to bermuda together?',2,212,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20007,3327,'2003-04-14 00:59:41',449,'s','dude, yeah. I don\\\'t like the mall thing though. I figure that I\\\'ll be a waitress at some cheap joint in Vegas and he\\\'ll come in for the strippers. We\\\'ll get drunk and get married by Elvis or something. ',3,214,17,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(20008,3327,'2003-04-14 01:08:06',17,'austin','that happens to allot of people, in Vegas.',2,42,449,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20009,3327,'2003-04-14 13:10:12',175,'Paul G','Fate is a jerk; don\\\'t hold your breath.  And I look like a Romulan.',0,70,0,0,'207.220.155.142','en'),(20010,3324,'2003-04-14 13:13:51',175,'Paul G','Hi gus.  When I read the title, I totally thought that it was going to be you and, you know: bowchickabowbow...mmm...oh yes!',0,124,0,0,'207.220.155.142','en'),(20011,3327,'2003-04-14 18:19:15',66,'Peter','<br><tt>reno is an even better place for that sort of bizness...</tt>',1,69,17,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(20012,3328,'2003-04-14 18:24:33',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>all my rowdy friends have settled down.</i><br><br>thanks. now i have that damn song in my head!</tt>',1,108,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(20013,3327,'2003-04-14 18:32:09',82,'simon','i was |__| this close to going to reno saturday night!',1,54,66,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(20014,3327,'2003-04-14 19:58:26',449,'s','Paul, give me my starry eyed idealism for now.=) Really, I don\\\'t actually hold much stock in fate and all that. \r\n\r\nI wonder if the Star Trek Experience performs weddings somewhere...',3,186,0,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(20015,3327,'2003-04-14 20:50:44',82,'simon','they do, i think. i\\\'ve heard of people having star trek weddings.',0,68,449,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(20016,3327,'2003-04-14 21:41:03',129,'moebius','<br>In Vegas the better chapels on the Strip let you choose the theme for the wedding. Anything\\\'s possible depending upon how much moolah you want to shovel out, I think. Elvis, Vulcans, beach blanket bimbos, Mouseketeers, brass bands....the sky\\\'s the limit. <br><br>Molls & I opted for the basic \\\"white stretch limo & a preacher\\\" package. the limo driver took the pictures. We were married the Friday after last Thanksgiving at 11:45 pm, at a chapel across the street from Circus Circus. ended up spending more money on dinner than the ceremony. all in all it was quite nice.',1,588,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(20017,3327,'2003-04-14 23:14:20',175,'Paul G','You\\\'re right.<br>\r\n( * _ * )<br>\r\nStarry-eyed idealism is more than a good thing: it\\\'s a needful thing for us.  And sometimes it\\\'s better to have the hope for a thing than it is to have the thing itself, eh?\r\n<p>\r\nI do believe in fate, or luck, or whatever.  I have both feet firmly planted in the mystical-spooky camp.  There seem to be an almost infinite number of variables upon which every event depends; and the means by which we understand the variables and attempt to control them are the products of our own minds rather than being things that are related to some objective reality.  Not that I would say the same thing about moral judgements.  Maybe that seems like a contradiction, but in either case it is my opinion that the \\\"feelings\\\" might be a better or more reliable guide than ideas. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt\\\'s irrational, I know, but different people seem to have different kinds of luck, too.  Fortune, danger, and love seem like distinct \\\"categories of luckiness\\\" haha.  But seriously...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI hope that you find someone that is right for you - somehow, somewhere.  And in the meantime, the memories of what was and what might have been are a good thing to cherish.  Cheers.\r\n</p>  ',1,1225,449,0,'205.185.2.130','en'),(20018,3327,'2003-04-15 00:21:01',66,'Peter','<br><tt>simon: we were rolling down 8th street in a cab in the rain on saturday night, talking about you. we invoked you on the LES.</tt>',1,137,82,0,'66.108.184.212','en'),(20019,3327,'2003-04-15 07:29:56',187,'kandrer','i loved that. and again i discovered that the writer is a she. it seems that when it\\\'s a girl writing, i get more emotions. hummm... sorry, i just realized this is perfectly normal ;)\r\n<br>\r\ns, you might not need to change your pseudo (and i am sure i can recognize u from s5, he he)',1,286,0,0,'210.198.199.158','en'),(20020,3331,'2003-04-15 17:53:38',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>a man cracked in sidewalks walked with walls</i><br><br>this sentence is driving me crazy.</tt>',1,106,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(20021,3330,'2003-04-15 17:57:24',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i think that maybe, when you encounter the sort of person you\\\'ll never lose (for all the right reasons), the detritus that remains from the lost... <i>just isn\\\'t important anymore</i>.</tt>',1,203,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(20022,3325,'2003-04-15 17:59:34',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>there is some purpose for each of us</i><br><br>i think that coming to this realization is a pretty memorable- if not inevitable- milestone in the process of growing up...</tt> ',1,188,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(20023,3327,'2003-04-15 20:46:02',449,'s','<p>\r\nI\\\'m actually quite happy with my current life, love or otherwise.=)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'ve often thought that \\\"feelings\\\" are generally a better guide than ideas, or at least a more \\\"natural\\\" one. Lao Tzu or some old chinese philosopher can back me up on that one if I bother to find the quote.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFate on the other hand, is something that I feel is rather detached from human instinct and/or control. Perhaps it is the realist in me that rejects it. The religious, idealistic side of me wants to believe that a part of my future is something completely out of my control and all I can do is go along with it.\r\n</p>',2,640,175,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(20024,3327,'2003-04-15 20:46:10',449,'s','Well, there\\\'s only one s as opposed to five.=) Thanks. Ironically, I always feel more emotion when something is told from a male viewpoint. Wonder why that is...',2,164,187,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(20025,3331,'2003-04-15 20:58:05',449,'s','The rhythm in this is so yummy. ',2,32,0,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(20026,3327,'2003-04-15 23:23:08',83,'adamajah','I like stories with pictures.',2,29,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(20027,3325,'2003-04-16 04:39:22',234,'etienne','yes, i believe this too, but the problem is that in some cases people can\\\'t see their purpose.  they can\\\'t see past the trials right in front of them, and they can\\\'t cope, and they feel alone, and.  well.  they feel so alone and they go and kill themselves.  <br><br>\r\nso what happens to that purpose? who fulfills that part of the world?  because whoever that person is, i\\\'d like them to come along and fill hte hole in my life, in the lives which have that hole.  <br><br>\r\nsometimes i wish i was religious and could believe that all this has happened for a reason.',1,579,66,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(20028,3327,'2003-04-16 05:32:52',421,'chimeres','I like stories that lead to good comments (and pictures.)  I knew a boy with a blue flannel shirt once and we parted saying the same sort of thing - \\\"if its meant to be we\\\'ll meet again later in life.\\\"  Funny, everyone, or the parts of them, that i\\\'ve loved since reminds me of him somehow.  But i did meet him again later and realized how hopeless faith in fate is.  It\\\'s a lazy excuse for those too tired of working to steer themselves in a certain direction (for right or wrong reasons), then again, fate is also a necessary starry eyed dream to hang on to, and feelings always lead much nicer than ideas.  Fate probably is more close to truth than anything else, but it doesn\\\'t do any good to rely on it, only does good to jump on it when you recognize it slipping away, or being chased off by silly \\\"ideas.\\\"',0,836,0,0,'64.156.229.104','en'),(20029,3325,'2003-04-16 05:57:21',421,'chimeres','I disagree.  It\\\'s great to feel as if everyone has a purpose, but (and maybe this is really the same thing in some backwards light) everything is divinely purposeless.  So much so that everything anyone does, it immediately has some ripple effect that looks like a purpose in comparison to the randomness of reality that we start abstracting and seeing it as a purpose.  REally it\\\'s just our way of organizing life\\\'s perfect insanity.  And to me that\\\'s great, because no one should feel like they aren\\\'t important.  Action is purpose.  When you bring that \\\"single\\\" purpose and religious like \\\"path\\\" into it, you just make others feel like they are \\\"lost\\\" while making others feel shallowly superior.  That\\\'s why it bothers me when people say \\\"everyone has a purpose\\\" - no one has \\\"A\\\" purpose, we just are purposes, like it or not, since we make finite effects in a life which is infinitely purposeless.  Hard part is deciding how to make the effects worth making.',0,1011,0,0,'64.156.229.104','en'),(20030,3332,'2003-04-16 13:30:12',93,'chiamattt','Dear Mr. Austin Swinney. \r\n\r\nFrom all of us at the General Chicken factory in ByungJae China; \r\n\r\nRe-heating the general chicken has been known to make it taste better. It has also been known to make people think they look hot with contacts in. There are strange effects when eating the general chicken. ',0,304,0,0,'61.40.66.67','en'),(20031,3325,'2003-04-16 14:48:40',477,'jehannum','you\\\'re all assuming that purpose and fulfillment are one and the same. who says that where we may be led will make us happy. what if some of us are walking down a path simply to make others walk with us, or turn another way.',2,227,0,0,'130.123.128.24','en'),(20032,3332,'2003-04-16 15:03:54',477,'jehannum','<p>ah yes, seeking inspiration and everlasting love from a kindred writer. the only problem being that so far, even the most desirable of angst fuelled auteurs, divine storytelling maidens, and divas of diatribe - all have one of two flaws</p>\r\n\r\n<p>either one; they are so similarly angst ridden and full of inspiration that they remind me far too much of myself, or...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>two; they are so perceptive and intelligent that they realise there is nothing to be gained from a romance with me.<p>',2,499,0,0,'130.123.128.24','en'),(20033,3332,'2003-04-16 20:32:26',449,'s','<p>\r\nThe problem I\\\'ve found with seeking inspiration and relationships with kindred writers is:\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\none: they\\\'re so angsty, it makes me want to slap them.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ntwo: they\\\'re so perceptive and intelligent - it makes me want to slap them.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSo between a lack of tolerance and an injured self esteem(it\\\'s usually pretty good, except in the company of exceedingly brilliant people), I\\\'ve decided that the perfect venue to \\\"TO LIVE AND LOVE AND FIND, PERHAPS, that perfect someone to spend the rest of your life with\\\" is generally in - oh shit, I don\\\'t know.\r\n</p>',2,607,477,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(20034,3332,'2003-04-16 21:05:56',129,'moebius','<br>i for one, wish to speak up for male writers in relationships all around the world: we are tired of getting slapped. come on, folks, this behavior is abusive. when we correct your word usage or grammar in public environments, you\\\'re supposed to <i>thank</i> us. or at least say \\\"i stand corrected,\\\" smile, and move on. the slaps, the uppercuts, the gutpunches while standing in line for the buffet: they are not helpful. this is not how civil people behave. how can we gather our wits for our strenuous writing endeavors if we\\\'re worried that you\\\'ll kickbox us in the groin the moment we leave the next dinner party? ',3,636,449,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(20035,3332,'2003-04-16 21:35:19',86,'egg','did you write this on a napkin and then transcribe',0,50,0,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(20036,3332,'2003-04-16 22:08:52',195,'matt','<BR><BR>dear austin:<BR><BR>i think you\\\'re hot with or without glasses.<BR><BR>big smooches,<BR>matt',0,103,0,0,'209.208.109.148','en'),(20037,3332,'2003-04-16 22:38:38',449,'s','I\\\'ve been trying to transition the slapping into a more subtle roll of the eyes. I\\\'m hoping it will be appreciated. At first, I was trying to transition into a subtle ass grab, but soon realised that it rarely communicated my actual intention.\r\n\r\n\r\n',2,255,129,0,'192.160.216.56','en'),(20038,3332,'2003-04-16 22:49:42',129,'moebius','<br>austin is especially hot when he\\\'s had a few shots and is blindfolded and then set loose on a piÃ±ata. he pulls off a sort of \\\"drunken blind ninja\\\" act that is SO CHARMING. <br><br>i saw him pull this off at my party last friday. when he finished, he pulled off his blindfold with a dramatic flourish. the young girls on the balcony swooned as one and tossed roses to his feet, where they mingled with the sweets that had fallen from the object of his wrath.',2,470,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(20039,3332,'2003-04-17 02:33:35',234,'etienne','inspiration isn\\\'t my only saviour, but it sure is a big one.  ',1,65,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(20040,3325,'2003-04-17 02:37:00',234,'etienne','hi chimeres.  if i was that kind of person i\\\'d say lets agree to disagree, but as its one of my most-hated phrases, can you email me?  etienne at vortex.net.nz.  xo.',1,168,421,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(20041,3332,'2003-04-17 03:04:17',17,'austin','squires, this one was written at my desk before bed.',3,52,86,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20042,3332,'2003-04-17 03:06:37',17,'austin','it is true, the best general chicken is that which is reheated in the pan with some fried rice and a bit of water to keep it from drying out.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nit also helps if you are drunk and hungry and you are stealing it from your gothic roomate.',1,244,93,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20043,3332,'2003-04-17 03:09:30',17,'austin','i tried not to get too ninja on the poor transformers pi&#164;ata.  that was an excellent night.  i run into that friend of yours everywhere now.  actually i dont run into her, i sort of hide in corners while she passes, or put my hood up.  i cannot remember her name, etc.',2,273,129,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20044,3332,'2003-04-17 03:11:07',17,'austin','also i am an angstly, layabout, good-for-little writer type who is not good for relationships at all.',4,101,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20045,3332,'2003-04-17 03:22:22',57,'weeb','i think austin looks just ducky in glasses.\r\n<br><br>\r\nand who\\\'s good for relationships, anyways? no one, that\\\'s who, and that includes me :(\r\n<br><br>\r\nrelationships should have little on off switches or a mute button so you can go and do something else for a while and then come back to it.\r\n<br><br>\r\n(hijeff!ilovesya!)',1,328,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(20046,3332,'2003-04-17 03:58:15',66,'Peter','<Br><tt>big ups to love in new york. yeeuh!</tt>',1,40,0,0,'66.108.184.212','en'),(20047,3332,'2003-04-17 05:06:30',477,'jehannum','yeah, but that naked photo of you with the cat should have them swooning for miles. :)',1,86,17,0,'130.123.128.24','en'),(20048,3330,'2003-04-17 05:27:32',298,'lurkinginsagas','i think you\\\'re right, and i\\\'d give you a kiss on the cheek for it if you weren\\\'t just a little name on a screen.',1,121,0,0,'207.218.209.201','en'),(20049,3326,'2003-04-17 12:16:01',477,'jehannum','<p>\\\"The proof of our altruism will depend upon what kind of democracy develops in this turbulent Arab nation.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>how can you even entertain the notion that anything that is being done by the coalition is altruistic?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>whatever the benefits the iraqi people will reap now that saddam is no longer in power (and i am not disputing that it will be better with him gone) the notion that the troops were sent in based on some kind of benevolent charity from the west is pure fiction, even entirely comical.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>there are two huge problems that face america because of the invasion of iraq.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>1) that the people of america (as intended by the bush regime) seem to have conveniently forgotten about the election, the bush family\\\'s financial dealings with the bin laden family, enron, the budget, and many things that should concern them greatly.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>2) the middle east was volatile enough without the sight of westerners killing arabs. did the u.s. media show the arab men jumping on busses to go to iraq and fight \\\"jews and westerners we do not want in our country\\\"? how many reports are you seeing about the huge political timebombs that are syria and turkey?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>this war was about money and obfuscation. to have americans cheering for bush is as farcical as iraqis cheering for hussein.</p>',3,1344,0,0,'130.123.128.24','en'),(20050,3332,'2003-04-17 15:22:55',86,'egg','\\\"do not fall in love with him, he is an artist.\\\"',0,54,17,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(20051,3332,'2003-04-17 16:58:07',66,'Peter','<br><tt>artist-love is underrated</tt>',1,38,0,0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(20052,3325,'2003-04-17 23:04:59',421,'chimeres','I don\\\'t think i\\\'ve made purpose and fulfillment out to be the same thing at all.  Certainly they are very different, but we can try to lead ourselves to effectuating some purpose that will fulfill us.  Not always of course, because sometimes we have to, or should, realize our purposes may be more important and worthwhile than personal fulfillment - - how do know when that is, though?  Sometimes you just do, but not usually.  How do you know you will be able to actually carryout the purpose you think you are carrying out?  Sometimes you can only carry out some purpose if you are fulfilled by doing it.  If you think too much about it you\\\'ll trip yourself up and accomplish nothing, neither fulfillment or purpose - then again, plenty of people felt that way, then died and later others found purpose out of their lives.  It\\\'s all fiction most of the time it seems. (ps, yeah, i\\\'ll email you, etienne)',0,921,0,0,'208.27.203.128','en'),(20053,3332,'2003-04-17 23:52:28',195,'matt','being an artist - that\\\'s like being at a carnival in hell, isn\\\'t it',1,73,0,0,'209.208.110.195','en'),(20054,3326,'2003-04-18 00:52:49',154,'ryan','humans generally act in their own interest, as do countries.  boo!',0,66,0,0,'216.99.204.31','en'),(20055,3326,'2003-04-18 01:04:10',477,'jehannum','once again we seem to have missed the point. bush is acting in his own interests, not the interests of america or americans. by letting bush get away with this, americans are acting against their own interests.',3,210,154,0,'203.109.147.165','en'),(20056,3336,'2003-04-18 04:52:18',57,'weeb','\r\nbusy poo?',0,11,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(20057,3326,'2003-04-18 05:57:27',421,'chimeres','What are the interests of America?',0,34,0,0,'64.156.228.152','en'),(20058,3332,'2003-04-18 06:14:41',421,'chimeres','this piece makes me feel inspired, even as i am tired, so sweetly :)',0,68,0,0,'64.156.228.152','en'),(20059,3326,'2003-04-18 08:21:41',154,'ryan','survival and wealth?',0,20,0,0,'216.99.204.104','en'),(20060,3327,'2003-04-18 10:12:38',175,'Paul G','It is funny, in a way, that the comments on this piece moved towards dreams - inasmuch as that is what your alias means in the French language: dreams.\r\n<p>\r\nYou follow a Nietzschean line at times, in your comments.  But who are you really?  I need a chart with names and pictures, to help keep the aliases straight...heh.\r\n</p>',1,328,421,0,'207.220.178.139','en'),(20061,3332,'2003-04-18 10:14:19',66,'~','being an artist is the least dull incarnation of human existence.',6,65,0,0,'66.108.184.212','en'),(20062,3330,'2003-04-18 11:16:15',175,'Paul G','Triggers: The sounds, smells, and other such things, that cause us to remember that which we have forgotten - or want to forget.\r\n<p>\r\nIt\\\'s hard.  I stopped reading the article the first time that I read it - because it made me remember things too...\r\n</p>',0,259,298,0,'207.220.178.139','en'),(20063,3332,'2003-04-18 22:48:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt>aint that the truth!</tt>',1,33,66,0,'66.108.184.212','en'),(20064,3332,'2003-04-18 23:09:29',17,'austin','i would kill myself if i were not an artist!',1,44,66,0,'64.209.168.14','en'),(20065,3331,'2003-04-19 01:53:37',17,'austin','i enjoyed this too!\r\n',1,21,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20066,3336,'2003-04-19 05:14:12',370,'bandaid nelly','even when i\\\'m with my poo, you know i\\\'m crazy over you.',6,61,0,0,'207.6.14.96','en'),(20067,3326,'2003-04-19 09:48:25',187,'kandrer','(it\\\'s a bit related - with the war at least)<br>\r\ni hope i am not too naive on that but i often had the feeling that Americans think that freedom is a kindof number one value of their country... and that most of the \\\"rest of the world\\\" does not know such a freedom. am i right on that?<br>\r\nwell, if i am, then if you look at all the \\\"democraties\\\" in the world, apart that you can pass their border (specially to go out of the country), and that you can buy things if you have the money for it, what other things are u free to do? (ok, i am too naive, please enlighten me)</p><p>\r\n\r\ni specially heard this \\\"our freedom is number one\\\" speech by people would could not figure out why anybody would feel angry at the US. this was easy found reason: these countries are jealous of (or feel threatened by) our freedom.<br>\r\nagain, i can think of many other free countries who have no enemies (or much fewer).\r\n</p>\r\nBTW i agree with Jehannum: altruism is such a... weird word to use here!',1,1004,0,0,'221.119.194.82','en'),(20068,3314,'2003-04-19 15:39:10',477,'jehannum','<p>well, what can i say tyler but \\\"hallelujah!\\\" in the loudest possible voice (and believe me, i have a loud voice...).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>as i have tried to point out to so many friends in america, who cannot seem to grasp that you can be patriotic while denouncing a president that is, let\\\'s face it, about as good for the human race as cancer and sars rolled into one.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the lecgacy of the bush family to the american people is horrific to say the least. the entire clan is basically co-shareholders in a number of companies with the (feign surprise now) bin-ladens of saudi-arabia.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>please, please, please. all of you that can vote against bush in america, please remember the rest of us who live in other countries and are completely terrified at the thought of someone so ignorant, misguided, deluded and downright evil, with his hands on so much power.</p>',1,880,0,0,'203.109.157.66','en'),(20069,3336,'2003-04-20 06:19:27',65,'le','<a href=\\\"http://www.washingtonmonthly.com/features/2003/0304.marshall.html\\\">practice to deceive</a>',4,105,0,0,'216.27.177.123',''),(20070,3336,'2003-04-20 06:20:45',65,'le','<a href=\\\"http://www.washingtonmonthly.com/features/2003/0304.marshall.html\\\"><b>Practice to Deceive</b> - \r\nChaos in the Middle East is not the Bush hawks\\\' nightmare scenario--it\\\'s their plan. </a>',4,208,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(20071,3336,'2003-04-21 03:07:03',93,'chiamattt','im currently in some town i will dub  HERTZVILLE, THAILAND. Its where we catch the ferry to some islands. I dont know. I just wanted to say that there are no signs of weapons of mass destruction here. Well at least none from IRaq. ps. this pc room is hillarious. ',1,263,0,0,'203.146.54.227','en'),(20072,3326,'2003-04-21 12:52:19',273,'Quinn','Ah...but I said \\\"the proof of our altruism.\\\"  Has not Bush declared this a war to \\\"liberate the Iraqi people?\\\"  What is the will to \\\"liberation\\\" but altruism?  Personally, I doubt Bush\\\'s motives, but who\\\'s to say?  A shadow government is a shadow government.  If things develop in Iraq so as to establish an independent state, a state not unduly beholden to the US in terms of meeting political debts with money or oil, a democratic free state, then perhaps an altruism does lurk under the actions of the United States.  And maybe, just maybe, the narrow-sighted \\\"no blood for oil crowd\\\" is wrong.  We simply don\\\'t know for sure yet.  Unless you\\\'re CIA, and if you are, please tell us what\\\'s really going on.',0,747,0,0,'63.150.109.234','en'),(20073,3336,'2003-04-21 19:06:16',273,'Quinn','Sir Chiamatt, there\\\'s a lot going on in the world that many of us are unhappy about-- \r\n\r\n<P>My mom is undergoing tests for cancer right now.  I\\\'m not happy about that. Why isn\\\'t their a pill she can take that fixes everything? \r\n\r\n<P>Many of my friends are unemployed, which I am also unhappy about.  If I had it my way I\\\'d give them all jobs.  \r\n\r\n<P>It\\\'s going to rain tomorrow.  I wish it wouldn\\\'t, I\\\'d planned a picnic.  One should be able to wash away rainclouds like weathermen do on their magic weatherwalls.  \r\n\r\n\r\n<P>Wars that you haven\\\'t even dreamed about are in the near and far future.  Our government does things we can\\\'t stop.  I wish we had more control over politicians.  \r\n\r\n<P>I have an itchy rash on my elbows that\\\'s persisted for three days now.  I can change my drycleaner, which I suspect did this to me, but for the time being it still itches.  That\\\'s just not fair.\r\n\r\n<P>I\\\'m at work today when most of my office is at home (it\\\'s Marathon Day in Boston, a city holiday).  I wish I could be at home.\r\n\r\n<P>I wish that movie stars were more accessible.  You should just be able to call up Drew Barrymore on the phone anytime you want.  Where does she get off, keeping her phone numbers secret?\r\n\r\n<P>Daisies should grow from gun barrels, not bullets. Cops should run donut shops, they\\\'re always there anyway. Movie Theatres should charge less for snacks, movies cost enough to begin with.  And we should organize the homeless to clean the trash of the streets, they\\\'re such a mess.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',0,1568,93,0,'63.150.109.234','en'),(20074,3326,'2003-04-22 12:54:05',175,'Paul G','<p>\r\nThere are no Iraqi people.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere really is no such thing as Iraq.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAfter WWI, the British created Iraq; the joke is that it was done after dinner one night - while taking brandy and cigars.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe states that now exist in the Middle East were, for the most part, created either by the British or by the French - and not the people native to the region.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe French said: \\\"Let there be Lebanon.\\\"<br>\r\nAnd lo: there was Lebanon. <br>\r\nThe British said: \\\"Let there be Iraq. <br>\r\nAnd lo: there was Iraq. <br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAnd after WWII, Israel came into being again - in much the same manner.  The only thing that\\\'s really missing from the current map of the region are a few pictures of \\\"Ronald McDonald\\\" and the \\\"Grimace\\\" chasing along a cartoon path that leads to \\\"fries\\\" and a \\\"shake.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWho is native to the region, anyway? <br>\r\nAt one time, people from Greece ruled the region. <br>\r\nAt another time, people from northern China ruled the region. <br>\r\nLooking at my face, you can see them both mixed together. <br>\r\nMy grandfather had a French first name, and a Roman last name; his father\\\'s name was Abraham.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe observation has been made more than once that American democracy and the American people do not deal well with things that are both subtle and also complex.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSome well-groomed man or lady on the television speaks of, \\\"The Iraqi people...\\\" And so we repeat, \\\"The Iraqi people...\\\"  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n(Think: we argue about imaginary things.)  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBut the people who live within the boundaries of the artificial state of Iraq never wanted to live together in the first place.  They share no common vision of the best way to live.  And to further complicate matters, many of the groups with Iraq are very much closer - in religion, and ethnicity - to citizens in other neighboring states than they are to each other.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNot everyone within the region is an Arab.  The Arabs are a relatively young people; the spread of the Arabic language, and the faith of Islam, were rather recent events: seventh century A.D. - in a place with cultures dating prior to the second millennium B.C.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe wouldn\\\'t speak of the United States as a German country; and yet the Germans are the most numerous ethnic group within the United States.  But we do repeat, \\\"The Arab state of...\\\" or, \\\"The Arab world...\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHow is it possible to \\\"liberate\\\" people that are not a people? <br>\r\nIf we maintain an Iraq, we maintain the will of the British - and not the will of the citizens of the state that they created.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere is no shortage of human capital in the region. <br>\r\nMost of the graduate students in the physical sciences in the United States are Asians; many of these students are from this region: Southwest Asia. <br>\r\nThe capacity to cause destruction lies within the human mind - and not in some product of human artifice. <br>\r\nHow will pursuing weapons solve the problem of the weapon maker? <br>\r\nEither the end game is a program far more drastic than has yet been mentioned, or the entire pursuit is a folly. <br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt\\\'s something worth worrying about. <br>\r\nAnd it has the potential to lay to waste everything that is good. <br>\r\n</p>',3,3350,0,0,'205.185.2.20','en'),(20075,3327,'2003-04-23 17:28:50',421,'chimeres','<p>Ha, you humor me.  Perhaps i\\\'d be more Nietzschean if i could understand him, damn guy seems to haunt me.  Dreams come up a lot in these posts... like a mysterious collective consciousness that we all work together on. A less lonely escape and a private show, hmm... more than that, it is a little more curious that i thought after all...</p><p>I like aliases more.  People become boring, too concrete.</p>',0,412,175,0,'166.90.45.19','en'),(20076,3340,'2003-04-24 01:19:48',195,'matt','this piece does something weird and pleasing and probing to my brain when i read it.  i like it very much.',1,106,0,0,'209.208.108.236','en'),(20077,2889,'2003-04-24 09:16:23',168,'laney','Dude!! I discovered \\\"the ring.\\\" It\\\'s called NuvaRing I think. It\\\'s this little ring you place strategically inside (it\\\'s actually easier than it sounds). The hormones are a lot less intense because they seep right into your body in little doses, not having to travel through other systems. We\\\'re pretty stoked on it. It also comes with this incredibly cute little timer to remind you to take it out when the 28 days are done. Then it\\\'s out for seven days and the timer clocks that, too. I\\\'m telling you, man, it\\\'s cool.',1,546,0,0,'4.42.121.176','en'),(20078,3100,'2003-04-24 10:11:44',234,'etienne','i miss you, x',1,13,0,0,'210.86.58.50','en'),(20079,3335,'2003-04-24 12:35:40',234,'etienne','ahhhh, wellington. <3',1,21,0,0,'203.96.111.237','en'),(20080,3335,'2003-04-28 01:18:21',17,'austin','beef wellington.<BR>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.leitesculinaria.com/images/beef_well.jpg\\\">',3,91,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20081,3335,'2003-04-28 13:55:37',477,'jehannum','that whooshing sound you can all hear is mr swinney\\\'s comments (once again) going right over my head at high speed. gotta love that guy. :)',1,142,0,0,'203.109.157.89','en'),(20082,2892,'2003-04-28 14:09:28',477,'jehannum','well, i\\\'ll avoid the condolences and hint at a comforting silence instead. suffice to say, you are a wonderful writer.',1,121,187,0,'203.109.157.89','en'),(20083,3335,'2003-04-28 15:29:46',17,'austin','oh, sorry.  that was dumb.  you can say it was dumb!  i\\\'m dumb.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni have been reading a book where the author is always interjecting things about beef wellington.  it had no business in your article.  which is a good article!  i have not been to where i was in the pre-k-3rd grade since i left there.  it would certainly be an interesting trip.  i\\\'d like to meet all the people i knew back then and have long since lost track of them.  ',1,451,477,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20084,3330,'2003-04-28 18:06:46',298,'lurkinginsagas','yes.  there are certain situations, certain stories that i\\\'ve learned to avoid.',1,82,175,0,'198.216.248.250','en'),(20085,2760,'2003-04-28 18:17:40',298,'lurkinginsagas','my sister\\\'s dog barking at the television\r\nsilverware scraping dishes\r\nthumping bass\r\nloud people talking noises in public places\r\nmy mom\\\'s telephone laughter\r\nthe air conditioning systems on buses\r\nsmacking\r\ndoor slamming\r\nnewspapers being repeatedly folded and unfolded',1,277,0,0,'198.216.248.250','en'),(20086,3335,'2003-04-28 20:59:31',66,'Peter','<br><tt>gross...</tt>',1,21,17,0,'66.108.184.212','en'),(20087,3340,'2003-04-28 22:11:38',239,'Poonlarp','thats sort of the point, my man.',1,45,195,0,'66.108.184.212','en'),(20088,3341,'2003-04-29 02:19:00',66,'Peter','<br><tt>way to keep it real, auntie</tt>',1,40,0,0,'66.108.184.212','en'),(20089,3341,'2003-04-29 04:29:53',234,'etienne','i think its a good idea.  maybe it will get further than the fat barbies did, at least.',1,87,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(20090,3341,'2003-04-29 06:50:07',82,'simon','heh. i love this idea. i\\\'m really into <a href=\\\"http://www.scholastic.com/titles/butt/bpbook.htm\\\">this book</a>. and also this one called My Weird Parents about this kid who is totally embarassed by his weird parents then he realizes that they arent that bad after all.',1,278,0,0,'12.236.164.112','en'),(20091,3341,'2003-04-29 10:27:43',154,'ryan','does anyone around here read a.m. homes?',0,40,0,0,'216.99.204.114','en'),(20092,3341,'2003-04-29 17:31:15',82,'simon','i have yes.',0,11,154,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(20093,3341,'2003-04-29 18:43:42',17,'austin','i have not, what should i read?',2,31,154,0,'64.209.168.14','en'),(20094,3341,'2003-04-29 18:50:40',82,'simon','<a href=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/tg/detail/-/0688170838/103-7910781-5532629?vi=glance#product-details\\\">the safety of objects</a>',0,144,17,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(20095,3341,'2003-04-29 19:32:17',74,'jo','luckily, there\\\'s always louis sachar. louis sachar has been keepin\\\' it real for 20 years and his books are GOLD. i have a particular fondness for there\\\'s a boy in the girls bathroom and pig city, but he\\\'s most famous for sideway stories at wayside school.',1,267,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(20096,3341,'2003-04-29 19:32:56',74,'jo','oh and that movie that just came out, holes, the movie advertised as \\\"the book is now the movie,\\\" is by louis sachar.',1,123,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(20097,3341,'2003-04-30 02:22:05',154,'ryan','also, <i>music for torching</i> is good.  it\\\'s based on one of the short stories in the above mentioned title -- the story about the suburban couple that goes on a crack binge.',0,179,17,0,'216.99.204.42','en'),(20098,3341,'2003-04-30 17:39:45',82,'simon','that was certainly my favorite of the stories anyway. i\\\'ll have to check it out.',1,83,154,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(20099,3343,'2003-05-02 18:53:12',17,'austin','hot girl on girl action, no doubt.  is it closer to wrestling or wrastling?',2,75,0,0,'66.35.239.94','en'),(20100,3343,'2003-05-02 19:16:58',176,'Scapegoat','<p>I wish more people could see this sort of activity in the positive light you have framed it.</p>',0,99,0,0,'159.33.2.41','en'),(20101,3342,'2003-05-02 21:10:23',421,'chimeres','Ha ha! So you are paramilitroopers after all!  Awesome.',0,55,0,0,'63.208.131.97','en'),(20102,3343,'2003-05-03 02:52:05',234,'etienne','that\\\'s why i wrote it.  <br><br>(this was written in december 2001 but posted again for austin.)',1,99,176,0,'210.86.56.79','en'),(20103,3338,'2003-05-03 08:10:23',187,'kandrer','again, test of a comment',1,24,0,0,'219.125.207.59','en'),(20104,3338,'2003-05-03 08:11:03',187,'kandrer','et avÃ© les accents: Ã¢Ã©ÃªÃ®Ã»Ã¹Ã¨Ã´Ã§Ã Ã¯...',1,34,0,0,'219.125.207.59','fr'),(20105,3343,'2003-05-03 18:22:02',195,'matt','this makes me wet',1,17,0,0,'24.129.129.219','en'),(20106,3342,'2003-05-03 21:40:21',195,'matt','man, i love me some samoas.  i wish i had one right now.',1,56,0,0,'209.208.109.159','en'),(20107,3343,'2003-05-03 22:44:49',17,'austin','don\\\'t read it in the rain then!',3,34,195,0,'66.35.239.94','en'),(20108,3343,'2003-05-04 04:22:09',477,'jehannum','i used to wrestle at university way back in... well it seems like aeons ago now. some of the images you have conjured up for me are haunting and yet comforting. all this was in the days long before i became skinny-pale-writer-man, naturally. a different life. and you set the whole piece to massive attack... gush, worship, an so forth...',1,338,0,0,'203.109.196.75','en'),(20109,3270,'2003-05-04 04:30:59',477,'jehannum','oh my god, can they PLEASE take that damned \\\"loyal\\\" billboard on fanshawe street down already!? hello, we lost! might have had something to do with them not ever testing the boat in race conditions (nice plan guys, you should run for office).',1,248,0,0,'203.109.196.75','en'),(20110,3343,'2003-05-04 21:32:33',17,'austin','by the way, why is jiu-jitsu ok and not boxing?',2,47,234,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20111,3320,'2003-05-05 02:45:14',75,'tyler','how far can it go before there\\\'s just a civil war?',1,53,0,0,'66.32.60.30','en'),(20112,3344,'2003-05-05 04:40:11',234,'etienne','<i>a white mask is no cause for alarm.</i> <br>\r\ni beg to differ on this point; if you go into just about any chemist on queen st there are packs of masks available for purchase accompanied by appropriately fearmongering signs.',1,227,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(20113,3344,'2003-05-05 18:04:02',82,'simon','on that note: louis vitton and gucci are selling designer masks in hong kong. oooh!',0,83,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(20114,3344,'2003-05-05 18:11:39',90,'leonard','That is too damn funny. I hope you don\\\'t mind but I will be using that line for the rest of the week.',1,104,82,0,'67.118.80.121','en'),(20115,3344,'2003-05-05 20:36:59',477,'jehannum','the only thing more depressing than that, will be the fact that they\\\'ll probably sell out of them...',1,103,82,0,'203.109.196.75','en'),(20116,3344,'2003-05-05 21:34:00',82,'simon','depressing? hmm. am i sick for thinking that was kind of fabulous? probably.',1,76,477,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(20117,3344,'2003-05-05 22:05:20',234,'etienne','paul frank\\\'s making them too...',1,34,82,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(20118,3342,'2003-05-05 22:10:21',234,'etienne','i wasn\\\'t allowed to join the girl guides because my mother had been one.  what\\\'s the deal with that?',1,106,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(20119,3344,'2003-05-05 22:32:22',82,'simon','trust, love, i\\\'ll be picking a few up to match <a href=\\\"http://www.luxuryfashion.com/lv_sportsneaker.jpg\\\">my shoes</a>!',1,128,234,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(20120,3344,'2003-05-06 00:22:03',90,'leonard','Those are nice!',1,15,82,0,'67.118.80.121','en'),(20121,3320,'2003-05-06 16:42:50',187,'kandrer','sigh... who will fix our democracies ?',1,38,0,0,'219.125.202.124','en'),(20122,3341,'2003-05-06 17:32:25',75,'tyler','just don\\\'t write another Bridge To Terabithia--i\\\'m still upset about that one.',1,84,0,0,'66.32.60.30','en'),(20123,3335,'2003-05-07 01:20:15',228,'belly','i used to love beef wellington. but when i moved to wellington i was already vegetarian. so i never got to eat beef wellington in wellington. ahh, what\\\'s a life without regrets?</p> \r\n<p>\r\nmaybe one day i will get to watch casablanca in casablanca. that would compensate, some.\r\n</p>',0,286,0,0,'210.173.56.166','en'),(20124,3344,'2003-05-07 01:55:37',228,'belly','<p>i\\\'ve been in japan 9 months and the masks still squick me out a little. people here generally wear them when they are sick, so as not to spread the germy jims, rather than as a precaution. but seeing a kid with his face half covered in white gauze just makes me think \\\"go home! if you\\\'re that bloody sick, go to bed\\\". but of course it doesn\\\'t work like that here. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni think it would be a racist reaction if the people you are talking about are chinese-new zealanders, or maori, or anyone else that grew up in the same culture as you. but that\\\'s not what you\\\'re talking about. i think you\\\'re talking about cultural differences, and observing your reaction to them is more anthropological than anything else. \r\n</p><p>\r\ndon\\\'t underestimate how much your culture informs how you feel about things. it\\\'s not racism, it\\\'s just a reaction to difference that is human.\r\n</p><p>\r\nin some ways living in japan has made me a lot more tolerant of some cultural differences, a lot more understanding. in other ways i find myself making more generalisations, less excuses for behaviours i can\\\'t get my head around as easily. in the end, you have to recognise that different cultures breed different behaviours. and it\\\'s ok for them to confuse or even annoy you, because it\\\'s not about the colour of their or your skin, it\\\'s because you grew up in a different place, with different social rules and different ways of interacting with the world. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni know that people here definitely get really annoyed with things i do that are totally fine in my own culture, but not so fine here. and most of the time i try to modify my behaviour, but sometimes i can\\\'t be arsed, because living in another culture and constantly monitoring and modifying your own behaviour can be really exhausting. \r\n</p><p>\r\nthe most any of us humans can do is try to understand that the confusing weird things aren\\\'t bad, they\\\'re just different, and cut each other a bit of slack.</p>',0,2015,0,0,'210.173.56.166','en'),(20125,3344,'2003-05-07 02:39:11',176,'Scapegoat','I would have to agree with the notion of cutting each other some slack. Was the motivation to cross the street because you can\\\'t stand being near asain people? That is not the impression I get. More likely you are being over sensitive to a reaction that was based on a health concern, that has been blown out of proportion to a hysterical degree by the media, and back filled it with a little self loathing. But thats just my take.<br><br>\r\n   ',0,447,0,0,'66.185.85.68','en'),(20126,3335,'2003-05-07 10:22:28',175,'Paul G','Almost every night I park my taxi, and eat at a diner on <a href=\\\"http://www.transitchicago.com/maps/maps/F2002N.html\\\">Wellington</a> (3000 N).<br>\r\nThe guy who owns the taxi is from Morocco; he just sold his home in <a href=\\\"http://www.theodora.com/maps/new/morocco.gif\\\">Casablanca</a>.',1,299,228,0,'209.109.226.48','en'),(20127,3341,'2003-05-07 14:20:37',74,'jo','SHOCK!\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\nSHOCK!',4,44,75,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(20128,3344,'2003-05-07 20:17:23',123,'dcc','<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/lvmask4.jpg\\\">',0,63,0,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(20129,3344,'2003-05-07 21:34:32',17,'austin','that is pretty sexy.',2,20,123,0,'66.128.224.196','en'),(20130,3347,'2003-05-07 22:56:59',66,'Peter','<br><tt>solid, paulg, solid.</tt>',1,33,0,0,'66.108.189.16','en'),(20131,3347,'2003-05-07 23:41:53',90,'leonard','I like Eddy\\\'s smile.',1,23,0,0,'67.118.80.121','en'),(20132,3347,'2003-05-08 01:49:43',239,'Poonlarp','eddy is hot. id fuck him.',1,25,90,0,'66.108.185.93','en'),(20133,3322,'2003-05-08 04:33:27',488,'xiolaBleu','<p>that was nice..better than nice, uplifting.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i was walking home from university one night, sleeping on this guys bedroom floor and surviving off boiled noodles and parmesan cheese, oh and it was raining...</P>\r\n\r\n<P>hard for california.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i was in the middle of thinking how awful humanity was that no one cared about silly me, walking at near 11pm in the pouring rain when this chick pulled up and asked me if i needed a ride. i was next to the apt. complex by this time, but it was a wonderful moment</P>',1,526,0,0,'66.59.238.140','en'),(20134,2788,'2003-05-08 04:46:58',488,'xiolaBleu','<P> lovely.tense</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i have been in situations the same. you want to apologize..well, at least i did. you want to say that what came out of your mouth were not the right words, but if you do you dig yourself deeper into a hole.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> i don.t think your friends are morons. <--someone\\\'s post mention this.nationality is a tricky issue. in one of my theory courses we have had yelling fights over it.</P>',1,416,187,0,'66.59.238.140','en'),(20135,3347,'2003-05-08 11:12:32',175,'Paul G','<p>\r\nSo this woman walks into the gas station.  And she\\\'s hot: she looks like <a href=\\\"http://www.wapers.com/people/carmenelectra/carmenelectra3.jpg\\\">Carmen Electra</a>.\r\nAnyway, she asks if there\\\'s a bathroom that she can use; so Eddy shows her where it is.\r\nThen the Carmen Electra woman pokes her head out of the bathroom door:<br>\r\nCEW: \\\"Hey, you want a blow-job?\\\"<br>\r\nEddy: \\\"My job is not blow; it\\\'s a good job.\\\"<br>\r\nCEW: \\\"?\\\"<br>\r\nSo this is a big joke between us, now.  Not a night passes when one of us isn\\\'t offered a blow-job in exchange for something.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSometimes Eddy is out of sight: he\\\'s on the floor praying, or in the back room getting supplies.<br>\r\nEddy: \\\"People say I was doing the jag-off.\\\"<br>\r\nSo this too is a big joke for us.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI told Eddy that he needs eight arms - like an octopus - so that he can work the register, clean the store, get the White Owls, and do the jag-off at the same time.\r\nAnd Eddy corrects me:<br>\r\nEddy: \\\"NO, the <i>fuckin\\\'</i> jag-off.\\\"<br>\r\nBecause that\\\'s the way that we speak, in America.\r\n</p>',1,1123,0,0,'209.109.238.9','en'),(20136,3306,'2003-05-08 15:04:57',187,'kandrer','well, i have to switch your code page to japanese (shift Jis) for you to see this post...\r\n<META http-equiv=Content-Type content=\\\"text/html; charset=shift_jis\\\">\r\n',1,168,0,0,'219.125.211.3','en'),(20137,3342,'2003-05-08 21:35:50',210,'may','samoas to be had for all. if you have 3.50 plus s&h, of course/',0,63,0,0,'134.74.74.164','en'),(20138,3347,'2003-05-08 22:29:10',83,'adamajah','I\\\'m glad I took the time to read this.',0,41,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(20139,3344,'2003-05-09 00:20:46',57,'weeb','are those really your shoes??',0,29,82,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(20140,2821,'2003-05-09 03:20:56',187,'kandrer','i checked a few definitions today and arranged them in interesting order :</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>patriotism</b> : <br>\r\nLove of country and willingness to sacrifice for it\r\n</p><p>\r\n<b>nationalism</b> : <br>\r\n1. Love of country and willingness to sacrifice for it<br>\r\n2. The conviction that the culture and interests of your nation are superior to those of any other nation\r\n</p><p>\r\n<b>racism</b> : <br>\r\n1. The prejudice that members of one race are intrinsically superior to members of other races<br>\r\n2. Discriminatory or abusive behavior towards members of another race\r\n</p>\r\nit does not seem to be such a long way to go, uh?',1,624,0,0,'219.125.202.44','en'),(20141,3347,'2003-05-09 05:22:24',421,'chimeres','this is really great to read.  it totally inspires exploring people more, and fighting those counter-tendencies that pop up, whatever they happen to be at the time.  But it is just good to read, too.',0,199,0,0,'67.74.51.182','en'),(20142,3347,'2003-05-09 06:53:28',187,'kandrer','yup! i can\\\'t wait for next post/part! :)',1,43,175,0,'219.125.214.87','en'),(20143,3344,'2003-05-10 03:29:20',477,'jehannum','i can see the campaign slogans now... \\\"SARS\\\" the sexy disease... \\\"wear one of our masks and she\\\'ll have you panting more than a severe respiratory attack\\\"... \\\"if you\\\'re gonna go, go in style...\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\ni\\\'m gonna laugh at this because it\\\'s the only reaction i can mount that doesn\\\'t involve depression or rage.',1,346,123,0,'203.109.157.66','en'),(20144,3347,'2003-05-10 05:26:10',17,'austin','Sweet candy, Paul G.',1,20,175,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20145,3228,'2003-05-12 01:45:25',234,'etienne','there\\\'s not really much to say other than that this is amazing, and that doesn\\\'t seem enough.',1,99,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(20146,3349,'2003-05-12 03:06:15',82,'simon','CHIA! chia chia chia! and stuff.',1,32,0,0,'67.121.84.106','en'),(20147,3083,'2003-05-12 09:14:50',234,'etienne','today was the perfect day to read this.',1,39,0,0,'210.86.59.133','en'),(20148,3349,'2003-05-12 09:45:43',175,'Paul G','Yeah - it made me remember <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2564\\\">this</a>, by austin, <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2901\\\">and this</a>, by gus.\r\n<br>\r\nAnd these were/are some of my favorite things.\r\n<p>\r\nThe number of references/connections to SE Asia, around the site, is interesting.\r\n</p>',1,357,0,0,'209.109.236.59','en'),(20149,1309,'2003-05-12 10:50:45',234,'etienne','<p>i did improv & comedy at a theatre here for two years.  comedians are even more permanently \\\"on stage\\\" than actors.  & yes, there are lots of hotties.</p>\r\n<p>the highlight was a workshop we did with <a href=\\\"http://www.keithjohnstone.com/\\\">keith johnstone</a>.  the man is a fucking god.  </p>',1,309,0,0,'210.86.56.48','en'),(20150,2901,'2003-05-12 12:52:38',93,'chiamattt','I liked this post a lot today. ',1,31,0,0,'203.144.64.10','en'),(20151,3349,'2003-05-12 20:04:43',17,'austin','what an adventure land thailand is.  i hope you make it out unscathed.  take lomo pics of the lightning, too.',3,109,0,0,'66.128.224.196','en'),(20152,3349,'2003-05-13 19:06:12',124,'gus','My driver was named Wanna (pronounced Vanna) and he was a pretty smart cookie. I agree it\\\'s best to have the same driver. Pay extra and go to the farther temples, do it all. \r\n<p>\r\nHalf the things you do in cambodia are a total scam if you\\\'re western. You\\\'re paying 100% tourist tax on everything pretty much. but oh well. \r\n<p>\r\nGo to the fancy hotel in town and have yourself a fancy cocktail in the aircon and play some pool. \r\n<p>\r\nAnd remember, dont go overland to Poipet from Siem Reap. If you want to share the experience, read my story about it, but there\\\'s no need to subject yourself to the ultimate torture for a fee. I was there in april, same hot season, what you need is a lightweight long sleeved light colored shirt, and a hat. and a water bottle IV drip.\r\n',0,785,0,0,'64.172.59.236','en'),(20153,3350,'2003-05-14 03:18:51',477,'jehannum','wow. dark and brooding with a terrifying familiarity. like it.',1,62,0,0,'203.109.157.66','en'),(20154,3352,'2003-05-14 09:48:41',234,'etienne','oh man, this made me think of my grandfather who was another of the new zealand porridge mafioso.  \r\nwhen i stayed with my grandparents in the lovely tauranga, he\\\'d wake me up every morning to ask me if i wanted porridge - almost as if i might not that morning or as if it was an anomaly that i might eat it for breakfast.  \r\nbreakfast time was our time, as my domineering grandmother would wake \\\"with the birds\\\", eat toast on the porch and then go back to bed until about 10.  when she did rise for the day it would be the end of any peace in the house until she went to sleep at night.  \r\ni never gave up the opportunity to have it and i have never had porridge as good ever since.  my mother tried valiantly (and still does) but nothing can beat the almost-silent white-haired man slowly and carefully preparing the porridge we savoured in the only \\\"us\\\" time we had during the day.',1,899,0,0,'210.86.56.187','en'),(20155,3350,'2003-05-14 11:42:05',175,'Paul G','It\\\'s very effective prose.  It almost all felt like something that was written by someone 10-20 years older than the person who wrote it.  It\\\'s not just the words...but I think a sophisticated understanding of the dynamics of such a relationship, from \\\"her\\\" perspective.  You must be very observant...',1,313,0,0,'209.109.224.224','en'),(20156,3352,'2003-05-14 21:33:19',477,'jehannum','god i can taste the brown sugar, freshly melted and stirred through the porridge. i can see my grandparents\\\' kitchen in christchurch. i remember alsways getting a kick out of seeing my own breath when i walked to the mailbox to get the paper for dad in the mornings. what is it about brown sugar and porridge being inextricably linked with childhood in new zealand?',1,368,0,0,'203.109.157.66','en'),(20157,3351,'2003-05-15 17:07:59',187,'kandrer','very nice!<br>\r\ni would say that what you called \\\"the understanding\\\" might in fact had been something else ; like you decided to take control of the dream because it was becoming too painful.<br>\r\nwithout being a fan of JLo, nor thinking that the movie is perfect, i enjoyed seeing \\\"the cell\\\" because it depicted the relations between dreams and consciousness/fantasms in a credible way. it showed a few close-to-normal-but-not-exactly scary sceneries, a bit like your recurrent dream.',1,497,0,0,'219.125.220.72','en'),(20158,3350,'2003-05-15 17:20:07',187,'kandrer','i got quite scared during this story... i am glad at the pessimistic-but-quiet ending.  even more scared than during the \\\"black crow\\\" one. i think that i am more OK to enter anybody\\\'s nightmare, than to enter strangers\\\' bedroom.',1,240,0,0,'219.125.220.72','en'),(20159,3350,'2003-05-16 06:55:02',234,'etienne','it\\\'s actually about two men i know.  the first (up until the bed) is my old flatmate who turned really weird; the second is an old boyfriend.  i\\\'m not in contact with either anymore but i\\\'m glad that they provided me with this.',0,236,0,0,'203.96.111.237','en'),(20160,3353,'2003-05-16 22:25:01',75,'tyler','well...for short periods of time, anyway.',1,41,0,0,'66.32.60.30','en'),(20161,3353,'2003-05-17 02:01:33',75,'tyler','i am filled with contradictions.',1,32,0,0,'66.32.60.30','en'),(20162,3350,'2003-05-17 07:40:07',120,'joyrider','i am thinking about the cold bedroom, and the woman rubbing her legs together to keep warm.  later she is shying away from the man\\\'s touch.  <p>lonliness has its root all wrong.</p>',0,184,0,0,'12.252.221.216','en'),(20163,2694,'2003-05-17 11:25:39',183,'lin','<p>When I was writing on my page this morning, I was thinking about my anxiety issues, and whether or not other people really deal with things the way I do. After reading your article I decided that I think you and I get along so well, and understand each other because we have different experiences that often lead us to the same conclusions. </p>\r\n<p> I\\\'ve often wondered where self doubt comes from. Is it fostered in us when we\\\'re children and we have some weird flaw that makes us one of the kids the others run away from? Is it due to our parents not understanding adolescence. I haven\\\'t really discovered what it is that makes me or anyone doubt their place, but over time, building on friendships, advice and experience I\\\'ve learned that letting people in again is often the bext way to get past things that kept them out for a very long time.</p> \r\n<p> I can remember conversations I had at a time with someone who ultimately did awful things to me, but in the end, became a positive and constructive force in my life.. one of them was just a comment to me, when I said \\\"well, who am I to...\\\" .. and the answer was, \\\"who would you need to be?\\\" ... \r\nThere is something in that for me, because who do we need to become before our opinions, experiences, selves, our love for others, is valid?</p>\r\n<p> I think we are who we need to be, because we can talk and love and joke around with each other. Sometimes we just need a little PR for the world that hasn\\\'t come to understand our valid points of view. And sometimes we just need to learn confidence, like as if it were a program. The more you run through it on a daily basis, the more you understand it and can impliment its uses in your daily operations.. Anyway. I think you\\\'re brave, among other fabulous qualities that you usually forget about. </p>',0,1842,70,0,'64.230.169.110','en'),(20164,3353,'2003-05-17 14:24:26',187,'kandrer','good luck! :)',1,13,75,0,'210.198.207.123','en'),(20165,3352,'2003-05-18 14:16:35',175,'Paul G','<p>\r\nporridge is a sort of warm, familiar, sustaining thing.\r\nand the style of the piece matched the subject: it was nostalgic.\r\nbut the piece was called dad, not porridge.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso, a conflation of the ideas of fatherhood and food took place.\r\nand it worked, well.\r\n8 paragraphs:\r\n6 contain a reference to food;\r\n6 contain a reference to fatherhood.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni think that it\\\'s easy for most people to identify a particular food product that reminds them of their home, and family.\r\njust like it\\\'s easy for most people to understand how it feels when some warm, familiar, sustaining thing is missing from their life.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit was good after the first reading; and it\\\'s held up after a few readings.\r\n</p>',1,728,0,0,'67.195.182.37','en'),(20166,3355,'2003-05-18 17:45:23',17,'austin','a read like orange juice on a saturday morning.',2,47,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20167,3356,'2003-05-18 20:40:53',75,'tyler','cool',1,4,0,0,'66.32.60.30','en'),(20168,3355,'2003-05-18 20:41:43',75,'tyler','pert ass',1,8,0,0,'66.32.60.30','en'),(20169,3137,'2003-05-19 07:51:34',120,'joyrider','\\\"chalice and blade\\\" - read some excerpts, mostly quotes in terrance mckenna books, but haven\\\'t read the whole thing.  i\\\'ve been meaning to, but the reading list is backed up.\r\n\r\ni\\\'ve read \\\"ishmael,\\\" though.  i\\\'d read things like it, so it wasn\\\'t a real paradigm shift, but i\\\'m glad it\\\'s out there.  a lot of people have read it and thought about the ideas in there, and that\\\'s a start. ',0,422,0,0,'12.252.221.216','en'),(20170,3356,'2003-05-19 16:10:11',90,'leonard','I love the title. Nice....',1,26,0,0,'67.118.80.121','en'),(20171,3070,'2003-05-20 16:19:13',232,'necklace','hey mima, how are you? how have you been? have you moved out? did you run away?',0,79,0,0,'193.1.100.103','en'),(20172,3357,'2003-05-22 01:23:08',90,'leonard','At least you didn\\\'t receive some Sean \\\"P Diddy\\\" Combs. That would have been fucked.',1,92,0,0,'67.118.80.121','en'),(20173,3357,'2003-05-22 05:29:11',187,'kandrer','i am depressed since yesterday because it seems (the dentist said so but i don\\\'t believe him) that i have a cavity.\r\nbut here, you see, i have no hope that Austin could send me anything to fix this, from Texas or anywhere, and even if he wanted to. i looked for comfort in phylosophy. socrates told me this morning : <i>know thyself</i>. i wonder if by this he means that i should let the dentist investigate further on my tooth...</p>\r\nanyway, nice story as ever, Paul. let us know when you figure the number\\\'s meaning (any relationship with my tooth that you may find is of first interest to me, thanks)',1,611,0,0,'219.125.215.234','en'),(20174,3357,'2003-05-22 05:32:09',187,'kandrer','hey, but this IS really Austin on the picture, dammit! fantastic! i like the comb\\\'s one too, though.',1,103,0,0,'219.125.215.234','en'),(20175,3357,'2003-05-22 10:48:38',93,'chiamattt','I liked this. ',1,14,0,0,'61.40.66.67','en'),(20176,3349,'2003-05-22 10:49:35',93,'chiamattt','I am pro 100% tourist price. Tourism is all they have. ',1,55,0,0,'61.40.66.67','en'),(20177,3357,'2003-05-22 11:02:41',175,'Paul G','<p>\r\nNo, no.\r\n<br>\r\nThis is <a href=\\\"http://www.dammit.com/photos/01-06-15%20dammit.jpg\\\">dammit</a>;\r\n<br>\r\nthis is <a href=\\\"http://www.kfki.hu/~arthp/art/m/michelan/1sculptu/david/david.jpg\\\">austin</a>; \r\n<br>\r\nand this is <a href=\\\"http://monograph.org/img/kandrer_01.jpg\\\">you</a>.\r\n</p>',1,306,187,0,'209.109.226.174','en'),(20178,3357,'2003-05-22 15:03:00',175,'Paul G','<a href=\\\"http://ak1.aka.eonline.com/7/1480/1218/0001/www.eonline.com/Features/Awards/Grammys2002/Pics/Images/pics.combs2.jpg\\\">Right on!</a>',4,145,90,0,'199.35.210.241','en'),(20179,3357,'2003-05-23 06:45:38',187,'kandrer','i was a bit sad that you drew me with a flag (in france, only nationalist morons display a flag, i think), but i admit that it looks like me a lot. :)\r\n</p><p>\r\ni take this (french flag) occasion to ask readers who can understand (easy-)written french to modify their settings so that they can see my posts in french... well, this is if you are not angry about that UN story, he he he...',1,387,175,0,'219.125.222.209','en'),(20180,3357,'2003-05-23 12:23:48',175,'Paul G','<a href=\\\"http://monograph.org/img/kandrer_02.jpg\\\">better?</a>',1,67,187,0,'209.109.236.167','en'),(20181,3357,'2003-05-23 14:51:52',187,'kandrer','yup !<br>\r\n[x] fromage<br>\r\n[x] wine<br>\r\n[x] complaining',1,57,175,0,'219.125.218.147','en'),(20182,3357,'2003-05-23 15:20:03',17,'austin','kandrer; this is some funny bits.  that really does look like him.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni have a headache from the bushmills.  and i am trying really really hard not to spend a bunch of money on something irrational.  it is the temptation.  i was in bed last night, after i closed the book American Gods, that i thought i could generally pray to The-God-Who-Is-Responsible-For-Me and it might work.  All the gods would sort out what percentage of effect for prayer should come from them.  i really thought this was a good idea.  i still do.',3,530,175,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20183,3357,'2003-05-23 15:23:15',17,'austin','i\\\'m sure there would be conflicting interests among the gods as to whether i should spend my money on irrational things that are expensive.  but the existance of gods is irrational, so would they explode just thinking about it?  i\\\'m not sure.  it worries me.\r\n<BR>*destabilizing*',4,285,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20184,3357,'2003-05-23 20:31:34',154,'ryan','i think it is interesting that most people of europe view displaying their flags as nationlistic (at least the people i know in germany, france, and holland) while in america it is viewed (generally) as the honorable thing to do.\r\n<p>\r\nthose pig-fucking americans.',0,264,0,0,'216.99.204.10','en'),(20185,3175,'2003-05-24 00:54:33',409,'rachel','i met the one i love for the first time at a club called 11:11. xep brought him there. \r\n',1,89,0,0,'64.81.241.248','en'),(20186,2976,'2003-05-24 03:20:16',228,'belly','did it get easier? \r\n<p>\r\ni still swallow back tears whenever i am with my ex and remember some moment of intimacy long gone, when i feel those invisible walls stopping me from touching him the way i used to touch him, when we hug and it doesn\\\'t comfort me the way it used to. i miss the smell of home and wonder if i will ever look at him again without tears stinging and pricking at my eyes. </p>\r\n<p>\r\ntell me it gets easier.\r\n</p>',0,437,0,0,'210.155.207.64','en'),(20187,2740,'2003-05-24 03:39:03',228,'belly','this article inspired my best mix cd ever. \r\n<p>\r\nthese songs make me feel better:<br>\r\nbeta band \\\'dry the rain\\\'<br>\r\nghetto boys, \\\'damn it feels good to be a gangster\\\'<br>\r\nshuggie otis, \\\'inspiration information\\\'<br>\r\nbrad, \\\'the day brings\\\'<br>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n ',0,282,0,0,'210.155.207.64','en'),(20188,3358,'2003-05-25 00:19:05',75,'tyler','i thought the one about the baby was cute.',1,42,0,0,'66.245.48.164','en'),(20189,3359,'2003-05-25 00:24:14',75,'tyler','be careful, dude!',1,17,0,0,'66.245.48.164','en'),(20190,3360,'2003-05-25 06:56:27',154,'ryan','maybe you\\\'re the cunt?',1,25,0,0,'216.99.204.96','en'),(20191,3361,'2003-05-25 17:43:51',17,'austin','<TT>:(</TT>',4,11,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20192,3361,'2003-05-25 17:55:10',82,'simon','sigh. :(',0,8,0,0,'67.121.84.106','en'),(20193,3359,'2003-05-25 19:08:32',93,'chiamattt','i wrote the next part and my computer crashed. blast!',6,53,0,0,'61.40.66.95','en'),(20194,3361,'2003-05-26 02:49:04',234,'etienne','this reminds me of why i don\\\'t tell my mother anything about my life.  she thinks that women are lesbians because \\\"they are more adaptable\\\".  i went to see a lesbian film at the outtakes fest with jo on friday night and decided it was best to tell her that i was seeing x-men2 again.  bleh.\r\n*hugs to alana*',1,316,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(20195,3360,'2003-05-26 11:33:13',477,'jehannum','<p>well there isn\\\'t any maybe about it. that was really the point of the whole story...</p>',3,94,154,0,'203.109.157.88','en'),(20196,3358,'2003-05-26 13:36:24',175,'Paul G','The form of the piece was different - and it worked, for me.  I wondered not so much about the issues raised in the individual articles, but rather about something like, \\\"citizen\\\'s responsibilty.\\\"  \r\n<p>\r\nIs it possible to, \\\"blame,\\\" people for not being aware of affairs that they cannot hope to affect - especially when the effort to keep abreast of the news results in some sort of psychic damage.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt\\\'s a very difficult thing: with only a limited amount of time and energy to spend, how ought we to spend it?  It\\\'s not possible to read everything - and 1/2 of what we read isn\\\'t likely to be true, anyway.  Maybe it\\\'s better to concentrate on small, meaningful acts?  But then we leave others to make the, \\\"big decisions,\\\" for us?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI don\\\'t know...\r\n</p> ',1,815,0,0,'209.109.226.67','en'),(20197,3361,'2003-05-26 13:43:58',231,'alana','shmmm. dup. mew.',3,16,0,0,'203.220.42.26','en'),(20198,3357,'2003-05-26 13:49:27',175,'Paul G','And to think: I only saw him once, in an mpeg.  If both you and he agree, then art school finally paid off.\r\n<p>\r\nSave your money.  Irrational?  Let\\\'s join the French Foreign Legion.  We can smoke with kandrer, someplace in Africa.  Everything will make sense then.  I will drink red wine, and call you by your nom de guerre.\r\n</p>  ',1,336,17,0,'209.109.226.67','en'),(20199,3357,'2003-05-26 22:16:53',195,'matt','ATTENTION:<BR><BR>do not spend money on something irrational. do not save it.  send it to:<BR><BR>Friends of Matt McCord<BR>22213 Croom Road<BR>Brooksville, FL 34601<BR><BR>postal money order(s) will be fine.  we thank you kindly. :)',1,233,17,0,'209.208.109.22','en'),(20200,3365,'2003-05-27 09:04:42',175,'Paul G','loving these, chia.\r\n<p>\r\nI thought of you tonight, when a guy in the back claimed to make his living playing the piano at the \\\"royal orchid sheraton\\\" in bangkok.  i have no idea if there is such a hotel, let alone a piano in it.  but he was a dollar short - so he may have been a fibber.\r\n</p>',1,300,0,0,'67.195.31.1','en'),(20201,3365,'2003-05-27 15:28:13',86,'egg','go to ao thong tha khian. ',0,26,0,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(20202,3364,'2003-05-27 18:37:49',195,'matt','nine is a magic number',1,22,0,0,'209.208.110.168','en'),(20203,2965,'2003-05-27 21:09:26',309,'slacker','ah yes, the humping incident.  how many churches will i taint with my love?',0,75,148,0,'64.230.138.5','en'),(20204,3364,'2003-05-28 00:05:30',298,'lurkinginsagas','i love you.',1,11,0,0,'207.218.215.208','en'),(20205,3365,'2003-05-28 04:37:38',124,'gus','do not go to pantong on phuket! koh phi phi was nice, there were a couple of really fantastic dive spots with light blue and lavander coral, but I heard some of them got damaged since I was there... <p>\r\nI just love those second class sleeper train cars. they make your bed all crisp and clean, and you can buy beer from it! hehe.',0,330,0,0,'64.163.215.46','en'),(20206,3364,'2003-05-28 06:41:34',449,'s','I love you and magic numbers.',2,29,0,0,'66.32.179.168','en'),(20207,3361,'2003-05-28 07:51:01',228,'belly','i kind of just wanted to email this to you, but i don\\\'t know where to send it, so...\r\n<p>\r\ni think she sounds scared. i think all mums want their kids to have easier, better lives than then. maybe your mum is scared your life isn\\\'t going to be easier than hers, and she doesn\\\'t know how to handle it. maybe your mum is scared people will think she did something wrong as a mother or wife to have raised a daughter who isn\\\'t into men (more likely, maybe she is the one who thinks she did something wrong). maybe your mum is just scared she won\\\'t get to be a grandma. \r\n</p><p>\r\nyeah, she\\\'s being selfish and she is handling it really badly. and i don\\\'t know anything about your family. but i think maybe if you could talk to her about her fears, it might make it easier for her to get used to? it seems like you might have to be the grown-up in this situation. \r\n</p>\r\ngood luck!!!\r\n',2,903,0,0,'61.125.243.253','en'),(20208,3364,'2003-05-28 13:58:20',175,'Paul G','2 and 9',1,7,0,0,'209.109.224.133','en'),(20209,3358,'2003-05-28 14:43:07',124,'gus','I didnt read or listen to the news for like a year after the OJ trial. Before that I listened to NPR everyday at my crappy temp job, then OJ happened and they pre-empted EVERY SINGLE BIT OF NEWS except traffic to cover OJ. I realized that none of that other news must actually be important if they can do that, so why pay any attention? ',0,337,0,0,'64.163.215.46','en'),(20210,3366,'2003-05-28 21:45:15',449,'s','wow, what an image. ',2,20,0,0,'66.32.179.168','en'),(20211,3367,'2003-05-28 23:50:31',477,'jehannum','very nice work. as someone who used to suffer from back pain it was very familiar... ouch.',1,90,0,0,'203.109.157.88','en'),(20212,3358,'2003-05-28 23:52:00',477,'jehannum','note to self; cross congo of list of potential hoilday destinations...',1,70,0,0,'203.109.157.88','en'),(20213,3364,'2003-05-28 23:53:33',477,'jehannum','wicked. plain and simple, it\\\'s purely wicked.',1,48,0,0,'203.109.157.88','en'),(20214,3365,'2003-05-29 00:04:35',477,'jehannum','oh man i want to travel this year. living in a backpackers\\\' and seeing only people who are travelling the world swapping stories does enough for the travel bug to bite me, but matt you are making it much worse! (oh and that was meant as a compliment dude).',1,259,0,0,'203.109.157.88','en'),(20215,3364,'2003-05-29 00:07:18',90,'leonard','the odd thumb out. do you like hats?',2,36,449,0,'67.118.80.121','en'),(20216,3365,'2003-05-29 00:22:00',124,'gus','Oh, that was Patong, not Pantong. Though the Thais will spell it however they want anyhow. \r\n<p>\r\nIf you dont mind, Please have some pomfret with 3 flavors sauce while you\\\'re in soutern thailand, and maybe a little spicey green papaya salad on the side. For desert if you can find Wall ice cream, and get the one in the green and purple wrapper.\r\n<p>\r\nsniff. i miss that food.',0,379,0,0,'63.198.55.58','en'),(20217,3367,'2003-05-29 00:28:28',124,'gus','It makes me want to read \\\"Talk To Me Like The Rain, And Let Me Listen\\\" by Tennesse Williams. if you havent, do it.',0,120,0,0,'63.198.55.58','en'),(20218,3364,'2003-05-29 01:39:17',449,'s','Muchly random. But yes, as a matter of fact, I collect them...\r\n<br>\r\nOh...holy shit - josh?',4,92,90,0,'66.32.176.149','en'),(20219,3357,'2003-05-29 03:58:33',187,'kandrer','<i>Everything will make sense then.</i> In deed. Thank you for making me laugh.',1,79,175,0,'219.125.210.122','en'),(20220,3358,'2003-05-29 04:33:07',187,'kandrer','Maybe my problem with the news is also due to the fact that I don\\\'t see people anymore. Seeing people regularly, hearing about their \\\"close to normal\\\" lives, would help me to identify these news as what they are or should be : exceptions.<br>\r\nIt is impossible for me (and, I guess, for any medium-sensitive person) to carry on a \\\"normal\\\" life if I am being told everyday about babies monitored through air traffic control, liver-eating rebels, etc (I am not familiar with OJ trial).<br>\r\nThe internet delivering of news was supposed to bring us better quality informations. We can still dream of having several viewpoints on each news, and links to related topics that would help to grasp the importance of a fact. Later, maybe...<br>\r\nAnd Paul, I did not think about it before your comment, but it\\\'s right that, after spending a while reading the news, I sometimes got the feeling that I <i>understand</i> what is happening (which is true and false, as you pointed out). That made me want to express myself more about any issue. That gave me the (unreasonable) hope of changing things. Since everything society gives me to express myself is a vote here and there, I will feel better minding about closer things that I can see and touch.',1,1256,0,0,'219.125.210.122','en'),(20221,3330,'2003-05-29 05:09:04',477,'jehannum','<p>the human body can\\\'t remember pain, they say. but right now i can feel that sort of empty ache along my arms and across my chest that i get whenever i think of one of two women that i am no longer with.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s an awful thing that we seem to able to remember, deep down, how to feel bad, just as if no time had passed, and even if it were never our fault in the first place. yet somehow, remembering happy times does not always make one feel strong, confident or even a little happy.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i intend to complain to the manufacturer immediately...></p>',1,570,298,0,'203.109.157.88','en'),(20222,3357,'2003-05-29 15:46:39',477,'jehannum','oooo, mr swinney is reading gaiman! let us know if it\\\'s any good austin, cos i loved smoke and mirrors, but there\\\'s just too many good books on my list at the moment. that much choice is just too damned stressful without adequate medication. *curse you bennets books and your fabulous multi-level selection*',1,313,17,0,'203.109.196.76','en'),(20223,3361,'2003-05-29 17:57:12',74,'jo','my friend amy is going through a similar thing. except her parents are from kentucky and want to send her to a camp where jesus will make her not a lesbian. she\\\'s been living with her girlfriend erin for 2+ years and her mom still addresses packages just to amy and when amy\\\'s parents came and visited last month, amy\\\'s mom didn\\\'t even look erin in the eye. it\\\'s so so devastating. i\\\'m sorry. :(',1,413,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(20224,3366,'2003-05-29 18:02:53',74,'jo','i enjoyed this but i wish i hadn\\\'t been eating lunch while reading it.',1,73,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(20225,3367,'2003-05-29 18:04:42',74,'jo','i want a metaphor less clich&eacute; than \\\"car crash waiting to happen.\\\" that\\\'s where you lost me. i really liked it up to there.',1,138,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(20226,3042,'2003-05-29 18:26:11',493,'hortondm','Where are you?  Did you move?  Please call me.',0,46,0,0,'40.0.40.10','en'),(20227,3361,'2003-05-29 19:09:03',82,'simon','it is totally devastating. i\\\'m so sorry too. my entire family except my parents and a couple cousins are wicked homophobic and it blows. i hate it.',1,150,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(20228,3365,'2003-05-29 22:03:14',449,'s','No, no, no...if you get Wall\\\'s ice cream, it\\\'s got to be the stuff that changes colour. ',2,94,124,0,'216.175.85.60','en'),(20229,3365,'2003-05-30 10:29:33',175,'Paul G','Food in Thailand - I got a letter today: \\\"i\\\'ve got a good case of roundworm that i picked up in thailand or burma.\\\"  Dude.  <a href=\\\"http://travel.roughguides.com/health/atozworms.html\\\">Quote:</a> \\\"Itâ€™s estimated that around a quarter of the worldâ€™s population is affected by roundworms.\\\"  I hope that her worms go away.  I hope that I did not get worms from her.  I hope that you do not get worms.  Chia!  Worms!\r\n<p>\r\n*destabilizing*\r\n</p>\r\n',4,464,0,0,'209.109.226.153','en'),(20230,3365,'2003-05-30 18:27:02',124,'gus','s: I cant eat real ice cream, milk kills me, the great thing about the one in the purple and green wrapper is that it\\\'s taro and coconut, NO MILK! I can eat it! its so good! eat it everyday! yum!\r\n<p>\r\nPaul, the food in most of thailand is totally safe, you just dont eat anywhere that\\\'s empty, if they\\\'ve got customers, especially locals, chances are they\\\'re serving good food.... However... I did get a parasite. I dont know what it was, it wanst roundworm, it had some fancy scientific name. I lost like 15 lbs. whoopsie!',0,536,0,0,'63.198.55.58','en'),(20231,3367,'2003-05-30 20:48:29',370,'seanthecon','i was in the passanger seat. i was sitting tense and nervous cause i knew the seatbelts don\\\'t work. i was watching you stare straight ahead, cold and calm. i could hear the senseless chatter from the back seat drivers that tell you want to do and what they don\\\'t like. i watched you drown it all out and just press harder on the gas. i watched you light up a smoke as the car flew over the cliff. you offered me one but i declined, i told you that that shit would kill you. you laughed as the others screamed.',6,515,0,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(20232,3365,'2003-05-30 20:54:27',370,'seanthecon','you are a man of the people chiamattt. keep telling it like it is. great travel stories. one day we will rewrite \\\'sean and dave\\\'s long drive\\\' with an appendix of blood.',6,177,0,0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(20233,3368,'2003-06-01 04:34:10',187,'kandrer','i loved it.<br>\r\n(i am a bit surprised that i am the first to comment here. well, i am not sure how long this post spent online)',1,128,0,0,'221.119.220.104','en'),(20234,3366,'2003-06-01 04:48:37',187,'kandrer','very... efficient, in deed :).<br>\r\nthis makes me notice that writers on swinney sometimes check the \\\"tragedy\\\" box for posts that are not that sad.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nthis one deserves the check:<br>\r\n[x] tragedy. no, really.',1,223,0,0,'221.119.220.104','en'),(20235,3361,'2003-06-01 05:33:33',187,'kandrer','please forgive me, i will just mind your own business for a few seconds, in my usual robot style.<br>\r\nby how it happened, it looks to me like your mother tried to tolerate it. she failed, that time at least. i think the violence of her reaction shows how much efforts she was putting <i>before</i> bursting.<br>\r\nmaybe you know her well enough to \\\'evaluate\\\' your chances that she will tolerate it one day (understanding would be another hard step for her). if these chances are very low, follow Etienne\\\'s advice. life is too short to change people, in many cases.<br>\r\nalso, by pure guess : the fact that she refused to talk to you for a while might mean she regrets her behavior (for sure she regrets it in some way) but also that she is scared of what she would tell you now (now that she gave up publicly her tolerance mask, i mean). she might have been talking (for long) about how she feels with your father or someone close to her. if she didn\\\'t yet, her problem is worse.<br>\r\nmay i repeat the excellent comment of Belly: you will have to be the grown-up here. my heart is with you.',1,1102,231,0,'221.119.220.104','en'),(20236,3344,'2003-06-01 06:18:07',187,'kandrer','to jehannum (and belly) :<br>\r\n<i>if the world was populated with identical clones, there would be wars over what colour t-shirts people chose to wear</i><br>\r\nfunny, when i read that one i thought \\\"welcome to japan !\\\"...\r\n</p><p>\r\nabout SARS, since most of you guys just pass occasionally a masked person or two... (except the endangered swinney writers in Toronto)<br>\r\nyou can imagine how happy i feel in the japanese trains and subways. each time i can spot 2 or 3 people coughing and/or sneazing around me and, believe me, i wish the bastards could wear masks at least! (even though it might not protect so well)\r\n</p><p>\r\nabout SARS and Belly\\\'s remark about behaviors :<br>\r\nthe other day i managed to refrain myself from coughing. i do not use to cough, but suddenly, i felt a crazy itching inside my throat. i knew how bad people around me would feel, so i refrained, for the first time in my life. my eyes got filled with tears. i thought i would go mad. i thought: \\\"15 minutes before my stop, i won\\\'t make it...\\\"\r\n</p>',1,1046,0,0,'221.119.220.104','en'),(20237,3368,'2003-06-01 07:26:04',477,'jehannum','fabulous. fabulous. fabulous. tyler this is the most beautiful thing i have read in a while. i feel like going out and cuddling someone that i have a sort-of crush on...',1,169,0,0,'203.109.157.66','en'),(20238,3365,'2003-06-01 11:25:56',93,'chiamattt','Sean, I try and forget that trip...those nights....the rope and your other personality. Greg was mean. ',0,103,370,0,'61.37.154.92','en'),(20239,2611,'2003-06-01 22:16:26',477,'jehannum','s, you just encapsulated about fifteen years of my life into a well-written article! my own postural bad habit was to prop my head up to watch tv in bed, until the top of my spine felt like an over-kicked football at the point where it joined my skull. light became razor-blades, sound was the screaming of banshees, and a large rioting crowd were helpfully kicking the backs of my eyeballs from inside my skull. the good parts were when a girlfriend would storke my hair. i used to have long curly locks and just having her running her fingers through them and gently playing her hands over my face, especially my eyes, was dreamy and beautiful despite the pain.',1,663,0,0,'203.109.157.88','en'),(20240,3365,'2003-06-02 21:40:23',370,'greg','i don\\\'t want you talking to that seanthecon. don\\\'t listen to his lies. i will find you again.',6,99,93,0,'216.129.68.74','en'),(20241,3365,'2003-06-02 21:41:02',370,'greg','stay away from chiamattt. that bitch is mine. find your own garden tool.',6,72,370,0,'216.129.68.74','en'),(20242,3365,'2003-06-02 21:41:42',370,'seanthecon','what the fuck is going on? what have you done with my medication greg?',6,70,370,0,'216.129.68.74','en'),(20243,3370,'2003-06-02 23:09:57',17,'austin','this poem is making me real hungry!\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nhi jay!',2,54,0,0,'66.128.224.196','en'),(20244,3158,'2003-06-02 23:12:48',477,'jehannum','don\\\'t try and be too much like robert ludlum dude, he is dead after all...\r\n\r\ni loved the piece though. had me thinking of that great scene in the movie version of high fidelity where cusack abuses and then beats up robbins in every way he can imagine.\r\n\r\nmaybe you can drop an air-conditioner on him next time?',1,314,17,0,'203.109.157.88','en'),(20245,3369,'2003-06-03 00:31:03',234,'etienne','you are not very pale at all.  i was expecting someone quite different :P',1,73,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(20246,3370,'2003-06-03 03:45:17',75,'tyler','pow',1,3,0,0,'66.245.48.249','en'),(20247,3370,'2003-06-03 04:05:55',57,'weeb','hi, i liked this. it reads well and possesses literary stamina.',0,63,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(20248,3369,'2003-06-03 06:05:54',449,'s','This was hilarious. \r\n\r\nOnce, I had a penis on the back of my neck for some time...',2,83,0,0,'66.32.190.6','en'),(20249,3369,'2003-06-03 09:30:17',228,'belly','i stayed in that backpackers for a few nights when i first moved to wellington. before that, i stayed there after travelling to wellington for a red hot chili peppers concert, and again after a soundgarden concert. i almost lost my viginity there after the chili peppers show. yeah.',4,282,0,0,'61.125.243.18','en'),(20250,3369,'2003-06-03 11:51:13',477,'jehannum','etienne; you were expecting a skinny goth akin to a jhonnen vasquez comic, perhaps? it\\\'s always weord meeting on-line people in the real world...',1,148,0,0,'203.109.196.71','en'),(20251,3369,'2003-06-03 11:53:09',477,'jehannum','s: you mean a <tt>picture</tt> of one don\\\'t you? otherwise i\\\'m trying to work out how you got around easily?\r\n\r\nbelly: in auckland dude, not in welly no more. *insert pouting expression here*',1,197,0,0,'203.109.196.71','en'),(20252,3367,'2003-06-03 13:23:41',309,'slacker','i haven\\\'t read that, but i will certainly be looking into it now.  thanks for the suggestion.',0,96,124,0,'66.185.84.196','en'),(20253,3367,'2003-06-03 13:25:33',309,'slacker','so you heard those voices too?  and all this time i thought i was the only one.  oh, and sorry about where i grabbed you, i swear it was \\\"accidental\\\".',0,156,370,0,'66.185.84.196','en'),(20254,3369,'2003-06-03 21:28:10',234,'etienne','it was the way you described yourself that was incorrect !',1,58,477,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(20255,3370,'2003-06-04 00:46:56',234,'etienne','i really like this, especially parts like <i>afternoons sailed by across\r\nstrings of words retouched and rearranged</i>.',1,120,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(20256,3369,'2003-06-04 10:15:17',228,'belly','you mean there\\\'s a backpackers near a bar-type place called globe in auckland too? ok. i thought i was being a sneaky stalker. oh well. ',0,139,477,0,'210.155.207.249','en'),(20257,3370,'2003-06-04 10:50:00',175,'Paul G','write more, please',0,18,0,0,'209.109.224.57','en'),(20258,3157,'2003-06-04 18:47:53',486,'procyon','<i>But maybe it will come in handy to one of you</i><br>it has to me..i am on the opposite end of a similar situation... thank you.',2,131,0,0,'64.12.97.12','en'),(20259,3371,'2003-06-05 02:48:26',82,'simon','i feel similarly',0,16,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(20260,3370,'2003-06-05 05:06:37',228,'belly','this is my favourite part:\r\n<p>\r\n<i>everyone I have ever loved becomes a single unknowing object, palmed, polished and adored, that lifts me more than not, keeps me coming back to\r\nkiss it.</i></p>\r\n<p>\r\nreading this makes me want to write and write and write until my words draw pictures.',0,289,0,0,'210.173.57.154','en'),(20261,3361,'2003-06-05 17:03:15',231,'alana','<p>meeeew. my mother\\\'s reaction was unacceptable. she has a dyke sister and a dyke daughter and she isn\\\'t comfortable with either of these things.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i have spoken to her since it happened. there was no regret or sadness or any other indication that she had hurt me. she just said she was disgusted it happened in front of her, in public, and she found our behaviour insulting.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the single good thing about this experience is that i know where i stand with my mother now. i\\\'m 28. i have little time for her dramas now. i\\\'ve done therapy and eaten the meds for over ten years and i\\\'m well adjusted i think. i don\\\'t want to change her. what i wish for is for her to love me, her daughter, completely.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>also, i understand that mothers are under no obligation to love their children unconditionally.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ok. i don\\\'t know what else to say. except that in melbourne it is 3am and i love my chosen family. mew.</p>',3,959,187,0,'203.194.54.77','en'),(20262,3371,'2003-06-05 19:35:12',57,'weeb','\r\nobligations. sometimes i feel like i should chuck it all and live in a hut in costa rica. but then i go and buy some expensive organic produce, make myself some pabulum, and get distracted by <a href=\\\"http://www.cabinetmagazine.org/issues/7/floatingisland.php\\\">something on the web.</a>',1,294,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(20263,3371,'2003-06-05 21:19:58',91,'jay','who categorized this as \\\"non-fiction/spiritual\\\"?  have I mentioned, dear swinney, how I dislike the categorization.  Like, for instance, how does a piece of writing qualify as \\\"gay\\\"?  What need we with these categories, I say.  The work should stand for itself.  ',3,275,0,0,'67.113.150.31','en'),(20264,3371,'2003-06-05 22:32:32',17,'austin','Hi Jay,\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThanks for writing in!  The categorization choices come from Vulcan Video in Austin, Texas.  They serve no purpose at all.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThanks!\r\n<BR>\r\nAustin',3,174,91,0,'66.128.224.196','en'),(20265,3371,'2003-06-05 22:36:57',124,'gus','I just want to say that I personally love the categorizations. I always sure to choose wisely when I make my swinney.org posts!',0,127,0,0,'63.198.55.58','en'),(20266,3371,'2003-06-05 22:54:54',234,'etienne','re categorisations: i\\\'m in favour.  i do wish everything wasn\\\'t american spelling though :P',1,97,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(20267,3361,'2003-06-06 21:59:15',187,'kandrer','well, the fact that she does not show regret does not mean that she does not regret. and she might also love you more than she seems. the unconditional love does not exist (yet?), i\\\'m afraid ; it would for example require that you love her completely, even though she cannot accept you entirely...<br>\r\nbut i am unable to juge anything about this. it\\\'s 7am here and i should not be writing anything about your life, drunk as i am. i (finally) guess you don\\\'t need advice anymore. btw, i don\\\'t try to fix things anymore with my mother.<br>\r\ntake care anyway.',1,569,231,0,'219.125.221.21','en'),(20268,3369,'2003-06-07 06:37:16',449,'s','Well, it made the orgys so much less of a contortionist effort.\r\n\r\nYes, it was a picture drawn by some jerk who sat behind me in class.',3,135,477,0,'216.175.85.216','en'),(20269,3375,'2003-06-09 00:10:05',195,'matt','hi there. there is a lot more than this, but i have limited computer access.  i have been consigned to an assisted living facility in the middle of nowhere.  my parents have control of my finances.  my disability checks go straight to them.  my life and my destiny are no longer my own.<BR><BR>i know exactly where this novel is going.  i know what happens to the characters along the way and how it ends.  i just need unimpeded time to write it.  if i could write as much as i want, 12-15 hours a day, it would take about a month to finish.  right now, i\\\'m required to go to group therapy for six hours a day, be in bed with lights out for twelve hours a day, and deal with my jabbering schizophrenic roommate the rest of the time.  so i have precious little time to write.<BR><BR>here\\\'s what i want:<BR>i\\\'d like to escape all this.  i want to go to Tampa, to a fleabag hotel across from the University of Tampa called Whitledge Hall, and write uninterrupted for a month.  i need money to do this, of which i have none, of which i will have none in the forseeable future.<BR><BR>here\\\'s how you can help:<BR>i know there are thousands of people who look at this website.  all i need is one thousand dollars.  if a fraction of the people who visit swinney.org send me one dollar (or unit of your local currency), i will be set.  the money will not only allow me to stay at Whitledge Hall for a month and eat, but also travel to New York when the work is done, secure an agent, and get the book published.<BR><BR>so, i\\\'m begging here.  i have no shame, only need. my address is:<BR><BR>Matt McCord<BR>22213 Croom Road<BR>Brooksville, FL USA  34601<BR><BR>my parents will not open my mail.  they have never done that.  i thank you in advance, and i thank you bounteously.  please help me if you will.<BR><BR>yours,<BR>matt',1,1834,0,0,'65.144.79.36','en'),(20270,3371,'2003-06-10 21:50:38',175,'Paul G','i wish that a, \\\"really gay,\\\" category existed...and also a penalty box for citizens of the commonwealth who engaged in gratutitous use of the letter, \\\"u.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nbut most of all i like your writing, jay.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndon\\\'t get stuck, i think to myself.  life is like a great wheel turning.  nothing here will ever be perfect, or permanent.  and as time passes, i understand myself and <a href=\\\"http://www.koausa.org/Gods/God9.html\\\">fire</a> better.\r\n</p>',1,473,0,0,'209.109.230.203','en'),(20271,3375,'2003-06-10 22:17:38',17,'austin','i swear to god, you have to be pulling my leg.  somehow i believe you though.   God\\\'s website blurs the line between reality and fiction to the point that i no longer care to distinguish between the two.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nleonard has a guest bedroom.',2,246,195,0,'66.128.224.196','en'),(20272,3034,'2003-06-11 12:52:27',295,'karenlee','<i>Sometimes, living a life in opposition to something can be every bit as limiting as simply accepting circumstances.</i> yep.',1,127,0,0,'203.194.11.174','en'),(20273,3377,'2003-06-13 20:33:01',17,'austin','i activated this for simon, but as you can see, for me as well.',1,63,0,0,'66.128.224.196','en'),(20274,3377,'2003-06-13 23:51:47',124,'gus','I fixed the format for simon, as well as for swinney, just now. whoops.',0,71,0,0,'63.198.55.58','en'),(20275,3377,'2003-06-14 13:13:27',75,'tyler','<pre>\r\nthrough interstellar spaces\r\nBaron Swinney he may fly\r\nhis interstitial areas \r\nawash in Spanish Fly\r\n\r\na groovy 80\\\'s beat pulsating\r\nas he swooshes by\r\nthe trail of exhaust gases\r\ndissipating in the sky\r\n\r\na vintage motorcycle, \r\nhe is riding through my mind--\r\nthough thoughts he has for company\r\nrepulsive he might find\r\n\r\nso now it\\\'s time to end this ode\r\nideas come cheap, but frighten\r\nin my heart i know he\\\'s out there\r\nbut i\\\'d never recognize him\r\n</pre>\r\n',1,483,0,0,'66.245.48.175','en'),(20276,3377,'2003-06-14 15:05:01',93,'chiamattt','sang sang wakes you up',0,22,0,0,'61.40.66.81','en'),(20277,3378,'2003-06-15 07:33:09',449,'s','Reading this made me remember the first two very clearly. It all makes for a very haunting and lovely trilogy. ',2,111,0,0,'66.32.182.219','en'),(20278,3377,'2003-06-16 20:43:38',82,'simon','hee  hee',1,8,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(20279,3378,'2003-06-17 04:12:33',370,'seanthecon','this is absolutely beautiful and more poetic than i could ever have dreamed. thank you ali.',6,91,0,0,'24.156.128.107','en'),(20280,3379,'2003-06-17 06:20:41',449,'s','this made me happy.',2,19,0,0,'66.32.176.74','en'),(20281,3379,'2003-06-17 10:03:22',175,'Paul G','i think that there are men who can wear slippers, pajamas, and robes, and then there men who cannot wear any of that stuff.  some things are just unnatural, and even comical, on some people - it\\\'s like a cat wearing shoes.  when my cat acts according to her nature, i\\\'m usually spared the angst that accompanies mouse-killing.',1,332,0,0,'209.109.226.61','en'),(20282,3377,'2003-06-17 10:11:27',175,'Paul G','i read this several times, and it always made me smile.  like characters that i know, but don\\\'t really, \\\"know,\\\" it is fun to imagine you doing things together...',1,170,0,0,'209.109.226.61','en'),(20283,3379,'2003-06-17 12:05:14',93,'chiamattt','fuck the mouse...where\\\'s saddam!?!',1,37,0,0,'218.145.25.15','en'),(20284,3379,'2003-06-17 16:38:20',492,'bmg','<pre>\"WEE, sleekit, cowâ€™rin, timâ€™rous beastie,\r\nO, what a panicâ€™s in thy breastie!  \r\nThou need na start awa sae hasty,  \r\n                Wiâ€™ bickering brattle!  \r\nI wad be laith to rin anâ€™ chase thee,\r\n                 Wiâ€™ murdâ€™ring pattle!\"\r\n</pre> - Burns, Robert, To a Mouse',0,270,0,0,'147.80.216.86','en'),(20285,3379,'2003-06-17 17:26:02',86,'egg','drug.',0,5,0,0,'199.4.104.129','en'),(20286,3379,'2003-06-17 18:04:55',17,'austin','thx',0,3,86,0,'66.128.224.196','en'),(20287,3379,'2003-06-17 22:05:06',175,'Paul G','more germane, perhaps: so these two beings meet, perchance.  one is invested with the power to decide the course of the other\\\'s existence.  why?  not good, nor right, nor wisdom, but rather the because of the manner in which they came into the world.\r\n<p>\r\nhmmm...\r\n</p>',1,273,0,0,'199.35.139.159','en'),(20288,3379,'2003-06-18 01:42:11',228,'belly','i imagined a naked guy, wearing just slippers. \r\n<p>\r\ni\\\'m glad he didn\\\'t step on the wee mouse. he should\\\'ve made a tiny top hat for the mouse, and maybe taught it to juggle. the mouse can talk, right?\r\n</p>\r\n',0,218,175,0,'210.173.56.226','en'),(20289,2453,'2003-06-18 01:59:10',228,'belly','i want to know what happened. did you keep the job? did it make you happy? did you throw a brick through the shop\\\'s window in the middle of the night, just to keep things interesting? ',0,187,0,0,'210.173.56.19','en'),(20290,3379,'2003-06-18 23:29:32',90,'leonard','Stuart Tribble',1,14,0,0,'67.118.80.121','en'),(20291,3381,'2003-06-19 07:36:55',75,'manuel','que?',1,4,0,0,'66.245.48.175','en'),(20292,3381,'2003-06-19 10:03:12',154,'ryan','give me the furries.',0,20,0,0,'216.99.204.118','en'),(20293,3381,'2003-06-19 18:51:59',177,'pony','mahoromatic made me cry',0,23,0,0,'12.233.252.245','en'),(20294,3381,'2003-06-19 23:04:59',449,'s','ooo...furries.\r\n\r\nthank you to whomever classified this as \\\"tragedy\\\".',2,75,0,0,'66.32.176.2','en'),(20295,3317,'2003-06-20 02:37:24',75,'tyler','just put on a coat and tie. shine your shoes.',1,45,17,0,'66.245.48.175','en'),(20296,3381,'2003-06-20 17:20:01',83,'adamajah','I still haven\\\'t seen a good Faye Valentine from Cowboy Bebop.  That\\\'s a hard one to pull off.',0,99,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(20297,3381,'2003-06-20 22:21:06',234,'etienne','yay mtv, adding \\\"of doom!!\\\" (and yes there has to be at least a couple of exclamation marks) is the best thing ever.  i will never tire of it.',1,148,0,0,'203.96.111.237','en'),(20298,3381,'2003-06-20 22:35:53',175,'Paul G','i\\\'m a fan.\r\n<p>\r\ni enjoyed the combination of literary and anime references in this piece.  and the thought of someone doing something somewhat odd for reasons that were not entirely clear to them also appealed to me.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbut you didn\\\'t mention my true love: aeon flux.  and so i am going to select, \\\"burn! burn!\\\" \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthat will show you.\r\n</p>',6,372,449,0,'209.109.237.140','en'),(20299,3375,'2003-06-21 21:32:10',195,'matt','have faith, little austie',1,25,17,0,'65.144.78.36','en'),(20300,3381,'2003-06-22 23:46:17',449,'s','http://images.cosplay.com/showphoto.php?photo=45311&papass=&sort=2&thecat=500',3,77,83,0,'66.32.181.82','en'),(20301,3381,'2003-06-22 23:47:17',449,'s','The literary references were unintentional(with the exception of Dylan Thomas mangling)...I think I can only find two, anyhow. \r\n\r\nRight, Aeon Flux. That was spiffy.',2,165,175,0,'66.32.181.82','en'),(20302,3381,'2003-06-23 02:37:44',175,'Paul G','sorry, dude.  please know that i only comment when i like the article and/or author.  i thought that phrases in the piece like, <i>\\\"taken away by a large tentacle monster ...\\\"</i> invited a bit of fun.  i tried to comment in a tone similar to that in which the piece was written.  my bad - i don\\\'t, \\\"really know,\\\" you or anyone here.  i forget.  maybe you\\\'re really a 65 y/o guy in new jersey?  sometimes, no matter what it is that you try to do, it turns out wrong.  maybe, there are even people waiting for bad things to happen.  and after that goes on long enough, you\\\'ve got to wonder: \\\"why bother?\\\"  ',3,632,449,0,'199.35.139.204','en'),(20303,3381,'2003-06-23 04:53:13',449,'s','Ooof. I\\\'m sorry my tone came out kinda wrong. I thought your reply was hilarious! You\\\'re spiffy! You have a pretty taxi! You may name my firstborn monkey! Maybe even my first born paper clip! Heh. Anyway, I didn\\\'t mean to sound like I did.\r\n\r\nI\\\'m a 19 year old girl in California. Maybe one day, I shall reach the ranks of a 65 year old guy in New Jersey.\r\n\r\n\r\nBy the way, for everyone\\\'s information, the convention went well and I was not raped by a large tentacle monster.',2,489,175,0,'66.32.181.82','en'),(20304,3381,'2003-06-23 10:47:44',175,'Paul G','don\\\'t change.',1,16,449,0,'209.109.236.85','en'),(20305,3384,'2003-06-25 07:04:39',75,'tyler','jesus, man. that was great. i mean seriously, i really enjoyed reading that. keep it up.',1,88,0,0,'66.245.49.48','en'),(20306,3385,'2003-06-26 01:46:09',195,'matt','this is a stand-alone story that goes between Book Two and Book Three.  i\\\'m going to write them for between all the books.  have to go back and write one for after Book One.<BR><BR>i\\\'m going to try to publish it.  it has a certain minimalist ernest hemingway/raymond carver je ne sais quoi:)<BR><BR><I><B>best friendly</I></B>',1,332,0,0,'65.144.78.60','en'),(20307,3381,'2003-06-26 22:04:35',234,'etienne','no one should change.  i love you all just as you are.',1,54,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(20308,3381,'2003-06-26 22:04:35',234,'etienne','no one should change.  i love you all just as you are.',1,54,0,1,'210.54.120.200','en'),(20309,3381,'2003-06-28 16:39:20',17,'austin','life is boring. change helps!',3,29,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20310,3381,'2003-06-28 19:52:03',175,'Paul G','sometimes, when i\\\'m bored, i talk to myself in a different voice.  i have two favorite voices: one scottish, and one japanese.  i only say one thing in my japanese voice: \\\"tasty penis made of candy.\\\"  i don\\\'t know why; i just do.  and, yeah, sometimes i forget that i\\\'m not alone in the car...',4,308,17,0,'199.35.139.39','en'),(20311,3381,'2003-06-29 18:06:39',17,'austin','\\\"Ohhhh, verrry GOOD! HA HA\\\"\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nlately i have been using a pirate voice on occasion.  it comes out most when i am cursing something.',1,145,175,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20312,2886,'2003-07-01 21:44:51',17,'austin','it is almost that time again!  dashed holiday hopes of beer and sparklers.  everyone prep their lawn chair and start exercising that beer arm!\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n<IMG SRC=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/art/galleries/misc/julyfirework2.gif\\\"><IMG SRC=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/art/galleries/misc/julyfirework2.gif\\\"><IMG SRC=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/art/galleries/misc/julyfirework2.gif\\\">',1,231,0,0,'66.128.224.196','en'),(20313,3386,'2003-07-01 23:38:22',124,'gus','this one was just a link from one section to another, i dont know if it made any sense. I had the writers blockage. ',0,116,0,0,'63.198.55.58','en'),(20314,3386,'2003-07-02 14:49:58',195,'matt','i like your stuff, gus. i do, i do.',1,35,0,0,'24.129.129.219','en'),(20315,2886,'2003-07-02 15:03:37',74,'jo','mmm, bursty.',2,12,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(20316,3386,'2003-07-02 19:51:58',175,'Paul G','i found myself dwelling on, \\\"...the twang of Willie Nelson.\\\"  it\\\'s usually, <i>Always on My Mind</i>, on the jukebox, in these parts.  but, wow, he wrote a lot of stuff.\r\n<p>\r\nsometimes, when i read the pieces in this series, i wonder exactly how much time you\\\'ve spent in the environment that you\\\'re writing about; the detail is so very good.\r\n</p>',1,364,0,0,'209.109.226.196','en'),(20317,3386,'2003-07-02 20:25:58',124,'gus','these are all made up places. Their details come from a mish mash of places I have and havent been.',0,99,175,0,'63.198.55.58','en'),(20318,3379,'2003-07-03 18:16:28',90,'leonard','http://www.hcs.harvard.edu/~golder/dialect/staticmaps/q_49.html',1,63,0,0,'67.118.80.121','en'),(20319,3379,'2003-07-03 20:08:08',17,'austin','dragged sounds bad.',1,19,90,0,'66.128.224.196','en'),(20320,3379,'2003-07-04 03:00:19',449,'s','but it is correct as the word \\\"drug\\\" is not and has never been the past tense of \\\"drag.\\\"',2,100,0,0,'216.175.108.154','en'),(20321,3381,'2003-07-04 03:06:41',449,'s','I like to act Chinese sometimes, because I am.\r\n\r\nBut it always comes out like \\\"twenty dollah! me rove you rong time!\\\"',4,124,175,0,'216.175.108.154','en'),(20322,3379,'2003-07-05 16:37:50',17,'austin','i say drug for dragged all the time.  never, ever, would i say, \\\"I dragged the thing over there.\\\"  i would always say, \\\"I drug it over there.\\\"  i would point as well.',3,178,449,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(20323,3381,'2003-07-05 23:38:06',175,'Paul G','you are very funny.\r\n<p>\r\nand there is a small chance that you are what you say that you are.  but, until it\\\'s disproved, i\\\'m sticking with my 65 y/o new jersey guy theory.  in fact, i\\\'m beginning to suspect that you drive a taxi: cigar in one hand, pastrami on rye in the other, steering the wheel with your beer-engorged belly.  and maybe if you heard a woman say, \\\"love you long time,\\\" it was in vietnam, and not in a <a href=\\\"http://www.google.com/search?hl=en&lr=&ie=UTF-8&oe=UTF-8&q=kubrick+me+so+horny&spell=1\\\">stanley kubrick film...</a>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n(ref. change: write in a way that\\\'s good for you; don\\\'t let any guy ever make you doubt or feel bad.  p.)\r\n</p> ',1,697,449,0,'209.109.236.82','en'),(20324,3388,'2003-07-08 02:05:01',64,'Joanna','I once went trick-or-treating at the American Embassy compound in Tokyo - that was surreal.  More surreal was Yokota airforce base - a whole little American town in the middle of the outskirts of Tokyo.  It was the first and only place I\\\'ve ever hitchhiked because when we were sneaking out we had to get across the flight path.  I\\\'m sure America isn\\\'t really like the embassy or the air force base, but that\\\'s always how I imagine it.  ',1,449,0,0,'156.62.9.21','en'),(20325,3353,'2003-07-08 02:05:06',75,'tyler','man. now i\\\'m like, \\\'who wrote that?\\\'',1,45,0,0,'66.32.62.54','en'),(20326,3388,'2003-07-09 03:41:14',449,'s','I am so happy, after reading the title, that it wasn\\\'t an \\\"American Pie\\\" reference.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\nI grew up in Malaysia and the Philippines. I\\\'d always wanted to be able to go trick o\\\' treating, but by the time I moved here, my friends all thought they were too old to go. I\\\'m not american, but I\\\'ve spent a lot of my life being the \\\"expatriate\\\" kid- it\\\'s funny how that term doesn\\\'t apply anymore now that I\\\'m an \\\"expatriate\\\" in America, because apparently there aren\\\'t any extra hardships to endure...',2,547,0,0,'216.175.83.57','en'),(20327,3388,'2003-07-12 20:53:17',234,'etienne','our version of the 4th of july is, i think, the 6th of february - waitangi day - and all it signals is racial discord.  *sigh*',0,126,0,0,'203.96.111.237','en'),(20328,3388,'2003-07-13 18:22:01',175,'Paul G','hey may.<br>\r\nthis piece was really tight: it had a beginning, a middle, and an end that looped back to the beginning; it felt really polished.<br>\r\nhow did you write it?<br>\r\nare you able to knock out something like this in a single sitting?  or, do you let your writing sit for a while, and then come back to it?  any thoughts on working style/method would be appreciated.  really nice read.  p. ',1,398,0,0,'209.109.226.101','en'),(20329,3390,'2003-07-14 01:38:49',228,'belly','i really like both of these, but i don\\\'t see the connection. were they intended to be two separate posts?',0,108,0,0,'210.173.56.74','en'),(20330,3390,'2003-07-14 05:05:32',234,'etienne','you can have soy chai lattes :P',0,31,0,0,'210.54.120.200','en'),(20331,3390,'2003-07-14 14:50:05',366,'eunoia','The opening narrator is the voice in the second part as well. His anal retention allows him to see everyone elses shit, hence his omnipotence. I guess the connection is not obvious since the second half has a definite mood change. Thanks for the point. WIP',0,256,0,0,'66.185.84.68','en'),(20332,3390,'2003-07-14 22:48:16',366,'eunoia','NEWLY edited, with that great soy chai late flavor!',0,51,0,0,'142.107.84.170','en'),(20333,3388,'2003-07-17 01:04:06',210,'may','thank you paul g.\r\nthis one came out in a rush after a particularly non-nostalgic phone conversation with my parents, who are several thousand miles away.',1,154,175,0,'205.188.209.138','en'),(20334,3174,'2003-07-17 21:46:09',195,'matt','this is masterful writing, moe.  very nice.',1,43,0,0,'209.208.110.23','en'),(20335,3390,'2003-07-19 04:14:15',93,'chiamattt','where are you now mark?',1,23,0,0,'218.145.25.79','en'),(20336,3390,'2003-07-20 04:07:06',366,'eunoia','Back in Toronto living with Czech gf working for corrections canada...missing you',0,81,0,0,'66.185.84.68','en'),(20337,3390,'2003-07-20 08:49:37',93,'chiamattt','corrections canada eh? you better hope they dont discover your link with alquaida. ',1,83,0,0,'61.40.66.78','en'),(20338,3388,'2003-07-21 22:53:00',124,'gus','A couple of years ago I spent celebrated my American independence on July 4 at the Australian embassy in Bangkok while visiting a Scottish friend, and polished it off by kissing a Thai boy. I felt very international.',0,216,0,0,'63.198.55.58','en'),(20339,3395,'2003-07-22 06:47:29',234,'etienne','because you believe it, and becuase you\\\'re CORMIE!',0,53,0,0,'210.86.57.118','en'),(20340,2468,'2003-07-23 21:31:35',75,'tyler','i accept loneliness as inevitable, but not silence. and that\\\'s rock and roll.',1,80,0,0,'66.245.50.154','en'),(20341,3397,'2003-07-24 19:43:58',82,'simon','<p>i miss houston.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.homorobotic.org/photologs/texASS/humps.jpg\\\">\r\n</p>',1,106,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(20342,3397,'2003-07-24 19:50:01',74,'jo','i don\\\'t. at all. ever. cesspool. oh wait&#151;i miss the museums. and empire cafe. and star pizza. that\\\'s it.',1,115,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(20343,3397,'2003-07-24 20:26:42',17,'austin','Houston, it is better than Dallas.\r\n\r\nHi jesse!',2,47,0,0,'66.128.224.196','en'),(20344,3397,'2003-07-24 20:57:00',74,'jo','houston IS better than dallas. but then again, so is swimming in a pit of venomous snakes, so.',1,94,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(20345,3397,'2003-07-24 21:22:15',82,'simon','i love houston. the museums. the driving around looking at stuff. value village. the ballet. i like being able to use the word \\\'bayou\\\' in everyday conversation. the endless photo opportunities. the weird looking stoplights in some parts of town. the cuban restaurant with the mango mojitos. the cheap apartments. hot weather. cheap beer. playing spot-the-molesting-oil-baron. the endless airbrushing nail salons. this one taco truck. the galleries. i want to move there someday.',1,484,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(20346,3397,'2003-07-25 22:24:37',501,'bigchin','<p>\r\noh good lord! here\\\'s what i think:\r\n</p><p>\r\ni hate the heat, the way it bounces off the endless cement and permeates\r\nyour every pore. it zaps my energy and makes the inside of my eyeballs\r\nsweat. i hate the all-american take-it-or-leave-it mentality that dares you\r\nto defy or even question. i hate the ticky-tacky apartments with omg\r\ncarpeting in the entry ways. i do however relish the thought of playing\r\nspot-the-molesting-oil baron.\r\n</p><p>\r\nok, ok, i also love this one coffee place, Brazil, that has dynamite\r\nbreakfasts, and actually i do love the museums, the ballet, and those cuban\r\nmojitos you can get by the pitcher full. hmm, not to mention the cheap\r\nclothing places. ok, i would visit you if you moved there!\r\n</p>',1,742,82,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(20347,3397,'2003-07-26 03:08:48',165,'joker','hi austin.  i think i like houston better than dallas too, though i haven\\\'t really spent much time there.',3,119,17,0,'216.141.190.191','en'),(20348,3397,'2003-07-26 03:10:30',165,'joker','<pre>oops!  i guess you better insert some  CRLFs if you\\\'re\r\ngonna use &lt;pre&gt; tags in a comment.</pre>',3,110,0,0,'216.141.190.191','en'),(20349,3397,'2003-07-27 16:06:10',75,'tyler','Brazil is a superb name for a coffee shop or even a bar or restaurant come to think of it. \r\n<p>\r\nit\\\'s weird that I\\\'ve been so many places outside the USA, and so relatively few inside. ',1,192,0,0,'66.245.49.23','en'),(20350,3397,'2003-07-28 05:23:35',65,'le','\\\"i think i\\\'m lonely sometimes.  but then i think that lonely is just what we call the condition of not being ecstatic\\\"\r\n\r\nYES',4,134,0,0,'216.27.177.123',''),(20351,3397,'2003-07-28 19:32:24',74,'jo','why do people love brazil so much? it was pretentious and overpriced and the seating was horribly uncomfortable. the bathrooms were nice, though.',1,145,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(20352,3397,'2003-07-29 16:27:01',82,'simon','i dont think brazil is all that great.',1,38,74,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(20353,3397,'2003-07-29 18:13:12',74,'jo','oh p.s. hi hesse :)',0,19,165,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(20354,3397,'2003-07-29 18:13:33',74,'jo','DAMMIT. JESSE. jesse jesse.',0,27,165,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(20355,3397,'2003-07-30 15:56:43',502,'chris','Hi Jesse. Hi Austin. Hi Jo. Hi hi. My first trip to houston I was overwhelmed by the power lines and concrete. I am not saying that its all bad, not at all actually. Observe that some streets, like roads, are not asphalt  but actually poured concrete. Concrete is superiour to asphalt, although much more expensive - but lasts much longer. One annoyance with concrete streets is that you get that thump-thump after every 50ft or so where the seems are. As for the power lines, Houston power lines seem to be on average 30ft taller than most that I have seen before. Another observation is that housing is much cheaper than in Austin, some anyway. This weekend is San Antonio for me to see the Dale Chihuly exhibits, http://www.chihuly.com/, and it reminds me that I saw his stuff in Houston. A granny was trying to get her granddaugther to pick a small, $5000, piece out for a pressy. The little girl was not impressed. O\\\'well... thats my houston experience... I like the Empire Cafe, I like the Menil (http://www.menil.org/), and of course there is Houston Garden Centers...  ',0,1080,0,0,'147.80.216.86','en'),(20356,3394,'2003-07-31 09:14:48',370,'seanthecon','so this is what goes on in your head. its a dangerous place that many don\\\'t return from, and those that do...well i\\\'m living testimony of your mental carnage. at any rate, i\\\'ll have whatever you\\\'re smoking and through in a few colt 45s for measure.',6,260,0,0,'193.91.64.34','en'),(20357,3390,'2003-07-31 09:18:57',370,'seanthecon','amazing. you are a depressed middle aged man trapped in the body of a young optomistic spaniard...er, african...er, phillipino. a true gonzo journalist. keep snatching up them chickens.',6,185,366,0,'193.91.64.34','en'),(20358,3398,'2003-07-31 17:51:18',17,'austin','<B>ablative</B> is a $.25 word.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nnice pics!  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\n<h1>i &hearts; the cold war.</H1>',1,100,0,0,'66.128.224.196','en'),(20359,3398,'2003-07-31 20:40:46',17,'austin','also, \r\n<BR>\r\nisn\\\'t not == ain\\\'t no',2,41,0,0,'66.128.224.196','en'),(20360,3398,'2003-08-01 15:26:32',486,'procyon','i know i should find some way not to phrase this as a one-line comment... but thanks :)',0,87,17,0,'128.156.10.11','en'),(20361,3399,'2003-08-01 18:21:58',74,'jo','(chris, sorry for not realizing that you were you. i was like, \\\"who\\\'s this chris guy saying hi?\\\" HI!)<BR>\r\ni haven\\\'t taken photos for several months now. my digital camera has even been put in my bag on multiple occasions but never used. i even just had a birthday party&#151;my biggest craziest party yet&#151;and i didn\\\'t even bring my camera (or think to). anyway, the point of saying this is that i\\\'m kind of enjoying not feeling the pressure to document my life as much anymore? and i\\\'m finding that my memories suffice&#151;are usually much <b>nicer</b> than any photo i would take anyhow&#151;and that maybe i was forgetting certain events in my life because i was supposed to. :) also, emdash is such my bitch.',1,739,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(20362,3400,'2003-08-01 20:05:51',123,'dcc','erm. i do this too. i\\\'m glad to see i\\\'m not the only freak that gets on the line up with the departure station exits.\r\n\r\nin the morning this works well. in the evening, not so much. at night i have to aim for the far end of my home station. it seems that people getting on the train seem to not understand the \\\"i must let people off before i can get on\\\" idea.\r\n\r\ni once purposely elbowed someone in the face getting off the train because they wouldn\\\'t move after i yelled \\\"excuse me\\\" 2x. serves them right.',2,527,0,0,'66.207.199.131','en'),(20363,3400,'2003-08-01 23:20:46',70,'Fuzzy','<p>I have three positions on the Eastbound platform at my station which I use depending on if I am transferring at one of two stations or riding straight along the line.  Even though it\\\'s nice to get onto the platform as soon as your train is arriving, this is slightly bothersome because you know you\\\'re not going to be at  the door you\\\'d like when you reach your destination.</p>\r\n<p>Throwing elbows while exiting the subway is a guilty pleasure of mine.</p>',2,469,0,0,'65.50.26.222','en'),(20364,3390,'2003-08-03 23:42:43',241,'linus van pelt','<p> this is good stuff. you paint an ugly picture of human existence, at least of urban human existence. if we believe the narrator, it would seem that we all suffer from some sickness, some disease. the shared reaction toward the death of the dog suggests that disease is indifference, or perhaps an acute case of self-centredness.</p> \r\n\r\n',0,341,0,0,'64.230.146.32','en'),(20365,3390,'2003-08-05 19:31:55',366,'eunoia','Thanks for the comments.  I especially like the comment about the the death of the dog. I am unsure of which dog you speak of, but it is a comical canine comment that cannot go unnoticed. Keep on barking.',3,204,241,0,'142.107.84.170','en'),(20366,3390,'2003-08-07 12:20:16',370,'seanthecon','does the dog represent your penis? and the bus your mother? and all the passangers are all the people that have had the horrifying experience of seeing your penis?',6,163,366,0,'193.91.64.34','en'),(20367,3390,'2003-08-07 15:17:25',241,'linus van pelt','<p>\\\"We all look at each other for an instant, recognizing that the bus may have run over a dog. At least I think so. I am sure some of the other people think so too.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>that dog. woof </p>\r\n\r\n',0,204,366,0,'64.230.147.26','en'),(20368,3405,'2003-08-07 20:50:57',82,'simon','i think i feel similarly.',1,25,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(20369,3407,'2003-08-08 03:20:03',17,'austin','You are a confrontational photographers, are you not?',2,53,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20370,3404,'2003-08-08 21:07:08',124,'gus','I liked this one. It had just the right amount of detail, but not too much.',0,75,0,0,'63.198.55.58','en'),(20371,3405,'2003-08-08 21:08:21',124,'gus','Im obsessed with my future, and I dont know how to get there.',0,61,0,0,'63.198.55.58','en'),(20372,3407,'2003-08-08 22:31:34',122,'hool','i think that all photography of this type (street) is confrontational.  i\\\'m actually quite friendly, i try to say thank you and smile when someone busts me shooting them.  it definitely helps to ease the tension of the moment after. ',0,236,17,0,'66.185.84.68','en'),(20373,3404,'2003-08-09 16:15:39',195,'matt','this was nice, tyler.  very erotic.<BR><BR>but next time, you\\\'ll want to work some graphic sex into it.  people like nothing more than to read about a good old-fashioned horsefucking.  it makes them hot.:P',1,208,0,0,'209.208.110.231','en'),(20374,3405,'2003-08-09 23:53:58',75,'tyler','\\\'Memory is nothing but a gimmick.\\\' -Col. Bruce Hampton, ret.',1,66,0,0,'66.245.48.228','en'),(20375,3407,'2003-08-11 12:41:59',370,'seanthecon','personally i think you\\\'re a pervert who gets off on taking photos of old women and young children. god knows what other photos you have that you don\\\'t post. but i was quite turned on by the old lady in the window. i\\\'ll trade you my entire collection of doctored olsen twin photos for that one.',6,302,122,0,'193.91.64.34','en'),(20376,3408,'2003-08-13 18:09:13',82,'simon','t-top. my uncle had an iroc z with a t-top. his son chris crashed it twice. his hair was longish. he was in a frat. his frat nickname was skeeter. when he was in his first year of college, i was 8. he made me drink my first two beers and played metallica for me. despite those two gems, i hated his guts.',1,304,0,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(20377,3397,'2003-08-15 16:15:15',165,'joker','hi chris, hi jo.  chris i think i saw you at starbucks at 45th and lamar last saturday.  possible?',2,98,502,0,'216.141.190.87','en'),(20378,3408,'2003-08-17 01:33:23',66,'Peter','<br><tt>my mom and dad used to have matching 1977 stingray t-tops. mom\\\'s was splitpea green metaflake with silvered windows while dad\\\'s was midnight blue with a white hood and roof and trunk.<br><br>one day, they decided to sell the cars and put a down-payment on a house and a bright orange pinto. i was born a few days later.<br><Br>oh well. its been pretty much downhill since then.</tt>',1,396,0,0,'24.193.78.146','en'),(20379,3407,'2003-08-17 01:34:20',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i want a hool action figure</tt>',1,40,122,0,'24.193.78.146','en'),(20380,3407,'2003-08-17 01:54:25',234,'etienne','ditto.  although i have had many hool hugs and that was even better.',1,68,66,0,'65.50.24.17','en'),(20381,3410,'2003-08-17 01:58:19',66,'Peter','<br><Tt>i stole these photos from my fiance, btw. also: the lights you see on in the buildings were mostly flickering candles and emergency backups...</tt>',1,155,0,0,'24.193.78.146','en'),(20382,3410,'2003-08-17 04:34:51',234,'etienne','i am sort of glad i was here for it, it was kind of exciting.  in the t-dot we were backyard-ing it up and drinking lots of beer.',1,129,0,0,'65.50.24.17','en'),(20383,3410,'2003-08-17 20:42:34',251,'Jamie','peter is engaged',1,16,0,0,'172.188.61.131','en'),(20384,3408,'2003-08-18 02:35:14',17,'austin','I submit these motorhead lyrics as an appendix to this post:\r\n<BR>\r\n<PRE>\r\nLove Me Forever\r\n\r\nLove me forever,\r\nor not at all,\r\nEnd of our tether, \r\nbacks to the wall,\r\nGive me your hand,\r\nDon\\\'t you ever ask why,\r\nPromise me nothing, live \\\'till we die,\r\n\r\n\r\nEverything changes,\r\nIt all stays the same,\r\nEveryone guilty, no one to blame,\r\nEvery way out,\r\nBrings you back to the start,\r\nEveryone dies\r\nTo break somebody\\\'s heart,\r\n\r\n\r\nWe are the system, we are the law,\r\nWe are corruption,worm in the core,\r\nOne of another, laugh\\\'til you cry,\r\nFaith unto death or knife in YOUR eye.\r\n\r\nLove me or leave me,\r\nTell me no lies,\r\nAsk me no questions, \r\nSend me no spies\r\nYou know love\\\'s a thief, \r\nSteal your heart in  the night,\r\nSlip through your fingers, \r\nBest hold on tight!\r\n</pre>',1,591,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20385,3410,'2003-08-18 04:49:39',93,'chiamattt','In Seoul, the lights never go out. Sometimes the masses need a good blackout. ',0,78,0,0,'220.73.165.14','en'),(20386,3409,'2003-08-18 14:26:07',370,'seanthecon','except for my chronic masterbation and alcoholism, you just described my life to the T. oh, if only i could talk to Tae Gue, i would tell him what i wished someone had told me at his age: \\\'being a fuck hole makes it difficult to sit down.\\\' but did i listen? i\\\'m writing this from my special zero gravity computer console from the planet xerox in the galaxy zebucon. i wish someone had also taken the time to tell my parents that incest breeds retardidomdom.',6,466,0,0,'193.91.64.34','en'),(20387,3410,'2003-08-19 02:52:28',129,'moebius','love the title of this piece. makes it sound like a yearly event: \\\"everybody get your flashlights primed for Blackout 2004! it\\\'ll be even darker and more powerless than last year!\\\"',1,189,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(20388,3409,'2003-08-19 10:54:14',370,'seanthecon','koreans could learn something from hungarians, who you a disdain for work, a distrust of authorit and an overall disinterest of not relaxing. they\\\'re not lazy, they just aren\\\'t whipped. however, i think the hungarians could learn something from the koreans and at least do something when you say you will. often things get pushed aside and never done ever. it gets frustrating. dave, i think we should create a utopian world called \\\'hungarea\\\' or \\\'korngy\\\' or \\\'canada\\\' or something like that.',6,514,0,0,'193.91.64.34','en'),(20389,3409,'2003-08-19 10:56:40',370,'seanthecon','that came out all wrong:\r\n\r\na disdain for work, a distruct of authority and an disinterest for anything but relaxing.\r\n\r\nor something like that. okay, is three too many posts for this piece. i digest.',6,200,0,0,'193.91.64.34','en'),(20390,3410,'2003-08-19 14:12:33',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yep, thats what theyre calling on fox news... all excitedly, you know... <i>\\\"its the supercrazy great blackout of oh three! whee!\\\"</i>... it makes it sound like the annaul aids walk or something.</tt> ',1,215,129,0,'24.193.78.146','en'),(20391,3412,'2003-08-19 19:53:09',239,'Poonlarp','...and thats when i do him up the ass alot.',6,43,0,0,'24.193.78.146','en'),(20392,3412,'2003-08-19 21:15:46',90,'leonard','Best of...',1,10,0,0,'67.118.80.121','en'),(20393,3409,'2003-08-20 03:16:01',93,'chiamattt','distrust you journalist!',6,24,0,0,'218.145.25.15','en'),(20394,3412,'2003-08-20 06:11:43',17,'austin','i\\\'m gonna go take another vicodin.',1,37,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20395,3413,'2003-08-21 17:39:06',17,'austin','i\\\'m reminded of the Gumho Pussy Crew.<BR><BR>i started trying to fix some of the sentences that didn\\\'t make sense, then i noticed that when they are fixed, they don\\\'t make sense.  so i left them.  the drunken style of Chiamattt is not to be tampered with.',3,264,0,0,'66.128.224.196','en'),(20396,3416,'2003-08-21 17:53:30',17,'austin','this is a great article. it is my favorite in quite some time: short, very descriptive, and interesting.',1,104,0,0,'66.128.224.196','en'),(20397,3413,'2003-08-24 19:03:43',195,'matt','does the cat still call you Plumpy?\r\n',1,37,0,0,'209.208.110.207','en'),(20398,3418,'2003-08-26 04:43:47',75,'tyler','burnout.',1,8,0,0,'66.245.48.68','en'),(20399,3418,'2003-08-26 04:57:48',93,'chiamattt','huh?',1,4,0,0,'61.40.66.81','en'),(20400,3418,'2003-08-27 03:15:14',75,'tyler','you seem to be suffering from \\\'burnout\\\' at your job.',1,58,0,0,'66.245.48.68','en'),(20401,3418,'2003-08-27 03:58:10',93,'chiamattt','I like my job. I just have nothing to go to when I finish at the end of the day. My weekends are boring and empty. I am not burned out. In fact in a week or so im going to be adding more hours to work. Fill more of my free and lonely time with teaching and children. ',0,267,0,0,'218.145.25.79','en'),(20402,3418,'2003-08-30 16:57:38',17,'austin','in any case, you look like a burnout.',2,37,93,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20403,3418,'2003-09-02 05:34:10',93,'chiamattt','I look burned out. I need to chinwag with a naked woman. ',0,57,0,0,'61.33.57.100','en'),(20404,3419,'2003-09-02 07:26:39',234,'etienne','no one had any fun just by standing still\r\n',0,43,0,0,'203.96.111.237','en'),(20405,3419,'2003-09-02 14:38:41',477,'jehannum','<p>least of all me. i mean how many times this year have i done this now? three, maybe four? i\\\'ve never lived in one house for as much as a year since i left home. one day i\\\'ll find out where i\\\'m supposed to be but i\\\'m sure not there yet...</p>',1,256,234,0,'203.109.157.88','en'),(20406,3420,'2003-09-03 18:29:52',17,'austin','does this mean i have to trade in my bitches and money?  damn.\r\n<br><br>\r\nhi brent, you philosophical ho.',2,105,0,0,'66.128.224.196','en'),(20407,3420,'2003-09-03 18:35:29',82,'simon','send all bitches and money my way, old man swinney.',1,51,17,0,'209.172.115.210','en'),(20408,3419,'2003-09-03 23:49:11',124,'gus','I had a room mate who was always going off to find himself. I always thought, if he couldn\\\'t find himself under his own bed, he wouldn\\\'t find himself in another city either. \r\n<p>\r\nHe always came back from these adventures more discouraged than when he\\\'d left. \r\n<p>\r\nIf you\\\'re never happy where you are, it might not be where you are that needs changing. \r\n<p>\r\nBut I\\\'m just sayin.',0,397,0,0,'63.198.55.58','en'),(20409,3420,'2003-09-03 23:51:43',124,'gus','<blockquote>True happiness isn\\\'t found in things. It is found in people.</blockquote>\r\n<p>\r\nOk, but don\\\'t tell Rilke.\r\n<p>',0,128,0,0,'63.198.55.58','en'),(20410,3419,'2003-09-04 09:55:39',477,'jehannum','never a truer word was spoken...',1,32,124,0,'203.109.196.74','en'),(20411,3420,'2003-09-04 19:31:20',510,'DissFunction','I\\\'m a philosophical whore for sure.  Who writes crap like that?  Only when I feel so alone.  Can you hear the violins in the background?  They\\\'re playing my song, \\\"Poor me.  The world sucks. Blah blah blah.\\\"  \r\n\r\nHi Austin!  Good to see you\\\'re ALIVE.',0,265,0,0,'65.197.77.2','en'),(20412,3420,'2003-09-05 02:35:53',477,'jehannum','<p>i think, technically, if you are allowed to seek happiness in <tt>people</tt> then you can indeed keep the bitches.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the money has to go though. sorry.</p>',1,167,17,0,'203.109.157.88','en'),(20413,3420,'2003-09-05 16:40:28',176,'Scapegoat','<i>True happiness isn\\\'t found in things. It is found in people. We must condition ourselves to not accept material objects as happiness.<i/><br><br> \r\nI must say that the happiness found in material objects isn\\\'t as satisfying but it is a hell of a lot more reliable. Pursuing happiness in people is a hell of a lot more dangerous then the material world. But, maybe I just haven\\\'t met the right type of people?<br><br> ',0,429,510,0,'159.33.2.41','en'),(20414,3420,'2003-09-05 17:09:18',510,'DissFunction','You hit the head on the nail, Scapegoat.  People are far less reliable than a lot of material things.  People will let you down.  But, I do believe that even if you find the right person, that person is going to let you down too.  It\\\'s our inherent nature to be shitheads.  We\\\'re all so busy with ourselves (including me!).  I guess that\\\'s why I say that people don\\\'t really change.  However, it\\\'s much easier to just say phuck it rather than take the time and energy to pursue something real.  Never give up!  Something is out there, and when you least expect it, it will kick you in the balls with no apologies.  ',2,630,0,0,'65.197.77.2','en'),(20415,3419,'2003-09-06 04:15:25',366,'eunoia','Being from where you are, New Zealand, can you explain the FART tax? Are you a proponent or a detractor. Or am I just full of hot air and bad sources.',4,150,477,0,'66.185.84.68','en'),(20416,3419,'2003-09-07 11:41:16',477,'jehannum','the fart tax is what they\\\'re calling the government initiative to try and tax farmers for the methane that their cattle produce. so now we\\\'ve got \\\"don\\\'t fart\\\" banners everywhere and angry farmers parading around, something i think people in europe can more than relate to. i think the whole thing\\\'s a circus. for more you could read <a href=\\\"http://xtramsn.co.nz/news/0,,3779-2640337,00.html>this</a> and <a href=\\\"http://xtramsn.co.nz/news/0,,3779-2642025,00.html\\\">this</a>.',1,501,366,0,'203.109.157.88','en'),(20417,2798,'2003-09-08 20:58:28',477,'jehannum','man i feel your pain, i really do. i\\\'ve been diagnosed borderline manic-depressive and a number of medical professionals have insisted it would be a good thing for me to go on medication, but i can\\\'t stand it. i need my freaky angst moments to get by, without them i simply can\\\'t write. if i need a pickup i occasionally drop ecstacy or a 2CB, but again the swings are hideous. as anyone who has read <a href=\\\"http://jehannum.diary-x.com\\\">my diary</a> can tell you, my moods are often violent polar opposites. despite all this though, i can\\\'t stand the numbness the medication gives me. best of luck with it brother.',1,634,0,0,'203.109.196.74','en'),(20418,3422,'2003-09-09 18:58:58',175,'Paul G','simon, nice.  if you need anything in chicago, let me know.  bon voyage.',1,72,0,0,'199.35.145.28','en'),(20419,3422,'2003-09-10 00:19:51',82,'simon','i need someone to join me for a beer at jimmy\\\'s woodlawn tap. on woodlawn and 53rd. the bar where my dad and his best friend spent their formative years.',0,156,175,0,'65.25.166.224','en'),(20420,3422,'2003-09-10 09:58:08',175,'Paul G','roger that.  i always have a car and cash.  need contact info?',1,62,82,0,'209.109.224.129','en'),(20421,3422,'2003-09-10 13:47:42',82,'simon','simon@homorobotic.org',0,21,175,0,'65.25.166.224','en'),(20422,2576,'2003-09-11 20:14:15',65,'le','i am going to print this and put it on the staff bulletin board at omega',4,72,0,0,'208.239.147.248','en'),(20423,3422,'2003-09-11 20:50:20',124,'gus','<blockquote>shooting dancers in midwest cities, including my sister</blockquote>\r\n<p>\r\nI imagine you on a rampage through the southwest, your semi automatic chopping down dancers one by one in every dusty town and then, you drive off, waving your gun over your head with a hysterical \\\"YEE HAW!\\\" as the wind fucks with your hair while you twist the giant white steering wheel of your faded aqua colored convertable boat of a car.',0,434,0,0,'63.198.55.58','en'),(20424,3422,'2003-09-12 00:37:07',82,'simon','email from a friend in ny: \\\"so how are you getting here? probably driving an el camino with 18 cameras and 3 hot girls.\\\" my response: \\\"no, 3 cameras and 18 hot girls, see ya soon!\\\"',0,192,124,0,'66.222.31.32','en'),(20425,3422,'2003-09-12 00:44:00',17,'austin','stop this masquerade and come home!',1,35,82,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20426,3404,'2003-09-15 03:56:01',75,'tyler','and then...the horses started fucking!',1,38,0,0,'66.245.49.136','en'),(20427,3426,'2003-09-15 05:04:15',17,'austin','The only foe we have to foe is foe himself.',1,43,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20428,3386,'2003-09-15 08:59:34',511,'Arnay','Inspiring amount of detailing. Thank you.',0,41,0,0,'203.109.254.40','en'),(20429,3424,'2003-09-16 14:21:27',93,'chiamattt','brilliant.\r\n<br>\r\nHey, did yall know that Bush Sr, and his wife are over in Russia have tea and fourwaydoublepenetration talks with Putin and his babooshka?\r\n\r\nWhats the deal? Are former heads of state allowed to do that? ',0,222,0,0,'61.40.66.81','en'),(20430,3418,'2003-09-17 03:22:46',75,'tyler','is that a korean word?',1,22,0,0,'66.245.49.136','en'),(20431,3418,'2003-09-17 04:48:05',93,'chiamattt','chin waggin man. like moving the chin. talking. chatting. shooting the shit. ',0,77,0,0,'61.33.57.97','en'),(20432,3422,'2003-09-18 09:57:49',175,'Paul G','woodlawn & 55th, nw corner.',1,27,82,0,'209.109.226.158','en'),(20433,3424,'2003-09-18 17:46:45',75,'tyler','talking politics is so early 2003.',1,34,0,0,'66.245.80.189','en