ss it. may it burn in hell.',6,381,0,0,'193.91.64.34','en'),(20534,3455,'2003-10-22 16:12:33',370,'outkast','i\\\'m sorry ms jackson',6,23,0,0,'193.91.64.34','en'),(20535,3455,'2003-10-22 16:12:47',502,'chris','did you hook up a date?',1,23,0,0,'147.80.216.122','en'),(20536,3455,'2003-10-22 20:31:21',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ok, so i made this comment simply because i wanted to check <i>\\\"burn! burn!\\\"</i> for once, pun intended.</tt>',6,123,17,0,'66.108.170.255','en'),(20537,3455,'2003-10-23 05:58:08',17,'austin','Negative.  No date.  I never contacted her.  I couldn\\\'t get outlook to send Hotmails and didn\\\'t want to give her this website.  You know how it is trying to keep things on the down low, cover your tracks, etc.',1,215,502,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20538,3459,'2003-10-23 06:13:02',69,'starlajo','writing is for writers. \r\n',0,26,0,0,'203.99.31.50','en'),(20539,3459,'2003-10-23 09:15:57',234,'etienne','i\\\'m thinking my wrists only made it to 35k two years ago so even though i\\\'m tempted - i doubt it.',1,103,0,0,'210.54.226.83','en'),(20540,3459,'2003-10-23 10:38:39',66,'Peter','<br><tt>pr0n</tt>',1,17,0,0,'66.108.170.255','en'),(20541,3459,'2003-10-23 12:11:14',93,'chiamattt','im going to have something ghostwriten. ',0,40,0,0,'218.39.19.38','en'),(20542,3459,'2003-10-23 14:50:25',57,'weeb','<p>i think you should write a romance novel, austie. i think you are secretly a softie first time lover at heart, beneath that crusty pirate shell. \r\n</p><p>\r\nin no particular order; everyone needs:\r\n</p><p>\r\nlove\r\nclean sheets\r\na nice bathtub</p><p>i\\\'m too busy to do it this year, but maybe next year. maybe i\\\'ll do a week of writing but i can\\\'t do a month.</p>\r\n\r\n',1,376,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(20543,3459,'2003-10-23 16:55:11',90,'leonard','Good luck with <u>Yeah, Right</u>. ',1,35,0,0,'67.118.80.121','en'),(20544,3458,'2003-10-24 15:14:26',93,'chiamattt','i shower with my eyes closed. i dont open them until i turn the water off. ',0,75,0,0,'218.39.19.10','en'),(20545,3455,'2003-10-24 15:57:25',93,'chiamattt','i just fit a lighter into my nostril\r\n',5,38,0,0,'218.39.19.10','en'),(20546,3459,'2003-10-24 20:04:27',129,'moebius','erotic action spy romance novel. go for the gusto, austie. include several bisexual motorcycle mechanics of both genders. and at least one good anarchist revolt. props if you set it in barcelona during the spanish civil war. ezra pound could make a guest appearance.\r\n\r\npersonally, i\\\'m lucky if i can find the time to write three consecutive paragraphs that make sense at any given time. a novel would be sort of pushing it.\r\n\r\nbut then again, perhaps. ',2,456,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(20547,1958,'2003-10-24 20:20:30',129,'moebius','<br>i\\\'m currently in a \\\"i love the city\\\" phase. and i\\\'ve been living in it for twelve years now. sometimes i get frusturated with aspects of it, but always i find other things to love. the ceaseless change is both frightening and invigorating.<br><br>i have to admit that the desire to own a big house with a yard, 2 car garage, flower garden, etc. just hasn\\\'t ever really blossomed within me the way it does for lot of other folk. i don\\\'t know why. i think i just had enough of that suburban lifestyle as a kid. when i was a teenager i told myself <i>never again</i> and i really, really meant it.<br><br>for now the \\\"moving out of the city to more affordable places\\\" discussions are on hold, but they will resume someday. and i\\\'m going to have to try not to flinch when it comes up.\r\n',0,813,66,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(20548,3459,'2003-10-25 00:35:16',195,'matt','you can finish mine, if you want',1,32,0,0,'209.208.108.109','en'),(20549,3458,'2003-10-25 05:05:35',57,'weeb','no way man, it\\\'s all about the tub. the thing that is superior about the tub is the superior toe scrubbing potential. you can stretch, and scrub and rub the legs, feet and toes. really get in there and massage them tootsies. i like the medusa: laying back in the water and swishing the hair back and forth like a gorgon and thinking large hair thoughts. large hair thoughts.',1,377,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(20550,3459,'2003-10-25 11:23:52',65,'le','FORGET IT',4,9,0,0,'216.27.177.123','en'),(20551,3459,'2003-10-25 17:56:03',17,'austin','Push it, push it real good!',0,27,129,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20552,3458,'2003-10-27 00:14:48',195,'matt','i got a secret compartment in my shower with a gat in it for lightin\\\' suckas up when they try to ambush a brother.',1,117,0,0,'209.208.109.1','en'),(20553,3459,'2003-10-27 16:00:49',74,'jo','i\\\'m doing it. i\\\'m not going to attempt a novel, however, i\\\'m just going to make myself write 2000 words a day. i know it\\\'s cheating but who cares.',0,158,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(20554,3408,'2003-10-27 22:55:41',57,'weeb','\r\nhere i am randomly commenting on things during my lunch break. i really want to say, that this is my favorite swinney swinney ever. pretty much. yeah. it made me teary. yeah.',1,176,0,0,'67.95.211.226','en'),(20555,3408,'2003-10-28 20:36:26',17,'austin','it is kind of teary ended.',1,26,57,0,'66.128.224.196','en'),(20556,3459,'2003-10-29 08:23:11',372,'mews','<p>i think maybe you should go for the untapped market of senior citizen erotic spy action romance? just because you lose pieces of yourself as you walk down the street, and fall over a lot, it doesnt mean you cant be as cool as every other hero out there.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ps. go easy on the romance bits though...</p>',0,312,17,0,'203.109.254.40','en'),(20557,3458,'2003-10-29 08:29:06',372,'mews','i have the bestest thought patterns in the shower. its like getting stoned and communing with the universe, only the shower doesnt spin quite so much.. is it something to do with the steam ?',0,190,0,0,'203.109.254.40','en'),(20558,3463,'2003-10-30 06:34:01',17,'austin','FUCK YEAH!',1,10,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20559,3463,'2003-10-30 07:06:16',154,'ryan','what if it would have been a guy getting beat up?',0,49,0,0,'216.99.204.69','en'),(20560,3458,'2003-10-30 14:01:28',370,'seanthecon','jesus. isn\\\'t anyone going to admit to jerking off in the shower. that\\\'s why god created shampoo for christ sake.',6,118,0,0,'193.91.64.34','en'),(20561,3463,'2003-10-30 14:02:57',370,'seanthecon','motherfucker! you\\\'re a mad man chia. saint fucking chiamattt!',6,64,0,0,'193.91.64.34','en'),(20562,3465,'2003-10-30 15:47:27',502,'chris','this morning, I was on the treadmill running my fastest ever 2 miler, when all the sudden the machine stopped, I lunged forward nearly smashing my head into the window... i got pissed, and all flustered, sweaty, and out of breath I proceeded to the manager to let them know that my machine is broken, and should be fixed or taken out of use. My girlfriend described my actions as like a four year old stomping off. Needless to say, that got me even more pissed, so I pouted for the next 15 minutes, haranguing  to my girlfriend about exercise equipment safety, thus ending my 4yr-old like tirade. She pretended to listen, then told me to give it up...',5,651,0,0,'147.80.216.122','en'),(20563,3463,'2003-10-30 16:10:38',502,'chris','sometimes I day dream about defending ones honor like you have done. at times I get carried away, like this one time I was day dreaming about beating up a big bully guy that I saw spit in a girls face. I dreamt that I broke both of his arms jet li style, and then proceeded to spit in his face... i awoke in horror thinking that I had taken it bit far when I realized I was clenching my fists and teeth... once i had shards of a dirt punks teeth in my knuckle, i fealt pretty shity but he did try to rob me.',0,505,0,0,'147.80.216.122','en'),(20564,3465,'2003-10-30 17:57:43',75,'tyler','i bought an ipod for windows the other day, and found out when i got home that actually, you need to buy an adapter for usb 2.0, OR a fifty-dollar firewire card for your laptop. oh--best buy doesnt carry the $10 adapter. they do, however, carry the $50 card. mother fuckers!i was really pissed.',1,294,0,0,'66.32.109.162','en'),(20565,3463,'2003-10-31 02:38:29',124,'gus','most times if you break up a domestic dispute, even if you stop a guy from beating up his gf, she\\\'ll defend him. that\\\'s just how those things go, it\\\'s the same way in america.',0,184,0,0,'64.160.54.2','en'),(20566,3465,'2003-10-31 05:39:42',90,'leonard','Junior Whopper. I like these kind of stories. ',6,46,0,0,'216.249.90.175','en'),(20567,3463,'2003-10-31 13:47:31',372,'mews','<p>hurrah, chivalry lives!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i was on the bus one time when the man in front of me (who had been arguing with himself at a moderate volume for about 10 minutes) dropped his tupperware container of wine gums all over the floor. it was rainbow carnage ladies and gentlemen! </p>\r\n\r\n<p>my giggles escaped before i could stifle them, and i suddenly became an unwitting target to a torrent of abuse and rapidly approaching wookie hamfists. i was rescued by a complete stranger who stood in between us and prepared to put up his own fisticuffs should the need arise. it was like suddenly becoming the star in a really low budget action movie</p>\r\n\r\n<p>all he told me is that his name was woody, and he was an art student. thanks woody, you made me a good memory on that day</p>\r\n\r\n ',0,784,0,0,'203.109.254.40','en'),(20568,3463,'2003-10-31 21:35:26',75,'tyler','way to go man! i am so impressed right now. you are the man. sometimes a man\\\'s gotta do what a man\\\'s gotta do. john wayne style.',1,134,0,0,'66.32.109.162','en'),(20569,3463,'2003-10-31 21:46:58',195,'matt','what are wine gums?',1,19,372,0,'209.208.110.224','en'),(20570,3463,'2003-11-01 04:42:35',372,'mews','<p>theyre close to the worlds most perfect candy!! i am appalled! </p>\r\n\r\n<p>in the chivalrous spirit of this thread, if you give me your address i will mail you a packet ? (i am secretly an international candy pimp)</p>',0,220,195,0,'203.109.254.40','en'),(20571,3459,'2003-11-01 06:12:35',180,'auntie','maybe this year i will do the thing and finish.  i vote for a spy novel.',2,72,0,0,'69.40.65.88','en'),(20572,3463,'2003-11-01 06:44:43',234,'etienne','omg matt.  wine gums are close to candy god.',0,44,195,0,'210.55.126.137','en'),(20573,3463,'2003-11-01 17:31:28',195,'matt','yeah, that would be great.  i like getting things in the mail.  how sweet of you! :)<BR><BR>my address is:<BR><BR>matt mccord<BR>15458 snow memorial highway<BR>brooksville, fl, usa  34601<BR><BR>thanks!',1,202,372,0,'209.208.109.87','en'),(20574,3468,'2003-11-04 04:52:28',241,'linus van pelt','<p>a moving requiem. i remember the first time i heard elliott smith. it was \\\'rose parade\\\'. his wispy voice seemed like a big blanket you could wrap yourself up in to counter any feelings of lonelieness that his lyrics may have inspired. it\\\'s sad that i was not surprised to hear of his death.</p>  ',0,308,0,0,'67.60.111.176','en'),(20575,3465,'2003-11-05 21:19:39',83,'adamajah','I thought that was the whole point of \\\"animal style\\\":  onions.',0,68,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(20576,3465,'2003-11-05 22:42:30',17,'austin','no, no, no, it is about the cheese and sauce.',1,45,83,0,'66.128.224.196','en'),(20577,3468,'2003-11-11 09:16:39',370,'seanthecon','i had a dream about elliot last night. that he was a massive coke head and finally he couldn\\\'t handle the come downs. i felt naively shocked when i found out, like when you\\\'re in kindergarden and you find out your teacher smokes. crack that is.',6,250,0,0,'193.91.64.34','en'),(20578,3407,'2003-11-11 15:32:17',223,'shanE','I think that I know that girl',3,29,0,0,'211.110.37.118','en'),(20579,3407,'2003-11-11 15:32:44',223,'shanE','Sure! She s in one of Chia s classes!',0,37,0,0,'211.110.37.118','en'),(20580,3468,'2003-11-12 17:47:34',65,'le','beautiful',4,9,0,0,'68.166.72.80','en'),(20581,3463,'2003-11-13 21:27:41',372,'mews','el candy on its way sir !',0,25,195,0,'203.109.254.40','en'),(20582,3470,'2003-11-14 05:55:11',176,'Scapegoat','I don\\\'t have a pair of jeans I want to put on a wall but I do have the fronts and backs of several concert t-shirts from my youth that I would love to sew onto a blanket. Just for sitting at home and staying warm while watching a movie or something but packed with memories.',0,277,0,0,'65.49.93.202','en'),(20583,3474,'2003-11-14 06:08:22',176,'Scapegoat','It would be an attempt no? The raising of the gun would that not be enough for intent? The aspect of his character that led to the situation being diffused doesn\\\'t seem relevant to her intent. Was his character trait pivotal in the disarming or is it coincidence? I\\\'m envious tyler wish I was enrolled this year.',0,318,0,0,'65.49.93.202','en'),(20584,3474,'2003-11-15 23:56:17',75,'tyler','just as it would be up to the jury in this case to decide, so it is up to the reader. attempt is when an outside force intervenes to stop someone from committing a crime, but the actor would have gone through with it otherwise. the thing sbout this story is, was the force that stopped her from shooting him internal or external? how much of her husband was \\\'inside\\\' her, stopping her from firing the gun? like the woman he was having sex with was immobilized physically, was ms. Smith immobilized too? for some of the same reasons?',1,538,0,0,'66.245.90.83','en'),(20585,3471,'2003-11-16 18:23:57',241,'linus van pelt','while i can appreciate how comfortable a state it would be being naked with bacon (whether eat be eating it whilst watching Buffy or just cradling it in your hand with the backdrop of your nudity), i must point out one flaw in your argument and that is the potential for pain infliction during the actual making of the bacon. once you throw those raw bacon strips on a hot frying pan they start poppin\\\' like corn kernels, firing grease in every direction. hot bacon grease plus bare skin- not so comfortable. ',0,512,0,0,'67.60.111.176','en'),(20586,3474,'2003-11-17 20:06:58',154,'ryan','menacing with a weapon?',0,23,0,0,'216.99.204.82','en'),(20587,2926,'2003-11-18 03:37:24',75,'tyler','\\\'Oobie can\\\'t play baseball.\\\'',1,37,0,0,'66.245.90.83','en'),(20588,3475,'2003-11-18 07:50:42',17,'austin','Tell me about it:\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/art/galleries/cars/67_charger.jpg\\\">',1,103,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20589,3473,'2003-11-18 15:44:56',75,'tyler','\\\'hell is where you make money.\\\'\r\n\r\n--now that is the very best non sequitor ever. \r\n \r\n',1,93,0,0,'66.245.90.83','en'),(20590,3471,'2003-11-18 18:44:39',392,'p.','Aw man, you\\\'re right.  Grease burns are no good.  So let\\\'s say you make the bacon and then get naked?',0,107,241,0,'12.13.167.78','en'),(20591,3475,'2003-11-19 18:30:20',90,'leonard','Now, that\\\'s a deal sealer.',1,29,17,0,'67.118.80.121','en'),(20592,3471,'2003-11-19 21:01:54',241,'linus van pelt','i can agree to that. to be honest with you, i really need little excuse to get naked with anything. or anyone, for that matter.',0,127,392,0,'67.60.111.176','en'),(20593,3476,'2003-11-20 08:58:11',175,'Paul G','*YES*\r\n<br>\r\n[best ever, hool.]',1,31,0,0,'209.109.237.233','en'),(20594,3476,'2003-11-20 14:52:52',93,'chiamattt','just be glad they didnt try and impose democracy on your ass. \r\n',1,64,0,0,'218.39.19.8','en'),(20595,3476,'2003-11-20 21:25:29',75,'tyler','i\\\'m no longer surprised to hear anything bad about the US. upset, yes.',1,73,0,0,'66.245.18.154','en'),(20596,3476,'2003-11-20 21:52:11',123,'dcc','land of the free ... to be exactly like your neighbour.',0,55,0,0,'64.26.177.65','en'),(20597,3475,'2003-11-20 23:21:21',129,'moebius','<br>mmm. warm leatherette, dude. warm leatherette.',0,50,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(20598,3476,'2003-11-20 23:53:52',129,'moebius','christ, i had no idea things had gotten that bad. but on the other hand i can\\\'t say i\\\'m terribly surprised either. i mean, even US citizens get their bungholes searched if they try to fly cross-country with a one-way ticket these days.<br><br>but from your experience it sounds like US Immigration isn\\\'t as concerned with the thought of terrorists crossing over into the US as they are about some loosely defined category of Canadian freeloader. because you know the US homeless problem is actually really the fault of a bunch of itinterant young Canadians. i\\\'m sure soon they\\\'ll even be trying to stop Canadian geese at the border.<br><br>but the wacky thing is, i\\\'m constantly hearing liberal-minded people down here talking about how they\\\'re on the verge of moving to Canada. i don\\\'t hear much talk of the opposite. so i think your immigration authorities and ours maybe have their priorities reversed--because before too long you\\\'re going to have a flood of filthy Americans!',1,1006,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(20599,3476,'2003-11-21 00:22:37',154,'ryan','<p>the british did basically the same thing to me when i was living in europe and was travelling broke and with no return tickets during my 18 month stay.  the british sent me back to france, which was ok.  i was 18 at the time, but the chubby british lady even asked if my parents had any idea where i had been for the past year.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\ni\\\'ve also been searched pretty harsh by some canadian border cops on my way to BC a couple of times.  doobies!\r\n</p>',0,462,0,0,'216.99.204.77','en'),(20600,3476,'2003-11-21 00:35:23',122,'hool','<p>thanks paul.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i feel stupid for just not thinking about the one way ticket thing.  i don\\\'t feel too stupid about saying i don\\\'t have a full time job.  as for travelling light, i wouldnt\\\' have thought of that anyway because i haven\\\'t travelled internationally before.  seems to me that travelling light would indicate a short stay? wish i could\\\'ve had a few pointers before hand, i\\\'m sure all of this could\\\'ve been avoided. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>if i try to go to the US again, i\\\'ll visit the US consulate here first, also i\\\'ll make sure to have a letter of invitation from whomever i\\\'m staying with. i\\\'ll pack a huge fucking trunk, and i might even shave.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i wonder how many canadians actually leave canada to freeload off of the american system?</p>',0,793,0,0,'66.185.84.68','en'),(20601,3476,'2003-11-21 01:55:28',234,'etienne','<3',0,2,0,0,'219.88.223.161','en'),(20602,3476,'2003-11-21 02:08:32',93,'chiamattt','alan thicke, alex trebeck, micheal j fox, celine dion, brian adams, oh there of lots of them. ',0,94,0,0,'218.39.19.9','en'),(20603,3476,'2003-11-21 07:01:44',154,'ryan','peter jennings.<p>\r\nfreeloading off of the american system sounds funny.',0,72,0,0,'216.99.204.50','en'),(20604,3471,'2003-11-21 14:08:43',392,'p.','mmmm, naked.',0,12,0,0,'12.13.167.78','en'),(20605,3476,'2003-11-21 22:52:59',129,'moebius','you know how it is...canadians hop over the border for, i don\\\'t know, cheap wine in bulk or something, and then they kind of go off the rails for awhile and before they know it they\\\'ve ended up hosting game shows or network news programs. it\\\'s a problem, and it\\\'s about time someone did something about it!',0,318,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(20606,3476,'2003-11-22 21:52:46',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yeah hool... canadians are turning down their socialized healthcare left and right to come partake of our wonderful american system! its so wonderful here. where else can you work a 60 hour week and still barely get by! heh.</tt>',1,237,122,0,'66.65.21.112','en'),(20607,3476,'2003-11-22 21:54:37',66,'Peter','<br><tt> yo and also: when we were returning from our wedding, we got the same schtick due to the fact that we were on one way returning tickets. ironically, our massive amount of luggage flagged us, too. they even threw out the nose-hair clippers i had in my checked bag. dangerous blades and all, you know.</tt>',1,313,122,0,'66.65.21.112','en'),(20608,3476,'2003-11-22 22:37:07',17,'austin','It is a total mania.',6,20,66,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20609,3477,'2003-11-23 16:49:54',17,'austin','Alright, now you need to tell us some stories!',1,46,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20610,3475,'2003-11-24 02:20:14',195,'matt','this article reminds me of one time when i was thirteen.  i found myself alone in my grandparents\\\' house one day and decided to do a little exploring.  grandma had an old vacuum cleaner with a horizontal tube body.  inspiration struck.  i took the hose off the front and stuck my thing in there and turned it on.  man.  talk about an orgasm.  i\\\'m still trying to top that one.  i couldn\\\'t wait for my next chance to be alone in the house, but by then, it had gotten too big to fit in there.  i still yearn for my little hoover nymphette.',1,546,0,0,'209.208.109.84','en'),(20611,3478,'2003-11-24 03:26:57',93,'chiamattt','loved it!',1,9,0,0,'211.214.6.90','en'),(20612,3478,'2003-11-24 10:30:22',370,'seanthecon','haha. i\\\'m pretty sure you\\\'ve had narcolepsy since i\\\'ve known ya. i once had a witch put a curse on me that left me celibate for a year. no fooling. wicked piece man.',6,174,0,0,'193.91.64.34','en'),(20613,3463,'2003-11-24 21:10:20',195,'matt','i got the candy and vegemite today (11/24).  thanks so much.  you shouldn\\\'t have spent all that money mailing it to me.',1,122,372,0,'24.129.129.219','en'),(20614,3463,'2003-11-25 01:45:04',372,'mews','haha, yeah i was a bit taken aback by that myself - christmas postage prices are insane, but what the hey :)\r\n\r\ni hope you liked them! (ok, i dont expect you to like the vegemite..cos it is pretty weird..)',0,205,195,0,'203.109.254.40','en'),(20615,3477,'2003-11-25 11:39:15',508,'edward','I will.. =)',0,11,17,0,'62.65.89.96','en'),(20616,3477,'2003-11-25 18:54:06',502,'chris','...I read a headline today, \\\'Hunger Rising Again, U.N. Report Says\\\'... then another...\\\'Economy Grew Even Faster Than First Thought in 3rd Quarter.\\\' I looked at it all with crossed eyes and a fleeting amount of care. I think I just realized that shit changes, sometimes just the names change, sometimes just numbers of things... I find it hard to care about much outside my own life... and thats shame on me.',0,419,0,0,'147.80.216.122','en'),(20617,3478,'2003-11-26 00:31:30',508,'edward','really funny.. =)',0,17,0,0,'62.65.88.47','en'),(20618,3477,'2003-11-26 00:51:17',508,'edward','nice that you write such a thing, because my mind was not only about swinney. but about everything. and you are completely true in what you are saying. it is really strange that people do not think about it, care about it, more. it just too easy not to. but then again, who could blame them? I think I might get back to the matter in another text sometime.. thanks.',0,365,502,0,'62.65.88.47','en'),(20619,3476,'2003-11-26 03:18:21',520,'OpheliaJones','it\\\'s a culture of intolerance, bred by fear of the unknown. which in this case was you...',1,92,122,0,'65.50.27.143','en'),(20620,3477,'2003-11-27 02:44:23',175,'Paul G','<ul>\r\n<li>\r\nmarriage\r\n<li>\r\nfamily\r\n<li>\r\ncompeting sites\r\n<li>\r\neconomic hardship\r\n<li>\r\nfragmentation of the core\r\n<li>\r\njealousy\r\n<li>\r\ngeneral loss of interest in reading/writing\r\n<li>\r\nlack of consensus\r\n<li>\r\nlaziness\r\n<li>\r\ndesire for celebrity\r\n<li>\r\nelitism\r\n<li>\r\nfear of ridicule\r\n<li>\r\ncomments/particular commentators\r\n<li>\r\nno graphic sexual content\r\n<li>\r\nworld events\r\n<li>\r\nimages: pro/con\r\n<li>\r\nsome bad experience on-line...\r\n<li>\r\nmaybe it\\\'s all cyclical?\r\n</ul>\r\n<p>\r\nhi ed.  can i call you ed?  thanks.  that was a nice piece that you wrote.  on-line stuff is funny - isn\\\'t it?  what we see here is like the tip of the proverbial iceberg: under the bit that is exposed, there lies the greater part.  yep.  \r\n</p>',3,741,508,0,'199.35.153.112','en'),(20621,3477,'2003-11-27 03:21:29',234,'etienne','yeah dude.  i agree.  in fact, i will post something this very afternoon.  (ps, thanks for the compliment).',1,107,0,0,'219.88.223.161','en'),(20622,3162,'2003-11-28 04:07:52',17,'austin','Hey, happy Thanksgiving.',1,24,0,0,'67.67.160.101','en'),(20623,3481,'2003-11-28 12:50:04',508,'edward','I like your short pieces.',0,25,0,0,'62.65.89.27','en'),(20624,3477,'2003-11-28 12:58:50',508,'edward','i prefer ed, paul. don\\\'t know why i didn\\\'t choose it as my nick. and you\\\'re right, this is just the tip, and i want more. guess we all want more.\r\n\r\nyour welcome etienne, i wrote a one sentence love letter to your lastest before reading your comment here.',0,264,0,0,'62.65.89.27','en'),(20625,3480,'2003-11-29 00:08:06',176,'Scapegoat','I\\\'ve had days like this. There isn\\\'t much you can do other then just chalk it up to a bad day and move on. I feel for you in the bike sitch though big time.',0,162,0,0,'159.33.2.41','en'),(20626,3481,'2003-11-30 00:20:56',234,'etienne','yeah, i\\\'m not sure i am able to write big long things.  it kind of worries me sometimes though because isn\\\'t the point of being a \\\"writer\\\" writing big things like novels?',1,182,508,0,'210.55.167.18','en'),(20627,3483,'2003-11-30 01:17:08',65,'le','i love it everytime you post something.',4,39,0,0,'64.81.201.13','en'),(20628,3481,'2003-11-30 15:29:57',508,'edward','hell no, the art of writing is to capture a moment, a feeling. tell something that needs to be told. i have a thing for fragments, where a text could be about different things. of course novels are nice too, but it\\\'s like short movies, they are really under rated compared to two hour movies.',0,295,234,0,'62.65.89.140','en'),(20629,3481,'2003-12-01 04:35:46',234,'etienne','thanks - i\\\'ll keep that in mind.',1,35,508,0,'219.88.223.161','en'),(20630,723,'2003-12-01 19:19:50',17,'austin','still highly relevant.',2,22,79,0,'66.128.224.196','en'),(20631,3476,'2003-12-01 20:03:17',156,'lotus','blargh. i must write about going to the US border and then coming back to canada. i love canada.',1,96,0,0,'134.117.202.50','en'),(20632,3483,'2003-12-01 23:48:20',75,'tyler','oh my god, this is really really really good. you should send this to magazines, next thanksgiving. New Yorker, Slate, Atlantic Monthly, etc.',1,141,0,0,'66.245.28.214','en'),(20633,3483,'2003-12-02 02:31:58',129,'moebius','stunning. this is the kind of writing that makes me want to write myself, and for me that is the highest compliment possible.',0,125,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(20634,3371,'2003-12-02 07:15:42',234,'etienne','why not the other way around?  the letter u is important.',1,57,175,0,'210.54.236.103','en'),(20635,3483,'2003-12-02 07:22:59',175,'Paul G','the end of the last paragraph puts to the reader one of the core questions regarding the human condition: \r\n<br>\r\ngiven that our struggle to exist is ultimately futile, how ought we to live?\r\n<p>\r\nwe consume - to what end?  we deny - to what end?\r\ndead flesh preserves life; dead flesh reminds us of the mortality of our own bodies.\r\nyour roommate abstains, and you partake; the two paths are thrown down side by side.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbut you call our cravings, \\\"cheap,\\\" and people like chairs, \\\"overstuffed.\\\"\r\nso, there is doubt; questions are left unanswered.\r\nis it better, or more true, to live one way or another?\r\nor is the best thing to continue to struggle - conscious of the dilemna - until the question is moot...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthank you for sharing.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n[is it ok with you to add this to the \\\"food\\\" series?]\r\n</p>',1,844,0,0,'209.109.226.20','en'),(20636,3483,'2003-12-02 15:37:09',156,'lotus','this is magnificent',1,19,0,0,'64.230.111.143','en'),(20637,3483,'2003-12-02 16:48:02',122,'hool','i loved the pace and the unhappiness of this. ',0,46,0,0,'65.49.92.218','en'),(20638,3483,'2003-12-03 06:41:22',75,'tyler','man, i just love the language, the sounds of the words. this is truly great writing.',1,84,0,0,'66.245.28.214','en'),(20639,3455,'2003-12-03 20:41:39',82,'simon','you thought it was that boring? weird. i read it. and then read it again almost right away.',0,91,17,0,'209.246.68.181','en'),(20640,3455,'2003-12-04 19:49:37',17,'austin','I can\\\'t even say I finished it.  I got about half way through.  It reminded me of reading book because I HAD TO when I was in college.',1,137,82,0,'66.128.224.196','en'),(20641,2472,'2003-12-04 20:52:01',74,'jo','chris isaak has no idea how much that one stupid quote of his from like five years ago has shaped my entire opinion of him.',3,123,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(20642,3486,'2003-12-05 06:04:16',75,'tyler','we don\\\' love them hos.',1,25,0,0,'66.245.28.214','en'),(20643,3486,'2003-12-05 15:14:49',370,'freddy forint','i don\\\'t know what chew heard about me, koszonom szepen servusz.',6,66,0,0,'193.91.64.34','en'),(20644,3490,'2003-12-05 17:01:34',228,'belly','there should be a pregnancy test for an immaculate conception.\r\n\r\none line = not pregnant.\r\n\r\ntwo lines = pregnant.\r\n\r\nthree lines, or maybe more mystical symbol like celtic cross = son of god.',0,193,0,0,'194.128.172.5','en'),(20645,3490,'2003-12-06 06:52:29',75,'tyler','damn.',1,5,0,0,'66.245.28.214','en'),(20646,3455,'2003-12-12 01:28:47',82,'simon','weird.',1,6,17,0,'209.246.80.188','en'),(20647,3491,'2003-12-16 08:21:27',176,'Scapegoat','Congrads on the first one down. I just got my letter saying the schools have everything I should know in the next couple of months if I\\\'m in.',0,144,0,0,'65.49.93.202','en'),(20648,3483,'2003-12-18 16:56:42',86,'egg','holy shit. im literally cryin\\\' ova hea/',0,42,0,0,'64.81.201.13','en'),(20649,3495,'2003-12-22 14:18:03',17,'austin','As one with a great many friends, I must say this is a great friend story.',2,74,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20650,3495,'2003-12-22 14:19:58',17,'austin','And thanks for coming back after a year and 4 months.',1,53,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20651,3491,'2003-12-22 18:13:23',75,'tyler','this was a really strange time for me. i feel ok now. ',1,54,0,0,'66.245.16.91','en'),(20652,3494,'2003-12-23 17:02:11',75,'tyler','good <i>clean</i> girls.',1,24,0,0,'66.245.16.91','en'),(20653,3494,'2003-12-23 23:07:42',195,'matt','baptist girls are full of the kink.\r\n',1,37,0,0,'209.116.42.16','en'),(20654,2952,'2003-12-24 01:21:35',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i just got a new job workign with microscopes again. awesome! i start jan 5, \\\'04. ergo, more of these sorts of entries may loom in the future...</tt>',1,160,0,0,'66.65.22.140','en'),(20655,3494,'2003-12-25 22:46:28',234,'etienne','marina girls!',1,13,0,0,'219.89.101.171','en'),(20656,3497,'2003-12-29 16:55:17',122,'hool','hello my name is hool and i liked this entry.  well concluded.  made me think about carrying a palmtop and a mini polaroid digital printer to make portraits of people in the streets. ',0,183,0,0,'65.49.92.218','en'),(20657,3497,'2003-12-29 17:56:44',195,'matt','<TT>And everyone that gets paid is happy when they finally get off.</TT><BR><BR>a whore told me that once.',1,106,0,0,'209.116.42.45','en'),(20658,3497,'2003-12-30 02:11:37',66,'Peter','<br><tt>hi hool! have you seen <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2228#12731\\\">this comment</a>? its similar to what you were saying. instead of printer and garb, pick up a nice polaroid. i know that its probably amateurish ilk for someone of your very genuine skills, but you\\\'d probably have alot of fun, or at the least, wow some folks with some <i>good point of view</i> shots, on the fly. the polaroid sx70 land camera is specifically suited for this type of thing, if you can find film for it...</tt>',1,536,122,0,'66.65.22.140','en'),(20659,3497,'2003-12-30 02:18:54',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i was waiting for someone to say something to this effect. i scrapped my originally pensive ending for that one sentence, knowing that if i did, the nasties would come out of the woodwork and speak up about this otherwise rather non double entendre ending, heh. </tt>',1,275,195,0,'66.65.22.140','en'),(20660,3498,'2003-12-30 05:17:57',239,'Poonlarp','<i>Everybody\\\'s building the big ships and the boats.\r\nSome are building monuments, others jotting down notes.\r\nEverybody\\\'s in despair, every girl and boy,\r\nBut when Quinn the Eskimo gets here everybody\\\'s gonna jump for joy.<br><br>\r\n\r\nCome all without, come all within.<br>\r\nYou\\\'ll not see nothing like the mighty Quinn.<br><br>\r\n\r\nNow I like to do just like the rest, I like my sugar sweet.\r\nBut guarding fumes and making haste, it ain\\\'t my cup of meat.\r\nEverybody\\\'s just standing around \\\'neath the trees\r\nfeeding pigeons on a limb.\r\nBut when Quinn the Eskimo gets here, all the pigeons gonna run to him.<br><br>\r\n\r\nCome all without, come all within.<br>\r\nYou\\\'ll not see nothing like the mighty Quinn.<br><br>\r\n\r\nA cat\\\'s meow and a cow\\\'s moo, I can\\\'t recite \\\'em all.\r\nJust tell me where it hurts and I\\\'ll tell you who to call.\r\nNobody can get any sleep, there\\\'s someone on everybody\\\'s toes.\r\nBut when Quinn the Eskimo gets here, everybody\\\'s gonna wanna doze.<br><br>\r\n\r\nCome all without, come all within.<br>\r\nYou\\\'ll not see nothing like the mighty Quinn.<br>\r\nCome all without, come all within.<br>\r\nYou\\\'ll not see nothing like the mighty Quinn.</i>\r\n\r\n',6,1209,0,0,'66.65.22.140','en'),(20661,3498,'2003-12-30 14:44:42',273,'Quinn','uh...not supposed to be classifed as \\\"gay.\\\"  not sure how that happened.',0,78,0,0,'63.150.109.234','en'),(20662,3497,'2003-12-31 14:17:01',195,'matt','oh, to be a nasty ;)',1,20,0,0,'209.116.42.43','en'),(20663,3491,'2003-12-31 14:56:18',195,'matt','ativan, son.  ask for it by name.',1,33,0,0,'209.116.42.43','en'),(20664,3497,'2004-01-02 03:25:05',75,'tyler','peter, how does Going do those effects with polaroid film?',1,58,0,0,'66.245.96.30','en'),(20665,3497,'2004-01-02 20:30:42',195,'matt','it looks like he scratches the picture while it\\\'s developing.  i\\\'m just guessing here.',1,92,0,0,'24.129.129.219','en'),(20666,3497,'2004-01-02 20:44:04',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yup, emulsion manipulation. older polaroid film- particularly the time-zero film used in the polaroid sx-70 land camera- has gushy emusions that dont fix immediately (newer polaroid film- suck as the 600 or spectra, etc isnt well suited for emulsion manipulation). so, you can use a popsicle stick or car key to push the emulsions around a bit while its developing, making your shot look more like an impressionist painting (sort of) and less like a photo...</tt>',1,471,75,0,'66.65.22.140','en'),(20667,3497,'2004-01-02 20:44:51',66,'Peter','<br><tt>also: <a href=\\\"http://users.frii.com/uliasz/photoart/polaroid/\\\">more info</a>...</tt>',1,99,75,0,'66.65.22.140','en'),(20668,3497,'2004-01-06 22:28:31',129,'moebius','<br>\r\none of my university photography teachers published a book of emulsion manipulations back when all of that stuff still seemed like brand new wild & crazy stuff (early \\\'70s). he was also an assistant to ansel adams at one point, but that\\\'s neither here nor there, i guess.<br><br>so we all had lots of fun in that class for a day or two making lots of polaroid manipulations and transfers and yadda yadda yadda.<br><br>anyway, great essay. it\\\'s been a long time since you last put this much effort into a piece, it seems--but but i know that working nights, getting married, and holidaze can really be distracting when it comes to the pursuit of the finer arts. glad to see you\\\'re keeping your form solid.',1,722,66,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(20669,3497,'2004-01-07 19:19:22',66,'Peter','<br><tt>thanks man, that wasa good comment. yep, now that i have a new job (avec internet), a day shift, and have accomplished Mission Wedding, I am happily returning to my old less-exhausted/more creative/happy/calm self. yay!</tt>',1,232,129,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20670,1099,'2004-01-08 18:09:45',66,'Peter','<br><tt>now that most of the good sf ppl live in nyc...</tt>',1,60,65,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20671,3499,'2004-01-08 21:27:30',65,'le','bukowski',4,8,0,0,'64.242.240.2','en'),(20672,1099,'2004-01-09 04:44:32',66,'sine','the great eastward migration of \\\'03.\r\n\r\nmore more more muwahahah\r\n',1,69,0,0,'66.108.168.210','en'),(20673,3499,'2004-01-09 18:22:07',129,'moebius','love is a dog from hell',1,23,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(20674,1099,'2004-01-09 20:56:04',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i always chuckle when i hear people call sf <i>the new detroit</i>.</tt>',1,80,66,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20675,3496,'2004-01-09 21:01:22',239,'Poonlarp','i read all of this. it gave me a headache.',6,42,0,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20676,1099,'2004-01-09 23:38:29',129,'moebius','<br>yo, them\\\'s fightin\\\' words!<br><br>sf will never become \\\"the new detroit.\\\" unless you plan to install some hills in detroit and fill it full of wealthy muthafuckahs.<br><br><a href=\\\"http://www.sfgate.com/cgi-bin/article.cgi?file=/chronicle/archive/2004/01/08/DDG1944M6M1.DTL\\\">\\\"Only a city as beautiful as San Francisco could survive what you people are doing to it.\\\" - <i>Frank Lloyd Wright</i></a>',0,425,66,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(20677,1099,'2004-01-10 02:43:07',441,'yummy','incitor was located in an undisclosed neighborhood of san francisco. he and kat enjoyed a lucious christmas day dinner of cheese cheese cheese fondue. followed by copiuous amounts of wine and chocolate. all served up by two french speaking foreign girls born in switzerland. ',1,275,0,0,'24.7.65.16','en'),(20678,3496,'2004-01-10 15:27:56',508,'edward','might just be something wrong with your head. =)',2,48,239,0,'62.65.90.27','en'),(20679,3499,'2004-01-12 17:24:45',122,'hool','i liked this a lot. very straight forward, so i don\\\'t have much input. some of the lines i found too short, but that\\\'s personal preference, i like the cadence to be just a little longer. you make me want to go get high.  i try to go with a one-week-on-one-week-off relationship with weed.  but on the weeks off, i wind up scraping resin. ',0,344,0,0,'65.49.92.218','en'),(20680,3496,'2004-01-12 17:37:32',239,'Poonlarp','in my haste to come up with something witty to reply to your witty comment, i came up with this- \\\"no, perhaps its something wrong with <i>your</i> head\\\". but i finally decided on this- \\\"well, that would only be possible if i actually had a head\\\". but then i realized that my first comment mentioned that i had a headache, which couldnt be the case without a head. so, i suppose ill have to go with the first option. therefore- <i>\\\"no, perhaps its something wrong with <i>your</i> head, to think that something is wrong with <i>my head</i> simply because a complicated piece gave me a headache\\\". so there.',6,622,508,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20681,3501,'2004-01-12 17:40:46',66,'Peter','<br><tt>were you stoned when you wrote this, by any chance?<br><br><i>*best friendly*</i></tt>',1,64,0,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20682,1099,'2004-01-12 17:48:55',66,'Peter','<br><tt>well moe, i dont actually say it myself. i just hear people say it occasionally, and it makes me chuckle as i theoretically superimpose each city into a mental metroglopolis i like to call <i>Detrancisco</i>. I suppose <i>San Frantroit</i>  would work as well, although i think the first sounds better. Imagine the <a href=\"http://detroityes.com/rise/01rencen.htm\">RenCen</a></i> filling soma, the east bay morphing into windosor, canada, and potrero hill being filled with elevated monorail lines, steaming manhole covers and old abandoned hotels. its all quite amusing, from an urban planning point of view... ahh, <A href=\"http://www.citynoise.org/index.php?article=353\">detroit</a>.</tt>',1,697,129,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20683,3499,'2004-01-12 17:58:10',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>off the wall</i> is probably one of the greatest albums of all time.</tt>',1,84,0,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20684,1099,'2004-01-12 18:27:11',129,'moebius','<br>well actually i imagine Potrero Hill looks more Detroitish (Detroity? Detroitistic?) than much of SF. One of the things I like best about it is the way it is sort of surrounded on three sides by industrial wasteland, empty lots, and projects, and bounded on two by elevated freeways. And if you\\\'re lucky enough to live on the northern side of the hill you get amazing views of abandoned shipyards, construction sites, mysterious warehouses, the oakland/berkeley hills, the bay bridge (lit up with strands of pearls at night), and the <a href=\\\"http://saturn5.com/~moebius/twilightcity.jpg\\\">downtown SF skyline</a>. <a href=\\\"http://saturn5.com/~moebius/citypix/city_landscapes/280horizon.jpg\\\"><i>San Detrancisco</i></a>, indeed.',0,745,66,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(20685,1099,'2004-01-12 19:02:38',66,'Peter','<br><tt>see? i knew you would get it :D. now, sf just needs casinos and to spawn some genre of music, and we have a perfect match.</tt>.',1,136,129,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20686,3500,'2004-01-13 18:14:43',75,'tyler','girls. it\\\'s like, all they want to do is fuck and cry.',1,57,0,0,'66.245.69.172','en'),(20687,3500,'2004-01-13 18:31:12',508,'edward','i saw an old school movie on tv the other day. colors with robert cuvall and sean penn. and penn went something like \\\"first sex, then kissing, then it gets into talking and then i\\\'m outta there..\\\"\r\n\r\ni do agree to some extent. \\\"girls have sex to get loved, men love to get sex\\\".\r\n\r\nhowever i do not place myself in that line of men, but there might be some truth in it. sad though..\r\n\r\n\r\naustin: what\\\'s the price for writing article no. 3500? =)',3,463,75,0,'62.65.89.24','en'),(20688,1099,'2004-01-13 18:32:06',129,'moebius','well, SF spawned plenty of psychedelic rock back in \\\'66-\\\'70, which gave it musical street cred for decades, and pretty much made it a natural beachhead for the UK rave scene in the early \\\'90s (for whatever that\\\'s worth). and the bay area has a pretty healthy indierock/hiphop/glitchpop/experimental noise scene going on at the moment. nothing as \\\"monumental\\\" as techno or electro, but so what?<br><br>and please: no casinos. ugh.<br><br>i think most detroit residents would rather have the exchange go the other way around: give them winters that rarely go below 50 degrees F, access to good surfing, the golden gate bridge, etc. but without raising their housing prices/rent to SF\\\'s insane heights. ha!',1,724,66,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(20689,3501,'2004-01-13 18:34:12',508,'edward','i echo that question... somekind of speedier drug seems to have been involved...',1,80,66,0,'62.65.89.24','en'),(20690,3500,'2004-01-13 21:06:06',508,'edward','um.. prize.. nothing else. damn typos.',5,38,0,0,'62.65.89.25','en'),(20691,3499,'2004-01-13 21:22:47',75,'tyler','<a href=\\\"http://bobanddavid.com/section_talent_pft_bukowski.html\\\">bukowski</a>',1,84,0,0,'66.245.69.172','en'),(20692,3501,'2004-01-14 18:24:16',90,'leonard','HAHAHAHAHAHA it hurts when I laugh.',1,35,0,0,'67.118.80.121','en'),(20693,3501,'2004-01-14 18:49:55',239,'Poonlarp','OUCHOUCHOUCH. it burns when i pee.',6,34,90,0,'156.145.58.12','en'),(20694,3502,'2004-01-14 18:55:12',66,'Peter','<br><tt>fuck it.</tt>',1,21,0,0,'156.145.58.12','en'),(20695,1099,'2004-01-14 18:57:02',66,'Peter','<br><tt>you mean, like <A href=\"http://saturn5.com/~peter/music/donovan-hurdy_gurdy_man.mp3\"><i>donovan</i></a>?</tt>',1,43,129,0,'156.145.58.12','en'),(20696,3501,'2004-01-14 19:47:54',90,'leonard','YESYESYESYES pooping rocks, shit a brick. Let\\\'s build a house together?',6,74,239,0,'67.118.80.121','en'),(20697,3501,'2004-01-14 19:51:12',239,'Poonlarp','lets build a glass house out of poop. we could call it <i>Crystal Shit</i>. bob villa would swoon, thats for sure.',6,113,90,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20698,1099,'2004-01-14 19:57:46',129,'moebius','<br>well, not Donovan exactly--he was Scottish. though he probably spent a fair amount of time in SF during that period. no, i\\\'m talking about Jefferson Airplane, the Grateful Dead, Janis Joplin, Santana, Moby Grape (!) and all the other performers whose temporary apartments are now pointed out to tourists during walking tours of the Haight/Ashbury district.....',0,367,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(20699,1099,'2004-01-14 20:20:13',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i know, i just wanted an excuse to post that tune :)</tt>',1,65,129,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20700,3502,'2004-01-14 22:20:03',370,'ballsackimo','your friends sound fucking cool. you, however, sound like a fucking downer.',0,75,0,0,'193.91.64.34','en'),(20701,3499,'2004-01-14 22:35:17',370,'fresh freddy forint','i like the style. to the point. it cuts. especially liked \\\'you moon. you faint suns. water and birds. i feel like a paper man cut out and pasted into this landspace...smoked joint and count money\\\' part. good shit. but two things. you should have stole something from the apartment. and lay off the crack man. that shit is stupid.',6,335,0,0,'193.91.64.34','en'),(20702,1099,'2004-01-14 23:47:31',129,'moebius','<br>oh. well anyway. time for everyone everywhere to <i>invent new music</i>. no more living in the past! no more sound revivals! the seventies, the eighties, the ninties, the early two thousands--all dead! all gone! invent it from the ground up or go home! and so on! (i\\\'d write a manifesto, but you get the idea)',0,317,66,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(20703,3496,'2004-01-15 08:41:44',508,'edward','touchÃ©. but i didn\\\'t realize it was the piece that made your head spin (yeah, right..), i thought you just wanted to mention, since we all felt like throw up after reading this that you felt the same, but because of a mismanufactured head. <br> as a matter of fact, it could however also be spiccoli\\\'s head. guess we\\\'ll never know..',3,341,239,0,'193.10.214.217','en'),(20704,1099,'2004-01-15 14:24:27',175,'Paul G','Nooooooooo!  Pry the 70\\\'s & 80\\\'s from my cold, dead fingers.  The only wax that I still have is circa that era: original 45rpm singles from Earth, Wind, and Fire; Blondie; etc.  I sold my punk!  To this day I cringe.  Three-letter bands!  MDC, DRI, <a href=\\\"http://www.furious.com/perfect/pil.html\\\">PIL</a>!  Look at <a href=\\\"http://www.publicimagelimited.com/\\\">Public Image Limited</a> now!  It\\\'s all so funny.  Ahhh...to be drinking Schlitz Malt Liquor in the alley, snorting coke on the floor of the Metro, head-banging a random skinhead, and then crashing the car on the way home one last time...\r\n<p>\r\nAnd what about The Cramps!\r\n</p>\r\n*destabilizing*',4,677,129,0,'209.109.224.203','en'),(20705,1099,'2004-01-15 17:43:38',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>pil live in tokyo</i> is one of my favorite vinyls. i agree with moe, though. time for something new. but then theres the quandry... how to come up with something new when most every new/marketable frontier has already been reached/saturated. hmm. what to do?</tt>',0,275,0,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20706,1099,'2004-01-15 19:05:22',129,'moebius','<br>but, see \\\"what to do?\\\" is exactly the question that was being asked just before punk broke, the first hip hop beats were dropped, and electro rhythms made their mark...there\\\'s always a new answer. the problem isn\\\'t finding the answer so much as having the courage to ask the question. ',0,301,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(20707,3502,'2004-01-15 19:05:36',57,'weeb','your profile says you live in ELko, CO, but it sounds like you live in NYC. embrace the language of sailors. it is poetry, but a stop glottal enhanced kind of poetry.',0,166,0,0,'66.92.219.22','en'),(20708,1099,'2004-01-15 19:30:47',175,'Paul G','<i>\\\"every new/marketable frontier has already been reached/saturated...\\\"</i>\r\n<br>\r\nburn down the net: it\\\'s the death of local color; live in a one-room shack; write a manifesto; take flying lessons; convert; etc.\r\n<br>\r\nfollow the fearless leader out into space, and make moon-music, or mars-music?  ever more dependent upon the technology that ever fewer people control?\r\n<br>\r\ni don\\\'t know. i like reading about people\\\'s small-scale, low-tech, solutions/responses that are well-integrated with the environment in which they exist.  they are usually highly personal, and don\\\'t reproduce well everwhere and always.\r\n<br>\r\nbackward?  hmmm.  if you\\\'re moving forward in the wrong direction, it\\\'s a good idea to hit the brakes.\r\n<br>\r\nthe biking, picture-making, sewing, bird-feeding, gardening, etc, is all hope-filled and life-sustaining activity - even if, in a sense, it is regressive/backward, or whatever.  maybe the goal is to sustain or preserve something, as opposed to seeing it win, or become popular?\r\n<br>\r\ni like you!',1,1053,66,0,'199.35.131.212','en'),(20709,3502,'2004-01-15 21:39:36',66,'Peter','<br><tt>what the fuck. are you saying that muthafucking new yorkers use a fucking shitload of fucking curse words? my ass! fucking new yorkers dont fucking curse any fucking more than any other fucking fuck in the whole fucking country. fuck!<br><br><i>:D :D :D</i></tt>',1,270,57,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20710,1099,'2004-01-15 21:46:45',66,'Peter','<br><tt>im working on a piece of <A href=\"http://www.improvision.com/products/Volocity/default.lasso\">software<a/> at work that does wonderful things with pixels. it massages them in all the right ways to bring out tons of data. it has the capability to generate random algorithms to do all sorts of crazy stuff to pixels.<br><br>when i was first learning to code the automations involved in the algorithm stuff, i kept thinking <i>\"wow, this is like tweaking a modular synth- setting up step by step microprocesses that do weird things to the imput to effect the output\"</i>. and i have these odd daydreams of generating sound patters from data in pixel form, and finessing it with the software.<br><br>but that would just make me one of a zillion other geeks who mess with computers and sound. i dunno. sometimes, i feel like its all ben done before. i mean, i agree with you about going in good/new directions, but until i can find out which direction, its all just very frustrating.<br><Br>\r\nfor now, i let my bike make music. it swishes along the avenues so lovely, so heartbreaking, so exciting at times...</tt>',1,1129,175,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20711,2976,'2004-01-16 14:23:07',228,'belly','it got easier! yay :-D',0,22,0,0,'194.128.172.5','en'),(20712,1099,'2004-01-17 23:44:41',234,'etienne','\\\"get off the internet\r\ndestroy the right wing\\\"',0,52,0,0,'203.118.173.187','en'),(20713,2976,'2004-01-17 23:49:37',234,'etienne','it does.  and i like that it does, but so often its really hard to wait for the time to pass and not will it forward without trying to enjoy even the bittersweet moments as it does.',0,181,0,0,'203.118.173.187','en'),(20714,2976,'2004-01-19 00:53:28',156,'lotus','it certainly did! i should write a little sequel. though everyone knows sequals suck. hello etienne!',1,100,228,0,'64.230.112.190','en'),(20715,3495,'2004-01-19 02:38:09',156,'lotus','i missed you.',1,13,0,0,'64.230.112.190','en'),(20716,3506,'2004-01-20 19:46:08',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\\\"...how long it\\\'s been since my arrangement of tongue and teeth scooped out the entire inner workings of an animal...\\\"</i><br><br>here\\\'s to keeping it up.</tt>',1,182,0,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20717,3505,'2004-01-20 19:48:15',239,'Poonlarp','if i wanted to have a coronary, id just eat a haggis!',1,53,0,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20718,3505,'2004-01-20 19:58:08',66,'Peter','<br><tt>the recipe nothwithstanding, i really did dig the last paragraph:<br><br><i>\"...for extra goodness, stir some tabasco into the ketchup. good accompanying beverage choices are orange juice, gatorade, milk, or champagne. follow with marijuana, plus video games or movies until digested, then go for a bike ride. take your camera...\"</i><br><br>thats bar none the best way to digest a fine meal. i highly reccommend this process for the holidays, also.</tt>',1,509,0,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20719,576,'2004-01-20 21:45:37',74,'jo','i should update sent.diaryland.com. i haven\\\'t touched it in months.',3,70,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(20720,3506,'2004-01-20 22:44:37',175,'Paul G','These pieces are really good: clean, smart, and richly textured.  There\\\'s a lot packed into each paragraph; yet it\\\'s easily readable, and not at all dense.  The writing has a whisper-like quality about it: soft, familiar, and...something else that I can\\\'t quite put my finger on.  It\\\'s like lotus?  I hope that you continue to submit similar work.',1,359,0,0,'199.35.132.53','en'),(20721,3505,'2004-01-21 22:57:53',57,'weeb','hey it\\\'s the poonster!!@\r\n\r\nwhassup, poooneroonie? ',1,54,0,0,'67.95.211.226','en'),(20722,3505,'2004-01-21 23:09:33',239,'Poonlarp','you know, a little of this, a little of that. still keeping it realer than a mofo, and checking \\\"burn! burn!\\\" at the end of every comment.',6,144,57,0,'66.65.23.42','en'),(20723,3507,'2004-01-23 04:56:37',239,'Poonlarp','why cant more people wrote about shit like orange phones?',6,57,0,0,'66.65.23.42','en'),(20724,3502,'2004-01-24 08:29:06',528,'oliver','I don\\\'t actually live in america. I didn\\\'t specify any country when I signed up, just kept what was on screen. I\\\'m Australian.',0,135,0,0,'211.29.0.61','en'),(20725,3502,'2004-01-24 08:30:03',528,'oliver','i\\\'ll change my profile thing now.',0,36,0,0,'211.29.0.61','en'),(20726,3507,'2004-01-24 18:09:12',520,'OpheliaJones','recently, i\\\'ve become intrigued by the  emotions physical objects seem to be imbued with... probably due to my packing frenzy. but this made me think- how many people have whispered their secrets lovingly into the orange mouth piece? trusting a piece of finely modled plastic to carry their hopes out into the world?',0,319,0,0,'65.50.27.143','en'),(20727,3507,'2004-01-25 05:15:29',66,'Peter','<br><tt>you should write about <i>your</i> physical objects,  then! i like that sort of writing...</tt>',1,103,0,0,'66.65.23.42','en'),(20728,2751,'2004-01-25 16:00:01',175,'Paul G','Dear Moll,<br>\r\nFinding this piece was like being hit with a hammer; that was the impact that it had.  This is everything that I remember about Illinois, Wisconsin, and Kentucky summers, being blue collar, and falling in love.  For me, this was the eqivilent - in text - of stumbling upon an old picture in my Mom\\\'s family album: the sort of thing that forces you to pause, and remember.<br>\r\nThanks, Paul.',1,409,0,0,'199.35.129.123','en'),(20729,2435,'2004-01-26 20:09:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i have black wranglers now. my mom gave them to me the day before i got married. i wore them to dinner on my wedding night.</tt>',1,136,123,0,'156.145.58.11','en'),(20730,3507,'2004-01-26 23:14:05',129,'moebius','<br>speaking of old phones, when i was growing up my parents had one of those really <i>old</i> phones, from back in the day when you\\\'d had to have the operator make your calls for you. (rotary dial? what?) it was an ungainly wooden box that hung on the wall in the kitchen. the two bronze bells and proboscis-like reciever on the boxfront gave it a face with a perpetually surprised expression. on the left side, an earpiece hung in a cradle; on the right, you\\\'d find a crank that rang the two bells. <br><center><img src=\\\"http://www.darkfunk.com/barrot/images/exported/old-telephone.jpg\\\"><center><br>the old phone didn\\\'t work, but that didn\\\'t stop my sister and i from having a blast with it, cranking up a ruckus that you could hear in the house next door and pretending to make time-travelling calls back to rum-running gangsters in 1922.',1,860,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(20731,3507,'2004-01-27 00:51:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yo! my grandmother had one of those too, and id play with it just the same. in my house, growing up, we had one of <A href=\\\"http://atcaonline.com/phone/20.jpg\\\">these</a>, which i actually used alot- it still works, thanks to my mom hacing it with a 4-prong plug, and then with a modular adaptor.<br><Br>my neighbor growing up, a very aged, retired engineer, had one of <a href=\\\"http://www.vintagephone.com/HB/AE50.jpg\\\">these</a>, which he said was army surplus from way back. i was always jealous of that phone!</tt>',1,536,129,0,'66.65.23.42','en'),(20732,3509,'2004-01-27 17:37:11',75,'tyler','the bitter wafer of virginity--i like it!',1,41,0,0,'66.245.8.143','en'),(20733,3509,'2004-01-27 17:38:46',75,'tyler','also--yes it is!',1,16,0,0,'66.245.8.143','en'),(20734,3511,'2004-01-28 17:59:57',75,'tyler','in my opinion: love is seeing something, and recognizing the expression of the fundamental organizing forces in the universe as they play out in it. it is the gasp of amazement that escapes involuntarily, when you realize that there\\\'s really no reason for anything being here, in existence, but somehow it is anyway, just for the joy of itself. when you look at a seashell, or watch a puppy play, or simply sit quietly, you see the creator winking at you, conspiratorily.\r\n\r\nalso, constructively--\r\n\\\'Advancement of technology today has allowed man to manipulate DNA attributes for the purpose of reproducing a better human species. The equal economic purchasing power of both male and female negates the need for dividing the tasks for nurturing, protecting and feeding the young.\\\'\r\n\r\n--neither of these statements is true.',1,832,0,0,'66.245.8.143','en'),(20735,3511,'2004-01-28 18:07:04',420,'thanatos','thanks for the constructive ... can u pls tell me why you think it is not true ... we can manipulate genes today, can\\\'t we... maybe i should do more due diligence on this one ... can u pls elaborate so I can correct this portion... yeah love is both beautiful and really annoyingly disruptive to peaceful solitude... ',1,320,0,0,'67.101.98.187','en'),(20736,3511,'2004-01-28 18:29:09',66,'Peter','<br><tt>wow. firstly, let me say that i respect  your efforts in trying to tackle this topic here... large books have been written about lesser topics, and you have written rather eloquently about your love odyssey.<br><br>\r\nbut id be remiss to say that i actually <i>buy</i> a huge bulk of what youre laying down here. as a scientist, i was cringing at the parallels you used to bisect love. the scientific method? evolution? i dunno. on a hyper-metaphysical sense, perhaps, but...<br><br>\r\ntechnology and dna? love isnt spawned from rna, nor does it live on chromosomes. i dunno. im persuaded to think that no matter how you slice it, such an abstract, personal topic is gonna be hard to qualify or refute via socioeconomic, cultural and scientific systems.<br><br>\r\ndont get me wrong. i only mean this constructive. perhaps its my own perspective that causes me to feel this way, but i think youve bit off alot here, made some good points, perhaps raised some eyebrows... yet it all rings profoundly hollow.<br><br>\r\n<i>\\\"How can I fathom the essense of sex and love?\\\"</i>... good question. but suffice to say, at least for me, no matter whata processes and logic i apply, no matter what religiosociopolitical/economical lens i put on it, the only way i can fathom love at is truest is when i experience the one person i truly love above all others.<br><br>\r\nbut kudos for writing about something you have/are struggling with. thats an intelligent part of the process of healing, learning about yourself, and defining some of the most fleeting elements of your human existence.</tt>',1,1590,0,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20737,3517,'2004-01-28 18:41:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt>bike with regard, cause being a <i>live</i> one is.<br><br>god, it was about fucking time for a good bike piece on here. ill write one. maybe if others do, you can make a series. i dug this alot. youre telling the same story i see every day when i ride. most of the same cast of characters, too. dont forget the bike cops who harass you, the doors that open out into you when youre squeezing between the parked truck and the city bus at about 20 mph, the unlicensed delivery drivers that mow you down while youre in the bike lane, the assholes that fuck with your bike, the cabbies, the rich fucks running redlights in their mercedes.<br><br>lets see your ride. this is mine:<br><br><center><img src=\"http://saturn5.com/~peter/bike/street3.jpg\" border=\"10\"></center></tt>',1,246,0,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20738,3517,'2004-01-28 19:12:52',17,'austin','i like this sort of writing.  i think SUVs are the size of serengeti elephants though.',1,86,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20739,3517,'2004-01-28 20:49:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt><A href=\\\"http://www.hsph.harvard.edu/ergonomics/bike/\\\">interesting</a></tt>',1,89,0,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20740,3511,'2004-01-29 01:09:51',420,'thanatos','peter ... thank you so much for your comments... i had to write all these down bec of the weight and burden i carry ... i hope to rid myself of this, I call \\\"affliction\\\" when i have produced something ... thanks about pointing out the scientific references - i still have to validate those ... i had to write fast, with just googl\\\'ing as my references, bec. i could no longer contain my emotions ... yeah, pathetic, huh, and it was just one night... phew.  thanks again ... i will definitely absorb and think about your advise tonight ...',2,547,0,0,'171.72.5.226','en'),(20741,3511,'2004-01-29 02:25:33',75,'tyler','<p>sure man. this might sting a little, but it\\\'ll be worth it in the long run.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<i>\\\'Advancement of technology today has allowed man to manipulate DNA attributes for the purpose of reproducing a better human species.\\\'</i>\r\n<br><br>\r\nok. first, we can\\\'t change human attributes through genetic manipulation yet, and even if a few scientists can mess around a bit here and there, they don\\\'t because that would be illegal, not to mention extremely immoral. also, if you are making something new, e.g., a \\\'better human species,\\\' you are \\\'creating\\\' or \\\'producing\\\' it; when something \\\'<b>re</b>produces,\\\' it makes a copy of itself (for humans, with the aid of a mating partner). also, if you created a new species, it wouldn\\\'t technically be \\\'human.\\\'</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i>\\\'The equal economic purchasing power of both male and female negates the need for dividing the tasks for nurturing, protecting and feeding the young.\\\'</i><br><br>\r\nwell, for some people, mainly the middle- to upper- middle class, this might be true. otherwise, typically, men make more money than women in a lot of the same jobs. sexual discrimination suits are filed and won every day against many of the largest employers in the USA. also, rasing kids is about more than economics, as my single yet fairly well-off mother will tell you. </p><p>\r\ni\\\'m just being anal, man. keep up the good work. just be careful when you write, what you state as fact, because a glaring factual error will cast doubt on the merits of the whole thing.',1,1558,0,0,'66.245.8.143','en'),(20742,3511,'2004-01-29 05:23:40',420,'thanatos','thanks so much tyler... did not sting at all... this will help me in working through the second draft.  i guess i made some points factual (ie, DNA, economic issue vis a vis male/female) without any supporting documents... i will rework further ...',2,248,0,0,'67.101.98.187','en'),(20743,3511,'2004-01-29 21:12:41',66,'Peter','<br><tt>several parts of this series have slipped through without showing on the front page; since thanatos has put much effort into these pieces, ive made a <i>\\\"lovebytes 2004\\\"</i> series. check it at the top right...</tt>',1,229,0,0,'156.145.58.11','en'),(20744,2741,'2004-01-29 21:18:01',239,'Poonlarp','where do all the random writers go? its sad that this site has so much flux in patronage. all the old timers have left. but maybe thats for the best, considering this writer.',6,174,0,0,'156.145.58.11','en'),(20745,3511,'2004-01-29 22:01:15',420,'thanatos','hey peter ... thank you so much!',2,32,0,0,'171.72.5.226','en'),(20746,3512,'2004-01-30 00:48:47',124,'gus','why couldnt a soulmate be platonic? \r\n<p>\r\nJust because someone is your soulmate, it doesnt mean you will always want to be with them in a romantic relationship.\r\n<p>\r\nIn my experience, it may be someone you dont even want to know. ',0,232,0,0,'67.101.113.170','en'),(20747,3515,'2004-01-30 00:57:54',124,'gus','a foriegn concept but one you sure spend a lot of time thinking about and writing about. not so free that you dont think about it. a lot.',0,137,0,0,'67.101.113.170','en'),(20748,3511,'2004-01-30 02:28:53',75,'tyler','thanatos: i think if you just said something like \\\'we are approaching...\\\' and then mention the dna and economics, that might be good.',1,139,0,0,'66.32.105.69','en'),(20749,3516,'2004-01-30 02:38:57',75,'loser','because you wanted the LD',1,25,0,0,'66.32.105.69','en'),(20750,3511,'2004-01-30 03:24:37',420,'thanatos','hi tyler ... i actually changed it to something like that: <i> The rapid advancement in science and technology may one day provide man the ability to manipulate genetic attributes for the purpose of producing a better human species.</i> .... also \\\"qualified\\\" my economic statement ... \\\" In certain economic strata, ...\\\"   thank u so much!!!!!',2,354,0,0,'67.101.98.187','en'),(20751,3516,'2004-01-30 03:36:57',420,'thanatos','LOL..',2,5,0,0,'67.101.98.187','en'),(20752,3512,'2004-01-30 03:38:43',420,'thanatos','yeah ... in fact i think that is the route i will take, as i am damn bored of fleeting moments .. hihi.. if that is the case, i have lots of soulmates including my sister, my son, and a whole bunch of bestfriends ... just got to control that stupid sexual desire ... ;-)',0,270,0,0,'67.101.98.187','en'),(20753,3512,'2004-01-30 15:44:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt>whats stupid about sexual desire? its one of my favorite things in life...</tt>',1,87,0,0,'156.145.58.13','en'),(20754,3512,'2004-01-30 21:04:30',420,'thanatos','hehe . . . that is another concept i have been pondering about ... why certain people are able to have great casual sex, without a dent in their emotions ... i don\\\'t know what it is about me ... or, not to generalize, quite a few females; though, some women have claimed that they are able to have sex, and go ...',3,316,0,0,'159.37.7.93','en'),(20755,3512,'2004-01-30 21:12:59',66,'Peter','<br><tt>who said anything about casual sex? married life is pretty damn good too :)</tt>',1,88,0,0,'156.145.58.13','en'),(20756,3518,'2004-01-30 23:48:31',129,'moebius','<br>Ayn Rand, oh dear. back in my single days, Rand was a litmus test for me as well...a negative one. whenever women who piqued my curiosity started gushing about Rand, i knew right then & there that it wouldn\\\'t work out. one girl even went so far as to say that i reminded her of Howard Roarke. yikes! time to exit, stage left.<br><br>but i don\\\'t mean to offend. oddly enough, the woman i ended up marrying has a fairly strong <a href=\\\"http://www.aynrand.org/\\\">objectivist</a> streak running through her, even though she\\\'s never read a whit of Rand. we make an interesting couple: a headstrong go-getter CEO-type and a quirky slacker poet/comedian/tech-writer. yin + yang. it works.',2,699,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(20757,3518,'2004-01-31 03:28:31',420,'thanatos','lol ... yeah ... ayn rand\\\'s objectivist philosophy was a paradigm shift for me ... i was obsessed over her for a while, then i read more ... of other things, so i explored through the entire spectrum of philosophers ... when i learned about her affair with nathaniel branden, while she still maintained her marriage with frank o\\\'connor, my blind admiration for her shattered ... then i realized that writers, artists, philosophers ... etc. attempt to express their ideal ... the work is not the creator ... it was easier to accept her imperfection ...',0,557,0,0,'67.101.98.187','en'),(20758,3518,'2004-01-31 03:29:20',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yup; heres to the yin/yang!</tt>',1,40,129,0,'66.65.23.42','en'),(20759,3512,'2004-01-31 03:32:06',420,'thanatos',':) ... perhaps ... i have seen sincerely honest couples who grow and evolve together ... are happy ... it must be me, ..',0,120,0,0,'67.101.98.187','en'),(20760,3361,'2004-01-31 04:47:47',420,'thanatos','still struggling to understand my uptight, very catholic, mother ... when i was growing up i wanted to be a tomboy and play with the boys, but she forced me to be a foo-foo girl ... i am straight, but i felt that same stern black and white, either/or attitude from uptight-rightist-fundamentalist mentalities ... as angry and frustrated it makes me, i also feel sorry for them, bec of their ignorance and ability to reflect, think and ponder, .. they can only follow rules and guidelines (ie., tenets of their religion, social mores, etc.)  i feel for you ... ',0,560,0,0,'67.101.98.187','en'),(20761,2741,'2004-01-31 18:46:33',175,'Paul G','It is good to say things directly, in your own name, in a way that allows the other party a chance to respond in kind.\r\n<p>\r\nIt is not that I would defend this piece of writing, or critique your own clever work, but rather that it seems wrong to mock someone who doesn\\\'t have the capacity to offer a defense - in this case because of absence.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhen people feel that there isn\\\'t room for dialogue, that their attempts to communicate are silenced, and that their risks are the subject of sport, they fall silent.  And then they go away.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEveryone who makes an effort to try new things in life looks the fool at one time or another.  Everyone who writes has written something weird or stinky at one time or another.  It\\\'s not fair to fixate upon a moment in time that may or may not reflect the person in question, now.  But, the more that we record things, the more that is bound to happen, I suppose...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhat people do when they have a type of power, or superior numbers, or anonymity, is revealing.\r\n</p>',3,1046,239,0,'199.35.136.47','en'),(20762,3517,'2004-01-31 22:18:35',75,'tyler','<a href=\\\"http://www.sheldonbrown.com/glossary.html\\\">this</a> is one of the best bicycle resources on the web. what a guy! also check out his personal site for tips and stories of his life in cycling.',1,205,0,0,'66.32.105.69','en'),(20763,3517,'2004-01-31 23:49:20',66,'Peter','<br><Tt>even before i clicked that link of yours, i knew it was gonna be to sheldon\\\'s site. good call. although, i am a bit partial to <a href=\\\"http://www.sheldonbrown.com/fixed/index.html\\\">this</a> area on that site.</tt>',1,231,75,0,'66.65.23.42','en'),(20764,3517,'2004-02-02 00:11:44',75,'tyler','love those handlebars',1,21,66,0,'66.32.105.69','en'),(20765,3517,'2004-02-02 15:43:31',66,'Peter','<br><tt>thanks. theyre trial bars (bullhorns) that i reverse-mounted; much tighter on city streets while still resembling the feel of racing drop bars by giving an option for a lower hand position when racing. well, and they look cool too...</tt>',1,246,75,0,'156.145.58.11','en'),(20766,3520,'2004-02-02 15:46:48',66,'Peter','<br><tt>and dont forget the <i>\\\"christian singles\\\"</i> one that fills my box with subject lines like <i>\\\"saw you at church, wanted to hook up\\\"</i>...</tt>',1,166,0,0,'156.145.58.11','en'),(20767,3520,'2004-02-02 17:01:12',75,'tyler','that was you?!?',1,15,0,0,'66.32.105.69','en'),(20768,3520,'2004-02-02 19:13:05',74,'jo','rad.',1,4,0,0,'146.6.172.208','en'),(20769,3520,'2004-02-02 20:51:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yeah, all the christian girls sweat me nowadays.</tt>',1,61,75,0,'156.145.58.13','en'),(20770,3520,'2004-02-03 01:11:25',17,'austin','fantastic.  the man looks at the ugly tedium of everyday life and finds, what, beauty? entertainment?  a good poem.',1,115,0,0,'64.2.58.24','en'),(20771,1099,'2004-02-03 04:13:34',66,'Peter','<br><tt>wow. what a thread. i wish some of those people were still as participatory here- it would be alot more interesting. also, i had forgotten about that whole <i>griece</i> thing.  that was a while back, yo.</tt>',1,217,0,0,'66.65.23.42','en'),(20772,3521,'2004-02-03 04:16:59',66,'Peter','<br><tt>hi. write us a story.</tt>',