om/performance_parts/store/catalog/Product.jhtmlPRODID=1115&CATID=1099.html\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.gmpartsdirect.com/performance_parts/store/dbimagecache/1160.gif\\\"></a>\r\n\r\nRam Jet 502, you either have one or you want one. With 502 horsepower at 5100 rpm and 565 ft-lbs of torque at 3200 rpm, the instantly throttle responsive Ram Jet 502 drives like no other big-block youâ€™ve ever driven. With over 500 lb. of torque from 2200 to 5200 rpm the ram Jet 502 is a tire shredding monster.\r\n\r\n\r\nDesigned for ease of installation the Ram Jet 502 needs only 12V, ground, fuel feed and fuel return to run. Included are an instruction sheet, service manual and diagnostic tool for ease of installation and service. As with any GM Performance Parts Ram Jet engine, an in tank fuel pump is recommended to avoid possible vapor lock. See the instruction sheet for fuel pump recommendations. Also included is complete throttle linkage that accepts your production throttle cable and transmission cable. Includes pre-programmed MEFI 3 ECM and wiring harness.\r\n\r\n\r\nThe Ram Jet manifold stands eleven inches tall as measured from the front and rear \\\"china wall\\\" on the cylinder block. That\\\'\\\'s just half an inch taller than a typical single plane high rise manifold, Holley carb and air cleaner combo.\r\n\r\n\r\nFor specific internal engine specifications refer to the ZZ502/502. Â ',6,1456,0,0,'209.179.238.253','en'),(20915,3562,'2004-04-02 19:11:35',538,'arcy','Whoa!! Do I ever! But I\\\'m afraid it would probably leave half my car on the road behind it. I suppose that shouldn\\\'t stop me.',2,131,0,0,'142.204.74.44','en'),(20916,3562,'2004-04-02 19:50:42',66,'Peter','<br><tt><a href=\\\"http://www.snopes.com/autos/dream/jato.asp\\\">this story</a> comes to mind...</tt>',1,103,538,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20917,3561,'2004-04-02 23:40:01',368,'haecceity','Amazing.  Simply amazing.\r\n\r\nI was wondering how you were doing, and thought I\\\'d check here.',1,95,0,0,'24.14.131.126','en'),(20918,3561,'2004-04-03 22:09:00',175,'Paul G','Hi!  Thank You!\r\n<p>\r\n<tt>\r\ns w i n n e y !\r\n</tt>\r\n</p>',1,56,0,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(20919,3561,'2004-04-05 23:06:35',124,'gus','yah I just read this whole thing too. nice.',0,43,0,0,'67.101.113.170','en'),(20920,3563,'2004-04-06 02:27:02',75,'tyler','poets of swinney unite! i liked it.',1,35,0,0,'66.32.93.64','en'),(20921,3567,'2004-04-07 04:27:24',156,'lotus','lovely. i\\\'d change a few things, but that\\\'s me. being a talentless picky girl.',2,84,0,0,'64.230.107.35','en'),(20922,3566,'2004-04-07 14:59:13',538,'arcy','This will be me in two weeks time. I\\\'ll keep my eyes peeled for yellow whales.',2,81,0,0,'142.204.75.31','en'),(20923,3565,'2004-04-07 16:39:50',239,'Poonlarp','smoking is bad for you while making money is good. theres a nice logic here. i guess theres some truth to the \\\"stop smoking, make $!\\\" ads you see, huh?',1,157,0,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20924,3568,'2004-04-08 01:03:59',175,'Paul G','nice.',1,5,0,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(20925,3568,'2004-04-08 16:55:48',17,'austin','indeed.',1,7,175,0,'68.6.213.49','en'),(20926,1561,'2004-04-09 19:55:23',66,'sine','bloody brilliant.\r\n\r\n\r\nmore please!',1,35,0,0,'66.108.171.152','en'),(20927,1561,'2004-04-09 20:36:29',543,'Herr Doktor','im the most brill cat on the block!',1,35,66,0,'156.145.58.13','en'),(20928,3569,'2004-04-11 02:16:19',195,'matt','it\\\'s just like when you sleep and you can\\\'t remember anything.  you just don\\\'t wake up.  it\\\'s easy.  nothing to be afraid of.',1,137,0,0,'64.12.117.21','en'),(20929,3569,'2004-04-11 02:17:30',195,'matt','but other people will miss you, yes.\r\n',0,38,0,0,'64.12.117.21','en'),(20930,3569,'2004-04-11 21:14:13',82,'simon','last night i couldn\\\'t sleep and i was all tossing and turning and feeling sick and i had a dream that i was slowly going deaf and i couldn\\\'t tell if it was real or not. no fun at all.',1,189,0,0,'4.237.246.74','en'),(20931,3569,'2004-04-12 17:08:29',66,'Peter','<br><tt>last night, i fell into a deep foodcoma, from whence i have not awaken until now. burp.</tt>',1,100,82,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20932,3573,'2004-04-15 02:32:47',65,'le','your mind is trying to figure something out in your sleep that it can\\\'t do during the waking hours. most likely it is just related to some kind of stress or anxiety. one way to investigate dreaming is to allow for dreamtime during the day. for a couple of days try setting aside 30 mins or an hour just to sit and stare into space and let your mind wander. this way your mind can be free to roam without pressure, and maybe there will be less pressure on it to figure things out while you are trying to sleep.\r\n\r\nthat\\\'s what i think!\r\n',1,541,0,0,'216.254.118.212','en'),(20933,3572,'2004-04-15 03:04:57',75,'tyler','this is fucking awesome.',1,24,0,0,'66.245.86.224','en'),(20934,3573,'2004-04-15 03:15:56',75,'tyler','my alarm clock talks to me in the morning too. no shit. i can never remember what it said.',1,90,0,0,'66.245.86.224','en'),(20935,3573,'2004-04-15 12:09:31',228,'belly','the alarm clock, and the grievances, and the snooze recording... that should be a short film. i would definitely want to see that film. ',0,136,0,0,'194.128.172.227','en'),(20936,3573,'2004-04-15 17:25:31',17,'austin','I would think the suspension bridge is the Golden Gate.  That should be where you get off.',1,90,0,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(20937,3476,'2004-04-16 07:32:17',266,'spleenless','Gosh, I sure do feel a lot better knowing that we\\\'re doing such a fantastic job of keeping our country safe.  I\\\'d be terrified to think that Canadians could just bring cameras into our blessed republic anytime they felt like it.  Do you know how many people are killed each year by camera weilding Canadians?  Well, neither do I but I\\\'m sure there\\\'s got to be lots.',1,377,0,0,'64.170.3.174','en'),(20938,3174,'2004-04-18 01:57:25',75,'tyler','this one stands the test of time.',1,33,0,0,'66.245.73.128','en'),(20939,3573,'2004-04-19 00:17:13',175,'Paul G','Ok.\r\n<i>\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"i boarded an airplane the size of a small city or cruise ship,\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"suddenly, without fail, the plane would start to jerk back and forth violently,\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"ropey.\\\"\r\n</i>\r\n<p>\r\nBut, the alarm clock: My guess is that it is linked to thoughts of the working day, or some scheduled task.  It is the first thing that you see, touch, and hear as you transition from the \\\"safe\\\" place of dream and sleep to the relatively harsh external world.  If the alarm clock records gossip, or complaints, maybe - on a conscious, or subconscious level - you have some sort of anxiety about a task/relationship/etc at work, or on the calendar.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHmmm.  A warning from a friend [alarm clock] that you have doubts about something, or someone, that you are not dealing with - or are not able to deal with right now, for some reason...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt doesn\\\'t seem crazy at all.  You do appear to have a strong visual/graphic orientation, and a thickly-layered personal history.  Movement, totemic/symbolic objects, decision making...\r\n</p>',1,1074,0,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(20940,3504,'2004-04-20 15:18:22',232,'necklace','<p>oh, my mima!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>here you are, still sparklingly beautiful, as you were during those atpasyears we shared, still writing special words and alive and well. amazing how we survived and grew bigger and stronger, losing particles of ourselves along the way but finding more and more each day. oh, my mima, here you are and here i am and there are so many memories blooming in my head now that i read your words and remember what we used to be. i want to read more from you, please tell me how you\\\'ve been, necklace_me@hotmail.com</p>\r\n\r\n<p>all my love,</p>\r\n\r\n<p>necklace.</p>',1,584,0,0,'194.35.212.53','en'),(20941,3573,'2004-04-20 22:10:59',91,'jay','god this is awesome.  i wish i had dreams like this.',1,52,0,0,'66.123.22.66','en'),(20942,3572,'2004-04-20 22:47:10',195,'matt','this is really top shelf stuff.  thanks for sharing it with us.',1,63,0,0,'209.116.42.21','en'),(20943,3573,'2004-04-21 19:19:26',195,'matt','taoists and others believe this reality stuff is a dream, too.',1,62,0,0,'209.116.42.29','en'),(20944,3573,'2004-04-22 01:19:56',175,'Paul G','since you\\\'re not pissed at me [yet] simon, i can tell you the whole truth: the airplane is austin.',1,101,0,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(20945,3573,'2004-04-22 01:19:56',175,'Paul G','since you\\\'re not pissed at me [yet] simon, i can tell you the whole truth: the airplane is austin.',1,101,0,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(20946,3573,'2004-04-22 14:27:39',82,'simon','by austin do you mean: a. the plane is a portable dream version of the city of Austin, Tex-ass or b. that i am inside a gigantic flying Austin Swinney?',1,151,175,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(20947,3564,'2004-04-22 16:00:04',187,'kandrer','i must have lost my memory, since when i try to think of it, all that comes out is \\\"<i>hair</i>\\\". hair, hair, hair, i have lost so much of my hair...<br>\r\n:) hello lotus, i am so glad to pass by here and read things from you that i could not resist writing something; didn\\\'t have to be that dumb, though, i admit ;)',1,324,0,0,'220.214.207.62','en'),(20948,3561,'2004-04-22 16:32:30',187,'kandrer','great stuff, Paul, and i am not surprised at all. i checked here because luckydevil seems down forever; send me your new email one day, OK?',1,139,175,0,'220.214.207.62','en'),(20949,3573,'2004-04-22 18:59:49',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\\\"i am inside a gigantic flying Austin Swinney\\\"</i><br><br>you win \\\'quote of the day\\\', simon!</tt>',1,120,82,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20950,3570,'2004-04-24 09:44:08',508,'edward','why givin\\\' up the friendship if you\\\'re that close?',3,56,0,0,'193.10.210.38','en'),(20951,3569,'2004-04-24 09:48:24',508,'edward','i like this, again. have you seen 21 grams, etienne?',1,52,0,0,'193.10.210.38','en'),(20952,3570,'2004-04-24 10:42:02',538,'arcy','I think he\\\'s \\\"simplifying everything\\\" as a means of punishment for not being the success he doesn\\\'t realize he already is. SHouldn\\\'t a mark left on the world be one made by the things that you charish, a by-product of what you love to do and who you are? So, maybe I could handle being a friend, but he wants neither lover or friend right now. Unfortunately, I\\\'m not a computer, or a piece of animation software, so I\\\'m getting benched like everyone else.',0,476,508,0,'69.195.29.221','en'),(20953,3570,'2004-04-24 17:50:31',508,'edward','i\\\'d like to answer you, but longer, preferably in an e-mail.. if it\\\'s ok with you. if so, send an empty letter to ghtx@mail.nu so i get your address..',1,156,538,0,'212.181.180.217','en'),(20954,3561,'2004-04-26 01:35:05',175,'Paul G','thank you too, gus!',1,19,124,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(20955,3561,'2004-04-26 01:42:19',175,'Paul G','thanks, k!  i sold/cancelled/deleted everything possible.  the only thing left is: paulgermanos@msn.com\r\n<p>\r\ncheers.\r\n</p>',1,123,187,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(20956,3564,'2004-04-26 02:07:36',175,'Paul G','metaphor: mostly hairless coming into the world; mostly hailess leaving it - at the end of a long life.  white to black, to white again.  innocence lost, and then regained.  the childish rush to maturity, and then the adult\\\'s remorse...\r\n<p>\r\nit seems in keeping with the piece - a very good piece...\r\n</p>',1,309,187,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(20957,3575,'2004-04-26 19:57:15',228,'belly','i don\\\'t like it when people say you have to let go completely. i think it\\\'s ok to still love someone enormously, even when you aren\\\'t in a relationship with them anymore. it\\\'s part of what made you who you are, after all. as long as you aren\\\'t waiting around for an improbable reunion, then i don\\\'t think there\\\'s anything wrong with not letting go of <i>everything</i>. you can want more than one thing, o rperson, or future at the same time, and love as many people as you want.\r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'m not sure if hearts ever really heal. i think we just get used to the broken bits. </p> ',0,601,0,0,'80.40.78.218','en'),(20958,3576,'2004-04-26 20:01:10',228,'belly','i really, really, really like this. a lot.',0,42,0,0,'80.40.78.218','en'),(20959,3576,'2004-04-27 20:34:21',492,'bmg','congratulations mr joker. glad you are so happy. ',2,49,0,0,'147.80.216.122','en'),(20960,3575,'2004-04-28 07:40:30',370,'freddy forint','all i know is that we should have drank more. and had we accomplished this simple, but very meaningful task, i\\\'m sure all would have been solved. i thought we\\\'d get another chance. let that be our lesson. drink now, before the opportunity passes you by. and by opportunity, i mean life. ps: you owe me a rusty trombone.',6,325,0,0,'24.69.255.245','en'),(20961,3579,'2004-04-28 13:53:13',75,'tyler','hey man, we\\\'re all socially awkward sometimes.',1,49,0,0,'66.245.116.94','en'),(20962,3579,'2004-04-28 13:53:51',75,'tyler','also--have you tried smoking pot and watching Chapelle\\\'s Show?',1,65,0,0,'66.245.116.94','en'),(20963,3580,'2004-04-28 23:18:06',75,'tyler','Butters: \\\'Sorry guys. I\\\'d rather be a crying little bitch than a faggy goth kid.\\\'',1,90,0,0,'66.245.116.94','en'),(20964,3576,'2004-04-29 14:09:07',165,'joker','hi bmg,\r\n\r\nthanks.  hope we get to see you soon.',2,48,492,0,'216.141.190.87','en'),(20965,3582,'2004-04-29 14:30:20',66,'Peter','<br><tt>the one-two punch; classic.</tt>',1,40,0,0,'156.145.58.11','en'),(20966,3585,'2004-04-29 23:16:43',83,'adamajah','Beckys and Chads.',2,17,0,0,'216.206.179.50','en'),(20967,3580,'2004-04-30 03:01:07',546,'pepon','<p> Seems you\\\'ve got a good handle on both.  Why so abrasively harsh? </p>',0,77,75,0,'132.239.112.74','en'),(20968,3585,'2004-04-30 06:11:21',17,'austin','Becky played in the surf while Chad watched longingly from the Jeep while doing database work for his employer via wireless connection through UCSD.',4,148,83,0,'68.6.213.49','en'),(20969,3290,'2004-05-03 03:35:45',175,'Paul G','a year later:<br>\r\nroughly 30,000 americans have been killed or severely wounded in iraq, thus far.\r\nthat\\\'s about 1/4 of the total number of american troops currently deployed, there.\r\nat the moment, we\\\'re pulling out of the iraqi city of falluja, and reconstituting the same iraqi army that we went there to fight - a year ago.\r\nand we\\\'ve decided to put an ex-republican guard general in charge of said force.\r\n<p>\r\nthere\\\'s a presidential election looming here, at home.\r\nthe democratic candidate: john kerry, has said that the problem with iraq is that we don\\\'t have enough troops there; if elected, he\\\'s promised to send more.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe important thing is that we have a choice:\r\na choice between two roughly 60 year-old, white, millionare, male, yale grad, skull and bonesmen.\r\ndon\\\'t worry: if \\\"your guy\\\" doesn\\\'t win, his distant relative will.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit\\\'s almost funny to stop, and think, that there really isn\\\'t any such thing as iraq.\r\nthe british just made it up, post ww1.\r\nthere\\\'s no iraqi ethnic group, no iraqi language, no iraqi religion.\r\niraq is like the old yugoslavia: a fiction composed of diverse people - who hate each other.  and like yugoslavia without tito, iraq without hussein is flying apart.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni keep waiting for someone to stand up and say: \\\"the emperor has no clothes.\\\"\r\nbut the show keeps going on - here in the fourth incarnation of the reagan administration - more strange than fiction.\r\n</p>',6,1490,93,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(20970,3585,'2004-05-03 03:56:43',175,'Paul G','there have been a number of references to things inside/outside boxes, and also to Hunter S. Thompson, in recent articles.  interesting.  i\\\'ve spent a lot of time with <i>Hell\\\'s Angels</i> in the past year - after a 20 year break - and have been struck by the fact that at the time of its writing HST was still on the inside, looking out, talking about \\\"them.\\\"  something happened during vietnam, and i wonder if it will happen again, now, as the 70\\\'s roll back around.  i liked it!',1,497,17,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(20971,3435,'2004-05-04 00:28:32',17,'austin','weebz.  talk to me.',1,19,57,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(20972,3332,'2004-05-04 04:46:58',549,'tousled','<p>I would endeavor to kill art if I weren\\\'t an artist.  Do people of other ilks hate the things they love as much as artists do?  Do lawyers sit around and say, \\\"fuck law, man.. just <i>fuck</i> it.\\\"?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I have to admit I\\\'ve said that about art a few times.  \\\'Course I then dragged myself out of that torpor with some art.</p>',2,349,0,0,'67.123.184.109','en'),(20973,3573,'2004-05-04 14:58:40',86,'egg','It makes sense that people\\\'s clocks would talk to them. you\\\'re probably not psychotic, and if you are, only mildly so. still functioning, like andy warhol or henry kissinger. the ghost of something is just trying to get you to investigate. basically you should be grateful for this sort of clairvoyance into the supernatural messages passed between yourselves. ',1,367,0,0,'64.81.201.107','en'),(20974,3561,'2004-05-04 17:49:12',66,'Peter','<br><tt>there are still some paul g chicago photos <a href=\\\"http://www.citynoise.org/search.php?for=paul+g&Submit=Search%21\\\">here</a>...</tt>',1,147,0,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20975,3585,'2004-05-04 23:54:49',57,'weeb','so yo what\\\'s up?! you\\\'re living in a hotel room?? last i heard, you moved out of town without telling ME and i have cut you out of my will. now you\\\'re talking skinny cigarillos in capris and maids talking nasty about dishes.\r\n\r\nwhat up yo! what up!!',0,258,0,0,'208.218.54.65','en'),(20976,3588,'2004-05-05 05:20:57',546,'pepon','A tribute to the insanity inducing effects of relationships-gone-distorted. I hope you continue to find yourself.',0,113,0,0,'216.199.210.45','en'),(20977,3589,'2004-05-05 20:04:41',75,'tyler','now this makes no goddamn sense whatsoever. i read it three times.',1,66,0,0,'66.245.116.94','en'),(20978,3589,'2004-05-05 20:13:19',66,'Peter','<br><tt>with comments like these, one has to wonder if tyler\\\'s <A href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/info_user.phtml?uid=75\\\">average nature</a> is really <i>\\\"best friendly\\\"</i> ;)</tt>',1,202,75,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20979,3589,'2004-05-05 20:17:56',534,'griece','go ahead and ask him what he does for a living then it oughta make more sense.',5,78,75,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(20980,3589,'2004-05-06 02:49:47',154,'ryan','isn\\\'t he a <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=3529\\\">loss preventiion \\\"specialist\\\"</a>?',0,124,0,0,'216.99.204.109','en'),(20981,3589,'2004-05-07 03:51:03',122,'hool','it made perfect sense to me, and i liked it very much.  thank you incitor.',0,74,0,0,'65.49.92.218','en'),(20982,3290,'2004-05-07 07:03:42',187,'kandrer','all these months, I kept remembering your comment about the inexistence of a thing that could be called \\\"Iraqi people\\\". And yes, it still explains clearly why things are not getting better over there. Proposing a \\\"democracy\\\" to people who don\\\'t want to leave together and share the same laws and beliefs is in deed very naive... It might work after a century or two (if the violence stops, that is).\r\nI was completely shocked that the word \\\"draft\\\" could be pronounced in the USA - about this war that wasn\\\'t needed - without more reaction against it (well I am not very sure since I sortof quitted reading the news, as I said before)',1,657,175,0,'219.125.211.78','en'),(20983,3590,'2004-05-07 07:04:29',17,'austin','Hi H.\r\n\r\nOnce I saw a homeless man in the mission asking for food outside the M<sup>c</sup>D\\\'s at 16th and Mission.  He had the sign about wanting some food from said fast food place.  In general, M<sup>c</sup>D\\\'s is repulsive, granted.  But I was headed there to buy fries to keep my sanity while I went with Weebz to do some errand.  I bough the guy a burger.  When I came out he was eating the food that somebody else had bought him.  The burger I had clearly smelled like M<sup>c</sup>BodyOdor.  I didn\\\'t want to give it to the homeless guy.  I didn\\\'t want him to glut himself on my behalf.  So I took the stinking burger thing with me to the Bart station (like the Metra) and left it on a bench.  Later I left the fries on a trash can, unable to eat what sounded like a good idea at the time.\r\n\r\nP.S. Hi, do you know Paul G.?',1,842,0,0,'68.6.213.49','en'),(20984,3589,'2004-05-07 07:07:04',17,'austin','Fuckin\\\' Griece and Incitor on the same post.  Yay.',6,53,0,0,'68.6.213.49','en'),(20985,3589,'2004-05-07 07:08:57',17,'austin','Also, I\\\'m fine with this post.  Makes sense.  But then I know you are a loss preventor. Or is it a harbinger of loss?  Depends on the side of the loss.',2,154,0,0,'68.6.213.49','en'),(20986,3589,'2004-05-07 09:09:20',370,'ballsackimo','yep. fucking good. works even if you dont\\\' connect it to the job.  ',6,70,0,0,'24.69.255.245','en'),(20987,3588,'2004-05-07 09:16:02',370,'seanthecon','i just saw \\\'ameros perros\\\' for the first time. the first story was great, the other two were boring as shit. one for three. that\\\'s what mike lowell is hitting right now. .333. that\\\'s life. ',6,201,0,0,'24.69.255.245','en'),(20988,3591,'2004-05-07 09:18:09',370,'fresh freddy forint','\\\"always carry a knife\\\"',6,28,0,0,'24.69.255.245','en'),(20989,3290,'2004-05-07 19:09:22',154,'ryan','FUBAR',0,5,0,0,'216.99.204.23','en'),(20990,3589,'2004-05-08 00:05:06',75,'tyler','i\\\'m sorry if i was a little frustrated by this piece. my response was short i admit-- alas, not really \\\'best friendly\\\', was it? incitor, i understand that you are trying to share your life experiences and insights, and i appreciate that. so. i will now list the things that confused me. \r\n<br><br>\r\neven if you know about his job, the first half still doesn\\\'t make sense. a whore\\\'s teeth are around his neck. how can teeth be around someone\\\'s neck? like a necklace of teeth? \r\n<br><br>\r\nthen he asks, \\\'who made me for this? me?\\\' the words and grammer read strictly say \\\'who created me for this purpose? myself?\\\' how can you create  yourself? he may be asking, in essence, \\\'who made me <i>suited</i> for this,\\\' or something like that which might make sense, but who knows?\r\n<br><br>\r\nthen the third paragraph makes sense, but it doesn\\\'t relate to any of the rest of it. also the definition of murder is basically \\\'an unlawful killing--\\\' if it\\\'s murder, its by definition illegal. i know that\\\'s being a little overcritical, but malapropism is deadly. <br><br>\r\nthen \\\'the best part of the job is the worst--\\\' ok, that sounds like it means something, but it really doesn\\\'t. if he had said \\\'sometimes the worst,\\\' that would make sense, but he didn\\\'t, so i don\\\'t know what he meant. also, if something is bad, and then something else \\\'makes up for it,\\\' that means that the something else is therefore \\\'better\\\' because it makes the first thing less bad. <br><br>\r\nalso--whose anxiety? the shoplifters? i would think that a store detective would be the gateway to anxiety for a criminal. \\\'constructive.\\\'',3,1685,0,0,'66.245.116.94','en'),(20991,3589,'2004-05-08 03:52:38',177,'pony','jeezue tyler, go down to your nearest drug store and buy some imagination.',4,74,75,0,'67.174.231.196','en'),(20992,3589,'2004-05-08 19:55:33',534,'griece','dont be the grumpy old man, tyler. dont dissect everything like its yer fuckin math homework, either. its just reading, writing, enjoying. not an analytical analysis fest. sorry if i sound mean, but you repeatedly follow this exact same pattern of criticism (saying something snarky, then coming back in another comment to give tit for tat explaination about why you think the piece sucks) and more often than not, it makes you look like a bit of an ass. we love you, but for real- relax a bit. its only swinney.org',5,515,75,0,'66.108.171.164','en'),(20993,3590,'2004-05-10 03:49:03',175,'Paul G','when i was a little boy, i crossed that bridge while holding my mother\\\'s hand.  a great gust of wind came and took my hat - a little white sailor\\\'s cap - off my head, and into the river below.  i cried.  but, it seemed somewhat fitting that the hat had gone to sea.',1,271,0,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(20994,3573,'2004-05-10 04:29:35',175,'Paul G','a week or so after i read this, i began to have a recurring dream: i was in a mexican western wear store, trying to buy boots.  but i needed to find a dark pair with long, pointy tips, and they weren\\\'t available.  i consoled myself with the thought that i would get them when i visited my family in austin, texas.',1,316,82,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(20995,3587,'2004-05-10 12:44:36',538,'arcy','I love the \\\"road-trippy\\\" feeling. I\\\'ll often get it when the day couldn\\\'t be nicer and I long for times spent with friends and past loves. From my 22nd storey bedroom, there is nothing I want more today than to be behind the wheel of a car crammed with camping paraphernalia and people that expect nothing from me but my sure footed speed and my steady steering. A car full of anticipated happiness and torn maps, thats what I want today. ',2,451,0,0,'69.195.29.221','en'),(20996,3595,'2004-05-11 18:14:08',17,'austin','Excellence.  I enjoy the descriptions of neglected home life.  ringed cups, etc.  and then the scuffs.  Isn\\\'t it fucked up how there is always another person though?  I wish humans mated for life and when it didn\\\'t work out, our need to mate disappeared.  Or like a crazed lemming, we ran off a cliff in droves of spurned, jilted, or \\\'psychotic\\\' lovers.',2,365,0,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(20997,2879,'2004-05-12 02:00:43',17,'austin','I have since become an Ikea ho.',1,31,0,0,'68.6.213.49','en'),(20998,3595,'2004-05-12 04:45:52',57,'weeb','we\\\'d all be at the bottom of the sea.',0,40,0,0,'66.92.219.22','en'),(20999,3595,'2004-05-12 19:54:00',66,'Peter','<br><tt>things will go better, down where its wetter, take it from me...</a>',1,76,57,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(21000,3573,'2004-05-13 00:39:16',82,'simon','WEIRD!',1,6,175,0,'4.237.244.65','en'),(21001,3539,'2004-05-13 19:19:50',17,'austin','Holy fuck this is good.  I liked it.  The only reason I am seeing it now is because it is responsible for allot of google search referrals with regards to hungarians and gypsies and whores.',1,189,0,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(21002,3603,'2004-05-14 17:02:02',17,'austin','Post the script!',1,16,0,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(21003,3603,'2004-05-14 17:38:37',552,'isobel','To imply hope would be to end without a final conclusion. To imply sentiment and perhaps pitty is to end things with death. I personally find the idea of spending time invested in a character only to see them die is rather frustrating. Too many loose ends that I have enough of. I guess it depends on what you are wanting people to experience. Frustration can be good, if it invokes a true feeling, but I like the imagination of my favorite characters walking the same streets I do.',2,482,0,0,'142.204.74.97','en'),(21004,3604,'2004-05-18 16:02:55',66,'Peter','<br><tt>bodies of water are indeed powerful; just reading this left some vivid images in my mind of what i imagine those lakes to be like- so vivid, in fact, that im surprised ive never actually seen most of those bodies of water. <br><Br>i also liked the contrast between sand/warmth/sun and ice/pollution/cities.</tt>',1,319,0,0,'156.145.58.11','en'),(21005,3589,'2004-05-19 02:03:18',75,'tyler','i don\\\'t think you should have to completely rely on your imagination to find meaning in something. also, the whole body of my criticism is basically me using my imagination to try and pry a meaning out of it, isn\\\'t it? and being frustrated? and trying to say why? how is that unimaginative?',1,296,0,0,'66.245.66.204','en'),(21006,3589,'2004-05-19 02:08:04',75,'tyler','also, greice, who are you to say what this \\\'is\\\' or \\\'isn\\\'t?\\\' if i am part of swinney.org, and i write ten page criticisms all the time, then part of swinney.org is ten page criticisms.   ',1,201,0,0,'66.245.66.204','en'),(21007,3589,'2004-05-19 04:07:20',534,'griece','yer a sourpuss, thats what you are. 99 out of 100 times, you offer naysaying opinions with no additional insights or constructive criticism. no one appreciates that. no offense, but for real. ever heard of tact?',5,211,75,0,'66.108.171.164','en'),(21008,3605,'2004-05-20 01:22:36',469,'patlevy','talk to xep and ask her about her sisters too.',1,46,0,0,'198.88.154.204','en'),(21009,3606,'2004-05-20 18:38:11',228,'god','pants, if you die, i\\\'ll fucking kill you. ',0,45,0,0,'81.178.120.191','en'),(21010,3589,'2004-05-20 19:07:45',228,'belly','it didn\\\'t make much sense to me either, for the same reasons tyler mentioned. \r\n\r\nto me, s.o is a place for story-telling and being told stories. it\\\'s interactive, so you have to expect criticism as well as agreement and conservations bouncing off what you\\\'ve written. if i ever get around to posting more writing here, bring on the criticism! how are you gonna get better otherwise? if you want unconditional praise, email your mum.',3,442,0,0,'81.178.120.191','en'),(21011,3606,'2004-05-20 21:21:41',492,'bmg','that is fucking crazy. ',0,23,0,0,'147.80.216.122','en'),(21012,3606,'2004-05-20 21:26:08',17,'austin','A day in the life, Yo.  A day in the life.',1,42,492,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(21013,3589,'2004-05-20 22:26:40',75,'tyler','i think if you click on my name and read my comments, you\\\'ll find that you are mistaken. its true that i have done that, but not true that it represents the majority of my contributions. for a prime example of me explaining myself, and apologizing for being short, see above. as for the \\\'butters\\\' comment on pepon\\\'s article, i remain gleefully unrepentant. i even said something nice once about something you wrote, which puts me in the minority. ',1,459,534,0,'66.245.66.204','en'),(21014,3589,'2004-05-21 04:28:04',533,'incitor','hell, i forgot i posted this.  \r\n\r\ntyler:  criticisms accepted with a smile.  frankly i can\\\'t remember the state i was in when i wrote it, but i was probably quite drunk.  ',2,175,0,0,'69.107.82.34','en'),(21015,3589,'2004-05-21 04:33:27',533,'incitor','<p>oh... the whole anxiety thing.  </p>\r\n<p>\r\n(and this might be somewhat relevant to some stuff that many of you may be thinking/reading/hearing about these days)</p?\r\n<p>\r\ni\\\'m the way out of the anxiety... this is because the art of interrogation is (when applied by reasonably moral people) the art of helping someone reduce their level of anxiety by telling you what you wish to know.  </p>\r\n<p>\r\ni\\\'ve been trained, and continue to study, the techniques that can be used to get someone to believe that the only way out is to tell me what I want to know.  it\\\'s a really strange thing, and utterly fascinating to me.  \r\n</p>',2,635,0,0,'69.107.82.34','en'),(21016,3589,'2004-05-21 04:42:49',533,'incitor','hehe.  my mom has mostly criticism for me.  \\\"why aren\\\'t you married?\\\"  \\\"why are you a virgin?\\\"  \\\"why do you insist on living in San Francisco?\\\"  \\\"why can\\\'t you write anything nice?\\\"  \\\"Why aren\\\'t you mormon yet?\\\"  and so on.  \r\n',2,266,228,0,'69.107.82.34','en'),(21017,3589,'2004-05-21 04:52:18',533,'incitor','i must be bored.  i shouldn\\\'t spam swinney, but...\r\n\r\ni think you should give me a little leeway for being artistic.  even when i\\\'m drunk, even when i\\\'m writing about nasty stuff.  (does anyone really care to hear about how I eat tempeh and edamame three meals a day, week after week?  I thought not.)  \r\n\r\ni admit to being sort of creepy.  mea culpa.  i\\\'m really fucking ugly, but hey, blame my parents and/or my socio-economic index.  i\\\'m kinda grumpy, it\\\'s true.  however, i write alot, and enjoy experimenting with grammar here and there.  i also write poetry, some of which is even almost but not quite decent.  i sit and read Owens and Seeger, Byron and Thomas, Keats and Yeats; sometimes all during a single cup of coffee soy milk.\r\n\r\ni suspect that those who \\\"like\\\" me, are those who noticed i\\\'m not quite a one dimensional character.  afterall, i\\\'m a real live boy.  full of terrible flaws and great beauty.  i do not expect most people to have a positive impression of me, but i would ask those who have more time than a two minute face to face offers, to at least consider my words and my existence.  \r\n\r\nbecause i do the same for those around me.  ',2,1190,75,0,'69.107.82.34','en'),(21018,3589,'2004-05-21 04:53:56',533,'incitor','oops.  my mother wants me to be a virgin.  therefore:  \\\"why AREN\\\'T you a virgin?\\\" though I admit that after these past 4.5 years, i rather feel like one.',3,162,0,0,'69.107.82.34','en'),(21019,3611,'2004-05-21 14:44:06',508,'edward','ground floor and basements trouble me too, there\\\'s something about living a bit higher up.. it feels somewhat safer. it could be a biological inheritance, since a lot of animals act the same way, like the lions on the savanna or the chimpanzees in the djungle..',3,264,0,0,'193.10.214.97','en'),(21020,1670,'2004-05-21 15:38:42',555,'Dan623','I would give anything to blow raspberries on Beyonce\\\'s belly',0,63,0,0,'205.188.117.21','en'),(21021,3589,'2004-05-21 22:25:34',75,'tyler','i didn\\\'t have a problem with your tone, darkness, nastiness or what have you--what you wrote, gramatically, just didn\\\'t mean anything, objectively. that isn\\\'t to say, though, that that approach is invalid from a postmodernist standpoint, i.e., you may feel that what you\\\'re writing about is pointless and frustrating, and so the piece itself is pointless and frustrating, while at the same time you provide enough colorful words to paint some sort of picture. \\\'through a glass, darkly\\\' and all that. to be honest, when i was an english undergrad i totally missed the point of a lot of postmodernist works, only to have the meaning occur to me weeks or months later, and then feel like an ass for having been a dick about it in class. sort of what happened here. appreciating art is a process, no doubt. so yeah. i think this piece does have merit, it just took me a while to get there. also, man, if you\\\'ve got something to say, please don\\\'t let fear of spamming stop you. i appreciate your followup and will gladly read more.',1,1050,0,0,'66.245.0.215','en'),(21022,3611,'2004-05-22 06:00:02',17,'austin','i\\\'ve never considered living in a basement.  it is too hobitlike for me.  i\\\'ve considered that my friends who did live in basements had a sort of personality for it.  we are talking about the kind where you have a sort of partial window opening up into a recessed area that is meant to off you some resemblance of a window even though the only view is up.\r\n\r\nhi arcy, i\\\'m austin.\r\n\r\nhi edward.',1,402,0,0,'68.6.213.49','en'),(21023,3589,'2004-05-22 07:15:26',533,'incitor','<p>I can\\\'t really claim that anything I write or do has merit.  I just write when compelled to, and whatever comes out... I trust it won\\\'t be too horrible for my eyes.  </p>\r\n<p> I\\\'ve never given any thought at all to where my writing lies in any spectrum.  To me it just is.  Almost nothing I\\\'ve written here at swinney has been...uhhh...my art.  It was just me sort of writing an edited down train of thought.  However, the vast majority of what I write is born of my artistic urge.  Something that, frankly, I refused to admit I had in me for most of my life. </p>\r\n<p>I think part of the reason you found a problem with this piece was that if indeed i ever truly write for an audience, it\\\'s for those who read my journal, most of whom know me.  It could also be that I very carefully don\\\'t care about what people on swinney think about me.  Part of the charm swinney exerts on me is that I feel like even more of an outsider here than I do in the real world.  Yet as far as i\\\'ve seen, the average entity here on swinney is more intelligent and/or reasonable and/or etc etc than the average entity in the real world.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSo i can be an outsider, and yet still receive a greater benefit than out there.  I get comments, have people dislike and like me, see what they think about, what they do.  All without once wondering if I\\\'m going to have to defend myself against them.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>I drifted.  sorry.  I feel that the piece made sense mostly, even without external contextual data.  That is because it was mostly emotional in nature.  I was not explaining anything, i was not showing you anything (other than the state of mind I was in), and I wasn\\\'t writing so you could read it.  I write because if i don\\\'t, i have to masturbate three times more often.  I can\\\'t afford the massive loss of time that would require.  Yes, I\\\'m being a little glib here.  However, if i don\\\'t write, I will progressively begin to devolve into a version of myself that I don\\\'t like, am not proud of, and that I find morally unacceptable.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI think it helps to know me.  Or just try to stick to my fiction or poetry.  (Which isn\\\'t an option here I suppose.) When I write about my life, what I did today or yesterday, it\\\'s going to be fragmented sometimes.  It\\\'s going to rely upon visualizations that have no meaning to anyone but me.  Partucularly when I\\\'m upset.  Particularly when I\\\'m drunk.  I know how horribly that violates the contract with the reader.  But then, I\\\'m not really a nice guy to those I don\\\'t love.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThanks, however, for your thoughts and the time you took to put them down.  I adore criticism of my writing.  Even the worst of it, even the dullest.  I can learn more about my art, my craft, at any time, from anyone.  I don\\\'t have to be trying to learn, I just have to keep my bloody eyes open and my mind in gear.\r\n</p> ',2,2914,75,0,'69.107.82.34','en'),(21024,2489,'2004-05-23 00:50:43',17,'austin','<a href=\\\"http://akamaipix.crutchfield.com/products/2004/257/h257HXGX7-f-1.jpeg \\\"><img width=30 src=\\\"http://akamaipix.crutchfield.com/products/2004/257/h257HXGX7-f-1.jpeg\\\"></A>',3,87,0,0,'68.6.213.49','en'),(21025,3589,'2004-05-23 09:36:25',17,'austin','bring on the writing, you poser!  I\\\'M CALLING YOU OUT.  New Zealanders are ALL TALK.',1,87,228,0,'68.6.213.49','en'),(21026,3611,'2004-05-24 02:27:41',175,'Paul G','<br>\r\n(1) chicago was/is a swamp.\r\n<br>\r\n(2) many of the older buildings in town had/have dirt floors.\r\n<br>\r\ni\\\'ve had friends who didn\\\'t make the connection between (1) & (2) until a good rain: black ooze began to rise up through every crack in their sub-grade dwelling.  tile~plywood~dirt.  hmmm.\r\n<p>\r\ndoing construction work in dirt-floored units always made me nervous: my co-workers were large, bearded men; and 15 years ago i looked like Tender Vittles.  the likeness of the basement apartment to the grave was not lost on me.  it was not an unreasonable fear.\r\n</p>  ',1,581,0,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(21027,3611,'2004-05-24 14:23:05',538,'arcy','Well in fact there are two windows in this apartment, both not much bigger than my computer screen. If I ever had a fire and the door was block, I would have just enough room to pull myself up with the bars and scream to the underside of an old set of stairs. Fire extinguisher a definite accessory.',1,299,17,0,'69.195.29.221','en'),(21028,3611,'2004-05-24 14:29:36',538,'arcy','I was once in the basement of an old house that my hairdresser work out of. When she had to go for a smoke I would follow her down into this very creepy, stone walled, dirt floored coal storage room. The thought crossed my mind that if something were to happen, nothing more than a shovel and a little effort and no one would ever know what happened to me. Chilling. I got the feeling like I was a small child in an Edward Gorey tale.',1,434,175,0,'69.195.29.221','en'),(21029,3612,'2004-05-24 15:07:27',66,'Peter','<br><tt>small world... i just got <a href=\\\"http://www.cjadvertising.com/samples/print/yp.jpg\\\">this</a> in the daily spam. if you make an ad too, be sure to show us!</tt>',1,175,0,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(21030,2489,'2004-05-24 19:00:07',195,'matt','<B>bring on the dancing horses</B>  smoking pot for the first time.  sixteen.  with a girl named susan on the dock at bayport.',1,126,0,0,'207.239.93.170','en'),(21031,3608,'2004-05-24 19:52:51',57,'weeb','that is my first place most favorite word in the world.\r\n<br>\r\nBI-otch. biiOTCH. BEAACH!@!\r\n<br>\r\noh dear.',0,106,0,0,'66.92.219.22','en'),(21032,3612,'2004-05-25 00:06:07',75,'tyler','we\\\'re on the inside cover of the phonebook. i have collected some catchphrases too.<br><br>\r\n\\\'one call, that\\\'s all.\\\'<br><br>\r\n\\\'turn your disaster into dollars.\\\'<br><br>\r\n\\\'turn that catastrophe into cold cash.\\\'\r\n<br><br>\r\n\\\'the state bar prohibits me from promising you a huge cash settlement. but read my lips. i promise <i>you</i>-- a huge cash settlement.\\\'',1,387,0,0,'66.32.73.52','en'),(21033,3612,'2004-05-25 01:34:55',75,'tyler','one man\\\'s dream is just another man\\\'s annoying spam.',1,58,0,0,'66.32.73.52','en'),(21034,3614,'2004-10-05 03:45:54',17,'austin','I feel gyped.<BR><BR>\r\nAt least the google ads are funny.',5,57,0,666,'63.80.97.254','en'),(21035,3614,'2004-10-05 03:45:54',234,'etienne','i wish this post had talked about google ads.  aren\\\'t these types of crises what livejournal is for? ;)',4,106,0,666,'219.88.223.161','en'),(21036,3611,'2004-05-25 04:06:12',234,'etienne','i\\\'ve lived in dark places and that was bad enough, especially if they were damp.  basement apartments don\\\'t have windows - they have slits.',1,145,0,0,'219.88.223.161','en'),(21037,3611,'2004-05-25 16:21:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt><img src=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2054_1.pjpeg\\\"><br><br>we lived in the basement for a long time. this was our \\\"window\\\". it was not fun living here, in the long run. i wrote an innitial take on the place <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1912\\\">here</a>...</tt>',1,321,0,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(21038,2158,'2004-05-25 16:29:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt>its been four years since that summer in brooklyn, indeed. forgotten much of what i so arduously wrote about above, changed my life alot, married a wonderful woman...<br><br>\r\nand this weekend, we move <b>back to brooklyn</b>...<br><br>\r\nim thinking this brooklyn theme is one i\'ll be continuing, or at least picking up with once again...</tt>',1,343,66,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(21039,3614,'2004-05-25 16:56:58',66,'Peter','<br><tt>come on, we <i>all</i> know courtney had kurdt killed.</tt>',1,67,0,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(21040,3617,'2004-05-25 20:20:01',66,'Peter','<br><tt>respiratory cancer high value class-action lawsuit! </tt>',1,52,0,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(21041,3618,'2004-05-26 14:20:56',66,'Peter','<br><tt>free Mesothelioma Lawyer Referral Service. We put you in contact with experienced Mesothelioma lawyers who have successfully tried cases in your state. Our goal is to help legal consumers get in touch legal professionals and to keep the public aware of new discoveries in treatments for Mesothelioma. We maintain a current Mesothelioma article library and we keep you up to date on current legal settlements for asbestos cases. Those dealing with asbestos face a number of challenges, including the tremendous costs of treatment. If you were unknowingly exposed to harmful levels of Mesothelioma, you have legal rights. YOU MAY BE ENTITLED TO MONETARY COMPENSATION. If you are in need of legal advice or services, or simply wish to speak to an attorney who has successfully handled asbestos cases in your state, you may use this Free Online Consultation Form Mesothelioma is a serious cancer that occurs in individuals exposed to asbestos. Mesothelioma victims have either inhaled or swallowed asbestos fibers which then travel through the lung and become lodged in the pleura (the thin, saran wrap-type membrane lining the lungs.) Each year 2,500 to 4,000 patients in the U.S. are diagnosed with mesothelioma and asbestos-related diseases. There is NO COST or obligation for this service.</tt>',1,1302,0,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(21042,3614,'2004-05-26 20:37:40',75,'tyler','if i could, i\\\'d give you your two minutes back. ',1,51,0,0,'68.222.239.6','en'),(21043,3619,'2004-05-27 03:44:06',17,'austin','This is easy to visualize.\r\n',3,28,0,0,'68.6.213.49','en'),(21044,3614,'2004-10-05 03:45:54',66,'Peter','<br><tt>so do the comments effect the googleadz too? like, if i say ghettoblaster ghettoblaster ghettoblaster ghettoblaster ghettoblaster ghettoblaster ghettoblaster, will it make ghettoblasters pop up in the ads?</tt>',1,218,17,666,'68.175.71.101','en'),(21045,3614,'2004-05-28 16:52:12',176,'Scapegoat','Shit I hope the lawyer thing ends up being worth it cuz I just paid my tuition deposit on my first year.<br><br>\r\nAlways figured life was kinda neutral - good and bad are relative concepts.<br><br>\r\nGhettoblaster!',0,213,0,0,'159.33.2.41','en'),(21046,3605,'2004-05-28 16:57:26',176,'Scapegoat','Second acts?<br><br>\r\nWhat other potential life paths are you thinking about? Other possible career paths perhaps... they don\\\'t strike me as the same thing life and career. Don\\\'t lose track of that, you can do things outside of work to realize other potential. Is there something specific that you are mourning for? ',0,322,0,0,'159.33.2.41','en'),(21047,3614,'2004-05-29 02:30:46',75,'tyler','also, etienne--i\\\'m pretty sure that invoking livejournal in conjunction with someone\\\'s swinney article is about the most stinging thing one can say. i hope i haven\\\'t offended you that way in the past. ',1,210,0,0,'66.245.94.70','en'),(21048,3614,'2004-05-29 08:23:34',508,'edward','yes tyler, i guess you should. what would life be if all days where on average better than 0 - you would not know how to appreciate the positive days, and how to handle the negative ones.\r\n\r\ni liked this one, a lot, and i somewhat found it ironic too. but i guess that people like hemmingway and kurt just drop out in the negative zone. and it is like the opposite of the people living 24/7 in the positive zone.\r\n\r\nsomehow, i think i rather kill myself than turn into one of them acting always happy people.\r\n\r\nrock on.',2,520,75,0,'212.181.180.15','en'),(21049,3174,'2004-05-29 20:49:19',562,'alissagrim','All comments thus far about Narcisse Dille, from what I can recall, were accurate. What I experienced was from a completely different perspective during the years 1993-1995. I suppose I was one of those \\\"nordic blondes,\\\" Although I am not tall or blonde by any means... maybe nordic, and without goth pedigree, but who cares. As strange as my relationship and experiences with Narcisse were, I cannot make light or joke of him. I must say he was the most gentle and well intentioned person I\\\'ve ever met, who\\\'s consistency in this could be an example for us all... regardless of how others may feel about his persona. For this I still admire him. Even my mom, who is normally a bit judgemental, took to liking him. She often recalls offering him a Calistoga Sparkling Juice (lemon,) not sure it would fit in his diet of white food and drink. He read aloud the ingredients to be sure they were acceptable, and exclaimed like a child  \\\"...clouding agent, oh how beautiful!\\\" He definately made an impression on people, usually positive, but sometimes on some people, the reaction was quite adverse (mostly in men, it seemed.) Anyway, I\\\'m a mom now and a 1st grade teacher. Possibly my maternal instincts have taken over, but I do worry about him from time to time. To me he\\\'s not \\\"Narcisse mystified,\\\" he was a very good and close friend who seemed to regularly find himself in trouble. Anyone seen or heared of him lately?',3,1450,0,0,'205.188.117.21','en'),(21050,3613,'2004-05-30 06:32:11',546,'pepon','My goodness, you\\\'ve been busy writing these two days!  And such different styles.  Whatever your muse is, I believe you\\\'ve found it, dammit.',1,146,0,0,'132.239.127.169','en'),(21051,3623,'2004-05-30 06:49:01',546,'pepon','I hate mixing competition with art.  Maybe because my drive to win is in direct conflict with my yearning to express myself.  But I wish you all luck, and lots of cash prizes. ',4,176,0,0,'132.239.127.169','en'),(21052,3623,'2004-05-30 06:56:17',17,'austin','Next!',3,5,546,0,'68.6.213.49','en'),(21053,3602,'2004-05-30 07:01:41',546,'pepon','It\\\'s interesting that you title this \\\"sigh,\\\" because when I sigh, it\\\'s a way to let out emotion - all sorts of emotion, instead of harboring it inside me, where it festers and turns to bitterness and depression.  It sounds like long, plentiful sighs are on your horizon.',2,282,0,0,'132.239.127.169','en'),(21054,3623,'2004-05-30 11:17:44',508,'edward','what are swinney.org/you going to use the art for? selling postcards? or just for give-away things advertizing the site? i don\\\'t get it.',3,139,17,0,'212.181.180.132','en'),(21055,3623,'2004-05-30 16:28:40',82,'simon','oh i am so in. look out, swinney.',0,33,0,0,'4.236.30.244','en'),(21056,3623,'2004-05-30 16:28:55',17,'austin','<P>These items are for the giving away, leaving in bars, book stores, etc.  Not for sale.</P>',2,93,508,0,'68.6.213.49','en'),(21057,3620,'2004-05-30 16:41:43',82,'simon','i read this a couple times and had a feeling of driving to napa a million times with a variety of family and friends. whether it was a booze-y early morning trip in rumpled formal wear after staying up all night, or a pleasant drive with my parents to drink wine and make fun of tourists, the feeling is sort of the same. i\\\'m not sure what it is.',1,349,0,0,'4.236.30.244','en'),(21058,3623,'2004-05-30 23:18:09',66,'Peter','<br>quit while you are ahead everyone. sine and i are gonna kick your asses in the art contest!</tt>',0,100,0,0,'162.83.210.121','en'),(21059,3623,'2004-05-30 23:23:07',17,'austin','rock on!',1,8,0,0,'68.6.213.49','en'),(21060,3626,'2004-06-01 18:08:10',508,'edward','lucky you found it worthwhile. welcome to swinney. i love them travel-stories.',1,78,0,0,'195.252.51.53','en'),(21061,3622,'2004-06-02 02:57:37',538,'arcy','Thats a difficult question. I have forgiven what to my knowledge was only a kiss, but it seems to hurt more the tranfer of excitment and attention.',2,147,0,0,'69.195.29.221','en'),(21062,3623,'2004-10-05 03:45:54',175,'Paul G','google\\\'s <a href=\\\"https://www.google.com/adsense/faq#start1\\\">AdSense</a> makes money for someone via a site\\\'s content.\r\n<p>\r\nthis site\\\'s content was built slowly, over the course of several years, through the collective - and not-for-profit - contributions of many people.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhow much revenue is being generated?\r\n<br>\r\nwhat is that money being used for?\r\n<br>\r\nwhat\\\'s next?\r\n<br>\r\nyou changed the title of one of my stories, so that google liked it better?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ntransparency, please.  i took the ads for a joke, at first.  but, hey, what\\\'s up?  \r\n</p>',4,589,0,666,'206.68.117.82','en'),(21063,3623,'2004-10-05 03:45:54',17,'austin','The ads started as a curiosity and have grown into a project.  Rest assured, there is no $200 of google\\\'s money at this time.  I\\\'m not pulling in hundreds of dollars as a result of these ads.  I do get some amount of money from about two out of three clicks.  Usually somewhere between 2 an 5 cents.  sometimes several dollars.  I dont know how this works yet, or what ads garner more money than others.<BR><BR>Currently googe\\\'s spidering amounts for 2/3 the traffic on the website.  the top 15 visitors are all google bots 1-15.  There is some Yahoo and MSN in there.  The largest referrals come from google\\\'s sites in the US and abroad.  <BR><BR>I saw an opportunity to make google pay for the traffic that it causes even if it is only a small amount of money for a large amount of traffic.  That money I plan to put into things having to do with this website that would otherwise not happen, such as promotional items to bring in some new people, and contributing for the hosting costs that heretofore I have paid out of my own pocket as donations to saturn5.com. <BR><BR>Which brings me to another item: moving from saturn5.com.  Eventually I would like to move the site from the current webserver because it is no longer supporting PHP\\\'s memory requirements to my liking.  Moving the site anywhere is going to cost more money than it does now, but I\\\'m fine with that so long as it is not money out of my pocket.<BR><BR>How is that?',1,1454,175,666,'63.80.97.254','en'),(21064,3623,'2004-10-05 03:45:54',66,'Peter','<br><tt>at first, i wondered about the ads too. then, i was talking to austin about it, and it sort of made sense. sort of as a curiosity, a joke as well as the very detailed reasons he mentioned above. i dunno; at first, i was sorta like <i>\\\"google ads? wtf?\\\"</i> then i realized it wasnt like austin was out to get a profit off of the site or something, i mean, ha, for real. so its no reason for offense, and i say that as a person who has added much content here, and sort of object to prying googlebots in general.<br><br>\r\nbut since i stand behind the opinion that php-driven sites deserve as much memory as they need (citynoise.org is currently fucked because of some related php issues), i agree with austin.<br><br>i hope the google ads dont stay forever, but im willing to be chill about austin fuckin around with em for a while, you know, especially if it makes the site self-sufficient. thats success.</tt>',1,924,175,666,'162.83.210.121','en'),(21065,3623,'2004-06-03 02:48:13',66,'Peter','<br><tt>and the art contest; thats a cool idea. it beats <a href=\\\"http://saturn5.com/~peter/rhodamine/images/swinney.jpg\\\">bathroom graffiti</a></tt>',1,154,17,0,'162.83.210.121','en'),(21066,3623,'2004-10-05 03:45:54',17,'austin','I feel like if this was an email thread, it would say, \\\"SUBJECT: Google Ads For Capitalist Pigdogs (Was: Art Contest)\\\".<BR><BR>\r\nAnother word about the ads...  I will probably/at some point make them only show up if you are coming in from a google search.  For example, if you google into swinney, you see an announcement at the top of the page that says, \\\"HI THERE GOOGLER, PLEASE CLICK ON A GOOGLE AD AND SUPPORT OUR SITE.\\\"<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nBut they are also serving as an exercise to see how I can get money out of google in general.  The fact that google is willing to give me money at all has me really psyched.  Sure it ain\\\'t shit compared to slangin\\\' rock back on the block, but it might pay for the hosting of the site someplace professional, and a handful of my pay-per-month web services.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nFurthermore, I\\\'m bored with doing anything for this website.  The fact that it is doing something for me now gets me excited enough to throw this art contest and try and build up the user base enough to warrant a redesign (which I would pay for with google money).  The days of people doing things for free are over.  Which is to say the days of people being unemployed and having too much time on their hands to give to projects like this one, are over.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nSee, I have not written this much on this website in years!',1,1350,175,666,'68.6.213.49','en'),(21067,3623,'2004-10-05 03:45:54',175,'Paul G','And if my comment was an e-mail thread, it would be entitled:\r\n<br> \r\n<b>\\\"SUBJECT: Angry Communist Asshole Throws Mud - and Calls it Chocolate.\\\"</b>\r\n<p>\r\nBut, per Peter, <i>\\\"i dunno; at first, i was sorta like \\\'google ads? wtf?\\\'\\\"</i>\r\nIt was a shock.  I understand that nothing is free, in this world.\r\nHosting, admin, etc, need to be accounted for.\r\nI\\\'ve been really grateful for the time, space, and energy that this site has provided.\r\nI\\\'ve tried to say, \\\"thank you,\\\" when appropriate, and give back in the way that I am best able.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNow, the car is at a fork in the road; your hand is on the wheel.\r\nI will hang on, jump, or be thrown out. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe sudden appearance of ads, in conjunction with a promotional contest, was bound to raise questions.\r\nI asked the questions that I had: in my own name, directly, in a way that allowed for a similar response.\r\nPeople haven\\\'t tended to show me the same courtesy, over the years.\r\nThank you for answering.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI don\\\'t want to be a drone - or to be surrounded by drones.\r\nIf I want to give honest affection, I need to speak out at less pleasant moments as well - or I will be nothing but a sycophant.\r\nOk?  \r\nI admire the work that a lot of people have put into this thing - because it is something that I am not able to do.\r\nBut, yeah, I do write for free, even though I don\\\'t have time, because I\\\'m trying to make something more from life than money.\r\nJust think about that stuff when you turn the wheel.\r\nPlease.\r\n<br>\r\nPaul.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\np.s. This was the best quality that I observed about Olivia: She would simply write, \\\"You are dicks.\\\"\r\n</p>',1,1670,17,666,'206.68.117.82','en'),(21068,3623,'2004-06-04 01:55:17',17,'austin','Often I found myself saying, \\\"Olivia, you bitch!\\\"',1,55,175,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(21069,3623,'2004-06-04 14:06:35',534,'griece','i was always fond of <i>\\\"can it, ho\\\"</i>',5,46,17,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(21070,3623,'2004-06-05 21:42:01',175,'Paul G','<a href=\\\"http://www.performit.co.uk/network/contact.htm\\\">pigdog</a>...i\\\'m shopping for a taxi, so that i can become capital.  things change - sometimes for the better, even.',1,182,0,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(21071,3631,'2004-06-07 01:35:11',17,'austin','YOU HAD SEX WITH PARIS HILTON????!',1,34,0,0,'68.6.213.49','en'),(21072,3627,'2004-06-07 05:08:14',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\nI flew into Vancouver just over five days ago. I flew into Toronto just earlier today. I am back at home. It is an strange feeling. really strange.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI do the same things I did when I was here last. Almost exact. I get home. I say hello. We all chit chat for about twenty minutes, and then it just becomes like it used to be. It is something my mom and grandmother both love, because it makes them feel safe. It brings back those days of \\\'just having me around\\\'. I was bored. How did I grow up in such a sleepy suburb of Toronto? What serious effects did that kind of life have on me? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTo be fair. Growing up in Korea must suck large size. The ammount of time I had away from regulated events was far more than any korean child growing up. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBut as I am now older, and out of the grips of regulated education, I can be at peace in other nations. Korea is that nation right now, and I miss my life back there already. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt is great to be back in that I can butt heads with my cat, and chit chat with my mom about random bullshit, and that I can hang out with my nephew and be the cool uncle. It is nice that I can do that right now, but I miss my life in Asia too much.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI do not think I am being selfish. I find it terribly hard to live in North America. I do not communicate with people well here. I have so much more self esteem living in Korea. I feel better about myself on the whole, and dont feel as bothered by the things I see on the street. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI will wait until I return to Korea to estimate on when I will return again, but so far I am just feeling ok about it. \r\n</p>',1,1654,0,0,'65.50.46.171','en'),(21073,3630,'2004-06-07 09:30:33',508,'edward','I do not know if I totally get your thoughts correctly. But don\\\'t let a downward trend make you think that love is gone, or was never there..',3,144,0,0,'213.67.51.28','en'),(21074,3627,'2004-06-07 16:58:43',546,'pepon','Hey chiamatt,\r\n\r\nthis is cool.  I feel a lot like this when I go home, too.  My mom always nags for me to come home.  Come home!  we miss you so much!  We feel so bad that we weren\\\'t here for you when you were a kid, that we want to try to make up for it now that you\\\'re a 20-something young adult making her way in the big world!  So I come home, and then nothing happens.  She goes to the grocery store, and gets excited about the apriocot fruit bars, and comes home and reads a book and does some gardening and complains about something.  We both want to talk, I think, but there\\\'s 20-something years of no communication behind us.  Like you can\\\'t get back to mending those childhood mistakes when you\\\'re older.  Something is changed.  The innocence is lost.  The trust wasn\\\'t built.\r\n\r\nI wonder what you found in Korea.  I wonder why that draws you.  Because I can relate to your sentiments about Toronto - maybe an answer lies for me in Korea or something similar, too? ',2,993,0,0,'132.239.127.169','en'),(21075,3623,'2004-10-05 03:45:54',234,'etienne','i say \\\"go google ads\\\" - we all use this site and if it went down would all cry into our hankies.  it\\\'s not free to have a site anymore, and austin shouldn\\\'t have to foot the bill.  yeah, maybe it takes something from the site to have them but i\\\'m surprised that anyone would think that austie was trying to just profit?  surely you all know its been around for too long etc etc.  sometimes you jsut gotta do what you gotta do.  it could be a lot worse.',1,467,0,666,'219.88.223.161','en'),(21076,3612,'2004-06-13 05:48:05',75,'tyler','also, Peter, that\\\'s pretty mean, man. really.',1,48,0,0,'66.32.133.11','en'),(21077,3632,'2004-06-13 07:17:24',82,'simon','i had a best friend in high school that went through all my shit with me and i loved her more than life itself. and now we aren\\\'t even in contact. sometimes i\\\'m like fuck god where did zoe go, i wish i knew her. but most of the time i just think that we did what we needed for each other at that moment and i wouldnt trade it for the world. how powerfully sappy, i know, but it\\\'s a nice feeling. maybe that\\\'s all we needed.\r\n',1,437,0,0,'162.83.252.42','en'),(21078,3612,'2004-06-13 23:11:42',234,'etienne','tyler...sometimes the truth hurts.  ',2,36,75,0,'219.88.75.170','en'),(21079,3612,'2004-06-14 00:01:08',17,'austin','Ha, Tyler is an ambulance chaser!',1,33,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21080,3612,'2004-06-15 00:45:05',534,'griece','so are ya gonna get a late night tv spot, too?',5,46,75,0,'162.83.210.121','en'),(21081,3623,'2004-10-05 03:45:54',175,'Paul G','it should be possible to like austin and the website - and to still have questions about the ads.\r\ninsofar as i understand things, i do like austin and the website - and i do have questions about the ads.\r\ni don\\\'t know.  what\\\'s the better course of action?\r\nask, under cover of an alias?  whisper the questions behind his back?  harbor suspicion, quietly?  spread rumors?  hmmm.\r\n<p>\r\nto be frank: there was no notice.  and the ads represent a fairly radical change in course - relative to the collective, not-for-profit ethos around which the older versions of the site grew.\r\nsome sort of warning would have been helpful: <i>\\\"hey gang - daddy\\\'s broke!  the well ran dry.  and in order to cover the cost of hosting, i need to...\\\"</i>\r\nbecause, keywords in the writing are what google wants; and everyone who has ever written anything for the site has contributed their time and effort, gratis, for a purpose other than google\\\'s.\r\nis daddy broke?  is the time for doing things for free, over?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyou know, i thought that howard dean was a dick.\r\nnevertheless, i cast my votes for his electors in the democratic primary - because he was the closest thing to a real alternative candidate.\r\nbut then he made a noise like the cookie monster, and subsequently was assassinated in the popular media.\r\nthe gb2 makes odd noises - that are supposed to be speech - every week, and no one calls him on it.\r\n*sniff*\r\nthe situation - as we transition from late republic to early tyranny - here, is really pretty bad.\r\nand we\\\'ll be very lucky if we don\\\'t have a draft immediately following the presidential election.\r\nthis is a period of increasing competition for diminishing natural resources: the capital investments in the petroleum economy are being preferred to life.\r\ni miss the company of the old hippies and young punks that i grew up with.\r\ni don\\\'t see a lot of people, now, standing up, asking questions, and acting according to their convictions.\r\nwhether i am one of them, or not, i admire the \\\"real\\\" communists, vegans, feminists, anti-globalists, neo-luddites, et al, who have the balls to break away from the herd and endure the sanctions of the hierarchy.\r\nat times it seems that this is an exceedingly materialistic generation, bent on pursuing nothing but its own selfish desires.\r\nwhy then, i suppose, be shocked that our elected officials are considering their personal interests more than the good of the whole?\r\nthere\\\'s a real need today for places like swinney.org - places for free expression, art, unusual perspectives, and real human stories.\r\nonce upon a time there was an admonition here, something like: \\\"don\\\'t do nothing.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nagain: the people in the u.s.a. who do have issues with the direction in which the country is headed, now, are called unpatriotic.\r\nthey are said not to be real americans.\r\ncorollary: if i am not in total agreement with everything on swinney.org, am i \\\"not a real swinney?\\\"\r\nshould i fly a little swinney.org flag on my taxi, and repeat, \\\"god bless swinney.org,\\\" ad nauseam?\r\nhaha.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhy should this be like a marriage with someone who\\\'s bipolar? - where everything is either perfect, or in ruins; jesus, or hitler.\r\nmost of the people who follow [and participate in] the site are - at least - in their mid-twenties; and they\\\'ve followed it for several years.\r\nthey\\\'re mature enough to know what\\\'s what.\r\ndialogue!  that\\\'s how relationships work - and last.\r\nsilence, or unilateral action, leads to divorce.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nover the past year i\\\'ve chosen to remove myself from every other forum/journal/site.\r\nyou\\\'re right about the emotional connection to this place.\r\nthe original attraction: colorful personalities, layers of interaction, rough edges, sometimes inspired writing, and that sense of...mystery/chaos/fuckery.\r\nwho knew what the hell you would find, or what the weirdos would be up to?  eh?\r\noh well.\r\n</p>',1,3967,234,666,'206.68.117.82','en'),(21082,3623,'2004-10-05 03:45:54',17,'austin','Paul, would you mind if I put ads on swinney.org?\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nthat bit about flying a god bless swinney.org flag made beer come out my nose!\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ngoogle ads!\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n<B>*destabilizing*</B>\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nnew server tomorrow!',4,232,175,666,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21083,3623,'2004-06-17 07:42:12',17,'austin','WAIT,\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nare you submitting GOD BLESS SWINNEY.ORG flags as your art contest contestant?',1,95,175,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21084,323,'2004-06-17 18:31:17',502,'chris','i am trying to remember...',1,26,0,0,'147.80.216.122','en'),(21085,323,'2004-06-17 18:40:30',66,'Peter','<br><tt>we\\\'re <i>all</i> devo.</tt>',1,38,0,0,'156.145.58.57','en'),(21086,3623,'2004-10-05 03:45:54',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\\\"...most of the people who follow [and participate in] the site are - at least - in their mid-twenties; and they\\\'ve followed it for several years. they\\\'re mature enough to know what\\\'s what. dialogue!\\\"</i><br><br>this is profound. i find that alot of the people here are new and mysterious and far less gregarious and fascinating as the 2001 crowd. im not saying that from my perspective so much as the shared perspective of some of the folks who have been around a while. times change. people flux.<br><br>things slow down. i see less banter and friendly faces here, i write less, i check the site less, things grind to a halt.<br><br>but then i see austin tooling around with some revitalization. the new host will make use/admin of the site alot easier, and ergo browsing it, contributing and getting re-involved will make spending time perusing the site a more productive and rewarding prospect one again. i, and hopefully more old friends, will start writing more. things may develop a new sort of energy. things happen in phases like this. its natural.<br><br>\r\naustin has been super laid-back about letting us all incorporate our own lives and personalities in what is ultimately his own personal site. he\\\'s been logical about the google bizness, honest about the money, and he\\\'s always back there tweaking shit and staying excited about the vast project that this site has become in the past several years.<br><br>i say more power to him and everyone here. im psyched about the new host. im psyched that more visitors are coming and reading my writing. im psyched that old faces are coming through a little more these days. im excited about seeing where this alll goes in the next year. yay swinney.org.</tt>',1,1747,175,666,'156.145.58.57','en'),(21087,3636,'2004-06-18 15:43:11',508,'edward','<p>I\\\'ve reflected about this lately too, in connection with the Hierarchy of Needs, stated by Maslow. But being better or doing things better, isn\\\'t that the same thing?</p><p>I guess we <i>are</i> satisfied with our current level, since it could always be worse, but that it\\\'s somewhat principles like Darwin\\\'s Survival of the Fittest that keep us striving for development, in material things, in love, anything.. money - for what you can buy.</p><p>I like your articles Arcy.</p>',3,493,0,0,'213.67.51.28','en'),(21088,3627,'2004-06-21 03:03:58',75,'tyler','what do you mean by regulated events?\r\n',1,39,93,0,'66.32.153.14','en'),(21089,3627,'2004-06-21 04:05:14',534,'griece','<A href=\\\"http://dictionary.reference.com/search?q=regulated\\\">this</a> might help. i may be reaching here, but id say he\\\'s referring to ethereal things like school and other such \\\'regulated\\\' events.',5,212,75,0,'162.83.210.121','en'),(21090,3637,'2004-06-22 07:04:03',17,'austin','Ha!  That bit about cancer is almost word for word a conversation I had with a friend of mine when he heard I put gooads on this shithole.',1,138,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21091,3637,'2004-06-22 19:48:23',195,'matt','and yet, when i first viewed it, none of the ads on this page mentioned mesothelioma.',1,85,0,0,'209.116.42.45','en'),(21092,3637,'2004-06-22 20:27:19',66,'Peter','<br><Tt>deja vu!<br><Br>in other news, the person who hijacked/\'returned\' <A href=\"http://www.citynoise.org\">citynoise.org</a>  to me has requested the following of me:<br><Br><i>\"If you could do one little thing for me in return for me returning your url, it would be very much appreciated. We publish sites on an informational topic and are just rolling out one on the deadly disease Mesothelioma. Mesothelioma is a disease caused by exposure to asbestos and really very little else; Mesothelioma exposure is usually at the expense of employees of major corporations. If you could give me a link to my mesothelioma site from your homepage once your site is back up and running to help spread the word about mesothelioma a bit and get the mesothelioma site a little exposure, that would be awesome. You can check out the mesothelioma site at mesothelioma.mesothelioma.mesothelioma.mesothelioma.com; it\'s about 80% complete, but the vast bulk of the information about mesothelioma is there. I would be great if you could link up to our mesothelioma site and send all your traffic our way so people can learn about mesothelioma...\"</i><br><Br>\r\nderrr. what a lamer. i will not link him. instead, i shall put his note here in order to bring in the <i>$big bux$</i> for herr swinney.</tt>',1,1263,17,0,'156.145.58.128','en'),(21093,3627,'2004-06-22 23:35:32',75,'tyler','i meant what kind of regulated events. <i>[ed. note:  removed snarky namecalling here]</i>',1,48,0,0,'66.32.120.136','en'),(21094,3623,'2004-06-23 01:55:29',75,'tyler','turning and turning in the widening gyre<br>\r\nthe falcon cannot hear the falconer;',1,82,0,0,'66.32.120.136','en'),(21095,3627,'2004-06-23 14:53:30',75,'tyler','what the fuck. thats not editing, thats censorship. how often do i call someone a name? the point of me calling him a dickhead is to be ironic--he is calling me a dickhead by putting the dictionary link in his comment, because it implies that i don\\\'t know what the word \\\'regulated\\\' means, i.e., i am an illiterate dickhead. so, in contrast to that underhanded \\\'snarkyness,\\\' i respond <i>overtly</i> by just calling him a name, which expresses both my dislike for his shitty comment and his shitty underhanded way of expressing it. if you have a problem with what i say, then say something about it here in the comments list and give me a chance to defend it and to know who it is that doesn\\\'t like it. otherwise what\\\'s the point of all this? your editor\\\'s password doesn\\\'t give you some kind of blanket moral/intellectual superiority.',1,861,0,0,'68.222.239.6','en'),(21096,3627,'2004-06-23 15:37:54',66,'Peter','<br><Tt>lighten up, tyler. no one\\\'s conspiring to censor you, make you a victim of \\\'intellectual superiority\\\' or really imply anything aside from the fact that <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/faq.phtml#howcome\\\">namecalling</a> is anti-intellectual and not necessary. youre a smart man. if you truly want a chance to defend/discuss/argue ideas, feel free- we know you have more eloquence up your sleeve than merely dismissing another user as a \\\'dickhead\\\'.<br><Br>i hope you know i mean no offense by this, but your increasingly blunt, inflamatory and argumentative tone may be having a bit of a backlash. if youre truly seeking a nicer reaction from people, you might consider proffering the same... just an idea from my own biased perspective...<br><Br>\r\n<i>*best friendly*</i></tt>',1,812,75,0,'156.145.58.128','en'),(21097,3632,'2004-06-24 12:10:57',508,'edward','Like when Ed Harcourt ironically sings \\\"I\\\'m not the one for nostalgia, I don\\\'t really like the past\\\".. You should be grateful for having that feeling because it makes you a more sensitive and caring person, as long as it\\\'s not too strong it\\\'s only good I think. Welcome to Swinney.',1,299,0,0,'213.67.51.28','en'),(21098,3614,'2004-06-24 20:16:14',57,'weeb','geez tyler, sensitive much?',6,27,0,0,'208.218.54.65','en'),(21099,3639,'2004-06-25 19:27:35',195,'matt','i\\\'m sorry i told you, in essence, to go fuck yourself.  that wasn\\\'t nice.',1,79,0,0,'207.239.93.170','en'),(21100,3627,'2004-06-25 20:57:00',75,'tyler','Peter...didn\\\'t i just write a whole long thing about how i wasn\\\'t \\\'merely dismissing another user as a dickhead\\\'? \r\n<br><br>\r\nand please don\\\'t say \\\'we\\\' (\\\'we know you have more eloquence up your sleeve...\\\') when you are talking to me one on one. we who? do you have a mouse in your pocket?',1,315,0,0,'68.222.239.6','en'),(21101,3357,'2004-06-26 05:55:26',17,'austin','Select here the comb that meets your styling need: \r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\n\r\n<B>Dressing Comb</B> <BR>\r\nLarge coarse & fine tooth comb\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\n<B>Detangling Comb</B><BR> \r\nA comb with a comfortable grip for detangling or working in conditioners. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\n\r\n<B>Rake Comb</B> <BR>\r\nA high-profile, wide-toothed comb \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\n<B>Tail Comb </B><BR>\r\nFine teeth and a pick. \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\n<B>Styling Comb</B> <BR>\r\nMedium size comb for styling and touchups.  <BR><BR>\r\n\r\n\r\n<B>Pocket Comb</B> <BR>\r\nA styling comb that there\\\'s always room for. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\n<B>Cutting Comb</B> <BR>\r\nA long, low-profile tapered styling comb ',1,627,175,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21102,3627,'2004-06-27 01:22:20',66,'Peter','<br><tt>no, actually, i have a dwarf hamster in my pocket; his name is <A href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=3640\">charles</a>.<br><br>in regards to how sensitive you appear to be about every comment directed to you, im just gonna peace out of any further nitpicking; i dont mean any offense man, its just getting sort of hard  to interact with you on here without expecting some sort of backlash.<br><Br>\r\n<i>*nice supportive*</i></tt>',1,388,75,0,'162.83.210.121','en'),(21103,3640,'2004-06-27 02:07:39',17,'austin','this article should be called \\\'ham on the loose\\\'!',0,55,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21104,3640,'2004-06-27 02:11:06',66,'Peter','<br><Tt>...or, <i>\\\"Ham on the Run!\\\"</i></tt>',1,50,17,0,'162.83.210.121','en'),(21105,3640,'2004-06-27 02:15:36',17,'austin','Or maybe blurbed \\\"Pressed Ham\\\".\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nglad you found you hammy!',1,78,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21106,3640,'2004-06-27 02:16:37',66,'Peter','<br><tt>oh look! al the ads to the right are about HAM. yay!</tt>',1,65,17,0,'162.83.210.121','en'),(21107,3636,'2004-06-27 03:16:10',70,'Fuzzy','<p>I like your articles as well.</p>\r\n<p>The thought about not being better but doing things better is nice.  I think the main problem is that people are too willing to let some external entity dictate to them what exactly better is and, quite often, for a fee.  It\\\'s simple enough to figure out how something can be done more efficiently or for less money but it is another matter entirely to step back and question the necessity of that \\\"improvement\\\".</p>\r\n<p>I suppose that being in a position to \\\"make things better\\\" and also being perceptive enough to realise that some things are fine the way they are; yet going ahead anyway probably does just come down to money.  If you know that the majoriy of the population will willingly accept something *NEW* [insert over-exclamation here], even if it is unecessary, then why not go ahead and make some money?  Sounds easy enough to me.  It\\\'s more difficult to be happy with things as they stand.  And for all the times you hear that \\\"people don\\\'t like change\\\", they sure seem to accept it so long as it is sufficiently marketed to them.</p>\r\n<p>I guess it comes down to more of a personal moral / ethical question regarding the exploitative nature of our society.  And how many people are in a position to actually make that kind of a choice anyway?  Is that too pessimistic?</p>',1,1355,0,0,'65.50.24.60','en'),(21108,3623,'2004-10-05 03:45:54',234,'etienne','hi again.  i missed paul g\\\'s comment, partly because my visits here of late have  been of a \\\"read new writing\\\" and \\\"skim swinchat for interesting thoughts/words\\\" not following threads and comments and shit.  its interesting in a way to come to a site which at first appears to move at a low hum but then realise that underneath the surface there is a lot more going on.\r\ni don\\\'t think the actual google ads are the issue here anymore though, i think its become about what people want from this site, how they view and use it and what it means to them.  maybe i\\\'m wrong, but i am excited for new servers and such - i haven\\\'t been writing much of late, but i know that when i am, i have a lot of time to write for/on swinney.',1,747,0,666,'219.88.206.243','en'),(21109,3614,'2004-06-27 11:06:08',234,'etienne','<p>tyler - i\\\'m not even sure you\\\'ve ever commented on anything i\\\'ve written.  ironic though that you have such a view of livejournal - i myself read/write there often.  i mostly view the difference between the writing there and teh writing here as the temporality of it - i can delete the bad mood or insecure moment i publically displayed there with a few clicks - here, i don\\\'t have that control.  lighten up dude and think about the other possibilities of peoples words.  if you think the worst of people all the time you\\\'ll get it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>*trying to be constructive*</p>',3,592,75,0,'219.88.206.243','en'),(21110,723,'2004-06-28 01:21:43',17,'austin','teakers suck, still.',1,20,79,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21111,3357,'2004-06-28 02:14:01',175,'Paul G','i\\\'m afraid that if there is too much stress and conflict in my life, i may lose my hair altogether.\r\n<p>\r\nwill you put on your poodle skirt, so that we can slow dance to lonnie mack\\\'s <i>i\\\'ll keep you happy</i>?  just like old times...but better.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni expect too much from you, in know.  i think that you should be a playboy model, betty crocker, and marie curie.  and the truth is that you have all that you can do to keep the house together, and the family entertained.  everyone wants something new; and they get tired of new things, quickly.  and it\\\'s easy to affect that \\\"leave it for the bitch\\\" attitude that puts all the burden on you.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas for me, work has got me down.  i feel like i\\\'m giving myself away for free - and i\\\'m tired of it.  the rules of the game are changing fast, and working class guys are hurting.  you know?  when i saw that you sold the things i gave you, i snapped.  i don\\\'t know.  it\\\'s not good to be too serious all the time.  but i see too many people who don\\\'t care about anything or anyone at all.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni\\\'m gonna start my shift now.  if i don\\\'t get back to you right away, it\\\'s \\\'cause i don\\\'t have an isp - and not \\\'cause i\\\'m ignoring you.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nxoxo\r\n<br>\r\npaul.\r\n</p>',1,1289,17,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(21112,723,'2004-06-28 02:36:48',66,'Peter','<Br><Tt>this is one of my all-time fav swinneys...</tt>',1,55,79,0,'162.83.210.121','en'),(21113,3357,'2004-06-28 05:47:19',17,'austin','You trippin\\\' Dog.  Why don\\\'t you buy a cop interceptor off ebay and paint it yellow and get a business card that says fuck the man?\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nburn, Tha Man, burn! ',6,172,175,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21114,3639,'2004-06-28 18:21:48',195,'matt','i wouldn\\\'t have said that to you if i had known who you were.  and that isn\\\'t right:  i shouldn\\\'t be talking like that to anybody, no matter who they are.  (conversely, i probably wouldn\\\'t have apologized if i hadn\\\'t ascertained your identity from your livejournal.  and that isn\\\'t right, either.)<BR><BR>which leads me to wonder why you addressed me under an alias, because, even though the question pissed me off (because it was too close to home), it was a perfectly valid question.  i <I><B>have</I></B> changed my life.  (more on the changes later)<BR><BR>i consider you my friend and deeply regret lashing out like that.  i\\\'m pretty thin-skinned and have a bad temper, but i\\\'m working on those things.  i hope we can put this behind us.  if not, that\\\'s cool, too.<BR><BR>see you around the block,<BR>mmm',1,836,0,0,'207.239.93.170','en'),(21115,3640,'2004-06-28 20:50:53',74,'jo','warning, warning: picture cannot fully represent how heart-breakingly adorable charles is. charles is more hamster cuteness than can possibly be captured in a jpg.',1,163,0,0,'216.142.229.132','en'),(21116,3636,'2004-06-28 22:42:38',508,'edward','You got a point, but I\\\'m not whether to take the question of exploitative nature seriously or not. It seems to me that my call for \\\'upgrades\\\' are stronger the calls for \\\"meditative satisfaction\\\", if you understand what I mean.\r\n\r\nI think the way of thinking is true if applied on a relation, but concerning material stuff and experiences my mind have been more commercialized lately.',3,398,70,0,'213.67.51.28','en'),(21117,3640,'2004-06-28 23:56:53',240,'xep','this is the best story i\\\'ve heard in a long time',0,51,0,0,'64.81.241.248','en'),(21118,3639,'2004-06-29 08:57:07',234,'etienne','so who is it?',0,13,195,0,'219.88.206.243','en'),(21119,3640,'2004-06-29 14:47:26',82,'simon','dude! PRESSED HAM! hahaha',0,25,17,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(21120,3640,'2004-06-29 21:39:19',57,'weeb','i just keep thinking about what if Peter bought shoes that were the right size.',0,79,0,0,'208.218.54.65','en'),(21121,3640,'2004-06-30 07:44:16',17,'austin','moons over my hammy.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.dennys.com/images/MenuItems/Breakfast/MoonsOverMyHammy.gif\\\">',1,119,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21122,3639,'2004-06-30 16:04:10',195,'matt','it\\\'s up to them to tell you, i guess.  i won\\\'t break their anonymity.',1,75,0,0,'207.239.93.170','en'),(21123,3631,'2004-07-01 17:01:11',180,'auntie','no, i think he had sunglass with her',3,36,0,0,'162.40.229.114','en'),(21124,3623,'2004-10-05 03:45:54',180,'auntie','money is a good thing, and google is a good thing, so fuck worrying about the ads.',1,82,0,666,'162.40.229.114','en'),(21125,3641,'2004-07-02 18:14:51',195,'matt','i will stop, i will stop at nothing.  say the right things when electioneering.  i trust i can rely on your vote.<BR><BR>when i go forwards you go backwards and somewhere we will meet<BR><BR>riot shields.  voodoo economics.  it\\\'s just business.  cattle prods and the IMF.  i trust i can rely on your vote.<BR><BR>when i go forwards you go backwards and somewhere we will meet',1,378,0,0,'207.239.93.170','en'),(21126,3641,'2004-07-02 19:32:24',180,'auntie','<p>\r\nthey\\\'re all liars.  bless their pointed little heads.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni\\\'ve said it before elsewhere, but here is a good place to repeat myself...\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\nnow that the masses are online, why the hell can\\\'t we have a candidate who doesn\\\'t need to collect millions to run for office, and in the process, owe favors to dozens of smoke-filled rooms full of scotch-drinking deal makers?\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nthere really isn\\\'t any reason why we couldn\\\'t search high and low and find someone who knows the difference between a flame war and a difference of opinion.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe need someone who knows what a real job is, someone who has wondered where the light bill money was gonna come from.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe also need someone strong enough to do something when evil is apparent, but not someone who uses apparent evil as an excuse to line his own power pockets, or those of someone he/she owes a favor to.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\ni\\\'d sorta like to have people in power who knew how to listen better.\r\n</p>',3,1011,195,0,'24.92.161.117','en'),(21127,3641,'2004-07-02 23:58:06',234,'etienne','i am so disillusioned about politics.  it used to be my passion, my goal in life - i wanted to effect change from within the system but the more i read and study and learn - it just wn\\\'t make a difference.  ',0,210,0,0,'219.88.206.243','en'),(21128,3627,'2004-07-04 02:46:12',93,'chiamattt','regulated events:  school, academies, family, studying.',1,55,0,0,'65.50.46.171','en'),(21129,2576,'2004-07-04 05:11:34',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i just used the warhol/\\\'your 15 minutes are up\\\' magnet to hang a snapshot of the trade center on my fridge at our new house. things are better here now.</tt>',1,171,0,0,'162.83.210.121','en'),(21130,3641,'2004-07-05 05:21:54',370,'seanthecon','the issues are very complicated. but people register things like binary codes. one\\\'s and zero\\\'s. black and white. good versus evil. one year your husband is a hero, the next he\\\'s a villain. one year iraq is an ally, the next they are the enemy. language is a very powerful tool. i noticed that you called them terrorists. not everyone fighting this war are alqada, just like not all american soldiers are baby killers. the american adminstration talks about the \\\'brutal savages\\\' which only causes the pendulum to come swinging back the other way - in many cases unjustifiably. i hope your husband returns safely. too many have already died - unjustifiably.',6,671,180,0,'65.92.122.62','en'),(21131,3642,'2004-07-07 18:32:58',195,'matt','if nothing else, vote for the people who aren\\\'t allowed to.  i\\\'m a convicted felon, so, in the state of Florida, i can\\\'t vote.  also am deemed <I>non compos mentis</I>, so then again.  i\\\'m fucked a-comin\\\' and a-goin\\\'.  and there are millions like me.<BR><BR>if nothing else, vote for the disenfranchised.  i wish i could vote.  i\\\'d vote every time the polls opened.  dog catcher or whatever.<BR><BR>and if decide not to vote, don\\\'t bitch when you don\\\'t like the way things turn out.',1,509,0,0,'207.239.93.170','en'),(21132,3646,'2004-07-08 21:50:25',17,'austin','The most anticipated return of 2004, Essemar! <BR><BR>\r\nEssemar Vs. Incitor\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nFIGHT!',1,93,0,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(21133,3646,'2004-07-09 04:26:07',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i ran into essemar a couple of months ago at burritoville in hell\\\'s kitchen. i was drunk and had stepped out of the belleview for some smokes and eats. he mentioned being in a play, i think.<br><br>we exchanged numbers and then never called each other.</tt>',1,268,17,0,'162.83.210.121','en'),(21134,3646,'2004-07-09 05:46:07',72,'essemar','ummm... actually... I did call you.  You never called me back.  :)',6,66,66,0,'66.95.134.2','en'),(21135,3646,'2004-07-11 01:08:44',82,'simon','heh, peter you flake',0,20,66,0,'207.237.42.182','en'),(21136,3647,'2004-07-11 05:17:32',234,'etienne','what kind of cue will it take?',1,30,0,0,'219.88.205.112','en'),(21137,3646,'2004-07-11 06:59:17',533,'incitor','hmmm...no one\\\'s ever accused me of being pompous before.  but really, your case against me only consisted of a sentence or two.  \r\n\r\ni guess i don\\\'t see what you\\\'re on about.  i look at most of my writing here, and elsewhere, and think that it expresses alot more than you suggest.  i think that i\\\'ve shown my confusion, my sorrows, my fear, self loathing, self pity...  I\\\'ve expressed my beliefs, my experiences, yeah.  But i don\\\'t remember telling anyone that their words are silly just because they\\\'ve never seen what i have, or done what i have.  that doesn\\\'t mean i haven\\\'t, i\\\'m an imperfect creature.  i lose my temper, i say things i don\\\'t mean, i write things i\\\'ll later wish i could take back.  ooops, i\\\'m human.\r\n\r\nshit, i\\\'m more flawed than almost anyone i know.  \r\n\r\nyou are welcome to insult me, or call me names if you wish.  i don\\\'t really care.  nothing you ever say can be worse than what i say and think about myself.  \r\n\r\nfrankly, i\\\'m sad that you think so poorly of me, but you know... tomorrow is still going to happen to me.\r\n\r\nto provide you with some amusement, if only to make up for my pomposity, i will share some funny/pathetic facts about myself now:\r\n\r\n-it\\\'s been nearly five years since i\\\'ve been kissed or made love.\r\n\r\n-the closest thing to a date i\\\'ve had in that time has been my two dinners with a high priced hooker i arrested.  she offered me a freebie, instead i took her to dinner (twice so far) and took notes as she told me about her life.\r\n\r\n-my boss is younger than me, he is better looking than me, his girlfriend is beautiful, very very intelligent, well educated, and crazy rich.  \r\n\r\n-my little brother is about to graduate college (after only 2.5 years) and is getting ready to head to grad school.  those who know me well, know how much i lament my lack of higher education.\r\n\r\n-i\\\'ve tried (and failed) to be chemically castrated for four years.  i\\\'ve begged doctors and urologists, practically on my knees, only to be told that since i\\\'m not a pedophile, it can\\\'t be done.  i\\\'ve tried because my sex drive is so strong, and it has a really painful negative effect on my rationality.\r\n\r\n-as i said, i can\\\'t get laid.  funnily, i\\\'m multiorgasmic.  i can easily jerk off five times, one after another.  i can crank off 20 times in a day.  that\\\'s not an exaggeration.  and no, i don\\\'t care if no one believes.  \r\n\r\n-i am losing most of my friends due to distance.  those i\\\'m not losing to distance, i\\\'m losing because i\\\'m such an unhappy person.\r\n\r\n-i\\\'ve had women who don\\\'t know me, and who had no reason to speak to me, tell me that i\\\'m ugly.  \r\n\r\nevery word is the truth, believe it or not.  that\\\'s all for now.\r\n\r\nby the way:  if you called me pompous to my face, i\\\'d probably grin and buy you a beer.  most of my closest friends initially didn\\\'t like me for one reason or another.  i admire people who are willing to tell me to my face that they don\\\'t like me, or something i said/did.',3,3051,72,0,'24.5.141.120','en'),(21138,3646,'2004-07-11 07:05:01',533,'incitor','apologies to all for the utter lack of formatting.  i\\\'m not not drunk, and i\\\'ve had a long day of holding babies and fingerprinting little kids.',0,150,0,0,'24.5.141.120','en'),(21139,3647,'2004-07-11 13:55:43',552,'isobel','I\\\'m not so sure I know, but any rash leeps of  emotional expression could truly deem fatal and horribly unfair to someone new that seems to be getting close. I\\\'m not satisfied that my engagement into something more with him wouldn\\\'t just be an attempt to solidify my past as my past.  I wish I could just say \\\"fuck it\\\" to the whole lot and just dive in; my brain works too hard some times.(squeaky, squeaky)',0,422,0,0,'69.195.31.236','en'),(21140,3647,'2004-07-11 21:18:17',17,'austin','I have these same dreams.  I also go out to bars, usually to see bands, and I see the sneakers-n-high heals-n-boots hanging from bar stools.  The closer I get to them, the further I want to be from them.  Desire draws me to them in the beginning and pushes me away in the end.',1,276,552,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21141,2829,'2004-07-17 20:55:50',195,'matt','yeah, i like <I><B>for your love</I></B>, when there\\\'s a lull, then the drums go, and the rest of the instruments kick in.  choice.',1,134,0,0,'209.116.42.32',