0,23,0,1,'69.195.31.216','en'),(21712,3753,'2004-12-16 20:41:58',86,'egg','i was just coming here to write a comment cause i know you want me to, but actually this poem is brilliant! shit ma! you continue to amaze me. am i going to get a christmas poem? I want it to rhyme.',0,198,0,0,'64.81.201.107','en'),(21713,3734,'2004-12-17 01:15:50',587,'ackman','As this covers a number of my own personal issues,I\\\'m having trouble martialing my thoughts around my response,so I\\\'ll probably address one or two points each across a couple of comments.\r\n\r\nHere goes:\r\n\r\na)  Okay, The first thing you mentioned -\\\"PRESTO! He\\\'s made you all disappear!\\\" and it\\\'s similarity to your \\\"Presto.\r\nPresto: I wish that you would appear, again.\\\" Take it from a true paranoid (more on that later),it\\\'s coincidence. Although I think have (ironically)read all of your articles (and a lot of comments as well) on this site (and other sites, as I find your writing [and what I think I can extrapolate about your character] intrigue me and are thus worth the tracking down)I didn\\\'t lift that. Firstly, several of my friends are widely published authors as well as keepers of high moral standards. It\\\'s why I\\\'m attracted to them as well as why I\\\'m forever mystified as to why the have anything to do with me. I couldn\\\'t possibly do that. It would be more shame than I could bear - and believe me - as a guy who used to eat out of the trash cans at the cablecar turn-around in downtown San Francisco (M.O.T.L.),I can bear a fair bit of it.\r\n\r\nb) Neither was it subconcious. I\\\'ve this weird kinda \\\"thing\\\" about an animated Mpeg based on aplay titled Bingo: http://ftp.supertux.com/pub/supertux/media/funny/bingo.mpg and I picked up this little Ringmaster/Stage Magician kinda routine I fall into when I\\\'m being sarcastic (what the jerk means is that he\\\'s so insecure he still enhances his verbal communication with dramatic behavior and/or characters. Christ, he even does Bullwinkle impressions; talk about enduring shame, I gotta live in this guy\\\'s HEAD!). \r\n\r\nBack in a couple minutes \r\n\r\n',1,1766,175,0,'67.122.211.15','en'),(21714,3734,'2004-12-17 01:45:36',587,'ackman','As to the Grewco-Gaulic thing, my mother named me Achilles Kasanis. The \\\"Achilles\\\" part apparently had to do with her hope that I would be invunerable to what life would dish at me \\\'cause she wouldn\\\'t there herself - I was up for adoption at birth. The Kasanis was the name of a guy she had been seeing. My father had had a brief thing with her, and had gone on about his life, none the wiser to her \\\"condition\\\". My last name, \\\"Gaubert\\\" is my natural fathers name (he finally found out he had a son - fifteen years later). Belmont? I\\\'ve lived here in Belmont, CA for the last nineyears, and it\\\'s a slow roasting, Hell (or at the very least, Purgatory) and I\\\'ll go back to S.F where I was born (1001 Portero) and lived for the previous thirty-odd years (coincidently, the foster family that adopted me were also in S.F)just as soon as circumstances (money and a dorsal mounted simian) allow.',1,923,175,0,'67.122.211.15','en'),(21715,3753,'2004-12-17 18:12:24',57,'weeb','sweet.',0,6,0,0,'66.92.219.22','en'),(21716,3753,'2004-12-18 06:29:07',17,'austin','there is a live band outside playing holiday hits.  this music does nothing goo d for me.',1,89,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21717,3356,'2004-12-18 16:07:53',120,'joyrider','i\\\'ve been writing in private journals for a while now.  a bunch of things i was writing about aren\\\'t for the net. i think i\\\'m about to start writing more around here.   i have an article in mind.',0,204,74,0,'69.146.193.5','en'),(21718,3734,'2004-12-19 01:44:36',17,'austin','what is a dorsal mounted simian?',1,32,587,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21719,3734,'2004-12-19 02:26:46',195,'matt','i think it means a monkey on your back.',1,39,0,0,'24.92.185.89','en'),(21720,3734,'2004-12-19 03:11:34',195,'matt','which they don\\\'t have.  none of it rings true.  none of the biographical info.<BR><BR>i\\\'m of the opinion that, despite the suspicions paul has, ackman is a meat puppet for somebody who\\\'s been here awhile and been lurking lately, maybe somebody who feels they\\\'ve been ostracized or not given the attention they deserve.  but that\\\'s just a theory.',1,360,0,0,'24.92.185.89','en'),(21721,3734,'2004-12-20 15:46:15',74,'jo','i don\\\'t know if it\\\'s fake but i do find the po-mo inflated-but-not-really language to be pretty irritating. not everyone is jonathan safran foer. thank god, because he irritates the shit out of me, too.',0,208,0,0,'216.142.229.132','en'),(21722,3711,'2004-12-20 18:08:47',538,'arcy','Maybe the whole point is that the expectations are imposed on the person themselves. How do you help someone out of that mind set? I know this sort of scenerio well. An all or nothing attitude can be very destructive, especially when you find yourself buying into their bullshit. Its fine to demand the best, but when you aren\\\'t willing to humbly assert yourself to work from the bottom up, you end up going no where. Its the same sort of attitude that blinds people into a belief that they\\\'ll win the lottery and so they put their life on hold until they win; completely unattainable.',1,591,0,0,'142.204.189.78','en'),(21723,3731,'2004-12-21 06:15:30',187,'kandrer','great! thinking he can see a bird <i>in there</i> justifies drug better than any social theory I ever heard.\r\n*I love your series*, Hool, fantastic!',1,148,0,0,'222.5.186.213','en'),(21724,3703,'2004-12-21 06:22:03',187,'kandrer','take good care, Paul. And if you are recovering of something, anything, I have no doubt you will soon be all right, because you are \\\"une force de la nature\\\"!<br />\r\n[x] love',1,179,175,0,'222.5.186.213','en'),(21725,3703,'2004-12-21 17:53:56',66,'Peter','<br><tt>everytime i see this article pop up in the comments, the pixie\\\'s <i>\\\'letter to memphis\\\'</i> always pops into my head.</tt>',1,139,0,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21726,3755,'2004-12-21 18:49:17',587,'ackman','Now I truly feel like a total loser/ass. My apology for this crap. I\\\'ve been at the end of my rope and feeling like throwing in the towel. Today, I destroyed my paraphernalia and gave away the last of my dope. Sure, I could always get more but I don\\\'t think that\\\'ll be the case. \r\n\r\n  Anyways, I just wanted say sorry for wallowing in self-pity in public. For now, I gotta go find the coin to keep the \\\'phone on. I\\\'ll be back as soon as I can with somethin\\\' worth readin\\\' (one hopes, anyways). ',3,515,0,0,'67.122.209.18','en'),(21727,3755,'2004-12-21 19:14:15',195,'matt','you\\\'re right.  you\\\'re listed in the belmont directory.  sorry, dude.  good luck with your program.',1,104,0,0,'24.92.185.89','en'),(21728,3753,'2004-12-23 02:06:57',93,'chiamattt','the most annoying christmas song to date is korean. to the beat of \\\'mary mary\\\' by RUN DMC.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nMERRY MERRY. MERRY CHRISTMAS \r\n<br>\r\nI hate it so much...but my coworkers love it so much. they just think its the coooolest.',0,233,0,0,'221.146.67.20','en'),(21729,3758,'2004-12-26 05:35:19',17,'austin','you are motorcycle obsessed.  where is the snow?  <BR><BR>HI MOM!',1,65,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21730,3758,'2004-12-26 08:30:51',82,'simon','heh. the snow! i\'m having a california christmas. yesterday we zoomed down 280 past golden hills listening to joni mitchell with the sun roof open on the way to christmas dinner! then tonight we had my friends and sort of brother and his wife over. my mom told stories of acid trips in tijuana and my sober brother looked extremely uncomfy and my sober dad thought it was hilarious. also the food was great. then we watched a laugh-in rerun. tomorrow is brunch at the olympic club in SF with the great aunts and uncles.',1,520,17,0,'24.5.110.153','en'),(21731,3758,'2004-12-26 14:54:47',74,'jo','was that bar called \"yeah baby\"?',0,34,0,0,'66.68.108.105','en'),(21732,3758,'2004-12-26 17:21:11',82,'simon','that business is called YEAH BABY! but its not a bar. just some little boutique. the bars in fairfax are called: nave\'s, 19 broadway, peri\'s and new amsterdam.',1,161,74,0,'24.5.110.153','en'),(21733,3758,'2004-12-27 05:20:44',65,'le','my riding instructor grew up in fairfax. she learned to vault before she learned to ride regular. vaulting is when you do gymastics on the horses back, sometimes leaping onto the horse while it\'s in mid stride. that\'s all i really know about fairfax.',0,252,0,0,'69.69.140.156','en'),(21734,3734,'2004-12-27 14:22:35',587,'ackman','Titles courtesy of The PoMo English Paper Title Generator:\r\nThe Feminism of Production and the Boundaried in Gaubert\'s In Response...\r\nDegeneration and Savagery in In Response...: Gaubert Perverting Heteronormative Identity\r\nMapping the Savage Objectification in Gaubert: In Response... and Periphery\r\nHomophobic Affliction and the Reclamation of Ethnocentric Artifice in Gaubert\'s In Response...\r\nHistoricizing Promiscuities: Erotic Initiation in Gaubert\'s In Response...\r\n\r\nSeriously, though. Had to do a google on PoMo. Don\'t know if I\'m post-modern but I\'m sure I\'m post-most things ;^)   I just read the first few pages of Foer\'s Everything Is Illuminated and all I can say is that if this came off like that, then I agree. However, no attempt at any particular style or method was attempted here. It pretty much comes outta my head, one way or another -SPLAT! I was simply tanked to the gills with whatever I could find to put a damper on some of the more harshly critical (and coincidentally the most accurate) voices in my head. rWhat \"writing\" I\'ve done - sober or otherwise - has more often than not served as a pressure release valve rather than a craft. And as I and Formal Education parted ways about the time I began my career in criminality as an apprentice hoodlum... well, let\'s say that I read a lot of books but leave a great deal to be desired as a curriculum director. Now I\'m merely a novice again at yet5 another artcraftdisciplinescience. Anyways, blahblahblah. Thanks for the crit\', I now have another vast realm of literature to peruse in my efforts to find voice/tone/style/whatever. However, I\'m afraid I\'ll probably keep barking my shins in the dark rooms of my ignorance as well as reinventing the wheel (yeah, I do things like - scribble down tales with only a period for punctuation and find that it\'s a \'style\' [one of my favorite novels \"Butcher Boy\" clued me on that one]) to my embarrassment but hey, Embarrassing myself is one of the few things I show much talent for. That and rambling when I\'m beatin\' down the Jonze, so I\'ll just dial down the iris here.\r\n\r\n\r\n',2,2124,74,0,'64.160.39.16','en'),(21735,3002,'2004-12-28 18:56:35',587,'ackman','Sean: Aw the @%#$!&*$@! an\'...if you\'re just a very gifted and imaginative writer, please don\'t ever tell a soul. I can put myself/and everyone else through my gauntlet-feast of guilt and greed, shame, pity and predation. and I can just see the soiled scalps of my brethren vermin, toiling, growling, laughing maniacally in their own pits of despair and sleazy, greasy, pleasure/pain. Where it always goes. Where it always begins - if...\r\nDon\'t tell cause I couldn\'t stand it if they all really knew IT that well and could tell it even better. fuck. my god. chocolate bar, diet coke and enough gas to get there | don\'t think about...shit. And yeah, I can still manage to go a little lower. after all the mess, to suddenly stop and say \'oh,my! someone may see/hear what I\'ve done!\" Ha, ha! I\'m such a fuckin\' mark. I\'m the easiest piece of ass my mind has ever had. Dude, I\'d go short on my wake-up hit just to pay some punk $5 to swipe a copy of your book from Frenchy\'s,Stacy\'s or the goddam public library. Please tell me you\'ll write it while\'s we still gotz the time! god, and The captain - like the Wreck of the Medusa...jeez. Good. ',2,1155,0,0,'67.117.146.146','en'),(21736,3760,'2004-12-29 03:20:54',195,'matt','since i can\'t edit the article, i want to tell you to disregard what i said about <I><B>spartan</B></I>.  i praised it on the strength of its roughly first half.  i got caught up in something else and didn\'t watch the rest of it until today.  it falls apart and totally sucks.  i was really disappointed.  fell apart just like <B><I>heist</B></I> did.<BR><BR><A HREF=\"http://rogerebert.suntimes.com/apps/pbcs.dll/article?AID=/20040312/REVIEWS/403120305/1023\">roger ebert</A> must have been high when he saw it.  or in somebody\'s pocket and flat-out lying.<BR><BR>guys, i\'m going to check out of the hotel for awhile.  i might post chapters from something i\'m working on, but i\'m mostly going to keep myself to myself.  see you all down the road.  hang loose.',1,766,0,0,'24.92.185.89','en'),(21737,3173,'2004-12-29 16:53:46',587,'ackman','Always found the very attractive to be fascinating, above and beyond the most common motivation - their being attractive.  ;^)\r\n\r\nThatâ€™s not to say that I haven\'t also been, at times, fascinated by the unattractive or grotesque. But, like the good lilâ€™ Von Neumann/SUV for DNA that I was designed to be and, -make no mistake about it!- controlled with the same calm, confident, hand of my Tiny Helical Masters as if I were a carriage-horse, born and bred to the harness, I find myself more frequently disposed to gaze upon the fair. \r\n\r\nIt seems that, when I bother to really try to plumb the dark, treacherous, labyrinthine paths to the very root of my motivations -whether I\'m deciding what shirt to by at Salvation Army, breathing, or anything in-between- I invariably find myself blinking in confusion at the simple, straightforward and logical designs of my double helix\'d whip-cracker (the confusion from the idea that anything so simple and elegant could reside at my core [okay, okay: and partially confused from whatever it was I Snorted.Shot.Swallowed.Smoked.or Shoved up one orifice or another, that got me contemplating my navel in the first place). \r\n\r\nMost of the time, I\'m unaware of the blinders and bridle I bear in \"my efforts\" (i.e. my tiny masters desires) to find good, new places and people to live at/with as well as procreate with/at (and yes, I manage to do my part for the team without actually, LITERALLY being reproduced: language can be fairly virulent if used appropriately and it can be infused with experiences that the PHYSICAL young of others can benefit from. In my case, a cautionary tale that begins with our hero being nearly as favored by the Gods as his namesake, having the gift of being a healthy, white, male, north-American who can think on his feet an\' has got maybe a little bit o\' wit over the average bear (Pretty damn well equipped for the ass-end of the XXth \'Sent\'). And even with these advantages, managed to. well... I\'m a damned sight more physically virulent than most of the creatures on this rock and right at this point in time, not much better off mentally, spiritually, emotionally and am having trouble remembering where I left the boundaries of my principles (Hmm. Coulda sworn I left it by a bloody great bronze somethin-er-other dedicated to my self respect, but as with a lot of folks, I found that it\'s not to solid, immovable or highly valued at all when the Jones come over to dinner. And BTW, contrary to the opinion of a great many, it\'s rare that the Jonze\' aren\'t invited over the first time they visit - not a lot of I.B.S\'ers in the self-destruction business. \r\n\r\nJesus Marimba! Iâ€™ve done it again; turned a genuine desire to communicate with someone about THEIR work, and end up turning it into a monologue, palimpsest-style. Whelp. No matter. For those who desire it, Iâ€™m happy to where the Clown Pattern Baldness of Bozoâ€™s great, orange, skull-mounted Hot-Wheels ramp and red rubber nose.\r\n\r\n  Anyways, what I was trying to get at is that research since this initial posting indicates that physical beauty is a matter of geometrical-angly-arcy-mathy-kinda ratios/relationships between ones features and that it appears that commonly considered racial differentials are irrelevant. Test subjects given representations of these â€˜optimalâ€™ designs sans hair, make-up, shading etc., more often than not chose these features as more attractive than others with features more closely associated with any stated racial preference by the subjects. \r\n\r\nAdditionally, taking the same â€œwig-model-headâ€™ or whatever ya call it and giving it a particular hairdo and make-up tint had them all easily identifying the very same head as obviously being Marilyn Monroe and Halle Barre (sorry â€˜bout a lot of the spelling) alternatively. Neat stuff. I think thereâ€™s a PBS?NOVA ep on it as well as stuff by Phil Zombardo and interestingly enough, John Cleese. \r\n\r\nYeah, Iâ€™m sure itâ€™s all old news to a lot of ya (itâ€™s pretty evident the average education level around exceeds my vaunted 9th grade (I also have an advanced Degree in G.E.D.), but hey, anything that gets any kinda legitimate cognitive spark outta my head-sponge these days Iâ€™m gonna go with - Even if I am a couple years late for the discussion. And of course as they say at NBC: If I havenâ€™t seen it before, it new to me (in ref to their summer re-run programming). Boy these speed-raps are gonna be the life oâ€™ me some day. Later, folk!\r\n\r\n',0,4477,0,0,'67.119.2.173','en'),(21738,3173,'2004-12-29 18:05:07',587,'ackman','Well, if THAT wasn\'t enough, here\'s some personal experience. BTW, I make a concious effort to not allow the physical beauty of an individual to influence how I see that person\'s \'self\' or insides \'stead of outsides. A concious effort because Im just like all those other humans that tend more oft than not to BE infkuenced unconciously in just that way. Boy, the heady heights of resource-for-paper I coulda acheived backin my Health Insurance adventures in\'n I\'d gotten those caps, tugs and tans. It\'s sad, but true.\r\n\r\nThe pisser is, for all this \"won\'t get fooled again\" attitude about the the pretty and graceful, for all the months I worked for Pierre making spinache and brie croissant snadwhiches for an entire agency of young,predomonantly female fashion/hair/body-part models -and I tell ya, it\'s like the rest of us- alot of nuts, assholes and those unfortunately rare few that make it a pleasure to interact with human beings (just looks a little harsher I guess when someone who has the lines of a statue meant to glorify a Deity open her talking end of the tube and present herself as an ignorant, racially bigoted creature). But man! I couldn\'t help but feel like Quasimodo when some 15 of them would come down en masse and swarm about the few of us bungled n\' botched awkwardly filling up space in \'our way\". \r\n\r\nYeah, even with a this I fell in love with a beautiful woman. Funny. The first few times I had seen her, it was from across the room at NA/AA meetings. I\'d criticize her silently as I gazed at her with thoughts like \"yeah, I\'m so SURE she roughed it on the street when she hit bottom... fer about ten minutes!\" And other such unkind and unworthy things. Jealous of imagined advantage enjoyed. Or rather, afraid. \r\n\r\nSee, the more I was learning about her -at a distance- the more I was starting to think it would be one extreme or another:\r\n1. She\'s witty and charming as a result of being treated like a little princess from stage to stage of life. Hard not to have a positive, friendly demeanor. Yeah, that\'s it.\r\n\r\n2. She\'s a genuinely nice person and has probably never depended upon her looks to get her by than I with my mouth or muscle, and although I had a couple o\' years on her sober and away from the street, she seemed to be a lot more balanced and, well, not sitting around obsessing on pointless jealosy issues like me.\r\n\r\nWell, it turned out that she was 2.++\r\nShe didn\'t have time to recognize or take advantage of her \'charms\' before her aunt and Uncle did. They were into some pretty tripped out Anton Levey kinda thing that they\'d added sexual abuse and pedophilia to. That\'s who babysat her.\r\n\r\nShe didn\'t come to accept her above average attractiveness until her late teens and she started getting modeling work. \'Course the drugs an\' self-loathing she\'d been packing around had her hanging with the wrong modelling crowd and after a brief run of legit gigs, she was one of the Exotic Erotic Ball when the were doin\' the photographic posters), she ended up in the \'business\". High-end, anyways. A good day was two-maybe three calls (always in-calls, prescreened by Agency and credit verified and reserved ata minimum of $300.00 an hour) and ironically, the guys who could afford this kinda treatment regular mostly didn\'t want to have penetration, and not a few could deal with only the merest physical contact. Go figger. Anyways, I fell for her the day that a mutual friend suggested I ask her to see a rare video she had re: a common interest, And no, I didn\'t know that she was workin\' at this point. It wouldn\'t matter anyway. She lived in a part of the City I love, \'round Green and van ness kinda. I instantly fell in love with her apartment and what sent the chillup the back of my neck was when I looked at her book shelves. Just about every book I owned had owned or wanted and damn few not in one of those categories. Like maybe ten outta a few hundred. Christ. The joke was on me. I was not one of the 13th steppen\' dawgs that haunted her around the AA/NA. I wouldn\'t salivate for that particular bell but this...! I thought it was a joke, a con, anything but that there was a duplicate of my what-I-thought-was-eclectic (I bet we all kinda think that way, maybe?)Library and, no, she was not of the silver spoon as I had assumed nor was she educated formally anymore than was I (that was really a surprise). From that point on the discoveries of similar...er, well, SAMNESs/DIFFERENTNESS with a jigsaw fit was the second event in my life for which I felt the term \'miraculous\' was suitably made use of. Also, a sense of something that I suspect was what had been meant by those I have heard mention GRACE. Anyways, yeah she was and, no doubt, still is beautiful. Yeah, I had to work some issues out with myself a bout adjusting to other guys hittin\' on her all the time (not to mention what she was doing for a living). We were in love, our respective \'family\' members approved, lived together for a couple of years, got into a nice renovated Victorian at Leavenworth and Pacific, had the housewarming party, set the date, and then she broke my heart. I promptly folded like an origami crane made of tissue. After a year of trying to find somewhere in the city I could go and not run into her (she still loved med..or anyways..nevermind. Unimportant), I sent myself into exile. A good friend let me rent the apartment over his video store in Millbrae. Fell into dope again, balh blah blah. \r\n\r\nOf the few stellar points that made what WE were when we were still WE, that she was beatiful was not one of them; merely the fine Platinum filigree that lined that wonderful cloud being part/one with someone, even for a little while. Yeah. It was good. Sorry fer rambling into the \'Achilles Show\' again.\r\n\r\nOh. Outside of the two women that I\'ve been in love with, I don\'t think anyone has referred to me as handsome or good looking.',0,5944,0,0,'67.117.144.135','en'),(21739,3761,'0000-00-00 00:00:00',17,'austin','we get both sides here.',1,22,0,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(21740,3760,'2004-12-30 00:59:54',587,'ackman','Don\'t know if you\'ve ever had to deal with this before, but I can tell you that the pain does fade. And without goin\' inta details, I can tell ya from where I\'ve stood that you\'re already way ahead of the game. For what it\'s worth: I found talking to complete strangers and asking them \'bout there day and just listening as long as they wanted to talk, took me away from it some. Look forward to readin\' ya soon, take care, Matt.',2,437,195,0,'67.117.146.211','en'),(21741,3758,'2004-12-30 01:01:47',175,'Paul G','cool.  austin: dual-sports.',1,27,82,0,'64.107.88.142','en'),(21742,3173,'2004-12-30 07:53:40',17,'austin','never\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nin\r\n<BR><BR>\r\na\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nmillion\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nyears\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ncould\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nwrite\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nthat\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nmuch\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nabout\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nthis\r\n<BR><BR>\r\narticle.',4,201,587,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21743,3762,'2004-12-31 01:50:15',17,'austin','<TT>this is the way that six-year-olds solve problems.</TT>, ha!  Paul G., aren\'t you getting too old to take pot shots at the man?  *glove*',1,141,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21744,3763,'2004-12-31 20:46:23',587,'ackman','Tried to upload an image of my signature \'til I ran into the fact that I don\'t knowe how. Sorry ya got the sucky days off. \'Course, if anyone ever asks what do YOU contribute to society? You can let \'em know. I\'d be pretty bummed if I couldn\'t go see a flick on a holiday, and am damn glad there\'s folks there to operate the place. Also, EVERYONE should go easy on service personnel on holidys. Then again, that\'s how I learned which of the patrons used to work on my kinda job. As to the rest? Give \'em the obligatory smile and greeting; fuck \'em.',2,558,0,0,'67.122.211.108','en'),(21745,2790,'2004-12-31 20:46:54',17,'austin','sometimes doing the laundry leads to <a href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=3031\">the death of kittens.</A>',1,128,59,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21746,3762,'2004-12-31 20:50:10',175,'Paul G','guilty as charged.  i was already 18 when i saw the smiths that year.  \r\nand my grandmother was born in england during the reign of queen victoria.\r\nbut this isn\'t about me, morrissey and the QV.  it\'s about you and TEXAS.\r\nright?  all of \"you people\" stick together.  alamo!\r\nyou probably HAD a pet armadillo.  what was his name?  mr. grey nuts?  lil\' bumpy?  \r\nor did you save those terms of affection for yourself?\r\ndo tell.\r\n<br>\r\n*other glove*',1,453,17,0,'192.203.136.254','en'),(21747,3762,'2004-12-31 21:28:30',17,'austin','<P>I used to catch armadilloes as a kid.  it goes like this:\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\n1. chase the dillo into a wall\r\n<BR>\r\n2. chase the dillo along the wall\r\n<BR>\r\n3. have second person at end of wall with a garbage bucket\r\n<BR>\r\n4. dillo rams into garbage bucket\r\n<BR>\r\n5. throw lid on can before dillo sprays feces everywhere\r\n<BR>\r\n6. dump dillo up the road in somebody else\'s yard\r\n<BR>\r\n7. fight over who will wash out the feces from the trash can\r\n</P>\r\n\r\n<P>there you have it.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>and stay off my people, yankee!</P>',1,518,175,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21748,3762,'2004-12-31 22:15:30',175,'Paul G','i would stand on the texas border with my PETA sign, and protest - but where would i stand?  arkansas?  mexico?  the iron curtain around the old ussr was nothing compared to what \"you people\" have surrounded yourselves with.  my little nephew pledges allegience to texas before the usa, every day.  all the hippie/punk power that sister #2 and i can summon barely breaks-through his conditioning.  soon he will be lost to us.\r\n<br>\r\np.s. <i>\"sprays feces everywhere\"</i> i feel like i understand you and the site so much better now.\r\n<br>\r\np.p.s. happy new year old man swinney - time to flush 2004, for real.',1,613,17,0,'64.107.88.142','en'),(21749,3654,'2004-12-31 22:29:46',175,'Paul G','at least he didn\'t chase you into a \"garbage bucket.\"  good luck in the new year!',1,84,63,0,'64.107.88.142','en'),(21750,3748,'2004-12-31 22:32:15',175,'Paul G','wien?',1,5,0,0,'64.107.88.142','en'),(21751,3748,'2005-01-01 09:05:57',234,'etienne','ear flaps are NOT the way to a girl\'s heart.  i repeat, NOT.',1,61,0,0,'222.152.113.214','en'),(21752,3763,'2005-01-01 22:14:09',508,'edward','i\'m always polite to the theater attendents, no matter if it\'s a holiday or not. and kind of polite to service personnel in general i think.',1,142,0,0,'213.67.51.18','en'),(21753,3748,'2005-01-02 13:19:48',17,'austin','elly was once wooed by ear flaps alone.',1,39,234,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21754,3764,'2005-01-02 14:19:14',93,'chiamattt','buy a daves quality gift basket while supplies last!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!',0,66,0,0,'222.234.108.37','en'),(21755,3764,'2005-01-02 22:50:40',175,'Paul G','hahaha!',1,7,93,0,'64.107.88.142','en'),(21756,3764,'2005-01-03 05:21:21',83,'adamajah','that reminds me, i need to read the B1BL3 and see what all this killing is about.',1,81,0,0,'4.43.160.212','en'),(21757,3764,'2005-01-03 05:27:41',57,'weeb','spreading the word of porn everywhere... the great, not late, and earnest: CHIAMATT!',0,84,0,0,'66.92.219.22','en'),(21758,3762,'2005-01-03 14:15:46',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i innitially read <i>dillo</i> as <i>dildo</i>. needless to say, that added an interesting twist to your comment!</tt>',1,126,17,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21759,3764,'2005-01-03 14:18:45',93,'chiamattt','<a href=\"http://inconduit.com/sets/\">hool does it again</a> with these sets. god fucking damn. this shit is awesome. jehovah itself would be proud. I cannot even begin to say how much these photos make feel good about life. ',1,226,0,0,'222.234.108.37','en'),(21760,3764,'2005-01-03 18:13:07',17,'austin','<P>this isn\'t a blog.  do that again and i\'ll cane you.</P>',1,61,93,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(21761,3762,'2005-01-03 19:36:35',91,'jay','this will be our year, man.  i hold out hope.',1,45,0,0,'207.69.137.207','en'),(21762,3764,'2005-01-03 22:51:12',93,'chiamattt','yow!',0,4,17,0,'222.234.108.37','en'),(21763,3749,'2005-01-05 04:59:23',618,'skwooshi1','sorry guys, will work on paragraphs :D',0,38,0,0,'216.161.222.95','en'),(21764,3762,'2005-01-06 05:27:32',82,'simon','i hope so.',0,10,91,0,'24.215.234.202','en'),(21765,3762,'2005-01-06 16:14:06',74,'jo','where are the pictures? store \'em on swinney for safekeeping. unless they WERE stored on swinney and that\'s why they\'re gone.\r\n<p>\r\n2005 is going to be the bomb. i love it already.',0,183,0,0,'216.142.229.132','en'),(21766,3762,'2005-01-08 23:50:24',17,'austin','i\'ll fix that later.',3,21,74,1,'63.80.97.254','en'),(21767,3762,'2005-01-08 23:50:24',17,'austin','i\'ll fix that later.',3,21,74,1,'63.80.97.254','en'),(21768,3766,'2005-01-08 23:50:02',17,'austin','asdfasdf',0,8,0,1,'63.80.97.254','en'),(21769,3766,'2005-01-08 23:50:02',17,'austin','test',2,4,0,1,'63.80.97.254','en'),(21770,829,'2005-01-09 00:50:08',82,'simon','dave, you look cute here. fyi.',0,30,0,0,'24.215.234.202','en'),(21771,3703,'2005-01-10 04:32:31',17,'austin','<P>\r\nGERMANOS:\r\n</P>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/art/galleries/bikes/BONNEVILLE_3.jpg\">\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/art/galleries/bikes/map_pictNew244.gif\">\r\n',1,176,175,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21772,3770,'2005-01-10 05:45:13',234,'etienne','ohhhh...this is still so close to home.  i wish i\'d had this opportunity for her.',0,82,0,0,'222.152.113.243','en'),(21773,3748,'2005-01-10 08:54:47',598,'themixtaper','i enjoy earflaps very much',0,26,234,0,'24.66.94.141','en'),(21774,3703,'2005-01-11 00:36:31',175,'Paul G','i\'m there.  this spring my big obligations to the uni and mom will end.  time for me.\r\nwhen weather allowed, i was going to head\r\nsouth to the sis\' in austin, then turn right\r\nand make for the uncle\'s place in blythe.\r\nbeyond that, i had no real plans.  the idea for me was to plan little and take even less.  whatever happens, happens.\r\n<p>\r\nmexico?  canada too!  how far can it go?!?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe feeling of motion is what puts my soul at ease.  i have learned that much about myself.  when i am moving, and free, i am happy: bike, car, truck, feet, horse, whatever...give me the sky and the wind, and let the world pass away.  it\'s what keeps me going.\r\n</p>',1,668,17,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(21775,310,'2005-01-11 16:23:23',195,'matt','i shall be released.',1,20,0,0,'24.92.185.89','en'),(21776,310,'2005-01-11 18:47:46',66,'Peter','<br><tt>you\'re actually a pretty good singer, if you ask me, sine.</tt>',1,72,261,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21777,3703,'2005-01-13 00:01:16',83,'adamajah','<br>\r\nmaybe i\'ll figure out how to make my bike reliable by then',0,65,0,0,'66.77.144.5','en'),(21778,3771,'2005-01-13 14:28:35',66,'Peter','<br><tt>big congrats, matt! may this year be merely one of many more to come. also: this is a very telling entry; i like it. as always, thanks for posting it here.</tt>',1,168,0,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21779,3762,'2005-01-13 14:36:15',628,'ktsmith',' I stumbled upon your journal somehow. I typed \"Valentine\'s Day\" into Google and wound up with your Valentine\'s Day entry. And then I couldn\'t stop reading...\r\n\r\n Just wanted to say that I enjoy your journal/stories. In fact, I believe you\'ve made my day. Thanks!',1,269,175,0,'68.44.209.162','en'),(21780,3771,'2005-01-13 20:41:43',82,'simon','way to go, sir. here\'s to 2005 being a great year for you and at least less of a mess for me! woo!',0,99,0,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(21781,3762,'2005-01-14 01:26:42',175,'Paul G','back at you.  perfect timing too.  sorry for the poor proof/edit work on my part.  cheers!',1,90,628,0,'192.203.136.254','en'),(21782,3771,'2005-01-14 10:53:56',587,'ackman','Happy Birthday! Glad yer fellin better,too.\r\nWhen asked how does one stay clean,Rusty D. used ta say \"Easy! Don\'t take nothin\' an\' ya won\'t get loaded.\" \r\n;^)',2,164,0,0,'66.127.54.26','en'),(21783,3771,'2005-01-16 00:44:32',177,'pony','<p>My mom and I have had conversations about the possibility that my grandmother was bipolar...along with my brother. Trouble seems to run in that side of the family, but nobody wants to face it. My grandmother had four husbands, and one child for each husband. One of the husbands left her while pregnant and she gave the baby up for adoption to my mom\'s aunt. Mom didn\'t know her \"cousin\" was her sister until about ten years ago. Grandma use to beat everyone. My mom beat us too...but she refused to see the similarities. My brother also beat me, and did other things. They were all very angry people. I haven\'t talked with any of them for years, except for my mom. We have something of a good relationship now...or at least better than it use to be. She still hates the way I dress, she still thinks I\'m something of a freak. It\'s not unknown for people in my family to disappear for months at a time. No phone numbers, no mail, no contact. I kind of like it that way.</p>',4,983,0,0,'24.7.71.79','en'),(21784,3773,'2005-01-16 00:52:33',66,'Peter','<br><tt>hi dr.jd! :)</tt>',1,25,0,0,'68.28.123.108','en'),(21785,3775,'2005-01-16 01:35:58',17,'austin','<P>in the future, seth alves thinks the cutting board will be self healing.  i tried to argue against this future.  seth accused me of believing in God.</P>',1,156,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21786,3771,'2005-01-16 01:37:35',195,'matt','yeah, my grandpa on my dad\'s side did that for seven years one time.  nobody knew where he was.  in a long phone conversation several years ago, my aunt told me that she and a few of her siblings suspect he had another family down in south florida someplace.  but to not tell my dad she said that.  my dad only wants to believe the best about people.  classic enabler.  but god only knows where i\'d be without him.<BR><BR>i did that for nine months one time, five months another.<BR><BR>it\'s no good.<BR><BR><BR><BR>guys, thanks for all your support.  i couldn\'t have made it through this last year without having you guys to talk to.',1,638,0,0,'24.92.185.89','en'),(21787,3774,'2005-01-16 02:19:20',632,'tristan','OK, where\'s the next one?  I\'ve been sucked in...',2,51,0,0,'208.185.179.20','en'),(21788,1948,'2005-01-16 02:51:53',604,'jdfalk','<p>I didn\'t read this until just now, but luckily I was not eaten by wild voles in the meantime.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>As I read it I\'m listening to \"Acknowledgement,\" from John Coltrane\'s <i>A Love Supreme.</i>  This song has no words, but I always cry a little at the beauty of it.</p>\r\n',1,282,0,0,'64.81.66.81','en'),(21789,1948,'2005-01-16 02:52:45',604,'jdfalk','(\"just now\" == January 15, 2005)',2,34,0,0,'64.81.66.81','en'),(21790,3772,'2005-01-17 02:26:00',82,'simon','my cousin\'s in jail again and i\'ve been sending letters and pictures. i want to get him some magazines though.',0,112,0,0,'24.215.233.23','en'),(21791,3776,'2005-01-17 02:26:49',175,'Paul G','(1) someone [you] was probably doing something bad, and they put the light there to discourage the \"behavior.\"\r\n<p>\r\n(2) you could use the light like batman, i.e., instead of the bat-signal, you would have the swinney-signal.\r\nyou know, like: \"help, i\'m out of whiskey!\"  or \"help, i\'m horny!\"  and, maybe, that would help to take care of (1) too.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n(3) lube.\r\n</p>',1,383,0,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(21792,3772,'2005-01-17 03:39:59',195,'matt','well, you know which kind would come in HANDy, but they won\'t let him have those.<BR><BR>used to read <B><I>the new yorker</B></I> a lot in there.  thank god somebody donated a shitload of them.  remember reading an essay about the beatles and the fundamental goodness of things.<BR><BR>what else?  most of the stuff on the library cart i\'d already read. read <B><I>a farewell to arms</B></I> again.  and <B><I>the sound and the fury</B></I>.  (was reading that the day my sister got married.  ha.)<BR><BR>the koran.  borrowed it from a dude in my cell named l.a. (from arkansas, not l.a.) who was facing twelve years in the federal pen for coke distribution.  he was always raising hell about his diet.  he got fed a lot of turkey products instead of swine.<BR><BR>i asked him if he\'d heard of the sufis, and he said, \"oh, yeah, they\'re militant.\"  everything had to be militant.  actually, i think he was just pretending.  he was really pretty kind when you got to know him.  i think islam was more of a survival strategy.',1,1030,0,0,'24.92.185.89','en'),(21793,3772,'2005-01-17 06:56:43',82,'simon','hm. yeah. mikey wants FHM and Maxim. I\'m like dude ew. But hey, what can I do but give the kid what he wants. He\'s in for two years this time. His mom got him national geographic. I promised her and him that i\'d come out to pick him up with them when he\'s done. He wants to come to New York after but I\'m not sure what I\'d do with him here. I guess we have another year to talk it all over and see if it\'d be a good idea or not. So far I\'m thinking not. At least not right away. But who knows. He was my best friend when we were kids.',0,542,0,0,'24.215.233.23','en'),(21794,3775,'2005-01-17 18:12:50',604,'jdfalk','In the future, alarm clocks will be capable of complicated logic like \"hmm, he clearly doesn\'t want to wake up now, but he <i>does</i> want to get to work on time\" and will adjust their alarmage accordingly.',3,210,0,0,'64.81.66.81','en'),(21795,3777,'2005-01-17 18:53:38',637,'missmeliss','so glad you had her as long as you did\r\nI spose it\'s worthy to say, I hope, as a\r\nvixenish mother type, to live as long as Rose.\r\n\r\n=]',2,135,0,0,'66.92.185.90','en'),(21796,3778,'2005-01-18 00:43:49',189,'cyantist','so sweet!  this is awesome j.d.!',1,32,0,0,'63.251.108.100','en'),(21797,3778,'2005-01-18 00:47:25',65,'le','wow!',0,4,0,0,'64.81.58.18','en'),(21798,3776,'2005-01-18 00:48:58',65,'le','what you need is a slingshot or bb gun. ',4,40,0,0,'64.81.58.18','en'),(21799,3776,'2005-01-18 00:53:44',17,'austin','the light has killed a branch on the tree.  i aimed it downward but such that it doesn\'t shine directly into my room.  it is still annoying, but i can live with it.\r\n',1,167,0,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(21800,3140,'2005-01-18 06:11:17',604,'jdfalk','it\'s been like a year, and it\'s still time for another printing.\r\n\r\nI try to thank bus drivers too, even though the N Judah rattled my office in my last apartment (our living room was closer to the tracks than the office, but only the office rattled...kinda strange.)  Now that I\'m back in Oaktown my office doesn\'t rattle anymore, but the bus that goes past is still loud.\r\n',3,379,0,0,'64.81.66.81','en'),(21801,3779,'2005-01-18 07:44:37',65,'le','i see fucked up things on the street a lot. i\'m glad you got away safe. sometimes confidence and willingness to behave in risky and abnormal ways is enough to save your ass.',4,174,0,0,'64.81.58.18','en'),(21802,3779,'2005-01-18 15:34:07',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this is a nice story, hool. i mean, at least from a reader\'s perspective. thanks for sharing it...</tt>',1,112,0,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21803,3776,'2005-01-18 15:37:52',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yeah, i gotta second le\'s advice. a bb gun is good for this sort of thing. or a can of ultra-flat black krylon. <br><br>on the bright side [pun intended], at least you wont have trouble with <A href=\"http://www.sada.org.uk/treatment.htm\">seasonal affective disorder</a> this winter! well, not that thats a big problem in your neck of the woods, but...</tt>',1,367,17,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21804,3782,'2005-01-18 15:53:12',66,'Peter','<br><tt><a href=\"http://home.freeuk.com/spook007/\">NUMBER STATIONS</A>! my favorite is <a href=\"http://www.simonmason.karoo.net/page14.html\">the lincolnshire poacher</a>. i often whistle it when im nervous. and sine whistles it at me when she wants to freak me out.</tt>',1,274,0,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21805,3772,'2005-01-18 15:54:52',66,'Peter','<br><tt>...i think that <i>\"bend over and look at my jackass...\"</i> wins \'quote of the day\' today.</tt>',1,108,195,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21806,3782,'2005-01-18 17:08:25',604,'jdfalk','Hee hee, that\'s a great one for whistling.  Much of my vision of encoded messages during the cold war comes from <a href=\"http://www.alibris.com/search/search.cfm?qwork=1537987\"><i>Declare</i></a> by Tim Powers, one of the most amazing books I\'ve ever read.\r\n',2,263,0,0,'64.81.66.81','en'),(21807,3782,'2005-01-18 20:14:36',195,'matt','MARKOVIAN PARALLAX DENIGRATE',1,28,0,0,'24.92.185.89','en'),(21808,2029,'2005-01-19 18:13:32',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\"...i make a fuckin mushroom cloud every time i smoke trees...\"</i> is just the best comment evar.</tt>',1,116,0,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21809,3779,'2005-01-20 02:56:17',175,'Paul G','setting my pro-hool bias aside, this piece is really effective in a purely technical sense: the crop & positioning of the images tells the story as well as the text.\r\n<p>\r\ni made a personal choice never to photograph addicts, prostitutes, the mentally ill, and members of several other categories that i worked with on the street - unless i was asked.  for you it is different: you\'re not providing a service, or placed in public trust.  having said that, do you have any self-imposed limits on subject matter?  if so, what are they?  why?  curious: wanting to understand your perspective, not judge you.\r\n</p> ',1,612,0,0,'64.107.88.142','en'),(21810,3783,'2005-01-21 04:57:04',82,'simon','finally! damn dude. it\'s you and shit. cool.',0,45,0,0,'24.215.235.185','en'),(21811,3783,'2005-01-21 05:00:34',17,'austin','\r\n<P>adam:</P>\r\n<P><img src=\"http://swinney.org/art/galleries/adam/adamajah.jpg\"></P>\r\n<P>bubba:</P>\r\n<P><img src=\"http://swinney.org/art/galleries/austin/austin.jpg\"></P>',1,175,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21812,3783,'2005-01-22 19:42:34',195,'matt','so is this anything like what you guys thought i looked like?  i\'m not really satisfied with the picture.  i look better than that.  well, depends.  some days are better than others.  will take other photos.',0,208,0,0,'207.239.93.170','en'),(21813,3783,'2005-01-22 23:11:39',82,'simon','for some reason i thought you\'d be blonde.',0,43,0,0,'24.215.233.23','en'),(21814,3783,'2005-01-23 00:14:27',195,'matt','i quit coloring it around sept. or oct., i guess.  i can\'t tell if you can see the grey in it or not in the photo.  it\'s there, anyway.',1,137,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21815,3783,'2005-01-23 00:50:58',195,'matt','most people don\'t notice it until after they\'ve been around me awhile, though.',1,80,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21816,3783,'2005-01-23 02:21:02',17,'austin','your photo makes me wonder what you looked like the night you went binging and lost your car and wound up at your friends apartment.  and they said something about how very bad you looked.  i want to know what you looked like then.  i can guess a little more now.  before seeing this picture, i guessed you had dark hair like that.',1,331,195,0,'4.43.160.212','en'),(21817,3783,'2005-01-23 02:21:43',17,'austin','also, that small one is your livejournal icon in case you didnt figure that out.',1,80,195,0,'4.43.160.212','en'),(21818,3534,'2005-01-23 03:23:19',638,'isrow','Im not goin to tell you not to hate your father i hate mine too. a lot of dads are jerks who dont deserve relationships with their children. i dont know your situation but write a letter to him tellin him that you hate him and how bad of a father he\'s been. that always shows them. hurting the ones that hurt you always makes you feel better. ',2,344,0,0,'24.50.91.19','en'),(21819,3783,'2005-01-23 04:01:38',195,'matt','i would never in ten thousand years have guessed that that\'s what that is.  i thought i had fucked up and submitted the photo twice or something and one of them ended up small.  i think there may have been a little brain damage incurred somewhere along the sunny garden path there.<BR><BR>actually, on the night you\'re speaking of, i looked substantially better than i do in this picture.  i looked good, just obviously very fucked up - unshaven, no sleep, and eyes that gave away the fact that i\'d been freebasing and eating oxycontins for two days and had just smoked two joints of hydro (do you have that where you are?  it\'s just about the best you can get around here, i guess.  i don\'t know what they call top of the line anywhere else.).<BR><BR>during that last escapade, i didnt\' eat for twelve days and lost a lot of weight all at once, and it fucked my face up.  it\'s kind of better now.  i used to have fatter cheeks, though, and the area around the eyes is different.  i can\'t explain it, really.',1,1016,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21820,3783,'2005-01-23 15:22:03',74,'jo','mmm, hydro.\r\n<p>this isn\'t at all what i expected you to look like, matt, but then again, i didn\'t think about it much.</p>',0,125,0,0,'66.68.108.105','en'),(21821,3783,'2005-01-23 15:23:02',74,'jo','we should have a post where everyone posts pictures of themselves.',0,66,0,0,'66.68.108.105','en'),(21822,3783,'2005-01-23 15:24:29',74,'jo','<a href=\"http://www.flickr.com/photos/joannavaught/tags/jo/\">mmm, flickr</a>',0,78,0,0,'66.68.108.105','en'),(21823,3779,'2005-01-24 06:08:37',122,'hool','<p>thanks paul.  it\'s hard to talk about limits when i\'m removed from the situation.  the limits become apparent as the scene materializes in front of me.  i prefer to make that decision in the moment rather than state an outright rule that i will not shoot a person who i classify as off limits.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>a situation where i decided not to shoot:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>walking down spadina.  a car races up to the curb and a woman gets out, carrying a little boy.  she sets him standing on the curb and pulls down his pants, and tells him to pee.  he does.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i chose not to shoot this for two reasons.  first, people get hysterical about children being photographed. this combined with naked little boy buttocks made me hesitate.  second, i felt bad for the kid, though he didn\'t seem to mind, he was watching the pee.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>we can divide this question into shooting and showing. i don\'t think the act of shooting is right or wrong,\r\n\r\n<p>i do photograph kids, just not naked ones.  kids are out on the street with everyone else, there are lots of them, so they wind up in the photos.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i photograph homeless people.  i also talk to them.  sometimes i ask, sometimes i don\'t.  but panhandling does not a homeless person make.  i don\'t go out of my way to avoid them.\r\n</p>',0,1290,0,0,'69.195.39.4','en'),(21824,3784,'2005-01-24 16:19:51',66,'Peter','<br><tt>...when one believes one has come to the <i>spleen</i> of the matter, however...</tt>',1,93,0,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21825,3784,'2005-01-24 19:04:08',604,'jdfalk','<p>sometimes the heart is the only part that matters</p>\r\n\r\n<p>other times, it\'s simply funny</p>\r\n',3,100,0,0,'216.145.54.158','en'),(21826,3790,'2005-01-27 16:38:50',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\"...The everyday orbits of my life seem profound, and I feel the urge to share them...\"</i><br><br>\r\n\r\nmaybe you have <a href=\"http://www.medterms.com/script/main/art.asp?articlekey=26483\">hypergraphia</a>?</tt>',1,226,0,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21827,3790,'2005-01-27 16:42:09',195,'matt','<I>the midnight disease</I><BR><BR>ha.  i think i have that.  only so much of the output makes sense, though.',1,109,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21828,3790,'2005-01-27 17:22:42',17,'austin','that would be crazy.  \'i can\'t stop writing!!!!asdfasdfasdf\'  i think justin hall has a strain of that.',1,106,66,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(21829,3790,'2005-01-27 17:27:10',17,'austin','i\'m going through the opposite.  nothing seems important to me.  sitting at a red light this morning, i considered..  my procrastination has been honed to something desireable.  i now have patience.  i dont have patience all the time, but for some important things, i have patience.  i make incremental progress when the time is right.  it feels good.',1,352,195,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(21830,3790,'2005-01-27 18:11:33',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i was watching an episode of <i>\"cold case files\"</i> this week that involved hypergraphia... seems alot of people with hypergraphia tend to think that theyre conveying something of great importance through their writing, and that what theyre saying  is alot more profound/important than it actually is.<br><br>i think alot of people experience this at times, <A href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2099\">myself included</a>. unfortunately, some can\'t seem to parse how/why others may not be as enchanted by the writing as they are... the disclaimer-ish first sentence to this piece made me think of all of this.</tt>',1,649,17,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21831,3790,'2005-01-27 18:21:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt>also: funny thing you mentioned justin, as sine and i made that *exact* same observation after seeing the aforementioned episode!</tt>',1,142,17,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21832,3788,'2005-01-27 18:33:26',604,'jdfalk','yes.',2,4,0,0,'64.81.66.81','en'),(21833,3790,'2005-01-27 18:37:26',604,'jdfalk','<P>when life seems busy/complicated, you have to make time to let stillness in.</P>\r\n\r\n<P><font size=-2>(...or so I keep reminding myself.)</font></P>',3,150,0,0,'64.81.66.81','en'),(21834,2948,'2005-01-27 19:11:07',604,'jdfalk','search for bechamel sauce.  add more cheese.  yum.',2,50,0,0,'64.81.66.81','en'),(21835,2948,'2005-01-27 19:33:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt>...never did a pound of butter taste so good!</tt>',1,58,604,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21836,3790,'2005-01-27 22:40:58',74,'jo','i didn\'t get from justin\'s breakdown video that he was saying that he had something profound to share and the world would be better off if they listened. i got that he had been doing it for so long and that it had been his form of release that even when he realized that a) no one really cared and b) it wasn\'t doing anything for him anymore, he couldn\'t find an analog for that release in his life. i equated it with seeing the same shrink for 10 years and then not doing talk therapy at all. typing into boxes is therapy for a lot of people in our modern age. i don\'t think most of them actually convince themselves that what they have to say is so fucking great. then again, <a href=\"http://www.cewebrity.net\">some do</a>.',0,732,0,0,'216.142.229.132','en'),(21837,3790,'2005-01-27 22:43:34',74,'jo','tyler, that\'s not really the only reason why you\'re breaking up with her, right? she\'s also a bad lay or she collects precious moments figurines, right? cause, tyler, lame fucking reason.\r\n\r\n<p>oh man, if my boyfriend got a dog, i\'d be like, \"YAY! PUPPY!\" and after anastasia sprayed everything in teh house in anger and after meatwad tried to ride the dog like a horse, i\'d probably see if i could get all three cats <em>and</em> the dog in the bed with me and matt.',0,474,0,0,'216.142.229.132','en'),(21838,3790,'2005-01-27 23:47:57',75,'tyler','i wonder if that disorder extends to other arts as well? i\'ve always thought there was something wrong with mimes. ',1,116,0,0,'66.32.141.167','en'),(21839,3790,'2005-01-28 02:03:47',17,'austin','i wasn\'t suggesting that.  i was suggesting he has a strain of hypergraphia.  no, definately not profound, more like embarrassing.  watching that video, i felt bad for him, his friends, and his family.  i felt bad for the internet as a whole.   that day should go down in history as the internet\'s saddest day.  the day the internet hit rock bottom.',1,351,74,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(21840,3788,'2005-01-28 17:51:05',66,'Peter','<br><tt>so now that youve thought all this out... whats next? not so much changing the world as <i>stopping the cart</i> and having a bite or three of the <i>carrot</i>? how do you think one might do this? im curious!</tt>',1,222,0,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21841,3790,'2005-01-28 17:55:10',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yeah, i dont think it was the video that we were alluding to so much as the years of alleged compulsion that led up to it... that was but one catharsis at the end of a *very* long string of hypertext.</tt>',1,213,74,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21842,3790,'2005-01-28 19:45:33',239,'Poonlarp','youve become less critical but are dumping someone cuz they got a dog? if thats \"less critical\", what were you like before?',1,125,75,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21843,3791,'2005-01-28 23:27:07',175,'Paul G','i would write that i envy your command of the language - but the words give the feeling that they grew into their right shape, without any commanding involved...',1,161,0,0,'64.107.88.156','en'),(21844,3790,'2005-01-29 05:31:31',75,'tyler','i guess i was an even bigger asshole, poon.',1,43,0,0,'66.32.105.179','en'),(21845,3791,'2005-01-29 06:45:25',604,'jdfalk','<p>Thanks...you\'re right, this one did appear almost exactly as you see it.  Much of what I write -- especially the poetry -- arrives as just a few lines and a feeling, and then I scramble to find some paper or a keyboard before the spark dissipates.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When I was younger I\'d often try to cram my thoughts into a specific meter, often with an absurdly complicated multi-layered rhyme structure.  This is the first poem where I\'ve had a clear rhythm in quite some time, though.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>As usual, I wish I knew where it came from -- and how to get back there.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>(And in this case, I also wish I knew who I was talking about.  Heh.)</p>\r\n',1,656,175,0,'64.81.66.81','en'),(21846,3790,'2005-01-29 06:52:20',604,'jdfalk','hm.  clearly I missed something.  whatever it was, I hope my earlier comment wasn\'t inappropriate.',2,99,0,0,'64.81.66.81','en'),(21847,3791,'2005-01-29 08:09:42',195,'matt','just make yourself available.<BR>if you wait long enough, it\'ll come along and bop you upside the head.<BR><BR>but you have to have the willpower to sit there long enough.<BR><BR>i guess it boils down to willpower.',1,215,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21848,3225,'2005-01-29 08:57:59',195,'matt','if you had just a minute to breathe, and they granted you one final wish, would you ask for something like another chance?  or something similar to this?  don\'t worry too much - it\'ll happen to you as sure as your sorrows are joys.<BR><BR>if i gave you everything that i owned and asked for nothing in return, would you do the same for me as i would for you?  or take me for a ride and strip me of everything including my pride?  but spirit is something that no one destroys.',1,477,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21849,3225,'2005-01-29 09:02:22',195,'matt','and the thing that you\'re hearing is only the sound of the low spark of high-heeled',1,84,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21850,3225,'2005-01-29 09:02:46',195,'matt','boys',1,4,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21851,3225,'2005-01-29 20:10:22',82,'simon','matt. you\'re great.',0,20,0,0,'24.215.233.23','en'),(21852,3704,'2005-01-30 01:32:04',625,'panic','woof.',1,5,17,0,'24.7.89.143','en'),(21853,3175,'2005-01-30 10:18:02',641,'walid','whenevr i look at the clock during the day, its always 10:10 for example, or 10:01 or 03:33 or whatever is the same number or two numbers.\r\nits making me crazy\r\ni want to know why?',0,287,0,0,'212.36.213.23','en'),(21854,3175,'2005-01-31 01:29:15',195,'matt','this is the first of two articles about 11:11.',1,46,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21855,3175,'2005-01-31 01:32:45',195,'matt','we watched <B><I>lost in translation</I></B>again (for me) last night and noticed that when bill murray\'s in bed, can\'t sleep, and looks at the clock, it says 4:20.<BR><BR>that\'s very subtle.  but i guess only stoners would get it.  a little wink wink nudge nudge.',1,267,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21856,3792,'2005-01-31 03:24:58',82,'simon','someone talked dirty to me on the phone the other day. i have to say it was great.',0,82,0,0,'24.215.233.23','en'),(21857,3792,'2005-01-31 17:37:49',66,'Peter','<br><tt>at the drive in<br>\r\nin the old mans ford<br>\r\nbehind the bushes<br>\r\ntil im screamin for more, more, more<br>\r\ndown in the basement<br>\r\nlock the cellar door<br>\r\nand baby<br>\r\ntalk dirty to me<br></tt>',1,211,0,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21858,3792,'2005-01-31 18:18:10',195,'matt','this guy i went to school and played adolescent rock and roll with in various garages and barns was a huge poison fan during the time that they were popular.  when we were eightteen or so, i guess.  the rest of us were into u2, r.e.m., etc., but he liked the bubblegum metal.<BR><BR>he went on to play with a novelty band (i guess is how you would describe it) called the impotent sea snakes.  he got really messed up on vitamin c and quit and ran an escort service in tampa then got out of that and opened a bar in athens, which is where i ran into him right after i moved there.  he sold that one, opened another one, and last time i checked, it wasn\'t there anymore.  lost track of him.<BR><BR>he\'s a drummer, so that might account for his taste.  i don\'t know.  probably not.',1,782,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21859,3792,'2005-01-31 18:23:03',66,'Peter','<br><tt>was his club, in athens, the 11:11 club?</tt>',1,53,195,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21860,3792,'2005-01-31 18:58:53',195,'matt','no, the 11:11 club was from late 70s-early 80s.  couldn\'t find exact dates.  i did, however, find out that the 11:11 club was the site of r.e.m.\'s <A HREF=\"http://homepages.ihug.com.au/~darryl74/1980.html\">first public appearance</A> and their second show overall, the first being at a private party they threw in the deconsecrated church they lived in.  i didn\'t know that.<BR><BR>no, the bar sam (beau to everybody besides me) (and sambo to me when i was taking the piss) owned was called the velvet elvis.  the one he opened after that was called boba fett\'s (cause his name\'s beau, get it?).  i don\'t know if it\'s not in the phone book anymore because it went out of business (maybe he went back on the butter), or if maybe george lucas caught wind of it.  i haven\'t been up there since summer of \'98, so who knows?',1,830,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21861,3792,'2005-01-31 21:31:32',66,'Peter','<br><tt>heh. \"taking the piss\" is such a good euphemism.</tt>',1,63,195,0,'162.83.196.119','en'),(21862,3792,'2005-02-01 22:43:19',644,'zombieniki','haha tighty whitey.',3,19,0,0,'67.124.63.179','en'),(21863,3792,'2005-02-02 04:04:13',17,'austin','i\'m wearing them right now.',1,28,644,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21864,3795,'2005-02-02 04:07:10',17,'austin','c\'mon, you made that ancient saying up.',1,40,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21865,3175,'2005-02-02 04:11:02',17,'austin','you are just a kid until you start to biologically  recognize 12:34, 23:45,21:09, 5:43,4:32, 3:21, 09:10, ...',1,109,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21866,3797,'2005-02-02 04:11:57',82,'simon','heh. let\'s see. i would sometimes do my homework upstairs at mitchell bros. in la i think michelle\'s xxx is kinda amazing. i\'ve spent a lot of time at various north beach and market street strip joints. i recently made friends in the bathroom with a stripper at the pussycat lounge here in ny. my girlfriend used to work at showgirls on broadway. and my old roommate works at the century and i used to bring her lunch all the time. her stip name was gretchen. i played a stripper extra in a movie and debby harry told me a had nice tits. oh and once i threw up on the floor of the lusty lady. then fell in it. ew.',0,616,0,0,'24.215.185.200','en'),(21867,3788,'2005-02-02 05:44:03',175,'Paul G','<p>\r\nSome 25-50% of my peers - bright, creative and sensitive people - are being told that\r\nwhen they worry about these things they are suffering from depression.\r\nThen they are given psychotropic meds.\r\nAfter that, even if the questions don\'t disappear, the questions\' urgency does disappear.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFor me, it has become very personal: not only because I \"felt\" the pressures during the past 2 years in the taxi, \r\nbut because I have been questioned about my ethnicity at the polls, at work, at uni, and by police during a traffic stop.\r\nAnd now I seem to be \"frozen out\" of my grad program (run by Straussians.)\r\n<p>\r\nI. What I have chosen to do:\r\nA. Think clearly, and answer the question: What is the good life?\r\nB. Be honest about what is possible, given the current situation.\r\nC. Communicate with others.\r\nD. Refuse to repeat the lie, or act in any way that stifles freedom.\r\nE. Remember: existence is finite, and imperfect.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI am not comforted by the myth that at some point in the future there will be a period of super-abundance\r\nas a result of scientific advances.  I do not believe that the lies being told are \"for my own good.\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nII. Population - Technology:\r\nA. Leading causes of death and disease in the world:\r\n1. lack of: clean water; healthy food; minimal care during pregnancy/chilbirth; and sanitation; \r\n2. presence of: war/conflict.\r\nB. We possess the (primitive) technology necessary to solve those problems - but we don\'t.  Why?  Money?\r\nWe had 300 billion (and counting) dollars to spend on Iraq, killing ~100,000 civilians in the process.  Answer:\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIII. Population - Malthus:\r\nA. We already have too many people (more than needed to make the system work.)\r\nB. Surplus labor drives down wages and working conditions (viewed as useful.)\r\nC. The bulk of the population is good for: consuming goods?  fighting war? the sex industry? nothing?\r\nD. The people being treated as \"surplus\" tend to be non-European.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIV.  Natural differences do exist; but:\r\nA. Environment heightens natural (genetic) differences:\r\n1. food;\r\n2. parenting;\r\n3. education;\r\n4. drugs;\r\n5. stress;\r\netc.\r\n(everything in paragraph [9] above.)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt begins to seem to me that we are being habituated for slavery.\r\nHave you seen what kids are eating?\r\nHave you noticed that history, philosophy, literature, politics, etc, have almost disappeared from the public schools?\r\nHave you heard how many young people are being put on Zoloft/Prozac/etc?\r\nHave you observed the children being raised by strangers in day care centers?\r\nIs it to be believed that no one has considered the cumulative effects?\r\nThere is a growing conviction within one group that it ought to be served;\r\nand there is a growing expectation within another (larger) group that it will have no choice but to serve.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nV. The crypto-facists in line w/ Nietzsche suppose that:\r\nNo rational investigation of the whole is possible.\r\nRather, what we call philosophy is but an effort to supply arguments that \"seem\" rational\r\nin support of our prejudices.\r\nEverything is, ultimately, an assertion: a discharge of the will.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHuman relations are (in that model) characterized by the use of force and fraud in opposition to weakness and ignorance.\r\nThis precludes the possibility of community, democracy, and any argument against \"man\'s inhumanity to man\" be it death camps, or \r\nfootbinding - you can\'t say it\'s wrong.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAny definition of \"rightness\" that I accept has to allow for living things to develop their natural potentiality.\r\nI don\'t see much room for that anymore.\r\nI am resisting as best I can.  I am not going to play master or slave.\r\n</p>',0,3712,66,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(21868,3797,'2005-02-02 14:21:44',66,'Peter','<br><Tt>dude, sims. you fell in your own <A href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2061\">puke </a>in a titty bar. sweet.<br><br>and re: the original post... ive never been to a titty bar!</tt>',1,210,82,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21869,3175,'2005-02-02 14:23:56',66,'Peter','<br><tt>did you ever type 0.7734 into your calculator, and flip it over, to make it spell out \"hello\"?</tt>',1,109,17,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21870,2434,'2005-02-02 14:34:01',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this is an amusing entry, looking back.</tt>',1,52,0,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21871,3797,'2005-02-02 15:53:43',82,'simon','i know. i\'m awesome!',1,21,66,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(21872,3797,'2005-02-02 16:31:54',66,'Peter','<br><tt>dude, like, totes, man.</tt>',1,36,82,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21873,2061,'2005-02-02 17:02:43',195,'matt','i threw up in the back of a police car once.  the officer had been summoned because i had parked my car in somebody\'s back yard at three in the morning then passed out at the wheel.',1,182,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21874,3175,'2005-02-02 17:10:17',195,'matt','58008',1,5,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21875,3795,'2005-02-02 17:55:18',122,'hool','<p>i was paraphrasing a bit from the old testament, but the spirit of the message remains intact</p>',0,100,0,0,'69.195.39.4','en'),(21876,3795,'2005-02-02 17:58:10',66,'Peter','<br><tt>they totally had puppies in the old testament times. cute, cuddly mosaic puppies, yo.</tt>',1,98,122,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21877,3798,'2005-02-04 22:38:10',57,'weeb','yeah, i\'m like, totally rocking that vegetarian lifestyle. ',0,60,0,0,'66.92.219.22','en'),(21878,3798,'2005-02-05 00:40:45',75,'tyler','I think there is probably a \'god\' in some sense, for several reasons. One, all things considered, when I read about physics and all that, the question strikes me: wouldn\'t it be a whole lot easier for there simply not BE anything? Take the Big Bang for example; all matter, energy and <i>space</i> was compressed to the point where there was no volume. So, if all of space was a simple point with no radius, then how did the point itself exist? Where was IT? If there was no time, then when did it erupt?<p>\r\nYou can catch the Creator winking at you if you look. For example, there is an experiment in physics called the \'double-slit experiment.\' Place a lightbulb  into an enclosed box, at one end. in the middle, place a solid sheet of wood with two narrow, symmetrical slits running parallel to each other, from top to bottom. Close the box and turn on the light. On the opposite end of the box from the light, after the light passes through the slits, what is called an \'interference pattern\' will form; that is, instead of two slit-shaped streaks of light, there will be about eight streaks, with some brighter than others. The shows that light travels as a wave (bear with me), because some of the peaks and troughs from each slit cancel some of those from the other slit, just like sound waves in noise-cancelling Bose headphones. One slit, no interference pattern--just a solid treak of light shows up on the far side of the box. OK. But light also is made of particles called \'photons,\' which have a (tiny) radius and mass. Little ping-pong balls. So, imagine that you can lower the light bulb until one photon alone comes off of it once per second. You would think that then, it would go through either one slit or the other. It would have to, right? It\'s a real object with mass, it can\'t go through both at once. BUT, if you follow the photon strikes over time, THEY BUILD UP AN INTERFERENCE PATTERN. That is, even moving one at a time through the slits, they KNOW THAT THERE ARE TWO SLITS. <p>\r\nHere\'s the really mind-blowing part. All along, each photon is travelling through one or the other slit. But if you put a device which can record the passage of the photons through it over one of the slits--NO INTERFERENCE PATTERN. You get a picture of two slits against the back wall of the box. Because you know which one it went through, it only went through one, right? The photons know that you are looking, and to avoid a logical paradox, they decide to go straight through.     <p>\r\nI can hear him laughing even now. \r\n',1,2546,0,0,'66.32.65.230','en'),(21879,3798,'2005-02-05 20:03:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this piece says \"god\" in it 7 times. they say 7 is god\'s number.</tt>',1,80,0,0,'162.83.196.119','en'),(21880,3799,'2005-02-06 20:06:22',625,'panic','<p>While I\'m a fan of vast conspiracy theories, I think the truth is, and has always been, that violence, sex, drugs, etc, sells.  It was true for Shakespeare and it\'s certainly true for Ludacris all the same.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Bel Biv Devoe just isn\'t as compelling a theme as Tupac, for obvious reasons. It has always seemed to me that what\'s being portrayed is a means of success in a given system, drugs and violence are the tools.  Rapping about going to school and staying off the streets will never sell.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Is hip-hop advocating selling drugs and killing to get ahead?  It\'d be hard to argue that Shakespeare was advocating adultery and murder to get ahead in his day.  Perhaps once it\'s not quite so <i>hard</i> for poor black kids to choose the right path, people will have a less tainted perspective on hip-hop and rap, and it\'ll be seen as the art that it is.</p>',3,881,0,0,'24.7.89.143','en'),(21881,3799,'2005-02-06 22:33:38',66,'Peter','<br><tt>instead of getting really pissed off at alot of points this article trys to make, both the outdated ones and the just plain opinionated ones, and saying something snarky, ill just say that i agree with what _p said. i think most of us here will.<br><Br>also, i wanted to make the point that i for one see a profound difference between what they call \"rap\" and what they call \"hip-hop\" as genres. its easier to hate \"rap\", you know, the gangsta-ridden g shit they show on mtv and bet, that is all about the bling and lowrider hummers and gold-plated blunts and shit... than it is to hate \"hip-hop\", the elevated *artform* of rap where its more about the lyrics, the freshness, the beats and *knowlege* that the mc is bringing to the music game, as opposed to the \"street\" game and whatnot.<br><br>there is a big difference, and i think its a shame to overlook all the redeeming artistic value in a tremendously faceted genre just because the commercial side of it is ludicrously tacky and low on the redeeming-value scale.<br><br>\r\nthis exact argument would also apply well, with a few tweaks, to the death-metal genre as well as dancemusic, indie rock, etc.</tt> ',1,1186,0,0,'162.83.196.119','en'),(21882,3799,'2005-02-06 23:24:02',180,'auntie','panic made many good points. sex and violence do sell.  but so does a good story that doesn\'t depend on it for the entire plot line. <br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\npeter, are you a sagittarius? because i never seem able to explain what i mean to sag\'s.<br><br>\r\n\r\nhave you heard <i>hope</i> from the coach carter movie? that is what i would like to hear more of...<br><br>\r\n\r\nbecause most of what i see on the bet top 20 is not hopeful, it\'s hurtful, at least when listened to by the young and impressionable.<br><br>\r\n\r\n',3,507,0,0,'69.69.233.253','en'),(21883,3799,'2005-02-07 04:13:53',57,'weeb','I just watched \'pimps up, hos down\' and one thing that seemed relevant to this discussion was the monologue by one of the pimps (they all blur together now) and please, I am not lumping all pimps and rap stars together, but it seems like what ties it all together is culture. The culture of someone that grows up in the streets is not the culture that auntie grew up in, so it\'s going to take a lot of work for auntie to understand the why behind why someone would be a rapper, would want flashy cars, money, jewelry, etc-- I think it\'s related to the flash representing the dream of security, freedom, and most of all, STATUS to someone who grew up on the streets.\r\n\r\nIf you open up a new york times, all you see is mink coats and diamond jewelry. I see the same desire behind the pink  suit for the pimp and the pink mink for paris hilton. It doesn\'t even have to be a mink. Even KMart sells dreams.\r\n\r\nEvery culture has a story. What stories are we teaching our kids when we tell them to go to school to be \"successful\" and \"live a better life\"? I wonder, what does modern society teach our children instead of a blind rush for the good life? ',1,1155,0,0,'66.92.219.22','en'),(21884,3799,'2005-02-07 21:30:57',648,'veeeyloova','Auntie, I think you that should find, rent/buy and watch a documentary called \'Scratch\'. It may help you out a little. \r\n\r\n',1,125,0,0,'68.192.138.148','en'),(21885,3799,'2005-02-08 00:53:39',66,'Peter','<br><tt>no, im a cancer, go figure. and i love hip-hop. and i also love being highly opinionated about completely subjective shit like music; hi!<br><br>i dunno. good music is good music and bad is bad. you dont like rap. i dont like salsa. the next guy loves  all-american butt-rock or hates opera... and we could discuss the nuances of these tastes all night. music one of those topics- like politics and religion- that are sure to like totally polarize a thread. but its all good.<br><br>i could go on and on about the merits of many\'a mc and tune and record and talk all sorts of shit, but what would be the point? you could just tell me about all sorts of g-thang-stylee drama thats happened in the hiphop community, or on the source, or on allhiphop.com. it would be just as valid!<br><br>but anyway, \r\nthere is an mc named <i>MF DOOM</i> who makes nice rhymes that arent gratuitously gangsta. but thats all secondary to the point that he also wears a robot mask everytime he makes a public appearance.<br><br>this point alone makes him worth mentioning. every rapper isnt a thug.<br><Br>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.breakbeatscience.com/images/items/41380_tn.gif\"></tt>\r\n\r\n',1,1177,180,0,'162.83.196.119','en'),(21886,3798,'2005-02-08 01:29:11',175,'Paul G','we fought for laws to protect workers, end child and slave labor,\r\nand protect the environment, here.\r\nso, factories moved.\r\n<a href=\"http://www.digitaljournalist.org/issue0501/leong_23.html\">this</a>\r\nis a part of the cost of producing cheap consumer goods, and maximizing investor profit.\r\nno empathy?\r\n<p>\r\ni don\'t know.\r\ni am a grown man.\r\ni have seen bad things.\r\nbut the following pictures still make me cry.\r\nwhat did these <a href=\"http://dirckhalstead.org/issue0401/pjg12.html\">children</a>\r\ndo to deserve their fate?\r\nwho cares about them? \r\nwho takes responsibility for the cost of war?\r\nthe weak?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\neverything that we do affects every other living thing to a greater or lesser degree.\r\nthe choices count; they add up.\r\nthe vegetarians, the pacifists, everyone trying to cause as little suffering as possible, and use as little energy as possible: they count.\r\nthey preserve the potential of system.\r\nyou benefit from their actions - even if you don\'t know it, or acknowledge it.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe wage war now, and pay for it by cutting social programs.\r\nharm more; care less.\r\nunemployment + \"guest workers\"\r\nwhere do you suppose this is going?\r\nwhat makes us civilized?  our technology? or the way that we use it?\r\n</p>',3,1248,0,0,'192.203.136.254','en'),(21887,3799,'2005-02-08 01:39:33',180,'auntie','peter, the problem is that i *do* like this music, i am just saddened by the message.<br><br>\r\n\r\nvee - i just added scratch to the top of my netflix queue, i will report back.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthanks to all for the interesting commentary.\r\n\r\n\r\n',3,238,0,0,'71.0.234.14','en'),(21888,3798,'2005-02-08 02:21:06',538,'arcy','Doesn\'t raising X dollars for surviving loved ones offer a relief to the person dying? Shit money in my life is more than precious, I work my ass to earn every cent. Ultimately didn\'t I offer my time then to that person? I\'ve held the hands of people before when all I really wanted to do was offer security, and love doesn\'t do that. Lets be honest \"good\" health care isn\'t cheap. But ya the praying thing is bullshit. ',1,427,0,0,'24.43.240.222','en'),(21889,3799,'2005-02-08 03:09:51',82,'mf doom','By the way,\r\nI read up on bad dreams\r\nbag up screams in fiftys\r\nbe up on mad schemes\r\nthat heat shop like jiffy-pop\r\npop in a instant\r\nget smoked like winston\r\ncigarettes\r\nho\'s get ripped off like nicorette\r\n(patch)\r\nin real life\r\nthe real trife scene\r\nmight snatch ya life like a-ssault machine\r\nrifle\r\ndead up setup like bull-fight\r\nbe blunted how we like\r\ncouldnt white or in full flight\r\nthe unemotional\r\ncall me anti-social\r\non the run off the gun\r\ndeath tally commercial\r\ndeath valley?\r\nis like rehearsal to the streets\r\nto my peeps\r\nG.M.\r\nMF on the beat\r\nrhymes\r\nis chosen like the weapons of war\r\nso keep from steppin on my floor\r\nor delivery\r\nfront door\r\nI bring it to ya\'ll motherfuckers\r\nmaster yours\r\nmy disaster cause-\r\nhell-\r\nand gas drawls\r\n',0,758,66,0,'24.215.234.173','en'),(21890,3799,'2005-02-08 03:11:20',82,'simon','arg. paragraph tags. i rule.',0,28,0,0,'24.215.234.173','en'),(21891,3799,'2005-02-08 18:23:35',538,'arcy','Hmmm, I know what auntie is talking about and it has very little to do with musical credit. Don\'t take it to heart its not selling the lifestyle its selling the image and the image sells the ekco and the fubu and the seanjohn. Most kids are wise enough to know that livin on the streets only seems cool when mommy packs your lunch and makes your bed. ',2,352,0,0,'142.204.189.13','en'),(21892,3799,'2005-02-08 18:43:41',66,'Peter','<br><tt>uh oh! i listen to hiphop... and wear ecko... so does that mean my next stop is going to be living on the streets? oh noes!!1</tt>\r\n\r\n',1,142,538,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21893,3796,'2005-02-09 00:40:00',57,'weeb','series.',1,7,0,0,'208.218.54.65','en'),(21894,3796,'2005-02-09 02:42:13',175,'Paul G','the bath here is for the body, not the mind.  whatever i am feeling, the water amplifies.  when i am frustrated, or lonely, i will exercise.  i exercise a lot.  when my head is empty, and only the ache in the thews is left, then i will take the tub: hot, with salts, after 1/2 bottle of cheap wine - and a good cleaning per your description.  the water heater has been dying slowly over the past few months, so that now i need to use the stove and a pot to help it along.  [altered states]',1,489,0,0,'64.107.88.156','en'),(21895,3796,'2005-02-09 03:23:22',66,'Peter','<br><tt>dude. ive been waiting for you to activate this. i read it last week and loved it. total awesome swintry by the man himself. word up.</tt>',1,146,0,0,'162.83.196.119','en'),(21896,3796,'2005-02-09 03:37:40',175,'Paul G','i didn\'t know how to write this...but the article felt \"comforting.\"  maybe, like hearing about someone else getting a massage and relaxing vicariouosly.  mood: safe/calm/close.',1,180,66,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(21897,3803,'2005-02-10 07:52:59',17,'austin','<P>that bit about no family values, HA!</P>\r\n<P>there were 3 puppies trying their paws at climbing today.  i thought they looked like floppy sock girls.  they were all showing off their bodies.  and yet, i dont think thy had grown out of their family values yet.  i referred to them as the Skin Tights.  i had to wait for them to get by me so i could continue what i was working on.  for the most part, i just oggled like i was watching a sea tank of playful furry little seal pups trying to forge for food but looking silly.</P>',1,529,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21898,3803,'2005-02-10 16:00:57',82,'simon','like i always say, honesty is not always the best policy.',0,57,0,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(21899,3799,'2005-02-10 20:09:18',82,'publicenemy','<p>Number one, not born to run<br>\r\nAbout the gun...<br>\r\nI wasn\'t licensed to have one<br>\r\nThe minute they see me, fear me<br>\r\nI\'m the epitome - a public enemy<br>\r\nUsed, abused, without clues<br>\r\nI refused to blow a fuse<br>\r\nThey even had it on the news<br>\r\nDon\'t believe the hype...\r\n</p>\r\n',1,301,0,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(21900,3803,'2005-02-10 20:09:35',66,'Peter','<br><tt>no wait, honesty is the best lubricant. no wait, thats a monkeywrench. wait. im confused.</tt>',1,102,82,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21901,3799,'2005-02-10 20:11:04',82,'cam\'ron','\"Pops gone/ Shit tragical/Moms on mission/My house is where the addicts chill/I\'m like a teacher/I need me a sabbatical/It\'s not irrational/I grew up radical.\"',1,163,66,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(21902,3799,'2005-02-10 21:07:38',82,'hov\'','<p>So fuck Chuck Phillips and Bill O\'Reilly/<br>\r\nIf they try to stop hip-hop, we all gon\' rally!</p>',1,103,82,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(21903,3799,'2005-02-10 23:03:00',17,'Jeru The Damaja','<PRE>\r\nOne day about six \'o clock I\'m woke up\r\nBy the sound of my buzzer and a car or a truck\r\nScreechin\' off, so I jump up, scratch my nuts\r\nBut when I\'m like \"Who\'s that?\" nobody speaks up\r\nSo I go to the door there\'s a note it says:\r\n\"We have Hip-Hop hostage with guns to his throat,\r\nDo the right thing and we might let him go,\r\nBut if you call the police, that\'s all she wrote\r\nYou know what the motive is, it\'s all about dough\r\nAnd in case ya think we bullshittin\' here\'s the photo.\"\r\nI couldn\'t recognize the clows because they was all hooded down\r\nBut I peeped Foxy Brown sippin\' Cristal in the background\r\nWith fake alligator boots on\r\nAnd smack dab in the middle was hip-hop with a Versace suit on\r\nI immediately called Primo\r\nI said \"Hip-Hop is in trouble, meet me at my rest on the double\r\nDon\'t even jump in the shower, matta\'fact scratch my rest\r\nMeet me and D & D in an half an hour\r\nAnd bring all ya shit wit\' you cuz you know what we got to do.\"\r\nYo Afu! (Whassup?) Lets jet-son like Elroy\r\nIf I recall correctly I last saw hip-hop down at Bad Boy\r\nWe\'ll see if Puff knows whassup\r\nCuz he\'s the one gettin\' him drunk and fuckin\' his mind up\r\nWe go to the office, he\'s nowhere to be found\r\nSo we snatch up Jay Black and beat his bitch ass down\r\n\"Now where\'s Hip-Hop?!\" \"Aaight, aaight...\" he confessed:\r\n\"Suge came and took him from Puff last night,\r\nHe said he\'d give him up if a real nigga came to retrieve \'em...\"\r\nSo we went to L.A. later that evenin\'\r\nWhen we got there, everything was aaight\r\nAnd we brought Hip-Hop back home that night.\r\nONE DAY...\r\n</PRE>',1,1615,0,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(21904,3799,'2005-02-11 17:43:28',538,'arcy','Oh no, thats not what I meant. Let me try again...the only people that would be impressionable enough to take the contents of cookie cutter hip hop for a credible reflection of urban society would have to be kids looking for an escape from their typical teenage lives. Isn\'t that what we all got into music for in the first place? Or I could be wrong and the bootylicious girls ARE true embassadors of the urban lifestyle? ',1,424,66,0,'142.204.189.13','en'),(21905,3799,'2005-02-11 18:19:46',66,'Masta Ace','<br><Tt>\r\nM.A, I ride these tracks wit no pedals<br>\r\nand hold medals, since I was born i\'ve been so ghetto<br>\r\nI grew up in the projects called Howitt<br>\r\nand if you ran from a fight, you was a coward, and got devoured<br>\r\nSince then, ain’t much change as far as I go<br>\r\nAccept that, I don’t eat pork and I do Tae-bo<br>\r\nStill ghetto like old sneakers an new strings<br>\r\nor a little baby with a chain, and a few rings<br>\r\nI\'m ghetto like a Lucy Newport<br>\r\nI\'m ghetto like smokin\' weed, drivin\' through Oakland pumpin\' Too $hort<br>\r\nAlong with a few quarts, and a six-pack of St.Ides<br>\r\nTha same guyz that always get high, off what you bought<br>\r\nI\'m ghetto like turning your channels with a pair of pliers<br>\r\nAlotta cats claim that they ghetto, but they\'re liars<br>\r\nIt\'s plain to see, I\'m so ghetto as I claim to be<br>\r\nWho’s got as much ghetto game as me, Nobody. <br><br>\r\n\r\nEvery night on my block police question the thiefs<br>\r\nI\'m ghetto like finding a bottle, and catchin bees<br>\r\nI\'m ghetto like rockin do-rag in a new jag<br>\r\nwith NO license and NO registration, no hesitation<br>\r\nI\'m ghetto like 4 chicken wings and french fries<br>\r\nand still wanna freestyle on the bench with the guyz<br>\r\nI\'m ghetto like red kool-aid with mad sugar<br>\r\nI\'m ghetto like my 3 niggas Smurf,Poonchy and Boogar<br>\r\nI still go back on the block to see friends<br>\r\nand ghetto like a Benz with a spare, and 3 rims<br>\r\nI\'m ghetto like medicade, I went to school in the ghetto<br>\r\nand I still gotta  better grade<br>\r\nthere\'s a whole lotta cheddar made, people are so broke<br>\r\nI\'m ghetto like taking a shower with no soap<br>\r\nAn from the heart this the way it gotta be<br>\r\nYou can take me outta it, but cant take it outta me<br>\r\nHear me out<br><br>\r\n\r\nI\'m ghetto like a big blonde weave and some Lee press-ons<br>\r\nand Rucker park with high heels and a new dress on<br>\r\nI\'m ghetto like them chicks that hate kobe\'s white fiance<br>\r\nI wanna try to fight her, Brandy and Beyonce<br>\r\nI\'m ghetto like them bitches who boost minx and a griddle<br>\r\nYO! I\'m more ghetto than a crab end on merdole<br>\r\nI\'m ghetto like goin\' to the grammys in a Va\'lore<br>\r\ntry the dress, than the next day taking it back to the store<br>\r\nincluding the shoes you wore, with the bottom taped up<br>\r\nso you could dance all night without getting the bottom scraped up<br>\r\nI\'m ghetto like going food shopping at house slippers and pink rollers<br>\r\npushing 2 babies in 1 stroller<br>\r\nCan a rapper tell you he more ghetto than me<br>\r\nM.A theres nothing in the world that I would rather be<br>\r\nThan Ghetto<br>\r\nYeah! Soooo Ghetto<br><br>\r\n\r\nI\'m Ghetto like...10 carrot gold rings and chains<br>\r\nI\'m Ghetto like...Pitty-pat and space games<br>\r\nI\'m Ghetto like...Mail boxes with 5 names<br>\r\nI\'m Ghetto like...you<br>\r\nand I\'m Ghetto like...you<br>\r\nI\'m Ghetto like...rockin a skully in mid-summer<br>\r\nI\'m Ghetto like...8-track tapes of Stevie Wonder<br>\r\nI\'m Ghetto like...4 TVs up in the hummer<br>\r\nI\'m Ghetto like...you<br>\r\nand I\'m Ghetto like...you<br>\r\nIm Ghetto like... <br><br>\r\n\r\nYeah, M.A<br>\r\nsend this one to all my niggas from the BK<br>\r\nBrownsville, Howitt houses<br>\r\nYeah, Saukrates Saukrates<br>\r\nHa, all my niggas from the 718<br>\r\nfilthy rich, domingo<br>\r\nhuh Dj Rob<br>\r\nit dont stop. <br>\r\n\r\n</tt>',1,3359,82,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21906,3799,'2005-02-11 18:35:06',82,'nas','<p>It drops deep as it does in my breath<br>\r\nI never sleep, cause sleep is the cousin of death<br>\r\nBeyond the walls of intelligence, life is defined<br>\r\nI think of crime when I\'m in a New York state of mind</p>\r\n',1,216,66,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(21907,3803,'2005-02-12 16:23:27',67,'trouble','two guys named matt with two entries entitled \'wednesday afternoon\'.  spooky.  i liked your wednesday afternoon though and i agree, honesty and being nice gets you nowhere.  ',1,176,0,0,'198.111.161.114','en'),(21908,3803,'2005-02-12 19:13:21',195,'matt','i think i might write one with the same title next week.  or not.',1,65,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21909,3803,'2005-02-12 19:23:18',67,'trouble','if next wednesday turns out like the last then you should.  you could turn it into a series.  matt\'s wednesdays.  the slice-of-life stuff works.',1,145,195,0,'152.163.100.200','en'),(21910,3803,'2005-02-12 22:11:33',195,'matt','i tell you, if next wednesday turns out like this one, well, i don\'t know that i want any more material like that.  i was on the outs about it until just a few minutes ago when i convinced her to come over for dinner.<BR><BR>i guess i would welcome events like those depicted here if they didn\'t include the last part and the consequences.  i have to learn to be a little more circumspect in the future.',1,405,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21911,2396,'2005-02-12 22:37:58',195,'matt','all those details in the last paragraph - did you remember them or did you note them (write them down) at the time?  if i was writing an article like this at home, and i wanted to put in some details about what the cleaning crew might encounter, i would have a hard time remembering those items or imagining items of sufficient verisimilitude.  little things like that.  i can remember broad, general details, but that\'s it.  i\'m really good at remebering conversations and events, though.<BR><BR>bobby burgess.  it would nice if stuff like his could be published.  i don\'t know if it could be done.  i don\'t know if it would translate.  the form and gist of it.<BR><BR>he should write a novel.  i bet it would kick some ass.  probably the easiest kind of novel for him would be the one that borrows heavily from the author\'s life, the kind henry miller, jean genet, charles bukowski, a thousand others, used to write.',1,923,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21912,2396,'2005-02-13 16:24:24',67,'trouble','no, i was at work when i wrote it.  all i had to do was look around and guess what\'s going to happen when i leave based on the way it often looked when i came back the next morning.  as i remember it we had an extremely good cleaning staff.  details in writing has always been my strong suit.  things like structure, whether it\'s interesting, cohesiveness... those things not so much.\r\n\r\ni think bobby burgess could be published by just about anybody.  (test it out in one of mcsweenyes special issues maybe) his style is different but there is undeniable appeal and i see people getting hooked rather quickly.  \r\n\r\ni agree with you, his novel should borrow from his life.  i remember during the time that i wrote this he was making a warehouse job of his sound interesting.\r\n\r\nwhat\'s your lj again?',0,802,195,0,'152.163.100.200','en'),(21913,2396,'2005-02-13 17:43:33',195,'matt','radiocity',1,9,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21914,3804,'2005-02-13 21:21:14',17,'austin','<P>i go through similiar times.  when i was in school it was a problem because of all the deadlines.  test.  papers.  but now a days in the work force, i just roll with it.  it gives me an opportunity to flit from one task to another, finishing one bit at a time, usually never finishing any of them completely.  i don\'t care though.  no one knows that i waste the time.  i have fun wasting it.</P>',1,399,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21915,3804,'2005-02-13 22:08:21',650,'jo23','when i feel like this i tend to simply retreat. if i can\'t hold onto the thread of a conversation, i try not to have too many conversations. it doesn\'t work so well when you are working and need to make yourself appear to be following a strict code of logical thought, but if you are able to take a short leave of a few days, do so. rest and let this time simply pass through you. it will indeed pass. try to use any free time letting go...of whatever is occupying your mind. walk or ride your bike in no particular direction, write stream of consciousness thoughts down with a pen and paper and then throw it away, or do something totally without consequence. and then forget it.',2,682,0,0,'24.80.168.3','en'),(21916,3788,'2005-02-13 22:26:48',175,'Paul G','so, peter, i hung myself out and said what i had to say in my own name.  i read the <a href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=3790\">tyler piece and comments</a> that followed immediately.\r\n<p>\r\nwhere are you at with the u.s.a. right now?  do you suppose that it\'s naive to attempt to resist the current of \"progress,\" or to worry about \"being responsible\" for things?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni realize that when i try to communicate in a sincere manner i expose myself to ridicule.  i mean i\'ve noticed a change in my own lifetime: it wasn\'t always considered cool to pretend to be something or someone other than yourself.  things are different now - especially on-line.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndo you have ethical questions about the science you do - or science in general?  i know that a lot of what i\'ve written must be contrary to your beliefs.  i\'m not claiming to possess the secret of life.  i\'m just asking you to respond honestly.  i, honestly, wonder what the hell to do.  and i wonder what\'s coming next.\r\n</p>',0,1027,66,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(21917,3805,'2005-02-14 07:03:52',231,'alana','<p>i think this is beautiful. thank you for sharing this. \r\n<p>your words honor pandora.',2,88,0,0,'150.101.157.235','en'),(21918,3805,'2005-02-14 15:57:54',82,'simon','oh another song i forgot to add: some velvet morning.',0,53,0,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(21919,3788,'2005-02-14 16:05:36',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\"...i, honestly, wonder what the hell to do. and i wonder what\'s coming next...\"</i><br><Br>i couldnt have said it better. i guess thats why i didnt have much to say; i was just curious to see you expand some of your thoughts, which you did very well and to a satisfying degree. where am i with the usa? rather frustrated... but thats another story for another day. i dont think its naive to resist \"progress\" in many ways, and i do this to some degree, i think we all do. new generations and new perceptions of what \"progress\" is are generated every single day. im not necessarily  sure that \"progress\" = \"technology\", though. sometimes technology can be an excellent tool to resist waves of meaningless so-called \"progress\" or revision or brainwashing, or whatever it may reveal itself to be in the final analysis. and as far as \"being responsible\" goes, i think thats a task that any redeemable human should be up to! :)</tt>\r\n\r\n',1,958,175,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21920,3805,'2005-02-14 18:14:58',17,'austin','that pirate show was awesome.  it was the greatest battle of the bands i\'ve ever seen.',1,87,0,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(21921,3805,'2005-02-15 00:53:44',57,'weeb','aw sims.<br>\r\n:(((((((((((((((((\r\n',1,34,0,0,'66.92.219.22','en'),(21922,3805,'2005-02-15 01:28:00',175,'Paul G','<i>\"I can\'t make anything sound as good or bad or weird or sloppy or beautiful as it actually was.\"</i>  here\'s to the hope that the living of things will always be better than the writing of them.  thank you for sharing, s.',1,228,0,0,'131.156.159.153','en'),(21923,3788,'2005-02-15 02:13:32',175,'Paul G','thanks.  so much has happened so quickly: <a href=\"http://www.aclu.org/SafeandFree/SafeandFree.cfm?ID=17432&c=206\">feb 08</a>, <a href=\"http://news.com.com/House+approves+electronic+ID+cards/2100-1028_3-5571898.html?part=rss&tag=5568415\">feb 10</a>. ',0,254,66,0,'131.156.159.153','en'),(21924,3175,'2005-02-16 00:45:11',658,'novel11','My b-day happens to be 11/11/1971 which may me considered one up on gus at least in this eleven eleven matter.\r\n\r\nI was not really obsessed with the numeric significance until my 20\'s. interestingly there was one girl in my third grade class named Sabrina born the same day.  I used her name as a psuedonym when writing pieces for an underground \'zine in the early 90\'s.  About that same time I came across Solara\'s 11:11 book and had to buy a copy just for shit\'s & giggles.  I soon after came in contact with some TOPY folk who tipped me off to the number 23 meme that took hold immediately, and started getting a lot of 23\'s, 11\'s and combinations thereof.  Incidentally my sister was born on a 23rd and I lived on route 23 for the latter half of childhood. (here\'s a primer on the 23 thingy,  http://www.disinfo.com/archive/pages/dossier/id327/pg1/).  \r\n\r\nThe number 11 is considered the number of magick while 23 is associated with coincidence.  After many years being tuned-in to those numbers, I have come to feel that sightings should not be taken too seriously, or one might get obsessed by them but accept that you have been initiated in one of the strange mindtrips that inhabit percieved reality.\r\n\r\nIt\'s also interesting, at least to me, the date that alana first posted this topic.',4,1304,0,0,'68.80.241.232','en'),(21925,3175,'2005-02-16 01:43:11',195,'matt','gamblers are very adverse to 51.  inauspicious.  for the obvious reason, and, then, just because.',1,97,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21926,3806,'2005-02-17 16:59:15',66,'Peter','<br><tt>peen! is my new favorite word. also: does anyone actually ever read <i>paradise lost</i>?</tt>',1,102,0,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21927,3807,'2005-02-17 18:10:33',17,'austin','He doesnt think pirates or riots should be a problem?',1,53,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21928,3807,'2005-02-17 18:20:36',66,'Peter','<br><Tt>did you hear the one about the pirate who walked into a bar with a steering-wheel attached to his crotch...?</tt>',1,121,17,0,'130.219.136.217','en'),(21929,3807,'2005-02-17 18:45:43',195,'matt','ufo attack.  i should think ALL contracts would be void at that point, clauses be damned.<BR><BR>also:<BR><BR><B><I>force majeure</B></I>.  that was a pretty good album.  tangerine dream.',1,187,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21930,3807,'2005-02-17 19:44:40',82,'simon','ARRR, it\'s drivin me nuts!',0,27,0,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(21931,3806,'2005-02-17 19:46:34',82,'simon','my dad. he also has a copy of it. second edition. 1674!',1,55,0,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(21932,3805,'2005-02-17 20:08:49',195,'matt','<I>pandora</I>.  that\'s a beautiful name.  for me, it conjures up animated images, maybe a greek mythology sequence with lots of deep blue.<BR><BR>i didn\'t know about ram jam and unitard.  thanks for mentioning them.  found out about a song called <B><I>d.o.a.</B></I> by a band called bloodrock through a link from the ram jam page on amazon.  going to go look for it.  supposed to be good.<BR><BR>i\'m sorry you lost somebody that meant a lot to you.  maybe you\'ll meet up her with her again somewhere down the line.',1,521,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21933,3807,'2005-02-18 00:23:15',175,'Paul G','i thought that you were writing about large quantities of marijuana until i reached the third paragraph...',1,106,0,0,'64.107.88.156','en'),(21934,3572,'2005-02-18 00:33:11',195,'matt','<I>exactly where the penis goes. i didn\'t know i had what i had down there. seriously. pretty amazing.</I><BR><BR>that\'s funny.  that never occured to me, that women have a place they aren\'t fully aware of until they have sex.  \'cause men, from boys up, are always aware of theirs.  it\'s always there.  swingin\'.<BR><BR>every once in awhile, in the middle of it, i think, <I>this is how we\'ve always done this.  for as far back as we\'ve done it this way.  millions of years.  when we started, we didn\'t look anything like we do now.  but it\'s always been us.  and before we were doing it this way, that was us, too.  we have always been here before.</I><BR><BR>sex is close to the very middle of things.  right there at it.',1,733,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21935,3807,'2005-02-18 02:27:55',82,'simon','ha ha',0,5,0,0,'24.215.185.18','en'),(21936,2798,'2005-02-18 07:40:01',115,'lulu','get over it.',0,12,0,0,'69.233.105.41','en'),(21937,3807,'2005-02-18 16:08:20',66,'Peter','<br><Tt>im reading a book about \"<A href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/tg/detail/-/0380899094/qid=1108755191/sr=1-3/ref=sr_1_3/102-3970509-2072143?v=glance&s=books\">air america</a>\" right now, and this piece reminds me alot of a section of the book discussing how hard it was for the pilots to make the required bills of lading for the \"customer\", especially when they were illegally ferrying bombs/ammo/agents into laos for the cia, against the agreements of nato, the geneva convention, et al.</tt>',1,355,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(21938,3807,'2005-02-18 19:17:09',195,'matt','also a pretty good <A HREF=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/tg/detail/-/0394582616/ref=pd_sim_b_4/102-3470958-1164130?%5Fencoding=UTF8&v=glance\">novel</A>',1,158,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21939,3807,'2005-02-19 02:26:17',175,'Paul G','oh the truth of these matters appears more mad than madness itself, eh?  i assume the only reason that <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/gp/product/product-description/0767919343/ref=dp_proddesc_0/002-8526103-1094408?%5Fencoding=UTF8&n=283155\">this guy</a> wasn\'t sued for libel was that howard hughes really did bribe nixon.  now i will think of chia <a href=\"http://www.coasttocoastam.com/shows/2005/01/16.html\">hughes-like</a> \"<i>living naked in hotel rooms with the windows blacked out, shooting up codeine, growing his hair down to his waist, and storing his urine in mason jars,</i>\" doing the deals that shape the course of history.',1,644,0,0,'64.107.88.156','en'),(21940,3798,'2005-02-19 03:12:17',420,'thanatos','to peter.. yes i have thought a lot about the concept of why should it be as opposed to not be... it is easier to not BE than to BE... from an individual\'s existential point of view as well as in pondering the existence of the universe ... i don\'t know and i am not sure, if this is attributable to some for of god, or prime mover... the fact that our human existence has forever been plagued with this question makes my own minute existence interesting ... entertains my mind from a thinking perspective while i am alive ... re: the double slit interference and the dual property of light (it has wave-like properties and is a photon too) ... there are many paradoxes in physics, and other sciences ... is god playing and fooling around with us?  i also don\'t know ...\r\n\r\nwhat is our goal anyway as humans ... to fully understand and comprehend existence in all facets and levels?  i guess that will keep us going for a very very long time, and ensure survival ... the quest is infinite ... as we approach answers and solutions... more questions and mysteries unravel ... is god possibly hiding somewhere in dark matter_land?  \r\n\r\ni don\'t know, and if there is one, he must be grinning at us ... thinking how interesting these creatures who thinks they can figure him out.',2,1277,0,0,'68.164.174.192','en'),(21941,3798,'2005-02-19 03:23:34',420,'thanatos','to paulG and the rest ... i would not disagree with your points, esp paulG... my statements and expressions were meant to be symbolic, in exaggerated form, if you will, to prove my conviction vis a vis the hypocrisy of the catholic church and the blind catholics that follow without discerning, self-reflection... who repeat chants, the rosaries, the decades, like robots, without incorporting teachings into actions.  Just a background and context of the piece, in reaction to email conversation with a buddy, and a newsgroup thread i saw from high school friends.  the latter are people from upper class society of a third world nation; having said that, upper class in third world means the aristorcracy; they are the wives and children of landowners who exploit the poor in such countries, as you have depicted in the links ....\r\n\r\nMy piece was just a dramatic cry ... a note to myself on First_Wives\'_Clubs hypocrisy ... the concern, perhaps to a certain point includes a degree of sincerity, however, I have also perceived a strong degree of self-exaltation... the condescending satisfaction (possibly unconscious) to be able to offer charity, as they continue with their daily oligarchic lives ...\r\n\r\nMy point was simple, action is better than words ... I am one who will not hesitate to offer monetary gifts, in my own terms, on my own volition and free will; I will not be coerced by moral and ethical blackmail.\r\n\r\nthanks for all the comments...',2,1456,0,0,'68.164.174.192','en'),(21942,3807,'2005-02-19 18:05:38',195,'matt','that book reminds me of <A HREF=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clifford_Irving\">clifford irving</A>.  he lived in <A HREF=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2796\">san miguel de allende</A> during both the times i was there.  he\'s probably there still.<BR><BR>the second time i was there, i lived in a converted guesthouse next to margaret, a south african in her early sixties, a former model and park avenue resident living in reduced circumstances.  she was a raging alcoholic, and i was a fervent junkie, and during our more lucid moments, we\'d sit in her courtyard and drink cokes and talk and look out at the passing traffic on calle del reloj, the street of the clock.<BR><BR>she said there was a subset of the expatriate population that buzzed around irving, that thought he pissed eau de cologne.<BR><BR>she detested american literature.  she pressed <B><I><A HREF=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Magic_Mountain\">the magic mountain</A></B></I> on me.  said she read it on the ocean liner from cape town to new york city.  said it reminded her of san miguel.<BR><BR>i passed through there a year later and couldn\'t find her.',1,1156,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21943,2638,'2005-02-19 18:38:17',195,'matt','some choice phrases here:<BR><BR><I>it rinsed the accumulated crud of pain and illwill<BR>slur and slush<BR>clots and masses gurgled within to expel what was there<BR>daylight hour landmines<BR>silence like an empty auditorium<BR>like a jazz horn in an extended session signalling the beginning or end of something<BR>clear perception is just the removal of obstacles preventing pure light from reaching the destination eye<BR>a drumfill for another descent<BR>the peaceful womb of the ocean</I><BR><BR>nice details.  it creates a very soothing atmosphere, like what it must be like to be underwater for an extended period.<BR><BR>there are so many of you who write better than i do.<BR><BR>posting stuff on here has definitely improved my writing.',1,748,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21944,3808,'2005-02-20 23:17:30',93,'chiamattt','I don\'t think there is a drunk tank in korea. The cops just put cardboard in the back of the squad car and drive you home. \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\ndui is bad! shame on you!',0,163,0,0,'222.234.108.37','en'),(21945,3808,'2005-02-20 23:30:53',640,'ndg','who are you to pass judgement?',6,30,93,0,'24.7.89.143','en'),(21946,3808,'2005-02-20 23:51:29',17,'austin','that is the problem with getting a DUI, everyone can pass judgement on you.  all of us, even you, are in danger of cars when we are out and about.  chiamatt is a fucking drunk bastard, no doubt.',1,194,640,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21947,3808,'2005-02-21 00:10:00',640,'ndg','if there are universal truths out there, i don\'t think this is one of them.\r\n\r\nyes, i made a mistake.  yes, i was wrong. a terrible miscalculation.\r\n\r\nbut how do you justify passing judgement?  you have never had a beer and gotten in a car?  never gone out drinking and soaked it up with a big greasy 3am breakfast and driven home?\r\n\r\nnever driven tired, for that matter?  used your cellphone behind the wheel?\r\n\r\ndo you actually think that i went out, got blitzed, and said \"hey let\'s go for a joyride?\"',6,508,17,0,'24.7.89.143','en'),(21948,3808,'2005-02-21 01:18:16',17,'austin','i\'m not passing judgement.  i\'m just sayin.  i\'m quite often in a situation where i could get a DUI.  i was in one last night.  i wasn\'t blitzed by any means, but they probably would have hauled me in.  i was coming home from a dinner party.  <BR><BR>\r\nmy friend down here in boring land and i decided we were going to actively try to not become one of society\'s outcasts.  thus, there is ruffly a two beer limit whenever we hang out and one of us has to drive home.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nnow you have to be extra careful.  i\'m trying to be extra careful in advance.',1,561,640,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21949,3808,'2005-02-21 05:29:41',195,'matt','<U>somebody\'s</U> gonna pass judgment, though.  in the very near future.  and then you will be what they call a <U>passenger</U> for awhile.<BR><BR>and <U>maybe</U> you\'ll get a little community service.  and some <U>fines</U>.  and, if you\'re lucky, some <U>misdemeanor</U> <U>probation</U>, so you can go in and talk to the nice people every month.  and give them a little money.  and perhaps a little pee-pee, if you ask them real nice.<BR><BR>the theory is that if they make you miserable enough, you won\'t do it again.<BR><BR>i got a DUI when i was nineteen and proceeded to drive in blackouts pretty frequently for the next fourteen years.  i did it ALL THE TIME.<BR><BR>i drove from brooksville to gainesville in a blackout once.  (hour and a half trip)  i have one distinct memory from it:  losing control of the car and almost hitting a tree.  i came to a stop, drove back off the side of the road, and continued on my way.<BR><BR>i wrecked cars; i lost cars.  i should be dead a hundred times over.  it\'s entirely possible that i killed someone.  who knows?<BR><BR>but i\'m not saying that any of that applies to you.  but odds are you\'ll do it again, so just be careful.',1,1187,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21950,3808,'2005-02-21 07:31:14',57,'weeb','there\'s also the small matter of perhaps creating corpses on your way home. but it\'s not my place to point that out and perhaps rain on your completely selfish and wack ass parade. \r\n\r\nbest friendly!',1,201,0,0,'66.92.219.22','en'),(21951,3808,'2005-02-21 17:41:54',122,'hool','<p>if you were convicted of DUI, that means by definition you have been judged.  by a judge.  the judge acts on behalf of society.  on the issue of DUI, you are judgable, by anyone. </p>',0,186,0,0,'69.195.39.4','en'),(21952,3808,'2005-02-21 20:21:57',175,'Paul G','jail does have the same look and smell 24/7/365.\r\nglad you got out unscathed.\r\ninstitutions = bad.\r\nref \"choke the first bitch that opens this door.\"\r\ni remember a guy who repeated similar phrases, and beat on his cell door as he did it.\r\nit sounded like a canon firing.\r\nhe was 5\'11\"/1.8m, 275lbs/125kg, and he worked out with 400lbs/182kg on the bench.\r\nconvicted of his third felony, he was going away for life and just didn\'t care.\r\nsometimes, people like that get mixed in with the dui\'s in holding.\r\np.s. he was an anal rapist.  no humor there.',1,556,0,0,'64.107.88.156','en'),(21953,3809,'2005-02-22 05:31:18',17,'austin','i\'m cracking up.  what the hell.  i think i must be drunk and not reading right.  i thought that said you had an italian sausage wrapped to your calf because you have the flu.',1,176,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21954,3809,'2005-02-22 07:36:53',195,'matt','note on text:  i revised the part dealing with the flue remedy.  i had called the roll of bedclothes a sausage.  now it\'s compared only to a jelly roll.',1,153,0,0,'24.92.186.239','en'),(21955,3808,'2005-02-22 15:13:11',93,'chiamattt','when you get a drivers license you sign a contract. The contract stipulates that you will abide by the laws of the road. one such law is that you do not drink and drive. as you DID drink and drive...anyone...even those without a license but who uses roads can tell you you are wrong.\r\n<br>\r\ndrinking and driving is a selfish act and has nothing to do with driving tired or while on a cellphone because as of yet...those instances are not part of the contract you sign when getting a license to drive a car.\r\n<br>\r\nyes I am a drunk fuck. I am a drunk fuck smart enough not to own a car...and even smarter enough not to drive one while under the influence...i might put peoples emotions in harm...but I never put ther lives in harm.\r\n<br>\r\nalso. fuck you austin. thanks. \r\n<br>',1,775,640,0,'222.234.108.37','en'),(21956,1975,'2005-02-22 20:07:07',86,'egg','i just googled \"heba hobba\" and found this. whatever happened with this project people?',0,89,0,0,'67.121.73.136','en'),(21957,1975,'2005-02-23 03:02:33',66,'Peter','<br><tt>it probably went off to the happy hunting-ground in the sky, where all good web-ideas go when the lack of follow-up outweighs the impetus of the initial good idea. which is a real shame, cause boy do i ever have me a hankering to take that photo of myself hanging upside down on the subway during rush-hour...</tt>',1,322,86,0,'162.83.164.235','en'),(21958,3810,'2005-02-23 06:39:34',86,'egg','<br>\r\n&lt;austin&gt; you do talk like you know a lot<br>\r\n&lt;egg&gt; is it all an act<br>\r\n&lt;egg&gt; do i really know nothing<br>\r\n&lt;egg&gt; am i an old doddering fool<br>\r\n&lt;egg&gt; making homemade beer on the porch<br>',0,171,0,0,'24.239.145.130','en'),(21959,3808,'2005-02-23 06:43:33',86,'egg','hi nic! you were in jail?? sucks! come to new york, we don\'t drive here, we just get wasted and hope someone can read the address embroidered on our underwear.',0,160,0,0,'24.239.145.130','en'),(21960,3805,'2005-02-23 08:30:13',651,'sonic','i used to listen to her dj at the attic with john boyd.  she played great music and was friendly and always looked like a dream.',0,128,0,0,'69.233.105.41','en'),(21961,3810,'2005-02-23 16:00:02',66,'Peter','<br><tt>hi egg. you talk a good game, but you definitely dont look old.</tt>',1,76,86,0,'130.219.8.171','en'),(21962,3810,'2005-02-23 16:25:38',86,'egg','that seems like it is some stream of consciousness bullshit above but i was trying to paste a chat with austin and it came out all goobery.',0,139,66,0,'24.239.145.130','en'),(21963,3810,'2005-02-23 16:46:15',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i just fixed it. you can pay me back with homemade beer.</tt>',1,69,86,0,'130.219.8.171','en'),(21964,3810,'2005-02-23 17:05:51',91,'jay','i\'m already 29, as of three days ago, and it hurts a little.  i mean, this year has started out pretty promising, i have to say, and maybe it\'s true what they say about the 20s being a bunch of confusion and bullshit and in your 30s you get centered and come into your own.  but shit, man, what is this thing called time.',1,323,0,0,'69.226.199.117','en'),(21965,3810,'2005-02-23 21:58:11',175,'Paul G','\"but *before* you came here, where were you from?  you look so foreign.  i mean that in a good way, like, exotic.  hawaii?  brazil?  you people have such lovely eyes.\"\r\n<p>\r\nthe good news is that all really old people look alike.\r\n</p>',1,237,0,0,'192.203.136.254','en'),(21966,3810,'2005-02-23 22:17:56',83,'adamajah','<p>What\'s a mid-life crisis called when you\'re only twenty eight?</p>',0,71,0,0,'66.77.144.5','en'),(21967,3810,'2005-02-23 23:04:06',86,'egg','i think it\'s called \"30ish angst\"\r\ni don\'t think it\'s a crisis, it\'s just an added social bristling. it\'s not like i\'m going to buy a porsche. unless i had the money. if i had the money I would probably buy a boat.',0,222,83,0,'24.239.145.130','en'),(21968,3810,'2005-02-24 02:43:00',82,'simon','hm. i wonder if i know anything i didn\'t know when i was 18. maybe. i had something to say here but i forget what it was.',0,122,0,0,'64.134.126.249','en'),(21969,3805,'2005-02-24 02:54:08',82,'simon','<p>this is the pandora megamix i ended up making:</p>\r\n<p>\r\ngimme danger-the stooges<br>\r\ndesperate but not serious-adam ant<br>\r\ndancing barefoot-patti smith<br>\r\nrelease the bats-the birthday party<br>\r\nget down, make love-queen<br>\r\nblack betty-ram jam<br>\r\nrhiannon-fleetwood mac<br>\r\namerican wheeze-16 horsepower<br>\r\nseven and seven is-love<br>\r\njust like honey-the jesus and mary chain<br>\r\nrepo man-iggy pop<br>\r\njolene-dolly parton<br>\r\nfever-peggy lee<br>\r\nsome velvet morning-nancy sinatra and lee hazelwood<br>\r\nthe year to be hated-unitard<br>\r\nla la love you-pixies<br>\r\nnight of the vampire-roky erickson<br>\r\nwe gotta get out of this place-the animals<br>\r\npeace in the valley-johnny cash and the carter family.\r\n</p>',0,734,0,0,'64.134.126.249','en'),(21970,3810,'2005-02-24 22:39:36',83,'adamajah','<p>I think I\'m having a \"30-ish angst crisis\".  I hate how corporate my job has gotten, I want a \"divorce\" from my girlfriend, and I bought a motorcycle.</p>',3,162,86,0,'66.77.144.5','en'),(21971,3812,'2005-02-26 01:47:35',66,'Peter','<br><tt>dr. thomas\' passing represents the loosing of yet one more of the holds that was keeping american culture from slipping, evermore quickly, into the daunting oblivion.<br><Br>\r\nready or not, our generation is being presented the helm. its high time for some of us to step up, grip the wheel, and pilot this careening vessel into some vast, bright and horrific future.<br><br>\r\ni, for one, am very much glad that i appreciated the hell out of that dead man while he was around, and lately my prayers have been that whatever judging god weilds death\'s mighty wrath might have an appropriate measure of mercy on his soul; god knows he needs all the help he can get.</tt>',1,676,0,0,'69.112.172.228','en'),(21972,3812,'2005-02-26 01:50:42',66,'Peter','<br><tt>uh, *thompson. wtf.</tt>',1,32,0,1,'69.112.172.228','en'),(21973,3812,'2005-02-26 02:16:59',66,'Peter','<br><tt>uh, *thompson\'s. wtf.</tt>',1,35,66,0,'69.112.172.228','en'),(21974,3812,'2005-02-27 01:12:29',175,'Paul G','<p>\r\nlast night, quite by accident, i caught a reel of hst clips on charlie rose: 97, 98, and 03 interviews.\r\nit was the first time that i had heard his voice.\r\nhe not only looked, but also sounded like a farmer from wisconsin, kentucky or the texas hill country.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbut he moved his hands in a circle a lot, and talked about karma.\r\nyou stop and think: in his adult life, the defining \"clash of civilizations\" was between europe and asia, specifically between\r\nengland\'s colonial territories [uk, usa, au, nz, cn] and resource-rich lands of southeastern and southwestern asia, after the cold war with russia. \r\nrose pressed him, on more than one occassion, for info about his \"role models.\"\r\nhe did mention hemingway - but only because he claimed to have learned from him that it was possible to \"get away with\" being a writer,\r\neven though one was not born into privilege.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni understand that even as he was hated by people on the Right, there will be those on the Left who will slap him with\r\nthe label of misogynist, or racist, and write off his life and his work.\r\nfor a straight anglo-saxon guy who came into the world in 1937, in kentucky, i think that he had a pretty healthy respect\r\nfor the \"other.\"  it\'s too much to expect anyone to be perfect.  so please don\'t hate me for writing \"lard sack\" or \"tits,\" either.\r\n</p>',0,1364,0,0,'63.164.145.198','en'),(21975,3812,'2005-02-27 01:13:32',175,'Paul G','<img src=\"http://www.germz.org/img/art/jello_01.jpg\" width=\"200\" height=\"300\" border=\"1\">',1,97,66,0,'63.164.145.198','en'),(21976,3804,'2005-03-01 00:11:49',618,'skwooshi1','thanks guys.....transitional periods suck....being in limbo is cool until it drags on for months....my dad once said i\'m like a fairy in a bubble..nothing can really touch me and i just kinda float through life....here lately i\'ve been more like a fairy in a bubble trapped in a windstorm....no direction...purpose...no clue as to where i\'m going or what i\'m going to do with my life.;...you were right, it is getting easier...helps to stay off of the dope and get away from crackheads..and to just kind of float through things and deal with them as they come instead of trying to deal with things that may happen but aren\'t anywhere near by yet....like my mom says deal with the wolf at your door and take care of the other howlers when they get there :D',0,760,0,0,'209.181.27.232','en'),(21977,3813,'2005-03-01 02:33:27',496,'chemicalpilate','<p>I understand why you made the hair comment to those two guys, but my filter usually catches stuff like that.  Go figure.</p>',3,127,0,0,'137.131.205.145','en'),(21978,3815,'2005-03-02 15:32:59',66,'Peter','<br><tt>when i was in the 8th grade, a senior at my school decided to off himself with an AK one morning, right in front of the school cafeteria, which was right below the windows of the middleschool english classroom where I was taking a vocabulary test. when they found the body, he had six 25-round clips in his blazer pockets. makes you wonder why. though we couldnt see him do it, we did hear two 5-round bursts right after the start of first period; then quiet. our teacher sent  us home and gave everyone 100\'s on the vocabulary test. the community was scandalized, wondering where a highschool boarding student got an AK. they were even more scandalized when a month later, our highschool\'s state wrestling champ was pulled over in the \'jects on a cocaine bust... and cops found the invoice for the rifle under his seat.</tt>',1,836,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(21979,3815,'2005-03-02 15:34:22',66,'Peter','<br><tt>strange. i hadnt thought of that story in years. looking back, it seems really fucked up.</tt>',1,102,66,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(21980,3799,'2005-03-02 20:22:43',662,'Tru7h','Look, the truth on this subject is that this kind of music, be rap or hip-hop, is sending out the wrong message to the American public. Especialy to the American kids....\r\n\r\nThis music idolizes sex, drugs, and violence. It makes the American people, that listen to it, think that the only way to be \"cool\" or \"in\" is to smoke weed or wear their pants under their asses or even make them think about killing someone...... \r\n\r\nThis kind of a thing should be faced, and maybe even stoped.',0,489,0,0,'168.221.27.195','en'),(21981,3815,'2005-03-02 20:35:03',662,'Tru7h','it does seem fucked up man. did that actualy happen to u in 8th grade? because if it did, i feel very sorry for u and the other kids that were there that day and heard or even saw it happen.',0,190,0,0,'168.221.27.195','en'),(21982,3799,'2005-03-03 02:17:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\"... idolizes sex, drugs, and violence...\"</i><br><br>you mean like opera? take <i>il barbieri di siviglia</i> for example... i mean, rosina becoming a forced sex-slave of bartolo... before getting double-teamed by a very drunk almaviva and figaro? thats some fucked up shit, man. and how about rossini\'s <i>la italiana in algheri</i>? even though it was a \"comedy\", there was more beef between mustafa and isabella than biggie and pac. hell, this opera actually had <i>slaves</i> in it, yo, not to mention death and scandal.<br><br>so my point? i hope your comment was a joke, because to me, it looks like you have your overly-reactionary head too far up your as to know what the <i>truth</i> actually is on this subject.</tt>',1,743,662,0,'162.83.164.235','en'),(21983,3815,'2005-03-03 02:23:52',66,'Peter','<br><tt>sorry for me? shielding yourself- or your kids- doesn\'t protect you from anything except for delaying the cathartic adult realization that in real life, shit happens.<br><br>that guy offing himself didnt bother me at all compared to my morbid curiosity as to what he had in mind with the other 150 bullets he didn\'t have the chance to use.</tt>',1,354,662,0,'162.83.164.235','en'),(21984,3799,'2005-03-03 02:47:41',534,'griece','yeah i agree this should be stopped! ya got the number for the local censor\'s office? while were at it lets ban all media that contains references to  violence or sex, from \"the bible\" all the way up to \"2-live-crew\". then we would have no media left to worry about! hahahahaha. protect yer 1st amendment rights people, and dont let people like \"tru7h\" sweet talk you into thinking that censorship is a good thing for society. it aint. if yer worried bout america\'s kids, then be a fucking better parent and teach them enough taste to not care for 50 cent or whoever the artist/target of the day is. teach em whats up and trust them. do it yourself dont wait on the feds or the conservatives to do it for you. dont micromanage yer kids down to the degree that yer tellin em what they can and cant listen to. that sort of control is what leads to serial-killers. oh and just for the record, lemme just say FUCK THE MUTHAFUCKERS UP THEIR ASSES! WITH A SAWED OFF SHOTGUN! WHILE SMOKIN WEEEEEED!</tt>',5,1004,662,0,'162.83.164.235','en'),(21985,3799,'2005-03-03 03:53:11',17,'Ghostface','<PRE>\r\nIf you shoot \r\nyou ain\'t the real pretty tone\r\n</PRE>',4,61,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21986,3808,'2005-03-03 08:12:45',640,'ndg','<p>funny thing is, you worthless shitbag, i didn\'t sign a contract with you.                      i signed a contract with the state of california.  which means that the state                       of california gets to tell me that i\'m wrong, arrest me and punish me, which i\r\nassure you they\'re in the process of doing.</p>\r\n<p>it\'s a good thing that society developed things like laws, police to enforce them, judges, et al, else self-righteous fucks like you would still be getting your jollies throwing rocks at sinners like me.  i guess the internet\r\nis the next best thing these days.</p>',6,599,93,0,'24.7.89.143','en'),(21987,3808,'2005-03-03 14:48:01',66,'Peter','<br><tt>its not <i>the state of california</i> that gets violently killed when mowed over by a drunk driver. its people like chia and me and everyone else here. drunk-driving is like wife-beating. its an act thats pretty much universally despised, no matter how you rationalize it or make light of it.<br><br>also: <a href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/faq.phtml#howcome\">worthless shitbag</a>? is that really necessary? i mean, you come here and write about your DUI then get all defensive when people call you out about your grossly antisocial act. what exactly did you expect? a pat on the back?<br><Br><i>*best friendly*</i></tt>\r\n\r\n\r\n',1,644,640,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(21988,3815,'2005-03-03 15:52:22',587,'ackman','Ironically, both the AK and the M-16 (a full-auto version of the AR-15) we\'re both selected by the respective world powers\' military for (along with obvious logistical elements like weight, variety of ammo that you could put in it -gotta take what you can find in the field- cost, ruggedness, etc) their ability to wound rather than kill. Purely Machiavellian of course - takes one or two guys to help the wounded man; dead we leave in the field till things calm down a bit. There\'s an interesting (if creepy) documentary on the AK making the rounds on sundance and IFC (if\'n ya actually wanted to know more \'bout this stuff). One interesting tidbit - both of these weapons are frequently the choice of mass-murdering wacko\'s. 400 rounds aminute does a lot of damage. However, it is seldom used in drive-by\'s or by dealers (assuming they don\'t use their wares)- too loud, hard to conceal, unwieldly in conventional vehicles. Damn. Just lost the rest of this somehow. Oh, well. L.S.S., if you don\'t have one, it\'s dollars to donuts you\'ll ever hurt somebody with one... \'course that leaves the Louisville slugger,\r\nGeez, senseless and need to rest.  \r\n   \r\n \r\n',2,1171,0,0,'66.127.53.22','en'),(21989,3799,'2005-03-03 20:04:01',662,'Tru7h','people like me? people like arent thinking about the entertainment of this music. we are thinking about the message its putting out to the kids! and what do u mean about neededing parents to teach their kids about not caring about 50 cent? THAT DOES NOT MAKE A DIFFRENCE! the music is still sending out a very negative message! and u know what? the whole meadia is at risk here, and it is not the thing we are adressing. its the future OF our kids that is at risk! each day this world is becomeing more and more violent, and im not saying that it is all because of artist like G-unit and 50 cent, but the message they do contribute to it with the message they put out. THE MESSAGE THEY MOSTLY PUT OUT IS VIOLENCE IS GOOD! AND ITS NOT!!!',0,736,0,0,'168.221.27.195','en'),(21990,3799,'2005-03-03 20:05:01',662,'Tru7h','one more thing griece, ur the perfect example of what i dont want my kids to act like.',0,86,534,0,'168.221.27.195','en'),(21991,3815,'2005-03-03 20:08:02',662,'Tru7h','that is true, the thought of what he had in mind for the other 150 bullets does seem very scary. and to be honest, i didnt really think about that until you told me.',0,165,66,0,'168.221.27.195','en'),(21992,3815,'2005-03-04 03:16:08',587,'ackman','BTW, the comma after slugger shoulda been a period. As to the extra mag\'s, he probably didn\'t want anyone to try an\' stop him in his final act (of stupidity), and would -with great zeal, no doubt- send them out as \'point\' to scout the nothingness for him. Life can really suck and I actually succeeded -briefly- in my suicide. When going on seems worse than nonexistence...well, it\'s stupid. Yeah, sometimes there ain\'t much here in the universe and even that can suck, but to not stick around and see what happens next...just stupid. Understandable but stupid. Geez, where\'d that come from? Too wrapped up in my own silliness - time for another nap.  ',2,660,0,0,'67.119.15.140','en'),(21993,3799,'2005-03-04 05:52:38',175,'Paul G','I think what you are saying is that we should create an opera-themed gang: \r\n<li>beards\r\n<li>wigs\r\n<li>velvet\r\n<li>capes\r\n<li>swords\r\nI hope that you are willing to go all the way and beef-up and smoke to give your voice a husky richness.  I\'m in.\r\n',1,250,66,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(21994,3799,'2005-03-04 06:56:33',82,'simon','Dear \"Tru7h,\"<br>\r\nPlease learn to spell.<br>\r\nRegards,<br>\r\nYour friendly critic',0,83,662,0,'24.215.185.237','en'),(21995,3810,'2005-03-04 09:52:02',261,'sine','30ish angst only happens in your late twenties. when you turn 30 everything is somehow golden.\r\nso far my 30\'s feel really comfortable. except for the hangovers being far, far worse.',1,183,0,0,'162.83.164.235','en'),(21996,3810,'2005-03-04 15:08:42',67,'trouble','for me, 30-ish angst is more uncomfortable than any other age-specific angst.  i feel like i should have my shit together at this age so when i sit and think about how i *don\'t* my angst goes through the roof.  i should be \'mr. well-adjusted 30-something\' guy by now.\r\n<br><br>\r\nhyphen-filled comment!',1,304,261,0,'198.111.161.114','en'),(21997,3799,'2005-03-04 16:44:08',195,'matt','i don\'t care how bad the message is.  at this point, it\'s just boring.<BR><BR>although there are exceptions in the mainstream and the underground, the whole Zeitgeist has grown stale as hell, and i cannot wait for the next thing to come along.  should happen in the next couple of years.  hopefully.',1,301,0,0,'207.239.93.170','en'),(21998,3810,'2005-03-04 16:56:37',17,'austin','<P>i think those hang overs are because of who you married. <TT>=-)</TT> the gallon whiskey jug king of brooklyn.</P>',1,117,261,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(21999,3799,'2005-03-04 19:04:33',662,'Tru7h','wow, i did suck at typing in that meassage, did\'nt it?',0,55,82,0,'168.221.27.195','en'),(22000,3799,'2005-03-04 19:18:03',662,'Tru7h','i mean did\'nt I?**',0,19,662,0,'168.221.27.195','en'),(22001,3813,'2005-03-04 19:19:05',587,'ackman','Some days I find that the simple raw, grating,minutiae of normal everyday life clean and sober makes me wanna \'rub back\' at it all or worse - I fall back into my fearless-and-can-give-a-damn-what-happens mode \'cause I know YOU won\'t be leaving this conflict unscarred, no matter my outcome - one of the reasons I don\'t own handguns or large knives anymore. The world\'s already full o\' weapons of opportunity - a can of tomatoes swung in a plastic bag with a handle fer instance (thank you Mother Corps for the brainwashin\') ;^)\r\n\r\nI guess sometimes I just gotta make that kinda fight-or-flight juice pump \'cause my environment/circumstances/goals/life/etc no longer provides it. Or at least not at the thresholds I\'m used to. I always feel sorry or at the least, embarrassed when I do these but it\'s probably a fair bit better than REALLY cuttin\' loose, ya know? Anyways, you were honest in your opinion re: their respective coiffure, as well as accepting 9and agreeing) with them re: your own acci-doo, and got to burn a few oz.\'s of adrena-jet fuel in yer veins without fuckin\' up your life. I say well done! and man, I don\'t think I wouda been able to keep a straight face with a matched set of faux-mohawks in line behind me. P.S. - the cool thing about the foodstamp cards is the lack of stigma the stamps seemed to have carried.  ',2,1351,0,0,'67.122.208.161','en'),(22002,3810,'2005-03-04 19:30:05',66,'Peter','<br><tt>dont blame me, blame cneill ;)</tt>',1,43,17,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22003,3810,'2005-03-04 19:30:57',66,'Peter','<br><tt>...though winter in nyc is a great impetus for getting schlockered if i ever saw one.</tt>',1,98,17,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22004,3811,'2005-03-04 19:32:12',587,'ackman','Very nice, and it helped, too. I\'ve the wingchairs to salvage, literally years of mail and every day an unknown potential; although providence ain\'t exactly been smilin\' down at me, she hasn\'t left me completely ass-out, either. It\'s just these last minute saves that get me panicky like a damned kid. Anyways, thanks from me and appluase for you',2,351,0,0,'67.122.208.161','en'),(22005,3799,'2005-03-04 19:36:23',66,'Peter','<br><tt>the only thing worse than your spelling/online-affect defects is this <i>\"the future of our children is at risk because of TEH EVIL RAP MUSICK!1one!!1ELEVENTYTEN!!\"</i> argument. they said the same about elvis in the 50s. suck it. also: say hell to my bozo list.</tt>',1,277,662,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22006,3815,'2005-03-04 19:37:33',66,'Peter','<br><tt>its ok, im sure theres *plenty* you havent thought about.</tt>',1,70,662,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22007,3815,'2005-03-05 04:05:20',93,'chiamattt','I think the AK is manufactured in over 15 countries because the Soviets did not use copyrights or trademarks on anything. The simple design and cleaning of the gun makes it a very suitable weapon for an army that does not have a well oiled supply network. \r\n<br>\r\nI shot a lot of guns in Cambodia. The AK on semi is quite accurate, but on fully automatic, its crazy. I also noticed that the kick on a M-16 was a lot more powerful than the kick from an AK. Needless to say, something has to be said about a gun that has few modifications since it was designed in 1947. \r\n<br>\r\nI like firing guns, but hope to never own one or need one. \r\n',1,637,0,0,'221.146.67.20','en'),(22008,3815,'2005-03-05 20:33:46',175,'Paul G','all the gun stuff aside, you are still my favorite thing to read on swinney.org',1,79,0,0,'64.107.88.156','en'),(22009,3799,'2005-03-05 21:02:50',175,'Paul G','<p>\r\n(1) sex & violence sell<br>\r\n(2) rap sells because it contains sex and violence<br>\r\n(3) opera and shakespeare contain sex and violence<br>\r\n(4) opera and shakespeare sell?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\neither raps sells for reasons other than sex and violence, or opera and shakespeare do treat sex and violence differently than rap, and therefore sell less.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nstill, i\'d be willing to bet that at any performance of \"high art\" one would find the same percentage of criminals as one would find at any rap/hip-hop performance; they\'d be a different sort of criminal though.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nif you kill one person, you\'re a murderer; give that murderer the gun that he uses, knowing that he will kill with it, and your an accessory to murder.  kill thousands, create the weapons used to kill thousands, and then you\'re called patriotic.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\npower, context, and such, are at issue.  once you have the ability to put your work in a museum, on stage, institutionalize it, your claim to represent the other/outsider/disenfranchised/et al diminshes, i think.  as rap popularizes, becomes profitable, formulaic, the claims of many of its practioners weaken.  i thought that auntie & weeb made good points about the distinction between moral/spiritual progress and financial progress: it is possible for one\'e position to advance in one sense, and decline in another sense - simultaneously.  but i don\'t know much about music.\r\n</p>  ',1,1435,0,0,'64.107.88.156','en'),(22010,3815,'2005-03-06 02:20:08',17,'austin','<P>Thanks for the kind words, PG.  I\'m all-the-year waiting for you to come out of retirement.</P>',1,99,175,0,'4.43.160.212','en'),(22011,3816,'2005-03-09 16:55:43',57,'weeb','baja.',0,5,0,0,'66.92.219.22','en'),(22012,3816,'2005-03-09 17:08:21',82,'simon','shit, mang, that\'s fierce.',0,27,0,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(22013,3816,'2005-03-09 18:35:25',83,'adamajah','<p>My bike could fit in your bike\'s trunk.</p>',1,47,0,0,'66.77.144.5','en'),(22014,3799,'2005-03-09 19:30:21',662,'Tru7h','glad im on asshole\'s bozo list, makes me feel a shitload better that jackass\'s like him are on this site',0,106,66,0,'168.221.27.194','en'),(22015,3816,'2005-03-09 22:11:01',175,'Paul G','All I could think was: \"It\'s huge.\"  And then I found myself wondering about Austin\'s height and weight.  \"How large a man must he be for that thing to look natural between his legs?\" I wondered.  Maybe he used PS to make it look bigger than it really is...    ',1,267,0,0,'64.107.88.156','en'),(22016,3799,'2005-03-10 05:42:05',17,'austin','ah, hazing.  such a healthy pass time.',1,38,66,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(22017,3816,'2005-03-10 07:18:17',65,'le','this is awesome.\r\n\r\njpegs?!~',4,28,0,0,'64.81.58.18','en'),(22018,3534,'2005-03-11 02:54:48',666,'acanofspam','i don\'t think i can ever hate anyone as much as i hate him. i blame him for everything, i blame him for making my mom so unhappy and for making me push people away for just f-ing me up in general. i screw up every relationship i\'v ever had and i scare myself thinking about how i never want to be like him and watching myself for signs of it. i wake up everyday knowing somethings wrong with me and somethings missing. i just wishhe would go away just leave us all alone. physically he\'s here but emotionally he\'s gone. we treat each other like strangers and its not the words that stings its the silence. the fact that we live in the same house yetdon\'t even know each other. while i\'ve made an effort to talk to him so many times before he didn\'t care. i\'m tired of setting myself up for a fall. all i ever wanted was to live my life without him in it at all a constant reminder of my pain.',0,900,0,0,'64.12.117.13','en'),(22019,3810,'2005-03-12 20:03:59',604,'jdfalk','30 seems like such a silly place for things to change.\r\n\r\n(and yet, they have for me too.)\r\n',3,92,0,0,'64.81.66.81','en'),(22020,3817,'2005-03-12 20:12:49',604,'jdfalk','thank you.  I\'ll be thinking about this one all week.',2,54,0,0,'64.81.66.81','en'),(22021,3819,'2005-03-15 19:07:55',195,'matt','what\'s the tag for making those engraved-looking lines that separate the text?  those are pretty boss.  lots more styley than my asterisks.',1,140,0,0,'207.239.93.170','en'),(22022,3819,'2005-03-15 21:29:17',83,'adamajah','Yeah, continue it!',2,18,0,0,'66.77.144.5','en'),(22023,3819,'2005-03-16 03:28:02',17,'austin','the tag is bracket HR bracket\r\n<HR><HR>i think',1,46,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(22024,3819,'2005-03-17 02:00:02',175,'Paul G','<br>\r\ndear matt,<br>\r\nreading the following, in the following order, helped me:<br>\r\n(1)<a href=\"http://www.links.net/webpub/\">http://www.links.net/webpub/</a><br>\r\n(2)<a href=\"http://www.mcli.dist.maricopa.edu/tut/lessons.html\">http://www.mcli.dist.maricopa.edu/tut/lessons.html</a><br>\r\n(3)<a href=\"http://www.w3schools.com/\">http://www.w3schools.com/</a><br>\r\npaul.',1,374,0,0,'64.107.88.156','en'),(22025,3819,'2005-03-17 19:53:22',195,'matt','thanks, man.  i appreciate it.',1,30,0,0,'207.239.93.170','en'),(22026,3819,'2005-03-20 18:07:36',604,'jdfalk','Thanks, adamajah!  I will, eventually...just not sure what happens next yet.  *grin*\r\n',2,86,83,0,'64.81.66.81','en'),(22027,3820,'2005-03-20 18:21:30',604,'jdfalk','I can picture some of this, I think--<br>\r\nmaybe happening on the outskirts<br>\r\nnear the only small town I know.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nit is an interesting inversion,<br>\r\nbetween small towns and large, where<br>\r\nin a small town, you notice the stranger and don\'t know what to say, while<br>\r\nin a large town, you notice the familiar and try to make something up.',3,355,0,0,'64.81.66.81','en'),(22028,3816,'2005-03-22 21:53:54',17,'austin','<a href=\"http://redirx.com/?33sb\"><img src=\"http://swinney.org/art/galleries/austin/sites/MyNewBMW/MyNewBMW-Thumbnails/5.jpg\"></A>',1,134,0,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(22029,723,'2005-03-24 01:07:46',65,'le','tweakers still suck.',1,20,0,0,'204.253.162.9','en'),(22030,3720,'2005-03-24 03:11:58',175,'Paul G','<p>\r\n\"...he who seeks to deceive will always find someone who will allow himself to be deceived,\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"...he may appear to him who sees and hears him altogether merciful, faithful, humane, upright, and religious. There is nothing more necessary to appear to have than this last quality, inasmuch as men judge generally more by the eye than by the hand, because it belongs to everybody to see you, to few to come in touch with you. Every one sees what you appear to be, few really know what you are, and those few dare not oppose themselves to the opinion of the many,\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"One prince of the present time, whom it is not well to name, never preaches anything else but peace and good faith, and to both he is most hostile...\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<a href=\"http://www.constitution.org/mac/prince18.htm\">http://www.constitution.org/mac/prince18.htm</a>\r\n</p>',1,870,0,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(22031,3046,'2005-03-25 23:10:07',251,'Jamie','elegance? she was a right minger!',5,33,0,0,'172.213.114.166','en'),(22032,3205,'2005-03-25 23:11:55',251,'Jamie','if only it lasted',4,17,0,0,'172.213.114.166','en'),(22033,3001,'2005-03-25 23:28:00',251,'Jamie','additional: jamie and chris have two children. things pan out',1,61,0,0,'172.213.114.166','en'),(22034,2595,'2005-03-25 23:37:32',251,'Jamie','the wierd thing is, right now (2005) i have no memory of this whatsoever. strange, i think mayhaps i was just a bit mental at the time of writing.',4,146,0,0,'172.213.114.166','en'),(22035,3816,'2005-03-28 20:06:13',90,'leonard','NICE!',1,5,17,0,'67.118.80.121','en'),(22036,3824,'2005-03-29 16:22:13',66,'Peter','<br><tt>great read, matt. i always love seeing new installments.</tt>',1,69,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22037,3816,'2005-03-30 23:11:29',66,'Peter','<br><tt>swinney, i am so jealous of your awesome bikes. now i wanna trade our brick for a coupla bikes! race to work! *kaboom!*</tt>',1,132,17,0,'162.83.176.30','en'),(22038,3001,'2005-03-31 19:08:00',74,'jo','this kind of makes me want to revisit old swintries and update the status of things that hung in the air but after an ex FLIPPED that i had written such personal stuff on here, some about him, i erased all content from this site. and most personal content from my site, too.\r\n\r\n<p>this isn\'t why i love my current boyfriend but it\'s worth noting that he has been the most vocal in the last two years about wishing i would go back to the way i wrote before. and being frustrated that i self-censor so much now. ',3,512,0,0,'216.142.229.132','en'),(22039,3001,'2005-04-02 03:46:21',17,'austin','i could perhaps reactivate them.',1,32,74,0,'72.14.140.105','en'),(22040,3826,'2005-04-03 21:51:26',175,'Paul G','though i took something away from the piece, it seemed wrong for many days after to leave a comment.',1,100,0,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(22041,3640,'2005-04-03 21:59:09',496,'chemicalpilate','This is good on sunny Sunday afternoons.',1,40,0,0,'69.17.112.135','en'),(22042,3828,'2005-04-04 04:33:09',148,'jeeff','i tried anti-depressants about two years ago (zoloft specifically) and i was scared away immediately by the side-effects.  strangely enough, going to japan was a kind of anti-depressant to me.  i enjoyed your photos of tokyo.  i recognize a lot of the stickers.  my favourite bit of tokyo graffiti is <a href=\"http://www.modernhistory.org/photos/120104/images/11.jpg\">this one</a>.',0,383,0,0,'24.103.202.204','en'),(22043,3001,'2005-04-04 08:36:46',74,'jo','well but i actually deleted the contents. i saved the really important ones to my server and i can access most of the rest with the wayback machine if i really want to see them but it\'s fine. this is how i learned that i don\'t do well when my content is somewhere where i can\'t make swift and permanent changes.',2,314,17,0,'68.64.54.250','en'),(22044,3828,'2005-04-04 17:40:12',74,'jo','holy. shit. thanks for sharing this. i hope you\'re feeling a whole lot better, dave.',1,85,0,0,'216.142.229.132','en'),(22045,3828,'2005-04-05 02:47:34',175,'Paul G','i really like your writing.',1,27,0,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(22046,3829,'2005-04-07 14:01:11',93,'chiamattt','I really really liked your skater story. I think you did an ultra cool thing by giving them music. ULTRA COOL. I don\'t much like skating, but I download a lot of skate videos because I really like their soundtracks. I think a lot of \'skaters\' have an open mind, and you were just unfortunate enough to meet some that didn\'t. But anyway...it was awesome of you to make an effort and try and open their mind. I guess everyone needs a push every so often. respect!',1,465,0,0,'222.234.109.143','en'),(22047,3829,'2005-04-07 18:33:08',17,'austin','nice.',1,5,0,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(22048,3829,'2005-04-07 19:40:24',195,'matt','thanks, dude.  i try.',1,21,93,0,'207.239.93.170','en'),(22049,3829,'2005-04-07 19:52:09',195,'matt','i forgot to put in there that i was kind of halfway hoping that they would take off with the guitar, and one of them would carry it home, and it might be the beginning of something.<BR><BR>like when i saw chet atkins on <B><I>austin city limits</B></I>, and he was talking about some virtuoso brazilian player who supposedly got his start when he found a guitar in a forest.<BR><BR>it was probably pretty foolish to leave it there, but i like making these little forays into providence.<BR><BR>(mainly, what i was doing was showing that i trusted him, in the hope that he would trust me in return and, by extension, my taste.)',1,626,0,0,'207.239.93.170','en'),(22050,3829,'2005-04-09 05:19:21',122,'hool','i liked this a lot matt.  thanks.',0,33,0,0,'24.42.73.142','en'),(22051,3829,'2005-04-09 19:02:15',175,'Paul G','Where is the political content in music, now?  I have been wondering.  I saw it move from punk to rap, and then I lost track of it.  There is feminist neo-folk, and right-wing country music, but after that...I don\'t know.  Green Day and Ludacris are not MDC and NWA.  It is cool that you were able to connect, Matt...',1,318,0,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(22052,3832,'2005-04-09 21:44:52',261,'sine','greyest neighborhood around.',1,28,0,0,'162.84.161.162','en'),(22053,3832,'2005-04-11 02:26:45',604,'jdfalk','<p>Nice slice of life, there.  You\'ve inspired me to do the same with my street -- always people walking past -- except that I don\'t have a camera worth the attempt.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ah well, eventually.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>What does the sign on the pole say?  Not the no parking sign, the other one....</p>\r\n',2,294,0,0,'64.81.66.81','en'),(22054,3832,'2005-04-11 17:50:28',66,'Peter','<br><tt>thanks, jd. i think it would be really great to see what your neighborhood (oakland, yeah?) looks like, too...<br><br>the sign youre referring to, despite the coincidental illusion, is actually on the side of the building behind the pole. its a business-card-like placcard put there by a company that did a renovation on that building a while back...</tt>\r\n\r\n',1,367,604,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22055,3831,'2005-04-11 17:58:04',66,'Peter','<br><tt>maybe they just got a really good deal on chef boyardee?</tt>',1,69,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22056,3831,'2005-04-11 20:41:02',195,'matt','and ran out of space in the pantry?  yeah, i could see that.',1,60,66,0,'207.239.93.170','en'),(22057,3736,'2005-04-12 02:30:49',175,'Paul G','thinking of the rash of plastic surgery programming on cable, now...',1,68,0,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(22058,3829,'2005-04-12 02:34:13',175,'Paul G','...if there is a Matt.',0,22,0,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(22059,3828,'2005-04-12 02:36:38',175,'Paul G','also: they put my father on paxil a few months before he died.',0,62,0,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(22060,3711,'2005-04-12 02:42:31',175,'Paul G','<i>\"...but when you aren\'t willing to humbly assert yourself to work from the bottom up, you end up going no where.\"</i>\r\n<p>\r\nwork on the dock lifting boxes.  reward?  more boxes to lift.  the game has changed.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyou exist for a little while, in an imperfect state, and then you die.  the big decisions have already been made.  drink up.\r\n</p>',0,357,0,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(22061,3828,'2005-04-12 11:37:31',93,'chiamattt','I am sorry to hear about your father. Do you think paxil was at fault at all regarding his death?  ',1,99,175,0,'222.234.109.143','en'),(22062,3828,'2005-04-12 17:48:35',175,'Paul G','chia, i just don\'t know.  while they do test these things on animals, and then human subjects,\r\nyou know that it\'s impossible to predict exactly what they\'ll do in a particular person\'s body - \r\nwhen combined with numerous other drugs.\r\n<p>\r\nmy dad continued to smoke, drink, and take various other meds for heart disease and diabetes -\r\nwhen being put on an antidepressant.  it was definately a \"whole health picture\" that should\r\nhave been looked at.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso often it seems to be the case that people don\'t really want to be honest about the \r\nroot causes of things.  or, people want a quick fix that is relatively effortless.\r\nor, there is a pharm corp that is eager to market products - etc.  i think that you were\r\ndead on about that in your article.  and it was brave to come out with it.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthat is one of the reasons that i totally enjoyed your piece, and one of the reasons i support your effort.  i have had way too many\r\nfriends/family/lovers who have struggled with this crap, when, what, 20 years ago these\r\nproducts didn\'t even exist...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni haven\'t used any sort of drug since i was a teen - in large part because of\r\nthe damage i saw stuff causing.  yes i still drink, but given my family history i try to be\r\ncareful with that too.  true: changes in diet, exercise and thinking won\'t \"fix\" everyone.\r\nbut it does help.  it\'s gotten me to 37...haha.  good luck to you!  \r\n</p>',1,1430,93,0,'64.107.88.142','en'),(22063,3829,'2005-04-12 18:46:07',195,'matt','what do you mean, paul?  yeah, i\'m real.  i\'m a real person, walking around, etc.  please clarify.',1,100,175,0,'207.239.93.170','en'),(22064,3828,'2005-04-13 14:23:03',93,'chiamattt','thanks man. :)',1,14,175,0,'222.234.109.143','en'),(22065,3835,'2005-04-13 17:19:53',66,'Peter','<br><tt>these are the schoolgirls in question, I believe:<br><br><img src=\"http://www.citynoise.org/upload/1022174819schoolgirls.jpg\" border=\"1\"></tt>',1,154,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22066,3837,'2005-04-13 18:39:34',175,'Paul G','beauty.',1,7,0,0,'64.107.88.142','en'),(22067,3832,'2005-04-13 18:46:56',175,'Paul G','one ponders the rite of initiation waiting at 100, when austin and chia welcome you into their fold.\r\n<p>\r\np.s. is that you or a Peter-replicant in pic one?\r\n</p>',1,162,66,0,'64.107.88.142','en'),(22068,3834,'2005-04-13 19:00:35',122,'hool','<p>i\'ve heard of other cats peeing in outlets as well.  i think it has something to do with the cats natural inclination to pee into holes.  i wonder if a cat has ever self-urine-electrocuted itself? </p>',0,205,0,0,'24.42.73.142','en'),(22069,3829,'2005-04-13 20:46:58',195,'matt','well, i thought about it, and i decided that if there isn\'t a matt, then i\'m doing a damned good imitation.<BR><BR>as pathetic as it may sound, i derive a large measure of my self-worth from this site (and now, lately, from livejournal).  i wrote, for the last four years, for this audience.  if you all hadn\'t been here, i wouldn\'t have had anyone to show my stuff to, and, consequently, wouldn\'t have done it, and wouldn\'t have gotten better at it.  i think i\'m really close to being able to make a living doing my schtick, if i can discipline myself.<BR><BR>so i don\'t know if it makes a difference this late in the game.<BR><BR>take it for whatever you think it is.  i suspect that probably most everyone here quit caring about the issue years ago.',1,760,0,0,'207.239.93.170','en'),(22070,3837,'2005-04-14 12:12:57',251,'Jamie','very citynoise :-)',1,18,0,0,'213.249.154.99','en'),(22071,3834,'2005-04-14 21:44:54',175,'Paul G','in chicago we do have what i think is an urban myth about dudes getting zapped when they stream off train platforms into the highly-electrified \"third rail\" that powers the el trains.  i will not be the one to test it.\r\n<p>\r\n<i>\"cats natural inclination to pee into holes\"</i>  she is the most talkative, territorial, demanding, possessive and affectionate being that i have ever lived with.\r\n</p>',1,401,122,0,'64.107.88.142','en'),(22072,3839,'2005-04-22 15:09:23',66,'Peter','<br><tt>arrrr, matey!</tt>',1,26,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22073,3839,'2005-04-24 06:52:05',606,'critic','is the captain gay? i bet he is',0,31,0,0,'162.84.161.162','en'),(22074,3838,'2005-04-24 06:52:26',606,'critic','oh no you cant write.',0,21,0,0,'162.84.161.162','en'),(22075,3837,'2005-04-24 06:52:47',606,'critic','a buncha broken images',0,22,0,0,'162.84.161.162','en'),(22076,3837,'2005-04-24 13:53:50',93,'chiamattt','they work for me. \r\n',0,20,0,0,'222.234.109.143','en'),(22077,3839,'2005-04-25 09:21:36',251,'Jamie','my gaydar says no. he was a pretty cool guy.',2,44,0,0,'213.249.154.99','en'),(22078,3840,'2005-04-25 09:26:56',187,'kandrer','hi all! just to let you know how serious is my life these days :}.\r\n<br>\r\nplease tell me if you see any typo/mistake.<br>\r\nNotes: <i>Nudic</i> is a club in Osaka that will be closing in two weeks. \"Ki o tsukete\" means something like \"take care\". <i>Replay</i> is also the title of a book.',1,292,0,0,'222.14.214.48','en'),(22079,3834,'2005-04-25 09:41:19',187,'kandrer','thanks Paul, nice story :). A funny thing is I read by mistake \"fiction\" instead of \"non-fiction\" (long time I haven\'t come to Swinney, I forgot there is no \"fiction\" category either). So I read while thinking \"weird that this is a fiction, I would imagine Paul\'s cat is just like that...\"<br>\r\nI was walking up the street just today and saw one more japanese lady with a little dog wearing a ridiculous dog-coat (it\'s really warm now, BTW). Never a cat will let you put him/her in such a idiot coat. Never a dog will look at you in the eyes until *you* feel stupid. Dogbert never existed.',1,600,0,0,'222.14.214.48','en'),(22080,3840,'2005-04-25 17:34:07',17,'austin','i liked this allot!\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nhi and all is well.  do you have any photos online?',1,82,187,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(22081,3839,'2005-04-25 23:59:42',82,'simon','ha',0,2,0,0,'24.215.235.231','en'),(22082,3839,'2005-04-27 01:17:20',175,'Paul G','(1) an accident in my youth nearly severed my tongue.\r\nthe only medical treatment i received was a salt water rinse.\r\nthe tongue healed, but grew back larger than normal.\r\nyears of speech therapy in public school followed.\r\nat the end, the therapist suggested an operation to \"trim\" the tongue.\r\ni freaked.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n(2) languages other than english were spoken in my home.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n(3) to this day i edit my speech because i understand that\r\nmost of the people to whom i speak are too stupid to make sense of what i say.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nand so my voice is strange to some ears.\r\nit took me a long time to figure out that if not my speech, then\r\nsomething else would be mocked.\r\nthere is a certain type of personality that will seize upon whatever\r\nis different about you, in order to secure or advance their own\r\nstatus within a group.\r\nand so if you\'re one of those weak-willed and cowardly people you\'ll conceal your own \"imperfections\"\r\nas deeply as possible, conforming to whatever has been put forth\r\nas the ideal, always pointing: pointing away from your true self...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbut anyone with any real understanding has knowledge apart from appearances.\r\n</p>',3,1176,0,0,'64.107.88.142','en'),(22083,3840,'2005-04-27 01:30:24',175,'Paul G','i ate dinner with a group of people that i did not know.\r\nthe meal was prepared by a guy who looked like austin,\r\nbut spoke with a thick french accent.\r\nhe was trained as a chef in geneva, and working abroad.\r\ni thought of you, and wondered again if you were real.\r\n<p>\r\nthe french-austin made a gigantic steak.\r\ni haven\'t eaten red meat in years, but somehow it was \"ok\"\r\nbecause everyone was european - and drinking a lot of wine.\r\n[yes, what a shock: france/wine]\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni was very sick for three days following the dinner.\r\nbut i do not blame you, or austin.\r\nand now french-austin\'s visa has expired, so he is no longer a threat either.  mooo.\r\n</p>',1,663,187,0,'64.107.88.142','en'),(22084,3839,'2005-04-27 01:32:02',175,'Paul G','before you ask, yes: i have jpgs of the tongue.',1,47,82,0,'64.107.88.142','en'),(22085,3373,'2005-04-27 19:54:19',400,'elaine','this is very very funny. you are a funny geezer. you make me laugh',3,66,0,0,'62.56.37.175','en'),(22086,3528,'2005-04-27 20:01:34',400,'elaine','that is terrifying now, i have to get unhexed about this very quickly away from the computer in a lead lined room',0,113,0,0,'62.56.37.175','en'),(22087,3839,'2005-04-27 21:58:05',82,'simon','funny you should say that as i was just about to ask.',0,53,175,0,'64.2.77.3','en'),(22088,3840,'2005-04-28 07:53:33',187,'kandrer','a friend was recently in business trip Chicago, and emailed me he was dead bored in his hotel room watching TV at night. i emailed back with a link on your website and a comment saying i would be happy to be in Chicago so i can meet <i>this guy</i>. i suppose he did not try to contact you; maybe he did not believe you are real.<br>\r\n<p>\r\nbtw, never eat dinner with people you don\'t know. you end-up forcing yourself to eat things you would never eat otherwise, just to please them. after that, when you are done with vomiting, you might discover that they did not pay attention to how polite you were during the dinner; instead, they focused on subtle expressions on your face, like how you moved the corner of your mouth when that girl said she\'d like to <i>try whale meat</i>.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthank you for passing by when i pass by, Paul! ;) always a pleasure to read from you.\r\n</p>',1,884,175,0,'220.214.6.81','en'),(22089,3841,'2005-05-03 09:49:35',400,'elaine','man made fibres will get burn holes. trackie bottoms are a social evil. i hope for your sake this is fiction',1,108,0,0,'62.56.43.30','en'),(22090,3731,'2005-05-03 10:12:21',400,'elaine','yes, it\'s like what people like S de Beauvoir were doing, making the text talk throught the dialogue and actions not conjecture from the author, it\'s nice and spare. i\'m not saying what i see',1,194,0,0,'62.56.43.30','en'),(22091,3664,'2005-05-03 10:27:09',400,'elaine','this made me really laugh. but then i am cruel. i dreamed i had really comfortable flip flops on last night',1,107,0,0,'62.56.43.30','en'),(22092,3664,'2005-05-03 13:55:52',66,'Peter','<br><tt>last night i took some benadryl for my allergies, which have been acting up lately... then later, sine and i did some vodka shots. needless to say, i slept dreamlessly, like a baby. waking up this morning was hard, though...</tt>',1,237,400,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22093,3841,'2005-05-03 13:56:27',66,'Peter','<br><tt>oi, oi bollock-chops!</tt>',1,34,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22094,3846,'2005-05-09 05:02:11',93,'chiamattt','That was awesome.',1,17,0,0,'221.146.67.25','en'),(22095,3846,'2005-05-10 07:22:30',370,'seanthecon','incredible.',6,11,0,0,'24.85.230.64','en'),(22096,3846,'2005-05-10 10:08:52',400,'elaine','night on earth, on and on... nice',0,33,0,0,'62.56.49.29','en'),(22097,3289,'2005-05-11 16:03:27',400,'elaine','your mum does use a computer if; she uses most phones, most cars, banks, microwaves, new cookers, new fridges, any office equipment, most televisions, most travel ticket buying stations... am i just being pedantic, maybe, but there is a message hidden somewhere within!',1,269,0,0,'62.56.56.37','en'),(22098,3845,'2005-05-11 23:54:48',496,'chemicalpilate','Is this fiction or non-fiction?  Whichever, it\'s damn good.',1,60,0,0,'137.131.205.145','en'),(22099,3846,'2005-05-12 07:54:58',496,'chemicalpilate','I wonder what would happen to stories like these if there were different soundtracks playing over different visualizations of the narrative.',1,140,0,0,'69.17.112.117','en'),(22100,3846,'2005-05-13 14:09:23',17,'austin','never get out of the boat... absolutely god-damned right.  <i>Paul G</I> got out of the boat.',1,93,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(22101,3846,'2005-05-14 14:57:14',122,'hool','<p>loved it. thanks for writing it</p>',0,38,0,0,'69.194.14.87','en'),(22102,3845,'2005-05-16 08:32:23',251,'Jamie','it\'s fiction matey. glad you liked it.',1,39,496,0,'213.249.154.99','en'),(22103,3715,'2005-05-18 20:37:51',228,'belly','this last pic needs adding! and my proper name needs taking away and replacing with a less-real one. please?',0,108,0,0,'81.179.245.8','en'),(22104,1200,'2005-05-27 14:36:37',534,'griece','yep!',6,4,534,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22105,3715,'2005-05-27 14:37:23',534,'griece','that old e is some ballsy shite.',6,32,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22106,3845,'2005-05-27 14:37:45',534,'griece','great story',6,11,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22107,3845,'2005-05-31 00:31:25',0,'xep','more more!',0,10,0,0,'69.17.112.135','en'),(22108,3810,'2005-05-31 18:15:34',90,'leonard','You look really old.',1,20,86,0,'67.118.80.121','en'),(22109,2812,'2005-06-01 04:26:12',120,'joyrider','<p>shared joy is increased;  shared pain is lessened - thus, entropy is refuted.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>a guy named spider robinson said that once to the internet.</p>',0,154,376,0,'69.146.250.111','en'),(22110,3848,'2005-06-01 04:37:32',83,'adamajah','<p>That is the most hysterical thing I\'ve ever read.  Laughing out loud.  *best friendly*</p>',1,94,0,0,'66.77.144.5','en'),(22111,3850,'2005-06-02 15:22:53',0,'austin','if you looked out your window right now, you would see me going to get donuts.',1,78,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(22112,3848,'2005-06-03 07:12:18',0,'austin','awesome transitions.  i suggest some paragraph breaks in the future.  it is a great read though!',1,96,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(22113,3848,'2005-06-03 14:59:54',195,'matt','el misterioso griece',1,20,0,0,'207.239.93.170','en'),(22114,3850,'2005-06-08 01:29:26',175,'Paul G','thoughts:\r\n<li>tobacco and coffee are the writer\'s vices.\r\nyou can drink a bottle of wine and then do the things\r\nthat you\'ll write about.  but you can\'t drink a bottle of\r\nwine and then start to write - well.\r\nthere is something about being chained to a particular\r\nspace/fate that goes with tobacco.\r\ni swear to god tobacco helps you remain calm,\r\nand focus.\r\n<li>why cigarettes?  why <a href=\"http://www.heavenlysmoke.com/html/dunhill_cigarette.html\">dunhill\'s</a>?  i have tried to start smoking several times - but failed. \r\n<br>\r\n- pipes are an invitation for the uc-oxbridge crowd to \r\nknock you down, jump you in, and sew patches on the elbows of your coats.  pipe dorks, represent.\r\n<br>\r\n- cigarettes smell, wicked.  it\'s all cheap and dirty, like you\'re the type who\'d consider trading blowjobs for shots of hard liquor.\r\n<br>\r\n- but the cigar!  ahh.  the cigar says: \"i am freeing my inner-german in order to be able to better understand nietzsche.\"  and i may operate heavy machinery.\r\n<li>everyone 60+ gets a knife: \r\n\"happy birthday - here ya\' go,\"\r\nand they get to threaten/stab any 15-25 y/o males who are dicks.\r\nfair game.  no questions asked.',1,1176,0,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(22115,3846,'2005-06-08 01:41:43',175,'Paul G','but if, in the course of your journey upstream, you should happen upon a french widow who, having drawn a curtain about you, reminds you not to question fate, then smoke.',1,170,17,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(22116,3846,'2005-06-08 05:57:58',17,'austin','damng,\r\n\r\nnow i have to put that on.\r\n\r\nshizzle\r\n',6,49,175,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(22117,3846,'2005-06-08 23:32:20',195,'matt','there are two of you, swinney.  don\'t you see?  one that kills and one that loves.',1,83,0,0,'207.239.93.170','en'),(22118,3640,'2005-06-09 18:44:23',66,'Peter','<br><tt><b>RIP Charles</b><br><br>charles passed away early this morning, from old age. he was a happy ham, and a good pet; we will miss him. incidentally, when i wrote this entry, he was very old already, and that was almost a year ago. he had a very long life, for an adventurous ham!</tt>',1,291,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22119,3853,'2005-06-13 14:49:47',0,'simon','matt. thank god. you\'re back.',0,30,0,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(22120,3853,'2005-06-13 16:56:40',0,'Peter','<br><tt>a fun something to read about, too. its nice to see some fresh stuff, matt. thanks.</tt>',1,96,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22121,2333,'2005-06-14 20:58:00',175,'Paul G','On Mother\'s Day, I live in the present.  On Father\'s Day, I live in the past.\r\n<p>\r\nThis story was good to read again, this week.  Roots.  History.  Legacy.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt\'s \"sponge fishing\" that makes me smile, everytime.  Under the sea...under the sea...\r\n</p>',1,267,0,0,'64.107.88.142','en'),(22122,3175,'2005-06-15 22:05:15',679,'dom','wow am i glad i found this thread !  Ive had the 11:11 thing since 1998 and it gets me at least once a month - i used to avoid the digital clock around that time but now im kind of embracing it ! It is a special number even in the world of numerology which can be dubious - there are whole websites devoted to eleven eleven and i have even met independently 3 eleven eleveners in real life as well !  if you mention it to someone you can pass it on like a virus as well\r\n ps the armistice for WW1 was signed at 11:11 on 11/11 ',1,526,0,0,'82.32.44.150','en'),(22123,3855,'2005-06-18 14:40:21',0,'simon','i could use some water right about now',0,38,0,0,'24.193.161.121','en'),(22124,3855,'2005-06-18 16:25:51',587,'ackman','Good to have to go to. The ocean simplifies things for me. Sometimes it simplifies me down to being just achilles and the pacific - all else currently irrelevent. Freedom from the tornado of my thouhgts. Although I have only the most limited access now, just reading your post helps me imagine that I am now creating and expanding a perimiter of peace, complete with the white noise of waves crashing, accent of gull calls. The occassional kite flits by winding and whipping it\'s brief but wildly colorful tail. \r\n\r\nfrom exile,\r\nthank you, matt\r\n\r\n(my apologies if I missed an obvious point; comes w/being self-obsessed and dense.\r\nAchilles',2,641,0,0,'67.119.13.49','en'),(22125,3855,'2005-06-18 17:18:37',17,'austin','library writing group?  i can never join stuff like that. it sounds like a growth experience, but i don\'t enjoy the company of strangers on more than a 2 people i know to 1 stranger ratio.',1,189,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(22126,3855,'2005-06-18 20:51:31',195,'matt','writers group ain\'t ready for me, anyway.',1,42,0,0,'64.128.138.54','en'),(22127,3087,'2005-06-19 05:02:47',0,'simon','wow, dude. this is cute.',1,24,82,0,'24.193.39.22','en'),(22128,3854,'2005-06-19 19:44:44',175,'Paul G','mystery, indeed.  while reading stories of this sort i like to settle into one character or another, and \"view\" the events from their perspective.\r\n<p>\r\ni do not know \"who to be\" here.  austin?  bouncer?  young asian woman?  the moral ambiguity of the events makes each perspective problematic.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndid the austin deserve a beat-down, or was he a patsy?  did the bouncer do his job, or overreact?  mystery!\r\n</p>  ',1,426,0,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(22129,3855,'2005-06-20 14:59:23',0,'Peter','<br><Tt>i also am on my last pair of contacts. its gonna be glasses soon for me too, unless i act quickly.<br><Br>i saqw a dolphin at the beach when i was last in fla too. cool!</tt>',1,182,195,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22130,3854,'2005-06-20 16:11:45',0,'austin','oh my, am i being held accountable to my writing for once?',1,58,175,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(22131,3854,'2005-06-20 17:32:00',0,'chemicalpilate','Austin Swinney, scourge of San Diego...',1,39,0,0,'137.131.205.145','en'),(22132,3854,'2005-06-20 23:57:04',175,'Paul G','...it\'s a thick scottish fog of alcohol and lonliness.  i know i\'m seeing something in there, but i can\'t quite tell what\'s real.  \r\n<p>\r\ni guess that\'s what the bar is like.\r\n</p>',1,185,0,0,'64.107.88.142','en'),(22133,3856,'2005-06-23 17:36:17',17,'austin','<P>i know this person, be nice!</P>',1,35,0,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(22134,3856,'2005-06-23 20:13:31',195,'matt','why would dancers need to pass a background check?  that makes absolutely no sense to me.',1,89,0,0,'64.128.138.100','en'),(22135,3856,'2005-06-23 22:02:00',175,'Paul G','state & fed maintain a database of prints, images, names, numbers, charges, convictions, etc.  municipality or state issues professional licenses: substitute teacher, massage therapist, taxi driver, etc.  identifiers of applicant compared to database - in hope of avoiding the issuance of a license to someone with priors for soliciting, pimping, contributing to delinquency, etc.  \r\n<p>\r\nthe system works - to a degree.  but, sometimes offenses in youth effectively prohibit pursuit of a good life.  and, while some licenses seem fairly rewading financially, i.e., medical doctor, attorney, in most cases the \"real money\" is in the fringe action: drugs, prostitution, gambling, etc.  so, going in clean and staying clean are two different things.  that\'s a theme in the story, eh?  and the screening process does fail...as anyone who has had a bad experience with a doctor or a cop will know...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin my own experience, the dancers are universally well-behaved, and their customers are somewhere in the \"worst three\" category, reference tipping and attitude.\r\n</p>',1,1079,195,0,'64.107.88.142','en'),(22136,3735,'2005-06-23 22:36:11',228,'belly','i found him again :-)',0,21,0,0,'84.51.149.3','en'),(22137,3856,'2005-06-24 04:41:10',82,'simon','hm. i edit a dance magazine/write about ballet and modern and jazz/my sister is a ballet dancer/i\'ve dated many dancers/and strippers/many of my best friends are also strippers. it\'s a funny world to be involved in. especially since i\'m such a dudey dude and feel weird about dancing in public. tho i do dj at an R&B club and get paid to keep people dancing.',1,361,0,0,'64.2.77.3','en'),(22138,3856,'2005-06-24 17:27:27',17,'turandot','This piece is just begging for pictures.',1,40,0,0,'206.19.233.2','en'),(22139,3857,'2005-06-30 17:15:38',0,'xep','oontz oontz, baby',0,17,0,0,'69.17.112.129','en'),(22140,3854,'2005-07-04 19:50:27',0,'jdfalk','seems like a good opening scene for some tough, hard-boiled noir',3,64,0,0,'64.81.66.81','en'),(22141,3856,'2005-07-05 06:33:21',176,'Scapegoat','Interesting to hear about the whole process of getting set up. I know that things work a little differently in Canada, fees and rules differ from province to province.\r\n\r\nIn Ontario there often isn\'t any cover to get in but the club will charge a patron a cover if they enter the area for private dances. After that though usually the clubs let the dancers keep the full amount for the private dances.\r\n\r\nThanks for the insight. \r\n',0,432,0,0,'69.194.68.63','en'),(22142,3856,'2005-07-06 00:05:00',175,'Paul G','hi again!  whenever i hear <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dominion_of_Canada\">Dominion of Canada</a> i expect riding crops and thigh-high vinyl boots to follow.  \"vice of the english,\" indeed.\r\n<p>\r\ni am sorry for using i.e. instead of e.g., above.  i was educated in america - without the proper discipline.\r\n</p> ',1,326,176,0,'192.203.136.254','en'),(22143,3856,'2005-07-06 05:23:22',176,'Scapegoat','Riding crops and thigh-high vinyl boots,  oddly enough this isn\'t as big a costume with the dancers up here as one might think. \r\n\r\nIt is very rare that I ever here it referred to as The Dominion of Canada these days, unless I\'m visiting my parents whom live on the Loyalist parkway.',0,285,175,0,'69.194.68.63','en'),(22144,2959,'2005-07-06 17:57:00',400,'elaine','too much complexity to go into right now, right here, but suffice to say i feel for you. and recently because of a bipolar inability to deal with SSRIs i was briefly on an anti epilepsy drugm topiromate, which was good in the short term for me since it got me back into being able to sleep, which is always nice. but even at this age, or maybe particularly, being at the end of fertility i was super aware that there was no option to get pregnant while on it - and the psych wanted me to be on for 5 years. luckily, only being mental and not epileptic i had the option of saying bollocks to that as soon as i had got what i wanted from it. not that i am in a position to be having babies right now, but i hated the \'cage\' of having the option taken away, so, like i say, much sympathy',1,786,0,0,'62.56.59.222','en'),(22145,3859,'2005-07-06 22:20:52',195,'matt','this brought john wayne gacy to mind for some reason.',1,53,0,0,'64.128.138.76','en'),(22146,3859,'2005-07-08 07:42:18',695,'Olie','I...... Wow....I\'m typing in the dark right now so any typeo\'s I\'m sorry but wow. I had no idea that so many people love writing. I just found swinney bey typing up Olivia the Gothe on google and found this place. sorry u don\'t wonna ear how I got hear anyhow.\r\n\r\n\r\nU r an amazing writer, I\'m sure you\'ve heard that plenty\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n                    Good bye',1,366,0,0,'12.65.54.67','en'),(22147,3858,'2005-07-09 10:59:34',0,'le','i love the part about the loquats.\r\n\r\n<img src=\"http://www.sol.dti.ne.jp/~sasaki-y/a005/b010505-photo-loquat.jpg\">',4,116,0,0,'64.81.58.124','en'),(22148,3861,'2005-07-11 07:01:25',17,'austin','Haha.  1082 vacation pictures being sorted through now.',1,55,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(22149,3859,'2005-07-11 12:37:54',231,'alana','<p>thanks for offering this up.</p>\r\n',1,37,0,0,'59.167.66.53','en'),(22150,3861,'2005-07-11 16:53:49',0,'turandot','I think I\'ve seen this black winged nuisance too, mostly at taco shops, Nunu\'s, bathtubs, and Blockbuster.',2,108,0,0,'206.19.233.2','en'),(22151,3861,'2005-07-11 17:06:50',682,'turandot','and rock climbing gyms.',2,23,0,0,'206.19.233.2','en'),(22152,3850,'2005-07-13 19:25:20',82,'simon','i look out the window in my living room and see the train starting to go over the williamsburg bridge. i like the time when its starting to move toward twilight and the train\'s inside lights are on and yellow against a super blue sky.',0,235,0,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(22153,3861,'2005-07-13 21:38:57',154,'ryan','that is a photo of crater lake i believe',6,40,0,0,'206.228.191.196','en'),(22154,3861,'2005-07-13 22:13:34',175,'Paul G','Well, \"god damn,\" back at you.  You are right.  That is Crater Lake as it was in 1959, captured on Kodak film, by my ancestors.  I thought that I could pull a fast one, now that the entire Pac-NW had been unificated into the single state of War-megon.  Motto: Stumps (Our Trees, Your Limbs.)  But, no.',1,303,154,0,'64.107.88.142','en'),(22155,2829,'2005-07-14 01:00:50',82,'simon','right now i\'m listening to we\'ve gotta get out of this place.',0,63,195,0,'24.193.160.250','en'),(22156,2829,'2005-07-14 20:31:51',195,'matt','<B><I>shapes of things</B></I> is my favorite yardbirds song.  i don\'t think i like any songs by the animals.  dunno.  could be mistaken.  can\'t think of any at the moment, though.',1,182,0,0,'64.128.138.48','en'),(22157,2829,'2005-07-16 13:09:58',82,'simon','i like that song. today i like \'its my life\' by the animals. i got up really early but now i don\'t know what to do with myself. i think i\'ll go sit on the stoop.',0,165,0,0,'24.193.37.143','en'),(22158,2829,'2005-07-18 15:10:56',0,'Peter','<br><tt>doy, sims. being bored and sitting on the stoop practically screams <i>malt liquor</i>...</tt>',1,102,82,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22159,3862,'2005-07-19 04:07:24',695,'Olie','u and me both!',0,14,0,0,'12.65.66.34','en'),(22160,3864,'2005-07-20 18:39:00',587,'ackman','Good stuff! Ya know, I couldn\'t remember a single line of Lovecraft if my life depended on it (haven\'t read him in over thirty years) but I DO remember the general feel/vibe it left me with and this has it. It stokes all that curiosity, fear, morbid fascination, sympathetic anticipation of fright and all the other stuff that hookeed me into reading -and wanting to write- fiction in the first place (I came in by way of the horror, Hitchcock and sci-fi entrance). Very refreshing, for me. Again, good stuff! Keep it up. Achilles',2,532,0,0,'66.127.53.83','en'),(22161,3858,'2005-07-20 19:04:46',587,'ackman','I get the flavor of Bradbury from this. Nice. I very much liked where the lines broke, as well (I gotta learn how ta upload that kinda stuff). Thanks,Matt!',2,155,0,0,'66.127.53.83','en'),(22162,3173,'2005-07-21 04:54:08',587,'ackman','Ah, but the special talent of old fools like me lies not in how much we write but how little we ultimately say.  ;^) ',1,117,17,0,'67.119.2.236','en'),(22163,3699,'2005-07-22 10:36:45',295,'karenlee','the matt person is right about taking action againt the manufacturer; i guess you\'ve probably looked into that. \'course trouble with that sorta stuff is you\'ve gotta have the energy for it. i work with much younger people (i am 40) who can\'t even see without their lenses/glasses and i think anybody who lives every day like that, like you, is incredibly brave.',1,365,231,0,'220.237.225.35','en'),(22164,3866,'2005-07-23 00:45:45',0,'ackman','You ARE Batman. BTW, Bruce Wayne called; he wants to know when you\'ll be through with the car.',1,95,0,0,'67.119.3.97','en'),(22165,3866,'2005-07-23 07:04:10',587,'ackman','Wow. How\'d I do that?',0,22,0,0,'67.117.131.207','en'),(22166,3866,'2005-07-25 14:13:42',534,'griece','dont be silly [activates secret bat bozo list]',6,46,587,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22167,3867,'2005-07-25 20:40:33',0,'turandot','Love is not a battleship, it is a battlefield.',2,46,0,0,'206.19.233.2','en'),(22168,3867,'2005-07-26 06:32:18',420,'thanatos','thanks for the editorial .. what alcohol can do to one\'s typing skills !! lol ... ok am changing it .. that\'s wat i mean - battlefield ... hahaha! good catch!',1,160,0,0,'67.101.151.170','en'),(22169,3867,'2005-07-26 06:34:21',420,'thanatos','ps ... turandot .. THANKS!',1,26,0,0,'67.101.151.170','en'),(22170,3861,'2005-07-27 00:02:28',175,'Paul G','all shit aside, the pictures were inspirational.  \r\ni was not ready for such beauty.\r\nalso, you are a great photographer.',1,121,17,0,'192.203.136.254','en'),(22171,3861,'2005-07-27 00:28:27',0,'ryan','the free state of cascadia.  not sure if SF is included in this plan or not, but BC is for sure.  the free state of jefferson would be a state within a state.',0,158,0,0,'206.228.191.196','en'),(22172,3866,'2005-07-27 09:34:57',587,'ackman','Seriously. Someone was being physically assaulted by someone sttronger than they were (probably the only reason the asshole did attack - no fear of like response), and you did for them what they could not do for themselves at a time when they had no useful legal recourse (they can only arrest your assailant AFTER he\'s hit you), and did so not for fee, fame or bloodlust. That\'s what Batman does. Only, Batman is a comic book/movie. Easy to write high principles, courage, etc., into a character. MUCH harder to have the character to live the principles. Thus you ARE Batman. OOHRAH! (Ol\' Corps thang).',1,606,534,0,'66.127.53.77','en'),(22173,3863,'2005-07-28 00:05:42',175,'Paul G','too dark, too familiar, and an excellent piece of writing.  penetrating psychology.',1,83,0,0,'64.107.88.142','en'),(22174,3870,'2005-08-11 03:26:47',0,'simon','i still feel like a boy too.',0,28,0,0,'24.193.163.235','en'),(22175,3870,'2005-08-11 16:14:39',66,'Peter','<br><tt>that second to last paragraph is the story of my life lately. but instead of drinking, its something else. im not sure what, but it definitely comes from inside my head as opposed to a bottle. some people tell me its some vague <i>late-twenties urban ennui</i>. i think its some sort of heretofore latent mania.<br><br>but whatever. im no freud. next time youre in brooklyn, wanna kill a half gallon of beam with me?</tt>',1,429,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22176,3870,'2005-08-11 22:05:52',0,'austin','two big men, drinking lots, together.',1,37,0,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(22177,3869,'2005-08-14 01:24:18',195,'matt','you just need a little vitamin c.',1,33,0,0,'205.188.117.7','en'),(22178,3869,'2005-08-15 01:25:41',0,'simon','who doesnt!',0,11,0,0,'24.193.160.96','en'),(22179,3868,'2005-08-15 03:22:20',538,'arcy','Whoa! I\'m all for womanly things, but good gravy!!',1,51,0,0,'24.43.240.222','en'),(22180,3871,'2005-08-16 16:33:53',66,'Peter','<br><tt>so... are the guys reenacting <i>goatse</i>?</tt>',1,57,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22181,3871,'2005-08-16 16:52:34',66,'Peter','<br><tt>that last comment was about the first part. i just finished reading the whole thing. you know its good when you can read that many pages of text and feel like it only took one minute. i like your writing.<br><br>and i like how you say stuff like <i>\"well, that took the red right off their candy\"</i>. im totally gonna bite that the first chance i get.</tt>',1,367,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22182,3871,'2005-08-16 17:34:32',195,'matt','no, they\'re fooling with the other aperature.  opening it wider intensifies things somewhat.<BR><BR>i guess i should have been a little more specific, huh?  i was trying to tell it all with just dialogue.',1,205,0,0,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22183,3872,'2005-08-17 04:44:21',682,'turandot','This is gastrointestinal distress of epic proportions.  Has Ridley Scott optioned this story yet?  Good job, I haven\'t been so disgusted and entertained at the same time since...Lardass.',2,187,0,0,'72.14.140.105','en'),(22184,3872,'2005-08-18 14:57:49',534,'griece','who the fuck is ridley scott',6,28,682,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22185,3872,'2005-08-18 18:28:28',0,'turandot','Ridley Scott directs big budget visually stunning films such as:\r\nAlien (the original)\r\nBlade Runner\r\nGladiator\r\n\r\nIt\'d be awesome to see your story acted out by someone like...Vince Vaugn, but totally vivid and in slow motion.',2,228,534,0,'206.19.233.2','en'),(22186,3872,'2005-08-18 20:21:11',0,'chemicalpilate','It\'s funny how universally recognized The Move is.',1,51,0,0,'137.131.205.145','en'),(22187,3871,'2005-08-20 20:53:09',195,'matt','you know, if i could ever manage to stay in the same tense, i\'d be a mess of hell.  jeez.',1,90,0,0,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22188,3801,'2005-08-21 00:52:33',477,'jehannum','hey alana. please do me a favour and if you haven\'t ever had a CT-scan done at a hospital, then arrange for one as soon as pobbile. i suffered terribly from migraines for 15 or more years, and it wasn\'t until last year that they found the cause of them. now, a year later they\'re gone. please please please get yourself scanned if you haven\'t already. i wish you good health and all the best dealing with the horrid things.',1,427,0,0,'222.153.39.219','en'),(22189,3801,'2005-08-21 00:53:22',477,'jehannum','er, that should read <tt>possible</tt>, don\'t know where pobble came from...',0,77,0,0,'222.153.39.219','en'),(22190,3872,'2005-08-21 22:06:29',0,'ryan','jesus fucking christ',0,20,0,0,'67.171.154.38','en'),(22191,3873,'2005-08-23 13:40:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt>sine has told me stories about ybor city which only the folks who grew up in florida in the 80s can fully appreciate.<br><br>our wedding party  escaped to ybor the afternoon we jetted for our honeymoon. they were there for at least two days, and somehow not a single person involved has any recollection of anything that happened, save for vague snapshots of dancing with trannies and getting the boot from a goth bar for bringing in a gallon of jack daniels.<br><br>sine tells me that such tales are laughingly tame as compared to what <i>really</i> went on there back in the day.</tt>',1,594,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22192,3873,'2005-08-23 14:31:22',0,'simon','sigh',0,4,0,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(22193,3868,'2005-08-24 00:45:19',175,'Paul G','i like alana.',1,13,538,0,'64.107.88.140','en'),(22194,3868,'2005-08-24 16:24:52',0,'Peter','<br><tt>-2 points for using \"whoa\" and \"good gravy\" in a pejorative comment...</tt>',1,87,538,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22195,3868,'2005-08-24 17:14:41',82,'simon','yeah. i\'m against \"good gravy!\"',0,34,0,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(22196,3868,'2005-08-24 17:30:20',0,'Peter','<br><tt>totes. i think im just not a big fan of gravy anyway.</tt>',1,66,82,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22197,3868,'2005-08-25 00:00:58',0,'austin','you guys have not had my aunt\'s sausage gravy.  it would turn a vegan wrong ways!',0,82,0,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(22198,3868,'2005-08-25 04:45:08',0,'austin','<BR>\r\nmy seasons\r\n<BR>\r\nmy ocean\r\n<br>\r\nmy moon\r\n<br>\r\nmylanta',1,62,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(22199,3868,'2005-08-25 17:07:47',0,'Peter','<br><tt>that sounds like some <i>good gravy</i>, austin!</tt>',1,61,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22200,3868,'2005-08-26 00:42:52',175,'Paul G','many chicago italians resent the use of the word \"sauce\" to describe the red, marinara, tomato/garlic liquid served with pasta.  they insist on calling it \"gravy.\"',1,167,0,0,'64.107.88.140','en'),(22201,3868,'2005-08-26 18:00:23',0,'Peter','<br><tt>i love silly haiku.<br><br>\r\n<i>menstruation sauce<br>\r\nsome call it marinara<br>\r\nothers say \"gravy\".</i><br></tt>',1,125,175,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22202,3868,'2005-08-26 20:14:33',175,'Paul G','to Peter: i just didn\'t want alana to feel like someone was laughing at her; or, if someone was laughing, that no one else here would stand up.  i am usually a big jerk, writing whatever i think.  but with alana, i am a spectator: shown things from a different perspective.  sucks to feel like people are going to put you down whenever you open up.  cool to feel like you have a safe, accepting space.  coming from alana, i take this writing seriously.  sorry if i am too serious!',1,481,0,0,'192.203.136.254','en'),(22203,3868,'2005-08-26 20:53:02',538,'arcy','My apologies. No harm intended. Just thought it was a fitting phrase.',0,69,0,0,'142.204.20.27','en'),(22204,3868,'2005-08-29 05:47:28',0,'austin','sorry... i like you, alana!',1,27,175,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(22205,3868,'2005-08-29 14:44:50',0,'Peter','<br><tt>i got mad love for alana. she knows it.</tt>',1,52,175,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22206,3175,'2005-08-31 20:24:49',469,'elevenalana','my name is alana and i was born on 11/11. i\'ve always felt very closely connected to elevens. but not ones.',0,108,0,0,'207.101.231.163','en'),(22207,3872,'2005-09-01 10:37:23',0,'elaine','that was an exhausting read... i love the way that in the midst of this exqisite detail you randomly put asterixes in sh**te. that made me laugh a lot',1,150,0,0,'62.56.46.25','en'),(22208,3871,'2005-09-01 11:02:41',0,'elaine','i loved the \'drafted\' bit - it punctures the whole prose really nicely. \r\ndunno where you are w the jean rhys, but i  started off reading her because i liked wide sargasso sea, but if anything the other stuff is better (probably because it is more \'real\' whatever that means...',1,281,0,0,'62.56.46.25','en'),(22209,3283,'2005-09-02 17:53:26',709,'marcemery','Margrine can be used in substitute to butter, along with any oil, as long as it has fat in it.  The THC just needs to be dissolved in fat.  And using good herb means good brownies.  Fuck using that shwag shit.  Can\'t even find that shit anymore.',4,246,0,0,'65.126.111.83','en'),(22210,3877,'2005-09-06 16:52:44',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this is really good, matt. your stuff is always worth reading.</tt>',1,75,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22211,3175,'2005-09-06 23:52:38',195,'matt','but elevens are nothing <U>but</U> ones.',1,40,469,0,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22212,3870,'2005-09-08 00:36:18',57,'weeb','nothing more exciting than a new girl.',1,38,0,0,'208.241.162.2','en'),(22213,3869,'2005-09-09 23:02:13',175,'Paul G','<a href=\"http://www.grtbooks.com/hesse.asp?idx=0&yr=1877\">\"for madmen only\"</a>',1,83,0,0,'64.107.88.140','en'),(22214,3313,'2005-09-10 00:16:49',710,'dee','i have just recovered from an hysterectomy and oopherectomy performed due to an ovarian cyst that weighed over a stone! Turns out it was cancerous along with the ovary it was attatched to. Lucky me, it was caught early and no chemo required. Like you, I know the meaning of life now.',0,283,0,0,'195.93.21.103','en'),(22215,3837,'2005-09-11 11:26:43',223,'shanE','looks cold. Nice moments.\r\n',0,27,0,0,'218.153.86.140','en'),(22227,3872,'2005-09-27 21:02:06',251,'Jamie','this is quite possibly the fucking funniest thing i\'ve read EVAR!',1,66,534,0,'172.214.57.140','en'),(22217,3876,'2005-09-19 04:07:03',234,'etienne','this story upsets me.',0,21,0,0,'210.86.25.254','en'),(22218,3868,'2005-09-19 04:16:31',231,'alana','yes. i knows it! it is all ok!',1,30,0,0,'59.167.81.165','en'),(22219,3801,'2005-09-19 04:18:07',231,'alana','oh i had a scan. lots of them. my grandma gave me the gift of migraines. also it is tied in with my epilepsy.<br><br>\r\nalso, pobble is a really good word!',1,154,477,0,'59.167.81.165','en'),(22220,3877,'2005-09-22 18:18:52',400,'elaine','this is very fucking good. i would like to hear it read out, but it shows how it would be read anyway, written so it can be read s.l.o.w.l.y. ',1,142,0,0,'62.56.58.21','en'),(22221,3878,'2005-09-23 18:49:24',66,'Peter','<br><tt>isnt <i>\"unbreak my heart\"</i> a toni braxton song? sorry, now i have that song stuck in my head, and i had to mention it.</tt>',1,137,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22222,3878,'2005-09-24 00:22:49',682,'turandot','Totally.  In the video her boyfriend dies in a motorcycle accident.',2,67,66,0,'206.19.233.2','en'),(22223,3878,'2005-09-26 01:35:09',175,'Paul G','when it\'s good like that, you\'re young.  and you don\'t really think it through with your head, and you don\'t really feel it between your legs - so much as it just pulls out at your chest, lifting you up, in circles.\r\n<p>\r\nand you lose your balance, and your coordination, and you can\'t look cool to save your life.  and if the object of your affection wants only an actor, all they see is you - stumbling over your words, and your feet, and everything you try to do.\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen i was young, i never understood why old, wrinkled people married a second time - late in life.  but, now, i think that they want to be children - again.  when it\'s real, the tugging at your heart never goes away.\r\n</p>',1,708,0,0,'206.68.117.82','en'),(22224,3878,'2005-09-26 05:29:45',420,'thanatos','d00ds ... LOL ...\r\n\r\nfunny ... love is the ultimate classic entity ... beats wine, literature, history ... and anything else that goes classic after a couple of decades .. only love (or lust?) remains classic, vintage and hopelessly timely ... man, can\'t we just have sex and shake hands?',1,289,0,0,'67.101.222.216','en'),(22225,3878,'2005-09-26 05:30:43',420,'thanatos','and yes, unbreak my heart is a toni braxton song ... while i wrote to express my daemons i was humming that tune, and thus the title, ..',0,136,0,0,'67.101.222.216','en'),(22226,3881,'2005-09-26 06:03:54',420,'thanatos','yeah .. we may be old farts, but the resonating tune of adolescent love stubbornly permeates through this tough hard heart .. it tears all the hard matter, transforms the hardened walls into soapy foam, and leaves us back in a way highschool time ...',1,250,0,0,'67.101.222.216','en'),(22228,3879,'2005-09-27 21:06:50',251,'Jamie','i copied and pasted this into my word processor. neatly formatted it. printed it. took it home. then read it. and i liked it. i liked it alot. not only are you a visual artist but you are also an accomplished wordsmith.',1,219,0,0,'172.214.57.140','en'),(22229,3879,'2005-09-28 07:49:07',195,'matt','when i was a freshman in college, the guy i bought pot from (a friend of a friend of my brother) was heavily into collecting vinyl.  he was one of the main dealers in The Student Ghetto, and he must have had four or five thousand lps.  the first time i was at his apartment, he got me high and let me look through it all and play what i wanted.<BR><BR>he had a bookcase full of record guides.  i looked through four or five, i guess.  one of them, i can\'t remember which, rated albums from one to five stars.  the reviewer\'s taste must have appealed to me, because i sat on his couch stoned for about an hour and a half and jotted down all the five-star entries.<BR><BR>and that\'s how i found out about <I>another green world</I> and <I>volunteers</I> and <I>chirpin\'</I> and <I>indeterminancy</I> and <I>trout mask replica</I> and <I>paradise and lunch</I> and <I>entertainment!</I> and <I>datapanik in the year zero</I> and <I>everything you know is wrong</I> and <I>i just can\'t stop it</I>.  love, the bothy band, ted hawkins, the move.<BR><BR>and <I>crocodiles</I>.<BR><BR><I>crocodiles</I> always intrigued me because i could never figure out why it was given five stars.  it didn\'t seem to be in the same class as the others.  it didn\'t resonate like they did, didn\'t have the same... <I>round</I> quality, i guess is how i would describe it.<BR><BR>i bet i listen to it just as much as the others, though.  i\'m still trying to figure it out.<BR><BR>i bet marcus never sold a smudge of that vinyl.  i bet he\'s still in that same apartment.  he was ten years ago, anyway, last time i saw him.  he was always kind of a homebody.  the nesting type.\r\n\r\n    ',1,1670,0,0,'71.99.200.114','en'),(22230,3879,'2005-09-28 12:13:19',400,'elaine','maybe it was reviewed highly because echo and the bunnymen are \'important\' rather than because it was such a great LP? or maybe it just scratched an itch at the time which is hard to pin down at a later date. i was inordinately fond of echo at the time and listened to them far more than other bands, except possibly the smiths, being a bit mono as i am. however, i really don\'t listen to them any more. but i still tend to listen to one thing at a time over and over and over and over. ',1,490,195,0,'62.56.44.253','en'),(22231,3879,'2005-09-28 12:15:03',400,'elaine','thank you jamie. i am glad it was worth all the fiddling and printing',1,69,251,0,'62.56.44.253','en'),(22232,3881,'2005-09-30 21:53:49',240,'xep','Some things never get old.',0,26,0,0,'69.17.112.129','en'),(22233,3872,'2005-10-03 06:38:56',177,'pony','bettah get a bucket',1,19,0,0,'24.7.104.23','en'),(22234,3872,'2005-10-07 13:42:12',228,'belly','google ads = best ever.',0,23,0,0,'86.138.236.180','en'),(22235,1483,'2005-10-08 16:55:23',400,'BrandonDNilles','Hey, I used to be Kloun@Nerve.com, and that is my name... but I have no idea who you people are?  This is vary strange...',1,121,0,0,'66.218.63.177','en'),(22236,1483,'2005-10-08 18:49:56',195,'matt','and yet we know all about <U>you</U>.  we\'re hiding in your shower right now.  go look.',1,88,400,0,'64.12.116.200','en'),(22237,1483,'2005-10-08 18:56:42',195,'matt','you rat bastard, you.',1,21,400,0,'64.12.116.200','en'),(22238,1483,'2005-10-08 23:37:51',195,'matt','oh.  look at that.  BrandonDNilles = elaine.  why does it say that on the comments page but not the article?',1,108,0,0,'64.12.116.200','en'),(22239,3875,'2005-10-09 09:46:51',176,'Scapegoat','I enjoyed this.',0,15,0,0,'70.30.232.219','en'),(22240,3703,'2005-10-12 23:12:32',175,'Paul G','Austin, Peter, Simon, Trouble, Adam, Canadians, everyone...it begins: <a href=\\\"http://germz.org/klr650/\\\">http://germz.org/klr650</a>.\r\n<p>\r\nThis weekend, October 16, I will leave Chicago on a light motorcycle to see as much of the North American continent as money and weather will allow.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIf you are able to offer a tour, lodging, advice, a cup of coffee in your city, anything at all, please reach out.  (I hope that Simon will testify that I am quite polite, in \\\"real life\\\".)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn October of 2002, I was embarking upon my journey in the taxi; in October of 2003, I left to see my ex and her daughter in San Fransisco; in October of 2004, I wrote this article; it\\\'s October of 2005, and I am leaving again.  Thank you for being here to share the thoughts and the adventures.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis year, this trip, is a response to <a href=\\\" http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=3299\\\">this</a>, as much as anything else.  At a certain critical juncture - to paraphrase Melville - you reach for one thing or another.  Choosing life, I\\\'m going to Sea.\r\n</p>',1,1108,0,0,'64.107.88.140','en'),(22241,3565,'2005-10-13 16:57:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt>im really glad i dont live in hell\\\'s kitchen anymore. that place has bad mojo.</tt>',1,94,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22242,3175,'2005-10-19 22:28:57',722,'murphy','I frequently see 11:11 on digital clocks( much, much more than you would expect form a random shuffle). i was born at 11:12 , I think that OB wrote the wrong time down. Is there a gathering of \\\"11:11 people \\\" in Germany each year at 11 of nov (11) ?',2,255,0,0,'86.129.68.214','en'),(22243,3868,'2005-10-19 22:31:12',722,'murphy','get off that and get back to something \r\ndiferent : 11:11',0,57,231,0,'86.129.68.214','en'),(22244,3868,'2005-10-19 22:34:02',722,'murphy','not that I have anything against lesbians, if I were a girl I would be one,but go back to what you were taking about before, I am perplexed.',0,140,0,0,'86.129.68.214','en'),(22245,3868,'2005-10-20 06:55:56',231,'alana','i\\\'m sorry murphy, i have no idea what you\\\'re talking about. and if it\\\'s about the topic, no offence, but i\\\'ll about whatever i damn well feel like. if you don\\\'t like the content, make some yerself.',3,212,722,0,'59.167.82.16','en'),(22246,3868,'2005-10-20 06:57:03',231,'alana','actually what i meant to say was that i\\\'ll write about whatever i want to.',3,77,231,0,'59.167.82.16','en'),(22247,3660,'2005-10-22 17:54:36',195,'matt','to find somebody from a dream and talk to them.  wow.',1,53,0,0,'71.99.153.210','en'),(22248,3885,'2005-10-22 23:33:50',195,'matt','i like that:<BR><BR>incantations fall<BR>like warm rain -<BR>Witches gather burdoch<BR>and butane.<BR><BR>it\\\'s even stronger because it\\\'s the only exact rhyme.  nice.\r\n',1,174,0,0,'71.99.153.210','en'),(22249,3886,'2005-10-23 02:14:38',83,'adam','<p>one needs to take special care when sleeping on grass, especially grass that is cheap, and poorly maintained to boot. specific care needs to be placed on eliminating all contact surfaces between yourself and said grass. as if you are not careful you will chafe your entire body into one gigiantic rash and the following day at work will be a living hell. <br>\r\n<br>\r\n;)\r\n</P>\r\n',1,380,0,0,'71.106.138.110','en'),(22250,3885,'2005-10-23 09:20:46',718,'wild-roses','excellent - the more i read it, the better it gets. enchanting really.',1,70,0,0,'86.137.246.239','en'),(22251,2972,'2005-10-23 13:25:49',718,'wild-roses','it\\\'s the smell that remembers you... follows you, one life to the next, hinting that you really should be doing something, somewhere, with someone',2,149,0,0,'86.137.246.239','en'),(22252,3838,'2005-10-25 09:44:36',718,'wild-roses','whoever said you cant write (above) is wrong - clearly they\\\'ve never had any creativity inorder to have a block',2,114,0,0,'86.137.246.239','en'),(22253,3838,'2005-10-27 01:29:16',195,'matt','it\\\'s the \\\"quick quality time\\\" that you deceive yourself into thinking is a requirement that trips you up.  every time.  because it has a way of expanding.',1,163,0,0,'71.98.174.77','en'),(22254,3888,'2005-10-30 17:35:28',240,'xep','Brilliant!',0,10,0,0,'69.17.112.129','en'),(22255,3890,'2005-10-31 21:00:15',66,'Peter','<br><tt>even though we have a dishwasher, i generally do most of our dishes by hand; its pleasant. even though we have a washer and dryer in the basement, i still send our dirty clothes out to the laundry, because i hate doing laundry. so much.</tt>',1,249,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22256,3889,'2005-10-31 21:00:57',66,'Peter','<br><tt>great story, chia... its always the little hole-in-the-wall places that make for the best adventures.</tt>',1,114,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22257,3890,'2005-11-01 00:30:51',82,'simon','me: i can\\\'t do laundry. i send it out.<br>\r\nnick: me too. it was a beautiful day when i realized i could afford it.<br>\r\nme: dude, me too. <br>\r\nnick: now i\\\'m at a place where i\\\'m looking for a service to come over and get it, take it away, wash it, fold it and return it to me.',1,287,0,0,'24.193.36.108','en'),(22258,3885,'2005-11-01 22:35:38',697,'keuch2','nice images...  I can visualize it happening.',0,45,0,0,'200.61.225.214','en'),(22259,3889,'2005-11-01 22:59:28',82,'simon','good one, chia!',1,15,0,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(22260,3890,'2005-11-02 14:34:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt>tote, simz. the day i started sending out laundry was indeed a memorable one. there is a place around the corner form us that does the pickup/dropoff service, but that just strikes me as overkill, as its like .25 block away. but you know what? ill probably end up doing that soon enough.<br><Br>i hate laundry that much.</tt>',1,333,82,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22261,3886,'2005-11-02 14:49:44',66,'Peter','<br><tt>thats why i prefer slkeeping in a bed.</tt>',1,51,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22262,3885,'2005-11-02 14:52:09',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>Under a blue light<br> \r\nbulb, getting all the hot deals<br>\r\nk-mart is the shit</tt></i>\r\n ',1,103,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22263,3881,'2005-11-04 07:49:28',420,'thanatos','perhaps xep, some things never grow old, unless the plaything is (irregardless of the age ..) after all the romance, by happenstance to be a bit challenged, from a tools-standpoint ..  then one realizes, romance and all that sh*t prolly is a mere illusion ..',1,258,0,0,'67.101.222.216','en'),(22264,3881,'2005-11-04 14:46:33',66,'Peter','<br><tt>irregardless</tt>',1,25,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22265,3890,'2005-11-09 03:00:42',122,'hool','<a href=\\\"http://www.craigslist.org/about/best/sfo/101753746.html\\\">best-of craigslist: do my laundry, get a blowjob</a>',0,124,0,0,'69.194.66.189','en'),(22266,3890,'2005-11-09 05:44:23',195,'matt','man, this is entrapment if i ever saw it.<BR><BR>probably bust upwards of fifty guys with this little setup.',1,108,122,0,'71.98.174.77','en'),(22267,3892,'2005-11-09 15:53:36',251,'Jamie','I hear ya. To create from something is, paradoxicly, oftentimes the best way to let that something go. If there\\\'s one thing you\\\'ve taught me lately elaine, it\\\'s how to let go and just <i>be</i>.',1,203,0,0,'213.249.154.99','en'),(22268,3892,'2005-11-09 17:14:58',400,'elaine','cheers peachy boy.\r\nby the way \\\'this is the time, and this is the record of the time is from laurie anderson, case she reads this and wants to sue me',0,152,0,0,'212.225.63.163','en'),(22269,3892,'2005-11-10 20:13:37',195,'big laur','yer busted.',1,11,400,0,'71.98.174.77','en'),(22270,3893,'2005-11-11 14:22:51',66,'Peter','<br><tt>what about, say, salad tongs? or, like, armatron? that shit must freak you out, eh?<br><br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.ece.uvic.ca/499/2004a/group08/images/armatron.jpg\\\"></tt>',1,184,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22271,3893,'2005-11-11 19:02:57',400,'elaine','i am afraid of blenders, in case i get a compulsion to plunge my hand in and mash up my fingers. still, if i ever did, you would be less afraid of my hands, as long as the bones were neatly surgically tidied up already, if not my fingers would be sort of really long and bloody and dripping with fat and skin and muscles and stuff',0,330,0,0,'62.56.9.249','en'),(22272,3893,'2005-11-12 16:09:59',82,'simon','um, barf!',0,9,400,0,'63.212.166.178','en'),(22273,3893,'2005-11-16 21:04:03',400,'elaine','thanks!',1,7,82,0,'62.56.30.162','en'),(22274,1483,'2005-11-16 21:08:23',400,'elaine','not sure who that was but it wasn\\\'t me',0,41,0,0,'62.56.30.162','en'),(22275,3895,'2005-11-24 21:28:17',195,'matt','oh, look, there\\\'s a google ad up there for funny quote t-shirts.  maybe they have one with <I>i love it when a plan comes together</I> on it.<BR><BR>so, jo, you know, if you were wondering what you could get me for christmas, wink, wink, hint, hint.',1,252,0,0,'71.98.174.77','en'),(22276,3896,'2005-12-01 18:40:45',82,'simon','i love that record. and also metal box. i think the first time i heard husker du was at epicenter records. where i hung out/worked after school.',1,144,0,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(22277,3896,'2005-12-02 21:30:58',66,'Peter','<br><tt>let me go on record as saying that you <i>must</i> write more of these. music, like the sense of smell, conjurs some fantastic memories...</tt>',1,151,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22278,3896,'2005-12-03 02:40:47',195,'matt','<BR><BR><B><CENTER>december again</B></CENTER><BR><BR>the loquat tree\\\'s in bloom.  little white flowers.  the bee\\\'s are all over it.  the citrus fruit around here\\\'s almost ripe.  i like to filch kumquats on my way to the grocery store or post office, if the trees are close enough to the sidewalk.  i\\\'m a fruit thief.<BR><BR>got my checks yesterday, so i had the bus fare to get around to some stores here and in tampa and unload all the books my money\\\'s been tied up in.  christmas presents are paid for, and i\\\'ve got one seventy-five burning a hole in my pocket.<BR><BR>so, i\\\'ve been looking for weed.  everybody thinks i\\\'m a cop, though.  i guess with my hair and build and clothes, i could be mistaken for a cop.  this happened the last time i lived here, too.  i didn\\\'t know anybody here then, either.  ended up hooking up with this guy in maastricht - is that right?  is that in the netherlands?  i\\\'m not about to go research it, so i\\\'ll say yes. - over the internet, and he bought weed and hash there and flattened it with a rolling pin and sandwiched it between two thin greeting card-sized pieces of cardboard and mailed it to me.  it was good stuff, about eighty bucks a quarter, if memory serves, but i see on some message boards that he\\\'s ripping people off now, so forget that idea, i guess.<BR><BR>so, i\\\'m smoking nutmeg tonight.  it isn\\\'t much of anything, but it takes the edge off.  i think it\\\'s a hoot that you can buy it with food stamps, a packet of four nuts for fifty-five cents.  i\\\'m listening to édith piaf at relatively high sound pressure level and writing some poems.  i think i\\\'m going to order a pizza in a second, white with spinach and tomatoes, and make some iced tea and open the front windows and sit out on the step and wait and make the young blood sitting on the trunk of his car across the street wonder what kind of cop listens to music like that, make him doubt his own better judgment.',1,1977,0,0,'71.98.174.77','en'),(22279,3897,'2005-12-05 18:53:56',66,'Peter','<br><tt>part of me feels like i should be holier than thou and be all like <i>\\\"come on, its not cool to make fun of someone just because theyre overweight\\\"</i>... but then the rest of me remembers how much this cracked me up when i just read it, so i guess i gotta just be honest- this was pretty damn funny.</tt>',1,319,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22280,3897,'2005-12-05 21:20:42',195,'matt','yeah, i thought the same thing when i saw it in admissions limbo.<BR><BR>the punch line gets you, doesn\\\'t it?  ha.',1,117,0,0,'71.98.174.77','en'),(22281,3900,'2005-12-12 19:28:05',240,'xep','Just remember that when  you run away, you\\\'re still there.  You can\\\'t run away from yourself.  Believe me, I\\\'ve tried.',2,127,0,0,'69.17.112.129','en'),(22282,3900,'2005-12-12 20:28:41',66,'Peter','<br><tt>so much technology, wisdom and futureshock available to humankind at the moment, yet we still cant figure out how to fix our problems, or even effectively distance ourselves from them enough to get proper perspective. what an ironic, sad and inevitable truth... its one thing i really hate about life.</tt>',1,314,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22283,3902,'2005-12-13 15:32:04',82,'simon','i do love a good home depot, however. especially the one on sunset and western. it smells like sawdust and paint and oil. and is full of hot boys. i went there almost daily while working on the movies.',1,201,0,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(22284,3902,'2005-12-13 21:16:58',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i hate lowe\\\'s too. home depot over lowe\\\'s any day...</tt>',1,71,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22285,3902,'2005-12-15 00:44:50',175,'Paul G','The charger for my digi-cam exploded, for no reason, in a random motel outlet.  I looked for a new one in San Diego.  And I wound up at Fry\\\'s.  Paying $50 was bad.  But standing in the sluiceway that shaped the check-out line was worse.  Nothing else makes me feel quite so much like a bull waiting for slaughter.    ',1,320,0,0,'192.203.136.254','en'),(22286,3903,'0000-00-00 00:00:00',17,'austin','<a href=\\\"http://www.webbikeworld.com/BMW-motorcycles/bmw-hp2/\\\"><img src=\\\"http://farc.ikami.com/~swinney/tmp/bmw-hp2.jpg\\\" width=\\\"400\\\"></a>\r\n<BR>\r\n<P>Good seeing you Paul Germanos, be sure and lift those heavy questions with your legs.</P>\r\n\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.linfield.edu/ehs/images/Lift%204.gif\\\">',1,335,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(22287,3903,'2005-12-15 16:59:29',66,'Peter','<br><tt>paul- great trip! theres not much i can add to what youve posted here... except that now i <i>really</i> want a klr650. toss some slicks on there, pull off all the body panels, and rock it all over brooklyn.</tt>',1,220,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22288,3903,'2005-12-15 17:04:24',66,'Peter','<br><tt>also:<br><br><img src=\\\"http://www.raptorsandrockets.com/images/KLR%20SM%20stoppie%2001_edited.JPG\\\" border=\\\"1\\\"></tt>',1,135,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22289,3903,'2005-12-15 22:29:04',175,'Paul G','Yeah in theory a motorcycle makes you look cool.  But I would suggest that we resemble <a href=\\\"http://germz.org/KLR650/rideamerica/swinney_paulg.jpg\\\">Howdy Doody and Eddie Munster gone bad</a>.  Like child stars on E!, it is.  <a href=\\\"http://www.digicamhistory.com/Howdy-Doody.jpg\\\">You</a> and <a href=\\\"http://www.perspectivas.com.mx/noticias/media/Eddie.jpg\\\">me</a>.  Yep.\r\n<p>\r\nPeter: <a href=\\\"http://www.suzukicycles.com/Products/DRZ400SMK6/Default.aspx\\\">Suzuki DR-Z400SM</a>.  But, maybe, only the KLR or XR650L would fit your skyscraper of a body.  And, maybe, I\\\'m only pretending to know anything about motorbikes...',1,651,0,0,'64.107.88.240','en'),(22290,3903,'2005-12-16 00:58:50',682,'turandot','The documentation and reflections on your cross country journey is awe inspiring. I also like your writing style.  And it was also very good to finally meet you in person!  I am going to take the MSP  course and get my license in the spring.',1,241,0,0,'206.19.233.2','en'),(22291,3905,'2005-12-16 19:01:37',17,'austin','&hearts;yorca of the floor seas!!!!!!&hearts;',1,45,0,0,'63.80.97.254','en'),(22292,3905,'2005-12-16 19:26:31',66,'Peter','<br><tt>sure beats being <i>\\\"yorca of the sore fleas\\\"</i>!</tt>',1,69,17,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22293,3903,'2005-12-19 05:07:32',17,'Max Fischer','Can I see some documentation?',1,29,682,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(22294,3903,'2005-12-20 02:35:49',175,'Paul G','Thank you for taking the time to write something nice.  I hope that the mtorcycle is everything that you want it to be.',1,119,682,0,'192.203.136.254','en'),(22295,3903,'2005-12-20 07:39:40',682,'turandot','<img src=\\\"http://www.lafraise.com/prod/xl/tee-shirt121.JPG\\\">',1,66,17,0,'72.14.140.105','en'),(22296,3903,'2005-12-20 07:58:39',17,'austin','<BR>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://farc.ikami.com/~swinney/tmp/RoflMao.jpg\\\">',6,70,682,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(22297,3903,'2005-12-21 00:31:29',66,'peter','<img src=\\\"http://saturn5.com/peter/ljphoto/duh/mao.jpg\\\">',1,62,17,0,'24.46.96.93','en'),(22298,3903,'2005-12-21 22:28:58',175,'Paul G','You are all Halford-licking wankers:<br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.mercadolibre.com.pe/jm/img?s=MPE&f=115839_8735.jpg&v=P\\\" width=\\\"250\\\" height=\\\"250\\\"><br>\r\nM-otorcycling<br>\r\nS-winney-style<br>\r\nP-roficiently:<br>\r\n(1) right hand on the throttle; (2) left hand on the crack pipe; (3) front wheel off the ground.\r\n<p>\r\nDude, I need a real job - one that does not involve melons or heroin.  I should have kept the tent, and lived on the beach...\r\n</p>',1,461,0,0,'192.203.136.254','en'),(22299,3903,'2005-12-21 23:44:12',17,'austin','<P>\r\nwhich i received back today!  thanks broham.\r\n</P>',1,55,175,0,'67.113.126.149','en'),(22300,3905,'2005-12-22 00:13:31',17,'austin','I just remembered, she also used to be <i>yorka the nut whale</I> after her propincity to want to be sleeping on my crotch, and others\\\' crotches.',1,148,66,0,'67.113.126.149','en'),(22301,3905,'2005-12-23 04:48:43',65,'le','yes, yorca the nut whale.\r\n\r\nalso, i don\\\'t really remember writing this. i think it was late at night.',6,105,0,0,'64.81.58.124','en'),(22302,3903,'2005-12-24 02:30:40',175,'Paul G','Very good.  The time to write the constitution for your outlaw motorcycle club is now:\r\n<br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://germz.org/img/friends/swingram_01.jpg\\\" width=\\\"300\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" border=\\\"1\\\" alt=\\\"Swinney\\\'s Slaves a/k/a The Meat Ogres\\\">\r\n<br>\r\nIn any case...I plan to ride from NY to SF, alone or with (?) company, in the Spring/Summer.\r\n<p>\r\nHappy Holidays!\r\n</p>',1,391,17,0,'64.107.88.240','en'),(22303,3903,'2005-12-24 06:56:45',17,'austin','it is called <H1>the BURNINATORS!</H1>\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ndon\\\'t forget it!',1,69,175,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(22304,3903,'2005-12-25 23:56:09',66,'peter','<br><tt>count me in for the ny->sf ride... for real. here\\\'s hoping i can score a ride better than 200cc before then, heh...</tt>',1,131,175,0,'24.46.96.93','en'),(22305,3903,'2006-01-02 03:14:30',83,'adamajah','<b>Just stay off our turf.</b><br>\r\n<img src =\\\"http://www.yeager.tv/images/misc/SantaMonicaMtnNinjaz.jpg\\\"><br>\r\n<br>\r\n-The Mtn Ninjaz',1,139,17,0,'71.106.138.110','en'),(22306,3905,'2006-01-02 14:13:18',93,'chiamattt','Hi le. remember me?',1,19,0,0,'58.226.158.76','en'),(22307,3907,'2006-01-02 17:55:47',626,'catintheattic','it\\\'s almost warm enough to swim right now.  if i do it, i\\\'m going to do it buck naked.<BR><BR>is that a racial slur, do you think?  <I>buck naked?</I>',1,156,0,0,'71.99.163.87','en'),(22308,3907,'2006-01-03 05:51:21',82,'simon','i like BUTT naked, myself.',0,26,0,0,'24.193.37.73','en'),(22309,3909,'2006-01-05 14:58:37',82,'simon','hee. i was just in a small midwestern city. and i had cheese curds. they squeak! it\\\'s weird.',1,95,0,0,'24.193.37.73','en'),(22310,3909,'2006-01-05 18:15:33',66,'Peter','<br><tt>im glad america has small midwestern towns. it keeps all the rednecks out of nyc... and im sure theyre glad that nyc keeps all the hipsters, queers, weirdos and all dem scary asians out of their town, heh heh...</tt>',1,224,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22311,3909,'2006-01-05 18:25:29',210,'may','add a piercing and a tattoo and it\\\'s as if i have to wear a sign: \\\"i will not mug you! i am just a boring phd student!\\\"\r\n\r\nmaybe i just need to move back to a coast.',0,174,66,0,'70.108.210.155','en'),(22312,3909,'2006-01-05 19:12:42',66,'Peter','<br><tt>the way you describe yourself sounds like much of my neighborhood here in brooklyn. its really odd to imagine that you would be such a conspicuous anomaly anywhere, but then again, im always having to remind myself that the world doesnt simply stop west of the hudson river... i guess theres jersey and shit. then a bunch of other shit. then california. <br><br>how did you end up stranded in such a landlocked place?</tt>',1,430,210,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22313,3909,'2006-01-05 21:47:33',682,'turandot','I\\\'m asian and I get the same thing in San Diego all the frigging time. What was that thing that drunk guy said to me that one time?\r\n\r\n\\\"You speak good English.\\\"\r\n\r\nCool, thanks.',1,186,210,0,'206.19.233.2','en'),(22314,3909,'2006-01-05 21:49:15',210,'may','i decided to go back to school, and picked the \\\"best\\\" one i got into. i\\\'m trying not to feel like it was a bad idea.',0,125,66,0,'70.108.210.155','en'),(22315,3909,'2006-01-05 21:49:43',210,'may','what?! san diego? i thought we were plentiful out there in california.',0,70,682,0,'70.108.210.155','en'),(22316,3909,'2006-01-05 22:18:38',682,'turandot','There are plenty of Asians in California, but people still find us exotic...possibly because a lot of the Asians don\\\'t frequent non-Asian owned establishments. California is a lot more ethnically segregated than people realize.',1,230,210,0,'206.19.233.2','en'),(22317,3909,'2006-01-06 00:38:23',82,'simon','what town?',1,10,0,0,'24.193.37.73','en'),(22318,3909,'2006-01-06 02:38:53',175,'Paul G','<p>\r\nInteresting.  \r\nI received no inquiries about my ethnicity or national origin - till I reached San Diego.\r\nEvery day that I spent in San Diego I was questioned about the place that I was, \\\"really from.\\\"\r\nBecause no one in San Diego would accept that I had come from Chicago.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI saw a \\\"Texas is for Whites only\\\" graffito outside Midland. \r\nI saw a \\\"Go home Yankee\\\" graffito in Arkansas. \r\nAnd Confederate battle flags were not an uncommon sight, in several states.\r\nBut the people in those place went to great lengths to assist me.\r\nTo my face, they were friendly.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt might be the case that racism, and xenophobia, is ubiquitous, in America;\r\nmaybe it manifests differently, in different locations.\r\nBut the end result of my own experience has been that I have felt most welcome in those places that have had a poor reputation.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nArriving in San Diego was like being slapped in the face.\r\nIt felt like Chicago: a place that was nominally diverse, but in which various communities did not intermingle.\r\nSeveral other parties (including turandot) reinforced that impression.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMore generally, there seems to be (1) a human penchant for tribalism; and (2) a Machiavellian understanding\r\nthat social achievement tends to depend upon one\\\'s ability to appear to maintain the \\\"norms\\\" of the particular society \r\nin which one hopes to succeed, e.g., though San Francisco is purported to honor individuality, it (apparently) rewards a certain\r\ntype of conformity.  On-line, too, people scramble to modify their appearance as need demands; true feelings are revealed only anonymously, on figurative bathroom walls.\r\nAnd even the University/Academy alternately rewards and punishes its members, and aspiring members,\r\nbased upon their ability to maintain the forms of its own creation.\r\n</p>',3,1854,0,0,'64.107.88.240','en'),(22319,3670,'2006-01-06 08:26:15',17,'austin','mike just said:\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n<B>\\\"Is that guy watching the challenger explode or something?</B>',1,95,0,0,'68.6.211.134','en'),(22320,3909,'2006-01-06 14:26:59',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\\\"...though San Francisco is purported to honor individuality, it (apparently) rewards a certain type of conformity...\\\"</i><br><br>ive heard several people say almost this exact thing lately. an interesting confluence...</tt>',1,241,175,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22321,3909,'2006-01-06 15:14:29',195,'matt','i wonder if the people at the german restaurant know where i can get some cheese curds.',1,87,0,0,'71.99.163.87','en'),(22322,3910,'2006-01-06 17:02:01',82,'simon','heh. so i\\\'m like, ew poem! but then i read it anyway and go, hey not bad!',1,76,0,0,'209.21.99.118','en'),(22323,3910,'2006-01-07 02:20:23',727,'netter','aww, blushing like mad.  <3',1,27,0,0,'67.117.164.171','en'),(22324,3826,'2006-01-07 02:23:26',727,'netter','shivers.  whoa.',1,15,0,0,'67.117.164.171','en'),(22325,3909,'2006-01-07 05:45:02',240,'xep','It\\\'s funny.  chemicalpilate\\\'s rural midwestern high school was way more racially integrated than my ultra liberal california town high school.  california is all a myth.',0,175,0,0,'69.17.112.129','en'),(22326,3909,'2006-01-07 22:07:45',175,'Paul G','<img src=\\\"http://www.importimagesnewyork.com/catalog/images/detail/1921.jpg\\\" alt=\\\"andy warhol\\\'s marilyn monroe\\\">',1,127,66,0,'64.107.88.237','en'),(22327,3909,'2006-01-08 05:06:22',66,'Peter','<br><tt><img src=\\\"http://www.clyffordstill.net/basquiat/bio/basquiatinstudio.jpg\\\" border=\\\"1\\\"></tt>',1,110,175,0,'24.46.99.165','en'),(22328,3909,'2006-01-08 05:19:25',82,'simon','<img src=\\\"ht