caisonally, then i ran into her downtown on the saturday night before halloween.  she was pretty messed-up.  she ended up spending the night, and i haven\\\'t seen her since.  too bad.  in my more lucid moments, i thought she might have been the one.<BR><BR>above all, i valued hunter s. thompson because he made me laugh.  is that shallow?  nah.  that\\\'s a rare thing, to be able to entertain people.  when i was nineteen, my grandmother told me, <i>you know, sometimes you have a very bleak outlook on life.  you\\\'re going to have to guard against that.</i><BR><BR>so i prize the humorous.  even hemingway had his wry, very deadpan humor.  and david letterman has saved my life so many times.  you laugh, and it doesn\\\'t seem so bad.  cheer up, keed.  don\\\'t let it get to you.',1,897,0,0,'65.35.194.157','en'),(22602,4069,'2007-02-21 06:54:15',195,'matt','i have every reason to believe that before it\\\'s all over with, everybody on this site is going to sex everybody else.  fish and fowl. it\\\'s like my dad told me the first time we went to one of those mega-buffet places:  <i>just taste a little bit of everything, and what you like, you can go for seconds on.  just don\\\'t make a pig of yourself.</i><BR><BR>i get this feeling that you could stand to get laid.  don\\\'t be afraid to speak up.  ask, and it shall be given unto you.  seek, and ye shall find.  knock, and it shall be opened unto you.  ah lay loo, ah lay loo yuh.',1,582,0,0,'65.35.194.157','en'),(22603,4069,'2007-02-22 01:24:29',195,'matt','on second thought, i don\\\'t have every reason to believe that.  some of my suspicions border only tangentially upon reality.<BR><BR>but from what i can gather, it seems like significant swaths of this project may have already been accomplished.  so who knows.<BR><BR>(don\\\'t pay any attention to me.  carry on.  i\\\'m just about a fool.)',1,342,0,0,'65.35.194.157','en'),(22604,4070,'2007-03-09 18:40:34',17,'austin','<P>I just read two depressive swinney articles back to back.  Somebody turn on the lights in here.</P>\r\n\r\n<P><B>*best friendly*</B></P>',1,135,0,0,'69.17.112.129','en'),(22605,4068,'2007-03-09 18:42:28',17,'austin','<P>I can see why J-LO dumped him.</P>',1,37,0,0,'69.17.112.129','en'),(22606,4045,'2007-03-09 18:49:21',17,'austin','<P>i completely forgot that shit-shtick and wasn\\\'t expecting it.</P> \r\n<P>why did i never know about this website, it is great!</P>',1,134,0,0,'69.17.112.129','en'),(22607,4022,'2007-03-09 18:51:33',17,'austin','word.',1,5,0,0,'69.17.112.129','en'),(22608,4071,'2007-03-20 22:26:12',93,'chiamattt','nice post. thanks dude.',1,23,0,0,'58.226.156.81','en'),(22609,4071,'2007-03-20 23:37:03',122,'hool','this was a great read. loved the juxtaposition of flow and interruption, calmness and violence.  gave me a vivid recollection of being in that apartment.',0,153,0,0,'74.100.11.231','en'),(22610,4071,'2007-03-21 06:09:39',175,'Paul G','not too long ago i wrote a letter to a friend.\r\nand in that letter i tried to describe a \\\'change\\\' that i had seen in my lifetime.\r\ni used the example of snow shovelling to illustrate my point.\r\n<p>\r\nhere in chicago, as in toronto, it\\\'s necesary to shovel the snow from paved walks and streets.\r\nit\\\'s a chore that becomes fatiguing when - as was the case this year - we have multiple snow falls per day. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni remembered when, maybe even ten years ago, people in the neighborhoods\r\nwould shovel every snowfall, and then salt those shoveled paths, and take a great sort of pride in having done the job well.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmaybe it sounds silly, but people - very old and very young people - depended upon having clean, safe surfaces.\r\nand if a property owner left \\\'unshoveled\\\' the little piece of the public way that they they were responsible for, eyebrows were raised.\r\nwho was going to slip?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnow, most of the walkways are left in poor repair:\r\nand as long as a homeowner can access his/her private vehicle, the rest of the public can go get fucked.\r\nso too it goes with the behavior of said driver once they\\\'re on the roadway: the rest of the public can go get fucked.\r\nwherever we once depended upon a public spiritedness in the people, we are now in a bad way. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nif you try to exhibit some sensitivity, some concern, you set yourself up to be ridiculed.\r\nno one cares - until it touches them.\r\nwhen you, or your child or someone else you love gets hurt - then it\\\'s real.\r\npeople slip - fuck them.\r\npeople crash their auto - fuck them, hope they die.\r\nwar someplace else - those people have always been fighting.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nand it\\\'s the way that we communicate, now, too.\r\nwhere\\\'s the polite and \\\'plain-spoken\\\' american?\r\nwhen i was young, if i wanted to date a young woman i had to go to her home and/or call her on the telephone.\r\nand there was one telephone used by the whole family, and the mother/father would answer, and i had to explain\r\nmyself and my intentions.  ditto for the door to the family home: invited in, sat down, intentions declared.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nif in the 1960\\\'s-1970\\\'s, in vietnam-era america, the counter-culture included \\\'free\\\' drugs and sex, they were just that: free.\r\nand now this too is all about the celebrity and money: heavily armed thugs [guys who are cool because they don\\\'t care] trafficking in huge quantities of narcotics, and the porn industry.\r\nand the images of that drug and porn activity repeated ad nauseum on mtv, ultimately being aped on-line by white office-monkeys for whom\r\nviolent oppression is a very distant thing...\r\n</p>',4,2655,0,0,'76.209.58.163','en'),(22611,4069,'2007-03-21 08:32:55',65,'le','been there.',0,11,0,0,'216.27.181.232','en'),(22612,4071,'2007-03-26 08:18:02',370,'seanthecon','things done changed. the rights and freedoms won in the 60s/70s were sold by the very people who fought for them. if this generation of youth rings hollow to you, then all you are hearing is the echo of shallow self-righteousness of previous eras. when the \\\"counter-culture\\\" sells out, why blame today\\\'s youth for \\\"buying in\\\"? ',6,342,175,0,'24.80.179.124','en'),(22613,4071,'2007-03-27 06:59:16',175,'Paul G','<p>\r\nyour comment was really tight.\r\nand your use of the language was most excellent.\r\ni especially liked the shift from\r\n\\\"youth rings hollow,\\\" to\r\n\\\"echo of shallow self-righteousness.\\\"\r\nit\\\'s like you speak english, or something.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nback to the point: the original article was entitled \\\"the accident\\\" and it did indeed describe such a scene.\r\nbut what i read had only little to do with an auto wreck, \r\nand very much more to do with how we know, and live, with each other.\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>the autos race along - their drivers viewing the world through the glass of the windscreen.\r\n<li>and you view the autos - through the glass of your window.\r\n</ul>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>the \\\"naked punks, the bull dykes and the old drunks,\\\" watch the wreckage.\r\n<li>and you watch the \\\"naked punks, the bull dykes and the old drunks.\\\"\r\n</ul>\r\nso that there are pairs of voyeurs engaged in a relationship.\r\nit\\\'s like sitting in a theatre watching kyle mclauglin as he watches dennis hopper\\\'s frank booth.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n(1)violence; (2)voyeur; (3)audience.<br>\r\n(1)war; (2)reporter; (3)television viewer.<br>\r\n(1)poseur; (2)camera; (3)flickr/myspace/tribe/etc.<br>\r\n(1)ambition; (2)rhetoric; (3)voter.<br>\r\nso that whomever it is that experiences (1) from position (3) is always dependent upon the device in (2)\r\nand is therefore subject to manipulation.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhow do you know your neighbors?  you spy on them.\r\nwhat\\\'s missing?  as i read the story: empathy; any sense of connectedness.\r\nyou get right up to the wreckage and you don\\\'t puke; you don\\\'t cry.\r\n\\\"I am not sick or sad. I want to get closer.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI am reminded of what hool wrote, here:\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=3299\\\">\r\nwe build houses to <i>insulate</i> ourselves from the physical nature we cannot control. \r\nwe accumulate worldly things as an act of <i>insulation</i> against the cold void of our mental nature, \r\nwhich we also cannot control.\r\n</a>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni would suggest that we are too well insulated.\r\nwe, in the modern west collectively, have too little direct experience.\r\nthis is the situation into which huxley thrust his savage in <u>brave new world</u>.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n[read in here the political parallel:\r\nmany see with their eyes what appears to be; but few know with their hands what really is.\r\nand the consequences:\r\nthe people behind the current administration - bush sr, cheney, rumsfeld, et al - came into power in the 60\\\'s.\r\nand they never went away.\r\nwith vietnam, as with the current war, no one cared until it touched them.\r\nas the draft expanded, the protests expanded.]\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbirth and death are the peak human events.\r\nbut it\\\'s not just the peak events from which we are disconnected: \r\nwhy don\\\'t people shovel their walkways in the winter?\r\nthey don\\\'t want to exert themselves, or feel the cold.\r\nbut, maybe, there is something to be said for actually feeling the chill of the wind and the weight of the snow?\r\nand maybe, just maybe, if you know that someone is giving birth, has been the victim of a crime,\r\nis mourning a loss, having surgery, is in an accident, whatever, you can be nice, you can care.\r\n</p>',0,3203,370,0,'76.209.53.144','en'),(22614,4071,'2007-03-27 07:40:14',370,'seanthecon','oh. i see. we\\\'re on the same page. don\\\'t take my piece too literally - cause that\\\'s the exact point i\\\'m trying to make. i\\\'m just using myself as the vehicle that drives into that tree of alienation. but i don\\\'t know how clear that comes through.',6,263,175,0,'64.59.144.87','en'),(22615,4071,'2007-03-27 17:41:02',175,'Paul G','<p>\r\nI was a hippie<br>\r\nI was a burnout<br>\r\nI was a dropout<br>\r\nI was out of my head<br>\r\nI was a surfer<br>\r\nI had a skateboard<br>\r\nI was so heavy man, I lived on the strand<br>\r\nI was so fucked up<br>\r\nI was so messed up<br>\r\nI was so screwed up<br>\r\nI was out of my head<br>\r\nI was so jacked up<br>\r\nI was so drunk up<br>\r\nI was so knocked out, I was out of my head<br>\r\nI was so wasted.\r\n</p>',0,400,370,0,'68.253.129.121','en'),(22616,4071,'2007-03-27 21:41:50',93,'chiamattt','I\\\'ve got the world up my ass\r\nand i\\\'m gonna move fast\r\nbe the first\r\nwon\\\'t be the last\r\ni\\\'ve got the world up my ass\r\n\r\nsociety is burning me up\r\ntake a bite, spit it out\r\ntake their rules\r\nrip \\\'em up, tear them down',1,231,0,0,'58.226.156.81','en'),(22617,4071,'2007-03-28 05:32:25',370,'seanthecon','I like big butts and I can not lie\r\nYou other brothers can\\\'t deny\r\nThat when a girl walks in with an itty bitty waist\r\nAnd a round thing in your face\r\nYou get sprung, wanna pull out your tough\r\n\\\'Cause you notice that butt was stuffed\r\nDeep in the jeans she\\\'s wearing\r\nI\\\'m hooked and I can\\\'t stop staring\r\nOh baby, I wanna get wit\\\'cha\r\nAnd take your picture\r\nMy homeboys tried to warn me\r\nBut with that butt you got makes me feel so horny',6,455,0,0,'24.80.179.124','en'),(22618,4068,'2007-04-08 04:08:56',180,'auntie','the trouble with ben affleck is that he is too preciously WHITE.  he has no shades of color.  both literally and figuratively.',2,126,0,0,'24.92.160.192','en'),(22619,4072,'2007-05-03 02:55:11',370,'seanthecon','beautiful piece.',6,16,0,0,'24.80.179.124','en'),(22620,4073,'2007-05-14 19:50:56',57,'weeb','would you really feel better by screwing everyone who depends on the money from this non-profit, and everyone who depends on this non-profit for a living? would sabatoging the future success of this charity really make you sleep better at night?',0,245,0,0,'66.93.131.112','en'),(22621,4073,'2007-05-15 03:10:22',180,'auntie','hmmm, nobody depends on this not-for-profit for a living.  i feel really good about telling them buh-bye after they voted to spend $10k that should have gone to the charity on frivolous bullshit.\r\n\r\nsince i located a donor for the cover the lost money, i feel good about that, too.\r\n\r\nmy hope is that my leaving will bring them all to their senses, and they will stop writing checks for stupid stuff and begin writing more and bigger checks to the charities they purport to support.',1,482,57,0,'24.92.160.192','en'),(22622,4069,'2007-05-26 22:03:41',251,'jamie','this brought back some difficult memories',0,41,0,0,'85.210.160.167','en'),(22623,3262,'2007-05-26 22:11:11',251,'jamie','heh, i don\\\'t remember writing this. she\\\'s burned, spurned and spat me out countless times since then. that\\\'s why i\\\'m marrying her ass.',1,146,0,0,'85.210.160.167','en'),(22624,4103,'2007-05-30 23:21:48',195,'matt','i think they need a beatdown.<BR><BR>just to seal the deal.',1,59,0,0,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22625,4092,'2007-06-01 15:35:47',496,'chemicalpilate','Keep on keepin\\\' on.  I always love reading these.',1,52,0,0,'69.17.112.117','en'),(22626,4092,'2007-06-01 17:52:42',195,'matt','thanks :)<BR><BR>thank you for reading and for your support.',1,60,496,0,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22627,4006,'2007-06-23 06:57:10',175,'Paul G','Whether I\\\'ve commented, I\\\'ve read everything that you\\\'ve written on this site.',1,87,0,0,'76.209.247.88','en'),(22628,3891,'2007-06-26 03:06:00',587,'ackman','Keep taking the high road, \\\'cause it pays off. My only qualification for offering advice is as a walking, talking cautionary tale so in an odd way, I know from where I speak.',1,177,0,0,'216.15.4.120','en'),(22629,4071,'2007-07-05 03:32:40',183,'ali','<p>Did you read concrete island? This makes me remember the style of J.G., though much more human/humaine.</p>',0,110,370,0,'74.105.115.126','en'),(22630,4092,'2007-07-06 21:51:21',17,'austin','word, good. i wonder to some small extent what is going on.  it is comforting.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nby the way, \\\"in case it started raining on the way over,\\\" never happens here in san diego.',1,186,0,0,'207.158.20.111','en'),(22631,3737,'2007-07-13 00:49:27',66,'Peter','<br><tt>23</tt>',1,15,0,0,'24.186.187.5','en'),(22632,3654,'2007-07-13 02:55:15',66,'Peter','<br><tt>come back? you posted this three years ago! post again already, o!</tt>',1,79,63,0,'24.186.187.5','en'),(22633,632,'2007-07-13 03:00:19',66,'Peter','<br><tt>still waiting...<br><br>\r\n\r\ni have an ace position in mind, but it would require drawing a diagram. some things are hard to condense into text...</tt>',1,158,17,0,'24.186.187.5','en'),(22634,3637,'2007-07-15 01:00:08',587,'ackman','I\\\'m hoping better late than never. I apologize for the delay. I very much wanted to answer your questions, but \\\'habits\\\' were causing a rapid deterioration of my circumstances, and I ultimately put myself in the position of having to spend the better part of last year in a homeless shelter, so online access was a low priority. If you don\\\'t mind, I\\\'d like to speak to you about the situation that had occurred back when it was all falling apart for me. The whole Paul G thing really hit me outta left field, and being the new, uninvited guy I really didn\\\'t know what to do. See, I had come to feel \\\"at home\\\" here. Yeah, I mostly lurked, but I read ALOT. It probably isn\\\'t the most healthy means of socialization (pretty pitiful, actually) but I had come to care about some of the people here, and logging on to read what new posts my be there or what banter abounded in the chat room was a high point of my day. \r\nThere aren\\\'t a lot of what I would call \\\'safe\\\' places for me and granted -I squatted here with no invititation- but I value this site accordingly. So you might imagine how off balance I was when I came to find that I was suspected of basically digitally stalking/harrassing/plagiarising a mainstay of the community...well, it was *almost* funny as I happen to be under treatment for a number of psych conditions that include paranoid delusions; *I\\\'M* supposed to be the guy worried about such things, normally (when I\\\'m off meds that is). Although I had wanted to do something to set PAul\\\'s mind at ease, I was at a loss (of resources)to do anything substantial and convincing, like fly out to where he is and take a polygraph. Sure, I\\\'m not responsible correcting the perceptions of others when I\\\'m not at fault but a) people do that for me when I\\\'m seein\\\' shadows where there aren\\\'t any and b) I had genuinely come to like Paul as well as respect the personal integrity comes through so clearly is his writing. Unfortunately, I was an unknown and all I had were words to offer. I thing I ultimately convinced Matt I was who I said I am, but for the most part, with everything else in my life going as bad as it was, This situation put the capper on it just as surely as if Rod Serling had come strolling through the door pullin\\\' on his perpetual cigarette, and offering ME for your consideration as I slipped steadily further into the Twilight Zone. Anyway, I\\\'m sure I\\\'m being rtedundant and unclear, so I\\\'ll shut up. I just wanted to ask for your help/advice on how I might resolve this mess (if possible). I really appreciate it.',1,2619,66,0,'64.121.59.122','en'),(22635,3637,'2007-07-15 01:05:05',587,'ackman','Wow. \r\nSorry \\\'bout the non-english grammar, there.\r\nI PROMISE that next time I\\\'ll actually get some sleep and edit my text prior to posting.',1,146,66,0,'64.121.59.122','en'),(22636,4092,'2007-07-17 16:55:58',195,'matt','i\\\'m glad i can be of some comfort.  ;)',1,41,17,0,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22637,4092,'2007-07-17 17:01:34',195,'matt','i don\\\'t know about that last sentence.  <i>a cloud in the shape of night.</i>  what the hell does that mean?  the cynic in me detects a whiff of bullshit.<BR><BR>i was going to say <i>a cloud in the shape of an elephant</i> or <i>a turtle</i>, because we were playing the cloud game on the way there, but i thought <i>night</i> was better.  for some reason.  i don\\\'t understand it.  i think it\\\'s one of those things that comes from something besides the rational mind and speaks mainly to that faculty in others.  couldn\\\'t tell you.<br><BR>the israelite reference was intentional, though.',1,600,0,0,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22638,3637,'2007-07-24 00:54:48',587,'ackman','btw my new e-mail is achilles.aflac@gmail.com (no connection to my former employer aflac),',3,90,66,0,'64.121.59.122','en'),(22639,4239,'2007-07-31 21:40:44',538,'arcy','I like this very much.',2,22,0,0,'216.191.144.135','en'),(22640,3895,'2007-08-01 19:11:59',195,'matt','a more stable lithium level seems to equal - less of an asshole, writing not as good.  not as crisp.  i don\\\'t know.',1,118,195,0,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22641,3895,'0000-00-00 00:00:00',66,'Peter','',1,130,195,1,'24.186.187.5','en'),(22642,3895,'0000-00-00 00:00:00',195,'matt','god, i\'ve been on so many meds.  was on depakote and risperdal (together) ten, eleven years ago.  dep made my hair fall out; they switched to lithium.  risp was phased out in favor of zyprexa.  gained about forty-five pounds from that.  ugh.<BR><BR>i was on both lith and lamictal until about a year ago, at which time they discontinued the lam and also the geodon.  all i take now is lith, wellbutrin, and the thyroid replacement.  things seem to be going well.  i\'ve been doing better than most with my diagnosis.  but, then again, i do a lot of things to supplement the meds:  the right diet, exercise, stress management, yoga, chi gung, nei gung, maintaining a support system.<BR><BR>not to mention abstaining from the things i need to.  not having big wads of cash sitting on the coffee table.  etc.  etc.  ;)<BR><BR>little by little, everything changes....',1,868,0,1,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22643,3895,'0000-00-00 00:00:00',66,'Peter','',1,372,195,1,'24.186.187.5','en'),(22684,4381,'2008-03-28 20:20:37',195,'matt','hey.  nobody told me that shit was toxic.  fuckers.<BR><BR>i wouldn\\\'t recommend <u>any</u> of them.  they\\\'re all horrid.  you start using them, and you\\\'re brain gets addicted.<BR><BR>if i had the means, i would look into some sort of alternative therapy.  that being said, your practitioner may still recommend that you get a psychiatrist and take the meds for a little while with an eye towards weaning you off of them eventually.<BR><BR>it\\\'s a long road, but you can get better if you want to.  some people don\\\'t want to.',1,538,0,0,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22644,3895,'0000-00-00 00:00:00',195,'matt','you have episodes?',1,18,66,1,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22645,4250,'2007-08-08 06:16:43',496,'chemicalpilate','The feeling I get that scares me the most is that there is nothing in the queue.',1,80,0,0,'69.17.112.117','en'),(22646,4250,'2007-08-11 04:11:15',538,'arcy','Well, tonight the Xbox caught the notorious red ring hardware failure notification. Maybe it was sending me distress calls during the week?',0,139,0,0,'74.123.71.194','en'),(22647,4250,'0000-00-00 00:00:00',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i went for a walk last night, and stopped by the bodega on the way home to pick up a couple of 32\'s of tecate. got home, unbagged the brews, and went to stick them in the fridge. i had one by the neck in my left hand, and used said clumsily full hand to open the fridge. i thought <i>\"geez, that would suck if i dropped this shit\"</i> and, of course, immediately dropped it, sending a flood of beerborne shattered glass all over my floor. it was as if i were watching it fall and shatter on the cement floor in slow-motion... thinking <i>\"yep, of course i just dropped it...\"</i> then i stepped on some glass and cut my foot.<br><br>sadly, this is about as intuitive as ive been in ages...</tt>',1,524,0,0,'24.186.187.5','en'),(22648,4250,'2007-08-16 15:55:44',538,'arcy','A couple of weeks ago, I did the same thing. I was parked and about to get out of my car, when I stopped to fish around in my bag for something. When I found it I hopped out and turned to close the door. In true David Cross form I realized that I was about to lock my keys in and me out. I was aware as my hand pushed against the door in that quick moment what I was doing but there was nothing to be done. ',0,407,0,0,'216.191.144.135','en'),(22649,2927,'2007-08-23 04:14:18',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i look back, reading this, and feel like such a different person, more than five years later. i dont think i could write like this again if i tried...</tt>',1,163,66,0,'24.186.187.5','en'),(22683,4383,'2008-03-26 13:50:27',66,'Peter','<br><tt>your morning routine sounds very similar to mine, particularly the long commute full of annoying spacey workbots. i definitely empathize...</tt>',1,152,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22650,3654,'2007-09-06 23:10:42',59,'s5','she\\\'s still a brat.',5,22,0,0,'74.95.2.241','en'),(22651,3868,'2007-09-15 17:01:20',829,'m3ub5','<A delightful variety of commenting However the descriptive words are much too factual and should be hidden>',0,108,0,0,'198.111.161.34','en'),(22652,3868,'2007-09-15 17:02:44',829,'m3ub5','In an effort to address the disease portion of the verse, I would suggest an expression of your heart\\\'s greatest desire at the local Bakery.',0,143,0,0,'198.111.161.34','en'),(22653,4045,'2007-09-15 17:05:03',829,'m3ub5','The 3-Year old from Cadillac Cemetery\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nI\\\'m sure she doesn\\\'t want to be there,\r\nShe likes my chair.\r\n\r\nSomeday soon she\\\'ll\r\nsee the moon\r\n\r\nAgain my chair will be mine again.',0,188,0,0,'198.111.161.34','en'),(22654,4308,'2007-09-18 17:42:42',122,'hool','i enjoyed this and i\\\'m glad to see your writing here again.  i re-read your old swintries from time to time.',0,111,0,0,'74.100.11.231','en'),(22655,4308,'2007-09-19 06:05:26',72,'essemar','Thanks.  I always love complements.  :)\r\n<br><br>\r\nYeah... it\\\'s gotten pretty barren around here these last many years.  In 2001, I used to spend over an hour a day keeping up on everything here.  Now... well, it doesn\\\'t take as much effort.<br><br>I guess when everyone started disappearing, it stopped being as much fun.  I do miss having an intelligent, supportive venue for creative output though.',0,407,122,0,'72.93.180.237','en'),(22656,4308,'2007-09-25 04:31:08',496,'chemicalpilate','We\\\'re here.  Just...searching for inspiration, maybe.',1,56,0,0,'69.17.112.117','en'),(22657,4326,'2007-10-19 22:55:57',90,'leonard','i already printed this out and my dog peed on it.',1,49,0,0,'67.120.31.137','en'),(22658,4326,'2007-10-24 00:36:53',175,'Paul G','Maybe, he loves you so much that he peed on the sofa BEFORE it burst into flame.  That is the type of thing that Lassie would do...\r\n<p>\r\nI have visions of Buckminster in a harness, suspended from a helicopter, fighting the conflagration.  The whole of San Diego is saved.  Hero.\r\n</p>',1,285,0,0,'76.210.9.92','en'),(22659,4340,'2007-10-25 22:23:34',195,'matt','<i>you wanna call me up?<BR>take my number down:<BR>it\\\'s two two two<BR>two two two two<BR>i got an answering machine<BR>that can talk to you.</i><BR><BR>this summer, when my cousin chris was staying with me, apt to roll in around four ay em loaded like the last freight train out of saigon, the phone rang one morning at seven pretty much on the dot, maybe one or two minutes after.  the caller i.d. read \\\"(727) 397-7777.\\\"<BR><BR>a very officious-sounding woman said, \\\"good morning.  did somebody call me from this number last night?\\\"<BR><BR>i said, \\\"i don\\\'t know.\\\"<BR><BR>and she said, \\\"well, i don\\\'t know, either.\\\"<BR><BR>and i said, \\\"i guess it\\\'s one of the great mysteries of our time.  see ya.\\\"  and hung up.<BR><BR>i thought, <i>this is just one more thing i need - him coming in drunk and dialing some monkeyass long-distance numbers.</i><BR><BR>i disconnected the phone recently.  i\\\'m loving it.  my number was (727) 236-9147.  i thought that was pretty cool - mnemonic as hell.  i almost kept the phone on just cause i wanted to keep that fuckin\\\' number.',1,1112,0,0,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22660,4339,'0000-00-00 00:00:00',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i hate to sound pedantic... but that second line only has 6 syllables.<br><br>i must admit it, im a haiku syllable-counter...</tt>',1,83,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22661,3715,'0000-00-00 00:00:00',66,'Peter','',1,87,0,1,'24.186.187.5','en'),(22662,3916,'0000-00-00 00:00:00',66,'Peter','',1,70,0,1,'24.186.187.5','en'),(22663,4339,'2007-11-13 18:08:06',210,'may','yeah...this is the first verse of a rengay that i wrote with two other english instructors. because that\\\'s what we do instead of writing papers, i guess.',1,156,66,0,'72.33.76.225','en'),(22664,4339,'2007-11-14 00:45:02',175,'Paul G','Garry Gay\\\'s <a href=\\\"http://www.brooksbookshaiku.com/ggayweb/rengay.html\\\">rengay</a>...',1,96,0,0,'76.209.241.93','en'),(22665,4344,'0000-00-00 00:00:00',66,'Peter','<br><tt>#4 makes my brain hurt... and on so many levels.<br><br>i mean, with the 7-tile maximum, someone would have to play something like <i>less</i> then add <i>regard</i> to that, then add <i>ir</i> to that, and thats assuming <i>irregardless</i> is even a word and... <br><Br><b>*head explodes*</b>...',1,301,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22666,4344,'2007-11-15 22:24:57',195,'matt','i have no idea.  i pulled \\\"seventy-eight\\\" out of my ass.',1,62,66,0,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22667,4344,'2007-11-15 22:25:56',195,'matt','(i don\\\'t play much scrabble.)',1,32,66,0,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22668,4341,'2007-11-22 12:26:04',93,'chiamattt','Thanks for the laugh. \r\n\r\nI have been editing a document on Solid Waste Management in Punjab, Pakistan for the past couple of weeks (btw your story has more to do with sludge and sewage, not solid waste), and I needed a chuckle. The document is dry, very long, and horribly written. \r\n\r\nIn the end it will be read by an administrator in Punjab, thrown away, and recycled or incinerated in one of their fancy new Solid Waste handling facilities. ',1,445,0,0,'58.230.166.31','en'),(22669,4344,'2007-12-10 20:22:03',697,'keuch2','mick jagger is, secretly, a duck sucker...',1,42,0,0,'200.61.226.190','en'),(22670,4344,'2007-12-10 22:04:34',195,'matt','not so secretly, apparently.<BR><BR>or at least he claims he can.',1,65,697,0,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22671,3931,'2007-12-12 01:31:37',17,'austin','shut it.\r\n<HR>\r\n[this is good]',1,30,17,0,'206.251.248.21','en'),(22672,4350,'2007-12-19 07:45:54',120,'joyrider','i\\\'m blessed to live in humboldt.  the stump of an ancient giant is right outside my window.  new redwoods grow from it\\\'s burls, and a sitka spruce and huckleberry bush share the space at the top of the stump.  <i>sempervirens</i> is a good name for coast redwoods.',0,270,0,0,'75.111.37.204','en'),(22673,3260,'2008-01-15 06:16:05',175,'Paul G','Yeah, ok, so it is 2008 now.  And Bush did serve two terms.  And there\\\'s another election looming.\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'m wondering, as I\\\'ve been wondering for a while now, if the Left was sort of fucked-up when it was out in the street, would the Right have been any better?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI mean, if you talked to someone carrying a FAGS - REPENT! sign, do you suppose that they\\\'d be able to present you with a logical argument when pressed for it?  Or, would their position, ultimately, be grounded upon a story that they had been told, and repeated, but did not understand?\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\n1970\\\'s: I remember being on vacation with my Mom and sisters in Wisconsin.  And there was an oil embargo.  We were stuck.  And it was like Mad Max -- long lines of cars, waiting for the tanker to arrive.  As a kid, scared the shit out of me.  Yep.  Jimmy Carter came on television and told us that it would be OK.  We\\\'d live in solar-powered windmills or some shit.  Haha.  Carter-Reagan-Bush-Clinton-Bush, no solar-powered windmill shit to be seen.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nNope.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nI\\\'m thinking, now, like, what would be the consequences of nobody protesting nothing -- no matter how bad it was.  And then I\\\'m like, \\\"Hey, we do kidnapping, and torture in secret prisons and shit.\\\"  And people basically don\\\'t say shit.  Not even disorganized people.  What sort of a country is that?\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nYou\\\'re all like, \\\"Well, the poets and the artists and the Starbuck\\\'s employees can\\\'t get their act together -- so fuck them.\\\"  I mean, what?  Huh?  Like if the least-powerful and most idealistic citizens can\\\'t GET HARD then fuck them?  What?\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nI like watching my animals, and reading my books, and growing my flowers and vegetables, and making my sculpture -- so unless I can FORCE the people driving H2\\\'s and toting guns to listen and be all, \\\"Wow man save the dolphins,\\\" I suck?\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nHELP.  OK?  You\\\'re smart.  What are you doing, now?  How are you changinf things?  They\\\'re going to install Romney I or Clinton II.  HELP.  None of this, \\\"This is a part of the World where they understand force,\\\" bullshit.  Because I don\\\'t know anywhere that force isn\\\'t understood.  And we seemed to pay a lot more attention to people after they blowed us up.  So what does that say about us?  And stuff.  And shit.  And so what if I\\\'m totally high.\r\n</p>',6,2407,0,0,'76.209.246.24','en'),(22674,4377,'2008-01-15 22:34:22',538,'arcy','I enjoyed reading this from the artificial shelter of a three-sided cubicle. ',2,77,0,0,'216.191.144.135','en'),(22675,3260,'2008-01-19 08:19:31',175,'Paul G','But, seriously: You\\\'ve identified the problem as a lack of voter participation in the electoral process -- even as you\\\'ve called those same voters, \\\"stupid.\\\"  So the trick to improving our government is involving more dumb people in the selection of our leaders?\r\n<p>\r\nBut, wait: It\\\'s not that the people are, really, stupid.  They\\\'re just \\\"picky consumers who are not satisfied with the inherent compromise of democracy.\\\"  Though having written that, while the marchers are wrong to have given up on the candidates presented to them, you\\\'re right to have given up on the marchers and public protest.  It\\\'s like, \\\"shopping for shoes, expecting to get exactly the pair [you] want.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIs there any relationship between the act of public protest and voting?  The Framers of the Constitution seemed to think so when they bundled the rights contained in the First Amendment: \\\"Congress shall make no law respecting an establishment of religion, or prohibiting the free exercise thereof; or abridging the freedom of speech, or of the press; or the right of the people peaceably to assemble, and to petition the government for a redress of grievances.\\\" \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe idea is that for a vaguely-democratic government to function there must be a lively, public exchange of ideas.  Not everyone is going to be a great orator or scribe.  No, some people will be hard-pressed just to schlep down to the town square and make noise.  That\\\'s OK.  Street-level: If you\\\'re being hurt, make noise.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhat is sick and deadly for a system such as out own is silence.  How dare you chastise activists -- and then rationalize your own inaction.  Hypocrisy?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThings are broken.  You know that Nixon wasn\\\'t voted out of office -- he resigned after being caught attempting to tamper with the electoral process!  If JFK was elected, he was killed.  If RFK looked like he was going to be elected, he was killed.  What about Carter and the hostages in Iran?  Do you remember the end of that Presidency?  The release of those same hostages and the Iran-Contra Affair after Reagan was elected?  What about Bush v. Gore being decided in our Supreme Court?  On and on.  The past 50 years haven\\\'t been so good for American democracy.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNow, the Nevada Supreme Court upholds MSNBC\\\'s right to exclude Dennis Kucinich from the Democratic debates in Nevada?  FOX excludes Ron Paul?  Why do private corporations have the ability to frame the choices presented to the people?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIf you have the ability to organize and inform large groups of people as the result of some prior work experience, why not apply that ability to the movement for change?\r\n</p>',5,2724,99,0,'76.209.60.56','en'),(22676,3052,'2008-01-23 23:17:44',183,'lin','Maybe I\\\'ll print this post and put it on linus van pelt\\\'s pillow. It\\\'s been a while since I thought about this, but it\\\'s still relevent\r\n\r\nI wonder if you are feeling more or less optimistic now that you have completed your law degree and have made your way into another equally socially challenging professional environment.\r\n\r\nThough I think I would revise my earlier post. Given the last few equalizing years. Everything social is economic to a poor student, but now the more I read and think about this post, the more I think about your questions about community and group identifiers.\r\n\r\nI\\\'ve no concrete answers for you, unfortunately, but my insights so far are that people band together (in twos, and then in larger couplings, and further into clusters) in society for the same reason they do so in highschool.. it\\\'s like a banding together of ideals... similar to what we\\\'re all doing, writing here and reading her. I don\\\'t know that this is necessarily bad. Just like girls only gyms are not \\\"bad\\\"... they respond to this clustering, a banding together of women who have a certain desire, to work out, not be gawked at.. or at least not by their male counterparts..\r\n\r\nI suppose my thoughts about individualism settle on the fact that it is DIFFICULT, and therefore not very many people can maintain their own self-image without a mirror of community members (or significant others?) to reinforce it. And those who perservere in this regard, are these people simply part of another community, a somewhat less tangible community of people who simply know each other by a certain strength of character? I think most people who are judging by community groups and generalizations (I\\\'m guilty of this from time to time) are looking for answers, and probably pretending or going about their lives as if they had found them... \r\n\r\nbeware of absolutes\r\n\r\n',3,1891,176,0,'99.239.214.191','en'),(22677,4378,'2008-01-28 22:09:08',900,'polished','Not sure \\\'romance\\\' was really what I was going for, but if thats what was illustrated then sure. ',0,103,0,0,'216.191.144.135','en'),(22678,4378,'2008-01-28 23:30:29',195,'matt','at least they didn\\\'t pick \\\"gay\\\".  that was a popular move for a while there.',1,85,900,0,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22679,4378,'2008-01-29 00:27:04',66,'Peter','<br><tt>what would you prefer? it can be changed. its sort of, well, up for grabs when you dont choose a category for yourself when you submit it...<br><br><i>*best friendly*</i></tt>',1,183,900,0,'70.107.213.240','en'),(22680,4378,'2008-01-29 18:42:17',195,'matt','but now that i think about it, nobody\\\'s pussy was hurting about that but mine....',1,84,195,0,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22681,4378,'2008-01-31 18:03:40',900,'polished','I\\\'m not stressing about it, just thought it was funny. Romance can stay.',0,75,0,0,'216.191.144.135','en'),(22682,4381,'0000-00-00 00:00:00',66,'Peter','<br><tt>hey man. i was going to say something like <i>\"how you been?\"</i>, but after reading this entry, that sounds like a rather cloying question, eh?<br><br>they say that needles also offer a better alternative to cutting. less scarring for later, when you get balanced and regret mutilating yourself.<br><Br>all that being said... what have you been up to the past several years leading up to this?',2,973,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22687,4385,'2008-04-03 13:06:05',66,'Peter','<br><tt>doesnt beer repel aphids? or maybe it was slugs. yeah, i think slugs. good thing we dont have those around here; nothing\\\'s worse than stepping on a slug while barefooted...</tt>',1,188,0,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22688,4385,'2008-04-06 03:17:57',57,'weeb','i\\\'ve drank more in the last two weeks than the previous two months. i think i\\\'m pulling out of it now, though. \r\n<br><br>\r\ngood luck on your wagon ride, senor roses. \r\n<br><br>\r\n',0,184,0,0,'66.93.131.112','en'),(22689,3972,'2008-04-06 03:21:20',57,'weeb','this is a really good poem. i want to paint it in big strokes on my wall.',0,73,0,0,'66.93.131.112','en'),(22685,4381,'0000-00-00 00:00:00',72,'essemar','<p>wow...</p><p>\r\n\r\nThanks for the concern, but you should really notice that I didn\'t mark the article as non-fiction. Yes, based somewhat on personal experience, but it was mostly me being in a dark mood one night and channeling all the emo angst of a thousand 16-year olds.\r\n</p><P>\r\nWell... it was also an extension of an ongoing argument regarding why people cut themselves. People who have never been seriously depressed can\'t understand it. (Heck, I don\'t think people who do it even understand it.) They make up some bullshit that makes sense to them - explains it in a context they understand - but ultimately get it wrong. The truth is more complex and counter-intuitive.\r\n</p><P>\r\nAnyways, for the record: I have never cut myself - it hurts and I\'m a wimp (although I gotta say that needle idea is absolutely brilliant). I\'m taking Lamictal and feeling much happier and more stable than I ever did in 2000 when I was writing on here a lot. I still think it\'s fun to be incredibly dark and depressive sometimes. I am a wannabe goth.</p><br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nOh... and yes, lithium is very toxic if you take too much, but it was the only effective treatment for bipolar until recently. My shrink does in fact liken Bipolar Disorder to diabetes or any other physiological defect with which one is born because that is what it is. It took me a couple years, but I\'ve come to accept the truth of that statement. My-life-is-falling-to-shit depression and bipolar depression are two very different things. Trust me; I\'ve experienced both. I currently live in Boston. I\'m taking classes and dancing on my school\'s ballroom dance team. I am not a monster. I am a human being.',0,1701,0,1,'71.126.233.247','en'),(22686,4381,'2008-04-02 07:38:28',72,'essemar','<p>wow...</p><p>\r\n\r\nThanks for the concern, but you should really notice that I didn\\\'t mark the article as non-fiction. Yes, based somewhat on personal experience, but it was mostly me being in a dark mood one night and channeling all the emo angst of a thousand 16-year olds.\r\n</p><P>\r\nWell... it was also an extension of an ongoing argument regarding why people cut themselves. People who have never been seriously depressed can\\\'t understand it. (Heck, I don\\\'t think people who do it even understand it.) They make up some bullshit that makes sense to them - explains it in a context they understand - but ultimately get it wrong. The truth is more complex and counter-intuitive.\r\n</p><P>\r\nAnyways, for the record: I have never cut myself - it hurts and I\\\'m a wimp (although I gotta say that needle idea is absolutely brilliant). I\\\'m taking Lamictal and feeling much happier and more stable than I ever did in 2000 when I was writing on here a lot. I still think it\\\'s fun to be incredibly dark and depressive sometimes. I am a wannabe goth.</p><br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nOh... and yes, lithium is very toxic if you take too much, but it was the only effective treatment for bipolar until recently. My shrink does in fact liken Bipolar Disorder to diabetes or any other physiological defect with which one is born because that is what it is. It took me a couple years, but I\\\'ve come to accept the truth of that statement. My-life-is-falling-to-shit depression and bipolar depression are two very different things. Trust me; I\\\'ve experienced both. I currently live in Boston. I\\\'m taking classes and dancing on my school\\\'s ballroom dance team. I am not a monster. I am a human being.',0,1701,0,0,'71.126.233.247','en'),(22690,3754,'2008-04-07 01:37:13',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this reminds me of something i read recently about exponential series, computing power, and singularity.</tt>',1,117,0,0,'70.107.184.37','en'),(22691,4385,'2008-04-08 18:40:46',175,'Paul G','Slug Trap -- Having turned it top side down, a jelly jar lid was pressed into the soil till flush with the surrounding surface.  Then it was filled with beer.\r\n<p>\r\nThe beer drew the slugs to the trap.  And drinking, they fell in and were drowned.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere\\\'s some metaphor or simile waiting to be suggested -- some reference to real versus apparent goods, or maybe an allusion to sex and death as engines or ends.  But Austin\\\'s not a slug.  OR IS HE?\r\n</p>',1,471,0,0,'76.209.59.39','en'),(22692,4384,'2008-04-08 18:45:42',175,'Paul G','This felt like a piece written for swinney.org 5+ years ago.  Tech-work.  Kittens.  Dreams.  Yep.\r\n<p>\r\nMaybe if Elly still read the site she\\\'d add this, and Austin\\\'s piece, to her \\\"Dreams\\\" series.\r\n</p>',1,215,0,0,'76.209.59.39','en'),(22693,4384,'2008-04-11 19:05:23',195,'matt','i bet elly still reads the site.  put up something like \\\"ELLY EATS BOOGERS\\\", and see how long it takes her to issue a comment on it.',1,138,175,0,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22694,4384,'2008-04-12 16:52:41',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i like kittens.</tt>',1,28,0,0,'70.107.184.37','en'),(22695,4384,'2008-04-12 16:53:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i also like limes.</tt>',1,31,0,0,'70.107.184.37','en'),(22696,4384,'2008-04-14 15:30:37',195,'matt','i like big butts.',1,17,0,0,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22697,4384,'2008-04-14 15:30:55',195,'matt','and i cannot lie.',1,17,0,0,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22698,4385,'2008-04-15 02:00:23',538,'arcy','I have a slug hole in the basement next to my closet. Whenever it rains heavily, one will appear and leave a shiny trail across the berber. ',0,140,0,0,'99.253.252.130','en'),(22699,4385,'2008-04-15 04:38:46',66,'Peter','<br><tt><img src=\\\"http://www.northcoastjournal.com/091307/SCI-Courting-slugs.jpg\\\"><br><br>\r\njust for the record, i think thats two banana slugs fucking.</tt>',1,163,0,0,'96.224.103.164','en'),(22700,4385,'2008-04-15 23:42:59',17,'austin','non-drinking experiment is going well.  consumption is down significantly.\r\n',1,76,0,0,'206.251.240.89','en'),(22701,4387,'2008-04-16 04:42:53',17,'austin','Good one.',1,9,0,0,'68.101.205.156','en'),(22702,4387,'2008-04-16 08:37:07',234,'etienne','oh man. i wanna go back to nyc.',1,31,0,0,'121.98.132.68','en'),(22703,4387,'2008-04-16 18:36:45',66,'Peter','<br><tt>you should! maybe theyll let you drive the subway over the brooklyn bridge again :)</tt>',1,96,234,0,'130.219.8.253','en'),(22704,4387,'2008-04-20 20:04:03',496,'chemicalpilate','<p>Every single day that we do something, we are increasing production.  Like measuring basal metabolic rate, <em>not</em> creating is far easier said than done.</p><p>This, however, is a spike in the FFT.</p>',1,209,0,0,'137.131.205.145','en'),(22705,4387,'2008-05-03 12:15:08',122,'hool','heh. this made me laugh. ',1,25,0,0,'85.178.41.94','en'),(22706,4385,'2008-06-05 22:23:13',17,'austin','Thu Jun  5 15:21:37 PDT 2008\r\n\r\ndoing ok with the experament.  bought a bicycle.  riding it 23 miles one way to work then back a couple days a week.  drinking less days a week.  feeling good about things.',1,204,0,0,'206.251.240.89','en'),(22707,3952,'2008-06-13 17:49:23',195,'matt','i\\\'m 95% sure that he\\\'d never heard of enb.  that\\\'s just the best way i could describe what his licks sounded like.',1,123,66,0,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22708,4388,'2008-07-11 02:49:00',66,'Peter','<br><tt>so how humid is it in florida these days?<br><br>there used to be a crazy guy on cn named jesse. reading this post made me think of him. he used to email me unsolicited over-photoshop-filtered jpgs of his nude body with his frightening poetry typed over it. come to think of it, ive known a few <i>jesses</i> in my time, and theyve all been exceptionally eccentric at best and fascinatingly damaged at worst. how doesw your jesse rate on that scale?<br><br>\r\noh and what prompted me to comment on this was that, as i was reading it, <a href=\\\"http://saturn5.com/peter/music/Needle%20in%20the%20Camel\\\'s%20Eye.mp3\\\">this song</a> was randomply playing on my itues... how are you cutting up the samples? that sounds really interesting.</tt>',1,752,0,0,'24.188.229.135','en'),(22709,4388,'2008-07-11 04:20:02',195,'matt','it\\\'s almost midnight in brooksville.  i just stepped out on the deck for a smoke, and the humidity is hovering around five hundred percent.  the weed almost wouldn\\\'t burn.<BR><BR>jesse\\\'s 20 and still fairly intact, which is pretty amazing considering he\\\'s surrounded by junkies.  active ones and a couple on parole.  his sister\\\'s entire salary goes in her arm.  luckily, the ones he lives with, his mother and brother, both got out of prison recently and are managing to stay clean.  (mostly by drinking like bastards.)<BR><BR>these days (all along really, but moreso now that he sleeps over in the office occaisonally) i\\\'m trying to figure out if i should make a move on him.  it seems like one of the vilest epithets he knows is \\\"gay\\\".  everything lame is gay.  he acts homophobic (to the extent that i had somebody else cut the solo out of the song for me before i played it for him so i wouldn\\\'t have to explain about brian eno - <i>he was just cross-dressing.  he has a wife and kids.</i>  - and even then had to flange it out because it sounded too GAY.), but he is forever doubling up on some girl with robbie and giving me these looks and likes to wrestle around with me, so i just do not know.  i don\\\'t want to grab it one night and have everything turn to shit.<BR><BR>we use fl studio and pro tools.  his brother\\\'s friend trina owns the copy of pro tools, so we have to go over to hers to work with it.  mostly they do the hands-on stuff.  i could if i absolutely had to, but the lithium makes it hard to concentrate and learn new skills.',1,1582,66,0,'68.214.125.150','en'),(22710,4389,'2008-07-12 05:46:23',195,'matt','am i mistaken, or is this part of a series?',1,43,0,0,'68.220.131.74','en'),(22711,4389,'2008-07-15 04:08:26',66,'Peter','<br><tt>probably!</tt>',1,22,195,0,'24.188.229.135','en'),(22712,4386,'2008-07-16 01:01:33',66,'Peter','<br><tt>im quite fond of the way the line <i>\\\"my Stravinsky road overexposed while Sagittarius unclothes...\\\"</i> sounds when read aloud...</tt>',1,149,0,0,'24.188.229.135','en'),(22713,4386,'2008-07-16 18:37:56',195,'matt','never underestimate the power of a little hot glucose.',1,54,66,0,'64.128.136.170','en'),(22714,4397,'2008-09-15 18:05:18',195,'matt','or disillusioned.',1,17,0,0,'24.173.124.66','en'),(22715,3537,'2008-09-16 22:46:29',477,'jehannum','it\\\'s also some very fond memories. four years on and lucky to be alive...',1,76,0,0,'203.214.150.236','en'),(22716,3366,'2008-09-16 23:06:46',477,'jehannum','wow, i\\\'d almost forgotten about this one.',1,44,0,0,'203.214.150.236','en'),(22717,3351,'2008-09-16 23:21:55',477,'jehannum','four years on and i now live in a suburb of sydney called crows nest. i\\\'ve been in my apartment for 14 months, longer than i\\\'ve lived in any one place since i left home at 17.\r\nthe weekend i moved in here there was a murder of crows circling the building, all cawing to one-another.\r\nit may sound cheesy, but it gave me comfort, like the demons of my childhood coming back to give me new strength.\r\nsince then i have felt nothing but safe here.',1,450,0,0,'203.214.150.236','en'),(22718,3325,'2008-09-16 23:36:39',477,'jehannum','nearly five years on it\\\'s ironic to look back knowing that i had a tumor the size of a golf ball \\\'living inside the four walls of my skull\\\'. weird.',0,156,0,0,'203.214.150.236','en'),(22719,4397,'2008-09-17 00:31:08',122,'hool','<p>hey peter, i liked this.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it reminded me of a reoccurring thought i have, when i pass by a place i used to live, or frequent, many years ago.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i think back to when i lived there, if i could imagine from that point where i am now.  usually i can\\\'t.  so then i think about where i am now, and where i will be, and nothing comes to mind but the feeling of distance, vague but satisfactory.</p>',0,414,0,0,'99.254.33.115','en'),(22720,4399,'2008-09-19 17:56:45',741,'mute','(on lj, elaine asked what this was.)<BR><BR>notes on process:  this is extracted from the index of the norton anthology of american literature (volume 2, 3rd edition).  i noticed that there were no author names beginning with \\\"Y\\\" and that the titles and first lines in that section made somewhat of a poem when strung together.  i picked out the ones i liked (mostly all first lines of poems) and, finding the result too short, cast around for more material.  as most of the lines began with \\\"you\\\", i looked in the \\\"I\\\" section and found it to be similarly devoid of author names.  selected my faves from there and then grouped them in stanzas and sections.<br><br>i left them in alphabetical order and tried to select lines with an eye toward groupings of theme.  (to the extent that \\\"theme\\\" can be imposed on a collection of random words.)  i have read many of these poems at one time or another but recognized only \\\"howl\\\" (<i>i saw the best minds of my generation destroyed</i>) and the one pound title (<i>in the metro</i>).<br><br>i then posted the result and only then saw that it could (albeit in some fairly tangential ways) apply to david foster wallace.  what really weirded me out a little was going back later and reading all the poems.  i should mark it up, dfw style, because some of the connections one could make are truly eerie.  there are quite a few writers here who killed themselves - plath, hart crane, john berryman, probably others whose biographies i don\\\'t know as well - but that part is highly coincidental.  what got to me were the poems like <a href=\\\"http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/I_walked_in_a_desert\\\">this</a> and <a href=\\\"http://www.bartleby.com/84/207.html\\\">this</a> and <a href=\\\"http://famouspoetsandpoems.com/poets/philip_levine/poems/19027\\\">this last one</a>.  but that\\\'s the thing about divinitory writing, visionary writing, whatever - sometimes there are many happy accidents, but you could take that same set of words and conform it to whatever subject or question you ask and make whatever meaning you want out of the miasma.  i don\\\'t know if it\\\'s good or if i\\\'m just high',1,2175,0,0,'70.152.55.23','en'),(22721,4399,'2008-09-20 04:41:15',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>your mind and you are our sargasso sea...</i></tt>',1,61,741,0,'24.186.185.18','en'),(22722,4400,'2008-09-30 17:03:23',195,'matt','i\\\'ve noticed that, too - milk going bad two or three days after i\\\'ve bought it.  i blamed it on the fridge and the summer.<BR><BR>when i got my stamps ($106) this month, i hit the grocery store as soon as they opened and paid sixty-five bucks for less than a week\\\'s worth of food, which represents about a 30% increase over the last six months.  i told the cashier, a middle-aged lady, \\\"i think people are going to start to revolt soon.\\\"  she said, \\\"i think i\\\'m going to be one of them.\\\"<BR><BR>(got a letter in the middle of the month that said the stamps will increase to $124 for october.  i hope they know that if the poor start to organize themselves, they are going to have some real problems.  this wall street shit will be nothing.)',1,764,0,0,'24.173.124.66','en'),(22723,4400,'2008-10-01 00:19:19',66,'Peter','<br><tt>funny how the market just crashed 777 points. when the end-times eventually come, im sure we\\\'ll all look back and laugh about how good we had it now with our expensive bread.</tt>',1,190,195,0,'24.185.248.176','en'),(22724,4400,'2008-11-04 18:10:59',17,'austin','that is raw boned.',1,18,0,0,'160.33.43.231','en'),(22725,4405,'0000-00-00 00:00:00',66,'Peter','<br><tt>its funny what a slight misreading can do:<br><br>\r\n\r\n<i>\"I\'ll still make out with a handful of clams from the practice, but no big score that night. But I\'m usually able to slip out with the goods.</i> ',1,236,0,0,'24.188.46.194','en'),(22726,4404,'0000-00-00 00:00:00',66,'Peter','<br><tt>\r\nwith your hands full of clams,<br> \r\ni am able to slip out<br><br>\r\n\r\nwith the goods<br>\r\n\r\n',1,117,0,0,'24.188.46.194','en'),(22727,4405,'2008-12-22 05:41:11',542,'glen','mmm, clams',3,10,0,0,'64.81.50.177','en'),(22728,4405,'2008-12-22 19:20:45',66,'Peter','<br><tt>indeed, but im not too sure about <i>making out</i> with them...</tt>',1,77,542,0,'130.219.8.250','en'),(22729,4402,'2009-01-06 17:03:22',741,'mute','01.06.09  circles have converged.  epiphany.  blinded by the light.  cut loose like a deuce, another runner in the night.  taking this down.',1,140,0,0,'24.173.124.66','en'),(22730,4400,'2009-01-11 00:13:07',66,'Peter','<br><tt>isnt it a shame that the poor will never organize?</tt>',1,63,195,0,'24.188.46.194','en'),(22731,4400,'2009-01-11 05:17:34',195,'matt','petitio principii.',1,18,66,0,'72.187.21.88','en'),(22732,4400,'2009-01-13 05:02:49',66,'Peter','<br><tt>not in the slightest. times have been worse, and they didnt organize then. so why now?',1,94,195,0,'24.188.46.194','en'),(22733,4400,'2009-01-13 17:10:57',195,'matt','garmonbozia.<BR><BR><img src=\\\"http://www.sanza.co.uk/apps/shop/pics/4066.jpg\\\"/>',1,85,66,0,'72.187.21.88','en'),(22734,4400,'2009-01-13 21:26:05',66,'Peter','<br><tt>do you know who i am? i am the ARM! and  sound like this: <i>WOO WOO WOO WOO WOO WOO WOO</i> ',1,101,195,0,'130.219.8.250','en'),(22735,4406,'0000-00-00 00:00:00',66,'Peter','<br><tt>the story of my life (give or take).</tt>',1,34,0,0,'24.188.46.194','en'),(22736,4406,'2009-01-20 23:54:12',195,'matt','s.o is my free online writing course.<BR><BR>quite a room. ;)',1,61,0,0,'72.187.21.88','en'),(22737,4404,'2009-01-21 17:10:46',122,'hool','this was good. i liked it.',1,26,0,0,'99.232.210.56','en'),(22738,4404,'0000-00-00 00:00:00',239,'Poonlarp','Everything I write is genius. Your most recent was genius too. Swinney.org isn\'t dead, only sleeping. ',6,104,122,0,'24.188.46.194','en'),(22739,4406,'2009-01-21 23:19:50',239,'Poonlarp','Not that it matters at all, but is this a true story? Inquiring minds want to know.',6,83,0,0,'24.188.46.194','en'),(22740,4408,'2009-01-28 14:32:17',195,'matt','i was born a month late.  my mom tells me that stuff, too.<BR><BR>and i\\\'m almost 39 and still have no idea what glass packs are.  i have the vague idea that they\\\'re something to do with mufflers, maybe.',1,208,0,0,'72.187.18.190','en'),(22741,4407,'2009-01-30 16:39:50',626,'catintheattic','that\\\'s it.  that\\\'s the end of the joke.',0,45,0,0,'72.187.18.190','en'),(22742,4407,'2009-02-06 04:06:19',66,'Peter','<br><tt>what joke?</tt>',1,23,626,0,'24.188.46.194','en'),(22743,4407,'2009-02-08 10:27:37',626,'catintheattic','why did the chicken cross the road?  <a href=\\\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Why_did_the_chicken_cross_the_road\\\">to get to the other side.</a>  that\\\'s like a fucking koan, isn\\\'t it?  that\\\'ll keep you up at night thinking about it.<BR><BR>the joke is:<BR>i recently read quite a few of the pieces i\\\'ve posted here and on lj, and after eight years of writing for the internet, i can tell that i\\\'ve grown somewhat as a person, but my development as a writer has been lagging, both in terms of craft and career.  so i\\\'m closing the door on anything which features me as a character.  i\\\'m not sure why i think this will improve the quality.  stay tuned.<BR><BR><blockquote><i>Jack Kerouac was a writer.  That is, he wrote.  Many people who call themselves writers and have their names on books aren’t writers and can’t write — the difference being a bullfighter who fights a bull is different than a bullshitter who makes passes with no bull there.  The writer has been there or he can’t write about it.  And going there he risks being gored.  To write they must go there and submit to conditions they may not have bargained for.  The only real thing about a writer is what he has written, and not his so-called life.</i><p align=right>--William Burroughs</p></blockquote><BR><BR>i was watching <a href=\\\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VGO-YrfLsPQ\\\">this video</a> the other day, and my attention snagged on the phrase \\\"salt of the earth\\\" and how it\\\'s one of those metaphors everybody uses without giving much thought to its meaning.  <a href=\\\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salt_and_Light\\\"><i>one interpretation of </i>salt of the earth<i> is that it orders the audience to take part in the world rather than withdraw from it.</i></a>  and in some crucial ways, that\\\'s what it feels like i\\\'ve been doing for awhile now - hiding what little light i have.<BR><BR>i think that guy sitting behind mick jagger\\\'s right shoulder is the tony that <a href=\\\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanley_Booth\\\">stanley booth</a> speaks of, bodyguard and designated bag man.  he who held the bags.  i guess the salt of the earth is okay just as long as it isn\\\'t parked right up under your ass.<BR><BR><i>we cool we got funny hats 60s crapola</i><BR><BR>ha.',1,2292,66,0,'72.187.18.190','en'),(22744,4408,'2009-02-08 18:16:10',17,'austin','I have a picture of my mom in scanty pajamas smoking a cig somewhere.  It predates my brother and I.  Nice read there sims. ',1,124,0,0,'67.113.125.243','en'),(22745,4406,'2009-02-13 06:55:04',183,'lin','that guys just wanted you to buy him a beer. just noticing. ',3,60,0,0,'24.80.239.46','en'),(22746,4409,'2009-02-18 16:08:04',66,'Peter','<br><tt>glad you want to start writing here again; maybe i will too...</tt>',1,75,0,0,'130.219.8.250','en'),(22747,4413,'2009-03-09 00:22:44',195,'matt','like, \\\"here, have some of my vintage backwash.\\\"',1,53,0,0,'72.187.31.238','en'),(22748,4385,'2009-03-11 14:50:05',477,'jehannum','sending positive vibes your way my inspirational friend. good luck avoiding the sauce and slaying the slugs.',1,108,0,0,'203.158.60.8','en'),(22749,4385,'2009-03-19 16:24:29',66,'Peter','<br><br>so... how long did the dry-spell last, dude? hahaha...</yy>',1,67,17,0,'130.219.8.250','en'),(22750,4413,'2009-03-23 05:00:15',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this phenomenon occurred again today, but this time i didnt think twice about accepting because it was offered by a tremendously attractive person. hmm. i never realized that my opinion on imbibing second-hand saliva was so subjective. oh, the things we do...</tt>',1,272,0,0,'24.188.46.194','en'),(22751,4416,'2009-04-20 04:54:50',65,'le','i always want  to tell a someone about the moon, too.',2,53,0,0,'76.191.219.159','en'),(22752,4416,'2009-04-22 04:57:53',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i always wondered if they really <i>did</i> land on the moon</tt>...',1,76,0,0,'24.188.46.194','en'),(22753,723,'2009-06-05 17:37:01',17,'austin','Still sucking in 2009.',1,22,0,0,'160.33.43.231','en'),(22754,4417,'2009-06-07 03:41:30',183,'lin','It\\\'s the eerily unsurprised scream that does it, when you hear it and kind of understand that it\\\'s all part of the routine. \r\n\r\nI was moved by this. ',1,155,0,0,'24.80.251.106','en'),(22755,4417,'2009-06-13 17:44:01',195,'matt','carroll, it sounds like this guy might benefit from a good all-around ass beating.  but you intimate that there would be repercussions, no?  what you need is an out-of-towner, somebody to slip up there and waylay him in the laundry room or something.  the unsheen asshashin.  then dip with a quickness, leave no witness.',1,320,0,0,'173.169.128.108','en'),(22756,4417,'0000-00-00 00:00:00',66,'Peter','<br><tt>theres a reason why hes missing some teeth.<br><br>i just saw your comment when i came here to make the comment that almost the exact same story as i posted above is going on again tonight.</tt>',1,206,195,0,'24.188.46.194','en'),(22757,4418,'2009-12-16 21:36:34',17,'austin','I went to Vancouver once.  I saw the police beat up a homeless man in broad daylight.',1,85,0,0,'68.101.206.200','en'),(22758,4419,'2009-12-17 10:10:47',65,'le','i forgot how to add images. or maybe they don\\\'t work. but i uploaded one.',1,76,0,0,'76.191.219.159','en'),(22759,4408,'2009-12-18 07:18:48',65,'le','<3',1,2,0,0,'76.191.219.159','en'),(22760,4397,'2009-12-18 20:40:05',66,'Peter','<br><tt><br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>matt:</b> definitely a lot of that, too. goes without saying.<br><br>\r\n<b>hool:</i> nice thought. i like the idea of distant, vague satisfaction...</tt>\r\n',1,177,0,0,'130.219.8.250','en'),(22761,480,'0000-00-00 00:00:00',982,'gsmjammeerforu','',0,1290,0,1,'64.191.50.99','en'),(22764,765,'2009-12-25 07:06:09',195,'matt','i think that is from Pale Fire, if i am not mistaken.  perhaps i am.',1,68,0,0,'74.179.118.80','en'),(22762,480,'2009-12-25 05:57:15',195,'matt','i\\\'m in the market for a spam jammer.  why was this article blessed with it?  funny.  and, in the above comment, \\\"dick-rode\\\".  hahaha.  s.o is ripe and ready for archaeological perusal.',1,193,0,0,'74.179.118.80','en'),(22763,765,'2009-12-25 07:04:33',195,'matt','If your hair is short and nappy,<BR>Conkaline will make it happy.',1,65,0,0,'74.179.118.80','en'),(22765,4419,'2010-03-30 16:20:58',187,'kandrer','pretty fingers :)\r\n(now i cannot say i care about the content either)',2,69,0,0,'115.179.221.153','en'),(22766,3840,'2010-05-10 09:01:22',65,'le','kandrer! please post a new update!',1,34,0,0,'76.191.219.159','en');
