rist. c-po what what',0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(28121,'2003-02-12 20:06:57',66,'Peter','im not an infidel.',0,'204.168.247.126','en'),(28122,'2003-02-12 20:49:49',175,'Paul G','As much as the Arab people might hate both Hussein and also Bin Laden, it is equally true that they perceive them as having the balls to stand up to the West; so, from that point of view, the two are the same.  And the U.S.A. is regarded as something of a, \\\"bitch-ass,\\\" that acts tough when dealing with a smaller man (Iraq, Serbia) but is timid when dealing with another man it\\\'s own size (China, Russia.)  Long-term, nothing that\\\'s happening now will do anything to end terrorism: the weapon favored by the weak against the strong; and that\\\'s scary.  It would be nice to hear SOMEONE ask, \\\"Why do they hate us?\\\"  But, no, they are simply, \\\"THE EVIL ONES.\\\" ',0,'199.182.166.2','en'),(28123,'2003-02-12 20:50:35',175,'Paul G','I\\\'ll sit quietly and wait to be deported now.',0,'199.182.166.2','en'),(28124,'2003-02-12 20:59:59',370,'seanthecon','I think you are overestimating arab unity. Despite their distrust of America, there is little evidence that points to an arab common front. This is proven with the Iraq-Iran conflict. Iraq-Kuwait war. Arab peace deals with Isreal depsite the continued occupation. The persecution of kurdish people. The hatred between Sunni and Shiite muslims in Iraq. Hatred between Pashtuns and Tajiks in Afghanistan. While they may all have their problems with the U.S. the enenmy of my enemy does not make it my friend. As highlighted with Sadamm and Osama, many have worked for or with the U.S. at some time.',0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(28125,'2003-02-12 21:03:02',370,'biggie clinton','as far as i can see, i ain\\\'t no infidel, but i be into infidelity ',0,'207.6.194.96','en'),(28126,'2003-02-12 21:18:10',175,'Paul G','Dude: The people of Iran are not Arabs; neither are the Kurds; neither are the Afghanis.  Seriously though, don\\\'t underestimate the tremen